export default JSON.parse("{\"lessons\":[{\"id\":\"0.1.1\",\"title\":\"What a market is: an auction\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"A market price is the last price a buyer and a seller agreed on, and it moves when one side is in more of a hurry.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain a market price as the price at which a buyer and a seller last agreed.\",\"Find the price at which the most units change hands in a simple auction.\",\"Predict which way price moves when buyers are more eager than sellers.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.1\",\"check\":[\"q-0.1.1-1\",\"q-0.1.1-2\",\"q-0.1.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.1.2\",\"title\":\"Who trades, and why\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Every trade has someone on the other side, trading for their own reasons and often with better tools than yours.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the main market participants and the reason each one trades.\",\"Explain that every trade has a counterparty with its own horizon and information.\",\"Describe why retail traders usually face better-equipped counterparties.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.2\",\"check\":[\"q-0.1.2-1\",\"q-0.1.2-2\",\"q-0.1.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.1.3\",\"title\":\"A tour of the asset classes\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Stocks, forex, futures, CFDs, bonds and crypto differ in hours, leverage, costs and counterparty; the habits you learn on LABX carry over to all of them.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the main asset classes and one feature that sets each apart.\",\"Compare asset classes by trading hours, typical leverage, costs and gap risk.\",\"Explain which habits transfer from LABX to any instrument and which numbers don't.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.3\",\"check\":[\"q-0.1.3-1\",\"q-0.1.3-2\",\"q-0.1.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.1.4\",\"title\":\"Investing, trading and the benchmark\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Any trading result has to beat buy-and-hold on the same market, after costs, and the more often you trade, the bigger the edge that takes.\",\"objectives\":[\"Distinguish investing from trading by holding period, turnover and source of return.\",\"Compare an active result with the buy-and-hold benchmark on the same market, after costs.\",\"Explain why frequent trading needs a larger edge to beat the benchmark.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.4\",\"check\":[\"q-0.1.4-1\",\"q-0.1.4-2\",\"q-0.1.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.1.5\",\"title\":\"The honest numbers\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Most retail CFD accounts and almost all persistent day traders lose money, and deciding not to trade real money is a valid result of this course.\",\"objectives\":[\"State the share of retail CFD accounts that lose money and where the figure comes from.\",\"Match the most common reasons retail traders fail to the parts of this course that address them.\",\"Explain why deciding not to trade with real money is a valid outcome of the course.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.5\",\"check\":[\"q-0.1.5-1\",\"q-0.1.5-2\",\"q-0.1.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.1.6\",\"title\":\"How this course and the simulator work\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"The simulator runs on fixed rules, grades your decisions and your risk discipline rather than your profit, and labels every method with its evidence grade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe the simulated world: LABX, vUSD, the 10,000.00 account, the spread and the commission.\",\"Tell the three scores apart and name the actions that move each one.\",\"Read an evidence grade and say what it does and doesn't promise.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.1\",\"courseId\":\"0.1.6\",\"check\":[\"q-0.1.6-1\",\"q-0.1.6-2\",\"q-0.1.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.2.1\",\"title\":\"Process versus outcome\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Judge a decision by what you knew and how you reasoned, not by how it turned out.\",\"objectives\":[\"Judge a decision by the information and reasoning available at the time, not by its result.\",\"Place any decision in one of four boxes: good or poor decision, good or bad outcome.\",\"Explain why people still grade decisions by their outcomes, even when told not to.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.2\",\"courseId\":\"0.2.1\",\"check\":[\"q-0.2.1-1\",\"q-0.2.1-2\",\"q-0.2.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.2.2\",\"title\":\"Expected value\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Weigh each outcome by its probability to find what a game returns per play, on average, over many plays.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the expected value of a simple game from its probabilities and payoffs.\",\"Show that a game that wins most of the time can still lose on average.\",\"Explain why expected value describes many plays, not the next one.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.2\",\"courseId\":\"0.2.2\",\"check\":[\"q-0.2.2-1\",\"q-0.2.2-2\",\"q-0.2.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.2.3\",\"title\":\"Variance and streaks\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Results scatter around the expected value, and long losing streaks are a normal part of random sequences.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain variance as the spread of results around the expected value.\",\"Estimate how long losing streaks get over 100 independent trials.\",\"Recognise that a streak on its own is not evidence that something has changed.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.2\",\"courseId\":\"0.2.3\",\"check\":[\"q-0.2.3-1\",\"q-0.2.3-2\",\"q-0.2.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.2.4\",\"title\":\"How many trades before you know?\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A win rate from a few trades is a wide range, so judge methods on hundreds of trades and single trades on their process.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why a win rate measured on 20 trades is a wide range, not a single number.\",\"Estimate how the plausible range narrows as the number of trades grows.\",\"Judge methods on large samples and single trades on their process.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.2\",\"courseId\":\"0.2.4\",\"check\":[\"q-0.2.4-1\",\"q-0.2.4-2\",\"q-0.2.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.2.5\",\"title\":\"Randomness draws patterns\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Coin flips draw trends, ranges and levels, so a pattern earns trust only by beating random data after costs.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe a random walk and why it produces trends, ranges and turning points.\",\"Admit when a chart can't be told apart from a random one.\",\"Ask how often a pattern works compared with random data before trusting it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.2\",\"courseId\":\"0.2.5\",\"check\":[\"q-0.2.5-1\",\"q-0.2.5-2\",\"q-0.2.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"0.2.6\",\"title\":\"Luck or skill?\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"In a large group, luck alone makes stars; skill shows only as a consistent process over many trades, after costs.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate how many people in a large group get impressive results by luck alone.\",\"Explain how showing only the winners makes luck look like skill.\",\"List the evidence that separates skill from luck: process, sample size, results after costs.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Psychology check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"0.2\",\"courseId\":\"0.2.6\",\"check\":[\"q-0.2.6-1\",\"q-0.2.6-2\",\"q-0.2.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.1.2\",\"title\":\"Long and short\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A long buys first and sells later; a short sells first and buys back later. Each opens on one side of the spread and closes on the other.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the P&L of a long and of a short from the entry and exit fills.\",\"Name the price, bid or ask, that opens and closes a long and a short.\",\"Explain why a short's possible loss has no fixed ceiling while a long's is limited to the price falling to zero.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bearish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bearish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.1\",\"courseId\":\"1.1.2\",\"check\":[\"q-1.1.2-1\",\"q-1.1.2-2\",\"q-1.1.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.1.3\",\"title\":\"Which price is \\\"the price\\\"?\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Last, bid, ask and mid are different prices: a chart plots one of them, and your stops trigger on the quote that closes the position.\",\"objectives\":[\"Distinguish last, bid, ask and mid, and say which one a chart is drawn from.\",\"Predict which quote triggers a long's stop and a short's stop.\",\"Explain how a wider spread can trigger a stop while the chart barely moves.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bearish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bearish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.1\",\"courseId\":\"1.1.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.1.3-1\",\"q-1.1.3-2\",\"q-1.1.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.1.4\",\"title\":\"Commission, fees and the cost stack\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Commission comes in several models; with the spread, slippage, financing and fees it forms the cost stack between a gross and a net result.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the commission on a fill under a percentage model, rounded up to the cent.\",\"List the costs that stand between a gross and a net result.\",\"Explain why \\\"zero commission\\\" doesn't mean zero cost.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.1\",\"courseId\":\"1.1.4\",\"check\":[\"q-1.1.4-1\",\"q-1.1.4-2\",\"q-1.1.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.1.5\",\"title\":\"Round trips and break-even\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A round trip must cover the spread and two commissions before it gains anything, and that cost takes a bigger share of small targets.\",\"objectives\":[\"Find the break-even exit price of a long and of a short, costs included.\",\"Express the round-trip cost as a share of a planned move.\",\"Explain why small targets and frequent trading magnify the share of costs.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.1\",\"courseId\":\"1.1.5\",\"check\":[\"q-1.1.5-1\",\"q-1.1.5-2\",\"q-1.1.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.1.6\",\"title\":\"P&L in ticks, points, pips and units\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Ticks, points and pips count a price move; the position size and the value per point turn it into money and a share of your account.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate P&L as units × price change, in vUSD and as a percentage of the account.\",\"Convert a price change into ticks, points or pips and into money with a value per point.\",\"Explain why the same price move is worth different amounts in different instruments.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.1\",\"courseId\":\"1.1.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.1.6-1\",\"q-1.1.6-2\",\"q-1.1.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.1.7\",\"title\":\"The quote screen and the order ticket\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Read a quote line field by field, tell balance from equity on the account panel, and check the order ticket before you send it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read last, bid, ask, the change and the day's range on a quote line.\",\"Tell balance, equity, realised and unrealised P&L apart on the account panel.\",\"Check side, size, price and stop on a ticket before sending it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.1\",\"courseId\":\"1.1.7\",\"check\":[\"q-1.1.7-1\",\"q-1.1.7-2\",\"q-1.1.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.2.2\",\"title\":\"Line, bar and candle charts\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"The same prices drawn as a line, as OHLC bars and as candles, and what each drawing keeps or drops.\",\"objectives\":[\"Draw the same OHLC data as a line, a bar and a candle.\",\"Identify what each chart type keeps and what it drops.\",\"Choose a chart type for a task: a long-term overview or reading single periods.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M01\",\"label\":\"Read the Candle\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.2\",\"courseId\":\"1.2.2\",\"check\":[\"q-1.2.2-1\",\"q-1.2.2-2\",\"q-1.2.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.2.3\",\"title\":\"Timeframes and aggregation\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"How short candles combine into longer ones, why one market looks different on each timeframe, and how to choose one.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a 4-hour candle from four 1-hour candles.\",\"Explain how the same market can look trending on one timeframe and choppy on another.\",\"Choose a timeframe that matches how long you plan to hold a trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.2\",\"courseId\":\"1.2.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.2.3-1\",\"q-1.2.3-2\",\"q-1.2.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.2.4\",\"title\":\"Volume\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"What volume bars measure, how to compare each one with its average, and why volume never shows direction.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read volume bars under a price chart and compare them with their average.\",\"Explain that volume measures activity, not direction.\",\"Recognise that spot FX and CFD charts usually show tick volume from one broker.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.2\",\"courseId\":\"1.2.4\",\"check\":[\"q-1.2.4-1\",\"q-1.2.4-2\",\"q-1.2.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.2.5\",\"title\":\"Normal candle size and gaps\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"How tall a normal candle is, what a gap is, and why no order can fill at the prices a gap jumps over.\",\"objectives\":[\"Measure a candle's range and the average range of recent candles.\",\"Spot a gap between one candle's close and the next candle's open.\",\"Explain why no order can fill at the prices a gap jumps over.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.2\",\"courseId\":\"1.2.5\",\"check\":[\"q-1.2.5-1\",\"q-1.2.5-2\",\"q-1.2.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.2.6\",\"title\":\"Linear and log scale\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Why a move in percent says more than a move in price, and how a log scale draws equal percentages as equal heights.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compare two price moves in percentage terms rather than in price units.\",\"Explain how a log scale draws equal percentage moves as equal distances.\",\"Choose a log scale for long histories with large price changes.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.2\",\"courseId\":\"1.2.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.2.6-1\",\"q-1.2.6-2\",\"q-1.2.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.2.7\",\"title\":\"What a chart can't tell you\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"What a price chart records, what it leaves out, and how hindsight and hand-picked examples make patterns look surer than they are.\",\"objectives\":[\"List what a price chart records and what it leaves out: who traded, why, the order inside a candle, the future.\",\"Recognise hindsight in chart examples that already show the outcome.\",\"Ask the base-rate question before trusting a pattern seen on a chart.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.2\",\"courseId\":\"1.2.7\",\"check\":[\"q-1.2.7-1\",\"q-1.2.7-2\",\"q-1.2.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.3.3\",\"title\":\"Stop entries and stop-limit orders\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A stop entry waits beyond a level and buys or sells at market once it breaks; a stop-limit adds a price cap that a gap can skip.\",\"objectives\":[\"Place a buy stop above the price to enter only if a level breaks.\",\"Predict where a stop entry fills when price gaps through its trigger.\",\"Explain why a stop-limit can leave you unfilled, or unprotected, in a gap.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.3\",\"courseId\":\"1.3.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.3.3-1\",\"q-1.3.3-2\",\"q-1.3.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.3.4\",\"title\":\"Brackets and OCO orders\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A bracket links a stop and a target so one cancels the other; OCO pairs can also enter a breakout in either direction, as long as nothing is left behind.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe the life of a bracket: entry pending, entry filled, stop and target active, one fills, the other is cancelled.\",\"Set up an OCO pair that enters on a break in either direction.\",\"Spot an orphaned order left behind after a manual exit.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.3\",\"courseId\":\"1.3.4\",\"check\":[\"q-1.3.4-1\",\"q-1.3.4-2\",\"q-1.3.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.3.5\",\"title\":\"Time in force\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Time in force sets how long an order lives, from a single session to until you cancel it, or only for an instant.\",\"objectives\":[\"Choose between day, good-till-cancelled and good-till-date for a resting order.\",\"Predict what IOC and FOK do with an order larger than what's available.\",\"Explain the risk of a forgotten good-till-cancelled order.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.3\",\"courseId\":\"1.3.5\",\"check\":[\"q-1.3.5-1\",\"q-1.3.5-2\",\"q-1.3.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.3.6\",\"title\":\"Fills, partial fills and the queue\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Orders in a book fill by price, then by time, so touching your price isn't a fill, and a large order walks the book to a worse average price.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain price-time priority in an order book.\",\"Predict whether a resting limit order fills when price only touches its level.\",\"Calculate the average price of an order filled across several price levels.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.3\",\"courseId\":\"1.3.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.3.6-1\",\"q-1.3.6-2\",\"q-1.3.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.3.7\",\"title\":\"Slippage and gaps\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Slippage is the distance between the fill you expected and the one you got; a gap through a stop turns 1R into more, and the size decides how much more.\",\"objectives\":[\"Measure slippage as the difference between the expected and the actual fill.\",\"Express a gap-through-stop loss in R.\",\"Size a position so that a plausible gap is still survivable.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.3\",\"courseId\":\"1.3.7\",\"check\":[\"q-1.3.7-1\",\"q-1.3.7-2\",\"q-1.3.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.3.8\",\"title\":\"Trailing stops\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A trailing stop follows price in your favour by a rule set before entry and never moves back; how tight it sits decides whether noise or the trend ends the trade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Move a stop only in the direction that reduces risk.\",\"Compare a tight trail, a wide trail and a structure trail on the same price path.\",\"Explain why a trail tighter than a normal candle tends to exit on noise.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.3\",\"courseId\":\"1.3.8\",\"check\":[\"q-1.3.8-1\",\"q-1.3.8-2\",\"q-1.3.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.4.2\",\"title\":\"R: one unit for every trade\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"R is the loss at the initial stop, costs included; measured in R, trades of any size can be compared.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate 1R from the entry, the stop, the size and the costs.\",\"Express any trade result as an R-multiple.\",\"Explain why R is fixed at entry and doesn't change when a stop is moved.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.4\",\"courseId\":\"1.4.2\",\"check\":[\"q-1.4.2-1\",\"q-1.4.2-2\",\"q-1.4.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.4.6\",\"title\":\"Costs in R and the real break-even\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Costs take a fixed amount per unit, so the tighter the stop, the bigger their share of every R and the higher the real break-even.\",\"objectives\":[\"Express a trade's round-trip cost as a share of its stop distance.\",\"Recalculate the break-even win rate of a 2:1 plan once costs are included.\",\"Explain why tighter stops make costs a larger part of every R.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.4\",\"courseId\":\"1.4.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.4.6-1\",\"q-1.4.6-2\",\"q-1.4.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.4.8\",\"title\":\"Drawdowns: depth, duration and what's normal\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Drawdown is measured from the equity peak; its depth and duration follow from your risk per trade and the losing runs a method normally has.\",\"objectives\":[\"Measure drawdown from the equity peak, not from the starting balance.\",\"Estimate how deep a normal losing run goes at a given risk per trade.\",\"Read an underwater curve for depth and time to recover.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.4\",\"courseId\":\"1.4.8\",\"check\":[\"q-1.4.8-1\",\"q-1.4.8-2\",\"q-1.4.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.4.9\",\"title\":\"Daily loss limits\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A daily loss limit, set before the session, ends the day once the next trade's risk no longer fits, before losses turn into revenge trades.\",\"objectives\":[\"Set a daily loss limit in R and in vUSD before the session starts.\",\"Stop trading for the day when the next trade's risk no longer fits the limit.\",\"Explain why a daily limit protects against the most expensive behaviour after losses.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.4\",\"courseId\":\"1.4.9\",\"check\":[\"q-1.4.9-1\",\"q-1.4.9-2\",\"q-1.4.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.5.1\",\"title\":\"Swing highs and swing lows\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Swing highs and swing lows, marked with one fixed rule, are the turning points of market structure, and each is confirmed only after the candles that follow it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Mark swing highs and swing lows with a fixed rule: a high above the two candles on each side.\",\"Explain why a swing is confirmed only two candles after it forms.\",\"Show how a different rule changes the structure you see.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.5\",\"courseId\":\"1.5.1\",\"check\":[\"q-1.5.1-1\",\"q-1.5.1-2\",\"q-1.5.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.5.3\",\"title\":\"Pullbacks\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A pullback is measured against the leg before it, keeps the trend only while it holds the last higher low, and lets you buy with a closer stop.\",\"objectives\":[\"Measure a pullback as a share of the prior leg and in normal candles.\",\"Tell a pullback that keeps the trend from one that breaks the last higher low.\",\"Explain why entering on a pullback gives a closer stop than chasing the move.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.5\",\"courseId\":\"1.5.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.5.3-1\",\"q-1.5.3-2\",\"q-1.5.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.5.5\",\"title\":\"Round numbers\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Orders cluster at and just beyond round numbers, so a stop belongs beyond the level and the cluster, and the documented effect is real but small.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why orders cluster at and just beyond round numbers.\",\"Place a stop beyond structure rather than exactly at, or just past, a round number.\",\"Describe the size of the documented effect without overstating it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.5\",\"courseId\":\"1.5.5\",\"check\":[\"q-1.5.5-1\",\"q-1.5.5-2\",\"q-1.5.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.5.6\",\"title\":\"Retests, role reversal and failed breakouts\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"After a breakout price often returns to the broken level; a retest that holds and a close back inside look alike at first, so you plan for both.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell a retest that holds from a close back inside that fails.\",\"Explain why old resistance can act as support after a breakout.\",\"Plan your action for both outcomes before the break happens.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.5\",\"courseId\":\"1.5.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.5.6-1\",\"q-1.5.6-2\",\"q-1.5.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.5.7\",\"title\":\"When a trend ends\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"An uptrend's structure breaks at the first close below its last higher low; a lower high and then a lower low confirm a downtrend.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify the first lower low and the first lower high after an uptrend.\",\"Tell a deep pullback from a change in structure.\",\"Stop trading the old direction once the structure has broken.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.5\",\"courseId\":\"1.5.7\",\"check\":[\"q-1.5.7-1\",\"q-1.5.7-2\",\"q-1.5.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.5.8\",\"title\":\"One market, several timeframes\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"The higher timeframe gives the context and the one you trade gives the timing; a 1-hour fall inside a 4-hour uptrend is read as a pullback while it holds.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read the trend on a higher timeframe before acting on a lower one.\",\"Explain a timeframe conflict, such as 4H up and 1H down, as a pullback inside a larger trend.\",\"Avoid switching timeframes until one agrees with your idea.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.5\",\"courseId\":\"1.5.8\",\"check\":[\"q-1.5.8-1\",\"q-1.5.8-2\",\"q-1.5.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.6.2\",\"title\":\"Your plan v1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Write a short, checkable plan v1 with one setup, your risk capital and limits written as numbers.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write a one-page plan v1: market, timeframe, one setup, risk rules, exits and limits.\",\"Set your risk capital and personal limits as numbers the app can check.\",\"Version the plan and change it only through its own rule for changes.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: trending market\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.6\",\"courseId\":\"1.6.2\",\"check\":[\"q-1.6.2-1\",\"q-1.6.2-2\",\"q-1.6.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.6.3\",\"title\":\"The pre-trade checklist\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Run a short yes-or-no checklist before every order, and treat any 'no' as no trade or a smaller size.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run a short go / no-go checklist before every order.\",\"Separate the items the app can verify from those only you can answer.\",\"Treat any 'no' as no trade, or resize within the limit.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"Mission: No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways market\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.6\",\"courseId\":\"1.6.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.6.3-1\",\"q-1.6.3-2\",\"q-1.6.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.6.4\",\"title\":\"The trade journal\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Record every trade and no-trade with its thesis, invalidation, plan check and emotion, written within minutes.\",\"objectives\":[\"Record a trade, or a no-trade, with its thesis, invalidation, plan check and emotion.\",\"Let the simulator fill in the numbers and add the 30-second written part.\",\"Explain why journaling only losers, or days later, distorts the record.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"Mission: No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: trending market\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.6\",\"courseId\":\"1.6.4\",\"check\":[\"q-1.6.4-1\",\"q-1.6.4-2\",\"q-1.6.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.6.6\",\"title\":\"Mistake or variance?\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Sort each loss into variance or a mistake, tag the mistakes, and change rules only for mistakes.\",\"objectives\":[\"Sort a losing trade into variance (a valid trade that lost) or a mistake (a broken process).\",\"Tag each mistake with the app's error name.\",\"Explain why only mistakes are worth fixing and why a streak on its own isn't one.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Mission: Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.6\",\"courseId\":\"1.6.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.6.6-1\",\"q-1.6.6-2\",\"q-1.6.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.6.7\",\"title\":\"Process KPIs\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Measure how well you follow your process, with KPIs that mean something long before profit does.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate stop integrity, risk accuracy and plan adherence from a list of trades.\",\"Explain why process KPIs mean something after 30 trades while profit doesn't.\",\"Set one process goal for your next 30 trades.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: trending market\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.6\",\"courseId\":\"1.6.7\",\"check\":[\"q-1.6.7-1\",\"q-1.6.7-2\",\"q-1.6.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.7.1\",\"title\":\"Emotions are normal\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Fear, greed, hope and regret are normal in trading: name them, rate them, and let the plan decide.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name fear, greed, hope and regret in common trading moments.\",\"Rate your emotional state from 1 to 5 before and after a trade.\",\"Explain why strong emotional reactions to gains and losses go with worse decisions.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.1\",\"check\":[\"q-1.7.1-1\",\"q-1.7.1-2\",\"q-1.7.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.7.3\",\"title\":\"The gambler's fallacy\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"A run of losses doesn't make a win due and a run of wins doesn't make you hot, so risk per trade stays the same.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why a run of losses doesn't make a win 'due'.\",\"Spot size changes driven by a streak rather than by the plan.\",\"Keep risk per trade the same through winning and losing streaks.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.7.3-1\",\"q-1.7.3-2\",\"q-1.7.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.7.4\",\"title\":\"Outcome bias in your own trades\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Grade your own trades' process before you see the result, so lucky wins don't train broken rules and unlucky losses don't kill sound ones.\",\"objectives\":[\"Grade your own trade's process before you see its result.\",\"Recognise when a lucky win is reinforcing a broken rule.\",\"Recognise when an unlucky loss tempts you to drop a sound rule.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.4\",\"check\":[\"q-1.7.4-1\",\"q-1.7.4-2\",\"q-1.7.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.7.5\",\"title\":\"Boredom and overtrading\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Trades taken out of boredom add costs without edge, and waiting for a setup is a valid, scored decision.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise trades taken to relieve boredom rather than because a setup appeared.\",\"Calculate what extra, off-plan trades cost in R.\",\"Treat sitting on your hands as a valid, scored decision.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.5\",\"check\":[\"q-1.7.5-1\",\"q-1.7.5-2\",\"q-1.7.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.7.6\",\"title\":\"If-then rules\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Turn urges into if-then rules with an observable trigger and a concrete action, and write them into your plan.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write an if-then rule with an observable trigger and a concrete action.\",\"Turn three common urges into if-then rules in your plan.\",\"Explain why decisions made in advance hold better than willpower in the moment.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.6\",\"check\":[\"q-1.7.6-1\",\"q-1.7.6-2\",\"q-1.7.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.7.7\",\"title\":\"The pause\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"Set a pause length, take it after a loss or a sharp move, and fill it with a fixed routine before any new decision.\",\"objectives\":[\"Set a pause length in your plan and take it after a loss or a sharp move.\",\"Use the pause for a fixed routine: step away, check in, reframe, run the checklist.\",\"Ask the 'fresh eyes' question before re-entering or holding a losing trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.7\",\"courseId\":\"1.7.7\",\"check\":[\"q-1.7.7-1\",\"q-1.7.7-2\",\"q-1.7.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.8.1\",\"title\":\"Martingale and averaging down\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Doubling after losses and adding to losers win small most of the time and lose catastrophically once; the maths of losing runs makes that loss a matter of time.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the size and the total loss of a doubling sequence after a run of losses.\",\"Explain why a very high win rate can hide a ruinous loss.\",\"Tell averaging down from a planned split entry with a fixed total risk.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.1\",\"check\":[\"q-1.8.1-1\",\"q-1.8.1-2\",\"q-1.8.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.8.2\",\"title\":\"Grid trading\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":5,\"summary\":\"A grid without stops collects small wins in a range and piles up units, and open losses, as soon as price trends away.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe how a grid without stops adds exposure as price moves away.\",\"Calculate the open loss of a grid after a one-way move.\",\"Explain why a grid's smooth equity curve hides the risk of ruin.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Exposure check\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.2\",\"check\":[\"q-1.8.2-1\",\"q-1.8.2-2\",\"q-1.8.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.8.3\",\"title\":\"Holy grails and survivorship bias\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"A performance claim means little until you know its sample, costs, period, worst loss and what was left out; even real edges shrink once published.\",\"objectives\":[\"Check a performance claim for sample size, costs, period, worst loss and selection.\",\"Explain how survivorship and cherry-picking make random results look skilled.\",\"Apply the publication-decay rule of thumb to any published edge.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.3\",\"check\":[\"q-1.8.3-1\",\"q-1.8.3-2\",\"q-1.8.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.8.4\",\"title\":\"Signal sellers, scams and unregulated brokers\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Check a broker's licence on the regulator's own register, know the red flags of scams and paid signals, and know who is on the other side of your trades.\",\"objectives\":[\"Check a broker's licence on the regulator's own register.\",\"Recognise the red flags of scams, signal services and pay-to-play challenges.\",\"Explain the conflict of interest when a broker is the counterparty to your trades.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.4\",\"check\":[\"q-1.8.4-1\",\"q-1.8.4-2\",\"q-1.8.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"1.8.5\",\"title\":\"The leverage illusion\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"beginner\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Effective leverage, position value divided by equity, sets how hard a move hits the account; a broker's close-out comes after the damage, not instead of a stop.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate effective leverage as position value ÷ equity.\",\"Work out the price move that wipes out an account at a given effective leverage.\",\"Explain why a broker's close-out is a failure of sizing, not a protective stop.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"1.8\",\"courseId\":\"1.8.5\",\"check\":[\"q-1.8.5-1\",\"q-1.8.5-2\",\"q-1.8.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.1\",\"title\":\"Measuring a candle against its neighbours\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"How the app sizes a candle against the 10 before it, and the names that follow: marubozu, spinning top, high-wave and doji.\",\"objectives\":[\"Classify a candle's body as long, short or doji by comparing it with the last 10 candles.\",\"Identify a marubozu, a spinning top, a high-wave candle and the four doji types from open, high, low and close.\",\"Explain why the same candle can count as long in a calm market and short in a volatile one.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M01\",\"label\":\"Read the Candle\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.1-1\",\"q-2.1.1-2\",\"q-2.1.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.2\",\"title\":\"Long shadows: hammers, shooting stars and their twins\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Two long-shadow shapes, four names set by the trend before them, and what testing says about each.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify a hammer, hanging man, inverted hammer and shooting star from the candle's shape and the trend before it.\",\"Explain why one shape gets two names, and name the two whose tested behaviour contradicts the name.\",\"Compare each pattern's reversal rate with the 50% coin-flip line.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bearish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bearish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.2-1\",\"q-2.1.2-2\",\"q-2.1.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.3\",\"title\":\"Context first: trend, location, confirmation\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Three checks that decide whether a candle pattern counts, what waiting for confirmation costs, and what the same rule does on a random walk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Apply three context checks (prior trend, location at a zone, a confirming close) to accept or reject a candle pattern.\",\"Calculate how waiting for confirmation changes the entry, the stop distance, the size and the reward-to-risk.\",\"Compare a pattern's results on a random-walk market with its results in context, using the tally lab.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.3-1\",\"q-2.1.3-2\",\"q-2.1.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.4\",\"title\":\"Engulfing and harami\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Two-candle patterns read from real bodies only, how they relate to inside and outside bars, and why a high hit rate can still rank low.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify bullish and bearish engulfing patterns from the real bodies only, ignoring shadows.\",\"Identify a harami and a harami cross, and relate them to the inside bar.\",\"Explain how a pattern can reverse 79% of the time and still rank 91st of 103.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.4-1\",\"q-2.1.4-2\",\"q-2.1.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.5\",\"title\":\"Piercing, dark cloud and the penetration scale\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Five two-candle patterns that differ only in how deep the second candle closes into the first, plus tweezers and kickers.\",\"objectives\":[\"Measure how far a second candle closes into the first candle's body, as a share of that body.\",\"Name the pattern at each depth: on-neck, in-neck, thrusting, piercing, engulfing.\",\"Identify tweezers and kickers, and state what the data says about each.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.5-1\",\"q-2.1.5-2\",\"q-2.1.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.6\",\"title\":\"Stars, doji stars and abandoned babies\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Three-candle turns with a small, gapped middle candle, where candle 3 must close, and what happens to the rule on markets that rarely gap.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify a morning star and an evening star, including the gap and where the third candle must close.\",\"Tell a star, a doji star and an abandoned baby apart by the middle candle and its gaps.\",\"Explain how relaxing the gap rule on markets that rarely gap changes the statistics.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M01\",\"label\":\"Read the Candle\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.6-1\",\"q-2.1.6-2\",\"q-2.1.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.7\",\"title\":\"Confirmation built in: three-candle patterns\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Patterns whose last candle confirms the turn, why their rates look higher, and what the late entry does to reward-to-risk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify three white soldiers, three black crows, three inside up/down and three outside up/down.\",\"Explain why patterns that include their own confirmation candle show higher reversal rates, and what that costs.\",\"Calculate the reward-to-risk of entering at the close of three white soldiers with a stop below the pattern.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.7-1\",\"q-2.1.7-2\",\"q-2.1.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.8\",\"title\":\"When names lie: the pattern zoo\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Patterns that tested against their own names, a five-question card for judging any pattern, and named shapes on a random walk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name three patterns whose tested behaviour contradicts their textbook meaning.\",\"Judge any unfamiliar pattern with a five-question reference card: rule, sample size, base rate, result after costs, scope.\",\"Show with the lab that named and unnamed random shapes perform alike on a random walk.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.8-1\",\"q-2.1.8-2\",\"q-2.1.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.1.9\",\"title\":\"Other ways to draw price: Heikin-Ashi, Renko, point & figure\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Heikin-Ashi, Renko and point & figure: how each is built, what it hides, and why fills always use real prices.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate a Heikin-Ashi candle from real open, high, low and close values.\",\"Build Renko bricks and point & figure columns from a list of prices, given the box size and the reversal.\",\"Explain why stops, fills and backtests must use real prices, not Heikin-Ashi or Renko prices.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M01\",\"label\":\"Read the Candle\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.1\",\"courseId\":\"2.1.9\",\"check\":[\"q-2.1.9-1\",\"q-2.1.9-2\",\"q-2.1.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.1\",\"title\":\"Drawing lines that mean something: trendlines and channels\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Draw trendlines and channels by fixed rules, and treat a break as a warning to confirm, not a signal.\",\"objectives\":[\"Draw a valid trendline through two swing lows and confirm it with a third touch.\",\"Draw a parallel channel, and explain why a fitted regression channel cannot be used in a backtest.\",\"Treat a trendline break as a warning to confirm, not as a signal on its own.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bearish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bearish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.1-1\",\"q-2.2.1-2\",\"q-2.2.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.2\",\"title\":\"Anatomy of a chart pattern\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Every chart pattern has the same eight parts; confirm its breakout with a filter and scale its target by how often it was reached.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the eight parts of any chart pattern, from the prior trend to what happens after the breakout.\",\"Confirm a breakout with a close beyond the boundary, and compare a 3% filter with an ATR filter on LABX.\",\"Calculate a pattern target from its height, scaled by the share of past patterns that reached the full target.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.2-1\",\"q-2.2.2-2\",\"q-2.2.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.3\",\"title\":\"Double tops and bottoms\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A double top or bottom exists only once price closes beyond the level between its two extremes; early and confirmed entries compare by expectancy.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify a double top or bottom and its confirmation level: the valley or the peak between the two extremes.\",\"Compare entering at the second peak with entering on confirmation, in hit rate and in R.\",\"Calculate the scaled target of a confirmed double bottom.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.3-1\",\"q-2.2.3-2\",\"q-2.2.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.4\",\"title\":\"Head and shoulders\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Label the shoulders and the head, draw the neckline through the lows between them, then confirm and measure by the rules.\",\"objectives\":[\"Label the left shoulder, head and right shoulder, and draw the neckline through the two lows between them.\",\"Apply the confirmation rule for an up-sloping and for a down-sloping neckline.\",\"Calculate the full and the scaled target for a top and for an inverse pattern.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.4-1\",\"q-2.2.4-2\",\"q-2.2.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.5\",\"title\":\"Triple tops, triple bottoms and rectangles\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A triple top is a range until a close under its lowest valley; a rectangle is measured both ways, and a partial move only tilts the odds.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why a triple top is only a range until price closes below its lowest valley.\",\"Draw a rectangle with at least three touches on one boundary and two on the other.\",\"Use a partial rise or partial decline inside a rectangle as a tilt in the odds, not a certainty.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.5-1\",\"q-2.2.5-2\",\"q-2.2.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.6\",\"title\":\"Triangles\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Name triangles by their slopes, take each one's breakout bias from the data, and distrust breakouts near the apex.\",\"objectives\":[\"Classify ascending, descending and symmetrical triangles by the slopes of their two boundaries.\",\"State each triangle's breakout bias from the data, including where the descending triangle contradicts the textbook.\",\"Explain why breakouts close to the apex deserve less trust.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.6-1\",\"q-2.2.6-2\",\"q-2.2.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.7\",\"title\":\"Flags, pennants and measured moves\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Spot flags and pennants by pole, shape and length; scale the half-mast target, and project measured moves at 60% and 100%.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify a flag and a pennant from the flagpole, the shape and the duration.\",\"Compare the \\\"half-mast\\\" target with how often it was reached, and scale it.\",\"Project a measured move from three swing points at 60% and 100% of the first leg.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.7-1\",\"q-2.2.7-2\",\"q-2.2.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.8\",\"title\":\"Wedges and broadening formations\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Tell wedges from channels by their slopes, know how weak rising-wedge breakdowns were, and why megaphones and diamonds are hard to trade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell a rising wedge from a falling wedge and from a channel by the slopes of the two lines.\",\"State what the data says about rising wedges that break down.\",\"Identify a broadening formation and a diamond, and explain why they are hard to trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.8-1\",\"q-2.2.8-2\",\"q-2.2.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.9\",\"title\":\"Slow turns and sharp turns: rounding, cup with handle, V\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Rounding bottoms and cups become visible late, V turns can't be seen in real time, and a rare pattern's numbers need their sample size checked.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify a rounding bottom and a cup with handle, and explain why both become visible only late.\",\"Explain why a V-bottom can't be recognised in real time, using the V's that failed.\",\"Use the pattern zoo card to compare any rare pattern by failure rate, rank and sample size.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.9\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.9-1\",\"q-2.2.9-2\",\"q-2.2.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.2.10\",\"title\":\"Failed, busted and random patterns\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Write the plan for works, fails and busts before the breakout; patterns appear in random walks, and published failure rates change with the era.\",\"objectives\":[\"Pre-commit your actions for the three outcomes of a breakout: it works, it fails, it busts.\",\"Show with the lab that pattern shapes appear in a random walk and that what follows them matches the base rate.\",\"Explain why Bulkowski's failure rates doubled after the 1990s and what that means for any published statistic.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.2\",\"courseId\":\"2.2.10\",\"check\":[\"q-2.2.10-1\",\"q-2.2.10-2\",\"q-2.2.10-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.1\",\"title\":\"Dow Theory and what \\\"trend\\\" means\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Dow Theory's three degrees of trend, the peak-trough rule for when a trend has ended, and four working definitions of trend that can disagree.\",\"objectives\":[\"List Dow Theory's tenets and its three degrees of trend.\",\"Apply the peak-trough rule to decide whether a trend has ended.\",\"Compare four working definitions of trend on the same chart and show where they disagree.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.1-1\",\"q-2.3.1-2\",\"q-2.3.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.3\",\"title\":\"EMA, WMA and the price of speed\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"How an EMA and a WMA are calculated, why they react faster than an SMA, and why speed is always paid for in whipsaws.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate one EMA step from the previous EMA, the new close and k = 2 ÷ (n + 1).\",\"Calculate a WMA, and rank SMA, EMA and WMA by how fast each reacts to the same close.\",\"Explain why no average removes the lag-versus-noise trade-off and why default periods are only conventions.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways, with EMA 20\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.3-1\",\"q-2.3.3-2\",\"q-2.3.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.4\",\"title\":\"Crossovers and the cost of whipsaws\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Crossovers as sign changes, why ranges produce many of them, and how costs turn a marginal crossover rule into a losing one.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define price crosses, double crosses and golden/death crosses as sign changes of fast minus slow.\",\"Count the crossover trades a range produces and what they pay in costs.\",\"Find, with the cost slider, the cost level at which a marginal crossover rule stops making money.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways, with SMA 20 and SMA 50\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.4-1\",\"q-2.3.4-2\",\"q-2.3.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.5\",\"title\":\"MACD: two averages in disguise\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"MACD, its signal line and histogram calculated by hand, why its zero cross is just a 12/26 EMA cross, and how PPO compares instruments.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the MACD line, signal line and histogram from given EMA values.\",\"Show that a MACD zero-line cross is the same event as a 12/26 EMA cross.\",\"Convert MACD to PPO to compare instruments with different prices.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.5-1\",\"q-2.3.5-2\",\"q-2.3.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.6\",\"title\":\"ADX and DMI: strength without direction\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Wilder's directional movement by hand, how ADX measures trend strength without direction, and what an ADX threshold catches and misses as a regime filter.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate +DM, −DM and true range for a candle, including inside and outside candles.\",\"Calculate +DI, −DI and DX from summed values, and read ADX as trend strength without direction.\",\"Use an ADX threshold as a regime filter and measure its hits and misses against hidden regime labels.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.6-1\",\"q-2.3.6-2\",\"q-2.3.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.7\",\"title\":\"Parabolic SAR and SuperTrend: trailing lines that flip\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"How the Parabolic SAR and SuperTrend lines are calculated, how they trail a trend, and why always-in versions whipsaw in ranges.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the next Parabolic SAR value from the previous SAR, the extreme point and the acceleration factor.\",\"Calculate a SuperTrend band from the candle midpoint and the ATR.\",\"Explain why indicators that are always in the market whipsaw when the market ranges.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.7-1\",\"q-2.3.7-2\",\"q-2.3.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.3.8\",\"title\":\"Ichimoku basics and look-ahead traps\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Ichimoku's five lines calculated from highs and lows, why they are mostly Donchian midpoints, and how its shifted lines create look-ahead bias in backtests.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate Tenkan, Kijun and the two cloud lines from period highs and lows.\",\"Explain why Ichimoku's lines are mostly Donchian midpoints, so they are not five independent confirmations.\",\"Spot look-ahead in a backtest that uses the forward-shifted cloud or the Chikou line.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.3\",\"courseId\":\"2.3.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.3.8-1\",\"q-2.3.8-2\",\"q-2.3.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.1\",\"title\":\"Momentum and rate of change\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Momentum and rate of change measure how far price has come in n candles; the strong evidence for momentum covers months, not hours.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate momentum and rate of change over n candles.\",\"Tell bounded oscillators from unbounded ones, and explain why unbounded ones have no fixed overbought level.\",\"Explain why the horizon matters: the strong momentum evidence covers 3–12 months, not 10 hourly candles.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.1-1\",\"q-2.4.1-2\",\"q-2.4.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.2\",\"title\":\"RSI by hand\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"RSI turns average gains and losses into a 0–100 reading: calculate it by hand, read its lines, and see why it can stay above 70 in a trend.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate RSI from average gains and losses, including one step of Wilder's smoothing.\",\"Read RSI's 70/30 lines, the 50 centreline and Brown's bull-market and bear-market ranges.\",\"Explain why RSI can stay above 70 for long stretches in a trend.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.2-1\",\"q-2.4.2-2\",\"q-2.4.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.3\",\"title\":\"Stochastic, Williams %R and CCI: one story told three times\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Stochastic %K, Williams %R and CCI rescale the same highs, lows and closes, so when they agree you have about one signal, not three.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate stochastic %K, Williams %R and a short CCI from the same candles.\",\"Show that Williams %R equals %K minus 100.\",\"Explain why three agreeing oscillators are not three confirmations, and pick indicators that add different information.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.3-1\",\"q-2.4.3-2\",\"q-2.4.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.4\",\"title\":\"Overbought is not a sell signal\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"The same RSI fade wins in a ranging market and loses in a trending one: overbought describes a move, it doesn't forecast a reversal.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run the same RSI fade rule on a trending and a ranging market, and compare the win rates.\",\"Explain the three reasons \\\"overbought\\\" fails as a sell signal: regime, horizon and arbitrary thresholds.\",\"Decide, for a given regime, whether an oscillator extreme suggests a fade, a strong trend, or nothing.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.4-1\",\"q-2.4.4-2\",\"q-2.4.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.5\",\"title\":\"Divergences, with honest grades\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A divergence is price and an oscillator disagreeing at the same swings; strong trends show several before the top, so wait for confirmation and know the evidence is weak.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify regular and hidden divergences by reading the oscillator at the same candles as the price swings.\",\"Apply a confirmation rule (a price structure break, or the oscillator crossing 50) before acting on a divergence.\",\"Count how many divergences appeared before the actual top in the lab, and say what that implies.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.5-1\",\"q-2.4.5-2\",\"q-2.4.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.6\",\"title\":\"Measuring volatility: true range, ATR and standard deviation\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"True range counts gaps, ATR smooths it Wilder's way, ATR % compares instruments, and because volatility clusters, ATR forecasts how much price moves, never which way.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate true range when a candle gaps, and update ATR with Wilder's smoothing.\",\"Express ATR as a percentage of price to compare instruments.\",\"Explain volatility clustering, and why ATR forecasts how much price will move, not which way.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.6-1\",\"q-2.4.6-2\",\"q-2.4.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.8\",\"title\":\"Bollinger Bands and the squeeze\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Bollinger Bands sit two standard deviations around a 20-period average: tags aren't signals, closes outside start as continuation, and a squeeze predicts size, not direction.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate Bollinger Bands, %b and BandWidth from a 20-period average and standard deviation.\",\"Apply Bollinger's own rules: a band tag is not a signal, and closes outside the bands are at first continuation.\",\"Use the squeeze to expect a bigger move, and show in the lab that it does not predict the direction.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.8-1\",\"q-2.4.8-2\",\"q-2.4.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.4.9\",\"title\":\"Keltner and Donchian channels\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Keltner Channels set ATR around an EMA and Donchian channels track recent extremes; Bollinger inside Keltner marks a squeeze, and a channel that includes the current candle leaks the future into a backtest.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a Keltner Channel from an EMA and the ATR, and a Donchian channel from the highest high and lowest low.\",\"Detect a TTM-style squeeze, where the Bollinger Bands sit inside the Keltner Channel.\",\"Find the look-ahead bug in a Donchian breakout test that includes the current candle in the channel.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.4\",\"courseId\":\"2.4.9\",\"check\":[\"q-2.4.9-1\",\"q-2.4.9-2\",\"q-2.4.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.1\",\"title\":\"Reading volume\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Relative volume as a ratio to the recent average, what volume can and can't tell you, and how to test the claim that volume confirms breakouts.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate relative volume as a candle's volume divided by its 20-candle average.\",\"Explain what volume can and cannot tell you: participation, not direction.\",\"Test \\\"volume confirms breakouts\\\" in the lab against the same breakouts without the filter.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.1-1\",\"q-2.5.1-2\",\"q-2.5.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.2\",\"title\":\"OBV, accumulation/distribution and money flow\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Two running totals built from volume, OBV and the accumulation/distribution line: how to calculate them, how they mislead, and why only their direction is read.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate OBV and the accumulation/distribution line for a few candles.\",\"Show how one huge-volume candle dominates OBV and how A/D ignores gaps.\",\"Explain why the level of OBV or A/D means nothing and only its direction is read.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.2-1\",\"q-2.5.2-2\",\"q-2.5.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.3\",\"title\":\"VWAP and anchored VWAP\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"VWAP as the volume-weighted average price of a session, its real job as an execution benchmark, and anchored VWAP from an event you choose.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate VWAP from typical price and volume, cumulated from the session open.\",\"Explain VWAP's real use as an execution benchmark, and why it resets every session.\",\"Anchor a VWAP to an event and read it as the average price paid since then.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.3-1\",\"q-2.5.3-2\",\"q-2.5.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.4\",\"title\":\"Volume profile basics\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Volume traded at each price: the point of control, the value area, high- and low-volume nodes, and the look-ahead trap.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a volume profile and find the point of control and the 70% value area.\",\"Tell high-volume nodes from low-volume nodes and state what each is claimed to do.\",\"Avoid look-ahead: never use a profile that includes later candles to explain earlier turns.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.4-1\",\"q-2.5.4-2\",\"q-2.5.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.5\",\"title\":\"Pivot points\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Pivot points from the previous session's high, low and close, the competing formulas, and why many lines guarantee a 'hit'.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the classic pivot, R1, S1, R2 and S2 from the previous session's high, low and close.\",\"Name the Fibonacci, Camarilla, Woodie and DeMark variants and what each changes.\",\"Explain why plotting several pivot systems at once guarantees that some line is always \\\"hit\\\".\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.5-1\",\"q-2.5.5-2\",\"q-2.5.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.6\",\"title\":\"Gaps: four kinds and an island\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Common, breakaway, runaway and exhaustion gaps, what counts as a fill, and the island reversal.\",\"objectives\":[\"Classify a gap as common, breakaway, runaway or exhaustion from where it appears and the volume around it.\",\"Define when a gap is filled, and explain why \\\"all gaps get filled\\\" is false for breakaway gaps.\",\"Identify an island reversal and compare it with an abandoned baby.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.6-1\",\"q-2.5.6-2\",\"q-2.5.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.7\",\"title\":\"Supply and demand zones\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Drawing demand and supply zones from the base before a strong move, the touches-versus-fresh contradiction, and how to test zones against random ones.\",\"objectives\":[\"Draw a demand or supply zone from the base before a strong move away, using one fixed convention.\",\"Explain the contradiction between \\\"more touches make a level stronger\\\" and \\\"fresh zones work best\\\".\",\"Compare first-touch reactions at your zones with reactions at random zones of the same width.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.7-1\",\"q-2.5.7-2\",\"q-2.5.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.5.8\",\"title\":\"Fibonacci levels and the confluence illusion\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Fibonacci retracements and extensions, why the 38.2–61.8% zone is hard to falsify, and why lines piling up at one price prove little.\",\"objectives\":[\"Draw Fibonacci retracements and extensions from a swing and calculate each level.\",\"Explain why 50% is not a Fibonacci ratio and why the 38.2–61.8% zone is almost impossible to falsify.\",\"Count how many \\\"levels\\\" sit near a random price, and decide which confluence factors are independent.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.5\",\"courseId\":\"2.5.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.5.8-1\",\"q-2.5.8-2\",\"q-2.5.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.1\",\"title\":\"Trend bars and doji bars\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Classify each bar by its body and close, then read runs of trend bars and the overlap between bars as signs of a trend or a range.\",\"objectives\":[\"Classify bars as trend bars or doji bars from body size and where the bar closed.\",\"Read runs of trend bars and the overlap between bars as signs of a trend or a range.\",\"Explain why the same bar means something different in a trend and in a range.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.1-1\",\"q-2.6.1-2\",\"q-2.6.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.2\",\"title\":\"Signal bars, entry bars and the trader's equation\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Grade a signal bar, enter one tick beyond it with a stop entry, and check the trade with the trader's equation before you take it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Rate a bull reversal signal bar with a five-point checklist.\",\"Place a stop entry one tick above a signal bar and the protective stop one tick below it.\",\"Check a setup with the trader's equation: chance of winning times reward against chance of losing times risk, after costs.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.2-1\",\"q-2.6.2-2\",\"q-2.6.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.3\",\"title\":\"Inside, outside and narrow-range bars\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Spot inside, outside and NR7 bars from highs and lows, bracket a quiet bar with OCO stop entries sized from the stop, and recognise a hikkake.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify inside bars, ii and iii sequences, outside bars and NR7 bars from highs and lows.\",\"Set up a one-cancels-the-other pair of stop entries around an inside bar, and size it so the stop-out loss stays within budget.\",\"Explain a hikkake: an inside bar, a false breakout, then a move through the other side.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.3-1\",\"q-2.6.3-2\",\"q-2.6.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.4\",\"title\":\"Two-legged pullbacks and bar counting\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Count H1 and H2 bars in a pullback, enter the second attempt with a stop entry, and know why the count means little outside a trend.\",\"objectives\":[\"Count H1 and H2 bars (L1 and L2 in a bear trend) in a pullback.\",\"Identify a two-legged pullback, and place an H2 stop entry with its protective stop.\",\"Explain why the second entry can trap counter-trend traders, and why bar counts mean little in a range.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.4-1\",\"q-2.6.4-2\",\"q-2.6.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.5\",\"title\":\"Channels and wedges, bar by bar\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Grade a trend by its channel, tight, broad or micro, spot three pushes with fading momentum, and count only the channel touches you could have traded.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell tight, broad and micro channels apart by the size of the pullbacks and the bars' lows.\",\"Identify a three-push wedge and a wedge flag.\",\"Explain why a channel line drawn after the fact scores touches you could not have traded.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.5-1\",\"q-2.6.5-2\",\"q-2.6.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.6\",\"title\":\"Traps and failed breakouts\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Call a breakout failed only when a bar closes back inside, find where the trapped traders' stops sit, and decide at the close with the invalidation written down.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify a failed breakout as a close back inside the range within one to three bars.\",\"Locate where trapped breakout traders' stops sit, and explain how their exits can fuel the move against them.\",\"Decide between \\\"breakout\\\" and \\\"failure\\\" only after the bar closes, and write the invalidation for either choice.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.6-1\",\"q-2.6.6-2\",\"q-2.6.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.7\",\"title\":\"Momentum or exhaustion?\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Score a move's strength against its signs of exhaustion, flag climax bars by their size and context, and state a probability you can score later.\",\"objectives\":[\"Score a move's strength with a checklist: runs of trend bars, overlap, pullback depth, closes near extremes, gaps between bars.\",\"Flag a climax bar as a bar more than 2.5 ATR tall late in an extended move.\",\"Explain why the same large bar can be a breakout or a climax, and record a calibrated probability instead of a certainty.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.7-1\",\"q-2.6.7-2\",\"q-2.6.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.6.8\",\"title\":\"Aligning timeframes\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Pick three timeframes about 4–6 times apart, rank them with a rule written before the trade, and catch timeframe shopping in your journal.\",\"objectives\":[\"Choose a higher, a trading and a lower timeframe about 4–6 times apart.\",\"Apply a rule written in advance for which timeframe wins when they disagree.\",\"Recognise timeframe shopping in your own journal.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.6\",\"courseId\":\"2.6.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.6.8-1\",\"q-2.6.8-2\",\"q-2.6.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.1\",\"title\":\"Entries: anticipate, confirm or wait for the retest\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"One setup can be entered early with a limit, after confirmation with a stop or market order, or on a retest; each trades price for certainty, so compare them by expectancy.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compare anticipation, confirmation and retest entries on the same setup in entry price, stop distance and reward-to-risk.\",\"Match each entry to its order type (limit, stop or market) and name each one's fill risk.\",\"Judge entry methods by expectancy over many trades, not by hit rate.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.1-1\",\"q-2.7.1-2\",\"q-2.7.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.2\",\"title\":\"Scaling in versus averaging down\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Adding to a position is safe only while the worst case, every stop hit, stays within the 1R you planned: split entries and pyramids do that, averaging down doesn't.\",\"objectives\":[\"Plan a split entry whose total risk stays within 1R.\",\"Add to a winning position and move the stops so the worst case stays below the original 1R.\",\"Show why adding to a losing position pushes risk beyond 1R, and why the app flags it as a rule breach.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"position_sizing\",\"label\":\"Position sizing check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.2-1\",\"q-2.7.2-2\",\"q-2.7.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.3\",\"title\":\"Targets: R-multiples, structure and volatility\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A target can come from a fixed R-multiple, the next structure or a multiple of ATR; which one pays best depends on how far trades travel, and that changes with the regime.\",\"objectives\":[\"Set a target three ways: a fixed R-multiple, the next structure level, or a multiple of ATR.\",\"Calculate the expectancy of each target from the share of trades whose maximum favourable move reached it.\",\"Explain why the best target depends on the market regime and must be measured, not assumed.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.3-1\",\"q-2.7.3-2\",\"q-2.7.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.4\",\"title\":\"Trailing stops\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A trailing stop follows price by a rule fixed before entry; a chandelier hangs a multiple of ATR under the highest high, and every trail trades giveback against holding time.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate a chandelier stop from the highest high since entry and the ATR.\",\"Compare ATR, structure, moving-average and Parabolic SAR trails by giveback, holding time and stop-outs.\",\"Keep the rule that a trailing stop only moves in the trade's favour, and never switches method mid-trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.4-1\",\"q-2.7.4-2\",\"q-2.7.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.5\",\"title\":\"Breakeven stops\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Moving the stop to the entry after a gain saves some losers and kills some winners; whether it helps depends on your own trades, and it never removes the risk of a gap.\",\"objectives\":[\"Move a stop to breakeven only by a written rule, and name the cost of putting it exactly at the entry price.\",\"Count how many winners a breakeven rule kills and how many losers it saves.\",\"Calculate the expectancy of a set of trades with and without the rule.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"stop_loss\",\"label\":\"Stop-loss check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.5-1\",\"q-2.7.5-2\",\"q-2.7.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.6\",\"title\":\"Partial exits\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A trade closed in parts has a blended R; partials raise the win rate and narrow the spread of results, but can cap the trades that pay for everything else.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate the blended R of a trade closed in parts.\",\"Compare an all-in exit with a half-off-at-1R plan in expectancy and in the spread of outcomes.\",\"Explain why partial exits raise the win rate and can quietly cap the best trades.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.6-1\",\"q-2.7.6-2\",\"q-2.7.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.7\",\"title\":\"Time stops and planned exits\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A time stop closes a trade that hasn't progressed in N bars; every other early exit needs a reason from the plan, and a stop only ever moves toward less risk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write a time stop: exit if the trade has not reached a set progress within N bars.\",\"Separate planned exits on an opposing signal from fear exits by tagging the reason for every exit.\",\"Keep stop discipline: a stop moves only toward less risk, never wider.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.7-1\",\"q-2.7.7-2\",\"q-2.7.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.7.8\",\"title\":\"MAE and MFE: learning from your own excursions\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"MAE and MFE measure how far each trade went against you and for you, in R; over enough of your own trades they test your stops and show how much of each move your exits keep.\",\"objectives\":[\"Calculate a trade's maximum adverse and maximum favourable excursion in R.\",\"Use an MAE scatter to test a tighter stop, and count the winners it would have cut.\",\"Calculate giveback and exit efficiency, and read them by setup.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.7\",\"courseId\":\"2.7.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.7.8-1\",\"q-2.7.8-2\",\"q-2.7.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.1\",\"title\":\"Matching the tool to the regime\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Every strategy is a bet on a market regime: label the chart as trend, range or no clear regime first, then pick the family built for it, or no trade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Label an unlabeled chart segment as trend, range or no clear regime before choosing a strategy.\",\"Match each strategy family to the regime where it works and the one where it fails.\",\"Choose \\\"No trade\\\" when the regime is unclear or the market behaves like a random walk.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.1-1\",\"q-2.8.1-2\",\"q-2.8.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.2\",\"title\":\"Trend following\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Trend following cuts losses short and lets winners run: a 30–45% win rate can pay when winners are several times the losers, but ranges whipsaw it.\",\"objectives\":[\"State the full rules of a moving-average or channel trend-following system.\",\"Explain why a 30–45% win rate can still give a positive expectancy.\",\"Predict where the system loses: ranges and sharp reversals.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.2-1\",\"q-2.8.2-2\",\"q-2.8.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.3\",\"title\":\"Pullback in a trend\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A pullback entry joins a trend after a dip, with a trend filter, a trigger and a stop under the pullback low; it buys a better reward-to-risk than chasing, at the price of missed trades.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define a pullback entry with a trend filter, a trigger and a stop below the pullback low.\",\"Compare the reward-to-risk of buying a pullback with chasing the same move.\",\"Recognise the pullback that turns into a reversal.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.3-1\",\"q-2.8.3-2\",\"q-2.8.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.4\",\"title\":\"Breakout from a range\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A range breakout buys a close beyond a tested level, with a stop back inside and a measured-move target; it needs only about 30% winners, and most of its trades are small losses.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write breakout rules: a tested level, a close beyond it, a stop back inside, a measured-move target.\",\"Compute the break-even win rate of a breakout before and after costs.\",\"Name the conditions in which breakouts fail most often.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.4-1\",\"q-2.8.4-2\",\"q-2.8.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.5\",\"title\":\"Volatility contraction breakout\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A volatility contraction bets on a bigger move, not a direction: an OCO bracket around an NR7 or a squeeze, with stops so tight that costs and two-sided whipsaws decide the result.\",\"objectives\":[\"Spot a volatility contraction: an NR7 candle, an inside bar or a Bollinger squeeze.\",\"Place an OCO bracket around the contraction and size each side to 1%.\",\"Explain why tight compressions raise the cost in R and why both sides can lose.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.5-1\",\"q-2.8.5-2\",\"q-2.8.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.6\",\"title\":\"Range trading\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Range trading buys near support and sells near resistance in a confirmed range, with stops just outside the edges, until the first close outside the range ends it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Confirm a range before trading it: two touches on each side and flat averages.\",\"Plan a range trade with a stop outside the edge and a target near the other side.\",\"Apply the flip rule: stop range trading on the first confirmed close outside the range.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.6-1\",\"q-2.8.6-2\",\"q-2.8.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.7\",\"title\":\"Mean reversion\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Mean reversion fades stretched prices back to an average: it wins often and loses rarely but big, so a high win rate can hide a negative expectancy unless a catastrophic stop caps the tail.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe two mean-reversion rules: Bollinger reversion and RSI(2).\",\"Explain why a high win rate can hide a negative expectancy (negative skew).\",\"Keep a catastrophic stop on every mean-reversion trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bearish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bearish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.7-1\",\"q-2.8.7-2\",\"q-2.8.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.8\",\"title\":\"Failed breakout\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A failed-breakout trade sells a new 20-candle high that falls back inside the range, with a stop just above the spike; it mirrors the breakout, and the regime evidence decides which one to play.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define a failed-breakout trade (Turtle Soup) with exact entry and stop rules.\",\"Explain why it mirrors the breakout strategy and when each one fits.\",\"Choose breakout, fade or no trade from the regime evidence, not from the last result.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.8-1\",\"q-2.8.8-2\",\"q-2.8.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.9\",\"title\":\"Confluence versus redundancy\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Five oscillators agreeing are one piece of evidence counted five times; real confluence combines independent kinds of evidence, and every filter you add shrinks the sample you can judge.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell independent evidence from the same evidence counted several times.\",\"Group indicators by the input they are built from.\",\"Explain why every extra filter shrinks the sample you can judge.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.9\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.9-1\",\"q-2.8.9-2\",\"q-2.8.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.8.10\",\"title\":\"Judging a strategy after costs\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A strategy's edge is what's left after spread, commission and slippage, judged against simply holding and against the same rule on a random walk, over enough trades to tell it from luck.\",\"objectives\":[\"Convert a strategy's gross edge into a net edge per trade with spread, commission and slippage.\",\"Compare the net result with the passive benchmark and with the same rule on a random walk.\",\"Decide what sample size you need before trusting the result.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision making check\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.8\",\"courseId\":\"2.8.10\",\"check\":[\"q-2.8.10-1\",\"q-2.8.10-2\",\"q-2.8.10-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.1\",\"title\":\"Market hours and time zones\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Each market has a main session where liquidity gathers; convert its hours to your own clock, and watch the weeks when US and European clocks change on different dates.\",\"objectives\":[\"Say when the main markets are open, in New York time and in Romanian time\",\"Convert an event time across time zones, including the weeks when US and EU clocks change on different dates\",\"Explain why a 24-hour market is not equally liquid all day\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.1-1\",\"q-2.9.1-2\",\"q-2.9.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.2\",\"title\":\"FX sessions and overlaps\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Currency trading moves from Sydney to Tokyo, London and New York; activity, volatility and spreads follow the sessions and peak in the London–New York overlap.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the four FX sessions and the London–New York overlap, in Romanian time\",\"Explain why activity, volatility and spreads change with the session\",\"Choose trading hours from movement per unit of cost, not from session folklore\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.2-1\",\"q-2.9.2-2\",\"q-2.9.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.3\",\"title\":\"The intraday rhythm\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Volume and volatility peak near the open and the close and dip at midday, so stops should follow the hour's volatility, and quiet hours make costs a bigger share of each R.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe the U-shaped pattern of intraday volume and volatility\",\"Match the stop distance to the volatility of the hour, not to a fixed number\",\"Explain why quiet hours raise the cost of a trade in R\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.3-1\",\"q-2.9.3-2\",\"q-2.9.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.4\",\"title\":\"The opening range\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"An opening range breakout trades a break of the first minutes' high or low; the pattern is real, but costs and slippage at the open decide whether its small edge survives.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define the opening range and the rules of an opening range breakout\",\"Show how costs and slippage at the open decide whether a small edge survives\",\"Filter out the days on which the opening range breakout tends to fail\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.4-1\",\"q-2.9.4-2\",\"q-2.9.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.5\",\"title\":\"Gap and go or gap fill\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A gap with a catalyst and heavy volume can run, a quiet gap can fade back, often the right call is to pass, and many 'fills' are what a random walk does anyway.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell a gap with a catalyst from a gap without one\",\"State the rules of gap-and-go and gap-fill, and when to pass\",\"Show that many \\\"gap fills\\\" are what a random walk produces anyway\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.5-1\",\"q-2.9.5-2\",\"q-2.9.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.6\",\"title\":\"Day trading or swing trading\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Day, swing and position trading trade costs against gap risk and screen time; pick the style your costs and your life can carry, and not day trading is a valid choice.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compare day, swing and position trading on holding time, costs, gap risk and time demand\",\"Compute how trade frequency and stop size change the weekly cost in R\",\"Match a style to your own constraints, including the choice not to day trade\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.6-1\",\"q-2.9.6-2\",\"q-2.9.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.7\",\"title\":\"Scalping and the cost wall\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Costs stay fixed while scalping targets shrink, so the break-even win rate climbs toward the impossible and cost drag swallows much of every gross profit.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the break-even win rate of a small-target trade after costs\",\"Use cost drag (cost ÷ average gross profit) to compare styles\",\"Explain why retail scalping against professional liquidity providers rarely survives costs\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.7-1\",\"q-2.9.7-2\",\"q-2.9.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.9.8\",\"title\":\"Weekends, holidays and rollover\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A stop can't protect against a gap while markets are closed, so positions held over weekends and holidays are sized for a worst-case gap, and FX stops keep clear of the rollover.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why a stop cannot protect you from a weekend or holiday gap\",\"Size a position you will hold over a weekend so a worst-case gap stays within 2R\",\"Know when rollover happens and why spreads widen around it\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.9\",\"courseId\":\"2.9.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.9.8-1\",\"q-2.9.8-2\",\"q-2.9.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.1\",\"title\":\"Expectations versus surprise\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Prices move on the surprise, actual minus consensus, measured against the usual size of surprises; small surprises are mostly noise.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why prices move on the surprise, not on whether the news is good or bad.\",\"Compute a surprise and a standardised surprise from consensus, actual and the usual size of surprises.\",\"Recognise small surprises that are usually noise.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.1-1\",\"q-2.10.1-2\",\"q-2.10.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.2\",\"title\":\"Reading the economic calendar\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A calendar row gives the time, impact, previous, revised, consensus and actual values; convert the time with care and triage the week before it starts.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read each field of a calendar row: time, impact, previous, revised, consensus, actual.\",\"Convert release times to your own time zone without the DST trap.\",\"Triage a week of events into flat, reduce or ignore, before the week starts.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.2-1\",\"q-2.10.2-2\",\"q-2.10.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.3\",\"title\":\"Spreads, slippage and stops at news time\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Around a release spreads widen, depth thins and stops fill at the first price after the jump; a widened spread can even trigger a stop the mid price never reached.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe what happens to spreads, depth and fills in the minute around a release.\",\"Compute the loss when a stop fills after a jump, and the slippage in vUSD and in R.\",\"Explain how a spread-triggered stop happens.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.3-1\",\"q-2.10.3-2\",\"q-2.10.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.4\",\"title\":\"Nonfarm payrolls\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"The US jobs report has three headline numbers and revisions; judge a surprise against the report's own noise, and call it hot, cold or mixed before reacting.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the three numbers in the US jobs report and the revisions that come with them.\",\"Judge whether a surprise is larger than the report's own sampling noise.\",\"Classify a report as clearly hot, clearly cold or mixed before reacting.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.4-1\",\"q-2.10.4-2\",\"q-2.10.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.5\",\"title\":\"Inflation: CPI\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Headline CPI includes everything, core leaves out food and energy; markets trade core month-on-month, and when the two disagree the reaction often comes in two waves.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain headline and core CPI and why markets focus on core month-on-month.\",\"Size a CPI surprise against the usual spread of CPI surprises.\",\"Wait for the details when headline and core disagree.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.5-1\",\"q-2.10.5-2\",\"q-2.10.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.6\",\"title\":\"Central banks: the basics\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Central banks move prices by how far a decision and its guidance differ from what was priced; weigh the outcomes by their probabilities to size the surprise.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain what a central bank decides, when, and what the market has already priced.\",\"Compute the surprise of a decision from market-implied probabilities.\",\"Tell hawkish from dovish language, relative to what was expected.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.6-1\",\"q-2.10.6-2\",\"q-2.10.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.7\",\"title\":\"Stand aside, reduce or trade the surprise\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Decide the news policy before the event: stand aside by default, reduce to a size that survives a gap, or trade only the reaction; the first seconds are no retail edge.\",\"objectives\":[\"Choose a news policy before the event: stand aside, reduce, or trade the reaction.\",\"Size a position you keep through an event so a gap of several ATRs stays within 1R.\",\"Explain why trading the first seconds of a release is not a retail edge.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.7-1\",\"q-2.10.7-2\",\"q-2.10.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.8\",\"title\":\"The second move\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Wait for spreads to normalise, mark the post-release range, and trade its break in the surprise's direction with a stop at its other side, or pass.\",\"objectives\":[\"Wait for spreads to normalise before acting on a release.\",\"Trade a break of the post-release range with a stop at its other side.\",\"Name the cases where the second move fails: conflicting details, small surprises, the press conference.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.8-1\",\"q-2.10.8-2\",\"q-2.10.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.10.9\",\"title\":\"Your news risk checklist\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Run a short checklist before any order near a scheduled release; against unscheduled shocks only size and leverage help; journal the slippage you actually paid.\",\"objectives\":[\"Apply a pre-event checklist before every order near a scheduled release.\",\"Explain why only size and leverage protect you from unscheduled shocks.\",\"Record the slippage you actually paid, per event type.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.10\",\"courseId\":\"2.10.9\",\"check\":[\"q-2.10.9-1\",\"q-2.10.9-2\",\"q-2.10.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.1\",\"title\":\"Sizing by volatility across instruments\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Volatility sizing gives every instrument the same cost per ATR of movement, so a calm market and a wild one carry the same weight in your account.\",\"objectives\":[\"Size positions so one ATR of movement costs the same share of equity in every instrument.\",\"Compare fixed-unit sizing with volatility sizing on two instruments.\",\"Explain the limits of ATR sizing: lag after volatility jumps, and correlation.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.1-1\",\"q-2.11.1-2\",\"q-2.11.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.2\",\"title\":\"Portfolio heat\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Portfolio heat adds up what every open stop would lose; capping it limits the damage of one bad afternoon when everything goes wrong together.\",\"objectives\":[\"Add up the open risk of all positions as a percentage of equity.\",\"Keep total heat under a cap by resizing or skipping new trades.\",\"Count gap risk and pending orders, even when a stop sits at breakeven.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: portfolio exposure\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.2-1\",\"q-2.11.2-2\",\"q-2.11.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.3\",\"title\":\"Correlation and concentration\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Positions that move together act like one bigger bet; correlation-adjusted risk and the effective number of bets show how much diversification you really have.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why two correlated positions behave like one larger position.\",\"Compute combined risk and the effective number of bets.\",\"Spot the same bet hidden in different instruments, and expect correlations to rise in stress.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: portfolio exposure\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.3-1\",\"q-2.11.3-2\",\"q-2.11.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.4\",\"title\":\"Exposure and loss limits\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Caps per instrument, direction and theme, plus weekly and monthly loss limits that count open risk, decide whether a new trade fits before you place it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Set caps per instrument, per direction and per theme.\",\"Apply weekly and monthly loss limits that count open risk.\",\"Decide whether a new trade fits all limits at once.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.4-1\",\"q-2.11.4-2\",\"q-2.11.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.5\",\"title\":\"Drawdown tiers\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Drawdown tiers cut risk at depths set in advance and calibrated to the system's normal drawdowns, and they say exactly when full risk comes back.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write drawdown tiers that cut risk at set depths and say when risk comes back.\",\"Calibrate the tiers to your system's normal drawdowns, not to a round number.\",\"Explain why risk only goes down as the drawdown deepens.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.5-1\",\"q-2.11.5-2\",\"q-2.11.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.6\",\"title\":\"Performance metrics\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Win rate, payoff, expectancy, profit factor and outlier dependence each describe a journal from one side and hide something; read them as diagnostics next to their sample size.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute win rate, payoff ratio, expectancy, profit factor and outlier dependence from a journal.\",\"Say what each metric hides.\",\"Keep outcome metrics as diagnostics with their sample size, and process metrics as the grade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.6-1\",\"q-2.11.6-2\",\"q-2.11.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.7\",\"title\":\"Expectancy with a confidence interval\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A 95% confidence interval shows where a system's true expectancy plausibly lies; on a small sample it is wide enough to include zero.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute a 95% confidence interval for a win rate and for an expectancy.\",\"Estimate how many trades you need before the interval excludes zero.\",\"Explain why a good-looking small sample proves little.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.7-1\",\"q-2.11.7-2\",\"q-2.11.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.11.8\",\"title\":\"SQN and comparing systems\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"SQN turns mean R, its spread and the number of trades into one t-like score; systems are compared per month after costs, and next to the passive benchmark.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute SQN and read it as a t-statistic of the mean R.\",\"Compare two systems by expectancy per month after costs, not per trade.\",\"Put any system next to the passive benchmark before calling it good.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.11\",\"courseId\":\"2.11.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.11.8-1\",\"q-2.11.8-2\",\"q-2.11.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.1\",\"title\":\"Prospect theory\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"People judge outcomes as gains or losses from a reference point and feel losses more, which pulls traders to cut winners and hold losers.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe the three parts of prospect theory: a reference point, diminishing sensitivity and loss aversion.\",\"Recognise your own risk-taking in gains versus in losses.\",\"Link prospect theory to holding losers, cutting winners and lottery-like targets.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.1-1\",\"q-2.12.1-2\",\"q-2.12.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.2\",\"title\":\"The disposition effect\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Selling winners early and holding losers is the disposition effect: measure it with PGR and PLR, and let exits come from rules.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define the disposition effect and measure it with PGR and PLR.\",\"Show how selling winners early and holding losers damages expectancy.\",\"Set exit rules that don't depend on whether the trade is in profit.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.2-1\",\"q-2.12.2-2\",\"q-2.12.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.3\",\"title\":\"Overconfidence and calibration\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Overconfidence is being sure more often than you're right: log your confidence, compare it with your hit rate, and watch what it does to your trade count.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain overconfidence as miscalibration: being sure more often than you are right.\",\"Measure your calibration with a confidence log.\",\"Link overconfidence to overtrading and its cost.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.3-1\",\"q-2.12.3-2\",\"q-2.12.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.4\",\"title\":\"Recency and small samples\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"The last few results feel like the truth, small samples mislead, and extreme runs drift back toward the base rate.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise recency bias: letting the last few results outweigh the base rate.\",\"Explain why small samples look more convincing than they are.\",\"Expect regression to the mean after an extreme run.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.4-1\",\"q-2.12.4-2\",\"q-2.12.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.5\",\"title\":\"Confirmation and anchoring\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Once you hold a trade you see what agrees with it and anchor to your entry; a written invalidation and a devil's-advocate check push back.\",\"objectives\":[\"Spot confirmation bias in how you read the chart of a trade you already hold.\",\"Recognise anchoring to your entry price or to an old high.\",\"Use a written invalidation and a devil's-advocate check before and during a trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.5-1\",\"q-2.12.5-2\",\"q-2.12.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.6\",\"title\":\"Sunk cost and house money\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"What's already lost or won shouldn't change your next decision: apply the fresh-eyes test and keep risk per trade flat.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise the sunk-cost trap in holding or adding to a loser.\",\"Recognise the house-money effect: taking more risk after gains.\",\"Apply the \\\"fresh eyes\\\" test and keep risk per trade flat after wins and after losses.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.6-1\",\"q-2.12.6-2\",\"q-2.12.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.7\",\"title\":\"Tilt and routines\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Tilt shows up as a cluster of rule breaks; guard-rails set before the session and a reset routine keep it from running the account.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise tilt as a cluster of rule breaks, not just a feeling.\",\"Set guard-rails before the session: a cool-down, a tilt threshold, a daily lock and a readiness check.\",\"Use reappraisal and a reset routine instead of suppressing emotion.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.7-1\",\"q-2.12.7-2\",\"q-2.12.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.12.9\",\"title\":\"Thinking in probabilities\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Each trade is one draw from a distribution: a positive-expectancy system still loses over short stretches, so judge blocks of trades by process.\",\"objectives\":[\"Treat each trade as one draw from a distribution, not as a test of being right.\",\"Show that a positive-expectancy system still loses over short stretches.\",\"Commit to executing a block of 20 setups by the rules and judge the block by process.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.12\",\"courseId\":\"2.12.9\",\"check\":[\"q-2.12.9-1\",\"q-2.12.9-2\",\"q-2.12.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.13.2\",\"title\":\"Building a playbook\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Write each setup as a checkable card, from regime to no-trade conditions, and judge it only on its own statistics and sample size.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write a setup card with regime, trigger, invalidation, management, re-entry and no-trade conditions\",\"Make every field checkable, so two people would agree on it\",\"Keep statistics per setup, with their sample size, before trusting or dropping a setup\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.13\",\"courseId\":\"2.13.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.13.2-1\",\"q-2.13.2-2\",\"q-2.13.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.13.3\",\"title\":\"The pre-market routine\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Run the same short routine before every session, so the event, limit or regime that changes today's plan is caught before the first order.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run a fixed routine before each session: readiness, calendar, regime, levels, scenarios, risk for the day\",\"Catch the event or limit that changes today's plan before the first order\",\"Write two or three if-then scenarios for the session\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.13\",\"courseId\":\"2.13.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.13.3-1\",\"q-2.13.3-2\",\"q-2.13.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.13.4\",\"title\":\"If-then plans\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Turn intentions into if-then rules with one observable trigger and one action, for market events and your own states, and check adherence after each session.\",\"objectives\":[\"Turn a vague intention into an if-then rule with an observable trigger and one action\",\"Write if-then rules for market events and for your own emotional triggers\",\"Check after the session which rules fired and whether you followed them\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Psychology check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.13\",\"courseId\":\"2.13.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.13.4-1\",\"q-2.13.4-2\",\"q-2.13.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.13.5\",\"title\":\"The error taxonomy\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Tag process errors from a fixed list, price each one in R against the same trade done by the rules, and fix the costliest one first.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tag process errors from a fixed list instead of free text\",\"Compute the cost of each error in R against the same trade done by the rules\",\"Separate mistakes, which you fix, from variance, which you accept\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.13\",\"courseId\":\"2.13.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.13.5-1\",\"q-2.13.5-2\",\"q-2.13.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.13.6\",\"title\":\"Daily and weekly reviews\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Review each day in minutes by grading decisions, review each week by setup, time and error tag to set one process goal, and replay trades blind.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run a five-to-ten-minute daily review that grades decisions, not profit\",\"Run a weekly review by setup, time of day and error tag, ending with one process goal\",\"Review in blind replay so hindsight doesn't rewrite your decisions\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.13\",\"courseId\":\"2.13.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.13.6-1\",\"q-2.13.6-2\",\"q-2.13.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.13.7\",\"title\":\"KPIs and goals\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Grade yourself on process KPIs, read outcome KPIs as diagnostics with their sample size, set weekly goals you control, and change rules only through a written process.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a balanced KPI set: process KPIs as the grade, outcome KPIs as diagnostics with their sample size\",\"Set weekly goals you fully control\",\"Change a rule only through a written process, not after a few losses\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.13\",\"courseId\":\"2.13.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.13.7-1\",\"q-2.13.7-2\",\"q-2.13.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.1\",\"title\":\"Stocks and ETFs\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Shares and ETFs trade on exchanges in set hours; a dividend moves value from the price into cash, a split changes only the share count, and a leveraged ETF decays in choppy markets.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain what a share and an ETF are, and where and when they trade\",\"Show why buying just before the ex-dividend date gains nothing before costs and taxes\",\"Explain why a leveraged ETF can lose value in a choppy market even when the index ends flat\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.1\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.1-1\",\"q-2.14.1-2\",\"q-2.14.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.2\",\"title\":\"Short selling\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A stock short sells borrowed shares: it pays a borrow fee and every dividend, can be recalled, has no loss cap, and must be sized so a squeeze past the stop is survivable.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how a short sale works: borrowed shares, borrow fee, dividends owed and recall\",\"Size a short so a gap or squeeze beyond the stop is survivable\",\"Name the US short-sale rules that change how orders fill\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bearish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bearish\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.2\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.2-1\",\"q-2.14.2-2\",\"q-2.14.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.3\",\"title\":\"Forex mechanics\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A currency pair prices the base currency in the quote currency; a pip's value per lot depends on the pair, and lots come from the risk budget divided by what the stop costs per lot.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a currency quote: base, quote, pip and pipette\",\"Compute pip value per lot for USD-quoted, yen and USD-base pairs\",\"Size an FX trade in lots from the stop distance in pips and your risk budget\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.3\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.3-1\",\"q-2.14.3-2\",\"q-2.14.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.4\",\"title\":\"Swap and rollover\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Holding FX past the daily rollover pays or earns the interest difference; Wednesday's swap counts three nights, and over a swing trade the total belongs in R.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain the overnight swap: the interest difference you pay or earn for holding FX past rollover\",\"Count the swap nights in a holding period, including the triple Wednesday\",\"Express financing in R for a swing trade, and see why carry has crash risk\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.4\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.4-1\",\"q-2.14.4-2\",\"q-2.14.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.5\",\"title\":\"Indices and CFDs\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Index exposure comes through futures, ETFs, options or CFDs; a CFD is an over-the-counter contract with the provider, funded every night it's held.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain the ways to get index exposure: futures, ETFs, options or CFDs\",\"Explain what a CFD is: an over-the-counter contract with the provider as counterparty\",\"Compute CFD overnight funding over different holding periods\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.5\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.5-1\",\"q-2.14.5-2\",\"q-2.14.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.6\",\"title\":\"Margin, margin call and negative balance protection\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Required margin is exposure divided by maximum leverage; equity, free margin and margin level show how near a close-out is, and a close-out means the size was wrong.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute required margin, free margin and margin level for a leveraged position\",\"Find the price at which a 50% margin close-out would hit\",\"Explain why a close-out is a sizing failure, and what negative balance protection does and doesn't cover\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.6\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.6-1\",\"q-2.14.6-2\",\"q-2.14.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.7\",\"title\":\"Costs by instrument\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Every trade pays a stack of costs; the tradability ratio compares movement per unit of cost, and the holding period decides whether a CFD or the stock itself is cheaper.\",\"objectives\":[\"List every cost of a trade: spread, commission, slippage, financing, borrow, taxes and opportunity cost\",\"Compare instruments by movement per unit of cost (the tradability ratio)\",\"Find the holding period at which the cheaper vehicle to enter becomes the more expensive one to hold\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.7\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.7-1\",\"q-2.14.7-2\",\"q-2.14.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"2.14.8\",\"title\":\"Rules by jurisdiction\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Retail rules on leverage, close-out, protection and day trading differ between the EU, the UK and the US and change over time: treat each as a dated fact, and check licences at the source.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the main retail rules for CFDs and FX in the EU, the UK and the US, with their dates\",\"Explain the history of the US pattern day trader rule and what replaced it in 2026\",\"Check a broker's licence on the regulator's own register before trusting it\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"2.14\",\"courseId\":\"2.14.8\",\"check\":[\"q-2.14.8-1\",\"q-2.14.8-2\",\"q-2.14.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.1\",\"title\":\"Liquidity is resting orders\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Chart 'liquidity' is resting orders: limits and take-profits that absorb flow and stop-losses that become market orders, sitting where people plausibly put them, without pulling price toward them.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the three kinds of resting orders behind chart \\\"liquidity\\\" (limit entries, take-profits, stop-losses) and say which of them become market orders when price reaches them.\",\"Mark at least four plausible clustering places on a chart (round numbers, just beyond swing extremes, prior-day high and low, range edges) before the reveal.\",\"Explain why liquidity being present at a level does not mean price is attracted to it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.1-1\",\"q-3.1.1-2\",\"q-3.1.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.2\",\"title\":\"Where stops cluster: the Osler evidence\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Osler's bank data put take-profits on round numbers and stop-losses just beyond them, so levels tend to stall or speed price, and your own stop belongs outside the crowd.\",\"objectives\":[\"State Osler's findings: take-profits cluster at round numbers, stop-loss buys just above them and stop-loss sells just below them.\",\"Predict whether a level is more likely to stall price (take-profit cluster) or to accelerate it (stop cluster), and explain why.\",\"Move a protective stop outside the crowded band and resize so the loss at the stop stays within 100.00 vUSD including costs.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.2-1\",\"q-3.1.2-2\",\"q-3.1.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.3\",\"title\":\"Cascades, sweeps and breakouts\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Triggered stops walk a thin book and can set off more stops; a break is a sweep or a breakout only once the bar closes, so the invalidation and the size come first.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how triggered stops become market orders that walk a thin book and can trigger further stops (a cascade).\",\"Classify a level break as a sweep or a breakout only after the close, with a written rule: close back inside within one bar, or close beyond with follow-through.\",\"Write an invalidation level and a size before acting on a break, instead of a guess about anyone's intent.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.3-1\",\"q-3.1.3-2\",\"q-3.1.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.4\",\"title\":\"Equal highs, equal lows and reference levels\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Equal highs and lows defined by a tolerance in ATR, the reference levels everyone plots, and why only a test against random levels at the same distance can show they matter.\",\"objectives\":[\"Detect equal highs and equal lows with an explicit tolerance in ATR instead of by eye.\",\"List the widely watched reference levels (prior-day and prior-week high and low, session high and low, opening price, round numbers) and plot them automatically.\",\"Compare how often price breaks or holds at equal highs with random levels at the same distance, and say whether the difference is beyond noise.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.4-1\",\"q-3.1.4-2\",\"q-3.1.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.5\",\"title\":\"Imbalances, gaps and voids\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The two meanings of imbalance, the three-candle gap read exactly from OHLC and measured in ATR, and why 'gaps fill' is mostly the base rate of revisiting nearby prices.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell apart the two meanings of \\\"imbalance\\\": order-flow imbalance (more aggressive buying than selling) and price imbalance (a fast one-sided move with little two-sided trading).\",\"Identify a three-candle gap (fair value gap) exactly from OHLC and measure it in ATR.\",\"Compare the revisit rate of gaps with that of random levels at the same distance and explain why \\\"gaps fill\\\" is mostly a base rate.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.5-1\",\"q-3.1.5-2\",\"q-3.1.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.6\",\"title\":\"Zone grading and hindsight: supply and demand under test\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Grade supply and demand zones with written criteria, lock them before the session, test the grades against random zones of the same width, and measure what hindsight adds.\",\"objectives\":[\"Grade supply and demand zones with written criteria (departure, time at base, freshness, location) and lock them before the session.\",\"Test whether higher-graded zones react more often than lower-graded zones and random zones of the same width.\",\"Measure how much drawing zones after the fact inflates their apparent success.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.6-1\",\"q-3.1.6-2\",\"q-3.1.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.1.7\",\"title\":\"Trapped inventory: stops, break-even exits and role reversal\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"After a failed breakout, trapped positions have a size, an average price and two exits, their stops and their break-even, which also explain role reversal at a retest.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read the size and average price of trapped positions from the x-ray after a failed breakout.\",\"Locate the two places where trapped traders exit (their stops and their break-even price) and say what each does to price.\",\"Explain role reversal (broken resistance acting as support, or the reverse) through break-even exits, and compare retests with random levels.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.1\",\"courseId\":\"3.1.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.1.7-1\",\"q-3.1.7-2\",\"q-3.1.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.1\",\"title\":\"Venues and market structures\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Orders meet in a central book, against a dealer, through a broker or in a mix of these, and in spot FX and CFDs no single book or volume figure exists.\",\"objectives\":[\"Classify a market as order-driven, quote-driven, brokered or hybrid from how you trade in it.\",\"Explain why spot FX and CFDs have no single central book and no consolidated volume.\",\"Name your counterparty in an exchange account, a dealer FX account and a CFD account.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.1-1\",\"q-3.2.1-2\",\"q-3.2.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.2\",\"title\":\"Reading the book: L1, L2, L3 and depth\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The book shows the best quotes (L1), the size at each price (L2) and every order (L3); a market order walks it, and the depth it walks can vanish before news.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read the best bid and ask, sizes and depth from L1 and L2, and say what L3 adds.\",\"Compute the average fill price of a market order that walks the book.\",\"Explain why displayed size is an intention, not a commitment, and name the illegal practice that exploits it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.2-1\",\"q-3.2.2-2\",\"q-3.2.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.3\",\"title\":\"Market makers and adverse selection\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A market maker earns the spread but loses to better-informed traders and carries inventory, so quotes widen, skew or vanish before news, and resting limits fill at the worst moments.\",\"objectives\":[\"Split a market maker's P&L into spread captured, adverse selection and inventory.\",\"Explain why quotes widen and skew before news and when inventory builds.\",\"Show why a resting limit order fills most often when the short-term move goes against it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.3-1\",\"q-3.2.3-2\",\"q-3.2.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.4\",\"title\":\"Queue priority\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"At one price, the matching rule decides who fills: under FIFO your place in the queue, under pro-rata your size, and some changes to an order send it to the back.\",\"objectives\":[\"Find your queue position at a price and predict whether a given traded volume fills you.\",\"Explain which order modifications reset your time priority.\",\"Compare how the same incoming volume is allocated under FIFO and under pro-rata.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.4-1\",\"q-3.2.4-2\",\"q-3.2.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.5\",\"title\":\"Hidden liquidity and order types\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Icebergs, hidden, pegged and post-only orders trade visibility, priority, price and fill certainty against each other, and maker-taker fees change what each fill costs.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how iceberg, hidden, pegged and post-only orders work and what each one trades off.\",\"Explain why a native iceberg and a synthetic one look different to the exchange and in the queue.\",\"Compute maker-taker fees on a round trip.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.5-1\",\"q-3.2.5-2\",\"q-3.2.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.6\",\"title\":\"Who fills you: broker models and last look\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Brokers pass your order on, match it in a shared book or take the other side; regulation manages the B-book conflict, and a reject report shows whether slippage only ever goes against you.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify an agency/STP, ECN, B-book or hybrid broker model from the client agreement.\",\"Explain the B-book conflict (client losses are broker revenue) and how regulation manages it.\",\"Read a last-look reject report and spot asymmetric slippage.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.6-1\",\"q-3.2.6-2\",\"q-3.2.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.7\",\"title\":\"NBBO, routing, PFOF and best execution\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"The NBBO is the best bid and offer across US exchanges; most retail orders go to wholesalers that pay brokers for them, and only execution quality shows what zero commission cost.\",\"objectives\":[\"Find the NBBO from several venues' quotes and explain why a direct feed can be faster than the consolidated quote.\",\"Describe how payment for order flow and internalisation work, and what best execution requires.\",\"Read an execution-quality report (price improvement, fill rate, speed) and compare two brokers net of commission.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.7-1\",\"q-3.2.7-2\",\"q-3.2.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.2.8\",\"title\":\"Opening and closing auctions\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A call auction collects orders and matches them at the one price that trades the most volume; imbalance messages invite offsetting orders, the close sets the index price, and halted markets reopen the same way.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the uncross price of a call auction as the price that maximises executed volume.\",\"Read indicative price, paired volume and imbalance messages and decide whether to supply liquidity against an imbalance.\",\"Explain why closing prices carry index flows and why a halted market reopens through an auction.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.2\",\"courseId\":\"3.2.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.2.8-1\",\"q-3.2.8-2\",\"q-3.2.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.1\",\"title\":\"The tape: time and sales\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Reading time-and-sales prints by price, size and aggressor, and why the rules that guess the aggressor misclassify a share of trades.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a time-and-sales print (time, price, size, aggressor) and classify it as buyer- or seller-initiated.\",\"Explain why every trade has a buyer and a seller, and what \\\"aggressor\\\" adds.\",\"Say how trade-classification rules (tick rule, Lee–Ready) go wrong and why spot FX \\\"volume\\\" is a tick count.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.1-1\",\"q-3.3.1-2\",\"q-3.3.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.2\",\"title\":\"The DOM ladder\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Reading a depth-of-market ladder, naming four ways displayed size behaves, and why a large displayed order is a clue, not a commitment.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a DOM ladder: resting size at each price, last trades and your own working orders.\",\"Name four size behaviours (pulling, stacking, reloading, absorption) and what each can and cannot show.\",\"Avoid trading against a large displayed order that may disappear.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.2-1\",\"q-3.3.2-2\",\"q-3.3.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.3\",\"title\":\"Footprint charts\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Reading a footprint bar's bid and ask volume at each price, its delta and POC, and the diagonal and stacked imbalances that describe the past, not the future.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a footprint bar: bid × ask volume per price, bar delta and bar POC.\",\"Find diagonal imbalances at a 3:1 threshold and stacked imbalances.\",\"Explain why an imbalance describes what happened, not what must happen next.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.3-1\",\"q-3.3.3-2\",\"q-3.3.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.4\",\"title\":\"Delta and cumulative delta\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Bar delta and cumulative volume delta from a chosen anchor, delta divergences that need price confirmation, and rallies built by sellers stepping back.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute bar delta and a cumulative volume delta from a chosen anchor.\",\"Spot a delta divergence and say what price confirmation it needs.\",\"Tell \\\"aggressive buying\\\" from \\\"sellers withdrawing\\\" as causes of a rally.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.4-1\",\"q-3.3.4-2\",\"q-3.3.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.5\",\"title\":\"Absorption and exhaustion\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Absorption as heavy aggressive volume without price progress, exhaustion as aggression drying up at an extreme, and the price or condition that invalidates each read.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise absorption: heavy aggressive volume at a level without price progress.\",\"Recognise exhaustion: aggressive volume drying up at the extreme of a move.\",\"State what invalidates each read: the level breaks, or volume returns.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.5-1\",\"q-3.3.5-2\",\"q-3.3.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.6\",\"title\":\"Icebergs: detection\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Spotting an iceberg from traded volume that exceeds the displayed size, estimating the minimum hidden size, and why a synthetic iceberg looks like many new orders.\",\"objectives\":[\"Detect an iceberg from traded volume exceeding displayed size and repeated refills at one price.\",\"Estimate the minimum hidden size from the tape.\",\"Explain why a synthetic iceberg looks like a series of new orders to the exchange.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.6-1\",\"q-3.3.6-2\",\"q-3.3.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.7\",\"title\":\"Spoofing and layering: illegal\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"What spoofing is and why it's illegal, the surveillance signature regulators look for, and why legitimate layering or one pulled order is no proof.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define spoofing (bidding or offering with the intent to cancel before execution) and say why it is illegal in US futures and under EU market-abuse rules.\",\"Recognise the surveillance signature: large orders away from the touch, frequent modifications, a high cancel-to-fill ratio and trades on the opposite side.\",\"Tell legitimate layering of genuine orders from spoofing, and explain why a pulled order alone is not proof.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.7-1\",\"q-3.3.7-2\",\"q-3.3.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.3.8\",\"title\":\"Market Profile and the auction\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Building a TPO profile from 30-minute periods, finding its POC, value area and initial balance, naming the day type, and testing the claims made about them.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a TPO profile from 30-minute periods and find the POC, the 70% value area and the initial balance.\",\"Classify a session as balance or imbalance and name its day type at the close.\",\"Test the \\\"80% rule\\\" and value-area claims against random bands and the R0 control.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.3\",\"courseId\":\"3.3.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.3.8-1\",\"q-3.3.8-2\",\"q-3.3.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.1\",\"title\":\"Putting a method on trial\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"How to put any trading method on trial: a testable claim, a random-walk control, a market with the structure, costs and a grade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Turn a method's claim into a testable statement with a rule, an event, an outcome, a horizon and a comparison.\",\"Explain why every method needs one run on the R0 control and one on a market with the relevant structure.\",\"Pre-register a test (rules, parameters, sample size) before seeing any result.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.1-1\",\"q-3.4.1-2\",\"q-3.4.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.2\",\"title\":\"Fibonacci in depth: projections, clusters and the anchor problem\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Three-point projections and clusters, why more anchors can explain any turn, and what a pre-registered test finds on R0 and on a market with Fibonacci watchers.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute three-point Fibonacci projections and build a cluster from several swings.\",\"Show how anchor choice multiplies the available levels, so a past reversal can almost always be \\\"explained\\\".\",\"Compare reversal rates at Fibonacci levels on R0 and on a market where some agents watch Fibonacci, with a mechanical anchor rule fixed in advance.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.2-1\",\"q-3.4.2-2\",\"q-3.4.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.3\",\"title\":\"Elliott Wave I: the rules\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The five-wave impulse and three-wave corrections, the three hard rules that can break a count, and what is only a guideline.\",\"objectives\":[\"Label a five-wave impulse and a three-wave correction.\",\"Check a count against the three hard rules and find the rule a count breaks.\",\"Separate rules (must hold) from guidelines (tendencies) and wave degrees.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.3-1\",\"q-3.4.3-2\",\"q-3.4.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.4\",\"title\":\"Elliott Wave II: the alternate-count lab\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"One random-walk path, three valid wave counts with opposite forecasts, and the only thing a count can defend: an invalidation level and a size.\",\"objectives\":[\"Produce a valid wave count on a random-walk path.\",\"Show that at least two other valid counts exist with opposite forecasts.\",\"Use a count only for what it can defend: an invalidation level and a position sized from it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.4-1\",\"q-3.4.4-2\",\"q-3.4.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.5\",\"title\":\"Wyckoff: reading ranges\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Wyckoff's accumulation events and three laws, why the Composite Man is a study device, and how often a 'spring' turns out to be a breakdown.\",\"objectives\":[\"Label Wyckoff events (selling climax, automatic rally, secondary test, spring, sign of strength, last point of support) on a generated accumulation range.\",\"State the three laws and explain why the Composite Man is a study heuristic, not a fact about manipulation.\",\"Label events in real time and count how many \\\"springs\\\" turned into breakdowns.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.5-1\",\"q-3.4.5-2\",\"q-3.4.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.6\",\"title\":\"Harmonic patterns\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"XABCD patterns and their ratios, how the tolerance decides how many you find, and what the potential reversal zone does against random depths.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a Gartley from its X, A, B, C and D points and check its ratios.\",\"Show how the number of detected patterns explodes as the ratio tolerance loosens.\",\"Compare reactions at the potential reversal zone with random five-point zigzags of the same leg lengths.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.6-1\",\"q-3.4.6-2\",\"q-3.4.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.7\",\"title\":\"Ichimoku as a complete system\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The full Ichimoku rule set as one mechanical system, tested after costs against its simplest part and across neighbouring parameters.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write the full Ichimoku trading rules (filter, trigger, confirmation, exit and stop) as a mechanical system.\",\"Compare the system with its simplest component, a Donchian-midline trend rule, after costs on trend, range and random-walk markets.\",\"Check whether the default parameters sit on a plateau or on a lucky spike by testing neighbouring settings.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.7-1\",\"q-3.4.7-2\",\"q-3.4.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.4.8\",\"title\":\"Gann, cycles and the esoteric\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Why Gann angles depend on chart scale, why spectral analysis finds 'cycles' in noise, and how to test an esoteric claim instead of mocking it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Show that a Gann 1×1 angle depends on chart scaling by rescaling the axes.\",\"Find \\\"cycles\\\" with spectral analysis on pure noise and explain why they appear.\",\"Evaluate an esoteric claim (astrology, numerology) with the trial protocol instead of accepting or mocking it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.4\",\"courseId\":\"3.4.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.4.8-1\",\"q-3.4.8-2\",\"q-3.4.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.5.1\",\"title\":\"Where SMC comes from and how to read it\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"SMC is a retail vocabulary with no official rulebook; sort each claim into mechanics, statistical tendency or narrative, and check who earns from selling it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe where ICT and SMC come from and why there is no official rulebook.\",\"Sort any trading claim into mechanics, statistical tendency or narrative.\",\"Spot the conflicts of interest in how a method is sold (courses, paid indicators, prop-challenge funnels).\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision making check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.5\",\"courseId\":\"3.5.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.5.1-1\",\"q-3.5.1-2\",\"q-3.5.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.5.2\",\"title\":\"Structure words: BOS, CHoCH, MSS, displacement\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"BOS, CHoCH, MSS and displacement rename Dow structure and momentum; their labels depend on the swing length and the close rule, and a CHoCH predicts nothing on a random walk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Map BOS, CHoCH, MSS and displacement to Dow-theory structure and momentum.\",\"Show that structure labels change with the swing length N and with the close-versus-wick rule.\",\"Test a CHoCH reversal rule against the R0 control.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.5\",\"courseId\":\"3.5.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.5.2-1\",\"q-3.5.2-2\",\"q-3.5.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.5.3\",\"title\":\"Liquidity words: BSL, SSL, sweeps, inducement\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"SMC's liquidity words rename stop clustering and failed breakouts: stops really do cluster and speed price up, but \\\"sweep, then reverse\\\" is contradicted by the evidence.\",\"objectives\":[\"Map buy-side and sell-side liquidity, equal highs and lows, sweeps, inducement, the Judas swing and turtle soup to stop clustering and failed breakouts.\",\"State which part is documented (stops cluster and accelerate price) and which is contradicted (sweeps reverse).\",\"Rewrite one liquidity claim as a testable hypothesis (H1).\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.5\",\"courseId\":\"3.5.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.5.3-1\",\"q-3.5.3-2\",\"q-3.5.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.5.4\",\"title\":\"Zone words: order blocks, FVGs, premium and discount\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Order blocks, breakers, FVGs, premium and discount and OTE rename supply and demand zones, role reversal, gaps, the 50% rule and Fibonacci; tested strictly, order blocks reacted like random zones.\",\"objectives\":[\"Map order block, breaker, mitigation block, FVG, consequent encroachment, premium and discount and OTE to supply and demand zones, role reversal, gaps, the 50% rule and Fibonacci 61.8–78.6%.\",\"Apply a strict order-block rule set and notice how \\\"mitigation\\\" can explain every outcome.\",\"Test order-block reactions against random candles at equal distance (H3).\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.5\",\"courseId\":\"3.5.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.5.4-1\",\"q-3.5.4-2\",\"q-3.5.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.5.5\",\"title\":\"Time and algorithm words: killzones, PO3, IPDA, SMT\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Killzones rename session volatility, which is real; the direction claimed for each window is not shown, PO3 fits any candle after it closes, and IPDA can't be falsified.\",\"objectives\":[\"Map killzones, the Silver Bullet, \\\"macros\\\", the Power of Three, the Asian range and SMT divergence to session volatility, Wyckoff cycles and Dow's confirmation principle.\",\"Separate the documented part (volatility follows the session clock) from the undocumented part (direction by time window).\",\"Explain why the IPDA claim cannot be falsified and conflicts with what is known about FX market structure.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"technical_analysis\",\"label\":\"Technical analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.5\",\"courseId\":\"3.5.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.5.5-1\",\"q-3.5.5-2\",\"q-3.5.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.5.6\",\"title\":\"SMC claim-test lab\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":9,\"summary\":\"Turn one SMC idea into frozen rules, run it in-sample, out-of-sample, on the random walk and after costs, and grade it yourself with the result that would change your mind.\",\"objectives\":[\"Turn one SMC idea into mechanical rules with every parameter exposed.\",\"Run it in-sample, out-of-sample, on R0 and with costs, and report the result with its sample size.\",\"Grade the claim yourself (A–F) and write which result would change your mind.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"false_breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: false breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.5\",\"courseId\":\"3.5.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.5.6-1\",\"q-3.5.6-2\",\"q-3.5.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.1\",\"title\":\"Rates: the price of money\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Bond prices fall when yields rise, long maturities most; subtract expected inflation for the real yield; and rate differentials push currencies.\",\"objectives\":[\"Convert between bond price and yield, and explain why long bonds move more for the same change in yield.\",\"Compute a real yield from a nominal yield and expected inflation.\",\"Explain how a change in the rate differential moves a currency pair.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.1-1\",\"q-3.6.1-2\",\"q-3.6.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.2\",\"title\":\"The yield curve\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Read the curve's slope and its four moves, and treat an inversion as a long-lead recession warning with false alarms, not a signal to sell stocks.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a yield curve and compute the 10y–2y and 10y–3m spreads.\",\"Name the four curve moves (bull or bear, steepener or flattener) from changes in short and long yields.\",\"Explain why an inversion is a long-lead recession signal with false positives, not a timing signal for equities.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.2-1\",\"q-3.6.2-2\",\"q-3.6.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.3\",\"title\":\"Inflation and regimes\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The inflation backdrop sets the central bank's reaction, so the same data move assets differently and the stock–bond correlation can change sign.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how the inflation backdrop changes a central bank's reaction function and therefore how assets react to the same data.\",\"Show that the stock–bond correlation depends on the monetary regime, using the 2022 case.\",\"Identify the regime from a pre-event dashboard before predicting a reaction.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.3-1\",\"q-3.6.3-2\",\"q-3.6.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.4\",\"title\":\"The dollar\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Rate and growth differentials drive the dollar over time; in global stress it tends to rise as a haven, except when a funding-currency unwind pulls it down.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain the main drivers of the dollar: rate and growth differentials, and safe-haven demand under stress.\",\"Place a world state on the dollar smile and predict the dollar's direction.\",\"Recognise an exception, such as a funding-currency unwind, where the smile fails.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.4-1\",\"q-3.6.4-2\",\"q-3.6.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.5\",\"title\":\"Commodities and commodity currencies\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Oil follows supply shocks, copper industrial demand, gold real yields and official buying; exporters' currencies follow their terms of trade, loosely.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the main driver of oil, copper and gold: supply shocks, industrial demand, real yields plus official demand.\",\"Link a commodity price to its exporter's currency through the terms of trade.\",\"Explain why the gold–real-yield relationship broke in 2022–24.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.5-1\",\"q-3.6.5-2\",\"q-3.6.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.6\",\"title\":\"Risk-on, risk-off and carry\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"In risk-off, risky assets fall together and havens rise; carry pays small steady gains and loses big ones fast, so size it for the crash.\",\"objectives\":[\"Predict the cross-asset reaction to a risk-off shock: equities, high-beta currencies, credit and safe havens.\",\"Explain why correlations rise toward 1 in a crisis and what that does to diversification.\",\"Show the negative skew of a carry trade: months of small gains erased in days.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.6-1\",\"q-3.6.6-2\",\"q-3.6.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.7\",\"title\":\"Business cycle and sector rotation\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Leading data hint at the cycle phase and the textbook sector leaders, but phases are clear only in hindsight, and rotation's edge mostly vanishes after costs.\",\"objectives\":[\"Classify the cycle phase from leading data (PMI new orders, jobless claims, curve slope, credit spreads).\",\"Name the textbook sector leaders in each phase and why they lead.\",\"Show that the rotation edge shrinks after costs and phase-timing errors.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.7-1\",\"q-3.6.7-2\",\"q-3.6.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.6.8\",\"title\":\"When correlations flip\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Correlations are footprints of the dominant shock: measure them on a rolling window, know its noise, and size hedged books as if both legs can lose together.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute and read a rolling correlation and say how many observations it rests on.\",\"Name three historical correlation breaks (stock–bond in 2022, gold–real yields in 2022–24, the yen as a safe haven) and their cause.\",\"Size a \\\"hedged\\\" portfolio for the case in which both legs lose together.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.6\",\"courseId\":\"3.6.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.6.8-1\",\"q-3.6.8-2\",\"q-3.6.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.1\",\"title\":\"Reaction functions: modelling the central bank\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A central bank's reaction function maps inflation and jobs into rate decisions; a simple rule shows how the data, or a change in the rule, reprice the whole expected path.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute a policy-rate prescription from a simple rule that uses inflation and unemployment.\",\"Estimate a synthetic central bank's rule weights from its past decisions and predict its next decision.\",\"Explain how a change in the reaction function reprices the whole expected rate path, not just the next meeting.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.1-1\",\"q-3.7.1-2\",\"q-3.7.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.2\",\"title\":\"Reading implied probabilities\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Rate futures price the month's average expected rate; weighting the days before and after a meeting turns that price into a hike probability, which is a price, not a poll.\",\"objectives\":[\"Convert a rate-futures price into an implied rate (100 minus the price).\",\"Compute the probability of a hike with day-weighting inside the meeting month.\",\"Explain why a market-implied probability is a price, not a poll, and contains risk premia.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.2-1\",\"q-3.7.2-2\",\"q-3.7.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.3\",\"title\":\"Statements, votes and tone\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Read a statement as edits against the last one, score each edit for tone, and judge the result against the tone the market had priced; votes and dissents hint at the next move.\",\"objectives\":[\"Find the changed phrases between two policy statements and rate their tone from −2 (very dovish) to +2 (very hawkish).\",\"Read a vote split and dissents as information about the next move.\",\"Explain why markets react to tone relative to what was expected, not to tone alone.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.3-1\",\"q-3.7.3-2\",\"q-3.7.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.4\",\"title\":\"Press conference, dots and forward guidance\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"One dot can move a dot plot's median, but prices move only where the dots differ from the priced path; plan the press conference in advance and treat guidance as conditional.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the median of a 19-dot plot and show how one participant can move it.\",\"Compare a dot-plot shift with what futures had already priced, and plan for the press conference's 30–60 minutes of headline whipsaw.\",\"Distinguish calendar, threshold and qualitative forward guidance, and what happens when guidance is abandoned.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.4-1\",\"q-3.7.4-2\",\"q-3.7.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.5\",\"title\":\"Balance sheets, caps and interventions\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"QE lowers long yields mainly through the term premium and signalling, QT is not its mirror image, caps and pegs store pressure that exits release as gaps, and intervention follows a ladder of warnings.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how QE lowers long yields through the term premium and signalling, and why QT is not its mirror image.\",\"Describe yield-curve control and why exiting a cap or a peg can gap the market.\",\"Recognise the ladder of verbal warnings that usually comes before currency intervention.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.5-1\",\"q-3.7.5-2\",\"q-3.7.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.6\",\"title\":\"Event strategies under test: fade and straddle\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":9,\"summary\":\"A pending-order straddle can fill on the spike and stop out on the reversal, fading spikes is practitioner lore, and every event policy is compared on the same events, after costs, against flat.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the result of a pending-order straddle that fills on a spike and stops out on the reversal, with slippage and commissions.\",\"Explain when fading a spike worked in the simulator (small surprise, conflicting details) and when it failed.\",\"Compare fading and straddling with the stand-aside, reduce and second-move policies from 2.10 on the same 30 events, after costs.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.6-1\",\"q-3.7.6-2\",\"q-3.7.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.7\",\"title\":\"Pre-event drift and positioning\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A documented pre-announcement drift needs hundreds of events to tell apart from noise and tends to weaken once published; how price reacts to news hints at positioning.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe the pre-FOMC drift finding and why a published anomaly should be expected to weaken.\",\"Compute how many events are needed before a small drift can be told apart from noise.\",\"Read positioning from reactions: a market that falls on good news is heavy.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.7-1\",\"q-3.7.7-2\",\"q-3.7.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.7.8\",\"title\":\"Tail events: when stops don't protect\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":9,\"summary\":\"In a tail event liquidity vanishes and stops fill at the first available price; size for the gap, cap leverage, and know your broker's protections before you need them.\",\"objectives\":[\"Retell what happened to liquidity and stop orders in the SNB floor removal (2015) and the Flash Crash (2010).\",\"Size a position for a gap scenario instead of for the stop, and compute the loss with and without negative balance protection.\",\"Write a tail-risk rule set for your plan: leverage cap, gap sizing, event exposure, broker protections.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.7\",\"courseId\":\"3.7.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.7.8-1\",\"q-3.7.8-2\",\"q-3.7.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.1\",\"title\":\"Three statements, one company\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Net income links the income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement, and receivables, inventory and capex explain why profit and cash can move apart.\",\"objectives\":[\"Trace how net income flows into the balance sheet and the cash-flow statement.\",\"Compute operating cash flow and free cash flow from given line items.\",\"Explain why profit and cash can move in opposite directions.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.1-1\",\"q-3.8.1-2\",\"q-3.8.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.2\",\"title\":\"Earnings quality and key ratios\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Adjusted EPS can hide recurring costs, dilution and capex; cash per share, growth without buybacks and leverage ratios show what the profit is worth.\",\"objectives\":[\"Rank companies by free cash flow per share when their adjusted EPS is identical.\",\"Separate EPS growth that comes from the business from growth that comes from buybacks.\",\"Use leverage and coverage ratios to flag a fragile balance sheet.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.2-1\",\"q-3.8.2-2\",\"q-3.8.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.3\",\"title\":\"Multiples: what the price already assumes\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"P/E and EV/EBITDA summarise what the price assumes; EV counts debt and cash, and fast growers are the most sensitive to discount rates.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute P/E, enterprise value and EV/EBITDA from price, shares, debt and cash.\",\"Explain why EV multiples compare companies with different debt fairly.\",\"Predict which stock falls more when discount rates rise one point.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.3-1\",\"q-3.8.3-2\",\"q-3.8.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.4\",\"title\":\"DCF and what the price implies\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A DCF values five years of free cash flow plus a terminal value; run in reverse, it shows the growth that today's price already assumes.\",\"objectives\":[\"Value a company as the present value of five years of free cash flow plus a terminal value.\",\"Measure how much of the value depends on the terminal value and on the discount rate.\",\"Reverse a DCF to find the growth that today's price implies.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.4-1\",\"q-3.8.4-2\",\"q-3.8.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.5\",\"title\":\"Earnings season: expectations, surprise and the gap\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A beat is the base case; the surprise's size and guidance move price, and a position held through a report is sized for the gap, not the stop.\",\"objectives\":[\"Treat a beat as the base case and judge a report by the size of the surprise and by guidance.\",\"Size a position held through earnings so a gap past the stop costs at most 2R.\",\"Name the extended-hours risks: thin liquidity, limit-only orders, stops that fill at the open.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.5-1\",\"q-3.8.5-2\",\"q-3.8.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.6\",\"title\":\"Guidance and the reaction matrix\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Results and guidance place a report in a 3×3 matrix: aligned cells lean clearly, mixed cells are wide coin flips, and a habitual sandbagger's raise is already priced.\",\"objectives\":[\"Place an earnings report in the results × guidance matrix.\",\"Predict the gap sign for the clear cells and flag the mixed cells as uncertain.\",\"Explain why a habitual sandbagger's raise is already in the price.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.6-1\",\"q-3.8.6-2\",\"q-3.8.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.7\",\"title\":\"Implied move and IV crush\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The at-the-money straddle prices the expected earnings move; after the report implied volatility collapses, so a call can lose even when the stock rises.\",\"objectives\":[\"Estimate the market's implied earnings move from the at-the-money straddle price.\",\"Explain why a call can lose money after the stock rises.\",\"Compare realised moves with implied moves before calling options cheap or rich.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.7-1\",\"q-3.8.7-2\",\"q-3.8.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.8\",\"title\":\"Post-earnings drift (PEAD)\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Prices once drifted for weeks after big earnings surprises; since publication the effect has shrunk, and it survives mainly where costs are highest.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute a standardised unexpected earnings (SUE) score.\",\"Describe PEAD and how much of it has disappeared since publication.\",\"Find the cost at which a drift portfolio stops paying, and where the drift still survives.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.8-1\",\"q-3.8.8-2\",\"q-3.8.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.8.9\",\"title\":\"Corporate events: buybacks, offerings, mergers, index changes\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Buybacks, offerings, takeovers and index changes have typical reactions; a merger spread prices the deal's odds, and splits and dividends must be adjusted out of price history.\",\"objectives\":[\"Predict the typical price reaction to a buyback, a share offering, a takeover bid and an index inclusion.\",\"Compute the deal-success probability implied by a merger spread.\",\"Adjust price history for splits and dividends so indicators and backtests show no fake gaps.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.8\",\"courseId\":\"3.8.9\",\"check\":[\"q-3.8.9-1\",\"q-3.8.9-2\",\"q-3.8.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.9.1\",\"title\":\"Sentiment is a condition, not a trigger\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Reading a weekly sentiment survey against its own history, and why extremes shift reversal odds without timing the turn.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read a weekly sentiment survey as a bull–bear spread against its own history.\",\"Explain why extremes raise the odds of a reversal but do not time it.\",\"Show that most survey swings follow price instead of leading it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"reversal\",\"label\":\"Arena: reversal\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.9\",\"courseId\":\"3.9.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.9.1-1\",\"q-3.9.1-2\",\"q-3.9.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.9.2\",\"title\":\"Commitments of Traders\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Who holds what in futures: the COT report families, a three-year COT index, the reporting lag, and why hedgers' shorts aren't bets.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the COT report families and their trader categories.\",\"Compute a three-year COT index from net positions.\",\"Explain the reporting lag and why commercial hedgers' shorts are not bearish bets.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.9\",\"courseId\":\"3.9.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.9.2-1\",\"q-3.9.2-2\",\"q-3.9.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.9.3\",\"title\":\"VIX and put/call: fear priced in options\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Turning the VIX into an expected move, why it spikes in sell-offs and mean-reverts, and reading put/call ratios against a shifting baseline.\",\"objectives\":[\"Convert a VIX level into an expected one-month and one-week move.\",\"Explain why the VIX jumps when stocks fall and then mean-reverts.\",\"Read a put/call ratio against its baseline and see why structural shifts break fixed thresholds.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.9\",\"courseId\":\"3.9.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.9.3-1\",\"q-3.9.3-2\",\"q-3.9.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.9.4\",\"title\":\"Positioning, short interest and squeezes\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Days to cover and short interest as squeeze fuel, how forced buying dwarfs the news that starts it, and how not to be exit liquidity.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute days-to-cover from short interest and average volume.\",\"Explain how forced buying turns a small surprise into a large squeeze.\",\"Spot a social-media pump and avoid becoming someone's exit liquidity.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.9\",\"courseId\":\"3.9.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.9.4-1\",\"q-3.9.4-2\",\"q-3.9.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.9.5\",\"title\":\"Contrarian signals that wait for price\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Writing a contrarian rule as a condition plus a price trigger, and comparing naive and confirmed fades after costs, in and out of sample.\",\"objectives\":[\"Combine a sentiment extreme with a price-reversal confirmation into one written rule.\",\"Compare naive fades with confirmed fades in the strategy lab, after costs and out of sample.\",\"Tell the contrarian use of extremes from the trend-confirming use of moderate sentiment.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.9\",\"courseId\":\"3.9.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.9.5-1\",\"q-3.9.5-2\",\"q-3.9.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.9.6\",\"title\":\"Calendar effects, graded\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Grading calendar effects by evidence and post-publication record, measuring how often noise looks seasonal, and why futures already price a commodity's seasons.\",\"objectives\":[\"Grade popular calendar effects by their evidence and post-publication record.\",\"Show how often pure noise produces a \\\"significant\\\" calendar pattern.\",\"Explain why a commodity's seasonal rise is usually already in the futures curve.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.9\",\"courseId\":\"3.9.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.9.6-1\",\"q-3.9.6-2\",\"q-3.9.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.1\",\"title\":\"Distributions, skew and fat tails\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"An average hides the shape of results: the median, the spread, the skew and the tails decide how a system feels and how badly it can surprise you.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe a return or R distribution by its mean, median, spread, skew and tails.\",\"Estimate how often a five-sigma move appears under normal versus fat-tailed assumptions.\",\"Match strategy styles to their typical skew and the kind of pain each skew brings.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.1-1\",\"q-3.10.1-2\",\"q-3.10.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.2\",\"title\":\"Volatility: measure, scale, cluster\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Volatility scales with the square root of time when days are independent, but it comes in clusters, so an estimate from a calm window fails when the regime switches.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute volatility from returns and scale it with the square root of time.\",\"Explain volatility clustering and why an estimate from a calm window fails after a regime switch.\",\"Distinguish realised volatility from implied volatility.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.2-1\",\"q-3.10.2-2\",\"q-3.10.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.3\",\"title\":\"Regression to the mean\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Every result is edge plus noise; after an extreme result the noise rarely repeats, so the next one tends to sit closer to the true edge.\",\"objectives\":[\"Predict how an extreme early result will look in the next sample when outcomes contain noise.\",\"Tell regression from \\\"the market changed\\\" when a hot setup cools down.\",\"Explain why the best backtest out of many is likely to disappoint.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"psychology\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: psychology\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.3-1\",\"q-3.10.3-2\",\"q-3.10.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.4\",\"title\":\"Base rates and Bayesian updating\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A passed test means little without the base rate: with 10% of ideas real, 80% power and 20% false alarms, a passed idea is real only 31% of the time.\",\"objectives\":[\"Turn a base rate, a test's hit rate and its false-alarm rate into the probability that an idea has a real edge.\",\"Build a natural-frequency tree out of 1,000 ideas.\",\"Update a belief about a setup as trades arrive, without jumping to certainty.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.4-1\",\"q-3.10.4-2\",\"q-3.10.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.5\",\"title\":\"Significance: is your edge distinguishable from zero?\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A t-statistic compares mean R with its standard error; small edges need hundreds of trades to show, and a Sharpe ratio means little without its standard error.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute a t-statistic for mean R and read its p-value correctly.\",\"Estimate how many trades a given edge needs before it is distinguishable from zero.\",\"Read a Sharpe ratio together with its standard error.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.5-1\",\"q-3.10.5-2\",\"q-3.10.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.6\",\"title\":\"Many tests, false discoveries\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Test enough useless rules and some will look significant; count every test, correct the threshold, and run the same search on random-walk data.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the chance that at least one of N useless strategies looks significant.\",\"Apply a simple Bonferroni correction and see what it costs.\",\"Use an R0 random-walk control to expose spurious \\\"discoveries\\\".\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.6-1\",\"q-3.10.6-2\",\"q-3.10.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.10.7\",\"title\":\"Autocorrelation and independence\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Autocorrelation fingerprints trending, mean-reverting and random markets; a runs test checks whether your wins and losses cluster, and clustering breaks tools that assume independence.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read the sign of return autocorrelation as a fingerprint of trending, mean-reverting or random markets.\",\"Run a runs test on a win–loss sequence to check whether outcomes cluster.\",\"Explain which tools break when trades are not independent.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.10\",\"courseId\":\"3.10.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.10.7-1\",\"q-3.10.7-2\",\"q-3.10.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.1\",\"title\":\"From hypothesis to rules\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A testable edge starts as a falsifiable hypothesis that names who is on the other side, then becomes rules exact enough that two people, or code, take the same trades.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write a falsifiable edge hypothesis that names who is on the other side.\",\"Turn it into rules precise enough that two people, or code, produce identical trades.\",\"Prepare data that matches the rules, and test in R before any sizing.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.1-1\",\"q-3.11.1-2\",\"q-3.11.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.2\",\"title\":\"Fills and costs in a backtest\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Conservative fill rules and every cost expressed in R turn a flattering backtest into an honest one; the breakeven cost shows how much room the edge has.\",\"objectives\":[\"Choose conservative fill rules: next-bar execution, stop first when stop and target share a bar, limits filled only when price trades through.\",\"Express every cost as a fraction of R and subtract it from gross expectancy.\",\"Find a strategy's breakeven cost and compare it with the costs you will actually pay.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.2-1\",\"q-3.11.2-2\",\"q-3.11.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.3\",\"title\":\"Look-ahead and survivorship\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Two bugs make worthless rules look brilliant: information that wasn't known yet, and a universe that contains only the survivors.\",\"objectives\":[\"Find the line in a backtest that uses information not available at decision time.\",\"Show how testing only today's survivors inflates results.\",\"Build a pre-test checklist against both biases.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.3-1\",\"q-3.11.3-2\",\"q-3.11.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.4\",\"title\":\"In-sample and out-of-sample\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Develop on in-sample data, then test once on an untouched later stretch; every extra look turns the test into more in-sample, and a short test says little.\",\"objectives\":[\"Split data into an in-sample part for development and an untouched out-of-sample part for one final test.\",\"Explain why peeking at the out-of-sample turns it into in-sample.\",\"Judge whether an out-of-sample result is long enough to mean anything.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.4-1\",\"q-3.11.4-2\",\"q-3.11.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.5\",\"title\":\"Overfitting and the random-walk control\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Many tuned parameters on few trades fit noise, and the best of many zero-edge tries looks impressive by chance; that's why every result runs next to an R0 control.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise an overfit backtest: many parameters, few trades, a perfect curve.\",\"Show that the best of N zero-edge configurations has an impressive in-sample Sharpe by chance alone.\",\"Run an R0 random-walk control next to every result you plan to trust.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.5-1\",\"q-3.11.5-2\",\"q-3.11.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.6\",\"title\":\"Walk-forward analysis\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Walk-forward analysis repeats tune-then-trade on rolling windows and keeps only the unseen segments, which shows how a system would really have been re-optimised.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run a walk-forward test: optimise on one window, trade the next unseen segment, roll forward.\",\"Compute walk-forward efficiency and read the parameter-stability chart.\",\"Choose between anchored and rolling windows, without tuning the walk-forward itself.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.6-1\",\"q-3.11.6-2\",\"q-3.11.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.7\",\"title\":\"Monte Carlo: one backtest, many futures\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Reshuffling and bootstrapping a trade list turn one equity curve into a range of futures, so drawdown plans come from the 95th percentile, not from the one path you happened to see.\",\"objectives\":[\"Reshuffle and bootstrap a trade list to turn one equity curve into a distribution.\",\"Set drawdown expectations from the 95th percentile, not from the single backtest.\",\"Draw an expectation corridor that tells normal variance from a broken system.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.7-1\",\"q-3.11.7-2\",\"q-3.11.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.8\",\"title\":\"Robustness: plateaus, not peaks\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A robust system sits on a broad plateau of parameter values and survives a battery of stress tests, growing weaker but staying positive; a fitted one collapses.\",\"objectives\":[\"Map performance across parameter values and pick a value on a broad plateau.\",\"Run a stress battery: double costs, drop the best trades, add noise, shift start dates, try other markets.\",\"Decide whether a system degrades gracefully or collapses.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.8-1\",\"q-3.11.8-2\",\"q-3.11.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.9\",\"title\":\"Random benchmarks and the edge ratio\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Random-entry twins with the same exits show what your entry adds beyond exits and market drift, and the edge ratio measures the entry on its own.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compare a strategy with 1,000 random-entry twins that use the same exits and holding time.\",\"Compute an edge ratio to test whether the entry beats random.\",\"Separate entry edge, exit effect and market drift, using the benchmark ghost.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"breakout\",\"label\":\"Arena: breakout\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.9\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.9-1\",\"q-3.11.9-2\",\"q-3.11.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.11.10\",\"title\":\"Incubation, kill switches and the research log\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A validated system earns full size in steps: go-live gates and kill criteria written from its Monte Carlo bands, a premortem, and rule changes only through a logged process.\",\"objectives\":[\"Write go-live gates and kill criteria before a system trades, using its Monte Carlo bands.\",\"Run a premortem: list the reasons the system will have failed six months from now.\",\"Change a rule only through the documented process: hypothesis, evidence threshold, test, versioned plan.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.11\",\"courseId\":\"3.11.10\",\"check\":[\"q-3.11.10-1\",\"q-3.11.10-2\",\"q-3.11.10-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.1\",\"title\":\"Geometric growth and volatility drag\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Returns compound by multiplying, so swings pull growth below the average return and the typical path below the average one.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the compound result of a sequence of gains and losses, and its geometric mean.\",\"Estimate volatility drag with g ≈ μ − σ²/2.\",\"Explain why the median trader's path can sit far below the average path.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.1-1\",\"q-3.12.1-2\",\"q-3.12.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.2\",\"title\":\"Same trades, different sizing\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Fixed units, fixed fractional and fixed ratio send the same trades down different paths; none of them can turn a losing list into a winner.\",\"objectives\":[\"Replay one trade list under fixed units, fixed fractional and fixed ratio sizing.\",\"Compute contract counts under fixed ratio for a given delta.\",\"Explain why sizing changes the path and the drawdown but cannot create an edge.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.2-1\",\"q-3.12.2-2\",\"q-3.12.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.3\",\"title\":\"The Kelly criterion\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The Kelly fraction maximises long-run growth for a known edge; above it growth falls, and near twice Kelly it stops.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the Kelly fraction from the win rate and the payoff ratio.\",\"Read the growth curve g(f) and place full, half and double Kelly on it.\",\"Explain why betting above Kelly lowers growth even with a positive edge.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"position_sizing\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: position sizing\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.3-1\",\"q-3.12.3-2\",\"q-3.12.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.4\",\"title\":\"Fractional Kelly and optimal f\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A fraction of Kelly survives an overestimated edge, and optimal f is fitted to the past list's worst loss; choose the size by the drawdown you accept.\",\"objectives\":[\"Show how an overestimated edge turns full Kelly into over-betting.\",\"Compute optimal f on a trade list and the account size per contract it implies.\",\"Choose a fraction of Kelly or of optimal f and justify it with drawdown, not with growth.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"position_sizing\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: position sizing\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.4-1\",\"q-3.12.4-2\",\"q-3.12.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.5\",\"title\":\"Risk of ruin, defined honestly\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Define ruin as the drawdown at which you'd stop, estimate its chance by Monte Carlo, and size so it stays under 1%.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute risk of ruin with the even-payoff formula and know when it stops applying.\",\"Define ruin as the drawdown at which you would stop trading, not as zero.\",\"Estimate risk of ruin by Monte Carlo for your own system and size so it stays under 1%.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.5-1\",\"q-3.12.5-2\",\"q-3.12.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.6\",\"title\":\"Volatility targeting\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Size a position so its normal daily swing fits a fixed budget, resize as the forecast changes, and cap exposure for calm spells.\",\"objectives\":[\"Size a position so its expected daily swing matches a volatility budget.\",\"Rescale exposure when the volatility forecast changes.\",\"Explain the lag problem when volatility jumps faster than the forecast.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.6-1\",\"q-3.12.6-2\",\"q-3.12.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.12.7\",\"title\":\"Equity-curve trading\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Pausing a system below its own equity average helps only when outcomes cluster; keep shadow trades, and fix the average in advance.\",\"objectives\":[\"Apply an equity-curve filter and keep tracking shadow trades while paused.\",\"Measure what the filter saved and what it missed.\",\"Predict when a filter helps (clustered outcomes) and when it hurts (trend following, independent trades).\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.12\",\"courseId\":\"3.12.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.12.7-1\",\"q-3.12.7-2\",\"q-3.12.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.1\",\"title\":\"Time-series momentum and the Turtles\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Time-series momentum is grade A across 58 futures markets; the Turtle rules are one crowded version of it, sized by N.\",\"objectives\":[\"State the time-series momentum rule and its evidence across 58 futures markets.\",\"Compute Turtle N, unit size and the 2N stop for a synthetic futures contract.\",\"Explain why the exact Turtle parameters faded while the underlying effect survived.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"bullish\",\"label\":\"Arena: bullish\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.1-1\",\"q-3.13.1-2\",\"q-3.13.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.2\",\"title\":\"Cross-sectional momentum and its crashes\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Rank by 12-1 return, buy the winners and sell the losers: grade A, with crashes when the losers rebound after a panic.\",\"objectives\":[\"Build a ranked winners-minus-losers portfolio with a 12-1 lookback.\",\"Describe momentum crashes and the market state that comes before them.\",\"Show how volatility scaling changes the depth of a crash.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.2-1\",\"q-3.13.2-2\",\"q-3.13.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.3\",\"title\":\"Carry and its crashes\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Carry pays you to hold an asset while prices sit still: grade A across asset classes, with crashes that arrive together.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the carry earned by an FX position, a rolled futures position and a spot–perp basis trade.\",\"Explain why carry has negative skew and crashes in the same periods across asset classes.\",\"Choose leverage from the crash scenario, not from the average carry.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.3-1\",\"q-3.13.3-2\",\"q-3.13.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.4\",\"title\":\"Testing a seasonal edge\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Scan for a calendar edge, confirm it once on hold-out data, count your false discoveries, and use what survives as a tilt.\",\"objectives\":[\"Test a calendar rule for significance in-sample and confirm it once on hold-out data.\",\"Measure your own detection rate and false-discovery rate across seeded and noise markets.\",\"Use a seasonal effect as a filter or tilt, not as a standalone system, and check the futures curve first.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision making check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.4-1\",\"q-3.13.4-2\",\"q-3.13.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.5\",\"title\":\"Pairs trading and statistical arbitrage\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Trade a spread only when the pair is cointegrated, with a z-score entry, a stop, and a time stop from its half-life.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell correlation from cointegration and test a candidate pair.\",\"Trade a spread with z-score entries, a mean exit, a stop and a time stop based on the half-life.\",\"Recognise a structural break and cap its loss.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.5-1\",\"q-3.13.5-2\",\"q-3.13.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.6\",\"title\":\"Event-driven strategies, systematically\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Measure an event effect as the average abnormal return over many events, with its band, and write the stop rule before the effect decays.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run a simple event study: average abnormal return around many events, not one anecdote.\",\"Build a rule-based PEAD portfolio and evaluate it net of costs over time.\",\"Decide in advance when to stop trading a decaying event effect.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.6-1\",\"q-3.13.6-2\",\"q-3.13.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.7\",\"title\":\"Decay, crowding and regime dependence\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Published edges shrink, crowded ones crash together, and a drawdown is judged against its corridor, regime and R0 control before an edge is called broken.\",\"objectives\":[\"Quantify typical post-publication decay and separate it from statistical selection bias.\",\"Explain crowding and why crowded strategies crash together.\",\"Tell a normal drawdown from a broken edge, using the Monte Carlo corridor and a regime breakdown.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.7-1\",\"q-3.13.7-2\",\"q-3.13.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.13.8\",\"title\":\"Reading a strategy study critically\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A ten-point checklist turns any paper, backtest or vendor claim into an evidence grade you can defend in two sentences.\",\"objectives\":[\"Apply a ten-point checklist to any strategy paper, backtest or vendor claim.\",\"Compare a headline result with an independent replication after costs.\",\"Assign an evidence grade from Mechanic to F and defend it in two sentences.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision making check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.13\",\"courseId\":\"3.13.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.13.8-1\",\"q-3.13.8-2\",\"q-3.13.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.1\",\"title\":\"Futures contracts: specs, margin and daily settlement\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A futures contract turns points into money through its multiplier, settles gains and losses in cash every day, and is sized in whole contracts from the stop, with micros when one contract is too big.\",\"objectives\":[\"Convert price moves into money with a contract's multiplier and tick value.\",\"Track initial, maintenance and variation margin through a losing day.\",\"Size in whole contracts from a stop, and switch to micros when one contract is too big.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.1\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.1-1\",\"q-3.14.1-2\",\"q-3.14.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.2\",\"title\":\"Expiry, roll and continuous charts\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Futures expire, so positions roll to the next contract with one calendar-spread order before expiry or first notice day, and continuous charts are back-adjusted so roll gaps don't fake signals.\",\"objectives\":[\"Read contract month codes and plan a roll before expiry or before first notice day.\",\"Execute a roll as a calendar spread and account for its cost.\",\"Back-adjust a continuous chart so a backtest sees no fake roll gaps.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.2\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.2-1\",\"q-3.14.2-2\",\"q-3.14.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.3\",\"title\":\"Contango, backwardation and roll yield\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A futures curve sits above spot in contango and below it in backwardation; a rolled position pays or earns the gap as each contract converges, which is why commodity ETFs can fall while spot is flat.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell contango from backwardation on a futures curve and name what causes each.\",\"Compute the roll yield a rolled long position earns or pays when spot doesn't move.\",\"Explain why commodity ETFs and VIX products can fall while spot is flat.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.3\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.3-1\",\"q-3.14.3-2\",\"q-3.14.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.4\",\"title\":\"Commodities: supply shocks, reports and limits\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Commodity futures move on supply shocks, storage and scheduled reports; limit moves can lock a market past your stop, so positions held through reports are sized for the gap, and seasons are already in the curve.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the supply, storage and report drivers that move a commodity future.\",\"Size a position held through a scheduled inventory report and a possible limit move.\",\"Explain why a famous seasonal pattern rarely pays once the curve has priced it.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.4\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.4-1\",\"q-3.14.4-2\",\"q-3.14.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.5\",\"title\":\"Crypto markets: 24/7, venues and custody\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Crypto trades around the clock with thin weekends and no closing auction; its larger volatility means smaller positions for the same risk, and coins left on a venue carry that venue's failure risk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe what changes when a market never closes: weekends, thin hours, no closing auction.\",\"Size a crypto position from its much larger volatility so risk per trade stays at 1%.\",\"Rank custody and counterparty risks: exchange failure, stablecoin de-pegs, self-custody.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.5\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.5-1\",\"q-3.14.5-2\",\"q-3.14.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.6\",\"title\":\"Perpetual swaps and funding\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A perpetual swap never expires; funding payments between longs and shorts keep it near spot, cost a leveraged long real money over time, and show crowded positioning without timing anything.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how funding payments keep a perpetual swap near its spot index.\",\"Compute the funding paid or received per interval and per year.\",\"Read persistently positive funding as crowded longs: a condition, not a signal.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.6\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.6-1\",\"q-3.14.6-2\",\"q-3.14.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.7\",\"title\":\"Leverage, liquidation and cascades\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"An isolated long is liquidated about 1 ÷ leverage minus the maintenance margin below entry; set the stop first, size from it, and choose leverage so liquidation sits well beyond the stop.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute an approximate liquidation price for an isolated long from leverage and maintenance margin.\",\"Place the stop well inside the liquidation price and let leverage set margin, not risk.\",\"Explain liquidation cascades, insurance funds and auto-deleveraging, and why cross margin spreads the damage.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.7\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.7-1\",\"q-3.14.7-2\",\"q-3.14.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"3.14.8\",\"title\":\"Bonds and rates for traders\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Bond prices move opposite to yields by about the duration per point; DV01 turns a yield move in basis points into money, and one rate surprise reaches FX carry, funding costs, futures and stock valuations.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain the inverse price–yield link and estimate a price change from duration.\",\"Compute a position's DV01 and its P&L from a yield move in basis points.\",\"Trace how one rate surprise reaches carry, CFD funding, futures fair value and equity valuations.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"3.14\",\"courseId\":\"3.14.8\",\"check\":[\"q-3.14.8-1\",\"q-3.14.8-2\",\"q-3.14.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.1\",\"title\":\"Calls and puts: the right, not the obligation\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"An option is a paid right to buy or sell at a set price: the buyer risks only the premium, and the premium splits into intrinsic and extrinsic value.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain the difference between buying a call and buying a put.\",\"Classify an option as in, at or out of the money from its strike and the underlying price.\",\"Split an option's premium into intrinsic and extrinsic value.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.1\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.1-1\",\"q-4.1.1-2\",\"q-4.1.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.2\",\"title\":\"Payoffs at expiry: four building blocks\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Long and short calls and puts are the four building blocks: buyers risk only the premium, writers can lose far more, and each has a breakeven you compute before trading.\",\"objectives\":[\"Draw the expiry payoff of a long call, short call, long put and short put.\",\"Compute the breakeven, maximum loss and maximum gain of each building block.\",\"Explain exercise and assignment, and the difference between American and European style.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.2\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.2-1\",\"q-4.1.2-2\",\"q-4.1.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.3\",\"title\":\"Put-call parity and synthetic positions\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Calls, puts, the underlying and cash are tied together by put-call parity, so any one can be built from the others, and small gaps rarely survive costs.\",\"objectives\":[\"State put-call parity and use it to find a missing option price.\",\"Build a synthetic long position from options and recognise that a covered call has the same payoff as a short put.\",\"Show why a small parity gap disappears after realistic costs.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.3\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.3-1\",\"q-4.1.3-2\",\"q-4.1.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.4\",\"title\":\"What sets an option's price\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Black–Scholes turns price, strike, time, volatility and rates into an option price; volatility is the only input you have to guess, and the model's assumptions break in real markets.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the five Black–Scholes inputs and the direction each one pushes a call price.\",\"Estimate an at-the-money option price with the 0.4 × S × σ × √T rule.\",\"Explain which model assumptions fail in real markets.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.4\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.4-1\",\"q-4.1.4-2\",\"q-4.1.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.5\",\"title\":\"Delta and gamma: how the price moves with LABX\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Delta says how much an option moves with LABX and how many units hedge it; gamma says how fast delta changes, and it explodes at the money near expiry.\",\"objectives\":[\"Estimate an option's price change from delta and gamma for a small move in LABX.\",\"Size a share hedge that makes an options position delta-neutral.\",\"Explain why gamma explodes for at-the-money options close to expiry.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.5\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.5-1\",\"q-4.1.5-2\",\"q-4.1.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.6\",\"title\":\"Theta, vega and the gamma–theta trade-off\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Theta charges an option holder for each day, vega reprices it when implied volatility moves, and a hedged long option wins only if LABX moves more than the market implied.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why time decay speeds up near expiry and which options carry the most vega.\",\"Decompose a one-day option P&L into delta, gamma, theta and vega.\",\"Find the daily move at which a delta-hedged long option breaks even, and link it to implied volatility.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"low_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: low volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.6\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.6-1\",\"q-4.1.6-2\",\"q-4.1.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.7\",\"title\":\"Implied volatility, skew and the term structure\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Implied volatility is the σ hidden in an option's price; it varies by strike and by expiry, and a straddle's price tells you the move the market is charging for.\",\"objectives\":[\"Back out implied volatility from an option price.\",\"Read skew and term structure on a volatility surface.\",\"Convert a straddle price into the move the market is pricing in.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"label\":\"Scenario analysis check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.7\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.7-1\",\"q-4.1.7-2\",\"q-4.1.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.8\",\"title\":\"Stock plus options: covered calls, protective puts and collars\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Adding options to a stock position reshapes its outcomes: a covered call sells the upside, a protective put buys a floor that holds through gaps, and a collar does both.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the maximum gain, maximum loss and breakeven of a covered call, a protective put and a collar.\",\"Compare a protective put with a stop-loss through an overnight gap.\",\"Match each structure to the regime where it helps and the regime where it hurts.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.8\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.8-1\",\"q-4.1.8-2\",\"q-4.1.8-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.9\",\"title\":\"Vertical spreads: defined risk, defined reward\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A vertical spread buys one option and sells another of the same type and expiry, so both the maximum loss and the maximum gain are known before you trade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the debit or credit, maximum gain, maximum loss and breakeven of the four vertical spreads.\",\"Explain why a credit spread's high win rate says nothing about its edge.\",\"Size a spread so its maximum loss fits a 1% risk budget.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.9\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.9-1\",\"q-4.1.9-2\",\"q-4.1.9-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.10\",\"title\":\"Buying movement: straddles, strangles and the event trap\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"A straddle or strangle profits only if LABX moves more than the move already priced in, so a correct big-move call can still lose once implied volatility collapses.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the breakevens, maximum loss and maximum gain of a long straddle and a long strangle.\",\"Show why a correct \\\"big move\\\" call can still lose after implied volatility collapses.\",\"Decide between long straddle, long strangle and no trade by comparing the implied move with your expected move.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.10\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.10-1\",\"q-4.1.10-2\",\"q-4.1.10-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.1.11\",\"title\":\"The volatility risk premium: getting paid to sell insurance\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Option sellers are paid on average because implied volatility tends to exceed realised volatility, but the pay comes as small steady gains and rare, very large losses.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain the volatility risk premium and why option sellers are paid on average.\",\"Compare the risk of a naked short straddle with a defined-risk iron condor.\",\"Size a short-volatility position from a stress loss, not from the premium collected.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.1\",\"courseId\":\"4.1.11\",\"check\":[\"q-4.1.11-1\",\"q-4.1.11-2\",\"q-4.1.11-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.2.1\",\"title\":\"Implementation shortfall: what execution really costs\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Implementation shortfall measures execution against the price when you decided, so it counts delay, spread, impact, commission and the units you never filled.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define implementation shortfall against the decision price.\",\"Split a shortfall into delay, spread, impact, commission and opportunity cost.\",\"Express execution cost in basis points of the intended trade.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.2\",\"courseId\":\"4.2.1\",\"check\":[\"q-4.2.1-1\",\"q-4.2.1-2\",\"q-4.2.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.2.2\",\"title\":\"Market impact and the square-root law\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Your own trading moves the price; the square-root law estimates that impact from order size, daily volume and volatility, and total cost grows faster than size.\",\"objectives\":[\"Distinguish temporary from permanent market impact.\",\"Estimate impact with the square-root law from order size, daily volume and volatility.\",\"Explain why doubling an order more than doubles its total impact cost.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.2\",\"courseId\":\"4.2.2\",\"check\":[\"q-4.2.2-1\",\"q-4.2.2-2\",\"q-4.2.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.2.3\",\"title\":\"Execution algorithms: TWAP, VWAP, POV and IS\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"TWAP, VWAP, POV and IS algorithms slice a parent order in different ways, trading market impact against timing risk; you choose by urgency, benchmark and liquidity.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe how TWAP, VWAP, POV and implementation-shortfall algorithms slice a parent order.\",\"Explain the trade-off between market impact and timing risk.\",\"Choose an algorithm for a given urgency and liquidity profile.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.2\",\"courseId\":\"4.2.3\",\"check\":[\"q-4.2.3-1\",\"q-4.2.3-2\",\"q-4.2.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.2.4\",\"title\":\"Latency, HFT and who is on the other side\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A resting order goes stale the instant news lands, and faster traders fill it before you can cancel; you can't win that race, so don't leave orders in the book at news.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how a stale limit order gets picked off after a fast price change.\",\"List the traits regulators use to describe high-frequency trading.\",\"Decide which of your own orders are exposed to latency and how to reduce that exposure.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"order_types\",\"label\":\"Order types check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.2\",\"courseId\":\"4.2.4\",\"check\":[\"q-4.2.4-1\",\"q-4.2.4-2\",\"q-4.2.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.2.5\",\"title\":\"Halts, limit up–limit down and circuit breakers\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Market-wide circuit breakers and single-stock LULD pauses stop trading after big moves; trading restarts by auction, where stops fill at the reopening price and only a limit caps it.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe US market-wide circuit breakers and single-stock limit up–limit down pauses.\",\"Predict where a stop order fills when a halted stock reopens through an auction.\",\"Choose order handling that caps your exit price during a halt, and state the risk of no fill.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: unexpected event\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.2\",\"courseId\":\"4.2.5\",\"check\":[\"q-4.2.5-1\",\"q-4.2.5-2\",\"q-4.2.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.2.6\",\"title\":\"Fixes, expiries and crowded clocks\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Fixes, month-ends, closing auctions and expiries move volume and spreads for mechanical reasons: they change your costs, not the market's direction.\",\"objectives\":[\"Identify the scheduled windows where liquidity, volume and volatility change for mechanical reasons.\",\"Explain option pinning near large open-interest strikes on expiry days.\",\"Tell a clock-driven volume spike from a directional signal.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.2\",\"courseId\":\"4.2.6\",\"check\":[\"q-4.2.6-1\",\"q-4.2.6-2\",\"q-4.2.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.1\",\"title\":\"Testing the winner of a search: Reality Check and the Sharpe haircut\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"The best rule of a search has to beat the best that luck produces across the whole search, not zero; the Reality Check, SPA and the Sharpe haircut set that bar.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why correlated variants make Bonferroni too strict and a single naive test far too lenient.\",\"Describe how White's Reality Check and Hansen's SPA test the best rule of a search against a benchmark.\",\"Apply a Sharpe haircut and count trials across the whole research process, not per backtest.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.1\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.1-1\",\"q-4.3.1-2\",\"q-4.3.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.2\",\"title\":\"The deflated Sharpe ratio\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"The deflated Sharpe ratio asks how likely a backtest's true Sharpe is to beat the best that luck gives all the trials run, allowing for skew and fat tails.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain why the expected best Sharpe of N zero-edge trials is well above zero.\",\"Read a probabilistic Sharpe ratio and a deflated Sharpe ratio card.\",\"Judge whether a backtest is likely a fluke from its DSR and the number of trials.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.2\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.2-1\",\"q-4.3.2-2\",\"q-4.3.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.3\",\"title\":\"How long must a backtest be? MinBTL and the Sharpe's error bars\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A Sharpe ratio's error bars shrink only with the square root of time, and every extra configuration you try raises the length of data you need.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute a 95% confidence interval for an annual Sharpe ratio from its track-record length.\",\"Use the minimum backtest length rule to cap the number of configurations you try.\",\"Explain why autocorrelation can inflate an annualised Sharpe.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.3\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.3-1\",\"q-4.3.3-2\",\"q-4.3.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.4\",\"title\":\"Probability of backtest overfitting\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"CSCV splits the data every way into in-sample and out-of-sample halves and counts how often the in-sample winner lands in the bottom half out of sample.\",\"objectives\":[\"Describe the CSCV procedure that estimates the probability of backtest overfitting.\",\"Interpret a PBO value and the in-sample vs out-of-sample degradation plot.\",\"Classify a strategy family as likely overfit, uncertain or worth further testing.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.4\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.4-1\",\"q-4.3.4-2\",\"q-4.3.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.5\",\"title\":\"Purged cross-validation and the embargo\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Labels built from the days ahead leak test information into training; purge the overlapping days and embargo the ones right after each test fold.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain how overlapping labels leak future information into standard k-fold cross-validation.\",\"Mark which training observations to purge and embargo around a test fold.\",\"Compare an inflated score with an honest one on the same data.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.5\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.5-1\",\"q-4.3.5-2\",\"q-4.3.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.6\",\"title\":\"Capacity: how much money an edge can carry\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Square-root impact eats a fixed edge as size grows: total profit peaks well before the edge reaches zero, and crowding pulls the peak lower.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute net edge per trade after fixed costs and square-root impact at a given size.\",\"Find the trade size that maximises total profit, and the larger size where the edge reaches zero.\",\"Explain how crowding reduces capacity.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"position_sizing\",\"label\":\"Position sizing check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.6\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.6-1\",\"q-4.3.6-2\",\"q-4.3.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.3.7\",\"title\":\"The validation dossier and the verdict\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Pre-register the thresholds and the decay haircut, audit the research log, then reach deploy, incubate or reject by criteria written before the results.\",\"objectives\":[\"Audit a research log for the violations that make a result unreportable.\",\"Pre-register the test plan, thresholds and decay haircut before seeing out-of-sample results.\",\"Complete a validation dossier and reach a deploy, incubate or reject verdict.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.3\",\"courseId\":\"4.3.7\",\"check\":[\"q-4.3.7-1\",\"q-4.3.7-2\",\"q-4.3.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.4.1\",\"title\":\"Value at Risk and Expected Shortfall\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"VaR marks the edge of the bad days, Expected Shortfall measures how deep they go, and fat tails can hide behind a reassuring 95% number.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute parametric and historical one-day VaR and Expected Shortfall.\",\"Explain why VaR is not a maximum loss and why fat tails can hide behind the 95% number.\",\"Backtest a VaR model by counting exceedances.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.4\",\"courseId\":\"4.4.1\",\"check\":[\"q-4.4.1-1\",\"q-4.4.1-2\",\"q-4.4.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.4.2\",\"title\":\"Volatility targeting for the whole book\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"A book's volatility comes from weights, volatilities and correlations, so a rise in correlation alone can call for a smaller book; caps and drawdown tiers still rule.\",\"objectives\":[\"Forecast a two-asset book's volatility from weights, volatilities and correlation.\",\"Rescale the whole book when correlation changes, even if no single volatility does.\",\"Combine a volatility target with a gross-exposure cap and drawdown tiers.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.4\",\"courseId\":\"4.4.2\",\"check\":[\"q-4.4.2-1\",\"q-4.4.2-2\",\"q-4.4.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.4.3\",\"title\":\"Gross, net and beta-adjusted exposure\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Net exposure measures the bet on the market, gross exposure the size of everything that can go wrong, and beta-adjusted exposure how hard the market really pulls on the book.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute gross, net and beta-adjusted exposure and effective leverage for a long–short book.\",\"Explain why a small net exposure can still hide a large risk.\",\"Rebalance a book to meet both a net and a gross limit.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.4\",\"courseId\":\"4.4.3\",\"check\":[\"q-4.4.3-1\",\"q-4.4.3-2\",\"q-4.4.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.4.4\",\"title\":\"Risk parity and risk budgets (concept)\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Equal capital is not equal risk: risk contributions show what really drives a book, inverse-volatility weights balance them, and matching a riskier book then takes leverage.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute each asset's contribution to portfolio risk.\",\"Build inverse-volatility weights that equalise risk between uncorrelated assets.\",\"Explain why risk parity needs leverage to match a riskier portfolio, and what that leverage risks.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.4\",\"courseId\":\"4.4.4\",\"check\":[\"q-4.4.4-1\",\"q-4.4.4-2\",\"q-4.4.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.4.5\",\"title\":\"Stress testing: what if the worst happens?\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Stress tests revalue the book under past crises and imagined shocks, reverse stress tests find the move that breaks it, and the worst scenario sets the size.\",\"objectives\":[\"Run historical and hypothetical stress scenarios on a book.\",\"Run a reverse stress test: find the move that would cost a given share of equity.\",\"Adjust a book so no scenario in a stress battery breaches a loss limit.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"event_shock\",\"label\":\"Arena: event shock\"},{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M04\",\"label\":\"Define Your Risk\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.4\",\"courseId\":\"4.4.5\",\"check\":[\"q-4.4.5-1\",\"q-4.4.5-2\",\"q-4.4.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.4.6\",\"title\":\"Correlation regimes: when diversification disappears\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Correlations rise toward one in crises, so five calm, diversified positions can become one bet; size with crisis correlations, not calm ones.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute correlation-adjusted risk and the effective number of bets for a set of positions.\",\"Explain why correlations rise in crises and which strategies fail together.\",\"Size positions with crisis correlations instead of calm ones.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"high_volatility\",\"label\":\"Arena: high volatility\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.4\",\"courseId\":\"4.4.6\",\"check\":[\"q-4.4.6-1\",\"q-4.4.6-2\",\"q-4.4.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.1\",\"title\":\"Regulators and the rules that apply to you\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Know who supervises your broker and your products, the dated retail limits that protect you, and why the US pattern day trader rule is gone.\",\"objectives\":[\"Name the regulators for retail trading in the EU, Romania, the UK and the US, and verify a firm on a regulator's own register.\",\"State the retail leverage limits and protections that apply in your jurisdiction, with their date.\",\"Explain what replaced the US pattern day trader rule in 2026 and why older course material is out of date.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"label\":\"Portfolio exposure check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.1\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.1-1\",\"q-4.5.1-2\",\"q-4.5.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.2\",\"title\":\"Client money, broker models and broker due diligence\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Know what protects your money if a broker fails, who profits from each dealing model, and how to rank brokers and flag the red flags.\",\"objectives\":[\"Explain client-money segregation and what investor compensation schemes do and do not cover.\",\"Describe A-book, B-book and hybrid dealing models and the conflict of interest in each.\",\"Rank brokers with a due-diligence checklist and flag the red flags.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Market fundamentals check\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.2\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.2-1\",\"q-4.5.2-2\",\"q-4.5.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.3\",\"title\":\"Prop firm evaluations: the rules and what they reward\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Retail evaluation firms sell attempts on simulated accounts under strict loss rules; know where each kind of floor sits and how risk per trade shifts the pass odds.\",\"objectives\":[\"Distinguish institutional prop firms from retail evaluation firms.\",\"Compute where a static, an intraday-trailing and an end-of-day-trailing loss limit sit during a trade.\",\"Explain how risk per trade changes the pass probability for traders with and without an edge.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.3\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.3-1\",\"q-4.5.3-2\",\"q-4.5.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.4\",\"title\":\"The maths and the business of evaluations\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Buying an evaluation is a bet with an expected value: pass probability times payouts, minus the fee. Estimate yours from your own trades, and know how thin the legal protection is.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute the expected value of buying an evaluation and the pass probability needed to break even.\",\"Estimate your own pass probability from your simulated trades.\",\"Explain what the CFTC case against My Forex Funds shows about regulatory protection for evaluation customers.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Risk management check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.4\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.4-1\",\"q-4.5.4-2\",\"q-4.5.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.5\",\"title\":\"Taxes (awareness only) and record-keeping\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":6,\"summary\":\"Tax depends on where you live, what you trade and the account you use, so the app gives no rates: it shows how tax slows compounding and which records you must keep.\",\"objectives\":[\"List the records a trader must keep for every trade and every cash movement.\",\"Show how a generic tax rate reduces long-run compounding.\",\"Explain why tax treatment differs by country, instrument and account type, and where to get an answer for your own case.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.5\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.5-1\",\"q-4.5.5-2\",\"q-4.5.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.6\",\"title\":\"From demo to live: the transition criteria\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Going live is a written decision on evidence, process, capital, broker and expectations; the interval around your results, not their average, decides.\",\"objectives\":[\"Apply a written go-live checklist covering evidence, process, capital, broker and expectations.\",\"Decide whether a sample of simulated results supports going live, using a confidence interval.\",\"Explain why live results are expected to be worse than simulated ones.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Decision-making check\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.6\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.6-1\",\"q-4.5.6-2\",\"q-4.5.6-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.5.7\",\"title\":\"When not to trade\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Standing aside is a decision with rules: know the personal, market, process and financial states that call for it, and let base rates decide whether active trading belongs in your plan.\",\"objectives\":[\"Recognise the personal, market, process and financial states in which you should stand aside.\",\"Use base rates on retail outcomes to judge whether active trading belongs in your plan at all.\",\"Treat abstaining as a scored skill, not a missed opportunity.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"arena\",\"ref\":\"sideways\",\"label\":\"Arena: sideways\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.5\",\"courseId\":\"4.5.7\",\"check\":[\"q-4.5.7-1\",\"q-4.5.7-2\",\"q-4.5.7-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.6.1\",\"title\":\"The constraints matrix\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Map your time, money, jurisdiction, drawdown tolerance, skills and measured temperament to the styles they allow, and rule out the rest with reasons.\",\"objectives\":[\"Fill in a constraints matrix covering time, capital, jurisdiction, drawdown tolerance, skills and temperament.\",\"Replace self-reported traits with measured behaviour from your journal.\",\"Name the styles your constraints rule out, with a reason for each.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M06\",\"label\":\"The FOMO Trap\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.6\",\"courseId\":\"4.6.1\",\"check\":[\"q-4.6.1-1\",\"q-4.6.1-2\",\"q-4.6.1-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.6.2\",\"title\":\"Choosing your market and your style\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Pick one or two markets that move a lot relative to their cost, are busiest in your hours, and fit your account and where you live.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute a tradability ratio (ATR ÷ round-trip cost) and the share of a target eaten by costs.\",\"Match session windows to your time zone, including daylight-saving mismatch weeks.\",\"Pick one or two instruments that fit your constraints, jurisdiction and account size.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: market fundamentals\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.6\",\"courseId\":\"4.6.2\",\"check\":[\"q-4.6.2-1\",\"q-4.6.2-2\",\"q-4.6.2-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.6.3\",\"title\":\"Specialise before you diversify\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Trade one style, one or two setups and one or two markets until the sample can show whether the edge is real, and write down when you won't trade.\",\"objectives\":[\"Compute how many trades you need before an edge of a given size is distinguishable from zero.\",\"Prune a playbook to one or two setups using sample size and confidence intervals.\",\"Write a no-trade list and the condition for adding a second strategy.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M10\",\"label\":\"No Trade Is a Trade\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.6\",\"courseId\":\"4.6.3\",\"check\":[\"q-4.6.3-1\",\"q-4.6.3-2\",\"q-4.6.3-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.6.4\",\"title\":\"The development pipeline and kill criteria\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":8,\"summary\":\"Every strategy moves through eight stages, from hypothesis to decay monitoring, with review and kill thresholds written from its Monte Carlo before it trades.\",\"objectives\":[\"Order the eight stages of a strategy's development pipeline and the gate at each stage.\",\"Write review and kill thresholds from a Monte Carlo drawdown distribution and an expectancy interval, before going live.\",\"Run a premortem on a strategy you are about to deploy.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M07\",\"label\":\"Stop or Stay?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"risk_management\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: risk management\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.6\",\"courseId\":\"4.6.4\",\"check\":[\"q-4.6.4-1\",\"q-4.6.4-2\",\"q-4.6.4-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.6.5\",\"title\":\"Scaling up without blowing up\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Raise size in stages unlocked by sample, adherence and drawdown, cut it automatically in drawdowns, and treat full Kelly as a ceiling far above your risk.\",\"objectives\":[\"Define capital stages with promotion and demotion rules based on sample size, adherence and drawdown, not on profit alone.\",\"Apply drawdown-based de-risking and step-ups after new equity highs.\",\"Explain why full Kelly is a ceiling, not a target, and why size growth runs into market impact.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M05\",\"label\":\"Size Matters\"},{\"kind\":\"calculator\",\"ref\":\"risk-calculator\",\"label\":\"Risk calculator\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.6\",\"courseId\":\"4.6.5\",\"check\":[\"q-4.6.5-1\",\"q-4.6.5-2\",\"q-4.6.5-3\"]}},{\"id\":\"4.6.6\",\"title\":\"Keep improving: reviews, decay and the yearly decision\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"level\":\"intermediate\",\"tier\":\"free\",\"minutes\":7,\"summary\":\"Tell decay from variance and regime change with intervals and Monte Carlo bands, change rules only through a logged process, and end each year with a decision.\",\"objectives\":[\"Tell decay from variance and regime change using a confidence interval and the Monte Carlo band.\",\"Change a rule only through a documented, evidence-based process.\",\"Run a yearly review that compares your net, risk-adjusted results with the passive benchmark and ends with a continue, reduce, retire or invest-passively decision.\"],\"blocks\":[],\"practice\":[{\"kind\":\"mission\",\"ref\":\"M02\",\"label\":\"Trend or Noise?\"},{\"kind\":\"check\",\"ref\":\"decision_making\",\"label\":\"Knowledge check: decision making\"}],\"scriptRef\":{\"unit\":\"4.6\",\"courseId\":\"4.6.6\",\"check\":[\"q-4.6.6-1\",\"q-4.6.6-2\",\"q-4.6.6-3\"]}}],\"questions\":[{\"id\":\"q-0.1.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A quote screen shows a share's last price as 25.40. What does that number tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The price the exchange set for the session\",\"rationale\":\"Exchanges match orders; they don't pick prices. The number comes out of the orders: it's where the latest buyer and seller agreed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The fair value of the share, worked out by analysts\",\"rationale\":\"Analysts' views only matter if they turn into orders. The last price records a trade, not anyone's estimate of value.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The price at which a buyer and a seller last agreed to trade\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The price you'll pay if you buy right now\",\"rationale\":\"Buying now means taking the lowest seller's price, the ask, which can differ from the last trade. The last price is history.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A last price is a record: the most recent price at which a buyer's order and a seller's order met. Nobody sets it, and it isn't a promise about the next trade, which happens at whatever price the next buyer and seller accept.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-as-auction\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"One unit per order. Buyers will pay at most 20.60, 20.50, 20.40 and 20.20. Sellers will accept at least 20.10, 20.30, 20.40 and 20.70. At what single price do the most units change hands?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":20.4,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"At 20.40, three buyers will pay at least 20.40 (20.60, 20.50, 20.40) and three sellers will accept at most 20.40 (20.10, 20.30, 20.40): 3 units. At 20.30 only 2 sellers accept, and at 20.50 only 2 buyers pay, so 2 units each.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-as-auction\"],\"hints\":[\"Pick a price and count the buyers who would pay at least that much.\",\"Then count the sellers who would accept at most that much. Units traded is the smaller count.\",\"Compare 20.30, 20.40 and 20.50. Which gives the largest smaller count?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX's best bid is 99.95 and its best ask is 100.05. Over the next minutes, buyers keep paying whatever sellers ask: they take every unit offered at 100.05, then at 100.10, then at 100.15.\",\"prompt\":\"Why is the last price rising?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because the exchange raises the price when trading gets busy\",\"rationale\":\"No one raises the price. Each trade prints where a buyer and a seller agree, and here buyers keep accepting higher offers.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because there are more buyers than sellers in the market\",\"rationale\":\"Each unit traded has exactly one buyer and one seller. What moves price is which side is eager enough to accept the other's price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because buyers are in a hurry: they keep taking the next seller's higher price\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because the sellers raised their prices to match demand\",\"rationale\":\"The sellers' prices were already waiting there. Buyers used up the cheaper offers and moved on to the next, higher ones.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Each trade prints at a price both sides accept. When buyers are eager enough to take sellers' prices, they use up the offers at 100.05 and move to 100.10, then 100.15, so the last price climbs. If sellers were the hurried side, they would hit lower bids and it would fall.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-as-auction\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask which side is accepting the other side's price.\",\"Every unit traded has one buyer and one seller, so counting people doesn't explain the move.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You buy 50 LABX. Who is your counterparty?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Whoever sold you the 50 units\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Anyone else who bought LABX at the same time\",\"rationale\":\"Other buyers are on your side of the market. Your counterparty is on the other side of your own trade: the seller.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The regulator that supervises your broker\",\"rationale\":\"A regulator supervises the market but never takes the other side of a trade. The counterparty is whoever sold to you.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The next person who buys LABX after you\",\"rationale\":\"They trade with someone else. Your counterparty is the one who took the opposite side of your trade when it happened.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Every trade has two sides. When you buy, your counterparty is whoever sold to you; when you sell, it's whoever bought. In retail FX and CFDs that is often a dealer or the broker itself.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"market-participants\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"In spring, a wheat farmer sells futures for her autumn harvest at a fixed price. She thinks wheat prices will probably rise by autumn, which would make the futures a loss for her.\",\"prompt\":\"Why might she sell the futures anyway?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"She's betting that prices will fall after all\",\"rationale\":\"Her own view is that prices rise. She sells to remove the risk of a fall in her harvest's value, not to bet on one.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"She wants a known price for her harvest more than the chance of a higher one\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Futures always pay more than selling the harvest later\",\"rationale\":\"Futures promise no extra. If prices rise she loses on the futures but sells her wheat for more; the total stays close to the fixed price.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"She has no wheat yet, so she carries no risk\",\"rationale\":\"Her coming harvest is the risk: its value drops if prices fall. The futures offset exactly that.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"She is a hedger. Her harvest already exposes her to falling prices, and the futures lock in a price whatever happens. If prices rise, the futures lose but her wheat sells for more, so the hedge did its job even though the contract lost.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"market-participants\"],\"hints\":[\"What risk does she already carry before she trades anything?\",\"A hedger judges the futures and the harvest together, not the futures alone.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After a quick drop fills his stop, a trader says: 'They hunted my stop.' Why isn't that a useful explanation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stops are invisible to everyone, so nobody could target them\",\"rationale\":\"Too strong: dealers and brokers can see their own clients' orders. The point is that a chart can't show anyone's intent.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Stops are only hit by news, never by normal moves\",\"rationale\":\"Normal moves hit stops all the time. Stops at obvious levels are reached simply because so many traders chose the same spot.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It blames someone a chart can't show; stops simply gather at obvious levels\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's fine if a single candle hit the stop\",\"rationale\":\"One fast candle shows a move, not a motive. The useful question is whether the stop sat at an obvious, crowded level.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The market isn't a person, and no chart shows who traded or why. Stops tend to gather just beyond obvious levels, so a move there fills many at once. Asking whether your stop was at a crowded level is something you can act on; blaming a hunter is not.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"market-participants\"],\"hints\":[\"Can a chart show who traded, or why?\",\"Think about where many traders put their stops, and what happens when price gets there.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these markets is open 24 hours a day, 7 days a week?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A US stock exchange\",\"rationale\":\"Stock exchanges keep weekday sessions, with extra hours before and after. They're closed at weekends.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Spot forex\",\"rationale\":\"Forex runs 24 hours a day from Sunday evening to Friday evening, then pauses for the weekend.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Gold futures\",\"rationale\":\"Futures trade nearly around the clock from Sunday to Friday, with a daily break, but not at the weekend.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Crypto spot exchanges\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Crypto spot trades 24/7, weekends included. Forex and most futures run almost around the clock on weekdays, while stock exchanges keep sessions. The fewer the hours, the more room for gaps when trading reopens.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"asset-classes\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold a futures contract that expires next week, and you want to keep the position. What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: futures don't expire if you keep holding them\",\"rationale\":\"Every futures contract has an expiry date. Holding past it means delivery or cash settlement, not a longer position.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Roll it: close the expiring contract and open the next one\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Ask the exchange to turn it into a CFD\",\"rationale\":\"Exchanges don't convert contracts. A CFD is a separate deal with a provider. To stay in, you roll to the next futures contract.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Wait: it renews itself at the same price\",\"rationale\":\"Contracts don't renew. The next one trades at its own price, so rolling can cost or earn the difference between them.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Futures expire on a set date. To keep the exposure you roll: close the contract that's about to expire and open the next one. The two usually trade at different prices, so the roll has a cost or a gain of its own.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"asset-classes\"],\"hints\":[\"Every futures contract has an end date.\",\"To keep the same exposure, you need a contract that's still alive next month.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Mara buys a CFD on gold from an online provider. She never owns any gold.\",\"prompt\":\"Who is on the other side of her trade, and why does it matter?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A gold miner selling its output\",\"rationale\":\"No gold changes hands. A CFD only pays the price change, and the contract is between Mara and her provider.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The exchange's clearing house, which guarantees the trade\",\"rationale\":\"That's how exchange futures work. A CFD is an over-the-counter contract with the provider itself.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The provider: it pays her gains, collects her losses and sets the spread and funding\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nobody: a CFD only tracks the price\",\"rationale\":\"Every trade has two sides. With a CFD, the provider is the other side of the contract.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A CFD is a contract with the provider, which is her counterparty. It pays her gains and collects her losses, sets the spread and the overnight funding, and her money depends on it staying sound. That is why regulation and the choice of provider matter.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"asset-classes\"],\"hints\":[\"Does Mara own gold, or a contract about gold's price?\",\"Who signed that contract with her?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is investing rather than trading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buying LABX because you expect a rise this week\",\"rationale\":\"That seeks profit from a short-term price change: trading. Investing holds for years for the asset's own return.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Opening and closing ten positions in one day\",\"rationale\":\"That's day trading: short holds and many trades. Investing means few trades held for years.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Holding a position for two weeks to catch one swing\",\"rationale\":\"That's swing trading. Two weeks is a short horizon aimed at one price move, not the long-run return of the asset.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Adding to an index fund every month and holding it for decades\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Investing holds assets for years for their own return, such as dividends and growth, with few trades. The other three seek profit from price changes over days or hours, which is trading.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-styles\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over a year, a trader made +12.0% before costs with 25 round trips, each costing about 0.2% of the account. Buy-and-hold on the same market made +8.5% after costs. By how many points did the trader beat the ghost? Use a minus sign if they trailed it.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-1.5,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"points\"},\"explanation\":\"Costs: 25 × 0.2 = 5.0 points. After costs: 12.0 − 5.0 = 7.0%. Against the ghost: 7.0 − 8.5 = −1.5 points. Before costs the trader looked 3.5 points ahead; after costs they trail.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"passive-benchmark\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare like with like: the ghost is after costs, so take costs off the trader's result first.\",\"Costs = number of round trips × 0.2 points.\",\"25 × 0.2 = 5.0, so the trader's result after costs is 12.0 − 5.0 = 7.0%. Now compare with 8.5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"gross\":[8,16,0.5],\"n\":[10,40,5],\"ghost\":[4,12,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(gross - n * 0.2 - ghost) >= 0.5\",\"gross > ghost\"],\"answer\":\"round(gross - n * 0.2 - ghost, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Over a year, a trader made +{gross:1}% before costs with {n} round trips, each costing about 0.2% of the account. Buy-and-hold on the same market made +{ghost:1}% after costs. By how many points did the trader beat the ghost? Use a minus sign if they trailed it.\",\"explanation\":\"Costs: {n} × 0.2 = {n * 0.2:1} points. After costs: {gross:1} − {n * 0.2:1} = {gross - n * 0.2:1}%. Against the ghost: {gross - n * 0.2:1} − {ghost:1} = {answer:1} points.\",\"hint\":\"{n} × 0.2 = {n * 0.2:1}, so the trader's result after costs is {gross:1} − {n * 0.2:1} = {gross - n * 0.2:1}%. Now compare with {ghost:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"On LABX, a scalper aims to make 0.30 per unit on each trade; a swing trader aims for 3.00. Both pay the same 0.10 spread on every round trip, plus commission.\",\"prompt\":\"Why does the scalper need a bigger edge to beat the benchmark?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The spread takes a third of each scalping target but a thirtieth of the swing target, and the scalper pays it far more often\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Regulators charge more per trade for short-term trading\",\"rationale\":\"Costs per round trip are the same here: 0.10 of spread plus commission. The difference is how big that cost is next to the target.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The swing trader pays no costs on positions held overnight\",\"rationale\":\"Both pay the spread and commission on every round trip. Holding longer just means fewer round trips and bigger targets.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Short-term trading can never be profitable\",\"rationale\":\"Too strong. It can be, but costs eat a much bigger share of each small target, so the edge per trade must be larger.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A 0.10 spread is 0.10 ÷ 0.30, a third, of the scalper's target, but 0.10 ÷ 3.00, a thirtieth, of the swing trader's. The scalper also pays it many more times, so a far larger share of any edge goes to costs before it can beat buy-and-hold.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-styles\",\"passive-benchmark\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the cost of a round trip with the size of each target.\",\"Then think about how many round trips each style makes in a month.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.5-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"ESMA found that 74% to 89% of retail CFD accounts lose money. A provider's risk warning says 78%. Out of 500 of its retail accounts, how many lose money?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":390,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"accounts\"},\"explanation\":\"78% of 500 is 500 × 78 ÷ 100 = 390. A risk warning turns the base rate into a count you can picture: 390 of every 500 accounts lose money.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"retail-loss-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"A percentage means 'out of 100'.\",\"Multiply the number of accounts by the percentage, then divide by 100.\",\"500 × 78 ÷ 100 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"pct\":[74,89,1],\"n\":[200,2000,100]},\"answer\":\"n * pct / 100\",\"prompt\":\"ESMA found that 74% to 89% of retail CFD accounts lose money. A provider's risk warning says {pct}%. Out of {n} of its retail accounts, how many lose money?\",\"explanation\":\"{pct}% of {n} is {n} × {pct} ÷ 100 = {answer}. A risk warning turns the base rate into a count you can picture: {answer} of every {n} accounts lose money.\",\"hint\":\"{n} × {pct} ÷ 100 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.5-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In Brazil, 97% of people who day traded futures for more than 300 days lost money. What does this figure show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"97% of everyone who ever tried day trading lost money\",\"rationale\":\"The figure covers only those who kept going for 300+ days, not everyone who tried. Many people stopped earlier.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Almost everyone who kept day trading for that long lost money\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Day traders learn and turn profitable after about 300 days\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: those who persisted still lost, and the study found no sign that they learned to win.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only Brazil's markets are this hard\",\"rationale\":\"Other base rates point the same way: under 1% of Taiwan's day traders reliably profit after fees, and 74–89% of EU retail CFD accounts lose.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The 97% describes persistent day traders: people who kept at it for more than 300 days. It doesn't cover everyone who tried, and it shows that sticking with it didn't turn them into winners. Studies elsewhere, such as Taiwan's, point the same way.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"retail-loss-rates\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Read who the figure counts: everyone, or a specific group?\",\"Did persistence help? Think about what 97% of the persistent group tells you.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.5-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After 60 simulated trades, Ana is up 12% in the Arena and well ahead of the benchmark ghost. She plans to open a real account next week and trade her savings the same way.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the soundest reading of her result?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"She has proven an edge, so real money will behave the same\",\"rationale\":\"60 trades in a synthetic market prove little. Real money adds real fear and different costs, and a short good run can be luck.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A good start, but 60 trades in a synthetic market, without real fear, say little about real money\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Her savings are safe as long as she keeps using stops\",\"rationale\":\"Stops limit each loss but don't create an edge. Most retail accounts lose money even with protections in place.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"She should size up with real money to make the most of her edge\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing up on a small, simulated sample adds risk to an edge that hasn't been shown to exist.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Simulator results don't predict live profits: the market is synthetic, there's no real fear, and 60 trades is a small sample. Not trading her savings, or only risking money she can afford to lose, is a sound decision.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"when-not-to-trade\"],\"hints\":[\"What's different about real money compared with the simulator?\",\"Is 60 trades enough to separate an edge from a lucky run?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In MARKET//LAB, a trade makes 300.00 vUSD of simulated profit. Which score does the profit itself raise?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Decision Quality\",\"rationale\":\"Decision Quality grades the reasoning with what you knew at the time. A profit can come from a poor decision and luck.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Risk Discipline\",\"rationale\":\"Risk Discipline grades your risk rules, such as a stop on every entry. A profit says nothing about whether you followed them.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"All three equally\",\"rationale\":\"The scores are kept apart on purpose, so that luck can't grade your process. Profit moves only the outcome.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only Simulated Outcome\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Profit moves only Simulated Outcome, which is shown but never graded. Decision Quality and Risk Discipline grade what you control: your reasoning and your risk rules.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"process-scores\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You enter a long on LABX without a stop. Price rises, and you close the trade for +150.00 vUSD.\",\"prompt\":\"How does MARKET//LAB score this trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Well on all three scores, because it made money\",\"rationale\":\"The scores are separate. The profit shows in Simulated Outcome, but entering without a stop breaks a risk rule whatever the result.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Simulated Outcome shows +150.00, but Risk Discipline drops: there was no stop\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Decision Quality is high, because the idea worked\",\"rationale\":\"Decision Quality judges the reasoning and information at the time, not whether price happened to rise.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It isn't scored, because it won\",\"rationale\":\"Every trade is scored. A win doesn't hide a missing stop; it only makes the mistake easier to repeat.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The profit appears in Simulated Outcome. Risk Discipline requires a stop with every entry, so this trade loses points there no matter how it ended. Grading the process stops a lucky win from teaching a risky habit.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"process-scores\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Which score grades whether you had a stop?\",\"Does the result change whether a rule was followed?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.1.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A method in the course carries evidence grade D. What does that tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's supported, but weaker since it was published\",\"rationale\":\"That's grade B: supported evidence that has faded or holds only in some regimes. D is weaker than that.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It loses money by design\",\"rationale\":\"That's grade F, such as doubling your size after each loss. D means unproven, not built to lose.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's true by construction, like the spread\",\"rationale\":\"That's Mechanic: facts about how markets and orders work. D is for claims that haven't held up.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's unproven: anecdotal, untestable, or not supported when tested\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"D means unproven: the claim rests on anecdotes, can't be tested, or failed when it was tested. Elliott wave forecasts are an example. A grade describes the evidence, not what will happen to you.\",\"lesson\":\"0.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"evidence-grades\"],\"hints\":[\"Grades run from Mechanic and A, the strongest, down to F.\",\"D sits between mixed evidence, C, and losing by design, F.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.1-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":1,\"scenario\":\"Mara buys LABX with a stop set before entry, risking 1% of her account, exactly as her plan says. Price falls to the stop and she loses 100.00 vUSD.\",\"prompt\":\"Which box does this decision belong in?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Deserved success\",\"rationale\":\"Deserved success needs a good outcome too. The decision was sound, but the result was a loss.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bad luck\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Poetic justice\",\"rationale\":\"Poetic justice is a poor decision punished. Hers followed the plan with a defined risk, so it wasn't poor.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Dumb luck\",\"rationale\":\"Dumb luck is a poor decision that turned out well. Here the decision was sound and the outcome was a loss.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Judge the decision first: a planned entry, a stop set in advance and 1% risk make it sound. The outcome was a loss. A sound decision with a bad outcome is bad luck, and the right response is to keep the process.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"outcome-bias\",\"decision-quality\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.1-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why can't a single result tell you how good a decision was?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because results are random and decisions never affect them\",\"rationale\":\"Decisions do shift the odds; that's why the process matters. One result just mixes the decision's effect with luck you can't separate.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because only losses carry information; wins are always luck\",\"rationale\":\"Wins and losses both mix decision and luck. Treating only losses as informative is outcome thinking turned upside down.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because each result mixes the decision with luck, and one result can't separate the two\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because a decision can only be judged after 10 results\",\"rationale\":\"No count of results grades a decision. You judge it by what was known and reasoned at the time.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A result comes from the decision and from luck together. A good decision can lose and a poor one can win, so one result can't show which part did the work. Judge the decision by what was known and how it was reasoned when it was made.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"outcome-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about what produces a result.\",\"A result has two ingredients. Can one result show how much came from each?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.1-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Dan reads a tip on a forum, buys LABX at ten times his usual size with no stop, and says 'it can only go up'. Two days later he's up 900.00 vUSD and says the trade proves he has a feel for the market.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most accurate description?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Deserved success: the profit shows the tip was good\",\"rationale\":\"The profit is the outcome. The decision broke every rule: a tip, ten times the size, no stop. That's a poor decision.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bad luck: he should have taken even more profit\",\"rationale\":\"Bad luck is a sound decision with a bad result. Here the result was good and the decision was poor.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Can't tell until he does it again\",\"rationale\":\"Repeating it would only add outcomes. The decision can be judged now, from what he knew and did: a tip, oversized, no stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Dumb luck: a poor decision with a good outcome\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A tip, ten times the usual size and no stop make the decision poor, whatever happened next. The good outcome makes it dumb luck. Reading it as skill invites the same bet again: that's outcome bias at work.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"outcome-bias\",\"luck-vs-skill\"],\"hints\":[\"Grade the decision before you look at the result.\",\"Was the decision sound, given what Dan knew? Then add the outcome to find the box.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A game pays 8.00 with a 30% chance and loses 4.00 with a 70% chance. What is its expected value per play, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.4,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Wins: 0.30 × 8.00 = 2.40. Losses: 0.70 × 4.00 = 2.80. EV = 2.40 − 2.80 = −0.40 per play. Each win is twice the size of each loss, but losses come more than twice as often.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"expected-value\"],\"hints\":[\"EV = chance of a win × the win − chance of a loss × the loss.\",\"Wins: 0.30 × 8.00. Losses: 0.70 × 4.00.\",\"2.40 − 2.80 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[20,80,10],\"win\":[2,20,1],\"loss\":[2,20,1]},\"constraints\":[\"round(pct(p) * win - (1 - pct(p)) * loss, 2) != 0\"],\"answer\":\"round(pct(p) * win - (1 - pct(p)) * loss, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A game pays {win:2} with a {p}% chance and loses {loss:2} with a {100 - p}% chance. What is its expected value per play, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Wins: {pct(p):2} × {win:2} = {pct(p) * win:2}. Losses: {1 - pct(p):2} × {loss:2} = {(1 - pct(p)) * loss:2}. EV = {pct(p) * win:2} − {(1 - pct(p)) * loss:2} = {answer:2} per play.\",\"hint\":\"{pct(p) * win:2} − {(1 - pct(p)) * loss:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A method wins 90% of its trades. Each win makes 20.00 vUSD; each loss costs 250.00. What can you say about it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's a strong method: it wins nine times in ten\",\"rationale\":\"That judges by how often it wins, not by how much. One loss wipes out more than twelve wins, so the average is negative.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It breaks even, because the wins and losses balance out\",\"rationale\":\"Balance needs the weighted amounts to match: 0.9 × 20.00 = 18.00 against 0.1 × 250.00 = 25.00. They don't.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It loses 7.00 per trade on average, despite winning 90% of the time\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"You can't say anything without knowing the next trade\",\"rationale\":\"EV doesn't predict the next trade, but it does describe the average over many. Here that average is negative.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"EV = 0.90 × 20.00 − 0.10 × 250.00 = 18.00 − 25.00 = −7.00 per trade. A high win rate says how often you win, not whether you make money. Small, frequent wins and rare, large losses can lose on average.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"expected-value\"],\"hints\":[\"Don't stop at the win rate. Weigh each amount by its chance.\",\"Wins: 0.90 × 20.00. Losses: 0.10 × 250.00. Which is bigger?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Game A pays +15.00 or −10.00 on a coin flip: an expected value of +2.50 per play. You play it once and lose 10.00. A friend says: 'So much for your expected value.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best reply?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Expected value describes the average over many plays; any single play can still lose\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Fair point: the expected value was wrong\",\"rationale\":\"One loss doesn't test an average. A coin-flip game loses half its plays; +2.50 describes the average over many.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The next play is now more likely to win, so I'll double the stake\",\"rationale\":\"Each flip is independent: a loss doesn't make a win due. Doubling after losses raises the risk and leaves the odds unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"I should stop: a positive game shouldn't lose on the first play\",\"rationale\":\"Half of all single plays of this game lose. Stopping after one loss judges the game on a sample of one.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"+2.50 is what game A returns per play on average, over many plays. A single play is either +15.00 or −10.00, each half the time. One loss is normal and says nothing against the expected value.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"expected-value\",\"variance\"],\"hints\":[\"What does expected value describe: the next play, or many plays?\",\"How often does a single play of game A lose?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.3-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You flip a fair coin 100 times. How likely is it that, somewhere in the sequence, you get at least 5 tails in a row?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About 3%: five in a row is rare\",\"rationale\":\"3.1% is the chance for five given flips. In 100 flips a run can start almost anywhere, so it shows up far more often.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"About 25%\",\"rationale\":\"That underestimates how many places 100 flips give a run to start. The exact figure is about 81%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exactly 50%, as with any coin flip\",\"rationale\":\"50% is the chance of one flip. A run of five needs many flips in a row, and 100 flips give it many places to start.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"About 81%\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Five given flips all land tails 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.5 × 0.5 ≈ 3.1% of the time. But a run can start at almost any of the 100 flips, so at least one run of five or more shows up in about 81% of sequences. Long streaks are normal.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"streaks-and-runs\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.3-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A method wins 40% of its trades, and each trade is independent. What's the chance that the next 4 trades all lose, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":12.96,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Each trade loses with a 0.60 chance. Four losses in a row: 0.60 × 0.60 × 0.60 × 0.60 = 0.1296, about 13.0%. That's for four given trades; somewhere in 100 trades, a run of four is close to certain (99.9%).\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"streaks-and-runs\"],\"hints\":[\"The chance of a loss is 100% minus the win rate.\",\"Independent trades: multiply the chances.\",\"0.60 × 0.60 × 0.60 × 0.60 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[30,70,5]},\"answer\":\"round((1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A method wins {w}% of its trades, and each trade is independent. What's the chance that the next 4 trades all lose, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Each trade loses with a {1 - pct(w):2} chance. Four losses in a row: {1 - pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2} = {answer:2}%. That's for four given trades; somewhere in 100 trades, a run of four is far more likely.\",\"hint\":\"{1 - pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.3-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your method has won 45% of its last 300 trades, and its average win is about twice its average loss. It has just lost 6 trades in a row. Every one of them followed your rules.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the streak tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The method has stopped working; switch to a new one\",\"rationale\":\"At a 45% win rate, a run of six losses somewhere in 100 trades is about 73% likely. The streak alone can't show that anything changed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"On its own, very little: runs like this are normal for this method. Keep checking that the rules are followed\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A win is now due, so the next trade should be bigger\",\"rationale\":\"Trades are independent: six losses don't make a win more likely. Sizing up after losses adds risk exactly when you're under strain.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Six losses prove the first 300 trades were luck\",\"rationale\":\"Six trades can't overturn 300. A short run of losses is expected even when a method works.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"At a 45% win rate, at least one run of six losses somewhere in 100 trades is about 73% likely. A streak is what randomness looks like, so on its own it doesn't show the method stopped working, and it doesn't make a win due. What you can check is the process.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"streaks-and-runs\",\"variance\"],\"hints\":[\"A method that loses 55% of the time: how often would six losses in a row turn up?\",\"A streak alone: evidence of change, or normal randomness?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.4-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A method won 12 of its first 20 trades (60%). Which range of true win rates fits that result?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About 58% to 62%\",\"rationale\":\"Far too narrow for 20 trades. Each single win or loss moves the result by 5 points.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"About 38% to 82%\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exactly 60%\",\"rationale\":\"60% is what happened in this sample, not the method's true rate. Another 20 trades could easily show 45% or 70%.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"About 55% to 65%\",\"rationale\":\"That's roughly the range after 400 trades. At 20 trades the plausible range is more than four times as wide.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"With only 20 trades, the plausible range around 60% runs from about 38.5% to 81.5%. That includes coin-flip methods (50%) and strong ones. The range narrows only as trades add up: about 50.4–69.6% at 100 trades.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.4-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A new method won 7 of its first 10 trades. You're thinking of doubling your risk per trade to make the most of it.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the evidence support?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Double the risk: 70% is a clear edge\",\"rationale\":\"Ten trades give a plausible range from about 42% to 98%. A coin-flip method wins 7 or more of 10 about 17% of the time.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Drop the method: 7 of 10 is too good to be real\",\"rationale\":\"A small sample can't prove it's bad either. The right response is the same: keep the risk fixed and gather more trades.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the risk only for the next 10 trades, then decide\",\"rationale\":\"Raising risk before the evidence exists bets more on an untested idea. Twenty trades still leave a wide range.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Keep the risk the same and keep collecting trades: 10 can't show an edge\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Seven of ten is a sample of ten. The plausible range runs from about 42% to 98%, and a pure coin flip wins 7 or more of 10 about 17% of the time. Keep the risk fixed and judge the method once it has hundreds of trades.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"How many trades is this result based on?\",\"How often might a coin-flip method win 7 of 10?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.4-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You've made 8 trades with a new method. What can those 8 trades fairly tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Whether the method has an edge\",\"rationale\":\"Eight trades leave a plausible range dozens of points wide. An edge takes hundreds of trades to show.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Your true win rate, to within a few percent\",\"rationale\":\"Each trade moves an 8-trade win rate by 12.5 points. A few percent of precision needs hundreds of trades.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Whether you followed your plan on each trade\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing at all, so there's no point reviewing them\",\"rationale\":\"They can't measure an edge, but each one can be checked against the plan: entry rules, stop, size.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Eight trades are far too few to measure an edge or a win rate. But each single trade can be judged on its process: did it follow the entry rules, have a stop and stay within the risk limit? That's what a small sample can fairly tell you.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"What can be checked on one trade, and what needs many?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is a random walk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A market with no trends or ranges\",\"rationale\":\"Random walks draw trends and ranges all the time, by chance. That's why a pattern alone doesn't prove anything.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A price path where each step up or down is decided by chance\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A chart that always returns to where it started\",\"rationale\":\"A random walk has no pull back to its start. It can drift far away and stay there.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A trend that reverses at random times\",\"rationale\":\"There is no underlying trend in a random walk. Each step is independent; trends only appear by chance.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In a random walk each step is like a coin flip, with no memory of earlier steps. It still draws trends, ranges and levels that look meaningful, which is why a chart alone can't prove a pattern.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"random-walk\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A friend shows you three LABX charts where price bounced off the same kind of level, and says: 'It worked all three times. This pattern is reliable.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's the most useful response?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three out of three is 100%: it's reliable\",\"rationale\":\"Three cases are a tiny sample, chosen after the fact. Random charts also bounce off levels two or three times.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Patterns never work, so ignore it\",\"rationale\":\"Some levels have shown a small real effect. The point isn't to reject patterns but to test them against chance.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How often does it 'work' on random charts, and does the difference survive costs?\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Try it with a bigger size to see if it keeps working\",\"rationale\":\"That tests the idea with money before there's any evidence. Count it on many charts, random ones included, first.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Random walks draw bounces at levels by chance, so three hand-picked examples prove little. The useful question is how often the pattern works compared with random data, over many cases, after costs.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"random-walk\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Could a coin-flip chart show the same three bounces?\",\"Compare with what happens by chance, over many cases.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Before you trust a chart pattern, which question matters most?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Does it work more often than on random data, after costs?\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Does it look clean on the chart?\",\"rationale\":\"Random walks draw clean-looking patterns too. Looks can't separate a real effect from chance.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Have experienced traders recommended it?\",\"rationale\":\"Recommendations may rest on remembered winners. The test is a count against chance, not who suggests it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Did it work the last time you saw it?\",\"rationale\":\"One case is a sample of one. A pattern can 'work' once by chance on any chart.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Any pattern can work sometimes by chance. In one test, chart gaps 'filled' 83.4% of the time, but random levels did as well (83.5%). A pattern earns trust only if it beats random data by enough to survive costs.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"random-walk\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Random charts draw patterns too. What would you compare against?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.6-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"500 people each guess LABX's direction for 10 days by flipping a coin. How many would you expect to get all 10 days right? Round to 1 decimal.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.5,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"people\"},\"explanation\":\"Each person has a 1 in 1,024 chance of 10 right (0.5 multiplied by itself 10 times). 500 ÷ 1,024 ≈ 0.5: about one perfect record for every two groups this size, from luck alone.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"luck-vs-skill\"],\"hints\":[\"One day right by luck: 1 in 2. Ten days in a row: multiply.\",\"0.5 multiplied by itself 10 times is 1 in 1,024.\",\"500 ÷ 1,024 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"people\":[200,5000,100]},\"answer\":\"round(people / 1024, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"{people} people each guess LABX's direction for 10 days by flipping a coin. How many would you expect to get all 10 days right? Round to 1 decimal.\",\"explanation\":\"Each person has a 1 in 1,024 chance of 10 right (0.5 multiplied by itself 10 times). {people} ÷ 1,024 ≈ {answer:1}, from luck alone.\",\"hint\":\"{people} ÷ 1,024 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.6-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is survivorship bias?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Holding on to losing trades so they can survive\",\"rationale\":\"That describes avoiding losses, a different trap. Survivorship is about which results you get to see.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The tendency of skilled traders to last longer\",\"rationale\":\"That assumes the survivors are skilled. The bias is that survival alone can come from luck when many try.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Judging from the winners you can see, while the many who failed are out of sight\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Preferring methods that have survived many years\",\"rationale\":\"Long testing is good evidence. The bias is seeing only the survivors and forgetting how many started.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"When many people try, some get great results by luck. If only those are shown, in ads or on social media, chance looks like talent. Ask how many started, not just how the winners did.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"survivorship-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about who you get to see, and who you don't.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-0.2.6-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"An online trader posts a screenshot of a +40% month and sells a course on his method. He says he has 'never had a losing month'.\",\"prompt\":\"Which evidence would best help you tell skill from luck?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"More screenshots of his best months\",\"rationale\":\"Chosen winners are the problem, not the proof. Best months say nothing about the months you don't see.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How many followers and students he has\",\"rationale\":\"Popularity measures marketing, not skill. Many followers can come from a few lucky months, shown well.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How confident he sounds about next month\",\"rationale\":\"Confidence isn't evidence. Lucky and skilled traders can sound equally sure.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Hundreds of trades under the same rules, results after costs, compared with the benchmark\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Skill shows as a consistent process over a large sample, with results after costs and next to the benchmark. A single great month, shown in an ad, fits luck just as well: among many traders, someone always has one.\",\"lesson\":\"0.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"luck-vs-skill\",\"survivorship-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"What would a lucky trader be unable to fake?\",\"Think process, sample size and costs.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You short 20 units of LABX at the bid, 99.95. Later the mid is 98.50 and you buy back at the ask. What is your P&L before commission, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":28,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A short buys back at the ask: 98.50 + 0.05 = 98.55. P&L = 20 × (99.95 − 98.55) = 20 × 1.40 = 28.00. Buying back at the mid would give 29.00, but no order fills there.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"long-and-short\"],\"hints\":[\"A short makes money when it buys back lower than it sold.\",\"A short closes with a buy, at the ask: mid + 0.05.\",\"Buy back at 98.55. P&L = 20 × (99.95 − 98.55).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[10,60,5],\"mid\":[97,99.5,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(units * (99.95 - (mid + 0.05)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You short {units} units of LABX at the bid, 99.95. Later the mid is {mid:2} and you buy back at the ask. What is your P&L before commission, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"A short buys back at the ask: {mid:2} + 0.05 = {mid + 0.05:2}. P&L = {units} × (99.95 − {mid + 0.05:2}) = {answer:2}. Buying back at the mid would give {units * (99.95 - mid):2}, but no order fills there.\",\"hint\":\"Buy back at {mid + 0.05:2}. P&L = {units} × (99.95 − {mid + 0.05:2}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You're short LABX and close the position with a market order. At which price does it fill?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The bid, mid − 0.05\",\"rationale\":\"The bid is where a short opens, because opening is a sale. Closing a short is a buy, and buys pay the ask.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The mid\",\"rationale\":\"The mid is what the chart draws and how open positions are valued, but no order fills there.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The ask, mid + 0.05\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your entry price\",\"rationale\":\"Your exit price comes from the market when you close, not from where you entered.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Closing a short means buying back, and every buy fills at the ask, half a spread above the mid. A short opens at the bid and closes at the ask: the mirror image of a long.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"long-and-short\",\"bid-ask-spread\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You short 50 LABX at 99.95 with no stop, planning to wait it out if price rises. Overnight, unexpected news lifts the mid from 100.00 to 112.00.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this show about a short with no stop?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It loses more because shorts pay a wider spread\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is the same 0.10 in both directions. The danger is the open-ended rise, not the cost.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The loss doesn't count until the short is closed\",\"rationale\":\"An open loss is already in your equity, and waiting can make it bigger. Without a stop nothing limits it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It can lose at most 99.95 a unit, the price it sold at\",\"rationale\":\"That's a long's limit turned around. A long can't lose more than its price; a short loses whatever price rises.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its loss has no fixed limit: price can keep rising\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"At a mid of 112.00 you'd buy back at 112.05: −12.10 a unit, −605.00 on 50 units before commission, about 6% of a 10,000.00 account. Price could keep rising. A short needs a stop above the entry, sized like any trade.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"long-and-short\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the most a long can lose with the most a short can lose.\",\"A long's loss stops when price reaches zero. What stops a rising price?\",\"Only a stop, set before entry, limits a short's loss.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which price are LABX candles drawn from?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The bid\",\"rationale\":\"Many FX and CFD platforms plot the bid, but LABX candles are drawn from the mid.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The mid, halfway between bid and ask\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The ask\",\"rationale\":\"The ask is what buyers pay. LABX plots the mid, 0.05 below it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The last trade\",\"rationale\":\"Stock charts usually plot the last trade. LABX plots the mid, halfway between bid and ask.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"LABX candles are drawn from the mid, halfway between the bid and the ask. Stock charts usually plot the last trade and many FX platforms the bid, so check which price a chart shows before reading your stop against it.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"price-types\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're long LABX with a stop at 97.40. What is the highest mid at which the stop triggers?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":97.45,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A long's stop sells, so it triggers when the bid reaches it. Bid = mid − 0.05, so the bid is 97.40 when the mid is 97.45. Any lower mid triggers it too; a higher one doesn't.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"price-types\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Which quote closes a long: the bid or the ask?\",\"A long's stop triggers when the bid reaches it. Bid = mid − 0.05.\",\"Solve mid − 0.05 = 97.40.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"stop\":[95,99.5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(stop + 0.05, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You're long LABX with a stop at {stop:2}. What is the highest mid at which the stop triggers?\",\"explanation\":\"A long's stop sells, so it triggers when the bid reaches it. Bid = mid − 0.05, so the bid is {stop:2} when the mid is {answer:2}. Any lower mid triggers it too; a higher one doesn't.\",\"hint\":\"Solve mid − 0.05 = {stop:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're short LABX with a stop at 102.00. The chart, drawn from the mid, peaked at 101.96 this hour. Your trade history says the short was stopped out at 102.00.\",\"prompt\":\"What happened?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A platform error: the chart never reached the stop\",\"rationale\":\"The chart shows the mid. A short's stop triggers on the ask, 0.05 higher: 101.96 + 0.05 = 102.01.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The stop triggered on the bid, which sits above the mid\",\"rationale\":\"The bid is below the mid, not above. A short's stop is a buy, and buys trigger on the ask.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The ask reached 102.00: it runs 0.05 above the mid the chart shows\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stops trigger on the close, and this hour closed above 102.00\",\"rationale\":\"The chart peaked at 101.96, so no close was above 102.00. Stops act on live quotes, not on closes.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A short's stop is a buy, so it triggers on the ask. With the mid at 101.96 the ask was 102.01, through the stop. The chart plots the mid, so it never showed 102.00. Wider spreads make the gap bigger.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"price-types\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Which price does the chart plot, and which price closes a short?\",\"A short's stop triggers on the ask: mid + 0.05.\",\"At a mid of 101.96, the ask is 102.01.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy 43 units of LABX at the ask, 100.05. Commission is 0.05% of the fill value, rounded up to the cent. What is the commission, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2.16,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Fill value: 43 × 100.05 = 4,302.15. 0.05% of it is 2.151075, and any fraction of a cent rounds up: 2.16. Rounding to the nearest cent would give 2.15.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-stack\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Commission is charged on the fill value: units × price.\",\"0.05% is 0.0005. Multiply, then round any fraction of a cent up.\",\"Fill value: 43 × 100.05 = 4,302.15. Times 0.0005 = 2.151075.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[11,99,1],\"ask\":[50.05,150.05,1]},\"answer\":\"commission(units * ask)\",\"prompt\":\"You buy {units} units of LABX at the ask, {ask:2}. Commission is 0.05% of the fill value, rounded up to the cent. What is the commission, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Fill value: {units} × {ask:2} = {units * ask:2}. 0.05% of it is {units * ask * 0.0005:6}, and any fraction of a cent rounds up: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Fill value: {units} × {ask:2} = {units * ask:2}. Times 0.0005 = {units * ask * 0.0005:6}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A trade's gross result is +200.00. Which costs can stand between that and your net result?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Spread, commission, slippage, financing and account fees\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only commission: the spread is already in the price\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is a cost too: you buy above the mid and sell below it. A gross result measured on the mid doesn't include it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Spread and commission, but only on losing trades\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are paid on every trade, winning or losing. They shrink winners and enlarge losers.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only the costs shown on the ticket before you send it\",\"rationale\":\"The ticket can show commission, but slippage and overnight financing appear later: at the fill or at the cut-off.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The cost stack: the spread, a commission on each fill, slippage when a fill comes worse than expected, financing on leveraged positions held overnight, and data or platform fees. Each comes off the gross result, win or lose.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-stack\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A broker advertises zero commission with a 0.40 spread. Your current broker charges 0.05% per fill with a 0.10 spread. You plan round trips of 50 LABX at a mid of 100.00.\",\"prompt\":\"Which statement is right?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The zero-commission broker is cheaper: there's no fee\",\"rationale\":\"No fee, but a 0.40 spread costs 50 × 0.40 = 20.00 a round trip, against 10.01 at your broker.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Your current broker is cheaper: 10.01 against 20.00 a round trip\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"They cost the same: brokers price to match each other\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing makes their costs equal. Add up each round trip: 10.01 against 20.00.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"You can't compare them until you know whether the trade wins\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are known before the trade: the spread and commission don't depend on the result.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Current broker: 50 × 0.10 = 5.00 of spread plus 2.51 + 2.50 commission = 10.01. Zero commission: 50 × 0.40 = 20.00, all in the spread. The cost didn't disappear; it moved into the spread and doubled.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-stack\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Add up each broker's full round trip, not just the commission line.\",\"Spread cost = units × spread. Commission is paid on both fills.\",\"Zero commission: 50 × 0.40 = 20.00. Current: 5.00 + 2.51 + 2.50.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.5-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX trades near a mid of 100.00, with a 0.10 spread and 0.05% commission per fill. Roughly how far must the mid move in your favour, per unit, to cover a round trip's costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The spread costs 0.10 a unit, crossed once. Each fee is about 0.05% of 100.00 = 0.05 a unit, paid twice. Together: about 0.20. Counting one fee would give 0.15.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"break-even-move\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the spread once and the commission twice.\",\"Each fee is about 0.05% of the price, per unit.\",\"0.10 + 0.05 + 0.05.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"mid\":[40,200,20]},\"answer\":\"round(0.1 + 0.001 * mid, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABX trades near a mid of {mid:2}, with a 0.10 spread and 0.05% commission per fill. Roughly how far must the mid move in your favour, per unit, to cover a round trip's costs?\",\"explanation\":\"The spread costs 0.10 a unit, crossed once. Each fee is about 0.05% of {mid:2} = {0.0005 * mid:2} a unit, paid twice. Together: about {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"0.10 + {0.0005 * mid:2} + {0.0005 * mid:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.5-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your LABX round trip costs about 0.20 a unit. Your target is a mid move of 0.80 a unit. What percentage of the gross gain at the target goes to costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Cost share = cost ÷ gross gain = 0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 0.25, or 25%. The same 0.20 would be 50% of a 0.40 target and 5% of a 4.00 one.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"break-even-move\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the cost by the gross gain, then turn it into a percentage.\",\"Cost share = round-trip cost ÷ planned move × 100.\",\"0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 0.25.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"target\":[0.4,4,0.2]},\"answer\":\"round(0.2 / target * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Your LABX round trip costs about 0.20 a unit. Your target is a mid move of {target:2} a unit. What percentage of the gross gain at the target goes to costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Cost share = cost ÷ gross gain = 0.20 ÷ {target:2} = {answer:1}%. A bigger target shrinks the share; a smaller one grows it.\",\"hint\":\"0.20 ÷ {target:2} = {0.2 / target:3}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.5-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Trader A takes 20 trades a day, aiming for 0.30 a unit each. Trader B takes 2 trades a day, aiming for 3.00. Both trade 100 LABX units, with round trips of about 0.20 a unit.\",\"prompt\":\"Whose results do costs hurt most, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A's: costs take about 67% of each planned gain, 20 times a day\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"B's: bigger targets mean bigger costs\",\"rationale\":\"Costs depend on size and price, not on the target. B pays the same 0.20 a unit, under 7% of a 3.00 target.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neither: each round trip costs the same\",\"rationale\":\"Each costs about the same, but A pays it ten times as often, against gains a tenth the size.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"B's: holding positions longer adds more spread\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is crossed once per round trip, however long you hold. LABX charges nothing for holding.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A pays about 0.20 to chase 0.30: two-thirds of each planned gain, 20 times a day, about 400.20 in costs on 100 units at 100.00. B pays the same 0.20 against 3.00, under 7%, twice a day: about 40.02.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"break-even-move\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare each trader's cost with the gain they're aiming for.\",\"Cost share = 0.20 ÷ target. Then count how often each pays it.\",\"A: 0.20 ÷ 0.30 ≈ 67%. B: 0.20 ÷ 3.00 ≈ 7%.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.6-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You're long 40 units of LABX and the price rises 0.85. What is your P&L before costs, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":34,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"P&L = units × price change = 40 × 0.85 = 34.00 vUSD. On LABX each point is worth 1.00 per unit, so 0.85 of a point on 40 units is 34.00.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"point-value\"],\"hints\":[\"P&L = units × price change.\",\"Each LABX point is worth 1.00 vUSD per unit.\",\"40 × 0.85.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[10,80,5],\"move\":[0.25,3,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(units * move, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You're long {units} units of LABX and the price rises {move:2}. What is your P&L before costs, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"P&L = units × price change = {units} × {move:2} = {answer:2} vUSD. On LABX each point is worth 1.00 per unit.\",\"hint\":\"{units} × {move:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.6-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy 20,000 units of EUR/USD at 1.0850 and sell at 1.0895. What is your P&L before costs, in USD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":90,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"USD\"},\"explanation\":\"1.0895 − 1.0850 = 0.0045, which is 45 pips. On 20,000 units a pip is worth 20,000 × 0.0001 = 2.00 USD, so the P&L is 45 × 2.00 = 90.00 USD.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"point-value\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the move in pips: one pip is 0.0001.\",\"Pip value = units × 0.0001.\",\"45 pips × 2.00 USD.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[10000,50000,10000],\"pips\":[10,80,5]},\"answer\":\"round(units * 0.0001 * pips, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You buy {units} units of EUR/USD at 1.0850 and sell at {1.085 + pips * 0.0001:4}. What is your P&L before costs, in USD?\",\"explanation\":\"{1.085 + pips * 0.0001:4} − 1.0850 = {pips * 0.0001:4}, which is {pips} pips. On {units} units a pip is worth {units} × 0.0001 = {units * 0.0001:2} USD, so the P&L is {pips} × {units * 0.0001:2} = {answer:2} USD.\",\"hint\":\"{pips} pips × {units * 0.0001:2} USD.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.6-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A friend says their trade 'made 10 points'. What do you need to know before you can say how much money that is?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: 10 points is always 10.00\",\"rationale\":\"A point is worth 1.00 only per LABX unit. On 30 units it's 30.00; on a futures contract it depends on the multiplier.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The direction of the trade\",\"rationale\":\"Direction says whether 10 points is a gain or a loss, not how much money it is.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only the instrument's name\",\"rationale\":\"The instrument gives the value per point, but not how many units or contracts were held.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The position size and the value of one point\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Money = points × size × value per point. 10 points on 1 LABX unit is 10.00 vUSD; on 30 units, 300.00; on one contract with a multiplier of 100, 1,000.00. The points alone say nothing about the money.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"point-value\"],\"hints\":[\"Think of the formula that turns a price move into money.\",\"Money = move × size × value per point.\",\"10 points on 1 unit and on 30 units are different amounts.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.7-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A quote line reads: XYZ · Last 24.31 · Bid 24.30 · Ask 24.33. You buy now with a market order. Which price do you expect to pay?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"24.30, the bid\",\"rationale\":\"The bid is what buyers offer, so it's what you'd get selling. A market buy pays the ask.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"24.31, the last price\",\"rationale\":\"The last price is a trade that already happened. Your buy fills against the sellers waiting at the ask.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"24.33, the ask\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"24.315, the midpoint\",\"rationale\":\"The mid is a reference halfway between bid and ask. No market order fills there.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A market buy fills against the sellers' best offer, the ask: 24.33. The last price, 24.31, is a trade that already happened, and the bid, 24.30, is what a seller would get.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"price-types\",\"bid-ask-spread\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.7-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your balance is 9,874.20. You hold one open long of 60 units bought at 101.45, and the mid is now 102.30. What is your equity, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":9925.2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Open P&L at the mid: 60 × (102.30 − 101.45) = +51.00. Equity = balance + open P&L = 9,874.20 + 51.00 = 9,925.20. The entry fee is already in the balance, so don't take it off again.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"balance-and-equity\"],\"hints\":[\"Equity = balance + the open P&L of positions, valued at the mid.\",\"Open P&L of a long = units × (mid − entry price).\",\"60 × (102.30 − 101.45) = 51.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"balance\":[9800,10200,0.01],\"units\":[10,80,10],\"entry\":[98.05,102.05,0.1],\"mid\":[97.5,104,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(balance + units * (mid - entry), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your balance is {balance:2}. You hold one open long of {units} units bought at {entry:2}, and the mid is now {mid:2}. What is your equity, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Open P&L at the mid: {units} × ({mid:2} − {entry:2}) = {units * (mid - entry):2}. Equity = balance + open P&L = {balance:2} + ({units * (mid - entry):2}) = {answer:2}. The entry fee is already in the balance.\",\"hint\":\"{units} × ({mid:2} − {entry:2}) = {units * (mid - entry):2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.1.7-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan: buy 25 LABX at market with a stop at 97.50. The ticket on screen reads: LABX · Sell · 25 units · Market · Stop 97.50.\",\"prompt\":\"What should you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Send it: the size and the stop match the plan\",\"rationale\":\"Size and stop match, but the side is Sell. That opens a short, a bet on a fall your plan doesn't make.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Change the side to Buy, read the ticket again, then send\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Send it, then fix the side once the order has filled\",\"rationale\":\"After the fill you hold a position you never planned and pay costs to undo it. Fix the ticket first.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Remove the stop so the order fills faster\",\"rationale\":\"A stop doesn't slow an entry. Removing it leaves the loss with no limit.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The side is wrong: Sell would open a short, a bet against your plan. Fix it before sending, then read the rest of the ticket once more. Fixing it after the fill means paying costs twice for a position you never planned.\",\"lesson\":\"1.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"order-ticket\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the ticket with the plan field by field.\",\"Side, units, type, stop: which one differs?\",\"The plan says buy; the ticket says Sell.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does a line chart of hourly LABX prices show for each hour?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The open and the close\",\"rationale\":\"A line has one point per period. There's no room for a second price, so only the close is kept.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The high and the low\",\"rationale\":\"Highs and lows need a vertical line or a wick. A line chart drops them, which is why spikes vanish from it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only the closing price\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"All four prices, drawn more thinly\",\"rationale\":\"That describes an OHLC bar. A line chart keeps a single price per period: the close.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A line chart joins each period's close and drops the open, high and low. It's clean for overviews, but a spike that closed lower never appears on it.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-types\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An OHLC bar's line runs from 99.40 to 100.60. Its left tick is at 100.30 and its right tick at 99.70. What happened in that hour?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It opened at 99.70 and closed at 100.30, a bullish hour\",\"rationale\":\"That swaps the ticks. The left tick is the open and the right tick is the close, so price fell from 100.30 to 99.70.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It opened at 100.30, traded between 99.40 and 100.60, and closed at 99.70\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It closed at 100.60, the top of the line\",\"rationale\":\"The top of the line is the high. The close is the tick on the right, 99.70.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It opened at 99.40 and closed at 100.60\",\"rationale\":\"Those are the ends of the line: the low and the high. Open and close are the side ticks.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"On an OHLC bar the left tick is the open (100.30), the right tick is the close (99.70) and the line spans the low (99.40) to the high (100.60). The close is below the open, so the hour was bearish.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-types\"],\"hints\":[\"An OHLC bar puts the open on one side and the close on the other.\",\"Left tick = open, right tick = close. The ends of the line are the high and the low.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You want one picture of how a stock index has moved over the last ten years, to compare it with another index.\",\"prompt\":\"Which chart fits the task best?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"1-hour candles, so no detail is lost\",\"rationale\":\"Ten years of hourly candles is thousands of candles. At that zoom each one is a sliver, so the detail is there but unreadable.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"OHLC bars, so each period's open is visible\",\"rationale\":\"Single-period opens don't matter for a ten-year view. The extra marks add clutter without answering the question.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Any type: they all show the same information\",\"rationale\":\"They draw the same data but keep different amounts of it. For a long overview, fewer marks per period make the path easier to read.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A line chart of the closes\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"For a long overview and a comparison, a line of closes shows the path clearly with one price per period. Bars and candles are for reading single periods, which a ten-year view doesn't need.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-types\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what the task needs: single periods, or the overall path?\",\"A ten-year comparison needs the path, not each period's high and low.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Four 1-hour candles have highs of 100.80, 101.25, 101.10 and 100.95. What is the high of the 4-hour candle they form, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101.25,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A 4-hour candle's high is the highest of its hourly highs: 101.25. Taking the last high or an average would miss the moment price traded highest.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"candle-aggregation\"],\"hints\":[\"The 4-hour high is one of the four hourly highs.\",\"Take the highest of the four, not the last one or the average.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"h1\":[100,102,0.05],\"h2\":[100,102,0.05],\"h3\":[100,102,0.05],\"h4\":[100,102,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"h1 != h2\",\"h1 != h3\",\"h1 != h4\",\"h2 != h3\",\"h2 != h4\",\"h3 != h4\"],\"answer\":\"max(h1, h2, h3, h4)\",\"prompt\":\"Four 1-hour candles have highs of {h1:2}, {h2:2}, {h3:2} and {h4:2}. What is the high of the 4-hour candle they form, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"A 4-hour candle's high is the highest of its hourly highs: {answer:2}. Taking the last high or an average would miss the moment price traded highest.\",\"hint\":\"Compare {h1:2}, {h2:2}, {h3:2} and {h4:2}, and take the highest.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over four hours LABX rises, drops 0.80 in the third hour, then recovers. The 4-hour candle is bullish. Where is the 0.80 drop on the 4-hour chart?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In the lower wick, which always shows each pullback\",\"rationale\":\"The lower wick only shows how far the lowest low sits below the body. A drop in the middle of the period has no mark of its own.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nowhere: the 4-hour chart removes bearish hours as noise\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing is removed. Every hour's prices are inside the 4-hour candle; the summary just can't show their order.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Inside the candle: it keeps only the first open, highest high, lowest low and last close\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It would have turned the 4-hour candle bearish\",\"rationale\":\"Direction compares the last close with the first open. A drop in the middle doesn't change that if price recovers.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Aggregation keeps four prices from the four hours. The drop happened, but it sits inside the 4-hour candle's range with no mark of its own, which is why longer timeframes look smoother.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"candle-aggregation\",\"timeframe\"],\"hints\":[\"List the four prices a 4-hour candle keeps from its hours.\",\"None of those four records what happened in the middle of the period.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You plan trades that you'll usually hold for three to five days.\",\"prompt\":\"Which timeframe fits best as your main chart?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"4-hour or daily candles\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"1-minute candles, to time every move precisely\",\"rationale\":\"A three-day hold spans thousands of one-minute candles. Most of what they show is noise for a trade of that length.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Whichever timeframe shows the clearest signal right now\",\"rationale\":\"Switching until a chart agrees with you is a bias. Fix the timeframe from your holding period first.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Weekly candles, because longer is always more reliable\",\"rationale\":\"A five-day hold fits inside one weekly candle, so you'd see one or two candles per trade: too coarse to manage it.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The timeframe should match the holding period. For trades of several days, 4-hour or daily candles give enough candles per trade without drowning it in minute-by-minute noise.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"timeframe\"],\"hints\":[\"Start from how long the trade lasts.\",\"You want several candles per trade, but not thousands.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does the volume bar under a 1-hour candle measure?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"How many buyers there were compared with sellers\",\"rationale\":\"Every unit traded has a buyer and a seller, so volume can't lean to one side. It counts units traded.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How many units traded during that hour\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How far price moved during the hour\",\"rationale\":\"That's the candle's range, high minus low. Volume counts units traded, which can be large on a tiny move.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"How many orders were waiting in the order book\",\"rationale\":\"Resting orders that haven't traded aren't volume. Only units that actually changed hands count.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Volume is the number of units traded in the period. Each unit has a buyer and a seller, so it measures activity, not which side was stronger.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"volume\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A 1-hour LABX candle closes 0.03 above its open. Its volume is three times the 20-hour average.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you conclude from these two facts alone?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buyers were in control, because it closed higher on high volume\",\"rationale\":\"A 0.03 rise is close to flat, and volume counts both sides equally. High volume doesn't say who won.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A big move is bound to follow within a few hours\",\"rationale\":\"High volume shows attention now. On its own it doesn't predict the size or timing of the next move.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The volume figure must be wrong, since price barely moved\",\"rationale\":\"Heavy trading with little net movement is common, for example around news. Volume and direction are separate.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Unusually many units changed hands, with no clear direction\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Three times the average means unusual activity. The candle barely moved, and volume has no direction of its own, so the honest reading is 'busy, but undecided'.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"volume\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate the two facts: one is about activity, the other about direction.\",\"Volume counts both sides of every trade. Does a 0.03 move show a winner?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The volume bars on a retail EUR/USD chart show tick volume. What is that?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The number of price changes at that broker during each period\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The total units of EUR/USD traded worldwide in each period\",\"rationale\":\"Spot forex has no central exchange, so no one sees that total. Tick volume counts one broker's price updates.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How many 0.0001 steps price moved in each period\",\"rationale\":\"That measures distance. Tick volume counts how often the price updated, whatever the size of each change.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The volume reported by a futures exchange for the euro\",\"rationale\":\"Exchange volume is real volume from futures, a different series. Tick volume comes from one broker's quotes.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Without a central exchange, retail FX and CFD charts count how many times one broker's price changed in each period. It often tracks activity, but it isn't the number of units traded.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"volume\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask who could know the total volume of spot forex. Is there a central exchange?\",\"Tick volume comes from a single broker's price feed.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.5-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"The last five LABX candles have ranges of 0.60, 0.45, 0.70, 0.55 and 0.50. What is their average range, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.56,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Add the five ranges: 2.80. Divide by 5: 0.56. That's what a normal candle looks like right now; a single big candle would not be.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"candle-range\"],\"hints\":[\"Add all five ranges first.\",\"Then divide the total by the number of candles, 5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"r1\":[0.2,1,0.05],\"r2\":[0.2,1,0.05],\"r3\":[0.2,1,0.05],\"r4\":[0.2,1,0.05],\"r5\":[0.2,1,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round((r1 + r2 + r3 + r4 + r5) / 5, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"The last five LABX candles have ranges of {r1:2}, {r2:2}, {r3:2}, {r4:2} and {r5:2}. What is their average range, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Add the five ranges: {r1 + r2 + r3 + r4 + r5:2}. Divide by 5: {answer:2}. That's what a normal candle looks like right now; a single big candle would not be.\",\"hint\":\"The total is {r1 + r2 + r3 + r4 + r5:2}. Divide it by 5.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.5-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX closes an hour at 100.00 and opens the next at 101.20. What does the space between 100.00 and 101.20 mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price traded through those prices very quickly\",\"rationale\":\"Fast trading still leaves prices inside a candle. Empty space between a close and the next open means no trades at all.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No trades happened at those prices between the two candles\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The data is missing, so the chart should be ignored\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing is missing. The last trade was at 100.00 and the next at 101.20: that jump is the gap.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A buy stop at 100.50 would fill at exactly 100.50\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing traded at 100.50, so nothing can fill there. A stop at 100.50 triggers at the next open, around 101.20.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A gap is a jump from one close to the next open with no trading in between. Any order resting inside the gap can only fill at the next available price, near the open.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"price-gaps\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask whether any trade happened at 100.50.\",\"A gap is empty space: the next trade after 100.00 was at 101.20.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.5-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A video says 'gaps always fill', so you should trade towards every gap you see.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the most accurate view?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's true: a gap is a magnet that price must revisit\",\"rationale\":\"Some gaps are never revisited. Nothing forces price back, and one counter-example breaks 'always'.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's false: price never returns to a gap\",\"rationale\":\"Price does return to many gaps, just not because they are gaps. 'Never' is as wrong as 'always'.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Price often returns, but mostly by chance, and some gaps never fill\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's true for small gaps, so trade towards those\",\"rationale\":\"No size rule makes a revisit certain. That's another untested claim built on the same myth.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Price returns to most nearby prices sooner or later, gaps included. The evidence that gaps pull price back is weak: revisits look close to what chance alone would produce.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"price-gaps\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask how often price would revisit any nearby price by chance.\",\"'Always' falls to one counter-example. Look for the answer that compares with chance.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.6-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX rises from 80.00 to 100.00. By what percentage did it rise?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"% change = (new − old) ÷ old = (100.00 − 80.00) ÷ 80.00 = 25%. Dividing by the new price instead (20%) understates the move.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"log-scale\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the move in price first: new − old.\",\"Divide the move by the old price, then multiply by 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"start\":[40,200,10],\"pct\":[10,150,10]},\"answer\":\"pct\",\"prompt\":\"LABX rises from {start:2} to {start * (1 + pct / 100):2}. By what percentage did it rise?\",\"explanation\":\"% change = (new − old) ÷ old = ({start * (1 + pct / 100):2} − {start:2}) ÷ {start:2} = {answer}%. Dividing by the new price instead understates the move.\",\"hint\":\"The move is {start * pct / 100:2}. Divide it by the old price, {start:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.6-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does a log price scale do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It draws equal price moves as equal heights\",\"rationale\":\"That's a linear scale. A log scale spaces prices by ratio, so equal percentage moves look equal.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It shows only the closes, joined by a line\",\"rationale\":\"That's a line chart, a chart type. Scale is separate: a line or a candle chart can use either scale.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It smooths prices to remove noise\",\"rationale\":\"A log scale changes the spacing of the axis, not the data. Every price is plotted as it is.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It draws equal percentage moves as equal heights\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"On a log scale, 50 → 100 and 100 → 200 take the same height because both are +100%. On a linear scale the second would look twice as tall.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"log-scale\"],\"hints\":[\"Think of 50 → 100 and 100 → 200. Which scale draws them the same?\",\"Linear spaces prices by difference; log spaces them by ratio.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.6-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You compare two indices over 20 years. One went from 1,000 to 4,000; the other from 100 to 500.\",\"prompt\":\"Which scale gives a fair picture of their growth?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A log scale, where equal percentage moves look equal\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A linear scale, because it shows the real price changes\",\"rationale\":\"On linear, the first index's +3,000 dwarfs the second's +400, though the second grew more: +400% against +300%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Either: the scale doesn't change what you see\",\"rationale\":\"Scale changes the heights. Over 20 years with big moves, linear and log tell very different visual stories.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A line chart instead of candles\",\"rationale\":\"That changes the chart type, not the scale. The question is how heights map to moves.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Growth is a percentage story: the first index rose 300%, the second 400%. A log scale draws those percentages as heights, while a linear scale makes the bigger index look far stronger.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"log-scale\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out each index's percentage change.\",\"Pick the scale on which the bigger percentage looks bigger.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.7-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these can a candle chart not tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The highest price traded in each hour\",\"rationale\":\"That's each candle's high, which every candle records.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Where each hour opened and closed\",\"rationale\":\"Open and close are two of the four prices every candle keeps.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Why the buyers in the last candle bought\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"How many units traded, when volume is shown\",\"rationale\":\"Volume bars record exactly that: the units traded in each period.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A chart records prices and, with volume, how much traded. It has no record of who traded or why, so any story about their motives is interpretation.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-limits\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.7-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A post shows four charts where a 'triple bottom' was followed by a strong rise, and says the pattern works every time.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the main problem with this evidence?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Four charts are enough, but they should share a timeframe\",\"rationale\":\"The timeframe isn't the issue. Four hand-picked winners say nothing about how often the pattern fails.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The charts were picked after the outcome, and the failures aren't shown\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Triple bottoms only work on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"That's another untested claim. The problem is the sample: only winners were shown.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The post should also show the volume\",\"rationale\":\"More detail on the same four winners doesn't fix the sample. What's missing are the cases where it failed.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"This is cherry-picking: the examples were chosen because they worked. Without the charts where a triple bottom failed, you can't tell how often it works, or whether it beats chance.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-limits\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask how the four charts were chosen.\",\"Which charts would you need to see to know how often it fails?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.2.7-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Before trusting a pattern you've seen on charts, which question matters most?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"How clean does the best example look?\",\"rationale\":\"The cleanest example was usually picked after the fact. Its looks say nothing about how often the pattern works.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Did it work the last time I saw it?\",\"rationale\":\"One recent case is a sample of one, and recency makes it feel bigger than it is.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How often does it work, on how many charts, after costs, compared with random?\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Do experienced traders talk about it?\",\"rationale\":\"Popularity isn't a base rate. A pattern can be widely quoted and still work no better than chance.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The evidence question asks for a base rate: how often, over a large sample, after costs, and against what random charts would produce. Without it, a pattern is a story.\",\"lesson\":\"1.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-limits\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Look for the option that counts, rather than describes.\",\"A base rate needs many cases and a comparison with chance.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.3-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX trades at 100.00, under resistance at 101.00. You want to buy only if price breaks above it. Which order fits?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A buy limit at 101.10\",\"rationale\":\"A buy limit above the ask fills at once, at the ask, about 100.05. You'd be in before any break.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A buy stop at 101.10\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A sell stop at 101.10\",\"rationale\":\"A sell stop sells, and it sits below the price. A breakout entry to buy needs a buy stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A buy limit at 99.90\",\"rationale\":\"That buys a dip below the price, the opposite of buying a break above resistance.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A buy stop waits above the price and becomes a market order only when the ask reaches 101.10, so you buy only after the break. A buy limit above the ask fills immediately, inside the range.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-entry-order\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.3-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your buy stop for 40 units sits at 105.20. The next candle opens with the mid at 105.65. Ignoring commission, how much does the slippage cost you, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":20,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Price gapped over the trigger, so the stop filled at the open's ask, 105.70: 0.50 above 105.20 per unit. 40 × 0.50 = 20.00. Measuring from the mid gives 18.00 and leaves out the half-spread.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-entry-order\",\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"A buy fills at the ask: the mid plus 0.05.\",\"After a gap the stop fills at the open's ask, not at the trigger. Slippage = fill − trigger.\",\"Ask 105.70 − trigger 105.20 = 0.50 per unit. Multiply by 40.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[10,60,5],\"trig\":[60,120,0.1],\"gap\":[0.1,1.2,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(units * (gap + 0.05), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your buy stop for {units} units sits at {trig:2}. The next candle opens with the mid at {trig + gap:2}. Ignoring commission, how much does the slippage cost you, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Price gapped over the trigger, so the stop filled at the open's ask, {trig + gap + 0.05:2}: {gap + 0.05:2} above {trig:2} per unit. {units} × {gap + 0.05:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Ask {trig + gap + 0.05:2} − trigger {trig:2} = {gap + 0.05:2} per unit. Multiply by {units}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.3-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long 50 LABX from 100.00. To avoid a bad fill, you protect it with a sell stop-limit: trigger 98.00, limit 97.90. After bad news, the next candle opens with the bid at 96.80 and keeps falling.\",\"prompt\":\"What is your position once the market opens?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Flat: sold at 97.90, the limit\",\"rationale\":\"A sell limit sells only at 97.90 or higher. The bid opened at 96.80, below it, so nothing sold.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Flat: sold at 96.80, the open\",\"rationale\":\"That's what a stop-market order would do. The limit forbids any sale below 97.90.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Flat: sold at 98.00, the trigger\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing traded at 98.00: the market opened below it. The trigger only turned the order into a limit.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Still long 50, with the loss growing\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The trigger fired and turned the order into a sell limit at 97.90. With the bid at 96.80 and falling, that limit can't fill, so the position stays open without protection. A protective stop should be a stop-market order: it fills worse in a gap, but it fills.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-limit-order\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Once triggered, a stop-limit becomes a limit order.\",\"A sell limit at 97.90 can only sell at 97.90 or higher. Where is the bid?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.4-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does an OCO order pair do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both orders fill together, at the same price\",\"rationale\":\"OCO means one cancels the other. Two orders filling at once is exactly what the link prevents.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The second order is placed only after the first one fills\",\"rationale\":\"That describes a sequence, like a bracket's exits waiting for the entry. OCO links two orders that are live at the same time.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When one order fills, the other is cancelled automatically\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Both orders stay active until you cancel them by hand\",\"rationale\":\"That's two separate orders. The point of OCO is that a fill cancels the other order for you.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"One-cancels-other links two live orders. The first to fill cancels the other, as with a bracket's stop and target, or a buy stop and a sell stop either side of a range.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"oco-order\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.4-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You bought 40 LABX at 100.00 with a bracket: stop 98.00, target 104.00. The target fills. An hour later LABX drops to 97.50.\",\"prompt\":\"Where do you stand?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Short 40, because the stop sold at 98.00\",\"rationale\":\"In a bracket the stop was cancelled when the target filled. Only an unlinked order would sell again.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Flat, with +160.00 before costs\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Flat, with +160.00 from the target and −80.00 from the stop\",\"rationale\":\"The stop never filled. Once the target closed the trade, it was cancelled, with nothing left to protect.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Long 40 again, bought back at 97.50\",\"rationale\":\"Exits don't re-enter. With the target filled, the trade is over and no order is working.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"40 × (104.00 − 100.00) = 160.00 before costs. When the target filled, the bracket cancelled the stop, so the drop to 97.50 doesn't touch you.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"bracket-order\",\"oco-order\"],\"hints\":[\"In a bracket, the exits are linked as one-cancels-other.\",\"The target closed the trade. What happened to the stop at that moment?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.4-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"On a platform that allows short selling, you bought 30 units with a separate sell stop at 97.00, not a bracket. At 101.50 you close the position by hand and forget the stop. A week later LABX falls through 97.00 to 96.00.\",\"prompt\":\"What is your position now?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Short 30, sold near 97.00\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Flat: the stop had nothing left to close\",\"rationale\":\"A separate stop doesn't know the position is gone. When triggered, it sells 30 units you no longer hold.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Long 30\",\"rationale\":\"You closed the long by hand, and nothing bought it back. The stop could only sell.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Short 60\",\"rationale\":\"The stop was for 30 units, so it sells 30. Your manual exit already sold the original 30.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The manual exit closed the long, but the separate stop stayed live. When the bid reached 97.00 it sold 30 units, opening a short you never decided on: an orphaned order. Check the order list after every manual exit.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"oco-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Was the stop linked to the position, or a separate order?\",\"A separate sell stop sells when triggered, whether or not you still hold the units.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.5-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You want a buy limit to cancel itself if it hasn't filled by the end of today's session. Which time in force?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"GTC\",\"rationale\":\"Good till cancelled stays active session after session, until it fills or you cancel it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Day\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"IOC\",\"rationale\":\"Immediate or cancel fills what it can at once and cancels the rest right away, not at the session's end.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"FOK\",\"rationale\":\"Fill or kill needs the whole order at once, or cancels it at once. It doesn't wait for the day.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A Day order rests in the book for the session and is cancelled automatically at its end if it hasn't filled. GTC would keep waiting; IOC and FOK don't wait at all.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"time-in-force\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.5-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You send a buy of 400 units at a limit of 100.10, IOC. Only 250 units are offered at 100.10 or better. How many units are cancelled?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":150,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"IOC fills the 250 units available now and cancels the other 150. With FOK nothing would fill, since 250 is less than the 400 ordered.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"time-in-force\"],\"hints\":[\"IOC fills what's available now and cancels the rest at once.\",\"Filled now: 250. The rest of the order doesn't wait.\",\"Ordered 400, filled 250. Cancelled: 400 − 250.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"size\":[200,900,50],\"avail\":[50,850,50]},\"constraints\":[\"avail < size\"],\"answer\":\"size - avail\",\"prompt\":\"You send a buy of {size} units at a limit of 100.10, IOC. Only {avail} units are offered at 100.10 or better. How many units are cancelled?\",\"explanation\":\"IOC fills the {avail} units available now and cancels the other {answer}. With FOK nothing would fill, since {avail} is less than the {size} ordered.\",\"hint\":\"Ordered {size}, filled {avail}. Cancelled: {size} − {avail}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.5-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Ten days ago you placed a buy limit for 40 LABX at 72.00, good till cancelled. You then changed your mind about the trade but never cancelled the order. Today LABX opens with a gap after bad news: the ask is 70.60.\",\"prompt\":\"What happens to the order?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: it expired at the end of the first session\",\"rationale\":\"That's a Day order. A GTC order stays active until it fills or you cancel it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It fills at 72.00, its limit price\",\"rationale\":\"A buy limit fills at its price or better. After a gap the first price is the open's ask, 70.60, which is better.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's skipped, because price jumped past the limit\",\"rationale\":\"A better price can't skip a limit. The ask at 70.60 is below 72.00, so it fills at once.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It fills at 70.60, and you hold 40 units you no longer wanted\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The GTC order was still live. A gap through a buy limit fills it at the open's ask, 70.60, better than the limit, but it buys a position you had decided against, in a falling market. Cancel orders you no longer want.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"time-in-force\",\"limit-order\"],\"hints\":[\"How long does a GTC order stay active?\",\"A buy limit fills at its price or better. Is 70.60 better than 72.00?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.6-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Buy limits A and B rest at 99.90; A arrived at 10:00, B at 10:05. Buy limit C rests at 99.95, placed at 10:10. A small market sell arrives. Which order fills first?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"C\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A\",\"rationale\":\"A is the oldest, but price comes before time. C bids more, so it's first in line.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"B\",\"rationale\":\"B is neither the best price nor the oldest order at 99.90.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"All three share it equally\",\"rationale\":\"Price-time priority doesn't share: the best price fills first, then the oldest order at that price.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Price-time priority ranks by price first: C's bid of 99.95 beats 99.90, so a seller meets C first. Between A and B, at the same price, A fills before B because it arrived earlier.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"order-queue\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.6-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A market buy of 500 units meets asks of 100 at 50.10, 200 at 50.15 and 200 at 50.20. What is the average fill price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":50.16,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Cost: 100 × 50.10 + 200 × 50.15 + 200 × 50.20 = 5,010.00 + 10,030.00 + 10,040.00 = 25,080.00. Divided by 500 units: 50.16. The plain average of the three prices, 50.15, ignores that most units paid more.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"order-queue\",\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"Average fill price = total cost ÷ total units.\",\"Multiply each price by the units filled there, then add the three amounts.\",\"Total cost 25,080.00 for 500 units.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[40,120,0.5],\"q1\":[100,300,50],\"q2\":[100,300,50],\"q3\":[50,300,50]},\"constraints\":[\"(q2 * 5 + q3 * 10) / (q1 + q2 + q3) == floor((q2 * 5 + q3 * 10) / (q1 + q2 + q3))\"],\"answer\":\"round(p + (q2 * 0.05 + q3 * 0.1) / (q1 + q2 + q3), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A market buy of {q1 + q2 + q3} units meets asks of {q1} at {p:2}, {q2} at {p + 0.05:2} and {q3} at {p + 0.1:2}. What is the average fill price?\",\"explanation\":\"Cost: {q1} × {p:2} + {q2} × {p + 0.05:2} + {q3} × {p + 0.1:2} = {q1 * p + q2 * (p + 0.05) + q3 * (p + 0.1):2}. Divided by {q1 + q2 + q3} units: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Total cost {q1 * p + q2 * (p + 0.05) + q3 * (p + 0.1):2} for {q1 + q2 + q3} units.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.6-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your sell limit at 103.00 rests in a real order book, with 500 units queued ahead of you at that price. Buyers take 350 units at 103.00, then price falls away.\",\"prompt\":\"How much of your order filled?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"All of it: price traded at your limit\",\"rationale\":\"Trading at your price reaches you only once the queue ahead is used up. Only 350 of the 500 ahead traded.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"None: 150 units were still ahead of you\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Part of it: 350 units\",\"rationale\":\"Those 350 went to the orders ahead of you, which arrived first at the same price.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Part of it: the book splits fills equally between orders\",\"rationale\":\"Under price-time priority fills aren't split equally. The earliest orders at the price fill first.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"At 103.00 the 500 units ahead of you fill first. Buyers took 350, so 150 were still in front of your order when price fell away. Price touched your level, but you weren't filled.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"order-queue\"],\"hints\":[\"At the same price, who fills first?\",\"Compare the 350 units traded with the 500 queued ahead of you.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.7-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your sell stop at 54.00 protects a long. The market opens with the bid at 53.35. How much slippage is that per unit, against the stop price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.65,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The stop expected 54.00 and filled at the open's bid, 53.35: 0.65 per unit worse than planned.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"Slippage is the gap between the price you expected and the fill.\",\"The stop expected 54.00. It filled at the open's bid.\",\"54.00 − 53.35 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"stop\":[40,120,0.5],\"gap\":[0.05,1.5,0.05]},\"answer\":\"gap\",\"prompt\":\"Your sell stop at {stop:2} protects a long. The market opens with the bid at {stop - gap:2}. How much slippage is that per unit, against the stop price?\",\"explanation\":\"The stop expected {stop:2} and filled at the open's bid, {stop - gap:2}: {answer:2} per unit worse than planned.\",\"hint\":\"{stop:2} − {stop - gap:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.7-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Long 25 LABX at 60.00, stop 56.00: 100.00 at risk before costs, your 1R. A gap fills the stop at 53.00. How many R did you lose?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.75,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Real loss: 25 × (60.00 − 53.00) = 175.00. In R: 175.00 ÷ 100.00 = 1.75R lost, against the 1R you planned.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-risk\",\"r-multiple\"],\"hints\":[\"R compares the real loss with the planned loss.\",\"Real loss: 25 × (60.00 − 53.00).\",\"175.00 ÷ 100.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"entry\":[40,120,1],\"dist\":[1,5,0.25],\"gap\":[0.5,4,0.25]},\"constraints\":[\"100 / dist == floor(100 / dist)\",\"100 / dist * entry <= 9900\"],\"answer\":\"round((dist + gap) / dist, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Long {100 / dist} LABX at {entry:2}, stop {entry - dist:2}: 100.00 at risk before costs, your 1R. A gap fills the stop at {entry - dist - gap:2}. How many R did you lose?\",\"explanation\":\"Real loss: {100 / dist} × ({entry:2} − {entry - dist - gap:2}) = {100 / dist * (dist + gap):2}. In R: {100 / dist * (dist + gap):2} ÷ 100.00 = {answer:2}R lost, against the 1R you planned.\",\"hint\":\"{100 / dist * (dist + gap):2} ÷ 100.00 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.7-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Trade A: long 25 units, stop 4.00 below entry. Trade B: long 125 units, stop 0.80 below entry. Both risk 100.00 before costs. Overnight news opens the market 5.00 below both entries, beyond both stops.\",\"prompt\":\"What happens?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both lose 100.00: the stops cap the loss\",\"rationale\":\"A stop caps the loss you planned for. In a gap it fills at the open, 5.00 below entry for both trades.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A loses more, because its stop was further away\",\"rationale\":\"A's far stop means fewer units. 25 × 5.00 = 125.00, against B's 625.00.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"B loses 625.00, −6.25R; A loses 125.00, −1.25R\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Both lose the same in R, since both risked 100.00\",\"rationale\":\"Same planned R, different size. The gap loss is units × gap: 125.00 for A, 625.00 for B.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"In a gap the loss is units × the gap: A, 25 × 5.00 = 125.00; B, 125 × 5.00 = 625.00. Both planned −1R, but B's tight stop allowed five times the units, and the gap hits every unit.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-risk\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"In a gap, both stops fill at the open, 5.00 below entry.\",\"Loss = units × 5.00 for each trade. Then divide by 100.00 for R.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.8-1\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What must a trailing stop on a long position never do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Move up as price makes new highs\",\"rationale\":\"That's its job: following price up cuts the risk and then locks in part of the gain.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Stay where it is while price pulls back\",\"rationale\":\"Holding still in a pullback is normal. A trail moves only when its rule says so, and only towards price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sit just under the last higher low\",\"rationale\":\"That's a common structure rule, not a mistake. The only forbidden move is away from price.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Move down, further from price\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A trailing stop only ever moves in the trade's favour. Lowering a long's stop is widening it: it raises the risk you agreed to, and MARKET//LAB counts it against Risk Discipline.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"trailing-stop\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.8-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"On LABX's 1-hour chart a normal candle spans about 0.80. You're long from 100.00 and trail the stop 0.30 under the highest price. The trend is intact: higher highs and higher lows.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely outcome?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"An ordinary pullback hits the stop early, often while the trend goes on\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The tight trail captures most of the trend, since it gives little back\",\"rationale\":\"It gives back little per exit, but ordinary 0.80 candles reach a 0.30 trail long before the trend ends.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing changes: a trail's distance doesn't affect when it exits\",\"rationale\":\"The distance decides which pullbacks reach the stop. At 0.30, most normal candles can.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The stop can't be hit while the trend makes higher lows\",\"rationale\":\"A higher low can still sit more than 0.30 under the high. A trail measured from the high ignores structure.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A trail tighter than a normal candle sits inside ordinary noise. Even in a healthy trend, a routine pullback of one candle reaches it, so the trade ends on noise rather than on a change of trend.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the trail's distance with the size of a normal candle.\",\"A pullback of one ordinary candle can be 0.80. Where is the stop?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.3.8-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Long from 50.00. You trail the stop 1.00 under the highest price reached. Price rises to 51.40, dips to 50.60, rises to 52.30, then falls to 50.90. At what price does the stop exit? Ignore the spread.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":51.3,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The stop follows the highest price: 51.40 puts it at 50.40, below the dip to 50.60. The new high, 52.30, lifts it to 51.30, and the fall to 50.90 passes through it there.\",\"lesson\":\"1.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"trailing-stop\"],\"hints\":[\"Track the highest price so far; the stop is always 1.00 under it.\",\"After 51.40 the stop is 50.40. Does the dip to 50.60 reach it?\",\"After 52.30 the stop is 51.30. Does the fall pass through it?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[40,120,1],\"up1\":[1,2,0.1],\"dip\":[0.3,0.9,0.1],\"up2\":[1,2,0.1],\"t\":[0.5,1.5,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"dip < t\",\"up2 > dip\"],\"answer\":\"round(e + up1 - dip + up2 - t, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Long from {e:2}. You trail the stop {t:2} under the highest price reached. Price rises to {e + up1:2}, dips to {e + up1 - dip:2}, rises to {e + up1 - dip + up2:2}, then falls to {e + up1 - dip + up2 - t - 0.4:2}. At what price does the stop exit? Ignore the spread.\",\"explanation\":\"The stop follows the highest price: {e + up1:2} puts it at {e + up1 - t:2}, below the dip to {e + up1 - dip:2}. The new high, {e + up1 - dip + up2:2}, lifts it to {answer:2}, and the fall passes through it there.\",\"hint\":\"After {e + up1 - dip + up2:2} the stop is {answer:2}. Does the fall pass through it?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You risked 80.00 on a trade, your 1R with costs included, and closed it for a net gain of 200.00. What was the result, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"An R-multiple divides the result by the initial risk: 200.00 ÷ 80.00 = +2.5R. The trade made two and a half times what it put at stake, whatever the position size.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"r-multiple\"],\"hints\":[\"R-multiple = result ÷ 1R.\",\"Put the result on top: 200.00 ÷ 80.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"risk\":[50,200,10],\"tenths\":[5,40,5]},\"answer\":\"tenths / 10\",\"prompt\":\"You risked {risk:2} on a trade, your 1R with costs included, and closed it for a net gain of {risk * tenths / 10:2}. What was the result, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"An R-multiple divides the result by the initial risk: {risk * tenths / 10:2} ÷ {risk:2} = +{answer}R. It shows the result as a multiple of what was at stake, whatever the position size.\",\"hint\":\"Put the result on top: {risk * tenths / 10:2} ÷ {risk:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy 60 units of LABX at 80.00 with a stop at 78.00. Commission is 0.05% of each fill, rounded up to the cent. What is 1R, in vUSD, with both commissions?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":124.74,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Price part: 60 × 2.00 = 120.00. Entry commission: 0.05% of 4,800.00 = 2.40. Exit commission at the stop: 0.05% of 4,680.00 = 2.34. 1R = 120.00 + 2.40 + 2.34 = 124.74. Leaving out the commissions understates the risk.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"initial-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"1R = units × distance to the stop, plus a commission at entry and another at the stop.\",\"The exit commission is charged on the stop price, not the entry: 60 × 78.00.\",\"120.00 + 0.05% of 4,800.00 + 0.05% of 4,680.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[20,100,10],\"entry\":[40,120,1],\"dist\":[1,4,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"units * entry <= 9900\"],\"answer\":\"round(units * dist + commission(units * entry) + commission(units * (entry - dist)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You buy {units} units of LABX at {entry:2} with a stop at {entry - dist:2}. Commission is 0.05% of each fill, rounded up to the cent. What is 1R, in vUSD, with both commissions?\",\"explanation\":\"Price part: {units} × {dist:2} = {units * dist:2}. Entry commission: 0.05% of {units * entry:2} = {commission(units * entry):2}. Exit commission at the stop: 0.05% of {units * (entry - dist):2} = {commission(units * (entry - dist)):2}. 1R = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{units * dist:2} + 0.05% of {units * entry:2} + 0.05% of {units * (entry - dist):2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You bought 50 units at 60.00 with a stop at 59.00: 1R was 52.98 with costs. When price dipped, you moved the stop to 58.00. It filled there, for a loss of 102.95.\",\"prompt\":\"How should your journal record the result?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"−1R: a stop-out is always −1R\",\"rationale\":\"A stop-out is −1R only at the initial stop. This loss, 102.95, is almost twice the 52.98 you planned to risk.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"−1R, because the moved stop became the new 1R\",\"rationale\":\"R is fixed when you enter. Letting a moved stop reset it would hide exactly the extra risk the move added.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"−1.94R: the loss measured against the initial 1R of 52.98\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Leave it out: trades with a moved stop don't count\",\"rationale\":\"Leaving it out hides the most expensive kind of mistake. The journal needs it, measured against the initial risk.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"R is fixed at entry: 102.95 ÷ 52.98 = 1.94R lost. Moving the stop nearly doubled the risk you had agreed to, and the journal must show it so the cost of the mistake is counted.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"initial-risk\",\"r-multiple\"],\"hints\":[\"Which 1R was agreed before the trade: the first stop or the moved one?\",\"Divide the actual loss by the initial 1R.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.6-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A round trip on LABX costs about 0.20 per unit. Your stop is 0.80 away. What share of the stop distance goes to costs, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Divide the costs by the stop distance: 0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 25%. A quarter of every R goes to costs before price does anything. With a 2.00 stop it would be 10%.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\"],\"hints\":[\"Costs ÷ stop distance.\",\"0.20 ÷ 0.80, then turn it into a percentage.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"dist\":[0.4,4,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(0.2 / dist * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A round trip on LABX costs about 0.20 per unit. Your stop is {dist:2} away. What share of the stop distance goes to costs, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Divide the costs by the stop distance: 0.20 ÷ {dist:2} = {answer:1}%. That share of every R goes to costs before price does anything.\",\"hint\":\"0.20 ÷ {dist:2}, then turn it into a percentage.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.6-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A 2:1 plan: stop 0.80 away, target 1.60 away. Costs are 0.20 per unit. What win rate do you need to break even after costs, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":41.7,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"After costs a loss costs 0.80 + 0.20 = 1.00 and a win pays 1.60 − 0.20 = 1.40. Break-even = 1.00 ÷ (1.00 + 1.40) = 41.7%, well above the 33.3% of a 2:1 plan before costs.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the costs to the loss and take them off the win.\",\"Break-even = loss ÷ (loss + win), both after costs.\",\"Loss 1.00, win 1.40: 1.00 ÷ 2.40.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"dist\":[0.4,3,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round((dist + 0.2) / (3 * dist) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A 2:1 plan: stop {dist:2} away, target {2 * dist:2} away. Costs are 0.20 per unit. What win rate do you need to break even after costs, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"After costs a loss costs {dist + 0.2:2} and a win pays {2 * dist - 0.2:2}. Break-even = {dist + 0.2:2} ÷ {3 * dist:2} = {answer:1}%, above the 33.3% of a 2:1 plan before costs.\",\"hint\":\"Loss {dist + 0.2:2}, win {2 * dist - 0.2:2}: divide the loss by their sum.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.6-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your setup wins 40% of the time at 2:1. On LABX at 100.00, costs are about 0.20 per unit. You can trade it with a 0.50 stop and a 1.00 target, or a 2.00 stop and a 4.00 target, sized to the same 1% risk.\",\"prompt\":\"Which version makes money after costs?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both: 40% beats the 33.3% break-even of any 2:1 plan\",\"rationale\":\"33.3% is the break-even before costs. After costs the 0.50 stop needs 46.7%, and 40% falls short.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only the 0.50 stop: it allows four times as many units\",\"rationale\":\"More units means more costs too. Sized to the same risk, what matters is the share of R that costs take: 40% against 10%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neither: costs make every 2:1 plan lose\",\"rationale\":\"With a 2.00 stop, break-even after costs is 36.7%. At 40% it earns about +0.10 per trade, in stop distances.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only the 2.00 stop: the tight stop's costs push its break-even to 46.7%\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Costs are the same 0.20 per unit, but 40% of a 0.50 stop and only 10% of a 2.00 stop. Break-even after costs: 0.70 ÷ 1.50 = 46.7% against 2.20 ÷ 6.00 = 36.7%. At a 40% win rate, only the wide version is positive.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out the break-even after costs for each stop.\",\"Break-even = (stop + costs) ÷ (stop + target).\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.8-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your account went 20,000.00 → 25,000.00 → 21,000.00 → 26,000.00 → 22,100.00. What was the maximum drawdown, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":16,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Measure each fall from the peak before it: 25,000.00 to 21,000.00 is 16%; 26,000.00 to 22,100.00 is 15%. The maximum is 16%. The account never fell below its 20,000.00 start, which is why measuring from the start misleads.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"Each drawdown is measured from the highest point before it.\",\"There are two falls: from 25,000.00 and from 26,000.00. Compute both and keep the deeper.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.8-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You risk 2% of current equity on every trade. After 10 losses in a row, how deep is the drawdown, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":18.3,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Each loss takes 2% of what is left, so 0.98 multiplied 10 times leaves 81.7% of the account: a drawdown of 18.3%. Adding 10 × 2% gives 20%, which overstates it because later losses are taken from a smaller account.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"expected-drawdown\",\"drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"Each loss takes 2% of what is left, not of the start.\",\"What's left after 10 losses = 0.98 multiplied by itself 10 times.\",\"0.98 to the 10th power is 0.817.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"r\":[0.5,3,0.25]},\"answer\":\"round((1 - (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100) * (1 - r / 100)) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You risk {r:2}% of current equity on every trade. After 10 losses in a row, how deep is the drawdown, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Each loss takes {r:2}% of what is left, so {1 - r / 100:4} multiplied 10 times leaves {100 - answer:1}% of the account: a drawdown of {answer:1}%. Adding 10 × {r:2}% overstates it, because later losses come from a smaller account.\",\"hint\":\"What's left after 10 losses = {1 - r / 100:4} multiplied by itself 10 times.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.8-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan risks 1% per trade and wins about 40% of the time. The journal shows every trade followed the plan. After 9 losses in a row you're 8.6% below your peak. The plan says to stop and review at 15%.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this drawdown most likely tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The method is broken: switch to another one now\",\"rationale\":\"At a 40% win rate, a run of 9 shows up in about a third of 100-trade stretches. Nothing here is outside the normal range.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's within the normal range: keep following the plan and review at 15%, as written\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the risk per trade to recover faster\",\"rationale\":\"That's doubling after losses. It deepens the hole if the run continues and breaks the plan's risk rule.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stop using stops until the market turns\",\"rationale\":\"Removing stops makes every loss open-ended. The drawdown is normal; the plan doesn't need fewer protections.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Nine losses at 1% each take 8.6%. At a 40% win rate, a run of 9 shows up in 32.4% of 100-trade stretches, so this is variance a valid method goes through. The review depth was set in advance for this moment: keep following it.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"expected-drawdown\",\"variance\"],\"hints\":[\"How common is a run of 9 losses at a 40% win rate?\",\"Compare the drawdown with the review depth written in the plan.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.9-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your account is 8,000.00 and your daily loss limit is 3% of it. What is the limit, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":240,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"3% of 8,000.00 = 240.00. Once the day's losses plus the next trade's risk would pass 240.00, the day is over.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.9\",\"concepts\":[\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"hints\":[\"Multiply the account by the percentage.\",\"8,000.00 × 0.03.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"acct\":[5000,20000,500],\"pct\":[2,5,1]},\"answer\":\"round(acct * pct / 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your account is {acct:2} and your daily loss limit is {pct}% of it. What is the limit, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"{pct}% of {acct:2} = {answer:2}. Once the day's losses plus the next trade's risk would pass {answer:2}, the day is over.\",\"hint\":\"{acct:2} × {pct / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.9-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Account 20,000.00, daily limit 2.5% of it. You risk 1% of current equity per trade and lose twice: 200.00, then 198.00. How much room is left today, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":102,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The limit is 2.5% of 20,000.00 = 500.00. Used: 200.00 + 198.00 = 398.00, so 102.00 is left. The next trade would risk 1% of 19,602.00 = 196.02, which doesn't fit: the day is over.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.9\",\"concepts\":[\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"hints\":[\"Start from the limit in vUSD: 2.5% of 20,000.00.\",\"Subtract what's already been lost today.\",\"500.00 − (200.00 + 198.00).\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.4.9-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your daily limit is 3R. Three trades have lost today, −3R in total. Twenty minutes later a textbook setup appears: it matches your playbook and the target is 3R away.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Skip it and log it with the reason 'daily limit'\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Take it at half size, so the risk is small\",\"rationale\":\"The limit is already used up. Any new risk, even half of 1R, takes the day past it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Take it: a valid setup is always a trade\",\"rationale\":\"A setup is valid only within the plan's limits. The daily limit is part of the plan, not a suggestion.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Raise today's limit to 4R, since the setup is so good\",\"rationale\":\"A limit is set before the session so it can't be argued with during one. Raising it after losses is how bad days grow.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The day ended when the limit was reached. Skipping the setup and logging why keeps the record honest and shows later what the rule cost and saved. A good-looking setup after three losses is exactly when the limit matters most.\",\"lesson\":\"1.4.9\",\"concepts\":[\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"hints\":[\"Is there any room left under the limit?\",\"When was the limit set, and can it change during the session?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Five 1-hour candles in a row have highs of 100.40, 100.70, 101.10, 100.90 and 100.60. Under the 2-candle rule, is the middle candle a swing high?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"No: it also needs the highest close\",\"rationale\":\"The rule compares highs only. A swing high is about where price reached, not where the candle closed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No: it needs three lower candles on each side\",\"rationale\":\"Three or five per side are other conventions. This course's rule uses two, and 101.10 passes it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Yes: 101.10 is above the two highs on each side\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only if the next candle is bearish\",\"rationale\":\"One candle's colour doesn't decide it. The rule asks whether the two neighbours on each side have lower highs.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"With two candles on each side, compare 101.10 with 100.40 and 100.70 before it and 100.90 and 100.60 after it. All four are lower, so it's a swing high. The rule looks at highs only, and the number of candles per side is a fixed choice.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"swing-points\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Candle 40 on LABX has just closed with a low of 98.60, the lowest of the last 20 hours. You use the 2-candle rule.\",\"prompt\":\"When can you mark candle 40 as a swing low?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Now, because it's the lowest low in 20 hours\",\"rationale\":\"Lowest so far isn't enough. The rule also needs two later candles with higher lows, and they haven't formed yet.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"After candles 41 and 42 both close with higher lows\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After candle 41 closes with a higher low\",\"rationale\":\"One candle is half the test. The rule needs two candles after it, so candle 42 has to close too.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Never: the newest candle can't be a swing low\",\"rationale\":\"It can't be one yet. Once two later candles close with higher lows, it qualifies.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A swing low needs higher lows on both sides: the two candles before it and the two after. Until candles 41 and 42 close above 98.60, candle 40 is only a candidate. That's why every swing is confirmed two candles late.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"swing-points\"],\"hints\":[\"Count how many candles the rule needs on each side.\",\"The two candles before are known. What about the two after?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Two traders mark swings on the same LABX chart. One finds 10, the other finds 4. What is the most likely reason?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"One of them misread the prices\",\"rationale\":\"Possible, but a gap this size usually comes from the rule, not the reading. Different rules give different swing maps.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only one of the two maps can be correct\",\"rationale\":\"Both can be correct under their own rule. Structure depends on the definition, and the definition is a choice.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The chart's prices changed between their looks\",\"rationale\":\"Closed candles don't change. The difference comes from how each trader defines a swing.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They use different rules, such as two versus five candles on each side\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A wider rule, such as five candles on each side, keeps only the bigger turns, so it finds fewer swings. Both maps are valid under their own rule. That's why you fix one rule and use it every time.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"swing-points\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about what decides whether a candle counts as a swing.\",\"A rule with more candles per side is harder to pass.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"An uptrend leg rises from 50.00 to 54.00. Price then pulls back to 53.00. How deep is the pullback, as a percentage of the leg?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"The leg is 54.00 − 50.00 = 4.00 and the pullback is 54.00 − 53.00 = 1.00. Depth = 1.00 ÷ 4.00 = 25%. Dividing by the price instead of the leg would give a much smaller, meaningless number.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pullback\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the size of the leg first, then the size of the pullback.\",\"Depth = pullback ÷ leg, as a percentage.\",\"Leg: 4.00. Pullback: 54.00 − 53.00 = 1.00. Now divide.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"start\":[40,120,1],\"leg\":[2,8,1],\"pct\":[20,80,5]},\"answer\":\"pct\",\"prompt\":\"An uptrend leg rises from {start:2} to {start + leg:2}. Price then pulls back to {start + leg - leg * pct / 100:2}. How deep is the pullback, as a percentage of the leg?\",\"explanation\":\"The leg is {start + leg:2} − {start:2} = {leg:2} and the pullback is {start + leg:2} − {start + leg - leg * pct / 100:2} = {leg * pct / 100:2}. Depth = {leg * pct / 100:2} ÷ {leg:2} = {answer}%. Dividing by the price instead of the leg would give a much smaller, meaningless number.\",\"hint\":\"Leg: {leg:2}. Pullback: {leg * pct / 100:2}. Now divide the pullback by the leg.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX is in an uptrend. Its last two swing lows were 98.40 and 99.30. From a high of 101.00, price pulls back to 99.10.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this pullback tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It broke the last higher low, 99.30, so the uptrend is in question\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A normal pullback, because price is still above 98.40\",\"rationale\":\"The low that counts is the last one, 99.30. An older low doesn't keep the pattern of higher lows intact.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A downtrend has started, so selling is the plan\",\"rationale\":\"One lower low is a warning, not a downtrend. A downtrend also needs a lower high and another lower low.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing yet: wait until it passes 61.8% of the leg\",\"rationale\":\"A ratio doesn't define the trend; structure does. The last higher low, 99.30, has already broken.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"In an uptrend, each pullback has to hold above the last higher low, here 99.30. A low of 99.10 breaks it: the pullback gave back 1.90 of a 1.70 leg, about 112%. That's a warning that the uptrend may be ending, not yet a downtrend.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pullback\",\"trend\"],\"hints\":[\"Which swing low does the uptrend's structure depend on right now?\",\"Compare 99.10 with the most recent swing low, not the older one.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your stop is at 48.00 and you will risk 100.00. Buying the pullback at 49.00 instead of chasing at 51.00, how many more units can you buy? Ignore costs.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":67,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"At the pullback the stop is 1.00 away: 100.00 ÷ 1.00 = 100 units. Chasing, it is 3.00 away: 100.00 ÷ 3.00 = 33.3, so 33 units. The pullback entry buys 67 more units for the same risk.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pullback\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Size each entry separately: budget ÷ distance to the stop, rounded down.\",\"Pullback: 49.00 − 48.00 = 1.00 per unit. Chase: 51.00 − 48.00 = 3.00 per unit.\",\"100 units versus 33 units. Now subtract.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"stop\":[40,120,1],\"pull\":[0.5,2,0.5],\"extra\":[1,3,0.5],\"budget\":[50,200,50]},\"constraints\":[\"floor(budget / pull) * (stop + pull) <= 10000\"],\"answer\":\"floor(budget / pull) - floor(budget / (pull + extra))\",\"prompt\":\"Your stop is at {stop:2} and you will risk {budget:2}. Buying the pullback at {stop + pull:2} instead of chasing at {stop + pull + extra:2}, how many more units can you buy? Ignore costs.\",\"explanation\":\"At the pullback the stop is {pull:2} away: {budget:2} ÷ {pull:2} = {budget / pull:1}, so {floor(budget / pull)} units. Chasing, it is {pull + extra:2} away: {budget:2} ÷ {pull + extra:2} = {budget / (pull + extra):1}, so {floor(budget / (pull + extra))} units. The pullback entry buys {answer} more units for the same risk.\",\"hint\":\"{floor(budget / pull)} units versus {floor(budget / (pull + extra))} units. Now subtract.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In Osler's study of a bank's currency orders, where did take-profit orders cluster most?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Just beyond round numbers, such as 100.07\",\"rationale\":\"That's where stop-loss orders gathered. Take-profits sat exactly on the round number.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Evenly across all prices\",\"rationale\":\"Orders were far from even. Prices ending in 00 drew about twice the share of take-profits as of stop-losses.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"At Fibonacci ratios of the last move\",\"rationale\":\"The study looked at price endings, not ratios. Take-profits clustered at round numbers.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Exactly at round numbers, such as 100.00\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Take-profits clustered exactly at round numbers: 9.3% ended in 00, against 4.4% of stop-losses. Stop-losses clustered just beyond round numbers instead. People choose easy prices for their targets.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"round-numbers\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long LABX at 50.40, just above support at 50.00, which is also a round number. A normal candle is about 0.30.\",\"prompt\":\"Where do you put the stop?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"50.00, on the support\",\"rationale\":\"Any ordinary test of support can touch it. The stop belongs where the idea is wrong, beyond the level.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"49.95, just under the level\",\"rationale\":\"That's where other traders' stops cluster. A quick push through 50.00 can set them all off, yours included.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"49.70, about one normal candle below the level\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"48.00, far below\",\"rationale\":\"Beyond the level is right, but this far adds loss without adding information. The idea is wrong well before 48.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The stop goes where the idea is wrong: beyond support, and past the cluster of stops just under 50.00. About one normal candle below, 49.70, does both. Closer stops can be swept; much wider ones only add loss.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"round-numbers\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"hints\":[\"Two things sit near 50.00: the support itself, and the stops other traders put just under it.\",\"Clear both, by about one normal candle.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Support and resistance levels published by analysts were tested against random levels. Which summary is accurate?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Published levels held about nine times in ten\",\"rationale\":\"The real rate was 60.8%, not 90%. Levels tilt the odds slightly; they don't hold reliably.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Published levels stopped price 60.8% of the time, random ones 56.2%: real but small\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"There was no difference between the two\",\"rationale\":\"There was a statistically significant difference. It was just small, under five percentage points.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Trading the published levels was shown to be profitable after costs\",\"rationale\":\"Profit after costs wasn't established. A small edge at turning points can vanish once you pay the spread and commission.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Osler (2000) found published levels interrupted intraday trends 60.8% of the time, against 56.2% for random levels. That's a real effect, but a modest one, and profitability after costs wasn't shown. It tilts the odds a little; it doesn't make a level a wall.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"round-numbers\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"hints\":[\"The effect was statistically real. Was it large?\",\"Look for the answer that is real but modest, and doesn't claim profit.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX broke above resistance at 80.00 and later dips back to 80.10. What does role reversal suggest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The old resistance near 80.00 may now act as support\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The level stopped mattering once it broke\",\"rationale\":\"Broken levels often stay in traders' minds. Role reversal says the old ceiling may now act as a floor.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Price must fall back below 80.00\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing must happen. Role reversal describes what may happen at the retest, and the retest can also fail.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"80.00 is now stronger resistance\",\"rationale\":\"Price is above 80.00 now, so the level sits below it. A level below price can act as support, not resistance.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Role reversal is the idea that a broken resistance may act as support on a retest, and a broken support as resistance. It's a practitioner pattern, not a guarantee: the retest can hold or fail.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"role-reversal\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX ranged under 60.00 for two days, then closed at 60.40. You bought at 60.45 with a stop at 59.60. Two candles later, a candle closes at 59.85, back inside the range. Your stop hasn't been hit.\",\"prompt\":\"What does that close tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The retest is holding: price is still above your stop\",\"rationale\":\"A retest that holds closes above the old zone. This one closed back inside the range, under 60.00.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The breakout has failed: the reason for the trade is gone\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A stronger breakout is likely next\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing in a close back inside points to a stronger break. It's the failure your stop was planned for.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing, until the stop is hit\",\"rationale\":\"The stop is the last line, not the only signal. A close back inside already says the breakout idea is wrong.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A close back inside the range within a few candles is what defines a failed breakout. The idea behind the trade, price holding above 60.00, is gone. Your plan decides the next step: exit, or let the stop do its job, but never widen it.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"false-breakout\",\"role-reversal\"],\"hints\":[\"Did the candle only wick into the zone, or close back inside the range?\",\"Compare the close, 59.85, with the broken level, 60.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your breakout buy stopped out for −1R when price closed back inside the range. It feels like the market tricked you, and price is still falling.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best next step?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Short at once to win the loss back\",\"rationale\":\"That's a new trade made out of frustration, not a setup from your plan. It's how one loss becomes two.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy again with a wider stop so it can't be hit\",\"rationale\":\"Widening the stop changes the risk after the fact. The setup failed, and the planned loss was the plan working.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the size on the next breakout\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing up after a loss raises risk when you're least objective. The next trade gets the same risk as any other.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Accept the planned loss and wait for the next setup in your plan\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A failed breakout is one of the two endings you planned for. The loss was sized in advance, so take it. Flipping direction, widening stops or sizing up after it are decisions made in frustration, not from the plan.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"false-breakout\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"Was the loss inside what you planned before the trade?\",\"Which choice is a setup from your plan, not a reaction?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What breaks the structure of an uptrend?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Any bearish candle\",\"rationale\":\"Uptrends contain plenty of bearish candles. Structure is about swing points, not single candles.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A pullback deeper than 50% of the last leg\",\"rationale\":\"Depth alone doesn't break a trend. A deep pullback that holds the last higher low keeps the structure.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A close below the last higher low\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"An hour with lower volume\",\"rationale\":\"Volume measures activity, not structure. The structure breaks when price closes below the last higher low.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"An uptrend is a sequence of higher highs and higher lows. A close below the last higher low breaks that sequence. It's a warning, not yet a downtrend: that also needs a lower high and a lower low.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"structure-break\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX's uptrend has made swing lows at 100.00 and 101.20 and a high at 103.00. Price has just closed at 100.90.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you know now?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A downtrend is confirmed\",\"rationale\":\"One close below the last higher low is a warning. A downtrend also needs a lower high and then a lower low.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The structure broke: a warning, not yet a downtrend\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing has changed while price stays above 100.00\",\"rationale\":\"The low that counts is the last one, 101.20. Older lows don't hold the structure together.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's a deep pullback, so buying it is the plan\",\"rationale\":\"A pullback buy needs an intact uptrend. The close under 101.20 broke it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The last higher low was 101.20, and price closed below it at 100.90, so the uptrend's structure is broken. That doesn't make a downtrend yet: watch whether the next rally stops below 103.00 and whether a new low follows.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"structure-break\",\"trend\"],\"hints\":[\"Which swing low is the most recent one?\",\"A downtrend needs lower highs and lower lows. How many of those exist yet?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your playbook: buy pullbacks in an uptrend, stop under the last higher low. Yesterday LABX closed below its last higher low. Today price dips, and the dip looks just like your usual setup.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Skip it: the first condition, an intact uptrend, fails\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy with half the usual size\",\"rationale\":\"Size isn't the problem. The setup's first condition, an intact uptrend, has failed, so there's no setup to size.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Buy, with the stop under yesterday's low\",\"rationale\":\"Moving the stop doesn't restore the structure. The playbook trades pullbacks in uptrends, and this isn't one.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Go short at once\",\"rationale\":\"A structure break is a warning, not a short setup. A short needs its own conditions in your plan.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Every condition in a playbook has to hold. The structure break removed the first one, so this dip isn't the setup, however similar it looks. A short would need its own setup; until the plan has one, standing aside is the decision.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"structure-break\",\"trading-plan\"],\"hints\":[\"Read the playbook's first condition again.\",\"Is the uptrend still intact after a close below the last higher low?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In a two-timeframe plan, what is the higher timeframe for?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Timing the exact entry\",\"rationale\":\"Timing is a detail, read on the timeframe you trade or a lower one. The higher timeframe gives the context.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Context: the bigger trend and the major levels\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Setting the stop distance\",\"rationale\":\"The stop belongs where the idea is wrong on the timeframe you trade. The higher timeframe frames the trade instead.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Replacing your trading timeframe when the two disagree\",\"rationale\":\"Switching charts when they disagree is the bias this lesson warns about. Both timeframes are fixed in the plan.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The higher timeframe shows the context: which way the bigger trend runs and where its major levels sit. Entries and stops come from the timeframe you trade. Both are chosen in advance.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.8\",\"concepts\":[\"multi-timeframe-analysis\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"On the 4-hour chart LABX makes higher highs and higher lows; the last higher low is 98.00. On the 1-hour chart, the last 12 candles fell from 101.50 to 99.20, with lower highs and lower lows.\",\"prompt\":\"How is this best read?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A new downtrend on both timeframes\",\"rationale\":\"The 4-hour structure hasn't broken: price is above its last higher low, 98.00. Only the 1-hour chart is falling.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The 4-hour chart is out of date, so trust the 1-hour\",\"rationale\":\"Both charts show the same prices. The 4-hour chart groups more of them, so it shows the bigger structure.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Noise: neither chart means anything\",\"rationale\":\"Both show clear structure: higher highs and lows on the 4-hour, lower highs and lows on the 1-hour.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A pullback inside the 4-hour uptrend, while it holds above 98.00\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Both charts are right at their own scale. The 1-hour downtrend is a pullback inside the 4-hour uptrend for as long as price holds above the 4-hour higher low, 98.00. A close below it would break the bigger structure.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.8\",\"concepts\":[\"multi-timeframe-analysis\",\"trend\"],\"hints\":[\"Check the 4-hour structure first: has its last higher low broken?\",\"Price is at 99.20; the 4-hour higher low is 98.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.5.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A trader checks the 5-minute, 15-minute, 1-hour and daily charts until one shows a buy signal, then buys. What's the problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Lower timeframes are always wrong\",\"rationale\":\"No timeframe is wrong. The problem is choosing one after seeing which agrees with you.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: more charts mean more information\",\"rationale\":\"More charts also mean more chances to find a signal by luck. Picking the one that agrees is selection, not information.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"With enough charts, one will agree by chance: that's confirmation bias\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Daily charts are the only valid ones\",\"rationale\":\"Any timeframe can work if it's chosen in the plan, before you look.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Each extra timeframe is another chance to find a chart that supports the idea you already had. Picking the one that agrees is confirmation bias. Fixing one context timeframe and one trading timeframe in the plan removes that freedom.\",\"lesson\":\"1.5.8\",\"concepts\":[\"multi-timeframe-analysis\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"What decided which chart the trader acted on?\",\"If you keep looking until something agrees, what are you measuring?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your savings are 15,000 EUR. Your emergency fund is six months of expenses at 1,500 EUR a month, and 2,000 EUR is already promised for a car repair. What is your risk capital, in EUR?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4000,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"EUR\"},\"explanation\":\"Risk capital is what's left after the money you can't afford to lose: 15,000 − 6 × 1,500 − 2,000 = 15,000 − 9,000 − 2,000 = 4,000 EUR. Leaving out the emergency fund or the promised repair would put money you need at risk.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-capital\"],\"hints\":[\"Start from savings and take out every amount you can't afford to lose.\",\"The emergency fund is six months of expenses: 6 × 1,500.\",\"15,000 − 9,000 − 2,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"savings\":[10000,40000,1000],\"monthly\":[800,2500,100],\"promised\":[500,5000,500]},\"constraints\":[\"savings - 6 * monthly - promised >= 1000\"],\"answer\":\"savings - 6 * monthly - promised\",\"prompt\":\"Your savings are {savings} EUR. Your emergency fund is six months of expenses at {monthly} EUR a month, and {promised} EUR is already promised for a car repair. What is your risk capital, in EUR?\",\"explanation\":\"Risk capital is what's left after the money you can't afford to lose: {savings} − 6 × {monthly} − {promised} = {savings} − {6 * monthly} − {promised} = {answer} EUR. Leaving out the emergency fund or the promised repair would put money you need at risk.\",\"hint\":\"{savings} − {6 * monthly} − {promised}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.2-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which line belongs in a plan v1, because it can be checked on every trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Only trade when the market feels calm\",\"rationale\":\"'Feels calm' changes with your mood and can't be checked on a ticket. A checkable line names a number or a test anyone could run.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep losses small\",\"rationale\":\"Everyone agrees with this, so it decides nothing. 'Small' needs a number: a percentage of equity per trade, costs included.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No new trade after a loss of 3R in a day\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Take profits when the move looks tired\",\"rationale\":\"'Looks tired' can be argued either way after the fact. An exit rule names a price, a multiple of R or a time.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A checkable line gives the same answer to anyone who looks: after −3R today, is a new trade allowed? No. Vague lines like 'keep losses small' leave room to bend the rule exactly when it matters.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-plan\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask of each line: could two people disagree about whether it was followed?\",\"Look for a number or a test, not a feeling.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.2-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan v1.0 puts the stop 0.20 below the pullback low and allows rule changes only after 30 trades, between sessions, with the reason written. Your first three trades under it followed every rule and were each stopped out at −1R. Mid-session, you want to move the stop rule to 0.60 below.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your rule for changes say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Change it now: three stop-outs prove the stop is too tight\",\"rationale\":\"Three trades are a tiny sample; a sound method can lose three in a row. The plan's own rule asks for 30 trades before judging.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Widen it just for the rest of today, then go back to v1.0\",\"rationale\":\"A change mid-session, after losses, is exactly what the rule forbids. Today's trades would no longer test v1.0.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Drop the setup and try a different one tomorrow\",\"rationale\":\"Switching setups after three losses resets your evidence to zero. Every setup has losing runs; the change rule exists to stop this.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Keep v1.0, note the idea in your journal, and judge it at a review once 30 trades are in\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Three losses with every rule followed is a normal run, not evidence. The rule for changes keeps v1.0 in place until 30 trades can show whether the stop rule works. The idea goes into the journal, and any change becomes v1.1, with its reason written.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-plan\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the plan's own rule for changes require before any rule changes?\",\"Three trades are a very small sample.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.3-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is a pre-trade checklist for?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Finding more trades to take\",\"rationale\":\"A checklist only filters trades you already see. Its job is to catch skipped steps, and it often ends in no trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Catching skipped steps, like a missing stop, before the order goes in\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Predicting whether the trade will win\",\"rationale\":\"No list can predict an outcome. It checks that the decision follows your plan, win or lose.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Replacing the playbook when markets move fast\",\"rationale\":\"The checklist asks whether the setup is in your playbook. It relies on the playbook; it doesn't replace it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A checklist is a short set of yes-or-no questions run at the moment of decision. It doesn't find or predict trades; it catches routine steps that get skipped under pressure, such as a missing stop or an oversized position.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pre-trade-checklist\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.3-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan risks 1% of equity per trade. Every checklist answer is yes except one: buying 60 units at 50.00 with the stop at 48.00, the loss at the stop is 122.94 with costs, 1.23% of 10,000.00.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Send it: six yeses out of seven is close enough\",\"rationale\":\"The checklist isn't a majority vote. One 'no' is a 'no', and small breaches of the risk rule add up over many trades.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Move the stop closer, so 60 units fit within 1%\",\"rationale\":\"That drags the stop off the invalidation and into the noise. The stop comes from the chart; the size adjusts to it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Cut the size until the loss at the stop fits within 1%, or skip the trade\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Send it, and move the stop away later if price comes close\",\"rationale\":\"That adds a second breach to the first. Moving a stop further away raises the risk even more.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Size is the one 'no' you can fix on the spot: at 48 units the loss at the stop is 98.36 with costs, within 1%. Moving the stop to fit the size changes the trade, and 'close enough' turns a limit into a suggestion.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pre-trade-checklist\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Which item failed, and can you fix it without changing the trade?\",\"The stop comes from the chart. What else can change?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.3-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why should a pre-trade checklist stay short, around 5 to 9 questions?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Fewer questions let more trades through\",\"rationale\":\"Letting trades through isn't the goal. The list should stop the trades that break your plan, whatever its length.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only the risk questions matter; the rest is decoration\",\"rationale\":\"Setup and state questions catch different mistakes. Short means each question earns its place, not that only risk counts.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A long list gets ticked without being read, which defeats its purpose\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The more questions, the lower your win rate\",\"rationale\":\"A checklist doesn't change how often trades win. Its length matters because of how you read it.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A checklist works only if each question is actually answered. Long lists invite ticking on autopilot, the main way checklists fail; a short one, tied to facts like the stop price and the risk in %, gets read every time.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pre-trade-checklist\"],\"hints\":[\"Picture your fiftieth trade with a 30-item list.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A month from now, which two journal fields best let you judge a trade's decision, not just its result?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Entry time and exit time\",\"rationale\":\"Times help you find a trade, but they don't say why you took it or whether the plan allowed it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Profit in vUSD and the day's high\",\"rationale\":\"Both describe what happened after the decision. A decision is judged by what you knew and whether you followed the plan.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Your thesis and whether you followed the rule\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The instrument and the timeframe\",\"rationale\":\"Useful context, but the same for many trades. They don't tell a sound decision from a lucky one.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The thesis records what you expected and why, at entry; 'Rule followed?' records whether the plan was respected. Together they let a review judge the decision. Profit, times and context describe the trade, not the quality of the choice.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"journaling\",\"decision-quality\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.4-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A pullback forms in an uptrend, but its low breaks below the previous swing low, so condition 2 of your playbook fails. You don't trade.\",\"prompt\":\"What goes in your journal?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A no-trade entry: the setup you saw, the condition that failed, and 'plan followed'\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: no trade, no entry\",\"rationale\":\"A no-trade is a decision too. Without it, your journal can't show how you behaved when the plan said no.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An entry only if price later rallies, to show what you missed\",\"rationale\":\"Logging only when price moved afterwards is selective, and it judges the decision by the outcome.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A note to take this setup next time even if condition 2 fails\",\"rationale\":\"That changes a rule from one example, mid-stream. Rule changes go through your plan's rule for changes.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Skipping a trade the plan rejects is a decision worth recording. A no-trade entry shows the plan working, keeps the record complete, and is written before you know what price does next.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"journaling\",\"trading-plan\"],\"hints\":[\"Was not trading a decision?\",\"Think about what a review a month later would need to see.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.4-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A trader writes journal entries only for losing trades, a few days after each one. What's the main problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"None: losses are where the lessons are\",\"rationale\":\"Losses teach, but so do wins that broke rules. Without them, the record is tilted and can't be compared.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It takes too long to be worth it\",\"rationale\":\"The written part takes about 30 seconds. The issue is what gets left out, and when it's written.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"None, as long as the simulator records the numbers\",\"rationale\":\"The simulator can't record your thesis, your emotion or what you knew. Written days later, those fill in with hindsight.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The record is tilted towards losses, and hindsight rewrites what was known at entry\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Two distortions stack up. Logging only losers hides rule-breaking wins and gives a one-sided picture of the setup. Writing days later lets the known outcome reshape what you remember thinking at entry.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"journaling\"],\"hints\":[\"Two things are wrong: which trades, and when.\",\"What happens to your memory of a decision once you know the outcome?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your stop was 0.20 below the pullback low, as your plan says. Size was 1% and every condition was met. Price dipped to the stop, filled you at −1R, then rallied to where your target was. Which bucket?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Mistake: the stop was too tight\",\"rationale\":\"That's hindsight: the rally came after the stop was hit. The stop was where the plan put it, so nothing was broken.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Mistake: you should have moved the stop when price got close\",\"rationale\":\"Moving the stop away is itself a tagged mistake. The plan was followed; the loss is the normal cost of trading.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Variance: a valid trade that lost\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither, because price later reached the target\",\"rationale\":\"What price did after the exit doesn't change the bucket. A loss with every rule followed is variance.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Every rule was followed: stop at the invalidation, 1% size, all conditions met. The loss is variance. The later rally tempts you to call the stop a mistake, but that's hindsight; a stop rule changes only through the plan's rule for changes, on many trades.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"mistake-vs-variance\",\"decision-quality\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"This week's journal: you moved a stop away and lost 1.8R where the plan allowed 1R; you entered without a stop and lost 2.5R where the plan allowed 1R; and you took a trade outside your playbook and lost 1.0R where the plan said no trade. How many R did the mistakes cost in total?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3.3,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Error cost is what you lost beyond what the plan allowed: (1.8 − 1) + (2.5 − 1) + (1.0 − 0) = 0.8 + 1.5 + 1.0 = 3.3R. The trade outside the playbook counts in full, because the plan allowed no trade at all.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"error-taxonomy\",\"mistake-vs-variance\"],\"hints\":[\"For each trade, subtract what the plan allowed from what you lost.\",\"A trade outside the playbook was allowed 0R, so its whole loss is error cost.\",\"0.8 + 1.5 + 1.0.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[1.2,3,0.1],\"b\":[1.2,3,0.1],\"c\":[0.5,1.5,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"a != b\"],\"answer\":\"round(a + b + c - 2, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"This week's journal: you moved a stop away and lost {a:1}R where the plan allowed 1R; you entered without a stop and lost {b:1}R where the plan allowed 1R; and you took a trade outside your playbook and lost {c:1}R where the plan said no trade. How many R did the mistakes cost in total?\",\"explanation\":\"Error cost is what you lost beyond what the plan allowed: ({a:1} − 1) + ({b:1} − 1) + ({c:1} − 0) = {a - 1:1} + {b - 1:1} + {c:1} = {answer:1}R. The trade outside the playbook counts in full, because the plan allowed no trade at all.\",\"hint\":\"{a - 1:1} + {b - 1:1} + {c:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Over 120 journaled trades, your setup has won 45% of the time at about +2R per win, against −1R per loss. You've now lost six in a row. The journal shows every rule followed and no error tags on any of the six.\",\"prompt\":\"What should you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Change the setup: six losses in a row prove it's broken\",\"rationale\":\"A run of six is common for a 45% setup: about 73% of 100-trade sequences contain one. Six trades can't outweigh 120.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep trading the plan at the same risk, and review at your scheduled time\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the next position to recover faster\",\"rationale\":\"That's sizing up after a loss, a tagged mistake. It turns variance into a deep drawdown.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Tighten your stops now, so each loss is smaller\",\"rationale\":\"That's a rule change driven by variance, made mid-streak. Changes go through the rule for changes, on evidence, between sessions.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"With no error tags, the six losses are variance. At a 45% win rate, a run of six or more losses shows up in about 73% of 100-trade sequences, so the streak alone is not evidence. Keep the risk fixed and let the scheduled review, on the whole record, decide.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"mistake-vs-variance\"],\"hints\":[\"Check the journal first: were any rules broken?\",\"How often does a 45% setup lose six in a row over 100 trades?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Over 40 trades, 37 had a stop at entry that was never moved further away. What is your stop integrity, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":92.5,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Stop integrity = trades with a stop at entry that was never widened ÷ all trades = 37 ÷ 40 = 92.5%. That's below the 95% the Level 1 gate asks for: 3 trades in 40 broke the rule.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"process-kpis\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the trades that passed, not the ones that failed.\",\"Divide the passing trades by all trades, then multiply by 100.\",\"37 ÷ 40 × 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[20,60,1],\"bad\":[1,6,1]},\"constraints\":[\"bad < n\"],\"answer\":\"round((n - bad) / n * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Over {n} trades, {n - bad} had a stop at entry that was never moved further away. What is your stop integrity, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Stop integrity = trades with a stop at entry that was never widened ÷ all trades = {n - bad} ÷ {n} = {answer:1}%. The Level 1 gate asks for at least 95%.\",\"hint\":\"{n - bad} ÷ {n} × 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.7-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After 20 trades, why does your stop integrity tell you more than your profit?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Profit never matters in trading\",\"rationale\":\"Over hundreds of trades, profit is the point. Over 20, chance swamps it, so the process is the better measure.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Profit over 20 trades owes a lot to luck; stop integrity measures only what you did\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"High stop integrity guarantees a profit over the next 20 trades\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing guarantees a short-run result. Even a good system ends 20 trades in the red about 1 time in 8.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stop integrity is easier to calculate\",\"rationale\":\"Both are easy to compute. The difference is noise: profit over 20 trades swings with luck, process doesn't.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A system making +0.4R a trade still ends 20 trades with a net loss 12.6% of the time, so a short profit record can't separate skill from luck. Stop integrity has no luck in it: each trade either had its stop kept or didn't.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"process-kpis\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of the two can chance change?\",\"Even a system making +0.4R a trade loses over 20 trades about 1 time in 8.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.6.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're setting a goal for your next 30 trades. Option 1: 'Make 5% this month.' Option 2: 'A stop at entry on every trade, and every trade journaled within 10 minutes.'\",\"prompt\":\"Which is the better goal, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Option 2: you control it fully, and it can be checked on every trade\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Option 1: profit is what trading is for\",\"rationale\":\"Over 30 trades, luck decides much of the result, and chasing a profit target pushes towards oversized positions.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Both are equally good, as long as you write them down\",\"rationale\":\"Writing helps, but an outcome goal still depends on luck. A process goal is met or missed by your choices alone.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither: goals only add pressure\",\"rationale\":\"A clear process goal lowers pressure: it tells you exactly what a good trade looks like, win or lose.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A process goal depends only on your actions, so meeting it is in your hands and you can check it trade by trade. An outcome goal over 30 trades is largely luck, and chasing it invites oversizing to catch up.\",\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"process-kpis\"],\"hints\":[\"Which goal could you meet even in a month of losing trades?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.1-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You skipped a valid setup and it ran to the target without you. Now you feel pulled to jump into the next move to make up for it. Which feeling is driving the pull?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Fear\",\"rationale\":\"Fear looks ahead at a possible loss. This feeling looks back at a trade you missed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Greed\",\"rationale\":\"Greed wants more than the plan allows. Here the push comes from the missed trade, looking back.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Regret\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Hope\",\"rationale\":\"Hope keeps a losing trade open on a wish. You have no trade open here.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Regret looks back at a choice and pushes you to make up for it, often by chasing the next move. Naming it is the first step; the plan still decides whether the next move is a setup.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"emotions-in-trading\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.1-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Lo, Repin and Steenbarger followed 80 day traders for five weeks. What did they find?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Traders who felt no emotions at all had the best results\",\"rationale\":\"No trader in the study felt nothing. The finding was about how intense the reactions were, not their absence.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Traders with more intense emotional reactions to gains and losses had worse results\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A clear 'trader personality' predicted who would do well\",\"rationale\":\"The study found the opposite: no single personality type stood out.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Strong reactions hurt only after losses; after gains they helped\",\"rationale\":\"Intense reactions went with worse results on both sides, to gains as well as to losses.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Traders whose reactions to gains and to losses were more intense performed significantly worse, and no 'trader personality' emerged. It's one small study, so the practical lesson is modest: notice strong reactions and let the plan decide.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"emotions-in-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about how strong the reactions were, not which emotion they were.\",\"The finding covered gains and losses alike.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.1-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You slept four hours, have a headache and rate yourself 4 out of 5 before the session. Your plan's fit-to-trade line says you're not fit below six hours of sleep or at a check-in of 4 or higher.\",\"prompt\":\"What does a process-based trader do today?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Trade as usual: the setups don't depend on how you feel\",\"rationale\":\"The setups don't, but your judgement does. That's why the plan has a fit-to-trade line, decided in advance.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Trade smaller or not at all, and note the reason in the journal\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trade more, to take your mind off the headache\",\"rationale\":\"More trades on a bad day mean more decisions under strain, plus costs. That's the opposite of the plan's line.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Use wider stops, to give trades more room on a bad day\",\"rationale\":\"Wider stops at the same size raise the risk. A bad day calls for less risk, not more room.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Fit-to-trade criteria are written while you're calm, so a bad day doesn't have to argue with itself. Both triggers are met, so the plan's action applies: a smaller size or no trading, logged with the reason.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"emotions-in-trading\",\"trading-plan\"],\"hints\":[\"Check the plan's line against this morning: sleep and check-in.\",\"Both triggers are met. What does the line say to do?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.3-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A fair coin has landed tails six times in a row. What's the chance the next flip lands heads?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"More than 50%: heads is due\",\"rationale\":\"That's the gambler's fallacy. The coin has no memory of the last six flips.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Less than 50%: tails is on a run\",\"rationale\":\"That's the hot-hand view, the mirror image. Each flip is independent of the ones before.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"50%\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It depends on how many flips came before\",\"rationale\":\"For a fair, independent coin, earlier flips don't change the next one, however many there were.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Each flip is independent, so the chance stays 50%. In a simulation of 1,000,000 flips, heads followed a run of five tails 49.7% of the time: the streak changed nothing.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"gamblers-fallacy\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.3-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You've won your last five trades at 1% risk each. You feel sharp and the next setup looks great. Your plan says 1% per trade.\",\"prompt\":\"What should the next trade risk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"2%: you're on a hot streak\",\"rationale\":\"That's the hot hand. Five wins can't show your edge has grown, and doubling makes the next normal loss −2%.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"1%, as the plan says\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"0.5%: after five wins, a loss is due\",\"rationale\":\"That's the gambler's fallacy turned around. Wins don't make a loss due any more than losses make a win due.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"3%, to make the most of the streak while it lasts\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing from a streak, not from the plan, is the error. And 3% breaks a 1% risk rule outright.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Risk per trade comes from the plan, not from recent results. Five trades are far too few to show the edge has changed, so the size stays at 1% after wins and after losses.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"gamblers-fallacy\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"hints\":[\"Who sets the size: the plan or the streak?\",\"Five trades are a tiny sample.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.3-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your pullback setup has lost four trades in a row. Your journal shows every condition was met, every stop was kept and risk was 1% each time. The market still trends the way the setup needs.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The setup is broken: replace it today\",\"rationale\":\"Four losses with every rule kept is what a normal run looks like. A streak alone can't show the setup broke.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A win is due, so take the next one at double size\",\"rationale\":\"That's the gambler's fallacy, and doubling after losses is the revenge pattern. The next trade is a new draw.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A normal run so far: keep the rules and review at the threshold your plan sets\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Skip the next valid setup, just to be safe\",\"rationale\":\"Skipping valid setups after a streak lets the streak decide. The plan's review rule decides instead.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"With rules kept and conditions unchanged, four losses fit normal variance: for a setup that wins half the time, a run of four or more somewhere in 100 trades is about 97% likely. A setup is judged by a review rule in the plan, not by the last few results.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"gamblers-fallacy\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate the process from the results: were the rules kept?\",\"What does your plan say should trigger a review?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":1,\"scenario\":\"You entered with no stop 'just this once'. Price dipped hard, then recovered, and you closed at +150.00 vUSD.\",\"prompt\":\"How do you grade this trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A good decision: it made +150.00\",\"rationale\":\"That's grading by the outcome. Trading without a stop broke the plan, and the profit doesn't undo that.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A poor decision with a good outcome: name the broken rule\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A good decision with a bad outcome\",\"rationale\":\"The outcome was the good part. The decision, a trade with no stop, was the poor part.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"With no stop, the risk was undefined: a broken rule, whatever the result. A profit on a broken rule is the most dangerous box, because it rewards the habit. Grade it as poor and write the rule down.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"decision-quality\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.4-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your last three trades met every playbook condition, risked 1% and were stopped out. You're tempted to drop condition 2, the one that keeps you out of deep pullbacks, because 'it's too strict'.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the process-based response?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Drop it now: three losses show it doesn't work\",\"rationale\":\"Three trades are a tiny sample. Sound rules lose three in a row regularly.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep it, and judge it only through the plan's rule for changes, over enough trades\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Keep it, but double the size to win back the three losses\",\"rationale\":\"That's revenge sizing. The question is about the rule, and the size stays at 1%.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Drop all the conditions until the losing stops\",\"rationale\":\"That throws out the plan because of three results: outcome bias at full strength.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Three rule-following losses are a bad outcome, not evidence of a bad rule. Changing rules after a few unlucky results lets the outcome grade the decision; the plan's own change rule, over a proper sample, decides instead.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Were any rules broken in these three trades?\",\"How many trades does it take to judge a rule?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.4-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why isn't it enough to know about outcome bias?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It only affects beginners, and knowing about it makes you an expert\",\"rationale\":\"Outcome bias affects experienced people too. Knowing its name doesn't change how results feel.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It still works on people who know about it, so you need a routine: grade the process before you see the result\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Results are the best evidence of how good a decision was\",\"rationale\":\"One result owes a lot to chance. That's exactly why judging decisions by results misleads.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Knowing about a bias removes it, but only after a few weeks\",\"rationale\":\"There's no such delay. A 2023 replication found the bias even among people who said results shouldn't count.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A 2023 pre-registered replication found outcome bias even among people who said outcomes shouldn't matter. Knowledge doesn't switch it off; structure does: hide the result, grade the process, then look.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Did the people in the 2023 replication know that outcomes shouldn't count?\",\"What can you change: what you know, or the order you do things in?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.5-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which trade was most likely taken out of boredom?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A small move outside your playbook, taken after three quiet hours 'just to be in'\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A pullback setup with every condition met, after a quiet morning\",\"rationale\":\"A quiet morning doesn't turn a valid setup into a boredom trade. Every condition was met, so it's a plan trade.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A logged 'no trade: no setup' after a slow session\",\"rationale\":\"That's not a trade at all. It's a decision to wait, and a good one.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A trade closed early by the plan's time limit\",\"rationale\":\"That's an exit rule doing its job, not an entry taken to relieve boredom.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A boredom trade comes from the wish to do something, not from a setup: action bias. The tell is an entry outside the playbook after a long quiet stretch.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"action-bias\",\"overtrading\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.5-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You add 3 off-plan trades a day. They have no edge, and costs take about 0.10R from each. Over 20 trading days, how much do they cost, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Per day: 3 × 0.10R = 0.30R. Over 20 days: 20 × 0.30R = 6R. That's six times your risk per trade, paid for trades with no reason behind them.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"overtrading\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the cost per day first: trades per day × cost per trade.\",\"Per day: 3 × 0.10R = 0.30R.\",\"Multiply the daily cost by the 20 trading days.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"trades\":[2,6,1],\"cost\":[0.05,0.15,0.05],\"days\":[10,25,5]},\"answer\":\"round(trades * cost * days, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You add {trades} off-plan trades a day. They have no edge, and costs take about {cost:2}R from each. Over {days} trading days, how much do they cost, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Per day: {trades} × {cost:2}R = {trades * cost:2}R. Over {days} days: {days} × {trades * cost:2}R = {answer:2}R. That's what trades with no reason behind them cost, before any edge.\",\"hint\":\"Per day: {trades} × {cost:2}R = {trades * cost:2}R. Multiply by {days}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.5-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A quiet session: no playbook setup appeared all morning. You took no trades and logged 'no trade: no setup' in your journal.\",\"prompt\":\"How does MARKET//LAB treat that morning?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It isn't scored: nothing happened\",\"rationale\":\"A decision happened: you chose to wait. Decision Quality scores decisions, including the decision not to trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"As a missed opportunity, since the account didn't grow\",\"rationale\":\"That's judging by activity and profit. With no setup, there was nothing to miss.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"As a good decision: a well-reasoned no-trade earns Decision Quality\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"As a weak session that lowers Decision Quality for inactivity\",\"rationale\":\"There's no penalty for waiting. The score rewards trades that fit the plan, and no-trades that fit it too.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Decision Quality judges whether a decision fitted the plan and the information at the time. With no setup, 'no trade' was the plan working, and the mission No Trade Is a Trade scores exactly that.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"action-bias\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"What does Decision Quality judge: activity, profit or the decision?\",\"Was there a setup to take?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.6-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is a well-formed if-then rule?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"If I feel nervous, then I'll be careful\",\"rationale\":\"'Careful' isn't an action you can check, and 'nervous' has no number. Both halves are vague.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Trade with discipline and respect the stop\",\"rationale\":\"That's an intention, not an if-then rule. There's no trigger saying when it applies.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"If a trade hits its stop, then I pause 20 minutes before the next decision\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"If the market looks bad, I'll trade less\",\"rationale\":\"'Looks bad' can't be observed, and 'less' isn't defined. Give both halves a number.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A good if-then rule has an observable trigger (a stop-out is an event you can see) and an action you can check (a 20-minute pause). The others leave the decision to the moment.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"if-then-rules\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.6-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After a winning morning you often take extra trades 'because you're sharp', and your journal shows those afternoon trades mostly lose.\",\"prompt\":\"Which if-then rule addresses the pattern?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stay humble after wins\",\"rationale\":\"A good intention, but with no trigger and no checkable action it won't hold in the moment.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"If I've taken 3 trades today, then I stop for the day\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"If I win in the morning, then I double my size in the afternoon\",\"rationale\":\"That's a rule, but it feeds the problem: sizing from a streak. Size stays fixed after wins.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"If I feel overconfident, then I try harder to focus\",\"rationale\":\"'Try harder' can't be checked. The rule needs an action you could see yourself doing.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The urge comes after wins, so the rule needs a trigger you can count and an action that ends it: a maximum number of trades per day. Rules about feelings or effort leave the decision to the moment the urge is strongest.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"if-then-rules\",\"overtrading\"],\"hints\":[\"What can you count in this pattern?\",\"The action should end the extra trades, not resize them.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.6-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do rules decided in advance tend to hold better than willpower in the moment?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"They remove emotions entirely\",\"rationale\":\"Emotions still arrive. The rule works because the decision was made earlier, not because the feeling is gone.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The decision is made while calm, and the trigger only has to start an action you already chose\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Willpower never works for anyone\",\"rationale\":\"Willpower can work, but it's weakest under stress, which is exactly when trading rules are tested.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Written rules guarantee profitable trades\",\"rationale\":\"No rule guarantees profit. Rules make the process consistent; results still vary.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In the moment, strong feelings and little time make good choices hardest. An if-then rule moves the choice to a calm moment, so under pressure you only have to notice the trigger. Guard-rails make the right action the easy one.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"if-then-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"With an if-then rule, when is the decision actually made?\",\"Rules don't stop feelings. What do they change?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.7-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"When does your cooling-off pause apply?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"After a loss or a sharp move, before the next decision\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only after three losses in a row\",\"rationale\":\"Three losses in a row may end the day under your limits. The pause comes earlier: after any loss or sharp move.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only after a winning trade, to avoid overconfidence\",\"rationale\":\"Wins can stir urges too, but the pause rule is set off by a loss or a sharp move.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whenever the market is quiet\",\"rationale\":\"A quiet market calls for patience, not a pause. The pause follows a loss or a sharp move.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The pause sits between a loss or a sharp move and the next decision, because that's when urges like revenge and FOMO are strongest. It has a set length and a fixed routine.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cooling-off-pause\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.7-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long at −0.7R. The higher lows that justified the trade have broken, and you wouldn't open this trade today. You think: 'I've already lost 70.00. I'll hold until it comes back.'\",\"prompt\":\"What does the 'fresh eyes' question tell you to do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Hold: closing now makes the loss real\",\"rationale\":\"The loss is already real on the account. Holding to avoid admitting it is loss aversion.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Exit or reduce, and write the decision down: you wouldn't open this trade now\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Buy more to lower the average price\",\"rationale\":\"Adding to a loser whose reason has broken raises the risk on an idea that has already failed.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Move the stop further away to give it room\",\"rationale\":\"Widening the stop raises the planned loss. It's the loss-aversion move the plan forbids.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Fresh eyes asks: if I had no position, would I open this one, at this size? Here the answer is no, so you exit or reduce. The 70.00 already lost is a sunk cost: it doesn't change what the trade is worth now.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cooling-off-pause\",\"loss-aversion\"],\"hints\":[\"Imagine you had no position: would you buy here?\",\"Does the money already lost change the trade's prospects?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.7.7-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"During a pause after a painful loss, which approach is most likely to help?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Telling yourself 'I feel nothing' and carrying on\",\"rationale\":\"That's suppression. It fights the feeling after it has grown, and tends to help less than changing the frame early.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Reframing early: 'one loss is one draw from many trades'\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Replaying the loss until you find who's to blame\",\"rationale\":\"Dwelling feeds the feeling. The routine reframes, checks in and moves on to the checklist.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Opening a small trade straight away to calm down\",\"rationale\":\"That's a trade taken for the feeling, not for a setup: the pattern the pause exists to stop.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Reappraisal changes how the situation is framed before the feeling grows; suppression fights it afterwards. In a qualitative study at four London investment banks, higher-performing traders relied more on early strategies like reframing.\",\"lesson\":\"1.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cooling-off-pause\",\"emotions-in-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"Which option changes the frame, and which hides the feeling?\",\"Early strategies act before the feeling grows.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.1-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A martingale bot starts with a first bet of 5.00 and doubles it after each loss. After 6 losses in a row, how big is its next bet, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":320,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The bets were 5, 10, 20, 40, 80 and 160: 315.00 lost. The next bet doubles the last one: 320.00, to win back 315.00 and make 5.00.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"martingale\"],\"hints\":[\"Each bet is twice the one before.\",\"Write out the six losing bets, then double the last one.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"base\":[1,20,1]},\"answer\":\"base * 64\",\"prompt\":\"A martingale bot starts with a first bet of {base:2} and doubles it after each loss. After 6 losses in a row, how big is its next bet, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"The bets were {base:2}, {base * 2:2}, {base * 4:2}, {base * 8:2}, {base * 16:2} and {base * 32:2}: {base * 63:2} lost. The next bet doubles the last one: {answer:2}, to win back {base * 63:2} and make {base:2}.\",\"hint\":\"The sixth losing bet was {base * 32:2}. Double it.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.1-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy 40 units at 50.00 with a stop planned at 49.00, so 1R is 40.00 before costs. At 49.00 you skip the stop and buy 40 more. Price falls to 47.50, where you finally exit. What was the loss, in R? Ignore costs.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Average price: (50.00 + 49.00) ÷ 2 = 49.50. Loss: 80 × (49.50 − 47.50) = 160.00, which is 160.00 ÷ 40.00 = 4R. The plan allowed 1R; adding to the loser and dropping the stop made it four times bigger.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"averaging-down\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the average price of all 80 units first.\",\"Loss = 80 × (average price − exit price).\",\"Average 49.50, exit 47.50: 80 × 2.00. Then divide by 40.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.1-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Ana plans 60 units in two halves: 30 at 100.00 and 30 at 99.00 if price dips, one stop at 97.50 for both, sized before the first order so the total loss at the stop fits her 1% budget. Ben buys 30 at 100.00 with a stop at 99.00; at 99.00 he cancels the stop and buys 30 more because it's 'cheaper now'.\",\"prompt\":\"Which statement is right?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both are averaging down: they both buy lower\",\"rationale\":\"Buying lower isn't the test. Ana fixed her total risk before the first order; Ben added risk after his idea had failed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Ana's is a planned split entry with a fixed total risk; Ben's is averaging down, with no limit\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Ben's is safer, because his average price is lower\",\"rationale\":\"His average is lower, but his position is bigger and has no stop. His possible loss has no limit at all.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Ana's is riskier, because her stop is further away\",\"rationale\":\"She sized both halves so the loss at 97.50 fits 1%. A wider stop with a smaller size isn't more risk.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A split entry is decided in full before the first order: sizes, levels and one stop, with the total loss at the stop inside the budget. Averaging down adds units after the idea starts failing, usually removing the stop, so the risk keeps growing.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"averaging-down\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask when each trader fixed the total risk.\",\"Which trader still has a stop after the second purchase?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A grid bot buys 20 units at every 0.50 step below 50.00 and has no stop. Price falls in one move from 50.00 to 46.00. How many units does it hold?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":160,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"It buys at 49.50, 49.00, 48.50, 48.00, 47.50, 47.00, 46.50 and 46.00: 8 levels × 20 units = 160 units, none of them sold, since price never rose 0.50 above a buy.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"grid-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the buy levels between 49.50 and 46.00.\",\"A 4.00 fall in 0.50 steps is 8 levels. Multiply by 20 units.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[5,20,5],\"fall\":[2,5,0.5]},\"answer\":\"units * fall * 2\",\"prompt\":\"A grid bot buys {units} units at every 0.50 step below 50.00 and has no stop. Price falls in one move from 50.00 to {50 - fall:2}. How many units does it hold?\",\"explanation\":\"A {fall:2} fall in 0.50 steps passes {fall * 2} buy levels, from 49.50 down to {50 - fall:2}: {fall * 2} × {units} = {answer} units, none of them sold.\",\"hint\":\"{fall:2} ÷ 0.50 = {fall * 2} levels. Multiply by {units} units.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The same grid: 20 units bought at every 0.50 step from 49.50 down to 46.00, 160 units in all. Price is now 46.00. What is the open loss, in vUSD? Ignore costs.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":280,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The average price of the eight equal lots is (49.50 + 46.00) ÷ 2 = 47.75. Open loss: 160 × (47.75 − 46.00) = 280.00. Using 50.00 as the cost of every unit would give 640.00 and overstate it.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"grid-trading\",\"exposure\"],\"hints\":[\"Equal lots at evenly spaced prices: the average is halfway between the first and the last.\",\"Open loss = units × (average price − current price).\",\"Average 47.75: 160 × 1.75.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[5,20,5],\"fall\":[2,5,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(units * fall * (fall - 0.5), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A grid bought {units} units at every 0.50 step from 49.50 down to {50 - fall:2}, {units * fall * 2} units in all. Price is now {50 - fall:2}. What is the open loss, in vUSD? Ignore costs.\",\"explanation\":\"The average price of the equal lots is (49.50 + {50 - fall:2}) ÷ 2 = {(99.5 - fall) / 2:2}. Open loss: {units * fall * 2} × ({(99.5 - fall) / 2:2} − {50 - fall:2}) = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Average {(99.5 - fall) / 2:2}: multiply {units * fall * 2} units by {(fall - 0.5) / 2:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A grid bot's equity curve rose smoothly for six months without a single losing week. Why is that weak evidence that it's safe?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Six months is too short for any strategy to be judged\",\"rationale\":\"Length helps, but isn't the point. A grid's risk sits in open positions, and a smooth curve can't show it until a trend arrives.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Smooth equity curves are always faked\",\"rationale\":\"Some are real. The issue isn't honesty: the curve leaves out the open risk the grid is carrying.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Grids only lose money on high-volatility days\",\"rationale\":\"Grids lose in trends, quiet or fast. A slow one-way move fills every level just the same.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its losing lots stay open instead of being closed, and they grow as price moves away until a trend turns them into one large loss\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A grid closes its winners and keeps its losers open, without a stop. Each week can look profitable while open losses build. When a trend comes, the lots pile up and one move can erase months of small wins.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"grid-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of the grid's trades get closed, and which stay open?\",\"What happens to the open lots when price keeps moving one way?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.3-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"An ad says: 'Our system: 85% win rate, +210% last year.' What do you need to know first?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Which indicator it uses\",\"rationale\":\"The method matters less than the evidence. Without sample, costs and worst loss, you can't tell skill from luck.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How many people have bought it\",\"rationale\":\"Popularity isn't evidence. Happy buyers speak up; the ones who lost usually don't.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How many trades, after which costs, and the largest loss and drawdown\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whether the price is discounted today\",\"rationale\":\"A deadline is a sales tactic. The first questions are about the evidence behind the claim.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A win rate and a return mean little alone. The sample says whether luck could explain them, the costs whether they survive trading, and the largest loss whether an 85% win rate hides rare, ruinous losses.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"performance-claims\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.3-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A signal group's ad shows 12 members who each turned 1,000 into more than 10,000 in a year. The group has had about 5,000 members.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the strongest reason to doubt the ad?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The 12 are survivors picked from 5,000; the members who lost aren't shown\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Turning 1,000 into 10,000 in a year is impossible\",\"rationale\":\"It's possible, often through luck and very large risk. The problem is that the ad shows only those who got lucky.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The members may not be real people\",\"rationale\":\"Maybe, but even if they're real, 12 picked from 5,000 prove nothing about the signals.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The ad should show monthly returns instead\",\"rationale\":\"Monthly figures for the same 12 would still be the survivors. You need the results of all 5,000.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"With 5,000 members taking big risks, some will hit extreme results by luck alone. Showing only them is survivorship and cherry-picking: the evidence you need is what happened to everyone who followed the signals.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"performance-claims\",\"survivorship-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"How many members are in the ad, and how many in the group?\",\"What would the ad look like if the signals had no edge at all?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.3-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A study publishes an edge that earned 1.0% a month in its test period. Using McLean and Pontiff's findings as a rule of thumb, what should you expect once it's published?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About 1.0% a month: published results are peer-reviewed\",\"rationale\":\"Review checks the method, not the future. Across 97 published predictors, returns fell about 58% after publication.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Roughly 0.4% a month, before your own costs\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Zero: published edges disappear completely\",\"rationale\":\"On average they shrank by about 58%, not to zero. Some edge often remains, but much less than the paper shows.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"More than 1.0%: publicity brings in more buyers\",\"rationale\":\"More traders using an edge usually shrinks it, as they compete for the same opportunities.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"McLean and Pontiff found that 97 published predictors earned 26% less out of sample and 58% less after publication. 1.0% × (1 − 0.58) ≈ 0.4% a month, and your costs still come off that.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"performance-claims\"],\"hints\":[\"How much did published predictors lose after publication, on average?\",\"1.0% × (1 − 0.58).\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Where should you check that a broker is licensed?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"On the broker's website, where the licence number is shown\",\"rationale\":\"Anyone can display a number. Only the regulator's register shows whether it's real and which company it belongs to.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In online reviews and forums\",\"rationale\":\"Reviews can warn you of problems, but they can't confirm a licence. The register can.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"On the regulator's own online register, searching the firm's name and licence number\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"By asking the account manager for a copy of the licence\",\"rationale\":\"Documents can be forged, and a salesperson isn't a neutral source. Go to the regulator directly.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Only the regulator's own register confirms that a licence exists, that it's current and which company and website it covers. Clone firms copy real licence numbers, so check that the name and web address match too.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-due-diligence\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.4-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A stranger messages you: 'Join our VIP signals. 30% a month guaranteed. Only 3 places left today. Deposit with our partner broker, licensed offshore, and get a 100% bonus.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's the right reading of this offer?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Only the offshore licence is a problem; the rest is normal marketing\",\"rationale\":\"Guaranteed returns, time pressure and deposit bonuses are red flags on their own, not marketing style.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's fine if the signals have a good track record\",\"rationale\":\"A track record from people selling signals is usually selective. And nothing makes a 30% monthly guarantee believable.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Every part is a red flag: unsolicited contact, guaranteed returns, time pressure, an offshore-only licence and a deposit bonus\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's safe if you start with a small deposit\",\"rationale\":\"A small deposit limits what you can lose, but it doesn't make the offer legitimate. Treat it as a scam until checked.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Each element is a known warning sign: contact you didn't ask for, a guaranteed return no market can give, pressure to act now, a licence with little protection and a bonus that ties up your money. Together they describe a likely scam.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"scam-red-flags\"],\"hints\":[\"Go through the message one claim at a time.\",\"Which of these would a regulated, honest firm never offer?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.4-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your broker runs a B-book model. What follows from that?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When you lose, the broker gains: a conflict of interest that rules and disclosures are meant to manage\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The broker is breaking the law\",\"rationale\":\"B-booking is legal and regulated in many places. It's a conflict to know about, not a crime.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It can't affect you, since prices come from the market\",\"rationale\":\"The broker is your counterparty, so your losses are its revenue. That's exactly why the model matters.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your orders are sent on to the exchange\",\"rationale\":\"That's an A-book broker. A B-book broker keeps your trades and takes the other side itself.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"In a B-book the broker takes the other side of client trades, so client losses become its income. That's a structural conflict of interest. It's legal and regulated, not automatically abusive, but you should know it before choosing a broker.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-due-diligence\"],\"hints\":[\"Who is on the other side of your trade in a B-book?\",\"If the broker is your counterparty, where does your loss go?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.5-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your equity is 4,000.00 and your open positions are worth 60,000.00. What is your effective leverage?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":15,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"Effective leverage = position value ÷ equity = 60,000.00 ÷ 4,000.00 = 15×. Whatever the broker allows, a 1% move now costs 15% of the account.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"effective-leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the position value by the equity.\",\"60,000.00 ÷ 4,000.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"equity\":[2000,20000,1000],\"lev\":[2,30,1]},\"answer\":\"lev\",\"prompt\":\"Your equity is {equity:2} and your open positions are worth {equity * lev:2}. What is your effective leverage?\",\"explanation\":\"Effective leverage = position value ÷ equity = {equity * lev:2} ÷ {equity:2} = {answer}×. Whatever the broker allows, a 1% move now costs {answer}% of the account.\",\"hint\":\"{equity * lev:2} ÷ {equity:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.5-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You run 15× effective leverage. How big a move against you wipes out the whole account, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6.67,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"The account loses the price move times the leverage. It's gone when that reaches 100%: 100% ÷ 15 = 6.67%. At 1× the same account would need the price to fall to zero.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"effective-leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"% of the account lost = % move × effective leverage.\",\"Set that equal to 100% and solve for the move: 100 ÷ 15.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lev\":[2,40,1]},\"answer\":\"round(100 / lev, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You run {lev}× effective leverage. How big a move against you wipes out the whole account, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"The account loses the price move times the leverage. It's gone when that reaches 100%: 100% ÷ {lev} = {answer:2}%.\",\"hint\":\"Set move × {lev} equal to 100% and solve: 100 ÷ {lev}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-1.8.5-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Mia trades CFDs at 20× effective leverage with no stop. A fast move takes her equity down to half the required margin and the broker closes all her positions. She says: 'At least the close-out worked like a stop.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with her view?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A close-out is a failure of sizing: it comes after most of the account is gone, at a price she didn't choose\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the close-out limited her loss the way a stop would\",\"rationale\":\"A stop limits the loss to a planned 1R. The close-out came only after most of her account was already gone.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The close-out should have come later, to give the trade room\",\"rationale\":\"Later would mean an even bigger loss. The problem is the size of the position, not the timing of the close-out.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"She should ask for more leverage, so close-outs come later\",\"rationale\":\"More leverage makes the same move cost more. It would make the next close-out arrive faster, not later.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A stop is a price chosen in advance where the idea fails, sized so the loss is 1R. A close-out happens when the account can no longer carry the position: by then most of the equity is gone, and it fills at whatever price is there.\",\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"margin-close-out\",\"effective-leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"Who chose the exit price, and when?\",\"How much of the account was gone when the close-out happened?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In the app's rules, what makes a candle's body 'long'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A body of more than 0.50 vUSD\",\"rationale\":\"A fixed size can't work: 0.50 is long in a calm hour and short in a busy one. The rule is relative.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A body bigger than the average body of the last 10 candles\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A body bigger than its own wicks\",\"rationale\":\"That compares the candle with itself. 'Long' compares the body with the bodies of recent candles.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A range bigger than the average range\",\"rationale\":\"Range includes the wicks. A long body is about open to close, measured against recent bodies.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The app follows the TA-Lib default: a body is long when it's above the average body of the 10 candles before it, and very long above 3 times that. The same candle can be long in a calm market and short in a busy one.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"candle-vocabulary\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Recent candles average a body of 0.30 and a range of 0.60.\",\"prompt\":\"A candle: open 100.40, high 100.75, low 100.15, close 100.55. Which name fits?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Doji\",\"rationale\":\"A doji's body is at most 10% of the average range, 0.06 here. This body is 0.15.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"High-wave candle\",\"rationale\":\"High-wave needs both wicks over twice the body, above 0.30. These wicks are 0.20 and 0.25.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Spinning top\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Long-legged doji\",\"rationale\":\"It isn't a doji: the body, 0.15, is well above the 0.06 line.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Body 100.55 − 100.40 = 0.15: short (under 0.30) but too big for a doji (over 0.06). Upper wick 100.75 − 100.55 = 0.20, lower wick 100.40 − 100.15 = 0.25: both longer than the body, but not twice as long. A spinning top, not a high-wave candle.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"candle-vocabulary\"],\"hints\":[\"Body = close − open. Upper wick = high − close; lower wick = open − low here.\",\"Body 0.15; wicks 0.20 and 0.25. Check the doji line: 10% of the average range.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After eight rising candles, a doji appears. A video calls it 'a clear sell signal: the buyers are exhausted'.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the most accurate reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sell: a doji after a rise marks the top\",\"rationale\":\"Tested doji types sit near 50%. A doji records one undecided hour; it doesn't mark tops.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy: the trend is still up\",\"rationale\":\"The doji doesn't say that either. It describes an hour with almost no net move.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An hour of indecision, not a signal: tested, doji sit near 50%\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It depends on the doji's colour\",\"rationale\":\"A doji has almost no body, so its colour carries next to no information.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A doji describes an hour where open and close nearly matched. As a signal it's unproven: in Bulkowski's data long-legged, dragonfly and gravestone doji sat at 50–51%. Context and testing, not the shape, decide anything.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"candle-vocabulary\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a doji record about the hour?\",\"Compare the tested rates with 50%.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"After ten rising candles, a candle has a small body near its high, a lower shadow three times the body and almost no upper shadow. What is it called?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Hammer\",\"rationale\":\"A hammer has this shape but follows a decline. After a rise, the same shape gets another name.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Hanging man\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Shooting star\",\"rationale\":\"A shooting star's long shadow is above the body. Here the long shadow is below.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Inverted hammer\",\"rationale\":\"An inverted hammer has a long upper shadow and follows a decline. This one has a long lower shadow after a rise.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Shape plus trend gives the name. A long lower shadow under a small body near the top is a hammer after a decline and a hanging man after a rise.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"rejection-candles\",\"trend\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Textbooks read the hanging man as bearish. What did Bulkowski's tests show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price fell after it about 70% of the time\",\"rationale\":\"That would make the name right. In the data the hanging man leaned the other way.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Exactly 50/50, no lean either way\",\"rationale\":\"It did lean, just not the way its name says: about 59% kept rising.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It was never tested\",\"rationale\":\"It was: Bulkowski ranks it 87 of 103, with a lean towards continuation.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Price kept rising about 59% of the time\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"As tested, the hanging man behaved more like a continuation: price broke upward first about 59% of the time, rank 87 of 103. Judge a pattern by its data, not its name.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"rejection-candles\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Two of the four long-shadow names point the wrong way in testing.\",\"The hanging man is one of them.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A LABX candle: open 100.30, high 100.32, low 99.70, close 100.20. What is its lower shadow divided by its body?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"Body: 100.30 − 100.20 = 0.10. The candle is bearish, so the lower shadow runs from the close, 100.20, down to the low: 0.50. 0.50 ÷ 0.10 = 5, well above the 2 a hammer needs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"rejection-candles\"],\"hints\":[\"The lower shadow starts at the bottom of the body, not at the open.\",\"This candle is bearish, so the bottom of its body is the close, 100.20.\",\"Lower shadow 100.20 − 99.70 = 0.50; body 0.10.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"o\":[99.5,101.5,0.1],\"b\":[0.05,0.3,0.05],\"lw\":[0.3,1.2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(lw / b, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A LABX candle: open {o:2}, high {o + 0.02:2}, low {o - b - lw:2}, close {o - b:2}. What is its lower shadow divided by its body?\",\"explanation\":\"Body: {o:2} − {o - b:2} = {b:2}. The candle is bearish, so the lower shadow runs from the close, {o - b:2}, down to the low: {lw:2}. {lw:2} ÷ {b:2} = {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"Lower shadow {o - b:2} − {o - b - lw:2} = {lw:2}; body {b:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A bullish engulfing forms after eight falling candles. The next candle closes above the pattern's high. The pattern's low is 1.50 above the nearest support zone. Which check fails?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Something to reverse\",\"rationale\":\"Eight falling candles came first, so there is something to reverse.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Confirmation\",\"rationale\":\"The next candle closed above the pattern's high: that is the confirmation.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Location\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"None: trade it\",\"rationale\":\"The pattern sits 1.50 above support, in open space, so the location check fails.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Trend and confirmation pass, but the pattern isn't at a zone: its low is 1.50 above support. With no level under it, the rule says skip.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pattern-context\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"hints\":[\"Run the three checks one by one.\",\"Where is the pattern's low compared with the support zone?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A hammer's low is 49.20. You wait for confirmation and buy at 49.85, with the stop 0.10 under the low. Resistance is at 51.00. What is the reward-to-risk, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.53,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Stop: 49.20 − 0.10 = 49.10. Risk: 49.85 − 49.10 = 0.75. Reward: 51.00 − 49.85 = 1.15. R:R = 1.15 ÷ 0.75 = 1.53.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pattern-confirmation\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the stop first: 0.10 under the hammer's low.\",\"Risk = entry − stop; reward = target − entry.\",\"Risk 0.75, reward 1.15. Divide reward by risk.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"low\":[48,52,0.05],\"gap\":[0.3,1,0.05],\"room\":[0.5,2.5,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(room / (gap + 0.1), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A hammer's low is {low:2}. You wait for confirmation and buy at {low + gap:2}, with the stop 0.10 under the low. Resistance is at {low + gap + room:2}. What is the reward-to-risk, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Stop: {low:2} − 0.10 = {low - 0.1:2}. Risk: {low + gap:2} − {low - 0.1:2} = {gap + 0.1:2}. Reward: {low + gap + room:2} − {low + gap:2} = {room:2}. R:R = {room:2} ÷ {gap + 0.1:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Risk {gap + 0.1:2}, reward {room:2}. Divide reward by risk.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You run a candle rule on 2,000 random-walk paths. It finds 1,200 patterns, and price is higher 10 candles later after 50.3% of them, give or take 2.8 points.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this tell you about the rule?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It has a small edge: 50.3% beats 50%\",\"rationale\":\"0.3 points is far inside the ±2.8 band. That's noise, not an edge.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's broken and should be thrown away\",\"rationale\":\"Every rule scores about 50% on a random walk. The test is a control, not a verdict on real markets.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It needs more random-walk data to reach 60%\",\"rationale\":\"More random-walk data only narrows the band around 50%. No sample size makes an edge out of noise.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"As expected: a shape alone creates no edge; test it on real data against random entries\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"On a random walk the future doesn't depend on the past, so every rule lands near 50%, as this one did. That's the control. Whether the rule has an edge can only be shown on real or structured markets, against random entries, after costs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\",\"random-walk\"],\"hints\":[\"Is 0.3 points bigger or smaller than the band?\",\"On a random walk, what rate should any rule get?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After a decline, candle 1 opens at 100.80 and closes at 100.30. Candle 2 opens at 100.35, closes at 100.90 and has a low of 100.20. Is it a bullish engulfing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes: candle 2's range covers candle 1\",\"rationale\":\"Ranges don't count in an engulfing. Compare bodies: candle 2's body starts at 100.35, above 100.30.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Yes: it closes above candle 1's open\",\"rationale\":\"That's one end. The other fails: the body opens at 100.35, above candle 1's close of 100.30.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No: candle 2's body opens above candle 1's close\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No: candle 2 should be bearish\",\"rationale\":\"A bullish engulfing ends with a bullish candle. The colour is right; the bottom of the body isn't.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Bodies only: candle 1's body is 100.30–100.80, candle 2's is 100.35–100.90. The top is covered, the bottom isn't, so it's not an engulfing. The low of 100.20 doesn't matter.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"engulfing-pattern\"],\"hints\":[\"Ignore highs and lows. Write down both bodies.\",\"Does candle 2's body reach down to 100.30?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What separates a harami from an inside bar?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A harami compares bodies; an inside bar compares whole ranges\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: they're one pattern with two names\",\"rationale\":\"They often coincide, but a harami's wicks can stick out while its body stays inside. An inside bar needs the whole range inside.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A harami needs a bullish second candle, an inside bar a bearish one\",\"rationale\":\"Neither is defined by colour. The difference is what's compared: bodies or full ranges.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"An inside bar must be a doji\",\"rationale\":\"That's a harami cross: a harami with a doji. An inside bar can have any body.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A harami asks whether the second body sits inside the first body. An inside bar asks whether the second candle's high and low sit inside the first one's range. Candle patterns compare bodies; price-action bars compare ranges.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"harami-pattern\"],\"hints\":[\"One looks at open and close; the other at high and low.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A blog calls bearish engulfing 'one of the most reliable patterns': it reverses price 79% of the time. Bulkowski ranks it 91st of 103 for overall performance.\",\"prompt\":\"How can both be true?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The rank must be a mistake\",\"rationale\":\"Both numbers come from the same tests. They measure different things: how often it turns, and how far.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The moves after it are small, so after costs little is left\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The 79% counts only the patterns that worked\",\"rationale\":\"The 79% counts all cases. The issue is the size of the move after the turn.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It works on daily charts but not on hourly ones\",\"rationale\":\"Both figures come from daily charts. Hit rate and overall performance simply measure different things.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A hit rate counts how often price breaks the expected way; overall performance also counts how far it goes. Frequent small moves rank low, and spread and commission take a bigger share of them. Judge a pattern by expectancy after costs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"engulfing-pattern\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a hit rate leave out?\",\"Think about the size of the move after the turn, and about costs.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Candle 1 is bearish: open 101.00, close 100.00. Candle 2 closes at 100.70. How far into candle 1's body does it close, in percent?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":70,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"(100.70 − 100.00) ÷ (101.00 − 100.00) = 0.70 ÷ 1.00 = 70%. Past the middle, so if candle 2 opened below candle 1's low, it's a piercing line.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"penetration-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"Measure up from candle 1's close, the bottom of its body.\",\"Divide by candle 1's body, open minus close.\",\"0.70 ÷ 1.00, as a percentage.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"o1\":[100.5,103,0.1],\"b\":[0.5,1.5,0.1],\"depth\":[0.05,1.45,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"depth < b\"],\"answer\":\"round(100 * depth / b, 0)\",\"prompt\":\"Candle 1 is bearish: open {o1:2}, close {o1 - b:2}. Candle 2 closes at {o1 - b + depth:2}. How far into candle 1's body does it close, in percent?\",\"explanation\":\"({o1 - b + depth:2} − {o1 - b:2}) ÷ ({o1:2} − {o1 - b:2}) = {depth:2} ÷ {b:2}, about {answer}%.\",\"hint\":\"{depth:2} ÷ {b:2}, then × 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Candle 1 is bearish, from 101.00 to 100.00, with a low of 99.90. Candle 2 opens at 99.70 and closes at 100.03. What is the pair called?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"On-neck line\",\"rationale\":\"On-neck closes at candle 1's low, 99.85 to 99.95. This one closes inside the body.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In-neck line\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Thrusting line\",\"rationale\":\"Thrusting needs a close more than 0.05 into the body, from 100.06. 100.03 is only just inside.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Piercing line\",\"rationale\":\"Piercing needs a close past the middle of the body, above 100.50.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"100.03 is within 0.05 of candle 1's close, 100.00: just inside the body. That's an in-neck line, read in theory as bearish continuation. Three cents separate it from a thrusting line.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"penetration-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"Find candle 1's close and the middle of its body.\",\"In-neck runs from 100.00 to 100.05.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What do Bulkowski's tests say about the kicker?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's one of the strongest signals, near the top of the rankings\",\"rationale\":\"That's the popular claim. The data rank it 96 of 103.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It reverses price more than 80% of the time\",\"rationale\":\"No: about 53%, close to a coin flip in this data.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"About 53%, rank 96 of 103, and one of the rarest patterns\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It was never tested\",\"rationale\":\"It was: 53%, rank 96 for performance, rank 100 of 103 for frequency.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The kicker: 53% in the expected direction, rank 96 of 103 for performance and 100 of 103 for frequency. A loud claim on weak, thin data: grade D.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the claim with the rate and the rank.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which middle candle makes a bullish abandoned baby?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A small body whose top is below candle 1's close\",\"rationale\":\"That's an ordinary morning star. An abandoned baby needs a doji gapped beyond both neighbours' wicks.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A doji whose body gaps below candle 1's close, its wicks overlapping\",\"rationale\":\"That's a morning doji star. In an abandoned baby even the wicks don't touch.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A long bearish body\",\"rationale\":\"A long middle body isn't a star at all. The middle candle of every star pattern is small.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A doji whose high is below the lows of candles 1 and 3\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"In an abandoned baby the middle doji is cut off on both sides: its high is below the lows of candles 1 and 3. That double gap is why the pattern is so rare.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"star-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the doji's high with its neighbours' lows.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Candle 1 of a morning star is bearish: open 103.40, close 102.60. By Bulkowski's rule, what is the lowest close for candle 3, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":103,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The middle of candle 1's body: (103.40 + 102.60) ÷ 2 = 103.00. Candle 3 must close there or higher. TA-Lib's default would accept anything above 102.60 + 0.3 × 0.80 = 102.84.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"star-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"Bulkowski uses the middle of candle 1's body.\",\"Add open and close, then halve.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"o1\":[100.5,105,0.1],\"b\":[0.4,1.6,0.2]},\"answer\":\"round(o1 - b / 2, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Candle 1 of a morning star is bearish: open {o1:2}, close {o1 - b:2}. By Bulkowski's rule, what is the lowest close for candle 3, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"The middle of candle 1's body: ({o1:2} + {o1 - b:2}) ÷ 2 = {answer:2}. Candle 3 must close there or higher.\",\"hint\":\"({o1:2} + {o1 - b:2}) ÷ 2.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do the published figures for the abandoned baby rest on few cases?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Researchers ignore it because it doesn't work\",\"rationale\":\"It was tested: 70%, rank 9. The problem is how seldom it appears.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It needs a doji gapped away from both neighbours, which rarely happens\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It only forms on weekly charts\",\"rationale\":\"It can form on any timeframe. It's rare because of the double gap it needs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its rule changes from book to book\",\"rationale\":\"Definitions vary a little for every pattern. The small sample comes from the double gap.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The pattern needs a doji gapped away from both neighbours' wicks. Gaps are uncommon, especially where trading runs around the clock, so the pattern is rare (frequency 92 of 103) and its rates come with a wide margin of error.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"star-patterns\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the gaps the pattern needs.\",\"Rare patterns mean small samples, and small samples mean wide margins.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Candle 1 is bearish, 101.00 to 100.00. Candle 2 is bullish, 99.90 to 101.20. Candle 3 closes at 101.60, above candle 2. What is the pattern?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three outside up\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Three inside up\",\"rationale\":\"Inside means candle 2's body sits inside candle 1's. Here candle 2's body covers it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Three white soldiers\",\"rationale\":\"Soldiers need three long up candles in a row. Candle 1 here is bearish.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Morning star\",\"rationale\":\"A star's middle candle is small and gapped. Candle 2 here is a long body that covers candle 1.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Candle 2's body, 99.90 to 101.20, covers candle 1's body, 100.00 to 101.00: a bullish engulfing. A higher close on candle 3 makes it three outside up.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"confirmed-candle-patterns\",\"engulfing-pattern\"],\"hints\":[\"Is candle 2's body inside candle 1's body, or around it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Three white soldiers end with a close at 51.20; their lowest low is 49.40. You buy at the ask (close + 0.05), stop 0.10 under that low. Resistance is at 52.00. What is the reward-to-risk?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.38,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Entry 51.20 + 0.05 = 51.25; stop 49.40 − 0.10 = 49.30; risk 1.95. Reward 52.00 − 51.25 = 0.75. R:R = 0.75 ÷ 1.95 = 0.38: far too little for the risk.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"confirmed-candle-patterns\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Entry at the ask: close + 0.05. Stop: low − 0.10.\",\"Risk 1.95, reward 0.75.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"close\":[48,55,0.05],\"height\":[1,3,0.05],\"room\":[0.3,2,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round((room - 0.05) / (height + 0.15), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Three white soldiers end with a close at {close:2}; their lowest low is {close - height:2}. You buy at the ask (close + 0.05), stop 0.10 under that low. Resistance is at {close + room:2}. What is the reward-to-risk?\",\"explanation\":\"Entry {close + 0.05:2}; stop {close - height - 0.1:2}; risk {height + 0.15:2}. Reward {close + room:2} − {close + 0.05:2} = {room - 0.05:2}. R:R = {room - 0.05:2} ÷ {height + 0.15:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Risk {height + 0.15:2}, reward {room - 0.05:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Bulkowski's three inside up shows 65%; the bullish harami inside it shows 53%. Why the jump?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Candle 3 adds new information about the future\",\"rationale\":\"On a random walk, where nothing predicts anything, three inside up still 'broke up first' 74% of the time. The jump is mostly built in.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Three-candle patterns are always more reliable\",\"rationale\":\"The number of candles isn't the reason. The last candle closing near the pattern's top is.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Candle 3 is already part of the move, so the pattern ends near its own breakout point\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The 65% counts only patterns at support\",\"rationale\":\"No location filter is in those figures. The difference comes from the third candle itself.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"When three inside up completes, candle 3 has closed above candle 1's open, near the pattern's top. The 'break up first' count then needs only a small further rise. Much of the higher rate is that geometry, paid for with a later entry.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"confirmed-candle-patterns\",\"pattern-confirmation\"],\"hints\":[\"Where does candle 3 close, compared with the pattern's high?\",\"What happened on the random walk in this lesson?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In Bulkowski's tests, which of these mostly did the opposite of what its name says?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Morning star\",\"rationale\":\"The morning star broke upward 78% of the time, the way its name says.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bearish engulfing\",\"rationale\":\"It broke down 79% of the time, as named, though the moves were small.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Three white soldiers\",\"rationale\":\"The soldiers broke upward 82% of the time, as named.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Bearish three-line strike\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The bearish three-line strike is taught as a continuation down, yet price broke upward 84% of the time, first of 103 patterns. The hanging man and the inverted hammer also lean against their names.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\",\"concepts\":[\"pattern-name-vs-evidence\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A forum post: 'This rare five-candle pattern is 86% accurate.' You can't find how many cases the figure comes from.\",\"prompt\":\"What should you ask first?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Whether the pattern has a famous name\",\"rationale\":\"A name proves nothing. The first open question is how many cases stand behind the 86%.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How many cases the 86% rests on\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Whether it worked on the last chart you saw\",\"rationale\":\"One chart is a sample of one. You need the full count.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Which stop to use with it\",\"rationale\":\"Risk comes later. First find out whether the 86% means anything at all.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A rate from a handful of cases has a wide margin of error. With 14 cases, 12 wins make 86%, and the true rate could be as low as about 60%. Rare patterns rarely give enough cases to judge.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\",\"concepts\":[\"pattern-name-vs-evidence\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Which card question comes straight after the rule?\",\"How wide is the margin of error with very few cases?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which order follows the five-question pattern card?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Rate vs 50% → rule → cases → scope → costs\",\"rationale\":\"A rate means nothing until you know the rule it counts and how many cases it has.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Scope → result after costs → rate → cases → rule\",\"rationale\":\"That's backwards: each answer depends on the one before, starting from the exact rule.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Rule → cases → rate vs 50% → result after costs → scope\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Cases → rule → costs → rate → scope\",\"rationale\":\"You can't count cases until the rule tells you what a case is.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Each question needs the one before it: the rule defines a case, the cases give the rate, the rate and the costs give the result, and the scope tells you whether any of it applies to your market.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\",\"concepts\":[\"pattern-name-vs-evidence\"],\"hints\":[\"What do you need before you can count anything?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.9-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A real candle: open 100.20, high 100.80, low 99.90, close 100.50. What is its Heikin-Ashi close, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":100.35,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"HA close = (100.20 + 100.80 + 99.90 + 100.50) ÷ 4 = 401.40 ÷ 4 = 100.35. It's an average, not a price that traded.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\",\"concepts\":[\"alternative-chart-types\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the four real prices.\",\"Divide the total by 4.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"o\":[98,102,0.05],\"up\":[0.1,0.8,0.05],\"dn\":[0.1,0.8,0.05],\"mv\":[-0.6,0.6,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(round((3 * o + 2 * mv + up - dn) * 25, 0) - (3 * o + 2 * mv + up - dn) * 25) < 0.000001\"],\"answer\":\"round((o + max(o, o + mv) + up + min(o, o + mv) - dn + o + mv) / 4, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A real candle: open {o:2}, high {max(o, o + mv) + up:2}, low {min(o, o + mv) - dn:2}, close {o + mv:2}. What is its Heikin-Ashi close, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"HA close = ({o:2} + {max(o, o + mv) + up:2} + {min(o, o + mv) - dn:2} + {o + mv:2}) ÷ 4 = {answer:2}. It's an average, not a price that traded.\",\"hint\":\"Add the four prices, then divide by 4.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.9-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A backtest of 'exit on the first red Heikin-Ashi candle' fills every exit at the HA close. Why are its results wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"HA charts always show more losses than candles\",\"rationale\":\"HA doesn't change outcomes by itself. The error is filling at prices that never existed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The rule should fill at the HA open instead\",\"rationale\":\"The HA open is an average too, not a traded price. Every fill must use a real price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing: HA prices are close enough to real ones\",\"rationale\":\"They can differ a lot. In this lesson the HA close was 0.30 above the real exit.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"HA closes are averages that never traded; real exits fill at real prices\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"An HA close is (O + H + L + C) ÷ 4, a number no one traded. A real exit fills at a real price, at the bid for a sale. In the lesson, HA showed 100.15 while the real sale filled at 99.85.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\",\"concepts\":[\"alternative-chart-types\"],\"hints\":[\"Could you actually have sold at an HA close?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.1.9-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A trader shows a Renko chart with a clean uptrend and says it formed in 'only a few bricks'. The brick size follows ATR, recalculated as new candles arrive.\",\"prompt\":\"What should make you cautious?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Renko hides time, and ATR-sized bricks can redraw past bricks\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Renko bricks are always too large\",\"rationale\":\"Brick size is a setting. The problems are the missing time axis and bricks that change with ATR.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Renko charts can't show downtrends\",\"rationale\":\"They draw down bricks too. The issue is time and redrawing, not direction.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: fewer bricks means a stronger trend\",\"rationale\":\"A few bricks could have taken hours or weeks. Renko's x-axis counts bricks, not time.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Renko's x-axis counts bricks, so a few bricks can hide a long, slow stretch. And when the box size follows ATR, a new ATR value can change how past bricks are drawn, so today's clean picture may not be what a trader saw at the time.\",\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\",\"concepts\":[\"alternative-chart-types\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a Renko chart's x-axis count?\",\"What happens to past bricks when the box size changes?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is a valid uptrend line?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A line through the two lowest closes on the chart\",\"rationale\":\"Closes aren't swing lows, and the two lowest can sit anywhere. Lines are drawn on the lows' wicks; closes judge the breaks.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A steep line through two lows, redrawn after each break so it keeps fitting\",\"rationale\":\"Redrawing after every break makes the line follow your hopes. A valid line stays fixed, and steep lines break on any pause.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A line through two swing lows, the second higher, confirmed by a third low that touches it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A line through two rising swing highs\",\"rationale\":\"An uptrend line runs under the lows. A line through rising highs can be a channel's upper edge, not the trendline.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Two swing lows draw the line, the second higher than the first; a third low that touches it confirms it. The points should be reasonably spaced, the line not forced through candles, and the steeper it is, the less it means.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"trendlines-and-channels\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX has risen for two days. Your uptrend line runs through three swing lows. A candle has just closed 0.30 under it. The last swing low is 102.00 and price is at 102.60.\",\"prompt\":\"What would confirm that the uptrend has ended?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing more: the close under the line already confirms it\",\"rationale\":\"A trendline break is a warning. Rises often slow and go sideways, breaking a rising line without turning down.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A close under the last swing low, 102.00, or a lower high followed by a lower low\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A second close under the same line\",\"rationale\":\"More closes under a sloped line still only say the rise slowed. A change of trend shows in the swing structure.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A break of a new line, redrawn through the latest low\",\"rationale\":\"Redrawing moves the goalposts. Keep the line fixed and confirm with horizontal structure instead.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Breaking a rising line shows the rise slowed. A change of trend needs structure: a close under the last swing low, or the peak-trough sequence turning to lower highs and lower lows. Until then, it can be a pause.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"trendlines-and-channels\",\"trend\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what the line break proves and what it doesn't.\",\"Trends are defined by swing highs and lows, not by the lines drawn on them.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why can't a regression channel fitted to the whole chart be used as a rule in a backtest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It is too wide to give clear signals\",\"rationale\":\"Width is a setting, plus or minus k standard deviations. The flaw is when the line is computed, not how wide it is.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It has too few touches to count as a valid line\",\"rationale\":\"A regression line isn't drawn through touches. It's fitted to every close on screen, including later ones.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Regression lines only work on a log scale\",\"rationale\":\"The scale changes the fit, not the problem. Any fit over the whole chart includes candles that came later.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its values at old candles change as new candles are added, so each signal uses later data\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A best-fit line through every close moves when a candle is added, so its value at an old candle depends on candles after it. A backtest that uses it reads the future: look-ahead. A parallel channel drawn from past swings stays fixed.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"trendlines-and-channels\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about which candles the fit uses to place the line at an old candle.\",\"In the lesson, refitting with 40 closes moved candle 30's value from 103.26 to 102.75.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In the life of a chart pattern, which of these can only come after the breakout?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Volume fading inside the pattern\",\"rationale\":\"Fading volume belongs to the pattern's build-up, while price still swings between the boundaries.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A throwback to the broken boundary\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A third touch of one boundary\",\"rationale\":\"Touches build the boundaries. They come before any breakout.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The prior trend into the area\",\"rationale\":\"The prior trend comes first: a pattern needs something to continue or reverse.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The order: prior trend, boundaries and touches, fading volume, the breakout and its confirmation; then the measured target, the invalidation level and a possible throwback, all read in context. A throwback needs a broken edge to return to.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"chart-pattern-anatomy\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Resistance sits at 104.00 and ATR(14) is 0.40. How much higher is the 3% confirmation level than the 0.5 × ATR level?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2.92,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"3%: 104.00 × 1.03 = 107.12. 0.5 × ATR: 104.00 + 0.20 = 104.20. They're 2.92 apart, more than 7 ATR. The 3% rule was built for daily stock charts; on LABX 1H it would confirm almost nothing.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"breakout-confirmation-filters\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out each confirmation level, then subtract.\",\"3%: 104.00 × 1.03. ATR filter: 104.00 + 0.5 × 0.40.\",\"107.12 − 104.20.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lvl\":[96,110,1],\"atr\":[0.3,0.6,0.02]},\"answer\":\"round(lvl * 0.03 - 0.5 * atr, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Resistance sits at {lvl:2} and ATR(14) is {atr:2}. How much higher is the 3% confirmation level than the 0.5 × ATR level?\",\"explanation\":\"3%: {lvl:2} × 1.03 = {lvl * 1.03:2}. 0.5 × ATR: {lvl:2} + {0.5 * atr:2} = {lvl + 0.5 * atr:2}. They're {answer:2} apart. On LABX 1H the 3% level is many ATRs away and would confirm almost nothing.\",\"hint\":\"{lvl * 1.03:2} − {lvl + 0.5 * atr:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A pattern 2.50 tall breaks out upward at 101.00. Past patterns of this kind reached the full target 60% of the time. Where is the scaled target?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":102.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Scaled distance = 2.50 × 0.60 = 1.50; target = 101.00 + 1.50 = 102.50. The full-height target, 103.50, was reached only 60% of the time, so the scaled target is the more honest plan.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"Scale the height first, then project it from the breakout.\",\"2.50 × 0.60 = 1.50.\",\"101.00 + 1.50.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"h\":[1.5,4,0.1],\"b\":[98,106,0.5],\"p\":[45,80,1]},\"answer\":\"round(b + h * p / 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A pattern {h:2} tall breaks out upward at {b:2}. Past patterns of this kind reached the full target {p}% of the time. Where is the scaled target?\",\"explanation\":\"Scaled distance = {h:2} × {p / 100:2} = {h * p / 100:2}; target = {b:2} + {h * p / 100:2} = {answer:2}. The full-height target, {b + h:2}, was reached only {p}% of the time.\",\"hint\":\"{b:2} + {h:2} × {p / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"When does a double top exist?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"As soon as a second peak forms near the first\",\"rationale\":\"Two peaks are only a range so far. In Bulkowski's data about 60% of unconfirmed twin peaks kept rising.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When the second peak's candle has a long upper wick\",\"rationale\":\"A wick shows one candle's rejection. The pattern needs the valley broken on a close.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When price touches the valley between the peaks\",\"rationale\":\"A touch is a test, not a break. Confirmation is a close beyond the level.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Once a candle closes under the valley between the two peaks\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The valley between the peaks is the confirmation level. Before a close under it, twin peaks are a range that often keeps rising; after it, the double top exists and its target and stop can be measured.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"double-tops-and-bottoms\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A double bottom has lows of 95.50 and 95.70, with a peak of 98.50 between them. It just closed above 98.50. Eve & Eve bottoms reached the full target 65% of the time. Where is the scaled target?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":100.45,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Height = 98.50 − 95.50 = 3.00, from the lower low to the peak. Scaled: 3.00 × 0.65 = 1.95, projected from the confirmation level: 98.50 + 1.95 = 100.45.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"double-tops-and-bottoms\",\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"The height runs from the lower low up to the peak between the lows.\",\"Height 3.00 × 0.65 = 1.95.\",\"98.50 + 1.95.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"low\":[94,100,0.1],\"h\":[1.5,4.5,0.1],\"d\":[0.05,0.3,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(low + h + h * 0.65, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A double bottom has lows of {low:2} and {low + d:2}, with a peak of {low + h:2} between them. It just closed above {low + h:2}. Eve & Eve bottoms reached the full target 65% of the time. Where is the scaled target?\",\"explanation\":\"Height = {low + h:2} − {low:2} = {h:2}. Scaled: {h:2} × 0.65 = {h * 0.65:2}, projected from the peak: {low + h:2} + {h * 0.65:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{low + h:2} + {h:2} × 0.65.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Two ways to trade the same double tops, tested after costs. A sells at the second peak: it wins 30% of the time, +3.0R on average, and loses 1R. B sells on the confirming close: it wins 65% of the time, +0.4R on average, and loses 1R.\",\"prompt\":\"Which method has the better expectancy?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: +0.20R a trade, against −0.09R for B\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"B, because it wins more than twice as often\",\"rationale\":\"Win rate ignores size. B's wins are small: 0.65 × 0.4 − 0.35 × 1 = −0.09R a trade.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"They're the same once costs are counted\",\"rationale\":\"These results are already after costs, and they differ: +0.20R against −0.09R.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They can't be compared without the targets\",\"rationale\":\"Average wins and losses in R already reflect where targets and stops sat. Expectancy compares them directly.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Expectancy = win rate × average win − loss rate × average loss. A: 0.30 × 3.0 − 0.70 × 1 = +0.20R. B: 0.65 × 0.4 − 0.35 × 1 = −0.09R. The higher win rate belongs to the worse method. The numbers are made up for the exercise.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"anticipation-vs-confirmation\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out each method's expectancy in R.\",\"A: 0.30 × 3.0 − 0.70 × 1.\",\"B: 0.65 × 0.4 − 0.35 × 1.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Peaks at 104.80 (candle 8), 106.00 (candle 17) and 104.70 (candle 26) follow a rise, with lows of 103.00 on candles 12 and 22. Which is the right shoulder?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"106.00, on candle 17\",\"rationale\":\"That's the head: the highest peak, in the middle.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"104.80, on candle 8\",\"rationale\":\"That's the left shoulder, the first of the three peaks.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"104.70, on candle 26\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"103.00, on candle 22\",\"rationale\":\"That's a low between the head and the right shoulder: one end of the neckline.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The right shoulder is the third peak, after the head: 104.70 on candle 26. The shoulders, 104.80 and 104.70, sit at almost the same height either side of the 106.00 head, and the neckline joins the lows between them.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"head-and-shoulders\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A head-and-shoulders top has its head at 106.50 and a flat neckline at 104.50. Tops reached the full target 51% of the time. Where is the scaled target after a close under the neckline?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":103.48,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Height = 106.50 − 104.50 = 2.00. Scaled: 2.00 × 0.51 = 1.02. A top breaks down, so subtract from the neckline: 104.50 − 1.02 = 103.48. The full target, 102.50, was reached only half the time.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"head-and-shoulders\",\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"Height: head minus neckline. Scale it, then subtract it from the neckline.\",\"2.00 × 0.51 = 1.02.\",\"104.50 − 1.02.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[98,106,0.1],\"h\":[1.5,4,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(n - h * 0.51, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A head-and-shoulders top has its head at {n + h:2} and a flat neckline at {n:2}. Tops reached the full target 51% of the time. Where is the scaled target after a close under the neckline?\",\"explanation\":\"Height = {n + h:2} − {n:2} = {h:2}. Scaled: {h:2} × 0.51 = {h * 0.51:2}. Subtract from the neckline: {n:2} − {h * 0.51:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{n:2} − {h:2} × 0.51.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An inverse head and shoulders has its head at 93.50 and a flat neckline at 96.50. Inverse patterns reached the full target 71% of the time. Where is the scaled target after a close above the neckline?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":98.63,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Height = 96.50 − 93.50 = 3.00. Scaled: 3.00 × 0.71 = 2.13. An inverse pattern breaks up, so add to the neckline: 96.50 + 2.13 = 98.63.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"head-and-shoulders\",\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"Height: neckline minus head. Scale it, then add it to the neckline.\",\"3.00 × 0.71 = 2.13.\",\"96.50 + 2.13.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[94,102,0.1],\"h\":[1.5,4,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(n + h * 0.71, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An inverse head and shoulders has its head at {n - h:2} and a flat neckline at {n:2}. Inverse patterns reached the full target 71% of the time. Where is the scaled target after a close above the neckline?\",\"explanation\":\"Height = {n:2} − {n - h:2} = {h:2}. Scaled: {h:2} × 0.71 = {h * 0.71:2}. Add to the neckline: {n:2} + {h * 0.71:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{n:2} + {h:2} × 0.71.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"After a rise, LABX made peaks at 102.00, 101.95 and 102.00, with valleys at 100.05 and 100.00. Price is now 101.10. What is this so far?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A range: it becomes a triple top only after a close under 100.00\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A triple top: three peaks at one price\",\"rationale\":\"Three peaks describe the shape so far. Without a close under the lowest valley it's a range, and many ranges break upward.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A sell signal, since the ceiling held three times\",\"rationale\":\"Holding three times says the ceiling exists, not which way the range will break. Rectangles after a rise broke up 63% of the time.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"An ascending triangle\",\"rationale\":\"An ascending triangle needs rising lows. These valleys are flat, 100.05 and 100.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Until a close under the lowest valley, 100.00, three peaks are a rectangle: flat support and resistance. Many such ranges break upward. The triple top exists only once the floor breaks on a close.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"rectangles-and-triples\",\"range\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A rectangle runs from 98.50 to 100.00. Upward breakouts from rectangles reached the full target 78% of the time. Where is the scaled target of an upward breakout?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101.17,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Height = 100.00 − 98.50 = 1.50. Scaled: 1.50 × 0.78 = 1.17, added to the top: 100.00 + 1.17 = 101.17. A downward breakout would use its own rate, 54%.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"rectangles-and-triples\",\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"Height: top minus bottom. Scale it, then add it to the top.\",\"1.50 × 0.78 = 1.17.\",\"100.00 + 1.17.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[96,104,0.1],\"h\":[1,3,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(lo + h + h * 0.78, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A rectangle runs from {lo:2} to {lo + h:2}. Upward breakouts from rectangles reached the full target 78% of the time. Where is the scaled target of an upward breakout?\",\"explanation\":\"Height = {h:2}. Scaled: {h:2} × 0.78 = {h * 0.78:2}, added to the top: {lo + h:2} + {h * 0.78:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{lo + h:2} + {h:2} × 0.78.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Inside a rectangle from 100.00 to 102.00, price rises from the floor to 101.20 and turns down without reaching the ceiling.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you read this partial rise?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"As a confirmed short: the breakout will be down\",\"rationale\":\"In Bulkowski's data about one in four still broke up. A tilt doesn't replace the close beyond the floor.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"As noise: moves inside a range mean nothing\",\"rationale\":\"It's one of the few quantified signs: after partial rises, 75% of breakouts went down in his data. Weak, but not nothing.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"As a sign the ceiling will break next\",\"rationale\":\"That's the tilt of a partial decline. A rise that stalls short of the top tilts the odds down.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"As a tilt toward a downward breakout, still waiting for a close under 100.00\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"After a partial rise, Bulkowski's rectangles broke down 75% of the time; after a partial decline, up 79%. That's a lean from one data set of US stocks, so you still wait for a close beyond the boundary and plan for being wrong.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"rectangles-and-triples\"],\"hints\":[\"Recall which way partial rises tilted the breakout in the data.\",\"A tilt changes the odds, not the need for a confirming close.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A pattern's swing highs are 104.00, 104.03 and 103.98. Its swing lows are 100.50, 101.40 and 102.30. What is it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A descending triangle\",\"rationale\":\"A descending triangle has a flat bottom under falling highs. Here the top is flat and the lows rise.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An ascending triangle\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A symmetrical triangle\",\"rationale\":\"A symmetrical triangle needs falling highs. These highs are flat, within 0.05 of each other.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A rising wedge\",\"rationale\":\"In a rising wedge both lines rise. Here the upper line is flat.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A flat top near 104.00 over lows that rise 0.90 a swing: an ascending triangle. The slopes give the name; the breakout's direction still comes from the data and the close.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"triangles\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Descending triangles are taught as bearish. What did Bulkowski's data show about the direction of their breakouts?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Most broke down, about three in four\",\"rationale\":\"That's the textbook story. The count was 53% up and 47% down.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Almost all broke down once volume faded\",\"rationale\":\"Volume fades in most triangles. It didn't make the descending ones break down.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"About half broke upward: 53%, and 63% when price had risen into the pattern\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They never broke upward\",\"rationale\":\"More than half did. The shape's reputation and its count disagree.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"53% of descending triangles broke out upward, 63% when price had risen into the pattern. The name suggests sellers; the count is close to a coin flip. Wait for the close beyond a boundary.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"triangles\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate the textbook label from the count.\",\"The count was close to a coin flip, slightly upward.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"An ascending triangle with a flat top at 104.00 breaks downward: a candle closes under its rising lower line at 102.80. You're considering a short.\",\"prompt\":\"What should your plan take into account?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The bias is 63% upward, so the breakdown will fail: buy instead\",\"rationale\":\"37% did break down. Buying against the close you just saw, on the bias alone, ignores the confirmation.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Breaking against the bias makes the move extra strong: size up\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing in the data says so. Downward breakouts from ascending triangles ranked 30th of 36.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing more: the confirming close settles the direction\",\"rationale\":\"Confirmation filters some false moves, not all. 38% of these downward breakouts still failed.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Downward breakouts here were weak: plan for a close back inside and for a bust up through 104.00\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Ascending triangles broke up 63% of the time. Those that broke down failed 38% of the time and reached the target only 44%, rank 30 of 36. A short is allowed, but the plan must cover a close back inside and a bust through the top.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"triangles\",\"busted-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"Look at how downward breakouts from this triangle performed.\",\"They failed 38% of the time and met the target 44%. What must the plan cover?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"After a steep rise, LABX pauses for 30 candles between two flat, parallel lines. What is the pause?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A flag\",\"rationale\":\"A flag is brief, 5–20 candles on LABX 1H, and slopes against the pole. Thirty flat candles is too long.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A pennant\",\"rationale\":\"A pennant's lines converge, like a small triangle. These are parallel.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A measured move\",\"rationale\":\"A measured move is a projection from three swing points, not the shape of a pause.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A rectangle: too long to be a flag\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Pole first, then shape, then length. Flags and pennants are brief pauses after a steep pole; a flat, parallel pause that runs for 30 candles is a rectangle, with its own statistics.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"flags-and-pennants\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A flagpole ran from 98.00 to 101.00. The flag breaks out upward at 100.60. Flags reached the half-mast target 46% of the time. Where is the scaled target?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101.98,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Pole = 101.00 − 98.00 = 3.00. Scaled: 3.00 × 0.46 = 1.38, from the breakout: 100.60 + 1.38 = 101.98. Half-mast, 103.60, was reached less than half the time.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"flags-and-pennants\",\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"Measure the pole, scale it, then add it to the breakout price.\",\"3.00 × 0.46 = 1.38.\",\"100.60 + 1.38.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"b\":[98,106,0.1],\"pole\":[2,5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(b + pole * 0.46, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A flagpole rose {pole:2}. The flag breaks out upward at {b:2}. Flags reached the half-mast target 46% of the time. Where is the scaled target?\",\"explanation\":\"Scaled: {pole:2} × 0.46 = {pole * 0.46:2}, from the breakout: {b:2} + {pole * 0.46:2} = {answer:2}. Half-mast, {b + pole:2}, was reached less than half the time.\",\"hint\":\"{b:2} + {pole:2} × 0.46.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A measured move: A 96.00, B 99.50, C 98.00. Project D at 60% of leg AB, measured from C.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":100.1,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"AB = 99.50 − 96.00 = 3.50. 60% of it is 2.10, added to C: 98.00 + 2.10 = 100.10. D100 would be 101.50; second legs usually ran shorter than the first.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"measured-move\"],\"hints\":[\"Measure leg AB, take 60% of it, and add it to C.\",\"AB = 3.50; 3.50 × 0.60 = 2.10.\",\"98.00 + 2.10.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[94,102,0.1],\"ab\":[2,5,0.1],\"bc\":[0.8,2.5,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"bc < ab * 0.8\"],\"answer\":\"round(a + ab - bc + ab * 0.6, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A measured move: A {a:2}, B {a + ab:2}, C {a + ab - bc:2}. Project D at 60% of leg AB, measured from C.\",\"explanation\":\"AB = {ab:2}. 60% of it is {ab * 0.6:2}, added to C: {a + ab - bc:2} + {ab * 0.6:2} = {answer:2}. Second legs usually ran shorter than the first.\",\"hint\":\"{a + ab - bc:2} + {ab:2} × 0.60.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Swing highs: 105.00, 104.00, 103.00. Swing lows: 103.50, 103.00, 102.50. What shape do the two lines make?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A falling wedge\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A rising wedge\",\"rationale\":\"In a rising wedge both lines rise. Here both fall.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A falling channel\",\"rationale\":\"Channel lines are parallel. Here the highs fall 1.00 a swing and the lows 0.50, so the gap shrinks from 1.50 to 0.50.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A descending triangle\",\"rationale\":\"A descending triangle has a flat bottom. These lows fall.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Both lines fall, the upper one faster: 1.00 a swing against 0.50. Same direction and converging is a wedge; falling, it's a falling wedge, which broke out upward 68% of the time in Bulkowski's data.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.8\",\"concepts\":[\"wedges-and-broadening\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Rising wedges are taught as bearish. What did Bulkowski's data show for those that broke down?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"They were among the most reliable downward patterns\",\"rationale\":\"The reverse: rank 36 of 36, last among downward breakouts.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Most of them reached the full target\",\"rationale\":\"Downward breakouts reached the full target only 32% of the time.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"60% broke down, but 51% of those failed to fall even 5%\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Hardly any broke down; they broke up\",\"rationale\":\"60% did break down. The weakness was in what followed: half of those breakdowns failed.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Most rising wedges broke down, 60%, but 51% of those breakdowns failed to fall 5% before turning, the weakest result of 36 downward patterns. With a stop beyond the upper line, the scaled target often pays less than the risk.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.8\",\"concepts\":[\"wedges-and-broadening\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate how often they broke down from what happened next.\",\"Failure here means falling less than 5% before turning back.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why are broadening formations and diamonds hard to trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"They form only in quiet markets\",\"rationale\":\"The reverse: their swings grow with volatility. That's what pushes stops away.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They're rare, drawn subjectively, and their widening swings push stops far away\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Their breakouts always fail\",\"rationale\":\"Diamonds ranked 3rd of 36 downward. The trouble is few, subjective cases, not certain failure.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Their targets can't be measured\",\"rationale\":\"Bulkowski gives targets for both. Spotting them consistently is the problem, not measuring them.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Megaphones and diamonds are rare, so their statistics rest on few cases; two people rarely draw them the same way; and growing swings put a sensible stop far from the entry. Bulkowski calls broadening formations poor performers.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.8\",\"concepts\":[\"wedges-and-broadening\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about how many cases, how they're drawn, and where the stop goes.\",\"Widening swings mean the opposite boundary keeps moving away.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.9-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why does a rounding bottom become clear only late?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Its volume can only be read once it's complete\",\"rationale\":\"Volume can be read candle by candle. The delay comes from the confirmation level, the left rim.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It forms only on weekly charts\",\"rationale\":\"Bowls appear on any timeframe. The issue is where it confirms, not which chart shows it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's confirmed only by a close above the left rim, after most of the rise from the low\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its lowest candle must be a hammer\",\"rationale\":\"No single candle defines the bowl. It's the slow turn, then the close above the rim.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A slow bowl looks like any decline until its right side is built. It confirms on a close above the left rim, and by then much of the rise from the bottom is done: in the lesson, 4.34 of it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.9\",\"concepts\":[\"rounding-and-v-patterns\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.9-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A cup with handle has its rim at 103.00 and its bottom at 98.00. Cups reached the full target 61% of the time. Where is the scaled target after a close above the rim?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":106.05,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Depth = 103.00 − 98.00 = 5.00. Scaled: 5.00 × 0.61 = 3.05, added to the rim: 103.00 + 3.05 = 106.05.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.9\",\"concepts\":[\"rounding-and-v-patterns\",\"pattern-target\"],\"hints\":[\"Depth: rim minus bottom. Scale it, then add it to the rim.\",\"5.00 × 0.61 = 3.05.\",\"103.00 + 3.05.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"rim\":[100,108,0.1],\"d\":[3,8,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(rim + d * 0.61, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A cup with handle has its rim at {rim:2} and its bottom at {rim - d:2}. Cups reached the full target 61% of the time. Where is the scaled target after a close above the rim?\",\"explanation\":\"Depth = {d:2}. Scaled: {d:2} × 0.61 = {d * 0.61:2}, added to the rim: {rim:2} + {d * 0.61:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{rim:2} + {d:2} × 0.61.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.9-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Two Library cards. Pattern X: rank 1 of 39, 3% failures, from 38 cases, its angles drawn by eye. Pattern Y: rank 7 of 39, 4% failures, from 900 cases, with a fixed drawing rule.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you weigh the two cards?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Trust X more: it ranks first\",\"rationale\":\"With 38 cases, a few lucky trades can lift a pattern to the top. A small sample makes the rank unstable.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Treat them as equal: both rank in the top ten\",\"rationale\":\"Ranks hide how much evidence stands behind them. 900 cases with a fixed rule beat 38 drawn by eye.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trust Y more: many more cases and a rule anyone can repeat\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Ignore both: rare patterns are random\",\"rationale\":\"Rare isn't random. Read the sample size and the drawing rule, and weigh the evidence accordingly.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A rank from 38 cases moves a lot with a few trades, and a pattern drawn by eye may not be the same pattern from one analyst to the next. Y's 900 cases and fixed rule make its numbers far more stable. Both still come from daily stock charts.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.9\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"How much could a few lucky trades move a rank built on 38 cases?\",\"Also ask whether two analysts would find the same cases.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.10-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A scanner finds head-and-shoulders tops in 10,000 random walks. What does the lab show about the moves that follow them?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Prices fall, as the textbook says\",\"rationale\":\"In a random walk each step ignores the past, so no shape can tilt the next move.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No patterns are found in random data\",\"rationale\":\"Random walks contain every shape. The scanner finds many.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Prices rise, because most patterns fail\",\"rationale\":\"Failure isn't built in either. Up and down stay equally likely after any shape.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They match what follows any candle: the base rate\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Random walks are full of patterns, but nothing in them predicts the next step, so what follows a pattern matches the unconditional distribution. A pattern earns attention only if, on real data and after costs, what follows it beats that base rate.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.10\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\",\"random-walk\"],\"hints\":[\"What does each step of a random walk depend on?\",\"Compare the moves after a pattern with the moves after any candle.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.10-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You bought an ascending triangle's breakout at 104.35 with a stop at 103.30. The next candle closed back inside at 103.85 and you exited, as planned. Two candles later price closes under the triangle's lower line: the pattern is busted.\",\"prompt\":\"What does a good plan say now?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy again: it's cheaper than your entry\",\"rationale\":\"That's buying into an idea the market has now rejected twice, with no plan behind it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Short twice your usual size to win back the loss\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing up to recover a loss is revenge trading. A new trade gets normal size and its own stop.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Stand aside, or trade the bust only with a new plan: its own entry, stop and size\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Re-enter the long with the old stop at 103.30\",\"rationale\":\"The old stop belonged to a breakout that failed. Reusing it means holding an idea that's already wrong.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A bust is a new situation, not a continuation of the old trade. Written in advance, the plan says: exit on a close back inside, then stand aside or trade the bust as a fresh setup with normal size and its own stop. Never add to recover.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.10\",\"concepts\":[\"busted-patterns\",\"scenario-planning\"],\"hints\":[\"The old trade ended at the failure. Treat what comes next as a new decision.\",\"Any new trade needs its own entry, stop and size.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.2.10-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Bulkowski's share of breakouts that failed to gain 10% roughly doubled from the 1990s to 2003–2007. What does that mean for a pattern statistic published today?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It describes its sample's period; expect it to differ, often for the worse, in another era\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's wrong and should be ignored\",\"rationale\":\"It was right for its sample. The lesson is scope: read it as that period's average, not as a law.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It holds in any market, since patterns reflect human nature\",\"rationale\":\"The doubling itself shows the rates move with the era and the market.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's more reliable now that more traders use patterns\",\"rationale\":\"Wider use tends to shrink edges: 97 published return predictors earned 58% less after publication.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Failure rates rose from 14% to 28% upward and from 26% to 49% downward between the two periods. Any published rate is that sample's average: market, years, timeframe. Edges also shrink once known, so expect less in your own tests.\",\"lesson\":\"2.2.10\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what period and market the statistic came from.\",\"If the rate doubled once, could it change again?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In Dow Theory, what is a secondary move?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A second average confirming the first\",\"rationale\":\"That's the confirmation tenet: two averages agreeing. A secondary move is a move against the main trend.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The day-to-day wiggles inside any trend\",\"rationale\":\"Those are minor moves. Secondary moves are bigger: corrections lasting weeks to months.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A correction against the primary trend, lasting weeks to months\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The last phase of a trend, when everyone piles in\",\"rationale\":\"That's a phase of the primary trend, its excess. Secondary moves are corrections against the trend.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Dow saw three degrees of trend at once: the primary trend over months to years, secondary moves against it over weeks to months, and minor day-to-day moves. A secondary move is a correction, not a reversal.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"dow-theory\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"In order, LABX makes a swing low at 100.20, a high at 101.50, a low at 100.90, a high at 102.30, a low at 101.40 and a lower high at 101.90. Price then falls to 101.10.\",\"prompt\":\"By the peak-trough rule, when did the uptrend end?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"At the 101.90 high: a lower high ends it\",\"rationale\":\"A lower high is a warning, not the end. The rule needs a low that breaks the previous swing low.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It hasn't: price is still above the earlier low, 100.90\",\"rationale\":\"The rule uses the most recent swing low, 101.40, not an earlier one.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only once a moving average turns down\",\"rationale\":\"That's another definition of trend, the slope of an average. The peak-trough rule uses swing lows.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"When price fell below 101.40, the last swing low\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The peak-trough rule ends an uptrend when a low breaks the previous swing low. The lower high at 101.90 was a warning; the fall below 101.40, on the way to 101.10, ended the trend.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"dow-theory\",\"trend\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the most recent swing low before the fall.\",\"An uptrend ends when price breaks the last swing low, not on a lower high.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On your chart, the peak-trough rule says the uptrend has ended, but the 50-period SMA is still rising. What should you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Use whichever definition agrees with the trade you want\",\"rationale\":\"Choosing the definition after seeing the chart lets you justify any trade. That's confirmation bias with extra steps.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Follow the definition you chose before you looked at the chart\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Wait until all four definitions agree\",\"rationale\":\"They rarely agree, and you'd still be choosing after looking. Fix one rule in advance.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Trust the SMA, because averages are smoother\",\"rationale\":\"Smoother means slower, not more correct. Each definition measures something different.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Definitions of trend measure different things and often disagree on the same candle. Pick one in advance, write it into your plan and keep it; switching after the fact turns the definition into a bias.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"trend-definitions\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask when the choice of definition should be made.\",\"A rule chosen after seeing the chart can justify anything.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Yesterday's 9-period EMA on LABX was 101.50. Today LABX closes at 103.00. What is today's EMA, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101.8,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"k = 2 ÷ (9 + 1) = 0.2. New EMA = 101.50 + 0.2 × (103.00 − 101.50) = 101.50 + 0.30 = 101.80. The EMA moves only part of the way toward the new close.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"weighted-moving-averages\"],\"hints\":[\"Find k first: k = 2 ÷ (n + 1).\",\"New EMA = old EMA + k × (close − old EMA).\",\"k = 0.2, so 101.50 + 0.2 × 1.50.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[4,19,5],\"prev\":[99,103,0.1],\"close\":[97,106,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(close - prev) >= 0.5\"],\"answer\":\"round(prev + 2 / (n + 1) * (close - prev), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Yesterday's {n}-period EMA on LABX was {prev:2}. Today LABX closes at {close:2}. What is today's EMA, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"k = 2 ÷ ({n} + 1) = {2 / (n + 1):3}. New EMA = {prev:2} + {2 / (n + 1):3} × ({close:2} − {prev:2}) = {answer:2}. The EMA moves only part of the way toward the new close.\",\"hint\":\"k = {2 / (n + 1):3}, so {prev:2} + {2 / (n + 1):3} × ({close:2} − {prev:2}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The last three LABX closes, oldest first, are 100.00, 101.00 and 104.00. What is their 3-period WMA, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":102.33,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Weights 1, 2 and 3, the newest heaviest: 100.00 × 1 + 101.00 × 2 + 104.00 × 3 = 614.00. The weights add to 6, so WMA = 614.00 ÷ 6 = 102.33, above the SMA of 101.67.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"weighted-moving-averages\"],\"hints\":[\"Give the newest close the biggest weight: 1, 2, 3.\",\"Multiply, add, then divide by the sum of the weights, 6.\",\"100.00 + 202.00 + 312.00 = 614.00, then ÷ 6.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"c1\":[98,102,0.5],\"c2\":[98,104,0.5],\"c3\":[98,106,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"c3 != c1\"],\"answer\":\"round((c1 + 2 * c2 + 3 * c3) / 6, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"The last three LABX closes, oldest first, are {c1:2}, {c2:2} and {c3:2}. What is their 3-period WMA, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Weights 1, 2 and 3, the newest heaviest: {c1:2} × 1 + {c2:2} × 2 + {c3:2} × 3 = {c1 + 2 * c2 + 3 * c3:2}. The weights add to 6, so WMA = {answer:2}. The SMA would be {(c1 + c2 + c3) / 3:2}.\",\"hint\":\"({c1:2} + {2 * c2:2} + {3 * c3:2}) ÷ 6.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On a ranging chart, you replace a 20-period EMA with a 10-period EMA. What should you expect?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Fewer false signals, because a shorter EMA filters noise better\",\"rationale\":\"A shorter period smooths less, so it follows more of the noise and crosses price more often.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The same signals, only earlier\",\"rationale\":\"A faster line doesn't just shift in time. It also follows wiggles the slower one ignored, so it adds signals.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It stops lagging\",\"rationale\":\"Every average is built from past closes, so every average lags. A shorter one lags less, not zero.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It turns sooner, and price crosses it more often\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"You can't have less lag and less noise at once. A 10-period EMA reacts sooner than a 20-period one, and in a range that means more crossings that soon reverse: more whipsaws, each paying costs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"indicator-literacy\",\"weighted-moving-averages\"],\"hints\":[\"Think of the trade-off between lag and noise.\",\"A shorter period reacts sooner. What else does it react to?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is a golden cross?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price crossing above any moving average\",\"rationale\":\"That's a price cross. A golden cross is a specific double cross: SMA 50 over SMA 200.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The 50-period average crossing above the 200-period average\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The 50-period average crossing below the 200-period average\",\"rationale\":\"That's the death cross, the bearish mirror.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"MACD crossing above its signal line\",\"rationale\":\"That's a MACD signal cross. The golden cross uses two price averages, 50 and 200.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A golden cross is SMA 50 crossing above SMA 200: the sign of SMA 50 − SMA 200 turns positive. Like every crossover, it arrives after the move it reports has started.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"ma-crossover\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A crossover rule averages +0.12R per trade before costs. A round trip costs 0.20 per unit, and your stop is 1.00 away. What does it average after costs, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.08,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Costs in R = 0.20 ÷ 1.00 = 0.20R per trade. After costs: 0.12R − 0.20R = −0.08R. The rule wins before costs and loses after them.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"ma-crossover\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Turn the cost into R first: cost ÷ stop distance.\",\"Then subtract it from the result before costs.\",\"0.12 − 0.20 ÷ 1.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"gross\":[0.05,0.4,0.01],\"cost\":[0.1,0.3,0.02],\"stop\":[0.5,2,0.25]},\"answer\":\"round(gross - cost / stop, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A crossover rule averages +{gross:2}R per trade before costs. A round trip costs {cost:2} per unit, and your stop is {stop:2} away. What does it average after costs, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Costs in R = {cost:2} ÷ {stop:2} = {cost / stop:2}R per trade. After costs: {gross:2}R − {cost / stop:2}R = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{gross:2} − {cost:2} ÷ {stop:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX has traded between 98.30 and 101.70 for three days. Your SMA 10/30 crossover rule has given 9 signals in that time.\",\"prompt\":\"Why is this rule likely to lose money here?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Crossover rules only work on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"Timeframe isn't the problem. A range on any timeframe makes the averages cross back and forth.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nine signals is too few to judge\",\"rationale\":\"Sample size isn't the issue. In a range, crossings reverse before any move develops, and each one pays costs.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The averages keep crossing as price swings inside the range, so signals reverse soon and each one pays costs\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The averages are too long\",\"rationale\":\"Shorter averages would cross even more often in a range, adding whipsaws and costs.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A range has no lasting direction, so the fast and slow averages keep meeting. Signals reverse within a few candles, and every one pays spread and commission: about 0.20R each in LABX terms.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"ma-crossover\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"What do two averages do when price goes sideways?\",\"Count what each signal costs.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"EMA 12 is 101.30 and EMA 26 is 100.85. The signal line was 0.30 one candle ago. Signal = previous + 0.2 × (MACD − previous). What is the histogram?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.12,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"MACD = 101.30 − 100.85 = 0.45. Signal = 0.30 + 0.2 × (0.45 − 0.30) = 0.33. Histogram = 0.45 − 0.33 = 0.12.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"macd\"],\"hints\":[\"Three steps: MACD, then the new signal, then the histogram.\",\"MACD = EMA 12 − EMA 26 = 0.45.\",\"Signal = 0.30 + 0.2 × 0.15 = 0.33; histogram = MACD − signal.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e26\":[98,104,0.05],\"m\":[0.1,1,0.05],\"prev\":[0,0.8,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(m - prev) >= 0.1\"],\"answer\":\"round(0.8 * (m - prev), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"EMA 12 is {e26 + m:2} and EMA 26 is {e26:2}. The signal line was {prev:2} one candle ago. Signal = previous + 0.2 × (MACD − previous). What is the histogram?\",\"explanation\":\"MACD = {e26 + m:2} − {e26:2} = {m:2}. Signal = {prev:2} + 0.2 × ({m:2} − {prev:2}) = {prev + 0.2 * (m - prev):2}. Histogram = {m:2} − {prev + 0.2 * (m - prev):2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"MACD = {m:2}; signal = {prev:2} + 0.2 × ({m:2} − {prev:2}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"MACD crosses above zero on candle 20. What happened on the price chart at the same candle?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"EMA 12 crossed above EMA 26\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Price crossed above EMA 26\",\"rationale\":\"MACD compares two averages, not price with one average. Price may have crossed EMA 26 earlier.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"MACD crossed its signal line\",\"rationale\":\"That's a different event, the signal cross. The zero line is where EMA 12 and EMA 26 meet.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing in particular: MACD is a separate indicator\",\"rationale\":\"MACD is EMA 12 − EMA 26, so its zero cross is exactly the 12/26 EMA cross.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"MACD = EMA 12 − EMA 26. It's positive exactly when EMA 12 is above EMA 26, so a zero-line cross and a 12/26 EMA crossover are the same event. Using both counts one signal twice.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"macd\",\"ma-crossover\"],\"hints\":[\"Write MACD as a subtraction.\",\"When is EMA 12 − EMA 26 equal to zero?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why doesn't MACD have fixed overbought and oversold levels, like 70 and 30?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because its signal line already marks the extremes\",\"rationale\":\"The signal line is an average of MACD; it marks crossings, not extremes. MACD itself is unbounded and in price units.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because it only works on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"MACD can be computed on any timeframe. The issue is that its values scale with price and have no bounds.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because 0.50 is its fixed overbought level\",\"rationale\":\"A MACD of 0.50 is large at a price of 10 and tiny at 1,000. No fixed level fits every price.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because it's measured in price units and has no upper or lower bound\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"MACD is EMA 12 − EMA 26 in price units, with no bounds: 0.70 at a slow EMA of 103.50 is the same momentum as 0.07 at 10.35. PPO divides by EMA 26 to compare, and even then 'overbought' MACD readings have no robust evidence (D).\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"macd\"],\"hints\":[\"What units is MACD in?\",\"Compare a MACD of 0.70 at 103.50 with 0.07 at 10.35.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The previous candle's high was 102.00 and its low 101.00. The current candle's high is 102.70 and its low 101.40. What is +DM?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.7,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Up move = 102.70 − 102.00 = 0.70. Down move = 101.00 − 101.40 = −0.40, which isn't positive. The up move is positive and larger, so +DM = 0.70 and −DM = 0.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"adx-dmi\"],\"hints\":[\"Up move = high − previous high; down move = previous low − low.\",\"+DM counts only if the up move is positive and bigger than the down move.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"ph\":[100,104,0.05],\"rng\":[0.6,1.4,0.05],\"up\":[-0.3,0.9,0.05],\"dn\":[-0.4,0.8,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"up != dn\",\"up + dn + rng > 0.2\"],\"answer\":\"(up > dn && up > 0) * max(up, 0)\",\"prompt\":\"The previous candle's high was {ph:2} and its low {ph - rng:2}. The current candle's high is {ph + up:2} and its low {ph - rng - dn:2}. What is +DM?\",\"explanation\":\"Up move = {ph + up:2} − {ph:2} = {up:2}. Down move = {ph - rng:2} − {ph - rng - dn:2} = {dn:2}. +DM is the up move only if it is positive and larger than the down move, so +DM = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Up move {up:2}, down move {dn:2}: does the up move count?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"+DI is 30 and −DI is 20. What is DX?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":20,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"DX = 100 × |30 − 20| ÷ (30 + 20) = 100 × 10 ÷ 50 = 20. It measures how far apart the two DIs are, not which one is on top.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"adx-dmi\"],\"hints\":[\"DX = 100 × |+DI − −DI| ÷ (+DI + −DI).\",\"The difference is 10 and the sum is 50.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[5,45,1],\"m\":[5,45,1]},\"constraints\":[\"p != m\"],\"answer\":\"round(100 * abs(p - m) / (p + m), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"+DI is {p} and −DI is {m}. What is DX?\",\"explanation\":\"DX = 100 × |{p} − {m}| ÷ ({p} + {m}) = 100 × {abs(p - m)} ÷ {p + m} = {answer:1}. It measures how far apart the two DIs are, not which one is on top.\",\"hint\":\"The difference is {abs(p - m)} and the sum is {p + m}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"During a strong rally, ADX falls from 50 to 40. What does that tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The rally is over\",\"rationale\":\"ADX falling from a high level only says the trend is less one-sided than it was. Price can keep rising.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Trend strength has eased; ADX says nothing about direction or the end of the trend\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A downtrend has started\",\"rationale\":\"ADX has no direction. Direction comes from price and the DI lines.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy more: ADX above 25 confirms the rally\",\"rationale\":\"ADX above 25 says the trend is strong in either direction. It isn't a buy signal, and it is late by construction.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"ADX is smoothed DX: strength without direction. A fall from 50 to 40 says the trend is less one-sided than it was, and still strong by Wilder's 25 convention. It doesn't say the trend is over, and it never says which way.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"adx-dmi\"],\"hints\":[\"What does ADX measure, and what doesn't it?\",\"Where does direction come from in Wilder's system?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A rising Parabolic SAR is at 98.50. The extreme point is 100.50 and the acceleration factor is 0.06. What is the next SAR?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":98.62,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Next SAR = 98.50 + 0.06 × (100.50 − 98.50) = 98.50 + 0.12 = 98.62, as long as it stays below the last two lows.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-and-reverse-indicators\"],\"hints\":[\"Next SAR = SAR + AF × (EP − SAR).\",\"The gap is 2.00; AF takes 6% of it.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"sar\":[97,100,0.05],\"gap\":[1,3,0.5],\"af\":[0.02,0.2,0.02]},\"answer\":\"round(sar + af * gap, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A rising Parabolic SAR is at {sar:2}. The extreme point is {sar + gap:2} and the acceleration factor is {af:2}. What is the next SAR?\",\"explanation\":\"Next SAR = {sar:2} + {af:2} × ({sar + gap:2} − {sar:2}) = {sar:2} + {af * gap:2} = {answer:2}, as long as it stays below the last two lows.\",\"hint\":\"{sar:2} + {af:2} × {gap:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A candle has a high of 102.40 and a low of 101.60. ATR is 0.50 and the SuperTrend multiplier is 2. Where is the basic lower band?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Midpoint = (102.40 + 101.60) ÷ 2 = 102.00. The band sits 2 × 0.50 = 1.00 away. Lower band = 102.00 − 1.00 = 101.00.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-and-reverse-indicators\"],\"hints\":[\"Start from the candle's midpoint, (high + low) ÷ 2.\",\"Subtract multiplier × ATR.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"h\":[100,106,0.1],\"rng\":[0.4,1.6,0.1],\"atr\":[0.2,0.8,0.05],\"m\":[1,4,1]},\"answer\":\"round(h - rng / 2 - m * atr, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A candle has a high of {h:2} and a low of {h - rng:2}. ATR is {atr:2} and the SuperTrend multiplier is {m}. Where is the basic lower band?\",\"explanation\":\"Midpoint = ({h:2} + {h - rng:2}) ÷ 2 = {h - rng / 2:2}. The band sits {m} × {atr:2} = {m * atr:2} away. Lower band = {h - rng / 2:2} − {m * atr:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"({h:2} + {h - rng:2}) ÷ 2, minus {m} × {atr:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You run the Parabolic SAR as a system: always in, long when price is above the line, short when it's below. LABX then trades sideways between 104.00 and 110.00 for 50 candles.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely result over those 50 candles?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"One long trade held through the range\",\"rationale\":\"The SAR keeps moving toward price and flips when price crosses it. In a range that happens again and again.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Many flips, mostly small losses, each paying spread and commission\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No trades: the SAR switches off in a range\",\"rationale\":\"An always-in system is never flat. It's long or short on every candle, so it trades every swing.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Steady profits, since the SAR speeds up with each swing\",\"rationale\":\"Acceleration helps only when a move continues. In a range the moves reverse, so the flips tend to buy high and sell low.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"An always-in line flips whenever price crosses it. In a range each swing crosses it, so the system keeps reversing near the edges, and every flip pays costs. In this lesson's range, the SAR flipped 6 times in 50 candles.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-and-reverse-indicators\",\"range\"],\"hints\":[\"What does an always-in system do when price crosses the line?\",\"How often does price cross a trailing line in a range?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over the last 26 candles, LABX's highest high is 104.60 and its lowest low is 99.80. What is the Kijun line?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":102.2,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Kijun = (104.60 + 99.80) ÷ 2 = 204.40 ÷ 2 = 102.20: the midpoint of the 26-candle range, the same as a Donchian 26 midpoint.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"ichimoku-basics\"],\"hints\":[\"Kijun is a midpoint, not an average of closes.\",\"(highest high + lowest low) ÷ 2.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[96,102,0.05],\"rng\":[1,8,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(lo + rng / 2, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Over the last 26 candles, LABX's highest high is {lo + rng:2} and its lowest low is {lo:2}. What is the Kijun line?\",\"explanation\":\"Kijun = ({lo + rng:2} + {lo:2}) ÷ 2 = {answer:2}: the midpoint of the 26-candle range, the same as a Donchian 26 midpoint.\",\"hint\":\"({lo + rng:2} + {lo:2}) ÷ 2.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Price is above the cloud, Tenkan is above Kijun, and Chikou is above price. How many independent confirmations is that?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three, one per condition\",\"rationale\":\"All three come from the same highs, lows and closes, over overlapping windows. They tend to agree because they're built alike.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Five, counting both cloud lines\",\"rationale\":\"Senkou A is itself the average of Tenkan and Kijun. Adding lines adds no new information.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Fewer than three: they're built from the same highs, lows and closes\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"None: Ichimoku can't be read at all\",\"rationale\":\"It can be read; it just isn't several separate views. Treat it as one channel-and-breakout picture.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Tenkan, Kijun and Senkou B are midpoints of highs and lows, Senkou A averages two of them, and Chikou is the close. When price rises, they tend to line up together, so their agreement is closer to one piece of information than three.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"ichimoku-basics\",\"indicator-literacy\"],\"hints\":[\"What is each line calculated from?\",\"Lines built from the same data tend to agree.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.3.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A backtest of an Ichimoku rule reports a 70% win rate. The rule buys at the close of candle t when the Chikou value plotted at t is above the cloud plotted at t.\",\"prompt\":\"What is wrong with this backtest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"70% is too high a win rate to be real\",\"rationale\":\"A high win rate is a reason to check, not a diagnosis. The flaw here is specific: the rule reads a future close.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The cloud is shifted ahead, so it uses future data\",\"rationale\":\"The cloud at t was calculated from candles up to t − 26. Shifting it forward uses old data, not future data.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Ichimoku needs daily candles\",\"rationale\":\"Its settings come from daily charts, but the flaw is timing: the Chikou value at t is a close from 26 candles later.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The Chikou value plotted at t is the close of t + 26, so the test trades on a future price\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Chikou plots each close 26 candles back, so the value at t is the close of t + 26. A rule that reads it at t knows the future and looks far better than any real trading could. Backtests must use values as they stood at the decision.\",\"lesson\":\"2.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"look-ahead-bias\",\"ichimoku-basics\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what the Chikou line at candle t is made of.\",\"Which candle's close does it show?\",\"A backtest may use only values that existed at candle t.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX closes at 103.20. Twelve candles ago it closed at 100.80. What is ROC(12), in percent?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2.38,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"ROC = (103.20 ÷ 100.80 − 1) × 100 = 2.38%. Momentum would be 2.40 vUSD; ROC turns that move into a share of the old price.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"momentum-roc\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide today's close by the close 12 candles ago.\",\"103.20 ÷ 100.80 = 1.0238. Subtract 1 and multiply by 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"now\":[95,110,0.05],\"then\":[95,110,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(now - then) >= 0.5\"],\"answer\":\"round((now / then - 1) * 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABX closes at {now:2}. Twelve candles ago it closed at {then:2}. What is ROC(12), in percent?\",\"explanation\":\"ROC = ({now:2} ÷ {then:2} − 1) × 100 = {answer:2}%. Momentum would be {now - then:2} vUSD; ROC turns that move into a share of the old price.\",\"hint\":\"Divide {now:2} by {then:2}, subtract 1 and multiply by 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does ROC have no standard overbought line, the way RSI has 70?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"ROC is less accurate than RSI\",\"rationale\":\"Accuracy isn't the issue. Both are exact formulas; they differ in their scale.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"ROC is unbounded: how high it goes depends on the market's volatility\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"ROC only works on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"ROC works on any timeframe. The missing line comes from its open-ended scale.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nobody has tested ROC yet\",\"rationale\":\"Momentum is among the most tested ideas in finance. The lack of a fixed line is about its scale.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"ROC is a percent change with no ceiling. +6% is extreme on a calm chart and routine on a volatile one, so no single line can mean 'too high'. Bounded oscillators like RSI rescale to 0–100, which is what makes a fixed 70 possible.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"oscillator\",\"momentum-roc\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the scales: RSI runs 0–100. What range does ROC have?\",\"A fixed line needs a fixed scale.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A friend cites a study showing that assets which rose over the past 12 months tended to keep rising. He plans to buy LABX whenever its ROC over the last 10 one-hour candles turns positive.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with his reasoning?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The study is about months; a 10-hour ROC rule is a different, untested claim\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the study proves momentum works on any timeframe\",\"rationale\":\"The study covers 3–12-month horizons. It says nothing about 10-hour windows.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Momentum has been disproven, so the study is wrong\",\"rationale\":\"Time-series momentum over 3–12 months is among the best-supported findings. The problem is the horizon, not the study.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"He should use 20 candles instead of 10\",\"rationale\":\"Changing the lookback doesn't bridge the gap from months to hours. Test the rule he actually plans to trade, after costs.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Evidence holds at the horizon it was measured on. The robust momentum findings cover 3–12 months; short intraday ROC rules are grade D, and on hourly candles costs weigh heavily. He'd need to test his own rule, after costs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"momentum-roc\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the study's horizon with his rule's horizon.\",\"12 months versus 10 hours: does evidence at one horizon cover the other?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over 14 candles the average gain is 0.60 and the average loss is 0.20. What is RSI(14)?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"RS = 0.60 ÷ 0.20 = 3. RSI = 100 − 100 ÷ (1 + 3) = 100 − 25 = 75. Gains three times the size of losses put RSI at 75.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"rsi\"],\"hints\":[\"RS is the average gain divided by the average loss.\",\"RS = 0.60 ÷ 0.20 = 3. Then RSI = 100 − 100 ÷ (1 + RS).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"g\":[0.2,0.9,0.05],\"l\":[0.1,0.6,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(100 - 100 / (1 + g / l), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Over 14 candles the average gain is {g:2} and the average loss is {l:2}. What is RSI(14)?\",\"explanation\":\"RS = {g:2} ÷ {l:2} = {g / l:2}. RSI = 100 − 100 ÷ (1 + RS) = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"RS = {g:2} ÷ {l:2}. Then RSI = 100 − 100 ÷ (1 + RS).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In a steady uptrend, RSI(14) keeps dipping to about 45 and rising to about 85. What does Brown's range idea say about the dips?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"They are oversold readings: buy each dip on RSI alone\",\"rationale\":\"Oversold usually means 30. Brown's point is that in uptrends RSI's floor rises, so 45 is ordinary.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They are normal pullback readings: in bull markets RSI tends to swing between about 40 and 90\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The trend is over once RSI falls below 50\",\"rationale\":\"Dips toward 40–50 are routine in Brown's bull range. A trend ends with price structure, not one RSI reading.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"RSI is miscalculated: in an uptrend it should stay above 70\",\"rationale\":\"RSI swings in trends too. Long stretches above 70 happen in strong legs, not throughout.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Brown observed RSI ranges shifting with the trend: about 40–90 in bull markets and 10–60 in bear markets, with 50 between them. In an uptrend, a dip to 45 is a pullback reading. These are observations, not tested rules.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"rsi\"],\"hints\":[\"Brown's ranges move with the trend.\",\"In bull markets RSI tends to stay above about 40.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX keeps making higher highs and higher lows. RSI(14) has been above 70 for the last 20 candles, and price is still climbing.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the RSI reading tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price must fall soon: 20 candles overbought is too long\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing forces RSI back. In a strong trend, long stretches above 70 are common.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The RSI is miscalculated: it can't stay above 70 that long\",\"rationale\":\"It can. Steady gains with small losses keep RS high, so RSI stays high.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sell half now and the rest when RSI drops below 70\",\"rationale\":\"That's a fade dressed as risk management. The reading describes the trend; it doesn't forecast a reversal.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Upward momentum is strong; on its own it's no reason to sell\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"RSI above 70 means recent gains have outweighed recent losses: momentum. In a trend that can last many candles. It describes strength; it doesn't time a top.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"rsi\",\"overbought-oversold\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what RSI measures: recent gains against recent losses.\",\"In a trend, gains keep outweighing losses, so RSI stays high.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over the last 14 candles the highest high is 102.40 and the lowest low is 100.40. LABX closes at 101.00. What is stochastic %K?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":30,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"%K = (C − LL) ÷ (HH − LL) × 100 = (101.00 − 100.40) ÷ 2.00 × 100 = 30. The close sits 30% of the way up the 14-candle range.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stochastic-family\"],\"hints\":[\"%K measures where the close sits between the lowest low and the highest high.\",\"(101.00 − 100.40) ÷ (102.40 − 100.40), times 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"ll\":[98,101,0.05],\"rng\":[1,4,0.1],\"pos\":[5,95,5]},\"answer\":\"round((round(ll + rng * pos / 100, 2) - ll) / rng * 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Over the last 14 candles the highest high is {ll + rng:2} and the lowest low is {ll:2}. LABX closes at {round(ll + rng * pos / 100, 2):2}. What is stochastic %K?\",\"explanation\":\"%K = (C − LL) ÷ (HH − LL) × 100 = ({round(ll + rng * pos / 100, 2):2} − {ll:2}) ÷ {rng:2} × 100 = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"({round(ll + rng * pos / 100, 2):2} − {ll:2}) ÷ {rng:2}, times 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Stochastic %K(14) reads 82. What does Williams %R(14) read on the same candles?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-18,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"%R = %K − 100 = 82 − 100 = −18. Both place the close in the same 14-candle range; %R measures down from the top.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stochastic-family\"],\"hints\":[\"%R and %K use the same high, low and close.\",\"%R = %K − 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"kk\":[5,95,1]},\"answer\":\"kk - 100\",\"prompt\":\"Stochastic %K(14) reads {kk}. What does Williams %R(14) read on the same candles?\",\"explanation\":\"%R = %K − 100 = {kk} − 100 = {answer}. Both place the close in the same 14-candle range; %R measures down from the top.\",\"hint\":\"Subtract 100 from %K: {kk} − 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"RSI is above 70, the stochastic above 80 and Williams %R above −20. A friend calls it 'triple confirmation'. What's wrong with that?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: three agreeing indicators make a reversal three times likelier\",\"rationale\":\"Agreement adds evidence only if the indicators could disagree. These share inputs and rarely do.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Williams %R would have to be below −80 to confirm\",\"rationale\":\"Above −20 is %R's overbought zone, matching %K above 80. The thresholds agree; that's the point.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"They are built from the same recent prices, so their agreement is close to one signal\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Three indicators are too few; use five\",\"rationale\":\"More collinear oscillators add more copies of the same signal, not more evidence.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"RSI, the stochastic and %R rescale the same highs, lows and closes. %R is %K minus 100, and on the lesson's chart RSI and %K correlated at 0.88. A real second opinion measures something different, such as volume or volatility.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"indicator-redundancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what each indicator is calculated from.\",\"If two readings can't disagree, the second adds no evidence.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does an 'overbought' RSI reading actually tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price must fall soon\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing forces a reversal. In trends RSI can stay above 70 for long stretches while price climbs.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buyers have run out of money\",\"rationale\":\"RSI only sees closes. It knows nothing about who is buying or how much money is left.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Price has risen a lot compared with its recent moves\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The asset is expensive compared with its value\",\"rationale\":\"RSI has no notion of value. It compares recent gains with recent losses, nothing more.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Overbought means gains have outweighed losses over the lookback: a description of the recent move. Whether it continues or reverses depends on the regime and the horizon.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"overbought-oversold\",\"rsi\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX has made three higher highs and three higher lows in two days. RSI(14) reads 78.\",\"prompt\":\"Which response fits the regime?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Short now: 78 is well above 70\",\"rationale\":\"That's the fade that lost 0.42R a trade in the lab's trend market. In a trend, 78 is a sign of strength.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Treat it as strength: look for trend entries such as a pullback, or do nothing\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Short half now and half at 85\",\"rationale\":\"Scaling into a fade adds to a losing idea in a trend. The regime, not the level, is the problem.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy at once: RSI above 70 is a buy signal\",\"rationale\":\"It's no signal either way. Strength favours trend trades, but an entry still needs a plan and a stop.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Higher highs and higher lows say trend. In a trend an RSI extreme is what strength looks like, and fading it lost in the lab. Trade with the trend on your own plan, or stand aside.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"overbought-oversold\",\"trend\"],\"hints\":[\"Identify the regime from price structure first.\",\"In the lab's trend market, fading RSI at 70 won 24.7% of the time.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Where do RSI's 70 and 30 lines come from?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Studies proving that prices reverse at 70 and 30\",\"rationale\":\"No robust study finds that. The lines came first; they are settings.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The statistical 95% range of RSI\",\"rationale\":\"RSI's spread depends on the market and the period. 70 and 30 aren't derived from it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exchange rules for overbought markets\",\"rationale\":\"No exchange defines overbought. It's an indicator setting.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Wilder's choice: a convention, like 80/20\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Wilder chose 70 and 30; traders also use 80/20, or 90/10 for RSI(2). All are conventions. None was derived from evidence that prices turn there.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"overbought-oversold\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In a downtrend, LABX makes a high at 99.10, then a lower high at 98.40. RSI reads 52 at the first high and 58 at the second. What kind of divergence is it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Regular bearish\",\"rationale\":\"Regular bearish needs a higher high in price. Here price made a lower high.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Regular bullish\",\"rationale\":\"Regular bullish compares lows: a lower price low with a higher RSI low.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Hidden bullish\",\"rationale\":\"Hidden bullish happens at the lows of an uptrend: a higher price low with a lower RSI low.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Hidden bearish\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A lower high in price with a higher high in RSI, inside a downtrend, is hidden bearish, read as continuation of the downtrend. Like every divergence, its evidence grade is D.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"divergence\"],\"hints\":[\"Check whether the swings are highs or lows, and which way the trend runs.\",\"Price: lower high. RSI: higher high. In a downtrend that's the continuation kind.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You spot a regular bearish RSI divergence in an uptrend. What should come before you act on it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the divergence is the signal\",\"rationale\":\"Strong trends can print several divergences before the top. Acting on each one means fighting the trend again and again.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Confirmation: price breaks its structure, such as the last pullback low, or RSI crosses below 50\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A second oscillator showing the same divergence\",\"rationale\":\"A collinear oscillator will usually agree. That's the same evidence twice, not confirmation.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"RSI falling to 30\",\"rationale\":\"Waiting for 30 means waiting for most of the fall. Structure or the 50 line come much earlier.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A divergence says momentum slowed. Confirmation, a break of price structure or RSI under 50, shows the trend actually changed. It costs part of the move: 1.49 below the top in the lesson.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"divergence\",\"structure-break\"],\"hints\":[\"A divergence is a warning, not a trigger.\",\"Look for evidence in price itself, or RSI crossing its centreline.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABX has trended up for a week. You count a third bearish RSI divergence in a row; each of the first two was followed by new highs.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the lab tally suggest about this third divergence?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three in a row make the top certain\",\"rationale\":\"In the lab, some uptrends showed four before the top. A count doesn't time the turn.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The first two failed, so ignore RSI from now on\",\"rationale\":\"The reading still says momentum is slowing. It just isn't a timing signal on its own.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Several are common in strong uptrends; wait for price to confirm before acting\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Short now with a larger size to make up for the first two\",\"rationale\":\"That doubles down on an unconfirmed signal with weak evidence. Size never fixes a timing problem.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"In 20 simulated uptrends, 13 showed three or more bearish divergences before the top, and some showed four. Several in a row are normal in strong trends; confirmation from price matters more than the count.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"divergence\"],\"hints\":[\"Recall how many divergences the lab's uptrends showed before their tops.\",\"13 of 20 simulated uptrends showed three or more before the real top.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The previous candle closed at 102.40. The next one gaps down and trades between a high of 101.90 and a low of 101.30. What is its true range, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.1,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"High − low is 0.60, but the previous close sits above the whole candle. Previous close − low = 102.40 − 101.30 = 1.10. True range is the largest of the three distances: 1.10.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"True range counts the gap from the previous close.\",\"Compare high − low with previous close − low: 102.40 − 101.30.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"pc\":[98,104,0.01],\"g\":[0.2,1.5,0.01],\"r\":[0.2,1.2,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(g + r, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"The previous candle closed at {pc:2}. The next one gaps down and trades between a high of {pc - g:2} and a low of {pc - g - r:2}. What is its true range, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"High − low = {r:2}; previous close − low = {pc:2} − {pc - g - r:2} = {answer:2}. True range takes the largest distance, {answer:2}: the gap counts.\",\"hint\":\"Compare high − low with previous close − low: {pc:2} − {pc - g - r:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"ATR(14) is 0.50. The next candle's true range is 0.92. What is the new ATR, Wilder's way?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.53,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"(0.50 × 13 + 0.92) ÷ 14 = 7.42 ÷ 14 = 0.53. A candle almost twice the average lifts ATR by only 0.03.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"Wilder's smoothing keeps 13/14 of the old ATR.\",\"(0.50 × 13 + 0.92) ÷ 14.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[0.3,0.9,0.01],\"tr\":[0.2,2,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round((a * 13 + tr) / 14, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"ATR(14) is {a:2}. The next candle's true range is {tr:2}. What is the new ATR, Wilder's way?\",\"explanation\":\"({a:2} × 13 + {tr:2}) ÷ 14 = {a * 13 + tr:2} ÷ 14 = {answer:2}. Each new candle gets a weight of 1/14.\",\"hint\":\"({a:2} × 13 + {tr:2}) ÷ 14.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After a quiet week, LABX's ATR(14) has doubled in two days, from 0.35 to 0.70. Price is about where it started.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you reasonably expect?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A rally: a rising ATR is bullish\",\"rationale\":\"ATR has no direction. Price is flat here, and big candles can go either way.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bigger candles for a while, in either direction\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Quiet again within hours: volatility always snaps back\",\"rationale\":\"Volatility does revert, but slowly. Busy stretches tend to be followed by more busy stretches first.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: past volatility says nothing about future volatility\",\"rationale\":\"Volatility clustering is one of the most solid facts in finance. Recent ATR is a fair forecast of near-term size.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Volatility clusters: busy periods tend to follow busy ones. A doubled ATR predicts bigger candles for a while, which matters for stops and size. It says nothing about direction.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-clustering\",\"volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"ATR measures size, not direction.\",\"Busy periods tend to follow busy periods.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"SMA(20) is 100.00 and σ is 0.40. LABX trades at 100.40. What is %b?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.75,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Bands: 100.00 ± 0.80, so 99.20 and 100.80. %b = (100.40 − 99.20) ÷ 1.60 = 0.75: three quarters of the way from the lower band to the upper.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"bollinger-bands\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the bands first: SMA ± 2σ.\",\"Bands 99.20 and 100.80. %b = (100.40 − 99.20) ÷ (100.80 − 99.20).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m\":[95,105,0.5],\"s\":[0.2,0.8,0.05],\"z\":[-2.5,2.5,0.25]},\"answer\":\"round((round(m + z * s, 2) - (m - 2 * s)) / (4 * s), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"SMA(20) is {m:2} and σ is {s:2}. LABX trades at {round(m + z * s, 2):2}. What is %b?\",\"explanation\":\"Bands: {m - 2 * s:2} and {m + 2 * s:2}. %b = ({round(m + z * s, 2):2} − {m - 2 * s:2}) ÷ {4 * s:2} = {answer:2}. 0 is the lower band, 1 the upper.\",\"hint\":\"Bands {m - 2 * s:2} and {m + 2 * s:2}. %b = (price − lower) ÷ (upper − lower).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Price touches the upper Bollinger Band. What does Bollinger himself say about the touch?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's a sell signal: price is two standard deviations too high\",\"rationale\":\"That's the most common misuse, and it contradicts Bollinger's own rule. In trends price walks the band.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It confirms the trend is ending\",\"rationale\":\"A touch says price is high compared with the last 20 closes. It doesn't say the trend is ending.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Buy: a touch leads to a breakout\",\"rationale\":\"Touches aren't signals either way. In ranges they often fade back to the middle.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Tags of the bands are just tags, not signals\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Bollinger's own rule: 'tags of the bands are just that, tags, not signals'. A touch places price within recent volatility. What it means depends on the regime: walking the band in a trend, fading in a range.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"bollinger-bands\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX's BandWidth has just hit its lowest level in 120 hours. A colleague says a breakout up is coming.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you honestly say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A breakout up: squeezes resolve in the trend's direction\",\"rationale\":\"In the lab's 20 squeezes, 10 went up and 10 down. Direction after a squeeze is grade D.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: a squeeze carries no information\",\"rationale\":\"It carries information about size: 16 of 20 squeezes were followed by a wider range.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A bigger move is likely soon, but its direction is unknown\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Prices will stay quiet: volatility is low and clusters\",\"rationale\":\"Quiet can persist for a while, but unusually low volatility tends to revert upward. That's the squeeze's one solid message.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Low volatility tends to be followed by higher volatility, so a squeeze forecasts a bigger move. Direction is another question: in the lab, up and down split 10 and 10. Plan for size, not side.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-squeeze\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate size from direction.\",\"In the lab, 16 of 20 squeezes widened; 10 went up and 10 down.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.9-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"EMA(20) is 104.50 and ATR(10) is 0.65. Where is the upper Keltner band, at 2 × ATR?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":105.8,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Upper = EMA + 2 × ATR = 104.50 + 1.30 = 105.80. The lower band sits the same distance below: 103.20.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.9\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-channels\"],\"hints\":[\"Keltner bands sit a multiple of ATR from the EMA.\",\"104.50 + 2 × 0.65.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[95,110,0.05],\"a\":[0.2,1.2,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(e + 2 * a, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"EMA(20) is {e:2} and ATR(10) is {a:2}. Where is the upper Keltner band, at 2 × ATR?\",\"explanation\":\"Upper = EMA + 2 × ATR = {e:2} + {2 * a:2} = {answer:2}. The lower band sits the same distance below: {e - 2 * a:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{e:2} + 2 × {a:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.9-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When is a TTM-style squeeze 'on'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When price closes outside the Keltner Channel\",\"rationale\":\"That's a channel breakout. The squeeze compares the two sets of bands, not price.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When BandWidth rises above its 120-hour average\",\"rationale\":\"That's volatility expanding, the opposite of a squeeze.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When the Keltner Channel sits inside the Bollinger Bands\",\"rationale\":\"That's the usual state when the σ of closes is large compared with ATR: no squeeze.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"When the upper Bollinger band is below the upper Keltner band and the lower Bollinger band is above the lower Keltner band\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Both Bollinger Bands inside Keltner means the σ of closes is small compared with ATR: unusually quiet closes. Like Bollinger's own squeeze, it forecasts size, not direction.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.9\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-squeeze\",\"volatility-channels\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the two upper bands, then the two lower bands.\",\"Bollinger must sit inside Keltner on both sides.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.4.9-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A backtest buys at the open of every candle whose high equals the highest high of the last 20 candles, that candle included. On a simulated random walk it makes +0.16R a trade after costs.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: +0.16R after costs is a real edge\",\"rationale\":\"A random walk has no edge to find. A profit there points to a flaw in the test.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The sample is too small\",\"rationale\":\"Size isn't the problem. Millions of trades with look-ahead would show the same false profit.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Look-ahead: at the open, the test already knows the candle will make a new high\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The costs are set too low\",\"rationale\":\"Higher costs would shrink the number, but the profit comes from information the test couldn't have had.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Whether a candle makes a new 20-candle high is known only at its close; buying at its open uses the future. Build the channel from previous candles and fill at the level: on the random walk that test lost 0.11R a trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.4.9\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-channels\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what the test knew at the moment it bought.\",\"A candle's high is known only after the candle closes.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A LABX candle trades 3,000 units. The 20 candles before it averaged 1,500 units. What is its relative volume (RVOL)?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"RVOL = 3,000 ÷ 1,500 = 2.0: twice the usual activity. It says the candle was busy; it says nothing about which way price went.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"relative-volume\"],\"hints\":[\"RVOL compares one candle's volume with its recent average.\",\"Divide the candle's volume by the average: 3,000 ÷ 1,500.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"vol\":[1500,4000,100],\"avg\":[800,2000,50]},\"answer\":\"round(vol / avg, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A LABX candle trades {vol} units. The 20 candles before it averaged {avg} units. What is its relative volume (RVOL)?\",\"explanation\":\"RVOL = {vol} ÷ {avg} = {answer:2}. It says how busy the candle was compared with normal; it says nothing about which way price went.\",\"hint\":\"Divide the candle's volume by the average: {vol} ÷ {avg}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these can a volume bar not tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"How many units traded in the period\",\"rationale\":\"That's exactly what a volume bar counts: units that changed hands.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Whether the period was busier than usual, compared with its average\",\"rationale\":\"Compare the bar with its average, as RVOL does, and you get this answer.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Whether a breakout drew more activity than the range before it\",\"rationale\":\"Comparing the breakout's volume with the range's volume answers this one.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whether buyers or sellers were stronger\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Every unit traded has a buyer and a seller, so volume measures participation, not who won. Direction comes from price, never from the volume bar.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"volume-analysis\",\"volume\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"In a simulated market whose generator makes heavy-volume moves likelier to continue, a breakout filter of RVOL ≥ 1.5 lifts the average result from −0.05R to +0.10R over 400 trades.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you conclude about volume filters in real markets?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing yet: the result reflects how the generator was built\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They fail, because simulated results never carry over\",\"rationale\":\"That overreaches the other way. This test simply can't speak to real markets, for or against.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"They work: 400 trades is a large sample\",\"rationale\":\"The sample is fine for that market. But the market was built so volume predicts continuation; the test rediscovered the design.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They work, but only on 1-hour candles\",\"rationale\":\"Narrowing the claim doesn't fix it. The edge came from the generator's design, not from the timeframe.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A test can only find what the data contains. This generator links volume to continuation by construction, so the filter had to help. Whether real markets behave that way needs real data.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"volume-analysis\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask where the filter's edge came from.\",\"The generator was built so that volume predicts continuation. What does that make the test measure?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"OBV stands at 5,200. The next candle closes 0.15 below the previous close, on 1,800 units. What is the new OBV?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3400,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"A lower close subtracts the candle's whole volume: 5,200 − 1,800 = 3,400. The size of the move, 0.15, plays no part.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cumulative-volume-indicators\"],\"hints\":[\"Higher close: add the volume. Lower close: subtract it.\",\"The close is lower, so subtract: 5,200 − 1,800.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"obv\":[2000,9000,100],\"vol\":[500,3000,100]},\"answer\":\"obv - vol\",\"prompt\":\"OBV stands at {obv}. The next candle closes below the previous close, on {vol} units. What is the new OBV?\",\"explanation\":\"A lower close subtracts the candle's whole volume: {obv} − {vol} = {answer}. The size of the move plays no part.\",\"hint\":\"The close is lower, so subtract: {obv} − {vol}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A candle has a high of 101.00, a low of 100.00 and a close of 100.25. What is its money flow multiplier, the weight the A/D line gives its volume?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"((C − L) − (H − C)) ÷ (H − L) = (0.25 − 0.75) ÷ 1.00 = −0.50. A close in the lower quarter of the range counts half the volume as distribution.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cumulative-volume-indicators\"],\"hints\":[\"The multiplier compares the close's distance from the low with its distance from the high.\",\"C − L = 0.25 and H − C = 0.75. Subtract, then divide by the range, 1.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"low\":[99,101,0.25],\"rng\":[0.5,2,0.25],\"x\":[0,2,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"x <= rng\"],\"answer\":\"round((x - (rng - x)) / rng, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A candle has a high of {low + rng:2}, a low of {low:2} and a close of {low + x:2}. What is its money flow multiplier, the weight the A/D line gives its volume?\",\"explanation\":\"((C − L) − (H − C)) ÷ (H − L) = ({x:2} − {rng - x:2}) ÷ {rng:2} = {answer:2}. Near the high the weight approaches +1; near the low, −1.\",\"hint\":\"C − L = {x:2} and H − C = {rng - x:2}. Subtract, then divide by the range, {rng:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your OBV for LABX reads 48,300. A friend's OBV for the same chart reads −12,500. How can both be right?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"You started counting on different days\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"OBV above zero means accumulation, below zero distribution\",\"rationale\":\"Zero has no meaning for OBV. The line's level depends on where counting began.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"One of you used tick volume instead of real volume\",\"rationale\":\"Possible in FX, but LABX has one volume series. The usual reason is simpler: different start dates.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They can't: OBV has a single correct value\",\"rationale\":\"OBV is a running total, so its value depends on the first candle counted. Both can be correct.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"OBV is a running total, so its level depends on the first candle counted. Change the start date and the whole line shifts up or down; its direction over the same stretch stays identical.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cumulative-volume-indicators\"],\"hints\":[\"OBV adds and subtracts from a running total. Where does that total start?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A session's first three candles have typical prices of 100.20, 100.60 and 100.00, on volumes of 1,000, 3,000 and 1,000. What is the VWAP so far?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":100.4,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Σ(TP × V) = 100,200 + 301,800 + 100,000 = 502,000. Σ V = 5,000. VWAP = 502,000 ÷ 5,000 = 100.40: the heavy middle candle pulls it above the plain average, 100.27.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"vwap\"],\"hints\":[\"Multiply each typical price by its volume, then add.\",\"Divide that total by the total volume, 5,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"t1\":[99.5,101,0.1],\"t2\":[99.5,101,0.1],\"t3\":[99.5,101,0.1],\"v1\":[500,3000,500],\"v2\":[500,3000,500],\"v3\":[500,3000,500]},\"constraints\":[\"t1 != t2\",\"t2 != t3\",\"v1 != v2\"],\"answer\":\"round((t1 * v1 + t2 * v2 + t3 * v3) / (v1 + v2 + v3), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A session's first three candles have typical prices of {t1:2}, {t2:2} and {t3:2}, on volumes of {v1}, {v2} and {v3}. What is the VWAP so far?\",\"explanation\":\"Σ(TP × V) = {t1 * v1 + t2 * v2 + t3 * v3:2}. Σ V = {v1 + v2 + v3}. VWAP = {answer:2}: each candle counts in proportion to its volume.\",\"hint\":\"Σ(TP × V) = {t1 * v1 + t2 * v2 + t3 * v3:2}. Divide it by {v1 + v2 + v3}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does a session VWAP start again at each session's open?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yesterday's prices become wrong overnight\",\"rationale\":\"They're still correct. They're just not part of today's trading, which is what VWAP describes.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It measures the average price of this session's trading, which fills are judged against\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Resetting makes VWAP a stronger support level\",\"rationale\":\"VWAP as support is unproven. The reset follows from its job: benchmarking today's execution.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Charting software can't store more than one session\",\"rationale\":\"It's a definition, not a storage limit. Anchored VWAP happily spans many sessions.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"VWAP answers one question: what was the volume-weighted average price of this session? That's the yardstick for today's fills, so the sums restart at the open. For longer spans, anchor a VWAP to an event instead.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"vwap\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about what VWAP is used for: judging a trader's fills during one session.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX dips to the session VWAP and bounces. What's the best-supported reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"VWAP is proven support, so buy every touch\",\"rationale\":\"There's no robust evidence that VWAP holds price. One bounce is one case, not a rule.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Institutions always defend VWAP\",\"rationale\":\"That's a story, not a finding. Large traders use VWAP to grade fills, which doesn't mean defending a line.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The bounce proves algorithms were buying there\",\"rationale\":\"A bounce can have many causes. Coordination is a possible mechanism, not something one candle proves.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Bounces at VWAP are unproven as a signal; its tested use is judging fills\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"VWAP as an execution benchmark is real market practice. VWAP as support or resistance is grade D: unproven, with coordination by VWAP-tracking algorithms as the only plausible mechanism.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"vwap\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate what VWAP is used for from what people claim it does.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A volume profile shows 101.50: 300 units, 101.25: 800, 101.00: 1,100, 100.75: 950 and 100.50: 400. Where is the point of control?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The point of control is the price with the most volume: 101.00, with 1,100 units. 100.75 is close, with 950, but second.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"volume-profile\"],\"hints\":[\"The point of control is a price, not a volume.\",\"Find the largest volume, 1,100, and read its price.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is claimed about a low-volume node, and how strong is the evidence?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price tends to move through it quickly; untested, grade D\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It acts as strong support; well proven\",\"rationale\":\"Support is the claim made for high-volume nodes, and even that is untested.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Price must come back to it; proven by the profile itself\",\"rationale\":\"That's the 'naked POC magnet' story, also untested. A profile can't prove what price will do.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It marks the session's fair price; well tested\",\"rationale\":\"'Fair price' is the story told about the POC and value area, not about low-volume gaps.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Few trades happened at a low-volume node, so the claim is that few positions defend it and price slices through. Like the high-volume node story, it's grade D: plausible, but not tested.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"volume-profile\"],\"hints\":[\"Low volume means few positions were opened at that price.\",\"Then ask which claims have actually been tested.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A video shows a weekly volume profile drawn on Friday. Its biggest node sits exactly where price bounced on Monday, and the presenter calls it proof that volume nodes act as support.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the main problem with this evidence?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Weekly profiles are too long; a daily profile would be reliable\",\"rationale\":\"Any window has the same problem if it includes candles after the bounce.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the node and the bounce line up exactly\",\"rationale\":\"They line up partly because the node was built from trading after the bounce. That's look-ahead.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The bounce should have come at a low-volume node instead\",\"rationale\":\"The problem isn't the node type. It's using later volume to explain an earlier turn.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The profile includes volume traded after Monday's bounce\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"On Monday, most of that node's volume hadn't traded yet. A profile that includes later candles 'explains' earlier turns by look-ahead. Only volume traded before the bounce could have influenced it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"volume-profile\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"When was the profile drawn, and when did the bounce happen?\",\"Which part of that node's volume existed on Monday?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Yesterday's session: high 104.00, low 101.00, close 103.40. What is today's classic pivot, P?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":102.8,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"P = (H + L + C) ÷ 3 = (104.00 + 101.00 + 103.40) ÷ 3 = 308.40 ÷ 3 = 102.80.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"pivot-points\"],\"hints\":[\"P averages three prices from yesterday.\",\"Add 104.00 + 101.00 + 103.40, then divide by 3.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"h\":[101,106,0.1],\"rng\":[1,4,0.1],\"x\":[0,4,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"x <= rng\"],\"answer\":\"round((h + (h - rng) + (h - rng + x)) / 3, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Yesterday's session: high {h:2}, low {h - rng:2}, close {h - rng + x:2}. What is today's classic pivot, P?\",\"explanation\":\"P = (H + L + C) ÷ 3 = ({h:2} + {h - rng:2} + {h - rng + x:2}) ÷ 3 = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Add {h:2} + {h - rng:2} + {h - rng + x:2}, then divide by 3.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Yesterday's session: high 104.00, low 101.00, close 103.40, so P = 102.80. What is the classic R1?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":104.6,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"R1 = 2P − L = 205.60 − 101.00 = 104.60. S1 would be 2P − H = 101.60.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"pivot-points\"],\"hints\":[\"R1 doubles the pivot and subtracts one of yesterday's prices.\",\"R1 = 2P − L = 2 × 102.80 − 101.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"h\":[101,106,0.1],\"rng\":[1,4,0.1],\"x\":[0,4,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"x <= rng\"],\"answer\":\"round(2 * (h + (h - rng) + (h - rng + x)) / 3 - (h - rng), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Yesterday's session: high {h:2}, low {h - rng:2}, close {h - rng + x:2}, so P = {(h + (h - rng) + (h - rng + x)) / 3:2}. What is the classic R1?\",\"explanation\":\"R1 = 2P − L = {2 * (h + (h - rng) + (h - rng + x)) / 3:2} − {h - rng:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"R1 = 2P − L = 2 × {(h + (h - rng) + (h - rng + x)) / 3:2} − {h - rng:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A chart shows classic, Fibonacci and Camarilla pivots together, and price reversed 'at a pivot' three times today. Why is that weak evidence?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Camarilla levels only work on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"That's another untested rule. The problem is how many lines there are, not which chart they're on.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"With so many lines, price is almost always near one; those touches would happen anyway\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Pivots can only work once a day, so three reversals is too many\",\"rationale\":\"No such rule exists. The real issue is that a dense grid of lines gets 'hit' by chance.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Fibonacci pivots cancel out the classic ones\",\"rationale\":\"Lines don't cancel. Adding systems just adds lines, and more lines mean more chance hits.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Three systems put about 20 lines around price. In the lesson, four systems put a line within half an ATR of 22 of 24 closes. A reversal 'at a pivot' is then close to guaranteed; only a comparison with random lines can tell.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"pivot-points\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the lines, then ask how often price would be near one by chance.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX has ranged between 99.50 and 100.50 for three days. This morning it opens at 101.10, above the range, on three times its usual volume, and keeps rising.\",\"prompt\":\"Which kind of gap is this most likely to be?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Common\",\"rationale\":\"Common gaps sit inside a range on ordinary volume. This one leaves the range on heavy volume.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Runaway\",\"rationale\":\"Runaway gaps come in the middle of an established trend. Here the trend is only starting.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Breakaway\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Exhaustion\",\"rationale\":\"Exhaustion needs a long move to exhaust. This gap starts the move.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A gap out of a range on heavy volume, at the start of a move, is a breakaway gap. Its edge is often watched as support, and it frequently never fills.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-types\"],\"hints\":[\"Where is the gap: inside a range, at its edge, mid-trend or late?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX gaps up: yesterday's high was 100.40 and today's low is 100.90. When is the gap filled?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When price trades back to 100.90\",\"rationale\":\"100.90 is the top edge of the gap. A fill means the whole gap: back down to 100.40.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When price closes below today's open\",\"rationale\":\"A close below the open is just a bearish candle. The fill depends on reaching the pre-gap high.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When price trades back to the midpoint, 100.65\",\"rationale\":\"That covers half the gap: a partial fill at most.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"When price trades back down to 100.40\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A gap up is filled when price returns to the last pre-gap extreme, yesterday's high of 100.40. Until then, some of the empty space is still open.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-types\",\"price-gaps\"],\"hints\":[\"The gap runs from yesterday's high to today's low.\",\"Filling it means covering all of that space.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"How did island reversals rank in Bulkowski's tests of chart patterns?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Among the strongest reversal patterns\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite. The dramatic shape doesn't translate into strong results in his data.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Among the weakest: 38th of 39 at bottoms, 31st of 36 at tops\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"In the middle of the table, around 20th\",\"rationale\":\"Lower than that: near the bottom of both tables.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They weren't tested; they're too rare\",\"rationale\":\"Bulkowski tested over 2,000 island trades. They're rare per chart, not rare in a big sample.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Over 2,000 island trades put them near the bottom: 38th of 39 bullish patterns and 31st of 36 bearish ones. Those figures come from US stocks on daily charts and don't transfer to LABX by default.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-types\"],\"hints\":[\"A striking shape isn't the same as strong results.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Price falls, pauses for three small candles, then rallies hard. What does the supply-and-demand method call the pause?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A supply zone, because the move into it was a drop\",\"rationale\":\"The zone is named by the move away from the base. Here that's a rally.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A demand zone: drop–base–rally\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No zone: zones need a rally before the base\",\"rationale\":\"Drop–base–rally is one of the two demand formations. The leg before the base doesn't decide.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A supply zone, because sellers stopped there\",\"rationale\":\"The method reads a rally away as buying waiting at the base: demand.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The departure decides the name. A rally away from a base makes a demand zone, whether the move into the base was a drop or a rally. A drop away makes a supply zone.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"supply-demand-zones\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A zone was tested twice and held both times. Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's stronger now: more touches mean more traders defending it\",\"rationale\":\"That's one school's view. The supply-and-demand school says the opposite, and neither is tested.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's weaker now: each touch used up resting orders\",\"rationale\":\"That's the other school's view, also untested. The two claims contradict each other.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The two popular rules disagree, and neither has tested evidence\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Two tests prove it will hold a third time\",\"rationale\":\"Two cases prove nothing about the third. Compare with random zones over many cases.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Support-and-resistance teaching says touches strengthen a level; supply-and-demand teaching says fresh zones are best. Both can explain any outcome after the fact, which is why only a test against random zones can settle it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"supply-demand-zones\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"hints\":[\"Recall the two schools' rules about touches.\",\"Can both be right? What would settle it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What did Osler (2000) find about support and resistance levels published by forecasting firms?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A small edge, 60.8% against 56.2%, and strength ratings that predicted nothing\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A large edge, about 90%, strongest at levels rated 'strong'\",\"rationale\":\"Far too high. The effect was small, and the strength ratings had no predictive value.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No edge at all over arbitrary levels, whatever the rating\",\"rationale\":\"There was a difference, 60.8% against 56.2%: small but significant.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A small edge, from firms that agreed on almost every level\",\"rationale\":\"The edge was real, but the firms agreed with each other only about 30% of the time.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Published levels had a real but small edge over arbitrary levels, 60.8% against 56.2%, in intraday currency trends. The firms rarely agreed, and their strength ratings were useless: a reason to test your own zones against random ones.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"The effect was real, but small.\",\"Think about what the firms' 'strength' ratings added.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A swing rises from 120.00 to 130.00. What is its 38.2% retracement level?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":126.18,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Retracement = high − ratio × move = 130.00 − 0.382 × 10.00 = 130.00 − 3.82 = 126.18.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.8\",\"concepts\":[\"fibonacci-levels\"],\"hints\":[\"Retracements are measured back down from the high.\",\"Subtract 0.382 × 10.00 from 130.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[90,150,1],\"mv\":[4,20,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(lo + mv - 0.382 * mv, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A swing rises from {lo:2} to {lo + mv:2}. What is its 38.2% retracement level?\",\"explanation\":\"Retracement = high − ratio × move = {lo + mv:2} − 0.382 × {mv:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Subtract 0.382 × {mv:2} from {lo + mv:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is 'the pullback held the 38.2–61.8% zone' almost impossible to prove wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Most pullbacks end between a third and two thirds of the swing anyway\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The zone is too narrow to test\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: it's so wide that most pullbacks land in it anyway.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Fibonacci ratios appear throughout nature, so markets follow them\",\"rationale\":\"That's the popular story, not evidence. Tests found the ratios in market swings no more often than chance.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Prices are random, so no level can ever be tested\",\"rationale\":\"Levels can be tested against random levels. This zone is just wide enough to pass almost any test of 'did it hold'.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The zone covers the middle of the swing, where most pullbacks stop anyway. With a small tolerance around each level, about two thirds of that stretch counts as 'at a level'. A rule that can't miss can't be tested.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.8\",\"concepts\":[\"fibonacci-levels\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Where do most pullbacks end, with or without Fibonacci?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.5.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"At 104.00 you count five reasons to buy: Fibonacci 61.8%, the 20-period SMA, the 20-period EMA, RSI oversold and Stochastic oversold.\",\"prompt\":\"At most, how many independent pieces of information is that?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Five: each tool is a different calculation\",\"rationale\":\"Different formulas can share inputs. SMA 20 and EMA 20 average the same closes; RSI and Stochastic read the same recent prices.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Four: only RSI and Stochastic overlap\",\"rationale\":\"SMA 20 and EMA 20 overlap too: two averages of the same 20 closes.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Three: the Fibonacci level, one average, one oscillator reading\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Two: levels and oscillators\",\"rationale\":\"The Fibonacci level comes from one swing's high and low, the average from 20 closes: different calculations, so count them separately at most.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"SMA 20 and EMA 20 are one opinion counted twice, and so are RSI and Stochastic. That leaves at most three, and even those all come from past prices. Each must also predict on its own before stacking adds anything.\",\"lesson\":\"2.5.8\",\"concepts\":[\"confluence\"],\"hints\":[\"Group the tools that use the same inputs.\",\"SMA 20 and EMA 20 average the same closes. Which other pair overlaps?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A bar opens at 100.40, trades up to 100.90 and down to 100.30, and closes at 100.85. How does the app classify it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Doji bar\",\"rationale\":\"A doji bar's body is a quarter of the range or less. Here the body is 0.45 of 0.60: 75%.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bull trend bar\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neither\",\"rationale\":\"Neither is for middling bodies or big bodies closing mid-range. This close, 100.85, is in the top third.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Bear trend bar\",\"rationale\":\"It closed above its open, so the body is bull. A bear trend bar closes near its low.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Body 100.85 − 100.40 = 0.45 of a 0.60 range: 75%, over half. The close, 100.85, is in the top third, which starts at 100.70. Both tests pass: a bull trend bar.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"bar-reading\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over the last 10 bars, each bar shares on average 65% of its range with the bar before. What does that suggest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: overlap can't be measured objectively\",\"rationale\":\"Overlap is mechanical: shared range ÷ the smaller range. Its meaning is the heuristic part.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A strong trend: price keeps returning to the same prices\",\"rationale\":\"Strong trends leave prices behind: low overlap, under 30%. Returning to the same prices is two-sided trading.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A breakout is about to happen\",\"rationale\":\"Overlap describes what has happened. It doesn't time a breakout or say which way one would go.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Two-sided trading, roughly in balance: a range reading\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Over 50% average overlap suggests a range: each bar covers mostly the same prices as the last, so neither side is winning. The measurement is mechanical; reading it as balance is Brooks' heuristic, without published statistics.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"bar-reading\",\"range\"],\"hints\":[\"Under 30% suggests a trend; over 50% suggests a range.\",\"What does it mean when bar after bar covers the same prices?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX has ranged between 99.00 and 101.00 for 30 hours, its bars overlapping about 60%. Now a big bull trend bar runs from 99.80 to 100.70 and closes near its high, in the middle of the range.\",\"prompt\":\"How do you read it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy now: a trend bar shows urgency\",\"rationale\":\"In a trend, a run of trend bars shows urgency. One bar mid-range is still inside two-sided trading.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Sell now: trend bars in ranges always fail\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing always fails. Brooks expects big bars in ranges to fade, but that's a heuristic, not a rule to trade blindly.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"As range behaviour until later bars show follow-through\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"As proof that the range will break upwards\",\"rationale\":\"It hasn't even reached the range high, 101.00. A breakout needs a close beyond the range and follow-through.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Context changes the bar. Mid-range, with 60% overlap behind it, one big bar hasn't changed the regime: the range is still the best description. Only follow-through, more trend bars and less overlap, would turn it into a trend reading.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"bar-reading\",\"range\"],\"hints\":[\"Where is the bar: at an edge or in the middle?\",\"One bar against 30 hours of overlap: which describes the market better?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A bull bar forms at the prior low: open 100.20, high 100.80, low 100.20, close 100.75. ATR(14) is 0.50. Which signal-bar test does it fail?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Close in the top third\",\"rationale\":\"The top third starts at 100.60, and it closed at 100.75. It passes.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An upper wick of at most 15% of the range\",\"rationale\":\"The upper wick is 0.05 of a 0.60 range, 8%. It passes.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A lower wick of at least 25% of the range\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A range between 0.5 and 2 × ATR\",\"rationale\":\"0.60 is 1.2 × ATR, inside the band. It passes.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"It opened at its low, so it has no lower wick: nothing shows sellers being rejected below. The close is in the top third, the upper wick is 8%, the range is 1.2 × ATR and it's at the prior low: 4 of 5.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"signal-and-entry-bars\"],\"hints\":[\"Measure each wick as a share of the 0.60 range.\",\"Where did the bar open, compared with its low?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A setup wins 45% of the time. Winners make 1.6R, losers lose 1R, and costs take 0.07R a trade. What is the expectancy per trade, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.1,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"0.45 × 1.6R − 0.55 × 1R − 0.07R = 0.72 − 0.55 − 0.07 = +0.10R a trade. Positive, but thin: a slightly lower win rate or higher costs would wipe it out.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"traders-equation\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Multiply each outcome by its chance, then subtract the costs.\",\"Wins: 0.45 × 1.6. Losses: 0.55 × 1.\",\"0.72 − 0.55 − 0.07.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[35,60,5],\"w\":[1.2,3,0.2],\"cost\":[0.03,0.1,0.01]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(p / 100 * w - (1 - p / 100) - cost) >= 0.01\"],\"answer\":\"round(p / 100 * w - (1 - p / 100) - cost, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A setup wins {p}% of the time. Winners make {w:1}R, losers lose 1R, and costs take {cost:2}R a trade. What is the expectancy per trade, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"{p / 100:2} × {w:1}R − {1 - p / 100:2} × 1R − {cost:2}R = {p / 100 * w:2} − {1 - p / 100:2} − {cost:2} = {answer:2}R a trade. Above zero is an edge after costs; below zero loses money however good the bars look.\",\"hint\":\"{p / 100 * w:2} − {1 - p / 100:2} − {cost:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A bull signal bar has a high of 101.36 and a low of 100.92. You place a buy stop one tick above it and a protective stop one tick below it. What is your risk per unit, before slippage and costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.46,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Buy stop 101.36 + 0.01 = 101.37; stop 100.92 − 0.01 = 100.91. Risk = 101.37 − 100.91 = 0.46 a unit: the bar's 0.44 range plus the two ticks.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"signal-and-entry-bars\",\"stop-entry-order\"],\"hints\":[\"One tick on LABX is 0.01.\",\"Buy stop: 101.36 + 0.01. Stop: 100.92 − 0.01.\",\"101.37 − 100.91.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[99,102,0.01],\"d\":[0.2,1.2,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(d + 0.02, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A bull signal bar has a high of {lo + d:2} and a low of {lo:2}. You place a buy stop one tick above it and a protective stop one tick below it. What is your risk per unit, before slippage and costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Buy stop {lo + d + 0.01:2}; stop {lo - 0.01:2}. Risk = {lo + d + 0.01:2} − {lo - 0.01:2} = {answer:2} a unit: the bar's {d:2} range plus the two ticks.\",\"hint\":\"{lo + d + 0.01:2} − {lo - 0.01:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The last seven bars have ranges 0.80, 0.65, 0.90, 0.55, 0.70, 0.60 and 0.50. The newest bar runs from 100.10 to 100.60; the one before ran from 100.15 to 100.75. What is the newest bar?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"An inside bar\",\"rationale\":\"Its low, 100.10, is under the previous low, 100.15, so it isn't contained.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An outside bar\",\"rationale\":\"Its high, 100.60, is under the previous 100.75. An outside bar must go beyond both ends.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An NR7 bar\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"An inside bar and an NR7 bar\",\"rationale\":\"It is NR7, but not inside: its low pokes 0.05 under the previous low.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"0.50 is the narrowest of the seven ranges, so it's NR7. It isn't an inside bar, because its low, 100.10, is under the previous low, 100.15; and it isn't an outside bar, because its high is lower too.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"inside-outside-bars\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare its range with the six before it.\",\"An inside bar needs both its high and its low within the previous bar.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.3-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An inside bar runs from 99.18 to 99.64. You bracket it with stop entries one tick beyond each side, and each leg's protective stop sits at the other entry. What is the risk per unit of either leg, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.48,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Buy stop 99.65, sell stop 99.17. The long's stop is at 99.17 and the short's at 99.65, so both legs risk 99.65 − 99.17 = 0.48 a unit.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"inside-outside-bars\",\"oco-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Buy stop: one tick above the high. Sell stop: one tick below the low.\",\"Each leg's stop is the other entry.\",\"99.65 − 99.17.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[98.5,101.5,0.01],\"d\":[0.2,1,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(d + 0.02, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An inside bar runs from {lo:2} to {lo + d:2}. You bracket it with stop entries one tick beyond each side, and each leg's protective stop sits at the other entry. What is the risk per unit of either leg, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Buy stop {lo + d + 0.01:2}, sell stop {lo - 0.01:2}. Each leg's stop is the other entry, so both risk {lo + d + 0.01:2} − {lo - 0.01:2} = {answer:2} a unit.\",\"hint\":\"{lo + d + 0.01:2} − {lo - 0.01:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is a hikkake?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Two inside bars in a row, followed by a breakout\",\"rationale\":\"That's ii and a breakout. A hikkake needs the first break to fail.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An outside bar that closes at its extreme\",\"rationale\":\"That's a strong outside bar. A hikkake starts with an inside bar.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An inside bar, a break of one side that fails, then a close beyond the other side\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A gap that fills within the same session\",\"rationale\":\"Gap fills are a different idea. A hikkake is a false break of an inside bar.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The first break traps traders on the wrong side; the move through the opposite side of the inside bar is the trade. By the app's convention, that close must come within 3 bars. No published statistics back the setup.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"inside-outside-bars\",\"false-breakout\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A bull pullback starts at the trend high. Bar highs, in order: 103.20 (the high), 102.90, 102.70, 102.95, 102.60, 102.40, 102.75. The sixth bar makes the pullback's lowest low. Which bar is H2?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The fourth, 102.95\",\"rationale\":\"That's H1: the first high above the bar before. H2 comes after the next lower low.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The sixth, 102.40\",\"rationale\":\"That's the pullback's low bar, the signal bar for H2. H2 is the next bar to trade above its high.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The fifth, 102.60\",\"rationale\":\"102.60 is under the fourth bar's 102.95, so it isn't a count.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The seventh, 102.75\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Count bars whose high beats the previous bar's high. The fourth (102.95 > 102.70) is H1. After the pullback makes its lower low on the sixth, the seventh (102.75 > 102.40) is H2.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"bar-counting\",\"two-legged-pullback\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare each high only with the one right before it.\",\"The first bar that beats its neighbour is H1.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An H2 signal bar has a high of 98.42, and the pullback low is 97.96. You place a buy stop one tick above the signal bar and a stop one tick under the pullback low. What is the risk per unit, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.48,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Buy stop 98.42 + 0.01 = 98.43; stop 97.96 − 0.01 = 97.95. Risk = 98.43 − 97.95 = 0.48 a unit.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"bar-counting\",\"signal-and-entry-bars\"],\"hints\":[\"One tick is 0.01: add it to the high, take it off the low.\",\"Buy stop 98.43; stop 97.95.\",\"98.43 − 97.95.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"low\":[96,102,0.01],\"d\":[0.2,0.9,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(d + 0.02, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An H2 signal bar has a high of {low + d:2}, and the pullback low is {low:2}. You place a buy stop one tick above the signal bar and a stop one tick under the pullback low. What is the risk per unit, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Buy stop {low + d + 0.01:2}; stop {low - 0.01:2}. Risk = {low + d + 0.01:2} − {low - 0.01:2} = {answer:2} a unit.\",\"hint\":\"{low + d + 0.01:2} − {low - 0.01:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do H1 and H2 counts mean little in a trading range?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Ranges have too few bars to count\",\"rationale\":\"Ranges can last hundreds of bars. The problem isn't the number of bars but what they'd be resuming.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Counting only works on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"Counting works on any timeframe. In a range, there's no trend for a second attempt to resume.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"In a range, H2 always fails\",\"rationale\":\"Not always. It just has no edge from the count: mid-range bars go both ways.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Without a trend there's nothing to resume, and every bounce produces counts\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"H1 and H2 are attempts to resume a trend. In a range there's no trend, and since a count is only a bar beating the previous high, counts appear on almost every bounce, random data included. The trend check comes first.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"bar-counting\",\"range\"],\"hints\":[\"What is an H2 an attempt to do?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In an uptrend, pullbacks last one or two bars and the deepest is 0.30, with an ATR of 0.50. Now and then a bar dips under the previous bar's low. Which channel is it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Tight channel\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Broad channel\",\"rationale\":\"Broad pullbacks give back more than half the leg before. These are shallow: 0.30 against an ATR of 0.50.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Micro channel\",\"rationale\":\"Some bars dip under the previous low, so it isn't micro.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A trading range\",\"rationale\":\"Short, shallow pullbacks in an uptrend are a strong trend, not two-sided trading.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Pullbacks of at most 1 ATR and at most 3 bars make a tight channel. It isn't micro, because some bars take out the previous low, and it isn't broad, because the pullbacks are shallow.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"price-channels\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the deepest pullback with the ATR.\",\"Micro needs no lower lows at all.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is a wedge bull flag?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three pushes up with fading momentum at the top of a rally\",\"rationale\":\"That's a wedge top, read as a possible reversal. A flag pushes against the trend it sits in.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A pullback in a bull trend that pushes down three times; its buy setup is an H3\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A flag whose lines converge, with no pushes counted\",\"rationale\":\"That's the classic wedge of unit 2.2. Brooks' version counts pushes.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A strong breakout followed by a slower channel\",\"rationale\":\"That's a spike and channel.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In a bull trend, a pullback that pushes down three times is a wedge bull flag; the third attempt up, H3, is the buy setup. It's Brooks' heuristic, with no published statistics.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"three-push-wedge\",\"bar-counting\"],\"hints\":[\"A flag is a pause against the trend.\",\"Count the pushes, then the attempts to resume.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Today you draw a channel line through three lows from last week, and note that the channel has been 'tested three times'. What's wrong with that count?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: three touches make a valid channel\",\"rationale\":\"Three touches you chose to draw through are a fit, not a test. Price never had to respect a line that didn't exist.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"You need at least five touches\",\"rationale\":\"More anchors don't help: you'd still be fitting the line to the past. The problem is when the touches happened.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The lows are the anchors you chose; only touches after you drew the line are evidence\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Channel lines only work on closes\",\"rationale\":\"Lows or closes, the problem is the same: touches chosen after the fact.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Drawn after the fact, a line always fits the lows you picked. Those touches couldn't have been traded, because the line didn't exist yet. Only touches after you draw it count as tests.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"price-channels\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"When did the line exist?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"By the app's rule, when has a breakout above a range failed?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When a wick pokes above the range and the bar closes inside\",\"rationale\":\"That bar never closed beyond, so it's a test, not a breakout. Failure needs a close beyond first.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When a bar closes above the level, then a bar closes back inside within one to three bars\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When price trades back to the level at any later time\",\"rationale\":\"Retests are common in breakouts that hold. Failure is a close back inside within one to three bars.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"When the breakout bar is a doji bar\",\"rationale\":\"A weak breakout bar raises doubt, but failure is defined by the close back inside.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A failed breakout needs two closes: one beyond the level, then one back inside within one to three bars. A wick that doesn't close beyond is a test; a later retest that holds isn't a failure.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"false-breakout\",\"trapped-traders\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A range runs from 98.00 to 100.00. A bar closes at 100.40, with a low of 99.90. The next bar closes at 99.60.\",\"prompt\":\"Where are the stops of the traders who bought that breakout most likely to sit?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Above the breakout bar's high\",\"rationale\":\"Stops above the high protect shorts. A long is protected by a sell stop below the entry.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"At 98.00, the bottom of the range\",\"rationale\":\"A few might sit there, but the breakout idea is wrong as soon as price is back inside: most stops sit just under the breakout bar.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Just under the breakout bar's low, around 99.89, back inside the range\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nowhere: breakout traders don't use stops\",\"rationale\":\"Some don't, but a breakout plan's stop belongs where the idea fails: back inside the range.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A breakout long is wrong once price is back inside, so its stop sits under the breakout bar or just inside the range. As the next bar falls to close at 99.60, those sell stops fill together and can push price lower: trapped traders fuelling the move against them.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"trapped-traders\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"hints\":[\"A long's stop is a sell order. Where is the breakout idea proven wrong?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why decide on a breakout only after the breakout bar closes?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Until it closes, a bar above the level can still close back inside: the close is its final verdict\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Spreads are wider before the close\",\"rationale\":\"On LABX the spread is a fixed 0.10. The reason is information: the close decides breakout or failure.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The close is always the best price to enter at\",\"rationale\":\"Waiting often costs some price. You accept that to know what the bar actually did.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stop orders only work at the close\",\"rationale\":\"Stop orders trigger whenever price reaches them. The point is your decision, not the order type.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Mid-bar, price above the level is only a possibility. The same bar can close back inside, turning a breakout into a failure. Deciding at the close, with the invalidation written, trades a little price for real information.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"false-breakout\",\"breakout\"],\"hints\":[\"What can still change before a bar closes?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In a rally, which of these adds a point of strength on the checklist?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A bar 3 ATR tall after 25 bars of trend\",\"rationale\":\"That's the climax flag: a huge bar late in a long move counts as exhaustion.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"8 of the last 10 bars closing in their top third\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Overlap rising over the last 5 bars\",\"rationale\":\"Rising overlap means bars stop moving on: an exhaustion flag.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Upper tails on each of the last 3 bars\",\"rationale\":\"Tails on top mean higher prices were rejected: exhaustion, in a rally.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Closes near the highs, bar after bar, show buyers in control: 7 or more of the last 10 in the top third adds a point. The other three are exhaustion flags.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Two bars are each 3 ATR tall and close at their highs. What makes one of them a climax candidate rather than a breakout candidate?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Its context: late in a long trend and far from the EMA 20, rather than right after a range\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Its colour and the size of its body\",\"rationale\":\"Both bars look the same. Bar shape alone can't separate them; the difference is where they come in the move.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Its volume, which is always higher at a climax\",\"rationale\":\"Volume can help, but nothing is always true here. The checklist's flag is about context: trend length and stretch.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: any bar over 2.5 ATR is a climax\",\"rationale\":\"The flag needs both size and context: 20 or more trend bars before it. After a range, the same bar is a breakout candidate.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The climax flag combines size with context: at least 2.5 ATR, after 20 or more trend bars, ideally far from the EMA 20. The same bar after a quiet range is a breakout candidate. Either way it's a probability, confirmed only by the bars after.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\",\"volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"The bars are identical. What differs is what came before them.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You gave a 0.70 probability that a move would continue. It didn't. What is the Brier score of that one forecast?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.49,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Brier = (probability − outcome)², with outcome 0 because it didn't happen: (0.70 − 0)² = 0.49. Over many forecasts you average these; always saying 50% scores 0.25.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\"],\"hints\":[\"The outcome is 1 if it happened, 0 if not.\",\"Square the gap between your probability and the outcome.\",\"(0.70 − 0)².\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[0.1,0.9,0.05],\"hit\":[0,1,1]},\"answer\":\"round((p - hit) * (p - hit), 4)\",\"prompt\":\"You gave a {p:2} probability that a move would continue. The outcome was {hit}, where 1 means it continued and 0 means it didn't. What is the Brier score of that one forecast?\",\"explanation\":\"Brier = (probability − outcome)² = ({p:2} − {hit})² = {answer:4}. Over many forecasts you average these; always saying 50% scores 0.25.\",\"hint\":\"({p:2} − {hit})².\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.8-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your trading timeframe is 1H. Which pair of higher and lower timeframes fits the 4–6× guideline?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"2H and 30m\",\"rationale\":\"2× steps are too close: the three charts tell nearly the same story.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"1W and 1m\",\"rationale\":\"168× and 60×: far too wide for the charts to connect.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"4H and 15m\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"1D and 5m\",\"rationale\":\"24× and 12×: too far apart to link a daily direction to a 5-minute entry.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"4H is 4 × 1H, and 1H is 4 × 15m: both steps sit in the 4–6× band. Each chart adds information without losing the link to the next.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.8\",\"concepts\":[\"timeframe-alignment\",\"timeframe\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.8-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan says: trade only in the 4H direction; set the stop on 1H structure; time entries on 15m. The 4H is in an uptrend. On the 1H, price has fallen for 12 bars and just broke a swing low.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your rule allow?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A 1H short: the 1H trend is down now\",\"rationale\":\"The 1H gives setups in the 4H direction only. A 1H downtrend against a rising 4H is a pullback, not a short.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A short at half size\",\"rationale\":\"Half a trade against the rule is still against the rule.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Switching the bias to the 1H for today\",\"rationale\":\"Changing the hierarchy mid-session is timeframe shopping. Rule changes belong in a review.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No short: wait for the 1H to set up a long, or stand aside\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The written hierarchy settles the conflict: the 4H gives the direction, so only longs are allowed. The 1H decline is, at most, a pullback to wait out until a 1H buy setup forms.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.8\",\"concepts\":[\"timeframe-alignment\"],\"hints\":[\"Which timeframe ranks first in your rule?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.6.8-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your rule: trade the 1H setup; use the 15m for timing only. Journal: '09:40 long on a 1H setup, stopped out at −1R. 09:48 opened the 15m, saw a fresh buy signal, entered long again.'\",\"prompt\":\"What does the journal show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Timeframe shopping: a switch right after a loss, to find a new signal\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Persistence: the idea was right, only the timing was off\",\"rationale\":\"The 1H setup had failed. The 15m was used to find a new reason to buy, not to time a valid setup.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A proper rule change\",\"rationale\":\"Rule changes happen in review, in writing, not eight minutes after a loss.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing wrong: the 15m is part of the rule\",\"rationale\":\"The 15m times a 1H setup. Here it supplied a new setup after the 1H one failed.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Eight minutes after a loss, the trader moved to a lower timeframe and found the agreement they wanted. That's timeframe shopping: the chart changed to fit the decision, not the other way round.\",\"lesson\":\"2.6.8\",\"concepts\":[\"timeframe-alignment\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"What happened just before the timeframe changed?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.1-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A double bottom's peak is 54.00. Your confirmation entry fills at 54.30, with the stop at 53.40 and the target at 56.10. How many R does the target pay, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Risk per unit: 54.30 − 53.40 = 0.90. Reward: 56.10 − 54.30 = 1.80. 1.80 ÷ 0.90 = 2.00R. An anticipation entry near the second low would show a far bigger ratio, and fail more often.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"entry-techniques\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Reward-to-risk = (target − entry) ÷ (entry − stop).\",\"Risk: 54.30 − 53.40. Reward: 56.10 − 54.30.\",\"1.80 ÷ 0.90 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[40,120,0.1],\"r\":[0.4,2,0.05],\"w\":[0.5,5,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(w / r, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your confirmation entry fills at {e:2}, with the stop at {e - r:2} and the target at {e + w:2}. How many R does the target pay, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Risk per unit: {e:2} − {e - r:2} = {r:2}. Reward: {e + w:2} − {e:2} = {w:2}. {w:2} ÷ {r:2} = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{w:2} ÷ {r:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.1-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX broke above resistance at 99.00 and now trades at 99.60. You want to buy only if price comes back to the broken level. Which order, and what is its main risk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A buy limit at 99.10: it may never fill\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A buy stop at 99.10: it may slip\",\"rationale\":\"A buy stop waits above the price. At 99.10, under the ask, it would trigger at once and buy now, not on the retest.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A market buy now: it may not fill\",\"rationale\":\"A market order fills at once, at the ask near 99.65. That isn't the retest, and filling is never its problem.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A buy limit at 99.10: it's certain to fill at 99.10\",\"rationale\":\"A limit controls the price, not the fill. If price never comes back to 99.10, nothing fills.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A retest entry waits under the price for a return to the broken level: a buy limit. It fills only if the ask comes back to 99.10, so its risk is missing the trade, often one of the strongest.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"entry-techniques\",\"limit-order\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.1-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A friend's entry method won 130 of its last 200 trades, 65%. Its expectancy after costs over those trades is −0.05R. He says it's a great entry because it's usually right.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you conclude?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's a strong method: winning two trades in three is what matters\",\"rationale\":\"Hit rate ignores size. If the wins are small and the losses full, a 65% method can lose on average, as this one does.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"On this record it loses about 0.05R a trade: frequent small wins don't cover the losses\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It will turn positive over the next 200 trades, since it wins most of the time\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing in the method changes with more trades. More data sharpens the estimate; it doesn't fix a negative one.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Expectancy can't be negative when the win rate is over 50%\",\"rationale\":\"It can, whenever the average win is small enough against the average loss.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Expectancy combines how often and how much: 0.65 × average win − 0.35 × average loss. With full 1R losses, wins averaging about 0.46R give −0.05R. Over 200 trades that's −10R from a method that is usually right.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy\",\"entry-techniques\"],\"hints\":[\"Expectancy uses the size of wins and losses, not only how often you win.\",\"Over 200 trades, −0.05R a trade is −10R in total.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.2-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Long 40 LABX at 30.00. Price rises to 32.00, and you add 20 more, moving the shared stop to 30.50. Before costs, how much do you lose if that stop is hit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Lot 1 at 30.50: 40 × 0.50 = +20.00. Lot 2: 20 × (30.50 − 32.00) = −30.00. Together: −10.00. The position grew by half, and the worst case is a small loss.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.2\",\"concepts\":[\"pyramiding\"],\"hints\":[\"Worst case = Σ units × (stop − entry), for each lot at the shared stop.\",\"Lot 1 gains at 30.50; lot 2 loses.\",\"+20.00 − 30.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"u1\":[20,100,10],\"u2\":[10,50,5],\"e\":[20,80,1],\"up\":[1,4,0.5],\"s\":[0.25,1.5,0.25]},\"constraints\":[\"s < up\",\"u2 * (up - s) - u1 * s > 0\"],\"answer\":\"round(u2 * (up - s) - u1 * s, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Long {u1} LABX at {e:2}. Price rises to {e + up:2}, and you add {u2} more, moving the shared stop to {e + s:2}. Before costs, how much do you lose if that stop is hit?\",\"explanation\":\"Lot 1 at {e + s:2}: {u1} × {s:2} = +{u1 * s:2}. Lot 2: {u2} × ({e + s:2} − {e + up:2}) = −{u2 * (up - s):2}. Together: −{answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"+{u1 * s:2} − {u2 * (up - s):2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.2-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Long 40 LABX at 25.00, stop 24.00: 1R is 40.00 before costs. At 24.40 you buy 60 more, same stop. If the stop is hit, how many R do you lose?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.6,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Lot 1: 40 × 1.00 = 40.00. Lot 2: 60 × (24.40 − 24.00) = 24.00. Total 64.00 ÷ 40.00 = 1.6R, against the 1R planned.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.2\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-per-trade\",\"r-multiple\"],\"hints\":[\"The loss at the stop counts every unit you hold, each from its own price.\",\"Lot 2 loses 60 × 0.40.\",\"(40.00 + 24.00) ÷ 40.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"u1\":[20,80,10],\"u2\":[20,100,10],\"e\":[20,100,1],\"d\":[0.5,3,0.25],\"x\":[0.1,2.5,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"x < d\"],\"answer\":\"round((u1 * d + u2 * (d - x)) / (u1 * d), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Long {u1} LABX at {e:2}, stop {e - d:2}: 1R is {u1 * d:2} before costs. At {e - x:2} you buy {u2} more, same stop. If the stop is hit, how many R do you lose?\",\"explanation\":\"Lot 1: {u1} × {d:2} = {u1 * d:2}. Lot 2: {u2} × ({e - x:2} − {e - d:2}) = {u2 * (d - x):2}. Total {u1 * d + u2 * (d - x):2} ÷ {u1 * d:2} = {answer:2}R, against the 1R planned.\",\"hint\":\"({u1 * d:2} + {u2 * (d - x):2}) ÷ {u1 * d:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.2-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After averaging down, your average entry price is better. Why isn't your risk lower?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It is lower: a better average means a smaller loss at the stop\",\"rationale\":\"The average improves, but more units sit above the same stop. 100 units 1.50 above it lose more than 50 units 2.00 above it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Commissions double when you add\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is a share of the value traded. The add costs a little more, but that isn't what turns 1R into 1.5R.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"More units now sit above the same stop, so the loss at the stop grows\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The stop then has to move closer to the price\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing forces the stop closer. With the same stop, the extra units alone raise the loss.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Risk is the loss if the stop is hit: units × (average price − stop). Averaging down lowers the average, but raises the units faster. 50 at 50.00 with a 48.00 stop risks 100.00; adding 50 at 49.00 makes it 150.00.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.2\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-per-trade\"],\"hints\":[\"Write the loss at the stop as units × (average price − stop).\",\"Compare 50 × 2.00 with 100 × 1.50.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.3-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In your last 50 trades, MFE reached 2.5R in 34% of them. With a 2.5R target, and every trade that misses it closing at −1R, what is the expectancy per trade, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.19,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Wins: 0.34 × 2.5R = 0.85R. Losses: 0.66 × 1R = 0.66R. Expectancy: 0.85 − 0.66 = +0.19R per trade, before costs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"profit-targets\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Expectancy = P × k − (1 − P) × 1R, with P the share that reached the target.\",\"Wins: 0.34 × 2.5. Losses: 0.66 × 1.\",\"0.85 − 0.66 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[10,70,1],\"kk\":[1,5,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(p * kk - round(p * kk, 0)) < 0.000001\"],\"answer\":\"round(p / 100 * kk - (1 - p / 100), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"In your last 50 trades, MFE reached {kk:1}R in {p}% of them. With a {kk:1}R target, and every trade that misses it closing at −1R, what is the expectancy per trade, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Wins: {p / 100:2} × {kk:1}R = {p / 100 * kk:2}R. Losses: {1 - p / 100:2} × 1R. Expectancy: {answer:2}R per trade, before costs.\",\"hint\":\"{p / 100 * kk:2} − {1 - p / 100:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.3-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"All else equal, what happens to the win rate when you move the target further away?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It rises, because each win pays more\",\"rationale\":\"What a win pays and how often you win are different things. Fewer trades travel the extra distance.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It stays the same; only the payoff changes\",\"rationale\":\"A trade counts as a win only if price reaches the target. A further target is reached by fewer trades.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It depends only on where the stop is\",\"rationale\":\"The stop matters, but with it fixed, a further target alone lowers the share of trades that reach it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It falls, because fewer trades travel that far before the stop\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Only trades whose MFE reaches the target win, and a further target is reached by a smaller share. Each win pays more, so expectancy can rise or fall: it depends on how fast that share drops.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"profit-targets\",\"expectancy\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.3-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long LABX at 100.00 with the stop at 98.00. Three rallies in the past month stalled at 103.80–104.20. Your fixed rule says 3R, so the target would be 106.00.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best way to handle the target?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Keep 106.00: the rule is the rule\",\"rationale\":\"A rule that ignores obvious opposing structure assumes a break nobody argued for. Rules can, and should, include structure.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Set it just under 103.80, or write down why price should break the zone\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Put it at 104.20, the top of the zone\",\"rationale\":\"That's inside the zone where rallies stalled. Price must push through most of the resistance to fill it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Move the stop to 99.00 so a target near 103.80 still pays 3R\",\"rationale\":\"The stop belongs where the idea fails. Moving it to fit a ratio raises the chance of being stopped by noise.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A target past clear resistance needs a break your plan hasn't argued for. Either take the structural target just under 103.80, a little under 1.9R, or treat the break as a separate scenario with its own evidence.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"profit-targets\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"hints\":[\"Where have rallies stopped before?\",\"A target belongs where the plan says price can reasonably go.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.4-1\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Highest high since entry 72.40, ATR 1.10, k = 3. Where is the chandelier stop for your long?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":69.1,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The chandelier hangs k × ATR under the highest high since entry: 72.40 − 3 × 1.10 = 72.40 − 3.30 = 69.10.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"chandelier-exit\"],\"hints\":[\"The chandelier hangs k × ATR under the highest high since entry.\",\"k × ATR = 3 × 1.10 = 3.30.\",\"72.40 − 3.30 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"hh\":[20,150,0.1],\"a\":[0.2,2,0.05],\"kk\":[2,4,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(kk * a * 100 - round(kk * a * 100, 0)) < 0.000001\"],\"answer\":\"round(hh - kk * a, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Highest high since entry {hh:2}, ATR {a:2}, k = {kk:1}. Where is the chandelier stop for your long?\",\"explanation\":\"The chandelier hangs k × ATR under the highest high since entry: {hh:2} − {kk:1} × {a:2} = {hh:2} − {kk * a:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{hh:2} − {kk * a:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.4-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You switch from a 2-ATR trail to a 4-ATR trail on the same setup. What should you expect, on average?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Less giveback and longer holds\",\"rationale\":\"A wider trail sits further from the high, so when it's hit it gives back more, not less.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Longer holds and bigger winners, with more of each peak given back\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"More stop-outs from noise\",\"rationale\":\"That's the tight trail's problem. A 4-ATR trail sits outside most normal pullbacks.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The same results: the trail doesn't change the edge\",\"rationale\":\"It doesn't create edge, but it changes how long you hold, how much you give back and the spread of results.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A wider trail survives pullbacks a tighter one wouldn't, so trades last longer and trends can pay more. The price is giveback: when it's finally hit, it sits 4 ATR under the high. Wide trails suit trends and give back nearly everything in ranges.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"chandelier-exit\"],\"hints\":[\"How far under the high does each trail sit when it's hit?\",\"Which one survives a 3-ATR pullback?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.4-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan trails a long with a 3-ATR chandelier, now at 61.40. Price pulls back to 61.60. You notice the 20-period EMA is at 60.70 and decide to trail with that instead, 'because it fits this trend better'.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"None: the EMA is a valid trailing method\",\"rationale\":\"It's valid if chosen before entry. Switched in mid-trade, as the stop gets close, it just moves your exit 0.70 further away.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The EMA lags, so it will exit too early\",\"rationale\":\"The EMA sits under the chandelier here, so it would exit later, not earlier. That's the point of the switch.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It widens the stop by 0.70 under cover of a new method, decided under pressure\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Trailing stops must use ATR\",\"rationale\":\"Structure, averages and ATR are all valid trails. The problem is changing between them mid-trade.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Changing the trail as the stop gets close is a widened stop in disguise: the exit moves from 61.40 to 60.70, decided under pressure rather than by the plan. Choose the trail before entry and keep it for the whole trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the two exits: 61.40 and 60.70.\",\"Which way did the exit move, and when was the new rule chosen?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.5-1\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"20 trades, target +2R, stop −1R. Without a breakeven rule: 8 winners, 12 losers. With breakeven at +1R, 2 winners came back to the entry first and 3 losers had reached +1R first. Expectancy with the rule, per trade, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.15,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Winners kept: 6 × 2R = 12R. Losers left: 9 × 1R. The five scratches count 0. (12 − 9) ÷ 20 = +0.15R per trade, down from +0.20R without the rule.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"breakeven-stop\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Killed winners and saved losers both become scratches at 0R.\",\"Winners left: 8 − 2 = 6. Losers left: 12 − 3 = 9.\",\"(6 × 2 − 9) ÷ 20 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[4,10,1],\"l\":[6,14,1],\"kd\":[0,4,1],\"sv\":[0,5,1]},\"constraints\":[\"kd <= w\",\"sv <= l\"],\"answer\":\"round(((w - kd) * 2 - (l - sv)) / (w + l), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"{w + l} trades, target +2R, stop −1R. Without a breakeven rule: {w} winners, {l} losers. With breakeven at +1R, {kd} winners came back to the entry first and {sv} losers had reached +1R first. Expectancy with the rule, per trade, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"Winners kept: {w - kd} × 2R = {(w - kd) * 2}R. Losers left: {l - sv} × 1R. Scratches count 0. ({(w - kd) * 2} − {l - sv}) ÷ {w + l} = {answer:2}R per trade.\",\"hint\":\"({(w - kd) * 2} − {l - sv}) ÷ {w + l} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.5-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You bought LABX at the ask, 100.00, and later moved the stop to exactly 100.00. Why does a stop like that get hit so often?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Brokers move prices to hit breakeven stops\",\"rationale\":\"The simulator has no such mechanism, and none is needed: the spread and normal noise explain the hits.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A breakeven stop fills at a worse price than other stops\",\"rationale\":\"It fills like any stop: at its price, or at the open after a gap. The problem is where it sits, not how it fills.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Moving the stop resets the trade's risk to 1R\",\"rationale\":\"Moving the stop doesn't change the initial 1R. It only changes where the trade can end.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It triggers when the bid falls to 100.00, a mid of 100.05: ordinary noise around your entry gets there\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A long's stop watches the bid. With a 0.10 spread, the bid reaches 100.00 when the mid is 100.05, above the 99.95 mid when you bought. Any normal wiggle around the entry gets there, and both commissions make the 'breakeven' a small loss.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"breakeven-stop\"],\"hints\":[\"Which price does a long's stop watch: the bid or the ask?\",\"At which mid is the bid 100.00?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.5-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your trade is up +0.8R. Nothing in your plan mentions breakeven, but you move the stop to your entry: 'I just can't stand watching this one turn into a loss.'\",\"prompt\":\"Which bias is steering this exit?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Confirmation bias\",\"rationale\":\"That's seeking evidence that agrees with you. Here the driver is the pain of a possible loss.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Loss aversion\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"FOMO\",\"rationale\":\"Fear of missing out pushes you into trades. This is about protecting one you're already in.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Overconfidence\",\"rationale\":\"Overconfidence loosens risk control. This tightens it, out of discomfort rather than a rule.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Losses hurt more than equal gains please. Moving the stop to avoid that pain, not because a written rule says so, is loss aversion steering the exit, and it can end valid trades in ordinary noise. Write a breakeven rule before entry, or leave the stop alone.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"loss-aversion\",\"breakeven-stop\"],\"hints\":[\"What feeling is behind 'I can't stand it'?\",\"Was the move in the plan before entry?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.6-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You close half the position at +1.5R and a quarter at +3R. The last quarter is stopped at breakeven. What is the trade's blended R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Each part's R times its share: 0.5 × 1.5 + 0.25 × 3 + 0.25 × 0 = 0.75 + 0.75 + 0 = +1.5R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"scaling-out\",\"r-multiple\"],\"hints\":[\"Weight each part's R by its share of the position.\",\"Half: 0.5 × 1.5. A quarter: 0.25 × 3.\",\"0.75 + 0.75 + 0 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[1,2,0.5],\"b\":[2,6,1]},\"answer\":\"round(0.5 * a + 0.25 * b, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You close half the position at +{a:1}R and a quarter at +{b:1}R. The last quarter is stopped at breakeven. What is the trade's blended R?\",\"explanation\":\"Each part's R times its share: 0.5 × {a:1} + 0.25 × {b:1} + 0.25 × 0 = {0.5 * a:2} + {0.25 * b:2} + 0 = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{0.5 * a:2} + {0.25 * b:2} + 0 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.6-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Compared with exiting all at once, what does taking half off at +1R usually do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Raises expectancy, because profits are locked in\",\"rationale\":\"Banking half early also caps half of every big winner. On the lesson's numbers expectancy fell from +0.10R to +0.05R.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Lowers the win rate but raises the average win\",\"rationale\":\"It's the reverse: more trades bank something, and the biggest wins shrink.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Raises the win rate and narrows the spread of results; expectancy may rise or fall\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Changes nothing, since the entries are the same\",\"rationale\":\"Same entries, different exits: the distribution of results changes, even if the edge doesn't.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Every trade that reaches +1R banks something, so more trades end positive, and the largest wins are halved, so results spread less. The average depends on how often runners go far: it can rise or fall.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"scaling-out\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens to trades that reach +1R and then reverse?\",\"And to the rare trade that runs to +5R?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.6-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your trend system wins 30% of its trades. Most of its profit comes from a few trades that run past +5R. A friend suggests taking half off at +1R on every trade, 'to smooth things out'.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the most likely effect?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Smoother results, but the big runners that pay for the losses are halved: expectancy likely falls\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Higher expectancy, since half of every winner is banked\",\"rationale\":\"Banking +1R on half caps half of each +5R runner, and that's where this system's profit comes from.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No effect on results, only on how the trades feel\",\"rationale\":\"The exits change real results: half of each big runner is sold at +1R.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Fewer winners, because half the position is gone\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: more trades end positive. What shrinks is the size of the big wins.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A system with rare big winners earns its expectancy in the right tail. Selling half at +1R raises the win rate and lowers the variance, but halves the large winners. Test the change on the system's own trades before adopting it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"scaling-out\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Where does this system make its money?\",\"What happens to a +5R runner when half is sold at +1R?\",\"Half × 1R + half × 5R = 3R, against 5R.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Eight trades are still under +0.5R after 12 bars. Held to the end, 2 would win +2R and 6 would lose 1R. A time stop at bar 12 would exit each at −0.15R. How much better (+) or worse (−) is the total with the time stop, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.8,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Held: 2 × 2R − 6 × 1R = −2.0R. Time stop: 8 × (−0.15R) = −1.2R. The time stop is 0.8R better here, at the cost of the two +2R winners.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"time-stop\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out both totals, then subtract.\",\"Held: 2 × 2R − 6 × 1R. Time stop: 8 × −0.15R.\",\"−1.2 − (−2.0) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[1,3,1],\"l\":[3,9,1],\"x\":[0.1,0.3,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"l > 2 * w\",\"abs((l - 2 * w) - (w + l) * x) > 0.001\"],\"answer\":\"round((l - 2 * w) - (w + l) * x, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"{w + l} trades are still under +0.5R after 12 bars. Held to the end, {w} would win +2R and {l} would lose 1R. A time stop at bar 12 would exit each at −{x:2}R. How much better (+) or worse (−) is the total with the time stop, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Held: {w} × 2R − {l} × 1R = −{l - 2 * w}R. Time stop: {w + l} × −{x:2}R = −{(w + l) * x:2}R. The difference is {l - 2 * w} − {(w + l) * x:2} = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{l - 2 * w} − {(w + l) * x:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.7-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What makes an exit 'planned'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It made money\",\"rationale\":\"A profitable exit can still be a whim. Planned means the reason existed before the trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"You thought about it for a while first\",\"rationale\":\"Thinking longer doesn't make a reason part of the plan. It has to be written before entry.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Its reason was written in the plan before entry, and you tag it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It happened at the stop or the target\",\"rationale\":\"Those are planned, but so are time stops and listed signals. The test is a reason written in advance.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A planned exit follows a reason written before entry: structure broke, a listed opposite signal, a time stop, scheduled news. Tagging each exit lets you check later whether rules or fear drove them.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"exit-rules\",\"trading-plan\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.7-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Long 40 LABX at 75.00, stop 73.50: 1R is 60.00 before costs. Price drops to 73.80, and you move the stop to 72.80 'to give it room'. Price then falls to 72.70.\",\"prompt\":\"How should this trade go into your journal?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"−1R: a normal stop-out\",\"rationale\":\"The loss is 40 × 2.20 = 88.00, about −1.47R against the initial 60.00. And the stop was widened.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"−1R, since the new stop set the trade's new 1R\",\"rationale\":\"R is fixed at entry. Moving the stop doesn't create a new 1R; it makes the loss a bigger multiple of the old one.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Leave it out: the stop was changed\",\"rationale\":\"Every trade counts. Leaving out the widened ones would make your journal lie about your risk.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"About −1.47R, flagged as a widened stop\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"R is set at entry: 60.00. Widening the stop to 72.80 made the loss 40 × 2.20 = 88.00, about −1.47R, and broke the rule that a stop only moves toward less risk. Record it against the initial R and tag it as a widened stop.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-loss-order\",\"initial-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Which 1R counts: the one at entry, or the one after the move?\",\"Loss: 40 × (75.00 − 72.80) = 88.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.8-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Long at 80.00, stop 78.00. The lowest price during the trade was 79.10. What was the MAE, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.45,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"MAE = (entry − lowest low) ÷ (entry − stop) = (80.00 − 79.10) ÷ (80.00 − 78.00) = 0.90 ÷ 2.00 = 0.45R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.8\",\"concepts\":[\"mae-mfe\"],\"hints\":[\"MAE measures how far price went against you, from the entry, in units of the stop distance.\",\"Distance: 80.00 − 79.10 = 0.90. Stop distance: 2.00.\",\"0.90 ÷ 2.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[20,150,0.5],\"d\":[0.5,4,0.25],\"f\":[5,95,5]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(d * f - round(d * f, 0)) < 0.000001\"],\"answer\":\"round(f / 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Long at {e:2}, stop {e - d:2}. The lowest price during the trade was {e - d * f / 100:2}. What was the MAE, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"MAE = ({e:2} − {e - d * f / 100:2}) ÷ ({e:2} − {e - d:2}) = {d * f / 100:2} ÷ {d:2} = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{d * f / 100:2} ÷ {d:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.8-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A trade's MFE was +2.40R. You exited at +1.80R. What was your exit efficiency, in percent?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Efficiency = result ÷ MFE = 1.80 ÷ 2.40 = 75%. The giveback was 0.60R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.8\",\"concepts\":[\"exit-efficiency\"],\"hints\":[\"Efficiency is the share of the best unrealised result you kept.\",\"Divide the exit result by the MFE.\",\"1.80 ÷ 2.40 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m\":[1,5,0.1],\"p\":[10,90,5]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(m * p - round(m * p, 0)) < 0.000001\"],\"answer\":\"p\",\"prompt\":\"A trade's MFE was +{m:2}R. You exited at +{m * p / 100:2}R. What was your exit efficiency, in percent?\",\"explanation\":\"Efficiency = result ÷ MFE = {m * p / 100:2} ÷ {m:2} = {answer}%. The giveback was {m - m * p / 100:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{m * p / 100:2} ÷ {m:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.7.8-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After 12 trades, a trader notices that no winner had an MAE above 0.5R. She tightens every stop to 0.5R from now on.\",\"prompt\":\"What's missing from her decision?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the data show winners never need more than 0.5R\",\"rationale\":\"Twelve trades can't show that. One winner with a deeper dip in the next 20 would be cut.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A wider stop, since tight stops always lose\",\"rationale\":\"Tight stops aren't always worse; the point is she can't know yet. What's missing is the test, not a rule against tight stops.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Enough trades, a re-test on new trades, and the higher cost in R of a tighter stop\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing, as long as she keeps her risk per trade at 1%\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing fixes the loss per trade, but doesn't make 12 trades a reliable guide. A tighter stop also means a bigger size and more cost in R.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Twelve trades are far too few to fit a stop: the pattern can be chance. She needs a larger sample of one setup, 30 or more, a re-test on trades that didn't shape the rule, and an allowance for costs, which grow in R as the stop tightens.\",\"lesson\":\"2.7.8\",\"concepts\":[\"mae-mfe\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"How many trades are behind the rule?\",\"Was the rule tested on trades that didn't help create it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which strategy family tends to lose in a range, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Range trading: it needs a trend to reach the other edge\",\"rationale\":\"Range trading is built for ranges: it buys near support and sells near resistance.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Trend following: its signals reverse before they pay\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Mean reversion: a range has no average to return to\",\"rationale\":\"A range has a stable average. That's exactly what mean reversion needs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"None: in a range every family breaks even\",\"rationale\":\"Families differ by regime. Trend rules bleed to whipsaws in a range while range rules do their best work.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In a range, a trend rule buys after a rise and sells after a fall, near the edges, and price turns straight back. Many small losses and few wins: the whipsaw pattern of a trend rule in the wrong regime.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-regime-fit\",\"market-regime\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.1-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Forty candles of LABX. Swing highs at 101.20, 100.60, 101.50, 100.90; swing lows at 99.40, 100.10, 99.20, 99.80. No level held twice, and the 20-candle average is flat.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Range trade between 99.20 and 101.50\",\"rationale\":\"Those are just the extremes. No edge was touched twice, so there's no range to trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy a breakout above 101.50\",\"rationale\":\"A breakout needs a tested level. 101.50 was reached once.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trend following: the last swing was up\",\"rationale\":\"One swing isn't a trend. The highs and lows went up and down in turn.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No trade: there's no regime you can name\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Swings that overlap with no edges and no direction leave no regime to name, and every strategy bets on one. On a random walk every rule loses about its costs. 'No trade' is a decision, not a failure.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-regime-fit\",\"random-walk\"],\"hints\":[\"Do the highs and lows step one way, or turn at the same levels?\",\"If you can't name the regime in one sentence, it isn't clear.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.1-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABX has made two higher highs, but its last two swing lows were equal, 99.50 and 99.50. You normally risk 1% on a trend-following entry.\",\"prompt\":\"How do you size this trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Full 1%: two higher highs are enough\",\"rationale\":\"The lows haven't stepped up. Half the evidence for a trend is missing, so the label could be wrong.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"2%, to make up for the trades you skipped\",\"rationale\":\"Raising risk on a weaker setup is the opposite of sizing for the regime you might be wrong about.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Half size, or skip it: the label is uncertain\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Full size, with a tighter stop to be safe\",\"rationale\":\"A tighter stop raises the cost in R and gets hit by normal noise. It doesn't fix an unclear label.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Size for the regime you might be wrong about. Equal lows look as much like a range floor as a higher low. With the label uncertain, trade smaller or skip; a full-size bet needs a clear regime.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-regime-fit\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"hints\":[\"A trend needs higher highs and higher lows. Are both there?\",\"What would two equal lows mean in a range?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A trend rule wins 40% of its trades, and its average loss is 1R. What is the smallest average win, in R, at which it breaks even before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"At break-even, 0.40 × win = 0.60 × 1R, so the win = 0.60 ÷ 0.40 = 1.5R. Any bigger average win makes the expectancy positive, even with most trades losing.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"trend-following\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Break-even: win rate × average win = loss rate × average loss.\",\"0.40 × win = 0.60 × 1.\",\"0.60 ÷ 0.40 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[25,45,5]},\"answer\":\"round((100 - w) / w, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A trend rule wins {w}% of its trades, and its average loss is 1R. What is the smallest average win, in R, at which it breaks even before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"At break-even, {w / 100:2} × win = {(100 - w) / 100:2} × 1R, so the win = {(100 - w) / 100:2} ÷ {w / 100:2} = {answer:2}R. Any bigger average win makes the expectancy positive, even with most trades losing.\",\"hint\":\"{(100 - w) / 100:2} ÷ {w / 100:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Where does a moving-average trend-following system lose most of its money?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In long, steady trends\",\"rationale\":\"That's where it makes its money: it holds the big winners while the trend runs.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"On the pullbacks inside a trend\",\"rationale\":\"Pullbacks give back some open profit, but a stop or trail wide enough lets the trade survive them.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nowhere much: its losses are always small\",\"rationale\":\"Each loss is small, but many small whipsaw losses in a range add up to the biggest drawdowns.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"In ranges, where crosses reverse quickly, and at sharp V-shaped reversals\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Trend rules lose in ranges, where each cross reverses before it pays, and at sharp reversals, where the lagging exit hands back open profit. Their losing runs come from regimes, not from bad luck alone.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"trend-following\",\"market-regime\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX is near 100.00 and a round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. Your trend-following stop sits 4.00 below the entry. What does the round trip cost in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.05,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Cost in R = cost per unit ÷ stop distance = 0.20 ÷ 4.00 = 0.05R. The same costs on a 1.00 stop would be 0.20R a trade: wide stops keep trend following's costs small in R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"trend-following\"],\"hints\":[\"Costs in R divide the cost per unit by the stop distance.\",\"0.20 ÷ 4.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s\":[1,5,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(0.2 / s, 3)\",\"prompt\":\"LABX is near 100.00 and a round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. Your trend-following stop sits {s:2} below the entry. What does the round trip cost in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Cost in R = cost per unit ÷ stop distance = 0.20 ÷ {s:2} = {answer:3}R. The wider the stop, the smaller the share of 1R that costs take.\",\"hint\":\"0.20 ÷ {s:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is a complete pullback entry?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price above a rising SMA 50; a dip to the EMA 20; buy on a close back above the prior candle's high; stop under the pullback low\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy whenever price touches the EMA 20; stop at the EMA\",\"rationale\":\"No trend filter and no trigger. In a downtrend the EMA is resistance, and a stop at the EMA sits inside normal noise.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A close back above the prior high; stop under the pullback low; no trend filter needed\",\"rationale\":\"Without the filter, the same trigger fires in ranges and downtrends, where a dip isn't a pullback.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Price above a rising SMA 50; buy at market as soon as it starts to dip; stop when the trend ends\",\"rationale\":\"A dip that has just started has no low to put the stop under, and 'when the trend ends' isn't a stop.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A pullback entry needs all three parts: a trend filter so you only buy dips in an uptrend, a trigger that shows buyers are back, and a stop under the pullback low, where the idea is wrong.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"trend-pullback\",\"invalidation-level\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.3-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX is trending up. Stop 98.40 under the last swing low, target 104.00. You buy the pullback at 99.20 instead of chasing at 101.60. What is the reward-to-risk of the pullback entry?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"Reward 104.00 − 99.20 = 4.80; risk 99.20 − 98.40 = 0.80; 4.80 ÷ 0.80 = 6.0. Chasing at 101.60 gives 2.40 ÷ 3.20 = 0.75: the same stop and target, a far worse trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"reward-to-risk\",\"trend-pullback\"],\"hints\":[\"Reward = target − entry; risk = entry − stop.\",\"(104.00 − 99.20) ÷ (99.20 − 98.40) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[98.8,99.8,0.2]},\"answer\":\"round((104 - e) / (e - 98.4), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABX is trending up. Stop 98.40 under the last swing low, target 104.00. You buy the pullback at {e:2} instead of chasing at 101.60. What is the reward-to-risk of the pullback entry?\",\"explanation\":\"Reward 104.00 − {e:2} = {104 - e:2}; risk {e:2} − 98.40 = {e - 98.4:2}; ratio {answer:2}. Chasing at 101.60 gives 2.40 ÷ 3.20 = 0.75.\",\"hint\":\"{104 - e:2} ÷ {e - 98.4:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"An uptrend with higher lows at 96.60 and then 98.05. A fourth pullback falls to 97.64, then a candle closes at 97.29, under the SMA 50.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this say about the pullback?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A deeper pullback: a better price to buy\",\"rationale\":\"Under the last higher low and the SMA 50, the trend's structure is broken. A cheaper price isn't a better trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It became a reversal: a lower low and a close under the 50 switch the filter off\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing yet: wait for the EMA 20 to turn down\",\"rationale\":\"The EMA lags. The lower low and the close under the 50 are the earlier, rule-based signs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy now, with a stop just under 97.29\",\"rationale\":\"Buying under a broken filter ignores the rule that makes pullbacks worth buying.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A pullback is only a pullback while the trend holds. A low under the last higher low breaks the structure, and a close under the SMA 50 fails the filter. Stop buying dips and wait for a new setup.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"trend-pullback\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the new low with the last higher low.\",\"Where did the close end up relative to the SMA 50?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX has ranged between 97.00 and 100.00. Which plan follows the breakout card?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Level touched once; buy a close above it at 100.15; stop 99.20; target 103.00\",\"rationale\":\"One touch doesn't make a tested level. The card asks for at least two.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy the first wick above 100.00; stop at 97.00, the range bottom\",\"rationale\":\"A wick isn't a close, and a stop at the far edge risks the whole range height on one trade.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Level touched three times; buy a close at 100.15; stop 99.20, back inside; target 103.00\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy a close at 100.15; stop 100.05, just under the level; target 103.00\",\"rationale\":\"A stop inside the level's noise gets hit by an ordinary retest. 'Back inside' means under the breakout candle.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The card needs a tested level, a close beyond it, a stop back inside the range under the breakout candle, and the measured move as the target: the 3.00 height added to 100.00.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"range-breakout-strategy\",\"support-and-resistance\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.4-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX ranged between 48.00 and 50.00, then closed at 50.30. Stop 49.80, target 52.00, the measured move. With costs of 0.20 per unit, what win rate does the trade need to break even?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":31.8,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Loss with costs: 50.30 − 49.80 + 0.20 = 0.70. Win after costs: 52.00 − 50.30 − 0.20 = 1.50. Break-even = 0.70 ÷ (0.70 + 1.50) = 31.8%. Before costs it would be 0.50 ÷ 2.20 = 22.7%.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"range-breakout-strategy\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the cost to the loss and take it off the win.\",\"Break-even = loss ÷ (loss + win).\",\"0.70 ÷ (0.70 + 1.50) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[50.1,50.5,0.1],\"s\":[49.5,49.9,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round((e - s + 0.2) / (52 - s) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"LABX ranged between 48.00 and 50.00, then closed at {e:2}. Stop {s:2}, target 52.00, the measured move. With costs of 0.20 per unit, what win rate does the trade need to break even?\",\"explanation\":\"Loss with costs: {e:2} − {s:2} + 0.20 = {e - s + 0.2:2}. Win after costs: 52.00 − {e:2} − 0.20 = {52 - e - 0.2:2}. Break-even = loss ÷ (loss + win) = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"{e - s + 0.2:2} ÷ ({e - s + 0.2:2} + {52 - e - 0.2:2}) = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In which situation does a range breakout fail most often?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A close beyond a level touched three times, on high volume\",\"rationale\":\"That's the setup the card wants: a tested level and participation. It can fail, but less often.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A first poke through a level touched once, on low volume, after a long run\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A breakout after a long, quiet range with tested edges\",\"rationale\":\"A long range with tested edges is the breakout's best case, not its worst.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A retest of the broken level that holds\",\"rationale\":\"A retest that holds is evidence for the breakout, not against it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Breakouts fail most from untested levels, on wicks and thin volume, and late in moves that have already run. Every one of those conditions is in option b.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"range-breakout-strategy\",\"false-breakout\"],\"hints\":[\"Which option has the fewest of the card's filters?\",\"Count touches, volume and how far the move has already run.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"The ranges of LABX's last 7 candles were 0.80, 1.20, 1.00, 0.70, 1.10, 0.95 and 0.75. Is the last candle an NR7?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes: 0.75 is below the average of the seven\",\"rationale\":\"An NR7 is narrower than each of the six before it, not just below the average.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Yes: it's narrower than the candle before it\",\"rationale\":\"That makes it an NR2. An NR7 must be the narrowest of seven.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No: an NR7 must also be an inside bar\",\"rationale\":\"They're separate signals. A candle can be an NR7 without sitting inside the one before.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No: 0.70, three candles earlier, is narrower\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"An NR7's range is smaller than each of the previous six. The fourth of the seven candles had a range of 0.70, under the last candle's 0.75, so the last one isn't an NR7.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-contraction-breakout\",\"volatility\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.5-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your OCO bracket has a buy stop at 100.65 and a sell stop at 99.65. The buy stop fills. What happens to the sell stop?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It stays live, as a second position\",\"rationale\":\"That would be two separate orders, not OCO. With OCO, one fill cancels the other.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's cancelled automatically\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It becomes the long's take-profit\",\"rationale\":\"A sell stop below the price can't take profit. OCO cancels it; the long needs its own protective stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's moved to the entry price\",\"rationale\":\"OCO doesn't move orders. It cancels the other one when the first fills.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"One-cancels-the-other links two orders: when one fills, the broker cancels the other. The long then needs its own protective stop, here at 99.65.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"bracket-order\",\"volatility-contraction-breakout\"],\"hints\":[\"What does 'one cancels the other' mean once one side has filled?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.5-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your volatility bracket re-arms the other side after a stop. In a sideways day, the long filled and was stopped, then the short filled and was stopped. Each side risked 99.02.\",\"prompt\":\"What went wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The stops were too wide\",\"rationale\":\"Wider stops lower the cost in R, but sideways chop can still hit both sides.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing in the execution: sideways chop is this strategy's known failure, and it costs about 2R\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You should have doubled the size after the first loss\",\"rationale\":\"Doubling after a loss is a martingale: it raises risk exactly when the setup has failed.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The NR7 was fake: real ones always break one way\",\"rationale\":\"No contraction promises a direction. The directional edge is weak after costs, grade C.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A contraction can resolve sideways. A plan that re-arms the other side then pays two losses, 198.04 here, about 2% of the account. It's a known cost of the strategy, so plan for it: fewer re-arms, or a smaller size per side.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-contraction-breakout\",\"costs-in-r\"],\"hints\":[\"Was any rule broken, or did the market do what this strategy fears most?\",\"Add up both sides' losses.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"When is a range confirmed for the range card?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"After one touch on each edge\",\"rationale\":\"One touch per edge could be the start of a trend or a triangle.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When price has moved sideways for 10 candles\",\"rationale\":\"Time alone doesn't make edges. The card wants touches on both sides.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"At least two touches on each edge, flat averages, ADX under 20\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"When ADX rises above 25\",\"rationale\":\"ADX above 25 points to a trend, not a range.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Range trading bets that the edges hold, so it needs evidence that they do: at least two touches on each side, flat averages and a low ADX. Anything less is a guess about edges that may not exist.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.6\",\"concepts\":[\"range-trading\",\"range\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.6-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A range trade: buy 98.20, stop 97.70, target 99.20. Costs are 0.20 per unit. What is the reward-to-risk after costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.14,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"After costs the win is 1.00 − 0.20 = 0.80 and the loss is 0.50 + 0.20 = 0.70: 0.80 ÷ 0.70 = 1.14. Before costs it was 2.0. Small targets make costs heavy.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.6\",\"concepts\":[\"range-trading\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Take the cost off the reward and add it to the risk.\",\"(1.00 − 0.20) ÷ (0.50 + 0.20) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"t\":[98.9,99.5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round((t - 98.4) / 0.7, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A range trade: buy 98.20, stop 97.70, target {t:2}. Costs are 0.20 per unit. What is the reward-to-risk after costs?\",\"explanation\":\"After costs the win is {t - 98.2:2} − 0.20 = {t - 98.4:2} and the loss is 0.50 + 0.20 = 0.70: {answer:2}. Before costs it was {(t - 98.2) / 0.5:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{t - 98.4:2} ÷ 0.70 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You've traded a 50.00–52.00 range four times with the range card. A candle closes at 52.35. On the next candle price dips back to 51.90, near your usual short entry.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the range card say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Short at 51.90: price is back under resistance\",\"rationale\":\"The close at 52.35 ended the range. Selling the old resistance now fights a possible breakout.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Short, with a wider stop above 52.35\",\"rationale\":\"Widening the stop to keep trading a broken range is a rule change made on the fly.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No new range trades: the first close outside ended it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Wait for one more touch of 52.00, then decide\",\"rationale\":\"The flip rule already fired on the close. Another touch doesn't bring the range back.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The flip rule: the first confirmed close outside ends range trading. From here the breakout card applies, and a fade needs its own setup and evidence.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.6\",\"concepts\":[\"range-trading\",\"range-breakout-strategy\"],\"hints\":[\"Did anything close outside the range?\",\"What does the card say happens after the first close outside?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which is the RSI(2) rule as taught?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"RSI(2) over 90: buy, momentum is strong\",\"rationale\":\"That's the opposite: RSI(2) buys short-term weakness inside an uptrend.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Close under the SMA 200 and RSI(2) under 10: buy\",\"rationale\":\"The SMA 200 filter keeps buys inside uptrends. Under it, oversold can keep falling.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"RSI(2) under 10: buy, and hold with no stop until RSI(2) is over 90\",\"rationale\":\"The exit is a close above the SMA 5, and the course requires a catastrophic stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Close above the SMA 200 and RSI(2) under 10: buy; exit on a close above the SMA 5; keep a catastrophic stop\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"RSI(2) buys a short, sharp dip inside an uptrend: the SMA 200 filter, RSI(2) under 10 to enter, a close above the SMA 5 to exit. The course adds a catastrophic stop, such as 3 ATR.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.7\",\"concepts\":[\"mean-reversion\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.7-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A mean-reversion rule wins 80% of its trades at +0.4R. What is the largest average loss, in R, at which it still breaks even?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.6,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"At break-even, 0.80 × 0.4 = 0.20 × loss, so the loss = 0.32 ÷ 0.20 = 1.6R. One rare loss well past that turns a high win rate into a losing rule.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.7\",\"concepts\":[\"return-skew\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Break-even: win rate × average win = loss rate × average loss.\",\"0.80 × 0.4 = 0.32. Divide by the loss rate.\",\"0.32 ÷ 0.20 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[65,85,5],\"aw\":[0.3,0.6,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(w * aw / (100 - w), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A mean-reversion rule wins {w}% of its trades at +{aw:1}R. What is the largest average loss, in R, at which it still breaks even?\",\"explanation\":\"At break-even, {w / 100:2} × {aw:1} = {(100 - w) / 100:2} × loss, so the loss = {w * aw / 100:3} ÷ {(100 - w) / 100:2} = {answer:2}R. One rare loss well past that turns a high win rate into a losing rule.\",\"hint\":\"{w * aw / 100:3} ÷ {(100 - w) / 100:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.7-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your RSI(2) backtest made more money without stops than with a 3-ATR catastrophic stop. A friend says you should drop the stop.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the reason to keep it anyway?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It caps the rare crash trade that the backtest may never have met\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It raises the win rate\",\"rationale\":\"Stops lower the win rate: some trades that would have recovered get stopped out.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It improves the backtest's results\",\"rationale\":\"Connors found the opposite in his tests. The stop is insurance, not an edge.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's needed only in trends, not in ranges\",\"rationale\":\"Gaps and crashes arrive in any regime. That tail risk is why the stop exists.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A negatively skewed rule's worst trade decides whether it survives. A backtest period without a crash flatters the no-stop version. The stop costs some return and caps the loss that could end the account.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.7\",\"concepts\":[\"mean-reversion\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a backtest show about crashes that didn't happen in its period?\",\"Which matters more for a negative-skew rule: the average trade or the worst one?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Range 97.00–100.00. Price spikes to 100.70, a new 20-candle high, then falls back. Where do Turtle Soup's entry and stop go?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A sell stop at 99.90; the stop at 100.80\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Sell at 100.70, the spike high; the stop at 101.70\",\"rationale\":\"You can't know the high while it's forming. The entry waits for price to fall back under the old high.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A sell stop at 99.90; the stop at 100.00\",\"rationale\":\"100.00 is the old high: any retest would stop you. The stop goes above the new extreme, 100.70.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A buy stop at 100.80; the stop at 99.90\",\"rationale\":\"That's the breakout side. Turtle Soup sells the failure.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Turtle Soup sells just under the old high, 99.90, only once price falls back through it, and puts the stop just above the new extreme, 100.80. The far edge of the range is the target.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.8\",\"concepts\":[\"failed-breakout-strategy\",\"false-breakout\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"How does the failed-breakout trade relate to the range breakout?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's the same trade with a tighter stop\",\"rationale\":\"It's the opposite direction: the fade sells where the breakout buys.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It only works in trends\",\"rationale\":\"The fade fails in a real trend, when the breakout keeps going. Its home is a range after a failed push.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's the mirror: same level, opposite bet; the breakout needs the range to end, the fade needs it to hold\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"One of the two must always win\",\"rationale\":\"On a random walk both lose about their costs.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Both trade the same level in opposite directions. The breakout pays when the range turns into a trend; the fade pays when the range holds after a failed push. The regime decides which card fits.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.8\",\"concepts\":[\"failed-breakout-strategy\",\"range-breakout-strategy\"],\"hints\":[\"Where does each trade enter, and in which direction?\",\"In which regime does each one win?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.8-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your last three breakout trades failed. Now LABX closes above a level touched three times, then closes above it again, both on twice the average volume.\",\"prompt\":\"Breakout, fade or pass?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Fade: breakouts have been failing lately\",\"rationale\":\"That's choosing from the last results. Three failures don't change what this chart shows.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Pass: three losses in a row mean the regime is random\",\"rationale\":\"At a 30% win rate, three losses in a row happen about a third of the time. The chart shows clear breakout signs.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Breakout: two closes outside with volume are breakout signs\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Fade with a smaller size, as a hedge\",\"rationale\":\"A smaller bet on the wrong card is still the wrong card. The signs point to the breakout.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Choose from the evidence on the chart, not from your last trades. At a 30% win rate, 0.7 × 0.7 × 0.7 ≈ 34%: three losses in a row are common and say little. Two closes outside on high volume are the breakout's signs.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.8\",\"concepts\":[\"failed-breakout-strategy\",\"market-regime\"],\"hints\":[\"What do the signs on this chart say, if you ignore your last trades?\",\"At a 30% win rate, how often do three losses in a row happen?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.9-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which pair adds independent evidence to a pullback setup?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"RSI under 30 and CCI under −100\",\"rationale\":\"Both measure recent price change. They agree for the same reason.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"MACD crossing zero and a 12/26 EMA crossover\",\"rationale\":\"MACD is built from those same two EMAs: one signal counted twice.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Stochastic and Williams %R\",\"rationale\":\"Williams %R is the Stochastic formula shifted by 100: their correlation is 1.00.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A tested support level and rising volume\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A level comes from structure; volume measures participation. They're built from different inputs, so when both agree, that's two reasons. The other pairs are one reason counted twice.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.9\",\"concepts\":[\"indicator-redundancy\",\"confluence\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.9-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which indicator is built from a different input than the other three?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"RSI\",\"rationale\":\"RSI uses closes, like ROC and MACD.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"OBV\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"ROC\",\"rationale\":\"ROC compares today's close with an earlier close: closes only, like RSI and MACD.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"MACD\",\"rationale\":\"MACD is built from EMAs of the close, like RSI and ROC.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"OBV adds or subtracts volume, so it carries information the close-based indicators don't. RSI, ROC and MACD are all built from closes and tend to move together.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.9\",\"concepts\":[\"indicator-redundancy\"],\"hints\":[\"What does each indicator take as its input?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.9-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After your filters, the setup has 25 trades, with results spread by 1.5R (one standard deviation). Using 1.96 × sd ÷ √n, how many R either side of the average does the 95% interval reach?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.59,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"1.96 × 1.5 ÷ √25 = 2.94 ÷ 5 = 0.59R either side. Unless the edge is bigger than that, 25 trades can't tell it from zero.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.9\",\"concepts\":[\"indicator-redundancy\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"√25 = 5.\",\"1.96 × 1.5 = 2.94.\",\"2.94 ÷ 5 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"q\":[4,10,1],\"sd\":[1,2,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(1.96 * sd / q, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"After your filters, the setup has {q * q} trades, with results spread by {sd:1}R (one standard deviation). Using 1.96 × sd ÷ √n, how many R either side of the average does the 95% interval reach?\",\"explanation\":\"1.96 × {sd:1} ÷ √{q * q} = {1.96 * sd:2} ÷ {q} = {answer:2}R either side. Unless the edge is bigger than that, {q * q} trades can't tell it from zero.\",\"hint\":\"{1.96 * sd:2} ÷ {q} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.10-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A backtest shows +0.15R per trade gross. The stop is 0.80 on LABX near 100.00, and a round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. What is the net expectancy per trade, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.1,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Cost in R = 0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 0.25R. Net = 0.15 − 0.25 = −0.10R per trade. A positive gross edge smaller than the cost in R is a losing strategy.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.10\",\"concepts\":[\"after-cost-evaluation\",\"costs-in-r\"],\"hints\":[\"First turn the cost into R: cost per unit ÷ stop distance.\",\"0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 0.25R.\",\"0.15 − 0.25 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"g\":[0.05,0.3,0.05],\"s\":[0.5,2,0.25]},\"answer\":\"round(g - 0.2 / s, 3)\",\"prompt\":\"A backtest shows +{g:2}R per trade gross. The stop is {s:2} on LABX near 100.00, and a round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. What is the net expectancy per trade, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Cost in R = 0.20 ÷ {s:2} = {0.2 / s:3}R. Net = {g:2} − {0.2 / s:3} = {answer:3}R per trade.\",\"hint\":\"{g:2} − 0.20 ÷ {s:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.10-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Over a year, your rule made +6R net. Holding LABX over the same year made the equivalent of +10R. In one run on a simulated random walk, the same rule made +5R net.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you conclude?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It works: +6R net is positive\",\"rationale\":\"Positive isn't enough. Holding did better, and random data did nearly as well.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It works, because it beat the random walk\",\"rationale\":\"By 1R, on one sample each: well within luck.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No evidence of skill: it trails holding, and random data did about as well\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy-and-hold doesn't matter for a trading rule\",\"rationale\":\"Holding is what you'd earn without trading. A rule has to beat it, after costs, to be worth the effort.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A rule earns its keep only if it beats the passive benchmark after costs and clearly beats the same rule on random data. This one does neither, so the +6R is no evidence of an edge.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.10\",\"concepts\":[\"after-cost-evaluation\",\"passive-benchmark\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the +6R with both benchmarks, not with zero.\",\"Would a 1R gap over one sample convince you?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.8.10-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A strategy shows +0.10R per trade over 40 trades, with results spread by 1.5R. What should you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Trade it at full size: +0.10R is a real edge\",\"rationale\":\"With 40 trades the interval is about ±0.46R. +0.10R can't be told from zero.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Drop it: 40 trades prove it doesn't work\",\"rationale\":\"40 trades can't prove it either way. The interval includes good edges and bad ones.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Raise the size to reach significance faster\",\"rationale\":\"Size doesn't add trades. It only adds risk while you're still unsure.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Keep testing: ±0.46R around +0.10R includes zero\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"1.96 × 1.5 ÷ √40 ≈ 0.46R, so the true expectancy could be anywhere from about −0.36R to +0.56R. Keep testing, at small size or in the simulator, until the interval excludes zero or the idea fails.\",\"lesson\":\"2.8.10\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\",\"after-cost-evaluation\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out 1.96 × 1.5 ÷ √40.\",\"Does the interval around +0.10R include zero?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"It's 20:00 in Bucharest on a Tuesday in January. Which market is in its regular session right now?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"US stocks\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The London Stock Exchange\",\"rationale\":\"London's session typically ends at 16:30 London time, 18:30 in Bucharest. By 20:00 it has closed.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neither: stock markets close in the evening\",\"rationale\":\"Evening in Bucharest is midday in New York. US stocks typically trade 16:30–23:00 in Bucharest time.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only the US pre-market\",\"rationale\":\"The pre-market ends when the regular session opens, at 16:30 in Bucharest. At 20:00 the regular session is running.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"In January, Bucharest is 7 hours ahead of New York, so 20:00 is 13:00 there: the middle of the US regular session, 09:30–16:00. London has closed; its session typically ends at 18:30 Bucharest time.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-hours\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Wednesday 28 October 2026. A US data release is due at 10:00 New York time, and your rule is to be flat 30 minutes before it.\",\"prompt\":\"By what time, in Bucharest, must you be flat?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"16:30\",\"rationale\":\"That uses the usual 7-hour gap. This week Europe is on winter time and the US isn't yet, so the gap is 6 hours.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"15:30\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"16:00\",\"rationale\":\"16:00 is the release itself in Bucharest time. You wanted to be flat 30 minutes earlier.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"17:00\",\"rationale\":\"That's the release under the usual 7-hour gap, and it skips the 30 minutes. Both steps need adjusting.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The EU left summer time on 25 October; the US only does on 1 November. That week New York is 6 hours behind Bucharest, so 10:00 there is 16:00 here, and flat 30 minutes earlier means 15:30. Most of the year the answer would be 16:30.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-hours\"],\"hints\":[\"Which clocks have changed by 28 October 2026: Europe's, the US's, or both?\",\"This week New York is 6 hours behind Bucharest, not 7.\",\"10:00 + 6 hours = 16:00. Then subtract 30 minutes.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"It's January. Your FX platform's clock reads 00:05, the same as your Bucharest clock, and a new daily candle has just started. EUR/USD's spread, 0.8 pips in the afternoon, is now 2.4 pips.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the most likely explanation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The broker raised its prices because you trade at night\",\"rationale\":\"Spreads widen at this hour for everyone: few orders arrive between New York's close and Asia's busy hours, so quotes are wider.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A major news release just came out\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing in the case points to news. A new daily candle on an FX platform marks 17:00 New York, the end of the FX day.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's just after 17:00 New York, the end of the FX day: a quiet hour when spreads often widen\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The platform's clock is wrong, so the candle is misplaced\",\"rationale\":\"Server time is a choice, not an error. FX platforms often set it so the daily candle closes at 17:00 New York.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Many FX platforms set their server clock so the daily candle closes at 17:00 New York time, which in winter is midnight in Bucharest. That hour sits between New York's close and Asia's busy hours. FX is open 24 hours, but it isn't equally liquid: spreads often widen there.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-hours\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"hints\":[\"In winter, what time is it in New York when Bucharest's clock reads 00:05?\",\"17:00 New York ends the FX trading day. Who is trading then?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Most of the year, when do London and New York overlap, in Bucharest time?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"10:00–14:00\",\"rationale\":\"10:00 is London's open in Bucharest time. New York hasn't started yet.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"15:00–19:00\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"16:30–23:00\",\"rationale\":\"That's the US stock market's regular session in Bucharest time, not the FX overlap.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"02:00–06:00\",\"rationale\":\"Those are Tokyo hours, the quieter Asian part of the FX day.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The overlap runs from 08:00 to 12:00 New York time, while both centres trade. Bucharest is usually 7 hours ahead: 15:00–19:00. In the weeks when US and EU clocks disagree it starts an hour earlier.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fx-sessions\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why are FX spreads usually tightest during the London–New York overlap?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Volatility is lowest then, so dealers take less risk\",\"rationale\":\"Volatility is usually highest in the overlap. Spreads are tight despite that, because so many orders arrive.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Regulators cap spreads during the main session\",\"rationale\":\"There's no such cap. Spreads are set by dealers and markets, and they tighten when order flow is heavy.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Many participants trade at once, so dealers compete and can offset orders quickly\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Brokers charge commissions instead of spreads in those hours\",\"rationale\":\"Pricing models don't switch by the hour. The spread narrows because more orders meet, not because the fee moves elsewhere.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Activity and volatility gather at the opens and in the overlap. With more trades, dealers can offset positions quickly and compete for the flow, so spreads narrow. In quiet hours fewer orders mean wider spreads and thinner moves.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fx-sessions\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about how many traders are active in each window.\",\"What happens to a dealer's spread when many orders compete?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A course sells 'killzones': it says the London open sets the day's direction. You test a breakout rule in each window on 200 synthetic days. The London open has bigger ranges, but long and short breakouts win equally often there.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your test support?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The killzone works: bigger ranges prove the direction is set there\",\"rationale\":\"Bigger ranges show more movement, not a direction. Longs and shorts won equally often, so there's no directional edge.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: sessions make no difference to trading\",\"rationale\":\"Ranges did differ. Movement per unit of cost is a real reason to prefer some hours.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trade only the London open, long, since it moves most\",\"rationale\":\"Moving most says nothing about which way. A one-sided rule needs evidence of direction, which the test didn't find.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The London open is a volatility window: more movement, no known direction\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Activity bunching at opens and overlaps is well documented; directional 'roles' for sessions aren't (grade D). Your test agrees: more range, no side favoured. Use the hours for movement per unit of cost, not for direction.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fx-sessions\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate two claims: 'moves more' and 'moves in a known direction'.\",\"Which claim did your test check, and what did it find?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.3-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"On a typical stock market day, when are volume and volatility usually lowest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In the first 30 minutes\",\"rationale\":\"The open is usually the busiest part of the day: overnight news and orders all arrive at once.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In the last 30 minutes\",\"rationale\":\"Activity usually picks up again before the close, helped by the closing auction.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Around midday\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They stay about the same all day\",\"rationale\":\"Intraday activity has a well-documented U shape: high at both ends, low in the middle.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Volume and volatility follow a U: highest after the open and before the close, lowest around midday. The open absorbs overnight news; the close draws orders aimed at the official closing price.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.3\",\"concepts\":[\"intraday-cycle\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.3-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You trade LABX with a fixed 0.30 stop. Your journal shows that 70% of your stop-outs happen in the first 30 minutes, when the 5-minute ATR is about 0.60.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best change?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Set the stop from the hour's ATR, 1.20 at the open, and size down so the loss stays within 1%\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep the 0.30 stop and add size to win back the losses faster\",\"rationale\":\"More size on a stop inside the noise only makes each loss bigger. The stop, not the size, is the problem.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Move the stop further away once price comes close to it\",\"rationale\":\"Moving the stop after entry breaks the 1R you planned. Set the right distance before you enter.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Keep the 0.30 stop: stop-outs are just bad luck\",\"rationale\":\"70% in one window isn't luck. At the open, 0.30 is half of one candle's normal move.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"At the open a 0.30 stop is half of one normal 5-minute candle, so noise hits it. A stop of 2 × ATR, 1.20, sits outside the noise, and the smaller size keeps the risk at 1%. The stop follows the hour's volatility.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.3\",\"concepts\":[\"intraday-cycle\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the stop with one candle's normal move at the open.\",\"A wider stop needs fewer units to keep the same risk.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.3-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In a quiet window LABX's 5-minute ATR is 0.15, and your stop is 2 × ATR. A round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. What are the costs, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.67,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"The stop is 2 × 0.15 = 0.30. Costs in R = 0.20 ÷ 0.30 = 0.67R: two thirds of every R goes to costs. At the open, with a 1.20 stop, it would be 0.17R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.3\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"intraday-cycle\"],\"hints\":[\"First find the stop distance: 2 × ATR.\",\"Costs in R = cost per unit ÷ stop distance.\",\"0.20 ÷ 0.30.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"atr\":[0.1,0.3,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(0.2 / (2 * atr), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"In a quiet window LABX's 5-minute ATR is {atr:2}, and your stop is 2 × ATR. A round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. What are the costs, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"The stop is 2 × {atr:2} = {2 * atr:2}. Costs in R = 0.20 ÷ {2 * atr:2} = {answer:2}R. The quieter the window, the tighter the stop and the bigger the share of R that costs take.\",\"hint\":\"0.20 ÷ {2 * atr:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which describes a 15-minute opening range breakout on the long side?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A buy limit at the range's low, betting the range holds\",\"rationale\":\"Buying the low of the range is range trading, the opposite bet. ORB enters on the break.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A buy stop just above the first 15 minutes' high, a stop-loss at the range's low, out by the close\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Buy at the open, before the range forms\",\"rationale\":\"The range has to form first: its high and low set both the entry and the stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A buy stop above the range, with N chosen after seeing which length worked today\",\"rationale\":\"N is fixed in advance. Choosing it after the fact fits the rule to the chart.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The opening range is the high and low of the first N minutes, with N fixed in advance. A long ORB places a buy stop just above the high, puts the stop-loss at the other edge and exits at a target or the close.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.4\",\"concepts\":[\"opening-range-breakout\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Opening range 99.70–100.30. Entry 100.35, stop 99.65: 0.70 away. A round trip costs 0.20 per unit and the stop entry slips 0.05. What do costs and slippage take per trade, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.36,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Costs plus slippage: 0.20 + 0.05 = 0.25 per unit. Divided by the 0.70 stop: 0.36R. A +0.13R gross edge would net −0.23R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.4\",\"concepts\":[\"opening-range-breakout\",\"costs-in-r\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the round trip and the slippage per unit.\",\"Divide by the stop distance.\",\"0.25 ÷ 0.70.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"dist\":[0.4,2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(0.25 / dist, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An ORB on LABX: stop {dist:2} below the entry. A round trip costs 0.20 per unit and the stop entry slips 0.05. What do costs and slippage take per trade, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Costs plus slippage: 0.20 + 0.05 = 0.25 per unit. Divided by the {dist:2} stop: {answer:2}R. A narrow range means a tight stop, and costs take a bigger share of every R.\",\"hint\":\"0.25 ÷ {dist:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your ORB card trades only days in play, with an opening range under half the daily ATR, and stands aside around scheduled releases. Four mornings:\",\"prompt\":\"Which day passes all the filters?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"No news, relative volume 0.8×, range 30% of the daily ATR\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing makes this day in play: no catalyst, below-average volume. Quiet days are where breaks fail.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Earnings beat overnight, relative volume 4×, range 95% of the daily ATR\",\"rationale\":\"In play, but the range is almost a full day's move. The stop would be too wide, with much of the move already spent.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A rate decision due 20 minutes after the open, normal volume\",\"rationale\":\"A scheduled release minutes away means stop orders can fill far past their price. The card stands aside.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Earnings beat overnight, relative volume 4×, range 35% of the daily ATR\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The earnings day with a 35% range has a catalyst, heavy relative volume and a modest range: the conditions in which ORB has a chance. Each of the others fails a filter: not in play, a range that's too big, or a release that makes fills unpredictable.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.4\",\"concepts\":[\"opening-range-breakout\"],\"hints\":[\"Check each day against every filter, not just one.\",\"A day needs a catalyst, high relative volume, a modest range and no release nearby.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":1,\"scenario\":\"LABX gaps up 3.5% at the open after an earnings beat. Relative volume in the first minutes is 4×.\",\"prompt\":\"Which setup does the card point to?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gap and go: a catalyst and heavy volume\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Gap fill: big gaps always come back\",\"rationale\":\"Gaps with a catalyst and 3× or more relative volume are the ones that tend not to fill. Fading them stands against real news.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Pass: never trade on earnings days\",\"rationale\":\"Passing is always allowed, but this is exactly the gap-and-go setup: catalyst first, then heavy volume.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Gap fill, because the gap is above 2%\",\"rationale\":\"Size alone doesn't decide. A fade needs no catalyst and modest volume; this gap has a catalyst and 4× volume.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The card checks the catalyst first and relative volume second. An earnings beat with 4× volume is a gap with a cause: the gap-and-go setup, entered on a break of the early range. It's still grade C to D, a rule from practice, so size it for slippage.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.5\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-strategies\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX gaps up 2.5% on an unconfirmed rumour. Relative volume is 1.1×, and the open is above last week's high.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the card say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gap and go: the gap is big\",\"rationale\":\"Size isn't a catalyst. A rumour with ordinary volume lacks both conditions for a Go.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Fade it back to yesterday's close\",\"rationale\":\"A fade needs a moderate gap with price still inside last week's range. This one opened above it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Buy half size as a compromise\",\"rationale\":\"Half a trade without a setup is still a trade without a setup. The card has a third answer for this.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Pass: neither setup's conditions are met\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"No real catalyst and ordinary volume rule out gap and go. A gap fill needs a moderate gap inside last week's range, and this one opened above it. When neither card fits, pass: a valid decision, not a missed one.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.5\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-strategies\"],\"hints\":[\"Check the catalyst and the volume for gap and go.\",\"Check the gap's size and last week's range for a fade.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"On a random walk with a daily standard deviation of 2.00, a stock opens 1.00 above yesterday's close. Φ(0.5) = 0.691. What's the chance price touches yesterday's close that day, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":61.8,\"tolerance\":0.3,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"d ÷ σ = 1.00 ÷ 2.00 = 0.5. The touch chance is 2 × (1 − Φ(0.5)) = 2 × 0.309 = 61.8%. On a volatile stock, a gap of this size 'fills' more often than not, with no edge at all.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.5\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-strategies\",\"random-walk\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the gap by the daily standard deviation.\",\"Touch chance = 2 × (1 − Φ(d ÷ σ)).\",\"2 × (1 − 0.691).\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Compared with swing trading, what's true of day trading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Lower costs, because positions are held for less time\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are paid per round trip. More trades with tighter stops make them a bigger share of each R.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"More gap risk, because it trades the open\",\"rationale\":\"Day traders are flat by the close, so overnight and weekend gaps can't reach them. Swing traders carry those.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Less screen time, because trades are short\",\"rationale\":\"Short trades need someone watching. Day trading demands the most screen time of the styles.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No overnight gap risk, but costs take a bigger share of each R\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Day traders close every position by the end of the session, so gaps can't reach them overnight. The price is many trades and tight stops: costs are paid often and take a big share of each R. Swing trading reverses the trade-off.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.6\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-styles\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You plan 15 trades a week on LABX with a 0.80 stop. A round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. What are your weekly costs, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3.75,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Per trade: 0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 0.25R. Over 15 trades: 15 × 0.25 = 3.75R a week. Your edge has to clear that before you keep anything.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.6\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"trading-styles\"],\"hints\":[\"Costs in R per trade = cost per unit ÷ stop distance.\",\"Then multiply by the number of trades.\",\"15 × (0.20 ÷ 0.80).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"trades\":[3,30,1],\"stop\":[0.4,4,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(trades * 0.2 / stop, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You plan {trades} trades a week on LABX with a {stop:2} stop. A round trip costs about 0.20 per unit. What are your weekly costs, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Per trade: 0.20 ÷ {stop:2} ≈ {0.2 / stop:2}R. Over {trades} trades: {answer:2}R a week. Your edge has to clear that before you keep anything.\",\"hint\":\"{trades} × (0.20 ÷ {stop:2}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Ana works 09:00–18:00 in Bucharest and can check her phone at lunch. Her account is 2,000. Her journal shows two revenge trades after losses last month.\",\"prompt\":\"Which plan fits her constraints?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Day trade the US open at 16:30 from her phone at work\",\"rationale\":\"She's at work until 18:00. Trading from a phone between tasks invites rushed entries, and revenge trades already show in her journal.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Scalp during her lunch break to keep each trade short\",\"rationale\":\"Scalping has the highest costs in R and needs full attention. A lunch break gives neither.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Swing trading on daily candles: orders set in the evening, small size, stops placed with the order\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Trade only at weekends, when she's free\",\"rationale\":\"Stock and FX markets are closed at weekends. Crypto trades, but thinly, and the plan ignores her real constraints.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Her hours rule out watching the market, her small account favours fewer trades, and revenge trades call for orders set calmly in advance. Swing trading with evening orders and stops attached fits all three. Not day trading is a sound choice for her.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.6\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-styles\"],\"hints\":[\"List her constraints: hours, account, behaviour.\",\"Which style lets her decide while the market isn't in front of her?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.7-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Stop 0.40, target 0.40, round-trip costs 0.20 per unit. What win rate breaks even after costs, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"A win nets 0.40 − 0.20 = 0.20; a loss costs 0.40 + 0.20 = 0.60. Break-even = 0.60 ÷ 0.80 = 75%. Before costs the same trade would need 50%.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-drag\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Take the costs off the win and add them to the loss.\",\"Break-even = loss ÷ (loss + win).\",\"0.60 ÷ (0.60 + 0.20).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"d\":[0.3,3,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round((d + 0.2) / (2 * d) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Stop {d:2}, target {d:2}, round-trip costs 0.20 per unit. What win rate breaks even after costs, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"A win nets {d - 0.2:2}; a loss costs {d + 0.2:2}. Break-even = {d + 0.2:2} ÷ {2 * d:2} = {answer:1}%. Before costs the same trade would need 50%.\",\"hint\":\"{d + 0.2:2} ÷ {2 * d:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.7-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your winning trades make 0.80 per unit on average before costs, and a round trip costs 0.20. What's the cost drag, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Cost drag = costs ÷ average gross profit = 0.20 ÷ 0.80 = 25%. A quarter of every win goes to costs. Doubling the average win to 1.60 would halve it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-drag\"],\"hints\":[\"Cost drag = costs ÷ average gross profit.\",\"0.20 ÷ 0.80, as a percentage.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"g\":[0.3,4,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(0.2 / g * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Your winning trades make {g:2} per unit on average before costs, and a round trip costs 0.20. What's the cost drag, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Cost drag = costs ÷ average gross profit = 0.20 ÷ {g:2} = {answer:1}%. The bigger the average win, the smaller the share costs take.\",\"hint\":\"0.20 ÷ {g:2}, as a percentage.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.7-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does retail scalping rarely survive costs?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Regulators forbid retail traders from holding positions for under a minute\",\"rationale\":\"There's no such ban. The problem is arithmetic and competition, not rules.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Small targets are hit more often, so it works with enough discipline\",\"rationale\":\"A higher hit rate doesn't help if break-even rises faster: 83% at a 0.30 target. Discipline can't change the arithmetic.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Brokers charge scalpers wider spreads than other clients\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is the same for everyone at that moment. It just weighs more on a small target.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Targets are small against fixed costs, and the other side is professionals who earn the spread with faster tools\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Costs per unit are fixed, so a small target pushes break-even toward 83% and cost drag past half of each win. On the other side sit market makers and high-frequency firms that earn the spread. Retail base rates agree: in Brazil, 97% of persistent day traders lost.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-drag\",\"retail-loss-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens to break-even as the target shrinks?\",\"Who earns the spread that the scalper pays?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.8-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why can't a stop-loss protect you from a weekend gap?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's an order that fills at the first available price; if Monday opens past it, it fills there\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Brokers cancel all stops over the weekend\",\"rationale\":\"Stops stay in place. The problem is that no price exists between Friday's close and Monday's open.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It can: a stop always fills at its own price\",\"rationale\":\"That's a guaranteed stop, a different product. A normal stop becomes a market order once triggered.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stops only work during the main session\",\"rationale\":\"Stops can trigger whenever the market trades. Over a weekend it doesn't trade, so price can jump past them.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A stop-loss becomes a market order when triggered. Over a weekend nothing trades, so if Monday's first price is past the stop, that's where it fills. 47 units with a 2.00 stop lost 216.10 when the first bid was 95.50.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.8\",\"concepts\":[\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\",\"gap-risk\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.8-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You'll hold LABX over a weekend: entry 50.00, daily ATR 1.00, worst-case gap 3 × ATR. Your gap budget is 2R, 200.00. Commission is 0.05% per fill, rounded up to the cent. How many units at most?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":65,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"The worst-case exit is 50.00 − 3.00 = 47.00. Each unit loses 3.00 plus about 0.05 in commissions. 65 units lose 195.00 + 1.63 + 1.53 = 198.16; 66 would lose 201.21, over 200.00.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.8\",\"concepts\":[\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"The gap exit is 50.00 − 3 × 1.00.\",\"Each unit loses 3.00 plus commissions on 50.00 and on 47.00.\",\"200.00 ÷ 3.0485 ≈ 65.6. Round down, then check.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.9.8-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When does FX rollover typically happen in Bucharest, and what should a short-term trader expect around it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"17:00 Bucharest time; spreads narrow as traders close positions\",\"rationale\":\"17:00 is the New York time. In Bucharest it's usually midnight, and spreads tend to widen, not narrow.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Usually 00:00, or 23:00 when US and EU clocks disagree; spreads often widen, so tight stops can be hit\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"At London's open, 10:00; it's the busiest moment of the day\",\"rationale\":\"Rollover marks the end of the FX day in New York, one of the quietest moments, not London's open.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Always 00:00 in Bucharest; nothing changes for traders\",\"rationale\":\"In the weeks when US and EU clocks disagree it's 23:00. And spreads around it often widen enough to hit tight stops.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Rollover follows New York: 17:00 there is 00:00 in Bucharest most of the year and 23:00 in the misaligned weeks. Few traders are active then, so spreads often widen for some minutes. Keep tight stops and new entries away from it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.9.8\",\"concepts\":[\"fx-rollover\"],\"hints\":[\"Rollover is set in New York time: 17:00.\",\"How many hours ahead is Bucharest, and what changes in the misaligned weeks?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does it mean that an expected number is 'priced in'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The release has been delayed, so price can't react yet\",\"rationale\":\"Priced in is about expectations, not timing. A delayed release simply isn't out yet.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The number is good for the economy, so price has to rise\",\"rationale\":\"Good news alone doesn't move price. If it was expected, traders already bought on it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Traders have already positioned for it, so its arrival adds little new\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Brokers have widened spreads, so the move is already paid for\",\"rationale\":\"Wider spreads raise your costs; they don't absorb the news. Priced in means expectations are already in the price.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Prices reflect what traders expect. When the actual matches the consensus, nothing new arrives, so there's little to trade on. The move comes from the surprise: actual minus consensus.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.1\",\"concepts\":[\"news-surprise\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A synthetic payrolls report: consensus 170,000 jobs, actual 125,000. Surprises in this report usually vary by 50,000. What is the standardised surprise z?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.9,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"z\"},\"explanation\":\"Surprise = 125,000 − 170,000 = −45,000. z = −45,000 ÷ 50,000 = −0.9: a sizeable miss, close to one usual surprise, and on the cold side.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.1\",\"concepts\":[\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"Surprise = actual − consensus. Keep the sign.\",\"z = surprise ÷ the usual size of surprises, σ.\",\"−45,000 ÷ 50,000 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"cons\":[100,250,10],\"diff\":[-120,120,5],\"sigma\":[40,80,10]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(diff) >= 10\",\"cons + diff >= 20\"],\"answer\":\"round(diff / sigma, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A synthetic payrolls report: consensus {cons * 1000} jobs, actual {(cons + diff) * 1000}. Surprises in this report usually vary by {sigma * 1000}. What is the standardised surprise z?\",\"explanation\":\"Surprise = {(cons + diff) * 1000} − {cons * 1000} = {diff * 1000}. z = {diff * 1000} ÷ {sigma * 1000} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{diff * 1000} ÷ {sigma * 1000} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Payrolls surprises usually vary by 60,000. Today's report beats consensus by 15,000. LABX ticks up 0.10, then drifts back. A trader buys anyway, calling it 'a strong beat'.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the best reading of this release?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A strong beat: any number above consensus is a buy signal\",\"rationale\":\"Above consensus isn't enough. 15,000 is a quarter of the usual surprise: z = 0.25.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A miss, because the move faded\",\"rationale\":\"The number did beat consensus. The fading shows the surprise was too small to matter, not that it was a miss.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Impossible to judge without last month's number\",\"rationale\":\"Revisions matter, but you have what you need here: the surprise is a quarter of the usual size.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Mostly noise: z = 0.25, where reactions are unreliable\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"z = 15,000 ÷ 60,000 = 0.25. Surprises that small happen all the time and give no reliable direction; a small tick that fades is what noise looks like.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.1\",\"concepts\":[\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"Standardise the surprise before reading anything into it.\",\"z = 15,000 ÷ 60,000. Under 0.5 is usually noise.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"On a calendar row, which value is the actual compared with to measure the surprise?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Previous\",\"rationale\":\"Previous is last period's number, already traded. What the market expects now is the consensus.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Consensus\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Revised\",\"rationale\":\"The revision corrects last period's number. It matters, but the surprise is measured against the consensus.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Impact\",\"rationale\":\"Impact is the calendar's rating of how much an event usually moves markets, not a number to compare with.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The consensus is the middle of the forecasts, roughly what's priced in. The surprise is actual minus consensus; the previous and revised values describe last period.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.2\",\"concepts\":[\"economic-calendar\",\"news-surprise\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A synthetic US release is at 08:30 New York time. It's the week after Europe's clocks went back in late October; the US clocks change next Sunday.\",\"prompt\":\"What time is the release in Bucharest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"13:30\",\"rationale\":\"That would need a 5-hour gap. New York is usually 7 hours behind Bucharest, and 6 this week.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"15:30\",\"rationale\":\"15:30 is the usual time, with a 7-hour gap. This week Europe has changed its clocks and the US hasn't: 6 hours.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"14:30\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"16:30\",\"rationale\":\"That adds 8 hours. New York is usually 7 hours behind Bucharest, and 6 this week.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Normally 08:30 in New York is 15:30 in Bucharest. Europe ends summer time on the last Sunday of October, the US on the first Sunday of November. In between, the gap is 6 hours: 14:30.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.2\",\"concepts\":[\"economic-calendar\"],\"hints\":[\"New York is usually 7 hours behind Bucharest.\",\"Europe has moved its clocks back but the US hasn't yet: the gap shrinks by one hour.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan: flat 15 minutes before red events, half size before orange ones, ignore yellow. Thursday has a yellow housing report at 15:30 and an orange jobless-claims report at the same minute. You hold a full-size LABX swing long.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your plan say to do before 15:30?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the housing report is yellow\",\"rationale\":\"Two events land at the same minute. The higher tier, orange, sets the policy.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Go flat: two events at once count as red\",\"rationale\":\"Your plan has no such rule. Stacked events raise volatility, but by your rules orange means half size.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Decide at 15:29, from the price action\",\"rationale\":\"Triage happens before the week starts. Deciding at T−1 minute is exactly what the plan prevents.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Cut to half size before 15:30\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"When events share a minute, the higher impact tier decides. Orange means half size, decided before the week and carried out at the planned time.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.2\",\"concepts\":[\"economic-calendar\",\"news-policy\"],\"hints\":[\"When events share a minute, the higher tier sets the policy.\",\"In this plan, orange means half size.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.3-1\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What typically happens to LABX's market in the minute around a big scheduled release?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Spreads narrow, because more traders are watching\",\"rationale\":\"Attention rises, but dealers widen quotes and pull orders before the number. Liquidity is thinnest exactly then.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Spreads widen several times over, resting orders thin out, and price can jump across levels\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trading pauses until the number is confirmed\",\"rationale\":\"Markets keep trading. The problem isn't a pause; it's that price jumps with few orders in between.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing changes for stops: they still fill at their price\",\"rationale\":\"A triggered stop becomes a market order. In a thin, jumping market it fills at the first price available.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Before a big number, dealers widen quotes and pull resting orders. At the release, price can jump through empty levels, and liquidity usually returns within a minute or two.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.3\",\"concepts\":[\"news-liquidity\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.3-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're long 40 LABX with a stop at 98.50. At the release, the first bid after the jump is 97.70. Ignoring commission, how much slippage did you pay, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":32,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The stop sold at 97.70, 0.80 below its 98.50 level. 40 × 0.80 = 32.00 of slippage on top of the planned loss.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.3\",\"concepts\":[\"news-liquidity\",\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"Slippage is the gap between the stop price and the fill.\",\"98.50 − 97.70 = 0.80 a unit. Multiply by 40.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[20,60,1],\"stop\":[96,99,0.1],\"slip\":[0.2,2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(units * slip, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You're long {units} LABX with a stop at {stop:2}. At the release, the first bid after the jump is {stop - slip:2}. Ignoring commission, how much slippage did you pay, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"The stop sold at {stop - slip:2}, {slip:2} below its {stop:2} level. {units} × {slip:2} = {answer:2} of slippage on top of the planned loss.\",\"hint\":\"{stop:2} − {stop - slip:2} = {slip:2} a unit. Multiply by {units}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.3-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long LABX with a stop at 99.00. At the release the mid price dips to 99.30, then recovers. For a few seconds the spread was 0.80. Your stop was filled.\",\"prompt\":\"Why did the stop fill when the mid never reached 99.00?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The broker hunted your stop\",\"rationale\":\"No intent is needed. A long stop triggers on the bid, and with a 0.80 spread the bid sat 0.40 under the mid.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Stops trigger on the mid with a 0.50 buffer\",\"rationale\":\"There's no buffer rule. A long stop triggers when the bid reaches it, and the bid is the mid minus half the spread.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The bid, 99.30 − 0.40 = 98.90, fell below your stop\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It couldn't have: the fill must be an error\",\"rationale\":\"It's normal at news time. 99.30 minus half of 0.80 puts the bid at 98.90, under 99.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A long stop sells, so it watches the bid. With the spread at 0.80, the bid was 0.40 below the mid: 98.90, under the 99.00 stop. That's a spread-triggered stop.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.3\",\"concepts\":[\"news-liquidity\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"hints\":[\"A long stop sells, so it watches the bid.\",\"Bid = mid − half the spread.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which numbers make up the headline of the US jobs report?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Payrolls, CPI and the policy rate\",\"rationale\":\"CPI is the inflation report and the rate is set by the central bank. The jobs report covers jobs, unemployment and wages.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The unemployment rate only, revised once a year\",\"rationale\":\"Payrolls lead the report, and each month is revised in the next two reports, plus a yearly benchmark.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Weekly jobless claims and payrolls, never revised\",\"rationale\":\"Claims are a separate weekly release, and payroll figures are revised, sometimes by a lot.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Payrolls, the unemployment rate and average hourly earnings, with revisions to the two prior months\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Payrolls come from a survey of firms, the unemployment rate from a survey of households, and hourly earnings track wages. Each report also revises the two previous months.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.4\",\"concepts\":[\"nonfarm-payrolls\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A synthetic payrolls report beats consensus by 70,000. In this report, the margin of error on the monthly change is about 120,000.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you read the beat?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A reliable sign the economy is accelerating\",\"rationale\":\"The beat is smaller than the report's own margin of error. Statistically, it can't be told apart from no surprise.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Inside the noise, though price may still react to it\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Meaningless, so price won't move\",\"rationale\":\"Markets trade the headline even when it's noisy. It's statistically weak, but price can still jump.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A miss, because 70,000 is less than 120,000\",\"rationale\":\"It's still above consensus. The margin of error tells you how reliable the beat is, not its sign.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A 70,000 beat inside a 120,000 margin of error is statistically noise. Markets still trade the headline, then reassess as revisions and details arrive.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.4\",\"concepts\":[\"nonfarm-payrolls\",\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the surprise with the report's own margin of error.\",\"A beat inside the margin of error is statistically noise; markets may still trade it.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Payrolls 80,000 above consensus. Unemployment rate 0.1 points below consensus. Hourly earnings 0.2% above. Revisions to the prior two months: −60,000.\",\"prompt\":\"How do you classify the report?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Clearly hot\",\"rationale\":\"Three readings are hot, but the revisions cut 60,000 jobs. Clear needs every reading to agree.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Clearly cold\",\"rationale\":\"Only the revisions point cold. Payrolls, unemployment and wages all point hot.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Mixed\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Can't classify it without the price reaction\",\"rationale\":\"Classify from the numbers before reacting. The first minutes of price action can mislead.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Payrolls, the unemployment rate and wages point hot, but the revisions point cold. Not every reading agrees, so the report is mixed, and two waves are likely.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.4\",\"concepts\":[\"nonfarm-payrolls\"],\"hints\":[\"Mark each reading hot or cold. Lower unemployment is hot.\",\"Clear only when all four readings agree.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.5-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why do markets focus on core CPI month-on-month rather than the headline?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Core leaves out volatile food and energy, so it shows the trend that rates respond to\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Core includes more prices than the headline\",\"rationale\":\"It's the opposite: core is the headline without food and energy.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Core is the index the Fed's 2% target is defined on\",\"rationale\":\"The Fed's 2% target uses a different index, PCE. Core CPI matters because it strips out volatile prices.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Core comes out a day before the headline\",\"rationale\":\"Both come out together, in the same release. Core matters because it removes the most volatile prices.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Food and energy prices swing from month to month. Without them, core shows the underlying trend, and that's what central banks, and so rates, respond to.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.5\",\"concepts\":[\"cpi-inflation\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.5-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Core CPI month-on-month: consensus 0.3%, actual 0.5%. Core surprises usually vary by 0.1 points. What is the standardised surprise z?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"z\"},\"explanation\":\"Surprise = 0.5 − 0.3 = +0.2 points. z = 0.2 ÷ 0.1 = +2.0: a large hot surprise.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.5\",\"concepts\":[\"cpi-inflation\",\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"Surprise = actual − consensus, in points.\",\"z = surprise ÷ the usual spread of surprises.\",\"0.2 ÷ 0.1 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"cons\":[0.1,0.5,0.1],\"diff\":[-0.3,0.3,0.1],\"sigma\":[0.1,0.2,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(diff) >= 0.1\",\"cons + diff >= -0.2\"],\"answer\":\"round(diff / sigma, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Core CPI month-on-month: consensus {cons:1}%, actual {cons + diff:1}%. Core surprises usually vary by {sigma:2} points. What is the standardised surprise z?\",\"explanation\":\"Surprise = {cons + diff:1} − {cons:1} = {diff:1} points. z = {diff:1} ÷ {sigma:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{diff:1} ÷ {sigma:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.5-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your plan: if headline and core CPI disagree, stay flat until 5 minutes after the release. Headline comes in 0.2 above consensus; core 0.1 below. One minute after the release, LABX has fallen 0.80 and looks like it's breaking down.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your plan say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sell now: the first wave shows the direction\",\"rationale\":\"The first wave follows the headline. With core disagreeing, a second wave the other way is common.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy now, betting on the reversal\",\"rationale\":\"Fading the first wave is a different strategy, not your plan. The rule is to stay flat and let the details settle.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Stay flat until at least 5 minutes after the release\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Sell half: a compromise\",\"rationale\":\"Half a position still breaks a rule written to keep you flat. Waiting costs nothing but a missed move.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Headline and core disagree, so the plan says flat until T+5. The first wave follows the headline; the second often follows core, and stops placed in the first can miss the turn.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.5\",\"concepts\":[\"cpi-inflation\",\"news-policy\"],\"hints\":[\"Do headline and core point the same way?\",\"If they disagree, the plan's answer doesn't depend on the chart.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.6-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Markets price a 100% chance of a 25 bp hike. The bank hikes 25 bp, with the guidance everyone expected. What is the likely reaction?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A big rally in the currency: rates went up\",\"rationale\":\"The hike was fully expected and already in the price. Without a surprise, there's little left to move it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The currency falls, because hikes hurt growth\",\"rationale\":\"That's a story, not a surprise. With everything as priced, the likeliest result is little net move.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A big move one way or the other: decisions always move markets\",\"rationale\":\"Decisions move markets by their surprise. A fully priced, as-expected decision usually moves them little.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Little reaction: the hike was already priced in\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Price moves on the gap between the decision and what was priced. A 100% priced hike delivered with the expected guidance adds nothing new.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\",\"concepts\":[\"central-bank-decisions\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.6-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Markets price a 40% chance of a 25 bp hike, otherwise no change. The bank holds. How large is the dovish surprise, in basis points?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"bp\"},\"explanation\":\"Expected change = 40% × 25 = 10 bp. A hold delivers 0, so it's 10 bp more dovish than priced.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\",\"concepts\":[\"central-bank-decisions\",\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"Expected change = probability × size of the move.\",\"The surprise is what happened minus what was expected.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[10,90,5],\"move\":[25,50,25]},\"answer\":\"round(p / 100 * move, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Markets price a {p}% chance of a {move} bp hike, otherwise no change. The bank holds. How large is the dovish surprise, in basis points?\",\"explanation\":\"Expected change = {p}% × {move} = {answer} bp. A hold delivers 0, so it's {answer} bp more dovish than priced.\",\"hint\":\"{p}% of {move} bp = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.6-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Markets price three rate cuts this year. The bank cuts once, as expected, and says 'one more cut may follow'.\",\"prompt\":\"Relative to expectations, how does the market read this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Dovish: the bank cut rates\",\"rationale\":\"It did cut, but the market expected three cuts in all. Signalling two is tighter than that.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Hawkish: fewer cuts than priced\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neutral: the cut was expected\",\"rationale\":\"The cut was expected; the guidance wasn't. Two cuts in all is fewer than the three priced.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Unknowable until the next meeting\",\"rationale\":\"Guidance moves prices on the day, against what's priced now.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Hawkish and dovish are relative. The market priced three cuts; the bank signals two. Fewer cuts than priced is a hawkish surprise, even on a day rates went down.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\",\"concepts\":[\"central-bank-decisions\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the cuts priced, then the cuts signalled.\",\"Tighter than priced is hawkish, whatever the rate did today.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.7-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"On Monday you wrote: 'CPI Wednesday 15:30: flat by 15:15.' At 15:10 on Wednesday your LABX long is up 1.5R and the chart looks strong.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Close the position by 15:15, as written\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep it: a winning trade can absorb a gap\",\"rationale\":\"Open profit doesn't cap a gap. A large gap can erase it and more; the policy was set for exactly this moment.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Keep it, and widen the stop so the spike can't hit it\",\"rationale\":\"A wider stop at the same size raises the money at risk, and a gap can skip it anyway.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Decide at 15:29, depending on the price\",\"rationale\":\"The point of the policy is not deciding under pressure at T−1 minute.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The policy was decided calmly, before the week. A strong chart and open profit don't change the gap risk at 15:30, so you follow the plan and go flat by 15:15.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.7\",\"concepts\":[\"news-policy\"],\"hints\":[\"Who decided the policy, and when?\",\"Open profit doesn't make a gap smaller.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.7-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"You want to keep a LABX long from 100.00 through a red event. ATR is 0.90, and you plan for a gap of 4 × ATR. With 0.05% commission on each side, how many whole units keep the gap loss within 100.00?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":27,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"The gap exit is 100.00 − 3.60 = 96.40. 27 units lose 97.20, plus 1.35 and 1.31 of commissions: 99.86. 28 units would lose 103.55.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.7\",\"concepts\":[\"news-policy\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"The gap is 4 × 0.90 = 3.60 below entry: an exit at 96.40.\",\"Each unit loses 3.60 plus about 0.10 of commissions.\",\"100.00 ÷ 3.70 ≈ 27. Check 27 and 28 with both commissions.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.7-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is trading the first seconds after a release not an edge for a retail trader?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The direction of the first move is random\",\"rationale\":\"Not quite: the first move does tend to follow the surprise. The problem is that you can't get there first.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Brokers forbid trading during releases\",\"rationale\":\"Some brokers restrict orders or raise margin around news, but the core problem is speed and cost.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Most of the repricing happens within seconds, before you can act, at the widest spreads\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The move always happens before the number is published\",\"rationale\":\"The big move comes after the number. The problem is speed: algorithms reprice in seconds, before you can click.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Algorithms reprice within a second or two. A person who reads, decides and clicks arrives late, after most of the move, and pays the widest spread. Retail news scalping rates D.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.7\",\"concepts\":[\"news-policy\",\"news-liquidity\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare how fast the price adjusts with how fast you can click.\",\"Think about what the spread is at that moment.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.8-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX's normal spread is 0.10. After a release, when does the second-move plan let you act?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"As soon as the number is out\",\"rationale\":\"That's trading the first seconds: the widest spreads and the jumps. The second move waits.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"After exactly one minute, whatever the spread\",\"rationale\":\"Time alone doesn't make fills fair. The rule watches the spread: at most 2 × normal.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only the next day\",\"rationale\":\"That misses the setup. The rule asks only for normal spreads and a break of the range.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Once the spread is back to 0.20 or less, on a break of the post-release range\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The second move waits until the spread is at most twice normal, 0.20 on LABX, then trades a break of the post-release range with a stop at its other side.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.8\",\"concepts\":[\"second-move\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.8-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Post-release range: 98.80–100.00. You buy the break at 100.05 with a stop at 98.75, below the range. With 0.05% commission on each side, how many whole units keep the loss at the stop within 100.00?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":71,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Risk per unit: 100.05 − 98.75 = 1.30. 71 units lose 92.30, plus 3.56 and 3.51 of commissions: 99.37. 72 units would lose 100.77.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.8\",\"concepts\":[\"second-move\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Risk per unit = 100.05 − 98.75 = 1.30, plus commissions.\",\"Without commissions, 100.00 ÷ 1.30 ≈ 76. Commissions cut that.\",\"Check 71 and 72 with both commissions.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.8-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A release beats consensus slightly: z = 0.4. Ten minutes later, spreads are normal and price pokes above the post-release range high. Your plan trades the second move only when |z| is over 1 and the details agree.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy the break: spreads are normal\",\"rationale\":\"Normal spreads are needed, but not enough. With z = 0.4, breaks of the range often fail and fall back in.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Pass: the surprise is too small, so a false break is likely\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sell: small surprises always reverse\",\"rationale\":\"They don't always reverse; they just give no reliable direction. Your plan says pass.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy at half size as a test\",\"rationale\":\"Half size still breaks the plan's rule. With |z| under 1, the plan says pass.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A z of 0.4 is close to noise, so the break has little behind it and often falls back into the range. The plan requires |z| over 1 with details agreeing: pass.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.8\",\"concepts\":[\"second-move\"],\"hints\":[\"Check the surprise against your plan's threshold.\",\"Small surprises are a known failure case for range breaks.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.9-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Friday, 15:20. Payrolls at 15:30 is on your calendar with the policy 'flat'. You spot a clean breakout on LABX and want to buy 40 units now with a 1.50 stop.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the checklist say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Take it: the setup is valid and the stop is set\",\"rationale\":\"A valid setup doesn't change the policy. In the last 15 minutes before a red event, no new orders.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Take it at half size\",\"rationale\":\"Half size is for orange events. For a red one, the policy is flat, with no new orders from T−15.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Skip it: no new orders in the last 15 minutes before a red event\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Take it and move the stop to break-even before 15:30\",\"rationale\":\"A gap can skip any stop. The checklist keeps you out, rather than hoping a stop holds.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The policy for payrolls was decided in advance: flat, with no new orders from 15:15. A good-looking setup ten minutes before a red event is exactly what the checklist screens out.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.9\",\"concepts\":[\"news-risk-checklist\",\"news-policy\"],\"hints\":[\"How far away is the red event?\",\"What did you decide for it before the week started?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.9-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which of these limits the damage from an unscheduled shock, such as a sudden currency de-peg?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Being flat before scheduled releases\",\"rationale\":\"That handles scheduled events. An unscheduled shock arrives at an hour nobody knew.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Small position size and low leverage\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A stop-loss order\",\"rationale\":\"In a gap, the stop fills at the first price after the jump, which can be far beyond its level.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Checking the calendar every morning\",\"rationale\":\"Unscheduled events never appear on the calendar. That's what makes them unscheduled.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"No timing rule dodges an event nobody scheduled, and a stop can fill far beyond its price. Only how much you hold, and how much leverage you use, decide how much a gap can take.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.9\",\"concepts\":[\"news-risk-checklist\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of these still works when nobody knew the shock was coming?\",\"Think about what a gap does to a stop.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.10.9-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You journal slippage per event type. At a CPI release, your stop on 50 LABX at 98.00 filled at 97.40. Your 1R is 100.00. How much slippage do you log, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.3,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Slippage: 50 × (98.00 − 97.40) = 30.00. In R: 30.00 ÷ 100.00 = 0.30R. Logged per event type, these numbers show what news really costs you.\",\"lesson\":\"2.10.9\",\"concepts\":[\"news-risk-checklist\",\"journaling\"],\"hints\":[\"Slippage = (stop price − fill) × units.\",\"Divide by your 1R, 100.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[20,70,1],\"slip\":[0.1,2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(units * slip / 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You journal slippage per event type. At a CPI release, your stop on {units} LABX at 98.00 filled at {98 - slip:2}. Your 1R is 100.00. How much slippage do you log, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Slippage: {units} × (98.00 − {98 - slip:2}) = {units * slip:2}. In R: {units * slip:2} ÷ 100.00 = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{units} × {slip:2} = {units * slip:2}. Divide by 100.00.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.1-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Equity 20,000.00. You let one ATR cost 0.5% of equity. The instrument's ATR is 2.50 per unit. How many whole units do you buy, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":40,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"One ATR may cost 0.5% of 20,000.00 = 100.00. Units = 100.00 ÷ 2.50 = 40. Each unit moves 2.50 on a typical day, so 40 units move 100.00. Costs then trim the size a little.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.1\",\"concepts\":[\"percent-volatility-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the money one ATR may cost, then divide by the ATR per unit.\",\"0.5% of 20,000.00 = 100.00; then 100.00 ÷ 2.50.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"eq\":[5000,50000,1000],\"vq\":[1,4,1],\"atr\":[0.2,5,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"floor(eq * vq * 0.25 / 100 / atr + 0.000001) >= 5\"],\"answer\":\"floor(eq * vq * 0.25 / 100 / atr + 0.000001)\",\"prompt\":\"Equity {eq:2}. You let one ATR cost {vq * 0.25:2}% of equity. The instrument's ATR is {atr:2} per unit. How many whole units do you buy, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"One ATR may cost {vq * 0.25:2}% of {eq:2} = {eq * vq * 0.25 / 100:2}. Units = {eq * vq * 0.25 / 100:2} ÷ {atr:2} = {answer}, rounded down. Costs then trim the size a little.\",\"hint\":\"{eq * vq * 0.25 / 100:2} ÷ {atr:2}, rounded down.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.1-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold 60 units of instrument A (ATR 3.00) and 60 units of instrument B (ATR 0.75). What does this fixed-unit sizing do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gives both the same weight, since the units are equal\",\"rationale\":\"Equal units aren't equal risk. One ATR costs 60 × 3.00 = 180.00 on A and 60 × 0.75 = 45.00 on B.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Makes A swing four times as much as B: 180.00 against 45.00 per ATR\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Makes B riskier, because cheaper instruments move more\",\"rationale\":\"The price level doesn't set the swing; the ATR does. B's ATR is a quarter of A's.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Can't be judged without the prices\",\"rationale\":\"ATR is already in price units per unit held, so units × ATR gives the daily swing without the price.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Units × ATR is what a typical day costs: 180.00 on A, 45.00 on B. Volatility sizing would hold four times as many units of B as of A, so both swing the same amount.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.1\",\"concepts\":[\"percent-volatility-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Multiply units by ATR for each instrument.\",\"Compare 60 × 3.00 with 60 × 0.75.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.1-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You hold 100 LABZ, sized when its 14-day ATR was 0.50. After overnight news, daily ranges jump to about 1.00. This morning your ATR reads 0.54.\",\"prompt\":\"What should you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Cut the size now, using the new daily range\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep 100 units: ATR has barely moved\",\"rationale\":\"ATR lags. It averages 14 days, so it reads 0.54 while the market moves about 1.00 a day: your swing has nearly doubled.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Add units: higher volatility means bigger profits\",\"rationale\":\"Bigger moves cut both ways. Adding doubles down on a risk that has already doubled.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Do nothing: the stop limits the loss\",\"rationale\":\"A stop set for a 0.50 ATR now sits inside normal noise, and gaps past it grow with volatility.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"ATR is a lagging average: with ranges at 1.00, a 14-day ATR reads 0.54 the next day and 0.76 after ten. Waiting for it leaves you with about twice the planned swing. Resize from what the market is doing now: about 50 units at 1.00 per ATR.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.1\",\"concepts\":[\"percent-volatility-sizing\",\"volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"How fast does a 14-day average react to a sudden jump?\",\"Compare the swing per ATR now, 100 × 1.00, with the 50.00 you planned.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.2-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Equity 12,000.00. Your open trades risk 120.00, 90.00 and 150.00. What is your portfolio heat, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Heat adds every open risk: 120.00 + 90.00 + 150.00 = 360.00. As a share of equity: 360.00 ÷ 12,000.00 = 3%. That's what you'd lose if all three stops were hit together.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.2\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-heat\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the open risks, then divide by equity.\",\"360.00 ÷ 12,000.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"eq\":[5000,30000,1000],\"a\":[50,300,10],\"b\":[50,300,10],\"c\":[50,300,10]},\"constraints\":[\"(a + b + c) / eq * 100 <= 8\"],\"answer\":\"round((a + b + c) / eq * 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Equity {eq:2}. Your open trades risk {a:2}, {b:2} and {c:2}. What is your portfolio heat, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Heat adds every open risk: {a + b + c:2}. As a share of equity: {a + b + c:2} ÷ {eq:2} = {answer:2}%. That's what you'd lose if every stop were hit together.\",\"hint\":\"{a + b + c:2} ÷ {eq:2}, as a percentage.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.2-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Equity 10,000.00, heat cap 5%. Your open trades already risk 3.6% in total. What is the most a new trade may risk, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":140,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Room under the cap = 5% − 3.6% = 1.4% of 10,000.00 = 140.00. A trade planned at 1% (100.00) fits; one planned at 1.5% (150.00) must be resized to 140.00 or skipped.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.2\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-heat\"],\"hints\":[\"Room = cap − current heat.\",\"1.4% of 10,000.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"eq\":[5000,30000,1000],\"cap\":[4,8,1],\"h10\":[10,70,1]},\"constraints\":[\"h10 < cap * 10\"],\"answer\":\"round(eq * (cap * 10 - h10) / 1000, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Equity {eq:2}, heat cap {cap}%. Your open trades already risk {h10 / 10:1}% in total. What is the most a new trade may risk, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Room under the cap = {cap}% − {h10 / 10:1}% = {(cap * 10 - h10) / 10:1}% of {eq:2} = {answer:2}. A larger trade is resized to that or skipped.\",\"hint\":\"{(cap * 10 - h10) / 10:1}% of {eq:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.2-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Equity 10,000.00. You hold three trades, each risking 1.5%. You've just moved one stop to breakeven. You also have a pending buy stop order that would risk 1%. A friend says your heat is now 3%.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the better count?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"3%: a breakeven stop can't lose, and pending orders aren't positions\",\"rationale\":\"A gap can open past a breakeven stop, and a pending order can fill while you're away. Both can still lose money.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"5.5%: moving a stop doesn't change the heat\",\"rationale\":\"Moving the stop to entry does remove its planned loss. What's left is gap risk, smaller than the original 1.5%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"4%, plus a gap allowance for the breakeven trade\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"3% now, plus 1% once the pending order fills\",\"rationale\":\"Once it fills, the risk is live at once, maybe while you're away. Count it before, so the cap holds when it fills.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Two trades still risk 1.5% each: 3%. The pending order counts as if filled: +1%. The breakeven trade's planned loss is gone, but a gap can still fill it below the entry, so it carries a gap allowance. Heat is at least 4%.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.2\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-heat\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Which items could still lose money if the market jumped right now?\",\"Count both remaining stops, the pending order and the gap risk.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.3-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You're long LABX and LABY, 1% risk each. Their correlation is 0.85. Which describes the pair best?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Two independent 1% bets: diversified\",\"rationale\":\"Independent positions would combine to 1.41%. At 0.85 they move together and combine to 1.92%, almost one bet.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Less risky than either alone, because they hedge each other\",\"rationale\":\"Positions hedge only when they move in opposite directions. Two longs with a positive correlation add up.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nearly one 2% bet: they combine to about 1.92%\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Exactly 1%, because the correlation cancels one of them\",\"rationale\":\"Positive correlation between two longs adds risk; it cancels nothing. At ρ = 1 the pair is a single 2% bet.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"√(1 + 1 + 2 × 0.85) = √3.7 = 1.92%. Two unrelated 1% positions would combine to 1.41%. Correlated longs are one idea held twice: if LABX falls on news, LABY likely falls too.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.3\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"hints\":[\"Do the two positions tend to hit their stops together?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.3-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold 6 positions with an average correlation of 0.5. How many effective bets is that?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.71,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"bets\"},\"explanation\":\"Effective bets = n ÷ (1 + (n − 1) × ρ) = 6 ÷ (1 + 5 × 0.5) = 6 ÷ 3.5 = 1.71. Six tickets add up to less than two independent bets.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.3\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"hints\":[\"Effective bets = n ÷ (1 + (n − 1) × ρ).\",\"6 ÷ (1 + 5 × 0.5).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[2,10,1],\"r10\":[1,9,1]},\"answer\":\"round(n / (1 + (n - 1) * r10 / 10), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You hold {n} positions with an average correlation of {r10 / 10:1}. How many effective bets is that?\",\"explanation\":\"Effective bets = n ÷ (1 + (n − 1) × ρ) = {n} ÷ (1 + {n - 1} × {r10 / 10:1}) = {answer:2}. Correlation shrinks {n} tickets to fewer independent bets.\",\"hint\":\"{n} ÷ (1 + {n - 1} × {r10 / 10:1}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.3-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You hold long vEUR/vUSD, short vUSD/vJPY and short vUSD/vCHF, 1% risk each. In calm months their correlations measured about 0.6, so your sheet counts them as 1.36 effective bets.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three currencies, three bets: well diversified\",\"rationale\":\"All three sell vUSD. They are one view on the dollar, held three times.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"1.36 bets is fine: the calm-period correlation is the one to use\",\"rationale\":\"Correlations measured in calm markets tend to rise in stress. A dollar shock would hit all three together.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The vJPY and vCHF trades hedge the vEUR trade\",\"rationale\":\"They'd hedge it only if they bet the other way on vUSD. Shorting vUSD/vJPY and vUSD/vCHF also sells vUSD.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"One short-vUSD bet held three times; in stress, expect them to move almost as one\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Each trade sells vUSD against another currency, so one dollar rally hits all three. Calm-period correlations understate how tightly they move in stress: plan as if it were close to one 3% bet, or keep one position per group.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.3\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"exposure\"],\"hints\":[\"What does each trade do to vUSD?\",\"In a currency pair, a long buys the first currency and a short sells it.\",\"Then ask what happens to correlations when markets turn.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.4-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":1,\"scenario\":\"Your plan caps risk at 2% per instrument. You already hold a LABX long risking 1.5%. A new LABX setup appears, planned at 1% risk.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Resize it to 0.5%, or skip it\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Take it at 1%: each trade is within the 1% rule\",\"rationale\":\"Each trade is, but LABX would then carry 2.5% in total, past the 2% instrument cap.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Take it and move the first stop closer to free up room\",\"rationale\":\"That bends a plan to fit a trade, and a tighter stop can be hit by normal noise.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Skip LABX for the rest of the month\",\"rationale\":\"The cap limits how much risk LABX carries at once, not whether you can trade it. 0.5% of room is left.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The instrument cap counts all the risk in LABX: 1.5% + 1% = 2.5%, over 2%. Resizing the new trade to 0.5% keeps LABX at exactly 2%. Skipping it is within the rules too.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.4\",\"concepts\":[\"exposure-limits\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the risk you already have in LABX.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.4-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Month-start equity 12,000.00, monthly cap 6% with open risk. You've lost 380.00 this month and have 240.00 of open risk. How much new risk can you take, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":100,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Cap: 6% of 12,000.00 = 720.00. Used: 380.00 realised plus 240.00 open = 620.00. Room: 100.00. Because open risk is already counted, a later stop-out doesn't take the limit by surprise.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.4\",\"concepts\":[\"period-loss-limits\"],\"hints\":[\"Work out the cap in vUSD first: 6% of month-start equity.\",\"Subtract the realised losses and the open risk.\",\"720.00 − 380.00 − 240.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"eq\":[5000,30000,1000],\"cap\":[4,8,1],\"lost\":[0,600,10],\"open\":[0,400,10]},\"constraints\":[\"lost + open < eq * cap / 100\"],\"answer\":\"round(eq * cap / 100 - lost - open, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Month-start equity {eq:2}, monthly cap {cap}% with open risk. You've lost {lost:2} this month and have {open:2} of open risk. How much new risk can you take, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Cap: {cap}% of {eq:2} = {eq * cap / 100:2}. Used: {lost:2} realised plus {open:2} open. Room: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{eq * cap / 100:2} − {lost:2} − {open:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.4-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Limits: 1% per trade, 3% per day, 6% per month with open risk, heat 5%, one position per correlated group. Equity 10,000.00. Today's closed trades lost 250.00; the month is −150.00 overall. Open: LABX and LABZ longs, 100.00 risk each.\",\"prompt\":\"A vEUR/vUSD long, planned at 100.00 risk, matches your setup. What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Accept it at 100.00: the month still has 250.00 of room\",\"rationale\":\"The month does, but today has only 50.00 left: 300.00 − 250.00. A trade must fit every limit.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Resize it to 50.00, or skip it\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Reject it: heat would pass the cap\",\"rationale\":\"Heat is 2% now and would be 3% with this trade, under the 5% cap.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Reject it: vEUR/vUSD is in the same group as LABX\",\"rationale\":\"Your groups are set by correlation, and vEUR/vUSD isn't grouped with LABX. The group rule doesn't block it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Check each limit. Per trade: 1% fits. Heat: 2% + 1% = 3% fits. Month: 150.00 + 200.00 + 100.00 = 450.00 of 600.00 fits. Day: 250.00 + 100.00 = 350.00 is over 300.00. The tightest limit, today's 50.00, sets the size.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.4\",\"concepts\":[\"exposure-limits\",\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"hints\":[\"Check each limit separately; the tightest one decides.\",\"Today's room: 3% of 10,000.00 minus what today's trades lost.\",\"300.00 − 250.00 = 50.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.5-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Peak equity 20,000.00. Tier 1 at −5% cuts risk from 1% to 0.5% of current equity. Equity is now 18,900.00. What do you risk per trade, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":94.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"18,900.00 is 5.5% below the 20,000.00 peak, past the −5% tier. Risk is 0.5% of current equity: 94.50. Full risk returns only once half the drawdown is recovered.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.5\",\"concepts\":[\"drawdown-tiers\"],\"hints\":[\"First check the drawdown: how far below the peak is equity?\",\"1,100.00 ÷ 20,000.00 = 5.5%, so tier 1 applies.\",\"0.5% of 18,900.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"peak\":[10000,40000,1000],\"dd10\":[55,95,5]},\"answer\":\"round(peak * (1000 - dd10) / 1000 * 0.005, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Peak equity {peak:2}. Tier 1 at −5% cuts risk from 1% to 0.5% of current equity. Equity is now {peak * (1000 - dd10) / 1000:2}. What do you risk per trade, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"{peak * (1000 - dd10) / 1000:2} is {dd10 / 10:1}% below the {peak:2} peak, past the −5% tier. Risk is 0.5% of current equity: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"0.5% of {peak * (1000 - dd10) / 1000:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.5-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A Monte Carlo of your system at 1% risk shows, over 100 trades: a median maximum drawdown of 8.3%, and about 1 run in 20 deeper than 14.8%. You have no other tiers yet.\",\"prompt\":\"Where should your 'stop and review' tier go?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"At −8%, a round number near the median\",\"rationale\":\"Normal runs pass −8% about half the time. You'd stop for plain variance in every other run.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"At about −15%, at or beyond the 95th percentile\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"At −10%: deeper than the median, so it's safe\",\"rationale\":\"About 31% of normal runs still reach −10%. With no tier before it, it fires in nearly one run in three.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nowhere: a drawdown is always just variance\",\"rationale\":\"Some drawdowns mean the edge is gone. A stop tier beyond normal runs is how you find out without guessing.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A stop tier should rarely fire when nothing is wrong. At about −15%, roughly 1 normal run in 20 gets there, so reaching it is a real signal. A shallower stop needs earlier tiers that slow the slide, written down with the reason.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.5\",\"concepts\":[\"drawdown-tiers\",\"expected-drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"How often do normal runs reach each depth?\",\"You want a stop that normal runs rarely reach: about 1 in 20.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.5-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're 9% below your peak. Tier 1 cut your risk to 0.5% at −5%. You've studied the losses and think the next setups look strong. Climbing back at 0.5% per trade will take a while.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your plan say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Raise risk to 2% to climb back faster\",\"rationale\":\"Raising risk in a drawdown is how drawdowns become blow-ups. If the run continues, each loss is four times bigger.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Return to 1% now: you've reviewed the losses\",\"rationale\":\"The return rule is written in advance so it can't be argued with mid-drawdown. Half the drawdown hasn't been recovered.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Stay at 0.5% until half the drawdown is recovered, then return to 1%\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Drop the tiers: they slow the recovery\",\"rationale\":\"Slowing you down in a drawdown is their job. A rule that switches off when it bites was never a rule.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Risk only goes down as the drawdown deepens, and it comes back by a rule set in advance: here, once half of the 9% is recovered. Raising risk to recover is the martingale trap in slow motion.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.5\",\"concepts\":[\"drawdown-tiers\"],\"hints\":[\"When does your plan say full risk returns?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A journal: 50 trades. 20 wins totalling +45R; 30 losses totalling −30R. What is the profit factor?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Profit factor = gross wins ÷ gross losses = 45 ÷ 30 = 1.5. Expectancy is (45 − 30) ÷ 50 = +0.30R a trade. Above 1 means the trades made money overall; whether 50 trades are enough to believe it is a separate question.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.6\",\"concepts\":[\"performance-metrics\"],\"hints\":[\"Gross wins go on top.\",\"45 ÷ 30.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"wins\":[10,30,1],\"losses\":[15,40,1],\"aw10\":[12,40,1]},\"constraints\":[\"wins * aw10 / 10 > losses\"],\"answer\":\"round(wins * aw10 / (10 * losses), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A journal: {wins + losses} trades. {wins} wins totalling +{wins * aw10 / 10:1}R; {losses} losses totalling −{losses}R. What is the profit factor?\",\"explanation\":\"Profit factor = gross wins ÷ gross losses = {wins * aw10 / 10:1} ÷ {losses} = {answer:2}. Above 1 means the trades made money overall.\",\"hint\":\"{wins * aw10 / 10:1} ÷ {losses}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which metric can be set by a single bad stretch, so that one month defines it for years?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Maximum drawdown\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Win rate\",\"rationale\":\"Win rate averages every trade, so one bad month moves it only a little. What it hides is the size of wins and losses.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Expectancy\",\"rationale\":\"Expectancy averages all trades. One bad month shifts it, and more trades dilute that month.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Payoff ratio\",\"rationale\":\"Payoff averages the wins and the losses. It hides how often you win, not a single episode.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Maximum drawdown is the deepest single fall from a peak. One unlucky run sets it, and it never improves afterwards, however the rest goes. Read it next to how many trades and how long a history it comes from.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.6\",\"concepts\":[\"performance-metrics\",\"drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"Which metric is a maximum rather than an average?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After 14 trades, your win rate is 29% and your expectancy −0.20R. Plan adherence is 100%, stop integrity 100% and risk accuracy 93%.\",\"prompt\":\"How do you grade these two weeks?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Failed: the outcome numbers are negative\",\"rationale\":\"14 trades are far too few for outcome metrics to mean much. A sound plan often looks like this over two weeks.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Change the strategy: 29% is below its break-even\",\"rationale\":\"On 14 trades the win rate's interval is very wide. Changing the rules now would react to noise.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Raise the risk to make up for the bad start\",\"rationale\":\"That's raising risk to recover, the trap drawdown tiers exist to prevent.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Good on process; the outcome numbers stay diagnostics until the sample is bigger\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Process KPIs depend on your actions and mean something after a few trades: here they are close to perfect. Outcome metrics on 14 trades are mostly noise, so they stay diagnostics until about 30 trades, always read with their sample size.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.6\",\"concepts\":[\"process-kpis\",\"performance-metrics\"],\"hints\":[\"Which numbers depend on your actions, and which on luck?\",\"How much can 14 trades say about a win rate?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your journal: 64 trades, with a standard deviation of results of 1.60R. What is the half-width of the 95% interval for your expectancy, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.39,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Standard error = 1.60 ÷ √64 = 0.20R. Half-width = 1.96 × 0.20 = 0.39R. With a mean of +0.30R, the interval would run from −0.09R to +0.69R: zero still inside.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.7\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy-confidence-interval\"],\"hints\":[\"Standard error = sd ÷ √n.\",\"√64 = 8, so 1.60 ÷ 8 = 0.20.\",\"Multiply the standard error by 1.96.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"rt\":[4,20,1],\"sd10\":[10,25,1]},\"answer\":\"round(1.96 * sd10 / 10 / rt, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your journal: {rt * rt} trades, with a standard deviation of results of {sd10 / 10:2}R. What is the half-width of the 95% interval for your expectancy, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Standard error = {sd10 / 10:2} ÷ √{rt * rt} = {sd10 / 10 / rt:3}R. Half-width = 1.96 × {sd10 / 10 / rt:3} = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"√{rt * rt} = {rt}, so the standard error is {sd10 / 10:2} ÷ {rt}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.7-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your edge looks like +0.25R a trade, with an sd of 1.50R. Using n = (2 × sd ÷ mean)², about how many trades do you need before the interval excludes zero?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":144,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"trades\"},\"explanation\":\"2 × 1.50 ÷ 0.25 = 12, and 12² = 144 trades. Halve the edge and you need four times as many: at +0.125R it would be 576.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.7\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Compute 2 × sd ÷ mean first.\",\"2 × 1.50 ÷ 0.25 = 12. Now square it.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m20\":[2,10,1],\"sd4\":[4,10,1]},\"answer\":\"round((2 * sd4 / 4 / (m20 / 20)) * (2 * sd4 / 4 / (m20 / 20)), 0)\",\"prompt\":\"Your edge looks like +{m20 / 20:2}R a trade, with an sd of {sd4 / 4:2}R. Using n = (2 × sd ÷ mean)², about how many trades do you need before the interval excludes zero?\",\"explanation\":\"2 × {sd4 / 4:2} ÷ {m20 / 20:2} = {2 * sd4 / 4 / (m20 / 20):2}, and squared that's about {answer} trades. Halve the edge and you need four times as many.\",\"hint\":\"2 × {sd4 / 4:2} ÷ {m20 / 20:2}, then square it.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A new setup has made +0.90R a trade over its first 12 trades, with an sd of 1.70R. A friend says it's proven and you should double your risk on it.\",\"prompt\":\"What do the numbers say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's proven: +0.90R is a big edge\",\"rationale\":\"Big averages are easy on 12 trades. The interval runs from −0.06R to +1.86R, so a true edge of zero is still possible.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It proves little: the 95% interval, −0.06R to +1.86R, still includes zero\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's proven if the win rate is above 50%\",\"rationale\":\"On 12 trades the win rate has its own wide interval; it can't rescue the sample.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's disproven: 12 trades are worthless\",\"rationale\":\"Small samples don't disprove anything either. They're weak evidence both ways: keep normal risk and keep counting.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Standard error = 1.70 ÷ √12 = 0.49R, so the interval is 0.90 ± 0.96R: from −0.06R to +1.86R, with zero inside. Keep normal risk and let the sample grow. Doubling risk on 12 trades of evidence is how lucky starts turn into drawdowns.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.7\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Compute the 95% interval: mean ± 1.96 × sd ÷ √n.\",\"1.70 ÷ √12 = 0.49; times 1.96, about 0.96.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.8-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"64 trades, mean +0.25R, sd 1.25R. What is the SQN?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.6,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"SQN = √N × mean ÷ sd = 8 × 0.25 ÷ 1.25 = 1.6. It's the t-statistic of the mean: 1.6 standard errors above zero, not yet enough to rule out luck.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.8\",\"concepts\":[\"sqn\"],\"hints\":[\"SQN = √N × mean ÷ sd, with N at most 100.\",\"√64 = 8.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"rt\":[5,10,1],\"m20\":[2,12,1],\"sd4\":[4,10,1]},\"answer\":\"round(rt * m20 / 20 / (sd4 / 4), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"{rt * rt} trades, mean +{m20 / 20:2}R, sd {sd4 / 4:2}R. What is the SQN?\",\"explanation\":\"SQN = √N × mean ÷ sd = {rt} × {m20 / 20:2} ÷ {sd4 / 4:2} = {answer:2}. It's the t-statistic of the mean: from about 2 up, luck starts to look unlikely.\",\"hint\":\"√{rt * rt} = {rt}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.8-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A system makes +0.35R a trade before costs and trades 10 times a month. Costs are 0.15R a trade. How many R does it earn a month after costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"(0.35 − 0.15) × 10 = 2.0R a month. Before costs it looked like 3.5R: costs take 1.5R, because they're paid on every trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.8\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Take the costs off each trade first.\",\"(0.35 − 0.15) × 10.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e20\":[4,20,1],\"c20\":[1,4,1],\"f\":[2,20,1]},\"constraints\":[\"e20 > c20\"],\"answer\":\"round((e20 - c20) / 20 * f, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A system makes +{e20 / 20:2}R a trade before costs and trades {f} times a month. Costs are {c20 / 20:2}R a trade. How many R does it earn a month after costs?\",\"explanation\":\"({e20 / 20:2} − {c20 / 20:2}) × {f} = {answer:2}R a month. Costs are paid on every trade, so they grow with the frequency.\",\"hint\":\"({e20 / 20:2} − {c20 / 20:2}) × {f}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.11.8-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Over two years, your system made +12% with a maximum drawdown of 9%. Simply holding the index made +25%, with a maximum drawdown of 14%. Your SQN is 1.1 over 90 trades.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the fairest conclusion?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It trailed the benchmark, and its edge isn't established: not good yet\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The system is good: it made money\",\"rationale\":\"Making money isn't the test. Holding the index made more, with no trading at all.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It beat the benchmark, because its drawdown was smaller\",\"rationale\":\"A smaller drawdown counts, but 13 points less return is a high price, and SQN 1.1 doesn't show an edge.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"SQN 1.1 proves the edge, since it's above 1\",\"rationale\":\"SQN reads like a t-statistic: 1.1 standard errors above zero is well within what luck produces.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Compare with the passive benchmark, return and drawdown together: the system made 13 points less and fell 5 points less. With an SQN of 1.1, its mean is only 1.1 standard errors above zero. It may be worth refining, but not calling good yet.\",\"lesson\":\"2.11.8\",\"concepts\":[\"passive-benchmark\",\"sqn\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare return and drawdown with the benchmark.\",\"Read SQN as a t-statistic: is 1.1 far from zero?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.1-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Prospect theory says outcomes are judged as gains or losses from a reference point. Which two ideas complete its three parts?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Diminishing sensitivity and loss aversion\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Expected value and the law of large numbers\",\"rationale\":\"Those describe what a bet is worth on average. Prospect theory describes how people feel and choose, which often departs from expected value.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Anchoring and confirmation bias\",\"rationale\":\"Both are biases, but other ones. Prospect theory's parts are about how gains and losses are felt.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Risk per trade and position size\",\"rationale\":\"Those are rules you set. Prospect theory describes the pulls those rules have to resist.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Prospect theory has three parts: a reference point that splits outcomes into gains and losses, diminishing sensitivity (each extra unit matters less) and loss aversion (losses weigh more than equal gains). Together they predict caution with gains and risk-taking with losses.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.1\",\"concepts\":[\"prospect-theory\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.1-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Two open trades. Trade A is +0.5R and you feel the urge to close it now. Trade B is −0.6R and you feel the urge to give it more room, hoping it comes back to zero.\",\"prompt\":\"What does prospect theory say about these two urges?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Risk-seeking in gains, risk-averse in losses\",\"rationale\":\"That's the reverse. Closing A early avoids risk; holding B for a comeback takes more risk.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Both show sound risk management\",\"rationale\":\"Neither urge comes from the plan. Both come from where each trade stands against your entry price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Risk-averse in gains, risk-seeking in losses\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Both are loss aversion alone; the frame plays no part\",\"rationale\":\"Loss aversion explains part of B, but A has no loss. The frame, gain or loss from entry, flips your attitude to risk.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Above the reference point, a sure gain feels better than a chance of more, so you close A early. Below it, locking in a loss hurts more than a gamble on a comeback, so you hold B. Same trader, opposite attitudes to risk, set by the frame.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.1\",\"concepts\":[\"prospect-theory\",\"loss-aversion\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare where each trade stands against its entry price.\",\"Which urge avoids risk, and which one takes more?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.1-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your journal over 40 trades: average winner +0.7R, average loser −1.3R. Your plan's stop is 1R and your target 2R. You moved 9 stops further away and closed 11 winners before the target.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best reading, and the process fix?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The method has no edge: switch to a new one\",\"rationale\":\"The journal shows exits that broke the plan, not a tested method failing. A new method would meet the same pulls.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bad luck: keep going unchanged\",\"rationale\":\"Moved stops and early exits are decisions, not luck. They explain why losers are bigger than 1R and winners smaller than 2R.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Use a closer target, so winners hit more often\",\"rationale\":\"That bakes the early exits into the plan. Winners shrink while losers stay large.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The prospect-theory pattern: set exits at entry and never move the stop away\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Losers averaging −1.3R against a 1R stop, and winners at +0.7R against a 2R target, show risk-seeking in losses and caution in gains. The fix is structure: stops and targets set at entry, stops never moved away, and early exits only by a rule such as a time stop.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.1\",\"concepts\":[\"prospect-theory\",\"exit-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the averages with the plan: a 1R stop and a 2R target.\",\"Which decisions made losers bigger and winners smaller?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.2-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your sample journal shows PGR 0.30 and PLR 0.15. What does that mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"You hold winners too long and sell losers too fast\",\"rationale\":\"That's the reverse. A PGR above PLR means gains are sold faster and losses kept.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Your win rate is 30%\",\"rationale\":\"PGR isn't a win rate. It's the share of available gains you chose to realise when you sold.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You realise gains at twice the rate of losses: a disposition effect of 0.15\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your losses are twice as large as your gains\",\"rationale\":\"PGR and PLR count how often you sell, not how big the results are.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"PGR is the share of paper gains you realised; PLR is the share of paper losses. 0.30 against 0.15 means gains were sold at twice the rate of losses: a disposition effect of 0.30 − 0.15 = 0.15. Zero would mean no difference.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.2\",\"concepts\":[\"disposition-effect\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.2-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A plan wins 40% of trades at a +2.5R target and loses 1R otherwise. You start closing winners at +1.0R, with the same trades winning. What's the expectancy per trade, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.2,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Wins: 0.40 × 1.0R = 0.40R. Losses: 0.60 × 1R = 0.60R. Expectancy: 0.40 − 0.60 = −0.20R per trade, against +0.40R with the target. Early exits on winners can turn a working plan into a losing one.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.2\",\"concepts\":[\"disposition-effect\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Expectancy = win rate × average win − loss rate × average loss.\",\"Use the early exit, not the target, as the average win.\",\"0.40 × 1.0 − 0.60 × 1 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[30,50,10],\"e\":[0.5,1.5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(pct(w) * e - (1 - pct(w)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A plan wins {w}% of trades at a +2.5R target and loses 1R otherwise. You start closing winners at +{e:1}R, with the same trades winning. What's the expectancy per trade, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Wins: {pct(w):2} × {e:1}R = {pct(w) * e:2}R. Losses: {1 - pct(w):2} × 1R = {1 - pct(w):2}R. Expectancy: {answer:2}R per trade, against {pct(w) * 2.5 - (1 - pct(w)):2}R with the target.\",\"hint\":\"{pct(w):2} × {e:1} − {1 - pct(w):2} × 1 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.2-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long at +1.1R. Your plan: stop 1R below entry, target +2.5R, time stop after 20 candles. It's candle 9, nothing in the setup has changed, and you feel a strong urge to lock it in.\",\"prompt\":\"Which exit decision follows the plan?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Close now: a profit is a profit\",\"rationale\":\"That's the disposition effect at work. None of the plan's exits has triggered, and the setup hasn't changed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Hold: let the stop, the target or the time stop end the trade\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Move the target closer, to 1.5R, since you're in profit\",\"rationale\":\"Changing the target because the trade is green lets P&L decide. Target changes belong in the plan, before entry.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Widen the stop, so a pullback can't stop you out\",\"rationale\":\"Widening a stop raises risk and is never a rule-based exit. A stop moves only in your favour, by rule.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Exits come from rules set at entry: stop, target, time stop, or an opposing signal named in the plan. None has triggered, so the plan says hold. The urge to lock in a gain is the disposition effect, and it shrinks average winners.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.2\",\"concepts\":[\"disposition-effect\",\"exit-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of the plan's exits has triggered?\",\"Is the urge coming from the chart or from the open profit?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.3-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which confidence log shows overconfidence as miscalibration?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Average confidence 85%, hit rate 62%\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Average confidence 55%, hit rate 70%\",\"rationale\":\"Here the hit rate beats the stated confidence. That's underconfidence, the opposite gap.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Average confidence 70%, hit rate 71%\",\"rationale\":\"A 1-point gap is calibrated: the stated confidence matches the record.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Ten winning trades in a row\",\"rationale\":\"A streak says nothing about calibration. Overconfidence is measured by comparing stated confidence with the hit rate.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Overconfidence as miscalibration means being sure more often than you're right: stated confidence above the hit rate. 62 − 85 = −23 points is a clear gap.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.3\",\"concepts\":[\"overconfidence\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.3-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over 40 trades you logged an average confidence of 80% and were right 26 times. What's your calibration gap, in points?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-15,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"points\"},\"explanation\":\"Hit rate: 26 ÷ 40 = 65%. Gap: 65 − 80 = −15 points. Negative means your confidence ran 15 points ahead of your record: overconfident.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.3\",\"concepts\":[\"overconfidence\"],\"hints\":[\"Gap = hit rate − average confidence.\",\"First turn 26 out of 40 into a percentage.\",\"26 ÷ 40 = 65%. 65 − 80 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"conf\":[60,90,5],\"hits\":[16,36,1]},\"answer\":\"round(hits / 40 * 100 - conf, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Over 40 trades you logged an average confidence of {conf}% and were right {hits} times. What's your calibration gap, in points?\",\"explanation\":\"Hit rate: {hits} ÷ 40 = {hits / 40 * 100:1}%. Gap: {hits / 40 * 100:1} − {conf} = {answer:1} points. Negative means confidence ran ahead of the record; positive means it lagged behind.\",\"hint\":\"{hits} ÷ 40 = {hits / 40 * 100:1}%. Then subtract {conf}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.3-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"After a winning month you feel sharp. Your confidence log averages 85%, but your hit rate is 58%. You've gone from 6 to 18 trades a week. Costs are about 0.20R per trade.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Good form: trade more while it lasts\",\"rationale\":\"A 58% hit rate against 85% confidence says the feeling of form outruns the record. Each extra trade adds 0.20R of cost.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Calibrated: the winning month proves it\",\"rationale\":\"A month of results can't show calibration. The log compares stated confidence with outcomes, and the gap is 27 points.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Underconfident: raise your size\",\"rationale\":\"Underconfident means right more often than you said. Here you're right less often: 58% against 85%.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Overconfident: the 12 extra trades cost about 2.4R a week. Back to playbook setups only\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"58 − 85 = −27 points: overconfident. The jump from 6 to 18 trades adds 12 × 0.20R = 2.4R of costs a week, for certain, while any edge in the extra trades is unproven. Overconfidence often shows first in the trade count.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.3\",\"concepts\":[\"overconfidence\",\"overtrading\"],\"hints\":[\"Compute the gap: hit rate − average confidence.\",\"Extra trades × cost per trade = extra cost a week.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.4-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A setup has won 45% of its 200 trades. After three losses in a row, a trader halves the size on the next valid signal, though the plan doesn't say to. Which bias is at work?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Anchoring\",\"rationale\":\"Anchoring ties judgement to a number such as your entry price. Here the last three results are doing the steering.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Recency bias\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"House-money effect\",\"rationale\":\"House money raises risk after gains. This is a cut in size after losses.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Disposition effect\",\"rationale\":\"The disposition effect is about exits on open trades. This is the size of a new trade after recent losses.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The base rate, 45% over 200 trades, hasn't changed, but the last three results feel more telling. Cutting size on a valid signal because of them is recency bias. A drawdown rule written in the plan is fine; an improvised cut isn't.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.4\",\"concepts\":[\"recency-bias\",\"base-rates\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.4-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which result is the stronger evidence of a real edge?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"System A: +1.20R per trade over 12 trades\",\"rationale\":\"A high average from 12 trades sits inside a very wide interval. Small samples look more convincing than they are.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Both are equally strong; A's average is just higher\",\"rationale\":\"Evidence depends on the sample size as well as the average. 300 trades narrow the interval far more than 12.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"System B: +0.25R per trade over 300 trades\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither: past results never show anything\",\"rationale\":\"Large samples do carry evidence: the interval narrows as trades add up. That's why 300 trades beat 12.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The interval around an average narrows with the square root of the number of trades. Over 12 trades, +1.20R can easily be luck; over 300 trades, +0.25R is much harder to get by chance. Trusting the short, hot sample is the law of small numbers.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.4\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Which sample has the narrower interval?\",\"An interval's width shrinks as the number of trades grows.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.4-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A new setup made +1.5R per trade over its first 15 trades. A long test, after costs, puts its edge at about +0.3R per trade.\",\"prompt\":\"What should you expect from the next 15 trades?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About +1.5R per trade again: the setup is hot\",\"rationale\":\"That treats the hot start as the edge. Part of it was luck, and luck doesn't carry over.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A loss, to balance out the good start\",\"rationale\":\"That's the gambler's fallacy. Results don't owe the average anything; they just stop being extreme.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing can be said at all\",\"rationale\":\"The long test is the best estimate available. Expect results near it, with wide variation over 15 trades.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Something closer to +0.3R per trade\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Extreme results mix edge and luck. The edge carries over and the luck doesn't, so the next stretch tends to sit closer to the long-run average: regression to the mean. A drop from +1.5R to around +0.3R isn't proof that the setup broke.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.4\",\"concepts\":[\"regression-to-the-mean\"],\"hints\":[\"Split the hot start into edge and luck.\",\"Which part carries over to the next 15 trades?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.5-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long LABX. Since entry you've saved three bullish posts, noted the rising moving average twice in your journal, and skipped the volume panel, which shows heavy selling on every drop.\",\"prompt\":\"What's going on, and what's the process fix?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Confirmation bias: list the evidence against the trade, starting with the volume\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Anchoring: sell when price is back to entry\",\"rationale\":\"Anchoring is about a reference number such as your entry. Here the problem is which evidence you look at.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Recency bias: ignore the last few candles\",\"rationale\":\"Recency overweights the latest results. Here you're filtering evidence by whether it agrees with your position.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: bullish evidence is what a long needs\",\"rationale\":\"A long needs an honest read of both sides. Skipping the panel that disagrees is the bias itself.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Collecting what agrees and skipping what doesn't is confirmation bias, strongest once you hold a position. The fix is a written devil's-advocate step: list the evidence against the trade and compare it with your invalidation.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.5\",\"concepts\":[\"confirmation-bias\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"hints\":[\"Which evidence did you look at, and which did you skip?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.5-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX traded at 120.00 last year and is now 95.00. A trader says: 'It's cheap here, it was 120.00.' What's the main problem with that reasoning?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"95.00 is too close to a round number\",\"rationale\":\"Round numbers can matter for orders, but the issue here is judging today's price against an old, irrelevant one.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's confirmation bias, not anchoring\",\"rationale\":\"Confirmation bias filters evidence. Here a single past number sets the yardstick: that's anchoring.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The old high is an anchor: 120.00 says nothing about what 95.00 is worth now\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: a 21% drop is always a chance to buy\",\"rationale\":\"A drop isn't a signal by itself. Without a setup, 'cheaper than before' is the anchor talking.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Anchoring lets an irrelevant number set the yardstick. Last year's high doesn't make today's price cheap; only the current setup, levels and invalidation can justify a trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.5\",\"concepts\":[\"anchoring\"],\"hints\":[\"What is 120.00 being used for?\",\"Would the trade still make sense if you'd never seen 120.00?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.5-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You plan a long at 101.20. The last swing low is 100.90. Your rule puts the invalidation 0.10 below the last swing low.\",\"prompt\":\"Which pre-trade note follows the process?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Invalidation: when it no longer feels right\",\"rationale\":\"A feeling can't be checked before entry. Invalidation is a price, written down in advance.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Invalidation 100.80; against the idea: lower highs and heavy selling volume\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Invalidation 101.00, so the loss stays small\",\"rationale\":\"101.00 sits above the 100.90 swing low, inside normal noise. The rule puts it 0.10 below the swing low.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No invalidation needed: the trend is up\",\"rationale\":\"Every idea needs a price that proves it wrong. Without one, confirmation bias decides when you exit.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A process note names a price, 100.90 − 0.10 = 100.80, and the evidence against the idea. Both are written before entry, when you have no position to defend.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.5\",\"concepts\":[\"invalidation-level\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Invalidation is a price, set by the rule.\",\"100.90 − 0.10 = ?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.6-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"'I've already lost 300.00 on this trade; selling now would make it real.' Which bias is speaking?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"House-money effect\",\"rationale\":\"House money is about taking more risk with profits. This is about a loss already taken.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Overconfidence\",\"rationale\":\"Overconfidence is a gap between confidence and hit rate. This is about what's already been lost.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Recency bias\",\"rationale\":\"Recency overweights the latest results across trades. Here one position's past cost drives the decision.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Sunk cost\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The 300.00 is gone whether you sell or hold; selling only records it. Letting it decide is the sunk-cost trap. The question that matters: would you open this trade now, at this size?\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.6\",\"concepts\":[\"sunk-cost\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.6-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After three wins your account is 10,400.00 vUSD. Your plan risks 1% per trade. The next setup looks great and you feel you're playing with the market's money.\",\"prompt\":\"How much should the next trade risk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"104.00: 1% of the current account\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"208.00: 2%, since 400.00 of it is profit\",\"rationale\":\"That's the house-money effect. The 400.00 is your money now, and the plan's 1% covers all of it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"100.00: 1% of the starting 10,000.00\",\"rationale\":\"Close, but fixed fractional sizing uses current equity. 1% of 10,400.00 is 104.00.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"400.00: the profit, since it was never really yours\",\"rationale\":\"Risking all the profit on one trade is house money at its extreme: about 3.8% of the account.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Fixed fractional sizing takes 1% of current equity: 10,400.00 × 0.01 = 104.00. Profits join the account like any other money; risking more because they feel free is the house-money effect.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.6\",\"concepts\":[\"house-money-effect\",\"fixed-fractional-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Is profit different from the rest of the account?\",\"Take 1% of the current equity.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.6-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You're long at −0.8R. The swing low your idea depended on broke an hour ago, but the stop hasn't been hit. You're tempted to buy more 'to lower the average'.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the fresh-eyes test say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy more: a lower average makes break-even easier\",\"rationale\":\"That's sunk cost with more size: the average only matters because of what's already lost. It adds risk to a broken idea.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Close or cut the position: you wouldn't open it now\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Hold until it gets back to entry\",\"rationale\":\"Your entry price is an anchor. The market doesn't care where you got in.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Move the stop lower to give it room\",\"rationale\":\"Widening the stop raises the loss you accepted, on an idea that has already failed its test.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Ask: if I were flat now, would I open this position at this size? The level the idea needed has broken, so the answer is no, and the position goes. What it has cost doesn't enter the decision.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.6\",\"concepts\":[\"sunk-cost\",\"averaging-down\"],\"hints\":[\"Pretend you have no position. Would you buy here, at this size?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.7-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which journal pattern is the clearest sign of tilt?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three losses in a row, every rule followed\",\"rationale\":\"Losses with the rules kept are variance. Tilt is about broken rules, not results.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Three rule breaks in your last four trades\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Feeling nervous before entry, then following the plan\",\"rationale\":\"A feeling handled by the plan isn't tilt. Tilt is when the feeling overrides the plan.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A single large winning trade\",\"rationale\":\"One result says nothing about the process. Tilt shows as clustered rule breaks.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Tilt is visible as a cluster of rule breaks, not as a feeling or a run of losses. Counting breaks over your last 10 trades, the tilt index, turns it into something a guard-rail can act on.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.7\",\"concepts\":[\"tilt\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.7-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your guard-rails lock trading at 3 rule breaks in 10 trades, or at a day's loss of 3R. Today: T1 −1R; T2 −1R, entered before the cool-down ended; T3 +1R; T4 −1R, no setup; T5 −1R.\",\"prompt\":\"After which trade does trading lock?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":5,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"trade\"},\"explanation\":\"Breaks come at trades 2 and 4: two, under the threshold. The day's running total is −1, −2, −1, −2 and −3R, so the daily lock triggers at trade 5. Either guard-rail can end the session.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.7\",\"concepts\":[\"behavioural-guard-rails\"],\"hints\":[\"Track two things: the number of rule breaks and the day's running total in R.\",\"Rule breaks come at trades 2 and 4 only.\",\"Running total: −1, −2, −1, −2, then?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.7-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You've just taken a loss that stung. Which response is reappraisal?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"'This loss is one draw from my distribution. The stop did its job.'\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"'I feel nothing. Next trade.'\",\"rationale\":\"That's suppression: hiding the feeling rather than reframing what happened.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"'I won't look at my positions for the rest of the day.'\",\"rationale\":\"That's avoidance. It looks away instead of reframing the loss.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"'I'll win it back on the next one.'\",\"rationale\":\"That's the breakeven pull behind revenge trading, not a reframe.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Reappraisal changes what the event means, early: one loss is one draw from many, and the stop worked as planned. In interview research better traders leaned on it, though the evidence is small and qualitative (grade C).\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.7\",\"concepts\":[\"tilt\",\"cooling-off-pause\"],\"hints\":[\"Which sentence changes what the loss means, rather than hiding it or looking away?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.9-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does it mean to treat a trade as 'one draw from a distribution'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A good analysis makes each trade's result predictable\",\"rationale\":\"No analysis makes one trade's result predictable. An edge is a higher probability across many trades.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A losing trade means the analysis was wrong\",\"rationale\":\"A losing trade can follow a sound analysis; it's one draw. Judge it by the process.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You should change method after each loss\",\"rationale\":\"Changing after each draw means never seeing the distribution. Judge a method over a series.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its result is one sample from many possible outcomes; the edge shows over a series\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"An edge is a higher probability over many trades, not a certainty on one. Each result is a single draw: a loss after a sound process is normal, and only a series of trades shows what the system does.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.9\",\"concepts\":[\"probabilistic-thinking\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.9-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A system wins 40% of trades at +2.5R and loses 60% at −1R. Over 5 independent trades, what's the chance of a net loss, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":33.7,\"tolerance\":0.2,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"One win (+2.5R) can't cover four losses (−4R), so a net loss means zero or one win. Zero wins: 0.6⁵ = 0.0778. One win: 5 × 0.4 × 0.6⁴ = 0.2592. Together: 33.7%, from a system worth +0.40R per trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.9\",\"concepts\":[\"probabilistic-thinking\",\"variance\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the most wins that still leave a net loss over 5 trades.\",\"Zero or one win: add those two chances.\",\"0.6⁵ = 0.0778 and 5 × 0.4 × 0.6⁴ = 0.2592.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[30,50,5]},\"answer\":\"round(((1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) + 5 * pct(w) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w)) * (1 - pct(w))) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A system wins {w}% of trades at +2.5R and loses {100 - w}% at −1R. Over 5 independent trades, what's the chance of a net loss, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"One win (+2.5R) can't cover four losses (−4R), so a net loss means zero or one win. Zero wins: {1 - pct(w):2}⁵. One win: 5 × {pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2}⁴. Together: {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"{1 - pct(w):2}⁵ + 5 × {pct(w):2} × {1 - pct(w):2}⁴ = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.12.9-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You've committed to a block of 20 trades at 1% risk. After 8 trades you're at −4R. Every trade followed the rules, and your journal shows no errors.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the commitment say to do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stop the block: −4R proves the system doesn't work\",\"rationale\":\"Eight trades can't judge a system: a +0.40R system loses over 10 trades 16.7% of the time. The journal shows a clean process.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Raise risk to 2% to finish the block in profit\",\"rationale\":\"That's the breakeven pull, and it doubles every loss. The block's risk was fixed before it started.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Keep executing the block by the rules, and judge it when it's done\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Switch to a different setup for the rest of the block\",\"rationale\":\"Changing the rules mid-block means the block measures nothing. Finish it, then review.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A block is judged as one result, by process first. With every rule followed, −4R after 8 trades is within normal variance for a positive-expectancy system. Finish the block, then review it.\",\"lesson\":\"2.12.9\",\"concepts\":[\"probabilistic-thinking\",\"decision-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Is there any process error in the journal?\",\"When does the commitment say to judge the block?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.2-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A trader's setup card lists the regime, the trigger, the stop rule and the target. Which field is still missing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Last week's profit on the setup\",\"rationale\":\"Results feed the card's statistics, but they aren't a rule. The card still doesn't say when not to trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A forecast for where price goes next\",\"rationale\":\"A card doesn't predict. It says what you do if its conditions are met, and when you stand aside.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The no-trade conditions\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A list of indicators to add later\",\"rationale\":\"Ideas for later go to the rule-change log. The missing field is when the card says no trade.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A complete card says when the setup is allowed, what triggers it, where it's wrong, how it's managed and re-entered, and when you don't take it at all. The no-trade conditions, such as a red event within 15 minutes, are missing here.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.2\",\"concepts\":[\"setup-card\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.2-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which card line could two traders check on the same chart and always agree on?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Enter when momentum looks strong\",\"rationale\":\"'Looks strong' is a judgement two people can argue about. A checkable line names a level, a count or a time.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Trigger: a 1H close above the high of the pullback's lowest candle\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Skip it if the market feels choppy\",\"rationale\":\"'Feels choppy' depends on the reader's mood. A regime rule needs a test, such as the swing highs and lows.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stop somewhere safe under support\",\"rationale\":\"'Somewhere safe' isn't a price. A stop rule names a level, such as the pullback low minus half an ATR.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A checkable field gives the same answer to anyone who looks. A 1H close above a named candle's high is either there or not; 'strong', 'choppy' and 'safe' leave the decision to the moment.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.2\",\"concepts\":[\"setup-card\",\"trading-plan\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask of each line: could two people disagree about whether it happened?\",\"Look for a level, a count or a time.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.2-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Setup A: 24 trades, average +0.60R, 95% interval −0.08R to +1.28R. Setup B: 150 trades, average +0.35R, interval +0.08R to +0.62R. Both spread about 1.7R a trade.\",\"prompt\":\"Which setup's record can you rely on more, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: its average is higher\",\"rationale\":\"On 24 trades A's interval still includes zero. A higher average on a small sample can be luck.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"B: its whole interval is above zero\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Both equally: both averages are positive\",\"rationale\":\"The averages are, but only B's interval stays above zero. The sample size decides how much an average means.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither: no setup can be judged before 1,000 trades\",\"rationale\":\"Too strict. B's 150 trades already put its interval above zero; A needs more trades, not B.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"An average means little without its sample size. A's 24 trades give an interval from −0.08R to +1.28R, which still includes no edge at all. B's 150 trades narrow the interval to +0.08R to +0.62R, entirely above zero.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.2\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\",\"setup-card\"],\"hints\":[\"Look at the intervals, not the averages.\",\"Does each interval include zero?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.3-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which step comes first in the pre-market routine, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Key levels, because price is what you trade\",\"rationale\":\"Levels matter, but if you aren't fit to trade, everything after runs at reduced risk. You come first.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The watchlist, so you find the trades early\",\"rationale\":\"The routine doesn't hunt for trades first. It checks you, then the facts, then which cards could trigger.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The readiness check, because a failure lowers the risk for everything after it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The day's maximum risk, so the limit is set before you look\",\"rationale\":\"The day's risk comes last, sized to what the routine finds, including a failed readiness check.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The routine runs from you to the market to your plan: readiness, overnight, calendar, regime, levels, scenarios, watchlist, and the day's risk last. A failed readiness check cuts the risk for the whole session, so it goes first.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pre-market-routine\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.3-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"At 14:50 your routine finds: fit to trade, regime range, levels 59.80 and 60.40, and a high-impact release at 15:30. You wrote: 'If a red event is within 15 minutes, then no new orders until the spread is normal.' At 15:05 your range card triggers, with a 0.40 stop.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Take it: the card triggered\",\"rationale\":\"The trade would still be open at 15:30. The routine found the release so this decision wouldn't be made in the moment.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Follow your scenario: no new order until the release is out and the spread is normal\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Take it with a wider stop to survive the release\",\"rationale\":\"A wider stop raises the risk, and at a release the stop can still fill past its price.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Take it, and close by hand if price moves fast at 15:30\",\"rationale\":\"A jump at a release is faster than a hand. That's why the routine plans the event before any order.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The routine's job was to catch the 15:30 release before the first order, and you wrote what to do about it. A trade from 15:05 with a 0.40 stop would sit through the release, so the plan says wait until it's out and the spread is back to normal.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.3\",\"concepts\":[\"pre-market-routine\",\"scenario-planning\"],\"hints\":[\"Would the trade still be open at 15:30?\",\"What did your written scenario say?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.3-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"US CPI is out at 15:30 today. Which if-then scenario belongs in your routine?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"If CPI is good, then I buy\",\"rationale\":\"'Good' isn't observable before the details are read, and it bets on a direction. Plan the risk first.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Be careful around CPI\",\"rationale\":\"No trigger and no action. Under pressure, 'careful' gets decided in the moment.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"If I feel confident, then I trade the release\",\"rationale\":\"A feeling isn't an observable trigger, and confidence at a release is exactly what the plan should not rely on.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"If CPI is within 15 minutes, then no new orders until the spread is back to 0.10\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A routine's scenarios name an observable trigger and one action. A time on the calendar and a spread you can read are both observable, and 'no new orders' leaves nothing to decide when the release arrives.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.3\",\"concepts\":[\"scenario-planning\",\"if-then-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"Look for a trigger anyone could see and one action you could check.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.4-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is a complete if-then rule?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"If the market looks heavy, then I sell\",\"rationale\":\"'Looks heavy' isn't observable: two traders could disagree. The trigger needs a price, time, count or event.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"If my check-in is 4 of 5 or higher, then I trade at half size\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"I will stay disciplined when trading gets hard\",\"rationale\":\"No trigger you can see and no action you can check. It's an intention, not a rule.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"If price breaks 60.50, then I buy or wait, depending on the candle\",\"rationale\":\"The trigger is fine, but 'buy or wait' leaves the choice to the moment. A rule has one action.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A complete if-then rule has one observable trigger, here a check-in number, and one action you can check afterwards, here half size. Vague triggers and open choices leave the decision to the moment the rule was meant to cover.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.4\",\"concepts\":[\"if-then-rules\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.4-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your if-then rules cover CPI releases, range breaks and the daily limit. Last month the journal shows four revenge trades, each within ten minutes of a stop-out.\",\"prompt\":\"Which rule is missing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A market trigger for another release\",\"rationale\":\"The revenge trades followed stop-outs, not releases. The missing trigger is about your state.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A higher daily limit, so revenge trades fit inside it\",\"rationale\":\"That makes room for the pattern instead of stopping it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"If a trade hits its stop, then I pause 15 minutes before the next order\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A rule to trade only when calm\",\"rationale\":\"'Calm' isn't observable. The stop-out is: use it as the trigger.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The journal shows a pattern after losses, so the missing rule has a state trigger: the stop-out itself. A pause decided in advance breaks the chain between a loss and the next order.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.4\",\"concepts\":[\"if-then-rules\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"What happened just before each revenge trade?\",\"Turn that event into the trigger.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.4-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After a week, your if-then log shows 12 rules that fired and 5 that never fired. You followed 9 of the 12. What is your adherence, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Adherence = rules followed ÷ rules fired = 9 ÷ 12 = 75%. Rules that never fired don't count either way: they cost nothing and tested nothing.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.4\",\"concepts\":[\"if-then-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"Count only the rules that fired.\",\"Divide the rules followed by the rules fired, then multiply by 100.\",\"9 ÷ 12 × 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"fired\":[6,30,1],\"missed\":[1,6,1],\"idle\":[1,8,1]},\"constraints\":[\"missed < fired\"],\"answer\":\"round((fired - missed) / fired * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"After a week, your if-then log shows {fired} rules that fired and {idle} that never fired. You followed {fired - missed} of the {fired}. What is your adherence, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Adherence = rules followed ÷ rules fired = {fired - missed} ÷ {fired} = {answer:1}%. Rules that never fired don't count either way: they cost nothing and tested nothing.\",\"hint\":\"{fired - missed} ÷ {fired} × 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.5-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You closed a trade at +0.4R because one candle went red; the plan held to a 2R target. Which tag does it get?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"E-EXIT-LATE\",\"rationale\":\"Late is holding past the plan's exit. This trade ended before the plan's target.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"E-SETUP\",\"rationale\":\"The setup was valid; nothing says otherwise. The rule broke on the way out.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Clean: it made money\",\"rationale\":\"A profit doesn't make a trade clean. The plan held to 2R, and the exit broke it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"E-EXIT-EARLY\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The plan's exit was the 2R target, and the trade was closed at +0.4R on a single candle. That's E-EXIT-EARLY, whatever the result: tags describe the process, not the profit.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.5\",\"concepts\":[\"error-taxonomy\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.5-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A month: 35 trades, net −1.2R. Trades tagged E-CHASE cost −2.6R against the same trades done by the rules. What would the month have been without E-CHASE, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.4,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Remove the error's cost: −1.2 − (−2.6) = +1.4R. Error cost compares each trade with the same trade done by the rules, so taking it away shows the month the plan would have produced: a winning one.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.5\",\"concepts\":[\"error-taxonomy\"],\"hints\":[\"The error made the month worse, so removing it helps.\",\"Subtract the error cost from the month: a negative minus a negative.\",\"−1.2 + 2.6.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"loss\":[0.5,4,0.1],\"cost\":[0.5,6,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"cost >= loss + 0.2\"],\"answer\":\"round(cost - loss, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A month: 35 trades, net −{loss:1}R. Trades tagged E-CHASE cost −{cost:1}R against the same trades done by the rules. What would the month have been without E-CHASE, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Remove the error's cost: −{loss:1} − (−{cost:1}) = +{answer:1}R. Error cost compares each trade with the same trade done by the rules, so taking it away shows the month the plan would have produced: a winning one.\",\"hint\":\"−{loss:1} + {cost:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.5-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Two losses this week, both sized at 1%. Trade 1, −1.4R: price gapped through the stop overnight, with nothing on the calendar. Trade 2, −1.2R: entered 10 minutes before a red release, which your card forbids.\",\"prompt\":\"Which loss is a mistake?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Trade 1 only: it lost more\",\"rationale\":\"The size of a loss isn't the test. Trade 1 followed every rule; the gap is the market.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Both: both lost more than 1R\",\"rationale\":\"Losing past 1R can be variance. The question is whether a rule was broken.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trade 2 only\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither: both were sized at 1%\",\"rationale\":\"The size was right in both, but Trade 2 broke the card's no-trade window around red events.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The test is the plan, not the result. Trade 1 had the right size and stop, and nothing was due: the gap is variance. Trade 2 broke a no-trade condition, so it's a mistake, tagged E-EVENT, even though it lost less.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.5\",\"concepts\":[\"mistake-vs-variance\",\"error-taxonomy\"],\"hints\":[\"Ignore the size of each loss.\",\"Which trade broke a rule written on the card?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does a daily review grade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The day's profit against a target\",\"rationale\":\"One day's profit is mostly noise. The review grades what you control: your decisions.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The decisions: the best and the worst, and each loss as mistake or variance\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How price moved after you closed\",\"rationale\":\"That's hindsight: you couldn't see it when you decided. It can't grade the decision.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Which indicator would have worked best today\",\"rationale\":\"Fitting an indicator to one day teaches nothing about the next. The review grades decisions.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A daily review takes five to ten minutes and grades decisions: the day's process KPIs, the best and worst decision, each loss sorted as mistake or variance, and one lesson for tomorrow. Profit over a single day says little.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.6\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-reviews\",\"decision-quality\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your weekly review shows error costs of E-CHASE −1.9R, E-EXIT-EARLY −1.1R and E-SIZE −0.3R. The win rate was 38% and the week ended at −0.8R.\",\"prompt\":\"Which goal should next week's review end with?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Raise the win rate to 50%\",\"rationale\":\"An outcome goal: which trades win is up to the market. A process goal names an action you control.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Fix all three tags next week\",\"rationale\":\"Three goals split your attention. The review ends with one goal, from the biggest cost.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Make back the −0.8R\",\"rationale\":\"An outcome goal that invites bigger positions. The review's goal targets the costliest error.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Enter only at the card's level, never after a run\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The weekly review ends with one process goal, taken from the costliest error. Chasing cost the most, −1.9R, and 'enter only at the card's level' is an action you fully control and can check every day.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.6\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-reviews\"],\"hints\":[\"Which tag cost the most?\",\"Pick the goal that is one action you control.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why replay a trade blind, with the chart stopped at the decision point?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"To practise predicting the next candles\",\"rationale\":\"Prediction isn't the point. The replay judges the decision on what you could see then.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"To check whether the outcome was good\",\"rationale\":\"The outcome is graded separately. Blind replay hides it, so it can't colour the decision.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"So hindsight can't turn what you know now into what you 'should have seen'\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"To make losing trades feel better\",\"rationale\":\"It may do that, but its job is fairness: a valid loss stays valid, and a lucky win gets caught.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Once the outcome is known, it feels as if it was predictable: hindsight bias. A blind replay shows only what you could see at the decision point, so a valid loss isn't turned into a 'mistake' and a lucky win isn't turned into skill.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.6\",\"concepts\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"trading-reviews\"],\"hints\":[\"What changes once you know how the trade ended?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"After 18 trades with a new card, your dashboard shows stop integrity 100% and a win rate of 33%, greyed out.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you read it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stop integrity is the grade; the win rate is greyed out because 18 trades say little\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The card is failing: 33% is too low\",\"rationale\":\"On 18 trades a win rate is mostly noise, which is why it's greyed out below 30 trades.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Ignore stop integrity: only results count\",\"rationale\":\"Process KPIs are the grade because they depend only on you and mean something even on small samples.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Move the target closer to lift the win rate\",\"rationale\":\"That changes a rule because of 18 trades, outside the rule-change process.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Process KPIs are the grade: a stop on every trade is fully in your hands, and 18 trades already measure it. Outcome KPIs are diagnostics, read with their sample size; below 30 trades the dashboard greys them out.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.7\",\"concepts\":[\"process-kpis\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of the two numbers depends only on you?\",\"What does the grey colour say about 18 trades?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.7-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which weekly goal is a process goal?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Make +3R\",\"rationale\":\"An outcome: the market decides much of one week's result.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Finish the week in profit\",\"rationale\":\"An outcome you can miss while doing everything right.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Run the pre-market routine before all 5 sessions\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Beat last week's win rate\",\"rationale\":\"Win rate swings with luck over a week. You can't choose which trades win.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A process goal names an action you fully control, with a number the app can measure: 5 routines in 5 sessions. Profit and win rate are outcomes; chasing them near the end of a week pushes size up.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.7\",\"concepts\":[\"process-goals\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.13.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your breakout card has lost 4 of its last 5 trades, every rule followed. A friend says to add an RSI filter now.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the rule-change process say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Add it now: four losses are enough evidence\",\"rationale\":\"Five trades can't show that. A valid card loses four of five often enough.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Drop the breakout card and trade another\",\"rationale\":\"Switching after a short run resets your evidence to zero. Every card has losing runs.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Add it for this week only, and see\",\"rationale\":\"A week of trades sets no evidence bar, and it mixes two versions of the card in one record.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Log the idea as a hypothesis, set an evidence bar, and test it before any new plan version\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Rules change through a written process: the idea is logged, turned into a hypothesis, given an evidence bar of 30 to 50 trades or a test, tested in the simulator, and only then adopted as a new plan version.\",\"lesson\":\"2.13.7\",\"concepts\":[\"rule-change-governance\"],\"hints\":[\"Were any rules broken in those five trades?\",\"What has to happen before a new plan version?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What keeps an ETF's market price close to the value of the assets it holds?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The exchange resets its price to that value every minute\",\"rationale\":\"Exchanges don't set prices. An ETF trades where buyers and sellers agree; dealers' arbitrage pulls that close to its value.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Large dealers create and redeem units against the basket\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Its yearly expense ratio\",\"rationale\":\"The expense ratio is the fund's fee. It slowly lowers returns; it doesn't hold the price to the holdings' value.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A promise from the fund to buy back at that value\",\"rationale\":\"Ordinary investors sell on the exchange, not to the fund. The link to value comes from dealers creating and redeeming units.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"If an ETF trades above its holdings' value, dealers buy the basket, create new units and sell them; below it, they buy units and redeem them for the basket. That arbitrage keeps the price close, though gaps can open when markets are stressed or closed.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\",\"concepts\":[\"stocks-and-etfs\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The mid is 29.95 the day before the ex-date. You buy 100 shares at the ask, 30.00. The dividend is 0.40; on the ex-date you sell at the bid, 29.50. Net result after commissions (0.05% per fill), before taxes, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-12.98,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Price: 100 × (29.50 − 30.00) = −50.00. Dividend: +40.00. Commissions: 1.50 and 1.48. Net: −50.00 + 40.00 − 1.50 − 1.48 = −12.98. The price dropped by the dividend, so only the costs remain.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\",\"concepts\":[\"corporate-actions\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"On the ex-date the price drops by about the dividend.\",\"Add the price result and the dividend, then subtract a commission on each fill.\",\"−50.00 + 40.00 − 1.50 − 1.48.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[50,300,10],\"div\":[0.2,1.5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(units * (29.9 - div - 30) + units * div - commission(units * 30) - commission(units * (29.9 - div)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"The mid is 29.95 the day before the ex-date. You buy {units} shares at the ask, 30.00. The dividend is {div:2}; on the ex-date the mid drops by it and you sell at the bid, {29.9 - div:2}. Net result after commissions (0.05% per fill), before taxes, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Price: {units} × ({29.9 - div:2} − 30.00) = {units * (29.9 - div - 30):2}. Dividend: +{units * div:2}. Commissions: {commission(units * 30):2} and {commission(units * (29.9 - div)):2}. Net: {answer:2}. The price dropped by the dividend, so only the costs remain.\",\"hint\":\"{units * (29.9 - div - 30):2} + {units * div:2} − {commission(units * 30):2} − {commission(units * (29.9 - div)):2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"An index rises 5% one day and falls 4.76% the next, ending where it started. A 2× daily leveraged ETF on it starts at 100.00.\",\"prompt\":\"Where does the ETF end, before fees?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"100.00, like the index\",\"rationale\":\"The ETF doubles each day's move, not the two-day result. +10% then −9.52% doesn't return to the start.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"About 100.48\",\"rationale\":\"Doubling daily moves doesn't help in a round trip: a rise and a fall back to the start always leave a 2× ETF lower.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"About 99.52\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"90.00, twice the index's loss\",\"rationale\":\"The index has no loss over the two days. The ETF's small loss comes from compounding doubled daily moves.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Day 1: +10%, 100.00 → 110.00. Day 2 the index falls 4.76%, so the ETF falls 9.52%: 110.00 → 99.52. The fund resets its leverage daily, so back-and-forth moves wear it down even when the index ends flat.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\",\"concepts\":[\"stocks-and-etfs\"],\"hints\":[\"Apply each day's doubled move in turn.\",\"Day 1: +10%. Day 2: the index falls 4.76%, so the ETF falls 9.52%.\",\"110.00 × (1 − 0.0952).\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're short 200 shares when the company pays a 0.80 dividend. What happens?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"You receive 160.00, like any holder\",\"rationale\":\"You don't own the shares; the lender does. A short owes the dividend to the lender.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: shorts are outside dividends\",\"rationale\":\"The lender still expects the dividend it would have received. Your account pays it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The borrow fee is waived for that day\",\"rationale\":\"The borrow fee keeps running. The dividend is an extra payment on top of it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"You pay 160.00 to the lender\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A short sold shares it borrowed, so the lender is still owed everything a holder gets: 200 × 0.80 = 160.00 comes out of your account. On the ex-date the price drops by about the dividend, so the short gains that drop and pays it back.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.2\",\"concepts\":[\"short-selling\"],\"hints\":[\"Who owns the shares you sold short?\",\"The lender is still owed whatever a holder would receive.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You plan a short at 50.00. You want an overnight jump to 56.00 to cost at most 200.00, before costs. What is the largest whole number of units?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":33,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"A jump to 56.00 costs 6.00 a unit. 200.00 ÷ 6.00 = 33.3, rounded down to 33: 198.00 before costs, 199.76 with commissions. 34 units would lose 204.00. Sizing for the gap, not just the stop, keeps a squeeze survivable.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.2\",\"concepts\":[\"short-selling\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the loss per unit if price opens at 56.00.\",\"6.00 a unit. Divide the cap by it.\",\"200.00 ÷ 6.00 = 33.3. Round down.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"gap\":[53,60,0.5],\"cap\":[100,300,50]},\"answer\":\"floor(cap / (gap - 50))\",\"prompt\":\"You plan a short at 50.00. You want an overnight jump to {gap:2} to cost at most {cap:2}, before costs. What is the largest whole number of units?\",\"explanation\":\"A jump to {gap:2} costs {gap - 50:2} a unit. {cap:2} ÷ {gap - 50:2} = {cap / (gap - 50):2}, rounded down to {answer}. Sizing for the gap, not just the stop, keeps a squeeze survivable.\",\"hint\":\"{cap:2} ÷ {gap - 50:2}, rounded down.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A US stock closed at 40.00 yesterday. Today it trades at 35.50, down 11.25%. You send a short sale at the bid.\",\"prompt\":\"What happens to your order?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It fills at the bid, like any sale\",\"rationale\":\"After a 10% fall from the prior close the short-sale restriction applies: a short can't sell at the bid.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Shorts are allowed only above the best bid, today and tomorrow\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Short sales are banned in the stock for a week\",\"rationale\":\"It's a price test, not a ban, and it lasts the rest of the day and the next day.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It fills, but the broker must close it out at once\",\"rationale\":\"Close-out rules deal with failed deliveries. Here the restriction is about the price a short may sell at.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A fall of 10% or more from the prior close triggers the US short-sale restriction (Rule 201) for the rest of the day and the next: shorts may only sell above the best bid, so they can't hit bids on the way down.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.2\",\"concepts\":[\"short-selling\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare today's fall with the 10% trigger.\",\"40.00 to 35.50 is an 11.25% fall.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.3-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"vEUR/vUSD is quoted at 1.0850. What does that mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"One vUSD costs 1.0850 vEUR\",\"rationale\":\"That reads the pair backwards. The first currency, the base, is the one priced, in units of the second.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"One vEUR costs 1.0850 vUSD\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The pair has moved 1,085 pips today\",\"rationale\":\"The quote is a price, not a move. Pips measure changes in that price, 0.0001 each.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The spread is 0.0850\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is the gap between bid and ask, a pip or two on major pairs. It isn't part of the price.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A pair prices one unit of the base currency, the first one, in the quote currency, the second. At 1.0850 one vEUR costs 1.0850 vUSD. A move to 1.0851 is one pip; a fifth decimal, if shown, counts pipettes.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.3\",\"concepts\":[\"forex-quotes\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.3-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"vUSD/vJPY is at 160.00. What is one pip worth on one standard lot, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6.25,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A yen pip is 0.01. On 100,000 units that's 1,000 vJPY a pip. Converted at 160.00: 1,000 ÷ 160.00 = 6.25 vUSD. When the dollar is the base currency, the pip value changes with the rate.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.3\",\"concepts\":[\"forex-quotes\"],\"hints\":[\"In yen pairs a pip is 0.01.\",\"100,000 × 0.01 = 1,000 vJPY a pip.\",\"Convert: 1,000 ÷ 160.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"rate\":[140,170,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(1000 / rate, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"vUSD/vJPY is at {rate:2}. What is one pip worth on one standard lot, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"A yen pip is 0.01. On 100,000 units that's 1,000 vJPY a pip. Converted at {rate:2}: 1,000 ÷ {rate:2} = {answer:2} vUSD. When the dollar is the base currency, the pip value changes with the rate.\",\"hint\":\"Convert: 1,000 ÷ {rate:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.3-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your risk budget is 150.00. On vEUR/vUSD your stop is 30 pips away. How many standard lots, rounded down to 0.01, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.5,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"lots\"},\"explanation\":\"One lot of vEUR/vUSD moves 10.00 a pip, so a 30-pip stop costs 300.00 a lot. 150.00 ÷ 300.00 = 0.50 lots. Adding the spread to the distance would trim it slightly.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.3\",\"concepts\":[\"position-sizing\",\"forex-quotes\"],\"hints\":[\"Find what the stop costs on one standard lot.\",\"30 pips × 10.00 = 300.00 a lot.\",\"150.00 ÷ 300.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"risk\":[50,200,10],\"stop\":[10,60,1]},\"answer\":\"floor(risk * 10 / stop) / 100\",\"prompt\":\"Your risk budget is {risk:2}. On vEUR/vUSD your stop is {stop} pips away. How many standard lots, rounded down to 0.01, before costs?\",\"explanation\":\"One lot of vEUR/vUSD moves 10.00 a pip, so a {stop}-pip stop costs {stop * 10:2} a lot. {risk:2} ÷ {stop * 10:2} = {risk / (stop * 10):4}, rounded down to {answer:2} lots.\",\"hint\":\"{risk:2} ÷ ({stop} × 10.00), rounded down to 0.01.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is the overnight swap on an FX position?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A fee for keeping the platform open overnight\",\"rationale\":\"Platforms don't charge for the clock. The swap comes from the interest rates of the two currencies.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The spread widening at rollover\",\"rationale\":\"Spreads often widen around rollover, but that's a separate cost. The swap is an interest payment.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The interest difference between the two currencies, paid or earned past rollover\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A tax on positions held overnight\",\"rationale\":\"It isn't a tax. It reflects the interest rates of the two currencies, plus the broker's markup.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Holding a pair past 17:00 New York time means holding one currency funded by the other. You pay or earn the rate difference, adjusted by the broker's markup, for each night held; Wednesday's counts three.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.4\",\"concepts\":[\"overnight-financing\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold 0.50 lots with a swap of −6.00 per lot per night, from Monday 09:00 to Thursday 09:00, Bucharest time. Your risk is 100.00. What does the swap cost, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.15,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Rollovers in between: Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, which counts three: 5 nights. 5 × 0.50 × 6.00 = 15.00, and 15.00 ÷ 100.00 = 0.15R.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.4\",\"concepts\":[\"overnight-financing\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the rollovers between the open and the close.\",\"Monday 1, Tuesday 1, Wednesday 3: 5 nights.\",\"5 × 0.50 × 6.00, then divide by 100.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lots\":[0.1,1,0.1],\"swap\":[2,12,1]},\"answer\":\"round(5 * lots * swap / 100, 3)\",\"prompt\":\"You hold {lots:2} lots with a swap of −{swap:2} per lot per night, from Monday 09:00 to Thursday 09:00, Bucharest time. Your risk is 100.00. What does the swap cost, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Rollovers in between: Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday, which counts three: 5 nights. 5 × {lots:2} × {swap:2} = {5 * lots * swap:2}, and {5 * lots * swap:2} ÷ 100.00 = {answer:3}R.\",\"hint\":\"5 nights × {lots:2} × {swap:2}, then divide by 100.00.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A pair has paid +6.00 per lot per night for a year. A forum post says: 'Hold 5 lots and collect 210.00 a week, risk-free.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with the claim?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the swap is fixed by the exchange\",\"rationale\":\"Spot FX has no exchange, and swaps follow interest rates, which change. Nothing about it is fixed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Swaps are only paid on Wednesdays\",\"rationale\":\"Swaps are paid at every rollover; Wednesday's just counts three nights.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Carry pays small amounts steadily, then can lose far more in a sharp unwind\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only that 5 lots ties up too much margin\",\"rationale\":\"Margin is a side issue. The real risk is the price: 5 lots move 50.00 a pip on a USD-quoted pair.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Carry has paid over long periods, but with negative skew: steady small income and rare, sharp losses when crowded positions unwind. At 5 lots, a 100-pip move against you on a USD-quoted pair costs 5,000.00, about 24 weeks of swap.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.4\",\"concepts\":[\"overnight-financing\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare a week's swap with what a normal price move costs at 5 lots.\",\"5 lots × 10.00 a pip × 100 pips = 5,000.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.5-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which route to index exposure charges overnight funding for every night you hold it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"An index ETF\",\"rationale\":\"An ETF charges a small yearly fee taken inside the fund, not nightly funding. You pay for it in full, so nothing is lent.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An index future\",\"rationale\":\"A future's financing is built into its price and its roll, not charged to you night by night.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Buying the index directly\",\"rationale\":\"An index is a number, not an asset, so you can't buy it. You need a vehicle that tracks it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A CFD on the index\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A CFD gives you the full exposure for a margin deposit, so the provider charges funding, a benchmark rate plus a markup, for every night it's held. ETFs charge a small yearly fee; futures build financing into their price.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.5\",\"concepts\":[\"cfds-and-indices\",\"overnight-financing\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.5-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold a CFD on an index. Who is your counterparty?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The exchange where the index's shares trade\",\"rationale\":\"The CFD never reaches that exchange. It's a private contract between you and the provider.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The CFD provider\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A clearing house that guarantees both sides\",\"rationale\":\"Clearing houses stand behind exchange-traded futures. CFDs are traded over the counter, with no clearing house.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whoever bought the opposite CFD at the same time\",\"rationale\":\"Other clients' trades may offset yours on the provider's book, but your contract is with the provider.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A CFD is an over-the-counter contract with the provider, which quotes the price and takes the other side. A provider that keeps client trades on its own book profits when clients lose: a conflict to know about before you trade.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.5\",\"concepts\":[\"cfds-and-indices\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask where the contract is traded: on an exchange, or over the counter?\",\"Over the counter, your contract is with the firm that sold it to you.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.5-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold 20,000.00 of an index through a CFD, with funding at 6% a year. What does it cost over 45 days, on a 365-day year, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":147.95,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Funding = exposure × rate × days ÷ 365 = 20,000.00 × 6% × 45 ÷ 365 = 147.95. It grows every night, so the holding period decides whether a CFD is cheap.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.5\",\"concepts\":[\"overnight-financing\"],\"hints\":[\"Funding = exposure × yearly rate × days ÷ 365.\",\"20,000.00 × 6% = 1,200.00 a year.\",\"1,200.00 × 45 ÷ 365.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"expo\":[5000,50000,1000],\"rate\":[3,8,0.5],\"days\":[5,120,5]},\"answer\":\"round(expo * pct(rate) * days / 365, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You hold {expo:2} of an index through a CFD, with funding at {rate:1}% a year. What does it cost over {days} days, on a 365-day year, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Funding = exposure × rate × days ÷ 365 = {expo:2} × {rate:1}% × {days} ÷ 365 = {answer:2}. It grows every night, so the holding period decides whether a CFD is cheap.\",\"hint\":\"{expo * pct(rate):2} a year, × {days} ÷ 365.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.6-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Equity 8,000.00. You buy 0.50 lots of vEUR/vUSD at 1.2000 with 30:1 maximum leverage. What is your free margin, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6000,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Exposure: 50,000 × 1.2000 = 60,000.00. Required margin: 60,000.00 ÷ 30 = 2,000.00. Free margin = equity − margin = 8,000.00 − 2,000.00 = 6,000.00. Margin level: 8,000.00 ÷ 2,000.00 × 100 = 400%.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.6\",\"concepts\":[\"margin-close-out\",\"leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"First find the exposure in vUSD: units × price.\",\"Required margin = exposure ÷ 30.\",\"Free margin = 8,000.00 − 2,000.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"equity\":[3000,20000,500],\"lots\":[0.1,1,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"equity > lots * 4000\"],\"answer\":\"round(equity - lots * 4000, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Equity {equity:2}. You buy {lots:2} lots of vEUR/vUSD at 1.2000 with 30:1 maximum leverage. What is your free margin, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Exposure: {lots * 100000:0} × 1.2000 = {lots * 120000:2}. Required margin: {lots * 120000:2} ÷ 30 = {lots * 4000:2}. Free margin = {equity:2} − {lots * 4000:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Required margin = {lots * 120000:2} ÷ 30. Subtract it from {equity:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.6-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Equity 6,000.00. You buy 1 standard lot of vEUR/vUSD at 1.2000 with 30:1 maximum leverage. The close-out comes at 50% of the required margin. At what price, to 4 decimals?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.16,\"tolerance\":0.0001,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Margin: 120,000.00 ÷ 30 = 4,000.00. The close-out comes at 2,000.00 of equity, after a 4,000.00 loss. At 10.00 a pip that's 400 pips: 1.2000 − 0.0400 = 1.1600.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.6\",\"concepts\":[\"margin-close-out\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the required margin, then the equity at which the close-out comes.\",\"Margin 4,000.00; close-out at 2,000.00 of equity, after a 4,000.00 loss.\",\"4,000.00 ÷ 10.00 = 400 pips below 1.2000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"equity\":[3000,10000,500]},\"answer\":\"round(1.2 - (equity - 2000) / 100000, 4)\",\"prompt\":\"Equity {equity:2}. You buy 1 standard lot of vEUR/vUSD at 1.2000 with 30:1 maximum leverage. The close-out comes at 50% of the required margin. At what price, to 4 decimals?\",\"explanation\":\"Margin: 120,000.00 ÷ 30 = 4,000.00. The close-out comes at 2,000.00 of equity, after a {equity - 2000:2} loss. At 10.00 a pip that's {(equity - 2000) / 10:0} pips: {answer:4}.\",\"hint\":\"({equity:2} − 2,000.00) ÷ 10.00 pips below 1.2000.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.6-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"An EU retail CFD account holds 2,000.00 and runs 25:1 effective leverage. A weekend gap closes everything at a loss of 2,600.00.\",\"prompt\":\"Which statement is true?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The trader owes the provider 600.00\",\"rationale\":\"Negative balance protection covers EU retail CFD accounts: the balance stops at 0.00, so nothing is owed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The close-out guaranteed an exit at 50% of margin\",\"rationale\":\"The close-out triggers at that level but fills at the next available price. After a gap that can be far worse.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Negative balance protection refunds the 2,000.00\",\"rationale\":\"The protection only stops the balance going below zero. Everything down to zero is still lost.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The balance stops at 0.00, and the 2,000.00 is lost\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Negative balance protection stops the account at 0.00, so the extra 600.00 isn't owed, but the 2,000.00 is gone. At 25:1, a 4% move takes the whole account. Sizing from a stop keeps effective leverage, and gaps, survivable.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.6\",\"concepts\":[\"negative-balance-protection\",\"margin-close-out\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate two questions: what's lost, and whether anything is owed.\",\"The protection limits the loss to the money in the account.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.7-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A swing trader holds CFD positions for about three weeks. Which layer of the cost stack usually grows largest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The spread\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is paid once per round trip. Over three weeks, nightly financing adds far more.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Overnight financing\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The borrow fee\",\"rationale\":\"A borrow fee is paid on stock shorts. CFD positions pay financing instead.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Slippage\",\"rationale\":\"Slippage hits fast markets at entry and exit. Holding for weeks, the nightly charge piles up instead.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Financing is charged for every night a CFD is held, so it grows with the holding period. At 5.5% a year, three weeks on 5,000.00 cost 15.82, while the CFD's spread was 5.00, paid once.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-stack\",\"overnight-financing\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.7-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An instrument's ATR is 45 pips. Its spread is 0.8 pips and commission adds 0.7 pips. What is its tradability ratio?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":30,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Tradability ratio = ATR ÷ (spread + commission) = 45 ÷ (0.8 + 0.7) = 45 ÷ 1.5 = 30. A typical day's move is 30 times the round-trip cost.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-stack\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the costs first, in pips.\",\"0.8 + 0.7 = 1.5 pips.\",\"45 ÷ 1.5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"atr\":[20,120,5],\"spread\":[0.4,2,0.1],\"fee\":[0,1,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(atr / (spread + fee), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"An instrument's ATR is {atr} pips. Its spread is {spread:1} pips and commission adds {fee:1} pips. What is its tradability ratio?\",\"explanation\":\"Tradability ratio = ATR ÷ (spread + commission) = {atr} ÷ ({spread:1} + {fee:1}) = {atr} ÷ {spread + fee:1} = {answer:1}.\",\"hint\":\"{atr} ÷ {spread + fee:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.7-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A stock round trip costs 12.00. The same exposure as a CFD costs 6.00 of spread plus 0.80 of funding a day. On which day does the CFD first cost more than the stock?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":8,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"day\"},\"explanation\":\"The CFD starts 6.00 cheaper and loses 0.80 a day: 6.00 ÷ 0.80 = 7.5 days. Day 7: 6.00 + 5.60 = 11.60, still cheaper. Day 8: 6.00 + 6.40 = 12.40, dearer.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.7\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-stack\",\"overnight-financing\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the CFD's head start, then how fast funding eats it.\",\"12.00 − 6.00 = 6.00 of head start, at 0.80 a day.\",\"6.00 ÷ 0.80 = 7.5 days. Which day crosses?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"stock\":[8,20,1],\"spread\":[2,6,0.5],\"cents\":[30,150,10]},\"constraints\":[\"stock > spread + 1\"],\"answer\":\"floor((stock - spread) * 100 / cents) + 1\",\"prompt\":\"A stock round trip costs {stock:2}. The same exposure as a CFD costs {spread:2} of spread plus {cents / 100:2} of funding a day. On which day does the CFD first cost more than the stock?\",\"explanation\":\"The CFD starts {stock - spread:2} cheaper and loses {cents / 100:2} a day: {stock - spread:2} ÷ {cents / 100:2} = {(stock - spread) * 100 / cents:2} days. The first day past that is day {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"{stock - spread:2} ÷ {cents / 100:2} days, then the next whole day.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.8-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Under the EU's retail CFD rules, in place since 2018, what is the maximum leverage on a CFD on a single share?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"30:1\",\"rationale\":\"30:1 is the cap for major currency pairs, the most liquid markets. Single shares are riskier and capped lower.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"20:1\",\"rationale\":\"20:1 applies to non-major pairs, gold and major indices. A single share is capped lower.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"5:1\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"2:1\",\"rationale\":\"2:1 is the cap for crypto CFDs, the most volatile category.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The EU caps retail CFD leverage by how volatile the underlying is: 30:1 on major pairs; 20:1 on non-major pairs, gold and major indices; 10:1 on other commodities and non-major indices; 5:1 on single shares; 2:1 on crypto.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\",\"concepts\":[\"retail-regulation\",\"leverage\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.8-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A US friend has 8,000 dollars in a margin account. He read that he can't make more than 3 day trades in 5 business days.\",\"prompt\":\"In late 2026, what's the accurate picture?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's still the rule at every US broker\",\"rationale\":\"The pattern day trader rule was replaced in 2026. Some brokers may still apply it during the phase-in, but not all.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It was replaced in 2026 by intraday margin; his broker may apply it until it switches, by late 2027\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"There are no limits now: any amount, any size\",\"rationale\":\"The 25,000 minimum is gone, but intraday margin deficits must still be covered promptly, or the account is restricted.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The rule only ever applied to cash accounts\",\"rationale\":\"It applied to margin accounts. Cash accounts have their own limits, set by settlement.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The pattern day trader rule (4 or more day trades in 5 business days, 25,000 dollars of equity) was replaced in 2026 by an intraday margin standard. Brokers may phase the change in until late 2027, so he should check his broker's current terms.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\",\"concepts\":[\"retail-regulation\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what the rule was, when it changed, and whether every broker switched at once.\",\"Brokers have a phase-in period until late 2027.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-2.14.8-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A broker's website shows a licence number and a regulator's logo. What is the most reliable check?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Ask the account manager to email the licence certificate\",\"rationale\":\"Documents from the firm itself can be forged. Only the regulator's own register is independent.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Click the regulator's logo on the broker's site\",\"rationale\":\"Logos and links on the broker's own site are easy to fake. Go to the regulator's site directly.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Search the number on the regulator's own register, and match the entity name and website\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Read reviews to see whether others got paid\",\"rationale\":\"Reviews can be bought, and scams often pay out early to build trust. The register is the authority.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Go to the regulator's site yourself, search its register for the number, and check that the legal entity, website and permissions, including the right to serve clients in your country, match. Clone firms copy real licence numbers, so the name and website must match too.\",\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-due-diligence\",\"retail-regulation\"],\"hints\":[\"Which source can the broker not control?\",\"Clone firms copy real numbers: check the name and website too.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.1-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX trades at 100.45. Price later reaches each of these resting orders. Which one becomes a market order when it's reached?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A buy limit at 99.60\",\"rationale\":\"A limit fills at its price or better and waits passively. It absorbs selling instead of adding to it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A long's take-profit, a sell limit at 101.00\",\"rationale\":\"A take-profit is a limit order: it fills passively at 101.00 and absorbs buying.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A long's stop-loss, a sell stop at 99.15\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A short's take-profit, a buy limit at 100.00\",\"rationale\":\"That's a limit too. It waits at 100.00 and absorbs the sellers who arrive there.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A stop order sleeps until price reaches its trigger, then becomes a market order and takes whatever bids are there. Limits, take-profits included, fill passively at their price: they absorb flow rather than push it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-pool\",\"stop-loss-order\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.1-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX has ranged between 49.20 and 50.80 for two days, with 50.00 in the middle. Price is at 50.30.\",\"prompt\":\"Where do sell stops most plausibly cluster?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Exactly at 50.00\",\"rationale\":\"Orders exactly on a round number are mostly take-profits. Stops tend to sit a little beyond the level.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"49.10–49.19, just under the range low\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"50.81–50.90, just above the range high\",\"rationale\":\"Stops above the range protect shorts. Those are buy stops, not sell stops.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"49.60, in the middle of the lower half\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing obvious sits at 49.60. Stops gather beyond levels everyone can see, such as the range low.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Longs who bought near the range low put their protective sell stops just beyond it, where their idea is wrong. That's the most obvious place on this chart; just under 50.00 is a second candidate. Plausible, not measured: no chart shows the real orders.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-pool\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"hints\":[\"Sell stops protect longs. Where did longs buy in this range?\",\"A long's stop goes where the idea is wrong: just beyond the level it bought on.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.1-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A video says: 'Price is drawn to the liquidity under the range low, so expect it to go there.' Which statement is accurate?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"True: large stop pools act like magnets\",\"rationale\":\"That's the 'magnet' claim, grade D. No study shows price is pulled toward resting orders.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Stops probably rest there and can speed a fall that reaches them; nothing shows price is pulled there\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"False: no stops rest under range lows\",\"rationale\":\"Stops beyond obvious levels are documented for round numbers (Osler, grade B). The claim fails on attraction, not on presence.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"True, but only near round numbers\",\"rationale\":\"Round numbers attract orders, not price. The order clustering is documented; a pull on price isn't, anywhere.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Two claims hide in one sentence. Orders clustering beyond obvious levels is documented (grade B). Price being attracted to them is not (grade D), and it treats the market as someone with a goal. Use the first; drop the second.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-pool\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Split the sentence into two claims: where the orders are, and where price goes.\",\"Which of the two has evidence behind it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.2-1\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Around a round number such as 100.00, where did Osler find each order type clustering?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Take-profits just above it, stop-loss buys exactly on it\",\"rationale\":\"Reversed. Take-profits sat on the number; stops avoided it and sat just beyond.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"All of them exactly at 100.00\",\"rationale\":\"Only take-profits favoured the number itself. Stop-losses ending in 00 were under half as common: 4.4% against 9.3%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Take-profits exactly on it, stop-loss buys just above, stop-loss sells just below\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stop-loss buys just below it, stop-loss sells just above\",\"rationale\":\"A buy stop protects a short, so it sits above the level, where the short is proven wrong. Sell stops mirror it below.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Take-profits clustered exactly on round numbers: 9.3% ended in 00, against 4.4% of stop-losses. Stop-loss buys sat just above, 14.4% in xx01–xx10 against 7.4% just below, and stop-loss sells mirrored them. One bank, FX data: grade B.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-clustering\",\"round-numbers\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.2-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"LABX rises toward 100.00. Longs' take-profits rest at 100.00; shorts' stop-loss buys rest at 100.01–100.10.\",\"prompt\":\"What does each cluster tend to do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both pull price up toward them\",\"rationale\":\"Resting orders don't pull. Take-profits sell into the rise; stops buy only once triggered.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The buy stops stall it; the take-profits speed it up\",\"rationale\":\"Reversed. A take-profit is a passive sell limit; a buy stop becomes a market buy when hit.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neither matters: only news moves price\",\"rationale\":\"Order flow moves price too. Passive limits absorb, triggered stops push, and Osler links that to trends speeding up through clusters.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The take-profits tend to stall the rise; if 100.00 gives way, the triggered buy stops can speed it up\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The take-profits are sell limits: they meet buyers head-on and tend to stall price at 100.00. The buy stops sit just beyond; once triggered they become market buys and add to the rise. Which wins depends on sizes a chart doesn't show.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-clustering\",\"liquidity-pool\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of the two orders is a limit, and which becomes a market order?\",\"A limit absorbs flow; a triggered stop adds to it.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.2-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're long LABX at 100.60. You move your stop below the crowded band, to 99.87. Your budget is 100.00, including 0.05% commission on entry and on exit. What is the largest whole number of units?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":120,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Loss per unit = 0.73 + 0.0005 × (100.60 + 99.87) = 0.8302. 100.00 ÷ 0.8302 = 120.4, so 120 units. Check with the commissions rounded up to the cent: 120 units lose 99.64 at the stop; 121 would lose 100.47.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-adjusted-sizing\",\"stop-clustering\"],\"hints\":[\"Loss per unit = distance to the stop + commission on both sides.\",\"0.73 + 0.0005 × (100.60 + 99.87) = 0.8302 a unit.\",\"100.00 ÷ 0.8302 = 120.4. Round down.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"entry\":[80,120,0.05],\"dist\":[0.5,1.5,0.01],\"budget\":[50,200,50]},\"constraints\":[\"floor(budget / (dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist))) * dist + commission(floor(budget / (dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist))) * entry) + commission(floor(budget / (dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist))) * (entry - dist)) <= budget\"],\"answer\":\"floor(budget / (dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist)))\",\"prompt\":\"You're long LABX at {entry:2}. You move your stop below the crowded band, to {entry - dist:2}. Your budget is {budget:2}, including 0.05% commission on entry and on exit. What is the largest whole number of units?\",\"explanation\":\"Loss per unit = {dist:2} + 0.0005 × ({entry:2} + {entry - dist:2}) = {dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist):4}. {budget:2} ÷ {dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist):4} = {budget / (dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist)):1}, so {answer} units. With the commissions rounded up to the cent, they lose {round(answer * dist + commission(answer * entry) + commission(answer * (entry - dist)), 2):2} at the stop.\",\"hint\":\"{budget:2} ÷ {dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist):4} = {budget / (dist + 0.0005 * (2 * entry - dist)):1}. Round down.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why can a stop cascade feed itself?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stops are limit orders that pull price toward them\",\"rationale\":\"Stops aren't limits. They wait until triggered, then become market orders.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Market makers chase the stops on purpose\",\"rationale\":\"That's an intent story. The mechanism needs nobody's intent: market orders walking a thin book are enough.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Each triggered stop widens the spread to 1.00\",\"rationale\":\"Spreads can widen in fast moves, but the feedback comes from fills moving price into the next trigger.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Triggered stops become market orders whose fills move price into the next stops\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A triggered stop is a market order. It takes what's offered, so each fill can be a little worse. If those fills reach the next cluster's triggers, more market orders follow: positive feedback. Osler (2005) links this to trends speeding up through stop clusters (grade B).\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stop-cascade\",\"slippage\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Yesterday's high is 101.00. A candle trades up to 101.14 and closes at 100.92. The next candle closes at 100.85. Your written rule: back inside within one bar is a sweep; a close beyond with follow-through is a breakout.\",\"prompt\":\"How do you classify the break?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A sweep: it closed back inside on the same bar\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A breakout: it traded 0.14 above the level\",\"rationale\":\"Trading above isn't closing above. The rule reads closes, and this bar closed at 100.92, inside.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A stop hunt by a large player\",\"rationale\":\"The chart shows a sweep, not who caused it or why. 'Stop hunt' assumes an intent nobody can see.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Unclassified: wait ten more bars\",\"rationale\":\"The rule decides within one bar. Changing the wait after seeing the chart turns a rule into a guess.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Price traded through 101.00, likely triggering the buy stops there, then closed back inside on the same bar: a sweep by the written rule. What the rule doesn't say is that a reversal now follows. That claim is grade D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-sweep\",\"false-breakout\"],\"hints\":[\"Look at the close, not the high.\",\"Back inside within one bar is the sweep condition.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does the evidence say about 'after a sweep comes a reversal'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's well established: sweeps mark turning points\",\"rationale\":\"No published study shows that. It's a practitioner claim, grade D.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It works in FX but not in stocks\",\"rationale\":\"There's no such split. The one test, on G10 FX, found continuation more often, not reversal.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The one systematic test, an unreviewed preprint, found continuation more often than reversal\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's proven, because stops are always hunted before reversals\",\"rationale\":\"That's intent attribution. A chart can show that stops were triggered, never that someone hunted them.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Trends speeding up through stop clusters is grade B (Osler 2005). Reversal after a sweep is grade D: the one systematic test, Mahadzva 2026, an unreviewed preprint on G10 FX, found swept levels followed more often by continuation. Treat a sweep as a classified event, not a signal.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-sweep\",\"stop-cascade\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about what a cascade does to a move: speed it up, or reverse it?\",\"Grade D means claimed, not shown.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Two swing highs sit at 101.48 and 101.52. ATR is 0.60, and your rule calls two highs equal if their gap is at most 0.1 × ATR. What is the tolerance, in price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.06,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Tolerance = 0.1 × 0.60 = 0.06. The gap, |101.52 − 101.48| = 0.04, is inside it, so these count as equal highs. The tolerance is written before you look, not chosen to fit the chart.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"equal-highs-lows\"],\"hints\":[\"The tolerance is a fraction of ATR.\",\"0.1 × 0.60.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"atr\":[0.3,1.5,0.1],\"h1\":[95,105,0.01],\"gap\":[0.01,0.12,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(0.1 * atr, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Two swing highs sit at {h1:2} and {h1 + gap:2}. ATR is {atr:2}, and your rule calls two highs equal if their gap is at most 0.1 × ATR. What is the tolerance, in price?\",\"explanation\":\"Tolerance = 0.1 × {atr:2} = {answer:2}. The gap is {gap:2}; the two highs count as equal only if it is {answer:2} or less. The tolerance is written before you look, not chosen to fit the chart.\",\"hint\":\"0.1 × {atr:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"'Equal highs will be swept.' What does the claim say, and what grade does it get?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"That stops rest above equal highs: grade A\",\"rationale\":\"That's a different, weaker claim about where orders sit, and even it is only plausible, grade C, never measured for swings.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"That price will trade above them, a forecast with no published probability: grade D\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Proven by every double top that later broke\",\"rationale\":\"Counting only the breaks is selection. You'd need the share that broke against random levels at the same distance.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A mechanical definition, like a swing high\",\"rationale\":\"A definition can't be wrong; this is a forecast about what price will do. Forecasts need tests.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The claim is a forecast: price will trade through the level. No study publishes how often that happens beyond the base rate, so it's grade D. The weaker claim, that stops plausibly rest there, is grade C.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"equal-highs-lows\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Is it about where orders sit, or about what price will do?\",\"Ask what data would back it, and whether that data exists.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why compare equal highs with random levels at the same distance from price?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because random levels are where stops really are\",\"rationale\":\"Random levels are a control, not a guess about orders. They show what any level gets.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Distance doesn't matter; any random level would do\",\"rationale\":\"Distance matters most. Close levels get hit more often, so the control must sit at the same distance.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Price reaches nearby levels often anyway; the control shows the base rate any level gets\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"To make the test harder to pass\",\"rationale\":\"The control isn't extra difficulty. Without it, a high hit rate could just reflect how close the level was.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"On a random walk, equal highs broke within 20 candles 69.3% of the time and random levels at the same distance 67.4%: a gap inside the noise band. Without the control, 69.3% looks like an edge. With it, it's the base rate.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\",\"equal-highs-lows\"],\"hints\":[\"What would a level get if it meant nothing at all?\",\"Closer levels are reached more often, whatever they are.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A trader says 'there's an imbalance at 100.50'. Which two meanings could that have?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"More aggressive buying than selling at the best prices, or a fast one-sided price move\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A gap between two trading sessions, or a weekend gap\",\"rationale\":\"Those are gaps between sessions. Imbalance means lopsided order flow or a fast one-sided move.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A wide spread, or a low-volume candle\",\"rationale\":\"Neither is the usual meaning. Imbalance is about which side dominates, in the orders or in the move.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A support level, or a resistance level\",\"rationale\":\"A level isn't an imbalance. The word describes order flow or how price moved.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Order-flow imbalance is more aggressive buying than selling at the best prices; it predicts the next few ticks (grade B), too fast to trade on for most people. Price imbalance is a fast one-sided move with little two-sided trading. Say which one you mean.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"price-imbalance\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Three LABX candles. Candle 1: high 100.40. Candle 2: open 100.35, close 101.20. Candle 3: low 100.70, close 101.40. How tall is the bullish three-candle gap?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.3,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"The gap runs from candle 1's high, 100.40, to candle 3's low, 100.70: 100.70 − 100.40 = 0.30. Candle 2's body doesn't set the edges; the neighbours' wicks do.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"fair-value-gap\"],\"hints\":[\"The gap sits between candle 1 and candle 3.\",\"Candle 3's low minus candle 1's high.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"h1\":[95,105,0.01],\"gap\":[0.05,0.6,0.01]},\"answer\":\"gap\",\"prompt\":\"Three LABX candles. Candle 1: high {h1:2}. Candle 2: open {h1 - 0.05:2}, close {h1 + gap + 0.5:2}. Candle 3: low {h1 + gap:2}, close {h1 + gap + 0.7:2}. How tall is the bullish three-candle gap?\",\"explanation\":\"The gap runs from candle 1's high, {h1:2}, to candle 3's low, {h1 + gap:2}: {h1 + gap:2} − {h1:2} = {answer:2}. Candle 2's body doesn't set the edges; the neighbours' wicks do.\",\"hint\":\"{h1 + gap:2} − {h1:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On a random walk, gaps were revisited within 20 bars 83.4% of the time, and random levels at the same distance 83.5%. What follows?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gaps fill reliably, so trade toward them\",\"rationale\":\"83.4% is what any level at that distance gets. A fill rate equal to the base rate is no edge.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Random walks are useless for testing\",\"rationale\":\"That's exactly their use: a market with no structure shows what a method gets from chance alone.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Real markets must be random walks\",\"rationale\":\"A null model shows what chance produces. It isn't evidence that real markets are random.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"'Gaps fill' is mostly the base rate: nearby prices get revisited, gap or no gap\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"When gaps and random levels at the same distance get the same rate, the gap adds nothing. The high fill rate comes from distance: nearby prices are revisited often. A real edge would need a rate beyond the base rate, on real data, after costs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\",\"fair-value-gap\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the two numbers, not the first one alone.\",\"What does a level with no meaning get?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which pairing of a zone-grading criterion and its measurement is right?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Departure: the number of candles in the base\",\"rationale\":\"That's time at base. Departure is the size of the move away from the zone, in ATR.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Freshness: how many times price has come back into the zone since it formed\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Location: the size of the move away, in ATR\",\"rationale\":\"That's departure. Location is where the zone sits relative to a higher-timeframe level.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Time at base: the distance to a daily level\",\"rationale\":\"That's location. Time at base counts the candles in the base.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Departure is the move away in ATR; time at base counts the base candles; freshness counts returns into the zone; location measures the distance to a higher-timeframe level. Each is a number, so a grade can be checked and locked.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"zone-grading\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Firms published support and resistance levels with 'strength' ratings. What did Osler (2000) find about the ratings?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Strong levels held far more often than weak ones\",\"rationale\":\"That's what the ratings promised, not what the data showed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The levels as a group had a small effect, but the strength ratings added nothing\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No published level beat random levels\",\"rationale\":\"The levels as a group did a little better: 60.8% against 56.2%. It was the ratings that added nothing.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Weak levels held more often than strong ones\",\"rationale\":\"There was no reversal either. The ratings simply carried no information.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Published levels stopped intraday trends 60.8% of the time against 56.2% for random levels: small but real. The firms' strength ratings didn't separate levels that held from levels that didn't. A grade is a claim that needs its own test.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"zone-grading\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"hints\":[\"Two findings: one about the levels, one about the ratings.\",\"Did a 'strong' label change how often a level held?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do zones drawn after a session look better than zones locked before it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Drawing after uses the outcome: failures get dropped or moved onto the real turns\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Traders draw better zones when they're calm\",\"rationale\":\"Calm isn't the difference. The redrawn zones were placed knowing where price turned.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Markets respect zones more in hindsight\",\"rationale\":\"Markets don't change after the fact; the drawings do.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Locked zones are drawn too wide\",\"rationale\":\"Width matters, but the gap here comes from knowing the result. Wider zones would 'react' more, not less.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A zone drawn after the session can be placed where price did turn, and the failures quietly vanish. In the lesson's example, 40% became 86% with the same rules. Only a locked drawing tests the method.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"zone-grading\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the trader know after the session that they didn't know before?\",\"Which zones tend to disappear from the after-drawing?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"After a failed breakout the x-ray shows trapped buyers: 600 units at 101.05, 500 at 101.10 and 300 at 101.15. What is their average entry price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":101.09,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Total cost: 600 × 101.05 + 500 × 101.10 + 300 × 101.15 = 141,525.00 for 1,400 units. 141,525.00 ÷ 1,400 = 101.089, about 101.09: their break-even.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"trapped-inventory\"],\"hints\":[\"Weight each price by its size.\",\"Add the three products, then divide by the total units.\",\"141,525.00 ÷ 1,400.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[95,105,0.01],\"a\":[100,900,100],\"b\":[100,900,100],\"c\":[100,900,100]},\"answer\":\"round((a * p + b * (p + 0.05) + c * (p + 0.1)) / (a + b + c), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"After a failed breakout the x-ray shows trapped buyers: {a} units at {p:2}, {b} at {p + 0.05:2} and {c} at {p + 0.1:2}. What is their average entry price?\",\"explanation\":\"Total cost: {a} × {p:2} + {b} × {p + 0.05:2} + {c} × {p + 0.1:2} = {a * p + b * (p + 0.05) + c * (p + 0.1):2} for {a + b + c} units. Divided by {a + b + c}, that's about {answer:2}: their break-even.\",\"hint\":\"{a * p + b * (p + 0.05) + c * (p + 0.1):2} ÷ {a + b + c}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Trapped longs from a failed breakout: some have stops under 100.60; the rest have no stop and average 101.09. Price is at 100.80.\",\"prompt\":\"What does each group's exit tend to do to price?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Both exits are buys that lift price\",\"rationale\":\"Longs exit by selling. Neither group buys when it gets out.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The stops stall a fall; the break-even exits lift a rally\",\"rationale\":\"Reversed. Triggered sell stops add to a fall; sellers at break-even lean against a rally.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Below 100.60 the stops become market sells that speed a fall; near 101.09 break-even sales can stall a rally\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither matters once the breakout has failed\",\"rationale\":\"The positions are still there. Their exits are orders, and orders move price.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Longs exit by selling. At 100.60 the stops turn into market sells that add to a fall, the cascade mechanism. Near 101.09 the unprotected longs sell to get out flat, which can stall a rally. Grade C: plausible, not directly tested.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"trapped-inventory\",\"stop-cascade\"],\"hints\":[\"How does a long get out: by buying or by selling?\",\"A stop sells at market; a break-even exit sells into a rally.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.1.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"How do break-even exits explain role reversal, and how strong is the evidence?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Traders wrong at the old level exit flat on the retest, adding orders that defend it; plausible, mixed evidence: grade C\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Institutions defend the level on purpose; proven: grade A\",\"rationale\":\"That's an intent story. The break-even mechanism needs no plan, and its evidence is mixed.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Broken levels always hold on the retest; grade B\",\"rationale\":\"Retests fail often. Nothing in the evidence makes them hold 'always'.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's random: role reversal has no possible mechanism\",\"rationale\":\"There is a plausible mechanism. What's missing is strong evidence that it beats random levels.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Shorts who sold at a broken resistance buy back near their entry on the retest, so buying meets the dip there. That's a plausible mechanism with mixed evidence, grade C. Only retests that hold more often than random levels at the same distance would support it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"role-reversal\",\"trapped-inventory\"],\"hints\":[\"Who is under water after the level breaks, and what do they want?\",\"Mechanism and evidence are two different questions.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You ask five dealers for a price on a corporate bond, then trade with the one that quotes best. What market structure is this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Order-driven: a central limit order book\",\"rationale\":\"No shared book holds resting orders here. You request quotes and trade against a dealer's price.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Quote-driven: a dealer market\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Brokered: a broker searches for the other side\",\"rationale\":\"No broker is searching for a counterparty: the dealers themselves take the other side.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Hybrid: a book plus market makers\",\"rationale\":\"There's no central book at all, only dealers' quotes on request.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A request for quote goes to dealers, who quote prices and take the other side themselves. That's a quote-driven, dealer market, the usual structure for corporate bonds.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-venue-types\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your spot FX chart shows a volume bar of 2,400 for the last hour. What does that number measure?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The euros traded worldwide in that hour\",\"rationale\":\"Spot FX has no central exchange, so no one publishes a consolidated volume figure.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Contracts traded on the FX exchange\",\"rationale\":\"Spot FX trades over the counter; there is no single FX exchange to count.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How many times your broker's price changed\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Lots traded by all of your broker's clients\",\"rationale\":\"A broker could count that, but retail FX chart volume is usually a count of price changes.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Spot FX is over the counter, with no central book and no consolidated volume. Retail FX charts show tick volume: the number of price changes at one broker, a rough stand-in for activity.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"venue-fragmentation\"],\"hints\":[\"Is there one central place where all spot FX trades?\",\"Without a central book, what can a single broker count?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You hold three accounts: shares on a stock exchange, spot FX with a dealer broker, and a CFD on an index with a CFD provider. You buy in each.\",\"prompt\":\"Who is your counterparty in each account?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Another trader; the dealer; the CFD provider\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The exchange in all three\",\"rationale\":\"An exchange runs the book; it isn't the seller. And FX and CFD trades never reach an exchange at all.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Another retail trader in all three\",\"rationale\":\"Only on the exchange do you meet another participant's order. The dealer and the CFD provider take the other side themselves.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Another trader; another retail client; the index's companies\",\"rationale\":\"A dealer may net clients against each other, but it is the one trading with you. A CFD is a contract with its provider.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"On an exchange your order matches another participant's, and a clearing house then stands between you. A dealer quotes and takes the other side. A CFD is a contract with its provider, which may hedge but remains your counterparty.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-venue-types\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask who quoted the price you traded at.\",\"A CFD is a contract. Who signed it with you?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.2-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which market data shows every individual order in the book and its place in the queue?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Level 1\",\"rationale\":\"L1 shows only the best bid, the best ask and their sizes.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Level 2\",\"rationale\":\"L2 adds the total size at each price, but not the separate orders inside it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Level 3\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The last-trade price\",\"rationale\":\"The last trade tells you what just happened, nothing about who is waiting.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"L1 is the best bid and ask with their sizes; L2 adds the total size waiting at each price; L3 shows each order separately, so you can see where an order sits in the queue.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"limit-order-book\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.2-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A market buy of 400 units meets asks of 150 at 80.10, 100 at 80.12 and 300 at 80.15. What is its average fill price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":80.12375,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"It takes 150 at 80.10, 100 at 80.12 and the last 150 at 80.15: 12,015.00 + 8,012.00 + 12,022.50 = 32,049.50. Divided by 400: 80.124, worse than the best ask because the order walked the book.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"market-depth\",\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"Fill the cheapest asks first until you reach 400 units.\",\"150 + 100 fill at the first two prices; the last 150 come from 80.15.\",\"Total cost 32,049.50 for 400 units.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[40,120,0.5],\"q1\":[100,300,50],\"q2\":[100,300,50],\"q3\":[50,250,50]},\"answer\":\"round(p + (q2 * 0.02 + q3 * 0.05) / (q1 + q2 + q3), 3)\",\"prompt\":\"A market buy of {q1 + q2 + q3} units meets asks of {q1} at {p:2}, {q2} at {p + 0.02:2} and {q3 + 150} at {p + 0.05:2}. What is its average fill price?\",\"explanation\":\"It takes {q1} at {p:2}, {q2} at {p + 0.02:2} and the last {q3} at {p + 0.05:2}: {q1 * p + q2 * (p + 0.02) + q3 * (p + 0.05):2} in all. Divided by {q1 + q2 + q3}: {answer:3}.\",\"hint\":\"Total cost {q1 * p + q2 * (p + 0.02) + q3 * (p + 0.05):2} for {q1 + q2 + q3} units.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.2-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A trader places large bids below the market, intending to cancel them before they can fill, so others think demand is strong. What is this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Legal layering: the orders are real\",\"rationale\":\"Orders at several prices are legal only when they're meant to trade. These are placed to be cancelled.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Spoofing, which is illegal\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An iceberg order\",\"rationale\":\"An iceberg hides size that is meant to trade. These orders show size that isn't.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Normal market making\",\"rationale\":\"Market makers quote to trade and earn the spread, not to cancel before anyone can hit them.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Displayed size is an intention, not a commitment, and spoofing exploits that: orders placed with the intent to cancel before execution, to mislead others about supply or demand. It is illegal, and was sanctioned in CFTC v. Sarao.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"limit-order-book\"],\"hints\":[\"The question is about intent: were the orders meant to trade?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You quote 0.10 wide and earn 0.05 a unit on every fill. You get 80 fills of 50 units. 10 of them come from an informed trader just before a 0.25 move against you. What is your P&L before commissions, with no inventory left?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Spread captured: 80 × 50 × 0.05 = 200.00. Adverse selection: 10 × 50 × 0.25 = 125.00. Net: 200.00 − 125.00 = 75.00.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"market-making\",\"adverse-selection\"],\"hints\":[\"P&L = spread captured − adverse selection ± inventory.\",\"Spread captured: 80 × 50 × 0.05.\",\"Adverse selection: 10 × 50 × 0.25.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[40,100,10],\"u\":[50,200,50],\"k\":[4,12,1],\"m\":[0.1,0.4,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"k < n\"],\"answer\":\"round(n * u * 0.05 - k * u * m, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You quote 0.10 wide and earn 0.05 a unit on every fill. You get {n} fills of {u} units. {k} of them come from an informed trader just before a {m:2} move against you. What is your P&L before commissions, with no inventory left?\",\"explanation\":\"Spread captured: {n} × {u} × 0.05 = {n * u * 0.05:2}. Adverse selection: {k} × {u} × {m:2} = {k * u * m:2}. Net: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Spread captured {n * u * 0.05:2}, adverse selection {k * u * m:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Shortly before a major release, a market maker that is long widens its quotes and shifts both of them down. Why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"To hunt its clients' stops\",\"rationale\":\"Widening and skewing are responses to informed flow and inventory. Nothing here needs stop hunting to explain it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Informed orders are likely, and it wants to shed its long inventory\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exchanges require wider quotes before news\",\"rationale\":\"No rule requires it. The market maker widens because the risk of trading with informed traders rises.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"To raise the commission it earns\",\"rationale\":\"Quotes aren't commissions. Wider quotes protect against adverse selection; the shift down helps sell inventory.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Before news, the chance that the next fill comes from someone who knows more rises, so the market maker widens. Shifting both quotes down makes its ask attractive and its bid less so, which sheds a long inventory.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"market-making\"],\"hints\":[\"Two separate moves: wider, and shifted down. Each answers a different risk.\",\"Who trades just before news, and what does a lower ask attract?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"On 50 days you rest a buy limit 0.10 below the price. Your journal: on the days it filled, price was on average 0.15 lower one minute later. On the days it missed, price rose.\",\"prompt\":\"What best explains this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Your broker sees your orders and trades against them\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing here needs that: any resting order shows this pattern, because it fills when price moves through its level.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bad luck that will even out\",\"rationale\":\"It's systematic: a limit fills when price comes to it and keeps going, and misses when price turns first.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A resting limit is a free option: it fills most surely when price keeps moving through it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Limit orders always fill at worse prices than market orders\",\"rationale\":\"A limit fills at its price or better. The issue is which moves fill it, not the fill price itself.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A resting limit gives anyone the option to trade with you when it suits them. Fills cluster on moves that continue through your price, and the moves that turn just before it are the ones you miss. That's adverse selection.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"adverse-selection\"],\"hints\":[\"Which days does a buy limit fill: when price keeps falling, or when it turns early?\",\"Think of the order as an option you gave the market.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.4-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You bid 150 units at 64.30. 520 units are queued ahead of you at that price. 600 units trade at 64.30, then price moves away. How many of your units fill?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":80,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"The first 520 units traded fill the orders ahead of you. The other 600 − 520 = 80 reach your order; 70 of your units keep waiting.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"queue-priority\",\"order-queue\"],\"hints\":[\"Under FIFO, the size ahead of you trades first.\",\"600 − 520 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[20,150,0.05],\"a\":[100,800,20],\"y\":[50,300,10],\"t\":[100,1200,20]},\"constraints\":[\"t > a\"],\"answer\":\"min(y, t - a)\",\"prompt\":\"You bid {y} units at {p:2}. {a} units are queued ahead of you at that price. {t} units trade at {p:2}, then price moves away. How many of your units fill?\",\"explanation\":\"The first {a} units traded fill the orders ahead of you. Of the other {t - a}, up to your {y} reach your order: {answer} fill.\",\"hint\":\"{t} − {a} = {t - a}. Can more than {y} fill?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.4-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your buy limit rests near the front of a long queue at 50.00. Which change keeps your place?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Raising the size from 200 to 300\",\"rationale\":\"A larger order counts as new size: it goes to the back of the queue.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Moving the price to 50.01\",\"rationale\":\"A new price is a new order at that price, at the back of its queue.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Cutting the size from 200 to 150\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Moving the order to another account\",\"rationale\":\"A different account means a new order, which joins the back.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"On most venues, raising the size, changing the price or changing the account resets time priority. Cutting the size usually keeps your place, though each venue sets its own rules.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"queue-priority\"],\"hints\":[\"Which change would let you jump ahead of orders that arrived after you?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.4-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A level uses pro-rata matching. 1,250 units are bid there, 200 of them yours. 500 units trade at that price. How many do you get, rounded down to whole units?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":80,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Pro-rata shares the volume by size: 500 × 200 ÷ 1,250 = 80. Under FIFO you could get anything from 0 to 200, depending on your place in the queue.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"queue-priority\"],\"hints\":[\"Pro-rata ignores time: your share follows your size.\",\"Your share of the level: 200 ÷ 1,250.\",\"500 × 200 ÷ 1,250 = ?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.5-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You want a better price than the best bid or ask and accept that your order may not fill. Which order type fits?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Iceberg\",\"rationale\":\"An iceberg hides size; it doesn't improve your price.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Midpoint peg\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Post-only\",\"rationale\":\"Post-only makes sure you add liquidity. It doesn't price you between the bid and the ask.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Market order\",\"rationale\":\"A market order fills for sure but pays the ask: the opposite of the trade-off you want.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A midpoint peg floats halfway between the best bid and ask: a better price than either, in exchange for a lower chance of a fill, since someone must meet you there.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"hidden-liquidity\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.5-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A venue pays makers 0.002 a unit and charges takers 0.003. You buy 2,000 units with a market order and later sell them with a resting limit that fills. What are your net exchange fees?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-2,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Entry as taker: 2,000 × 0.003 = −6.00. Exit as maker: 2,000 × 0.002 = +4.00. Net: −2.00.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"maker-taker\"],\"hints\":[\"Taking liquidity pays a fee; a resting order that fills earns a rebate.\",\"Entry: −2,000 × 0.003. Exit: +2,000 × 0.002.\",\"−6.00 + 4.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"u\":[500,5000,500],\"r\":[0.001,0.003,0.001],\"f\":[0.002,0.004,0.001]},\"answer\":\"round(u * (r - f), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A venue pays makers {r:3} a unit and charges takers {f:3}. You buy {u} units with a market order and later sell them with a resting limit that fills. What are your net exchange fees?\",\"explanation\":\"Entry as taker: {u} × {f:3} = −{u * f:2}. Exit as maker: {u} × {r:3} = +{u * r:2}. Net: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"−{u * f:2} + {u * r:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.5-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your platform offers a synthetic iceberg on a venue with no native icebergs. What does the exchange see?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A series of separate small orders, each sent after the previous one fills\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"One large order with a hidden reserve\",\"rationale\":\"That's a native iceberg. A synthetic one is run by your platform; the exchange never learns the total.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing until the full size has filled\",\"rationale\":\"Each slice is a real displayed order; the exchange sees every one of them.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A hidden order that keeps its queue place on refills\",\"rationale\":\"No refill keeps its place: each new slice joins the back of the queue.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A synthetic iceberg lives on your platform, which sends a new order after each fill. The exchange sees independent small orders with short gaps between them, each joining the back of the queue. A native iceberg is held and refilled by the exchange itself.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"iceberg-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Who holds the reserve: the exchange or your platform?\",\"If your platform holds it, what must it send after each fill?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.6-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A client agreement says: 'We act as principal to all your trades and may requote in fast markets.' Which broker model is this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Agency/STP\",\"rationale\":\"Agency brokers pass orders on and charge commission. 'Principal' means the broker itself takes the other side.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"ECN\",\"rationale\":\"An ECN matches many participants in one book; it doesn't act as principal or requote.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"B-book (dealer)\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It can't be told from the agreement\",\"rationale\":\"'Principal' and 'requote' are exactly the clauses that tell you: the broker is the dealer.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Acting as principal means the broker is your counterparty, and requotes mean it sets its own prices: a dealer, or B-book, model. Agency and ECN brokers pass orders to others or match them in a shared book.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-models\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.6-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is the core conflict in a B-book, and how does EU regulation handle it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Client losses are broker revenue; rules cap leverage, close out at 50% margin and stop balances going negative\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The broker earns commissions; regulation bans B-books\",\"rationale\":\"B-books are legal. Their income comes from the other side of client trades, and regulation manages that conflict rather than banning it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"There's no conflict, because the broker always hedges\",\"rationale\":\"A B-book keeps at least some client risk by definition; that's where the conflict comes from.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"B-books hunt stops, so regulators make them hide stop levels\",\"rationale\":\"Stop hunting by B-books is an unproven claim (grade D). The documented conflict is that client losses are broker gains.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"In a B-book the broker takes the other side, so what clients lose it gains. EU rules since 2018 manage this with leverage caps, a margin close-out at 50%, negative balance protection and a mandatory loss-rate warning.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-models\",\"leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"Who gains when a B-book client loses?\",\"Regulation manages this conflict; it doesn't ban the model.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.6-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your broker's report for 200 market orders: 141 filled at the quoted price, 59 filled worse by 0.01 to 0.05, and none filled better. Last-look rejects: 22, all when price moved in your favour during the window.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the report show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Normal noise: slippage happens\",\"rationale\":\"Noise goes both ways. No improvements at all and one-sided rejects are a pattern, not noise.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Proof that the broker hunts your stops\",\"rationale\":\"It shows one-sided slippage and rejects, a cost worth challenging. It says nothing about stops or intent.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Asymmetric slippage and rejects: gains are kept, losses passed on\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your orders are too large for the market\",\"rationale\":\"Size can raise slippage, but it wouldn't stop every single fill from ever being better than quoted.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"In a fair process prices move both ways during any delay, so some fills improve. None better, and rejects only when price moved your way, mean the dealer keeps the upside and passes on the downside: a red flag worth raising.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"last-look\",\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"During a delay, which way can price move?\",\"Compare the fills that got worse with those that got better.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.7-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Three exchanges quote a stock. X: 50.12 / 50.18. Y: 50.14 / 50.20. Z: 50.11 / 50.17. How wide is the NBBO spread?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.03,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The NBBO takes the highest bid, 50.14 on Y, and the lowest ask, 50.17 on Z: 50.17 − 50.14 = 0.03, tighter than any single exchange's 0.06.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"nbbo\"],\"hints\":[\"NBBO = the highest bid and the lowest ask, from any exchange.\",\"Highest bid 50.14, lowest ask 50.17.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[20,150,0.05],\"b1\":[0,4,1],\"b2\":[0,4,1],\"b3\":[0,4,1],\"s1\":[5,12,1],\"s2\":[5,12,1],\"s3\":[5,12,1]},\"answer\":\"round((min(b1 + s1, b2 + s2, b3 + s3) - max(b1, b2, b3)) / 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Three exchanges quote a stock. X: {p + b1 / 100:2} / {p + (b1 + s1) / 100:2}. Y: {p + b2 / 100:2} / {p + (b2 + s2) / 100:2}. Z: {p + b3 / 100:2} / {p + (b3 + s3) / 100:2}. How wide is the NBBO spread?\",\"explanation\":\"The NBBO takes the highest bid, {p + max(b1, b2, b3) / 100:2}, and the lowest ask, {p + min(b1 + s1, b2 + s2, b3 + s3) / 100:2}: a spread of {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Highest bid {p + max(b1, b2, b3) / 100:2}, lowest ask {p + min(b1 + s1, b2 + s2, b3 + s3) / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.7-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which statement about payment for order flow (PFOF) and best execution in the US is correct?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"PFOF is banned, so best execution is automatic\",\"rationale\":\"PFOF is legal in the US; the EU is the one that banned it. Best execution is a separate, continuing duty.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Disclosing PFOF to clients satisfies best execution\",\"rationale\":\"FINRA has said disclosure isn't enough: the broker must still seek the best terms reasonably available.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"PFOF is allowed, but it can't compromise best execution, which needs regular, rigorous review\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Best execution means every order gets the NBBO or better\",\"rationale\":\"It's a duty of reasonable diligence, not a guaranteed price on every order.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"In the US, accepting PFOF isn't a violation in itself, but it must not interfere with best execution: reasonable diligence and a regular, rigorous review of execution quality, at least quarterly (FINRA 5310). Disclosure alone doesn't satisfy the duty.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"best-execution\",\"payment-for-order-flow\"],\"hints\":[\"Is best execution a guarantee or a duty?\",\"What did FINRA say about disclosure?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.7-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Two brokers' execution reports for the same 10,000 shares. Broker A: zero commission, price improvement 0.001 a share. Broker B: 15.00 commission, price improvement 0.004 a share. Both filled every order within a second.\",\"prompt\":\"Which gave the better execution, net of commission?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: it costs nothing\",\"rationale\":\"A's improvement is 10.00. B's is 40.00, and after 15.00 of commission B is still 15.00 ahead.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"B, by 15.00\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"B, by 25.00\",\"rationale\":\"25.00 is B's net benefit. Compare it with A's 10.00: the gap is 15.00.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They're equal\",\"rationale\":\"B's 40.00 minus 15.00 is 25.00, against A's 10.00.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A: 10,000 × 0.001 = 10.00 of improvement, no commission. B: 10,000 × 0.004 = 40.00, minus 15.00 = 25.00. B is better by 15.00. Fill rate and speed are equal, so net price decides.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\",\"concepts\":[\"best-execution\"],\"hints\":[\"Net benefit = price improvement − commission.\",\"A: 10,000 × 0.001. B: 10,000 × 0.004 − 15.00.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.8-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A call auction's cumulative orders. At 40.00: buy 1,500, sell 300. At 40.10: buy 1,200, sell 700. At 40.20: buy 800, sell 1,100. At 40.30: buy 500, sell 1,600. What is the uncross price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":40.2,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The volume that can trade is the smaller side at each price: 300, 700, 800 and 500. The most, 800, trades at 40.20, the uncross price, with 300 left unmatched on the sell side.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\",\"concepts\":[\"call-auction\"],\"hints\":[\"At each price, the volume that can trade is the smaller of buy and sell.\",\"300, 700, 800, 500: where is the largest?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[20,150,0.1],\"d4\":[2,6,1],\"x3\":[1,5,1],\"x2\":[1,5,1],\"x1\":[1,5,1],\"s1\":[1,5,1],\"y2\":[1,5,1],\"y3\":[1,5,1],\"y4\":[1,5,1]},\"constraints\":[\"min(d4 + x3 + x2, s1 + y2) > min(d4 + x3 + x2 + x1, s1)\",\"min(d4 + x3, s1 + y2 + y3) > min(d4, s1 + y2 + y3 + y4)\",\"min(d4 + x3 + x2, s1 + y2) != min(d4 + x3, s1 + y2 + y3)\"],\"answer\":\"round(p + 0.1 + 0.1 * (min(d4 + x3, s1 + y2 + y3) > min(d4 + x3 + x2, s1 + y2)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A call auction's cumulative orders. At {p:2}: buy {100 * (d4 + x3 + x2 + x1)}, sell {100 * s1}. At {p + 0.1:2}: buy {100 * (d4 + x3 + x2)}, sell {100 * (s1 + y2)}. At {p + 0.2:2}: buy {100 * (d4 + x3)}, sell {100 * (s1 + y2 + y3)}. At {p + 0.3:2}: buy {100 * d4}, sell {100 * (s1 + y2 + y3 + y4)}. What is the uncross price?\",\"explanation\":\"The volume that can trade is the smaller side at each price: {100 * min(d4 + x3 + x2 + x1, s1)}, {100 * min(d4 + x3 + x2, s1 + y2)}, {100 * min(d4 + x3, s1 + y2 + y3)} and {100 * min(d4, s1 + y2 + y3 + y4)}. The most trades at {answer:2}, the uncross price.\",\"hint\":\"Volumes that can trade: {100 * min(d4 + x3 + x2 + x1, s1)}, {100 * min(d4 + x3 + x2, s1 + y2)}, {100 * min(d4 + x3, s1 + y2 + y3)}, {100 * min(d4, s1 + y2 + y3 + y4)}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.8-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Ten minutes before the close, the exchange publishes: indicative price 75.20, paired volume 40,000, imbalance 8,000 to buy. You hold shares you'd be happy to sell near 75.20.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sell with a limit at 75.20 or lower in the closing auction, offsetting the imbalance\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy at market to ride the imbalance\",\"rationale\":\"Buying adds to the imbalance: more buyers chasing the same sellers, which can push the closing price up against you.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sell with a limit at 76.00\",\"rationale\":\"A sell limit above the indicative price doesn't take part at 75.20. It won't fill unless the price jumps.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the closing price is already fixed\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing is fixed until the cross. Orders entered now change the indicative price and the imbalance.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A buy imbalance means 8,000 more shares are wanted than offered at 75.20. A sell limit at the indicative price or better takes part and reduces the imbalance, the way liquidity providers use auctions. Rules on late orders differ by exchange.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\",\"concepts\":[\"auction-imbalance\"],\"hints\":[\"Which side is short of orders at 75.20?\",\"A sell limit above the indicative price: does it take part?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.2.8-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does so much trading happen in the closing auction, and how does a halted stock restart?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Index funds trade at the official close; a halted stock reopens through an auction\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Closing prices are the day's best; a halted stock resumes at its last trade\",\"rationale\":\"The close isn't the best price, it's the benchmark price. After a halt, orders collect and an auction sets a new price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Market makers must trade at the close; halted stocks reopen with the old book\",\"rationale\":\"Index funds, not obligations, drive the close. A halt ends with an auction, not by resuming the old book.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The close has the lowest spreads; halts end at a random price\",\"rationale\":\"The reopening price is the auction's uncross price, set from the collected orders, not at random.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Index funds and many valuations use the official closing price, so their orders gather in the closing auction. A halted market collects orders and reopens through an auction, and stops can trigger at that reopening price, far from their levels.\",\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\",\"concepts\":[\"call-auction\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"What price do index funds track?\",\"After a halt, how is one fair price found from many waiting orders?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Bid 100.20, ask 100.30. A print of 800 units appears at 100.20. Who was the aggressor?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A buyer, because 800 is a large print\",\"rationale\":\"Size doesn't set the side. A print at the bid means a seller accepted the bid price.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A seller, who hit the bid\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Neither: large prints are always negotiated blocks\",\"rationale\":\"Some are, but a print at the bid on the tape is classified as seller-initiated by the quote rule.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"You can't tell without the next print\",\"rationale\":\"The next print doesn't matter. The quotes at the time of the trade settle it: 100.20 is the bid.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The quote rule compares the print with the quotes at that moment. At the bid, the seller crossed the spread to trade with a resting buyer, so the print is seller-initiated.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"trade-classification\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Every trade has a buyer and a seller. So what does knowing the aggressor add?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Whether more units were bought than sold\",\"rationale\":\"Units bought always equal units sold. The aggressor says who initiated, not who traded more.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Who will win the next move\",\"rationale\":\"Urgency isn't direction. The passive side can be better informed, and the tape's forecasting value is grade D.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Which side was in a hurry: who crossed the spread to trade now\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the aggressor and the passive side are the same\",\"rationale\":\"They differ: the aggressor crossed the spread and paid it; the passive side was resting in the book.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Volume can't be lopsided: each unit has a buyer and a seller. The aggressor flag tells you which side chose to pay the spread for immediacy. That's urgency, and it doesn't make the aggressor right.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"tape-reading\",\"trade-classification\"],\"hints\":[\"Can more units be bought than sold?\",\"Who pays the spread, and why would they?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your retail FX chart shows a volume bar of 2,400 on EUR/USD. What is it most likely counting?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Euros traded across the whole market\",\"rationale\":\"Spot FX has no central exchange or consolidated tape, so no single feed sees total market volume.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Contracts traded on a futures exchange\",\"rationale\":\"That would be futures volume. A spot FX chart from a broker usually counts ticks.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Aggressive buy volume minus aggressive sell volume\",\"rationale\":\"That's delta. Retail FX volume is usually tick volume, with no aggressor information at all.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Price changes in that period on your broker's feed\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Spot FX trades over the counter across many dealers and venues, with no central tape. Retail platforms usually show tick volume: how many times the price changed at that broker. It tracks activity roughly, not units or aggressors.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"volume\",\"trade-classification\"],\"hints\":[\"Is there one central exchange for spot FX?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"On a DOM ladder, where do you find your own working buy limit order?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In the bid size at its price, marked as yours\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In the last-trades column\",\"rationale\":\"Last trades are executions that already happened. A working order hasn't traded yet.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Among the offers above the best ask\",\"rationale\":\"A buy limit rests on the bid side, at or below the best bid, not among the offers.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nowhere: the ladder shows only other traders' orders\",\"rationale\":\"A trading ladder shows your working orders at their prices, so you can see where you stand.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A DOM has three layers of information: resting size at each price, recent executions, and your own working orders, shown at their price on the side where they rest. A buy limit waits on the bid side.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\",\"concepts\":[\"depth-of-market\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"The offer at 100.40 shows 300. A buyer takes all 300. Half a second later, 300 show at 100.40 again. This repeats four times.\",\"prompt\":\"Which size behaviour is this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Pulling\",\"rationale\":\"Pulling is size leaving without trading. Here the size traded, then came back.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Stacking\",\"rationale\":\"Stacking adds new size before trading. Here the same size returns after each fill.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Reloading\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Spoofing\",\"rationale\":\"Spoofing means orders meant to be cancelled. These orders filled, again and again.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The same size reappearing at the same price after each fill is reloading, the usual trace of an iceberg order. It shows a persistent seller at 100.40, not how much is left or what price does next.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\",\"concepts\":[\"depth-of-market\"],\"hints\":[\"Did the 300 trade, or vanish untouched?\",\"Same size, same price, back after each fill.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why isn't a large displayed bid proof of strong demand?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Large bids are always placed by spoofers\",\"rationale\":\"Most large orders are genuine. The point is that you can't tell from the ladder, and it may leave.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It can be cancelled at any moment, at no cost, before it trades\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Displayed size is delayed by several minutes\",\"rationale\":\"On a live ladder, displayed size is current. The issue is that it isn't committed: it can be cancelled.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Bids never trade; only offers do\",\"rationale\":\"Both sides trade whenever an aggressor crosses the spread. The bid may well fill, or vanish first.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Displayed size shows what someone is willing to do right now, and it can change in an instant. Executed volume is a record. That's why reading intent from displayed size is grade D, and a trade taken because of a wall needs its own invalidation.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\",\"concepts\":[\"depth-of-market\"],\"hints\":[\"What does it cost to cancel a resting order?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A footprint bar shows 640 units traded at the ask and 410 at the bid. What is its bar delta?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":230,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Delta = ask volume − bid volume = 640 − 410 = +230. More volume crossed the spread to buy; it says nothing about where price goes next.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"footprint-chart\"],\"hints\":[\"Delta compares aggressive buying with aggressive selling.\",\"Ask volume minus bid volume: 640 − 410.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"ask\":[200,1500,10],\"bid\":[200,1500,10]},\"constraints\":[\"ask != bid\"],\"answer\":\"ask - bid\",\"prompt\":\"A footprint bar shows {ask} units traded at the ask and {bid} at the bid. What is its bar delta?\",\"explanation\":\"Delta = ask volume − bid volume = {ask} − {bid} = {answer}. It measures aggressive buying against aggressive selling inside the bar, not what comes next.\",\"hint\":\"Ask volume minus bid volume: {ask} − {bid}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Footprint rows, bid × ask: 100.12: 30 × 45; 100.11: 15 × 90; 100.10: 40 × 25. Where is there a buy diagonal imbalance at 3:1 or more?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"100.11: 90 at the ask against 15 at the bid on the same row\",\"rationale\":\"That compares one row with itself. The diagonal compares 90 with the bid one row lower, 40: only 2.25:1.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"100.10: 40 at the bid against 25 at the ask\",\"rationale\":\"That's the bid side, a sell comparison, and a sell imbalance compares bid at 100.10 with ask one tick up, 90.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"None of them\",\"rationale\":\"45 ÷ 15 = 3.0 at 100.12 meets a 3:1 threshold exactly.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"100.12: 45 at the ask against 15 at the bid one tick lower\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A buy diagonal imbalance compares the ask at one price with the bid one tick lower, the two sides that were quoted together. 45 ÷ 15 = 3.0 qualifies; 90 ÷ 40 = 2.25 at 100.11 doesn't, despite the biggest ask.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"footprint-imbalance\"],\"hints\":[\"Diagonal: the ask at a price against the bid one row below it.\",\"Check 45 ÷ 15 and 90 ÷ 40.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A course says stacked buy imbalances 'become support'. Which evidence grade fits that claim, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Mechanic: the imbalance is computed from real trades\",\"rationale\":\"The computation is mechanic. The claim that it becomes support is a forecast, and that part is untested.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"D: a practitioner heuristic; the imbalance records what traded, not what must happen next\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A: it's how professional order-flow traders work\",\"rationale\":\"Popularity isn't evidence. A claim earns a grade from tests against random zones, not from how many people use it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"F: footprints are fake data\",\"rationale\":\"Footprints are real trade data when the feed flags the aggressor. The weak part is the forecast, not the data.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Separate the data from the reading. The footprint itself is mechanic. 'Stacked imbalances become support' predicts future behaviour and hasn't been shown to beat random zones of the same width: grade D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"footprint-imbalance\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"hints\":[\"Split the claim into the data and the forecast.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"CVD stands at 1,250. The next three bars have deltas +180, −320 and +90. What is CVD now?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1200,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Add each bar's delta to the running total: 1,250 + 180 − 320 + 90 = 1,200. The level depends on the anchor; the changes are what you read.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"cumulative-volume-delta\"],\"hints\":[\"CVD is a running total of bar deltas.\",\"1,250 + 180 − 320 + 90.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s\":[500,3000,50],\"a\":[50,400,10],\"b\":[50,400,10],\"c\":[50,400,10]},\"answer\":\"s + a - b + c\",\"prompt\":\"CVD stands at {s}. The next three bars have deltas +{a}, −{b} and +{c}. What is CVD now?\",\"explanation\":\"Add each bar's delta to the running total: {s} + {a} − {b} + {c} = {answer}. The level depends on the anchor; the changes are what you read.\",\"hint\":\"Add the deltas in order: {s} + {a} − {b} + {c}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Price breaks above its last high; CVD stays below its own last high. What's the sound way to use this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sell at once: the divergence shows the reversal\",\"rationale\":\"A divergence is grade D. Many moves with weaker delta keep going; acting before price confirms means fighting a trend on a hint.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Ignore it: delta never matters\",\"rationale\":\"It describes the move: less aggressive buying behind the new high. That's useful context, just not a signal by itself.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Treat the move as fragile and wait for price to confirm a turn, such as a break of the last swing low\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Buy more: higher prices on less buying mean the sellers are gone\",\"rationale\":\"That reads the opposite story into the same data. Neither story is confirmed until price acts.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A delta divergence flags a move with less aggressive buying behind it. It isn't a timing signal: confirmation comes from price, such as a break of the last swing low. Test any rule with and without that confirmation.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"delta-divergence\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the divergence say about the move, and what doesn't it say?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"How does OBV differ from CVD?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"OBV gives each bar's whole volume the sign of its close; CVD signs each trade by its aggressor\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They're the same line with different names\",\"rationale\":\"Both are running totals, but OBV uses closes and whole-bar volume, CVD uses each trade's aggressor.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"CVD uses closes; OBV uses each trade's aggressor\",\"rationale\":\"Reversed. OBV only needs candles; CVD needs trade-level data with the aggressor.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"OBV is cumulative; CVD resets every bar\",\"rationale\":\"Both are cumulative. CVD can restart at a session anchor, but resetting every bar would make it bar delta.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"OBV needs only candles: a higher close adds the bar's whole volume, a lower close subtracts it. CVD needs every trade: buys at the ask add, sells at the bid subtract. A bar can close higher with negative delta.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"cumulative-volume-delta\",\"cumulative-volume-indicators\"],\"hints\":[\"Which one can you compute from candles alone?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A DOM shows a 300-unit bid at 99.50. Over four minutes, sellers trade 1,500 units at 99.50. The bid still shows about 300, and no trade prints below 99.50.\",\"prompt\":\"What best describes this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Exhaustion: sellers have run out\",\"rationale\":\"Sellers traded 1,500 units, so aggression hasn't dried up. The selling meets a buyer who doesn't move.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Spoofing: the bid is fake\",\"rationale\":\"The bid traded 1,500 units. Orders that fill aren't spoofing; that's real size.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Absorption: a passive buyer is taking the selling\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A bearish breakout\",\"rationale\":\"No trade has printed below 99.50. A breakout needs price through the level, and that hasn't happened.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Heavy aggressive selling at one price without progress, with the bid refilling, is absorption. It describes a passive buyer with size, for now. The read is invalidated if price trades through the level.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"absorption\"],\"hints\":[\"Who is aggressive, and did price make progress?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which bar best fits order-flow exhaustion at the top of a rally?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A new high on the biggest volume and delta of the move\",\"rationale\":\"Rising volume and delta at a new high show aggression increasing: momentum, the opposite of exhaustion.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A new high on the lowest volume of the move and a small positive delta\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A large bar down from the high on heavy selling\",\"rationale\":\"That's a reversal bar after the fact, driven by aggressive sellers. Exhaustion is about buyers fading at the high.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A bar inside the previous bar's range on average volume\",\"rationale\":\"An inside bar doesn't reach a new extreme, and average volume shows nothing drying up.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Exhaustion means aggressive volume fading at the extreme: a new high on thin volume and little delta, like bar 6 in the lesson, 340 units and delta +30. It's a grade D read, invalidated if a new high comes with rising delta.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"order-flow-exhaustion\"],\"hints\":[\"Exhaustion is about aggression fading. What does that look like at a new high?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You read absorption by a passive seller at 101.20. Which event invalidates the read?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price dips to 101.05\",\"rationale\":\"A dip away from 101.20 fits the seller defending it. The read fails only if buyers trade through the level.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Delta turns negative\",\"rationale\":\"Sellers turning aggressive would fit the seller winning. The read is wrong when price gets through, not when it falls.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Volume at 101.20 doubles\",\"rationale\":\"More buying absorbed at 101.20 strengthens the read, as long as the level holds.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Trades print above 101.22\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A passive seller at 101.20 is a hypothesis about a level. It dies when buyers trade through it, here with a two-tick margin, above 101.22. Write that price down before acting on the read.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"absorption\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"hints\":[\"What would show that the seller at 101.20 has gone?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.6-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An offer at 101.50 shows 80 units. 1,000 units trade there, and 80 still show. What is the minimum hidden size?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":920,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"At most 80 of the 1,000 came from the slice showing at the start, so at least 1,000 − 80 = 920 were hidden. The 80 showing now may be someone else's new order, so it isn't counted.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"absorption\"],\"hints\":[\"Only the slice showing at the start was visible.\",\"Traded minus displayed: 1,000 − 80.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"d\":[20,200,10],\"t\":[300,2000,50]},\"constraints\":[\"t > 2 * d\"],\"answer\":\"t - d\",\"prompt\":\"An offer at 101.50 shows {d} units. {t} units trade there, and {d} still show. What is the minimum hidden size?\",\"explanation\":\"At most {d} of the {t} came from the slice showing at the start, so at least {t} − {d} = {answer} were hidden. The {d} showing now may be someone else's new order.\",\"hint\":\"Traded minus displayed: {t} − {d}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.6-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which tape pattern is the signature of an iceberg order?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A very large displayed order that never trades\",\"rationale\":\"That's displayed size, possibly a wall. An iceberg hides size; it reveals itself only by trading.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Traded volume at one price far above the displayed size, with the same slice refilling\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A single large print at the midpoint\",\"rationale\":\"That could be a block or a hidden midpoint order. An iceberg shows a pattern of repeated fills at one price.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Price moving fast through several levels\",\"rationale\":\"That's a market order walking thin liquidity, the opposite of size resting at one price.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"An iceberg displays a small slice and refills it from a hidden reserve. On the tape, that shows as much more volume trading at one price than was ever displayed, with the same size reappearing after each fill.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"absorption\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.6-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does a synthetic iceberg look like many separate orders to the exchange?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The exchange splits large orders automatically\",\"rationale\":\"That describes a native iceberg, which the exchange manages as one order. A synthetic one is managed outside the exchange.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Synthetic icebergs are illegal, so they're disguised\",\"rationale\":\"Icebergs, native or synthetic, are legal order types. The difference is only where the hidden reserve is kept.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The platform sends a new order each time a slice fills; the exchange never sees the parent order\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because each slice gets better queue priority\",\"rationale\":\"Each new slice usually joins the back of the queue. Being separate orders doesn't improve priority.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A native iceberg is one exchange order with a display quantity and a reserve. A synthetic iceberg is kept by a platform or broker, which submits a fresh order after each fill. The exchange sees unrelated orders, each joining the back of the queue.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"order-queue\"],\"hints\":[\"Where is the hidden reserve kept in each case?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.7-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What makes an order spoofing under US futures law?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Cancelling any order within a second\",\"rationale\":\"Fast cancels are normal, especially for market makers. The law targets intent at placement, not speed.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Placing an order larger than the displayed market\",\"rationale\":\"Large orders are legal. Size isn't the issue; placing an order you never mean to fill is.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Bidding or offering with the intent to cancel before execution\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Placing orders at several prices at once\",\"rationale\":\"Genuine orders at several prices are legitimate layering. They become illegal only if they're meant to be cancelled.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The Commodity Exchange Act, as amended by Dodd-Frank, bans 'bidding or offering with the intent to cancel the bid or offer before execution'. EU market-abuse rules also treat misleading orders as manipulation. Intent separates it from normal trading.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"spoofing\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.7-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which combination is the surveillance signature of spoofing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A high cancel-to-fill ratio on its own\",\"rationale\":\"Market makers cancel heavily too. The ratio alone doesn't separate them from a spoofer.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Many small fills at the best bid and ask\",\"rationale\":\"That's ordinary trading or market making: orders that fill aren't the problem.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A single large order that is pulled\",\"rationale\":\"One pulled order fits a changed mind. Surveillance needs a pattern, and intent.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Large orders away from the touch, frequent modifications, a very high cancel-to-fill ratio and trades on the opposite side\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"No single statistic proves spoofing. The signature is the combination: big orders that stay out of reach, constant re-pricing, almost no fills, and trading done on the other side of the book.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"spoofing\"],\"hints\":[\"Would a market maker match that description too?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.7-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"On the DOM you watch a 1,000-unit offer at 100.30. When price reaches 100.27, it disappears without trading.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you conclude?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing about intent: one pulled order fits a changed mind, a risk limit or a spoof\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's spoofing and should be reported as such\",\"rationale\":\"From outside you see one cancel and no intent. Surveillance needs the whole pattern and records only investigators see.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It was a hidden iceberg\",\"rationale\":\"An iceberg reveals itself by trading beyond its displayed size. This order never traded.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Sellers are weak, so price will rise\",\"rationale\":\"Reading direction from one cancel is the grade D intent read this lesson warns against.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Cancelling is normal. Spoofing needs intent to cancel at the moment of placing, inferred from a pattern of conduct and from records only investigators see. From the ladder, note the event and don't trade because of it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"spoofing\",\"depth-of-market\"],\"hints\":[\"What can you see from outside, and what can't you?\",\"Intent at the moment of placing is the legal line.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"How does a Market Profile (TPO) differ from a volume profile?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It counts units traded at the ask only\",\"rationale\":\"That's closer to a footprint's ask column. Market Profile ignores units entirely and counts time.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It counts 30-minute periods at each price, not units traded\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's the same count drawn with letters\",\"rationale\":\"The letters count periods. A price visited briefly by heavy volume gets one letter but a big volume bar.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It counts only the first hour of trading\",\"rationale\":\"That's the initial balance, one part of the profile. The profile covers every period of the session.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A TPO profile adds one letter for each 30-minute period that traded at a price, so it measures time at price. A volume profile adds up units at each price. They often look alike but can disagree, for example after a short burst of heavy trading.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"market-profile\",\"volume-profile\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Building a value area, your running total is 21 TPOs. The next candidate rows hold 5 above and 4 below. Adding one row at a time, what is the running total after the next step?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":26,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"TPOs\"},\"explanation\":\"Compare the two neighbours and add the larger: 21 + 5 = 26. In the lesson's profile, this step added 100.7. Repeat until the total reaches 70% of all TPOs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"market-profile\"],\"hints\":[\"The rule adds the neighbouring row with more TPOs.\",\"21 + 5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"run\":[10,40,1],\"up\":[1,9,1],\"dn\":[1,9,1]},\"constraints\":[\"up != dn\"],\"answer\":\"run + max(up, dn)\",\"prompt\":\"Building a value area, your running total is {run} TPOs. The next candidate rows hold {up} above and {dn} below. Adding one row at a time, what is the running total after the next step?\",\"explanation\":\"Add the larger neighbouring row: {run} + {max(up, dn)} = {answer}. Repeat until the total reaches 70% of all TPOs.\",\"hint\":\"Add the larger of {up} and {dn} to {run}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.3.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A lab tests the 80% rule on 200 LABX sessions in which price re-entered the value area and stayed two periods. Price crossed to the other side in 58% of them. Random bands of the same width, placed by chance, were crossed in 55% of comparable cases.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this test show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The rule works: 58% beats a coin flip\",\"rationale\":\"The fair comparison is the random bands at 55%, not a coin flip. Three points on 200 cases is within noise.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The rule adds little beyond any band of that width, and nowhere near 80%\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The rule works 80% of the time, as its name says\",\"rationale\":\"The test measured 58%. A rule's name isn't its hit rate.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Value areas are useless for anything\",\"rationale\":\"The test is about one claim, the 80% rule. Value areas still describe where the session traded.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Compare a rule with a control, not with its own name. 58% against 55% for random bands of the same width is a small gap on 200 cases, likely noise: one standard error is about 3.5 points. The claimed 80% doesn't appear.\",\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\",\"concepts\":[\"market-profile\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"What should 58% be compared with?\",\"Random bands of the same width are the baseline.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.1-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these claims is falsifiable as stated?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price respects key levels when the market is ready\",\"rationale\":\"'When the market is ready' can be fitted to any outcome after the fact, so no result could count against it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The trend continues unless smart money decides otherwise\",\"rationale\":\"Any reversal can be blamed on 'smart money', so the claim survives every result.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After a close above the 20-day high, the next 10 days rise more often than after a random day\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Wave 3 will be strong, unless the count needs relabelling\",\"rationale\":\"A built-in relabelling escape means no outcome can prove it wrong.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Option c names a rule, an event, an outcome, a horizon and a comparison, so some result could prove it wrong. The others carry an escape clause that absorbs any outcome.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"falsifiability\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.1-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why run every method trial on R0, a random walk with the same volatility, as well as on the market you care about?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Random walks are what real markets are, so R0 results transfer directly\",\"rationale\":\"R0 is a control, not a claim about real markets. Its job is to show what noise alone produces.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"R0 shows how often the 'signal' appears and 'works' by chance, so you can see whether the real result beats it\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A good method should earn the most on R0, because nothing interferes there\",\"rationale\":\"On R0 no method can have an edge. A method that 'earns' there is showing you luck or a bug.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's faster to simulate, so it saves time\",\"rationale\":\"Speed isn't the point. Without the control you can't tell a real effect from patterns that appear in any noise.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Patterns appear in noise: a head-and-shoulders detector found 3,091 of them on 10,000 R0 paths, and they did no better than any candle. R0 sets the base rate a method must beat on the structured market.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"method-trial\",\"random-walk\"],\"hints\":[\"What can any method earn on a market with no structure at all?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.1-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A trader tests a 50% retracement rule. After the first run he widens the touch band from 0.25 to 0.5 ATR, then switches from 1-hour to 4-hour candles, then drops the losing months. The final result looks strong.\",\"prompt\":\"What would pre-registration have protected him against?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Too few candles in the backtest\",\"rationale\":\"Sample size matters, but the problem here is the changes made after seeing results, each one another chance at a fluke.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Ignoring costs\",\"rationale\":\"Costs aren't the issue here. Band, timeframe and sample all changed after the results came in.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Picking a method with a weak evidence grade\",\"rationale\":\"The grade comes after the test. Pre-registration fixes the test before it runs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Letting the results choose the rules: each change was another try at a hit\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Every rule changed after a result is a degree of freedom. Writing the band, timeframe and sample down first makes the final number one test, not the best of many.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"method-trial\"],\"hints\":[\"Count how many times the rules changed after a result came in.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A swing runs from A = 80.00 to B = 92.00, and the pullback ends at C = 87.00. What is the 1.618 three-point projection?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":106.42,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Projection = C + 1.618 × (B − A) = 87.00 + 1.618 × 12.00 = 87.00 + 19.42 = 106.42.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fibonacci-clusters\"],\"hints\":[\"A projection starts from C, not from A.\",\"Add 1.618 × (B − A) to C.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[40,150,1],\"mv\":[4,20,1],\"back\":[1,8,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"back >= 0.3 * mv\",\"back <= 0.8 * mv\"],\"answer\":\"round(a + mv - back + 1.618 * mv, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A swing runs from A = {a:2} to B = {a + mv:2}, and the pullback ends at C = {a + mv - back:2}. What is the 1.618 three-point projection?\",\"explanation\":\"Projection = C + 1.618 × (B − A) = {a + mv - back:2} + 1.618 × {mv:2} = {a + mv - back:2} + {1.618 * mv:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Add 1.618 × {mv:2} to C, {a + mv - back:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does drawing grids from more swing highs and lows make it easier to 'explain' a past turn?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"More swings give a more accurate grid\",\"rationale\":\"There's no 'accurate' grid to converge on. More grids just add lines: four cover 68% of prices within 0.15, one covers 26%.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Markets respect clusters of levels more than single levels\",\"rationale\":\"Clusters are guaranteed once you have enough lines. That they exist says nothing about whether price respects them.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Each anchor pair adds five levels, so more prices sit near some level by chance\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It doesn't: levels from different swings cancel out\",\"rationale\":\"They don't cancel; they add up. Four grids give 20 levels where one gave 5.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Two lows and two highs give four grids and 20 levels, putting 68% of prices between 102.00 and 108.00 within 0.15 of a level. Picking anchors after the turn is hindsight: some level will be near.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"anchor-selection\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"Count the levels each new anchor pair adds.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"In the lab, Fibonacci levels showed a small extra reversal rate only on the market where 30% of agents traded them. Where would a real Fibonacci effect most plausibly come from?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The golden ratio's role in nature\",\"rationale\":\"Shells and flowers don't place orders. Tests on market swings found Fibonacci ratios no more often than chance.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Coordination: enough traders acting at the same levels\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The mathematics of random walks, which produce Fibonacci ratios\",\"rationale\":\"On R0 the Fibonacci levels did no better than control ratios: 48.1% against 48.0%.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Wave theory, which shows pullbacks end at 61.8%\",\"rationale\":\"Elliott Wave assumes the ratios; it doesn't demonstrate them. Its forecasts are graded D too.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"On R0 the levels matched the control ratios. With watchers, both rose, and Fibonacci by only about a point more. Any edge comes from people acting on the same lines (grade C), not from the numbers (grade D).\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fibonacci-levels\"],\"hints\":[\"What was different between the two markets?\",\"On R0, did the ratios help at all?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is one of Elliott's three hard rules?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Wave 3 is always 161.8% of wave 1\",\"rationale\":\"That's a guideline, extension. A count with wave 3 at 150% of wave 1 is still valid.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Waves 2 and 4 alternate: one sharp, one sideways\",\"rationale\":\"Alternation is a guideline. Counts that don't alternate can still pass all three rules.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Wave 3 is never the shortest of waves 1, 3 and 5\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Wave 5 must make a new high beyond wave 3\",\"rationale\":\"That's not one of the three rules. Elliott even has a name for a wave 5 that falls short: a truncation.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The three rules: wave 2 retraces less than 100% of wave 1; wave 3 is never the shortest; wave 4 stays out of wave 1's territory, except in diagonals. Ratios, alternation and equality are guidelines.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"elliott-wave\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A count has wave 2 retracing 45% of wave 1, wave 3 at 130% of wave 1, wave 5 at 90% of wave 1, waves 2 and 4 both sharp, and wave 4 ending 0.20 above wave 1's high.\",\"prompt\":\"Does the count break a rule?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Yes: wave 3 should be 161.8% of wave 1\",\"rationale\":\"161.8% is a guideline. The rule is only that wave 3 isn't the shortest, and at 130% it's the longest here.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Yes: waves 2 and 4 must alternate\",\"rationale\":\"Alternation is a guideline. Two sharp corrections make a less typical count, not an invalid one.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Yes: wave 4 overlaps wave 1\",\"rationale\":\"Wave 4 ends 0.20 above wave 1's high, so it stays out of wave 1's territory.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No: it misses guidelines, but all three rules hold\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Only three things break an impulse: wave 2 beyond 100% of wave 1, wave 3 the shortest, wave 4 overlapping wave 1. None happens here. Missed ratios and no alternation are guidelines, so the count stands.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"elliott-wave\"],\"hints\":[\"List the three rules, then check each one.\",\"Ratios and alternation are tendencies, not rules.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Wave 1 runs from 50.00 to 55.00, and wave 2 ends at 51.90. What percentage of wave 1 does wave 2 retrace?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":62,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Wave 2 gives back 55.00 − 51.90 = 3.10 of wave 1's 5.00: 3.10 ÷ 5.00 = 62%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"elliott-wave\"],\"hints\":[\"The retracement is the part of wave 1 that wave 2 gives back.\",\"Divide 3.10 by 5.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[40,120,1],\"w1\":[2,10,1],\"back\":[0.6,9.5,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"back >= 0.3 * w1\",\"back <= 0.9 * w1\"],\"answer\":\"round(100 * back / w1, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Wave 1 runs from {lo:2} to {lo + w1:2}, and wave 2 ends at {lo + w1 - back:2}. What percentage of wave 1 does wave 2 retrace?\",\"explanation\":\"Wave 2 gives back {lo + w1:2} − {lo + w1 - back:2} = {back:2} of wave 1's {w1:2}: {back:2} ÷ {w1:2} = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"Divide {back:2} by {w1:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do alternate counts make Elliott forecasts close to unfalsifiable?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Whatever happens, one of the valid counts already 'predicted' it, so the method never fails\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Counts are too complex for a computer to check\",\"rationale\":\"The rules can be checked mechanically. The trouble is that several counts pass them at once, with opposite forecasts.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Elliott waves only appear on long timeframes\",\"rationale\":\"Waves are labelled at every degree, from minutes to decades. The issue is multiple valid counts, not the timeframe.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The three rules are often broken\",\"rationale\":\"Counts that break a rule simply get relabelled. The trouble is the many counts that break none.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"On one R0 path, bullish, bearish and triangle counts all passed the rules. Every outcome confirms one of them, and relabelling after a surprise keeps the method alive. A claim no result can refute can't be tested.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"elliott-alternate-count\",\"falsifiability\"],\"hints\":[\"How many counts passed the rules on the lab's path?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What can a wave count defensibly give you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"How far wave 3 will run\",\"rationale\":\"Wave targets are guidelines, and the forecasts are graded D. Even on R0, where nothing can be forecast, counts pass the rules.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Permission to size up in wave 3\",\"rationale\":\"Wave 3 is a label, not a probability. Sizing up on it is the costliest Elliott mistake.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A price where the count is wrong, and a position size from it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The direction of the next move, once the count is clear\",\"rationale\":\"Alternate counts point in opposite directions. The 'clear' count is often just the one you saw first.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A count's rules define where it fails: an invalidation level. Put the stop just beyond it and size from the distance. The forecast part is graded D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"invalidation-level\",\"elliott-alternate-count\"],\"hints\":[\"Which part of a count can price prove wrong?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.4-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Account 10,000.00, risk 1% (100.00). Your count is wrong below 80.00, so your stop is 79.90. You buy at the ask, 82.40. Commission is 0.05% of each fill, on entry and at the stop. How many units can you buy?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":38,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Distance 2.50. Commission per unit: 0.05% × (82.40 + 79.90) = 0.08115, so each unit can lose 2.58115. 100.00 ÷ 2.58115 ≈ 38.74, rounded down: 38. 38 units lose 98.09 at the stop; 39 would lose 100.67.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"position-sizing\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"hints\":[\"The stop sits 0.10 under the count's invalidation, 80.00.\",\"Loss per unit = distance + 0.05% × (entry + stop).\",\"Loss per unit: 2.50 + 0.08115 = 2.58115.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"inv\":[40,150,1],\"gap\":[1,4,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1)) - floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1))) >= 0.05\",\"100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1)) - floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1))) <= 0.95\",\"floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1))) < floor(100 / (gap + 0.1))\"],\"answer\":\"floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1)))\",\"prompt\":\"Account 10,000.00, risk 1% (100.00). Your count is wrong below {inv:2}, so your stop is {inv - 0.1:2}. You buy at the ask, {inv + gap:2}. Commission is 0.05% of each fill, on entry and at the stop. How many units can you buy?\",\"explanation\":\"Distance {gap + 0.1:2}. Commission per unit: 0.05% × ({inv + gap:2} + {inv - 0.1:2}) = {0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1):5}, so each unit can lose {gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1):5}. 100.00 ÷ that ≈ {100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (inv + gap + inv - 0.1)):2}, rounded down: {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"Loss per unit: {gap + 0.1:2} + 0.05% × ({inv + gap:2} + {inv - 0.1:2}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In a Wyckoff accumulation, which event is a sharp drop on very heavy volume that sets the range low?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Spring\",\"rationale\":\"A spring is a later dip below the range low that closes back inside. The low itself is set earlier, by the climax.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Selling climax\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Secondary test\",\"rationale\":\"The secondary test revisits the climax low later, usually on lighter volume.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Last point of support\",\"rationale\":\"That comes late, after the sign of strength, as a shallow pullback near the top of the range.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The selling climax ends the fall on heavy volume and sets the low; the automatic rally sets the high. Springs, tests and signs of strength are all named relative to that range.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"wyckoff-method\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"How should you treat Wyckoff's Composite Man?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"As a real group of insiders who move prices\",\"rationale\":\"Wyckoff offered it as a way to study the market, not as a fact. Nothing in a range proves one operator is behind it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"As another name for market makers\",\"rationale\":\"Market makers are real and quote both sides. The Composite Man is an imagined single operator, a heuristic.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"As a myth with nothing useful in it\",\"rationale\":\"Mocking it misses its use: imagining motives can organise how you read a range. Just don't treat it as evidence.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"As a study device: imagine one operator to make sense of the tape\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The Composite Man is a heuristic: picture one large operator to read a range. Later vocabularies turned him into 'smart money', but there's no evidence that a single actor runs ranges.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"wyckoff-method\"],\"hints\":[\"Did Wyckoff present it as a fact, or as a way of studying?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A trader's journal lists 12 'springs' from last month, each followed by a rally. Dips below range lows that kept falling aren't in the journal.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with concluding that springs work?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"12 trades are too few; 30 would prove it\",\"rationale\":\"More trades won't help while the failures keep getting dropped. The sample is picked by the outcome.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The dips that failed were renamed or left out, so the 12 were chosen by their outcome\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Springs only work in uptrends\",\"rationale\":\"That's a new claim with no test behind it. The problem is how the 12 were picked.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: a spring followed by a rally is the definition\",\"rationale\":\"That's exactly the problem. If only rallies count as springs, the label can never fail.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A dip becomes a 'spring' only if it rallies; otherwise it's a 'breakdown'. Counting only the named ones guarantees success. On R0, 74% of such dips broke the low first.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"wyckoff-spring\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"When does a dip get the name 'spring'?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.6-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In a bullish Gartley, X = 40.00 and A = 52.00. Where does D sit, at 0.786 of XA?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":42.57,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"D = A − 0.786 × XA = 52.00 − 0.786 × 12.00 = 52.00 − 9.432 = 42.57.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"harmonic-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"D retraces from A, back towards X.\",\"Subtract 0.786 × 12.00 from 52.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"x\":[20,150,1],\"mv\":[4,20,1]},\"answer\":\"round(x + mv - 0.786 * mv, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"In a bullish Gartley, X = {x:2} and A = {x + mv:2}. Where does D sit, at 0.786 of XA?\",\"explanation\":\"D = A − 0.786 × XA = {x + mv:2} − 0.786 × {mv:2} = {x + mv:2} − {0.786 * mv:3} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Subtract 0.786 × {mv:2} from {x + mv:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.6-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On 400 random-walk charts, a harmonic detector finds 29 patterns at ±1% tolerance and 1,026 at ±10%. What does that show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Loose tolerance finds the real patterns that strict settings miss\",\"rationale\":\"These are random walks: no pattern is real. The extra 997 come from the looser dial, not from hidden structure.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Harmonic patterns are common, so they must matter\",\"rationale\":\"Frequency isn't evidence. Detections on pure noise show how easily the shapes appear by chance.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"±10% is too loose, but ±1% gives valid patterns\",\"rationale\":\"Strict settings find fewer patterns, not better ones. On R0, the reversal rate at D matched random depths.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Tolerance is a free parameter that decides how many patterns exist, even in noise\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The same noise holds 29 or 1,026 'patterns', depending on the dial. A method whose output you can tune this much needs a pre-registered tolerance and a control before any result means anything.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"harmonic-patterns\",\"falsifiability\"],\"hints\":[\"These charts are random walks. Where did the extra patterns come from?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.6-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What evidence grade fits harmonic patterns as reversal forecasts, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"D: no peer-reviewed support, many free parameters, and no edge over random depths in the lab\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A: Fibonacci ratios appear throughout nature\",\"rationale\":\"Ratios in nature aren't tests in markets. In the lab, the Gartley D did no better than random depths.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"C: they work when enough traders use them\",\"rationale\":\"Coordination could lift any watched level, but the lab found no gap even on R2. C needs evidence of the mechanism at work.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Mechanic: the ratios are exact definitions\",\"rationale\":\"The definitions are exact, but the claim is a forecast that price turns at D. Forecasts need tests, and these failed.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Grade D: plausible-sounding, not supported by peer-reviewed tests, with free ratios, ranges and tolerances. On R0 and R2, reversals at the Gartley D matched random depths.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"harmonic-patterns\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate the pattern's definition from the forecast it makes.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.7-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In the full Ichimoku system, which condition must hold before a Tenkan-Kijun cross counts as a long trigger?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The close is below Kijun\",\"rationale\":\"A close below Kijun is the exit for a long, not a condition for entering one.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Price is above the cloud\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The stop has been hit\",\"rationale\":\"A stop only exists once you're in a trade. The filter comes first.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Chikou is below the price 26 candles back\",\"rationale\":\"That's the opposite of the long confirmation, which wants Chikou above the price 26 candles back.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Filter first (price above the cloud), then the trigger (Tenkan crosses above Kijun), then confirmation (Chikou above the price 26 candles back). The stop and the exit come once you're in.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.7\",\"concepts\":[\"ichimoku-system\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.7-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"An Ichimoku long fills at the ask, 64.20. Kijun is 63.10 and the stop goes 0.10 below it. Risk 1% of 10,000.00 (100.00); commission 0.05% of each fill, on entry and at the stop. How many units?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":79,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Stop 63.00, distance 1.20. Commission per unit: 0.05% × (64.20 + 63.00) = 0.0636, so each unit can lose 1.2636. 100.00 ÷ 1.2636 ≈ 79.14, rounded down: 79. 79 units lose 99.83 at the stop; 80 would lose 101.09.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.7\",\"concepts\":[\"position-sizing\",\"ichimoku-system\"],\"hints\":[\"The stop is Kijun minus 0.10: 63.00.\",\"Loss per unit = distance + 0.05% × (entry + stop).\",\"Loss per unit: 1.20 + 0.0636 = 1.2636.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"kij\":[40,150,0.5],\"gap\":[0.5,3,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1)) - floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1))) >= 0.05\",\"100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1)) - floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1))) <= 0.95\",\"floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1))) < floor(100 / (gap + 0.1))\"],\"answer\":\"floor(100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1)))\",\"prompt\":\"An Ichimoku long fills at the ask, {kij + gap:2}. Kijun is {kij:2} and the stop goes 0.10 below it. Risk 1% of 10,000.00 (100.00); commission 0.05% of each fill, on entry and at the stop. How many units?\",\"explanation\":\"Stop {kij - 0.1:2}, distance {gap + 0.1:2}. Commission per unit: 0.05% × ({kij + gap:2} + {kij - 0.1:2}) = {0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1):5}, so each unit can lose {gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1):5}. 100.00 ÷ that ≈ {100 / (gap + 0.1 + 0.0005 * (kij + gap + kij - 0.1)):2}, rounded down: {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"Loss per unit: {gap + 0.1:2} + 0.05% × ({kij + gap:2} + {kij - 0.1:2}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.7-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Rule A's parameter grid shows net results of 8.1, 8.6, 9.0, 8.8 and 8.4 per 1,000 candles across five neighbouring settings. Rule B's shows −1.2, −0.8, 6.5, −1.1 and −0.9.\",\"prompt\":\"Which rule's best setting would you trust more?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Rule B: its best result, 6.5, is far ahead of the rest\",\"rationale\":\"A lone peak among losing neighbours is the mark of a lucky fit. Small changes wipe it out.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Both equally: each has a best setting\",\"rationale\":\"Every grid has a best setting by construction. What matters is whether the neighbours agree.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Rule A: its neighbours do about as well, a plateau\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither: grids tell you nothing\",\"rationale\":\"Grids can't prove an edge, but they flag fragile ones. A plateau is necessary, though not sufficient.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A plateau means the result doesn't hinge on one exact number, so it's less likely to be fitted noise. A spike surrounded by losses usually disappears on new data.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.7\",\"concepts\":[\"ichimoku-system\"],\"hints\":[\"Look at what happens right beside each rule's best setting.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.8-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why can't a Gann 1×1 angle be tested as drawn on a chart?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Its slope depends on how the chart is scaled, so the line changes with the axes\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Gann kept the exact angles secret\",\"rationale\":\"The angles are published. The issue is that '45°' on screen means a different price per candle at every scale.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Angles only work on daily charts\",\"rationale\":\"That's an untested restriction. On any timeframe, a drawn angle still depends on the scale.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It can: 45° is always one point per day\",\"rationale\":\"Only if you fix the unit first. Most charts rescale automatically, so the 45° line moves.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A 45° line rises one price unit per time unit, and the chart's scale picks the unit. Stretch the axes and a different line looks like 45°. Fix the price per candle before testing, or the screen sets the result.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"scale-dependence\",\"gann-methods\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.8-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Spectral analysis of 128 candles of a random walk reports a dominant 43-candle cycle. What's the best reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A 43-candle cycle exists and should repeat\",\"rationale\":\"On a random walk nothing repeats. Change the window and the 'dominant' cycle changes: 16, 43 and 128 candles in the lesson.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"On noise, the strongest 'cycle' is mostly set by the window length; test the claim on R0 first\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The method is broken and finds nothing\",\"rationale\":\"The method works as designed: it always reports a strongest frequency. On noise, that peak is an artefact.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Cycles need at least 1,000 candles to be real\",\"rationale\":\"More data doesn't make a noise cycle real. On a random walk, longer windows just report longer 'cycles'.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In 82% of 128-candle R0 windows, the strongest cycle was one of the two longest allowed. A cycle claim must beat that base rate, out of sample, before it means anything.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"spurious-cycles\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the same method report on R0?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.4.8-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A friend says Mercury retrograde periods are bad for stocks. Following the trial protocol, what's your first step?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Check whether the last retrograde was a down month\",\"rationale\":\"One period is an anecdote, picked after the fact. Write the rule and the comparison first, then look.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Dismiss it: astrology can't affect markets\",\"rationale\":\"Mocking skips the test. The protocol can reject a false claim on evidence, as it rejected the full-moon claim.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Run it on real data straight away\",\"rationale\":\"Running before writing the rule lets the results choose it. Pre-register, then run on R0 and the market.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Rewrite it as a testable rule: which days, which outcome, which horizon, compared with what\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"First the testable version, pre-registered; then R0 and the market; then costs and a grade. The same steps found a planted month-start effect and rejected the full-moon claim.\",\"lesson\":\"3.4.8\",\"concepts\":[\"method-trial\",\"falsifiability\"],\"hints\":[\"Which step of the trial comes before any data?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.1-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these claims is mechanics, true by how orders work?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stop-losses cluster just beyond round numbers\",\"rationale\":\"That's a measured tendency from order data: true more often than not, with exceptions.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Institutions defend their order blocks\",\"rationale\":\"That's a narrative about intent. If the zone breaks, the story simply changes.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A triggered stop-loss becomes a market order\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Volatility rises at session opens\",\"rationale\":\"That's a documented tendency, an average by hour, not a rule that holds every day.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A stop order turns into a market order when its price trades: that's how the order type works, every time. Clustering beyond round numbers and busier opens are measured tendencies. \\\"Defending\\\" a block is a story about intent.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"claim-layers\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.1-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A level holds, and a video says \\\"institutions defended it\\\". A week later the same level breaks, and the video says \\\"they were inducing liquidity\\\".\",\"prompt\":\"Why can't this explanation be tested?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It fits both outcomes, so no result could count against it\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only because retail traders can't see order-book data\",\"rationale\":\"Data would test order flows, not this story. As told, it fits a hold and a break alike.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Two examples are too few to judge it\",\"rationale\":\"More examples wouldn't help: every one, hold or break, already has a story ready.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It can: price moved, which confirms the institutions acted\",\"rationale\":\"Price moves either way. A claim confirmed by every outcome predicts nothing.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A testable claim says in advance what would prove it wrong. Here a hold means defence and a break means inducement, so every outcome confirms it. That's intent attribution: it explains everything and predicts nothing.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"intent-attribution\"],\"hints\":[\"What would the video say if the level had done the opposite?\",\"A testable claim names a result that would count against it.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.1-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A channel teaches SMC for free. Each video links a paid indicator and a prop-firm challenge with a discount code.\",\"prompt\":\"What matters most when you judge its claims?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Free lessons can't be accurate\",\"rationale\":\"Price isn't the issue. Free or paid, the question is what the claims are based on and who gains.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It earns from your purchases whether or not the method works\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Prop-firm challenges are illegal\",\"rationale\":\"They're legal products. The point is the channel earns from your sign-up, not from your results.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Indicators can't show SMC concepts\",\"rationale\":\"Vendors do code them. That makes them standard-setters with an interest in the method looking good.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Course, indicator and challenge sales pay the seller whether or not the method has an edge: a conflict of interest. It doesn't make every claim false, but it means the claims need independent tests, with base rates and costs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"smart-money-concepts\",\"performance-claims\"],\"hints\":[\"Follow the money: when does the channel get paid?\",\"Does it get paid only if your trading works?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In classical terms, a change of character (CHoCH) in an uptrend is…\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A new higher high, so the trend continues\",\"rationale\":\"That's a BOS, a with-trend break. A CHoCH breaks against the trend.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A fast run of large trend bars\",\"rationale\":\"That's displacement, which is momentum renamed.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The first break of the last higher low: Dow's warning\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A gap between candle 1 and candle 3\",\"rationale\":\"That's a fair value gap, a zone word, not a structure word.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A CHoCH is the first counter-trend break of the last protected swing: in an uptrend, the last higher low. Dow theory and the structure break of 1.5 describe the same event. Only the name is new.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"change-of-character\",\"structure-break\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.2-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Two traders run a CHoCH detector on the same 60 candles. One sees 3 CHoCH labels, the other 1.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely reason?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"One detector has a bug\",\"rationale\":\"Possible, but rarely needed. Different settings already give 3 or 1 on the same candles.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They use a different swing length N, or close versus wick\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The candles changed between their looks\",\"rationale\":\"Closed candles don't change. The settings do.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only one count can be right\",\"rationale\":\"Both are right under their own settings. Structure depends on definitions you choose.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"On one 60-candle chart the close rule gave 3 CHoCH labels with N = 2, 2 with N = 3 and 1 with N = 5; the wick rule gave more. Each setting is a choice, and every choice is another degree of freedom.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"change-of-character\",\"swing-points\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a detector have to decide before it labels anything?\",\"A larger N keeps fewer swings; a wick rule breaks levels more often.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.2-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which grade fits the claim \\\"a CHoCH signals a reversal\\\"?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: trend following is well documented\",\"rationale\":\"That's monthly trend following in futures. Intraday swing-label rules haven't been tested as such.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Mechanic: a CHoCH is a defined event\",\"rationale\":\"The definition is Mechanic. The prediction that a reversal follows is a separate claim.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"F: it loses by design\",\"rationale\":\"F is for rules built to lose, such as martingale. This one is unproven, not doomed by construction.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"D: unproven when tested\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Intraday swing rules haven't been tested as such, and on a random walk the CHoCH rule matched any candle: 49.8% against 49.7%. Where it did better, in a trending market, the edge came from the trend, as with any trend-following rule.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"change-of-character\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate the definition from the prediction.\",\"What did the CHoCH rule do on the random walk?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.3-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In classical terms, sell-side liquidity is…\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sell stops resting below swing lows\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Large sellers waiting to sell at the highs\",\"rationale\":\"SMC's sell-side is below the lows: the sell stops of longs and breakout traders.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The bid side of the order book\",\"rationale\":\"The term isn't about resting bids. It names stop orders below lows, which become sells when triggered.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A failed breakout below a low\",\"rationale\":\"That's the sweep. Sell-side liquidity is the orders the sweep would trigger.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Longs put stop-losses under swing lows, and breakout traders put sell stops there too. Triggered, they become market sells. SMC calls that pool sell-side liquidity; classically it's stop clustering.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-pool\",\"stop-clustering\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.3-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"\\\"They swept the stops, now we reverse.\\\" Which part is documented?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The reversal after the sweep\",\"rationale\":\"That's the contradicted part: the only systematic test found continuation, not reversal.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"That someone planned the sweep\",\"rationale\":\"Intent can't be seen in prices. It's the untestable part.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Stops cluster beyond levels and speed price up when triggered\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"All of it, given enough examples\",\"rationale\":\"Examples chosen after the fact show nothing. Two of the three parts fail or can't be tested.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Osler's order data show stops clustering beyond obvious levels and trends speeding up through them: grade B. The reversal is contradicted (grade D), and the plan behind it can't be observed.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-sweep\",\"stop-clustering\"],\"hints\":[\"Split the sentence into what happened, why, and what comes next.\",\"Which part has order data behind it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.3-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which version of \\\"sweeps reverse\\\" is a testable hypothesis?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Smart money reverses price after taking the highs\",\"rationale\":\"No event rule, no horizon, no baseline, and an actor nobody can observe.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"After a sweep, price falls over the next 10 candles\",\"rationale\":\"Close, but with no baseline: price falls after half of all candles anyway.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sweeps usually reverse, so watch for them\",\"rationale\":\"\\\"Usually\\\" has no number, no horizon and no comparison. Nothing could count against it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"After a trade 1 tick above the prior-day high and a close back under it, the next 10 candles fall more often than after a random candle\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A testable hypothesis names the event exactly, the outcome over a fixed horizon and a baseline to beat. Run it on the random walk and with stops on and off, and the data can say no.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"testable-reformulation\"],\"hints\":[\"Look for an exact event, a fixed horizon and a comparison.\",\"Only one option says what the result is compared with.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.4-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A breaker block is SMC's name for…\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A zone that broke and now acts the opposite way: role reversal\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The last opposite candle before a strong move\",\"rationale\":\"That's the order block itself. A breaker is what SMC calls it after it fails.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A three-candle gap\",\"rationale\":\"That's a fair value gap.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The 50% point of a range\",\"rationale\":\"That's equilibrium, the line between premium and discount.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A broken support acting as resistance, or the reverse, is role reversal from 1.5. SMC calls a failed order block that flips this way a breaker. The rename also turns a failure into a new zone.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"order-block\",\"role-reversal\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.4-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A dealing range runs from 80.00 up to 92.00. Where is the OTE \\\"sweet spot\\\", the 70.5% retracement, for a long?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":83.54,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"The range is 12.00. A long's retracement is measured down from the high: 92.00 − 0.705 × 12.00 = 92.00 − 8.46 = 83.54. It sits inside Fibonacci's 61.8–78.6% band: OTE is that band with rounded ratios.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"optimal-trade-entry\",\"fibonacci-levels\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the size of the range first.\",\"Measure down from the high: high − 0.705 × range.\",\"92.00 − 0.705 × 12.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"low\":[50,150,1],\"size\":[4,20,1]},\"answer\":\"round(low + size - 0.705 * size, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A dealing range runs from {low:2} up to {low + size:2}. Where is the OTE \\\"sweet spot\\\", the 70.5% retracement, for a long?\",\"explanation\":\"The range is {size:2}. A long's retracement is measured down from the high: {low + size:2} − 0.705 × {size:2} = {low + size:2} − {0.705 * size:2} = {answer:2}. It sits inside Fibonacci's 61.8–78.6% band: OTE is that band with rounded ratios.\",\"hint\":\"{low + size:2} − 0.705 × {size:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.4-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An order block holds: \\\"institutions defended it\\\". It breaks: \\\"it was mitigated\\\", or \\\"it's a breaker now\\\". What does this do to the claim \\\"order blocks hold\\\"?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It makes the claim stronger, because it explains more cases\",\"rationale\":\"Explaining every case is the problem: a claim that fits every outcome predicts none.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It makes it unfalsifiable: no outcome counts against it\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing, as long as the zones are drawn carefully\",\"rationale\":\"Careful drawing doesn't help if failures are renamed afterwards. The rule must say in advance what failure is.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It proves order blocks fail\",\"rationale\":\"It proves nothing either way. It removes the possibility of a test.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"If a hold confirms the claim and a break gets a new name, no result can count against it. A testable version fixes the rules and says in advance when a block has failed, then compares blocks with random zones.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"order-block\",\"intent-attribution\"],\"hints\":[\"What result would the claim accept as a failure?\",\"A testable claim says in advance what failure looks like.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"SMT divergence, two related markets disagreeing at a swing, is a new name for…\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The Wyckoff cycle\",\"rationale\":\"That's what the power of three and the market maker models rename.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Session opens and overlaps\",\"rationale\":\"That's what killzones rename.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An RSI divergence\",\"rationale\":\"Close, but RSI divergence compares price with an oscillator. SMT compares two markets.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Dow's confirmation principle\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Dow wanted the Industrials and the Transports to confirm each other's highs. SMT divergence applies the same check to pairs like two stock indices or two currency pairs. As a signal it grades D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"smt-divergence\",\"dow-theory\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which part of the killzone idea is documented?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"London sets the day's high or low\",\"rationale\":\"A directional role for a session: testable, but no published test supports it as a rule.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Volatility and activity rise around session opens and overlaps\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Setups inside the windows win more often\",\"rationale\":\"No published test shows it. In our test, direction stayed near 50/50 in and out of the windows.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Breakouts inside the windows are more predictable\",\"rationale\":\"They travel further, about a third, because volatility is higher. Their direction isn't more predictable.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Volatility follows the session clock, with U-shapes in Tokyo and London hours (Ito and Hashimoto): grade A. Directional roles for the windows are grade D. Bigger moves in both directions aren't a direction.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"killzones\",\"fx-sessions\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate how much price moves from which way it moves.\",\"Which part has been measured in market data?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.5-3\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is the IPDA claim graded D and called unfalsifiable?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Its code is secret, but it could be leaked one day\",\"rationale\":\"The problem isn't secrecy. As stated, any price path can be called \\\"delivered\\\", so no result could count against it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Algorithms are banned in FX\",\"rationale\":\"Algorithms trade FX all the time. What's missing is one central algorithm that steers price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"No result could disprove it, and FX is fragmented across many dealers\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It was tested and lost money\",\"rationale\":\"It can't be tested at all as stated. That's worse than losing money in a test.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Any price path can be described as delivered between liquidity and gaps, so nothing could count against the claim. It also clashes with what's known: FX runs through hundreds of dealers and platforms, and dealers internalise over 80% of client trades (BIS).\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"ipda-claim\"],\"hints\":[\"What price path would the claim fail to explain?\",\"Think about how many dealers and platforms make up FX.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In a claim-test report, what does the random-walk (R0) run tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"What the rule scores where no edge can exist\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How the rule did on the data used to tune it\",\"rationale\":\"That's the in-sample run.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"How the rule did on fresh data it wasn't tuned on\",\"rationale\":\"That's the out-of-sample run.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"How much the costs take\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are a separate step, applied to every run.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"On a random walk with the same volatility, no rule can have an edge. Whatever the rule scores there is what chance and the rule's geometry give. A real edge has to beat it out of sample and after costs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"testable-reformulation\",\"random-walk\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A tight-stop rule: average win 0.40, stop 0.20, win rate 45%, price 80.00. Spread 0.10; commission 0.05% of the price each way. What is the net result per unit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.11,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Gross: 0.45 × 0.40 − 0.55 × 0.20 = 0.18 − 0.11 = 0.07. Costs: spread 0.10 + commission 2 × 0.0005 × 80.00 = 0.08, so 0.18. Net: 0.07 − 0.18 = −0.11 per unit.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-costs\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Gross first: win rate × win − loss rate × stop.\",\"Costs: the spread once, commission on both executions.\",\"0.07 gross minus 0.10 + 0.08 of costs.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"win\":[0.2,0.6,0.05],\"stop\":[0.1,0.3,0.05],\"rate\":[30,60,5],\"price\":[50,200,10]},\"answer\":\"round(rate / 100 * win - (1 - rate / 100) * stop - 0.1 - 0.001 * price, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A tight-stop rule: average win {win:2}, stop {stop:2}, win rate {rate}%, price {price:2}. Spread 0.10; commission 0.05% of the price each way. What is the net result per unit?\",\"explanation\":\"Gross: {rate / 100:2} × {win:2} − {1 - rate / 100:2} × {stop:2} = {rate / 100 * win - (1 - rate / 100) * stop:3}. Costs: spread 0.10 + commission 2 × 0.0005 × {price:2} = {0.001 * price:2}, so {0.1 + 0.001 * price:2}. Net: {answer:2} per unit.\",\"hint\":\"{rate / 100 * win - (1 - rate / 100) * stop:3} gross minus {0.1 + 0.001 * price:2} of costs.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.5.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A report on H3: in-sample 60% (N 250); out-of-sample 52.0% ± 4.4 (N 480); random walk 50%; net −0.05R after costs. Parameters were frozen before the out-of-sample run.\",\"prompt\":\"Which grade is consistent with this report?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"B: out of sample is above 50%\",\"rationale\":\"52.0 ± 4.4 includes 50, the random-walk result, and the net is negative. B needs an edge clear of noise.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"D: unproven when tested\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A: the in-sample result is strong\",\"rationale\":\"In-sample results are inflated by tuning. Grades rest on out-of-sample results after costs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Mechanic: the rule is fully specified\",\"rationale\":\"Mechanic means true by construction, like the spread. A specified rule still needs evidence.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The out-of-sample interval, 47.6% to 56.4%, includes the random-walk 50%, and the net is negative after costs. The test ran properly and found nothing: grade D. A clear out-of-sample edge after costs, repeated, would change it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"evidence-grades\",\"testable-reformulation\"],\"hints\":[\"Does the out-of-sample interval include the random-walk result?\",\"Check the sign of the net result.\",\"52.0 − 4.4 = 47.6, below 50.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A zero-coupon bond pays 100 in 5 years. Its yield is 3.0%. What is its price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":86.26,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"per 100\"},\"explanation\":\"Price = 100 ÷ (1 + y)^n = 100 ÷ 1.03^5 = 100 ÷ 1.1593 = 86.26. A higher yield or a longer maturity would lower it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"bond-price-yield\"],\"hints\":[\"Price = 100 ÷ (1 + y)^n, with y as a decimal.\",\"1.03 multiplied by itself 5 times is 1.1593.\",\"100 ÷ 1.1593 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"y\":[1,8,0.5],\"n\":[2,5,1]},\"answer\":\"round(100 / ((1 + y / 100) * (1 + y / 100) * (1 + y / 100 * (n >= 3)) * (1 + y / 100 * (n >= 4)) * (1 + y / 100 * (n >= 5))), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A zero-coupon bond pays 100 in {n} years. Its yield is {y:1}%. What is its price?\",\"explanation\":\"Price = 100 ÷ (1 + y)^n = 100 ÷ {1 + y / 100:3}^{n} = {answer:2}. Each extra year divides by {1 + y / 100:3} once more.\",\"hint\":\"100 ÷ {1 + y / 100:3}^{n} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LB10 yields 3.80%. Breakeven inflation over the same 10 years is 2.30%. What is the approximate real yield?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Real yield ≈ nominal yield − expected inflation = 3.80% − 2.30% = 1.50%. The exact figure, 1.038 ÷ 1.023 − 1, is 1.47%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"real-yield\"],\"hints\":[\"Inflation eats into the nominal yield.\",\"Subtract expected inflation from the nominal yield.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"nom\":[2,6,0.05],\"inf\":[1,4,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(nom - inf, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LB10 yields {nom:2}%. Breakeven inflation over the same 10 years is {inf:2}%. What is the approximate real yield?\",\"explanation\":\"Real yield ≈ nominal yield − expected inflation = {nom:2}% − {inf:2}% = {answer:2}%.\",\"hint\":\"{nom:2} − {inf:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LCB unexpectedly raises its rate by 0.50 points; VFUN's central bank holds. Other things equal, what does LFX, vUSD priced in VFUN, tend to do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Fall: higher rates slow the economy, so the currency weakens\",\"rationale\":\"That's a later, slower effect. On the day, holding vUSD now pays more, which tends to lift it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Rise: the rate differential widens in vUSD's favour\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing: rate decisions move bonds, not currencies\",\"rationale\":\"Rate changes are one of the main drivers of currencies. Holding vUSD now pays more than holding VFUN.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Fall: vUSD becomes costlier to borrow, so traders sell it\",\"rationale\":\"Costlier borrowing discourages funding with vUSD and makes holding it more attractive. Both push vUSD up.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A currency pays its holder its interest rate. A surprise hike widens the differential, so money tends to flow into vUSD and LFX tends to rise. Grade B: risk appetite and flows can dominate on a given day.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"rate-differential\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what holding each currency pays after the hike.\",\"Money tends to move toward the higher yield.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LB2 yields 3.95% and LB10 yields 4.30%. What is the 10y–2y spread, in basis points?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":35,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"bp\"},\"explanation\":\"10y–2y = 4.30 − 3.95 = +0.35 percentage points = +35 bp. Positive: the curve slopes up between 2 and 10 years.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"yield-curve\"],\"hints\":[\"Spread = the 10-year yield minus the 2-year yield.\",\"1 percentage point = 100 basis points.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"two\":[2,6,0.05],\"ten\":[2,6,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(ten - two) >= 0.1\"],\"answer\":\"round((ten - two) * 100, 0)\",\"prompt\":\"LB2 yields {two:2}% and LB10 yields {ten:2}%. What is the 10y–2y spread, in basis points?\",\"explanation\":\"10y–2y = {ten:2} − {two:2} = {ten - two:2} percentage points = {answer} bp. A negative spread means the curve is inverted between 2 and 10 years.\",\"hint\":\"({ten:2} − {two:2}) × 100 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over a month, LB2 falls 0.50 points and LB10 falls 0.15 points. Which curve move is this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Bull flattener\",\"rationale\":\"Both fell, so it's bull. But the short end fell more, so the 10y–2y spread widened by 0.35: a steepener.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bear steepener\",\"rationale\":\"Bear means yields rose. Here both fell.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Bull steepener\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Bear flattener\",\"rationale\":\"Both yields fell, so it's bull, and the spread widened, so it's a steepener.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Yields fell: bull. The 2-year fell more, so 10y–2y rose by 0.35 points: a steepener. That's what a market pricing in rate cuts usually does.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"curve-moves\"],\"hints\":[\"Bull or bear: did yields rise or fall?\",\"Then compare the changes: did 10y − 2y widen or narrow?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The 10y–2y and 10y–3m spreads both turn negative. What does the evidence support?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sell stocks now: a crash usually follows within weeks\",\"rationale\":\"As a timing signal for stocks the inversion is grade D: equities have often risen for months after one.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the 2022–24 inversion proved the signal broken\",\"rationale\":\"One long inversion without a quick recession fits a long, variable lag. It lowers confidence but doesn't erase the record.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A recession within 12 months is certain\",\"rationale\":\"Lags have run from about 6 to 24 months, and there have been false alarms. A warning, not a certainty.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A long-lead recession warning, with a variable lag and some false alarms\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Inversions have come before every US recession since the 1950s, typically 6 to 24 months ahead, with false alarms such as the mid-1960s (grade B). They don't time stocks (grade D). Use them for scenarios and risk budgets.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"yield-curve\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Separate two claims: a recession warning, and a signal to time stocks.\",\"How long has the lead been, and has it always been right?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.3-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Payrolls beat consensus by the same margin in March and in September. LABX rose in March and fell in September. What best explains it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The market's main fear moved from growth to inflation, changing how it reads the central bank's response\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Payrolls are random, so the reactions are noise\",\"rationale\":\"Large surprises move markets reliably. The direction flipped because the market's reading of the bank changed.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Traders forgot the March reaction\",\"rationale\":\"Memory isn't the mechanism. The same number implied different policy, because the regime changed.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Strong jobs are always bad for stocks; March was an exception\",\"rationale\":\"Neither direction is always right. Strong jobs help stocks in a growth scare and hurt them in an inflation fight.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Data move markets through the expected policy response. In a growth scare, strong jobs ease recession fears. When the bank is fighting inflation, the same beat means more hikes: good news is bad news (grade B).\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"regime-dependent-reaction\",\"inflation-regime\"],\"hints\":[\"What does strong data imply for the central bank in each case?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.3-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Historical case: in 2022 the S&P 500 lost 18.1% and 20-year US Treasuries lost 26.1%, double-digit losses for both in the same year. Inflation was high and central banks were raising rates fast.\",\"prompt\":\"What does this case show about the stock–bond correlation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Bonds stopped working as a hedge forever\",\"rationale\":\"One regime doesn't settle forever. When growth shocks dominate again, bonds can cushion stocks again.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bonds always fall when stocks fall\",\"rationale\":\"For most of 2000–2020 the two moved opposite or barely together. The sign depends on the dominant shock.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It was a one-off with no economic cause\",\"rationale\":\"There was a cause: rising inflation and rate hikes push stock and bond prices down together.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Its sign depends on the regime: positive when inflation and tight policy dominate\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Decades of data show the stock–bond correlation is negative when inflation is low and stable, and positive when inflation is high and policy tight (grade A). 2022 was the second kind, so the bond leg added to losses.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"stock-bond-correlation\"],\"hints\":[\"What was the dominant shock in 2022: growth or inflation?\",\"Which way do inflation shocks push stock prices and bond prices?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.3-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A pre-CPI dashboard: core inflation has been falling for six months. The dot-plot median shows two cuts next year. The curve is steepening out of an inversion. Last week, strong retail sales lifted LABX 0.8% and barely moved LB2.\",\"prompt\":\"Which regime is the market trading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Disinflation, with growth the main focus\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Inflation fight: good news is bad news\",\"rationale\":\"In an inflation fight, strong data would have sunk LABX and lifted LB2. Here LABX rose and LB2 barely moved.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Crisis: haven demand dominates\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing here signals stress: no widening spreads, no haven bid. Strong data lifting stocks is a calm-market reaction.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Can't tell before the CPI number\",\"rationale\":\"The regime is read before the release, from recent reactions and policy signals. Here they all point one way.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Falling inflation, cuts in the dots and strong data lifting stocks without lifting the 2-year all say the bank isn't fighting inflation. With growth the focus, a hot CPI print would matter less than in an inflation fight.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"inflation-regime\"],\"hints\":[\"Start with the last reaction: what did strong data do to LABX and LB2?\",\"Then check the backdrop: inflation's direction and the dots.\",\"Good news lifting stocks without lifting LB2 rules out an inflation fight.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Over several years, which most consistently pushes a currency higher?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Rate and growth differentials moving in its favour\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A rising stock market at home, whatever happens abroad\",\"rationale\":\"Stocks can rise everywhere at once. What matters is the home economy relative to others, and the rate gap.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Higher inflation at home\",\"rationale\":\"Higher inflation erodes a currency's value unless rates rise even faster. The differential is what counts.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A bigger economy\",\"rationale\":\"Size isn't the driver. Growth and rates relative to other economies are.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Money flows toward higher expected returns, so rate and growth differentials drive currencies over time (grade B). In stress, haven and funding demand can dominate.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"rate-differential\",\"dollar-smile\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Growth accelerates in every major economy at once. Volatility is low and commodity prices are rising. US growth is steady but no longer the fastest.\",\"prompt\":\"Where is the world on the dollar smile, and what does LDX tend to do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Left side: LDX rises\",\"rationale\":\"The left side needs the US to outgrow the rest. Here the rest of the world is catching up.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Right side: LDX rises\",\"rationale\":\"The right side is a global crisis. Low volatility and rising commodities describe calm, broad growth.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Bottom: LDX tends to fall\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Off the smile: LDX rises as a haven\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing here calls for a haven. Calm, synchronised growth sends money abroad.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Synchronised global growth is the bottom of the smile: returns abroad improve, money flows out of the dollar, and LDX tends to fall. The smile is a grade C tendency.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"dollar-smile\"],\"hints\":[\"Is this a US boom, broad growth or a crisis?\",\"In calm, broad growth, where does money look for better returns?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Risk-off: LABX falls 6% in two days, yet LDX also falls. What is the most likely reason?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The dollar has lost its haven role for good\",\"rationale\":\"One episode doesn't end a role. A funding-currency jump is a known, temporary exception.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"US growth is accelerating\",\"rationale\":\"A growth spurt wouldn't fit a 6% two-day fall in stocks. The stress points to a carry unwind.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"LDX always falls when stocks fall\",\"rationale\":\"In most crises the dollar has risen. Falling with stocks is the exception, here driven by the funding currency.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A funding currency in LDX's basket is jumping as carry trades unwind\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"When a low-rate funding currency jumps, carry traders who borrowed it must buy it back. If it weighs heavily in LDX's basket, LDX falls even in risk-off. Historical case: the yen in summer 2024.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"dollar-smile\",\"carry-unwind\"],\"hints\":[\"Which currency rises when carry trades close?\",\"Check what's in LDX's basket.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.5-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which pairing of a commodity and its main driver is right?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gold: industrial demand from manufacturing\",\"rationale\":\"That's copper. Gold is driven by real yields, the dollar, haven demand and central-bank buying.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Oil: central-bank buying\",\"rationale\":\"Central banks buy gold, not oil. Oil is dominated by supply shocks and global growth.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Copper: industrial demand, which makes it a growth gauge\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Copper: real yields and haven demand\",\"rationale\":\"That describes gold. Copper tracks building and manufacturing activity.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Oil: supply shocks and growth (grade B). Copper: industrial demand, hence 'Dr. Copper'. Gold: real yields, the dollar, haven demand and official buying, a mix that shifts over time.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"commodities-in-macro\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.5-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A country's main export, iron ore, rises 20% while its import prices stay flat. What happens to its terms of trade, and what does its currency tend to do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"They worsen; the currency tends to fall\",\"rationale\":\"Terms of trade are export prices over import prices. Dearer exports improve them.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: terms of trade depend only on volumes\",\"rationale\":\"Terms of trade are about prices, not volumes. Export prices up 20% with import prices flat: they improve.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"They improve; the currency must rise\",\"rationale\":\"It tends to rise; it doesn't have to. Rate differentials and risk appetite often dominate commodity currencies (grade C).\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They improve; the currency tends to rise, unless rate differentials dominate\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Terms of trade = export prices ÷ import prices, here up 20%. National income rises and foreign buyers need more of the currency, so it tends to strengthen. The link is grade C: rate differentials often matter more.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"commodity-currencies\"],\"hints\":[\"Terms of trade compare two sets of prices.\",\"Is the link to the currency a rule or a tendency?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.5-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Historical case: from 2022, real yields rose well above zero. For years before, gold had tended to fall when real yields rose. This time gold climbed.\",\"prompt\":\"Which explanation fits the evidence best?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Heavy central-bank buying and reserve diversification outweighed the opportunity cost\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Gold has no relationship with real yields at all\",\"rationale\":\"The inverse link held for years (grade B). It broke because another source of demand overwhelmed it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Real yields actually fell in that period\",\"rationale\":\"Real yields rose clearly above zero. That's what makes the break notable.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Gold always rises in years of high inflation\",\"rationale\":\"Gold's record as an inflation hedge is mixed. The cited driver of this break is official buying.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Holding gold costs the real yield you give up, so higher real yields usually weigh on it. From 2022, heavy central-bank buying, linked to reserve diversification after sanctions and to geopolitics, outweighed that cost. Relationships drift when a new buyer arrives.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"commodities-in-macro\",\"real-yield\"],\"hints\":[\"What usually makes gold costlier to hold?\",\"Who else buys gold, besides investors?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.6-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A sudden risk-off shock hits the lab's base regime: volatility doubles and credit spreads widen.\",\"prompt\":\"Which set of first moves is most typical?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Everything falls, including LBND and LGLD\",\"rationale\":\"Forced selling can hit havens briefly, but in the base regime bonds and gold are bought for safety.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"LABX falls; LFX rises as carry looks more attractive\",\"rationale\":\"In stress, carry traders buy back the funding currency, VFUN, so LFX falls.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"LABX, LCOM and LFX fall; LDX, LBND and LGLD rise\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"LDX falls; LCOM rises as a haven\",\"rationale\":\"A commodity currency is high-beta: it falls with risk appetite. In the base regime LDX rises.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Risk assets and high-beta currencies fall; carry unwinds push LFX down; havens such as government bonds, gold and, in the base regime, the dollar rise. These are tendencies (grade B); 3.6.4 and 3.6.8 show the exceptions.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-on-risk-off\"],\"hints\":[\"Sort the assets into risk assets and havens.\",\"What do carry traders do with the funding currency in stress?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.6-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do correlations between risky assets tend to rise toward 1 in a crisis?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Investors suddenly agree on fundamentals\",\"rationale\":\"Fundamentals differ across assets. What's shared is the need to cut risk at the same time.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Correlations never change; crises just make them visible\",\"rationale\":\"Correlations do change, with the dominant shock and with forced flows. That's why diversification fails in stress.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Central banks buy all assets equally\",\"rationale\":\"Central-bank support comes later and isn't uniform. The rise in correlation comes from forced selling.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Forced, simultaneous selling: margin calls, volatility targeting and funding stress\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"In a crisis, leverage, volatility-targeting rules and funding needs force many holders to sell at once. They sell what they can, so assets that usually move apart fall together (grade B: documented, but uneven across crises).\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"risk-on-risk-off\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about who is forced to sell, not who wants to.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.6-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A carry position of 40,000 vUSD earns 3% a year. The funding currency jumps 4% in a day. How many months of carry does that day erase?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":16,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"months\"},\"explanation\":\"Carry: 40,000 × 3% = 1,200 a year. Loss: 40,000 × 4% = 1,600. 1,600 ÷ 1,200 = 1.33 years = 16 months. The position size cancels out: months = jump ÷ carry rate × 12.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"carry-trade\"],\"hints\":[\"Compute a year of carry and the one-day loss.\",\"Months lost = loss ÷ yearly carry × 12.\",\"1,600 ÷ 1,200 × 12 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"pos\":[20000,100000,5000],\"r\":[1,6,0.5],\"j\":[2,10,1]},\"answer\":\"round(j / r * 12, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A carry position of {pos} vUSD earns {r:1}% a year. The funding currency jumps {j}% in a day. How many months of carry does that day erase?\",\"explanation\":\"Carry: {pos} × {r:1}% = {pos * r / 100:2} a year. Loss: {pos} × {j}% = {pos * j / 100:2}. Months = {pos * j / 100:2} ÷ {pos * r / 100:2} × 12 = {answer:1}.\",\"hint\":\"{pos * j / 100:2} ÷ {pos * r / 100:2} × 12 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.7-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"PMI new orders are 47 but up 5 points in two months. Jobless claims are falling from a peak, the curve is steep and credit spreads are narrowing from wide levels. Which phase fits best?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Mid\",\"rationale\":\"Mid-cycle has new orders steadily above 50 and spreads already tight. Here orders are below 50 but rebounding fast.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Late\",\"rationale\":\"Late cycle has orders falling from high levels and spreads starting to widen. These readings are improving from lows.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Recession\",\"rationale\":\"Orders below 50 fit, but everything has turned up from its worst. That's the start of the early phase.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Early\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Rebounding orders, falling claims, a steep curve and narrowing spreads are the textbook early phase. In real time, though, phase calls are grade C: wait for several readings to agree.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"business-cycle\"],\"hints\":[\"Look at the direction of each indicator, not only its level.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.7-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In the textbook map, which sectors lead in the late phase, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Technology: steady growth rewards long-run investment\",\"rationale\":\"That's the textbook mid-cycle leader.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Energy and materials: commodity prices tend to peak as capacity runs tight\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Staples and utilities: demand for essentials holds up\",\"rationale\":\"Defensive sectors are the textbook recession leaders.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Financials and discretionary: cheap money revives lending and spending\",\"rationale\":\"That's the textbook early phase, after rates have been cut.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Late in the cycle, demand presses on tight capacity and commodity prices tend to peak, which favours energy and materials. It's a textbook map; the evidence that trading it pays is weak (grade C).\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"sector-rotation\"],\"hints\":[\"What tends to be scarce, and pricey, late in an expansion?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.7-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A backtest shows sector rotation beating buy-and-hold by 2.5 points a year. The phases were dated using data published years later. Each switch costs 0.30% round trip, with about four switches a year.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the main caveat?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"None: 2.5 points a year is a robust edge\",\"rationale\":\"The phases were dated with hindsight. Real-time calls come late, and costs come off the top.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The costs alone wipe out the edge\",\"rationale\":\"Costs take about 1.2 of the 2.5 points. The hindsight dating is the bigger problem.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Phase dating used hindsight; after 1.2 points of costs and late calls, little edge may remain\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Rotation fails because sectors don't differ across the cycle\",\"rationale\":\"Sector leadership does shift. The problem is that you can't date the phases in real time.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Four switches at 0.30% cost 1.2 points a year. More importantly, phases dated afterwards are recognised only late in real time. Research finds the modest edge mostly disappears after costs and timing errors (grade C).\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\",\"concepts\":[\"sector-rotation\",\"business-cycle\"],\"hints\":[\"When were the phases known: at the time, or later?\",\"Compute the yearly costs: 4 × 0.30%.\",\"Then ask what late phase calls would do to the rest.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.8-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your 20-day rolling correlation between LABX and LBND reads +0.35 today, up from −0.10 a week ago. Over the past year it averaged −0.40.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you read it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The correlation has flipped to +0.35 for good: re-hedge now\",\"rationale\":\"With 20 observations, readings within about ±0.44 of zero are common by chance. One reading proves little.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It rests on 20 pairs of daily returns, so +0.35 is within the noise band around zero: check a longer window and the news flow\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It rests on a year of data, so it's reliable\",\"rationale\":\"A 20-day window uses only the last 20 days. The yearly average is a different number.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Ignore it: rolling correlations carry no information\",\"rationale\":\"They carry information, with noise. A rising reading is a reason to check, not to ignore.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A 20-day rolling correlation uses 20 pairs of returns. At a true correlation of zero, 95% of such readings fall within about ±0.44, so +0.35 alone isn't proof. Check a longer window and the dominant shock, and size for correlation 1 meanwhile.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.8\",\"concepts\":[\"rolling-correlation\"],\"hints\":[\"How many observations sit behind a 20-day correlation?\",\"What range does chance alone produce with 20 pairs?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.8-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which pairing of a historical correlation break and its cause is right?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stocks and bonds, 2022: inflation became the dominant shock, so both fell together\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Gold and real yields, 2022–24: real yields fell, so gold rose\",\"rationale\":\"Real yields rose clearly. Gold climbed anyway, as central banks bought heavily.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The yen's haven role: Japan raised rates far above other countries'\",\"rationale\":\"The reverse: Japan's rates stayed far below others'. Wide rate gaps made the yen a funding currency and weakened its haven role.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stocks and bonds, 2022: a deep recession pushed both down\",\"rationale\":\"The driver was inflation and fast rate hikes, not a deep recession.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Correlations follow the dominant shock. In 2022 inflation and hikes pushed stocks and bonds down together. Gold and real yields broke on official buying. The yen's haven role weakened while its rate gap was wide.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.8\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-instability\"],\"hints\":[\"For each, ask what the dominant shock, or the new buyer, was.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.6.8-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your account is 20,000.00 vUSD. In stress you assume both legs of your hedged book fall 8% together, and you accept at most a 2% account loss. What is the largest total value of the two positions?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":5000,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Maximum loss = 2% × 20,000.00 = 400.00. With correlation 1 the book falls 8% as one: 400.00 ÷ 8% = 5,000.00 in total, 2,500.00 per leg if split evenly.\",\"lesson\":\"3.6.8\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-instability\",\"exposure\"],\"hints\":[\"Treat both legs as one position.\",\"Maximum loss = 2% of the account.\",\"400.00 ÷ 8% = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"acc\":[5000,50000,5000],\"f\":[5,20,1],\"L\":[1,3,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(acc * L / f, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your account is {acc:2} vUSD. In stress you assume both legs of your hedged book fall {f}% together, and you accept at most a {L:1}% account loss. What is the largest total value of the two positions?\",\"explanation\":\"Maximum loss = {L:1}% × {acc:2} = {acc * L / 100:2}. With correlation 1 the book falls {f}% as one: {acc * L / 100:2} ÷ {f}% = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{acc * L / 100:2} ÷ {f}% = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The LCB's rule: 2.00 + inflation + 0.5 × (inflation − 2.00) − 0.5 × (unemployment − 4.50). Inflation is 2.20%, unemployment 5.30%. What rate does the rule prescribe, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3.9,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"2.00 + 2.20 = 4.20. Inflation gap: +0.5 × 0.20 = +0.10. Unemployment gap: −0.5 × 0.80 = −0.40. Prescribed rate: 3.90%. Slack in the job market outweighs slightly high inflation.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"reaction-function\"],\"hints\":[\"Start from 2.00 + inflation.\",\"Add half the inflation gap; subtract half the unemployment gap.\",\"4.20 + 0.10 − 0.40 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"pi\":[2,4.5,0.1],\"u\":[4.5,6.5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(2 + pi + 0.5 * (pi - 2) - 0.5 * (u - 4.5), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"The LCB's rule: 2.00 + inflation + 0.5 × (inflation − 2.00) − 0.5 × (unemployment − 4.50). Inflation is {pi:2}%, unemployment {u:2}%. What rate does the rule prescribe, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"2.00 + {pi:2} = {2 + pi:2}. Add half the inflation gap, 0.5 × ({pi:2} − 2.00) = {0.5 * (pi - 2):2}. Subtract half the unemployment gap, 0.5 × ({u:2} − 4.50) = {0.5 * (u - 4.5):2}. Prescribed rate: {answer:2}%.\",\"hint\":\"{2 + pi:2} + {0.5 * (pi - 2):2} − {0.5 * (u - 4.5):2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The LCB signals that it will now react more strongly to unemployment and less to inflation. No new data has come out. What should reprice?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing until the next data release\",\"rationale\":\"The rule itself is news. Markets reprice expected rates as soon as they see the reaction function change, before any new data.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only the next meeting's expected decision\",\"rationale\":\"The rule applies at every meeting, so the whole expected path shifts, and the 2-year yield with it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The whole expected path of rates, and yields like LB2 with it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only long-term yields, because the rule is about the long run\",\"rationale\":\"The rule sets each policy rate. Short yields such as LB2 track the expected path most directly.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A reaction function applies at every meeting. New weights change the expected rate at each future meeting, so the whole path reprices, and the 2-year yield, roughly the path's average, moves at once.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"reaction-function\",\"expected-policy-path\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask which meetings the rule applies to.\",\"LB2 tracks the average expected rate over two years.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which central bank has a dual mandate: maximum employment and stable prices?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The ECB\",\"rationale\":\"The ECB's primary objective is price stability; other goals come second.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The Bank of England\",\"rationale\":\"The Bank of England puts price stability first, with an inflation target set by the government.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The Bank of Japan\",\"rationale\":\"The Bank of Japan's mandate is price stability, with a 2% target.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The Fed\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The Fed's mandate names two goals, maximum employment and stable prices, with a 2% inflation target. The ECB, the Bank of England and the Bank of Japan put price stability first. A mandate tells you which data a reaction function weighs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.1\",\"concepts\":[\"reaction-function\",\"central-bank-decisions\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"An LRF contract trades at 95.62. What average policy rate does it imply, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4.38,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Rate futures are quoted as 100 minus the average rate: 100 − 95.62 = 4.38%. A lower price means a higher expected rate.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-policy-probability\"],\"hints\":[\"Rate futures are quoted as 100 minus the rate.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"price\":[94.5,97.5,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(100 - price, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An LRF contract trades at {price:2}. What average policy rate does it imply, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"100 − {price:2} = {answer:2}%. A lower price means a higher expected rate.\",\"hint\":\"100 − {price:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"LRF trades at 95.96. The rate is 4.00%; the meeting is on day 11 of a 30-day month, and the new rate applies from that day. What is the implied chance of a 25 bp hike, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":24,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Average: 100 − 95.96 = 4.04. Rate-days: 4.04 × 30 = 121.2. Days 1–10 at 4.00: 40. New rate: (121.2 − 40) ÷ 20 = 4.06. Probability: 0.06 ÷ 0.25 = 24%. The naive formula would give 16%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-policy-probability\"],\"hints\":[\"Implied average = 100 − price. Multiply by 30 for the month's rate-days.\",\"Take out the days before the meeting at 4.00, then divide by the days after it.\",\"(121.2 − 40) ÷ 20 = 4.06. Now (4.06 − 4.00) ÷ 0.25.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"price\":[95.9,96,0.01],\"d\":[11,21,5]},\"constraints\":[\"(((100 - price) * 30 - (d - 1) * 4) / (31 - d) - 4) * 400 >= 5\",\"(((100 - price) * 30 - (d - 1) * 4) / (31 - d) - 4) * 400 <= 95\"],\"answer\":\"round((((100 - price) * 30 - (d - 1) * 4) / (31 - d) - 4) * 400, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"LRF trades at {price:2}. The rate is 4.00%; the meeting is on day {d} of a 30-day month, and the new rate applies from that day. What is the implied chance of a 25 bp hike, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Average: 100 − {price:2} = {100 - price:2}. Rate-days: {(100 - price) * 30:2}. Days before the meeting: {d - 1} × 4.00 = {(d - 1) * 4:2}. New rate: ({(100 - price) * 30:2} − {(d - 1) * 4:2}) ÷ {31 - d} = {((100 - price) * 30 - (d - 1) * 4) / (31 - d):3}. Probability: {answer:0}%.\",\"hint\":\"r = ({(100 - price) * 30:2} − {(d - 1) * 4:2}) ÷ {31 - d}. Then (r − 4.00) ÷ 0.25.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Rate futures imply a 60% chance of a hike at the next LCB meeting. What does the 60% tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"60% of economists expect a hike\",\"rationale\":\"It isn't a survey. It comes from futures prices, set by traders with money at stake and different reasons to trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A market price that includes risk premia; your edge is only where your view differs\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The hike will happen, since 60% is above 50%\",\"rationale\":\"60% leaves a 40% chance of no hike. A probability above 50% is not a certainty.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The chance the LCB has already decided to hike\",\"rationale\":\"The committee decides at the meeting. The 60% is the market's priced view, not inside information.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Implied probabilities are backed out of prices. They mix expectations with risk premia and can swing late. They tell you what's priced, so a trade only makes sense where your own view differs from it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-policy-probability\"],\"hints\":[\"Where does the number come from: a survey or a price?\",\"What else, besides expectations, is in a price?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Last statement: 'Some further firming may be appropriate. The risks are balanced. Spending grew moderately.' New statement: 'Further firming may be appropriate. The risks are balanced. Spending continued to grow moderately.'\",\"prompt\":\"Which edit carries the policy signal?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"'Spending continued to grow moderately': it adds 'continued'\",\"rationale\":\"That restates the same data. 'Continued' says nothing new about policy: it scores 0.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"None: the risks sentence didn't change, so the tone didn't\",\"rationale\":\"One unchanged sentence doesn't make the text unchanged. Dropping 'some' firms up the bias toward hikes.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Dropping 'some' before 'further firming'\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Both edits count equally\",\"rationale\":\"Only one edit is about policy. The spending line is a description of data, scored 0.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Dropping 'some' turns a hedged hint of further hikes into a firmer one: a hawkish shift in strength, +1 on this lesson's scale. 'Continued to grow' restates the data. Read each edit for what it says about the next move.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"policy-communication\"],\"hints\":[\"Which edit is about the bank's next move, and which about data?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The LCB holds rates. The vote is 7–2, with both dissenters wanting a cut. The market had expected a unanimous hold. What does the split suggest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The next move is now more likely a cut\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the decision was a hold, as expected\",\"rationale\":\"The decision matched, but the vote adds information: two members already want lower rates.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The next move is now more likely a hike\",\"rationale\":\"The dissents were for a cut. Dissents tilt the next move toward the dissenters' side.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The bank has lost credibility, so its currency must rise\",\"rationale\":\"Dissent is normal committee debate. Two votes for a cut read dovish, which tends to weigh on the currency, not lift it.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Dissents show the spread of views. Two votes for a cut, when none was expected, tilt the expected path lower: a dovish surprise, even though the decision itself was as priced.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"policy-communication\",\"central-bank-decisions\"],\"hints\":[\"Which way did the dissenters want to move?\",\"Compare the vote with what was expected.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"The market had priced a strongly hawkish LCB statement, a tone of +2. The statement scores +1: it keeps the bias toward hikes but drops some of the urgency.\",\"prompt\":\"What is LB2's most likely first reaction?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Up: the statement is hawkish\",\"rationale\":\"It's hawkish in absolute terms, but less than priced. Price moves on tone minus priced tone: −1.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No move: hawkish was expected, and hawkish came\",\"rationale\":\"The market expected +2 and got +1. That gap is a surprise, and LB2 trades it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Up, then down when the minutes come out\",\"rationale\":\"The first reaction follows the gap to what was priced, and that gap is dovish. The minutes come weeks later.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Down: it's less hawkish than priced\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Markets react to tone relative to what was expected. +1 against +2 priced is −1: a dovish surprise, so yields tend to fall even though the text still leans hawkish.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\",\"concepts\":[\"policy-communication\",\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"Subtract the priced tone from the statement's tone.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Nineteen dots: 1 at 3.75%, 5 at 4.00%, 4 at 4.25%, 6 at 4.50% and 3 at 4.75%. What is the median, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4.25,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Count up to the 10th dot: 1 by 3.75%, 6 by 4.00%, 10 by 4.25%. The 10th dot is the last one at 4.25%, so the median is 4.25%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"dot-plot\"],\"hints\":[\"With 19 dots, the median is the 10th.\",\"Add up the counts from the lowest level until you reach 10.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[0,4,1],\"b\":[2,7,1],\"c\":[1,5,1],\"d\":[2,7,1]},\"constraints\":[\"a + b + c + d <= 19\"],\"answer\":\"3.75 + 0.25 * ((a < 10) + (a + b < 10) + (a + b + c < 10) + (a + b + c + d < 10))\",\"prompt\":\"Nineteen dots: {a} at 3.75%, {b} at 4.00%, {c} at 4.25%, {d} at 4.50% and {19 - a - b - c - d} at 4.75%. What is the median, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Count up to the 10th dot: {a} by 3.75%, {a + b} by 4.00%, {a + b + c} by 4.25%, {a + b + c + d} by 4.50%. The 10th dot sits at {answer:2}%.\",\"hint\":\"Running totals: {a}, {a + b}, {a + b + c}, {a + b + c + d}. Where does the 10th dot fall?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LRF prices a year-end rate of 4.25%. The LCB's new dot plot moves the median from 4.25% to 4.50%. What is the likely reaction?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Little: the dots matched what was priced\",\"rationale\":\"That holds only when the priced path matches the dots. Here the market priced 4.25%, so 4.50% is new.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"LB2 falls: higher dots mean slower growth\",\"rationale\":\"Higher projected rates push short yields up, not down. The 25 bp gap to the priced path is hawkish.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"LB2 rises: the dots add 25 bp the market hadn't priced\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"None: dots are forecasts, not promises\",\"rationale\":\"They aren't promises, but they shift expectations. A median 25 bp above the priced path still moves LB2.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Dots move prices through their gap to the priced path. Futures priced 4.25%; the new median says 4.50%: a hawkish 25 bp surprise, so LB2 tends to rise.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"dot-plot\",\"expected-policy-path\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the new median with what futures had priced.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"'Rates will stay at this level until unemployment falls below 5.00%.' What kind of forward guidance is this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Calendar guidance\",\"rationale\":\"Calendar guidance names a date. This one names a level in the data.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Threshold guidance\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Qualitative guidance\",\"rationale\":\"Qualitative guidance uses words like 'patient' or 'data dependent', with no number to cross.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A promise the bank must keep\",\"rationale\":\"Guidance is conditional, not a promise. Banks can abandon it, and markets reprice hard when they do.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Threshold guidance ties policy to a level in the data: here, unemployment below 5.00%. Calendar guidance names a date; qualitative guidance uses words. None of them is a promise.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\",\"concepts\":[\"forward-guidance\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.5-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Through which channels does QE mainly lower long-term yields, and why isn't QT its mirror image?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Mostly by cutting the policy rate; QT raises it again\",\"rationale\":\"QE buys bonds; it doesn't set the policy rate. It works through the term premium and by signalling the path.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Mostly through the term premium and signalling; QT is gradual and pre-announced, with smaller, disputed effects\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Through inflation, which QE raises at once and QT lowers at once\",\"rationale\":\"Any inflation effect is slow and uncertain. The fast channel is bond prices: the term premium and signals about rates.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"QE and QT have equal and opposite effects, by construction\",\"rationale\":\"Estimates find QT's effects smaller and disputed: runoff is slow, announced in advance, and arrives in different conditions.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"QE takes long bonds off the market, so investors accept a lower term premium, and it signals that rates will stay low. QT runs off slowly and predictably, so its estimated effects are smaller and disputed.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"qe-qt\"],\"hints\":[\"Split a long yield into the expected path and the term premium.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.5-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"The LCB caps LB10 at 1.00%. LBND, a 10-year zero-coupon bond, sits at 90.53. A trader holds 200 units with a stop at 90.20, sure that the cap limits the downside.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the main risk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The stop fills at 90.20, so the loss stays small\",\"rationale\":\"A stop fills at the first available price. An exit gaps LBND past 90.20 with nothing trading in between.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"None: the cap holds forever\",\"rationale\":\"Caps end, and holding them gets more expensive for the bank as pressure builds.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The term premium rises slowly, giving the stop time to work\",\"rationale\":\"Under a cap, pressure builds out of sight and releases at once. The exit is a jump, not a drift.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"An exit can gap LBND far below the stop: to 86.17 if LB10 jumps to 1.50%\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A cap stores pressure. When the bank exits, the yield can jump straight to where the market wants it. At 1.50%, LBND is worth 86.17, 4.36 a unit below the cap: 872.00 on 200 units before costs, far past the stop.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"yield-curve-control\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Where does a stop fill when price gaps?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.5-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Officials say: 'We stand ready to take decisive action.' Where does this sit on the usual ladder of warnings before currency intervention?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The first step: they are watching\",\"rationale\":\"Watching is the mildest step. 'Decisive action' is the strongest wording before acting.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The last step before intervention\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The second step: naming moves as one-sided\",\"rationale\":\"Naming the problem comes earlier. Readiness for decisive action signals intervention may be close.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Not a warning: it means no action\",\"rationale\":\"It's the strongest of the usual warnings. After it, intervention can come at any time.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Officials usually escalate: watching closely, then naming moves as one-sided, then not ruling out options, then readiness for decisive action. The last step means intervention may be close, so size any exposed position for a sudden jump.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"concepts\":[\"fx-intervention\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A straddle on 80 units: the buy stop at 100.40 fills at 100.50 on the spike, and the protective stop at 100.00 fills at 99.85 on the reversal. Commission 0.05% per fill. What do you lose, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":60.02,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Price: 80 × (100.50 − 99.85) = 52.00. Commissions: 4.02 on 8,040.00 and 4.00 on 7,988.00. Loss: 52.00 + 4.02 + 4.00 = 60.02.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"news-straddle\",\"slippage\"],\"hints\":[\"Loss on price = units × (entry fill − exit fill).\",\"Commission is 0.05% of each fill's value, rounded up to the cent.\",\"52.00 + 4.02 + 4.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"units\":[50,150,10],\"s\":[0.05,0.3,0.05],\"e\":[0.05,0.2,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(units * (0.4 + s + e) + commission(units * (100.4 + s)) + commission(units * (100 - e)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A straddle on {units} units: the buy stop at 100.40 fills at {100.4 + s:2} on the spike, and the protective stop at 100.00 fills at {100 - e:2} on the reversal. Commission 0.05% per fill. What do you lose, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Price: {units} × ({100.4 + s:2} − {100 - e:2}) = {units * (0.4 + s + e):2}. Commissions: {commission(units * (100.4 + s)):2} + {commission(units * (100 - e)):2}. Loss: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{units * (0.4 + s + e):2} + {commission(units * (100.4 + s)):2} + {commission(units * (100 - e)):2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In this course's simulator, when did fading the first spike pay after costs, and how does the evidence grade spike fading in real markets?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"After big surprises; grade A\",\"rationale\":\"In the simulator, big clean surprises kept going and fades lost. And spike fading is practitioner lore, not an A-grade finding.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Always, since spikes overshoot; grade B\",\"rationale\":\"Fades of large, clean surprises lost in the simulator. Real-market evidence for fading is weak: D.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After small surprises with conflicting details; grade D in real markets\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Never; grade F\",\"rationale\":\"In this generator fades paid in one narrow case. The real-market evidence is weak, D, not proven false.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The simulator's hidden rule let fades pay only when |z| was under 1 and the details disagreed. That's a design setting, not a fact. In real markets, fading spikes is practitioner lore: evidence grade D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"spike-fade\"],\"hints\":[\"Recall the generator's hidden rule.\",\"A simulator result isn't a fact about real markets.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"On the same 30 synthetic events, after costs: flat 0R, reduce +1.2R, second move +2.4R, fade −1.8R, surprise trade −3.6R, straddle −6.3R. The second move's results per event vary with a standard deviation of 1.10R.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the soundest conclusion?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The second move has an edge: adopt it at full size\",\"rationale\":\"+0.08R per event, with a standard error of 0.20R, is well inside the noise. 30 events can't separate it from flat.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The second move did best, but its interval includes zero: keep testing on more events\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Straddles will win next time: they're due\",\"rationale\":\"Past losses don't make a win due. The straddle paid the news spread twice on every event.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing can be learned from 30 events\",\"rationale\":\"The run shows a lot: what costs do to each policy, and which ones lost heavily. It just can't prove a small edge.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Expectancy: 2.4 ÷ 30 = 0.08R. Standard error: 1.10 ÷ √30 = 0.20R. The 95% interval runs from about −0.32R to +0.48R and includes zero. The run ranks the costs clearly, but a small edge needs many more events.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.6\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy\",\"news-policy\"],\"hints\":[\"Compute the expectancy per event and its standard error.\",\"Does the 95% interval exclude zero?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.7-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What did the original pre-announcement drift study find, and what should you expect after publication?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A 49 bp average loss after each announcement; expect it to grow\",\"rationale\":\"The finding was a gain before scheduled announcements, not a loss after. Published anomalies tend to shrink, not grow.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A gain of 49% a year from trading every meeting; expect it to last\",\"rationale\":\"The 49 was basis points in the day before each announcement, not % a year. And published effects tend to fade.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A 49 bp average gain in the 24 hours before scheduled Fed announcements; expect it to weaken\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No effect at all in stocks\",\"rationale\":\"The study did find a sizeable effect in stocks. The open questions are its cause, and its size after publication.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"In the original sample, a broad US stock index rose 49 bp on average in the 24 hours before scheduled Fed announcements. Published predictors have tended to earn much less after publication, so expect a famous drift to weaken.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"pre-event-drift\"],\"hints\":[\"The effect was measured in basis points, in the day before scheduled announcements.\",\"What tends to happen to an anomaly once everyone can read about it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.7-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A synthetic drift of +0.20% per event, with a standard deviation of 1.50%. How many events do you need for t = 2?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":225,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"events\"},\"explanation\":\"t = drift × √N ÷ SD. For t = 2: √N = 2 × 1.50 ÷ 0.20 = 15, so N = 15 × 15 = 225.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"sample-size\",\"pre-event-drift\"],\"hints\":[\"t = drift × √N ÷ SD. Solve for √N with t = 2.\",\"√N = 2 × 1.50 ÷ 0.20 = 15. Square it.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"drift\":[0.05,0.25,0.05],\"sd\":[0.5,2,0.25]},\"constraints\":[\"2 * sd / drift >= 4\"],\"answer\":\"ceil(round(2 * sd / drift * (2 * sd / drift), 6))\",\"prompt\":\"A synthetic drift of +{drift:2}% per event, with a standard deviation of {sd:2}%. How many events do you need for t = 2?\",\"explanation\":\"t = drift × √N ÷ SD. For t = 2: √N = 2 × {sd:2} ÷ {drift:2} = {2 * sd / drift:2}. Square it and round up to whole events: N = {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"√N = 2 × {sd:2} ÷ {drift:2}. Square it, and round up.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.7-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over a week, LABX falls on three pieces of good news and drops sharply on two bad ones. What is the most likely reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Washed out: the sellers are exhausted\",\"rationale\":\"A washed-out market shrugs off bad news. This one can't even rally on good news.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Heavy: positioning is crowded long\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing: reactions carry no information\",\"rationale\":\"One reaction says little, but a consistent pattern is a hint: weak evidence, D, worth checking against positioning data.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Bullish: good news always wins in the end\",\"rationale\":\"Good news that fails to lift price suggests buyers are already in. That's a heavy market, not a bullish one.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"When good news can't lift price and bad news hits hard, buyers are likely already positioned and sellers are waiting: a heavy market. It's a discretionary tell, evidence D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.7\",\"concepts\":[\"market-tell\"],\"hints\":[\"What does it mean when good news can't lift price?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.8-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What happened to liquidity and stop orders when the Swiss National Bank removed its EUR/CHF floor in 2015?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Brokers filled every stop at its level, as promised\",\"rationale\":\"Ordinary stops promise a trigger, not a price. With no quotes near the floor, they filled far beyond their levels.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Quotes vanished, the rate fell far below the floor within minutes, and stops filled far beyond their levels\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The rate drifted down slowly over several days\",\"rationale\":\"The move came within minutes of the announcement, with almost no prices in between.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the floor was removed while markets were closed\",\"rationale\":\"It came during trading hours, without warning, and the rate gapped at once.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"When the floor went, liquidity vanished. EUR/CHF fell far below 1.20 within minutes, stops became market orders that filled far beyond their levels, some traders ended up owing money, and at least one retail broker failed. The 2010 Flash Crash showed the same mechanism in stocks.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.8\",\"concepts\":[\"tail-event\",\"liquidity-vacuum\"],\"hints\":[\"A triggered stop becomes a market order. Where does it fill when no one is quoting near it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.8-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A pegged pair trades at 130.00. You plan for a 10% gap. Your budget is 200.00 vUSD, and commission is 0.05% per fill. What is the most whole units you can hold?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":15,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Gap exit: 130.00 × 0.90 = 117.00, a loss of 13.00 a unit. 15 units lose 195.00 + 0.98 + 0.88 = 196.86; 16 would lose 208.00 before commissions, over 200.00.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.8\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-risk\",\"position-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"The gap exit is the price × (1 − gap).\",\"Each unit loses the gap, plus a little commission on both fills.\",\"200.00 ÷ 13.00 is just over 15. Check 15 with costs.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[100,150,1],\"g\":[10,20,5],\"b\":[100,300,50]},\"constraints\":[\"floor(b / (e * g / 100 + (2 * e - e * g / 100) * 0.0005)) * e * g / 100 + commission(floor(b / (e * g / 100 + (2 * e - e * g / 100) * 0.0005)) * e) + commission(floor(b / (e * g / 100 + (2 * e - e * g / 100) * 0.0005)) * (e - e * g / 100)) <= b\"],\"answer\":\"floor(b / (e * g / 100 + (2 * e - e * g / 100) * 0.0005))\",\"prompt\":\"A pegged pair trades at {e:2}. You plan for a {g}% gap. Your budget is {b:2} vUSD, and commission is 0.05% per fill. What is the most whole units you can hold?\",\"explanation\":\"Gap exit: {e:2} × {1 - g / 100:2} = {e - e * g / 100:2}, a loss of {e * g / 100:2} a unit, plus commissions. {answer} units fit within {b:2}; one more would not.\",\"hint\":\"{b:2} ÷ {e * g / 100:2} = {b / (e * g / 100):2}. Round down, then check the commissions.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.7.8-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On a leveraged account of 10,000.00, a gap costs 14,560.00. What does negative balance protection change?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It refunds the loss\",\"rationale\":\"It refunds nothing. It stops the loss at your deposit: the 10,000.00 is still gone.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It fills your stop at its level\",\"rationale\":\"That's a guaranteed stop, a different product with a fee. Balance protection only stops the balance going below zero.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It lowers your leverage automatically\",\"rationale\":\"It doesn't limit leverage. Only your own cap, or the broker's margin rules, do that.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It caps the loss at the 10,000.00 deposit, so you don't owe the broker 4,560.00\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Without protection, the balance would be −4,560.00: a debt to the broker. With it, the loss stops at the deposit. Either way the account is wiped out: the protection is a backstop, not a risk rule.\",\"lesson\":\"3.7.8\",\"concepts\":[\"negative-balance-protection\",\"leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the loss with the deposit: 14,560.00 against 10,000.00.\",\"What would the balance be without protection?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABA's operating cash flow for the year is 140m vUSD and its capex is 55m. What is its free cash flow, in millions?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":85,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"m\"},\"explanation\":\"Free cash flow = operating cash flow − capex = 140 − 55 = 85m: the cash left after the business has paid for its own investment.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"free-cash-flow\"],\"hints\":[\"Free cash flow is what's left after the company invests in itself.\",\"FCF = operating cash flow − capex.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"ocf\":[50,300,5],\"capex\":[10,120,5]},\"constraints\":[\"capex < ocf\"],\"answer\":\"ocf - capex\",\"prompt\":\"LABA's operating cash flow for the year is {ocf}m vUSD and its capex is {capex}m. What is its free cash flow, in millions?\",\"explanation\":\"Free cash flow = operating cash flow − capex = {ocf} − {capex} = {answer}m: the cash left after the business has paid for its own investment.\",\"hint\":\"FCF = {ocf} − {capex}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABA earns net income of 30m and pays no dividend. Where does that 30m show up on its balance sheet?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Cash rises by exactly 30m\",\"rationale\":\"Profit isn't cash. Receivables, inventory and capex decide how much cash actually arrives.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Retained earnings, part of shareholders' equity, rise by 30m\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Receivables rise by 30m\",\"rationale\":\"Receivables rise only by sales not yet paid for. That's one reason cash differs from profit, not where profit goes.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Liabilities rise by 30m\",\"rationale\":\"Profit belongs to the owners. It adds to equity, not to what the company owes.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Net income that isn't paid out accumulates in retained earnings, inside equity. The assets that match it can be cash, receivables, inventory or machines: that's why the balance sheet balances while cash moves differently.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"financial-statements\"],\"hints\":[\"Who does a company's profit belong to?\",\"Profit not paid out is kept: retained.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABE's net income has risen for three quarters in a row: 20m, 24m, 29m. Over the same quarters its operating cash flow fell: 18m, 9m, −4m.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely explanation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Large depreciation charges\",\"rationale\":\"Depreciation lowers profit, not cash, and it's added back in operating cash flow. It can't push cash below a rising profit.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A share buyback\",\"rationale\":\"A buyback is a financing cash flow. It reduces cash, but not operating cash flow.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Receivables and inventory growing, so sales and stock tie up cash\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Lower interest costs\",\"rationale\":\"Lower interest raises both profit and operating cash flow. It can't make them move apart.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"When profit rises but operating cash flow falls, working capital is usually absorbing the cash: sales booked but unpaid, goods bought but unsold. It's the first line to check in the cash-flow statement, and an early sign of weak earnings quality.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"concepts\":[\"financial-statements\",\"free-cash-flow\"],\"hints\":[\"Which items in operating cash flow can make cash fall while profit rises?\",\"Look at the working-capital lines: receivables and inventory.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABA's net income stays at 50m vUSD. A buyback cuts its shares from 25m to 24m. What is its EPS after the buyback, to 2 decimals?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2.08,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"EPS = 50 ÷ 24 = 2.083, up from 50 ÷ 25 = 2.00: +4.2% with zero business growth. Same profit, fewer shares.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"EPS = net income ÷ shares, after the buyback.\",\"50 ÷ 24 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"ni\":[20,200,5],\"shares\":[10,80,1],\"cut\":[1,5,1]},\"constraints\":[\"cut * 4 < shares\"],\"answer\":\"round(ni / (shares - cut), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABA's net income stays at {ni}m vUSD. A buyback cuts its shares from {shares}m to {shares - cut}m. What is its EPS after the buyback, to 2 decimals?\",\"explanation\":\"EPS = {ni} ÷ {shares - cut} = {ni / (shares - cut):3}, up from {ni} ÷ {shares} = {ni / shares:2}: +{(shares / (shares - cut) - 1) * 100:1}% with zero business growth. Same profit, fewer shares.\",\"hint\":\"{ni} ÷ {shares - cut} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A friend says: 'Adjusted EPS is the real number; GAAP EPS is just accounting noise.' What's wrong with that?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: adjusted EPS removes one-off items, so it shows the true run rate\",\"rationale\":\"Only if the items really are one-off. Companies choose the exclusions, and many come back every year.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"GAAP EPS always includes buybacks, so it's always higher\",\"rationale\":\"Buybacks affect both through the share count. And GAAP EPS is usually lower than adjusted, not higher.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Adjusted EPS is audited more strictly than GAAP EPS\",\"rationale\":\"It's the reverse: GAAP figures follow the accounting rules; adjusted figures are the company's own definition.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The company chooses what adjusted EPS leaves out, and recurring costs or share-based pay are still real costs\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Adjusted EPS is a company-defined number. It can be useful, but read what it excludes: share-based pay is paid in new shares, and 'one-off' costs that recur every year are part of running the business.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Who decides what adjusted EPS excludes?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABD reports ROE of 25%, net debt of 5× EBITDA and interest coverage of 1.8×.\",\"prompt\":\"What do the three ratios together signal?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Borrowed profitability: the balance sheet is fragile if rates rise or earnings dip\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A high-quality business: 25% ROE is excellent\",\"rationale\":\"ROE alone can't tell skill from leverage. With debt at 5× EBITDA, much of that 25% is borrowed.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A cheap stock: high ROE and debt mean a low valuation\",\"rationale\":\"These are quality and risk ratios, not valuation. Nothing here says what the price is.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing worrying: coverage above 1 means the debt is safe\",\"rationale\":\"1.8× leaves little room. A 45% fall in operating profit would leave too little to pay the interest.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"High ROE with high net debt/EBITDA and thin coverage means the return rests on borrowed money. If rates rise or profit dips, interest eats the earnings first. Leverage shows in net debt/EBITDA and coverage, not in ROE.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-quality\"],\"hints\":[\"Which of the three ratios measure debt?\",\"Coverage of 1.8× means operating profit is under twice the interest bill.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABD: market cap 2,400m vUSD, debt 900m, cash 300m, EBITDA 300m. What is its EV/EBITDA, to 1 decimal?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"EV = market cap + debt − cash = 2,400 + 900 − 300 = 3,000m. EV/EBITDA = 3,000 ÷ 300 = 10×.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"valuation-multiples\"],\"hints\":[\"EV = market cap + net debt.\",\"Net debt = 900 − 300 = 600m.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"cap\":[1000,6000,100],\"debt\":[0,2000,100],\"cash\":[0,1000,50],\"ebitda\":[100,800,20]},\"constraints\":[\"cap + debt - cash > ebitda * 3\"],\"answer\":\"round((cap + debt - cash) / ebitda, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"LABD: market cap {cap}m vUSD, debt {debt}m, cash {cash}m, EBITDA {ebitda}m. What is its EV/EBITDA, to 1 decimal?\",\"explanation\":\"EV = market cap + debt − cash = {cap} + {debt} − {cash} = {cap + debt - cash}m. EV/EBITDA = {cap + debt - cash} ÷ {ebitda} = {answer:1}×.\",\"hint\":\"Net debt = {debt} − {cash} = {debt - cash}m.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABE trades at a P/E of 6, against 18 for its sector. Why isn't that automatically a sign it's undervalued?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because P/E ignores the share price\",\"rationale\":\"P/E is the share price divided by EPS. It ignores debt, not price.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because a low P/E always means profits are about to rise\",\"rationale\":\"A low P/E often means the market expects profits to fall, not rise.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A low P/E can reflect slow growth, high risk, heavy debt or falling profits, so the price may be right\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It is automatically undervalued: low-P/E stocks always catch up\",\"rationale\":\"That's the value-trap mistake. Cheap stocks can stay cheap, or get cheaper, for years.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A multiple is the market's summary of growth, risk and discount rate. A P/E of 6 says the market expects little from LABE's profits. It can be wrong, but you need a reason, not just the low number.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"valuation-multiples\"],\"hints\":[\"What has to be true for a P/E to be low?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABE makes steel. At the bottom of the cycle its profit has collapsed, and its P/E is 45 against a long-run average of 12.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the P/E of 45 most likely show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Profit is temporarily depressed, so the P/E is high without the stock being expensive\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The stock is very expensive and due to fall\",\"rationale\":\"The 45 comes from a tiny E, not a big P. At a cyclical low, a high P/E often means depressed profit.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The market expects steel to become a fast-growth business\",\"rationale\":\"Possible, but unlikely. The usual reason at a cycle low is profit far below its normal level.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"P/E doesn't apply to cyclical companies\",\"rationale\":\"P/E applies whenever profit is positive. The issue is that this year's profit isn't a normal year's.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"P/E divides by this year's earnings. For a cyclical company at the bottom of its cycle, earnings are far below normal, so the P/E is high while the price may be low. Cyclicals often look cheapest on P/E at the top, just before profits fall.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\",\"concepts\":[\"valuation-multiples\"],\"hints\":[\"P/E = price ÷ earnings. Which of the two has changed?\",\"Where in the cycle are this year's earnings?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You will receive 110 vUSD in one year. With a 10% discount rate, what is it worth today, to 2 decimals?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":100,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Present value = 110 ÷ 1.10 = 100.00. Put 100.00 away at 10% and you have 110 in a year: that's why the two are worth the same today.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"discounted-cash-flow\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the future amount by 1 + the rate.\",\"110 ÷ 1.10 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"amount\":[50,500,5],\"rate\":[2,15,1]},\"answer\":\"round(amount / (1 + pct(rate)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You will receive {amount} vUSD in one year. With a {rate}% discount rate, what is it worth today, to 2 decimals?\",\"explanation\":\"Present value = {amount} ÷ {1 + pct(rate):2} = {answer:2}. Put {answer:2} away at {rate}% and you have {amount} in a year: that's why the two are worth the same today.\",\"hint\":\"{amount} ÷ {1 + pct(rate):2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does the terminal value usually dominate a five-year DCF?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Analysts add a safety premium to it\",\"rationale\":\"No premium is added. It's large because it sums all the cash flows after the forecast years.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It covers every year after year five, forever, while the forecast covers only five\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It isn't discounted\",\"rationale\":\"It is discounted, back from year five. Even so, it's often over 60–70% of the total.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It dominates only for loss-making companies\",\"rationale\":\"It dominates for most companies, profitable ones included: all the years after five outweigh five.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The terminal value stands for all cash flows after the forecast, forever. Even discounted, it's often over 60–70% of the total (a rule of thumb). So a DCF depends heavily on long-run growth and the discount rate, its two least knowable inputs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"discounted-cash-flow\"],\"hints\":[\"How many years does the terminal value stand for?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your DCF says LABB is worth 2,000m. The market values it at 2,600m. A friend says: 'The DCF says 2,000, the price is 2,600, so short it.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's missing from that reasoning?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the model shows the stock is 30% overvalued\",\"rationale\":\"That treats the model as the truth. It's an opinion built on guessed inputs.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only that the discount rate should be lowered until the model matches\",\"rationale\":\"Fitting the inputs to the price hides the real question: what growth does the price assume, and is it believable?\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The DCF is an opinion with no timing, and the market's higher growth assumption may be right\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Shorting is fine, but only after the next earnings report\",\"rationale\":\"Waiting adds a date, but the model is still an opinion, not a signal.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A DCF turns guesses about growth and rates into a value. The gap to the price is a disagreement, not a signal: the market may assume more growth for good reasons, and nothing says when the gap closes. As a trading signal, a DCF is grade D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-expectations\",\"discounted-cash-flow\"],\"hints\":[\"What would have to be true for 2,600m to be right?\",\"Does the model say when the gap closes?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.5-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're long LABA at 50.00 with a stop at 48.00. After a report it opens at 44.00, and your stop fills there. What was your loss in R, before costs?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"1R = 50.00 − 48.00 = 2.00 a unit. The fill at 44.00 loses 6.00 a unit: 6.00 ÷ 2.00 = 3R. The stop didn't fail; the gap skipped it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"1R is the distance from entry to stop.\",\"The loss runs from entry to the actual fill, 44.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"entry\":[20,150,0.5],\"dist\":[1,5,0.5],\"gap\":[2,15,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"gap > dist * 1.2\",\"gap * 3 < entry\"],\"answer\":\"round(gap / dist, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You're long LABA at {entry:2} with a stop at {entry - dist:2}. After a report it opens at {entry - gap:2}, and your stop fills there. What was your loss in R, before costs, to 2 decimals?\",\"explanation\":\"1R = {entry:2} − {entry - dist:2} = {dist:2} a unit. The fill at {entry - gap:2} loses {gap:2} a unit: {gap:2} ÷ {dist:2} = {answer:2}R. The stop didn't fail; the gap skipped it.\",\"hint\":\"Loss = {entry:2} − {entry - gap:2} = {gap:2} a unit.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.5-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is 'LABB beat the consensus' weak information on its own?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because consensus estimates are always wrong\",\"rationale\":\"Consensus is often close. The point is that companies usually clear it, so clearing it says little.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Most companies beat consensus, so a beat is the usual case; the surprise's size and guidance carry the news\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because only revenue matters, not EPS\",\"rationale\":\"Both matter, along with guidance. The issue is that a beat of either is the norm.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It isn't weak: a beat reliably sends the stock up\",\"rationale\":\"Beats are so common that they're expected. Stocks often fall on a beat smaller than hoped.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"About 78% of S&P 500 companies beat on average over five years (FactSet data), so a beat is the base rate. What's informative is how big the surprise is, compared with usual surprises, and what guidance says.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-surprise\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"What share of companies usually beat?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.5-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABE reports at 22:05, after the close. You hold a long with a stop at 30.00; LABE closed at 32.50.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you usually do tonight, and what happens to your stop if LABE opens at 27.00?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Place a market order tonight; the stop fills at 30.00 regardless\",\"rationale\":\"Outside regular hours, brokers often accept limit orders only. And a stop can't fill at a price the market skipped.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing tonight; the stop fills at 30.00 at the open\",\"rationale\":\"Limit orders are often possible after hours. But the stop fills at the open's price, about 27.00, not at 30.00.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Any order type; the stop fills at the after-hours low\",\"rationale\":\"Stops usually trigger in the regular session only, and fill at the first price there, not at an overnight low.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Usually only limit orders tonight; the stop waits for the session and fills near 27.00\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Extended hours usually allow limit orders only, with thin liquidity and no NBBO protection. A stop is a market order waiting for its trigger: when the session opens below it, it fills near the open, 27.00, 3.00 below the stop.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Which order type do brokers usually allow after hours?\",\"A stop becomes a market order at the first price after its trigger.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.6-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABB beats the EPS consensus but cuts its full-year guidance. What is the typical reaction in this cell?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A sure gap up: the beat comes first\",\"rationale\":\"Results and guidance disagree. Guidance often weighs more, because it changes the future, not one quarter.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"No reaction: the two cancel out\",\"rationale\":\"Mixed cells rarely mean quiet. They mean big moves in either direction.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Uncertain, with a wide spread: the cut can outweigh the beat, and sharp drops happen\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A sure gap down of at least 10%\",\"rationale\":\"Sharp drops happen in this cell, but not reliably. Its outcomes spread widely around zero.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Beat-and-lower is a mixed cell: results say up, guidance says down. Guidance often weighs more because it changes future cash flows, and sharp drops happen, but outcomes spread widely. Flag it as uncertain and size for a big move either way.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.6\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-guidance\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.6-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABE has guided low and then raised at every report for two years. Why does its latest raise barely move the stock?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Traders expect its habitual raise, so the raise is already in the price\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Raises never move stocks\",\"rationale\":\"Raises often move stocks when they're unexpected. LABE's aren't.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Guidance matters less than the quarter's results\",\"rationale\":\"Guidance usually matters more. Here, the expectation of a raise takes away the news.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Analysts stopped covering LABE\",\"rationale\":\"Coverage isn't the issue. The market has learned LABE's pattern and prices the raise in advance.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A habitual sandbagger teaches the market that its first guidance is too low. Traders price in the usual raise, so only a raise bigger than usual is news, and a smaller one can disappoint.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.6\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-guidance\"],\"hints\":[\"What do traders expect from LABE before the report?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.6-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LABD reports results exactly in line with consensus and cuts its full-year guidance by 6%. The stock opens 4% higher.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely explanation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The market misread the report\",\"rationale\":\"A misread usually reverses within minutes. A 4% gain that holds needs a reason.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Traders expected a bigger cut, so a 6% cut was a relief\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Guidance cuts usually lift prices\",\"rationale\":\"In-line with a cut leans down: in the simulator, 31 of 40 such reports gapped down. This one is an exception.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The cut doesn't matter because results were in line\",\"rationale\":\"Guidance often matters more than one quarter. The reaction depends on what was expected.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Reactions are measured against expectations, positioning included. If traders feared a 10% cut and sold beforehand, a 6% cut is better than feared. The cell says down; the expectation says relief.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.6\",\"concepts\":[\"earnings-guidance\",\"earnings-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"The reaction compares the news with what traders expected, not with zero.\",\"What might traders have expected before the report?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.7-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABE trades at 90.00. The at-the-money straddle for the expiry just after its report costs 6.30. What move, in %, is the market pricing either way, to 1 decimal?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":7,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Implied move ≈ straddle ÷ price = 6.30 ÷ 90.00 = 7.0%. The market prices a move of about ±7% through the report.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-move\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the straddle price by the share price.\",\"6.30 ÷ 90.00 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"price\":[20,300,1],\"move\":[3,15,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(round(price * pct(move), 2) / price * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"LABE trades at {price:2}. The at-the-money straddle for the expiry just after its report costs {round(price * pct(move), 2):2}. What move, in %, is the market pricing either way, to 1 decimal?\",\"explanation\":\"Implied move ≈ straddle ÷ price = {round(price * pct(move), 2):2} ÷ {price:2} = {answer:1}%. The market prices a move of about ±{answer:1}% through the report.\",\"hint\":\"{round(price * pct(move), 2):2} ÷ {price:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.7-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You bought a LABB call before earnings. The stock rose 3% on the report, yet your call lost 12%. Why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The broker mispriced the option\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing was mispriced. The call fell because the uncertainty it was pricing disappeared.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Calls lose value whenever the stock rises\",\"rationale\":\"A call gains from a rise, all else equal. Here, implied volatility fell from 72% to 30%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Implied volatility collapsed, and the uncertainty premium vanished faster than the rise added value\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The rise came after expiry\",\"rationale\":\"The option still had 6 days left. Its value fell because volatility collapsed, not because it expired.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Before the report, most of the call's price was a premium for uncertainty. The report resolved it, implied volatility fell, and the premium vanished: IV crush. The 3% rise added less than that loss.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\",\"concepts\":[\"iv-crush\"],\"hints\":[\"What was most of the call's price paying for before the report?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.7-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A forum post says: 'Earnings straddles usually lose, so always sell them.' What's missing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: if buyers usually lose, sellers usually win, so it's an edge\",\"rationale\":\"Winning often isn't positive expectancy. The rare losses can outweigh all the wins.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only the commissions, which make every option trade lose\",\"rationale\":\"Costs matter, but they're not the main issue. The tail risk of a big gap is.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Selling straddles is safe as long as you use a stop-loss\",\"rationale\":\"A stop can't fill at your price through an overnight gap. The open sets the loss.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The rare big moves: sellers win often but can lose many crushes' worth at once, and the evidence is mixed\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Selling straddles collects the IV crush most of the time, but a gap far beyond the implied move can wipe out many wins at once. The evidence is mixed: one dataset found actual moves about 85% of implied, another found fair pricing in aggregate (grade C).\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\",\"concepts\":[\"iv-crush\",\"implied-move\"],\"hints\":[\"How often does a seller win, and how much can one loss cost?\",\"Can a stop protect a short straddle through an overnight gap?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.8-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABE reports EPS of 1.42 against an expected 1.30. Its past surprises have a standard deviation of 0.08. What is its SUE, to 2 decimals?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.5,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"SUE = (actual − expected) ÷ the standard deviation of past surprises = (1.42 − 1.30) ÷ 0.08 = 0.12 ÷ 0.08 = 1.50.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.8\",\"concepts\":[\"post-earnings-drift\"],\"hints\":[\"First the surprise: actual − expected.\",\"Then divide by the usual size of surprises, 0.08.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"exp\":[0.5,4,0.05],\"surprise\":[-0.4,0.4,0.01],\"sd\":[0.04,0.2,0.01]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(surprise) >= 0.03\",\"exp + surprise > 0.1\"],\"answer\":\"round(surprise / sd, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABE reports EPS of {exp + surprise:2} against an expected {exp:2}. Its past surprises have a standard deviation of {sd:2}. What is its SUE, to 2 decimals?\",\"explanation\":\"SUE = ({exp + surprise:2} − {exp:2}) ÷ {sd:2} = {surprise:2} ÷ {sd:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Surprise = {exp + surprise:2} − {exp:2} = {surprise:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.8-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Where has PEAD survived best, and why is it hard to capture there?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In small, illiquid, thinly covered stocks, where spreads, impact and borrowing costs are highest\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In large caps, because they're the most traded\",\"rationale\":\"The reverse: heavy trading and coverage make prices adjust fast. In large caps the drift is about zero since around 2006.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"In every stock equally, so it's easy to capture\",\"rationale\":\"The drift is concentrated, not spread evenly, and it has shrunk sharply since publication.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"In stocks with no earnings reports\",\"rationale\":\"PEAD follows earnings reports by definition. It survives in stocks with reports but few followers.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The drift lives where information spreads slowly: small, illiquid stocks with little analyst coverage. Those are exactly the stocks with wide spreads, high market impact and costly shorting, so costs can eat the edge.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.8\",\"concepts\":[\"post-earnings-drift\",\"anomaly-decay\"],\"hints\":[\"Where does news spread slowest?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.8-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A course advert says: 'The PEAD spread was positive in 41 of 48 quarters. Trade it!' What does that statistic say, and what doesn't it?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It proves the strategy works today, with 85% reliability\",\"rationale\":\"41 of 48 describes 1974–1985. It says nothing direct about markets after publication, costs or today's large caps.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It means each trade wins 85% of the time\",\"rationale\":\"It counts quarters in which the whole top-minus-bottom portfolio was positive, not single trades.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It describes 1974–1985, before costs; it doesn't show the drift survives today, after publication and costs\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: academic results never apply to real trading\",\"rationale\":\"Too strong. The result was real for its period; the question is what's left after publication and costs.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Bernard and Thomas measured a gross top-minus-bottom portfolio in 1974–1985. Since then the spread has shrunk, large caps show about zero since 2006, and published edges typically lose much of their return after publication. Grade B, strongly decayed.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.8\",\"concepts\":[\"anomaly-decay\",\"post-earnings-drift\"],\"hints\":[\"Which years does the 41 of 48 cover?\",\"Was it measured before or after costs?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.9-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABE receives a cash offer of 30.00 a share. It trades at 28.50; if the deal fails, it should fall to 22.00. What deal-success probability does the price imply, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":81.25,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"p = (28.50 − 22.00) ÷ (30.00 − 22.00) = 6.50 ÷ 8.00 = 81.25%. The 1.50 spread pays for the 18.75% chance of a 6.50 fall.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.9\",\"concepts\":[\"corporate-actions\"],\"hints\":[\"Set p × offer + (1 − p) × fail price equal to the price.\",\"p = (price − fail price) ÷ (offer − fail price).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"offer\":[20,100,1],\"down\":[3,30,0.5],\"up\":[0.25,3,0.25]},\"constraints\":[\"up * 2 < down\",\"down * 2 < offer\"],\"answer\":\"round((down - up) / down * 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABE receives a cash offer of {offer:2} a share. It trades at {offer - up:2}; if the deal fails, it should fall to {offer - down:2}. What deal-success probability does the price imply, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"p = ({offer - up:2} − {offer - down:2}) ÷ ({offer:2} − {offer - down:2}) = {down - up:2} ÷ {down:2} = {answer:2}%. The {up:2} spread pays for the {100 - answer:2}% chance of a {down - up:2} fall.\",\"hint\":\"p = {down - up:2} ÷ {down:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.9-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABA splits 4-for-1. Before the split you held 100 shares at 120.00. What changed?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Your position is worth four times as much\",\"rationale\":\"The price is divided by 4 as the shares are multiplied by 4. The value stays 12,000.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"You lost 75% of your money\",\"rationale\":\"The price fell 75%, but you hold four times the shares. The value is unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You now hold 400 shares at about 30.00: the same 12,000 of value\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The company's profit per share rose\",\"rationale\":\"Profit is split over four times as many shares, so EPS falls to a quarter, along with the price.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A split cuts each share into smaller pieces: 4 times the shares at a quarter of the price. The company, its profit and your stake are unchanged. Only unadjusted charts and indicators see a crash.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.9\",\"concepts\":[\"corporate-actions\",\"adjusted-price-data\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.8.9-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A video suggests: 'Buy LABA before the ex-dividend date, collect the 1.00 dividend, and sell the next day. Free money, every quarter.'\",\"prompt\":\"Why isn't it a profit?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The dividend is paid only to long-term holders\",\"rationale\":\"Anyone who holds on the record date gets it. The catch is that the price drops by about the dividend.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It is a profit, as long as you buy at the open\",\"rationale\":\"The time of day doesn't remove the ex-date drop in price.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Brokers keep the dividend on short holdings\",\"rationale\":\"Brokers pass dividends on to holders. The value simply moves from the price into your cash.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The price usually drops by about the dividend on the ex-date, and taxes and trading costs come on top\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"On the ex-date the share drops by about the dividend: value moves from the price into cash. Collecting 1.00 while the price falls about 1.00 is no gain, and the spread, commissions and tax on the dividend make it a loss.\",\"lesson\":\"3.8.9\",\"concepts\":[\"corporate-actions\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens to the price on the ex-dividend date?\",\"Add the costs of two trades and the tax.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.1-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A weekly survey shows 24% bullish and 51% bearish. Its bull–bear spread has averaged +6, with a standard deviation of 15. What is this week's z-score?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-2.2,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Spread = 24 − 51 = −27. z = (−27 − 6) ÷ 15 = −33 ÷ 15 = −2.2. Beyond −2, so a bearish extreme by this survey's own history.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-sentiment\"],\"hints\":[\"First the spread: % bullish minus % bearish.\",\"z = (spread − mean) ÷ standard deviation.\",\"(−27 − 6) ÷ 15.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"bull\":[15,50,1],\"bear\":[15,60,1],\"m\":[0,10,1],\"s\":[10,20,1]},\"constraints\":[\"bull + bear <= 90\",\"abs(bull - bear - m) >= s\"],\"answer\":\"round((bull - bear - m) / s, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A weekly survey shows {bull}% bullish and {bear}% bearish. Its bull–bear spread has averaged +{m}, with a standard deviation of {s}. What is this week's z-score?\",\"explanation\":\"Spread = {bull} − {bear} = {bull - bear}. z = ({bull - bear} − {m}) ÷ {s} = {answer:2}. Beyond ±2 counts as an extreme for this survey.\",\"hint\":\"z = ({bull - bear} − {m}) ÷ {s}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.1-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A survey shows 70% of respondents bearish, a record. What does that reading tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The market will rise within a month\",\"rationale\":\"That treats a condition as a trigger. Extremes shift reversal odds; they don't date a turn, and they can deepen for weeks.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How one-sided the crowd's expectations are right now\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"That 70% of investors have sold their holdings\",\"rationale\":\"A survey records opinions, not positions. Many bearish respondents may still be fully invested.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: surveys are pure noise\",\"rationale\":\"They mostly echo recent price, but readings at multi-year extremes carry weak-to-moderate evidence (grade C).\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A survey measures expectations. A record bearish reading says the crowd is unusually one-sided, which raises reversal odds modestly (grade C). It's a condition to note, not a signal to act on.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.1\",\"concepts\":[\"market-sentiment\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.1-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your playbook says: 'Fade sentiment extremes only after a price trigger.' The survey hits +40, beyond +2σ, and you sell short at once anyway. Over the next six weeks the index rises 7% and the spread climbs to +47.\",\"prompt\":\"What does the rule 'condition, not trigger' say about this trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The rule failed: extremes should reverse\",\"rationale\":\"Extremes can deepen, as here. The rule exists for exactly this case.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Selling on the reading alone used the condition as a trigger; the plan said to wait for price\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Add to the short: a higher spread means a stronger signal\",\"rationale\":\"A deeper extreme isn't a better timer. Adding to a losing fade with no price trigger compounds the error.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: one trade can't test a rule\",\"rationale\":\"True for the rule's edge, but this is a process error: the trade skipped the plan's own trigger.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The extreme was the condition: it made a fade allowable. The trigger, a price reversal, never came, and the trend ran on with the spread climbing further. Judge the decision by its process: it skipped the trigger.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.1\",\"concepts\":[\"contrarian-signal\",\"market-sentiment\"],\"hints\":[\"Which part of the playbook did the trade skip?\",\"Condition: the spread. Trigger: a price event.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over the past 156 weeks, managed money's net position in a futures market ranged from −30k to +110k contracts. This week it is +75k. What is the COT index?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"COT index = (net − low) ÷ (high − low) × 100 = (75 + 30) ÷ (110 + 30) × 100 = 105 ÷ 140 × 100 = 75.0. Upper part of the range, but not an extreme.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\",\"concepts\":[\"commitments-of-traders\"],\"hints\":[\"The index places this week inside the three-year range, from 0 to 100.\",\"Subtracting a negative low adds it: 75 − (−30) = 105.\",\"105 ÷ 140 × 100.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lo\":[-60,20,5],\"hi\":[80,200,5],\"net\":[-50,190,1]},\"constraints\":[\"net > lo\",\"net < hi\",\"hi - lo >= 60\"],\"answer\":\"round((net - lo) / (hi - lo) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Over the past 156 weeks, managed money's net position in a futures market ranged from {lo}k to {hi}k contracts. This week it is {net}k. What is the COT index?\",\"explanation\":\"COT index = ({net} − ({lo})) ÷ ({hi} − ({lo})) × 100 = {net - lo} ÷ {hi - lo} × 100 = {answer:1}.\",\"hint\":\"({net} − ({lo})) ÷ ({hi} − ({lo})) × 100.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In the Legacy COT report, who are the 'non-commercials'?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Retail traders with small accounts\",\"rationale\":\"Small traders fall under non-reportable positions. Non-commercials are large enough to report.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Large reportable speculators, such as funds trading for profit\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Producers hedging their output\",\"rationale\":\"Producers hedging business risk are the commercials.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The exchange's market makers\",\"rationale\":\"Market makers aren't a Legacy category; the split is commercial, non-commercial and non-reportable.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Non-commercials hold positions above the reporting threshold without a commercial hedging purpose: mostly funds and other large speculators. Small traders, retail among them, sit in the non-reportable positions.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\",\"concepts\":[\"commitments-of-traders\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"The latest COT report shows commercials in synthetic crude holding their biggest net short in three years. A forum post calls it 'smart money betting on a crash'.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A strong bearish signal: commercials know the market best\",\"rationale\":\"Commercials are mostly hedgers. Their shorts lock in prices for output they will sell; they aren't directional bets.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Producers are hedging more of their output; it says little about direction\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A buy signal: commercials are always wrong\",\"rationale\":\"That flips the error. Hedging flows aren't forecasts in either direction.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The data are wrong: commercials can't be net short\",\"rationale\":\"They often are: producers sell futures against future output, so commercial net shorts are normal in commodities.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A producer sells futures to lock in a price for oil it will pump, so a large commercial net short usually means more hedging, not a forecast. Treat it as positioning on the other side of the speculators.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\",\"concepts\":[\"commitments-of-traders\",\"positioning\"],\"hints\":[\"Why does a producer sell futures?\",\"Think hedging, not betting.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"The VIX is 24 and the index is at 5,000. Using one standard deviation, how many index points either side does the options market price for the coming week?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":166.4,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"points\"},\"explanation\":\"Weekly move = 24 ÷ √52 = 24 ÷ 7.211 = 3.328%. 5,000 × 3.328% = 166.4 points, so the one-sigma week runs from about 4,833.6 to 5,166.4.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.3\",\"concepts\":[\"vix\"],\"hints\":[\"The VIX is annualised; scale it to one week.\",\"Divide by √52 ≈ 7.211.\",\"24 ÷ 7.211 = 3.328%, then take that share of 5,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"vix\":[12,45,1],\"idx\":[3000,6000,100]},\"answer\":\"round(idx * vix / 7.2111 / 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"The VIX is {vix} and the index is at {idx}. Using one standard deviation, how many index points either side does the options market price for the coming week?\",\"explanation\":\"Weekly move = {vix} ÷ √52 = {vix} ÷ 7.211 = {vix / 7.2111:3}%. {idx} × {vix / 7.2111:3}% = {answer:1} points.\",\"hint\":\"{vix} ÷ 7.211 = {vix / 7.2111:3}%, then take that share of {idx}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"The VIX has sat near 11 for months, one of its lowest readings. What does that most likely mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The market is safe: big drops are unlikely\",\"rationale\":\"A low VIX prices small moves; it doesn't prevent large ones. Calm periods can end abruptly.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Options price small moves: protection is cheap, and complacency is possible\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The index will fall soon\",\"rationale\":\"A low VIX isn't a timer. It can stay low for a long time.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Options traders expect a strong rally\",\"rationale\":\"The VIX measures the expected size of moves, not their direction.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The VIX measures the volatility priced into options. A low reading means protection is cheap and traders expect calm. That can reflect complacency, but it's neither a safety guarantee nor a sell signal.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.3\",\"concepts\":[\"vix\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"After same-day options volume surged, the total put/call ratio reads 1.30, above the 1.23 extreme that marked fear in 2007–2022. A newsletter calls it a contrarian buy signal.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you treat the 1.30 reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy: 1.30 is beyond the historical extreme\",\"rationale\":\"That threshold came from an older baseline. 0DTE volume shifted the ratio, so the old cut-off no longer marks the same fear.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Compare it with the ratio's recent history, and wait for a price confirmation before acting\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sell: heavy put buying means insiders expect a fall\",\"rationale\":\"Put volume mixes hedging and speculation. It isn't insider information, and it doesn't call direction.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Ignore put/call data completely\",\"rationale\":\"Put/call is grade C, not useless. Read against its recent baseline, at extremes, it's a condition worth noting.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Structural changes such as the 0DTE boom move the ratio's baseline, so fixed thresholds from 2007–2022 stop meaning what they did. Judge the reading against recent history, treat it as a condition, and let price give the trigger.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.3\",\"concepts\":[\"put-call-ratio\",\"contrarian-signal\"],\"hints\":[\"Did the 1.23 threshold come from today's market?\",\"A condition isn't a trigger.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.4-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A stock has 6.3 million shares sold short and trades 0.9 million shares a day on average. What is its days to cover?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":7,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"days\"},\"explanation\":\"Days to cover = short interest ÷ average daily volume = 6.3m ÷ 0.9m = 7.0 days of normal trading.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.4\",\"concepts\":[\"positioning\",\"short-squeeze\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide short interest by average daily volume.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"si\":[1,30,0.1],\"adv\":[0.3,6,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"si / adv >= 1\",\"si / adv <= 20\"],\"answer\":\"round(si / adv, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A stock has {si:1} million shares sold short and trades {adv:1} million shares a day on average. What is its days to cover?\",\"explanation\":\"Days to cover = {si:1}m ÷ {adv:1}m = {answer:1} days of normal trading.\",\"hint\":\"{si:1} ÷ {adv:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.4-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A heavily shorted stock jumps 30% on news worth perhaps 3%. What mostly drives the extra move?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The news was better than it looked\",\"rationale\":\"Possible, but the gap between a 3% surprise and a 30% move is the signature of forced buying.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Short sellers forced to buy back: stops, margin calls and recalls\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Long holders selling into the rise\",\"rationale\":\"Selling would dampen the move, not extend it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Market makers pushing the price up for profit\",\"rationale\":\"Dealers' hedging can add to moves, but the core fuel in a squeeze is shorts who must buy.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Short sellers who are stopped out, get margin calls or face share recalls must buy at whatever price. That buying isn't a judgement on the news, so the move can be many times the surprise.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.4\",\"concepts\":[\"short-squeeze\"],\"hints\":[\"Who has to buy, whatever the price?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.4-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A small-cap ticker is trending on social media. Volume is five times normal and the price has doubled in two weeks. A filing shows two directors sold shares yesterday.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the most likely reading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A breakout with insider confirmation: buy before it runs further\",\"rationale\":\"Insiders selling into the rise is the opposite of confirmation. They are using the demand to exit.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A likely pump, with late buyers serving as exit liquidity for insiders and early holders\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A short squeeze: shorts are covering\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing here shows short interest or days to cover. Hype plus insider sales points to a pump.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neutral: volume and social buzz cancel out\",\"rationale\":\"Rising buzz, rising volume and insider selling all point the same way: distribution to late buyers.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Social hype plus insiders selling into strength is the classic pump-and-dump shape. Social sentiment is grade D and easy to manipulate. Buying here likely means supplying liquidity to people who want out.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.4\",\"concepts\":[\"fomo\",\"positioning\"],\"hints\":[\"Who is selling into the rise?\",\"What does a late buyer give the sellers?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.5-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do naive fades of sentiment extremes tend to lose in strong trends?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Extremes in a trend often deepen, so selling at once keeps hitting stops\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Sentiment surveys are published too late to trade\",\"rationale\":\"Weekly surveys do lag, but instant data wouldn't fix it: strong trends keep producing extreme readings.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trends never reverse after extremes\",\"rationale\":\"They sometimes do: extremes raise reversal odds modestly. The issue is timing, not impossibility.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Costs are higher on contrarian trades\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are the same per trade. The losses come from repeated stops while the extreme persists.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Strong trends produce extreme readings and keep producing them. A rule that sells on the reading alone keeps entering against the trend and getting stopped. In the lab, the naive fade hit its stop in 150 of 237 in-sample trades.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.5\",\"concepts\":[\"contrarian-signal\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a strong trend do to the survey, week after week?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.5-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You add a price confirmation to a contrarian rule. What do you typically gain, and what do you give up?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gain fewer stop-outs on deepening extremes; give up part of the move on fast reversals\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Gain a higher win rate; give up nothing\",\"rationale\":\"Waiting has a price: on fast turns you enter later, at a worse level, often with a wider stop.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Gain more trades; give up accuracy\",\"rationale\":\"Confirmation filters trades out, so you usually get fewer, not more.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Gain certainty that the extreme marked the top; give up flexibility\",\"rationale\":\"No confirmation gives certainty. It shifts the odds and the timing, at a cost.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Confirmation keeps you out while the extreme deepens, which cuts stop-outs. On fast turns it enters after the first leg: in the lesson's fast episode, +0.49R for the confirmed rule against +1.79R for selling at once.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.5\",\"concepts\":[\"contrarian-signal\",\"anticipation-vs-confirmation\"],\"hints\":[\"Think of the lesson's two episodes: a slow turn and a fast one.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.5-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your confirmed-fade rule, tested out of sample once, shows an expectancy of +0.08R a trade with a 95% confidence interval of −0.05R to +0.21R.\",\"prompt\":\"What is the right verdict?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It works: the expectancy is positive\",\"rationale\":\"The interval includes zero, so the data can't tell this rule from no edge at all.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It can't be told from zero yet: gather more data or reject it, and don't trade it as an edge\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Re-run the out-of-sample test with tweaked settings until the interval clears zero\",\"rationale\":\"Each tweak tested on the hold-out turns it into in-sample data. That's data mining.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's a losing rule: the low end is negative\",\"rationale\":\"The interval also includes positive values. Not proven is not the same as proven wrong.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A confidence interval that spans zero means the result is consistent with no edge. The honest options are more data or rejecting the rule. Tuning on the hold-out would spoil it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.5\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"contrarian-signal\"],\"hints\":[\"Does the interval include zero?\",\"What happens to a hold-out you tune on?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.6-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You test 20 independent calendar patterns at the 5% level on data with no real effect. What is the chance, in %, that at least one looks significant?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":64.2,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Each test stays quiet with probability 0.95. For 20 independent tests, all stay quiet with probability 0.95²⁰ = 0.358. So at least one false 'hit' = 1 − 0.358 = 64.2%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.6\",\"concepts\":[\"calendar-effects\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the chance that no test passes, then subtract it from 1.\",\"Each test stays quiet with probability 0.95.\",\"0.95²⁰ = 0.358.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[5,40,5]},\"answer\":\"round(100 - 100 * ((n == 5) * 0.773781 + (n == 10) * 0.598737 + (n == 15) * 0.463291 + (n == 20) * 0.358486 + (n == 25) * 0.27739 + (n == 30) * 0.214639 + (n == 35) * 0.166083 + (n == 40) * 0.128512), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You test {n} independent calendar patterns at the 5% level on data with no real effect. What is the chance, in %, that at least one looks significant?\",\"explanation\":\"Each test stays quiet with probability 0.95. For {n} independent tests, all stay quiet {100 - answer:1}% of the time. So at least one false 'hit' = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"1 − 0.95 to the power {n}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.6-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What evidence grade does the 'January barometer' ('as January goes, so goes the year') get, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"B: it has worked in most years\",\"rationale\":\"A hit rate on a few decades, with no mechanism, is folklore. Few independent years can't support a B.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"D: folklore, with few independent years and no robust support\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"C: it works in small caps only\",\"rationale\":\"That's the January effect, a different claim about small-cap returns in January.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A: it's in every trading almanac\",\"rationale\":\"Popularity isn't evidence. Almanacs repeat it; robust studies don't support it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The January barometer rests on a handful of decades, so few independent observations, and no mechanism. The course grades it D, with the Santa Claus rally and the presidential cycle.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.6\",\"concepts\":[\"calendar-effects\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.9.6-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A newsletter says: 'Natural gas rises every winter, so buy the December future in June and collect the seasonal move.' In June, spot is 2.80 and the December future is 3.55.\",\"prompt\":\"Why is this unlikely to work as described?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because winter demand is unpredictable\",\"rationale\":\"It's fairly predictable, which is exactly why it's already priced. The problem is the price, not the season.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The December future already prices the expected winter rise; only surprises move it from 3.55\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because futures can't be held until December\",\"rationale\":\"You can hold the December contract until near its expiry. The issue is what its price already includes.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It will work: seasonal patterns are mechanical\",\"rationale\":\"The physical pattern is mechanical. As a trade it's grade C, because the curve has already priced it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Everyone knows gas demand peaks in winter, so the December contract already trades well above June spot: 3.55 against 2.80. Buying it earns only the surprise relative to that price, not the seasonal rise itself.\",\"lesson\":\"3.9.6\",\"concepts\":[\"calendar-effects\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the June spot price with the December future's price.\",\"What does the market already know?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.1-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A system wins 40% of trades at +2.5R and loses 60% at −1R. Its median trade is −1R, yet its expectancy is +0.40R. How can both be true?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Rare large wins pull the mean above the median\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"One of the two numbers must be miscalculated\",\"rationale\":\"Both are right. The median is the middle trade, a loss; the mean also weighs how large the wins are.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Expectancy counts only the winning trades\",\"rationale\":\"Expectancy counts every trade: 0.4 × 2.5 − 0.6 × 1 = +0.40R. The losses are in it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The median includes costs and the mean doesn't\",\"rationale\":\"Neither number here includes costs. They differ because the results are lopsided, not because of fees.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"More than half the trades lose, so the middle trade is −1R. The mean weighs each result by its size: 0.4 × 2.5 − 0.6 × 1 = +0.40R. Rare big wins make the distribution positively skewed, with the mean above the median.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\",\"concepts\":[\"return-distribution\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.1-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which system is most likely to have negative skew in its R results?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A trend follower with a tight stop and no profit target\",\"rationale\":\"Tight stops and open targets make many small losses and rare big wins: positive skew.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A breakout system that lets winners run\",\"rationale\":\"Letting winners run creates rare large wins on top of many small losses: positive skew.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A mean-reversion system with a small target and a far stop\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A coin-flip entry with stop and target at the same distance\",\"rationale\":\"Equal distances give equal-sized wins and losses: a symmetric distribution, close to zero skew.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A small target hit often and a far stop hit rarely produce many small wins and an occasional large loss: the long tail sits on the loss side. Option selling is the extreme case.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\",\"concepts\":[\"skewness\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask where the long tail sits: on the win side or the loss side?\",\"Small target, far stop: which results are frequent, and which are large?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.1-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"An option-selling strategy shows 18 months of steady gains, no losing month and a Sharpe ratio of 2.5. It collects small premiums and would lose heavily on a sharp market move, which hasn't happened in those 18 months.\",\"prompt\":\"Why might the Sharpe ratio flatter this strategy?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Its rare large losses may be missing from the sample, so the measured volatility is too low\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Sharpe ignores returns, so steady gains don't count\",\"rationale\":\"Sharpe divides the average excess return by volatility, so the gains count. The trouble is the denominator, which never saw a crash.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"18 months is too long a window for a Sharpe ratio\",\"rationale\":\"Longer windows help. The trouble is that 18 months may hold none of the rare losses that define this strategy's risk.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Option selling has positive skew, which Sharpe penalises\",\"rationale\":\"Option selling has negative skew: small frequent gains, rare large losses. Sharpe hides that tail rather than penalising it.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Sharpe sees only the volatility in the sample. A negatively skewed strategy can go many months without its rare large loss, so its measured volatility, and its risk, look small. Judge it by its worst plausible loss, not by its Sharpe.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\",\"concepts\":[\"skewness\",\"sharpe-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the standard deviation measure if the big loss never happened in the sample?\",\"Negative skew: frequent small gains, rare large losses.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABZ has a daily volatility of 0.8%. Assuming independent days, what is its volatility over a 5-day week, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.79,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Volatility scales with the square root of time: 0.8% × √5 = 0.8% × 2.236 = 1.79%. Multiplying by 5 would assume every day moves the same way.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.2\",\"concepts\":[\"square-root-of-time\"],\"hints\":[\"Multiply by the square root of the number of days, not by the days.\",\"√5 = 2.236.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"d\":[0.5,3,0.1],\"h\":[1,4,1]},\"answer\":\"round(d * ((h == 1) * 2.23607 + (h == 2) * 3.16228 + (h == 3) * 4.58258 + (h == 4) * 7.93725), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An instrument has a daily volatility of {d:1}%. Assuming independent days, what is its volatility over {((h == 1) * 5 + (h == 2) * 10 + (h == 3) * 21 + (h == 4) * 63)} trading days, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Volatility scales with the square root of time: {d:1}% × √{((h == 1) * 5 + (h == 2) * 10 + (h == 3) * 21 + (h == 4) * 63)} = {d:1}% × {((h == 1) * 2.23607 + (h == 2) * 3.16228 + (h == 3) * 4.58258 + (h == 4) * 7.93725):3} = {answer:2}%. Multiplying by the days would assume every day moves the same way.\",\"hint\":\"√{((h == 1) * 5 + (h == 2) * 10 + (h == 3) * 21 + (h == 4) * 63)} = {((h == 1) * 2.23607 + (h == 2) * 3.16228 + (h == 3) * 4.58258 + (h == 4) * 7.93725):3}. Multiply {d:1}% by it.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does volatility clustering mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Big moves tend to follow big moves, and quiet days follow quiet days\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Prices cluster around round numbers\",\"rationale\":\"That's about price levels. Clustering is about the size of moves over time: busy stretches follow busy ones.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After a big up day, a big down day is due\",\"rationale\":\"Clustering predicts size, not direction. In the lesson's seeded market, today's return said nothing about tomorrow's sign.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Volatility stays about the same over time\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: volatility comes in calm and turbulent stretches, which is why a calm-window estimate fails after a switch.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Large moves bunch together in time. In the lesson's seeded clustering market, the day after one of the biggest 5% of moves averaged 1.49 times the usual size, while its direction stayed unpredictable.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.2\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-clustering\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A market's daily returns have a correlation of +0.2 with the previous day's return. What does the √t rule do to its weekly volatility?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gets it right: √t works for any market\",\"rationale\":\"√t assumes independent days. With positive day-to-day correlation, moves build on each other and weeks get wider.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Understates it: moves build on each other, so weeks are wider than daily × √5\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Overstates it: the correlation cancels moves out\",\"rationale\":\"Cancelling is what negative correlation does, as in a range. Positive correlation makes moves continue, so weeks are wider.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Doubles it\",\"rationale\":\"The error is real but smaller: with +0.2, weekly volatility is about 17% above the rule, 2.62% against 2.24%.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Variances add only when days are independent. With a day-to-day correlation of +0.2, a 1%-a-day market moves about 2.62% a week, not 1% × √5 = 2.24%. Annualising a Sharpe ratio with √252 has the same flaw (Lo).\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.2\",\"concepts\":[\"square-root-of-time\",\"autocorrelation\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the √t rule assume about one day and the next?\",\"If today's move tends to continue tomorrow, does a week get wider or narrower?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.3-1\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your setup just made +4R in five trades, far above its long-run +0.3R a trade. What should you expect from the next five?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About +4R again: it's in form\",\"rationale\":\"Five trades are mostly noise. The 'form' was luck on top of the edge, and luck doesn't carry over.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Results closer to its long-run +0.3R a trade\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A losing run to balance it out\",\"rationale\":\"Luck doesn't pay itself back; it's redrawn. Expect the long-run edge, not a payback.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing can be said\",\"rationale\":\"The long-run edge from many trades is the best guide. Expect results near it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"An extreme result over a few trades is edge plus a lot of luck. The luck is redrawn on the next trades, so results tend back toward the true edge, about +0.3R a trade. That's regression to the mean, not a payback.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.3\",\"concepts\":[\"regression-to-the-mean\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.3-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A setup's R results have a standard deviation of about 1.5R. It averaged +0.9R over its first 20 trades and +0.1R over the next 20. Its 400-trade backtest shows +0.25R.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best first step?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Drop it: the edge is gone\",\"rationale\":\"+0.1R over 20 trades is well within the noise around a +0.25R edge. Twenty trades can't show the edge is gone.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Raise the size to win back the lost edge\",\"rationale\":\"Size doesn't fix an edge. If nothing changed, the edge is still about +0.25R; if something did, more size makes it worse.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Check both samples against the 400-trade edge, and look for a nameable cause before deciding\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Re-optimise its parameters on the last 20 trades\",\"rationale\":\"Fitting 20 trades chases noise, the same mistake as trusting the hot start.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"With an R spread of 1.5R, a 20-trade average has a standard error of about 0.34R. Both samples sit within the noise around +0.25R; the hot start was the outlier. Call it a real change only with a cause you can name, such as higher costs, and confirm it on new trades.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.3\",\"concepts\":[\"regression-to-the-mean\"],\"hints\":[\"How far can a 20-trade average wander from the true edge?\",\"Standard error = 1.5R ÷ √20 ≈ 0.34R.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.3-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You tested 30 parameter settings of a breakout rule on the same five years of data. The best earned +0.6R a trade in-sample; the average setting earned +0.1R.\",\"prompt\":\"What should you expect from the best setting on new data?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About +0.6R: it's the best setting\",\"rationale\":\"It came out on top partly thanks to luck that fit those five years. That part won't repeat.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Less than +0.6R, possibly close to the average setting\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exactly +0.1R, the average of all settings\",\"rationale\":\"Some of its edge may be real, so it could beat the average. But expect it to fall well short of +0.6R.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"More than +0.6R, as it improves with fresh data\",\"rationale\":\"Selection works the other way: the winner's luck is redrawn on new data, so its result shrinks.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Picking the best of 30 picks the most luck as well as any real edge: the winner's curse. On new data the luck is redrawn, so expect a result between its in-sample score and the average, often near the average. 3.11 tests this out of sample.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.3\",\"concepts\":[\"regression-to-the-mean\",\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"Why did this setting come out on top of 30?\",\"Which part of its result repeats on new data: the edge or the luck?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.4-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"20% of your setup ideas have a real edge. Your test passes 90% of real edges and 10% of useless ones. An idea passes. What's the chance it's real, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":69.2,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Out of 1,000 ideas: 200 real, 180 pass; 800 useless, 80 pass. Real passes ÷ all passes = 180 ÷ 260 = 69.2%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\",\"concepts\":[\"bayesian-updating\",\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Count 1,000 ideas: how many are real, and how many of those pass?\",\"Then count the useless ideas that pass, and divide real passes by all passes.\",\"Real passes: 180 of 1,000. Useless passes: 80.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"p\":[5,30,1],\"pw\":[50,95,5],\"fa\":[2,30,1]},\"answer\":\"round(100 * p * pw / (p * pw + (100 - p) * fa), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"{p}% of your setup ideas have a real edge. Your test passes {pw}% of real edges and {fa}% of useless ones. An idea passes. What's the chance it's real, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Out of 10,000 ideas: {p * 100} real, {p * pw} pass; {10000 - p * 100} useless, {(100 - p) * fa} pass. Real passes ÷ all passes = {p * pw} ÷ {p * pw + (100 - p) * fa} = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"Out of 10,000 ideas, real passes: {p * pw}. Useless passes: {(100 - p) * fa}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.4-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is base-rate neglect?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Judging new evidence while ignoring how common the thing was to begin with\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Refusing to update a belief after new evidence\",\"rationale\":\"That's under-reacting to evidence. Base-rate neglect over-reacts to it and forgets the starting odds.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Drawing conclusions from too small a sample\",\"rationale\":\"That's the law of small numbers. Base-rate neglect ignores the prior, even when the evidence itself is good.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Comparing your results with the market's\",\"rationale\":\"Comparing with a benchmark is good practice. Base-rate neglect is forgetting how rare the thing you test for is.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A vivid pass, a hot streak or a convincing backtest pulls attention away from the base rate. With 10% of ideas real, 80% power and 20% false alarms, a pass means 31%, not 80%. The base rate sets where you start.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\"],\"hints\":[\"Base rate: how common something is before any evidence.\",\"What happens to the 31% if you forget the 900 useless ideas?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.4-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A trader says: 'My backtest passed at the 5% level, so I'm 95% sure the strategy works.'\",\"prompt\":\"What's missing from that reasoning?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: a 5% test means 95% confidence that it works\",\"rationale\":\"5% is the chance a useless strategy passes. The chance a passing strategy is real depends on how many tested ideas are real.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The share of real ideas and the test's power, needed to turn a pass into a probability\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only a longer backtest\",\"rationale\":\"More data raises the test's power, but a pass still needs the prior. If few ideas are real, a pass is far from 95%.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The strategy's maximum drawdown\",\"rationale\":\"Drawdown matters for sizing, but it doesn't say whether the edge is real. That takes the prior and the error rates.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A 5% level is the false-alarm rate on useless ideas, not the chance of being right. With a 10% prior and 80% power, 1,000 ideas give 80 real passes and 45 false ones, so a pass is 64% likely to be real, not 95%. Fewer real ideas, lower still.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\",\"concepts\":[\"bayesian-updating\",\"statistical-significance\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the 5% level measure: passes among useless ideas, or real edges among passes?\",\"Try it on 1,000 ideas with a 10% prior and 80% power.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.5-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your journal: 64 trades, mean +0.25R, standard deviation 1.6R. What is the t-statistic of the mean?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.25,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"Standard error = 1.6 ÷ √64 = 1.6 ÷ 8 = 0.2R. t = 0.25 ÷ 0.2 = 1.25: under 2, so the edge isn't yet distinguishable from zero.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\",\"concepts\":[\"statistical-significance\"],\"hints\":[\"Standard error = sd ÷ √n; then divide the mean by it.\",\"√64 = 8, so the standard error is 1.6 ÷ 8 = 0.2R.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m\":[0.05,0.6,0.05],\"s\":[0.8,2.5,0.1],\"q\":[5,15,1]},\"answer\":\"round(m * q / s, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your journal: {q * q} trades, mean +{m:2}R, standard deviation {s:1}R. What is the t-statistic of the mean?\",\"explanation\":\"Standard error = {s:1} ÷ √{q * q} = {s:1} ÷ {q} = {s / q:3}R. t = {m:2} ÷ {s / q:3} = {answer:2}. About 2 or more suggests more than noise.\",\"hint\":\"√{q * q} = {q}, so the standard error is {s:1} ÷ {q} = {s / q:3}R.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.5-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your setup's mean R has a p-value of 0.03. What does that mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"There's a 97% chance the edge is real\",\"rationale\":\"That reads p backwards. The chance the edge is real needs a prior, as in 3.10.4.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"If the true edge were zero, a result this far from zero would happen about 3% of the time\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Future trades will be profitable 97% of the time\",\"rationale\":\"A p-value says nothing about future win rates. It measures how surprising this sample would be with no edge.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"3% of your trades were lucky\",\"rationale\":\"p isn't a count of lucky trades. It's the probability of data this extreme if the edge were zero.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A p-value assumes no edge and asks how often chance alone would produce a result this extreme. p = 0.03 says that's uncommon; it does not say the edge is 97% likely to be real. That depends on the prior and on how many things you tested.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\",\"concepts\":[\"statistical-significance\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a p-value assume is true?\",\"p is computed under 'no edge'. It isn't the probability of 'no edge'.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.5-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A strategy shows an annualised Sharpe ratio of 3.0 over its first three months, measured on daily returns.\",\"prompt\":\"What can you conclude?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's exceptional: a Sharpe of 3 is rare\",\"rationale\":\"Over three months the standard error is about 2. A zero-edge strategy shows 3 or more in about 7% of three-month samples.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Very little: the standard error is about 2, so the plausible range runs from below zero to about 7\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Its true Sharpe is at least 2\",\"rationale\":\"A 95% range of about −0.8 to 7.2 fits a true Sharpe near zero as well as a high one.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Three months is enough, since the data are daily\",\"rationale\":\"More data points barely help: a Sharpe's standard error depends on years of history, here 1 ÷ √0.25 = 2.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"With daily data, the standard error of an annual Sharpe is about 1 ÷ √years = 1 ÷ √0.25 = 2. In 10,000 seeded three-month histories with a true Sharpe of 3, the middle 95% of measured values ran from −0.82 to 7.15; with a true Sharpe of zero, 6.7% still showed 3 or more.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\",\"concepts\":[\"sharpe-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"Standard error of an annual Sharpe ≈ 1 ÷ √years.\",\"Three months is 0.25 years: 1 ÷ √0.25 = 2.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You test 30 rules with no real edge, each at the 5% level. What's the chance at least one looks significant, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":78.5,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Each useless rule stays quiet with probability 0.95. All 30 stay quiet: 0.95³⁰ = 0.215. At least one 'works': 1 − 0.215 = 78.5%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\",\"concepts\":[\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the chance that every rule stays quiet first.\",\"Multiply 0.95 by itself once per rule, then subtract from 1.\",\"0.95³⁰ = 0.215.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[2,8,1]},\"answer\":\"round(100 * (1 - ((n == 2) * 0.598737 + (n == 3) * 0.463291 + (n == 4) * 0.358486 + (n == 5) * 0.27739 + (n == 6) * 0.214639 + (n == 7) * 0.166083 + (n == 8) * 0.128512)), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You test {5 * n} rules with no real edge, each at the 5% level. What's the chance at least one looks significant, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Each useless rule stays quiet with probability 0.95. All {5 * n} stay quiet: 0.95 to the power {5 * n} = {((n == 2) * 0.598737 + (n == 3) * 0.463291 + (n == 4) * 0.358486 + (n == 5) * 0.27739 + (n == 6) * 0.214639 + (n == 7) * 0.166083 + (n == 8) * 0.128512):3}. At least one 'works': 1 − {((n == 2) * 0.598737 + (n == 3) * 0.463291 + (n == 4) * 0.358486 + (n == 5) * 0.27739 + (n == 6) * 0.214639 + (n == 7) * 0.166083 + (n == 8) * 0.128512):3} = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"0.95 to the power {5 * n} = {((n == 2) * 0.598737 + (n == 3) * 0.463291 + (n == 4) * 0.358486 + (n == 5) * 0.27739 + (n == 6) * 0.214639 + (n == 7) * 0.166083 + (n == 8) * 0.128512):3}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You ran 25 tests and want at most a 5% chance of any false discovery. Under Bonferroni, what p-value threshold should each test use, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.2,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Bonferroni divides the overall level by the number of tests: 5% ÷ 25 = 0.2%. A rule needs p below 0.2% to count, not below 5%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\",\"concepts\":[\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the overall level by the number of tests.\",\"5% ÷ 25.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[1,3,1],\"n\":[2,10,1]},\"answer\":\"round(((a == 1) * 1 + (a == 2) * 5 + (a == 3) * 10) / (5 * n), 4)\",\"prompt\":\"You ran {5 * n} tests and want at most a {((a == 1) * 1 + (a == 2) * 5 + (a == 3) * 10)}% chance of any false discovery. Under Bonferroni, what p-value threshold should each test use, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Bonferroni divides the overall level by the number of tests: {((a == 1) * 1 + (a == 2) * 5 + (a == 3) * 10)}% ÷ {5 * n} = {answer:3}%. A rule needs p below that to count.\",\"hint\":\"{((a == 1) * 1 + (a == 2) * 5 + (a == 3) * 10)}% ÷ {5 * n}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A trader tried 25 configurations of an indicator on the same data. One was significant at p = 0.03; the other 24 were not. The trader reports only that one.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the fair verdict?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A real edge: p = 0.03 is below 5%\",\"rationale\":\"Below 5% for one test. Among 25 useless configurations, at least one under 5% turns up 72% of the time.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Compatible with luck: after 25 tries the bar is 0.2%, and 0.03 is far above it\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Significant, because 24 failures show the test is strict\",\"rationale\":\"The 24 failures are what chance predicts. They make the one hit less impressive, not more.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Can't judge without the drawdown\",\"rationale\":\"Drawdown matters for sizing, not for whether one hit in 25 is luck. Count the tries.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"One hit in 25 is roughly what chance delivers: 1 − 0.95²⁵ = 72% for at least one. Bonferroni puts the bar at 5% ÷ 25 = 0.2%. p = 0.03 doesn't clear it, and reporting only the hit hides the 24 tries.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\",\"concepts\":[\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"How many configurations were tried in all?\",\"Divide 5% by the number of tries and compare.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your journal: 30 wins and 50 losses. If outcomes were independent, how many runs would you expect?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":38.5,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"runs\"},\"explanation\":\"Expected runs = 2 × 30 × 50 ÷ 80 + 1 = 37.5 + 1 = 38.5. Clearly fewer runs than that means wins and losses bunch together.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.7\",\"concepts\":[\"autocorrelation\"],\"hints\":[\"Expected runs = 2 × wins × losses ÷ trades + 1.\",\"2 × 30 × 50 ÷ 80 = 37.5. Then add 1.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[20,70,1],\"l\":[20,70,1]},\"answer\":\"round(2 * w * l / (w + l) + 1, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your journal: {w} wins and {l} losses. If outcomes were independent, how many runs would you expect?\",\"explanation\":\"Expected runs = 2 × {w} × {l} ÷ {w + l} + 1 = {2 * w * l / (w + l):2} + 1 = {answer:2}. Clearly fewer runs than that means wins and losses bunch together.\",\"hint\":\"2 × {w} × {l} ÷ {w + l} = {2 * w * l / (w + l):2}. Then add 1.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.7-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"In a market stuck in a range, what sign does the lag-1 autocorrelation of daily returns usually have?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Positive: moves continue\",\"rationale\":\"Continuation is the trending fingerprint. In a range, a move up tends to be followed by a move back down.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Negative: moves tend to reverse\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exactly zero\",\"rationale\":\"Near zero is the random-walk fingerprint. A range pulls price back, so the sign turns negative.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No sign: only its size matters\",\"rationale\":\"The sign is the useful part: positive for trends, negative for mean reversion.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In a range, an up day tends to be followed by a down day as price is pulled back toward the middle: negative autocorrelation, like the −0.51 of the mean-reverting market in the lesson.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.7\",\"concepts\":[\"autocorrelation\",\"market-regime\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.10.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your wins and losses cluster: 38 runs in 100 trades where 49 were expected. You estimate your worst drawdown by reshuffling your trade list 10,000 times.\",\"prompt\":\"What does that Monte Carlo get wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: reshuffling keeps every trade, so the drawdowns are right\",\"rationale\":\"It keeps the trades but breaks their order. Clustering lives in the order, so the bunches of losses disappear.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It underestimates drawdowns, because reshuffling breaks up the bunches of losses\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It overestimates drawdowns, because clustering also bunches wins\",\"rationale\":\"Bunched wins don't soften bunched losses. In the lesson's seeded test, reshuffling cut the median drawdown from 13R to 8.5R.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It only matters for the Sharpe ratio\",\"rationale\":\"Sharpe annualisation suffers too, but drawdown estimates, Kelly and ruin formulas all assume independence.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Reshuffling assumes independent trades. Clustered losses arrive together, so real drawdowns run deeper: in 10,000 seeded clustered sequences the median max drawdown was 13R, but 8.5R after reshuffling. Resample blocks of trades instead.\",\"lesson\":\"3.10.7\",\"concepts\":[\"autocorrelation\"],\"hints\":[\"What does reshuffling do to the order of trades?\",\"Where do clustered losses do their damage: in how many there are, or in when they come?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.1-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which hypothesis could a test prove wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price respects key levels whenever smart money defends them.\",\"rationale\":\"It has an escape clause: any failed level can be blamed on smart money not defending it. No result could refute it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"After a 20-bar breakout with a rising 50-bar average, +2R comes before −1R in over 40% of trades within 10 bars.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Markets move in waves, though the count can be revised as new prices arrive.\",\"rationale\":\"Revising the count after the fact fits any path. A claim that adapts to every outcome can't be wrong.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Good setups work when the trader is in the right state of mind.\",\"rationale\":\"Neither 'good setup' nor 'right state' is defined, so every loss can be explained away.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Only b names a condition, an outcome, a horizon and a threshold that the data could fail to meet. The others can explain any result after the fact, so no test can reject them.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.1\",\"concepts\":[\"edge-hypothesis\",\"falsifiability\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.1-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why test a new rule in R, with a fixed 1R per trade, before adding any position sizing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sizing can hide a weak edge or inflate a strong one, so the signal must be judged on its own first\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"R results are always larger than percentage results\",\"rationale\":\"R is a unit of risk. It doesn't make results bigger; it makes trades comparable, whatever their stop distance.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sizing doesn't matter once the edge is positive\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing matters a lot: it sets drawdowns and growth (3.12). It's designed second, not ignored.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A fixed size removes the need to count costs\",\"rationale\":\"Costs still apply to every trade. In R they're the cost per unit divided by the stop distance.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Compounding and sizing rules change the equity curve without changing the signal. A weak edge with aggressive sizing can look strong, and a strong one can look poor after a bad run. Judge the signal in R first, then design the sizing.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.1\",\"concepts\":[\"codified-rules\",\"r-multiple\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about what compounding does to an equity curve.\",\"Separate 'does the signal work?' from 'how much do I bet?'\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.1-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your breakout test on LABA shows its best trade on 3 March: the price jumped from 40.00 to 80.00 overnight, far above every earlier high, and the trade made +12R. LABA did a 1-for-2 reverse split that day.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with this result?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The prices weren't adjusted for the split: the breakout never happened, and the trade must go\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: reverse splits often come before rallies\",\"rationale\":\"A reverse split merges shares and doubles the price. Nothing of value changed, so no holder earned +12R from it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The stop would have filled worse after the gap\",\"rationale\":\"There was no real gap. The price doubled on paper because each new share replaced two old ones.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's survivorship bias\",\"rationale\":\"Survivorship is about missing instruments. This is a data error inside an instrument that is in the test.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Unadjusted data make a corporate action look like a price move. Adjust the history for splits and dividends, or the test trades events that never happened. The biggest single trades are the first place to look for data errors.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.1\",\"concepts\":[\"corporate-actions\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a reverse split do to the share price?\",\"Would anyone holding the stock have earned that move?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.2-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX trades near 80.00. The spread is 0.10, commission is 0.05% of each fill, and your stop is 1.60 away. What do costs take from each trade, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.11,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Per unit: the spread, 0.10, plus 0.05% of 80.00 on entry and on exit, 0.04 each: 0.18. Divided by the 1.60 stop: about 0.11R. A tighter stop would make the same costs a bigger share of R.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\",\"concepts\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"breakeven-cost\"],\"hints\":[\"Add the spread and both commissions, per unit.\",\"Commission per fill: 0.05% of 80.00 = 0.04. Then divide by the stop distance.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"px\":[20,200,5],\"sp\":[0.04,0.3,0.02],\"st\":[0.5,5,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"(sp + 2 * px * 0.0005) / st >= 0.03\",\"(sp + 2 * px * 0.0005) / st <= 0.6\"],\"answer\":\"round((sp + 2 * px * 0.0005) / st, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LABX trades near {px:2}. The spread is {sp:2}, commission is 0.05% of each fill, and your stop is {st:2} away. What do costs take from each trade, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Per unit: the spread, {sp:2}, plus 0.05% of {px:2} on entry and on exit, {px * 0.0005:3} each: {sp + 2 * px * 0.0005:3}. Divided by the {st:2} stop: {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"({sp:2} + 2 × {px * 0.0005:3}) ÷ {st:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.2-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In your backtest, one daily bar touched both your stop and your target. The bar data can't say which came first. What should the test assume?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The target, since the bar closed above the entry\",\"rationale\":\"The close says nothing about the order inside the bar. Assuming the better outcome flatters every ambiguous trade.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The stop, unless finer data show otherwise\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Half a win and half a loss\",\"rationale\":\"No real trade ends half and half, and splitting the difference still flatters when stops come first.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Skip the trade\",\"rationale\":\"Dropping trades you can't score removes losers selectively. Score it the conservative way instead.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Intrabar ambiguity is settled the conservative way: the stop first. If the system survives that, it survives reality. With intraday data you can check the real order.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fill-assumptions\"],\"hints\":[\"Which assumption could only make the test look worse than reality, never better?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.2-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A backtest fills every buy limit as soon as a bar's low touches the limit price. In the test, 70% of the limit entries filled at the exact low of the bar.\",\"prompt\":\"Why is this optimistic?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Limit prices are always worse than the market\",\"rationale\":\"A buy limit is a better price than the market. The question is whether it would have filled at all.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"At a touch, orders ahead of yours in the queue may take all the volume; you might get nothing\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Limit orders pay a higher commission\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is the same 0.05% per fill. The issue is the fill itself.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It isn't: a touch always means a fill\",\"rationale\":\"A touch means some volume traded at that price, not that it reached your order. Many orders at the low never fill.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Orders at one price fill in queue order. When price only touches your limit, the orders ahead of you may absorb the volume, and the touch is often the turn: the fills you'd miss are the best trades. Count a limit filled only when price trades through it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fill-assumptions\",\"queue-priority\"],\"hints\":[\"Who gets filled first at a price?\",\"Which limit orders fill at the exact low, and which trades would you miss?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.3-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which rule uses information from the future?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy at the next open when today closes above the 20-day high\",\"rationale\":\"Today's close is known before the next open. This one is clean.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Buy at today's open when today's range is above 2 ATR\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Sell at the next open when the close falls below the 10-day low\",\"rationale\":\"The signal uses closes up to today and acts after the close: no leak.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stop 1 ATR(14) below the entry price\",\"rationale\":\"The entry and the past ATR are both known when the order is placed.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Today's range is known only when the day ends, but the rule buys at the open. A backtest can do that; a trader can't. Ask of every input when exactly it was known.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.3\",\"concepts\":[\"look-ahead-bias\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.3-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A value rule is tested only on stocks listed today. Compared with a test on the full history, delisted stocks included, how will its results tend to look?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Better than the truth\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Worse than the truth\",\"rationale\":\"The missing stocks are mostly failures. Removing them removes losses, so results look better, not worse.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The same: delisted stocks are a small share\",\"rationale\":\"In the lesson's universe, 12 of 50 were delisted, and the rule's return fell from +18.9% to +4.8% a year with them included.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It depends only on the costs\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are a separate check. Survivorship changes which stocks are in the test.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Survivors are the stocks that didn't fail. Rules that buy what has fallen, such as value and mean reversion, gain the most from dropping the dead: in the lesson, +18.9% a year on survivors against +4.8% on the full universe.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.3\",\"concepts\":[\"survivorship-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"What do the delisted stocks have in common?\",\"Which stocks would a 'buy the cheapest' rule have held?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.3-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A test buys stocks whose quarterly earnings beat the same quarter a year earlier by 20%, on the report date. It uses a data file of earnings as they stand today, after several companies restated past quarters.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Look-ahead: on the report date, only the first, unrevised figures existed\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Survivorship: restated companies are missing\",\"rationale\":\"The companies are in the test. The problem is which version of their numbers it used.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"None, as long as costs are on\",\"rationale\":\"Costs don't fix timing. The test trades on numbers nobody had on the report date.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Overfitting to the 20% threshold\",\"rationale\":\"That may be a second problem, but the first is the data: revised figures used as if they were known earlier.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Restated figures are later information. Point-in-time data keep each number as first published, with its date, so the test sees only what a trader could have seen.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.3\",\"concepts\":[\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"When were the restated figures published?\",\"Which version of the number existed on the report date?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does 'peeking' at the out-of-sample mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Looking at its results, then changing the rules\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Running the final test once, with the rules fixed in advance\",\"rationale\":\"That's the proper use of the hold-out: one run, rules frozen.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Splitting the data in time order\",\"rationale\":\"A split in time order is the right way to build a hold-out.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Testing on a second market\",\"rationale\":\"Another market is a robustness check, not a look at the hold-out.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Any decision made after seeing hold-out results, a tweak, a filter, a new parameter, lets those results shape the rules. The hold-out becomes in-sample, and the log counts another trial.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.4\",\"concepts\":[\"in-sample-out-of-sample\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.4-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your system's Sharpe ratio is about 1.0. Its standard error is about √((1 + Sharpe² ÷ 2) ÷ years). How many years of out-of-sample data bring the standard error down to 0.5?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"years\"},\"explanation\":\"Square both sides: 1.5 ÷ years = 0.25, so years = 1.5 ÷ 0.25 = 6. Even then, the 95% range is 1.0 ± 0.98. Short out-of-sample periods can't tell a good system from a lucky one.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.4\",\"concepts\":[\"in-sample-out-of-sample\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"Square both sides to remove the root.\",\"1 + 1.0² ÷ 2 = 1.5, and 0.5² = 0.25. Then 1.5 ÷ 0.25.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s10\":[5,20,5],\"e100\":[30,70,5]},\"answer\":\"round((1 + s10 * s10 / 200) / (e100 * e100 / 10000), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Your system's Sharpe ratio is about {s10 / 10:1}. Its standard error is about √((1 + Sharpe² ÷ 2) ÷ years). How many years of out-of-sample data bring the standard error down to {e100 / 100:2}?\",\"explanation\":\"Square both sides: {1 + s10 * s10 / 200:3} ÷ years = {e100 / 100:2}² = {e100 * e100 / 10000:4}. Years = {1 + s10 * s10 / 200:3} ÷ {e100 * e100 / 10000:4} = {answer:1}.\",\"hint\":\"{1 + s10 * s10 / 200:3} ÷ {e100 * e100 / 10000:4}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.4-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Dana tuned a system on 2016–2022. The 2023–2025 hold-out lost money. She changed the stop from 1.5 ATR to 2 ATR, re-ran the hold-out, and it now shows a profit. Her report says: 'validated out of sample'.\",\"prompt\":\"What should her research log show, and what are 2023–2025 now?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"One more trial; 2023–2025 are now in-sample and can't validate anything\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing new; 2023–2025 are still a clean hold-out\",\"rationale\":\"The new stop was chosen because of the hold-out's result. Those years now helped build the rule.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"One more trial, but 2023–2025 still count as out-of-sample\",\"rationale\":\"Logging the trial is right, but the years are spent: they shaped the new stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: a stop change isn't a new trial\",\"rationale\":\"Every change tested on the data is a trial. A stop change can turn a loser into a winner purely by fitting.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Re-optimising after a failed hold-out uses it for development. The log adds a trial, and the system needs data it has never seen: a forward test (3.11.10) or a walk-forward (3.11.6).\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.4\",\"concepts\":[\"in-sample-out-of-sample\",\"research-log\"],\"hints\":[\"Did the hold-out influence the new stop?\",\"Once data shape a rule, can they still test it?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.5-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which backtest shows the classic signs of overfitting?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three parameters, 400 trades over ten years, similar results on nearby settings\",\"rationale\":\"Few parameters, many trades and a plateau are the signs of a sound test.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Seven tuned parameters, 38 trades, an almost straight equity curve\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Two parameters, a modest Sharpe of 0.6, tested on two markets\",\"rationale\":\"Modest, simple and tested across markets: the opposite of the warning signs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A rule that loses about its costs on R0\",\"rationale\":\"That's what a clean rule should do on a random walk: no edge, minus costs.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Many tuned parameters, few trades and a curve that's too smooth: seven parameters on 38 trades leave about 5 trades for each. Real edges in markets are noisy.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\",\"concepts\":[\"overfitting\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.5-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You try 10 versions of a strategy, all with zero true edge, on one year of daily data, and keep the best. What Sharpe ratio should you expect the winner to show, by luck alone?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"About 0\",\"rationale\":\"Each version's true Sharpe is 0, but you keep the best of 10. The maximum of ten noisy results sits well above zero.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"About 0.5\",\"rationale\":\"Too low. With one year of data, each Sharpe has a standard error near 1, so the best of 10 lands around 1.5.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"About 1.5\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"About 3\",\"rationale\":\"Too high for 10 tries: even the best of 100 averages about 2.5.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"In 10,000 simulated trials, the best of 10 zero-edge strategies averaged a one-year Sharpe of 1.55; Bailey and colleagues' formula gives 1.57. The best of 100 reaches about 2.5. Count the tries before you judge the winner.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\",\"concepts\":[\"overfitting\",\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"One year of data gives each Sharpe a standard error of about 1.\",\"You keep the maximum of 10 draws, not a typical one.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.5-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A system made +0.20R a trade over 150 trades. Its three worst losses all came on Fridays, so you add 'no trades on Fridays'. The backtest now shows +0.29R.\",\"prompt\":\"What happened?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Data snooping: the filter was chosen to remove known losers, so most of the gain is fit\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A real discovery: Fridays are bad for this system\",\"rationale\":\"Three trades are not evidence of a Friday effect. The filter was picked after seeing which trades lost.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing important: one filter can't overfit\",\"rationale\":\"A single filter chosen from the data can fit noise, especially one aimed at particular trades.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The system is now more robust\",\"rationale\":\"Robustness means surviving changes. Removing chosen losers does the opposite: it tailors the rule to past noise.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The data chose the rule. Treat 'no Fridays' as a new hypothesis: log it as a trial and test it on data that played no part in finding it, or on R0, where a filter like this should add nothing.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\",\"concepts\":[\"data-snooping\",\"overfitting\"],\"hints\":[\"Was the filter suggested by a theory, or by the losing trades themselves?\",\"Where could you test it fairly?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Over six walk-forward windows, the in-sample settings earned 20% a year on average, and the stitched out-of-sample segments 9% a year. What is the walk-forward efficiency?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.45,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"WFE = out-of-sample annual return ÷ in-sample annual return = 9 ÷ 20 = 0.45. That's below the practitioners' rule of thumb of 0.5: a warning, not a verdict.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\",\"concepts\":[\"walk-forward-analysis\"],\"hints\":[\"The unseen result goes on top.\",\"9 ÷ 20.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"is\":[8,30,1],\"oos\":[-4,24,1]},\"constraints\":[\"oos < is\"],\"answer\":\"round(oos / is, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Over six walk-forward windows, the in-sample settings earned {is}% a year on average, and the stitched out-of-sample segments {oos}% a year. What is the walk-forward efficiency?\",\"explanation\":\"WFE = out-of-sample ÷ in-sample annual return = {oos} ÷ {is} = {answer:2}. Practitioners often ask for 0.5 or more; it's a rule of thumb, not a standard.\",\"hint\":\"{oos} ÷ {is}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When does an anchored walk-forward window make more sense than a rolling one?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When you expect the market's behaviour to stay stable, and want settings that fit all the history\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When the market changes regime often and old data mislead\",\"rationale\":\"That's the case for rolling windows: they drop old data to adapt.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When it gives the higher walk-forward efficiency\",\"rationale\":\"Choosing the window type by its result is meta-overfitting. Choose it for a reason, before running.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Always: more data is always better\",\"rationale\":\"More data helps only if the past still resembles the present. Neither window type is better in general.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Anchored windows keep the start fixed and grow, favouring stability. Rolling windows keep a fixed length and drop old data, favouring adaptation. Decide which assumption fits your market before you run the test.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\",\"concepts\":[\"walk-forward-analysis\"],\"hints\":[\"What does each window type do with the oldest data?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A researcher ran walk-forward tests with windows of 1, 2, 3, 4 and 5 years. The 3-year run had a WFE of 0.72; the others were between 0.18 and 0.41. The report shows only the 3-year run.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Meta-overfitting: the walk-forward itself was tuned, and the other trials weren't reported\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: 3 years is simply the best window\",\"rationale\":\"The best of five runs is a selection. Its 0.72 includes luck, like any tuned parameter.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A WFE of 0.72 is too high to be real\",\"rationale\":\"0.72 isn't impossible. The problem is that it was picked as the best of five.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Walk-forward tests need 10-year windows\",\"rationale\":\"No window length is right in general. The error is choosing it after seeing the results.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The walk-forward has settings of its own, and tuning them is overfitting one level up. Fix the window length for a stated reason, log all five runs as trials, and report all of them.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\",\"concepts\":[\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"data-snooping\"],\"hints\":[\"How many walk-forward runs were tried?\",\"Is the best of five a fair estimate of one run?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.7-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What's the difference between reshuffling and bootstrapping a trade list?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Reshuffling keeps the same trades in a new order; bootstrapping draws trades with replacement, so the total changes too\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They are the same thing\",\"rationale\":\"Reshuffling never changes which trades are in the list; bootstrapping can repeat some and leave others out.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Bootstrapping keeps the order; reshuffling changes the trades\",\"rationale\":\"It's the other way round: reshuffling changes only the order.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Reshuffling adds new random trades\",\"rationale\":\"No new trades are created. Monte Carlo reuses the trades you have.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A reshuffle keeps the final profit and changes the path, so it's used to plan drawdowns. A bootstrap samples with replacement, so the final result varies as well: it shows how far the result itself could have landed.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\",\"concepts\":[\"monte-carlo-resampling\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.7-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your Monte Carlo puts the 95th-percentile worst drawdown at 13.5R. You risk 1% of the account per trade. Roughly how deep a drawdown, in %, should your plan accept?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":13.5,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"1R = 1% of the account, so 13.5R ≈ 13.5%, a little less with risk taken on a shrinking account. Plan for that, not for the single backtest's drawdown.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\",\"concepts\":[\"monte-carlo-resampling\",\"drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"1R is your risk per trade as a share of the account.\",\"13.5 × 1%.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"dd2\":[16,50,1],\"r4\":[1,8,1]},\"answer\":\"round(dd2 / 2 * r4 / 4, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your Monte Carlo puts the 95th-percentile worst drawdown at {dd2 / 2:1}R. You risk {r4 / 4:2}% of the account per trade. Roughly how deep a drawdown, in %, should your plan accept?\",\"explanation\":\"1R = {r4 / 4:2}% of the account, so {dd2 / 2:1}R ≈ {answer:2}%, a little less with risk taken on a shrinking account. Plan for that, not for the single backtest's drawdown.\",\"hint\":\"{dd2 / 2:1} × {r4 / 4:2}%.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.7-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"The reference system's Monte Carlo: 95th-percentile worst drawdown 13.5R, 99th 16.5R. Its backtest's worst was 7R. Live, after 60 trades executed by the rules, you're 11R below your peak.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Keep executing, with the plan's drawdown tiers: 11R is inside normal variance\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Stop the system: 11R is deeper than the backtest's 7R\",\"rationale\":\"The backtest was one ordering. 71.4% of reshuffles of the same trades went deeper than 7R.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the size to recover faster\",\"rationale\":\"Raising size in a drawdown turns variance into ruin risk. The tiers cut risk here; they never raise it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Change the stop and the target\",\"rationale\":\"Changing rules mid-drawdown fits the plan to noise. Changes go through the governance process (3.11.10).\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"11R sits below the 95th percentile, 13.5R: painful, but inside the corridor. Follow the plan's drawdown tiers, check your process, and keep executing. The kill switch waits beyond 16.5R.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\",\"concepts\":[\"expectation-corridor\",\"drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare 11R with the percentiles, not with the backtest.\",\"Where does the kill switch sit?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.8-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Setting A earns +0.18R a trade, but its neighbours earn far less. Setting B earns +0.13R, and its neighbours earn the same. Which do you trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: it has the higher result\",\"rationale\":\"The best cell of a grid is partly luck. Neighbours far below it suggest a spike that won't repeat.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"B: it sits on a plateau\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Either: both are positive\",\"rationale\":\"Both are positive in this sample. The question is which is more likely to stay positive, and the neighbours answer it.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neither: optimise further\",\"rationale\":\"More optimisation makes the spike problem worse. Choose on a plateau, then stress-test.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A plateau, where nearby settings behave alike, suggests the rule captures structure. An isolated peak suggests it captured noise. The course's design rule asks for neighbours averaging at least 80% of the chosen cell.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\",\"concepts\":[\"parameter-plateau\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.8-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You step a lookback by 10% each time: 10, 11, 12.1, 13.3 and so on. What's the next value after 13.3?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":14.6,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"bars\"},\"explanation\":\"Each step multiplies by 1.1: 13.3 × 1.1 = 14.6. Steps in percent give short and long lookbacks the same relative attention.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\",\"concepts\":[\"parameter-plateau\"],\"hints\":[\"A 10% step multiplies by 1.1.\",\"13.3 × 1.1.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"x10\":[100,600,1]},\"answer\":\"round(x10 / 10 * 1.1, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You step a lookback by 10% each time. What's the next value after {x10 / 10:1}?\",\"explanation\":\"Each step multiplies by 1.1: {x10 / 10:1} × 1.1 = {answer:1}. Steps in percent give short and long lookbacks the same relative attention.\",\"hint\":\"{x10 / 10:1} × 1.1.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.8-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A system's profit factor is 2.4. Without its best 5% of trades, it falls to 1.1.\",\"prompt\":\"How should you read this?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Normal for a trend follower, whose edge lives in rare big winners; a warning for a mean reverter, which should earn through many small gains\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The system is broken, whatever its type\",\"rationale\":\"Trend following depends on a few large winners by design. Losing most of the profit factor without them is expected.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Fine for any system: 1.1 is still above 1\",\"rationale\":\"For a mean reverter, needing a handful of outliers means the edge isn't where its design says it is.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The best trades should be removed from the results for good\",\"rationale\":\"Outliers are part of the return. The test measures how much the system depends on them; it doesn't delete them.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Outlier dependence has to match the system's type. Trend followers live on rare large winners, so the drop is normal. A mean reverter's edge is many small wins; if it needs its best 5% to stay above 1, it's fragile.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\",\"concepts\":[\"robustness-testing\",\"performance-metrics\"],\"hints\":[\"Where should each type's profit come from?\",\"Many small wins, or a few large ones?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.9-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Ten bars after its entries, a setup's average MFE is 1.8 ATR and its average MAE is 1.2 ATR. What's its edge ratio?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.5,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"E-ratio = average MFE ÷ average MAE = 1.8 ÷ 1.2 = 1.5. Compare it with random entries on the same data: about 1 in a flat market, more in a rising one.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.9\",\"concepts\":[\"edge-ratio\",\"mae-mfe\"],\"hints\":[\"The favourable excursion goes on top.\",\"1.8 ÷ 1.2.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"mfe\":[0.8,3,0.1],\"mae\":[0.8,2.5,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(mfe / mae, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Ten bars after its entries, a setup's average MFE is {mfe:1} ATR and its average MAE is {mae:1} ATR. What's its edge ratio?\",\"explanation\":\"E-ratio = average MFE ÷ average MAE = {mfe:1} ÷ {mae:1} = {answer:2}. Compare it with random entries on the same data.\",\"hint\":\"{mfe:1} ÷ {mae:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.9-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What do random-entry twins keep the same as your system?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The exits, the market and about the number of trades; only the entries are random\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The entries; only the exits are random\",\"rationale\":\"It's the other way round. Twins test the entry, so the entry is what's randomised.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Nothing: they're completely random strategies\",\"rationale\":\"Twins keep your exits, so any difference comes from the entry alone.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The final profit\",\"rationale\":\"The profit is what's being compared, so it can't be held fixed.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Twins share everything except the entry: the same exits, the same market, a similar number of trades. Your percentile among them then measures what the entry adds.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.9\",\"concepts\":[\"random-entry-benchmark\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.9-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A breakout system made +25% over five years. Against 1,000 random-entry twins with the same exits, it ranks at the 60th percentile. The market rose steadily over the period.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the verdict?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The entry adds little or nothing: exits and the market's drift explain most of the profit\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A strong entry edge: +25% is a good result\",\"rationale\":\"The twins made similar money with random entries. The result is good; the entry isn't what made it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The exits are useless\",\"rationale\":\"Twins with the same exits did well too, so if anything, the exits and the drift did the work.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Not enough information: percentiles don't measure edge\",\"rationale\":\"The percentile among twins is exactly the measure of entry edge, and 60 is close to the 50 of a random entry.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"At the 60th percentile, the system did about as well as random entries with its exits. In a rising market, buying almost anywhere paid. Credit the drift and the exits, not the entry, and compare with buy-and-hold.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.9\",\"concepts\":[\"random-entry-benchmark\",\"passive-benchmark\"],\"hints\":[\"Where would a random entry rank among its twins?\",\"What did the market do over those years?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.10-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which condition should trigger the reference system's kill switch?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A drawdown beyond the 99th percentile of its Monte Carlo, 16.5R\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Three losses in a row\",\"rationale\":\"With 60% losers, a run of three is common: from any trade, the chance is 0.6³ = 21.6%.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A drawdown deeper than the backtest's 7R\",\"rationale\":\"The backtest's 7R was one ordering; 71.4% of reshuffles went deeper. A kill there stops a healthy system.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A losing month\",\"rationale\":\"Losing months are part of variance. The kill switch watches for results that variance can't explain.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A kill switch fires on breakage, not bad luck: a drawdown past the 99th percentile, or an expectancy interval entirely below zero. Milder setbacks belong to the drawdown tiers.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\",\"concepts\":[\"kill-switch\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.10-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which order follows rule-change governance?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Hypothesis → evidence threshold → simulator test → new plan version\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Simulator test → hypothesis → new plan version → evidence threshold\",\"rationale\":\"Testing before stating a hypothesis invites fishing. The hypothesis comes first, and the threshold before the test.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"New plan version → simulator test → hypothesis → evidence threshold\",\"rationale\":\"Changing the plan first and justifying it later is exactly what governance prevents.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Evidence threshold → new plan version → hypothesis → simulator test\",\"rationale\":\"The plan changes last, after the test has met the threshold.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"State the change and why it should help; fix how much evidence you'll need, such as 30–50 trades or a walk-forward; test it in the simulator; then release a new version and compare before and after with intervals.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\",\"concepts\":[\"rule-change-governance\"],\"hints\":[\"What should come before any test?\",\"When does the plan itself change?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.11.10-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Three trades in a row hit their stops. You think: 'The stops are too tight. I'll widen them from 1 ATR to 1.5 ATR from tomorrow.'\",\"prompt\":\"What does rule-change governance say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Log it as a hypothesis, set an evidence threshold, test it in the simulator, and change the plan only through a new version\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Widen them: three stops in a row prove they're too tight\",\"rationale\":\"Three losses are ordinary for a system that wins 40% of the time. Changing rules on them fits the plan to noise.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Tighten them instead, to lose less\",\"rationale\":\"Any change made because of the last three trades is the same mistake, in the other direction.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Never change a rule\",\"rationale\":\"Rules can change, through a process: hypothesis, threshold, test, new version.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Changing a rule after a few losses is data snooping on your own results. A wider stop may be better, but only a test with enough trades can say so. Until then, the current version runs.\",\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\",\"concepts\":[\"rule-change-governance\",\"mistake-vs-variance\"],\"hints\":[\"How unusual are three losses for this system?\",\"What steps does a change go through?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.1-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your account of 10,000.00 gains 30%, then loses 20%. What is it worth after both periods, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10400,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Returns multiply: 10,000.00 × 1.30 × 0.80 = 10,400.00, a 4% gain. Adding the returns would suggest 10%, but the 20% loss is taken from a bigger balance than the one the gain started from.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.1\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-drag\"],\"hints\":[\"Turn each return into a growth factor and multiply them.\",\"10,000.00 × 1.30 × 0.80.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"up\":[10,60,5],\"down\":[10,50,5]},\"answer\":\"round(10000 * (1 + up / 100) * (1 - down / 100), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your account of 10,000.00 gains {up}%, then loses {down}%. What is it worth after both periods, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Returns multiply: 10,000.00 × {1 + up / 100:2} × {1 - down / 100:2} = {answer:2}. Adding the returns ignores that the loss is taken from a different balance than the gain.\",\"hint\":\"10,000.00 × {1 + up / 100:2} × {1 - down / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.1-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A strategy averages 12% a year with 25% volatility. Using g ≈ μ − σ²/2, what is its approximate compound growth rate, in % a year?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":8.875,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Drag = σ²/2 = 0.25² ÷ 2 = 0.03125, or 3.125 points. g ≈ 12% − 3.125% = 8.875%, about 8.9% a year. The swings cost a quarter of the average return.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.1\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-drag\"],\"hints\":[\"Square the volatility as a decimal, then halve it.\",\"0.25 × 0.25 ÷ 2 = 0.03125, or 3.125 points.\",\"Subtract the drag from the 12% average.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"mu\":[6,20,1],\"sig\":[10,40,5]},\"answer\":\"mu - sig * sig / 200\",\"prompt\":\"A strategy averages {mu}% a year with {sig}% volatility. Using g ≈ μ − σ²/2, what is its approximate compound growth rate, in % a year?\",\"explanation\":\"Drag = σ²/2 = {sig / 100:2}² ÷ 2 = {sig * sig / 20000:4}, or {sig * sig / 200:3} points. g ≈ {mu}% − {sig * sig / 200:3}% = {answer:3}% a year.\",\"hint\":\"Drag = {sig / 100:2} × {sig / 100:2} ÷ 2; subtract it from {mu}%.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.1-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"\\\"The average of 1,000 simulated traders made +20%, yet most of them lost money.\\\" How is that possible?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It isn't: an average of +20% means most traders gained\",\"rationale\":\"An average can sit far above the typical result when outcomes are skewed. A few huge gains can outweigh many small losses.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Costs cut every trader's result by the same amount\",\"rationale\":\"Equal costs would lower the average and the typical result together. They can't open a gap between them.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A few very large winners pull the average up; the median shows what happened to the typical trader\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Most traders traded too rarely for the average to apply\",\"rationale\":\"The number of trades isn't the point. Compounding makes results skewed: a few paths grow enormously while most lag.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Compounded results are skewed: a few paths grow enormously and pull the mean up, while the median path lags. At 16% risk on the reference system, the mean of 100,000 paths ended at 481×, the median at 20×. You live one path, so judge sizing by the median and the bad tail.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.1\",\"concepts\":[\"time-vs-ensemble-average\",\"volatility-drag\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about what a few enormous results do to an average.\",\"Which statistic tells you what happened to the typical trader?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.2-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Fixed ratio with Δ = 5,000. Your cumulative profit is 12,000. How many lots do you trade?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"lots\"},\"explanation\":\"Lot N needs Δ × N(N − 1) ÷ 2 of profit: 2 lots at 5,000, 3 at 15,000. With 12,000 you trade 2. The formula ½ × (1 + √(1 + 8 × 12,000 ÷ 5,000)) gives 2.75, rounded down to 2.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fixed-ratio-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Lot N needs Δ × N(N − 1) ÷ 2 of profit.\",\"Thresholds: 2 lots at Δ, 3 at 3Δ, 4 at 6Δ.\",\"5,000 and 15,000: where does 12,000 fall?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"delta\":[2000,6000,1000],\"profit\":[1000,60000,1000]},\"constraints\":[\"profit < 21 * delta\"],\"answer\":\"1 + (profit >= delta) + (profit >= 3 * delta) + (profit >= 6 * delta) + (profit >= 10 * delta) + (profit >= 15 * delta)\",\"prompt\":\"Fixed ratio with Δ = {delta}. Your cumulative profit is {profit}. How many lots do you trade?\",\"explanation\":\"Lot N needs Δ × N(N − 1) ÷ 2 of profit: 2 lots at {delta}, 3 at {3 * delta}, 4 at {6 * delta}, 5 at {10 * delta}. With {profit} of profit you trade {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"Thresholds: {delta}, {3 * delta}, {6 * delta}, {10 * delta}. Where does {profit} fall?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.2-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You risk 3% of current equity per trade. How many losses in a row halve the account? Answer with ln(0.5) ÷ ln(1 − r), to one decimal.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":22.8,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"losses\"},\"explanation\":\"Each loss leaves 0.97 of the account, so n losses leave 0.97^n. Setting it to 0.5: n = ln(0.5) ÷ ln(0.97) = 22.8. Adding up 3% steps would say 16.7, too few, because each loss comes from a smaller account.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fixed-fractional-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"Each loss leaves (1 − r) of the account: solve (1 − r)^n = 0.5.\",\"n = ln(0.5) ÷ ln(1 − r).\",\"ln(0.5) = −0.693 and ln(0.97) = −0.0305.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"r\":[1,10,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(0.693147 / ((r / 100) + (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 2 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 3 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 4 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 5 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 6 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 7 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 8 + (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) * (r / 100) / 9), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You risk {r}% of current equity per trade. How many losses in a row halve the account? Answer with ln(0.5) ÷ ln(1 − r), to one decimal.\",\"explanation\":\"Each loss leaves {1 - r / 100:3} of the account, so n losses leave {1 - r / 100:3}^n. Setting it to 0.5: n = ln(0.5) ÷ ln({1 - r / 100:3}) = {answer:1}. Adding up {r}% steps would say {50 / r:1}, too few, because each loss comes from a smaller account.\",\"hint\":\"n = ln(0.5) ÷ ln({1 - r / 100:3}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.2-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A trader's system breaks even before costs. He switched from fixed units to 1% fixed fractional sizing, and over the last 100 trades the account ended up 6%.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the best explanation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Fixed fractional added an edge by betting more after wins\",\"rationale\":\"It also bets less after losses. With an average R of zero or below, it can't make the expected result positive.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The order of wins and losses was lucky: sizing changed the path, not the edge\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Compounding turns any breakeven system into a winner over time\",\"rationale\":\"Compounding works against a zero-edge system: volatility drag pulls its typical path below the start.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Fixed units had hidden an edge that fixed fractional revealed\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing can't reveal an edge that isn't in the trades. Check the average R of the list itself.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Sizing multiplies each trade's R by a size; it can't change the average R. A profit from a breakeven list comes from the order of the trades. In the simulation of a slightly losing system, about a third of 100-trade lists still ended up, under every sizing rule.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.2\",\"concepts\":[\"position-sizing\",\"fixed-fractional-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"What does sizing change: the trades' results in R, or how much each one counts?\",\"Would the same rule win on a different order of the same trades?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.3-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A system wins 60% of trades; winners pay 1R and losers cost 1R. What is the Kelly fraction, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":20,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b = 0.60 − 0.40 ÷ 1 = 0.20, or 20%. It's the risk per trade that maximises long-run growth if the edge is exactly right; in practice it's a ceiling.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.3\",\"concepts\":[\"kelly-criterion\"],\"hints\":[\"f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b, with W as a decimal.\",\"0.60 − 0.40 ÷ 1.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[40,65,1],\"b\":[1,3,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"w / 100 - (1 - w / 100) / b >= 0.02\"],\"answer\":\"round((w / 100 - (1 - w / 100) / b) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A system wins {w}% of trades; winners pay {b:1}R and losers cost 1R. What is the Kelly fraction, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b = {w / 100:2} − {1 - w / 100:2} ÷ {b:1} = {answer:1}%. It maximises long-run growth only if the edge is exactly right; in practice it's a ceiling.\",\"hint\":\"{w / 100:2} − {1 - w / 100:2} ÷ {b:1}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.3-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A trader with a real, positive edge risks twice the Kelly fraction on every trade. What happens to long-run growth?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It doubles, because the edge works on twice the stake\",\"rationale\":\"Growth isn't proportional to size. Past Kelly, the bigger losses on a shrinking account cost more than the extra gains bring.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's slightly above full Kelly's, with deeper drawdowns\",\"rationale\":\"Full Kelly is the peak of the growth curve. Any fraction above it grows more slowly, not faster.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It falls to about zero, even though every trade has a positive expectancy\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's unchanged: only the drawdowns get deeper\",\"rationale\":\"The growth rate itself drops above Kelly. At twice Kelly it's close to zero.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The growth curve peaks at Kelly and crosses zero close to twice Kelly. For the reference system, 32% risk per trade leaves the median path at 1.45× after 100 trades, against 20× at 16%, and 46% of paths end below the start.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.3\",\"concepts\":[\"kelly-criterion\"],\"hints\":[\"Picture the growth curve: where is its peak?\",\"Where does the curve cross zero?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.3-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"\\\"Kelly says 16% for my system, so I'll risk 16% per trade.\\\" The system's 40% win rate and 2.5R payoff come from a backtest.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with this plan?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The edge is only an estimate, full Kelly's median drawdown is near 83%, and its assumptions don't hold\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: Kelly is the optimal size by definition\",\"rationale\":\"Optimal only if the edge is known exactly and trades are independent. A backtest gives an estimate, usually too high.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"16% is too low: Kelly should be doubled to make up for costs\",\"rationale\":\"Costs shrink the edge, which lowers Kelly. Doubling it moves toward zero growth.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Kelly applies to position value, so holding 16% of equity in the position is fine\",\"rationale\":\"In trading, f is the share of equity lost at the stop, not the position's value. 16% at risk per trade is enormous.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Kelly maximises growth only for a known edge with independent trades and fixed payoffs. A backtest estimate is usually too optimistic, and even with the true edge, full Kelly's median maximum drawdown over 100 trades is about 83%. Treat Kelly as a ceiling and bet a fraction of it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.3\",\"concepts\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"fractional-kelly\"],\"hints\":[\"Where did the 40% and the 2.5R come from?\",\"How deep are full Kelly's drawdowns over 100 trades?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.4-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Optimal f is 0.25 and the list's largest loss is 200 per contract. A trade made +300 per contract. What is its HPR?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.375,\"tolerance\":0.001,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"HPR = 1 + f × trade ÷ |largest loss| = 1 + 0.25 × 300 ÷ 200 = 1.375. Multiply the HPRs of every trade in the list to get its TWR.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\",\"concepts\":[\"optimal-f\"],\"hints\":[\"HPR = 1 + f × trade ÷ |largest loss|.\",\"1 + 0.25 × 300 ÷ 200.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"f\":[0.1,0.4,0.05],\"worst\":[100,400,50],\"trade\":[-400,800,50]},\"constraints\":[\"trade >= 0 - worst\",\"trade != 0\"],\"answer\":\"round(1 + f * trade / worst, 4)\",\"prompt\":\"Optimal f is {f:2} and the list's largest loss is {worst} per contract. A trade made {trade} per contract. What is its HPR?\",\"explanation\":\"HPR = 1 + f × trade ÷ |largest loss| = 1 + {f:2} × {trade} ÷ {worst} = {answer:4}. Multiply the HPRs of every trade in the list to get its TWR.\",\"hint\":\"1 + {f:2} × {trade} ÷ {worst}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.4-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do many traders bet a fraction of Kelly rather than full Kelly?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A fraction grows faster than full Kelly over the long run\",\"rationale\":\"Full Kelly is the peak of long-run growth for a known edge. A fraction grows a little slower; it's chosen for safety.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Kelly only works for coin flips, not for trading\",\"rationale\":\"Kelly applies to any known payoff distribution. The trouble is that a trading edge is only estimated.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A smaller size raises the win rate\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing doesn't change the win rate or the payoff. It only changes how much each trade counts.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It keeps most of the growth with much shallower drawdowns, and survives an overestimated edge\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Half Kelly keeps about three quarters of the growth rate with far smaller drawdowns: on the reference system, a median maximum drawdown of 53% instead of 83%. And if the edge is overestimated, a fraction may still grow where full Kelly shrinks.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\",\"concepts\":[\"fractional-kelly\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare growth and drawdown at half Kelly.\",\"What happens to full Kelly if the true edge is smaller?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.4-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You computed optimal f = 0.30 from your last 40 trades. The largest loss among them was 250 per contract, so the rule says one contract per 833 vUSD of equity.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the main danger in sizing your next trades this way?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Little: 40 trades is plenty to fix the size\",\"rationale\":\"40 trades rarely contain the worst loss you'll see. Optimal f is fitted to this sample and its largest loss.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The next large loss can exceed 250, and an f fitted to 40 trades overstates the edge\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Optimal f is too cautious and wastes growth\",\"rationale\":\"Optimal f is the most aggressive useful point: the peak of the past list's TWR. Beyond it, growth falls.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"HPRs can't be multiplied over 40 trades\",\"rationale\":\"TWR is exactly the product of the HPRs. The problem is what the past list leaves out.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Optimal f maximises growth on the past list only. A loss 1.5 times 250 at f = 0.30 would cost 45% of equity in one trade, and a small sample flatters the edge. Use a fraction of it, set by the single loss you can survive.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\",\"concepts\":[\"optimal-f\",\"fractional-kelly\"],\"hints\":[\"Is 250 the worst loss your system can produce?\",\"At f = 0.30, what would a loss of 375 per contract cost?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.5-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Even payoffs and a fixed stake: you win 55% of bets and your capital covers N = 20 stakes. What is the risk of ruin, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.81,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Risk of ruin = ((1 − W) ÷ W)^N = (0.45 ÷ 0.55)^20 = 0.818^20 = 1.8%. The formula assumes a fixed stake and independent bets, so for fixed fractional sizing it's only a starting point.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.5\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-of-ruin\"],\"hints\":[\"Risk of ruin = ((1 − W) ÷ W)^N.\",\"0.45 ÷ 0.55 = 0.818; raise it to the 20th power.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[52,58,1],\"n\":[10,20,5]},\"answer\":\"round(100 * ((n == 10) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) + (n == 15) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) + (n == 20) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w) * ((100 - w) / w)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Even payoffs and a fixed stake: you win {w}% of bets and your capital covers N = {n} stakes. What is the risk of ruin, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Risk of ruin = ((1 − W) ÷ W)^N = ({1 - w / 100:2} ÷ {w / 100:2})^{n} = {answer:2}%. The formula assumes a fixed stake and independent bets, so for fixed fractional sizing it's only a starting point.\",\"hint\":\"({1 - w / 100:2} ÷ {w / 100:2}) to the power {n}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.5-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does this course define ruin as a drawdown threshold rather than a zero balance?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"With fixed fractional sizing the account never reaches zero, and you'd stop trading long before it did\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Brokers close accounts at a 30% drawdown\",\"rationale\":\"There's no such rule here. The threshold is your own: the drawdown at which you'd stop, or be made to stop.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A zero balance is impossible when every trade has a stop\",\"rationale\":\"Gaps and fixed stakes can still wipe an account out. The reasons are fixed fractional sizing and where people really stop.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A threshold makes the risk of ruin smaller\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: a threshold above zero is easier to reach, so the honest risk of ruin is larger.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Risking a fraction of current equity, each loss is smaller than the last, so the balance approaches zero without reaching it. In practice you stop much earlier, at 25%, 30% or 50%. Measuring ruin there gives a number that matches how you'd actually stop.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.5\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-of-ruin\",\"fixed-fractional-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"What does 1% of a shrinking account do as losses pile up?\",\"Where would you really stop trading?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.5-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A trader computes risk of ruin with even payoffs, N = 10 and the 60% win rate of an in-sample backtest: 1.7%. Out of sample, the system wins 52%.\",\"prompt\":\"What does that do to the risk of ruin?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Little: 52% is still above 50%\",\"rationale\":\"Near 50% the formula is very sensitive. With N = 10, the risk of ruin jumps from 1.7% to about 45%.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It falls, because out-of-sample results are more reliable\",\"rationale\":\"More reliable, yes, but worse. A lower win rate raises the risk of ruin.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It rises sharply: with the same N = 10, from 1.7% to about 45%\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: risk of ruin depends only on the stake\",\"rationale\":\"It depends on the win rate, the payoff and the stake. The win rate is what moved here.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"((1 − W) ÷ W)^N with N = 10: (0.40 ÷ 0.60)^10 = 1.7%, but (0.48 ÷ 0.52)^10 = 44.9%. Near a 50% win rate the result swings wildly, so an in-sample win rate makes the risk of ruin look far smaller than it is.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.5\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-of-ruin\"],\"hints\":[\"Recompute ((1 − W) ÷ W)^N with the out-of-sample win rate.\",\"(0.48 ÷ 0.52)^10.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.6-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Account 20,000.00, daily volatility budget 0.5% of it. LABX's forecast daily move is 2.00 per unit. How many units fit the budget?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":50,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Budget: 0.5% of 20,000.00 = 100.00 a day. Units = 100.00 ÷ 2.00 = 50. On a normal day the position then moves about 100.00 either way.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.6\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-targeting\"],\"hints\":[\"Turn the percentage into a daily budget in vUSD first.\",\"Units = budget ÷ forecast daily move, rounded down.\",\"0.5% of 20,000.00 = 100.00; 100.00 ÷ 2.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"acct\":[5000,30000,1000],\"pct\":[0.4,1,0.1],\"sig\":[0.8,3,0.1]},\"answer\":\"floor(round(acct * pct / 100, 2) / sig + 0.000001)\",\"prompt\":\"Account {acct:2}, daily volatility budget {pct:1}% of it. LABX's forecast daily move is {sig:2} per unit. How many units fit the budget?\",\"explanation\":\"Budget: {pct:1}% of {acct:2} = {round(acct * pct / 100, 2):2} a day. Units = {round(acct * pct / 100, 2):2} ÷ {sig:2} = {round(acct * pct / 100, 2) / sig:2}, rounded down to {answer}.\",\"hint\":\"{round(acct * pct / 100, 2):2} ÷ {sig:2}, rounded down.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.6-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your LABX position is sized to a 20-day volatility forecast. Overnight, volatility jumps to twice its old level. What happens before the forecast catches up?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: the forecast resizes the position the same day\",\"rationale\":\"A 20-day forecast moves only a little each day. It takes weeks to reflect a jump fully.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The position stays too big, so daily swings and losses run above the budget\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The position becomes too small, so you miss the move\",\"rationale\":\"That can happen later, while the jump sits in the window. Right after it, units are still sized for calm.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The stop-loss stops working\",\"rationale\":\"The stop still works. The issue is size: units set for the old volatility swing twice as hard.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Units were set for the old volatility, and a 20-day forecast only creeps up. In the lesson's simulation, the typical daily swing over the 15 days after the jump was 113.41, against a 63.00 budget. Faster forecasts and exposure caps narrow the gap; nothing removes it.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.6\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-targeting\",\"volatility-clustering\"],\"hints\":[\"How quickly does a 20-day average react to one big day?\",\"Units = budget ÷ forecast. Which of the two is out of date?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.6-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Volatility has been falling for months. Your volatility-targeted LABX position has grown from 40 to 90 units, and the forecast keeps dropping.\",\"prompt\":\"What should your plan add?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: lower volatility means lower risk, so more units are safe\",\"rationale\":\"Calm can end abruptly. The bigger position then meets the jump at full size, sized for calm.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A higher volatility target, so units grow faster\",\"rationale\":\"That raises exposure further. Leverage creep is already the problem.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A longer forecast window, so units never change\",\"rationale\":\"A longer window lags even more after a jump. The fix for calm spells is a ceiling on exposure.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A cap on units, exposure or leverage, whatever the forecast says\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"As forecasts fall, volatility targeting raises exposure: leverage creep. When calm ends with a jump, the forecast lags and the oversized position takes the hit. A fixed cap limits the damage. In MARKET//LAB, buying power caps LABX at 99 units at 100.00.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.6\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-targeting\"],\"hints\":[\"What does units = budget ÷ forecast do as the forecast keeps shrinking?\",\"What happens to that position when calm ends?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.7-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why must shadow trades continue while an equity-curve filter has paused the system?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"So the system's live record looks better\",\"rationale\":\"Shadow trades aren't live results. They're recorded to keep measuring the system, not to dress up its record.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They don't need to: you resume after a fixed number of trades\",\"rationale\":\"The filter's rule is the equity average. Without shadow trades the curve stops, and you can't tell when it recovers.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The shadow equity decides when the system climbs back above its average and you resume\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"To double the sample for the next backtest\",\"rationale\":\"They're part of the same system's record, not extra data. Their job is to tell you when to resume.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The filter compares the system's own equity with its average. That curve has to keep moving while you're paused, so every signal is recorded as a shadow trade. Without it you'd resume at random, or never.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.7\",\"concepts\":[\"equity-curve-filter\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.7-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When is an equity-curve filter most likely to help?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When wins and losses come in long streaks\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"When trades are independent of each other\",\"rationale\":\"Then the recent past says nothing about the next trade. The filter only skips trades with the same edge.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"For trend following, where big wins follow losing runs\",\"rationale\":\"That's where it hurts: it's paused right when the big wins arrive.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whenever the system has a positive edge\",\"rationale\":\"An edge alone isn't enough: skipping its trades costs profit unless the skipped ones are worse than the rest.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The filter bets that a falling equity curve keeps falling, which is true only when outcomes cluster. In the simulation, clustered lists went from +61R to +87R and their median drawdown from 37R to 14R; independent lists gave up 38.5R.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.7\",\"concepts\":[\"equity-curve-filter\"],\"hints\":[\"The filter assumes the recent past predicts the next trades. When is that true?\",\"Compare clustered and independent lists.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.12.7-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A trader tests equity-curve filters with averages from 5 to 50 trades on one backtest. The 37-trade average gives the best result, so it goes into the plan.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"None: the backtest found the best length\",\"rationale\":\"It found the best length for this sample. With 46 lengths tried, the winner partly fits noise.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The average should be longer than 50 trades\",\"rationale\":\"No length is right in advance. The issue is choosing it by trying many on the same data.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Filters only work on intraday systems\",\"rationale\":\"Timeframe isn't the issue. Choosing the length by searching the past is.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's overfitting: the best of 46 tries fits past noise and will likely disappoint\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Searching 46 lengths and keeping the best is the overfitting of 3.11.5: the winner fits this sample's noise. Fix a length in advance, check whether outcomes cluster at all, with a runs test, and judge the filter out of sample.\",\"lesson\":\"3.12.7\",\"concepts\":[\"equity-curve-filter\"],\"hints\":[\"How many versions were tried before one was picked?\",\"Would the 37-trade average still be best on new data?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.1-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Account 100,000 vUSD. LXF's N is 2.0 points and each point is worth 50 vUSD. How many contracts is one Turtle unit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"contracts\"},\"explanation\":\"Unit = 1% of equity ÷ (N × value per point) = 1,000 ÷ (2.0 × 50) = 1,000 ÷ 100 = 10 contracts. One N then moves the account by 1,000 vUSD, 1%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.1\",\"concepts\":[\"percent-volatility-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"A unit is sized so that one N moves the account by 1%.\",\"Unit = 1% of equity ÷ (N × value per point).\",\"1,000 ÷ (2.0 × 50) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"acct\":[50000,200000,10000],\"n\":[1,4,0.5],\"pv\":[10,50,5]},\"constraints\":[\"acct * 0.01 / (n * pv) >= 1\"],\"answer\":\"floor(acct * 0.01 / (n * pv))\",\"prompt\":\"Account {acct} vUSD. A synthetic future's N is {n:1} points and each point is worth {pv} vUSD. How many whole contracts is one Turtle unit?\",\"explanation\":\"Unit = 1% of equity ÷ (N × value per point) = {acct * 0.01} ÷ ({n:1} × {pv}) = {acct * 0.01 / (n * pv):2}, rounded down to {answer} contracts.\",\"hint\":\"{acct * 0.01} ÷ ({n:1} × {pv}) = ?, then round down.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.1-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does the time-series momentum rule size each market to the same volatility?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because volatile markets trend more, so they deserve more money\",\"rationale\":\"Volatility isn't a trend signal. Sized by money alone, the wildest market would carry most of the risk, not most of the edge.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"To make every position the same size in vUSD\",\"rationale\":\"That's equal notional, the opposite. Equal money means a 40%-volatility market moves the account five times as much as an 8% one.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"So each market adds similar risk, instead of the wildest one dominating\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"To cut costs, because scaled positions trade less\",\"rationale\":\"Scaling can add trades as volatility changes. Its purpose is equal risk per market, not lower costs.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Scaling makes each market move the account by about the same amount. In the seeded test, the most volatile of 12 markets carried 21.6% of an equal-notional portfolio's risk, and 8.5% of the scaled one's: close to 1 in 12.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.1\",\"concepts\":[\"time-series-momentum\",\"percent-volatility-sizing\"],\"hints\":[\"At the same notional, compare how much a 40%-volatility market and an 8% one move the account.\",\"What share of the risk should each of 12 markets carry?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.1-3\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your trend system, validated out of sample, has just lost 12 trades in a row. Its backtest win rate is 35%, and the Monte Carlo corridor built from its trades puts a 12-loss run inside the 95% band over its trade count.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stop trading it: 12 losses in a row means the edge is gone\",\"rationale\":\"At a 35% win rate, long losing runs are normal. The seeded System 2 had one of 12 in 621 trades while its edge was intact.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Tighten the stops to 1N to lose less per trade\",\"rationale\":\"Changing the rules mid-drawdown creates an untested system. Tighter stops cut the winners too; the backtest no longer describes what you trade.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the size: a win is overdue\",\"rationale\":\"Trades are close to independent, so no win is 'due'. Doubling size into a drawdown raises the risk of ruin.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Check the run against the corridor and the regime, keep following the rules, and log it\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A run inside the corridor is variance, not evidence of a broken edge. Check the regime too: trend systems lose in ranges. Then follow the rules; abandoning a system at its worst point is the classic way to miss its recovery.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.1\",\"concepts\":[\"streaks-and-runs\",\"time-series-momentum\"],\"hints\":[\"Is a 12-loss run inside or outside what the system produces by chance?\",\"What does a 35% win rate imply for losing runs?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.2-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Why do momentum strategies rank on 12-1, skipping the latest month?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because the latest month's prices aren't published yet\",\"rationale\":\"They're available. The month is skipped because one-month returns tend to partly reverse, which would dilute the signal.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because one-month returns tend to reverse, which works against the signal\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"To trade less often\",\"rationale\":\"Turnover is set by how often you rebalance. The skip removes the short-term reversal effect.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because the latest month is the strongest predictor\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: over one month, winners tend to give some back. That's why it's left out.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Short-horizon returns tend to partly reverse, so the latest month works against a 12-month momentum signal. Ranking on months 12 to 2 keeps the persistent part.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cross-sectional-momentum\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When are momentum crashes most likely?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"After a steady, calm bull market\",\"rationale\":\"Calm uptrends are momentum's good times. Crashes come after a deep fall, with volatility high, as the market rebounds.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"During any sharp fall\",\"rationale\":\"During the fall, the short leg holds the falling losers and gains. The damage comes at the turn, when they rebound.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After a deep fall with high volatility, when the market rebounds\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"At random: no market state comes before them\",\"rationale\":\"Daniel and Moskowitz linked them to a state: after market falls, with volatility high, at the rebound.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"After a deep fall, the losers are the high-beta assets. When the market turns up, they rise most, and the short leg loses heavily: past losers +163% against past winners +8% in March–May 2009.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.2\",\"concepts\":[\"momentum-crash\"],\"hints\":[\"Which assets end up as 'losers' after a market crash?\",\"What do high-beta assets do when the market rebounds?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.2-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You compare two momentum versions: (1) hold the top 3 sector ETFs, long only; (2) long the top tenth and short the bottom tenth of 500 stocks.\",\"prompt\":\"How does the risk of version 1 differ from version 2?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It has no crash risk at all: it only buys winners\",\"rationale\":\"It drops the short leg's crash, but it keeps the market's full beta, so a bear market still hits it fully.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It avoids the short-leg crash but carries the market's full beta\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It's riskier only because three ETFs are less diversified than 100 stocks\",\"rationale\":\"Sector ETFs are diversified inside. The bigger change is losing the short leg and taking on the whole market's beta.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"No difference: both are momentum\",\"rationale\":\"Same signal, different exposures. Long-only holds market beta; long–short is roughly market-neutral but carries the short-leg crash.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Long–short momentum is close to market-neutral but crashes when losers rebound. Long-only top-3 has no short leg to crash, but it rises and falls with the market, so it carries bear-market risk instead.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cross-sectional-momentum\",\"momentum-crash\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the short leg do in a rebound?\",\"Without a short leg, what exposure is left?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.3-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You hold 50,000 vUSD long a currency that yields 3.5% a year more than the one you're short. Ignoring the broker's markup, how much carry do you earn per calendar day?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4.79,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"50,000 × 3.5% = 1,750 vUSD a year; ÷ 365 = 4.79 vUSD a day. Wednesday's swap pays three days.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.3\",\"concepts\":[\"carry\"],\"hints\":[\"Yearly carry = exposure × rate differential.\",\"Divide by 365 calendar days.\",\"1,750 ÷ 365 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"x\":[10000,200000,10000],\"d\":[0.5,6,0.25]},\"answer\":\"round(x * d / 100 / 365, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You hold {x} vUSD long a currency that yields {d:2}% a year more than the one you're short. Ignoring the broker's markup, how much carry do you earn per calendar day?\",\"explanation\":\"{x} × {d:2}% = {x * d / 100} vUSD a year; ÷ 365 = {answer:2} vUSD a day.\",\"hint\":\"{x * d / 100} ÷ 365 = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.3-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do carry strategies in currencies, bonds and commodities tend to crash at the same time?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because they all use the same brokers\",\"rationale\":\"Brokers don't drive it. Carry is payment for holding crash risk, and the crash comes in global recessions and liquidity crises for all of them.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Coincidence: their returns are independent\",\"rationale\":\"Koijen and co-authors found the bad periods coincide, in global recessions. That shared timing is why carry diversifies less than it looks.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because central banks change rates in every country on the same day\",\"rationale\":\"Policy rarely moves in lockstep. The common factor is global risk appetite and liquidity, which collapse together.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Each is paid for holding risk that pays out in global crises, when everyone de-risks at once\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Carry pays for bearing crash risk: it's the insurer's side. In a global recession or liquidity crisis, funding dries up and crowded carry positions unwind together, across asset classes. Spreading carry across markets helps less than it seems.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.3\",\"concepts\":[\"carry\",\"carry-unwind\"],\"hints\":[\"Whose risk is a carry trader paid to hold?\",\"What happens to funding and risk appetite everywhere in a global crisis?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.3-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A carry fund shows three years of smooth monthly gains, a Sharpe ratio of 2 and a worst month of −1.5%. It runs at 3× leverage.\",\"prompt\":\"What does that record leave out?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The rare large jump: a negative-skew strategy looks safest just before its crash\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: three years at Sharpe 2 is strong evidence of low risk\",\"rationale\":\"Three calm years can't show a jump that comes once every few years. Negative skew stays invisible until it hits.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only the costs, which lower the Sharpe a little\",\"rationale\":\"Costs matter, but the bigger gap is jump risk. A 10% move at 3× leverage costs 30% at once.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"That a Sharpe of 2 is too low to matter\",\"rationale\":\"A Sharpe of 2 is high. The problem is that it was measured in a calm sample with no crash in it.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Carry earns small steady gains and rarely loses big. A calm sample measures the small gains and misses the crash, so volatility and Sharpe look better than the risk. In the seeded path, a Sharpe of 2.02 over three years, then one jump took the account below where it started.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.3\",\"concepts\":[\"carry\",\"return-skew\"],\"hints\":[\"How often does the jump come, and how long is the record?\",\"Which tail does a calm sample miss?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You scan 4 markets with a real calendar drift, tested with 60% power, and 16 noise markets at the 5% level. What share of your flags do you expect to be false?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"False flags: 16 × 0.05 = 0.8. True flags: 4 × 0.6 = 2.4. False-discovery rate = 0.8 ÷ (0.8 + 2.4) = 0.8 ÷ 3.2 = 25%.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.4\",\"concepts\":[\"false-discovery-rate\"],\"hints\":[\"False-discovery rate = false flags ÷ all flags.\",\"False flags = noise markets × 5%; true flags = real markets × power.\",\"0.8 ÷ (0.8 + 2.4) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"real\":[2,6,1],\"noise\":[5,60,5],\"pw\":[50,90,10]},\"answer\":\"round(noise * 0.05 / (noise * 0.05 + real * pw / 100) * 100, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You scan {real} markets with a real calendar drift, tested with {pw}% power, and {noise} noise markets at the 5% level. What share of your flags do you expect to be false?\",\"explanation\":\"False flags: {noise} × 0.05 = {noise * 0.05:2}. True flags: {real} × {pw / 100:1} = {real * pw / 100:2}. False-discovery rate = {noise * 0.05:2} ÷ {noise * 0.05 + real * pw / 100:2} = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"{noise * 0.05:2} ÷ ({noise * 0.05:2} + {real * pw / 100:2}) = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.4-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A turn-of-month effect passes your hold-out test. Why use it as a filter or tilt rather than a system on its own?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because calendar effects are always fake\",\"rationale\":\"Some, like the turn of the month, have real evidence (grade B). The issue is size: the edge is small against costs.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Its edge per day is small against the costs of trading it alone; as a tilt it adds without new trades\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because the hold-out proves it will continue\",\"rationale\":\"A hold-out lowers the chance it's noise. It proves nothing about the future, and the edge stays small against costs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because filters always beat systems\",\"rationale\":\"Not always. Here, a thin edge traded alone pays costs on every entry; as a tilt on trades you already take, it doesn't.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"About 4 trading days in 21 sit in the window, and the extra return per day is small. Traded alone, it pays spread and commission every month for a thin edge. Used to time or size signals you already take, it adds the edge without adding many trades.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.4\",\"concepts\":[\"after-cost-evaluation\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the extra return per day with the cost of a round trip.\",\"What does a tilt cost that a separate system doesn't?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.4-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A synthetic gas market: every year, spot tends to rise from spring to winter. In April, the December contract already trades 0.55 above spot.\",\"prompt\":\"You plan to buy the December contract in April to catch the seasonal rise. What's the problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"None: the seasonal rise is reliable, so the contract will rise with spot\",\"rationale\":\"The contract's price already includes the expected rise. It gains only if winter spot ends more than 0.55 above today's.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Futures can't be bought that far ahead\",\"rationale\":\"They can. The issue is that the curve already prices the season, so buying it earns only the surprise.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The expected rise is already in the December price; you gain only if winter beats it\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Seasonal patterns only exist in stocks\",\"rationale\":\"Commodity seasons come from real supply and demand. The catch is that the futures curve prices them in advance.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"When a seasonal move is predictable, the futures curve prices it: the December contract already trades near the expected winter level. Buying it earns only the gap between winter spot and that price. Check the curve before trading a commodity season (grade C as a trade).\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.4\",\"concepts\":[\"news-surprise\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the 0.55 premium already contain?\",\"Where does the December contract end up at expiry?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.5-1\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A pair's spread is 1.80 above its mean, and its standard deviation is 0.75. What is the z-score?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":2.4,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"z = (spread − mean) ÷ standard deviation = 1.80 ÷ 0.75 = 2.40. Past +2, so the rule sells the spread.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\",\"concepts\":[\"pairs-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"A z-score measures distance from the mean in standard deviations.\",\"z = (spread − mean) ÷ standard deviation.\",\"1.80 ÷ 0.75 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"dev\":[0.5,4,0.1],\"s\":[0.4,2,0.05]},\"answer\":\"round(dev / s, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A pair's spread is {dev:2} above its mean, and its standard deviation is {s:2}. What is the z-score?\",\"explanation\":\"z = (spread − mean) ÷ standard deviation = {dev:2} ÷ {s:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{dev:2} ÷ {s:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.5-2\",\"topic\":\"technical_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A spread reverts at θ = 0.05 a day. What is its half-life, in days?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":13.9,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"days\"},\"explanation\":\"Half-life = ln 2 ÷ θ = 0.693 ÷ 0.05 = 13.9 days. A time stop near two half-lives is about 28 days.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\",\"concepts\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"cointegration\"],\"hints\":[\"Half-life = ln 2 ÷ θ.\",\"ln 2 ≈ 0.693.\",\"0.693 ÷ 0.05 = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"th\":[0.02,0.3,0.01]},\"answer\":\"round(0.6931 / th, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A spread reverts at θ = {th:2} a day. What is its half-life, in days?\",\"explanation\":\"Half-life = ln 2 ÷ θ = 0.693 ÷ {th:2} = {answer:1} days. A time stop near two half-lives is about {2 * answer:0} days.\",\"hint\":\"0.693 ÷ {th:2} = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.5-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You're short a cointegrated pair's spread, entered at z = 2.1, with a stop at |z| = 3.5. A merger is announced for one of the two companies, and the spread jumps to z = 4.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Add to the position: it's even more stretched\",\"rationale\":\"Stretched only if the old relationship still holds. A merger rewires it, so the mean you're betting on may no longer exist.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Hold until the time stop: it should revert within two half-lives\",\"rationale\":\"The half-life was measured on the old relationship. After a structural break, it no longer describes the spread.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exit at the stop, and re-test the pair before trading it again\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Remove the stop until the merger news settles\",\"rationale\":\"That turns a capped loss into an open one, exactly when the relationship is most likely broken.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A merger is a structural break: the economic link that held the spread together has changed. The stop exists for this. Exit, then re-test the relationship on new data before trading the pair again.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\",\"concepts\":[\"structural-break\",\"pairs-trading\"],\"hints\":[\"What has the merger done to the relationship the spread depended on?\",\"What is the stop for?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.6-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"On the day of its earnings, a stock rose 3.5% while the index fell 0.5%. Its beta is 1.4. What was its abnormal return?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4.2,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Expected from the market: 1.4 × (−0.5%) = −0.7%. Abnormal = actual − expected = 3.5% − (−0.7%) = 4.2%. The stock beat a falling market.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.6\",\"concepts\":[\"event-study\"],\"hints\":[\"Abnormal = actual − beta × market.\",\"1.4 × (−0.5%) = −0.7%.\",\"3.5 − (−0.7) = ?\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"r\":[-6,8,0.5],\"m\":[-2,2,0.25],\"b\":[0.5,2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(r - b * m, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"On the day of its earnings, a stock returned {r:1}% while the index returned {m:2}%. Its beta is {b:1}. What was its abnormal return, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Expected from the market: {b:1} × ({m:2}%) = {b * m:2}%. Abnormal = actual − expected = {r:1} − ({b * m:2}) = {answer:2}%.\",\"hint\":\"{r:1} − ({b * m:2}) = ?\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does an event study average the abnormal returns of many events instead of studying one closely?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because single events are hard to find\",\"rationale\":\"Events are easy to find. One event's move includes news and noise unique to it; only an average over many separates the effect.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because averaging makes the effect bigger\",\"rationale\":\"Averaging doesn't enlarge the effect; it shrinks the noise around it, so the band narrows.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"To avoid having to estimate beta\",\"rationale\":\"Beta is still needed for each abnormal return. Averaging deals with the noise, not the market adjustment.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"One event mixes the effect with its own noise; averaging cancels noise, and the band shows what's left\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Each stock's reaction carries its own news and noise. In the seeded study, single bad-news stocks ranged from −26.55% to +10.55% by day +60. The average over many events, with its confidence band, tells you whether an effect exists.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.6\",\"concepts\":[\"event-study\",\"sample-size\"],\"hints\":[\"What else moves one stock on one day besides the event?\",\"What happens to unrelated noise when you average many cases?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A course sells a method for trading breaking headlines: read the news on social media, then buy or sell within a minute.\",\"prompt\":\"What evidence grade fits retail headline trading, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: news moves prices, so trading the news works\",\"rationale\":\"News does move prices, but by the time a retail trader reads and clicks, faster traders have already moved them.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"D: professionals and machines react first, so a late reaction earns about zero before costs\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"B: it works in some regimes\",\"rationale\":\"B needs peer-reviewed support that has weakened. For retail headline trading the evidence points the other way: latency leaves about zero minus costs.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"F: it loses by design\",\"rationale\":\"F is for negative expectancy built into the structure, like doubling after losses. Headline trading isn't built to lose; it's simply too slow to win.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Unscheduled headlines are traded first by machines with direct feeds. A retail trader enters after the move, with wide spreads and whipsaws, so the expected value is about zero before costs and negative after: grade D.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.6\",\"concepts\":[\"evidence-grades\"],\"hints\":[\"Who sees and trades the headline first?\",\"What's left of the move a minute later?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"McLean and Pontiff found published predictors earned 26% less out of sample, before publication. What does that drop mostly reflect?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Traders copying the strategies after reading the papers\",\"rationale\":\"That's the larger drop after publication, 58%. Before publication nobody could copy it, so the 26% reflects luck in the original search.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Statistical bias: part of each original result was luck from searching the data\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Higher trading costs in later years\",\"rationale\":\"Costs can matter, but this drop mainly measures how much of each finding was selection luck.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Markets turning random\",\"rationale\":\"Most predictors kept some return. The drop says the original estimates were inflated, not that markets turned random.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Out of sample but before publication, nobody was trading on the paper. The 26% drop is the data-mining part: picking results that looked best in one sample. The bigger 58% drop after publication adds traders learning from the papers.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.7\",\"concepts\":[\"evidence-grades\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.7-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do crowded strategies tend to lose together?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because crowded strategies are usually bad ideas\",\"rationale\":\"Many crowded trades had real edges. The problem is the shared exit, not the idea.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because regulators target popular strategies\",\"rationale\":\"Regulation isn't the driver. Forced selling by holders of the same positions is.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Many holders own the same positions, so when some must sell, all sell into the same few buyers\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They don't: more traders means more liquidity\",\"rationale\":\"More traders on the same side adds liquidity only until they all want out. Then liquidity vanishes on that side.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Crowding shrinks the edge and links the exits. A shock forces a few holders to sell, prices move against everyone in the same trade, and more stops and margin calls follow: LTCM in 1998, momentum in 2009, the yen carry unwind in 2024.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.7\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-crowding\"],\"hints\":[\"Who's on the other side when everyone wants to exit?\",\"What happens to stops and margin calls when prices gap?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your system is down 14R. Its Monte Carlo corridor puts the 99th-percentile drawdown at 17R, where your kill switch sits. The market has moved from trending to ranging, a regime where the system has always done worse.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Stop now: 14R is a big loss\",\"rationale\":\"Big, but inside what the system produces by chance and short of your kill switch. Stopping here acts on emotion, not on your rule.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Add a regime filter right away, tuned on the last few months\",\"rationale\":\"An ad-hoc change mid-drawdown, fitted to recent data, is a new untested system. Run it through your rule-change process and test it out of sample.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Keep trading under the rules; propose a regime filter through your rule-change process, tested out of sample first\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Double the size to recover faster\",\"rationale\":\"More size in a drawdown raises the chance of hitting the kill switch. Size follows the rules, not the drawdown.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"At 14R you're inside the corridor and short of the 17R kill switch, and the regime shift explains the losses. The rule says keep trading. A regime filter may help, but it's a rule change: write it down, test it out of sample, and adopt it only if it holds.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.7\",\"concepts\":[\"market-regime\",\"rule-change-governance\"],\"hints\":[\"Where is 14R relative to the corridor and the kill switch?\",\"How should a rule change be made?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.8-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A signal service advertises: '92% winning trades over the last 4 months!' What's the most important thing missing?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The average win and loss after costs, and the number of trades\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The name of the trader\",\"rationale\":\"Who runs it matters less than what the record shows. Without the average loss, costs and trade count, the 92% can't be read.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Which broker they use\",\"rationale\":\"Execution matters, but first you need the record: average win and loss, costs and sample size.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A testimonial from a happy client\",\"rationale\":\"Testimonials are selected stories. They add nothing about expectancy.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A win rate alone can't tell you whether a strategy makes money: 92% small wins and 8% large losses can be negative. Four months is also a small sample. Ask for the average win and loss after costs, the number of trades, and the worst drawdown.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.8\",\"concepts\":[\"performance-claims\",\"evidence-appraisal\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.8-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A study you like is labelled 'working paper'. What does that tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It's wrong\",\"rationale\":\"Many working papers later pass review. The label means unchecked, not false.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It's newer, so more reliable than published studies\",\"rationale\":\"Newer isn't more reliable. A working paper hasn't had independent experts check its method.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Its data has been verified by a journal\",\"rationale\":\"That's what review would add. A working paper hasn't had it yet.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It hasn't been peer-reviewed yet; treat it as provisional until reviewed or replicated\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A working paper circulates before peer review. Its method, data and costs haven't been checked by independent experts, so its grade usually stays at C until it's reviewed and, better, independently replicated.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.8\",\"concepts\":[\"evidence-appraisal\"],\"hints\":[\"What step comes between writing a study and a journal publishing it?\",\"Who has checked its method so far?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.13.8-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A peer-reviewed study found a weekly reversal effect in small stocks. Two independent replications found it too, but about 60% smaller after publication, and mostly gone after realistic spreads in the smallest stocks.\",\"prompt\":\"Which grade fits, and why?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A: peer-reviewed and replicated\",\"rationale\":\"A needs robustness that survives out of sample. Here the effect shrank sharply and depends on costs in the very stocks where it lives.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"B: real and peer-reviewed, but decayed since publication and limited by costs\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"D: it doesn't work\",\"rationale\":\"It was replicated, so it isn't unsupported. Weaker and cost-limited is B, not D.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"F: it loses by design\",\"rationale\":\"F means negative expectancy built in. This effect is real but shrinking.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Peer review and independent replications support the effect, so it isn't C or D. But it shrank after publication and depends on costs: real, decayed, segment-bound. That's B. In two sentences: what's shown, what's missing.\",\"lesson\":\"3.13.8\",\"concepts\":[\"evidence-appraisal\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"hints\":[\"Was it replicated? Then it's above C and D.\",\"What does decay since publication mean on the grade scale?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.1-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LXO, the synthetic crude future, is 1,000 units a contract, with a 0.01 tick worth 10.00 vUSD. You're long 1 LXO at 72.40 and it settles at 73.25. Your gain, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":850,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"73.25 − 72.40 = 0.85, or 85 ticks. 85 × 10.00 = 850.00, the same as 0.85 × 1,000 units. The multiplier turns every price move into money.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\",\"concepts\":[\"futures-contract\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the move in price, then in ticks.\",\"0.85 is 85 ticks of 0.01.\",\"85 × 10.00.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"entry\":[65,80,0.05],\"ticks\":[20,150,5],\"n\":[1,3,1]},\"answer\":\"ticks * 10 * n\",\"prompt\":\"LXO, the synthetic crude future, is 1,000 units a contract, with a 0.01 tick worth 10.00 vUSD. You're long {n} LXO at {entry:2} and it settles at {entry + ticks * 0.01:2}. Your gain, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"{entry + ticks * 0.01:2} − {entry:2} = {ticks * 0.01:2}, or {ticks} ticks. {ticks} × 10.00 × {n} = {answer:2}. The multiplier turns every price move into money.\",\"hint\":\"{ticks} ticks × 10.00 × {n}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.1-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're long 3 LXF (50 vUSD a point). Yesterday's settlement was 5,010.25; today's is 4,987.75. How much variation margin leaves your account today, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3375,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"The fall is 22.50 points. 22.50 × 50 × 3 = 3,375.00, debited in cash tonight whatever your stop says. Tomorrow's P&L starts from 4,987.75.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\",\"concepts\":[\"mark-to-market\"],\"hints\":[\"Variation margin is the day's move, settlement to settlement, in money.\",\"5,010.25 − 4,987.75 = 22.50 points.\",\"22.50 × 50 × 3.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[1,4,1],\"drop\":[5,60,0.25]},\"answer\":\"drop * 50 * n\",\"prompt\":\"You're long {n} LXF (50 vUSD a point). Yesterday's settlement was 5,010.25; today's is {5010.25 - drop:2}. How much variation margin leaves your account today, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"The fall is {drop:2} points. {drop:2} × 50 × {n} = {answer:2}, debited in cash tonight whatever your stop says.\",\"hint\":\"{drop:2} × 50 × {n}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.1-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"LXM's margin is 1,250.00 a contract. A trader with 100,000.00 buys 6 LXM, posts 7,500.00 of margin and sets no stop. 'The margin is the most I can lose,' he says.\",\"prompt\":\"What's wrong with his reasoning?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: losses stop at the margin posted\",\"rationale\":\"Margin is a deposit. Losses settle in cash every day and keep coming out of the account beyond it, until he closes or is closed out.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Losses settle daily in cash and can exceed the margin; only a stop and the size limit the risk\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The margin is a fee, so he loses it whatever happens\",\"rationale\":\"Margin comes back when the position closes, plus gains or minus losses. It's collateral, not a cost.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The exchange closes the trade before losses pass the margin\",\"rationale\":\"Falling below maintenance brings a margin call, not a guaranteed exit at that level. Gaps and limit moves can carry losses far past it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Margin is collateral held against daily losses. Each evening the loss is debited in cash, and below maintenance he must top up. Without a stop, nothing caps the loss: 6 LXM lose 30.00 for every point.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\",\"concepts\":[\"mark-to-market\",\"leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what happens to a loss bigger than the margin.\",\"Variation margin is paid daily, in cash, from the account.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does the contract name LXFU7 tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The LXF contract expiring in June 2027\",\"rationale\":\"June is M. U is September: H, M, U and Z are the quarterly months.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The LXF contract expiring in September 2027\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An unadjusted continuous LXF chart\",\"rationale\":\"Month codes name single contracts. Continuous charts are built from many of them.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The seventh LXF contract listed this year\",\"rationale\":\"The digit is the year of expiry, here 2027; the letter is the month.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Root LXF, month code U for September, year 7 for 2027. Index futures list March (H), June (M), September (U) and December (Z).\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\",\"concepts\":[\"contract-roll\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"At the roll, the old LXF contract closes at 4,880.00 and the new one at 4,904.50. You back-adjust by adding the gap to earlier prices. A bar before the roll closed at 4,812.25. Its adjusted close?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4836.75,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"The gap is 4,904.50 − 4,880.00 = 24.50. Every price before the roll shifts up by it: 4,812.25 + 24.50 = 4,836.75. Prices after the roll stay as traded.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\",\"concepts\":[\"continuous-contract\"],\"hints\":[\"Find the roll gap first.\",\"4,904.50 − 4,880.00 = 24.50.\",\"Add it to 4,812.25.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"old\":[4500,5500,0.25],\"gap\":[5,45,0.25],\"d\":[10,150,0.25]},\"answer\":\"old - d + gap\",\"prompt\":\"At the roll, the old LXF contract closes at {old:2} and the new one at {old + gap:2}. You back-adjust by adding the gap to earlier prices. A bar before the roll closed at {old - d:2}. Its adjusted close?\",\"explanation\":\"The gap is {old + gap:2} − {old:2} = {gap:2}. Every price before the roll shifts up by it: {old - d:2} + {gap:2} = {answer:2}. Prices after the roll stay as traded.\",\"hint\":\"{old - d:2} + {gap:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A breakout system tested on an unadjusted continuous LXF chart shows its best trades entering on roll days, buying 20–40-point jumps and keeping the gains.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: roll days are liquid, so those fills are realistic\",\"rationale\":\"Liquidity isn't the issue. The jumps are where the chart switches contracts; no holder ever saw that price change.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The roll gaps are jumps no position ever earned, so those signals and gains are fake\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The test should hold each contract until delivery\",\"rationale\":\"Holding to delivery isn't an option for most traders, and it doesn't fix the chart. Back-adjusting removes the gaps.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing: a gap is a gap, and real traders saw it too\",\"rationale\":\"A real gap moves one contract's price. A roll gap exists only where the chart joins two contracts.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Each roll joins two contracts that trade at different prices. Unadjusted, the joint looks like a jump, and a breakout rule buys it. Back-adjusting shifts earlier prices by each gap, so signals see only moves a holder could have had.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\",\"concepts\":[\"continuous-contract\",\"adjusted-price-data\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask what happens to the chart on roll day.\",\"Did anyone holding a contract see that jump?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Spot is 80.00 and the one-month future 81.20. If both stay where they are, what does a long earn per monthly roll, in %? Use a minus sign for a cost.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-1.48,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"The future converges from 81.20 to 80.00: 80.00 ÷ 81.20 − 1 = −1.48%. That's contango: the long pays roll yield every month, even with spot flat.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\",\"concepts\":[\"roll-yield\"],\"hints\":[\"The future ends the month at spot.\",\"Return per roll = spot ÷ future − 1.\",\"80.00 ÷ 81.20 − 1.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"spot\":[50,100,1],\"diff\":[-2,2,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(diff) >= 0.2\"],\"answer\":\"round((spot / (spot + diff) - 1) * 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Spot is {spot:2} and the one-month future {spot + diff:2}. If both stay where they are, what does a long earn per monthly roll, in %? Use a minus sign for a cost.\",\"explanation\":\"The future converges from {spot + diff:2} to {spot:2}: {spot:2} ÷ {spot + diff:2} − 1 = {answer:2}%. Futures above spot (contango) cost a long; futures below spot (backwardation) pay it.\",\"hint\":\"{spot:2} ÷ {spot + diff:2} − 1.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What most often puts a commodity's futures curve into backwardation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"High storage costs\",\"rationale\":\"Storage is part of the cost of carry. It lifts futures above spot: contango.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"High interest rates\",\"rationale\":\"Financing is a carrying cost too. Higher rates lift futures relative to spot.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Low inventories, so having the commodity now is worth a premium\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Traders expecting prices to rise\",\"rationale\":\"Expected rises would lift futures above spot, not below. Backwardation signals scarcity now.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"When stocks are low, users pay up for the barrel or bushel in hand: the convenience yield. It can outweigh financing and storage, so spot trades above the futures. Ample stocks do the opposite.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\",\"concepts\":[\"term-structure\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"A commodity ETF that holds front-month futures and rolls monthly fell 20% in a year. Spot was flat over the same year. The fund's fee is 0.75% a year.\",\"prompt\":\"What best explains the gap?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The fee\",\"rationale\":\"0.75% a year explains less than one point of a 20-point gap.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Backwardation made it overpay for each contract\",\"rationale\":\"In backwardation futures sit below spot, so a rolled long gains as they converge up.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Spot was quoted wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The fund never held spot; it held futures, and their curve explains the loss.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Steep contango: each roll bought a contract above spot that then converged down\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A long that rolls in contango buys each contract above spot and loses the premium as it converges. About 1.8% a month compounds to roughly −20% a year with spot flat. Check a fund's holdings and their curve, not just its name.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\",\"concepts\":[\"roll-yield\",\"term-structure\"],\"hints\":[\"The fund holds futures, not spot.\",\"Ask what a monthly roll costs when futures sit above spot.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.4-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Long a synthetic grain future at 800.00, stop at 780.00. The daily limit is 5%. It locks limit-down two days in a row; trading reopens at the second day's limit price, where your stop fills. Loss in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3.9,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Day 1: 800.00 × 0.95 = 760.00. Day 2: 760.00 × 0.95 = 722.00. Loss 78.00 a unit ÷ 1R of 20.00 = 3.9R. The stop couldn't fill while the market was locked.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.4\",\"concepts\":[\"gap-risk\",\"commodity-drivers\"],\"hints\":[\"Each limit day is 5% below the previous settlement.\",\"800.00 → 760.00 → 722.00.\",\"Loss per unit ÷ (800.00 − 780.00).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"entry\":[200,1000,10],\"stopPct\":[1,4,0.5],\"limit\":[3,7,1]},\"constraints\":[\"stopPct < limit\"],\"answer\":\"round((1 - (1 - pct(limit)) * (1 - pct(limit))) / pct(stopPct), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Long a synthetic grain future at {entry:2}, stop at {entry * (1 - pct(stopPct)):2}. The daily limit is {limit}%. It locks limit-down two days in a row; trading reopens at the second day's limit price, where your stop fills. Loss in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Day 1: {entry * (1 - pct(limit)):2}. Day 2: {entry * (1 - pct(limit)) * (1 - pct(limit)):2}. Loss {entry - entry * (1 - pct(limit)) * (1 - pct(limit)):2} a unit ÷ 1R of {entry * pct(stopPct):2} = {answer:2}R. The stop couldn't fill while the market was locked.\",\"hint\":\"Loss per unit ÷ {entry * pct(stopPct):2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.4-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does a weekly inventory report do for a commodity market?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Publishes scheduled data on how much of the commodity is in storage\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Sets the official price for the coming week\",\"rationale\":\"Prices come from trading. The report adds supply information, and the market reprices on the surprise.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Shows how speculators are positioned\",\"rationale\":\"That's the COT report. An inventory report counts the commodity in storage.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Announces the exchange's new margin levels\",\"rationale\":\"Margin changes come from the exchange or clearing house, not from supply data.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Inventory reports are scheduled supply information. Prices move on the surprise against expectations: a build when a draw was expected pushes price down. Spreads widen around the release, as with any scheduled event.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.4\",\"concepts\":[\"commodity-drivers\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.4-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"It's June. You buy the January natural gas future, expecting winter demand to lift gas prices.\",\"prompt\":\"Why is this a weak trade on its own?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Gas prices don't rise in winter\",\"rationale\":\"Winter demand is real. The issue is that the January contract's price already includes it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"January contracts can't be traded in June\",\"rationale\":\"Gas futures list many months ahead; you can buy January in June.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The curve already prices winter: January trades above summer contracts, so you profit only if winter beats that\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The January future won't follow winter gas prices\",\"rationale\":\"It converges to spot in January, so it does. What it already contains is the expected winter premium.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Seasonal demand is public, so the curve builds it in: winter contracts trade at a premium to summer ones. Buying January in June earns only if winter turns out tighter than priced. As a trade, seasonality rates C.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.4\",\"concepts\":[\"term-structure\",\"commodity-drivers\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the January price with the summer contracts.\",\"What does the market already expect for winter?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.5-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LXC's daily volatility is 4%. It trades every day of the year. Annualised volatility, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":76.42,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Volatility scales with the square root of time. Crypto trades 365 days a year: 4% × √365 = 4% × 19.10 = 76.4%. Markets that trade about 252 days a year use √252.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\",\"concepts\":[\"crypto-market-structure\"],\"hints\":[\"Volatility grows with the square root of the number of trading days.\",\"√365 ≈ 19.10.\",\"4% × 19.10.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"daily\":[2,6,0.5]},\"answer\":\"round(daily * 19.105, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"LXC's daily volatility is {daily:1}%. It trades every day of the year. Annualised volatility, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Volatility scales with the square root of time. Crypto trades 365 days a year: {daily:1}% × √365 = {daily:1}% × 19.10 = {answer:1}%.\",\"hint\":\"{daily:1}% × 19.10.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.5-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A US broker that is a SIPC member fails. What does SIPC protection cover?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Any losses in the account, market losses included\",\"rationale\":\"SIPC replaces missing assets at a failed broker. It never covers market losses.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Crypto held at any US-licensed exchange\",\"rationale\":\"Crypto that isn't a security is outside SIPC, whoever holds it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Missing securities and cash, up to a limit, but not crypto that isn't a security\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Futures positions at the broker\",\"rationale\":\"SIPC doesn't cover futures. Futures customers rely on the segregation of their funds instead.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"SIPC restores customers' missing securities and cash when a member broker fails, up to set limits. It doesn't cover market losses, futures, or crypto that isn't a security. Venue failure risk on crypto stays with you.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\",\"concepts\":[\"counterparty-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"SIPC is about missing assets, not losses.\",\"Which assets count as securities?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.5-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You hold a mid-sized token. Next week a scheduled unlock releases tokens equal to 8% of the supply now trading.\",\"prompt\":\"What's the sound plan?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Buy more now: unlocks rally\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing about an unlock forces a rally. New supply that holders may sell is the usual concern.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Treat it as a scheduled supply event: cut size or stay flat, and watch funding and open interest\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Change nothing: scheduled events are priced in\",\"rationale\":\"Being expected doesn't make the day calm. Like an earnings date, it fixes when a gap may come.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Short as much as you can: the price must fall\",\"rationale\":\"The unlock may already be priced, and crowded shorts pay funding and get squeezed. 'Must' isn't a plan.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"An unlock adds supply on a known date, like a report or an earnings release. You can't know the reaction, but you know when risk rises: size down or stand aside, and check positioning, such as funding and open interest, going in.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\",\"concepts\":[\"crypto-market-structure\"],\"hints\":[\"Treat it like any scheduled event.\",\"You know when risk rises, not which way price goes.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.6-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You're short 40,000.00 vUSD of the LXC perpetual. Funding for this 8-hour interval is +0.02%. What do you receive, in vUSD? Use a minus sign if you pay.\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":8,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Positive funding: longs pay shorts. 40,000.00 × 0.02% = 8.00, received by your short for this interval. If the rate turned negative, you'd pay instead.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\",\"concepts\":[\"funding-rate\"],\"hints\":[\"Positive funding flows from longs to shorts.\",\"Funding is charged on the position value.\",\"40,000.00 × 0.0002.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"size\":[10000,200000,5000],\"rate\":[-0.05,0.05,0.005]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(rate) >= 0.005\"],\"answer\":\"round(size * pct(rate), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You're short {size:2} vUSD of the LXC perpetual. Funding for this 8-hour interval is {rate:3}%. What do you receive, in vUSD? Use a minus sign if you pay.\",\"explanation\":\"Positive funding: longs pay shorts; negative funding: shorts pay longs. {size:2} × {rate:3}% = {answer:2} for your short this interval.\",\"hint\":\"{size:2} × {rate:3}%.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.6-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Funding on the LXC perpetual has been positive for three weeks, often 0.05% per 8 hours. What does that tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Price will fall soon, so short now\",\"rationale\":\"Crowded longs can stay crowded for weeks. Funding describes positioning; it doesn't time the turn.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Shorts are paying longs to hold\",\"rationale\":\"Positive funding flows from longs to shorts.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The perpetual trades below its spot index\",\"rationale\":\"Positive funding means the perpetual trades above the index; funding pulls it back down.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Longs are crowded and paying to stay in: a condition that raises squeeze risk, not a signal\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Positive funding means the perpetual trades above spot and longs pay to hold. Persistent, high funding marks a crowded long side, which can unwind fast. It's a condition to size for, not a trigger to trade against (grade C).\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\",\"concepts\":[\"funding-rate\"],\"hints\":[\"Who pays when funding is positive?\",\"Does crowding tell you when it ends?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.6-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You run a basis trade: long 50,000.00 of LXC spot, short 50,000.00 of the LXC perpetual. Funding, positive for months, flips to −0.05% per 8 hours.\",\"prompt\":\"What happens to the trade?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: it's hedged\",\"rationale\":\"The legs offset on price, not on funding. Funding is the trade's income, and it just changed sign.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The short perpetual now pays funding: about 0.15% a day, 75.00, until the rate turns\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It earns more: negative funding favours shorts\",\"rationale\":\"Negative funding means shorts pay longs, and your perpetual leg is the short.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The spot leg pays the funding\",\"rationale\":\"Spot has no funding. Only the perpetual leg pays or receives it.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The basis trade earns funding on its short perpetual while the rate is positive. At −0.05% the short pays: 50,000.00 × 0.05% × 3 = 75.00 a day. A carry trade works until the carry flips.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\",\"concepts\":[\"funding-rate\",\"perpetual-swap\"],\"hints\":[\"Which leg has funding at all?\",\"Negative funding: shorts pay longs.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.7-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An isolated long at 2,000.00, 20× leverage, maintenance margin 0.5%. Approximate liquidation price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1910,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"2,000.00 × (1 − 1 ÷ 20 + 0.005) = 2,000.00 × 0.955 = 1,910.00, a 4.5% fall. The margin covers 5%; liquidation comes when only the 0.5% maintenance is left.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidation-price\"],\"hints\":[\"Liquidation ≈ entry × (1 − 1 ÷ leverage + maintenance).\",\"1 ÷ 20 = 0.05.\",\"2,000.00 × 0.955.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"entry\":[1000,5000,50],\"lev\":[5,50,5],\"mmr\":[0.4,1,0.1]},\"constraints\":[\"1 / lev > pct(mmr)\"],\"answer\":\"round(entry * (1 - 1 / lev + pct(mmr)), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"An isolated long at {entry:2}, {lev}× leverage, maintenance margin {mmr:1}%. Approximate liquidation price?\",\"explanation\":\"{entry:2} × (1 − 1 ÷ {lev} + {pct(mmr):3}) = {answer:2}. The margin covers {100 / lev:2}%; liquidation comes when only the {mmr:1}% maintenance is left.\",\"hint\":\"{entry:2} × (1 − {1 / lev:3} + {pct(mmr):3}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.7-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does cross margin change, compared with isolated margin?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A losing position can draw on the whole account, so one loss can drain everything\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Positions on cross margin can't be liquidated\",\"rationale\":\"They can. Cross margin delays liquidation by sharing the balance, so when it comes, it can take more.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Isolated margin guarantees that your stop fills\",\"rationale\":\"Margin mode doesn't affect fills. Stops can slip in fast markets either way.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Cross margin always means higher leverage\",\"rationale\":\"Leverage is chosen separately. The mode decides which money backs each position.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Isolated margin caps what one position can lose at its own margin. Cross margin lets every position use the account's balance: fewer liquidations from small moves, but a large one can take the whole account.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidation-price\",\"leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"Which money backs a position in each mode?\",\"Can one position reach the rest of the account?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.7-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"During a crash, the venue closes your profitable LXC short at the market price, with a note: auto-deleveraging. You never hit a stop or a liquidation price.\",\"prompt\":\"Why did it happen?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Your short reached its own liquidation price\",\"rationale\":\"A short in profit during a crash is far from its liquidation price, and the note says ADL, not liquidation.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Liquidated longs closed below zero equity and the insurance fund couldn't cover it, so the venue closed opposite winners\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The venue took a fee for the profit\",\"rationale\":\"Fees don't close positions. ADL is a loss-sharing step when the insurance fund runs short.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your stop triggered on the mark price\",\"rationale\":\"No stop was hit. ADL closes positions by the venue's ranking, usually most profit and leverage first.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In a cascade, some liquidations fill below zero equity. The insurance fund absorbs those losses; when it can't, the venue auto-deleverages, closing opposite positions, usually the most profitable and levered first. Even winners carry the venue's rules.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidation-cascade\"],\"hints\":[\"Who pays when a liquidation closes below zero?\",\"What happens when that reserve runs out?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.8-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A bond has a duration of 4.5. Yields rise 0.5 percentage points. Approximate price change, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-2.25,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Price change ≈ −duration × yield change = −4.5 × 0.5 = −2.25%. Yields up, price down. Convexity makes the real move slightly better than the estimate.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.8\",\"concepts\":[\"bond-duration\"],\"hints\":[\"Price moves opposite to yield.\",\"−duration × change in yield, in points.\",\"−4.5 × 0.5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"dur\":[2,12,0.5],\"dy\":[-1.5,1.5,0.25]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(dy) >= 0.25\"],\"answer\":\"round(-dur * dy, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A bond has a duration of {dur:1}. Yields change by {dy:2} percentage points. Approximate price change, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Price change ≈ −duration × yield change: {dur:1} × {abs(dy):2} = {abs(answer):2}%, opposite to the yield move, so {answer:2}%. Convexity makes the real move slightly better than the estimate.\",\"hint\":\"{dur:1} × {abs(dy):2}, with the sign opposite to the yield move.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.8-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You're long 4 LXN contracts, DV01 80.00 vUSD each. Yields fall 12 basis points. Your P&L, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3840,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"4 × 80.00 = 320.00 per basis point. Yields fell, so prices rose: 320.00 × 12 = +3,840.00.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.8\",\"concepts\":[\"dv01\"],\"hints\":[\"Total DV01 = contracts × DV01 each.\",\"Yields down means bond prices up.\",\"4 × 80.00 × 12.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[2,6,1],\"bp\":[-25,25,1]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(bp) >= 2\"],\"answer\":\"-n * 80 * bp\",\"prompt\":\"You're long {n} LXN contracts, DV01 80.00 vUSD each. Yields change by {bp} basis points (positive = rise). Your P&L, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"{n} × 80.00 = {n * 80:2} per basis point. Yields moved {bp} bp, and a long gains when yields fall and loses when they rise: {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{n * 80:2} per basis point × {abs(bp)} bp; check the sign.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-3.14.8-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"You trade only stocks. Why should a surprise jump in interest rates matter to you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It doesn't: rates matter only to bond traders\",\"rationale\":\"Rates set the discount rate in valuations and the cost of leverage. Stocks react within seconds of rate surprises.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Higher rates always lift stocks, because they signal a strong economy\",\"rationale\":\"Growth news can help, but a higher discount rate weighs on valuations. The sign depends on the regime (3.6.3).\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Rates feed the discount rate, funding costs and futures fair value, so one surprise moves them all\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only through dividend yields\",\"rationale\":\"Dividends are one channel. Valuations, CFD and margin funding, and index futures pricing all move with rates.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A rate surprise changes the discount rate in every valuation, most for long-duration growth stocks, raises the cost of leveraged positions and moves index futures' fair value. Stocks react through all of these at once.\",\"lesson\":\"3.14.8\",\"concepts\":[\"bond-duration\",\"equity-duration\"],\"hints\":[\"List what a stock price depends on that involves a rate.\",\"Think valuation, financing and futures.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You sell a LABX call and collect the premium. Who carries the obligation?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The buyer: they must buy LABX at the strike at expiry\",\"rationale\":\"The buyer holds a right, not a duty. They exercise only when it pays and can let the option lapse.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The exchange, which takes both sides\",\"rationale\":\"Clearing guarantees the trade, but the obligation to deliver falls on a writer like you, once assigned.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You, the writer: you must sell LABX at the strike if assigned\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nobody: an option is only a bet on direction\",\"rationale\":\"An option is a contract that settles. The writer's obligation is exactly what the premium pays for.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Buying an option buys a right; selling one sells an obligation. As the call's writer you collected the premium, and you must sell LABX at the strike if the holder exercises and you are assigned.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"option-contract\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX is at 100.00. A put with strike 95 is:\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In the money: its strike is below the price\",\"rationale\":\"That's the rule for calls. A put is the right to sell; selling at 95 what trades at 100 is worth nothing now.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Out of the money\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"At the money\",\"rationale\":\"At the money means the strike is at or very near the price. 95 is 5.00 away.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"In the money, because puts profit from falls\",\"rationale\":\"It would profit if LABX fell below 95, but today it has no intrinsic value, so it's out of the money.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A put's intrinsic value is max(K − S, 0) = max(95 − 100, 0) = 0. With no intrinsic value it's out of the money: its whole 0.97 premium is extrinsic.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"option-moneyness\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX is at 100.00. A put with strike 105 is quoted at 6.08. What is its extrinsic value, in vUSD per unit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.08,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A put's intrinsic value is max(K − S, 0) = 105 − 100 = 5.00. Extrinsic value = premium − intrinsic = 6.08 − 5.00 = 1.08, the part paid for time and volatility.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"concepts\":[\"option-moneyness\"],\"hints\":[\"Split the premium into intrinsic and extrinsic value.\",\"A put's intrinsic value is the strike minus the price, if positive.\",\"105 − 100 = 5.00. Subtract it from 6.08.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"k\":[101,115,1],\"e\":[0.3,2.5,0.01]},\"answer\":\"e\",\"prompt\":\"LABX is at 100.00. A put with strike {k} is quoted at {k - 100 + e:2}. What is its extrinsic value, in vUSD per unit?\",\"explanation\":\"A put's intrinsic value is max(K − S, 0) = {k} − 100 = {k - 100:2}. Extrinsic value = premium − intrinsic = {k - 100 + e:2} − {k - 100:2} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"{k} − 100 = {k - 100:2}. Subtract it from {k - 100 + e:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.2-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy a LABX put with strike 95 for 0.97. What is its breakeven at expiry?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":94.03,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A long put gains as LABX falls, and it must fall far enough below the strike to repay the premium: 95 − 0.97 = 94.03. Below that, profit; above it, a loss of up to 0.97.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"option-payoff\"],\"hints\":[\"A put pays only below its strike.\",\"Breakeven of a long put = strike − premium.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"k\":[80,120,5],\"p\":[0.2,4,0.01]},\"answer\":\"k - p\",\"prompt\":\"You buy a LABX put with strike {k} for {p:2}. What is its breakeven at expiry?\",\"explanation\":\"A long put must fall far enough below the strike to repay the premium: {k} − {p:2} = {answer:2}. Below that, profit; above it, a loss of up to {p:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Strike − premium: {k} − {p:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.2-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You sell the 100-strike LABX put for 2.86. What is your maximum loss per unit?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"2.86\",\"rationale\":\"That's your maximum gain: the premium you keep if LABX stays above 100.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"100.00\",\"rationale\":\"That forgets the 2.86 you collected, which offsets part of the loss.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Unlimited\",\"rationale\":\"An unlimited loss belongs to the short call. A put's loss stops when LABX reaches zero.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"97.14\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"If LABX falls to zero you must buy it at 100 and it's worth nothing: −100.00, offset by the 2.86 premium. Maximum loss = 100 − 2.86 = 97.14 a unit, 971.40 a contract.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"option-payoff\"],\"hints\":[\"How low can LABX go?\",\"At zero you pay the strike for something worthless, minus the premium you kept.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.2-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You wrote a LABX call and receive an assignment notice. What does it mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"You must sell LABX at the strike\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"You may buy LABX at the strike if you wish\",\"rationale\":\"That's the holder's kind of right, not yours. Assignment is where the writer's obligation is enforced.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You must return the premium you collected\",\"rationale\":\"The premium is yours whatever happens. Assignment obliges you to deliver LABX at the strike.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The option has expired worthless\",\"rationale\":\"Worthless options aren't exercised, so no one is assigned. A notice means the holder exercised.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The holder exercised the call and you, a writer, were assigned: you must deliver LABX at the strike. If you don't own it, you sell at the strike what you must buy at the market price.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\",\"concepts\":[\"exercise-and-assignment\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.3-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Rates are zero and LABX is at 100.00. The 105-strike call costs 1.08. What should the 105-strike put with the same expiry cost?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":6.08,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Parity at r = 0: C − P = S − K, so P = C − S + K = 1.08 − 100 + 105 = 6.08. That's 5.00 of intrinsic value plus the same 1.08 of extrinsic value as the call.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"put-call-parity\"],\"hints\":[\"Put-call parity at zero rates: C − P = S − K.\",\"Rearrange for the put: P = C − S + K.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"k\":[101,115,1],\"c\":[0.2,3,0.01]},\"answer\":\"c + k - 100\",\"prompt\":\"Rates are zero and LABX is at 100.00. The {k}-strike call costs {c:2}. What should the {k}-strike put with the same expiry cost?\",\"explanation\":\"Parity at r = 0: P = C − S + K = {c:2} − 100 + {k} = {answer:2}. That's {k - 100:2} of intrinsic value plus the call's {c:2} of extrinsic value.\",\"hint\":\"P = C − S + K = {c:2} − 100 + {k}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.3-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which position has the same payoff at expiry as a covered call: long LABX plus a short call at strike K?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A long put at K\",\"rationale\":\"A long put gains as LABX falls, while the covered call loses then. Their payoffs point opposite ways.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A long call at K\",\"rationale\":\"Long stock plus a long put copies a long call. A covered call contains a short option.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A short put at K with the cash to buy the stock: a cash-secured put\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A short call at K on its own\",\"rationale\":\"Without the stock, the short call loses without limit as LABX rises. The stock is what covers it.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Rearrange parity: S − C = K·e^(−rT) − P. Long stock plus a short call equals cash plus a short put. Same payoff, same downside: a covered call is no safer than a cash-secured put.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"synthetic-position\",\"put-call-parity\"],\"hints\":[\"Start from C − P = S − K and move the terms around.\",\"What's left when you write S − C?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.3-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Rates are zero. The LABX 100-call is quoted at 2.86 and the 100-put at 2.50. Buying LABX, buying the put and selling the call locks in 0.36 a unit before costs.\",\"prompt\":\"Why isn't this free money for a retail account?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The 0.36 depends on where LABX ends\",\"rationale\":\"The combination is worth exactly 100.00 at expiry whatever LABX does. That certainty is what makes it an arbitrage.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Parity holds only for American options\",\"rationale\":\"Parity holds exactly for European options like LABX's. American options add early-exercise caveats, not the reverse.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Brokers forbid holding a put and a short call together\",\"rationale\":\"It's a standard combination, called a conversion. The obstacle is cost, not permission.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Spreads and commissions on three legs eat most or all of it, and such gaps close in milliseconds\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Half-spreads of 0.05 on three legs and two commissions of 0.05 on LABX cost 0.25, leaving 0.11. With option spreads of 0.40 the trade loses 0.19. And in liquid markets faster traders close such gaps first.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\",\"concepts\":[\"put-call-parity\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Count every leg you have to trade.\",\"Each leg pays half its spread; LABX also pays commission in and out.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.4-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which Black–Scholes input can't you read off your screen?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"LABX's price\",\"rationale\":\"The price is quoted live. The input you have to estimate is how much LABX will move.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The strike\",\"rationale\":\"The strike is written in the contract. The unknown is the volatility over the option's life.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The volatility over the option's life\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The time to expiry\",\"rationale\":\"The expiry date is in the contract, so the time left is a simple count.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Price, strike, time and the rate are all known. Future volatility is not: you must estimate it, which is why the market quotes options by their implied volatility.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"option-pricing-model\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.4-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Use the quick rule 0.4 × S × σ × √T. LABX is at 100.00, implied volatility is 20% and expiry is one year away. What is the estimated at-the-money call price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":8,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"0.4 × 100 × 0.20 × √1 = 8.00. The full model gives 7.97: near the money, the rule is close.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"option-pricing-model\"],\"hints\":[\"For one year, √T = 1.\",\"0.4 × 100 × 0.20.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s\":[50,200,10],\"v\":[10,60,5]},\"answer\":\"round(0.4 * s * v / 100, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Use the quick rule 0.4 × S × σ × √T. LABX is at {s:2}, implied volatility is {v}% and expiry is one year away. What is the estimated at-the-money call price?\",\"explanation\":\"0.4 × {s:2} × {v / 100:2} × √1 = {answer:2}. For one year √T = 1, so the estimate is 0.4 × price × volatility.\",\"hint\":\"0.4 × {s} × {v / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.4-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"LABX gaps 8% overnight on news. Which Black–Scholes assumption does that break?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"European exercise\",\"rationale\":\"Exercise style is about when the holder may exercise. A gap breaks the model's picture of how the price moves.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Continuous trading with no jumps\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A fixed strike\",\"rationale\":\"The strike stays as written. What fails is the smooth, continuous path the model assumes for LABX.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Zero interest rates\",\"rationale\":\"Rates are an input you can set. The gap breaks the assumption that the price moves continuously.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The model assumes LABX moves continuously, with lognormal returns and constant volatility. A gap is a jump: no trading in between and no chance to hedge. Real markets also show fat tails and skew that the model leaves out.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.4\",\"concepts\":[\"option-pricing-model\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens between the close and the next open during a gap?\",\"The model pictures a price path with no breaks in it.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.5-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is the delta of the 30-day at-the-money LABX put, strike 100 with LABX at 100.00?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"+0.51\",\"rationale\":\"That's the call. A put gains as LABX falls, so its delta is negative.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"−1.00\",\"rationale\":\"That's a deep in-the-money put, moving one for one with LABX. At the money it's about half.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"−0.49\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"0\",\"rationale\":\"Zero is a far out-of-the-money put. At the money the put moves about half as much as LABX, the other way.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Put delta = call delta − 1 = 0.51 − 1 = −0.49. For each 1.00 LABX rises, the put loses about 0.49, and gains about as much when LABX falls 1.00.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"option-delta\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.5-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold 20 contracts of a LABX call with delta 0.26; a contract covers 10 units. How many LABX do you sell short to be delta neutral?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":52,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"units\"},\"explanation\":\"Position delta = contracts × 10 × delta = 20 × 10 × 0.26 = 52. Short 52 LABX, and small moves barely change the combined value.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"delta-hedging\"],\"hints\":[\"Position delta = contracts × units per contract × delta.\",\"20 × 10 × 0.26.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"c\":[2,40,1],\"d\":[0.1,0.9,0.01]},\"constraints\":[\"abs(c * 10 * d - floor(c * 10 * d) - 0.5) > 0.05\"],\"answer\":\"round(c * 10 * d, 0)\",\"prompt\":\"You hold {c} contracts of a LABX call with delta {d:2}; a contract covers 10 units. How many LABX do you sell short to be delta neutral?\",\"explanation\":\"Position delta = {c} × 10 × {d:2} = {c * 10 * d:1}. Short {answer} LABX, and small moves barely change the combined value.\",\"hint\":\"{c} × 10 × {d:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.5-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Where is an option's gamma largest?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Deep in the money, months from expiry\",\"rationale\":\"Deep in the money, delta is already near 1 and barely changes. Gamma is small there.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Far out of the money, close to expiry\",\"rationale\":\"Near expiry a far out-of-the-money option stays worthless through small moves: its delta, and its gamma, stay near zero.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"At the money, months from expiry\",\"rationale\":\"At the money is right, but with months left the hump is low and wide: 0.028 at 120 days.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"At the money, close to expiry\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Near expiry, a small move decides whether an at-the-money option ends worth something or nothing, so its delta swings between 0 and 1. The 100-call's gamma: 0.056 with 30 days left, 0.30 with one day.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\",\"concepts\":[\"option-gamma\"],\"hints\":[\"Gamma is how fast delta changes.\",\"Where can a small move flip an option from worthless to valuable?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.6-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What sign does theta have for a short put position?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Negative: time costs you money\",\"rationale\":\"That's the buyer's side. The writer collected the premium and gains as it decays.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Positive: time decay works for you\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Zero: only direction matters\",\"rationale\":\"All else equal, the option loses value each day, and that loss is the writer's gain.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It depends on whether LABX rises\",\"rationale\":\"Theta measures time alone, all else equal. A short option's theta is positive whatever LABX does.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A long option loses extrinsic value each day: negative theta. The writer is on the other side, so a short put or short call has positive theta. The price of that steady income is negative gamma.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"option-theta\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.6-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which LABX option has the most vega?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"At the money, one day to expiry\",\"rationale\":\"With one day left there's little time for volatility to matter: vega 0.021, against 0.114 at 30 days.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"At the money, one year to expiry\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Deep in the money, 30 days to expiry\",\"rationale\":\"Deep in the money options are mostly intrinsic value and behave like stock: little to reprice when volatility changes.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Far out of the money, 30 days to expiry\",\"rationale\":\"It has some vega, 0.050 for the call 110, but less than the at-the-money option and far less than a long-dated one.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Vega grows with time to expiry and peaks at the money. At IV 25%, the 100-strike option's vega is 0.021 with one day left, 0.114 with 30 days and 0.396 with a year.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"option-vega\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.6-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"The 100-call has delta 0.51, gamma 0.056, theta −0.048 a day and vega 0.114. Over one day LABX falls 1.00 and implied volatility rises 2 points. Estimated change in the call's price?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.3,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Delta: −0.51 × 1 = −0.51. Gamma: ½ × 0.056 × 1² = +0.028, positive whichever way LABX moves. Theta: −0.048. Vega: 0.114 × 2 = +0.228. Total ≈ −0.30; full repricing gives −0.31.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\",\"concepts\":[\"option-vega\",\"option-theta\"],\"hints\":[\"Add four terms: delta, gamma, theta and vega.\",\"The gamma term is ½ × gamma × move², positive either way.\",\"−0.51 + 0.028 − 0.048 + 0.228.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m\":[1,3,1],\"v\":[1,4,1]},\"answer\":\"round(-0.51 * m + 0.028 * m * m - 0.048 + 0.114 * v, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"The 100-call has delta 0.51, gamma 0.056, theta −0.048 a day and vega 0.114. Over one day LABX falls {m}.00 and implied volatility rises {v} points. Estimated change in the call's price?\",\"explanation\":\"Delta takes 0.51 × {m} = {0.51 * m:2}. Gamma adds ½ × 0.056 × {m}² = {0.028 * m * m:3}, whichever way LABX moves. Theta takes 0.048. Vega adds 0.114 × {v} = {0.114 * v:3}. Net: a loss of about {0 - answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"−0.51 × {m} + 0.028 × {m * m} − 0.048 + 0.114 × {v}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.7-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is implied volatility?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The volatility LABX actually showed over the past 30 days\",\"rationale\":\"That's realised, or historical, volatility. Implied volatility is read from option prices, looking forward.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The volatility that makes a pricing model match the option's market price\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A volatility the exchange sets for margin\",\"rationale\":\"Exchanges set margins, not option prices. Implied volatility comes from the prices traders agree on.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A forecast that is right on average\",\"rationale\":\"On average it has run above the volatility that followed: it includes a risk premium.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Put the market price into the model and solve for σ: that's implied volatility. It mixes the market's forecast with a premium, which is why it has tended to sit above the volatility that followed.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-volatility\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.7-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On a 30-day LABX expiry, implied volatility is 31% at the 90 strike, 25% at 100 and 22% at 110. What does this shape show?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The market expects LABX to rise\",\"rationale\":\"Skew prices the size and fear of falls, not a forecast of direction.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A put skew: downside protection is priced richer\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Calls at 110 are a bargain to buy\",\"rationale\":\"Lower IV isn't a mispricing by itself. The shape reflects demand for crash protection on the put side.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A pricing error: IV should be equal across strikes\",\"rationale\":\"Only in the Black–Scholes world. Real surfaces have skew, and that's information, not error.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Higher implied volatility on low strikes is a put skew, typical of index and stock options: investors pay up for protection against crashes, so downside puts carry richer volatility than calls.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-surface\"],\"hints\":[\"Which strikes carry the highest implied volatility: the low ones or the high ones?\",\"Low strikes are where downside puts sit.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.7-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX is at 100.00 and the at-the-money straddle costs 4.20. What move is the market pricing in, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":4.2,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Implied move ≈ straddle ÷ price = 4.20 ÷ 100.00 = 4.2%: the market prices a move of about ±4.20 by expiry.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\",\"concepts\":[\"implied-move\",\"implied-volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"Divide the straddle by the price.\",\"4.20 ÷ 100.00, as a percentage.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s\":[40,200,10],\"m\":[20,120,1]},\"answer\":\"m / 10\",\"prompt\":\"LABX is at {s:2} and the at-the-money straddle costs {s * m / 1000:2}. What move is the market pricing in, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"Implied move ≈ straddle ÷ price = {s * m / 1000:2} ÷ {s:2} = {answer:1}%: the market prices a move of about ±{s * m / 1000:2} by expiry.\",\"hint\":\"{s * m / 1000:2} ÷ {s:2}, as a percentage.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.8-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You own LABX at 100.00 and sell a 110-strike call for 0.32. What is the covered call's maximum gain per unit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10.32,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Above 110 the stock is called away at 110: (110 − 100) + 0.32 = 10.32. Any rise beyond 110 belongs to the call's buyer.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.8\",\"concepts\":[\"covered-call\"],\"hints\":[\"Above the strike, you sell LABX at the strike.\",\"Gain on the stock up to the strike, plus the premium.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"k\":[101,120,1],\"c\":[0.1,3,0.01]},\"answer\":\"k - 100 + c\",\"prompt\":\"You own LABX at 100.00 and sell a {k}-strike call for {c:2}. What is the covered call's maximum gain per unit?\",\"explanation\":\"Above {k} the stock is called away at {k}: ({k} − 100) + {c:2} = {answer:2}. Any rise beyond {k} belongs to the call's buyer.\",\"hint\":\"({k} − 100) + {c:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.8-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does a protective put hold through an overnight gap when a stop-loss doesn't?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Puts are executed before stops at the open\",\"rationale\":\"It isn't about queue order. The put's value doesn't depend on any fill: it pays the strike.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The put lets you sell at the strike whatever price LABX opens at; a triggered stop just becomes a market order\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The put's premium is refunded after a gap\",\"rationale\":\"The premium is spent either way. What the put gives back is the right to sell at the strike.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Stops are cancelled overnight\",\"rationale\":\"Stops stay working; the trouble is their fill price after the gap, not their existence.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"A stop at 95.00 triggers on the gap and fills near the open, say 88.00. A 95 put lets you sell at 95 however far LABX opens: the loss stays at 5.00 plus the 0.97 premium.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.8\",\"concepts\":[\"protective-put\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a stop become once it triggers?\",\"Does the put's payout depend on any fill price?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.8-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"In which regime does a covered call fall furthest behind simply owning LABX?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A quiet range\",\"rationale\":\"In a range the covered call beats the stock by the premium it collected.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A slow decline\",\"rationale\":\"In a decline the premium cushions the loss, so the covered call does a little better than the stock.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A crash\",\"rationale\":\"In a crash both lose almost the same; the covered call loses slightly less, by the premium.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A strong rally\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The short call caps the gain at the strike plus the premium: 6.08 with the 105 call. In a +12% rally the stock makes 12.00, so the covered call lags by 5.92. It sells the upside of the distribution.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.8\",\"concepts\":[\"covered-call\",\"market-regime\"],\"hints\":[\"The short call caps the gain at strike plus premium.\",\"Where does a cap cost the most?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.9-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy the 100/95 bear put spread for a debit of 1.89: long the 100 put, short the 95 put. What is its breakeven at expiry?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":98.11,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A bear put spread profits as LABX falls below the long strike by more than the debit: 100 − 1.89 = 98.11. Its max gain is 5 − 1.89 = 3.11 and its max loss the 1.89 debit.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\",\"concepts\":[\"vertical-spread\"],\"hints\":[\"The long put does the work; the short put only caps it.\",\"Breakeven = long strike − debit.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"k\":[90,120,1],\"d\":[0.5,4.5,0.01]},\"answer\":\"k - d\",\"prompt\":\"You buy the {k}/{k - 5} bear put spread for a debit of {d:2}: long the {k} put, short the {k - 5} put. What is its breakeven at expiry?\",\"explanation\":\"A bear put spread profits as LABX falls below the long strike by more than the debit: {k} − {d:2} = {answer:2}. Its max gain is 5 − {d:2} = {5 - d:2} and its max loss the {d:2} debit.\",\"hint\":\"Long strike − debit: {k} − {d:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.9-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A credit spread with strikes 5 apart collects a credit of 1.20. What is its maximum loss per unit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3.8,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"At worst the spread is worth its full width, 5.00, against you; the credit you kept offsets part of it: 5 − 1.20 = 3.80. A contract risks 38.00.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\",\"concepts\":[\"vertical-spread\"],\"hints\":[\"The most a vertical can be worth is its width.\",\"Max loss of a credit spread = width − credit.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[2,10,1],\"c\":[0.2,4,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"c < w - 0.5\"],\"answer\":\"w - c\",\"prompt\":\"A credit spread with strikes {w} apart collects a credit of {c:2}. What is its maximum loss per unit?\",\"explanation\":\"At worst the spread is worth its full width, {w:2}, against you; the credit you kept offsets part of it: {w} − {c:2} = {answer:2}. A contract risks {answer * 10:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Width − credit: {w} − {c:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.9-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A bull put spread wins about 79% of the time at expiry. Why doesn't that prove an edge?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"79% is too low; an edge needs 90% or more\",\"rationale\":\"No win rate proves an edge by itself. What matters is the win rate together with the size of wins and losses.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Win rates only matter for debit spreads\",\"rationale\":\"Win rate means the same for any strategy: nothing without the payoffs.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Its wins of up to 0.76 against losses of up to 4.24 make the model's expected value about zero\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Model probabilities are always wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The model may be imperfect, but the problem here is the payoff sizes, which wipe out the win rate.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The spread wins about 0.74 on average four times in five and loses about 2.72 on average the fifth: about zero before costs. A high win rate bought with large losses is a payoff shape, not an edge.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\",\"concepts\":[\"vertical-spread\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"Expectancy needs both the win rate and the payoff sizes.\",\"Compare the most it can win with the most it can lose.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.10-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You buy the 30-day 100-strike LABX straddle for 5.72. What is its upper breakeven at expiry?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":105.72,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"A long straddle needs LABX to move by its whole cost, either way: 100 + 5.72 = 105.72 above, and 100 − 5.72 = 94.28 below.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.10\",\"concepts\":[\"long-volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"Only one leg pays at expiry, and it must cover both premiums.\",\"Strike + total cost.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"k\":[50,150,5],\"c\":[1,12,0.01]},\"answer\":\"k + c\",\"prompt\":\"You buy the 30-day {k}-strike LABX straddle for {c:2}. What is its upper breakeven at expiry?\",\"explanation\":\"A long straddle needs LABX to move by its whole cost, either way: {k} + {c:2} = {answer:2} above, and {k} − {c:2} = {k - c:2} below.\",\"hint\":\"{k} + {c:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.10-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You bought a straddle before an event and the big move came, 4%. Why can it still lose?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Straddles lose whenever direction is guessed\",\"rationale\":\"A straddle doesn't care about direction. It cares whether the move beats the price paid.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The move was smaller than the one already priced in, and implied volatility collapsed after the event\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"One day of theta ate the gain\",\"rationale\":\"A day of decay is small. The collapse of implied volatility after the event did most of the damage.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The put leg expired worthless, so the trade failed\",\"rationale\":\"One leg always ends worthless. The question is whether the other covers the whole cost.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The 6.62 straddle priced a 6.6% move at IV 60%. After the report IV fell to 25%, and a 4% move left it worth 4.35: −2.27. Being right about movement isn't enough; you must beat the implied move.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.10\",\"concepts\":[\"iv-crush\",\"long-volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"What did the straddle's price already assume?\",\"What happens to implied volatility once the news is out?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.10-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Strangle or straddle on the same event: what do you gain with the strangle, and what do you give up?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A higher cost but closer breakevens\",\"rationale\":\"That describes the straddle: 6.62 with breakevens at 93.38 and 106.62.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The same cost, with more vega\",\"rationale\":\"The strangle is cheaper: 2.80 against 6.62. Out-of-the-money options carry less vega, not more.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A lower cost and maximum loss, but LABX must move further to profit\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A strangle can't lose more than half its premium\",\"rationale\":\"If LABX ends between the strikes, both legs expire worthless: the whole 2.80 is lost.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The 95/105 strangle costs 2.80 against the straddle's 6.62, so less is at risk. But it needs LABX below 92.20 or above 107.80, against 93.38 and 106.62 for the straddle.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.10\",\"concepts\":[\"long-volatility\"],\"hints\":[\"Out-of-the-money options are cheaper than at-the-money ones.\",\"Where do the strangle's breakevens sit compared with the straddle's?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.11-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is the volatility risk premium?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The extra return from buying options before events\",\"rationale\":\"Event options usually have the premium priced in. Buyers pay it rather than earn it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A fee exchanges charge on volatile days\",\"rationale\":\"It's not a fee. It's the gap between implied volatility and the volatility that follows.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A guaranteed profit from selling options\",\"rationale\":\"Paid on average, not guaranteed: sellers are paid for bearing rare, large losses like February 2018.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The tendency of implied volatility to exceed the volatility that follows, which pays option sellers on average\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Option buyers pay for insurance. On index options, implied volatility has on average exceeded realised volatility (Carr and Wu, 2009), so sellers collect a premium: the compensation for bearing crash risk.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\",\"concepts\":[\"volatility-risk-premium\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare implied volatility with the volatility that actually followed.\",\"Who is paid on average, the insurance buyer or the seller?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.11-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An iron condor has wings 5 wide and collects a credit of 1.10. What is its maximum loss per unit?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":3.9,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Only one side can lose at expiry, and at worst it loses the wing width, 5.00, less the total credit kept: 5 − 1.10 = 3.90. A contract risks 39.00.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\",\"concepts\":[\"short-volatility-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"LABX can only end beyond one wing.\",\"Max loss = wing width − total credit.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[2,10,1],\"c\":[0.2,3,0.05]},\"constraints\":[\"c < w - 0.5\"],\"answer\":\"w - c\",\"prompt\":\"An iron condor has wings {w} wide and collects a credit of {c:2}. What is its maximum loss per unit?\",\"explanation\":\"Only one side can lose at expiry, and at worst it loses the wing width, {w:2}, less the total credit kept: {w} − {c:2} = {answer:2}. A contract risks {answer * 10:2}.\",\"hint\":\"Wing width − credit: {w} − {c:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.1.11-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Why is a high Sharpe ratio no argument for a short-volatility strategy?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Sharpe ratios can't be computed for options\",\"rationale\":\"They can be computed for any return series. The trouble is what the Sharpe ratio leaves out.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Its smooth returns may not yet include the rare large loss, and Sharpe ignores skew\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Short-volatility strategies always have low Sharpe ratios\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: steady premiums make the Sharpe look excellent until the tail arrives.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A high Sharpe means too little risk was taken\",\"rationale\":\"A high Sharpe can hide large risk. Short volatility's risk sits in a tail the sample may not contain.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The Sharpe ratio uses the average and the standard deviation of returns. A record of small steady gains can miss the rare crash entirely, so the ratio looks best just before the loss. Judge by stress loss and Expected Shortfall instead.\",\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\",\"concepts\":[\"short-volatility-risk\",\"sharpe-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the Sharpe ratio measure, and what does it ignore?\",\"What happens to the ratio before the first big loss?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.1-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which cost does implementation shortfall count that slippage on your fills never shows?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The spread you crossed on filled units\",\"rationale\":\"Fill prices already include the spread you crossed, so slippage on fills shows it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The levels your order walked through in the book\",\"rationale\":\"Walking the book worsens your fill prices, so it shows up in slippage too.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The move you missed on units that never filled\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The gap between the price you expected and your fill\",\"rationale\":\"That gap is the definition of slippage itself.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Slippage is measured on fills, so units that never filled leave no trace. Implementation shortfall prices them at the move they missed from the decision price: the opportunity cost, often the largest line.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"implementation-shortfall\",\"slippage\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.1-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You decide to buy 20,000 LABX at 50.00. You fill 15,000 at an average of 50.08; the rest never fills, and the price closes at 50.30. With 0.05% commission, what is the implementation shortfall in bps?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":30.8,\"tolerance\":0.2,\"unit\":\"bps\"},\"explanation\":\"Filled: 15,000 × 0.08 = 1,200.00. Commission: 0.05% × 751,200.00 = 375.60. Opportunity: 5,000 × 0.30 = 1,500.00. Total 3,075.60 on the 1,000,000.00 you meant to trade: 30.8 bps.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"implementation-shortfall\"],\"hints\":[\"Measure every price against the decision price, 50.00.\",\"Unfilled units cost the move they missed: 5,000 × 0.30.\",\"Total 3,075.60; divide by 20,000 × 50.00 and multiply by 10,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"qk\":[10,40,5],\"p\":[40,120,10],\"fill\":[50,90,5],\"a\":[0.04,0.12,0.02],\"b\":[0.2,0.6,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round((qk * 10 * fill * a + commission(qk * 10 * fill * (p + a)) + qk * 10 * (100 - fill) * b) / (qk * 1000 * p) * 10000, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"You decide to buy {qk * 1000} LABX at {p:2}. You fill {qk * 10 * fill} at an average of {p + a:2}; the rest never fills, and the price closes at {p + b:2}. With 0.05% commission, what is the implementation shortfall in bps?\",\"explanation\":\"Filled: {qk * 10 * fill} × {a:2} = {qk * 10 * fill * a:2}. Commission: 0.05% × {qk * 10 * fill * (p + a):2} = {commission(qk * 10 * fill * (p + a)):2}. Opportunity: {qk * 10 * (100 - fill)} × {b:2} = {qk * 10 * (100 - fill) * b:2}. Divided by the {qk * 1000 * p:2} you meant to trade: {answer:1} bps.\",\"hint\":\"Total {qk * 10 * fill * a + commission(qk * 10 * fill * (p + a)) + qk * 10 * (100 - fill) * b:2}; divide by {qk * 1000 * p:2} and multiply by 10,000.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.1-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A portfolio manager decides to buy at 10:00, with LABX at 100.00. The trader sends the order at 10:30, with LABX at 100.40. Why measure the shortfall from 100.00?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The 0.40 move while the order waited is a real cost of the decision, and the arrival price would hide it\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The arrival price can't be known after the trade\",\"rationale\":\"Arrival prices are recorded. The problem is that measuring from them leaves the delay out.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The decision price is always the lower of the two\",\"rationale\":\"Price can rise or fall during a delay. Either way, the delay is part of the cost.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Commission is charged on the decision price\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is charged on fill value. The reference price decides which costs get counted.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The paper portfolio's return starts when the decision is made. Measured from the arrival price, the 0.40 delay vanishes and slow hand-offs look free. From the decision price, every cost of turning the idea into a position is counted.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\",\"concepts\":[\"implementation-shortfall\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask which price the paper portfolio would have paid.\",\"What happens to the 0.40 if you start measuring at 10:30?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.2-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A fund finishes buying LABX at 100.32. Over the next hour the price eases to 100.21 and stays there. What was the 0.11 that faded?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Permanent impact\",\"rationale\":\"Permanent impact is the part that stays: here, the 0.21 that held.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Temporary impact\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The bid-ask spread\",\"rationale\":\"The spread is the width of the quotes at any time; it doesn't fade after an order.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"New negative news\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing new happened. The fade is the book refilling after the buying stopped.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Temporary impact is the price pressure of demanding liquidity, and it fades when you stop and the book refills. The 0.21 that stayed is permanent impact: what the market learned from the buying.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"market-impact\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.2-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An order is 1% of the day's volume, and daily volatility is 3%. With Y = 1, what market impact does the square-root law predict, in bps?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":30,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"bps\"},\"explanation\":\"√1% = √0.01 = 0.1. Impact ≈ 3% × 0.1 = 0.3%, or 30 bps.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"square-root-impact\"],\"hints\":[\"Impact ≈ Y × σ × √(Q ÷ V).\",\"√0.01 = 0.1.\",\"3% × 0.1 = 0.3%. Convert to bps: 1% = 100 bps.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s\":[1,4,0.5],\"r\":[1,4,1]},\"answer\":\"10 * s * r\",\"prompt\":\"An order is {r * r}% of the day's volume, and daily volatility is {s:1}%. With Y = 1, what market impact does the square-root law predict, in bps?\",\"explanation\":\"√{r * r}% = {r / 10:1}. Impact ≈ {s:1}% × {r / 10:1} = {s * r / 10:2}%, or {answer} bps.\",\"hint\":\"{s:1}% × {r / 10:1} = {s * r / 10:2}%. Convert to bps: 1% = 100 bps.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.2-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does a 10,000 vUSD account barely feel market impact on LABX, while a fund buying 200,000 units pays about 63 bps?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Impact grows with order size relative to daily volume, and 100 units is a tiny share\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Brokers protect retail orders from impact\",\"rationale\":\"No broker can remove impact: it comes from the size you ask the market to absorb.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Funds pay higher commissions\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is a fee on each fill. Impact is a separate price move caused by your size.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Impact only applies to market orders\",\"rationale\":\"Large limit orders worked over hours move the price too: impact follows size, not order type.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Impact ≈ σ × √(Q ÷ V). 100 units is 0.005% of the 2,000,000 daily volume: 1.4 bps, below the 5 bps half spread. 200,000 units is 10%: about 63 bps. Retail costs are spread and commission; a fund's is impact.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\",\"concepts\":[\"square-root-impact\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare each order with the 2,000,000 units LABX trades in a day.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.3-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You must buy 50,000 LABX today, and daily volume is 2,000,000. You expect the price to drift up during the day as others learn what you know.\",\"prompt\":\"Which algorithm fits best?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"VWAP over the whole day\",\"rationale\":\"VWAP leaves much of the order for the afternoon, exposed to the rise you expect.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An IS algorithm that front-loads the order\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"TWAP over the whole day\",\"rationale\":\"Even slices until the close keep most of the order waiting while the price drifts up.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"POV at 2%\",\"rationale\":\"At 2% of volume, 50,000 units need 2,500,000 traded: more than a whole day.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"When you expect the price to move against you, timing risk dominates: front-loading pays more impact but leaves less of the order exposed. That's what an implementation-shortfall algorithm does.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"execution-algorithm\",\"impact-timing-tradeoff\"],\"hints\":[\"Which cost grows when the price is expected to run away: impact or timing risk?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.3-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You switch a 50,000-unit LABX order from POV 20% to VWAP over the whole day. Which cost rises?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Market impact\",\"rationale\":\"Slower trading lowers impact: VWAP's participation is 2.5% of volume, not 20%.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Commission\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is 0.05% of each fill, whatever the schedule.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Timing risk\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The half spread\",\"rationale\":\"Each child order still crosses half the spread; the schedule doesn't change that.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"VWAP spreads the order over the day, so impact falls, but the unfilled part stays exposed to price moves for hours. In this lesson's race, the 95th percentile rose from 151.5 to 213.6 bps.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"impact-timing-tradeoff\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens to the unfilled units while you wait?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.3-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A trader's algorithm buys exactly 1,000 LABX every 5 minutes, on the minute. Why is that a problem?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Others can spot the pattern and buy ahead of the next slice, raising its price\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Exchanges reject orders sent at regular times\",\"rationale\":\"Exchanges accept them. The danger is what other traders learn from the pattern.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Equal slices always pay more commission\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is a share of fill value; slicing barely changes the total.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Regular slices can't fill in thin markets\",\"rationale\":\"They can fill. The problem is that their rhythm advertises a large buyer.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"A regular rhythm leaks information: others infer a large buyer and trade ahead, so each child order pays more. Real algorithms randomise sizes and timing to hide their footprint.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\",\"concepts\":[\"execution-algorithm\"],\"hints\":[\"Who else watches the tape, and what does a steady rhythm tell them?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.4-1\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is a stale quote?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A resting order whose price no longer reflects the latest information\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A quote that has been in the book for more than a day\",\"rationale\":\"Age isn't the point: a quote goes stale the moment news changes value, even a millisecond after it was placed.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"An order the exchange has cancelled\",\"rationale\":\"A cancelled order can't trade. A stale quote is dangerous because it is still live.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A price shown late on a delayed data feed\",\"rationale\":\"A delayed feed is a display problem. A stale quote is a live order at an outdated price.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"When news changes value, resting orders priced before it are stale. Faster traders trade against them before their owners can cancel, which is why a resting order is a free option for anyone faster.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"latency-arbitrage\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.4-2\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Which of these is one of the traits the SEC uses to describe high-frequency trading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Holding positions for weeks\",\"rationale\":\"HFT holds positions for very short periods, often seconds or less.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Ending the trading day close to flat\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trading only at the open and the close\",\"rationale\":\"HFT trades all day; its speed matters most when quotes change.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Always taking the other side of retail orders\",\"rationale\":\"That's not a trait in the SEC's list. HFT trades with everyone, and many orders never meet a retail one.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The SEC's 2010 list: very fast programs; co-location and direct data feeds; very short holding periods; many orders cancelled soon after they're sent; and ending the day close to flat.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"latency-arbitrage\"],\"hints\":[\"Think about how long HFT firms want to carry risk.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.4-3\",\"topic\":\"order_types\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Payrolls are due at 14:30. You have a limit buy 0.10 below the market that would rest through the release. Your platform takes 150 ms to cancel an order.\",\"prompt\":\"What is your realistic fix?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Move to a faster broker so you can cancel in time\",\"rationale\":\"Races between the fastest firms are decided in microseconds. A retail setup can't win them.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Widen the limit to 0.30 below the market\",\"rationale\":\"A bigger move still finds it stale. Any order resting through news is a free option for someone faster.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Cancel it or let it expire before 14:30, and decide after the release\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Switch it to a GTC order\",\"rationale\":\"Time in force doesn't help if the order still rests at 14:30. GTC rests through the release just the same.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"You can't be faster than professionals, so don't race them. Make sure nothing rests at the release: cancel or let orders expire before it, then price new orders off the new quotes.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.4\",\"concepts\":[\"latency-arbitrage\",\"limit-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Which orders can go stale? Only the ones resting when the news lands.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.5-1\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"At 14:10 ET, the S&P 500 is down 13% from yesterday's close. What happens?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Trading stops for the rest of the day\",\"rationale\":\"That's Level 3, at −20%. Level 2, at −13%, is a 15-minute halt.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: only Level 1 can halt the market\",\"rationale\":\"Level 1 (−7%) and Level 2 (−13%) each halt for 15 minutes when hit before 15:25 ET.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"All US stocks halt for 15 minutes (Level 2)\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only the stocks that fell most are paused\",\"rationale\":\"Single-stock pauses are LULD. A 13% index fall triggers a market-wide halt.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Market-wide circuit breakers use the S&P 500's fall from the previous close: −7% (Level 1) and −13% (Level 2) halt all US stocks for 15 minutes if hit before 15:25 ET; −20% (Level 3) closes the market for the day.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-halts\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.5-2\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A stock halts at 50.00 after a fast fall. Your sell stop is at 49.50. Trading reopens by auction at 46.00. Where does your stop most likely fill?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"49.50, the stop price\",\"rationale\":\"No trade printed between 50.00 and 46.00, so 49.50 was never available.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"50.00, the halt price\",\"rationale\":\"Your stop hadn't triggered at 50.00. It triggers at the first trade after the halt.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Near 46.00, the reopening price\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nowhere: stops cancel during a halt\",\"rationale\":\"Stops usually stay live through a halt. They trigger at the reopening print and become market orders.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A stop is a trigger, not a price. The reopening auction prints 46.00, the stop triggers there and becomes a market order, filling near 46.00: 3.50 worse than the stop on every unit.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-halts\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"hints\":[\"Where is the first trade after the halt?\",\"Once triggered, a stop is a market order.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.5-3\",\"topic\":\"stop_loss\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Before a halt-prone event, you replace your sell stop at 49.50 with a stop-limit: stop 49.50, limit 48.00. What do you gain, and what do you risk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"You're guaranteed to sell at 49.50\",\"rationale\":\"No order guarantees a price through a gap. The limit only sets the worst price you accept.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"You never sell below 48.00, but you may not sell at all\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"You sell at 48.00 whatever happens\",\"rationale\":\"If the stock reopens below 48.00, the limit can't fill, and you keep the position.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Nothing changes: both fill the same way\",\"rationale\":\"A triggered stop becomes a market order; a triggered stop-limit becomes a limit order with a floor.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Once triggered, a stop-limit becomes a limit order: it fills only at 48.00 or better. If the stock reopens at 46.00, you keep the position and its risk. The limit caps the price, not the loss.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\",\"concepts\":[\"trading-halts\",\"stop-limit-order\"],\"hints\":[\"What does a triggered stop-limit become?\",\"What if the reopening price is below the limit?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.6-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is the 16:00 London FX fix?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A daily limit on how far a currency may move\",\"rationale\":\"Spot FX has no daily price limits. The fix is a reference price, not a cap.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The rate a central bank sets each afternoon\",\"rationale\":\"No central bank sets it: it's calculated from market trades in the window.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The moment FX positions roll to the next day\",\"rationale\":\"That's the rollover, at 17:00 New York, which is 22:00 in London.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A benchmark rate set from trades in a short window around 16:00, used by funds\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Funds value and trade currencies at the WM/Reuters benchmark, set from trades in a 5-minute window around 16:00 London. Their orders crowd into the window, so volume jumps for a mechanical reason.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-clock\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.6-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On a quarterly witching day, LABX trades twice its normal volume and closes 0.4% higher. Why is that weak evidence of a buyer with a view?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Volume on Fridays doesn't count\",\"rationale\":\"All volume is real trading. The question is why it happened, and the calendar explains it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Much of the volume is expiring futures and options being closed, rolled or hedged on schedule\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Prices can't move on expiry days\",\"rationale\":\"They can and do move; hedging flows can push prices or hold them near strikes.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"High volume always means sellers are in control\",\"rationale\":\"Volume has no direction by itself. On witching days it's mostly scheduled flow.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"On witching days many futures and options expire together, and positions are closed, rolled and re-hedged on schedule. That flow had to trade then, whatever anyone thought about value, so the spike says little about direction.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"liquidity-clock\"],\"hints\":[\"What is scheduled to happen on that date?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.2.6-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"A dealer is short gamma on LABX options and hedges its delta. LABX rises. What does its hedging do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It sells LABX, damping the rise\",\"rationale\":\"That's a long-gamma dealer: its delta grows as price rises, so it sells to rebalance.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing until expiry\",\"rationale\":\"Delta hedgers rebalance as price moves, not only at expiry.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It buys more options\",\"rationale\":\"Delta hedging trades the underlying, LABX, not more options.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It buys LABX, which can amplify the rise\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Short gamma means the position's delta falls as price rises: the dealer gets shorter. To stay hedged it buys LABX into the rise, which can amplify the move. Long-gamma dealers do the opposite and damp moves, one explanation for pinning.\",\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\",\"concepts\":[\"option-pinning\"],\"hints\":[\"As LABX rises, does a short-gamma position get longer or shorter?\",\"To stay hedged, the dealer trades against that change.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.1-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does White's Reality Check test?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Whether each rule, on its own, is significant at 5%\",\"rationale\":\"That's the single test it replaces. Judged one at a time, the best of many rules usually looks significant by luck.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Whether the best rule of the whole search beats the benchmark by more than the best rule would by luck alone\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Whether the rule still works on out-of-sample data\",\"rationale\":\"Out-of-sample testing is a separate step. The Reality Check corrects for the search on the data that were searched.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whether the backtest has look-ahead bias\",\"rationale\":\"Look-ahead is a coding error. The Reality Check assumes clean data and asks how much of the winner is luck from searching.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The Reality Check resamples the returns of every rule tried, recentred to no edge, and records the best result each time. The real winner must beat that distribution of lucky maxima: on R0, a winner with |t| = 2.07 got p = 0.40.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"reality-check-testing\",\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"Which rule does it judge, and against what?\",\"It builds the distribution of the best result when no rule has an edge.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.1-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You test 200 lookbacks of one momentum rule. Why is a Bonferroni correction too strict here?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It treats 200 near-copies as 200 independent chances, but the search behaves like far fewer tests\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Bonferroni only works for fewer than 20 tests\",\"rationale\":\"It works for any number of tests. The issue is that it assumes the worst case: fully independent tests.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Momentum rules can't be tested for significance\",\"rationale\":\"They can, with a t-statistic on their returns. The question is how to correct for 200 related tries.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It isn't too strict: 200 variants need the full correction\",\"rationale\":\"On R0 the best of these 200 crossed Bonferroni's 3.66 in 1.3% of searches, not the 5% it allows: the bar is too high.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Neighbouring lookbacks trade almost the same days, so their results move together. In 2,000 seeded R0 searches, the 200 lookbacks behaved like about 48 independent tests: the best crossed Bonferroni's 3.66 in only 1.3%, against a 5% target.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"multiple-testing\",\"reality-check-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"Do neighbouring lookbacks give independent results?\",\"Bonferroni assumes every test is a fresh, independent chance.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.1-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Over six months you tried 12 ideas on LABX. You dropped 9 after a quick look, swept 30 settings of one of the others, and now present its best setting.\",\"prompt\":\"Which tries belong in your trial count, N?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Only the 30 settings of the final idea\",\"rationale\":\"The quick looks and the other ideas were tries on the same data too. Leaving them out understates N.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only the 3 ideas you kept\",\"rationale\":\"Dropped ideas count: each was a chance for luck to hand you a winner.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Every try: all 12 ideas, the quick looks and all 30 settings\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Just 1: you present one strategy\",\"rationale\":\"That's the naive single test. The winner was selected from many tries, and the bar must reflect them.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"N counts every configuration that could have become the winner: the 9 ideas dropped after a quick look, the others, and all 30 settings of the sweep. Your research log is where the count comes from; without it, the risk of overfitting can't be judged.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\",\"concepts\":[\"reality-check-testing\",\"research-log\"],\"hints\":[\"Would a dropped idea have been presented if it had looked good?\",\"Every configuration that could have become the winner is a trial.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.2-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does the expected best Sharpe of N zero-edge trials rise as N grows?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Each trial is another draw of noise, and the best of more draws is higher\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Later trials learn from earlier ones\",\"rationale\":\"Zero-edge trials learn nothing. The best rises only because more noisy results are compared.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Longer searches use more data\",\"rationale\":\"N counts tries on the same data. The rise comes from keeping the best of more noisy results.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It doesn't: zero-edge trials average zero\",\"rationale\":\"Their average is zero, but you keep the best. The best of 50 one-year trials averaged 2.26 in seeded runs.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"With no edge, each trial's Sharpe is noise around zero. Keep the best and you keep the luckiest draw: about 1.57 spreads above zero for N = 10, 2.28 for 50 and 2.53 for 100.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.2\",\"concepts\":[\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\",\"multiple-testing\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens to the maximum of a set of random numbers as the set grows?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.2-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A validation card shows a deflated Sharpe ratio of 0.73. What does it say?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The true Sharpe is 73% of the measured one\",\"rationale\":\"DSR is a probability, not a shrink factor. A haircut lowers the Sharpe; the DSR judges it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"73% of the trials were profitable\",\"rationale\":\"DSR is about the winner against luck's benchmark, not the share of profitable trials.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"There's a 73% probability the true Sharpe beats the best Sharpe luck would give the trials run\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It passes: anything above 0.5 is significant\",\"rationale\":\"At 0.5 the Sharpe only equals luck's benchmark. The usual convention asks for 0.95.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"DSR is the probabilistic Sharpe ratio measured against SR*, the best Sharpe the trials are expected to show by luck, with skew and fat tails included. 0.73 is below the conventional 0.95: the result may be a fluke.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.2\",\"concepts\":[\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\",\"probabilistic-sharpe-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"Is DSR a probability or a reduced Sharpe?\",\"Its benchmark is the best Sharpe the trials would show with no edge.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.2-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Two backtests have the same Sharpe ratio and length. One has negative skew, the other positive. Why is the negative-skew PSR lower?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Negative skew lowers the average return\",\"rationale\":\"Both have the same Sharpe, so the same average for their volatility. Skew changes how reliable that Sharpe is.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Rare large losses make a measured Sharpe less reliable, which widens its error\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"PSR ignores skew; the gap is rounding\",\"rationale\":\"PSR includes skew directly: the term 1 − skew × SR grows when skew is negative.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Positive skew means more trades\",\"rationale\":\"Skew describes the shape of returns, not how many there are.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"In the PSR's denominator, √(1 − skew × SR + (kurtosis − 1) ÷ 4 × SR²), negative skew makes the term larger, so the same Sharpe gives a smaller z-score. In the lesson's backtest, skew −0.5 gives a PSR of 0.9942 against 0.9959 at +0.5.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.2\",\"concepts\":[\"probabilistic-sharpe-ratio\",\"skewness\"],\"hints\":[\"Look at the term 1 − skew × SR in the denominator.\",\"A larger denominator means a smaller z-score.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.3-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A strategy shows an annual Sharpe of 1.0 over 5 years of daily data. Using SE ≈ 1 ÷ √years, what is the lower end of its 95% interval?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.12,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"\"},\"explanation\":\"SE = 1 ÷ √5 = 0.447. Lower end = 1.0 − 1.96 × 0.447 = 0.12; the upper end is 1.88. Zero is only just outside.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"sharpe-ratio-uncertainty\"],\"hints\":[\"SE of an annual Sharpe ≈ 1 ÷ √years.\",\"Lower end = Sharpe − 1.96 × SE.\",\"1 ÷ √5 = 0.447.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"s\":[0.6,2,0.1],\"y\":[2,12,1]},\"answer\":\"round(s - 1.96 / sqrt(y), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"A strategy shows an annual Sharpe of {s:1} over {y} years of daily data. Using SE ≈ 1 ÷ √years, what is the lower end of its 95% interval?\",\"explanation\":\"SE = 1 ÷ √{y} = {1 / sqrt(y):3}. Lower end = {s:1} − 1.96 × {1 / sqrt(y):3} = {answer:2}.\",\"hint\":\"1 ÷ √{y} = {1 / sqrt(y):3}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.3-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"With 100 configurations tried and a target Sharpe of 1, the minimum backtest length is 6.4 years. What does that mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The strategy needs 6.4 years to become profitable\",\"rationale\":\"MinBTL is about how much data a test needs, not about when profits arrive.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Each of the 100 configurations needs its own 6.4 years\",\"rationale\":\"All 100 run on the same history. MinBTL is how long that one history must be.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After 6.4 years of data, the edge is proven\",\"rationale\":\"MinBTL is a minimum, a necessary condition. Passing it proves nothing on its own; DSR and PBO still apply.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"With less than 6.4 years of data, the best of 100 zero-edge tries is expected to reach a Sharpe of 1 by luck\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"E[max] for 100 zero-edge tries is 2.53, so the best shows an annual Sharpe near 2.53 ÷ √years. That stays above 1 until years exceed 2.53² = 6.4. Shorter data can't tell a Sharpe of 1 from the luck of 100 tries.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"minimum-backtest-length\"],\"hints\":[\"MinBTL compares a target Sharpe with the best that luck gives N tries.\",\"Luck's best shrinks with √years.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.3-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"You tried 20 variants on the last 3 months of daily data. The best shows an annual Sharpe of 3.0.\",\"prompt\":\"What does that tell you?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A lot: a Sharpe of 3 is rare\",\"rationale\":\"Rare for one test. The best of 20 zero-edge 3-month tries averaged 3.80 in seeded runs and reached 3.0 in 76% of them.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Almost nothing: luck alone would be expected to give the best of 20 about 3.8 over 3 months\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The edge is real but smaller, around 1.5\",\"rationale\":\"Nothing here separates a Sharpe of 1.5 from zero. The data are far too short for 20 tries.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Enough to start trading at full size\",\"rationale\":\"MinBTL for 20 tries at a target of 3 is about 4.8 months: 3 months can't carry this search.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"E[max] for 20 tries is 1.90; over 0.25 years that's an annual Sharpe near 1.90 ÷ √0.25 = 3.8. In 10,000 seeded searches the best averaged 3.80. MinBTL at a target of 3 is (1.90 ÷ 3)² = 0.40 years, about 4.8 months.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.3\",\"concepts\":[\"minimum-backtest-length\",\"sharpe-ratio-uncertainty\"],\"hints\":[\"How high is the best of 20 tries expected to be by luck alone?\",\"Over 0.25 years, divide E[max] by √0.25 = 0.5.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does the probability of backtest overfitting (PBO) measure?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The chance the strategy loses money next year\",\"rationale\":\"PBO is about ranks inside the family, not future profit. A family with a low PBO can still lose to costs.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How often the in-sample winner of a family ranks at or below the median out of sample\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The share of variants that are profitable\",\"rationale\":\"That counts winners. PBO asks whether picking the in-sample best helps out of sample.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"How much the Sharpe falls from in-sample to out of sample\",\"rationale\":\"That's the degradation, read beside PBO. PBO counts the splits where the winner's rank falls to the bottom half.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"CSCV splits the data every way into in-sample and out-of-sample halves. PBO is the share of splits where the in-sample winner's out-of-sample rank is at or below the median (λ ≤ 0).\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"backtest-overfitting-probability\"],\"hints\":[\"It's about the winner's rank inside its family.\",\"Count the splits where that rank falls to the bottom half.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.4-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"CSCV with 8 blocks gives 70 splits. In 21 of them the in-sample winner ranks at or below the median out of sample. What is the PBO, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":30,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"PBO = splits with λ ≤ 0 ÷ all splits = 21 ÷ 70 = 30%. By the app's convention, 20% to 50% means incubate.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"backtest-overfitting-probability\"],\"hints\":[\"PBO is a share of the splits.\",\"Divide the splits with λ ≤ 0 by 70.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[3,60,1]},\"answer\":\"round(100 * n / 70, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"CSCV with 8 blocks gives 70 splits. In {n} of them the in-sample winner ranks at or below the median out of sample. What is the PBO, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"PBO = {n} ÷ 70 = {answer:1}%. App convention: 50% or more reject, 20% to 50% incubate, under 20% keep testing.\",\"hint\":\"{n} ÷ 70.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.4-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does PBO sit near 0.5 on R0, random-walk data?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"R0 data have exactly half winning days\",\"rationale\":\"PBO counts the winner's ranks, not winning days. Any no-edge family gives about one half.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Half the variants are overfit\",\"rationale\":\"On R0 every variant is noise. The winner's out-of-sample rank is random, so half the splits put it in the bottom half.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"On a random walk the in-sample winner is a coin toss out of sample\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"CSCV uses half the data in-sample\",\"rationale\":\"The halves don't fix PBO. A family with a real, varied edge scores far lower on the same splits: 0.105 on R1.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"With no edge, in-sample rank says nothing about out-of-sample rank, so the winner lands above or below the median with equal odds. Across 1,000 seeded R0 families, PBO averaged 0.50.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.4\",\"concepts\":[\"backtest-overfitting-probability\",\"random-walk\"],\"hints\":[\"Does an in-sample rank carry any information when there is no edge?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.5-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does shuffled k-fold cross-validation overstate results on daily data with 10-day labels?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It uses too few folds\",\"rationale\":\"More folds don't help. The leak comes from labels that overlap between training and test days.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Daily data are too noisy for any model\",\"rationale\":\"Noise would pull the score toward 50%, not lift it above.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Shuffling removes the trend\",\"rationale\":\"Shuffling folds doesn't change the prices. It puts each test day's neighbours, with overlapping labels, into training.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Neighbouring days share most of their label days, so training holds near-copies of the test labels\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Day t's label uses days t+1 to t+10; day t+1's uses t+2 to t+11, nine days shared. Shuffled folds put such neighbours on both sides, and the model copies near-identical labels. On R0 that gave 56.0% accuracy, and 50.0% after purge and embargo.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"purged-cross-validation\",\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"hints\":[\"How many label days do two neighbouring days share?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.5-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is the difference between purging and an embargo?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Purging deletes outliers; the embargo delays trading\",\"rationale\":\"Neither is about outliers or live trading. Both remove training days near the test fold.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Purging drops training days whose labels overlap the test period; the embargo drops a gap right after the test fold\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"They're the same thing, on opposite sides\",\"rationale\":\"They sit on opposite sides here, but for different reasons: label overlap before, the test labels' days and serial correlation after.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Purging is for prices, the embargo for news\",\"rationale\":\"Both apply to any time series whose labels are built over a horizon.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Purging removes training observations whose labels reach into the test period. The embargo removes the days just after the fold, which the last test labels used and which share their serial correlation.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"purged-cross-validation\"],\"hints\":[\"One deals with labels reaching forward into the test fold; the other with the days just after it.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.5-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Labels use the next 5 days' return. The test fold is days 201–300. Which training days do you drop?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Purge 191–200 and embargo 301–310\",\"rationale\":\"That uses 10 days. With 5-day labels, only days 196–200 reach into the fold.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only 196–200\",\"rationale\":\"That's the purge. The days after the fold fed the last test labels: embargo 301–305 too.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Purge 196–200 and embargo 301–305\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only 301–305\",\"rationale\":\"Days 196–200 have labels reaching into days 201–205, inside the fold. Purge them.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Day 196's label covers days 197–201 and reaches the fold; day 195's ends at 200. The last test labels use days 301–305, so those are embargoed. Train on days up to 195 and from 306.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\",\"concepts\":[\"purged-cross-validation\"],\"hints\":[\"Which training labels reach day 201 or later?\",\"Then protect the days the last test labels used.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.6-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Gross edge 40 bps a trade, fixed costs 10 bps, impact 200 × √(Q ÷ V) bps per side. At Q ÷ V = 0.25%, what is the net edge per trade, in bps?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"bps\"},\"explanation\":\"√0.0025 = 0.05. Impact per side = 200 × 0.05 = 10 bps, so 20 bps round trip. Net = 40 − 10 − 20 = 10 bps.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-capacity\",\"square-root-impact\"],\"hints\":[\"Round-trip impact is twice the per-side figure.\",\"√0.0025 = 0.05.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"g\":[30,60,5],\"c\":[5,15,5],\"q\":[1,6,1]},\"constraints\":[\"g - c - 4 * q > 0\"],\"answer\":\"round(g - c - 4 * q, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Gross edge {g} bps a trade, fixed costs {c} bps, impact 200 × √(Q ÷ V) bps per side. At Q ÷ V = {q * q / 100:2}%, what is the net edge per trade, in bps?\",\"explanation\":\"√{q * q / 10000:4} = {q / 100:2}. Impact per side = 200 × {q / 100:2} = {2 * q} bps, so {4 * q} bps round trip. Net = {g} − {c} − {4 * q} = {answer:2} bps.\",\"hint\":\"√{q * q / 10000:4} = {q / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.6-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is the size that maximises total profit per trade smaller than the size where the edge reaches zero?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Brokers cap position size\",\"rationale\":\"No cap is involved. The peak comes from impact growing with size.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Fixed costs rise with size\",\"rationale\":\"Fixed costs are a constant 10 bps per trade here. Impact is what grows with size.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Past the peak, each extra unit raises impact on every unit, so total profit falls long before the edge hits zero\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They're the same size\",\"rationale\":\"Where the edge per unit is zero, total profit is zero. The peak, at 5,000 units, comes much earlier.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Total profit = size × net edge. More size adds units but lowers the edge on all of them. Marginal profit reaches zero at 5,000 units (500 vUSD); the edge itself reaches zero only at 11,250 units, where total profit is zero.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-capacity\"],\"hints\":[\"Total profit is size × net edge; both change as size grows.\",\"Find where one more unit stops adding profit.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.6-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Another fund starts trading your signal at the same moments. What happens to your capacity?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It falls: impact depends on the combined size, so your profitable size shrinks\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing, as long as you keep your size\",\"rationale\":\"Your size is the same, but impact is set by everyone trading. In the lesson, a twin fund cut 500 vUSD per trade to 86.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It rises: more volume makes the market deeper\",\"rationale\":\"Their orders don't add depth on your side; they compete for it at the same moments.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only their capacity falls\",\"rationale\":\"Impact is shared: both of you pay the price move set by the combined size.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"With a twin trading 5,000 units alongside your 5,000, impact is set by 10,000: your profit per trade falls from 500 to 86 vUSD, and your best size drops to about 2,985 units. Crowding shrinks capacity without you changing anything.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.6\",\"concepts\":[\"strategy-capacity\",\"strategy-crowding\"],\"hints\":[\"Which size sets the impact: yours alone, or everyone's trading at that moment?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why must a strategy report state the number of trials, N?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Regulators require it\",\"rationale\":\"This is about statistics, not regulation: N sets the bar that luck would reach.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"More trials show a more thorough strategy\",\"rationale\":\"More trials raise the bar luck sets, so the same result means less.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It sets the position size\",\"rationale\":\"Sizing comes from risk and expectancy. N tells readers how to judge the backtest.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Without N, nobody can judge how much of the result could be luck from searching\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The best of N tries is expected to look good by luck alone, and that bar rises with N. DSR, MinBTL and PBO all need it: without N, the risk of overfitting can't be assessed.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"research-hygiene\",\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"What does the bar set by luck depend on?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.7-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your research log shows a second out-of-sample unlock, after a rule change. What does it mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The out-of-sample data have become in-sample: the result is no longer an out-of-sample test\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing, if the second result was better\",\"rationale\":\"Better is the problem: the rule was changed after seeing those data, so they helped choose it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The out-of-sample result counts double\",\"rationale\":\"It counts less, not more: the data were used to pick the rule.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It's fine as long as the hypothesis stayed the same\",\"rationale\":\"The rules changed between the unlocks, guided by the first look. The hold-out is spent.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"An out-of-sample set tests a rule once. After you look, change the rule and look again, those data helped choose the rule. Report the result as in-sample and test on fresh data or forward.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"research-hygiene\",\"in-sample-out-of-sample\"],\"hints\":[\"Did the out-of-sample data help choose the final rule?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.3.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A published idea shows +0.24R per trade in your backtest. With a 50% decay haircut, what expectancy goes in your plan, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":0.12,\"tolerance\":0.005,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"0.24 × (1 − 0.50) = 0.12R. Sizing, gates and kill criteria use 0.12R, not 0.24R.\",\"lesson\":\"4.3.7\",\"concepts\":[\"research-hygiene\",\"anomaly-decay\"],\"hints\":[\"Cut the backtest figure by the haircut.\",\"0.24 × 0.5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"e\":[0.1,0.6,0.02],\"h\":[25,75,25]},\"answer\":\"round(e * (1 - h / 100), 3)\",\"prompt\":\"A published idea shows +{e:2}R per trade in your backtest. With a {h}% decay haircut, what expectancy goes in your plan, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"{e:2} × (1 − {h / 100:2}) = {answer:3}R. Sizing, gates and kill criteria use the planned figure.\",\"hint\":\"{e:2} × {1 - h / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.1-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Your book is 10,000.00 vUSD and its daily volatility is 2%. With the normal model (z = 1.645), what is the one-day 95% VaR, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":329,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"σ = 2% × 10,000.00 = 200.00 a day. VaR = 1.645 × 200.00 = 329: a loss exceeded on about 1 day in 20 if the model is right. It isn't the most you can lose.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"value-at-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"VaR = z × the daily volatility in vUSD.\",\"2% of 10,000.00 is 200.00. Multiply by 1.645.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"book\":[5000,50000,5000],\"vol\":[0.5,3,0.25]},\"answer\":\"round(1.645 * vol * book / 100, 0)\",\"prompt\":\"Your book is {book} vUSD and its daily volatility is {vol}%. With the normal model (z = 1.645), what is the one-day 95% VaR, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"σ = {vol}% × {book} = {vol * book / 100:2} a day. VaR = 1.645 × {vol * book / 100:2} = {answer}: a loss exceeded on about 1 day in 20 if the model is right. It isn't the most you can lose.\",\"hint\":\"1.645 × {vol * book / 100:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.1-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is Expected Shortfall a better limit than VaR for a strategy that sells options?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"ES is always smaller, so the limit is easier to meet\",\"rationale\":\"ES is never smaller than VaR at the same level: it averages the losses beyond VaR.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Its losses are rare and large; they sit beyond the VaR threshold, where only ES looks\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"VaR can't be computed for options\",\"rationale\":\"It can, from simulated or historical P&L. The problem is what VaR ignores: the size of losses past the threshold.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"ES uses a lower confidence level, so it reacts faster\",\"rationale\":\"ES is set at a high level too, such as 99%. Its advantage is that it measures how deep the tail goes.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Selling options earns small gains on most days and loses big on rare ones. A 95% VaR can look small because the big losses happen on fewer than 5% of days. ES averages the losses beyond VaR, so the tail shows up in the number.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"expected-shortfall\",\"value-at-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Where do a short-option strategy's big losses fall: inside or beyond the 95% threshold?\",\"Which measure looks past the threshold?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.1-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your 95% one-day VaR model was exceeded on 22 of the last 250 trading days.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you conclude?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Normal luck: 22 is close enough to 12.5\",\"rationale\":\"If the model were right, 22 or more would happen only about 0.8% of the time. That's not luck worth betting on.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The model is too cautious\",\"rationale\":\"A cautious model is exceeded less often than expected, not more: fewer than 12.5 times in 250 days.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Exceedances say nothing about a VaR model\",\"rationale\":\"Counting exceedances is exactly how VaR models are backtested.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The model understates risk; recalibrate it, for example with fat tails or recent volatility\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A correct 95% model is beaten on about 12.5 of 250 days. The chance of 22 or more is about 0.8%, so the model almost surely understates risk: volatility has risen, or the tails are fatter than it assumes.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\",\"concepts\":[\"value-at-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"How many exceedances does a correct 95% model produce in 250 days?\",\"If the model were right, 22 or more would happen less than 1% of the time.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.2-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Two assets, each at 10% volatility, held 50/50 with a correlation of 0.5. What is the book's volatility, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":8.66,\"tolerance\":0.02,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"σ_p² = 0.25 × 0.01 + 0.25 × 0.01 + 2 × 0.25 × 0.5 × 0.01 = 0.0075, so σ_p = √0.0075 = 8.66%. It's below 10% because the assets don't move in lockstep, and above 7.07%, the uncorrelated case.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-volatility-targeting\"],\"hints\":[\"σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂.\",\"0.0025 + 0.0025 + 0.0025 = 0.0075. Take the root.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"vol\":[5,30,1],\"r10\":[0,9,1]},\"answer\":\"round(vol * sqrt((1 + r10 / 10) / 2), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Two assets, each at {vol}% volatility, held 50/50 with a correlation of {r10 / 10:1}. What is the book's volatility, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"With equal weights and volatilities, σ_p = σ × √((1 + ρ) ÷ 2) = {vol} × √({1 + r10 / 10:1} ÷ 2) = {answer:2}%. The less the assets move together, the further it falls below {vol}%.\",\"hint\":\"σ_p = {vol} × √({1 + r10 / 10:1} ÷ 2).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.2-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your book's forecast volatility rose from 8% to 10% this month, yet no asset in it became more volatile. What most likely happened?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The book's weights fell\",\"rationale\":\"Smaller weights lower the book's volatility; they can't raise it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Nothing: book volatility can't rise unless an asset's does\",\"rationale\":\"Book volatility also depends on how the assets move together. Higher correlation raises it with every single volatility unchanged.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The correlations between the positions rose\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Volatility drag\",\"rationale\":\"Drag lowers compound growth. It doesn't change a volatility forecast.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"σ_p² includes the term 2·w₁·w₂·ρ·σ₁·σ₂. When correlations rise, positions stop offsetting each other and the book swings more. Two 10% assets at 50/50 go from 7.75% to 9.75% as ρ moves from 0.2 to 0.9.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-volatility-targeting\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"hints\":[\"Which input to a book's volatility isn't any single asset's volatility?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.2-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Your volatility target asks for 1.4× exposure. Your multiplier cap is 1.5×. Today the book reached −10%, where your drawdown tier halves it.\",\"prompt\":\"What exposure do you run?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"1.4×: the target is the main rule\",\"rationale\":\"The target sets the size only when no other rule asks for less. The tier was written for exactly this day.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"0.7×: the tier halves the target's size\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"1.5×: the cap\",\"rationale\":\"The cap is a ceiling, not a setting. It never raises the book.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"1.05×: the average of the two rules\",\"rationale\":\"Rules aren't averaged. Each can only shrink the book, so the smallest size wins.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Risk rules stack as limits: the target sets the size, and the cap and the tier can only shrink it. Here the tier halves 1.4× to 0.7×. When rules disagree, apply the smallest size.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-volatility-targeting\",\"drawdown-tiers\"],\"hints\":[\"Can a cap or a drawdown tier ever make the book bigger?\",\"Start from the target's 1.4×, then apply every rule that shrinks it.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.3-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Equity 50,000. You are long 80,000 vUSD of LABX and short 50,000 vUSD of LABZ. What is your gross exposure, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":130000,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"Gross adds absolute sizes: 80,000 + 50,000 = 130,000, or 2.6× equity. Net subtracts the short: +30,000. The book bets 30,000 on the market, but 130,000 can lose when the legs split.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"gross-and-net-exposure\"],\"hints\":[\"Gross counts longs and shorts as positive.\",\"80,000 + 50,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"long\":[20000,150000,10000],\"short\":[10000,150000,10000]},\"answer\":\"long + short\",\"prompt\":\"Equity 50,000. You are long {long} vUSD of LABX and short {short} vUSD of LABZ. What is your gross exposure, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"Gross adds absolute sizes: {long} + {short} = {answer}, or {answer / 50000:2}× equity. Net subtracts the short: {long - short}. Gross is what can lose when the legs split.\",\"hint\":\"{long} + {short}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.3-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A book's net exposure is exactly zero. Which risk is still there?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"None: the longs and the shorts cancel\",\"rationale\":\"They cancel only while they move together. Zero net says nothing about how big the two sides are.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Only market risk\",\"rationale\":\"Market risk is what net exposure measures, and here it's near zero. What's left is the risk that the legs diverge.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Leg risk: the longs can fall while the shorts rise, and the loss scales with gross exposure\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only the commissions on rebalancing\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are real, but small next to a 5% split between the legs on a large gross book.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Net zero removes the bet on the market as a whole, not the bet on the legs. If the longs fall and the shorts rise, every vUSD of gross loses: a 5% split on a 200,000 gross book costs 10,000.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"gross-and-net-exposure\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens if the long side falls 5% while the short side rises 5%?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.3-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"LABX has a beta of 1.2. What does that mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"LABX is 1.2 times as volatile as the market, whatever the correlation\",\"rationale\":\"Beta mixes volatility and correlation: β = ρ × σ_LABX ÷ σ_market. A volatile stock with a low correlation can have a low beta.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"LABX has tended to move about 1.2% for each 1% move in the market\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"LABX is 20% more likely to rise than the market\",\"rationale\":\"Beta is about the size of the co-movement, not the odds of going up.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"LABX returns 1.2% a year more than the market\",\"rationale\":\"That would be a return. Beta measures sensitivity to market moves.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Beta is LABX's sensitivity to the market, estimated from past returns: a 1% market fall has gone with about a 1.2% LABX fall. That's why 60,000 of LABX counts as 72,000 of beta-adjusted exposure.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\",\"concepts\":[\"beta-adjusted-exposure\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.4-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Two uncorrelated assets: one at 8% volatility, one at 24%. With inverse-volatility weights, what percentage of capital goes to the 8% asset?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":75,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"Weights are proportional to 1/σ: 1/8 = 0.125 and 1/24 = 0.0417. Scaled to 100%: 0.125 ÷ 0.1667 = 75%, and 25% for the other. Each then brings the same risk: 0.75 × 8% = 0.25 × 24% = 6%.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-parity\"],\"hints\":[\"Weight each asset by one over its volatility.\",\"Then scale the weights to add up to 100%.\",\"1/8 ÷ (1/8 + 1/24).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"a\":[4,20,1],\"b\":[10,40,2]},\"constraints\":[\"b > a\"],\"answer\":\"round(100 * b / (a + b), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"Two uncorrelated assets: one at {a}% volatility, one at {b}%. With inverse-volatility weights, what percentage of capital goes to the {a}% asset?\",\"explanation\":\"Weights are proportional to 1/σ. The {a}% asset gets (1/{a}) ÷ (1/{a} + 1/{b}) = {b} ÷ ({a} + {b}) = {answer:1}%. The calmer asset gets the larger weight, and each brings the same risk.\",\"hint\":\"(1/{a}) ÷ (1/{a} + 1/{b}), which equals {b} ÷ ({a} + {b}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.4-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why isn't a 50/50 split of capital a 50/50 split of risk?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because the cheaper asset buys more units\",\"rationale\":\"Unit prices don't matter; vUSD and volatility do. 5,000 vUSD of exposure is 5,000 vUSD at any price.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It is, whenever the two assets are uncorrelated\",\"rationale\":\"Uncorrelated is the clearest case of the gap: 10% and 30% assets at 50/50 carry 10% and 90% of the risk.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Because each asset's risk grows with its weight times its volatility, so the more volatile one dominates\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because commissions differ between the two\",\"rationale\":\"Costs are tiny next to the gap between volatilities, which decides risk shares.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"An asset's risk share depends on its weight times its volatility, squared when the assets are uncorrelated. At 50/50, a 30% asset brings nine times the variance of a 10% one: 90% of the risk.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-contribution\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the variance terms: weight² × volatility² for each asset.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.4-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"What is the main risk of running risk parity with leverage?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The weights drift away from 50/50 capital\",\"rationale\":\"Equal capital isn't the goal; equal risk is. Drift is fixed by rebalancing.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In a crisis the calm assets and the risky ones can fall together, and leverage multiplies the loss\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It holds too little of the calm asset\",\"rationale\":\"It holds more of the calm asset than a capital split would, then levers the whole book.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Leverage always lowers the Sharpe ratio\",\"rationale\":\"Leverage scales return and volatility together, so before costs the Sharpe ratio stays the same. The danger is the size of the losses.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Risk parity holds a lot of the calm asset and borrows to reach a return target. It relies on the assets offsetting each other. When they fall together, as stocks and bonds did in 2022, the levered book loses more than an unlevered one, and financing costs add to it.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-parity\",\"leverage\"],\"hints\":[\"What does risk parity rely on the assets doing?\",\"What does leverage do to a loss?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.5-1\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your book runs at 2.5× effective leverage. What adverse move, in %, would cost 25% of your equity?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":10,\"tolerance\":0.05,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"At 2.5×, each 1% move costs 2.5% of equity. 25% ÷ 2.5 = 10%. A reverse stress test turns the question around: not what a move costs, but which move breaks your limit.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"reverse-stress-test\"],\"hints\":[\"Loss as a share of equity = leverage × move.\",\"Divide the loss by the leverage: 25 ÷ 2.5.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"lev\":[1.5,6,0.5],\"loss\":[10,50,5]},\"answer\":\"round(loss / lev, 2)\",\"prompt\":\"Your book runs at {lev}× effective leverage. What adverse move, in %, would cost {loss}% of your equity?\",\"explanation\":\"At {lev}×, each 1% move costs {lev}% of equity. {loss}% ÷ {lev} = {answer:2}%. A reverse stress test turns the question around: not what a move costs, but which move breaks your limit.\",\"hint\":\"{loss} ÷ {lev}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.5-2\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does a stress test do that VaR doesn't?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It gives the probability of each scenario\",\"rationale\":\"Stress tests usually attach no probability. They ask what if, not how often.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It measures the average loss beyond VaR\",\"rationale\":\"That's Expected Shortfall. A stress test applies chosen shocks instead of averaging the data's tail.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It replaces VaR, which is no longer used\",\"rationale\":\"Risk managers use both: VaR and ES for everyday risk, stress tests for the tail the data hasn't shown.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It revalues the book under chosen extreme scenarios, including ones the recent data never showed\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"VaR and ES estimate risk from the data you have. A stress test applies chosen shocks, past crises or imagined ones such as a 20% gap with correlations at 1, to today's book. It finds losses your data never contained.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-stress-testing\",\"value-at-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Where do VaR's numbers come from, and what can't that source contain?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.5-3\",\"topic\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your stress test assumes you can close the whole book at the first price after a 10% gap.\",\"prompt\":\"Why is that optimistic?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In a crisis liquidity thins: you may need days to exit, while prices keep moving against you\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Gaps always reverse within the day\",\"rationale\":\"Some do, as in the 2010 Flash Crash; many don't, as with the Swiss franc in 2015. A stress test can't count on a rebound.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Stops guarantee the stop price\",\"rationale\":\"A stop becomes a market order. After a gap it fills at the next available price, often far beyond the level.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It isn't optimistic: the first price after a gap is the worst case\",\"rationale\":\"Selling a large book into a thin market costs more, and every day you still hold it, prices can fall further.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Liquidity disappears when everyone sells. If a 15,000 book can sell only a third a day while prices fall 3% a day, a 10% gap costs 19% of 10,000 of equity instead of 15%. Include the time to exit in every scenario.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.5\",\"concepts\":[\"portfolio-stress-testing\",\"gap-risk\"],\"hints\":[\"Can a large book be sold at one price when everyone is selling?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.6-1\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You hold 4 equal positions with an average correlation of 0.5. How many effective bets is that?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":1.6,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"bets\"},\"explanation\":\"Effective bets = n ÷ (1 + (n − 1)ρ) = 4 ÷ (1 + 3 × 0.5) = 4 ÷ 2.5 = 1.6. Four tickets, fewer than two independent bets, and fewer still if correlation rises in a crisis.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-regimes\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"hints\":[\"Effective bets = n ÷ (1 + (n − 1) × ρ).\",\"4 ÷ (1 + 3 × 0.5).\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[2,10,1],\"r10\":[1,9,1]},\"answer\":\"round(n / (1 + (n - 1) * r10 / 10), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"You hold {n} equal positions with an average correlation of {r10 / 10:1}. How many effective bets is that?\",\"explanation\":\"Effective bets = n ÷ (1 + (n − 1)ρ) = {n} ÷ (1 + {n - 1} × {r10 / 10:1}) = {answer:2}. Correlation shrinks {n} tickets to fewer independent bets, and a crisis shrinks them further.\",\"hint\":\"{n} ÷ (1 + {n - 1} × {r10 / 10:1}).\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.6-2\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why do carry strategies in different asset classes tend to lose at the same time?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"They trade the same instrument\",\"rationale\":\"Carry appears in currencies, bonds, commodities and stocks. The instruments differ; the risk they're paid for is shared.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"They don't: different asset classes are uncorrelated\",\"rationale\":\"In calm periods they look unrelated. In global recessions, carry strategies across asset classes lost together.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Carry is a form of trend following\",\"rationale\":\"Trend following has often gained in crises. Carry is paid for holding a risk that shows up in crashes.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They are paid for the same crash risk, and lose together when investors cut risk everywhere at once\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Carry pays for bearing a risk that shows up in bad times. In global recessions and liquidity crises, investors unwind leveraged positions everywhere at once, so carry in currencies, bonds and commodities falls together (Koijen et al. 2018, grade A). Size them as one bet.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"carry\",\"correlation-regimes\"],\"hints\":[\"What do carry trades earn their small, steady returns for?\",\"What do investors do everywhere at once in a crisis?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.4.6-3\",\"topic\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You are sizing five correlated positions. Which correlation should set the size?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The one measured over the last 20 calm days\",\"rationale\":\"Calm correlations understate a crisis: 0.2 measured today can be 0.8 when it matters.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A crisis correlation, such as 0.8, so the book still fits your limit when correlations jump\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The long-run average\",\"rationale\":\"An average mixes calm and crisis. The loss you must survive comes from the crisis.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Zero, since the positions are in different instruments\",\"rationale\":\"Different tickers can share one driver. In a crisis, that driver is the market.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Correlations rise toward 1 in crises, exactly when losses are large. Size so the book fits your limit at crisis correlation: five positions at 0.65% each carry about 3% even at ρ = 0.8.\",\"lesson\":\"4.4.6\",\"concepts\":[\"correlation-regimes\"],\"hints\":[\"When do you need diversification most, and what are correlations doing then?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.1-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Who supervises investment firms in Romania?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"ESMA\",\"rationale\":\"ESMA coordinates EU rules but doesn't license firms. Each country's own authority does.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The ASF (Autoritatea de Supraveghere Financiară)\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The National Bank of Romania\",\"rationale\":\"The central bank supervises banks. Investment firms and the capital market fall under the ASF.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The Bucharest Stock Exchange (BVB)\",\"rationale\":\"The BVB is the market where shares trade, not the supervisor of brokers.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The ASF licenses and supervises investment firms and the capital market in Romania, applying EU rules such as ESMA's retail CFD measures. A firm passported from another EU state appears on its home register and on the ASF's.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"regulatory-perimeter\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.1-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"On 22 October 2026, what is the status of the US pattern day trader rule?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"In force: 4 day trades in 5 business days need 25,000 dollars\",\"rationale\":\"That was the rule until June 2026. Many older courses still teach it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Raised to a 50,000-dollar minimum\",\"rationale\":\"The minimum was removed, not raised. Intraday margin deficits replaced it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Replaced by intraday margin from 4 June 2026; brokers may phase it in until 20 October 2027\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Abolished, with no margin rule in its place\",\"rationale\":\"Margin rules remain: brokers now track intraday margin deficits, which must be covered.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"FINRA's change, approved by the SEC in April 2026, took effect on 4 June 2026: the pattern day trader section is gone and intraday margin applies. Brokers may keep the old rule until 20 October 2027, so check your broker's terms.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"us-intraday-margin\"],\"hints\":[\"The rule changed in 2026, with a transition.\",\"The 25,000 minimum is gone, but margin rules aren't.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.1-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"An EU broker emails: 'Upgrade to professional status and unlock 200:1 leverage. Just tick the box.' Ioana has a 6,000-euro account and an office job outside finance.\",\"prompt\":\"What would Ioana give up if she opted up?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Nothing: only the leverage changes\",\"rationale\":\"Leverage caps are one of several retail protections. Negative balance protection and some warnings go too.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Her right to withdraw money\",\"rationale\":\"The client category doesn't affect withdrawals. What changes is the set of retail protections.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Only the ban on bonuses\",\"rationale\":\"Bonuses are banned for retail clients, but opting up removes much more: leverage caps and negative balance protection.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The retail leverage caps, negative balance protection and some risk warnings\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"Professional clients lose retail protections: leverage caps, negative balance protection and some warnings. Ioana doesn't meet two of the three criteria (frequent large trades, a portfolio over €500,000, finance experience), so a 'tick the box' offer is itself a warning sign.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"concepts\":[\"client-categorisation\"],\"hints\":[\"List what retail clients get that professionals don't.\",\"Leverage caps are only one of them.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which of these does SIPC not cover?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Cash in a failed US broker-dealer account, up to $250,000\",\"rationale\":\"Cash is covered, up to $250,000 within the $500,000 limit, when a member broker-dealer fails.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A 30% fall in the price of your shares\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Shares a failed broker-dealer can't return\",\"rationale\":\"Missing securities are what SIPC exists for, up to $500,000 per customer.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Shares the broker misused before it failed\",\"rationale\":\"Client assets a failed firm misused and can't return are what SIPC restores, within its limits.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"SIPC restores missing securities and cash when a US member broker-dealer fails: up to $500,000 per customer, of which $250,000 in cash. It never covers market losses, and it doesn't cover futures, forex or crypto that isn't a security.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"client-money-protection\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Where is the conflict of interest in a B-book broker?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It charges a commission on every trade\",\"rationale\":\"Commission is how A-book brokers earn: paid per trade, whatever you make. It isn't tied to your result.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It sends your orders to outside liquidity providers\",\"rationale\":\"That's the A-book model. A B-book broker keeps your trades in-house.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It is your counterparty, so your losses are its revenue\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It must segregate client money\",\"rationale\":\"Segregation protects you whatever the dealing model. It isn't a conflict.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A B-book broker takes the other side of your trades, so it gains what you lose and pays what you win. That's legal and regulated, but it creates an incentive against your success; disclosure, execution data and controls are what to check.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-models\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask who takes the other side of your trade.\",\"If you win, who pays?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You look a broker up on the regulator's register. What must match?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The broker's logo and its brand name\",\"rationale\":\"Clone firms copy logos and names. The legal entity and licence number are what count.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Its online review score\",\"rationale\":\"Reviews can help, but the register is what confirms who is licensed, and for what.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The licence number and the exact legal entity you'd sign with, with its permissions and websites\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The licence number shown on the broker's own website\",\"rationale\":\"That's the claim, not the check. Clone firms quote real numbers, so the entity and websites must match too.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A licence is only meaningful for the entity that holds it. Check the number, the legal name of the company that will hold your account, its permissions and its listed websites on the regulator's own register.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\",\"concepts\":[\"broker-due-diligence\"],\"hints\":[\"Clone firms copy real licence numbers.\",\"Which company would actually hold your money?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.3-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A 50,000 evaluation account has a 2,000 maximum loss limit that trails the end-of-day balance. Day 1 closes at 50,800. Where is the floor for day 2, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":48800,\"tolerance\":0,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"An end-of-day trailing limit follows the highest closing balance: 50,800 − 2,000 = 48,800. Intraday peaks don't count; only the close does.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"prop-evaluation-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"Use the closing balance, not the intraday peak.\",\"Subtract the limit from the highest close.\",\"50,800 − 2,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"limit\":[1500,3000,500],\"gain\":[200,1400,100]},\"constraints\":[\"gain < limit\"],\"answer\":\"50000 + gain - limit\",\"prompt\":\"A 50,000 evaluation account has a {limit} maximum loss limit that trails the end-of-day balance. Day 1 closes at {50000 + gain}. Where is the floor for day 2, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"An end-of-day trailing limit follows the highest closing balance: {50000 + gain} − {limit} = {answer}. Intraday peaks don't count; only the close does.\",\"hint\":\"{50000 + gain} − {limit}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.3-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"An evaluation's daily loss limit is 'measured on equity, including open positions'. What does that mean?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Only closed trades count toward the limit\",\"rationale\":\"That's a balance-based limit. Equity includes open losses, so a trade can breach the limit before you close it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"An open trade's unrealised loss can breach the limit, even if the trade later recovers\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The limit resets each time you close a trade\",\"rationale\":\"A daily limit resets once a day, at the firm's cut-off, not per trade.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Open profits count, but open losses don't\",\"rationale\":\"Equity counts open profit and open loss alike. The point is that an open loss counts in real time.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Equity is the balance plus open profit or loss. If an open trade is down far enough at any moment, the limit is breached, even if the trade would have recovered. Size and stops must fit the limit with open trades included.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"prop-evaluation-rules\",\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"hints\":[\"Equity = balance + open profit or loss.\",\"When is equity measured: at the close, or all the time?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.3-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does a consistency rule such as 'best day under 50% of total profit' penalise?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Trading every day\",\"rationale\":\"It doesn't limit trading days. It limits how much of the profit one day can supply.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Losing days\",\"rationale\":\"Loss limits handle losing days. The consistency rule looks at the best day's share of the profit.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Passing on one lucky, oversized day\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Using stops\",\"rationale\":\"Stops are encouraged. The rule targets profit concentrated in a single day.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"A trader who sizes up and gets one huge day can hit the target fast. The rule makes any single day count for less than half the profit, rewarding a steady process over one lucky spike.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\",\"concepts\":[\"prop-evaluation-rules\"],\"hints\":[\"Think of a trader who passes in a single day.\",\"What share of the profit came from that day?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.4-1\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"An evaluation costs 300. Your pass probability is 15% and the expected payouts if funded are 1,200. What is the expected value of one attempt, in vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-120,\"tolerance\":0.5,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"EV = 0.15 × 1,200 − 300 = 180 − 300 = −120. The fee is paid on every attempt; the payouts only after a pass.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"prop-challenge-ev\"],\"hints\":[\"EV = pass probability × payouts − fee.\",\"0.15 × 1,200 = 180. Then subtract the fee.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"fee\":[200,600,50],\"p\":[5,30,5],\"pay\":[800,3000,100]},\"answer\":\"pct(p) * pay - fee\",\"prompt\":\"An evaluation costs {fee}. Your pass probability is {p}% and the expected payouts if funded are {pay}. What is the expected value of one attempt, in vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"EV = {pct(p):2} × {pay} − {fee} = {pct(p) * pay} − {fee} = {answer}. The fee is paid on every attempt; the payouts only after a pass.\",\"hint\":\"{pct(p):2} × {pay} = {pct(p) * pay}. Then subtract {fee}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.4-2\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What does the CFTC case against My Forex Funds show evaluation customers, as of October 2026?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Evaluation fees are insured by the CFTC\",\"rationale\":\"No scheme insures evaluation fees. The case shows how unclear the protection is.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Evaluation firms must send funded traders' orders to real markets\",\"rationale\":\"No such rule came out of it. Many firms keep funded accounts simulated.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Prop evaluations are illegal in the US\",\"rationale\":\"The case was dismissed; it didn't make evaluations illegal. It left their status unclear.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Regulatory protection for fee payers is unclear: the case was dismissed in 2025\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The 2023 complaint alleged over 135,000 customers and at least $310 million in fees, with the firm as counterparty. In May 2025 the case was dismissed, with sanctions against the CFTC's team according to the defence. Fee payers can't count on a regulator to step in.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"prop-challenge-ev\"],\"hints\":[\"How did the case end, and when?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.4-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Dan's simulated trades have an expectancy of −0.1R. He reads that such a trader passes an evaluation more often at 2% risk than at 0.5%, so he plans to buy evaluations and trade them at 2%.\",\"prompt\":\"Why isn't this a strategy?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It is: more passes mean more payouts\",\"rationale\":\"More passes, but still mostly failures: about 15% pass at 2% in the lesson. Each pass is luck, and the funded account then loses.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because 2% risk always breaches the daily limit\",\"rationale\":\"Three 2% losses would breach a 5% daily limit, but stopping after two avoids it. The problem is the missing edge.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Higher risk only buys more lottery tickets: most attempts still fail, and without an edge a funded account drifts to its floor\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because the pass rate falls with risk for everyone\",\"rationale\":\"Only with an edge. Without one, more risk raised the pass rate in the simulation, from 0.3% to 15%.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Without an edge, variance is the only way to the target, so more risk raises the pass chance: from 0.3% at 0.5% risk to 15% at 2% in the lesson's simulation. But 85% of attempts still fail, and −0.1R trades lose in a funded account too.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\",\"concepts\":[\"prop-challenge-ev\",\"expectancy\"],\"hints\":[\"What happens to a −0.1R trader after a pass?\",\"Compare the pass chance with the share of attempts that still fail.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.5-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does the app do about taxes?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"It calculates the tax you owe in your country\",\"rationale\":\"It doesn't: rules differ by country and change often. Only your tax authority or an adviser can answer for your case.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"It applies a 20% rate to every user's results\",\"rationale\":\"The 20% in the lesson is a made-up example. You choose any generic rate yourself.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It tells you which account type pays the least tax\",\"rationale\":\"That would be tax advice. The app shows why treatment differs, not which choice suits you.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It shows the effect of a generic rate you choose and helps you keep complete records, but gives no country rates or advice\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The app is about awareness: a generic rate shows how tax slows compounding, and the records export keeps every money fact. Your actual tax depends on country, product, timing and account; ask your tax authority or a qualified adviser.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"tax-awareness\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.5-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"Mara's record for one trade: opened 14 March 2026 at 10:42, closed at 15:10; LABX; bought 50 at 97.30, sold at 102.40; commissions 2.44 and 2.56; net +250.00 vUSD; converted to 1,150.00 local units.\",\"prompt\":\"What is missing from the record?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The exchange rate used for the conversion, and its source\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"How she felt before the trade\",\"rationale\":\"That's useful in a journal, but the record needs the money facts. Look at the conversion.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The commissions\",\"rationale\":\"Both commissions are there: 2.44 and 2.56. Look at how 250.00 became 1,150.00.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The instrument\",\"rationale\":\"LABX is named. What's missing is how the result was converted.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Without the rate, 4.60 here, and its source, nobody can check how 250.00 vUSD became 1,150.00 local units. A record must let you, or a tax authority, rebuild every number.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"trade-records\"],\"hints\":[\"Try to rebuild every number from the record alone.\",\"How did 250.00 become 1,150.00?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.5-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"You start with 10,000 and grow 6% a year after costs and tax, for 10 years. What do you end with, to the nearest whole vUSD?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":17908,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"vUSD\"},\"explanation\":\"10,000 × 1.06¹⁰ = 17,908.48. Each year's growth is added before the next year's 6% is applied, so the gain is 7,908, not 6,000.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\",\"concepts\":[\"tax-awareness\"],\"hints\":[\"Multiply by 1.06 once for each year.\",\"1.06¹⁰ ≈ 1.7908. Multiply by 10,000.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"start\":[5000,20000,1000],\"r\":[4,8,1]},\"answer\":\"round(start * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)) * (1 + pct(r)), 0)\",\"prompt\":\"You start with {start} and grow {r}% a year after costs and tax, for 10 years. What do you end with, to the nearest whole vUSD?\",\"explanation\":\"{start} × {1 + pct(r):2}¹⁰ = {answer}. Each year's growth is added before the next year's {r}% is applied.\",\"hint\":\"Multiply {start} by {1 + pct(r):2}, ten times.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"100 simulated trades average +0.25R with a standard deviation of 1.5R. What is the lower bound of the 95% confidence interval for the average, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.04,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Standard error: 1.5 ÷ √100 = 0.15. Half-width: 1.96 × 0.15 = 0.294. The interval is 0.25 ± 0.294, from −0.04R to +0.54R. Zero is inside, so the answer is not yet.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"go-live-criteria\"],\"hints\":[\"Standard error = standard deviation ÷ √n.\",\"Multiply the standard error by 1.96, then subtract it from the average.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"n\":[50,400,10],\"m\":[0.1,0.5,0.05],\"sd\":[1,2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"round(m - 1.96 * sd / sqrt(n), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"{n} simulated trades average +{m:2}R with a standard deviation of {sd:1}R. What is the lower bound of the 95% confidence interval for the average, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Standard error: {sd:1} ÷ √{n} ≈ {sd / sqrt(n):3}. Half-width: 1.96 × the standard error ≈ {1.96 * sd / sqrt(n):3}. Lower bound: {m:2} − {1.96 * sd / sqrt(n):3} ≈ {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"{m:2} − 1.96 × {sd:1} ÷ √{n}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which money is risk capital?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Savings for a home deposit due next year\",\"rationale\":\"Money with a job to do soon isn't risk capital: losing it would change your plans.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A loan at a low interest rate\",\"rationale\":\"Borrowed money is never risk capital. You owe it back whatever happens.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Money you could lose entirely without touching living costs, debts or emergency savings\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your emergency fund, if you plan to refill it\",\"rationale\":\"The emergency fund exists for emergencies. Trading it away leaves you exposed.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Risk capital is what's left after living costs, debts, emergency savings and money already promised to a goal. Only that money belongs in a live trading account.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"risk-capital\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why are live results expected to be worse than simulated ones?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Real costs, slippage and partial fills, plus fear and greed with real money\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Brokers trade against every live client\",\"rationale\":\"Some brokers are counterparties, but the gap exists with any broker: costs, fills and emotions.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Simulators are designed to make you win\",\"rationale\":\"This simulator charges realistic costs. The gap comes from real execution and real emotions.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"They aren't: good simulator results carry over exactly\",\"rationale\":\"Real fills, real costs and real fear all differ. Plan for a smaller edge live.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Live trading adds wider and variable spreads, slippage, partial fills and outages, and above all the pressure of real money, which pushes you off your rules. The simulator builds skill; it doesn't predict real profits.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.6\",\"concepts\":[\"go-live-criteria\"],\"hints\":[\"List what's different when the money is real.\",\"Think of both execution and emotions.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.7-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your account reaches its first drawdown tier. Your plan says: half size until a new equity high. What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Keep full size to win it back faster\",\"rationale\":\"That's the revenge-trading urge dressed up as a plan. The tier exists for exactly this moment.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Halve the risk per trade, as written, and keep following the playbook\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the size: you're due a winner\",\"rationale\":\"Losing runs don't make wins more likely. Doubling after losses is martingale thinking.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Change the strategy at once\",\"rationale\":\"A tier is about size, not a verdict on the system. Changes go through your review process.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Drawdown tiers are set in advance so the decision is already made: cut the size at the tier and restore it by the written rule. If the drawdown reaches the deeper tier, stop.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"drawdown-tiers\"],\"hints\":[\"The rule was written before the drawdown.\",\"What does the plan say, word for word?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.7-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What did the study of Brazilian day traders find?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Most day traders became profitable after a year\",\"rationale\":\"The opposite: persistence didn't help. Of those who kept going over 300 days, 97% lost.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"97% of those who day traded futures for over 300 days lost money\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Day traders matched the market after costs\",\"rationale\":\"They lost money overall, and persistence didn't help.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Only beginners lost; experienced traders won\",\"rationale\":\"The 97% counts only those who persisted over 300 days. Experience didn't save them.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"The study followed everyone in Brazil who day traded mini index futures for over 300 days: 97% lost money. Persistence didn't produce winners, which makes it a base rate to start from.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"base-rates\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.5.7-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why is staying out of a no-edge stretch of noise a skill, not a missed opportunity?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because noise always reverses\",\"rationale\":\"Noise has no reliable direction at all. That's why trades there have no edge.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Because you'll make more trades later to compensate\",\"rationale\":\"Making up trades later is overtrading. Each no-edge trade loses its costs on average.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"It isn't a skill: the Arena only rewards profit\",\"rationale\":\"The Arena scores correct abstention in no-edge segments. Profit alone isn't the measure.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Any trade there has negative expected value after costs, so not trading is the better decision\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"In noise your setups have no edge, so each trade's expectancy is about minus its costs. Not trading saves that cost, which is why the Arena scores abstention there.\",\"lesson\":\"4.5.7\",\"concepts\":[\"no-trade-conditions\"],\"hints\":[\"What is a trade's expectancy when there's no edge?\",\"Costs are paid on every trade.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.1-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"You work fixed hours, 09:00–17:00, and can't look at a screen during them. Which style do your hours rule out?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Swing trading with orders set in the evening\",\"rationale\":\"Swing orders can be set after work and run on their own. Fixed hours point towards this style, not away from it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Scalping during the session\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Position trading with weekly reviews\",\"rationale\":\"Position trades need a few decisions a week, all of them possible outside work.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A systematic strategy with automated end-of-day orders\",\"rationale\":\"Automated end-of-day orders need no screen time during work. That's what fixed hours point to.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Scalping needs your full attention while the market is busy, which is exactly when you're at work. Fixed hours point towards swing or position trading and systematic rules with end-of-day orders.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"constraints-matrix\",\"trading-styles\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.1-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What is the difference between a declared and a measured constraint?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"A declared one is in the plan; a measured one is only in your head\",\"rationale\":\"That's backwards. Declared means self-described; measured means counted from your journal.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"None: if you know yourself, the two agree\",\"rationale\":\"Self-reports are biased by overconfidence (2.12). People who call themselves patient often close winners in minutes; only the journal shows it.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A declared one is how you describe yourself; a measured one comes from your journal, such as how often you revenge trade\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Measured constraints are about money; declared ones are about time\",\"rationale\":\"Either kind can cover anything. The difference is the source: your description or your recorded behaviour.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The behaviour audit replaces self-description with counts from the journal: trades opened right after a loss, stops moved, overtrading, holding time. Where the two disagree, the measured one goes in the matrix.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"behaviour-audit\"],\"hints\":[\"Ask where each one comes from.\",\"Think of someone who calls themselves patient but closes winners within 20 minutes.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.1-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"A 5% loss makes you want to rewrite your rules, and you've abandoned a plan twice after drawdowns of about that size.\",\"prompt\":\"What does your constraints matrix point you towards?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"0.25–0.5% risk per trade, mean reversion with stops, a diversified book\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Selling options for steady premium\",\"rationale\":\"Short volatility pays small and often, then rarely loses very large amounts. Low tolerance rules it out.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Trend following with a 35% win rate\",\"rationale\":\"A 35% win rate brings long losing runs and deep drawdowns, exactly what low tolerance can't sit through.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"1–2% risk per trade, to recover losses faster\",\"rationale\":\"Bigger risk makes drawdowns deeper. Low tolerance calls for smaller risk, not larger.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"Low drawdown tolerance is a measured constraint here: two abandoned plans. Small risk per trade keeps drawdowns shallow, and higher-win-rate approaches with stops avoid long losing runs. Styles with long losing streaks or rare huge losses are ruled out.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.1\",\"concepts\":[\"constraints-matrix\",\"drawdown\"],\"hints\":[\"Which styles bring long losing runs or rare, very large losses?\",\"Lower risk per trade makes every drawdown shallower.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.2-1\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"A currency pair has a daily ATR of 0.0080 and a round-trip cost of 0.0002. What is its tradability ratio?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":40,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"×\"},\"explanation\":\"Tradability ratio = ATR ÷ round-trip cost = 0.0080 ÷ 0.0002 = 40. The pair moves 40 times its cost in a typical day, a comfortable margin for most styles.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"tradability-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"Ratio = ATR ÷ round-trip cost.\",\"Both are in price units, so they cancel: 0.0080 ÷ 0.0002.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"atr\":[40,120,5],\"cost\":[1,4,1]},\"answer\":\"round(atr / cost, 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A currency pair has a daily ATR of {atr / 10000:4} and a round-trip cost of {cost / 10000:4}. What is its tradability ratio?\",\"explanation\":\"Tradability ratio = ATR ÷ round-trip cost = {atr / 10000:4} ÷ {cost / 10000:4} = {answer:1}. Higher means more movement for each unit of cost.\",\"hint\":\"Both are in price units, so they cancel: {atr / 10000:4} ÷ {cost / 10000:4}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.2-2\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why does scalping need a much higher tradability ratio than swing trading?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Scalpers pay a higher commission rate\",\"rationale\":\"The rate per fill is the same. What changes is the target: the same cost against a far smaller move.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Its targets are a small part of the ATR, so the same round-trip cost takes a much bigger share of each one\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Short-term moves are more predictable, so brokers charge more\",\"rationale\":\"Costs don't depend on predictability. A 0.5 × ATR target simply leaves less room for the same cost.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Swing trades have no costs\",\"rationale\":\"Swing trades pay the same spread and commission, plus any overnight financing. The point is the share of the target.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"On LABX, 0.20 a round trip is 4% of a 2 × ATR swing target (4.80) but about 17% of a 0.5 × ATR scalp target (1.20). Short styles need a much bigger raw edge, or a market that moves far more per unit of cost.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"cost-drag\",\"tradability-ratio\"],\"hints\":[\"Compare the cost with the size of the target, not with the price.\",\"0.20 against 4.80, then against 1.20.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.2-3\",\"topic\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":2,\"scenario\":\"You live in Romania and are free between 15:00 and 19:00, Bucharest time, on weekdays.\",\"prompt\":\"Which window fits you best?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The Asian session\",\"rationale\":\"Tokyo's busy hours fall in the night and early morning in Bucharest, far outside 15:00–19:00.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The US close\",\"rationale\":\"The US close is 23:00 in Bucharest, four hours after your window ends.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"The European index open\",\"rationale\":\"European indices open at 10:00 Bucharest time, while you're busy.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The London–New York overlap\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"The London–New York overlap runs about 15:00–19:00 Bucharest time, typically the busiest FX window, and the US open, 16:30, falls inside it. In the weeks when US and EU clocks disagree, it starts an hour earlier.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.2\",\"concepts\":[\"fx-sessions\"],\"hints\":[\"Convert each window to Bucharest time first.\",\"London and New York overlap from 08:00 to 12:00 New York time.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.3-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Your setup averages +0.25R with a standard deviation of 1.5R. How many trades do you need for a t-statistic of about 2?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":144,\"tolerance\":1,\"unit\":\"trades\"},\"explanation\":\"n = (2 × sd ÷ mean)² = (2 × 1.5 ÷ 0.25)² = 12² = 144 trades. Halve the edge and you need four times as many.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"specialisation\",\"statistical-significance\"],\"hints\":[\"n = (2 × sd ÷ mean)².\",\"2 × 1.5 ÷ 0.25 = 12. Now square it.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m\":[0.15,0.5,0.05],\"s\":[1,2,0.1]},\"answer\":\"ceil(round((2 * s / m) * (2 * s / m), 4))\",\"prompt\":\"Your setup averages +{m:2}R with a standard deviation of {s:1}R. How many trades do you need for a t-statistic of about 2?\",\"explanation\":\"n = (2 × sd ÷ mean)² = (2 × {s:1} ÷ {m:2})² = {2 * s / m:2}² ≈ {answer} trades. Halve the edge and you need four times as many.\",\"hint\":\"2 × {s:1} ÷ {m:2} = {2 * s / m:2}. Now square it.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.3-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What is a no-trade list?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The setups that lost money last month\",\"rationale\":\"That's a results list. A no-trade list is written in advance and doesn't depend on recent outcomes.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Markets your broker doesn't offer\",\"rationale\":\"Those are unavailable, not ruled out by you. The list covers situations in the markets you do trade.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A written list of situations in which you never open a trade, such as 15 minutes before a tier-1 release\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Trades you skipped and now regret\",\"rationale\":\"Those are missed trades. The no-trade list sets the conditions in which skipping is the rule.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"The no-trade list is part of the playbook: checkable conditions in which no new trade is opened, whatever the setup shows. A trade that breaks it is an error, even if it wins.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"specialisation\",\"when-not-to-trade\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.3-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When should you add a second strategy to your playbook?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"When the first one is in a losing streak\",\"rationale\":\"Adding a strategy to escape a drawdown splits your sample just when you need it, and is a decision made under stress.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"As soon as you find a setup with a higher average\",\"rationale\":\"A higher average on a small sample is weak evidence. Each strategy needs its own meaningful sample.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"When the two are highly correlated, so they confirm each other\",\"rationale\":\"Highly correlated strategies are the same bet twice. Diversifying needs low correlation.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"When each has positive expectancy on a meaningful sample, and the two are weakly correlated\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"A second strategy helps only if it adds an independent edge. Each needs its own evidence, and their results must be weakly correlated; otherwise it splits your sample or doubles the same bet.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.3\",\"concepts\":[\"specialisation\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"hints\":[\"What does each strategy need before it counts as an edge?\",\"Two strategies that move together are one bet.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.4-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"Which pipeline stage first tests real execution and your psychology with real money?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"The backtest with costs\",\"rationale\":\"A backtest uses assumed fills and has no emotions in it.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"The forward test in the simulator\",\"rationale\":\"The simulator tests your execution, but no real money is at stake, so real fills and real nerves aren't tested yet.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Live at minimum size\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"The robustness checks\",\"rationale\":\"Robustness tests the rules across parameters, markets and periods, still on past data.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Minimum live size is the first stage with real fills, real costs and real money at stake, at a size where mistakes are cheap. Scaling begins only after it.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"development-pipeline\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.4-2\",\"topic\":\"psychology\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"Why are kill criteria written before a strategy goes live?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Because brokers require them\",\"rationale\":\"No broker asks for them. They protect you from your own judgment in a drawdown.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"In a drawdown, judgment is biased; thresholds set while calm can't be bent to hope or fear\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"So they can be loosened if the strategy recovers\",\"rationale\":\"Loosening thresholds mid-drawdown is exactly what writing them in advance prevents.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Because a Monte Carlo can only be run before trading starts\",\"rationale\":\"You can rerun it at any time. What matters is when you decide, not when you compute.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Mid-drawdown, sunk cost, loss aversion and hope push you to move the line. Written beforehand from the Monte Carlo, the thresholds are a decision already made; the drawdown only checks it.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"kill-criteria\",\"sunk-cost\"],\"hints\":[\"Who decides: you while calm, or you during a drawdown?\",\"Which biases from 2.12 push a trader to move a limit?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.4-3\",\"topic\":\"risk_management\",\"kind\":\"scenario\",\"difficulty\":3,\"scenario\":\"Your system's written thresholds: review past 13.5R (its 95th Monte Carlo percentile), kill past 16.5R (the 99th). It's now 15R below its peak.\",\"prompt\":\"What do you do?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Kill it: 15R is a deep drawdown\",\"rationale\":\"15R is past the review line but short of the kill line, 16.5R. Killing now overrides your own written rules.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Keep trading at the same size: it's above the kill line\",\"rationale\":\"The review line has been crossed. That means a stage down and a full review, not business as usual.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Double the size to recover faster\",\"rationale\":\"Raising size in a drawdown is martingale thinking, and breaks every scaling rule.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Step down one capital stage and run a full review\"}],\"correct\":\"d\",\"explanation\":\"15R lies between the 95th and 99th percentiles: unusual, but not proof of breakage. The written response is review: one capital stage down and a full review. Kill only past 16.5R, on an interval entirely below zero, or if the regime is gone.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.4\",\"concepts\":[\"kill-criteria\",\"monte-carlo-resampling\"],\"hints\":[\"Place 15R between the two written lines.\",\"Review comes past the 95th percentile; kill past the 99th.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.5-1\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"A system wins 50% of its trades at +2R and loses 1R otherwise. What is its full Kelly fraction, in %?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":25,\"tolerance\":0.1,\"unit\":\"%\"},\"explanation\":\"f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b = 0.50 − 0.50 ÷ 2 = 0.25, or 25%. That's a ceiling: a 1% risk is a twenty-fifth of it, room for an overestimated edge.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"kelly-criterion\"],\"hints\":[\"Kelly: f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b.\",\"0.50 ÷ 2 = 0.25. Subtract it from 0.50.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"w\":[0.35,0.6,0.05],\"b\":[1.5,3,0.5]},\"constraints\":[\"w - (1 - w) / b > 0.02\"],\"answer\":\"round(100 * (w - (1 - w) / b), 1)\",\"prompt\":\"A system wins {w * 100:0}% of its trades at +{b:1}R and loses 1R otherwise. What is its full Kelly fraction, in %?\",\"explanation\":\"f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b = {w:2} − {1 - w:2} ÷ {b:1} = {answer:1}%. That's a ceiling, far above a 1% risk.\",\"hint\":\"{1 - w:2} ÷ {b:1} = {(1 - w) / b:3}. Subtract it from {w:2}.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.5-2\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"When do you increase your position size?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"After a losing streak, to win the losses back\",\"rationale\":\"That's martingale: size grows exactly when the system may be broken.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"After a great week\",\"rationale\":\"A week is no sample. A step up needs trades at the stage, adherence and a drawdown inside the band.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"After a new equity high, once the stage's criteria are met: sample, adherence and drawdown\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Whenever the account grows by 10%\",\"rationale\":\"Profit alone isn't the criterion. A 10% gain can come with poor adherence or a tiny sample.\"}],\"correct\":\"c\",\"explanation\":\"Size steps up about 25% at a time, only after a new equity high and once the stage's evidence is in: enough trades, adherence of 90% or more, and the drawdown inside the Monte Carlo band.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"capital-staging\"],\"hints\":[\"Profit alone never unlocks a stage.\",\"Think of the three criteria every stage needs.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.5-3\",\"topic\":\"position_sizing\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":3,\"prompt\":\"Why does scaling into an illiquid instrument erode your edge?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Illiquid instruments never trend\",\"rationale\":\"Liquidity says nothing about trends. The problem is the cost your own size creates.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Your own orders move the price: impact grows with order size against daily volume, so each unit costs more as you grow\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Brokers charge higher commission rates on larger orders\",\"rationale\":\"Commission rates usually fall with volume. Impact is the cost that grows with size.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"It doesn't: costs per unit are the same at any size\",\"rationale\":\"Spread and commission per unit may be, but market impact rises with your order size against the market's volume.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Market impact grows roughly with the square root of your order size relative to daily volume (unit 4.2). An edge that pays at 100 units can vanish at 10,000, because each fill moves the price further against you.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.5\",\"concepts\":[\"capital-staging\",\"trading-costs\"],\"hints\":[\"Which cost depends on your own size?\",\"Compare your order with the market's daily volume.\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.6-1\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"numeric\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"100 trades, expectancy +0.15R, standard deviation 1.2R. What is the lower end of the 95% interval of the expectancy, in R?\",\"numeric\":{\"answer\":-0.09,\"tolerance\":0.01,\"unit\":\"R\"},\"explanation\":\"Standard error = 1.2 ÷ √100 = 0.12R. Half-width = 1.96 × 0.12 = 0.24R. Interval: 0.15 − 0.24 = −0.09R to 0.15 + 0.24 = +0.39R. Zero is inside, so the edge isn't proven yet.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"decay-vs-variance\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\"],\"hints\":[\"Standard error = sd ÷ √n.\",\"Lower end = mean − 1.96 × standard error.\"],\"vary\":{\"fields\":{\"m\":[0.05,0.4,0.05],\"s\":[0.8,2,0.1],\"n\":[50,200,10]},\"answer\":\"round(m - round(1.96 * s / sqrt(n), 2), 2)\",\"prompt\":\"{n} trades, expectancy +{m:2}R, standard deviation {s:1}R. What is the lower end of the 95% interval of the expectancy, in R?\",\"explanation\":\"Standard error = {s:1} ÷ √{n} = {s / sqrt(n):3}R. Half-width = 1.96 × {s / sqrt(n):3} = {1.96 * s / sqrt(n):2}R. Lower end = {m:2} − {1.96 * s / sqrt(n):2} = {answer:2}R.\",\"hint\":\"Standard error = {s:1} ÷ √{n} = {s / sqrt(n):3}R. Multiply by 1.96, then subtract.\"}},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.6-2\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":2,\"prompt\":\"What must exist before you change a rule in your plan?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Three losses in a row with the current rule\",\"rationale\":\"Three losses are noise for most systems. A change needs a hypothesis and evidence, not a streak.\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"A written hypothesis, an evidence threshold met, a simulator test, and a new logged plan version\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"A strong sense that the rule is wrong\",\"rationale\":\"A feeling isn't evidence. It can start a hypothesis, which then has to pass a test.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"A better result for the new rule over the last 10 trades\",\"rationale\":\"Ten trades fit noise. The new version needs a threshold set in advance, such as 30–50 trades or a walk-forward.\"}],\"correct\":\"b\",\"explanation\":\"Rule changes follow the governance from 3.11: state the hypothesis, set the evidence threshold in advance (30–50 trades, or a backtest with walk-forward), test it in the simulator, then release a new plan version and log the reason.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"rule-change-governance\",\"continuous-improvement\"],\"hints\":[\"Think of the four steps from 3.11.10.\",\"When is the evidence threshold set: before or after the test?\"]},{\"id\":\"q-4.6.6-3\",\"topic\":\"decision_making\",\"kind\":\"mcq\",\"difficulty\":1,\"prompt\":\"What does the yearly review compare?\",\"options\":[{\"id\":\"a\",\"label\":\"Your net, risk-adjusted result with the passive benchmark's, after every cost\"},{\"id\":\"b\",\"label\":\"Your gross profit with last year's\",\"rationale\":\"Gross results ignore costs, and last year isn't the alternative. The alternative is a passive benchmark.\"},{\"id\":\"c\",\"label\":\"Your win rate with other traders'\",\"rationale\":\"A win rate means nothing without the payoff, and other traders' results aren't your alternative.\"},{\"id\":\"d\",\"label\":\"Your best month with your worst\",\"rationale\":\"The gap between months shows variance, not whether trading beat the simple alternative.\"}],\"correct\":\"a\",\"explanation\":\"The yearly question is whether your trading beat what buy-and-hold would have earned with no effort, after all costs and adjusted for risk. It ends in a decision: continue, reduce, retire the strategy, or invest passively.\",\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\",\"concepts\":[\"continuous-improvement\",\"passive-benchmark\"]}],\"terms\":[{\"id\":\"market\",\"term\":\"Market\",\"definition\":\"A place or system where buyers and sellers trade an instrument. The price is where they agree.\",\"plain\":\"Where people who want to buy meet people who want to sell.\",\"related\":[\"auction\",\"order\",\"instrument\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"auction\",\"term\":\"Auction\",\"definition\":\"A way of matching buyers and sellers at the price where the most units can change hands.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone names a price, and the price that lets the most trades happen wins.\",\"related\":[\"market\",\"order\",\"order-book\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"order\",\"term\":\"Order\",\"definition\":\"An instruction to buy or sell a quantity of an instrument, at the market price or at a chosen price.\",\"plain\":\"Your request to buy or sell: how many, and at what price.\",\"related\":[\"market-order\",\"limit-order\",\"order-book\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"counterparty\",\"term\":\"Counterparty\",\"definition\":\"The person or firm on the other side of your trade: the seller when you buy, the buyer when you sell.\",\"plain\":\"Whoever takes the other side of your trade.\",\"related\":[\"market-maker\",\"hedger\",\"speculator\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"hedger\",\"term\":\"Hedger\",\"definition\":\"Someone who trades to reduce a risk they already have, such as a future cost or income.\",\"plain\":\"A business or person using the market as insurance.\",\"related\":[\"hedge\",\"speculator\",\"futures\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"speculator\",\"term\":\"Speculator\",\"definition\":\"Someone who trades to profit from price changes and accepts the risk of losing.\",\"plain\":\"Someone betting on where the price goes next. In this course, that's you.\",\"related\":[\"hedger\",\"retail-trader\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"retail-trader\",\"term\":\"Retail trader\",\"definition\":\"An individual trading their own money, usually in small amounts, through a broker.\",\"plain\":\"A private person trading for themselves, not a professional firm.\",\"related\":[\"speculator\",\"counterparty\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"asset-class\",\"term\":\"Asset class\",\"definition\":\"A group of instruments with similar features, such as stocks, bonds, currencies, commodities or crypto.\",\"plain\":\"A family of things you can trade that follow similar rules.\",\"related\":[\"stock\",\"bond\",\"forex\",\"futures\",\"cryptocurrency\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"stock\",\"term\":\"Stock\",\"definition\":\"A share of ownership in a company, usually traded on an exchange.\",\"plain\":\"A small piece of a company.\",\"related\":[\"etf\",\"index\",\"asset-class\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Share\",\"Equity\"]},{\"id\":\"etf\",\"term\":\"ETF\",\"definition\":\"Exchange-traded fund: a fund holding a basket of assets, traded like a single share.\",\"plain\":\"One share that holds many investments at once.\",\"related\":[\"index\",\"stock\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Exchange-traded fund\"]},{\"id\":\"index\",\"term\":\"Index\",\"definition\":\"A number that tracks the value of a group of assets, such as 500 large companies.\",\"plain\":\"A scoreboard for a whole group of shares.\",\"related\":[\"etf\",\"stock\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"forex\",\"term\":\"Forex\",\"definition\":\"The market for exchanging currencies, quoted in pairs such as EUR/USD.\",\"plain\":\"Trading one currency for another.\",\"related\":[\"asset-class\",\"leverage\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"FX\",\"Foreign exchange\"]},{\"id\":\"futures\",\"term\":\"Futures\",\"definition\":\"Exchange-traded contracts to buy or sell an asset at a set price on a future date.\",\"plain\":\"A deal made now for a trade later, at a price fixed today.\",\"related\":[\"derivative\",\"commodity\",\"hedger\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Futures contract\"]},{\"id\":\"commodity\",\"term\":\"Commodity\",\"definition\":\"A raw material traded in bulk, such as oil, gold or wheat, usually through futures.\",\"plain\":\"Physical goods like oil, metals or crops.\",\"related\":[\"futures\",\"asset-class\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"cfd\",\"term\":\"CFD\",\"definition\":\"Contract for difference: a contract with a provider that pays the price change of an asset you don't own.\",\"plain\":\"A deal with your provider on a price, without owning the asset. The provider is on the other side.\",\"related\":[\"derivative\",\"leverage\",\"counterparty\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Contract for difference\"]},{\"id\":\"cryptocurrency\",\"term\":\"Cryptocurrency\",\"definition\":\"A digital asset recorded on a blockchain and traded on exchanges around the clock.\",\"plain\":\"Digital money like Bitcoin, traded 24/7.\",\"related\":[\"asset-class\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Crypto\"]},{\"id\":\"bond\",\"term\":\"Bond\",\"definition\":\"A loan to a government or company that pays interest. Its price falls when interest rates rise.\",\"plain\":\"An IOU that pays you interest.\",\"related\":[\"asset-class\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"derivative\",\"term\":\"Derivative\",\"definition\":\"An instrument whose value comes from another asset, such as a futures contract, an option or a CFD.\",\"plain\":\"A contract based on the price of something else.\",\"related\":[\"futures\",\"cfd\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"benchmark\",\"term\":\"Benchmark\",\"definition\":\"A reference result to compare against, such as buying and holding the same market.\",\"plain\":\"The result you'd have got without trying: the score to beat.\",\"related\":[\"buy-and-hold\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"buy-and-hold\",\"term\":\"Buy-and-hold\",\"definition\":\"Buying an asset and keeping it for a long time, whatever it does in the short term.\",\"plain\":\"Buy once and sit tight.\",\"related\":[\"benchmark\",\"position-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"scalping\",\"term\":\"Scalping\",\"definition\":\"Trading tiny moves held for seconds or minutes, many times a day. Costs weigh most here.\",\"plain\":\"Lots of very quick trades for very small gains.\",\"related\":[\"day-trading\",\"spread\",\"commission\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"day-trading\",\"term\":\"Day trading\",\"definition\":\"Opening and closing positions within the same day, with nothing held overnight.\",\"plain\":\"Every trade is closed before the day ends.\",\"related\":[\"scalping\",\"swing-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"swing-trading\",\"term\":\"Swing trading\",\"definition\":\"Holding positions for days to a few weeks to capture one swing in price.\",\"plain\":\"Riding one move up or down over several days.\",\"related\":[\"day-trading\",\"position-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"position-trading\",\"term\":\"Position trading\",\"definition\":\"Holding positions for weeks to months, usually to follow longer trends.\",\"plain\":\"Holding a trade for months to follow a big trend.\",\"related\":[\"swing-trading\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"risk-warning\",\"term\":\"Risk warning\",\"definition\":\"A broker's required notice stating what share of its retail clients lose money.\",\"plain\":\"The line on a CFD provider's site saying what share of its clients lose money.\",\"related\":[\"regulator\",\"cfd\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"regulator\",\"term\":\"Regulator\",\"definition\":\"A public authority that licenses and supervises brokers and markets, such as ESMA, the FCA or the ASF.\",\"plain\":\"The official watchdog that brokers must answer to.\",\"related\":[\"risk-warning\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"risk-discipline\",\"term\":\"Risk Discipline\",\"definition\":\"The MARKET//LAB score for following risk rules: a stop on every entry, risk within limits, no widened stops.\",\"plain\":\"Did you stick to your safety rules, whatever the result?\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"simulated-outcome\",\"stop-loss\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"simulated-outcome\",\"term\":\"Simulated Outcome\",\"definition\":\"The simulated profit or loss of your trades. It is shown, but never used to grade decisions.\",\"plain\":\"What you'd have made or lost. Shown, never scored.\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"risk-discipline\",\"simulation\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"evidence-grade\",\"term\":\"Evidence grade\",\"definition\":\"A label, from Mechanic and A to F, showing how well a method has held up when tested.\",\"plain\":\"A report card for an idea: how well it held up in tests.\",\"related\":[\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"0.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"outcome-bias\",\"term\":\"Outcome bias\",\"definition\":\"Judging a decision by how it turned out rather than by how it was made: the information and reasoning available at the time.\",\"plain\":\"Calling a choice good because it worked, or bad because it didn't.\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"cognitive-bias\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"expected-value\",\"term\":\"Expected value\",\"definition\":\"The average result of a bet over many plays: each outcome times its probability, added up.\",\"plain\":\"What a game pays per play, on average, if you play it many times.\",\"related\":[\"probability\",\"expectancy\",\"variance\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"EV\"]},{\"id\":\"probability\",\"term\":\"Probability\",\"definition\":\"How likely something is, from 0 (never) to 1 (certain), often written as a percentage.\",\"plain\":\"The chance that something happens: 0.5, or 50%, is a coin flip.\",\"related\":[\"expected-value\",\"sample-size\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"losing-streak\",\"term\":\"Losing streak\",\"definition\":\"Several losses in a row. In random sequences they are normal and often longer than expected.\",\"plain\":\"A run of losses one after another, which chance alone produces regularly.\",\"related\":[\"variance\",\"drawdown\",\"win-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"sample-size\",\"term\":\"Sample size\",\"definition\":\"How many trades or observations a figure is based on. Small samples can mislead.\",\"plain\":\"How much evidence is behind a number: 20 trades is little, 500 is a lot more.\",\"related\":[\"law-of-large-numbers\",\"win-rate\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"law-of-large-numbers\",\"term\":\"Law of large numbers\",\"definition\":\"As the number of trials grows, the average result moves closer to the expected value.\",\"plain\":\"Play long enough and the average settles near what the odds say; play briefly and it can be far off.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"expected-value\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"random-walk\",\"term\":\"Random walk\",\"definition\":\"A price path in which each move is random. It still produces trends and levels by chance.\",\"plain\":\"A chart drawn by coin flips. It can look just like a real market.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"support\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"survivorship-bias\",\"term\":\"Survivorship bias\",\"definition\":\"Looking only at the winners that remain and forgetting the many that failed or disappeared.\",\"plain\":\"Seeing the lucky few who made it and thinking their way must work.\",\"related\":[\"cognitive-bias\",\"sample-size\"],\"lesson\":\"0.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"borrow-fee\",\"term\":\"Borrow fee\",\"definition\":\"The fee a short seller pays to borrow shares, charged for as long as the short stays open.\",\"plain\":\"Rent on the shares you borrowed so you could sell them.\",\"related\":[\"short\",\"commission\",\"overnight-financing\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"last-price\",\"term\":\"Last price\",\"definition\":\"The price of the most recent trade. Stock charts usually plot it; LABX charts plot the mid.\",\"plain\":\"Where the latest deal happened, not necessarily a price you can trade at now.\",\"related\":[\"bid\",\"ask\",\"mid-price\",\"quote\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Last\"]},{\"id\":\"quote\",\"term\":\"Quote\",\"definition\":\"The current bid and ask for an instrument, often shown with the last price and the day's range.\",\"plain\":\"The two prices on offer right now: one to sell at, one to buy at.\",\"related\":[\"bid\",\"ask\",\"spread\",\"last-price\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"overnight-financing\",\"term\":\"Overnight financing\",\"definition\":\"Interest charged or paid for holding a leveraged position, such as a CFD or forex trade, past the daily cut-off.\",\"plain\":\"A nightly charge for keeping a position open with borrowed money.\",\"related\":[\"leverage\",\"commission\",\"borrow-fee\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.4\",\"aka\":[\"Swap\",\"Rollover\"]},{\"id\":\"payment-for-order-flow\",\"term\":\"Payment for order flow\",\"definition\":\"Money a broker receives for sending clients' orders to a particular firm to be filled.\",\"plain\":\"How some 'zero commission' brokers get paid: by the firm that fills your orders.\",\"related\":[\"commission\",\"spread\",\"market-maker\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.4\",\"aka\":[\"PFOF\"]},{\"id\":\"round-trip\",\"term\":\"Round trip\",\"definition\":\"Opening a position and closing it again. You pay commission twice and cross the spread once.\",\"plain\":\"In and out of a trade: its full cost is paid only once you've done both.\",\"related\":[\"commission\",\"spread\",\"break-even\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"break-even\",\"term\":\"Break-even\",\"definition\":\"The exit price, or win rate, at which a trade or method neither makes nor loses money after costs.\",\"plain\":\"The point where the costs are covered: no gain, no loss.\",\"related\":[\"round-trip\",\"commission\",\"win-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"point\",\"term\":\"Point\",\"definition\":\"A price move of 1.00. On LABX, one point on one unit is worth 1.00 vUSD.\",\"plain\":\"One whole step of price: from 100.00 to 101.00 is one point.\",\"related\":[\"tick\",\"pip\",\"contract-multiplier\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"pip\",\"term\":\"Pip\",\"definition\":\"The usual price step in most currency pairs, 0.0001. On 10,000 units of EUR/USD it is worth 1.00 USD.\",\"plain\":\"The small step forex prices are counted in.\",\"related\":[\"point\",\"tick\",\"lot\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"lot\",\"term\":\"Lot\",\"definition\":\"A standard block of units, such as 100,000 units of a currency pair in forex.\",\"plain\":\"A ready-made trade size: you order 1 lot instead of 100,000 units.\",\"related\":[\"unit\",\"pip\",\"position-size\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"contract-multiplier\",\"term\":\"Contract multiplier\",\"definition\":\"The money one futures contract gains or loses for each point of price movement.\",\"plain\":\"What one point is worth on one contract.\",\"related\":[\"point\",\"tick\",\"notional-value\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"order-ticket\",\"term\":\"Order ticket\",\"definition\":\"The form where you set an order's instrument, side, size, type, price, stop and target before sending it.\",\"plain\":\"The order form you check before pressing Send.\",\"related\":[\"market-order\",\"limit-order\",\"stop-loss\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"fat-finger-error\",\"term\":\"Fat-finger error\",\"definition\":\"A typing mistake in an order, such as an extra zero in the size or the wrong side.\",\"plain\":\"A slip of the finger that sends the wrong order.\",\"related\":[\"order-ticket\",\"position-size\"],\"lesson\":\"1.1.7\",\"aka\":[\"Fat finger\"]},{\"id\":\"line-chart\",\"term\":\"Line chart\",\"definition\":\"A chart that joins each period's closing price with a line, leaving out the opens, highs and lows.\",\"plain\":\"One point per period, at the close, joined up. Clean, but it hides how far price reached.\",\"related\":[\"close\",\"candle\",\"ohlc-bar\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"ohlc-bar\",\"term\":\"OHLC bar\",\"definition\":\"A chart bar showing open, high, low and close: a vertical line from low to high, a tick on the left for the open and one on the right for the close.\",\"plain\":\"The same four prices as a candle, drawn as a line with two small side ticks instead of a body.\",\"related\":[\"candle\",\"open\",\"close\",\"line-chart\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"Bar chart\"]},{\"id\":\"tick-volume\",\"term\":\"Tick volume\",\"definition\":\"The number of price changes in a period at one broker, used in spot forex and CFDs where total traded volume isn't available.\",\"plain\":\"How often one broker's price moved, used as a stand-in for real volume.\",\"related\":[\"volume\",\"tick\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"candle-range\",\"term\":\"Candle range\",\"definition\":\"A candle's height, high minus low. Not the same as a range, the sideways market.\",\"plain\":\"How far price travelled from bottom to top within one candle.\",\"related\":[\"candle\",\"high\",\"low\",\"range\",\"atr\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"log-scale\",\"term\":\"Log scale\",\"definition\":\"A chart scale on which equal percentage moves take up equal heights, useful for long histories with large price changes.\",\"plain\":\"A doubling looks the same size wherever it happens on the chart.\",\"related\":[\"linear-scale\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.6\",\"aka\":[\"Logarithmic scale\"]},{\"id\":\"linear-scale\",\"term\":\"Linear scale\",\"definition\":\"A chart scale on which equal price moves take up equal heights.\",\"plain\":\"Every 1.00 of price takes the same space, whether price is at 20 or at 200.\",\"related\":[\"log-scale\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.6\",\"aka\":[\"Arithmetic scale\"]},{\"id\":\"cherry-picking\",\"term\":\"Cherry-picking\",\"definition\":\"Showing only the examples that support a claim and leaving out the ones that don't.\",\"plain\":\"Showing the charts where it worked and hiding the ones where it didn't.\",\"related\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"1.2.7\"},{\"id\":\"stop-limit-order\",\"term\":\"Stop-limit order\",\"definition\":\"A stop order that becomes a limit order when its trigger is reached, so it fills only at the limit price or better and may not fill at all if price jumps past it.\",\"plain\":\"A stop with a price cap: it won't chase price past your limit, even if that means no fill.\",\"related\":[\"stop-order\",\"limit-order\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.3\",\"aka\":[\"Stop limit\"]},{\"id\":\"oco-order\",\"term\":\"OCO order\",\"definition\":\"One-cancels-other: two linked orders where the fill of one cancels the other automatically, as with a bracket's stop and target or a two-sided breakout entry.\",\"plain\":\"Two orders tied together: whichever fills first, the other disappears.\",\"related\":[\"bracket-order\",\"pending-order\",\"stop-order\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.4\",\"aka\":[\"OCO\",\"One-cancels-other\"]},{\"id\":\"pending-order\",\"term\":\"Pending order\",\"definition\":\"An order that has been placed but not yet filled, waiting for its price, such as a limit order or a stop entry.\",\"plain\":\"An order that's still waiting for its price.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order\",\"stop-order\",\"time-in-force\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.4\",\"aka\":[\"Resting order\",\"Working order\"]},{\"id\":\"time-in-force\",\"term\":\"Time in force\",\"definition\":\"How long an order stays active before it fills or is cancelled: Day, good till cancelled (GTC), good till date (GTD), immediate or cancel (IOC) or fill or kill (FOK).\",\"plain\":\"The order's expiry setting: how long it's allowed to wait.\",\"related\":[\"day-order\",\"gtc\",\"ioc\",\"fok\",\"pending-order\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"TIF\"]},{\"id\":\"day-order\",\"term\":\"Day order\",\"definition\":\"An order that is cancelled automatically at the end of the trading session if it hasn't filled.\",\"plain\":\"An order that's good for today only.\",\"related\":[\"time-in-force\",\"gtc\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"gtc\",\"term\":\"GTC\",\"definition\":\"Good till cancelled: an order that stays active until it fills or you cancel it. Some brokers still cancel such orders after a set period.\",\"plain\":\"An order that keeps waiting, for days or weeks, until it fills or you cancel it.\",\"related\":[\"time-in-force\",\"day-order\",\"pending-order\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"Good till cancelled\"]},{\"id\":\"ioc\",\"term\":\"IOC\",\"definition\":\"Immediate or cancel: an order that fills whatever is available now, at its price or better, and cancels the rest.\",\"plain\":\"Take what you can right now and forget the rest.\",\"related\":[\"fok\",\"partial-fill\",\"time-in-force\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"Immediate or cancel\"]},{\"id\":\"fok\",\"term\":\"FOK\",\"definition\":\"Fill or kill: an order that fills in full at once, at its price or better, or is cancelled entirely.\",\"plain\":\"All of it right now, or nothing.\",\"related\":[\"ioc\",\"time-in-force\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"Fill or kill\"]},{\"id\":\"queue-priority\",\"term\":\"Queue priority\",\"definition\":\"The rule that decides which resting orders fill first: the best price first and, at the same price, the earliest order.\",\"plain\":\"First in line at the best price gets filled first.\",\"related\":[\"order-book\",\"limit-order\",\"partial-fill\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.6\",\"aka\":[\"Price-time priority\"]},{\"id\":\"average-fill-price\",\"term\":\"Average fill price\",\"definition\":\"The size-weighted average price of an order that filled in parts at several prices: the total cost divided by the total units.\",\"plain\":\"What you paid per unit, on average, when an order filled at more than one price.\",\"related\":[\"fill\",\"partial-fill\",\"market-depth\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.6\",\"aka\":[\"Average price\"]},{\"id\":\"market-depth\",\"term\":\"Market depth\",\"definition\":\"How many units are waiting to be bought and sold at each price near the current one, as listed in the order book.\",\"plain\":\"How much is on offer at each price, not just at the best one.\",\"related\":[\"order-book\",\"liquidity\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.6\",\"aka\":[\"Depth\",\"Level 2\"]},{\"id\":\"guaranteed-stop\",\"term\":\"Guaranteed stop\",\"definition\":\"A stop a broker promises to fill at its exact price, even through a gap, in exchange for an extra fee.\",\"plain\":\"Insurance on your stop: you pay a premium, and the broker takes on the gap.\",\"related\":[\"stop-loss\",\"gap\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"1.3.7\",\"aka\":[\"Guaranteed stop-loss\",\"GSL\"]},{\"id\":\"initial-risk\",\"term\":\"Initial risk\",\"definition\":\"The loss a trade would take if the stop set at entry filled as planned, with both commissions included. It stays the trade's 1R even if the stop is moved later.\",\"plain\":\"What you stood to lose when you entered. Every result of that trade is measured against it.\",\"related\":[\"r\",\"r-multiple\",\"stop-loss\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"1.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"cost-drag\",\"term\":\"Cost drag\",\"definition\":\"The share of a trade's gross result taken by costs such as the spread and commissions. It grows as stops and targets get smaller.\",\"plain\":\"How much of what you make or lose goes to costs. Small targets lose a bigger share.\",\"related\":[\"spread\",\"commission\",\"round-trip\",\"break-even\",\"r\"],\"lesson\":\"1.4.6\"},{\"id\":\"underwater-curve\",\"term\":\"Underwater curve\",\"definition\":\"A chart of how far equity is below its previous peak at each point in time. It shows each drawdown's depth and how long it lasted.\",\"plain\":\"A picture of every stretch spent below your best account value: how deep, and for how long.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"max-drawdown\",\"equity\"],\"lesson\":\"1.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"daily-loss-limit\",\"term\":\"Daily loss limit\",\"definition\":\"The most you allow yourself to lose in one day, set before the session in R or as a percentage of equity. Once the next trade's risk no longer fits, trading stops until the next session.\",\"plain\":\"A cap on a bad day. Reach it and you stop until tomorrow.\",\"related\":[\"risk-per-trade\",\"r\",\"revenge-trading\",\"trading-plan\"],\"lesson\":\"1.4.9\"},{\"id\":\"swing-high\",\"term\":\"Swing high\",\"definition\":\"A candle whose high is above the highs of the candles on each side, two on each side under this course's rule. It marks a local peak.\",\"plain\":\"A peak in price: a candle that reached higher than its neighbours on both sides.\",\"related\":[\"swing-low\",\"market-structure\",\"high\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Pivot high\"]},{\"id\":\"swing-low\",\"term\":\"Swing low\",\"definition\":\"A candle whose low is below the lows of the candles on each side, two on each side under this course's rule. It marks a local trough.\",\"plain\":\"A dip in price: a candle that reached lower than its neighbours on both sides.\",\"related\":[\"swing-high\",\"market-structure\",\"low\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Pivot low\"]},{\"id\":\"market-structure\",\"term\":\"Market structure\",\"definition\":\"The pattern of swing highs and swing lows that shows whether price is trending up, trending down or moving sideways.\",\"plain\":\"The shape of the swings: rising highs and lows, falling ones, or a band price keeps returning to.\",\"related\":[\"swing-high\",\"swing-low\",\"trend\",\"range\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.1\"},{\"id\":\"leg\",\"term\":\"Leg\",\"definition\":\"A move from one swing point to the next, such as from a swing low up to the next swing high.\",\"plain\":\"One stretch of the zigzag: a run up from a low to a high, or down from a high to a low.\",\"related\":[\"pullback\",\"retracement\",\"swing-high\",\"swing-low\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.3\"},{\"id\":\"round-number\",\"term\":\"Round number\",\"definition\":\"A price ending in 00 or 50, such as 100.00 or 100.50. Orders tend to cluster at and just beyond such prices.\",\"plain\":\"An easy, memorable price that many traders pick for their orders.\",\"related\":[\"stop-cluster\",\"support\",\"resistance\",\"take-profit\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"stop-cluster\",\"term\":\"Stop cluster\",\"definition\":\"Many stop orders resting just beyond an obvious level, such as a round number. Triggered together, they become market orders and can speed up a move.\",\"plain\":\"A crowd of stops in the same spot. When price reaches it, they go off together.\",\"related\":[\"stop-loss\",\"stop-order\",\"round-number\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"retest\",\"term\":\"Retest\",\"definition\":\"A return of price to a level it has just broken, before it moves on or falls back through it.\",\"plain\":\"Price coming back to check the level it just broke.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"role-reversal\",\"false-breakout\",\"support\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"role-reversal\",\"term\":\"Role reversal\",\"definition\":\"When a broken resistance starts acting as support, or a broken support as resistance.\",\"plain\":\"The old ceiling becomes the new floor, or the old floor the new ceiling.\",\"related\":[\"retest\",\"support\",\"resistance\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.6\",\"aka\":[\"Polarity\"]},{\"id\":\"false-breakout\",\"term\":\"False breakout\",\"definition\":\"A move beyond a level that soon closes back inside, trapping traders who acted on the break.\",\"plain\":\"Price escapes its range, then slips straight back in.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"retest\",\"support\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.6\",\"aka\":[\"Failed breakout\"]},{\"id\":\"structure-break\",\"term\":\"Structure break\",\"definition\":\"A close below the last higher low of an uptrend, or above the last lower high of a downtrend. The first warning that the trend may be ending.\",\"plain\":\"The moment the pattern of rising lows, or of falling highs, is broken.\",\"related\":[\"market-structure\",\"trend\",\"reversal\",\"swing-low\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.7\",\"aka\":[\"Break of structure\"]},{\"id\":\"higher-timeframe\",\"term\":\"Higher timeframe\",\"definition\":\"A longer candle interval used for context, such as 4-hour candles when you trade on 1-hour ones.\",\"plain\":\"The zoomed-out chart that shows the bigger trend.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"trend\",\"market-structure\"],\"lesson\":\"1.5.8\",\"aka\":[\"HTF\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-capital\",\"term\":\"Risk capital\",\"definition\":\"Money you could lose entirely without affecting living costs, debts or emergency savings. Only risk capital belongs in a trading account.\",\"plain\":\"Savings minus your emergency fund and money already promised: the most you could afford to lose.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"equity\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"pre-trade-checklist\",\"term\":\"Pre-trade checklist\",\"definition\":\"A short list of yes-or-no questions run before every order. Any 'no' means no trade, or a smaller size when the size is the only problem.\",\"plain\":\"The last check before you send an order: setup, stop, size, limits and your state.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"playbook\",\"stop-loss\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"error-tag\",\"term\":\"Error tag\",\"definition\":\"A fixed name for a process mistake on a trade, such as moving the stop further away or entering without one, used to count mistakes and what they cost in R.\",\"plain\":\"A label for what went wrong in your process, not in the market.\",\"related\":[\"journal\",\"variance\",\"r\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"process-kpi\",\"term\":\"Process KPI\",\"definition\":\"A measure of how well you follow your process, such as the share of trades with a stop at entry. It depends on your actions, not on luck.\",\"plain\":\"A score for following your own rules, which means something even after a few trades.\",\"related\":[\"stop-integrity\",\"risk-accuracy\",\"plan-adherence\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"stop-integrity\",\"term\":\"Stop integrity\",\"definition\":\"The share of trades that had a stop at entry that was never moved further away.\",\"plain\":\"How often your stop was there from the start, and stayed put.\",\"related\":[\"process-kpi\",\"stop-loss\",\"risk-accuracy\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"risk-accuracy\",\"term\":\"Risk accuracy\",\"definition\":\"The share of trades sized correctly: the loss at the stop, costs included, within the plan's limit.\",\"plain\":\"How often your position size matched your risk rule.\",\"related\":[\"process-kpi\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"position-size\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"plan-adherence\",\"term\":\"Plan adherence\",\"definition\":\"The share of trades that matched a setup in your playbook.\",\"plain\":\"How often you traded only what your plan allows.\",\"related\":[\"process-kpi\",\"playbook\",\"trading-plan\"],\"lesson\":\"1.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"fit-to-trade\",\"term\":\"Fit to trade\",\"definition\":\"A check of sleep, stress and health before a session. If it fails, you trade smaller or not at all.\",\"plain\":\"Are you in a fit state to make decisions today? If not, the plan says trade less, or not at all.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"journal\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"gamblers-fallacy\",\"term\":\"Gambler's fallacy\",\"definition\":\"Believing a result is 'due' after a streak, when each outcome is independent of the last.\",\"plain\":\"Thinking a win must come soon because you've lost several times in a row.\",\"related\":[\"hot-hand\",\"variance\",\"cognitive-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.3\"},{\"id\":\"hot-hand\",\"term\":\"Hot hand\",\"definition\":\"Believing a winning streak will keep going, and sizing up because of it.\",\"plain\":\"Feeling 'on fire' after a few wins and betting bigger, though the next result is a new draw.\",\"related\":[\"gamblers-fallacy\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"cognitive-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.3\",\"aka\":[\"Hot-hand fallacy\"]},{\"id\":\"action-bias\",\"term\":\"Action bias\",\"definition\":\"Preferring to do something rather than wait, even when waiting is the better choice.\",\"plain\":\"The itch to trade because nothing is happening.\",\"related\":[\"overtrading\",\"cognitive-bias\",\"playbook\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.5\"},{\"id\":\"if-then-rule\",\"term\":\"If-then rule\",\"definition\":\"A rule decided in advance: if a named trigger happens, then you take a named action.\",\"plain\":\"A plan for a moment you can see coming: 'if this happens, I do that.'\",\"related\":[\"guard-rail\",\"trigger\",\"trading-plan\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.6\",\"aka\":[\"Implementation intention\"]},{\"id\":\"guard-rail\",\"term\":\"Guard-rail\",\"definition\":\"A setting that makes the right action easy and the wrong one hard, such as a daily lock.\",\"plain\":\"A safety barrier in your set-up, like a required stop, so a tired moment can't break your rules.\",\"related\":[\"if-then-rule\",\"cooling-off-pause\",\"stop-loss\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.6\"},{\"id\":\"cooling-off-pause\",\"term\":\"Cooling-off pause\",\"definition\":\"A set pause after a loss or a sharp move, taken before any new decision.\",\"plain\":\"A timed break after a loss, so the next decision isn't made in the heat of the moment.\",\"related\":[\"revenge-trading\",\"if-then-rule\",\"reappraisal\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.7\",\"aka\":[\"Cool-down\"]},{\"id\":\"reappraisal\",\"term\":\"Reappraisal\",\"definition\":\"Reframing a situation to change how it feels, for example: one loss is one draw from many.\",\"plain\":\"Telling yourself a truer, calmer story about what just happened.\",\"related\":[\"cooling-off-pause\",\"cognitive-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"1.7.7\"},{\"id\":\"martingale\",\"term\":\"Martingale\",\"definition\":\"A betting system that doubles the stake after every loss, so that one win recovers all the losses plus the first stake. With limited capital, a long enough losing run wipes it out.\",\"plain\":\"Doubling up after each loss. It wins small most of the time and eventually loses a huge amount at once.\",\"related\":[\"averaging-down\",\"blow-up\",\"position-size\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.1\",\"aka\":[\"Doubling up\"]},{\"id\":\"averaging-down\",\"term\":\"Averaging down\",\"definition\":\"Adding to a losing position to lower its average price, usually without a limit set in advance. It raises the risk on an idea that is already going wrong.\",\"plain\":\"Buying more because the price fell. It makes the bet bigger exactly when it's going wrong.\",\"related\":[\"martingale\",\"scaling-in\",\"blow-up\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.1\"},{\"id\":\"blow-up\",\"term\":\"Blow-up\",\"definition\":\"Losing most or all of an account, usually through oversizing, leverage or doubling after losses.\",\"plain\":\"When an account is wiped out, or damaged so badly it can't realistically recover.\",\"related\":[\"martingale\",\"leverage\",\"drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.1\"},{\"id\":\"grid-trading\",\"term\":\"Grid trading\",\"definition\":\"Placing buy and sell orders at fixed price steps around a price, often without stops, so positions are added as price moves away.\",\"plain\":\"A net of orders every so many points. It earns small amounts in a range and piles up losses in a trend.\",\"related\":[\"averaging-down\",\"martingale\",\"exposure\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.2\",\"aka\":[\"Grid bot\"]},{\"id\":\"out-of-sample\",\"term\":\"Out-of-sample\",\"definition\":\"Data a method was not built or tuned on. Results there are a fairer test than results on the data used to design it.\",\"plain\":\"New data the method has never seen. A system that only fits the past it was built on fails here.\",\"related\":[\"backtest\",\"cherry-picking\",\"survivorship-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.3\"},{\"id\":\"signal-service\",\"term\":\"Signal service\",\"definition\":\"A paid service that tells subscribers when to buy or sell. Its published track record is often selective and rarely audited.\",\"plain\":\"Someone sells you trade alerts. The results they show are usually their best ones.\",\"related\":[\"pump-and-dump\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\"},{\"id\":\"segregated-funds\",\"term\":\"Segregated funds\",\"definition\":\"Client money that a regulated broker must keep in accounts separate from its own, so it can't use it to run its business.\",\"plain\":\"Your deposit is kept apart from the broker's own money, which protects it if the broker fails.\",\"related\":[\"b-book\",\"margin\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\",\"aka\":[\"Client money segregation\"]},{\"id\":\"pump-and-dump\",\"term\":\"Pump-and-dump\",\"definition\":\"Hyping an asset to push its price up, then selling to the buyers the hype attracted. It is illegal market manipulation.\",\"plain\":\"Someone talks a price up, sells to you at the top, and leaves you holding it.\",\"related\":[\"signal-service\",\"liquidity\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\"},{\"id\":\"b-book\",\"term\":\"B-book\",\"definition\":\"A broker model in which the broker takes the other side of client trades instead of passing them to the market, so a client's loss is the broker's gain.\",\"plain\":\"The broker is your counterparty: when you lose, it earns.\",\"related\":[\"market-maker\",\"segregated-funds\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.4\"},{\"id\":\"effective-leverage\",\"term\":\"Effective leverage\",\"definition\":\"The total value of open positions divided by equity. It, not the broker's maximum, sets how hard a price move hits the account.\",\"plain\":\"How big your positions really are compared with your money. At 10×, a 1% move is 10% of your account.\",\"related\":[\"leverage\",\"exposure\",\"margin\",\"margin-close-out\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"margin-call\",\"term\":\"Margin call\",\"definition\":\"A broker's warning or demand for more funds when equity falls below the margin needed to keep positions open.\",\"plain\":\"The broker telling you that your account is too small for the positions you hold.\",\"related\":[\"margin\",\"margin-close-out\",\"leverage\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"margin-close-out\",\"term\":\"Margin close-out\",\"definition\":\"The broker closing positions automatically when equity falls too far below the required margin. For EU retail CFD accounts it happens at 50% of the required margin.\",\"plain\":\"The broker closes your trades for you, at whatever price is there, because your money ran out.\",\"related\":[\"margin-call\",\"margin\",\"effective-leverage\",\"negative-balance-protection\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\",\"aka\":[\"Stop-out\"]},{\"id\":\"negative-balance-protection\",\"term\":\"Negative balance protection\",\"definition\":\"A rule for retail clients in the EU and the UK that limits losses on leveraged products such as CFDs to the money in the account.\",\"plain\":\"You can't end up owing the broker more than you put in.\",\"related\":[\"margin-close-out\",\"leverage\"],\"lesson\":\"1.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"marubozu\",\"term\":\"Marubozu\",\"definition\":\"A candle with a long body and wicks under a tenth of the average range at both ends. White if it closed up, black if it closed down.\",\"plain\":\"A candle with a long body and almost no shadows: one side controlled the whole period.\",\"related\":[\"candle\",\"body\",\"wick\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"spinning-top\",\"term\":\"Spinning top\",\"definition\":\"A candle whose body is shorter than the average body of the last 10 candles and whose two wicks are both longer than the body.\",\"plain\":\"A small-bodied candle with shadows longer than the body on both sides.\",\"related\":[\"candle\",\"doji\",\"high-wave-candle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"high-wave-candle\",\"term\":\"High-wave candle\",\"definition\":\"A short-bodied candle whose upper and lower wicks are both more than twice the body.\",\"plain\":\"A small-bodied candle with shadows more than twice the body on both sides.\",\"related\":[\"spinning-top\",\"doji\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"doji\",\"term\":\"Doji\",\"definition\":\"A candle whose body is at most 10% of the average range of the last 10 candles, so its open and close are almost equal.\",\"plain\":\"A candle whose open and close are almost equal, so it has almost no body.\",\"related\":[\"candle\",\"body\",\"long-legged-doji\",\"dragonfly-doji\",\"gravestone-doji\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"long-legged-doji\",\"term\":\"Long-legged doji\",\"definition\":\"A doji whose upper and lower wicks are both at least 40% of the average range (an app convention).\",\"plain\":\"A doji with long shadows on both sides.\",\"related\":[\"doji\",\"high-wave-candle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"dragonfly-doji\",\"term\":\"Dragonfly doji\",\"definition\":\"A doji with almost no upper wick and a lower wick of at least half the average range: open and close sit at the high.\",\"plain\":\"A doji that opens and closes at its high, with a long lower shadow.\",\"related\":[\"doji\",\"hammer\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"gravestone-doji\",\"term\":\"Gravestone doji\",\"definition\":\"A doji with almost no lower wick and an upper wick of at least half the average range: open and close sit at the low.\",\"plain\":\"A doji that opens and closes at its low, with a long upper shadow.\",\"related\":[\"doji\",\"shooting-star\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"hammer\",\"term\":\"Hammer\",\"definition\":\"After a decline, a short body near the top of the candle, a lower shadow at least twice the body and almost no upper shadow.\",\"plain\":\"After a decline, a small body near the top of the candle with a long lower shadow.\",\"related\":[\"hanging-man\",\"pin-bar\",\"takuri-line\",\"wick\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"hanging-man\",\"term\":\"Hanging man\",\"definition\":\"The hammer's shape after a rise. Taught as bearish; in Bulkowski's tests price kept rising about 59% of the time.\",\"plain\":\"The hammer shape after a rise. In tested data it more often came before further rises.\",\"related\":[\"hammer\",\"shooting-star\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"inverted-hammer\",\"term\":\"Inverted hammer\",\"definition\":\"After a decline, a short body near the low with an upper shadow at least twice the body and almost no lower shadow. Taught as bullish; tested, it more often came before further falls.\",\"plain\":\"After a decline, a small body near the low with a long upper shadow.\",\"related\":[\"shooting-star\",\"hammer\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"shooting-star\",\"term\":\"Shooting star\",\"definition\":\"After a rise, a short body near the low with an upper shadow at least twice the body and almost no lower shadow.\",\"plain\":\"After a rise, a small body near the low with a long upper shadow.\",\"related\":[\"inverted-hammer\",\"pin-bar\",\"gravestone-doji\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"belt-hold\",\"term\":\"Belt hold\",\"definition\":\"A long candle with almost no wick at its opening end: a bullish belt hold opens at its low after a decline, a bearish one at its high after a rise.\",\"plain\":\"A long candle that opens at one extreme and closes near the other.\",\"related\":[\"marubozu\",\"candle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"pin-bar\",\"term\":\"Pin bar\",\"definition\":\"The price-action name for a candle with one long shadow that rejects a price, such as a hammer or a shooting star.\",\"plain\":\"Price-action name for a candle with one long rejecting shadow, like a hammer or shooting star.\",\"related\":[\"hammer\",\"shooting-star\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"takuri-line\",\"term\":\"Takuri line\",\"definition\":\"A hammer whose lower shadow is at least three times its body.\",\"plain\":\"A hammer with an extra-long lower shadow.\",\"related\":[\"hammer\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"confirmation\",\"term\":\"Confirmation\",\"definition\":\"Waiting for a close beyond a pattern, above its high for a bullish one or below its low for a bearish one, before acting on it.\",\"plain\":\"Waiting for a close beyond a pattern before acting on it.\",\"related\":[\"entry\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"base-rate\",\"term\":\"Base rate\",\"definition\":\"How often an outcome occurs across all comparable cases, used as the benchmark before judging any single case.\",\"plain\":\"How often an outcome happens across all similar cases, before you look at the current one.\",\"related\":[\"win-rate\",\"sample-size\",\"random-walk\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"engulfing-pattern\",\"term\":\"Engulfing pattern\",\"definition\":\"Two opposite-coloured candles where the second real body covers the first: bullish after a decline, bearish after a rise. Wicks don't count.\",\"plain\":\"Two candles where the second body fully covers the first, opposite-coloured body.\",\"related\":[\"harami\",\"body\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"harami\",\"term\":\"Harami\",\"definition\":\"Two candles where a short second body sits inside the previous long, opposite-coloured body.\",\"plain\":\"Two candles where a small body sits inside the previous, larger body.\",\"related\":[\"harami-cross\",\"engulfing-pattern\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"harami-cross\",\"term\":\"Harami cross\",\"definition\":\"A harami whose second candle is a doji.\",\"plain\":\"A harami whose second candle has almost no body.\",\"related\":[\"harami\",\"doji\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"piercing-line\",\"term\":\"Piercing line\",\"definition\":\"After a decline, a long bearish candle, then a bullish one that opens below its low and closes above the middle of its body, but below its open.\",\"plain\":\"After a long down candle, an up candle that opens lower and closes above the first body's midpoint.\",\"related\":[\"dark-cloud-cover\",\"thrusting-line\",\"engulfing-pattern\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"dark-cloud-cover\",\"term\":\"Dark cloud cover\",\"definition\":\"After a rise, a long bullish candle, then a bearish one that opens above its high and closes below the middle of its body, but above its open.\",\"plain\":\"After a long up candle, a down candle that opens higher and closes below the first body's midpoint.\",\"related\":[\"piercing-line\",\"engulfing-pattern\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"on-neck-line\",\"term\":\"On-neck line\",\"definition\":\"After a long bearish candle, a bullish candle that opens lower and closes at about the first candle's low.\",\"plain\":\"An up candle after a long down candle that closes at about the down candle's low.\",\"related\":[\"in-neck-line\",\"thrusting-line\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"in-neck-line\",\"term\":\"In-neck line\",\"definition\":\"After a long bearish candle, a bullish candle that opens lower and closes just inside the first body, within a few cents of its close.\",\"plain\":\"An up candle after a long down candle that closes just inside its body.\",\"related\":[\"on-neck-line\",\"thrusting-line\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"thrusting-line\",\"term\":\"Thrusting line\",\"definition\":\"A bullish candle that opens below a long bearish candle's low and closes into its body, but not past the middle.\",\"plain\":\"An up candle that closes into the previous down candle's body, but below its midpoint.\",\"related\":[\"piercing-line\",\"in-neck-line\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"tweezers\",\"term\":\"Tweezers\",\"definition\":\"Two neighbouring candles with matching lows after a decline (tweezer bottom) or matching highs after a rise (tweezer top).\",\"plain\":\"Two neighbouring candles with matching highs (tweezer top) or matching lows (tweezer bottom).\",\"related\":[\"support\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"kicker\",\"term\":\"Kicker\",\"definition\":\"A marubozu followed by an opposite-coloured marubozu that gaps beyond the first one's open.\",\"plain\":\"Two opposite-coloured candles with no shadows, separated by a gap.\",\"related\":[\"marubozu\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"morning-star\",\"term\":\"Morning star\",\"definition\":\"After a decline: a long bearish candle, a short body gapped below it, then a bullish candle closing at or above the middle of the first body.\",\"plain\":\"At a low: a long down candle, a small gapped candle, then a strong up candle.\",\"related\":[\"evening-star\",\"doji-star\",\"abandoned-baby\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"evening-star\",\"term\":\"Evening star\",\"definition\":\"After a rise: a long bullish candle, a short body gapped above it, then a bearish candle closing at or below the middle of the first body.\",\"plain\":\"At a high: a long up candle, a small gapped candle, then a strong down candle.\",\"related\":[\"morning-star\",\"doji-star\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"doji-star\",\"term\":\"Doji star\",\"definition\":\"A long candle followed by a doji whose body gaps away from it in the direction of the trend.\",\"plain\":\"A long candle followed by a doji that gaps away from it.\",\"related\":[\"doji\",\"morning-star\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"abandoned-baby\",\"term\":\"Abandoned baby\",\"definition\":\"A star pattern whose middle doji gaps beyond the wicks of both neighbours: in the bullish version, its high is below both their lows.\",\"plain\":\"A star pattern whose middle doji gaps beyond the shadows of both neighbouring candles.\",\"related\":[\"morning-star\",\"doji-star\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"tri-star\",\"term\":\"Tri-star\",\"definition\":\"Three doji in a row, the middle one gapped above or below the other two.\",\"plain\":\"Three doji in a row, the middle one gapping above or below the other two.\",\"related\":[\"doji\",\"doji-star\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"window\",\"term\":\"Window\",\"definition\":\"The candlestick name for a gap: a rising window when a candle's low is above the previous high, a falling window when its high is below the previous low.\",\"plain\":\"The candlestick name for a gap: a price range with no trading between two candles.\",\"related\":[\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"three-white-soldiers\",\"term\":\"Three white soldiers\",\"definition\":\"After a decline, three long bullish candles in a row, each opening inside the previous body and closing near its high.\",\"plain\":\"Three long up candles in a row, each opening inside the previous body and closing near its high.\",\"related\":[\"three-black-crows\",\"advance-block\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"three-black-crows\",\"term\":\"Three black crows\",\"definition\":\"After a rise, three long bearish candles in a row, each opening inside the previous body and closing near its low.\",\"plain\":\"Three long down candles in a row, each closing near its low.\",\"related\":[\"three-white-soldiers\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"three-inside-up-down\",\"term\":\"Three inside up / down\",\"definition\":\"A harami followed by a third candle that closes beyond the first candle's open: above it for three inside up, below it for three inside down.\",\"plain\":\"A harami followed by a candle that confirms the turn by closing beyond the pattern.\",\"related\":[\"harami\",\"three-outside-up-down\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\",\"aka\":[\"Three inside up\",\"Three inside down\"]},{\"id\":\"three-outside-up-down\",\"term\":\"Three outside up / down\",\"definition\":\"An engulfing pattern followed by a third candle that closes further in the new direction.\",\"plain\":\"An engulfing pattern followed by a candle that closes further in the new direction.\",\"related\":[\"engulfing-pattern\",\"three-inside-up-down\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\",\"aka\":[\"Three outside up\",\"Three outside down\"]},{\"id\":\"advance-block\",\"term\":\"Advance block\",\"definition\":\"Three rising bullish candles with shrinking bodies and growing upper shadows, read as a rise losing strength. No published statistics.\",\"plain\":\"Three rising candles with shrinking bodies and growing upper shadows: a rise losing strength.\",\"related\":[\"three-white-soldiers\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"three-line-strike\",\"term\":\"Three-line strike\",\"definition\":\"Three candles in one direction, then a fourth that opens beyond the third and closes beyond the first one's open. Taught as continuation; the bearish version broke upward 84% of the time in Bulkowski's tests.\",\"plain\":\"Three candles in one direction, then one long candle that wipes out all three.\",\"related\":[\"engulfing-pattern\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"rising-three-methods\",\"term\":\"Rising three methods\",\"definition\":\"A five-candle pause: a long bullish candle, three small candles inside its range, then a long bullish candle closing at a new high.\",\"plain\":\"A five-candle pause: a long up candle, three small ones inside its range, another long up candle.\",\"related\":[\"consolidation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"tasuki-gap\",\"term\":\"Tasuki gap\",\"definition\":\"In a trend, a gap followed by a candle in the opposite direction that moves into the gap without closing it.\",\"plain\":\"A gap in a trend that the next, opposite candle moves into but does not close.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"window\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"matching-low\",\"term\":\"Matching low\",\"definition\":\"Two bearish candles with the same close during a fall. Taught as support; in Bulkowski's tests price kept falling 61% of the time.\",\"plain\":\"Two down candles that close at the same price during a fall.\",\"related\":[\"tweezers\",\"support\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"heikin-ashi\",\"term\":\"Heikin-Ashi\",\"definition\":\"Candles drawn from averages: close = (open + high + low + close) ÷ 4 of the real candle, open = the average of the previous Heikin-Ashi open and close. The values drawn are not traded prices.\",\"plain\":\"Smoothed candles built from averages of real prices; their values are not prices anyone traded.\",\"related\":[\"candle\",\"renko\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\",\"aka\":[\"HA\"]},{\"id\":\"renko\",\"term\":\"Renko\",\"definition\":\"A chart of equal bricks, each drawn only when price closes a full box size beyond the last brick; time is ignored.\",\"plain\":\"A chart of equal bricks, drawn only when price moves a full brick; time is ignored.\",\"related\":[\"box-size\",\"heikin-ashi\",\"point-and-figure\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"point-and-figure\",\"term\":\"Point & figure\",\"definition\":\"A chart of X columns for rises and O columns for falls, built from a box size and a reversal amount, with no time axis.\",\"plain\":\"A chart of X columns for rises and O columns for falls, built from a box size and reversal.\",\"related\":[\"box-size\",\"reversal-amount\",\"renko\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\",\"aka\":[\"P&F\"]},{\"id\":\"box-size\",\"term\":\"Box size\",\"definition\":\"The price step that one Renko brick or one point & figure box stands for.\",\"plain\":\"The price step that one brick or box stands for on a Renko or point & figure chart.\",\"related\":[\"renko\",\"point-and-figure\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"reversal-amount\",\"term\":\"Reversal amount\",\"definition\":\"The number of boxes price must move against the current point & figure column before a new column starts.\",\"plain\":\"How many boxes price must move back before a point & figure chart starts a new column.\",\"related\":[\"point-and-figure\",\"box-size\"],\"lesson\":\"2.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"trendline\",\"term\":\"Trendline\",\"definition\":\"A straight line through rising swing lows (an uptrend line) or through falling swing highs (a downtrend line). Two points draw it; a third touch confirms it.\",\"plain\":\"A sloping line under the lows of a rise, or over the highs of a fall.\",\"related\":[\"swing-low\",\"swing-high\",\"channel\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"Trend line\"]},{\"id\":\"channel\",\"term\":\"Channel\",\"definition\":\"A trendline plus a parallel line through the most extreme swing on the opposite side, with price moving between the two.\",\"plain\":\"Two parallel sloping lines that contain price, like the banks of a river.\",\"related\":[\"trendline\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"throwback\",\"term\":\"Throwback\",\"definition\":\"A return to a broken boundary soon after an upward breakout. Bulkowski calls the same return after a downward breakout a pullback, which is a different sense from a pullback within a trend.\",\"plain\":\"Price comes back to test the level it just broke through.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"pullback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"Retest\"]},{\"id\":\"breakout-filter\",\"term\":\"Breakout filter\",\"definition\":\"A rule a breakout must pass before it counts, such as a close beyond the level by part of the ATR, by a percentage, two closes in a row, or a gap through it.\",\"plain\":\"An extra test that weeds out some false breakouts, at the price of a later entry.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"false-breakout\",\"atr\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"Confirmation filter\"]},{\"id\":\"pattern-height\",\"term\":\"Pattern height\",\"definition\":\"The distance from a pattern's extreme to its breakout line, such as from a head to the neckline. Projected from the breakout, it gives the pattern's target.\",\"plain\":\"How tall the pattern is; it's used to estimate how far price might travel after the breakout.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"neckline\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"double-top\",\"term\":\"Double top\",\"definition\":\"Two peaks near the same price with a valley between them. It is confirmed only when price closes below that valley.\",\"plain\":\"Two failed pushes at the same high, which count as a pattern only once the low between them breaks.\",\"related\":[\"double-bottom\",\"swing-high\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"double-bottom\",\"term\":\"Double bottom\",\"definition\":\"Two lows near the same price with a peak between them. It is confirmed only when price closes above that peak.\",\"plain\":\"Two bounces from the same low, which count as a pattern only once the high between them breaks.\",\"related\":[\"double-top\",\"swing-low\",\"support\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"head-and-shoulders\",\"term\":\"Head and shoulders\",\"definition\":\"Three peaks after a rise, the middle one (the head) highest, with a neckline through the lows between them. It is confirmed by a close beyond the neckline; the inverse pattern forms at bottoms.\",\"plain\":\"Three peaks where the middle one stands tallest, like a head between two shoulders.\",\"related\":[\"neckline\",\"double-top\",\"pattern-height\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.4\",\"aka\":[\"H&S\"]},{\"id\":\"neckline\",\"term\":\"Neckline\",\"definition\":\"The line through the two lows between a head-and-shoulders top's peaks, or through the two highs in the inverse pattern. A close beyond it confirms the pattern.\",\"plain\":\"The line under the shoulders and the head that price has to break for the pattern to count.\",\"related\":[\"head-and-shoulders\",\"pattern-height\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"triple-top\",\"term\":\"Triple top\",\"definition\":\"Three peaks near one price, confirmed only by a close below the lowest valley between them. The triple bottom is its mirror.\",\"plain\":\"Three failed pushes at the same high. Until the floor breaks, it's just a range.\",\"related\":[\"double-top\",\"rectangle\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"Triple bottom\"]},{\"id\":\"rectangle\",\"term\":\"Rectangle\",\"definition\":\"A sideways range between nearly flat support and resistance, with at least three touches on one boundary and two on the other.\",\"plain\":\"A box that price bounces around in until it breaks out of one side.\",\"related\":[\"range\",\"support\",\"resistance\",\"triple-top\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"partial-rise\",\"term\":\"Partial rise\",\"definition\":\"A swing inside a range that turns back before reaching the top. A partial decline is the mirror: it turns back before reaching the bottom.\",\"plain\":\"A move across the box that gives up halfway, a hint about which side may break.\",\"related\":[\"rectangle\",\"range\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"Partial decline\"]},{\"id\":\"ascending-triangle\",\"term\":\"Ascending triangle\",\"definition\":\"A triangle with a flat top and rising lows, with at least three touches on one line and two on the other.\",\"plain\":\"Buyers keep stepping in higher under a ceiling that stays put.\",\"related\":[\"descending-triangle\",\"symmetrical-triangle\",\"apex\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"descending-triangle\",\"term\":\"Descending triangle\",\"definition\":\"A triangle with a flat bottom and falling highs. Despite its bearish reputation, about half broke out upward in Bulkowski's data.\",\"plain\":\"Lower highs pressing down on a floor that stays put.\",\"related\":[\"ascending-triangle\",\"symmetrical-triangle\",\"apex\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"symmetrical-triangle\",\"term\":\"Symmetrical triangle\",\"definition\":\"A triangle whose upper line falls and lower line rises, converging toward the apex.\",\"plain\":\"The swings get smaller from both sides until price has to pick a direction.\",\"related\":[\"ascending-triangle\",\"descending-triangle\",\"apex\",\"pennant\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"apex\",\"term\":\"Apex\",\"definition\":\"The point where a triangle's two boundary lines meet when extended.\",\"plain\":\"The tip of the triangle, where the two lines cross.\",\"related\":[\"symmetrical-triangle\",\"ascending-triangle\",\"descending-triangle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"flag\",\"term\":\"Flag\",\"definition\":\"A brief pause with parallel lines that slopes against a steep prior move, the flagpole. On LABX 1H the app counts 5 to 20 candles.\",\"plain\":\"A short, small channel after a sharp move, tilted against it.\",\"related\":[\"flagpole\",\"pennant\",\"channel\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\"},{\"id\":\"pennant\",\"term\":\"Pennant\",\"definition\":\"A brief pause with converging lines after a steep move, like a small symmetrical triangle.\",\"plain\":\"A short pause that narrows to a point after a sharp move.\",\"related\":[\"flag\",\"flagpole\",\"symmetrical-triangle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\"},{\"id\":\"flagpole\",\"term\":\"Flagpole\",\"definition\":\"The steep move that comes before a flag or a pennant. The half-mast rule projects its length from the breakout.\",\"plain\":\"The sharp run that the flag hangs from.\",\"related\":[\"flag\",\"pennant\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\",\"aka\":[\"Pole\",\"Mast\"]},{\"id\":\"measured-move\",\"term\":\"Measured move\",\"definition\":\"A projection that a second leg, starting after a correction, will roughly equal the first leg. In Bulkowski's data the second leg was usually shorter.\",\"plain\":\"The idea that the next push will be about as long as the last one; usually it's a bit shorter.\",\"related\":[\"swing-high\",\"swing-low\",\"flagpole\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\",\"aka\":[\"ABCD\"]},{\"id\":\"high-tight-flag\",\"term\":\"High tight flag\",\"definition\":\"A flag after price nearly doubled within about two months. A stock-chart pattern with no equivalent on LABX 1H.\",\"plain\":\"A rare stock pattern: a huge run, then a short pause.\",\"related\":[\"flag\",\"flagpole\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.7\"},{\"id\":\"wedge\",\"term\":\"Wedge\",\"definition\":\"A pattern with two lines sloping the same way and converging: rising, with the lower line steeper, or falling, with the upper line steeper.\",\"plain\":\"A channel that narrows as it climbs or falls.\",\"related\":[\"channel\",\"trendline\",\"broadening-formation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.8\",\"aka\":[\"Rising wedge\",\"Falling wedge\"]},{\"id\":\"broadening-formation\",\"term\":\"Broadening formation\",\"definition\":\"A pattern with diverging lines: higher highs and lower lows, so the swings keep growing.\",\"plain\":\"Swings that get wider each time, like the mouth of a megaphone.\",\"related\":[\"wedge\",\"diamond-pattern\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.8\",\"aka\":[\"Megaphone\"]},{\"id\":\"diamond-pattern\",\"term\":\"Diamond\",\"definition\":\"A pattern that first widens like a broadening formation, then narrows like a symmetrical triangle.\",\"plain\":\"Swings that grow and then shrink, drawing a diamond shape.\",\"related\":[\"broadening-formation\",\"symmetrical-triangle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.8\"},{\"id\":\"rounding-bottom\",\"term\":\"Rounding bottom\",\"definition\":\"A slow, bowl-shaped turn from falling to rising prices, confirmed by a close above the left rim. The rounding top is its mirror.\",\"plain\":\"A gentle U-turn at the bottom that you can only name once it's nearly done.\",\"related\":[\"cup-with-handle\",\"v-reversal\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.9\",\"aka\":[\"Saucer\"]},{\"id\":\"cup-with-handle\",\"term\":\"Cup with handle\",\"definition\":\"A U-shaped base with rims at similar prices, a short pullback (the handle) in its upper half, then a breakout above the rim.\",\"plain\":\"A bowl, a small dip near the top edge, then a push through the edge.\",\"related\":[\"rounding-bottom\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.9\"},{\"id\":\"v-reversal\",\"term\":\"V reversal\",\"definition\":\"A sharp turn with no pause: a steep fall followed at once by a steep rise, or the reverse. Its turning point can't be told from a pause in real time.\",\"plain\":\"A sudden about-turn that is obvious afterwards and invisible while it happens.\",\"related\":[\"rounding-bottom\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.9\",\"aka\":[\"V-bottom\",\"V-top\"]},{\"id\":\"busted-pattern\",\"term\":\"Busted pattern\",\"definition\":\"A pattern that breaks out, fails to travel (less than 1 × ATR on LABX 1H), then closes beyond its opposite boundary.\",\"plain\":\"A breakout that turns around and breaks out the other side instead.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.2.10\",\"aka\":[\"Bust\"]},{\"id\":\"dow-theory\",\"term\":\"Dow Theory\",\"definition\":\"The founding trend framework: markets move in three degrees of trend at once, and a trend is assumed to continue until it is clearly reversed.\",\"plain\":\"The oldest rulebook for trends: big trends contain smaller moves, and a trend stands until a clear signal ends it.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"primary-trend\",\"peak-trough-rule\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"primary-trend\",\"term\":\"Primary trend\",\"definition\":\"The main, longest-lasting direction of the market, inside which shorter secondary and minor moves happen.\",\"plain\":\"The big direction you'd see if you zoomed all the way out.\",\"related\":[\"dow-theory\",\"trend\",\"timeframe\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"peak-trough-rule\",\"term\":\"Peak-trough rule\",\"definition\":\"An uptrend ends when a low breaks the previous swing low; a downtrend ends when a higher low forms and the next rally rises above the previous swing high.\",\"plain\":\"A trend is over when price breaks the last swing against it, not just because it made a weaker high or low.\",\"related\":[\"dow-theory\",\"trend\",\"pullback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"wma\",\"term\":\"Weighted moving average\",\"definition\":\"A moving average that gives linearly more weight to more recent closes: weights 1, 2 … n, with the newest close weighted n.\",\"plain\":\"An average in which the latest prices count the most, step by step.\",\"related\":[\"moving-average\",\"sma\",\"ema\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\",\"aka\":[\"WMA\"]},{\"id\":\"lag\",\"term\":\"Lag\",\"definition\":\"How far an indicator trails price, because it is calculated only from past data.\",\"plain\":\"The delay between price turning and the indicator turning.\",\"related\":[\"moving-average\",\"whipsaw\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"whipsaw\",\"term\":\"Whipsaw\",\"definition\":\"A signal quickly reversed by the opposite signal, usually costing a small loss plus costs.\",\"plain\":\"Getting in, then straight back out the other way, and paying both times.\",\"related\":[\"lag\",\"crossover\",\"range\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"wilder-smoothing\",\"term\":\"Wilder smoothing\",\"definition\":\"An average where each new value is the previous one times (n − 1) ÷ n, plus the new value divided by n: an EMA with k = 1 ÷ n.\",\"plain\":\"A slow average used inside ATR and ADX.\",\"related\":[\"ema\",\"atr\",\"adx\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.3\",\"aka\":[\"Wilder's moving average\"]},{\"id\":\"crossover\",\"term\":\"Crossover\",\"definition\":\"The moment one line crosses another, such as price crossing a moving average or a fast average crossing a slow one: a change of sign of their difference.\",\"plain\":\"Two lines swap places. Many indicator signals are just this.\",\"related\":[\"moving-average\",\"golden-cross\",\"death-cross\",\"whipsaw\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.4\"},{\"id\":\"golden-cross\",\"term\":\"Golden cross\",\"definition\":\"The 50-period moving average crossing above the 200-period moving average.\",\"plain\":\"A slow, widely watched sign that a rise has been under way for a while.\",\"related\":[\"death-cross\",\"crossover\",\"moving-average\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.4\"},{\"id\":\"death-cross\",\"term\":\"Death cross\",\"definition\":\"The 50-period moving average crossing below the 200-period moving average.\",\"plain\":\"The golden cross in reverse: a late sign that a decline has been going on.\",\"related\":[\"golden-cross\",\"crossover\",\"moving-average\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.4\"},{\"id\":\"macd\",\"term\":\"MACD\",\"definition\":\"The 12-period EMA minus the 26-period EMA: a momentum line built from two averages, usually shown with a signal line and a histogram.\",\"plain\":\"The gap between a fast and a slow average, drawn as its own line.\",\"related\":[\"ema\",\"signal-line\",\"macd-histogram\",\"ppo\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"Moving average convergence divergence\"]},{\"id\":\"signal-line\",\"term\":\"Signal line\",\"definition\":\"A moving average of an indicator, such as the 9-period EMA of MACD.\",\"plain\":\"A smoothed copy of an indicator; crossings with it are read as signals.\",\"related\":[\"macd\",\"macd-histogram\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"macd-histogram\",\"term\":\"MACD histogram\",\"definition\":\"MACD minus its signal line, usually shown as bars around zero.\",\"plain\":\"How far MACD is from its own average: growing bars mean MACD is speeding up.\",\"related\":[\"macd\",\"signal-line\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"ppo\",\"term\":\"PPO\",\"definition\":\"MACD divided by the slower EMA, times 100, so instruments at different prices can be compared.\",\"plain\":\"MACD as a percentage instead of in price units.\",\"related\":[\"macd\",\"ema\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"Percentage price oscillator\"]},{\"id\":\"adx\",\"term\":\"ADX\",\"definition\":\"Wilder's Average Directional Index: a smoothed measure of how strong a trend is, whatever its direction.\",\"plain\":\"A trend-strength gauge that says nothing about up or down.\",\"related\":[\"directional-indicator\",\"trend\",\"wilder-smoothing\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.6\",\"aka\":[\"Average directional index\"]},{\"id\":\"directional-indicator\",\"term\":\"Directional indicator\",\"definition\":\"Wilder's +DI and −DI: smoothed upward and downward movement as a share of true range. The higher one shows the direction.\",\"plain\":\"Two lines, one for up moves and one for down moves; the higher one shows which side is winning.\",\"related\":[\"adx\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.6\",\"aka\":[\"+DI / −DI\",\"DMI\"]},{\"id\":\"parabolic-sar\",\"term\":\"Parabolic SAR\",\"definition\":\"Wilder's stop-and-reverse line, which trails price, speeds up as a trend extends and flips sides when price crosses it.\",\"plain\":\"A trailing line that chases price faster the longer a trend runs.\",\"related\":[\"acceleration-factor\",\"trailing-stop\",\"supertrend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.7\",\"aka\":[\"PSAR\"]},{\"id\":\"acceleration-factor\",\"term\":\"Acceleration factor\",\"definition\":\"The Parabolic SAR step: it starts at 0.02, rises 0.02 at each new extreme and is capped at 0.20.\",\"plain\":\"How fast the SAR closes in on price.\",\"related\":[\"parabolic-sar\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"supertrend\",\"term\":\"SuperTrend\",\"definition\":\"A trailing line set a multiple of ATR away from the candle midpoint, which only ratchets in the trend's direction and flips when a close crosses it.\",\"plain\":\"A volatility-based trailing line that flips between below and above price.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"trailing-stop\",\"parabolic-sar\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"ichimoku-cloud\",\"term\":\"Ichimoku cloud\",\"definition\":\"The shaded area between Ichimoku's two leading lines, Senkou A and Senkou B, drawn 26 periods ahead.\",\"plain\":\"A band drawn ahead of price from older highs and lows.\",\"related\":[\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.8\",\"aka\":[\"Kumo\"]},{\"id\":\"look-ahead-bias\",\"term\":\"Look-ahead bias\",\"definition\":\"Using information in a test that wasn't available when the decision would have been made.\",\"plain\":\"A backtest that peeks at the future, so it looks better than reality.\",\"related\":[\"backtest\",\"repainting\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.8\"},{\"id\":\"repainting\",\"term\":\"Repainting\",\"definition\":\"When an indicator changes its past values as new data arrive, making past signals look better than they were in real time.\",\"plain\":\"An indicator that redraws its own history after the fact.\",\"related\":[\"look-ahead-bias\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"2.3.8\"},{\"id\":\"oscillator\",\"term\":\"Oscillator\",\"definition\":\"An indicator that swings between fixed bounds, such as 0 and 100, or around a centre line, such as 0. Most are built from recent price changes.\",\"plain\":\"An indicator that swings up and down on a scale, showing how strong recent moves were.\",\"related\":[\"momentum\",\"rate-of-change\",\"rsi\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"momentum\",\"term\":\"Momentum\",\"definition\":\"Today's close minus the close n periods ago, in price units. More broadly, the tendency of assets that rose over some horizon to keep rising.\",\"plain\":\"How far price has moved over a set number of candles.\",\"related\":[\"rate-of-change\",\"oscillator\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"rate-of-change\",\"term\":\"Rate of change (ROC)\",\"definition\":\"The percent change over n periods: (today's close ÷ the close n periods ago − 1) × 100.\",\"plain\":\"Momentum in percent, so moves at different prices can be compared.\",\"related\":[\"momentum\",\"oscillator\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.1\",\"aka\":[\"ROC\"]},{\"id\":\"rsi\",\"term\":\"RSI\",\"definition\":\"Relative Strength Index: 100 − 100 ÷ (1 + RS), where RS is the average gain ÷ the average loss over n periods, smoothed with Wilder's method. It runs from 0 to 100.\",\"plain\":\"A 0–100 score comparing recent gains with recent losses.\",\"related\":[\"oscillator\",\"overbought\",\"oversold\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.2\",\"aka\":[\"Relative Strength Index\"]},{\"id\":\"overbought\",\"term\":\"Overbought\",\"definition\":\"An oscillator reading in its upper zone, such as RSI above 70, after a strong rise. It describes the recent move; it doesn't mean price must fall.\",\"plain\":\"An indicator reading after a strong rise. It does not mean price must fall.\",\"related\":[\"oversold\",\"rsi\",\"fade\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"oversold\",\"term\":\"Oversold\",\"definition\":\"An oscillator reading in its lower zone, such as RSI below 30, after a strong fall. It describes the recent move; it doesn't mean price must rise.\",\"plain\":\"An indicator reading after a strong fall. It does not mean price must rise.\",\"related\":[\"overbought\",\"rsi\",\"fade\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"stochastic-oscillator\",\"term\":\"Stochastic oscillator\",\"definition\":\"%K = (close − lowest low) ÷ (highest high − lowest low) × 100 over n periods, usually 14; %D is a 3-period average of %K.\",\"plain\":\"Where the close sits within the recent high-low range, from 0 to 100.\",\"related\":[\"williams-r\",\"oscillator\",\"rsi\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\",\"aka\":[\"Stochastic\",\"%K\"]},{\"id\":\"williams-r\",\"term\":\"Williams %R\",\"definition\":\"(highest high − close) ÷ (highest high − lowest low) × −100 over n periods. It always equals stochastic %K minus 100.\",\"plain\":\"The stochastic %K shifted to run from 0 to −100.\",\"related\":[\"stochastic-oscillator\",\"oscillator\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\",\"aka\":[\"%R\"]},{\"id\":\"cci\",\"term\":\"CCI\",\"definition\":\"Commodity Channel Index: (typical price − its n-period average) ÷ (0.015 × mean deviation). It is unbounded; ±100 are the usual reference lines.\",\"plain\":\"How far typical price is from its average, in units of its usual distance.\",\"related\":[\"typical-price\",\"oscillator\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\",\"aka\":[\"Commodity Channel Index\"]},{\"id\":\"collinearity\",\"term\":\"Collinearity\",\"definition\":\"Indicators moving together because they are built from the same inputs, so their agreement adds little independent information.\",\"plain\":\"Indicators moving together because they use the same prices, so their agreement confirms nothing new.\",\"related\":[\"oscillator\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"fade\",\"term\":\"Fade\",\"definition\":\"A trade against the latest move, expecting it to reverse: selling after a rise or buying after a fall.\",\"plain\":\"Trading against the latest move, expecting it to reverse.\",\"related\":[\"overbought\",\"reversal\",\"range\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.4\"},{\"id\":\"divergence\",\"term\":\"Divergence\",\"definition\":\"Price and an oscillator moving in different directions at matching swing points, such as a higher price high with a lower RSI high.\",\"plain\":\"Price and an indicator disagreeing at the same swing points.\",\"related\":[\"hidden-divergence\",\"rsi\",\"swing-high\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"hidden-divergence\",\"term\":\"Hidden divergence\",\"definition\":\"In an uptrend, a higher price low with a lower oscillator low; in a downtrend, a lower price high with a higher oscillator high. It is read as continuation.\",\"plain\":\"A divergence read as a sign that the trend will continue, not reverse.\",\"related\":[\"divergence\",\"pullback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"failure-swing\",\"term\":\"Failure swing\",\"definition\":\"Wilder's RSI-only pattern: RSI goes below 30 (or above 70), comes back, fails to retest that extreme on the pullback, then breaks its previous swing. Price is ignored.\",\"plain\":\"An RSI pattern where RSI fails to reach its extreme again, then breaks out.\",\"related\":[\"rsi\",\"divergence\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"true-range\",\"term\":\"True range\",\"definition\":\"The largest of: high − low, |high − previous close| and |low − previous close|. Unlike high − low, it counts gaps between candles.\",\"plain\":\"A candle's full movement, including any gap from the previous close.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"gap\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\"},{\"id\":\"atr-percent\",\"term\":\"ATR %\",\"definition\":\"ATR divided by price, times 100. It compares volatility across instruments, or across time when prices differ.\",\"plain\":\"ATR as a share of price, so different instruments can be compared.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\",\"aka\":[\"ATR%\",\"normalised ATR\"]},{\"id\":\"standard-deviation\",\"term\":\"Standard deviation\",\"definition\":\"The square root of the average squared distance of values from their mean. Bollinger Bands use it on closes, historical volatility on returns.\",\"plain\":\"A measure of how spread out values are around their average.\",\"related\":[\"historical-volatility\",\"volatility\",\"bollinger-bands\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\",\"aka\":[\"σ\",\"sigma\"]},{\"id\":\"historical-volatility\",\"term\":\"Historical volatility\",\"definition\":\"The standard deviation of past log returns, usually annualised: daily σ × √252.\",\"plain\":\"How much prices have been moving, expressed as a yearly percentage.\",\"related\":[\"standard-deviation\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\",\"aka\":[\"HV\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-clustering\",\"term\":\"Volatility clustering\",\"definition\":\"The tendency of large price moves to follow large ones and small moves to follow small ones, so recent volatility helps forecast near-term volatility.\",\"plain\":\"Busy periods tend to follow busy ones, and quiet periods tend to follow quiet ones.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"atr\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.6\"},{\"id\":\"bollinger-bands\",\"term\":\"Bollinger Bands\",\"definition\":\"A 20-period simple moving average with bands two standard deviations of the closes above and below it.\",\"plain\":\"Lines above and below an average that widen when prices swing more.\",\"related\":[\"standard-deviation\",\"sma\",\"percent-b\",\"bandwidth\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"percent-b\",\"term\":\"%b\",\"definition\":\"(price − lower band) ÷ (upper band − lower band): 0 at the lower Bollinger Band, 1 at the upper, beyond 0 or 1 outside them.\",\"plain\":\"Where price sits between the Bollinger Bands: 0 at the lower, 1 at the upper.\",\"related\":[\"bollinger-bands\",\"bandwidth\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\",\"aka\":[\"percent b\"]},{\"id\":\"bandwidth\",\"term\":\"BandWidth\",\"definition\":\"(upper band − lower band) ÷ middle band. At the default settings it equals 4σ ÷ SMA, a measure of volatility.\",\"plain\":\"How wide the Bollinger Bands are, relative to price.\",\"related\":[\"bollinger-bands\",\"squeeze\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"squeeze\",\"term\":\"Squeeze\",\"definition\":\"Unusually narrow bands, such as BandWidth at its lowest in 120 periods: low volatility that tends to come before a bigger move in either direction.\",\"plain\":\"Very narrow bands: a quiet market that often gets busier, in either direction.\",\"related\":[\"bandwidth\",\"bollinger-bands\",\"keltner-channel\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"keltner-channel\",\"term\":\"Keltner Channel\",\"definition\":\"An exponential moving average, usually 20 periods, with bands a multiple of ATR above and below it, usually 2 × ATR(10).\",\"plain\":\"An average with bands set by the average true range.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"ema\",\"bollinger-bands\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.9\"},{\"id\":\"donchian-channel\",\"term\":\"Donchian channel\",\"definition\":\"The highest high and the lowest low of the previous n periods, with their midpoint. A break above the upper line is the classic channel breakout.\",\"plain\":\"The highest high and the lowest low of the previous n candles.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.4.9\"},{\"id\":\"relative-volume\",\"term\":\"Relative volume\",\"definition\":\"A candle's volume divided by the average volume of the candles before it, usually 20. A value of 2.0 means twice the usual activity.\",\"plain\":\"How busy this candle was compared with normal; 2.0 means twice the usual volume.\",\"related\":[\"volume\",\"tick-volume\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"RVOL\"]},{\"id\":\"obv\",\"term\":\"On-balance volume (OBV)\",\"definition\":\"A running total that adds a candle's volume when it closes above the previous close and subtracts it when it closes below.\",\"plain\":\"Volume added up on up closes and taken away on down closes. Only its direction means anything.\",\"related\":[\"volume\",\"accumulation-distribution-line\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"OBV\"]},{\"id\":\"accumulation-distribution-line\",\"term\":\"Accumulation/distribution line\",\"definition\":\"A running total of volume weighted by where each candle closed within its range: ((C − L) − (H − C)) ÷ (H − L) × volume. It ignores gaps between candles.\",\"plain\":\"Volume added up according to whether candles closed near their highs or near their lows.\",\"related\":[\"obv\",\"chaikin-money-flow\",\"volume\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"A/D line\"]},{\"id\":\"chaikin-money-flow\",\"term\":\"Chaikin Money Flow\",\"definition\":\"The money flow volume of the last 20 candles divided by their total volume; it usually stays between −0.5 and +0.5.\",\"plain\":\"The accumulation/distribution idea averaged over a window, usually 20 candles.\",\"related\":[\"accumulation-distribution-line\",\"money-flow-index\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"CMF\"]},{\"id\":\"money-flow-index\",\"term\":\"Money Flow Index\",\"definition\":\"An oscillator from 0 to 100 that applies the RSI formula to typical price times volume, split into rising and falling candles.\",\"plain\":\"A volume-weighted version of RSI, from 0 to 100.\",\"related\":[\"chaikin-money-flow\",\"typical-price\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"MFI\"]},{\"id\":\"vwap\",\"term\":\"VWAP\",\"definition\":\"Volume-weighted average price: the sum of typical price × volume divided by the sum of volume, counted from the session open.\",\"plain\":\"The average price everyone paid in today's session, with busy candles counting more. Traders use it to grade their fills.\",\"related\":[\"typical-price\",\"anchored-vwap\",\"average-fill-price\",\"benchmark\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"Volume-weighted average price\"]},{\"id\":\"anchored-vwap\",\"term\":\"Anchored VWAP\",\"definition\":\"A VWAP whose sums start at a candle the trader chooses, such as a swing low, a gap or a news event, instead of the session open.\",\"plain\":\"The average price paid since a moment you pick.\",\"related\":[\"vwap\",\"swing-low\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"AVWAP\"]},{\"id\":\"typical-price\",\"term\":\"Typical price\",\"definition\":\"The average of a candle's high, low and close: (H + L + C) ÷ 3.\",\"plain\":\"One price that stands for the whole candle, used by VWAP, pivots and money flow.\",\"related\":[\"vwap\",\"high\",\"low\",\"close\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"TP\"]},{\"id\":\"volume-profile\",\"term\":\"Volume profile\",\"definition\":\"A sideways histogram of the volume traded at each price over a chosen window, with its point of control and value area.\",\"plain\":\"A bar for every price showing how much traded there, whatever the time.\",\"related\":[\"point-of-control\",\"value-area\",\"high-volume-node\",\"low-volume-node\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"point-of-control\",\"term\":\"Point of control\",\"definition\":\"The price with the most traded volume in a volume profile.\",\"plain\":\"The busiest price in the window.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"value-area\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\",\"aka\":[\"POC\"]},{\"id\":\"value-area\",\"term\":\"Value area\",\"definition\":\"The range of prices around the point of control that holds about 70% of a profile's volume, built by adding the larger neighbouring level each step.\",\"plain\":\"Where most of the trading happened: roughly 70% of the volume.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"point-of-control\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"high-volume-node\",\"term\":\"High-volume node\",\"definition\":\"A peak in a volume profile: a price where much more traded than at the prices around it.\",\"plain\":\"A price where a lot of trading happened, seen as a peak in the profile.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"low-volume-node\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\",\"aka\":[\"HVN\"]},{\"id\":\"low-volume-node\",\"term\":\"Low-volume node\",\"definition\":\"A dip in a volume profile: a price where much less traded than at the prices around it.\",\"plain\":\"A price where little trading happened, seen as a dip in the profile.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"high-volume-node\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.4\",\"aka\":[\"LVN\"]},{\"id\":\"pivot-point\",\"term\":\"Pivot point\",\"definition\":\"A level calculated from the previous session's high, low and close, such as the classic P = (H + L + C) ÷ 3 and the R and S levels around it.\",\"plain\":\"Lines worked out before the session opens, from yesterday's high, low and close.\",\"related\":[\"typical-price\",\"support\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.5\",\"aka\":[\"Pivot\"]},{\"id\":\"common-gap\",\"term\":\"Common gap\",\"definition\":\"A gap inside a range or congestion area, on ordinary volume, usually filled soon.\",\"plain\":\"A small gap in a sideways market that usually closes quickly.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"gap-fill\",\"breakaway-gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"breakaway-gap\",\"term\":\"Breakaway gap\",\"definition\":\"A gap out of a range or pattern, usually on heavy volume, that can start a new move and often stays unfilled.\",\"plain\":\"A gap that jumps out of a range and can start a new trend.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"breakout\",\"common-gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"runaway-gap\",\"term\":\"Runaway gap\",\"definition\":\"A gap in the middle of an established trend, with no congestion before it. The claim that it marks the halfway point of the move is untested.\",\"plain\":\"A gap in the middle of a strong trend, also called a measuring gap.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"exhaustion-gap\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\",\"aka\":[\"Measuring gap\"]},{\"id\":\"exhaustion-gap\",\"term\":\"Exhaustion gap\",\"definition\":\"A gap late in a trend, often on very heavy volume, that is filled soon after. It can be identified with confidence only once the fill has happened.\",\"plain\":\"A late-trend gap that is quickly filled; you can be sure of it only afterwards.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"runaway-gap\",\"gap-fill\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"island-reversal\",\"term\":\"Island reversal\",\"definition\":\"A group of candles cut off by a gap on each side, with both gaps at overlapping prices, marking a sharp turn.\",\"plain\":\"A cluster of candles left stranded by one gap in and one gap out.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"reversal\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"gap-fill\",\"term\":\"Gap fill\",\"definition\":\"Price returning to the last pre-gap extreme: the previous high for a gap up, the previous low for a gap down.\",\"plain\":\"When price comes back and covers the whole empty space of a gap.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"partial-gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"partial-gap\",\"term\":\"Partial gap\",\"definition\":\"An open beyond the previous close while the two candles' ranges still overlap, so no price range is left untraded.\",\"plain\":\"The open jumps past yesterday's close, but the candles still overlap.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"gap-fill\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"supply-zone\",\"term\":\"Supply zone\",\"definition\":\"A zone drawn at the base of a strong drop, between the base's lowest body edge (proximal) and its highest wick (distal).\",\"plain\":\"A zone at the start of a sharp fall, where some traders expect selling again.\",\"related\":[\"demand-zone\",\"fresh-zone\",\"resistance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.7\"},{\"id\":\"demand-zone\",\"term\":\"Demand zone\",\"definition\":\"A zone drawn at the base of a strong rally, between the base's highest body edge (proximal) and its lowest wick (distal).\",\"plain\":\"A zone at the start of a sharp rise, where some traders expect buying again.\",\"related\":[\"supply-zone\",\"fresh-zone\",\"support\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.7\"},{\"id\":\"fresh-zone\",\"term\":\"Fresh zone\",\"definition\":\"A supply or demand zone that price has not returned to since it formed.\",\"plain\":\"A zone price hasn't touched again yet.\",\"related\":[\"supply-zone\",\"demand-zone\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.7\"},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-retracement\",\"term\":\"Fibonacci retracement\",\"definition\":\"Levels at 23.6%, 38.2%, 50%, 61.8% and 78.6% of a swing, measured back from its end, where some traders expect pullbacks to stall.\",\"plain\":\"Lines at fixed fractions of a move, drawn from its swing low to its swing high.\",\"related\":[\"retracement\",\"pullback\",\"fibonacci-extension\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.8\"},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-extension\",\"term\":\"Fibonacci extension\",\"definition\":\"Levels beyond a swing at ratios such as 127.2%, 161.8% and 261.8% of it, used as targets.\",\"plain\":\"Lines past the end of a move, used as targets.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-retracement\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.8\"},{\"id\":\"confluence\",\"term\":\"Confluence\",\"definition\":\"Several independent reasons pointing to the same price or direction. It adds information only when each factor predicts on its own and the factors don't share inputs.\",\"plain\":\"Several separate reasons agreeing at one price; easy to fake with lines that say the same thing.\",\"related\":[\"confirmation-bias\",\"fibonacci-retracement\",\"pivot-point\"],\"lesson\":\"2.5.8\"},{\"id\":\"trend-bar\",\"term\":\"Trend bar\",\"definition\":\"A bar with a large body that closes near its extreme. In the app: a body of at least half the range, with the close in the third of the range nearest the extreme.\",\"plain\":\"A bar where one side clearly won.\",\"related\":[\"doji-bar\",\"bar-overlap\",\"body\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"doji-bar\",\"term\":\"Doji bar\",\"definition\":\"In price action, a bar with a small body compared with its range. In the app: a body of at most a quarter of the range.\",\"plain\":\"A bar where neither buyers nor sellers won.\",\"related\":[\"trend-bar\",\"bar-overlap\",\"body\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"bar-overlap\",\"term\":\"Bar overlap\",\"definition\":\"How much of a bar's range is shared with the previous bar's range: the shared part divided by the smaller bar's range, 0 if they don't touch.\",\"plain\":\"How much two neighbouring bars cover the same prices.\",\"related\":[\"trend-bar\",\"doji-bar\",\"range\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"signal-bar\",\"term\":\"Signal bar\",\"definition\":\"The bar whose high or low sets the price of a stop entry, usually one tick beyond it.\",\"plain\":\"The bar that tells you where to place your entry order.\",\"related\":[\"entry-bar\",\"stop-order\",\"invalidation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"entry-bar\",\"term\":\"Entry bar\",\"definition\":\"The bar during which your entry order fills.\",\"plain\":\"The bar in which you get into the trade.\",\"related\":[\"signal-bar\",\"entry\",\"fill\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"traders-equation\",\"term\":\"Trader's equation\",\"definition\":\"Win chance times reward must beat loss chance times risk, after costs: P(win) × reward − P(loss) × risk − costs > 0.\",\"plain\":\"A quick check that a setup can pay for its losers and its costs.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"win-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"inside-bar\",\"term\":\"Inside bar\",\"definition\":\"A bar whose high and low stay within the previous bar's range; equal highs or lows still count. Two in a row are called ii, three iii.\",\"plain\":\"A bar that fits completely inside the bar before it.\",\"related\":[\"outside-bar\",\"nr7\",\"hikkake\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\",\"aka\":[\"ii\",\"iii\"]},{\"id\":\"outside-bar\",\"term\":\"Outside bar\",\"definition\":\"A bar whose high is above the previous bar's high and whose low is below the previous bar's low.\",\"plain\":\"A bar that is bigger than the bar before it at both ends.\",\"related\":[\"inside-bar\",\"nr7\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"nr7\",\"term\":\"NR7\",\"definition\":\"The bar with the narrowest range of the last seven bars. NR4 is the same idea over four bars.\",\"plain\":\"The quietest bar of the last seven.\",\"related\":[\"inside-bar\",\"volatility\",\"candle-range\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\",\"aka\":[\"narrow range 7\"]},{\"id\":\"hikkake\",\"term\":\"Hikkake\",\"definition\":\"An inside bar, a false break out of one side, then a close beyond the other side; within three bars, by the app's convention.\",\"plain\":\"A fake breakout of a quiet bar that turns into a move the other way.\",\"related\":[\"inside-bar\",\"failed-breakout\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"high-1-high-2\",\"term\":\"H1 / H2\",\"definition\":\"In a bull pullback, the first and second bars whose high goes above the previous bar's high. L1 and L2 are the bear-trend mirror, using lows.\",\"plain\":\"The first and second attempts to restart an uptrend after a dip.\",\"related\":[\"two-legged-pullback\",\"second-entry\",\"pullback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.4\",\"aka\":[\"H1\",\"H2\",\"L1\",\"L2\"]},{\"id\":\"two-legged-pullback\",\"term\":\"Two-legged pullback\",\"definition\":\"A pullback against the trend in two legs, separated by a small bounce.\",\"plain\":\"A dip that comes in two steps instead of one.\",\"related\":[\"pullback\",\"high-1-high-2\",\"second-entry\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"second-entry\",\"term\":\"Second entry\",\"definition\":\"A trade taken on the second attempt in one direction, after the first attempt failed.\",\"plain\":\"Getting in on the second try, after the first one didn't work.\",\"related\":[\"high-1-high-2\",\"two-legged-pullback\",\"entry\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"micro-channel\",\"term\":\"Micro channel\",\"definition\":\"A run of bars where no bar's low goes below the previous bar's low; in a bear trend, no high goes above the previous high.\",\"plain\":\"A trend so steady that no bar dips under the bar before it.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"three-push-wedge\",\"spike-and-channel\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"three-push-wedge\",\"term\":\"Three-push wedge\",\"definition\":\"Three pushes in the same direction with fading momentum, each leg shorter than the last.\",\"plain\":\"Three tries to go further, each one weaker.\",\"related\":[\"micro-channel\",\"reversal\",\"high-1-high-2\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"spike-and-channel\",\"term\":\"Spike and channel\",\"definition\":\"A strong breakout, the spike, followed by a slower trending channel in the same direction.\",\"plain\":\"A fast burst, then a slower drift the same way.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"micro-channel\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"trapped-trader\",\"term\":\"Trapped trader\",\"definition\":\"A trader caught on the wrong side of a failed move, whose exit can fuel the move against them.\",\"plain\":\"Someone who entered just before the market turned, and now has to get out.\",\"related\":[\"failed-breakout\",\"stop-loss\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"failed-breakout\",\"term\":\"Failed breakout\",\"definition\":\"A close beyond a level followed by a close back inside within one to three bars.\",\"plain\":\"A break out of a range that quickly falls back in.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"trapped-trader\",\"hikkake\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.6\",\"aka\":[\"false breakout\"]},{\"id\":\"climax\",\"term\":\"Climax\",\"definition\":\"An unusually large bar late in a long move, often followed by a pause or reversal. The app flags bars at least 2.5 ATR tall after 20 or more trend bars.\",\"plain\":\"A huge bar at the end of a long run, when the last buyers or sellers rush in.\",\"related\":[\"exhaustion\",\"atr\",\"reversal\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"exhaustion\",\"term\":\"Exhaustion\",\"definition\":\"Signs that a move is tiring: more overlap, tails against it, no follow-through after a big bar.\",\"plain\":\"Clues that a move is running out of energy.\",\"related\":[\"climax\",\"trend\",\"reversal\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"brier-score\",\"term\":\"Brier score\",\"definition\":\"The average squared gap between your stated probabilities and what happened, scored 1 or 0. Lower is better: 0 is perfect, and always saying 50% scores 0.25.\",\"plain\":\"A score for how well your probabilities match what actually happens.\",\"related\":[\"probability\",\"sample-size\",\"variance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"timeframe-shopping\",\"term\":\"Timeframe shopping\",\"definition\":\"Switching timeframes until one agrees with what you already wanted to do.\",\"plain\":\"Changing charts until one tells you what you want to hear.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"triple-screen\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.8\"},{\"id\":\"triple-screen\",\"term\":\"Triple Screen\",\"definition\":\"Alexander Elder's method: the trend on a longer timeframe, a pullback on the trading one, and the entry timed on a shorter one.\",\"plain\":\"Three charts, each with one job: direction, setup, timing.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"timeframe-shopping\",\"pullback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.6.8\"},{\"id\":\"anticipation-entry\",\"term\":\"Anticipation entry\",\"definition\":\"An entry taken before a pattern or signal is confirmed, usually with a limit order near the expected turn: a better price and a tighter stop, for a lower hit rate.\",\"plain\":\"Getting in early, before the pattern has proved itself.\",\"related\":[\"confirmation-entry\",\"retest-entry\",\"limit-order\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"confirmation-entry\",\"term\":\"Confirmation entry\",\"definition\":\"An entry taken after a pattern or signal is confirmed, for example by a close beyond a level, usually with a stop or market order: a higher hit rate at a worse price.\",\"plain\":\"Getting in once the pattern has proved itself, at a higher price.\",\"related\":[\"anticipation-entry\",\"retest-entry\",\"stop-order\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"retest-entry\",\"term\":\"Retest entry\",\"definition\":\"An entry with a limit order at a level price has just broken, filled only if price comes back to it. The price is better than a confirmation entry's, but the trade can be missed entirely.\",\"plain\":\"Waiting for price to come back to the level it broke. It may never come.\",\"related\":[\"confirmation-entry\",\"adverse-selection\",\"limit-order\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"adverse-selection\",\"term\":\"Adverse selection\",\"definition\":\"The tendency of resting limit orders to fill most surely when price keeps moving through them, and to miss the moves that never come back to them.\",\"plain\":\"Your limit order fills on the trades that go wrong and misses the best ones.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order\",\"fill\",\"retest-entry\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"pyramiding\",\"term\":\"Pyramiding\",\"definition\":\"Adding to a winning position while moving the stops so that the total result, if every stop is hit, stays within the original 1R.\",\"plain\":\"Adding to a winner, and lifting the stops so the risk doesn't grow.\",\"related\":[\"scaling-in\",\"open-risk\",\"split-entry\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.2\"},{\"id\":\"split-entry\",\"term\":\"Split entry\",\"definition\":\"Entering a planned position in several parts, with the stop and the total risk set for the full size before the first part is bought.\",\"plain\":\"Buying a planned position in pieces, with the risk planned for all of it.\",\"related\":[\"scaling-in\",\"pyramiding\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.2\"},{\"id\":\"open-risk\",\"term\":\"Open risk\",\"definition\":\"The loss your open positions would take, counted from their entry prices, if every stop were hit now. Stops raised above the entry shrink it, and can turn it into a locked-in gain.\",\"plain\":\"What you'd lose overall if all your stops were hit right now.\",\"related\":[\"r\",\"pyramiding\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.2\"},{\"id\":\"structural-target\",\"term\":\"Structural target\",\"definition\":\"A profit target set just before the next support or resistance zone in the trade's direction, rather than at a fixed multiple of R.\",\"plain\":\"A target at the next level where price has turned before.\",\"related\":[\"take-profit\",\"resistance\",\"mfe\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.3\"},{\"id\":\"mfe\",\"term\":\"MFE\",\"definition\":\"Maximum favourable excursion: the best unrealised result a trade reached before its exit, usually in R. For a long: (highest high − entry) ÷ (entry − stop).\",\"plain\":\"How far a trade went in your favour, at its best.\",\"related\":[\"mae\",\"giveback\",\"exit-efficiency\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.3\",\"aka\":[\"Maximum favourable excursion\"]},{\"id\":\"chandelier-exit\",\"term\":\"Chandelier exit\",\"definition\":\"A trailing stop for a long at the highest high since entry minus a multiple of ATR, often 3. It rises with new highs and never moves down.\",\"plain\":\"A stop that hangs a few ATRs under the trade's highest point.\",\"related\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"atr\",\"giveback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.4\",\"aka\":[\"Chandelier stop\"]},{\"id\":\"giveback\",\"term\":\"Giveback\",\"definition\":\"The part of a trade's best unrealised gain given back before the exit: the MFE minus the realised result, in R.\",\"plain\":\"How much of the best profit you handed back before you got out.\",\"related\":[\"mfe\",\"exit-efficiency\",\"trailing-stop\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.4\"},{\"id\":\"breakeven-stop\",\"term\":\"Breakeven stop\",\"definition\":\"A stop moved to the entry price after the trade has gained a set amount, often +1R. Spread and commissions still make an exit there a small loss.\",\"plain\":\"Moving the stop to where you got in, once the trade is up.\",\"related\":[\"scratch-trade\",\"stop-loss\",\"trailing-stop\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"Stop to breakeven\"]},{\"id\":\"scratch-trade\",\"term\":\"Scratch trade\",\"definition\":\"A trade closed at about zero before costs, often by a breakeven stop.\",\"plain\":\"A trade that ends roughly flat.\",\"related\":[\"breakeven-stop\",\"commission\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"Scratch\"]},{\"id\":\"blended-r\",\"term\":\"Blended R\",\"definition\":\"The total R of a trade closed in parts: each part's R multiplied by its share of the position, then added up.\",\"plain\":\"The overall R of a trade you sold in pieces.\",\"related\":[\"scaling-out\",\"runner\",\"r-multiple\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.6\"},{\"id\":\"runner\",\"term\":\"Runner\",\"definition\":\"The part of a position kept open after a partial exit, usually under a trailing stop.\",\"plain\":\"The piece you leave running after taking some profit.\",\"related\":[\"scaling-out\",\"blended-r\",\"trailing-stop\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.6\"},{\"id\":\"time-stop\",\"term\":\"Time stop\",\"definition\":\"A written rule to exit a trade that hasn't made a set progress, such as +0.5R, within a set number of bars.\",\"plain\":\"Getting out of a trade that's going nowhere, after a set time.\",\"related\":[\"exit-reason\",\"stop-loss\",\"trading-plan\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.7\"},{\"id\":\"exit-reason\",\"term\":\"Exit reason\",\"definition\":\"A tag recording why a trade was closed, chosen from reasons written in the plan before entry: structure broke, opposite signal, time stop or event ahead.\",\"plain\":\"A label saying why you got out, picked from your plan.\",\"related\":[\"time-stop\",\"journal\",\"playbook\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.7\"},{\"id\":\"mae\",\"term\":\"MAE\",\"definition\":\"Maximum adverse excursion: the worst unrealised result a trade reached before its exit, usually in R. For a long: (entry − lowest low) ÷ (entry − stop).\",\"plain\":\"How far a trade went against you, at its worst.\",\"related\":[\"mfe\",\"stop-loss\",\"edge-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.8\",\"aka\":[\"Maximum adverse excursion\"]},{\"id\":\"exit-efficiency\",\"term\":\"Exit efficiency\",\"definition\":\"The share of a trade's MFE kept at the exit: the realised result divided by the MFE.\",\"plain\":\"How much of the best profit you actually kept.\",\"related\":[\"mfe\",\"giveback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.8\"},{\"id\":\"edge-ratio\",\"term\":\"Edge ratio\",\"definition\":\"Average MFE divided by average MAE over a set of entries, both measured in the same unit, such as R or ATR. Random entries score about 1.\",\"plain\":\"How much further trades go for you than against you, on average.\",\"related\":[\"mae\",\"mfe\"],\"lesson\":\"2.7.8\",\"aka\":[\"E-ratio\"]},{\"id\":\"market-regime\",\"term\":\"Market regime\",\"definition\":\"The prevailing behaviour of a market over a stretch of time, such as trending, ranging or moving at random. It decides which strategies can work.\",\"plain\":\"How the market is behaving right now: trending, ranging or moving at random.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.1\"},{\"id\":\"trend-following\",\"term\":\"Trend following\",\"definition\":\"A family of rules that enters in the direction of an established trend, keeps losses small with a stop and lets winners run with a trailing exit, usually at a 30–45% win rate.\",\"plain\":\"Trading in the direction of an established trend, cutting losses fast and letting winners run.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"trailing-stop\",\"moving-average\",\"whipsaw\",\"market-regime\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.2\"},{\"id\":\"trend-filter\",\"term\":\"Trend filter\",\"definition\":\"A condition, such as price above a rising 50-period moving average, that allows trades only in the direction of the larger trend.\",\"plain\":\"A rule that allows trades only in the direction of the larger trend.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"moving-average\",\"pullback\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.3\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-contraction\",\"term\":\"Volatility contraction\",\"definition\":\"A stretch of unusually narrow candles, such as an NR7, an inside bar or a Bollinger squeeze. Ranges tend to return to their usual size afterwards, in either direction.\",\"plain\":\"A quiet stretch of unusually small candles, which often comes before larger moves.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"nr7\",\"inside-bar\",\"squeeze\",\"volatility-clustering\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"range-trading\",\"term\":\"Range trading\",\"definition\":\"Buying near support and selling near resistance in a confirmed range, with stops just outside the edges, until the first close outside the range.\",\"plain\":\"Buying near the bottom of a range and selling near the top, until it breaks.\",\"related\":[\"range\",\"support\",\"resistance\",\"mean-reversion\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.6\"},{\"id\":\"mean-reversion\",\"term\":\"Mean reversion\",\"definition\":\"A family of rules that fades short-term extremes, betting that price returns toward an average. Typically a high win rate, with rare large losses.\",\"plain\":\"Betting that a stretched price will move back toward its average.\",\"related\":[\"range-trading\",\"negative-skew\",\"bollinger-bands\",\"win-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.7\"},{\"id\":\"negative-skew\",\"term\":\"Negative skew\",\"definition\":\"A distribution of trade results with many small wins and rare large losses, so the average can be negative despite a high win rate.\",\"plain\":\"Many small wins and rare large losses; it looks smooth until the big loss arrives.\",\"related\":[\"mean-reversion\",\"win-rate\",\"expectancy\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.7\"},{\"id\":\"turtle-soup\",\"term\":\"Turtle Soup\",\"definition\":\"Connors and Raschke's failed-breakout trade: after a new 20-candle high whose previous 20-candle high is at least 4 candles old, sell just under the old high, with a stop above the new extreme. Mirrored for lows.\",\"plain\":\"Trading against a breakout of a 20-candle extreme that fails and returns inside the range.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"failed-breakout\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.8\"},{\"id\":\"indicator-redundancy\",\"term\":\"Indicator redundancy\",\"definition\":\"Counting indicators built from the same input as separate evidence. Their readings are highly correlated, so together they add little to one of them alone.\",\"plain\":\"Several indicators built from the same prices, telling you the same thing more than once.\",\"related\":[\"confluence\",\"collinearity\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.9\"},{\"id\":\"after-cost-expectancy\",\"term\":\"After-cost expectancy\",\"definition\":\"Expectancy per trade once spread, commissions and slippage are taken off. In R: gross expectancy minus the cost per unit divided by the stop distance.\",\"plain\":\"The average result per trade once spread, commission and slippage are taken off.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"spread\",\"commission\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"2.8.10\"},{\"id\":\"trading-session\",\"term\":\"Trading session\",\"definition\":\"The hours during which a market is open for trading, and in particular its main session, when most orders arrive and liquidity is deepest.\",\"plain\":\"The hours a market is open, and the main window when it is most active.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity\",\"session-overlap\",\"daylight-saving-time\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.1\",\"aka\":[\"session\"]},{\"id\":\"daylight-saving-time\",\"term\":\"Daylight saving time\",\"definition\":\"The seasonal one-hour clock change. The US switches on the second Sunday of March and the first Sunday of November, the EU on the last Sundays of March and October, so for a few weeks a year the gap between them is one hour smaller.\",\"plain\":\"The seasonal clock change; the US and Europe switch on different dates, shifting market hours.\",\"related\":[\"trading-session\",\"server-time\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.1\",\"aka\":[\"DST\",\"summer time\"]},{\"id\":\"server-time\",\"term\":\"Server time\",\"definition\":\"The time zone a trading platform uses to stamp prices and close daily candles. FX platforms commonly use GMT+2 or GMT+3, so the day ends at 17:00 New York time, which may differ from your own clock.\",\"plain\":\"The clock your trading platform shows, which may differ from your local time.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"daylight-saving-time\",\"candle\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.1\"},{\"id\":\"session-overlap\",\"term\":\"Session overlap\",\"definition\":\"Hours when two major trading sessions are open at once. In FX the London–New York overlap, about 08:00–12:00 New York time, is typically the busiest window of the day.\",\"plain\":\"Hours when two major sessions are open together, usually the busiest of the day.\",\"related\":[\"trading-session\",\"liquidity\",\"spread\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.2\"},{\"id\":\"opening-auction\",\"term\":\"Opening auction\",\"definition\":\"A call auction at the start of an exchange's regular session: orders collected beforehand are matched at one price, which becomes the official open.\",\"plain\":\"A single-price matching of orders that starts an exchange's regular session.\",\"related\":[\"closing-auction\",\"auction\",\"open\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.3\"},{\"id\":\"closing-auction\",\"term\":\"Closing auction\",\"definition\":\"A call auction at the end of the regular session that matches orders at one price: the official close, used by index funds and for valuations.\",\"plain\":\"A single-price matching of orders at the end of the session that sets the official close.\",\"related\":[\"opening-auction\",\"auction\",\"close\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.3\"},{\"id\":\"opening-range\",\"term\":\"Opening range\",\"definition\":\"The high and low of the first N minutes of the main session, with N, often 5, 15, 30 or 60, fixed in advance.\",\"plain\":\"The high and low of the first minutes of the main session.\",\"related\":[\"orb\",\"range\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.4\"},{\"id\":\"orb\",\"term\":\"ORB\",\"definition\":\"Opening range breakout: entering with a stop order just beyond the opening range's high or low, with the stop-loss at the other edge, usually exiting by the close.\",\"plain\":\"Opening range breakout: trading a break of the opening range's high or low.\",\"related\":[\"opening-range\",\"breakout\",\"stop-order\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.4\",\"aka\":[\"opening range breakout\"]},{\"id\":\"gap-and-go\",\"term\":\"Gap and go\",\"definition\":\"Trading in the direction of a large opening gap that has a real catalyst and heavy relative volume, entering on a break of the early range.\",\"plain\":\"Trading in the direction of a large opening gap that has a real catalyst.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"gap-fill\",\"relative-volume\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.5\"},{\"id\":\"rollover\",\"term\":\"Rollover\",\"definition\":\"The daily roll of open spot FX positions to the next value date, by convention at 17:00 New York time. Spreads often widen around it, and financing is charged or paid on positions held through it.\",\"plain\":\"The daily roll of open FX positions at about 17:00 New York time.\",\"related\":[\"overnight-financing\",\"spread\",\"forex\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.8\"},{\"id\":\"weekend-gap\",\"term\":\"Weekend gap\",\"definition\":\"The difference between Friday's close and the next session's open, caused by news while the market was closed. Stops that price jumps past fill at the first available price.\",\"plain\":\"A jump between Friday's close and the next open, caused by news while markets were shut.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"stop-loss\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"2.9.8\"},{\"id\":\"consensus\",\"term\":\"Consensus\",\"definition\":\"The middle of economists' forecasts for an economic release, published before it; it approximates what the market already expects.\",\"plain\":\"The middle of economists' forecasts for a release; roughly what the market already expects.\",\"related\":[\"surprise\",\"priced-in\",\"economic-calendar\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.1\",\"aka\":[\"Forecast\"]},{\"id\":\"surprise\",\"term\":\"Surprise\",\"definition\":\"The released value minus its consensus. Divided by the usual size of past surprises, it becomes a standardised surprise, z, comparable across releases.\",\"plain\":\"The released number minus the consensus. Prices react mostly to this.\",\"related\":[\"consensus\",\"priced-in\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.1\",\"aka\":[\"News surprise\",\"Standardised surprise\"]},{\"id\":\"priced-in\",\"term\":\"Priced in\",\"definition\":\"Already reflected in the price, because traders positioned for it in advance, so its arrival moves price little.\",\"plain\":\"Already in the price, so it moves little when it happens.\",\"related\":[\"consensus\",\"surprise\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.1\"},{\"id\":\"economic-calendar\",\"term\":\"Economic calendar\",\"definition\":\"A schedule of upcoming economic releases, central bank decisions and other events, showing the time, impact tier, previous and revised values, consensus and, once published, the actual.\",\"plain\":\"The schedule of upcoming data releases, central bank decisions and other market events.\",\"related\":[\"impact-tier\",\"consensus\",\"revision\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.2\"},{\"id\":\"impact-tier\",\"term\":\"Impact tier\",\"definition\":\"A calendar's rating of how much an event usually moves markets, often red, orange and yellow; the ratings are editorial and shift with the market regime.\",\"plain\":\"A calendar's rough rating of how much an event usually moves markets.\",\"related\":[\"economic-calendar\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.2\",\"aka\":[\"Impact level\"]},{\"id\":\"revision\",\"term\":\"Revision\",\"definition\":\"A change to a previously published figure, released with later data; US payrolls, for example, are revised in the two following reports.\",\"plain\":\"A change to a number published earlier, released alongside the new one.\",\"related\":[\"economic-calendar\",\"nfp\",\"surprise\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.2\"},{\"id\":\"spread-widening\",\"term\":\"Spread widening\",\"definition\":\"An increase in the bid-ask spread, often several times over, as dealers widen their quotes and pull orders around news or in thin markets.\",\"plain\":\"The gap between bid and ask growing, often sharply, around news or in thin markets.\",\"related\":[\"spread\",\"liquidity\",\"spread-triggered-stop\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.3\"},{\"id\":\"spread-triggered-stop\",\"term\":\"Spread-triggered stop\",\"definition\":\"A stop triggered because a widened spread pushed the bid (for a long) or the ask (for a short) through it, although the mid price never reached the stop.\",\"plain\":\"A stop hit because the spread widened, although the mid price never reached it.\",\"related\":[\"spread-widening\",\"stop-loss\",\"bid\",\"mid-price\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.3\"},{\"id\":\"nfp\",\"term\":\"NFP\",\"definition\":\"Nonfarm payrolls: the monthly US employment report, with payrolls from a survey of firms, the unemployment rate from a survey of households, and average hourly earnings; usually released on the first Friday of the month at 08:30 New York time.\",\"plain\":\"Nonfarm payrolls: the monthly US jobs report, usually released on a Friday at 08:30 New York time.\",\"related\":[\"revision\",\"consensus\",\"economic-calendar\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.4\",\"aka\":[\"Nonfarm payrolls\",\"Jobs report\"]},{\"id\":\"cpi\",\"term\":\"CPI\",\"definition\":\"Consumer price index: a monthly measure of the change in prices consumers pay for a basket of goods and services. The headline figure covers all items.\",\"plain\":\"Consumer price index: the main monthly measure of inflation in consumer prices.\",\"related\":[\"core-inflation\",\"consensus\",\"hawkish\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.5\",\"aka\":[\"Consumer price index\",\"Headline CPI\"]},{\"id\":\"core-inflation\",\"term\":\"Core inflation\",\"definition\":\"Inflation excluding food and energy, the most volatile prices, used to judge the underlying trend; for CPI, markets focus on its month-on-month change.\",\"plain\":\"Inflation without food and energy prices, which markets watch more closely.\",\"related\":[\"cpi\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.5\",\"aka\":[\"Core CPI\"]},{\"id\":\"central-bank\",\"term\":\"Central bank\",\"definition\":\"The institution that sets a currency's policy interest rate and manages its money supply, such as the Federal Reserve, the ECB, the Bank of England or the Bank of Japan.\",\"plain\":\"The institution that sets a currency's interest rates, such as the Fed or the ECB.\",\"related\":[\"rate-decision\",\"hawkish\",\"dovish\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\"},{\"id\":\"rate-decision\",\"term\":\"Rate decision\",\"definition\":\"A central bank's scheduled announcement of its policy interest rate, usually with a statement and often a press conference; it moves prices by how far it differs from what was priced.\",\"plain\":\"A central bank's announcement of its policy interest rate.\",\"related\":[\"central-bank\",\"basis-point\",\"priced-in\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\",\"aka\":[\"Policy decision\"]},{\"id\":\"hawkish\",\"term\":\"Hawkish\",\"definition\":\"Leaning toward higher interest rates or tighter policy; for prices, what counts is being more hawkish than the market expected.\",\"plain\":\"Leaning toward higher interest rates or tighter policy.\",\"related\":[\"dovish\",\"central-bank\",\"rate-decision\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\"},{\"id\":\"dovish\",\"term\":\"Dovish\",\"definition\":\"Leaning toward lower interest rates or easier policy; for prices, what counts is being more dovish than the market expected.\",\"plain\":\"Leaning toward lower interest rates or easier policy.\",\"related\":[\"hawkish\",\"central-bank\",\"rate-decision\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.6\"},{\"id\":\"stand-aside\",\"term\":\"Stand aside\",\"definition\":\"A news policy of placing no new orders, and holding no position or only what the plan allows, from a set time before an event until after it.\",\"plain\":\"Choosing to hold no new trades or positions around an event. A valid strategy.\",\"related\":[\"flat\",\"economic-calendar\",\"second-move\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.7\"},{\"id\":\"second-move\",\"term\":\"Second move\",\"definition\":\"The price move after a release once spreads have normalised and the details are digested; traded on a break of the post-release range, with a stop at its other side.\",\"plain\":\"The price move after spreads normalise, once the details of a release are digested.\",\"related\":[\"post-release-range\",\"stand-aside\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.8\"},{\"id\":\"post-release-range\",\"term\":\"Post-release range\",\"definition\":\"The high and low of the first minutes after a release, often the first 5 or 15, used to set a second-move entry and its stop.\",\"plain\":\"The high and low of the first minutes after a news release.\",\"related\":[\"second-move\",\"range\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.8\"},{\"id\":\"unscheduled-event\",\"term\":\"Unscheduled event\",\"definition\":\"News that arrives without warning, such as a geopolitical shock, an emergency policy move, a dropped currency peg or a flash crash. No calendar or timing rule avoids it; only position size and leverage limit the damage.\",\"plain\":\"News that arrives without warning, so no calendar or timing can protect you.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"leverage\",\"economic-calendar\"],\"lesson\":\"2.10.9\",\"aka\":[\"Unscheduled shock\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-sizing\",\"term\":\"Volatility sizing\",\"definition\":\"Sizing each position so that one ATR of movement costs the same share of equity in every instrument: units = equity × v ÷ ATR per unit.\",\"plain\":\"Sizing so a typical move (one ATR) costs the same share of equity in every instrument.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"position-size\",\"volatility\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.1\",\"aka\":[\"ATR sizing\"]},{\"id\":\"portfolio-heat\",\"term\":\"Portfolio heat\",\"definition\":\"The sum of the open risk of all positions and pending orders, as a percentage of equity: what would be lost if every stop were hit at once.\",\"plain\":\"The total you would lose if every open stop were hit, as a percentage of equity.\",\"related\":[\"open-risk\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"exposure\",\"pending-order\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.2\",\"aka\":[\"Heat\"]},{\"id\":\"effective-bets\",\"term\":\"Effective bets\",\"definition\":\"The number of independent positions a portfolio is worth once correlation is counted: n ÷ (1 + (n − 1) × ρ) for n positions with average correlation ρ.\",\"plain\":\"How many truly independent positions you hold once correlation is counted.\",\"related\":[\"correlation\",\"diversification\",\"portfolio-heat\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.3\"},{\"id\":\"exposure-limit\",\"term\":\"Exposure limit\",\"definition\":\"A cap on the risk allowed in one instrument, one direction or one theme, such as 2% per instrument or one position per correlated group.\",\"plain\":\"A cap on how much risk you allow in one instrument, direction or theme.\",\"related\":[\"exposure\",\"portfolio-heat\",\"correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.4\"},{\"id\":\"monthly-loss-limit\",\"term\":\"Monthly loss limit\",\"definition\":\"A cap on the month's realised losses plus the open risk of current positions, measured from month-start equity. Once it's reached, no new trades until the next month.\",\"plain\":\"A cap on the month's losses plus open risk; once reached, no new trades that month.\",\"related\":[\"daily-loss-limit\",\"open-risk\",\"drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.4\"},{\"id\":\"drawdown-tier\",\"term\":\"Drawdown tier\",\"definition\":\"A rule set in advance that cuts risk, or stops trading, when equity falls a set depth below its peak, together with a rule for when risk returns.\",\"plain\":\"A preset step that cuts your risk when the account falls a set amount below its peak.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"max-drawdown\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.5\"},{\"id\":\"profit-factor\",\"term\":\"Profit factor\",\"definition\":\"Gross profit divided by gross loss over a set of trades. Above 1 the trades made money overall; it ignores their order and the sample size.\",\"plain\":\"Gross profit divided by gross loss. Above 1 means the trades made money overall.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"win-rate\",\"payoff-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.6\",\"aka\":[\"PF\"]},{\"id\":\"payoff-ratio\",\"term\":\"Payoff ratio\",\"definition\":\"The average winning trade divided by the average losing trade, both in R or both in money.\",\"plain\":\"The average winning trade divided by the average losing trade.\",\"related\":[\"win-rate\",\"expectancy\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"profit-factor\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.6\",\"aka\":[\"Win/loss ratio\"]},{\"id\":\"recovery-factor\",\"term\":\"Recovery factor\",\"definition\":\"Net profit divided by the maximum drawdown over the same period. It grows with the length of the record, so it compares fairly only over equal periods.\",\"plain\":\"Net profit divided by the maximum drawdown.\",\"related\":[\"max-drawdown\",\"drawdown\",\"profit-factor\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.6\"},{\"id\":\"confidence-interval\",\"term\":\"Confidence interval\",\"definition\":\"A range computed from a sample that would contain the true value in a stated share of samples, such as 95%. For an average: mean ± 1.96 × sd ÷ √n.\",\"plain\":\"A range that likely contains the true value, given how much data you have.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"expectancy\",\"variance\",\"base-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.7\",\"aka\":[\"CI\"]},{\"id\":\"sqn\",\"term\":\"SQN\",\"definition\":\"System quality number: √N × mean R ÷ standard deviation of R, with N capped at 100. Uncapped, it's the t-statistic of the mean R.\",\"plain\":\"System quality number: average R divided by its spread, scaled by the number of trades.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"r-multiple\",\"confidence-interval\"],\"lesson\":\"2.11.8\",\"aka\":[\"System quality number\"]},{\"id\":\"prospect-theory\",\"term\":\"Prospect theory\",\"definition\":\"A model of choice under risk in which outcomes are judged as gains or losses from a reference point, sensitivity shrinks as amounts grow, and losses weigh more than equal gains.\",\"plain\":\"The finding that people judge gains and losses from a reference point and feel losses more.\",\"related\":[\"reference-point\",\"loss-aversion\",\"disposition-effect\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.1\"},{\"id\":\"reference-point\",\"term\":\"Reference point\",\"definition\":\"The value an outcome is compared with, such as your entry price; it decides whether a result feels like a gain or a loss.\",\"plain\":\"The value you compare an outcome with, such as your entry price.\",\"related\":[\"prospect-theory\",\"anchoring\",\"break-even\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.1\"},{\"id\":\"disposition-effect\",\"term\":\"Disposition effect\",\"definition\":\"The tendency to sell winning positions too early and hold losing ones too long, measured as the proportion of gains realised minus the proportion of losses realised (PGR − PLR).\",\"plain\":\"Selling winners too soon and holding losers too long.\",\"related\":[\"prospect-theory\",\"loss-aversion\",\"exit-reason\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.2\"},{\"id\":\"overconfidence\",\"term\":\"Overconfidence\",\"definition\":\"Being more sure of your judgement than your record justifies; in a confidence log, an average stated confidence above the hit rate.\",\"plain\":\"Being more sure of your judgement than your track record justifies.\",\"related\":[\"calibration\",\"overtrading\",\"cognitive-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.3\"},{\"id\":\"calibration\",\"term\":\"Calibration\",\"definition\":\"How closely stated confidence matches the actual hit rate. A calibrated trader's 70% calls come true about 70% of the time.\",\"plain\":\"How well your stated confidence matches how often you are actually right.\",\"related\":[\"overconfidence\",\"brier-score\",\"probability\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.3\"},{\"id\":\"law-of-small-numbers\",\"term\":\"Law of small numbers\",\"definition\":\"The mistaken belief that a small sample closely mirrors the process behind it, so a few results seem to show what a method can do.\",\"plain\":\"The mistaken belief that a few results reliably show what a method can do.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"regression-to-the-mean\",\"recency-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.4\"},{\"id\":\"regression-to-the-mean\",\"term\":\"Regression to the mean\",\"definition\":\"When results contain noise, an extreme result tends to be followed by one closer to the average, because its luck doesn't repeat.\",\"plain\":\"After an extreme result, the next one tends to be closer to average.\",\"related\":[\"law-of-small-numbers\",\"variance\",\"sample-size\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.4\"},{\"id\":\"anchoring\",\"term\":\"Anchoring\",\"definition\":\"Letting an initial or irrelevant number, such as your entry price or an old high, pull later estimates and decisions toward it.\",\"plain\":\"Letting an irrelevant number, like your entry price, pull your judgement.\",\"related\":[\"reference-point\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"invalidation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.5\"},{\"id\":\"sunk-cost\",\"term\":\"Sunk cost\",\"definition\":\"Money or effort already spent that can't be recovered. The sunk-cost trap is letting it decide whether to hold or add to a position.\",\"plain\":\"Money already lost. It shouldn't change what you do next, but often does.\",\"related\":[\"house-money-effect\",\"averaging-down\",\"loss-aversion\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.6\"},{\"id\":\"house-money-effect\",\"term\":\"House-money effect\",\"definition\":\"Taking more risk after gains because the profits feel like someone else's money. Its mirror, the breakeven effect, seeks risk after losses to get back to even.\",\"plain\":\"Taking more risk after a win because profits feel like free money.\",\"related\":[\"sunk-cost\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.6\"},{\"id\":\"tilt\",\"term\":\"Tilt\",\"definition\":\"A state, named in poker, in which emotion overrides the plan; in a journal it shows as a cluster of rule breaks.\",\"plain\":\"A state where emotion overrides your plan, shown by several broken rules in a row.\",\"related\":[\"guard-rail\",\"revenge-trading\",\"cooling-off-pause\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.7\"},{\"id\":\"probabilistic-thinking\",\"term\":\"Probabilistic thinking\",\"definition\":\"Treating each trade as one draw from a distribution of outcomes, and judging a method across a series rather than by any single result.\",\"plain\":\"Seeing each trade as one draw from many, not as a test of being right.\",\"related\":[\"broad-framing\",\"expectancy\",\"variance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.9\"},{\"id\":\"broad-framing\",\"term\":\"Broad framing\",\"definition\":\"Evaluating results across a series of decisions, such as a block of 20 trades, instead of one at a time (narrow framing).\",\"plain\":\"Judging results across a series of trades rather than one trade at a time.\",\"related\":[\"probabilistic-thinking\",\"sample-size\",\"expectancy\"],\"lesson\":\"2.12.9\"},{\"id\":\"setup-card\",\"term\":\"Setup card\",\"definition\":\"A playbook entry for one setup: its regime, trigger, order type, stop rule, management, re-entry and no-trade conditions, evidence status and its own statistics.\",\"plain\":\"A playbook entry describing one setup's conditions, stop, management and statistics.\",\"related\":[\"playbook\",\"trading-plan\",\"invalidation\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.2\"},{\"id\":\"pre-market-routine\",\"term\":\"Pre-market routine\",\"definition\":\"A fixed sequence run before each session: readiness, overnight news, the calendar, the regime, key levels, if-then scenarios, the watchlist and the day's maximum risk.\",\"plain\":\"The fixed checks you run before each session, from the calendar to your risk for the day.\",\"related\":[\"fit-to-trade\",\"if-then-rule\",\"trading-plan\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.3\"},{\"id\":\"error-cost\",\"term\":\"Error cost\",\"definition\":\"The difference, in R, between a trade's result as traded and the same trade done by the rules. Variance has no error cost.\",\"plain\":\"How many R a mistake cost compared with the same trade done by the rules.\",\"related\":[\"error-tag\",\"r\",\"variance\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.5\"},{\"id\":\"daily-review\",\"term\":\"Daily review\",\"definition\":\"A five-to-ten-minute review at the end of a session that grades decisions: the day's process KPIs, the best and worst decision, each loss as mistake or variance, and one lesson.\",\"plain\":\"A short end-of-day check of your decisions, not your profit.\",\"related\":[\"weekly-review\",\"journal\",\"decision-quality\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.6\"},{\"id\":\"weekly-review\",\"term\":\"Weekly review\",\"definition\":\"A weekly look at results by setup, time of day, instrument and error tag, set against the range expectancy predicts, ending with one process goal.\",\"plain\":\"A weekly look at results by setup, time and error, ending with one process goal.\",\"related\":[\"daily-review\",\"process-goal\",\"error-tag\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.6\"},{\"id\":\"blind-replay\",\"term\":\"Blind replay\",\"definition\":\"Replaying a past trade with every candle after the decision point hidden, so the decision is judged on what could be seen at the time.\",\"plain\":\"Reviewing a trade with the future hidden, as you saw it when deciding.\",\"related\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"daily-review\",\"decision-quality\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.6\"},{\"id\":\"process-goal\",\"term\":\"Process goal\",\"definition\":\"A goal set on an action you fully control, with a target that can be measured, such as a stop before entry on 100% of trades.\",\"plain\":\"A goal you fully control, such as placing every stop before entry.\",\"related\":[\"process-kpi\",\"kpi\",\"outcome-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.7\"},{\"id\":\"kpi\",\"term\":\"KPI\",\"definition\":\"Key performance indicator: a number tracked over time to judge progress, such as stop integrity or expectancy.\",\"plain\":\"A number you track to judge progress, such as stop integrity.\",\"related\":[\"process-kpi\",\"process-goal\",\"expectancy\"],\"lesson\":\"2.13.7\",\"aka\":[\"Key performance indicator\"]},{\"id\":\"dividend\",\"term\":\"Dividend\",\"definition\":\"A cash payment a company makes to its shareholders, usually per share and on a set schedule. Holders who own the share before the ex-dividend date receive it.\",\"plain\":\"A share of the company's profits, paid to you in cash.\",\"related\":[\"ex-dividend-date\",\"stock\",\"stock-split\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\"},{\"id\":\"ex-dividend-date\",\"term\":\"Ex-dividend date\",\"definition\":\"The first trading day on which a share trades without its next dividend. On that day the price typically drops by about the dividend.\",\"plain\":\"Buy before this day and you get the dividend; buy on it and you don't.\",\"related\":[\"dividend\",\"stock\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\",\"aka\":[\"Ex-date\"]},{\"id\":\"stock-split\",\"term\":\"Stock split\",\"definition\":\"A corporate action that divides each share into several, lowering the price in proportion; a reverse split merges shares instead. The total value is unchanged.\",\"plain\":\"Cutting the same pizza into more slices: more pieces, same pizza.\",\"related\":[\"stock\",\"dividend\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\",\"aka\":[\"Split\",\"Reverse split\"]},{\"id\":\"leveraged-etf\",\"term\":\"Leveraged ETF\",\"definition\":\"An ETF that aims to return a multiple, such as 2× or −1×, of an index's daily move. Because it resets daily, its return over longer periods drifts from that multiple.\",\"plain\":\"A fund that doubles each day's move, and loses value in choppy markets.\",\"related\":[\"etf\",\"leverage\",\"index\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.1\"},{\"id\":\"short-squeeze\",\"term\":\"Short squeeze\",\"definition\":\"A sharp rise in a heavily shorted stock as short sellers buy back to cut their losses, which pushes the price higher still.\",\"plain\":\"Short sellers rushing to buy back at once, sending the price up faster.\",\"related\":[\"short\",\"borrow-fee\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.2\"},{\"id\":\"locate\",\"term\":\"Locate\",\"definition\":\"In the US, a broker's reasonable belief, before a short sale, that the shares can be borrowed and delivered.\",\"plain\":\"The broker checking that shares can be borrowed before you short them.\",\"related\":[\"short\",\"borrow-fee\",\"short-sale-restriction\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.2\"},{\"id\":\"short-sale-restriction\",\"term\":\"Short-sale restriction\",\"definition\":\"A US rule (Rule 201): after a stock falls 10% or more from the prior close, short sales are allowed only above the best bid, for the rest of that day and the next.\",\"plain\":\"After a big fall in a day, short sellers may only sell above the best bid for a while.\",\"related\":[\"short\",\"locate\",\"bid\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.2\",\"aka\":[\"SSR\",\"Alternative uptick rule\"]},{\"id\":\"currency-pair\",\"term\":\"Currency pair\",\"definition\":\"Two currencies quoted together, such as EUR/USD: the price of one unit of the first, the base, in the second, the quote.\",\"plain\":\"How much of the second currency one unit of the first costs.\",\"related\":[\"forex\",\"pip\",\"pip-value\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"pip-value\",\"term\":\"Pip value\",\"definition\":\"The money one pip of movement is worth for a given position size. On a standard lot it is 10.00 when the account currency is the quote currency; otherwise it's converted at the current rate.\",\"plain\":\"How much you make or lose for each pip price moves.\",\"related\":[\"pip\",\"lot\",\"currency-pair\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"triple-swap\",\"term\":\"Triple swap\",\"definition\":\"The rollover, usually on Wednesday, that charges or pays three days of swap, because spot settlement two days later spans the weekend.\",\"plain\":\"Wednesday's swap counts three nights, to cover the weekend.\",\"related\":[\"overnight-financing\",\"rollover\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.4\",\"aka\":[\"Triple rollover\",\"Triple Wednesday\"]},{\"id\":\"carry\",\"term\":\"Carry\",\"definition\":\"Holding a higher-yielding currency against a lower-yielding one to earn the interest difference. It pays steadily but can lose sharply when crowded positions unwind.\",\"plain\":\"Getting paid interest to hold a position, until a sudden move takes it all back.\",\"related\":[\"overnight-financing\",\"negative-skew\",\"forex\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.4\",\"aka\":[\"Carry trade\"]},{\"id\":\"margin-level\",\"term\":\"Margin level\",\"definition\":\"Equity divided by used margin, times 100. Brokers issue margin calls and close positions out when it falls to set levels.\",\"plain\":\"How well your money covers the margin your positions need; the lower it is, the closer a close-out.\",\"related\":[\"free-margin\",\"margin\",\"margin-call\",\"margin-close-out\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.6\"},{\"id\":\"free-margin\",\"term\":\"Free margin\",\"definition\":\"Equity minus the margin already used by open positions: what's left to absorb losses or to open new trades.\",\"plain\":\"The part of your money not tied up as margin.\",\"related\":[\"margin-level\",\"margin\",\"equity\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.6\"},{\"id\":\"tradability-ratio\",\"term\":\"Tradability ratio\",\"definition\":\"An instrument's typical movement, such as its ATR, divided by its round-trip trading cost in the same units.\",\"plain\":\"How much the market moves for each unit of cost; higher is better for short trades.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"spread\",\"commission\",\"cost-drag\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.7\"},{\"id\":\"cost-stack\",\"term\":\"Cost stack\",\"definition\":\"All the costs between a trade's gross and net result: spread, commission, slippage, financing, borrow fees, taxes and opportunity cost.\",\"plain\":\"Every cost of a trade added up, not just the ones on the ticket.\",\"related\":[\"spread\",\"commission\",\"slippage\",\"overnight-financing\",\"borrow-fee\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.7\"},{\"id\":\"esma\",\"term\":\"ESMA\",\"definition\":\"The European Securities and Markets Authority, the EU body that coordinates securities regulation. In 2018 it restricted CFDs and banned binary options for retail clients.\",\"plain\":\"The EU's markets watchdog, above the national regulators.\",\"related\":[\"regulator\",\"fca\",\"asf\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\"},{\"id\":\"fca\",\"term\":\"FCA\",\"definition\":\"The Financial Conduct Authority, the UK regulator of financial firms and markets, including brokers that offer CFDs.\",\"plain\":\"The UK's financial watchdog.\",\"related\":[\"regulator\",\"esma\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\"},{\"id\":\"finra\",\"term\":\"FINRA\",\"definition\":\"The Financial Industry Regulatory Authority, the US self-regulatory body that oversees broker-dealers and sets rules such as margin requirements, under SEC supervision.\",\"plain\":\"The body that polices US brokers and their margin rules.\",\"related\":[\"regulator\",\"pattern-day-trader-rule\",\"intraday-margin\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\"},{\"id\":\"asf\",\"term\":\"ASF\",\"definition\":\"Autoritatea de Supraveghere Financiară, Romania's financial supervisor, which licenses and supervises investment firms there.\",\"plain\":\"Romania's financial watchdog: check brokers in its register.\",\"related\":[\"regulator\",\"esma\",\"licence-register\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\"},{\"id\":\"pattern-day-trader-rule\",\"term\":\"Pattern day trader rule\",\"definition\":\"A former US margin rule: 4 or more day trades in 5 business days in a margin account made a client a pattern day trader, who needed 25,000 dollars of equity. Replaced in 2026 by intraday margin.\",\"plain\":\"An old US rule that required 25,000 dollars to day trade often.\",\"related\":[\"intraday-margin\",\"finra\",\"day-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\",\"aka\":[\"PDT rule\"]},{\"id\":\"intraday-margin\",\"term\":\"Intraday margin\",\"definition\":\"The US margin standard approved in 2026 to replace the pattern day trader rule: brokers check for margin deficits during the day, and clients must cover them promptly.\",\"plain\":\"Margin checked during the day, not just at the close, for every margin account.\",\"related\":[\"pattern-day-trader-rule\",\"finra\",\"margin\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\"},{\"id\":\"licence-register\",\"term\":\"Licence register\",\"definition\":\"A regulator's official public list of authorised firms, with their licence numbers, legal names, websites and permissions.\",\"plain\":\"The regulator's own list, where you check that a broker is real and allowed to serve you.\",\"related\":[\"regulator\",\"asf\",\"esma\"],\"lesson\":\"2.14.8\",\"aka\":[\"Register of authorised firms\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-pool\",\"term\":\"Liquidity pool\",\"definition\":\"A cluster of resting orders near one price, such as stop-losses just beyond a swing high or take-profits on a round number.\",\"plain\":\"A crowd of waiting orders in one spot on the chart.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity\",\"resting-order\",\"stop-cluster\",\"take-profit-cluster\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"resting-order\",\"term\":\"Resting order\",\"definition\":\"An order waiting in the book, or at the broker, until price reaches it: a limit order, a take-profit or a stop.\",\"plain\":\"An order that sits and waits for its price.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order\",\"stop-order\",\"take-profit\",\"liquidity-pool\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"conditional-order\",\"term\":\"Conditional order\",\"definition\":\"An order that acts only when price reaches a trigger, such as a stop-loss or a take-profit.\",\"plain\":\"An 'if price gets here, do this' order.\",\"related\":[\"stop-loss\",\"take-profit\",\"stop-order\",\"resting-order\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"take-profit-cluster\",\"term\":\"Take-profit cluster\",\"definition\":\"Many take-profit orders at the same price, often exactly on a round number. As passive limits, they tend to slow price there.\",\"plain\":\"A crowd of profit targets on one price, usually a round one.\",\"related\":[\"take-profit\",\"round-number\",\"stop-cluster\",\"liquidity-pool\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"stop-cascade\",\"term\":\"Stop cascade\",\"definition\":\"Triggered stops become market orders whose fills push price into more stops, so the move speeds up.\",\"plain\":\"One batch of stops sets off the next, like dominoes.\",\"related\":[\"stop-cluster\",\"stop-order\",\"slippage\",\"liquidity-sweep\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"liquidity-sweep\",\"term\":\"Liquidity sweep\",\"definition\":\"Price trades through a level, triggers the stops there, then closes back inside, within one bar under this course's rule. It is known only after the close.\",\"plain\":\"A quick poke through a level that sets off the stops, then a close back inside.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"stop-cascade\",\"breakout\",\"stop-cluster\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.3\",\"aka\":[\"Stop run\"]},{\"id\":\"equal-highs-lows\",\"term\":\"Equal highs / equal lows\",\"definition\":\"Two or more neighbouring swing highs, or lows, within a stated tolerance of each other, often a fraction of ATR.\",\"plain\":\"Two peaks, or two dips, at almost the same price.\",\"related\":[\"swing-high\",\"swing-low\",\"double-top\",\"double-bottom\",\"detection-tolerance\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"reference-level\",\"term\":\"Reference level\",\"definition\":\"A price many traders watch, such as the prior-day high or low, the session high or low, the opening price or a round number.\",\"plain\":\"A line almost everyone has on their chart.\",\"related\":[\"round-number\",\"opening-range\",\"trading-session\",\"liquidity-pool\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"detection-tolerance\",\"term\":\"Detection tolerance\",\"definition\":\"How close two prices must be to count as equal under a rule, written before looking, often as a fraction of ATR.\",\"plain\":\"The 'close enough' setting of a rule.\",\"related\":[\"equal-highs-lows\",\"atr\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"fair-value-gap\",\"term\":\"Fair value gap\",\"definition\":\"A three-candle gap: candle 1's high sits below candle 3's low, or candle 1's low above candle 3's high. The gap is the price range between them.\",\"plain\":\"A hole left by one fast candle, between its neighbours' wicks.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"imbalance\",\"liquidity-void\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.5\",\"aka\":[\"FVG\",\"Three-candle gap\"]},{\"id\":\"imbalance\",\"term\":\"Imbalance\",\"definition\":\"Either more aggressive buying than selling at the best prices (order-flow imbalance), or a fast one-sided price move (price imbalance). Say which one you mean.\",\"plain\":\"A lopsided market: in the orders, or in the way price moved.\",\"related\":[\"fair-value-gap\",\"liquidity-void\",\"volume\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"liquidity-void\",\"term\":\"Liquidity void\",\"definition\":\"A price area crossed quickly with little trading, such as a low-volume node or single prints.\",\"plain\":\"A stretch of price that was skipped through in a hurry.\",\"related\":[\"low-volume-node\",\"fair-value-gap\",\"volume-profile\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"zone-grade\",\"term\":\"Zone grade\",\"definition\":\"A rating given to a supply or demand zone from written criteria, such as departure, time at base, freshness and location, before seeing what happens.\",\"plain\":\"A score for a zone, set by rules, not by feel.\",\"related\":[\"supply-zone\",\"demand-zone\",\"fresh-zone\",\"locked-drawing\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"locked-drawing\",\"term\":\"Locked drawing\",\"definition\":\"A level or zone fixed before the session, so it can't be redrawn after the result.\",\"plain\":\"A drawing you can't move once the market starts.\",\"related\":[\"zone-grade\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"trapped-inventory\",\"term\":\"Trapped inventory\",\"definition\":\"Positions caught on the wrong side of a failed move, described by their size and average price.\",\"plain\":\"How much got caught, and at what price.\",\"related\":[\"trapped-trader\",\"break-even-exit\",\"failed-breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"break-even-exit\",\"term\":\"Break-even exit\",\"definition\":\"Closing a losing position as soon as price returns to the entry price.\",\"plain\":\"Getting out flat, with no gain and no loss, after being under water.\",\"related\":[\"trapped-inventory\",\"loss-aversion\",\"break-even\",\"role-reversal\"],\"lesson\":\"3.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"venue\",\"term\":\"Venue\",\"definition\":\"One place where orders meet: an exchange, a dark pool or other alternative trading system, a dealer platform or a broker's internal book.\",\"plain\":\"Any place where a trade can happen.\",\"related\":[\"clob\",\"otc-market\",\"dark-pool\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"Trading venue\"]},{\"id\":\"clob\",\"term\":\"Central limit order book\",\"definition\":\"A shared book in which buy and sell limit orders rest and match by price, then by time.\",\"plain\":\"One list of everyone's orders, matched by fixed rules.\",\"related\":[\"order-book\",\"venue\",\"fifo\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"CLOB\",\"Order-driven market\"]},{\"id\":\"otc-market\",\"term\":\"Over-the-counter market\",\"definition\":\"Trading directly between two parties or with dealers, without one central exchange book, as in spot FX and most bonds.\",\"plain\":\"Deals done with a dealer or another firm, not on an exchange.\",\"related\":[\"dealer-market\",\"venue\",\"forex\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"OTC\"]},{\"id\":\"dealer-market\",\"term\":\"Dealer market\",\"definition\":\"A market in which dealers quote bid and ask prices and customers trade against them.\",\"plain\":\"You trade with the dealer, at the prices it shows you.\",\"related\":[\"otc-market\",\"market-maker\",\"quote\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"Quote-driven market\"]},{\"id\":\"internalisation\",\"term\":\"Internalisation\",\"definition\":\"A dealer, broker or wholesaler filling client orders from its own book instead of sending them to an exchange.\",\"plain\":\"Your order is filled in-house, not on the open market.\",\"related\":[\"wholesaler\",\"dealer-market\",\"b-book\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"Internalization\"]},{\"id\":\"dark-pool\",\"term\":\"Dark pool\",\"definition\":\"A trading venue that doesn't display its orders before they execute; its trades are reported afterwards.\",\"plain\":\"A venue where nobody sees the orders until they've traded.\",\"related\":[\"venue\",\"hidden-order\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"level-1\",\"term\":\"Level 1 (L1)\",\"definition\":\"Market data with the best bid, the best ask, their sizes and the last trade.\",\"plain\":\"The top of the book: the best prices and how much is there.\",\"related\":[\"level-2\",\"bid\",\"ask\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"L1\",\"Top of book\"]},{\"id\":\"level-2\",\"term\":\"Level 2 (L2)\",\"definition\":\"Market data with the total size waiting at each price level of the order book.\",\"plain\":\"How much is waiting at every price, not only the best one.\",\"related\":[\"level-1\",\"level-3\",\"market-depth\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"L2\",\"Market by price\"]},{\"id\":\"level-3\",\"term\":\"Level 3 (L3)\",\"definition\":\"Market data showing every individual order in the book, with its place in the queue.\",\"plain\":\"Each order on its own, in line.\",\"related\":[\"level-2\",\"queue-position\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"L3\",\"Market by order\"]},{\"id\":\"depth-chart\",\"term\":\"Depth chart\",\"definition\":\"A chart of cumulative bid and ask size at each distance from the current price.\",\"plain\":\"How much you could buy or sell before price moves a given distance.\",\"related\":[\"market-depth\",\"level-2\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"walking-the-book\",\"term\":\"Walking the book\",\"definition\":\"A market order larger than the best level filling across several price levels, so its average price gets worse.\",\"plain\":\"A big order eats through one price after another.\",\"related\":[\"average-fill-price\",\"market-depth\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"resiliency\",\"term\":\"Resiliency\",\"definition\":\"How fast an order book refills after a large order has used up its depth.\",\"plain\":\"How quickly the book recovers after a big trade.\",\"related\":[\"market-depth\",\"liquidity\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"inventory-risk\",\"term\":\"Inventory risk\",\"definition\":\"The risk a market maker carries from the positions it builds up while quoting, which can lose value before it can offload them.\",\"plain\":\"Being stuck holding what you only meant to pass on.\",\"related\":[\"market-maker\",\"quote-skew\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"quote-skew\",\"term\":\"Quote skew\",\"definition\":\"Shifting both the bid and the ask in one direction to attract trades that reduce a dealer's inventory.\",\"plain\":\"Moving both prices to encourage the trades you need.\",\"related\":[\"inventory-risk\",\"market-maker\",\"spread\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"high-frequency-trading\",\"term\":\"High-frequency trading\",\"definition\":\"Very fast automated trading with short holding periods and many orders placed and cancelled; many modern market makers use it.\",\"plain\":\"Computers trading in fractions of a second.\",\"related\":[\"market-maker\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.3\",\"aka\":[\"HFT\"]},{\"id\":\"queue-position\",\"term\":\"Queue position\",\"definition\":\"How much size sits ahead of your limit order at the same price.\",\"plain\":\"Your place in line at your price.\",\"related\":[\"queue-priority\",\"fifo\",\"level-3\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"fifo\",\"term\":\"FIFO (price-time priority)\",\"definition\":\"Matching rule: the best price first; at one price, the earliest order fills first.\",\"plain\":\"First in line fills first.\",\"related\":[\"queue-priority\",\"pro-rata\",\"queue-position\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\",\"aka\":[\"Price-time priority\",\"First in, first out\"]},{\"id\":\"pro-rata\",\"term\":\"Pro-rata\",\"definition\":\"Matching rule that splits incoming volume among the resting orders at a price in proportion to their size.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone in line gets a share by size, not by arrival.\",\"related\":[\"fifo\",\"queue-priority\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\",\"aka\":[\"Pro-rata matching\"]},{\"id\":\"tick-size\",\"term\":\"Tick size\",\"definition\":\"The smallest allowed price step for an instrument; a tick that is large relative to the price makes queues longer.\",\"plain\":\"How finely prices can be set.\",\"related\":[\"tick\",\"queue-position\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"iceberg-order\",\"term\":\"Iceberg order\",\"definition\":\"An order showing only part of its size; the visible slice refills from a hidden reserve after each fill.\",\"plain\":\"A big order that shows only its tip.\",\"related\":[\"display-quantity\",\"hidden-order\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"Reserve order\"]},{\"id\":\"display-quantity\",\"term\":\"Display quantity\",\"definition\":\"The visible slice of an iceberg order.\",\"plain\":\"The part of an iceberg you can see.\",\"related\":[\"iceberg-order\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"Display size\",\"Peak\"]},{\"id\":\"hidden-order\",\"term\":\"Hidden order\",\"definition\":\"A resting order that doesn't appear in the visible book; on most venues, displayed orders at the same price fill first.\",\"plain\":\"An order that waits where nobody can see it.\",\"related\":[\"iceberg-order\",\"dark-pool\",\"midpoint-peg\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"midpoint-peg\",\"term\":\"Midpoint peg\",\"definition\":\"An order that floats at the midpoint between the best bid and the best ask, usually hidden.\",\"plain\":\"An order that waits exactly halfway between bid and ask.\",\"related\":[\"hidden-order\",\"mid-price\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"post-only\",\"term\":\"Post-only order\",\"definition\":\"An order that is rejected or repriced if it would take liquidity instead of resting in the book.\",\"plain\":\"An order that only waits; it never trades on arrival.\",\"related\":[\"maker-taker\",\"limit-order\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"maker-taker\",\"term\":\"Maker-taker fees\",\"definition\":\"Fee schedules that pay a rebate to orders that add liquidity and charge orders that remove it.\",\"plain\":\"Resting orders get paid a little; orders that trade at once pay a little.\",\"related\":[\"post-only\",\"commission\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"a-book\",\"term\":\"A-book\",\"definition\":\"A broker model that passes client orders on to outside liquidity providers, so the broker doesn't hold the client's market risk.\",\"plain\":\"The broker sends your trade on instead of taking it.\",\"related\":[\"b-book\",\"stp\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"stp\",\"term\":\"STP (straight-through processing)\",\"definition\":\"Client orders sent automatically to outside liquidity providers, without a dealing desk; the broker earns a commission or a markup.\",\"plain\":\"Your order goes straight through to the market.\",\"related\":[\"a-book\",\"ecn\",\"b-book\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\",\"aka\":[\"STP\",\"Agency model\"]},{\"id\":\"ecn\",\"term\":\"ECN\",\"definition\":\"Electronic communication network: a venue where many participants' orders meet in one book.\",\"plain\":\"A shared electronic book of many traders' orders.\",\"related\":[\"clob\",\"stp\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\",\"aka\":[\"Electronic communication network\"]},{\"id\":\"last-look\",\"term\":\"Last look\",\"definition\":\"A short window in which a liquidity provider may reject a trade request at its quoted price.\",\"plain\":\"The dealer gets one last moment to say no.\",\"related\":[\"requote\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"requote\",\"term\":\"Requote\",\"definition\":\"A dealer declining the price you requested and offering another one.\",\"plain\":\"The broker says the price has changed and offers a new one.\",\"related\":[\"last-look\",\"dealer-market\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"nbbo\",\"term\":\"NBBO\",\"definition\":\"The national best bid and offer: the highest bid and the lowest offer across all US exchanges for a stock.\",\"plain\":\"The best buy and sell prices available anywhere in the US.\",\"related\":[\"sip\",\"bid\",\"ask\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\",\"aka\":[\"National best bid and offer\"]},{\"id\":\"sip\",\"term\":\"SIP (consolidated feed)\",\"definition\":\"The securities information processor: the public US feed that combines quotes and trades from all exchanges and computes the NBBO.\",\"plain\":\"The official feed that puts every exchange's prices together.\",\"related\":[\"nbbo\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\",\"aka\":[\"SIP\",\"Consolidated feed\"]},{\"id\":\"best-execution\",\"term\":\"Best execution\",\"definition\":\"A broker's duty to seek the most favourable terms reasonably available for each client order, and to review its routing regularly.\",\"plain\":\"The broker must make a real effort to get you a good fill.\",\"related\":[\"price-improvement\",\"payment-for-order-flow\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\"},{\"id\":\"price-improvement\",\"term\":\"Price improvement\",\"definition\":\"A fill at a better price than the best quote shown when the order arrived, such as below the NBBO ask for a buy.\",\"plain\":\"You got a slightly better price than the screen showed.\",\"related\":[\"nbbo\",\"wholesaler\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\"},{\"id\":\"wholesaler\",\"term\":\"Wholesaler\",\"definition\":\"A firm that fills large volumes of retail orders against its own book, often paying brokers for the flow.\",\"plain\":\"A big firm that buys brokers' order flow and trades against it.\",\"related\":[\"internalisation\",\"payment-for-order-flow\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.7\",\"aka\":[\"Retail market maker\"]},{\"id\":\"call-auction\",\"term\":\"Call auction\",\"definition\":\"A way of trading in which orders collect without matching, then all execute at one price that maximises the volume traded.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone's orders are gathered, then filled at one price.\",\"related\":[\"uncross\",\"opening-auction\",\"closing-auction\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\"},{\"id\":\"uncross\",\"term\":\"Uncross\",\"definition\":\"The moment a call auction matches its orders at the single clearing price; also that price.\",\"plain\":\"The moment the auction actually trades.\",\"related\":[\"call-auction\",\"indicative-price\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\",\"aka\":[\"Auction cross\"]},{\"id\":\"indicative-price\",\"term\":\"Indicative price\",\"definition\":\"The price at which an auction would clear if it ended now, published during the call period.\",\"plain\":\"The auction's price so far.\",\"related\":[\"paired-volume\",\"auction-imbalance\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\"},{\"id\":\"paired-volume\",\"term\":\"Paired volume\",\"definition\":\"The quantity that would match at the current indicative auction price.\",\"plain\":\"How much would trade if the auction ended now.\",\"related\":[\"indicative-price\",\"auction-imbalance\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\"},{\"id\":\"auction-imbalance\",\"term\":\"Auction imbalance\",\"definition\":\"Buy or sell quantity left unmatched at the indicative auction price, published before the cross.\",\"plain\":\"Orders on one side with no partner yet.\",\"related\":[\"indicative-price\",\"paired-volume\",\"call-auction\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\",\"aka\":[\"Order imbalance\"]},{\"id\":\"market-on-close\",\"term\":\"Market-on-close (MOC) order\",\"definition\":\"An order to trade only in the closing auction, at whatever price it sets; market-on-open (MOO) is the opening version.\",\"plain\":\"Buy or sell at the official closing price, whatever it turns out to be.\",\"related\":[\"closing-auction\",\"call-auction\"],\"lesson\":\"3.2.8\",\"aka\":[\"MOC\",\"MOO\"]},{\"id\":\"time-and-sales\",\"term\":\"Time and sales\",\"definition\":\"A running list of trades with their time, price and size, and often the side that initiated each one.\",\"plain\":\"The market's receipt roll: every trade as it happens.\",\"related\":[\"print\",\"aggressor\",\"tick-rule\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\",\"aka\":[\"T&S\",\"the tape\"]},{\"id\":\"aggressor\",\"term\":\"Aggressor\",\"definition\":\"The side that crossed the spread to trade: a buyer paying the ask or a seller hitting the bid. The other side was resting in the book.\",\"plain\":\"The trader who was in a hurry and took the price on offer.\",\"related\":[\"time-and-sales\",\"bid\",\"ask\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"print\",\"term\":\"Print\",\"definition\":\"One trade as reported on the tape, with its time, price and size.\",\"plain\":\"A single trade on the time-and-sales list.\",\"related\":[\"time-and-sales\",\"aggressor\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"tick-rule\",\"term\":\"Tick rule\",\"definition\":\"Guessing a trade's side from the price change: an uptick counts as a buy, a downtick as a sell, and an unchanged price repeats the last direction.\",\"plain\":\"If the price went up, assume a buyer started the trade. It's often wrong.\",\"related\":[\"aggressor\",\"time-and-sales\",\"tick\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"dom\",\"term\":\"DOM (depth of market)\",\"definition\":\"A price ladder showing resting size at each price, recent trades and your own working orders, updated as orders are added and cancelled.\",\"plain\":\"A live ladder of who is waiting to buy and sell at each price.\",\"related\":[\"order-book\",\"market-depth\",\"pulling\",\"stacking\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\",\"aka\":[\"Depth of market\",\"price ladder\"]},{\"id\":\"pulling\",\"term\":\"Pulling\",\"definition\":\"Resting orders cancelled as price approaches them, before they trade.\",\"plain\":\"Size on the ladder that vanishes just before price gets there.\",\"related\":[\"dom\",\"stacking\",\"spoofing\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\"},{\"id\":\"stacking\",\"term\":\"Stacking\",\"definition\":\"Resting size added at a price level, usually before it trades.\",\"plain\":\"More and more orders piling up at one price.\",\"related\":[\"dom\",\"pulling\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\"},{\"id\":\"reloading\",\"term\":\"Reloading\",\"definition\":\"The same size reappearing at a level after it trades, often the trace of an iceberg order.\",\"plain\":\"A level that fills, then shows the same size again, and again.\",\"related\":[\"dom\",\"iceberg-refill\",\"absorption\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\"},{\"id\":\"liquidity-heatmap\",\"term\":\"Liquidity heatmap\",\"definition\":\"A chart that colours resting order size by price and time, so displayed size and its changes show as bands.\",\"plain\":\"A picture of the order book over time: bright where many orders wait.\",\"related\":[\"dom\",\"market-depth\",\"pulling\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.2\"},{\"id\":\"footprint\",\"term\":\"Footprint\",\"definition\":\"A bar split by price that shows, at each price, the volume traded at the bid and at the ask.\",\"plain\":\"A candle opened up to show who traded at each price inside it.\",\"related\":[\"bar-delta\",\"diagonal-imbalance\",\"point-of-control\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\",\"aka\":[\"Footprint chart\"]},{\"id\":\"bar-delta\",\"term\":\"Bar delta\",\"definition\":\"Aggressive buy volume minus aggressive sell volume within one bar: ask volume minus bid volume.\",\"plain\":\"How much more was bought than sold by traders in a hurry, in one bar.\",\"related\":[\"footprint\",\"volume-delta\",\"cvd\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"diagonal-imbalance\",\"term\":\"Diagonal imbalance\",\"definition\":\"Ask volume at one price several times the bid volume one tick lower (a buy imbalance), or bid volume several times the ask volume one tick higher (a sell imbalance), often at 3:1.\",\"plain\":\"A spot in the footprint where one side traded far more than the other.\",\"related\":[\"footprint\",\"stacked-imbalance\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"stacked-imbalance\",\"term\":\"Stacked imbalance\",\"definition\":\"Three or more same-side diagonal imbalances at consecutive prices in one footprint bar.\",\"plain\":\"Several imbalances in a row, all on the same side.\",\"related\":[\"diagonal-imbalance\",\"footprint\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"unfinished-auction\",\"term\":\"Unfinished auction\",\"definition\":\"A bar extreme with trading on both the bid and the ask, which practitioners claim price tends to revisit. The claim is untested.\",\"plain\":\"A high or low where both sides were still trading, as if the auction didn't finish there.\",\"related\":[\"footprint\",\"exhaustion-print\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"exhaustion-print\",\"term\":\"Exhaustion print\",\"definition\":\"Very thin one-sided volume at a bar's extreme, read by practitioners as the aggressors running out.\",\"plain\":\"Almost nothing traded at the very top or bottom of a bar.\",\"related\":[\"footprint\",\"unfinished-auction\",\"exhaustion\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"volume-delta\",\"term\":\"Volume delta\",\"definition\":\"Aggressive buy volume minus aggressive sell volume over a period, from trades classified by their aggressor.\",\"plain\":\"Buying in a hurry minus selling in a hurry.\",\"related\":[\"bar-delta\",\"cvd\",\"aggressor\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.4\",\"aka\":[\"Delta\"]},{\"id\":\"cvd\",\"term\":\"Cumulative volume delta (CVD)\",\"definition\":\"The running total of volume delta from a chosen starting point, the anchor. Its level depends on the anchor; only its changes compare.\",\"plain\":\"Delta added up over time, from a start you pick.\",\"related\":[\"volume-delta\",\"bar-delta\",\"obv\",\"delta-divergence\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.4\",\"aka\":[\"CVD\",\"Cumulative delta\"]},{\"id\":\"delta-divergence\",\"term\":\"Delta divergence\",\"definition\":\"Price makes a new high or low while cumulative delta does not. It flags a move with less aggression behind it, not a reversal.\",\"plain\":\"Price goes further, but the buying (or selling) behind it doesn't.\",\"related\":[\"cvd\",\"divergence\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.4\"},{\"id\":\"absorption\",\"term\":\"Absorption\",\"definition\":\"Heavy aggressive trading at a level that doesn't move price, because passive orders take all of it.\",\"plain\":\"Lots of selling (or buying) hits one price and price doesn't budge.\",\"related\":[\"effort-vs-result\",\"reloading\",\"iceberg-refill\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"effort-vs-result\",\"term\":\"Effort vs result\",\"definition\":\"Wyckoff's comparison of volume, the effort, with price progress, the result. Big effort with little result suggests size on the other side.\",\"plain\":\"A lot of trading that gets price nowhere is a clue.\",\"related\":[\"absorption\",\"volume\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"iceberg-refill\",\"term\":\"Iceberg refill\",\"definition\":\"The visible slice of an iceberg order reappearing after it trades, drawn from the hidden reserve and usually placed at the back of the queue.\",\"plain\":\"The small visible part of a big hidden order coming back after each fill.\",\"related\":[\"reserve-quantity\",\"reloading\",\"queue-priority\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.6\",\"aka\":[\"Refill\"]},{\"id\":\"reserve-quantity\",\"term\":\"Reserve quantity\",\"definition\":\"The hidden part of an iceberg order still waiting to be shown. From outside, only a minimum can be estimated: traded minus displayed.\",\"plain\":\"How much of a hidden order is still left. You can't see it.\",\"related\":[\"iceberg-refill\",\"reloading\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.6\"},{\"id\":\"spoofing\",\"term\":\"Spoofing\",\"definition\":\"Bidding or offering with the intent to cancel before execution, to mislead other traders about supply or demand. Illegal in US futures and treated as market manipulation in the EU.\",\"plain\":\"Placing fake orders you never mean to fill. It's illegal.\",\"related\":[\"layering\",\"cancel-to-fill-ratio\",\"market-abuse\",\"pulling\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"layering\",\"term\":\"Layering\",\"definition\":\"Orders at several prices on one side of the book. Genuine layers are legal; layers meant to be cancelled are a form of illegal spoofing.\",\"plain\":\"Orders stacked at several prices. Legal if real, illegal if fake.\",\"related\":[\"spoofing\",\"stacking\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"cancel-to-fill-ratio\",\"term\":\"Cancel-to-fill ratio\",\"definition\":\"Orders cancelled divided by orders filled. Market makers run high ratios too, so a high value alone doesn't prove manipulation.\",\"plain\":\"How many orders someone cancels for each one that trades.\",\"related\":[\"spoofing\",\"market-maker\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"market-abuse\",\"term\":\"Market abuse\",\"definition\":\"Illegal conduct that distorts prices or exploits inside information, such as spoofing, manipulation or insider dealing.\",\"plain\":\"Cheating the market: fake orders, rigged prices or trading on secrets.\",\"related\":[\"spoofing\",\"regulator\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"tpo\",\"term\":\"TPO\",\"definition\":\"Time-price opportunity: one letter in a Market Profile for each 30-minute period that traded at a price.\",\"plain\":\"A letter marking that price traded during one half-hour.\",\"related\":[\"initial-balance\",\"single-prints\",\"value-area\",\"point-of-control\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\",\"aka\":[\"Time-price opportunity\"]},{\"id\":\"initial-balance\",\"term\":\"Initial balance\",\"definition\":\"The price range of the first hour of the regular session, the first two 30-minute periods in Market Profile.\",\"plain\":\"The high and low of the first hour of trading.\",\"related\":[\"tpo\",\"opening-range\",\"day-type\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\",\"aka\":[\"IB\"]},{\"id\":\"single-prints\",\"term\":\"Single prints\",\"definition\":\"Prices inside a Market Profile traded in only one 30-minute period, the trace of a fast move.\",\"plain\":\"Prices the market passed through only once, quickly.\",\"related\":[\"tpo\",\"day-type\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\"},{\"id\":\"day-type\",\"term\":\"Day type\",\"definition\":\"A Market Profile label for a session's shape, such as normal, trend, double distribution or neutral, assigned partly by eye.\",\"plain\":\"A name for what kind of day it was, judged at the close.\",\"related\":[\"initial-balance\",\"tpo\",\"single-prints\"],\"lesson\":\"3.3.8\"},{\"id\":\"falsifiable\",\"term\":\"Falsifiable\",\"definition\":\"A claim is falsifiable if some possible result would show it is wrong. Claims with escape clauses, such as relabelling after the fact, are not.\",\"plain\":\"There's a result that would prove it wrong, and you can say which one in advance.\",\"related\":[\"pre-registration\",\"base-rate\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"pre-registration\",\"term\":\"Pre-registration\",\"definition\":\"Writing a test's rules, parameters, sample size and comparison before running it, so the results can't steer them.\",\"plain\":\"Deciding exactly how you'll test something before you see any result.\",\"related\":[\"falsifiable\",\"backtest\",\"cherry-picking\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-projection\",\"term\":\"Fibonacci projection\",\"definition\":\"A target from three points: C plus a ratio times the move from A to B, for example C + 1.618 × (B − A).\",\"plain\":\"Measure the first move, multiply it by a ratio, and add it to the end of the pullback.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-extension\",\"measured-move\",\"fibonacci-cluster\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-cluster\",\"term\":\"Fibonacci cluster\",\"definition\":\"A zone where Fibonacci levels drawn from different swings overlap. With enough swings, clusters always exist.\",\"plain\":\"Several Fibonacci grids agreeing near one price, which is easy when you draw many grids.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-retracement\",\"confluence\",\"fibonacci-projection\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-time-zones\",\"term\":\"Fibonacci time zones\",\"definition\":\"Vertical lines at Fibonacci-numbered bar counts from a turn (1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21…), claimed to mark turning times.\",\"plain\":\"Lines in time instead of price, spaced by Fibonacci numbers.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-retracement\",\"fibonacci-cluster\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"impulse-wave\",\"term\":\"Impulse wave\",\"definition\":\"In Elliott Wave, a five-wave move in the direction of the trend, labelled 1 to 5, that must obey three hard rules.\",\"plain\":\"The five-step move with the trend.\",\"related\":[\"corrective-wave\",\"wave-degree\",\"trend\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"corrective-wave\",\"term\":\"Corrective wave\",\"definition\":\"In Elliott Wave, a move against the trend, usually in three waves labelled A-B-C: a zigzag, flat, triangle or combination.\",\"plain\":\"The pullback after an impulse, in three steps.\",\"related\":[\"impulse-wave\",\"wave-degree\",\"retracement\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"wave-degree\",\"term\":\"Wave degree\",\"definition\":\"The size class of an Elliott wave, from Grand Supercycle down to Subminuette, defined by its form rather than its duration.\",\"plain\":\"Waves sit inside bigger waves; the degree says which size you mean.\",\"related\":[\"impulse-wave\",\"corrective-wave\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"diagonal-triangle\",\"term\":\"Diagonal\",\"definition\":\"An Elliott impulse shaped like a wedge, in which wave 4 may overlap wave 1: the one exception to the overlap rule.\",\"plain\":\"A wedge-shaped five-wave move where the usual no-overlap rule is relaxed.\",\"related\":[\"impulse-wave\",\"wedge\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.3\",\"aka\":[\"Diagonal triangle\",\"Ending diagonal\"]},{\"id\":\"alternate-count\",\"term\":\"Alternate count\",\"definition\":\"Another valid Elliott labelling of the same chart, often with the opposite forecast.\",\"plain\":\"A second or third way to count the same waves that also follows the rules.\",\"related\":[\"impulse-wave\",\"corrective-wave\",\"invalidation\",\"falsifiable\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.4\"},{\"id\":\"composite-man\",\"term\":\"Composite Man\",\"definition\":\"Wyckoff's study device: imagine one large operator behind all the buying and selling in a range. A heuristic, not a fact about manipulation.\",\"plain\":\"An imaginary big player you picture to make sense of a chart.\",\"related\":[\"spring\",\"upthrust\",\"selling-climax\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"selling-climax\",\"term\":\"Selling climax\",\"definition\":\"A Wyckoff event: a sharp drop on very heavy volume that may end a downtrend and sets the low of a range.\",\"plain\":\"The panicky low that starts a Wyckoff range.\",\"related\":[\"climax\",\"sign-of-strength\",\"spring\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"spring\",\"term\":\"Spring\",\"definition\":\"A Wyckoff event: a dip below a range's low that closes back inside the range. Usually named only after the rally that follows.\",\"plain\":\"A break of the range low that fails, called a spring if price then rises.\",\"related\":[\"upthrust\",\"false-breakout\",\"selling-climax\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"upthrust\",\"term\":\"Upthrust\",\"definition\":\"A Wyckoff event: a push above a range's high that closes back inside the range. The mirror image of a spring.\",\"plain\":\"A poke above the range high that fails and falls back in.\",\"related\":[\"spring\",\"false-breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"sign-of-strength\",\"term\":\"Sign of strength\",\"definition\":\"A Wyckoff event: a strong rally on rising volume out of an accumulation range.\",\"plain\":\"The rally that's supposed to show buyers have taken control.\",\"related\":[\"selling-climax\",\"spring\",\"breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.5\",\"aka\":[\"SOS\"]},{\"id\":\"xabcd\",\"term\":\"XABCD\",\"definition\":\"The five labelled turning points of a harmonic pattern; its four legs must meet set Fibonacci ratios.\",\"plain\":\"The five points used to draw a harmonic pattern.\",\"related\":[\"gartley\",\"potential-reversal-zone\",\"fibonacci-retracement\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.6\"},{\"id\":\"potential-reversal-zone\",\"term\":\"Potential reversal zone\",\"definition\":\"The price area around point D where a harmonic pattern claims price will turn, often where several ratios overlap.\",\"plain\":\"The zone where a harmonic pattern says the turn should happen.\",\"related\":[\"xabcd\",\"gartley\",\"fibonacci-cluster\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.6\",\"aka\":[\"PRZ\"]},{\"id\":\"gartley\",\"term\":\"Gartley pattern\",\"definition\":\"A harmonic pattern with B at 0.618 and D at 0.786 of the XA leg, and CD 1.27 to 1.618 times BC.\",\"plain\":\"The best-known harmonic pattern, defined by fixed ratios between its legs.\",\"related\":[\"xabcd\",\"potential-reversal-zone\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.6\"},{\"id\":\"ichimoku-system\",\"term\":\"Ichimoku system\",\"definition\":\"The full set of Ichimoku rules (cloud filter, Tenkan-Kijun trigger, Chikou confirmation, Kijun stop and exit) tested together as one system.\",\"plain\":\"All the Ichimoku rules written out so they can be backtested as one.\",\"related\":[\"ichimoku-cloud\",\"donchian-channel\",\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.7\"},{\"id\":\"parameter-grid\",\"term\":\"Parameter grid\",\"definition\":\"Results of one rule tested across nearby parameter settings, to see whether the best setting sits on a plateau or a lucky spike.\",\"plain\":\"A table of results for the settings around the one you use.\",\"related\":[\"backtest\",\"out-of-sample\",\"ichimoku-system\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.7\"},{\"id\":\"gann-angle\",\"term\":\"Gann angle\",\"definition\":\"A line rising one price unit per time unit (the 1×1) or another ratio. Its slope on screen depends on the chart's scale.\",\"plain\":\"Gann's diagonal lines; stretch the chart and a different one looks like 45°.\",\"related\":[\"trendline\",\"square-of-nine\",\"linear-scale\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"square-of-nine\",\"term\":\"Square of Nine\",\"definition\":\"Gann's spiral table of numbers, claimed to give price and time targets, with no testable mechanism stated.\",\"plain\":\"A number spiral used to pick targets, without evidence that it works.\",\"related\":[\"gann-angle\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"spectral-analysis\",\"term\":\"Spectral analysis\",\"definition\":\"A method that splits a series into cycles of different lengths and reports their strength. It finds peaks even in pure noise.\",\"plain\":\"A tool that looks for repeating cycles; it always finds some, even in random data.\",\"related\":[\"random-walk\",\"synthetic-data\"],\"lesson\":\"3.4.8\"},{\"id\":\"smc\",\"term\":\"SMC (smart money concepts)\",\"definition\":\"A retail trading vocabulary built on a story of institutions hunting liquidity. It renames stop clustering, Dow structure, supply and demand zones and session timing, with no official rulebook.\",\"plain\":\"A popular set of trading words that tells a story about big players. Most of the words are old ideas with new names.\",\"related\":[\"ict\",\"intent-attribution\",\"order-block\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Smart money concepts\"]},{\"id\":\"ict\",\"term\":\"ICT\",\"definition\":\"\\\"Inner Circle Trader\\\", the online mentorship run by Michael J. Huddleston whose terms SMC repackaged.\",\"plain\":\"The online teacher and course where most SMC words started.\",\"related\":[\"smc\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Inner Circle Trader\"]},{\"id\":\"intent-attribution\",\"term\":\"Intent attribution\",\"definition\":\"Explaining a price move by what \\\"they\\\" wanted, which no chart can show. Because a story can be told for any outcome, it predicts nothing.\",\"plain\":\"Saying the market moved because someone planned it, without any way to check.\",\"related\":[\"smc\",\"hindsight-bias\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.1\"},{\"id\":\"bos\",\"term\":\"BOS (break of structure)\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a with-trend break of the latest swing: a new high in an uptrend or a new low in a downtrend, Dow's continuation.\",\"plain\":\"The trend makes its next new high, or new low, as expected.\",\"related\":[\"choch\",\"market-structure\",\"dow-theory\",\"swing-high\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"Break of structure\"]},{\"id\":\"choch\",\"term\":\"CHoCH (change of character)\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for the first break against the trend of its last swing, such as a close under the last higher low of an uptrend. Classically, a structure break.\",\"plain\":\"The first sign that the trend's pattern of swings has broken.\",\"related\":[\"bos\",\"mss\",\"structure-break\",\"swing-low\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"Change of character\"]},{\"id\":\"mss\",\"term\":\"MSS (market structure shift)\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a failed breakout followed by a sharp reversal, close to the classical 1-2-3 reversal. Some authors use it for any CHoCH.\",\"plain\":\"Price breaks out, fails, and turns hard the other way.\",\"related\":[\"choch\",\"failed-breakout\",\"displacement\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"Market structure shift\"]},{\"id\":\"displacement\",\"term\":\"Displacement\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a fast run of large candles in one direction, with no fixed size threshold. Classically, momentum: trend bars or a breakout spike.\",\"plain\":\"A burst of big candles all going the same way.\",\"related\":[\"momentum\",\"trend-bar\",\"spike-and-channel\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.2\"},{\"id\":\"buy-side-liquidity\",\"term\":\"Buy-side liquidity\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for buy stops resting above swing highs: shorts' stop-losses and breakout traders' buy stops.\",\"plain\":\"The buy orders waiting just above old highs.\",\"related\":[\"sell-side-liquidity\",\"liquidity-pool\",\"stop-cluster\",\"swing-high\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"BSL\"]},{\"id\":\"sell-side-liquidity\",\"term\":\"Sell-side liquidity\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for sell stops resting below swing lows: longs' stop-losses and breakout traders' sell stops.\",\"plain\":\"The sell orders waiting just below old lows.\",\"related\":[\"buy-side-liquidity\",\"liquidity-pool\",\"stop-cluster\",\"swing-low\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"SSL\"]},{\"id\":\"inducement\",\"term\":\"Inducement\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a small swing said to be bait before the real move. Classically a shake-out of trapped traders; the bait is intent nobody can prove.\",\"plain\":\"A small move said to be a trap set on purpose. The move is visible; the purpose isn't.\",\"related\":[\"trapped-trader\",\"intent-attribution\",\"false-breakout\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"IDM\"]},{\"id\":\"judas-swing\",\"term\":\"Judas swing\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for an early-session false move against the day's later direction: classically, a failed break of the opening range.\",\"plain\":\"The day starts one way, then goes the other. You only know which was which afterwards.\",\"related\":[\"opening-range\",\"failed-breakout\",\"inducement\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\"},{\"id\":\"swing-failure-pattern\",\"term\":\"Swing failure pattern\",\"definition\":\"Price trades beyond a prior swing extreme, then closes back inside. The classical 2B pattern.\",\"plain\":\"Price pokes past an old high or low and closes back on the near side.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity-sweep\",\"failed-breakout\",\"turtle-soup\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"SFP\",\"2B\"]},{\"id\":\"draw-on-liquidity\",\"term\":\"Draw on liquidity\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for the high or low that price is expected to reach next. Classically, a target at a prior extreme, not a force acting on price.\",\"plain\":\"The old high or low a trader expects price to go to next.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity-pool\",\"buy-side-liquidity\",\"take-profit\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"DOL\"]},{\"id\":\"order-block\",\"term\":\"Order block\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for the last opposite candle before a strong move, used as a zone: a renamed supply or demand base.\",\"plain\":\"The last down candle before a big rally, or the last up candle before a big drop, marked as a zone.\",\"related\":[\"demand-zone\",\"supply-zone\",\"breaker-block\",\"mitigation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\",\"aka\":[\"OB\"]},{\"id\":\"breaker-block\",\"term\":\"Breaker block\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a failed order block that flips its role: classic role reversal.\",\"plain\":\"A zone that broke and is now expected to work the other way.\",\"related\":[\"order-block\",\"role-reversal\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"mitigation\",\"term\":\"Mitigation\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for price returning to an earlier zone, said to let institutions finish their orders. Classically, a retracement into a prior trading area.\",\"plain\":\"Price coming back to an old zone. The story about why can't be checked.\",\"related\":[\"order-block\",\"retracement\",\"intent-attribution\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"consequent-encroachment\",\"term\":\"Consequent encroachment\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for the 50% point of a gap or a wick.\",\"plain\":\"The middle of a gap.\",\"related\":[\"fair-value-gap\",\"window\",\"premium-discount\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\",\"aka\":[\"CE\"]},{\"id\":\"premium-discount\",\"term\":\"Premium and discount\",\"definition\":\"SMC terms for the upper and lower halves of a range, split at 50%: buy in discount, sell in premium. The old 50% retracement rule.\",\"plain\":\"The top half of a range is \\\"expensive\\\", the bottom half \\\"cheap\\\".\",\"related\":[\"dealing-range\",\"optimal-trade-entry\",\"fibonacci-retracement\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"optimal-trade-entry\",\"term\":\"Optimal trade entry (OTE)\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a 62–79% retracement zone with a 70.5% \\\"sweet spot\\\", close to Fibonacci 61.8–78.6%.\",\"plain\":\"A deep pullback zone, the same area as the Fibonacci 61.8% to 78.6% levels.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-retracement\",\"premium-discount\",\"dealing-range\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\",\"aka\":[\"OTE\"]},{\"id\":\"dealing-range\",\"term\":\"Dealing range\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for the swing range used to measure premium and discount: classically, the trading range or the last impulse leg. Which swing to use is a choice.\",\"plain\":\"The high and low a trader picks to split into a top and bottom half.\",\"related\":[\"premium-discount\",\"range\",\"leg\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"pd-array\",\"term\":\"PD array\",\"definition\":\"SMC list of zone types, such as order blocks, gaps and old highs, ranked by importance: a catalogue of support and resistance zones.\",\"plain\":\"A ranked list of the kinds of zones SMC traders mark.\",\"related\":[\"order-block\",\"fair-value-gap\",\"premium-discount\",\"support\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"killzone\",\"term\":\"Killzone\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for a session window, such as the London open, said to give better setups. Its higher volatility is documented; a directional edge is not.\",\"plain\":\"A busy time of day that SMC traders watch. Busier, yes; more predictable, no.\",\"related\":[\"trading-session\",\"session-overlap\",\"silver-bullet\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"silver-bullet\",\"term\":\"Silver Bullet\",\"definition\":\"An SMC setup: a gap entry after a sweep, inside a fixed one-hour window. No published test exists.\",\"plain\":\"An SMC trade allowed only during one hour of the day.\",\"related\":[\"killzone\",\"window\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"power-of-three\",\"term\":\"Power of three (AMD)\",\"definition\":\"SMC claim that each bar runs accumulation, manipulation, then distribution. Any closed bar with a wick against its close can be narrated this way.\",\"plain\":\"A three-act story told about every candle after it closes.\",\"related\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"candle\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\",\"aka\":[\"PO3\",\"AMD\"]},{\"id\":\"ipda\",\"term\":\"IPDA\",\"definition\":\"SMC claim of an \\\"interbank price delivery algorithm\\\" steering price between liquidity and gaps. There is no evidence it exists, and as stated no result could disprove it.\",\"plain\":\"A claimed secret algorithm that moves prices. Nobody has shown it exists.\",\"related\":[\"smc\",\"intent-attribution\",\"market-maker\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"smt-divergence\",\"term\":\"SMT divergence\",\"definition\":\"SMC term for two correlated markets disagreeing at a swing high or low: Dow's confirmation principle.\",\"plain\":\"One market makes a new high and its usual partner doesn't.\",\"related\":[\"dow-theory\",\"divergence\",\"correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\",\"aka\":[\"SMT\"]},{\"id\":\"asian-range\",\"term\":\"Asian range\",\"definition\":\"The high-low range of the Asian session, used as a reference later in the day: classically, the overnight range.\",\"plain\":\"How high and low price went during the quiet Asian hours.\",\"related\":[\"trading-session\",\"opening-range\",\"killzone\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.5\",\"aka\":[\"CBDR\"]},{\"id\":\"testable-hypothesis\",\"term\":\"Testable hypothesis\",\"definition\":\"A claim written as an exact event, an outcome over a fixed horizon and a baseline, so data can reject it.\",\"plain\":\"A claim phrased so that a test could prove it wrong.\",\"related\":[\"backtest\",\"out-of-sample\",\"base-rate\",\"random-walk\"],\"lesson\":\"3.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"yield\",\"term\":\"Yield\",\"definition\":\"The annual return a bond pays if bought at today's price and held to maturity. For a zero-coupon bond it is the y in price = 100 ÷ (1 + y)^n, so it moves opposite to the price.\",\"plain\":\"The yearly return a bond pays at its current price. When the price falls, the yield rises.\",\"related\":[\"zero-coupon-bond\",\"real-yield\",\"bond\",\"basis-point\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\",\"aka\":[\"Bond yield\",\"Yield to maturity\"]},{\"id\":\"real-yield\",\"term\":\"Real yield\",\"definition\":\"A nominal yield minus the inflation expected over the same horizon: what lending earns after inflation.\",\"plain\":\"What a bond pays after taking off expected inflation.\",\"related\":[\"yield\",\"breakeven-inflation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"breakeven-inflation\",\"term\":\"Breakeven inflation\",\"definition\":\"The inflation rate priced by markets: the yield on a nominal government bond minus the yield on an inflation-protected bond of the same maturity.\",\"plain\":\"The inflation rate the bond market is expecting.\",\"related\":[\"real-yield\",\"yield\",\"cpi\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"zero-coupon-bond\",\"term\":\"Zero-coupon bond\",\"definition\":\"A bond that pays no coupons, only its face value at maturity. Its price is the face value discounted at its yield: 100 ÷ (1 + y)^n.\",\"plain\":\"A bond that pays 100 at the end and nothing before, so it costs less than 100 today.\",\"related\":[\"yield\",\"bond\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\",\"aka\":[\"Zero\"]},{\"id\":\"rate-differential\",\"term\":\"Rate differential\",\"definition\":\"The difference between two currencies' interest rates. Changes in it, measured against what was expected, are a main driver of exchange rates.\",\"plain\":\"How much more one currency's interest rate pays than another's.\",\"related\":[\"carry-trade\",\"yield\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.1\",\"aka\":[\"Interest rate differential\"]},{\"id\":\"yield-curve\",\"term\":\"Yield curve\",\"definition\":\"Government bond yields plotted by maturity, from months to decades. Its slope reflects expected policy rates, growth, inflation and the term premium.\",\"plain\":\"A line of government bond yields from short to long maturities.\",\"related\":[\"inversion\",\"steepener\",\"flattener\",\"term-premium\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"inversion\",\"term\":\"Curve inversion\",\"definition\":\"A yield curve with short-term yields above long-term ones, such as a negative 10y–2y spread. Historically a long-lead recession warning, with a variable lag and false alarms.\",\"plain\":\"When short-term bonds pay more than long-term ones: a classic recession warning, but not a timer.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve\",\"flattener\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\",\"aka\":[\"Inverted yield curve\"]},{\"id\":\"steepener\",\"term\":\"Steepener\",\"definition\":\"A curve move that widens the gap between long and short yields: a bull steepener when short yields fall more, a bear steepener when long yields rise more.\",\"plain\":\"When long-term yields rise relative to short-term ones.\",\"related\":[\"flattener\",\"yield-curve\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"flattener\",\"term\":\"Flattener\",\"definition\":\"A curve move that narrows the gap between long and short yields: a bear flattener when short yields rise more, a bull flattener when long yields fall more.\",\"plain\":\"When the gap between long and short yields shrinks.\",\"related\":[\"steepener\",\"yield-curve\",\"inversion\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"term-premium\",\"term\":\"Term premium\",\"definition\":\"The extra yield investors demand to hold a long bond instead of rolling short ones. Bond supply and central-bank balance sheets can move it.\",\"plain\":\"Extra pay for locking money into a long bond.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve\",\"yield\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"disinflation\",\"term\":\"Disinflation\",\"definition\":\"A fall in the rate of inflation while it stays positive: prices still rise, but more slowly.\",\"plain\":\"Inflation that is still positive but falling.\",\"related\":[\"cpi\",\"policy-regime\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"policy-regime\",\"term\":\"Policy regime\",\"definition\":\"The central bank's current stance, and the market's reading of it, such as fighting inflation or supporting growth. It decides how assets react to the same data.\",\"plain\":\"What the central bank is mainly trying to do right now.\",\"related\":[\"hawkish\",\"dovish\",\"central-bank\",\"disinflation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"dollar-index\",\"term\":\"Dollar index\",\"definition\":\"An index of the US dollar against a weighted basket of major currencies. In this lab, LDX tracks vUSD the same way.\",\"plain\":\"One number for the dollar's value against several big currencies.\",\"related\":[\"dollar-smile\",\"safe-haven\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\",\"aka\":[\"LDX\"]},{\"id\":\"dollar-smile\",\"term\":\"Dollar smile\",\"definition\":\"A framework in which the dollar tends to rise in US booms and in global crises and to fall in synchronised global growth. A tendency with known exceptions, such as carry unwinds.\",\"plain\":\"The idea that the dollar rises in US booms and in global crises, and weakens in between.\",\"related\":[\"dollar-index\",\"safe-haven\",\"funding-currency\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"safe-haven\",\"term\":\"Safe haven\",\"definition\":\"An asset that tends to hold its value or rise when risky assets fall. Which assets play the role changes with the regime.\",\"plain\":\"Where investors put money when markets get scary.\",\"related\":[\"risk-off\",\"dollar-smile\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\",\"aka\":[\"Haven\"]},{\"id\":\"funding-currency\",\"term\":\"Funding currency\",\"definition\":\"A low-interest currency borrowed to finance carry trades. It tends to jump when those trades are unwound.\",\"plain\":\"A cheap currency people borrow to buy higher-paying ones.\",\"related\":[\"carry-trade\",\"carry-unwind\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"terms-of-trade\",\"term\":\"Terms of trade\",\"definition\":\"A country's export prices divided by its import prices. When they rise, national income rises and the currency tends to strengthen.\",\"plain\":\"How much a country's exports buy in imports.\",\"related\":[\"commodity-currency\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"commodity-currency\",\"term\":\"Commodity currency\",\"definition\":\"A currency that tends to move with the prices of its country's main commodity exports, through the terms of trade. Rate differentials often matter more.\",\"plain\":\"A currency that tends to follow the price of what its country sells abroad.\",\"related\":[\"terms-of-trade\",\"risk-on\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"copper-gold-ratio\",\"term\":\"Copper-gold ratio\",\"definition\":\"The copper price divided by the gold price: a practitioner gauge of growth expectations that tends to move with long-term yields.\",\"plain\":\"Copper's price over gold's: a rough gauge of growth.\",\"related\":[\"yield\",\"commodity-currency\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"risk-on\",\"term\":\"Risk-on\",\"definition\":\"A market phase in which investors favour equities, credit and high-yield or commodity currencies, and risky assets tend to rise together.\",\"plain\":\"When investors feel brave and buy riskier assets.\",\"related\":[\"risk-off\",\"carry-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"risk-off\",\"term\":\"Risk-off\",\"definition\":\"A market phase in which investors cut risk, selling equities, credit and high-beta currencies and buying government bonds and safe havens. Correlations among risky assets tend to rise.\",\"plain\":\"When investors get scared and move to safer assets.\",\"related\":[\"risk-on\",\"safe-haven\",\"correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"carry-trade\",\"term\":\"Carry trade\",\"definition\":\"Borrowing a low-interest currency to hold a high-interest one and earn the difference through the swap: small steady gains, with a risk of large, fast losses.\",\"plain\":\"Borrowing cheap money to hold a better-paying currency, and pocketing the gap.\",\"related\":[\"carry\",\"funding-currency\",\"carry-unwind\",\"overnight-financing\",\"negative-skew\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"carry-unwind\",\"term\":\"Carry unwind\",\"definition\":\"Many carry trades closed at once, usually after a jump in volatility or a rally in the funding currency, which drives the funding currency sharply higher.\",\"plain\":\"Everyone exiting carry trades at the same time.\",\"related\":[\"carry-trade\",\"funding-currency\",\"risk-off\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"business-cycle\",\"term\":\"Business cycle\",\"definition\":\"The economy's recurring but irregular sequence of expansion and contraction, often split into early, mid, late and recession phases. Phases are dated reliably only afterwards.\",\"plain\":\"The economy's ups and downs over several years.\",\"related\":[\"sector-rotation\",\"credit-spread\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\",\"aka\":[\"Economic cycle\"]},{\"id\":\"sector-rotation\",\"term\":\"Sector rotation\",\"definition\":\"Shifting money between industry sectors as the business cycle changes. Its edge shrinks after costs and real-time errors in dating the phase.\",\"plain\":\"Moving between sectors as the economy's phase changes.\",\"related\":[\"business-cycle\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"credit-spread\",\"term\":\"Credit spread\",\"definition\":\"The extra yield a company bond pays over a government bond of similar maturity. It widens when default fears rise.\",\"plain\":\"The extra interest companies pay to borrow, compared with the government.\",\"related\":[\"financial-conditions\",\"yield\",\"business-cycle\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"financial-conditions\",\"term\":\"Financial conditions\",\"definition\":\"How easy it is to borrow and raise money, judged from interest rates, credit spreads, stock prices and the currency together.\",\"plain\":\"How easy money is to get right now.\",\"related\":[\"credit-spread\",\"yield\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.7\"},{\"id\":\"rolling-correlation\",\"term\":\"Rolling correlation\",\"definition\":\"A correlation recomputed over a moving window of the most recent observations. Short windows are noisy; long ones lag.\",\"plain\":\"A correlation recalculated as each new bar arrives, using only recent data.\",\"related\":[\"correlation\",\"correlation-breakdown\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.8\"},{\"id\":\"intermarket-analysis\",\"term\":\"Intermarket analysis\",\"definition\":\"The study of how bonds, stocks, commodities and currencies affect each other. Its relationships hold only within a regime.\",\"plain\":\"Studying how bonds, stocks, commodities and currencies affect each other.\",\"related\":[\"correlation\",\"rolling-correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.8\"},{\"id\":\"correlation-breakdown\",\"term\":\"Correlation breakdown\",\"definition\":\"A familiar relationship between two assets weakening or reversing its sign, usually because the dominant shock or buyer has changed.\",\"plain\":\"When two assets that used to move one way start moving another.\",\"related\":[\"correlation\",\"rolling-correlation\",\"intermarket-analysis\"],\"lesson\":\"3.6.8\"},{\"id\":\"reaction-function\",\"term\":\"Reaction function\",\"definition\":\"How a central bank changes its policy rate in response to inflation, jobs and financial conditions; markets estimate it from past decisions and words.\",\"plain\":\"The way a central bank reacts to the data.\",\"related\":[\"taylor-rule\",\"central-bank\",\"rate-decision\",\"expected-policy-path\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"taylor-rule\",\"term\":\"Taylor rule\",\"definition\":\"A simple formula that sets a policy rate from a neutral rate, inflation, the gap between inflation and its target, and a measure of economic slack. Central banks use such rules as a reference, not a recipe.\",\"plain\":\"A formula that suggests an interest rate from inflation and how busy the economy is.\",\"related\":[\"reaction-function\",\"neutral-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"expected-policy-path\",\"term\":\"Expected policy path\",\"definition\":\"The policy rates markets price for each upcoming meeting, read from rate futures and swaps; short-term yields track roughly its average.\",\"plain\":\"Where the market thinks rates will be after each of the next few meetings.\",\"related\":[\"rate-futures\",\"ois\",\"reaction-function\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.1\"},{\"id\":\"rate-futures\",\"term\":\"Rate futures\",\"definition\":\"Futures on a short-term interest rate, quoted as 100 minus the average rate expected over the contract period.\",\"plain\":\"Futures whose price shows the rate the market expects: 100 minus the rate.\",\"related\":[\"futures\",\"implied-rate\",\"implied-probability\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\"},{\"id\":\"implied-rate\",\"term\":\"Implied rate\",\"definition\":\"The interest rate implied by a rate-futures price: 100 minus the price. For a monthly contract, it's the month's expected average rate.\",\"plain\":\"The rate a futures price points to: 100 minus the price.\",\"related\":[\"rate-futures\",\"implied-probability\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\"},{\"id\":\"implied-probability\",\"term\":\"Implied probability\",\"definition\":\"The probability of an outcome backed out of market prices, such as a rate hike from rate futures. It includes risk premia, so it is a price, not a poll.\",\"plain\":\"The chance of something happening, as priced by the market.\",\"related\":[\"rate-futures\",\"implied-rate\",\"priced-in\",\"probability\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\"},{\"id\":\"ois\",\"term\":\"OIS\",\"definition\":\"Overnight index swap: a contract that exchanges a fixed rate for the average overnight rate over its term. Its fixed rate shows the expected average policy rate.\",\"plain\":\"A swap whose rate shows where the market expects overnight rates to average.\",\"related\":[\"rate-futures\",\"expected-policy-path\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.2\",\"aka\":[\"Overnight index swap\"]},{\"id\":\"policy-statement\",\"term\":\"Policy statement\",\"definition\":\"The written text a central bank releases with its rate decision. Markets compare it, edit by edit, with the previous one.\",\"plain\":\"The short text a central bank publishes with each decision.\",\"related\":[\"rate-decision\",\"hawkish\",\"dovish\",\"minutes\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\",\"aka\":[\"Statement\"]},{\"id\":\"dissent\",\"term\":\"Dissent\",\"definition\":\"A policymaker's vote against the committee's decision. A dissent for a hike or a cut hints at the direction of the next move.\",\"plain\":\"A vote against the majority.\",\"related\":[\"vote-split\",\"rate-decision\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\"},{\"id\":\"vote-split\",\"term\":\"Vote split\",\"definition\":\"How a policy committee's votes divide between options, such as 7–2 for a hold with two votes for a hike.\",\"plain\":\"How the committee voted, counted by option.\",\"related\":[\"dissent\",\"rate-decision\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\"},{\"id\":\"minutes\",\"term\":\"Minutes\",\"definition\":\"A detailed record of a policy meeting, released weeks later. Quantifiers such as 'many' or 'a few participants' show how widely a view was held.\",\"plain\":\"The written record of the meeting, published weeks later.\",\"related\":[\"policy-statement\",\"rate-decision\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.3\"},{\"id\":\"dot-plot\",\"term\":\"Dot plot\",\"definition\":\"A chart of each policymaker's projected policy rate for coming year-ends. Markets watch its median, which a single dot can move.\",\"plain\":\"A chart where each dot is one policymaker's view of future rates.\",\"related\":[\"neutral-rate\",\"forward-guidance\",\"rate-decision\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\"},{\"id\":\"neutral-rate\",\"term\":\"Neutral rate\",\"definition\":\"The policy rate thought neither to speed up nor to slow down the economy. It can't be observed, only estimated.\",\"plain\":\"The interest rate that neither pushes nor brakes the economy.\",\"related\":[\"taylor-rule\",\"dot-plot\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\"},{\"id\":\"forward-guidance\",\"term\":\"Forward guidance\",\"definition\":\"What a central bank says about its likely future policy: by a date, by a threshold in the data or in qualitative words. It is conditional, not a promise.\",\"plain\":\"Hints from the central bank about what it will do next.\",\"related\":[\"dot-plot\",\"press-conference\",\"hawkish\",\"dovish\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\"},{\"id\":\"press-conference\",\"term\":\"Press conference\",\"definition\":\"The live question session after a rate decision, which often moves markets a second time; its headlines can whipsaw prices for 30 to 60 minutes.\",\"plain\":\"The central bank chief's live questions and answers after the decision.\",\"related\":[\"rate-decision\",\"second-move\",\"forward-guidance\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.4\"},{\"id\":\"quantitative-easing\",\"term\":\"Quantitative easing (QE)\",\"definition\":\"A central bank buying bonds at scale to push long-term yields down, mainly by lowering the term premium and by signalling low rates for longer.\",\"plain\":\"A central bank buying lots of bonds to lower long-term interest rates.\",\"related\":[\"quantitative-tightening\",\"bond\",\"yield-curve-control\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"QE\"]},{\"id\":\"quantitative-tightening\",\"term\":\"Quantitative tightening (QT)\",\"definition\":\"A central bank shrinking its bond holdings, usually by letting them mature without replacing them. Its estimated effects are smaller than QE's, and disputed.\",\"plain\":\"A central bank slowly letting its pile of bonds shrink.\",\"related\":[\"quantitative-easing\",\"bond\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"QT\"]},{\"id\":\"yield-curve-control\",\"term\":\"Yield curve control\",\"definition\":\"A central bank capping a long-term yield by buying whatever bonds it must to hold the cap. An exit can release the stored pressure as a gap.\",\"plain\":\"A central bank promising to keep a long-term rate below a cap.\",\"related\":[\"quantitative-easing\",\"currency-peg\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"YCC\"]},{\"id\":\"fx-intervention\",\"term\":\"FX intervention\",\"definition\":\"Authorities buying or selling their own currency to move its price, usually after escalating verbal warnings.\",\"plain\":\"A government or central bank trading its own currency to push its price.\",\"related\":[\"forex\",\"currency-peg\",\"carry\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"Currency intervention\"]},{\"id\":\"currency-peg\",\"term\":\"Currency peg\",\"definition\":\"An official promise to hold an exchange rate at a level, above a floor or within a band. When it ends, the rate can gap far within minutes.\",\"plain\":\"A promise to keep a currency at a fixed level.\",\"related\":[\"fx-intervention\",\"yield-curve-control\",\"gap\",\"tail-event\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.5\",\"aka\":[\"Peg\",\"Currency floor\"]},{\"id\":\"news-straddle\",\"term\":\"News straddle\",\"definition\":\"A buy stop above and a sell stop below the price before a release, often linked as OCO, to catch either direction. A spike and a reversal can fill one side and stop it out.\",\"plain\":\"Orders on both sides of the price before news, to catch whichever way it moves.\",\"related\":[\"oco-order\",\"stop-order\",\"slippage\",\"fade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.6\"},{\"id\":\"pre-announcement-drift\",\"term\":\"Pre-announcement drift\",\"definition\":\"A tendency for prices to move before a scheduled announcement, such as the documented rise in US stocks before scheduled Fed decisions.\",\"plain\":\"Prices moving one way in the run-up to a scheduled announcement.\",\"related\":[\"buy-the-rumour-sell-the-news\",\"sample-size\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.7\",\"aka\":[\"Pre-FOMC drift\"]},{\"id\":\"buy-the-rumour-sell-the-news\",\"term\":\"Buy the rumour, sell the news\",\"definition\":\"A pattern in which prices rise before expected good news, then fall once it is confirmed, as early buyers take profits.\",\"plain\":\"Prices go up while people expect good news, then fall when it arrives.\",\"related\":[\"priced-in\",\"pre-announcement-drift\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.7\"},{\"id\":\"optional-stopping\",\"term\":\"Optional stopping\",\"definition\":\"Ending a test as soon as results look significant, or checking repeatedly and stopping at the first good look. It inflates false findings.\",\"plain\":\"Stopping a test the moment it looks good, which makes luck look like an edge.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"confidence-interval\",\"cherry-picking\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.7\"},{\"id\":\"tail-event\",\"term\":\"Tail event\",\"definition\":\"A rare, extreme move far outside normal ranges, in which liquidity vanishes and stops fill far from their levels.\",\"plain\":\"A rare, huge move that breaks the usual assumptions.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity-vacuum\",\"flash-crash\",\"gap\",\"guaranteed-stop\",\"negative-balance-protection\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.8\"},{\"id\":\"liquidity-vacuum\",\"term\":\"Liquidity vacuum\",\"definition\":\"A moment when buyers or sellers vanish from the book and price jumps to distant resting orders.\",\"plain\":\"When almost nobody is quoting, and price skips to wherever orders are left.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity\",\"market-depth\",\"tail-event\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.8\"},{\"id\":\"flash-crash\",\"term\":\"Flash crash\",\"definition\":\"A very fast, deep price fall that largely reverses within minutes or hours, as liquidity disappears.\",\"plain\":\"A sudden collapse in prices that mostly bounces back the same day.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity-vacuum\",\"tail-event\",\"high-frequency-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"3.7.8\"},{\"id\":\"income-statement\",\"term\":\"Income statement\",\"definition\":\"A financial statement of revenue, costs and profit over a period, ending in net income.\",\"plain\":\"Report of revenue, costs and profit over a period.\",\"related\":[\"balance-sheet\",\"cash-flow-statement\",\"eps\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"aka\":[\"Profit and loss statement\"]},{\"id\":\"balance-sheet\",\"term\":\"Balance sheet\",\"definition\":\"A statement of what a company owns (assets), what it owes (liabilities) and the owners' equity on one date. Assets always equal liabilities plus equity.\",\"plain\":\"Snapshot of what a company owns, what it owes and the owners' equity on one date.\",\"related\":[\"income-statement\",\"cash-flow-statement\",\"net-debt\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\"},{\"id\":\"cash-flow-statement\",\"term\":\"Cash-flow statement\",\"definition\":\"A statement of the cash that came in and went out over a period, split into operating, investing and financing cash flows.\",\"plain\":\"Report of cash coming in and going out from operations, investing and financing.\",\"related\":[\"operating-cash-flow\",\"free-cash-flow\",\"income-statement\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\"},{\"id\":\"operating-cash-flow\",\"term\":\"Operating cash flow\",\"definition\":\"Cash generated by the business itself: net income plus non-cash costs such as depreciation, minus increases in working capital such as receivables and inventory.\",\"plain\":\"Cash produced by the business itself after paying suppliers, staff and taxes.\",\"related\":[\"cash-flow-statement\",\"free-cash-flow\",\"capex\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"aka\":[\"OCF\",\"Cash from operations\"]},{\"id\":\"free-cash-flow\",\"term\":\"Free cash flow\",\"definition\":\"Operating cash flow minus capital spending: the cash a business generates after investing in itself.\",\"plain\":\"Operating cash flow minus spending on equipment: the cash left after investing.\",\"related\":[\"operating-cash-flow\",\"capex\",\"dcf\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"aka\":[\"FCF\"]},{\"id\":\"capex\",\"term\":\"Capex\",\"definition\":\"Capital expenditure: spending on long-lasting assets such as machines and buildings. It leaves cash at once and reaches profit slowly, through depreciation.\",\"plain\":\"Money spent on long-lasting assets such as machines and buildings.\",\"related\":[\"free-cash-flow\",\"operating-cash-flow\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"aka\":[\"Capital expenditure\"]},{\"id\":\"eps\",\"term\":\"EPS\",\"definition\":\"Earnings per share: net income divided by the number of shares outstanding. Diluted EPS also counts shares that options and convertible securities could create.\",\"plain\":\"Earnings per share: net profit divided by the number of shares.\",\"related\":[\"adjusted-eps\",\"pe-ratio\",\"dilution\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.1\",\"aka\":[\"Earnings per share\"]},{\"id\":\"adjusted-eps\",\"term\":\"Adjusted EPS\",\"definition\":\"EPS as defined by the company, leaving out items it calls non-recurring or non-cash, such as share-based pay or restructuring. Accounting rules don't set it.\",\"plain\":\"Company-defined EPS that leaves out some costs; check what was excluded.\",\"related\":[\"eps\",\"dilution\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\",\"aka\":[\"Non-GAAP EPS\"]},{\"id\":\"dilution\",\"term\":\"Dilution\",\"definition\":\"The fall in each existing share's claim on profit and assets when a company issues new shares, for example to pay staff or raise money.\",\"plain\":\"New shares shrinking each existing share's slice of the profit.\",\"related\":[\"eps\",\"share-buyback\",\"secondary-offering\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\"},{\"id\":\"share-buyback\",\"term\":\"Share buyback\",\"definition\":\"A company buying back its own shares, usually on the open market. It lowers the share count, so EPS rises even when profit doesn't.\",\"plain\":\"A company buying back its own shares, which lowers the share count.\",\"related\":[\"eps\",\"dilution\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\",\"aka\":[\"Buyback\",\"Share repurchase\"]},{\"id\":\"roe\",\"term\":\"ROE\",\"definition\":\"Return on equity: net income divided by shareholders' equity. Debt can inflate it, because borrowing shrinks the equity behind the same profit.\",\"plain\":\"Return on equity: profit divided by shareholders' equity; debt can inflate it.\",\"related\":[\"net-debt\",\"interest-coverage\",\"balance-sheet\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\",\"aka\":[\"Return on equity\"]},{\"id\":\"net-debt\",\"term\":\"Net debt\",\"definition\":\"Total borrowings minus cash. Net debt ÷ EBITDA is roughly how many years of operating earnings the debt equals.\",\"plain\":\"Total debt minus cash.\",\"related\":[\"enterprise-value\",\"interest-coverage\",\"roe\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\"},{\"id\":\"interest-coverage\",\"term\":\"Interest coverage\",\"definition\":\"Operating profit (EBIT) divided by interest expense: how many times the company's operating earnings cover its interest bill.\",\"plain\":\"Operating profit divided by interest costs: how easily the debt is serviced.\",\"related\":[\"net-debt\",\"roe\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.2\"},{\"id\":\"pe-ratio\",\"term\":\"P/E ratio\",\"definition\":\"Share price divided by earnings per share: how many years of current profit the price pays for.\",\"plain\":\"Share price divided by earnings per share.\",\"related\":[\"eps\",\"ev-ebitda\",\"value-trap\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\",\"aka\":[\"P/E\",\"Price-to-earnings ratio\"]},{\"id\":\"enterprise-value\",\"term\":\"Enterprise value\",\"definition\":\"The market value of the shares plus net debt: what a buyer would pay for the whole business, taking on its debt and receiving its cash.\",\"plain\":\"Market value of the shares plus net debt: the price of the whole business.\",\"related\":[\"net-debt\",\"ev-ebitda\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\",\"aka\":[\"EV\"]},{\"id\":\"ev-ebitda\",\"term\":\"EV/EBITDA\",\"definition\":\"Enterprise value divided by EBITDA, earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortisation. Because EV includes debt, it compares companies with different capital structures.\",\"plain\":\"Enterprise value divided by operating profit before depreciation; compares companies with different debt.\",\"related\":[\"enterprise-value\",\"pe-ratio\",\"net-debt\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\"},{\"id\":\"value-trap\",\"term\":\"Value trap\",\"definition\":\"A stock that looks cheap on multiples but stays cheap or falls further, because the low price reflects real problems.\",\"plain\":\"A stock that looks cheap and stays cheap, or gets cheaper.\",\"related\":[\"pe-ratio\",\"ev-ebitda\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\"},{\"id\":\"discount-rate\",\"term\":\"Discount rate\",\"definition\":\"The annual return used to turn future cash into today's value; higher when interest rates or risk are higher.\",\"plain\":\"The return used to convert future cash into today's value.\",\"related\":[\"present-value\",\"wacc\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.3\"},{\"id\":\"present-value\",\"term\":\"Present value\",\"definition\":\"The value today of money received in the future: the future amount divided by (1 + discount rate) for each year of waiting.\",\"plain\":\"What a future amount of money is worth today.\",\"related\":[\"discount-rate\",\"dcf\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\",\"aka\":[\"PV\"]},{\"id\":\"dcf\",\"term\":\"DCF\",\"definition\":\"Discounted cash flow: valuing a business as the sum of the present values of its expected free cash flows, plus a terminal value.\",\"plain\":\"Valuing a business as the present value of its future cash flows.\",\"related\":[\"present-value\",\"terminal-value\",\"wacc\",\"reverse-dcf\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\",\"aka\":[\"Discounted cash flow\"]},{\"id\":\"terminal-value\",\"term\":\"Terminal value\",\"definition\":\"In a DCF, the value of all cash flows after the forecast years, often from a perpetual-growth formula: last cash flow × (1 + g) ÷ (r − g).\",\"plain\":\"The value of all cash flows after the forecast years, often most of a DCF.\",\"related\":[\"dcf\",\"present-value\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\"},{\"id\":\"wacc\",\"term\":\"WACC\",\"definition\":\"Weighted average cost of capital: the blend of the returns that lenders and shareholders require, used as the discount rate for a company's free cash flow.\",\"plain\":\"The blended return that lenders and owners require.\",\"related\":[\"discount-rate\",\"dcf\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\",\"aka\":[\"Weighted average cost of capital\"]},{\"id\":\"reverse-dcf\",\"term\":\"Reverse DCF\",\"definition\":\"Running a DCF backwards: fixing the value at today's price and solving for the growth that price implies.\",\"plain\":\"Solving a DCF for the growth that today's price implies.\",\"related\":[\"dcf\",\"terminal-value\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.4\"},{\"id\":\"consensus-estimate\",\"term\":\"Consensus estimate\",\"definition\":\"The average or median of analysts' forecasts for a reported figure, such as EPS or revenue, published before the report.\",\"plain\":\"The average analyst forecast for a reported figure.\",\"related\":[\"consensus\",\"earnings-beat\",\"whisper-number\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"whisper-number\",\"term\":\"Whisper number\",\"definition\":\"The unofficial expectation traders actually hold for a report, often above the published consensus. It can't be checked: treat it as a reminder that the real bar is unknown.\",\"plain\":\"The unofficial expectation traders actually hold, often above the consensus.\",\"related\":[\"consensus-estimate\",\"earnings-beat\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"earnings-beat\",\"term\":\"Earnings beat\",\"definition\":\"Reported results above the consensus estimate. Most companies beat in a typical season, so a beat is the base case, not news by itself.\",\"plain\":\"Reported results above the consensus; the usual case, not news by itself.\",\"related\":[\"consensus-estimate\",\"surprise\",\"base-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\",\"aka\":[\"Beat\"]},{\"id\":\"extended-hours\",\"term\":\"Extended hours\",\"definition\":\"Trading before the regular open or after the close, with fewer participants, wider spreads, usually limit orders only and no NBBO protection.\",\"plain\":\"Trading before the open or after the close, with thin liquidity and wider spreads.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"nbbo\",\"limit-order\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\",\"aka\":[\"Pre-market\",\"After-hours\"]},{\"id\":\"estimate-revision\",\"term\":\"Estimate revision\",\"definition\":\"An analyst raising or cutting a forecast. Revisions cluster around reports, and price targets often follow the price.\",\"plain\":\"An analyst raising or cutting a forecast.\",\"related\":[\"consensus-estimate\",\"revision\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.5\"},{\"id\":\"guidance\",\"term\":\"Guidance\",\"definition\":\"Management's own forecast for coming results, such as full-year revenue or EPS. Changes in it often move price more than the quarter just reported.\",\"plain\":\"Management's own forecast for the coming results.\",\"related\":[\"beat-and-raise\",\"sandbagging\",\"consensus-estimate\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.6\",\"aka\":[\"Outlook\"]},{\"id\":\"beat-and-raise\",\"term\":\"Beat and raise\",\"definition\":\"A report with results above the consensus together with higher guidance: the most reliably positive combination, though not a guarantee.\",\"plain\":\"Results above expectations together with higher guidance.\",\"related\":[\"guidance\",\"earnings-beat\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.6\"},{\"id\":\"sandbagging\",\"term\":\"Sandbagging\",\"definition\":\"Setting guidance low on purpose so it's easy to beat. Once traders learn the habit, they expect the beat and the raise.\",\"plain\":\"Setting guidance low on purpose so it is easy to beat.\",\"related\":[\"guidance\",\"beat-and-raise\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.6\"},{\"id\":\"implied-volatility\",\"term\":\"Implied volatility\",\"definition\":\"The volatility, as an annual percentage, that makes an option-pricing model match an option's market price: the uncertainty the market is pricing in.\",\"plain\":\"The volatility the options market is pricing in.\",\"related\":[\"historical-volatility\",\"straddle\",\"iv-crush\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\",\"aka\":[\"IV\"]},{\"id\":\"straddle\",\"term\":\"Straddle\",\"definition\":\"Buying a call and a put with the same strike and expiry. It profits if price moves further, either way, than the total premium paid.\",\"plain\":\"Buying a call and a put at the same strike: a bet on a big move.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"implied-move\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\"},{\"id\":\"implied-move\",\"term\":\"Implied move\",\"definition\":\"The move the options market prices for an event, roughly the at-the-money straddle price divided by the share price.\",\"plain\":\"The move options price for an event: straddle price divided by share price.\",\"related\":[\"straddle\",\"implied-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\"},{\"id\":\"iv-crush\",\"term\":\"IV crush\",\"definition\":\"The sharp fall in implied volatility, and so in option prices, once an event such as an earnings report resolves the uncertainty.\",\"plain\":\"The drop in option prices after an event removes the uncertainty.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"straddle\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.7\"},{\"id\":\"sue\",\"term\":\"SUE\",\"definition\":\"Standardised unexpected earnings: actual minus expected EPS, divided by the standard deviation of past surprises, so surprises are comparable across companies.\",\"plain\":\"The earnings surprise divided by the usual size of surprises.\",\"related\":[\"pead\",\"surprise\",\"standard-deviation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.8\",\"aka\":[\"Standardised unexpected earnings\"]},{\"id\":\"pead\",\"term\":\"PEAD\",\"definition\":\"Post-earnings announcement drift: the tendency of prices to keep moving in the direction of an earnings surprise for weeks. Much weaker since publication.\",\"plain\":\"Post-earnings announcement drift: prices keep moving in the surprise's direction for weeks.\",\"related\":[\"sue\",\"anomaly\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.8\",\"aka\":[\"Post-earnings drift\"]},{\"id\":\"anomaly\",\"term\":\"Anomaly\",\"definition\":\"A return pattern that standard risk explanations don't account for. Published anomalies tend to shrink out of sample and after publication.\",\"plain\":\"A return pattern that standard risk explanations do not account for.\",\"related\":[\"pead\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.8\"},{\"id\":\"secondary-offering\",\"term\":\"Secondary offering\",\"definition\":\"A listed company selling new shares to investors. It dilutes existing holders and adds supply, so the price usually falls on the announcement.\",\"plain\":\"A listed company selling new shares to investors.\",\"related\":[\"dilution\",\"stock\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.9\",\"aka\":[\"Follow-on offering\"]},{\"id\":\"merger-arbitrage\",\"term\":\"Merger arbitrage\",\"definition\":\"Buying a takeover target below the offer price to earn the spread if the deal closes, accepting a large loss if it fails. The spread implies the market's odds for the deal.\",\"plain\":\"Trading the gap between a takeover target's price and the offered price.\",\"related\":[\"stock\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.9\",\"aka\":[\"Merger arb\",\"Risk arbitrage\"]},{\"id\":\"index-inclusion\",\"term\":\"Index inclusion\",\"definition\":\"A stock being added to an index, which makes funds that track the index buy it; a deletion makes them sell.\",\"plain\":\"A stock joining an index, which makes index funds buy it.\",\"related\":[\"index\",\"etf\"],\"lesson\":\"3.8.9\"},{\"id\":\"sentiment-survey\",\"term\":\"Sentiment survey\",\"definition\":\"A regular poll asking investors whether they expect prices to rise, fall or stay flat, such as the weekly AAII survey.\",\"plain\":\"A poll of how investors feel about the market's next move.\",\"related\":[\"bull-bear-spread\",\"contrarian\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.1\"},{\"id\":\"bull-bear-spread\",\"term\":\"Bull–bear spread\",\"definition\":\"The percentage of bullish answers minus the percentage of bearish answers in a sentiment survey.\",\"plain\":\"How many more people expect a rise than a fall, in percentage points.\",\"related\":[\"sentiment-survey\",\"sentiment-extreme\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.1\"},{\"id\":\"contrarian\",\"term\":\"Contrarian\",\"definition\":\"Leaning against the crowd when its views or positions are extreme.\",\"plain\":\"Going the other way when nearly everyone agrees.\",\"related\":[\"sentiment-extreme\",\"fade\",\"crowded-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.1\"},{\"id\":\"cot-report\",\"term\":\"COT report\",\"definition\":\"The CFTC's weekly Commitments of Traders report: futures positions by trader category, measured on Tuesday and published on Friday.\",\"plain\":\"A weekly list of who is long and who is short in each futures market.\",\"related\":[\"open-interest\",\"managed-money\",\"commercial-hedger\",\"cot-index\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\",\"aka\":[\"Commitments of Traders\"]},{\"id\":\"open-interest\",\"term\":\"Open interest\",\"definition\":\"The number of futures or options contracts still open, not yet closed out or delivered.\",\"plain\":\"How many contracts are still out there.\",\"related\":[\"futures\",\"cot-report\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\",\"aka\":[\"OI\"]},{\"id\":\"commercial-hedger\",\"term\":\"Commercial hedger\",\"definition\":\"A producer, merchant or user of a commodity who trades futures to reduce business risk, such as locking in a selling price.\",\"plain\":\"A business using futures as insurance, not as a bet.\",\"related\":[\"hedger\",\"hedge\",\"cot-report\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\"},{\"id\":\"managed-money\",\"term\":\"Managed money\",\"definition\":\"In the Disaggregated COT report, funds that trade futures for profit, such as CTAs and hedge funds.\",\"plain\":\"The big funds speculating in futures.\",\"related\":[\"speculator\",\"cot-report\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\"},{\"id\":\"cot-index\",\"term\":\"COT index\",\"definition\":\"Where a group's net position sits within its range over a look-back, usually three years: (net − low) ÷ (high − low) × 100.\",\"plain\":\"0 means as short as they've been in three years, 100 as long.\",\"related\":[\"cot-report\",\"crowded-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.2\"},{\"id\":\"vix\",\"term\":\"VIX\",\"definition\":\"The Cboe index of the 30-day volatility priced into S&P 500 options, quoted as an annualised percentage.\",\"plain\":\"The options market's price of fear: how big it expects moves to be.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"put-call-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.3\",\"aka\":[\"fear index\"]},{\"id\":\"put-call-ratio\",\"term\":\"Put/call ratio\",\"definition\":\"Put volume divided by call volume over a period, for equity options, index options or both.\",\"plain\":\"How many bets on a fall for every bet on a rise.\",\"related\":[\"vix\",\"zero-dte\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.3\",\"aka\":[\"P/C ratio\"]},{\"id\":\"zero-dte\",\"term\":\"0DTE\",\"definition\":\"Options that expire on the same day they are traded.\",\"plain\":\"Options that live for a single day.\",\"related\":[\"put-call-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.3\",\"aka\":[\"zero days to expiry\"]},{\"id\":\"short-interest\",\"term\":\"Short interest\",\"definition\":\"The number of shares currently sold short and not yet bought back, often shown as a % of the float.\",\"plain\":\"How many shares are borrowed and sold, waiting to be bought back.\",\"related\":[\"short\",\"days-to-cover\",\"short-squeeze\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.4\"},{\"id\":\"days-to-cover\",\"term\":\"Days to cover\",\"definition\":\"Short interest divided by average daily volume: how many days of normal trading short sellers would need to buy back.\",\"plain\":\"How long it would take the shorts to get out.\",\"related\":[\"short-interest\",\"short-squeeze\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.4\",\"aka\":[\"short ratio\"]},{\"id\":\"exit-liquidity\",\"term\":\"Exit liquidity\",\"definition\":\"Late buyers whose orders let earlier holders sell, typically near the top of a hyped move.\",\"plain\":\"The people others sell to on the way out.\",\"related\":[\"pump-and-dump\",\"fomo\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.4\"},{\"id\":\"crowded-trade\",\"term\":\"Crowded trade\",\"definition\":\"A position held by so many traders that their exit moves the price.\",\"plain\":\"A trade everyone is in, so the door out is narrow.\",\"related\":[\"short-squeeze\",\"cot-index\",\"sentiment-extreme\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.5\"},{\"id\":\"sentiment-extreme\",\"term\":\"Sentiment extreme\",\"definition\":\"A sentiment reading far outside its usual range, often beyond two standard deviations of its own history.\",\"plain\":\"A reading the survey rarely reaches.\",\"related\":[\"bull-bear-spread\",\"contrarian\",\"standard-deviation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.5\"},{\"id\":\"turn-of-the-month\",\"term\":\"Turn of the month\",\"definition\":\"The last trading day of a month plus the first few days of the next: the window of the best-documented calendar effect.\",\"plain\":\"The few days around the change of the month.\",\"related\":[\"sell-in-may\",\"data-mining\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.6\",\"aka\":[\"TOM\"]},{\"id\":\"sell-in-may\",\"term\":\"Sell in May\",\"definition\":\"The claim that November–April stock returns beat May–October returns, also called the Halloween effect.\",\"plain\":\"The saying that the summer months are weaker for stocks.\",\"related\":[\"turn-of-the-month\",\"data-mining\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.6\",\"aka\":[\"Halloween effect\"]},{\"id\":\"data-mining\",\"term\":\"Data mining\",\"definition\":\"Searching data through many tests until a pattern appears, whether or not it is real.\",\"plain\":\"Testing until something looks good by luck.\",\"related\":[\"cherry-picking\",\"out-of-sample\",\"sample-size\"],\"lesson\":\"3.9.6\"},{\"id\":\"distribution\",\"term\":\"Distribution\",\"definition\":\"The full set of possible outcomes and how often each occurs, such as the spread of a system's R results or of daily returns.\",\"plain\":\"How often each possible outcome occurs.\",\"related\":[\"median\",\"skew\",\"fat-tails\",\"standard-deviation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\"},{\"id\":\"median\",\"term\":\"Median\",\"definition\":\"The middle value of a sorted set of results: half are above it and half below. Unlike the mean, it ignores how large the extremes are.\",\"plain\":\"The middle value: half the outcomes are above it, half below.\",\"related\":[\"distribution\",\"expectancy\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\"},{\"id\":\"skew\",\"term\":\"Skew\",\"definition\":\"The asymmetry of a distribution. Positive skew has a long tail of rare large gains; negative skew has a long tail of rare large losses.\",\"plain\":\"Lopsided outcomes, with a longer tail on one side.\",\"related\":[\"distribution\",\"kurtosis\",\"fat-tails\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\",\"aka\":[\"skewness\"]},{\"id\":\"kurtosis\",\"term\":\"Kurtosis\",\"definition\":\"A measure of how heavy a distribution's tails are. Excess kurtosis above zero means more extreme outcomes than a normal distribution.\",\"plain\":\"How heavy the tails are; high kurtosis means more extreme outcomes.\",\"related\":[\"fat-tails\",\"skew\",\"distribution\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\"},{\"id\":\"fat-tails\",\"term\":\"Fat tails\",\"definition\":\"Extreme outcomes that occur far more often than a normal distribution predicts. Daily market returns have them: multi-sigma days recur within years.\",\"plain\":\"Extreme moves that happen far more often than a bell curve predicts.\",\"related\":[\"kurtosis\",\"distribution\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.1\",\"aka\":[\"heavy tails\"]},{\"id\":\"realised-volatility\",\"term\":\"Realised volatility\",\"definition\":\"Volatility measured from actual past returns, usually the standard deviation of daily returns over a window.\",\"plain\":\"Volatility measured from actual past price changes.\",\"related\":[\"historical-volatility\",\"annualised-volatility\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.2\",\"aka\":[\"realized volatility\"]},{\"id\":\"annualised-volatility\",\"term\":\"Annualised volatility\",\"definition\":\"Volatility scaled to a one-year horizon, usually daily volatility × √252 when days are independent.\",\"plain\":\"Volatility scaled to a one-year horizon.\",\"related\":[\"square-root-of-time-rule\",\"realised-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.2\",\"aka\":[\"annualized volatility\"]},{\"id\":\"square-root-of-time-rule\",\"term\":\"Square-root-of-time rule\",\"definition\":\"For independent returns, volatility over n periods equals one-period volatility × √n, because variances add up.\",\"plain\":\"Volatility over n days is about daily volatility times the square root of n.\",\"related\":[\"annualised-volatility\",\"autocorrelation\",\"independence\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.2\",\"aka\":[\"√t rule\"]},{\"id\":\"winners-curse\",\"term\":\"Winner's curse\",\"definition\":\"The best-scoring choice among many was selected partly for its luck, so its future results tend to fall short of its past score.\",\"plain\":\"The best-looking pick among many was partly lucky, so it tends to disappoint.\",\"related\":[\"regression-to-the-mean\",\"multiple-testing\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.3\"},{\"id\":\"prior\",\"term\":\"Prior\",\"definition\":\"The probability you give a hypothesis before seeing new evidence, often taken from a base rate.\",\"plain\":\"Your probability for something before seeing new evidence.\",\"related\":[\"posterior\",\"base-rate\",\"base-rate-neglect\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\"},{\"id\":\"posterior\",\"term\":\"Posterior\",\"definition\":\"The probability of a hypothesis after combining the prior with new evidence, by Bayes' rule.\",\"plain\":\"Your updated probability after seeing the evidence.\",\"related\":[\"prior\",\"false-positive\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\"},{\"id\":\"false-positive\",\"term\":\"False positive\",\"definition\":\"A test result that says 'yes' when the true answer is 'no', such as a useless strategy passing a backtest.\",\"plain\":\"A test saying 'yes' when the true answer is 'no'.\",\"related\":[\"p-value\",\"multiple-testing\",\"posterior\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\",\"aka\":[\"false alarm\"]},{\"id\":\"base-rate-neglect\",\"term\":\"Base-rate neglect\",\"definition\":\"Judging new evidence while ignoring how common the thing judged was to begin with, which overstates what a positive result means.\",\"plain\":\"Ignoring how common something is when judging new evidence about it.\",\"related\":[\"base-rate\",\"prior\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.4\"},{\"id\":\"t-statistic\",\"term\":\"t-statistic\",\"definition\":\"An estimate divided by its standard error, such as mean R ÷ (sd ÷ √n). Values around 2 or more suggest more than noise.\",\"plain\":\"An average divided by its standard error; about 2 or more suggests more than noise.\",\"related\":[\"standard-error\",\"p-value\",\"sqn\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\",\"aka\":[\"t-stat\"]},{\"id\":\"p-value\",\"term\":\"p-value\",\"definition\":\"The probability of a result at least as extreme as the one observed if there were truly no effect. It is not the probability that the effect is real.\",\"plain\":\"The chance of a result this extreme if there were really no edge.\",\"related\":[\"t-statistic\",\"multiple-testing\",\"false-positive\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\"},{\"id\":\"standard-error\",\"term\":\"Standard error\",\"definition\":\"The standard deviation of an estimate from sample to sample. For a mean, the standard deviation ÷ √n.\",\"plain\":\"How much an estimate, such as an average, would vary from sample to sample.\",\"related\":[\"confidence-interval\",\"t-statistic\",\"sample-size\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\",\"aka\":[\"SE\"]},{\"id\":\"sharpe-ratio\",\"term\":\"Sharpe ratio\",\"definition\":\"Average return above cash divided by the standard deviation of returns, usually annualised. With daily data, its standard error is about 1 ÷ √years.\",\"plain\":\"Return above cash divided by volatility.\",\"related\":[\"standard-error\",\"skew\",\"annualised-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\"},{\"id\":\"bootstrap\",\"term\":\"Bootstrap\",\"definition\":\"Estimating how uncertain a statistic is by resampling the observed data with replacement many times and recomputing the statistic each time.\",\"plain\":\"Resampling your own results many times to estimate how uncertain they are.\",\"related\":[\"confidence-interval\",\"standard-error\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.5\"},{\"id\":\"multiple-testing\",\"term\":\"Multiple testing\",\"definition\":\"Running many tests on the same data, which makes some look significant by chance unless the threshold accounts for every test.\",\"plain\":\"Running many tests, which makes chance 'discoveries' likely.\",\"related\":[\"bonferroni-correction\",\"p-hacking\",\"false-positive\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\",\"aka\":[\"data snooping\"]},{\"id\":\"bonferroni-correction\",\"term\":\"Bonferroni correction\",\"definition\":\"Dividing the significance threshold by the number of tests run, so the chance of any false discovery stays at or below the original level.\",\"plain\":\"Dividing the significance threshold by the number of tests run.\",\"related\":[\"multiple-testing\",\"p-value\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\"},{\"id\":\"p-hacking\",\"term\":\"p-hacking\",\"definition\":\"Trying analyses, rules or parameters until one produces a significant p-value, then reporting only that one.\",\"plain\":\"Trying analyses until one gives a significant p-value.\",\"related\":[\"multiple-testing\",\"p-value\",\"spurious-correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\"},{\"id\":\"spurious-correlation\",\"term\":\"Spurious correlation\",\"definition\":\"A correlation that appears by chance or through a shared trend and vanishes on new data.\",\"plain\":\"A correlation that appears by chance and disappears on new data.\",\"related\":[\"correlation\",\"multiple-testing\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.6\"},{\"id\":\"autocorrelation\",\"term\":\"Autocorrelation\",\"definition\":\"The correlation of a series with its own past values. The lag-1 autocorrelation of returns is positive in trending markets and negative in mean-reverting ones.\",\"plain\":\"How much a value depends on the values just before it.\",\"related\":[\"independence\",\"runs-test\",\"market-regime\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.7\",\"aka\":[\"serial correlation\"]},{\"id\":\"runs-test\",\"term\":\"Runs test\",\"definition\":\"A test of whether a sequence, such as wins and losses, has as many runs as independence predicts: expected runs = 2·n1·n2 ÷ (n1 + n2) + 1.\",\"plain\":\"A test of whether wins and losses alternate as randomly as expected.\",\"related\":[\"autocorrelation\",\"independence\",\"losing-streak\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.7\",\"aka\":[\"Wald–Wolfowitz test\"]},{\"id\":\"independence\",\"term\":\"Independence\",\"definition\":\"Outcomes are independent when one gives no information about the next. Kelly, risk-of-ruin formulas, reshuffled Monte Carlo and the √t rule all assume it.\",\"plain\":\"One outcome gives no information about the next.\",\"related\":[\"autocorrelation\",\"runs-test\",\"square-root-of-time-rule\"],\"lesson\":\"3.10.7\"},{\"id\":\"edge-hypothesis\",\"term\":\"Edge hypothesis\",\"definition\":\"A falsifiable statement of why a pattern should pay: the mechanism, the conditions, the expected effect and horizon, and who is on the other side of the trade.\",\"plain\":\"A testable reason why a pattern should pay, and who loses to you.\",\"related\":[\"falsifiable\",\"testable-hypothesis\",\"research-log\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.1\"},{\"id\":\"research-log\",\"term\":\"Research log\",\"definition\":\"A permanent, append-only record of every hypothesis, test, parameter change and result, with the running number of trials.\",\"plain\":\"A permanent record of every idea, test and change, including the number of trials.\",\"related\":[\"edge-hypothesis\",\"data-snooping\",\"journal\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.1\"},{\"id\":\"point-in-time-data\",\"term\":\"Point-in-time data\",\"definition\":\"Data stored exactly as it was known on each past date, with publication dates, so later revisions and index changes can't leak into a test.\",\"plain\":\"Data exactly as it was known on each past date, without later corrections.\",\"related\":[\"look-ahead-bias\",\"survivorship-bias\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.1\"},{\"id\":\"next-bar-execution\",\"term\":\"Next-bar execution\",\"definition\":\"A fill rule that executes a signal at the open of the bar after the one that produced it, because the signal bar's close is known only when the bar ends.\",\"plain\":\"Filling a signal at the next bar's open, not in the bar that produced it.\",\"related\":[\"intrabar-ambiguity\",\"look-ahead-bias\",\"backtest\",\"fill\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\"},{\"id\":\"intrabar-ambiguity\",\"term\":\"Intrabar ambiguity\",\"definition\":\"When one bar's range contains both the stop and the target, bar data can't show which was hit first; conservative tests assume the stop.\",\"plain\":\"Not knowing whether the stop or the target was hit first inside one bar.\",\"related\":[\"next-bar-execution\",\"stop-loss\",\"take-profit\",\"backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\"},{\"id\":\"breakeven-cost\",\"term\":\"Breakeven cost\",\"definition\":\"The cost per trade at which a strategy's net expectancy falls to zero. Divided by the current cost, it gives the breakeven cost multiple.\",\"plain\":\"The cost per trade at which a strategy stops making money.\",\"related\":[\"cost-drag\",\"cost-stress\",\"expectancy\",\"after-cost-expectancy\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\"},{\"id\":\"cost-stress\",\"term\":\"Cost stress\",\"definition\":\"Re-running a backtest with costs raised, for example doubled, to check whether the edge survives wider spreads and slippage.\",\"plain\":\"Re-running a backtest with higher costs to see whether the edge survives.\",\"related\":[\"breakeven-cost\",\"stress-test\",\"slippage\",\"cost-drag\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.2\"},{\"id\":\"delisting\",\"term\":\"Delisting\",\"definition\":\"The removal of a security from its exchange, often after failure, a takeover or a breach of listing rules. Delisted securities vanish from lists of current instruments.\",\"plain\":\"A stock removed from its exchange, often after failure or a takeover.\",\"related\":[\"survivorship-bias\",\"point-in-time-data\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.3\"},{\"id\":\"in-sample\",\"term\":\"In-sample\",\"definition\":\"The part of the data used to develop and tune a strategy. Results there are biased upward by the tuning.\",\"plain\":\"The data used to build and tune a strategy.\",\"related\":[\"out-of-sample\",\"hold-out\",\"overfitting\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.4\"},{\"id\":\"hold-out\",\"term\":\"Hold-out\",\"definition\":\"A later stretch of data set aside, untouched, for one final out-of-sample test once development is finished.\",\"plain\":\"The part of the data set aside for the out-of-sample test.\",\"related\":[\"in-sample\",\"out-of-sample\",\"research-log\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.4\"},{\"id\":\"overfitting\",\"term\":\"Overfitting\",\"definition\":\"Tuning rules to the noise of one sample, so they describe past accidents rather than a lasting structure and fail on new data.\",\"plain\":\"Tuning rules to past noise so they fail on new data.\",\"related\":[\"degrees-of-freedom\",\"data-snooping\",\"out-of-sample\",\"parameter-plateau\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\",\"aka\":[\"Curve fitting\"]},{\"id\":\"data-snooping\",\"term\":\"Data snooping\",\"definition\":\"Testing many ideas or tweaks on the same data until one looks good by chance. The more trials, the better the best one looks.\",\"plain\":\"Testing so many ideas on the same data that one works by luck.\",\"related\":[\"overfitting\",\"research-log\",\"random-walk-control\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\"},{\"id\":\"degrees-of-freedom\",\"term\":\"Degrees of freedom\",\"definition\":\"The number of choices a strategy can tune, such as parameters, filters, anchors and timeframes. Each one gives a test another way to fit noise.\",\"plain\":\"The number of choices and parameters a strategy can tune.\",\"related\":[\"overfitting\",\"data-snooping\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\"},{\"id\":\"random-walk-control\",\"term\":\"Random-walk control\",\"definition\":\"Running the same test on random-walk data, where no rule has an edge, to check that the apparent edge disappears. An edge there signals a leak or a fit.\",\"plain\":\"Running the same test on random data to check that the 'edge' disappears.\",\"related\":[\"random-walk\",\"look-ahead-bias\",\"overfitting\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.5\"},{\"id\":\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"term\":\"Walk-forward analysis\",\"definition\":\"Repeatedly tuning a strategy on one window and testing it on the next unseen window, then rolling forward and joining only the unseen segments.\",\"plain\":\"Tune on one period, test on the next unseen one, then roll forward.\",\"related\":[\"walk-forward-efficiency\",\"anchored-window\",\"rolling-window\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\"},{\"id\":\"walk-forward-efficiency\",\"term\":\"Walk-forward efficiency\",\"definition\":\"The annualised out-of-sample return of a walk-forward test divided by the annualised in-sample return. 0.5 or more is a practitioners' rule of thumb, not a standard.\",\"plain\":\"Out-of-sample return divided by in-sample return.\",\"related\":[\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"in-sample\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\",\"aka\":[\"WFE\"]},{\"id\":\"anchored-window\",\"term\":\"Anchored window\",\"definition\":\"A walk-forward window that always starts at the beginning of the data and grows by one step each time.\",\"plain\":\"A walk-forward window that always starts at the beginning of the data.\",\"related\":[\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"rolling-window\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\"},{\"id\":\"rolling-window\",\"term\":\"Rolling window\",\"definition\":\"A walk-forward window of fixed length that drops its oldest data as it moves forward.\",\"plain\":\"A walk-forward window of fixed length that drops old data as it moves.\",\"related\":[\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"anchored-window\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.6\"},{\"id\":\"monte-carlo\",\"term\":\"Monte Carlo\",\"definition\":\"Running many random simulations, such as reshuffles or resamples of a trade list, to see the range of outcomes a process can produce.\",\"plain\":\"Running many random simulations to see the range of possible outcomes.\",\"related\":[\"reshuffle\",\"fan-chart\",\"percentile\",\"drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\"},{\"id\":\"reshuffle\",\"term\":\"Reshuffle\",\"definition\":\"Putting the same list of trades in a new random order. The total stays the same, while the path and the drawdowns change.\",\"plain\":\"Putting the same trades in a new random order.\",\"related\":[\"monte-carlo\",\"max-drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\"},{\"id\":\"fan-chart\",\"term\":\"Fan chart\",\"definition\":\"A chart of many simulated equity paths, summarised by percentile bands such as the 5th, 50th and 95th.\",\"plain\":\"A chart of many simulated paths with percentile bands.\",\"related\":[\"monte-carlo\",\"percentile\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\"},{\"id\":\"expectation-corridor\",\"term\":\"Expectation corridor\",\"definition\":\"The band of results a validated system should produce by chance alone, taken from its Monte Carlo percentiles. Results inside it are variance; results outside it need investigating.\",\"plain\":\"The range a system's results should stay within by chance alone.\",\"related\":[\"monte-carlo\",\"percentile\",\"variance\",\"kill-switch\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\"},{\"id\":\"percentile\",\"term\":\"Percentile\",\"definition\":\"The value below which a given percentage of outcomes fall. A 95th-percentile drawdown is deeper than 95% of the simulated drawdowns.\",\"plain\":\"The value below which a given percentage of outcomes fall.\",\"related\":[\"monte-carlo\",\"fan-chart\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.7\"},{\"id\":\"robustness\",\"term\":\"Robustness\",\"definition\":\"The quality of results that hold up when conditions change a little: other parameters, higher costs, added noise, other periods or markets.\",\"plain\":\"Results that hold up when conditions change a little.\",\"related\":[\"stress-test\",\"parameter-plateau\",\"overfitting\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\"},{\"id\":\"parameter-plateau\",\"term\":\"Parameter plateau\",\"definition\":\"A broad region of parameter values with similar results, preferred to an isolated peak that its neighbours don't support.\",\"plain\":\"A wide area of settings that all give similar results.\",\"related\":[\"parameter-grid\",\"robustness\",\"overfitting\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\"},{\"id\":\"stress-test\",\"term\":\"Stress test\",\"definition\":\"Re-running a backtest under harsher conditions, such as doubled costs, removed best trades, noisier prices or a later start, to see whether the edge survives.\",\"plain\":\"Checking results under harsher conditions, such as double costs.\",\"related\":[\"robustness\",\"cost-stress\",\"outlier-dependence\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\"},{\"id\":\"outlier-dependence\",\"term\":\"Outlier dependence\",\"definition\":\"The share of a strategy's profit that comes from a few very large trades, measured by removing the best trades, for example the top 5%.\",\"plain\":\"How much of the total profit comes from a few very large trades.\",\"related\":[\"profit-factor\",\"stress-test\",\"trend-following\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.8\"},{\"id\":\"random-entry-benchmark\",\"term\":\"Random-entry benchmark\",\"definition\":\"Many twins of a strategy that keep its exits, market and trade count but enter at random. The strategy's percentile among them measures what its entry adds.\",\"plain\":\"The same strategy with random entries, used to judge whether your entry adds anything.\",\"related\":[\"edge-ratio\",\"benchmark\",\"buy-and-hold\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.9\"},{\"id\":\"incubation\",\"term\":\"Incubation\",\"definition\":\"Forward-testing a validated system at small size before scaling up, with gates based on its Monte Carlo bands and its process measures.\",\"plain\":\"Trading a new system small and forward before trusting it with full size.\",\"related\":[\"go-live-gate\",\"kill-switch\",\"expectation-corridor\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\"},{\"id\":\"kill-switch\",\"term\":\"Kill switch\",\"definition\":\"A condition written in advance that stops a system when its results fall outside what variance can explain, such as a drawdown past the 99th Monte Carlo percentile.\",\"plain\":\"A rule written in advance that stops a system that has stopped working.\",\"related\":[\"expectation-corridor\",\"incubation\",\"percentile\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\"},{\"id\":\"premortem\",\"term\":\"Premortem\",\"definition\":\"Imagining that a plan has already failed and listing the reasons, before committing to it. Each reason becomes a check or a rule.\",\"plain\":\"Imagining that a plan has already failed and listing why.\",\"related\":[\"incubation\",\"trading-plan\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\"},{\"id\":\"go-live-gate\",\"term\":\"Go-live gate\",\"definition\":\"The conditions a system must meet before its size increases, such as results inside its Monte Carlo band and process KPIs on target after a set number of trades.\",\"plain\":\"The conditions a system must meet before its size increases.\",\"related\":[\"incubation\",\"kill-switch\",\"kpi\"],\"lesson\":\"3.11.10\"},{\"id\":\"geometric-mean\",\"term\":\"Geometric mean\",\"definition\":\"The constant return per period that, compounded, gives the same end result as a sequence of returns: the n-th root of the product of the growth factors, minus 1.\",\"plain\":\"The average growth per period once gains and losses are compounded.\",\"related\":[\"cagr\",\"volatility-drag\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.1\"},{\"id\":\"cagr\",\"term\":\"CAGR\",\"definition\":\"Compound annual growth rate: the steady yearly rate that turns the starting value into the ending value over the period, (end ÷ start)^(1 ÷ years) − 1.\",\"plain\":\"The steady yearly rate that would give the same end result.\",\"related\":[\"geometric-mean\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.1\",\"aka\":[\"Compound annual growth rate\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-drag\",\"term\":\"Volatility drag\",\"definition\":\"The gap between the average return and the compound growth rate caused by swings: roughly σ²/2 per period, so g ≈ μ − σ²/2.\",\"plain\":\"Swings lowering compound growth even when the average return stays the same.\",\"related\":[\"geometric-mean\",\"volatility\",\"leveraged-etf\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.1\"},{\"id\":\"fixed-ratio-sizing\",\"term\":\"Fixed-ratio sizing\",\"definition\":\"A sizing method that adds one contract or lot each time profit grows by a further step: lot N needs Δ × N(N − 1) ÷ 2 of profit, where Δ is the delta.\",\"plain\":\"Adding contracts only after profit grows by set, ever larger steps.\",\"related\":[\"anti-martingale\",\"position-size\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.2\"},{\"id\":\"anti-martingale\",\"term\":\"Anti-martingale\",\"definition\":\"Any sizing that increases size after wins and reduces it after losses, such as fixed fractional sizing. It shapes the account's path but cannot create an edge.\",\"plain\":\"Sizing that grows after wins and shrinks after losses.\",\"related\":[\"martingale\",\"fixed-ratio-sizing\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.2\"},{\"id\":\"kelly-criterion\",\"term\":\"Kelly criterion\",\"definition\":\"The fraction of equity to risk per bet that maximises long-run growth when the edge is known: f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b for a win rate W and a payoff of b.\",\"plain\":\"The bet size that grows money fastest in the long run, if you know your edge exactly.\",\"related\":[\"growth-rate\",\"full-kelly\",\"fractional-kelly\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.3\",\"aka\":[\"Kelly fraction\"]},{\"id\":\"growth-rate\",\"term\":\"Growth rate\",\"definition\":\"The average compound gain per bet or period, measured as the expected log return. Risking f per trade: g(f) = W · ln(1 + f · b) + (1 − W) · ln(1 − f).\",\"plain\":\"How fast money grows per bet or period, once results compound.\",\"related\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"geometric-mean\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.3\"},{\"id\":\"full-kelly\",\"term\":\"Full Kelly\",\"definition\":\"Betting the whole Kelly fraction. It maximises long-run growth for a known edge but brings very deep drawdowns, and over-bets when the edge is overestimated.\",\"plain\":\"Betting the entire Kelly amount: the fastest growth, with very deep drawdowns.\",\"related\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"fractional-kelly\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.3\"},{\"id\":\"fractional-kelly\",\"term\":\"Fractional Kelly\",\"definition\":\"Betting a fixed fraction of the Kelly fraction, often a half or a quarter. Half Kelly keeps about three quarters of the growth rate with much shallower drawdowns.\",\"plain\":\"Betting part of the Kelly amount, such as a half or a quarter.\",\"related\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"full-kelly\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\"},{\"id\":\"optimal-f\",\"term\":\"Optimal f\",\"definition\":\"Ralph Vince's fixed fraction that would have maximised terminal wealth over a past trade list, with each trade scaled by the list's largest loss. It implies one contract per largest loss ÷ f of equity.\",\"plain\":\"The bet fraction that would have grown a past list of trades the most.\",\"related\":[\"hpr\",\"twr\",\"kelly-criterion\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\"},{\"id\":\"hpr\",\"term\":\"HPR\",\"definition\":\"Holding period return: 1 plus a trade's gain or loss as a fraction of equity. For optimal f, HPR = 1 + f × trade ÷ |largest loss|.\",\"plain\":\"One trade's result as a growth factor, such as 1.05 for +5%.\",\"related\":[\"twr\",\"optimal-f\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\",\"aka\":[\"Holding period return\"]},{\"id\":\"twr\",\"term\":\"TWR\",\"definition\":\"Terminal wealth relative: the product of all the HPRs in a trade list, the factor by which equity would have grown.\",\"plain\":\"All the trade results multiplied together: how many times the account grew.\",\"related\":[\"hpr\",\"optimal-f\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.4\",\"aka\":[\"Terminal wealth relative\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-of-ruin\",\"term\":\"Risk of ruin\",\"definition\":\"The probability that losses reach the ruin threshold, the drawdown at which you would stop trading. With even payoffs and a fixed stake it is ((1 − W) ÷ W)^N.\",\"plain\":\"The chance of losing enough that you must stop trading.\",\"related\":[\"ruin-threshold\",\"drawdown\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.5\"},{\"id\":\"ruin-threshold\",\"term\":\"Ruin threshold\",\"definition\":\"The drawdown, set in advance, at which you would stop trading. Risk of ruin is measured against it rather than against a zero balance.\",\"plain\":\"The drawdown at which you'd stop, used to define ruin.\",\"related\":[\"risk-of-ruin\",\"max-drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.5\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-target\",\"term\":\"Volatility target\",\"definition\":\"The level of volatility a position or account is sized to, such as 10% a year. Divided by √252 it becomes a daily budget.\",\"plain\":\"How much you want a position to swing, set in advance.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-forecast\",\"leverage-creep\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.6\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-forecast\",\"term\":\"Volatility forecast\",\"definition\":\"An estimate of how much prices will move in the coming period, often the standard deviation of recent daily changes over a window such as 20 days. It lags sudden jumps.\",\"plain\":\"A guess of how much prices will move next, based on recent moves.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-target\",\"standard-deviation\",\"atr\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.6\"},{\"id\":\"leverage-creep\",\"term\":\"Leverage creep\",\"definition\":\"Exposure rising quietly as volatility forecasts fall in calm markets, because a volatility target buys more units with the same budget.\",\"plain\":\"Positions growing bigger and bigger while markets stay calm.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-target\",\"leverage\",\"exposure\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.6\"},{\"id\":\"equity-curve-filter\",\"term\":\"Equity-curve filter\",\"definition\":\"A rule that pauses live trading while a system's equity is below its own moving average and resumes when it climbs back above, tracked with shadow trades.\",\"plain\":\"Pausing a system while its results are below their own average.\",\"related\":[\"shadow-trades\",\"drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.7\"},{\"id\":\"shadow-trades\",\"term\":\"Shadow trades\",\"definition\":\"Trades a paused system would have taken, recorded but not executed, so its equity curve and the filter's signal keep updating.\",\"plain\":\"Trades written down but not taken while a system is paused.\",\"related\":[\"equity-curve-filter\"],\"lesson\":\"3.12.7\"},{\"id\":\"time-series-momentum\",\"term\":\"Time-series momentum\",\"definition\":\"A rule that goes long an asset whose own past return, usually over 12 months, was positive and short one whose return was negative, with positions typically scaled to equal volatility.\",\"plain\":\"Long what has been rising and short what has been falling, judged on each asset's own past.\",\"related\":[\"momentum\",\"trend-following\",\"cross-sectional-momentum\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.1\",\"aka\":[\"TSMOM\",\"Absolute momentum\"]},{\"id\":\"turtle-unit\",\"term\":\"Turtle unit\",\"definition\":\"A position size of 1% of equity divided by N times the value of one point, where N is the 20-day smoothed average true range, so that a move of one N changes the account by about 1%.\",\"plain\":\"A position sized so one average daily range moves the account by 1%.\",\"related\":[\"atr\",\"volatility-sizing\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.1\"},{\"id\":\"crisis-alpha\",\"term\":\"Crisis alpha\",\"definition\":\"Gains a strategy tends to make during market crises, a label often given to trend following; how large it is depends on the period and the crises studied.\",\"plain\":\"Gains a strategy tends to make during market crises.\",\"related\":[\"time-series-momentum\",\"tail-event\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.1\"},{\"id\":\"cross-sectional-momentum\",\"term\":\"Cross-sectional momentum\",\"definition\":\"Ranking the assets in a group by past return, typically over 12 months skipping the latest, then buying the top of the ranking and selling the bottom.\",\"plain\":\"Buying the recent best performers in a group and selling the worst.\",\"related\":[\"momentum\",\"time-series-momentum\",\"momentum-crash\",\"formation-period\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.2\",\"aka\":[\"Relative momentum\",\"Winners minus losers\"]},{\"id\":\"momentum-crash\",\"term\":\"Momentum crash\",\"definition\":\"A sudden large loss for a long–short momentum portfolio, typically when beaten-down past losers rebound sharply after a market fall with high volatility.\",\"plain\":\"A sharp loss for momentum when past losers rebound strongly.\",\"related\":[\"cross-sectional-momentum\",\"short-squeeze\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.2\"},{\"id\":\"formation-period\",\"term\":\"Formation period\",\"definition\":\"The look-back window over which assets are ranked by past return before a momentum portfolio is formed, such as 12 months skipping the latest one (12-1).\",\"plain\":\"The look-back window used to rank assets by past return.\",\"related\":[\"cross-sectional-momentum\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.2\",\"aka\":[\"Look-back period\",\"12-1\"]},{\"id\":\"basis-trade\",\"term\":\"Basis trade\",\"definition\":\"Buying an asset in the spot market and selling its future or perpetual swap to earn the price difference or the funding payments, with little exposure to the price itself.\",\"plain\":\"Buying spot and selling the related future or perpetual to earn the difference.\",\"related\":[\"carry\",\"futures\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.3\",\"aka\":[\"Cash-and-carry\"]},{\"id\":\"uncovered-interest-parity\",\"term\":\"Uncovered interest parity\",\"definition\":\"The theory that a high-yielding currency's expected fall offsets its interest-rate advantage, so carry shouldn't pay on average. In the data, it often fails for long periods.\",\"plain\":\"The theory that currency moves should cancel interest-rate differences; in practice they often don't.\",\"related\":[\"carry\",\"carry-trade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.3\",\"aka\":[\"UIP\"]},{\"id\":\"false-discovery-rate\",\"term\":\"False discovery rate\",\"definition\":\"The share of results flagged as significant that are actually false: false flags divided by all flags. It rises as more noise is searched.\",\"plain\":\"The share of your 'discoveries' that are actually false.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.4\",\"aka\":[\"FDR\"]},{\"id\":\"seasonal-tilt\",\"term\":\"Seasonal tilt\",\"definition\":\"Using a calendar effect to filter entries or adjust the size of an existing strategy slightly, instead of trading the calendar on its own.\",\"plain\":\"Adjusting size or filters slightly by calendar instead of trading the calendar alone.\",\"related\":[\"false-discovery-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.4\"},{\"id\":\"pairs-trading\",\"term\":\"Pairs trading\",\"definition\":\"Trading the spread between two related instruments, long one and short the other, in the expectation that the spread returns to its mean.\",\"plain\":\"Buying one related instrument and selling another, betting that their gap returns to normal.\",\"related\":[\"cointegration\",\"z-score\",\"hedge-ratio\",\"half-life\",\"mean-reversion\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\",\"aka\":[\"Pairs trade\"]},{\"id\":\"cointegration\",\"term\":\"Cointegration\",\"definition\":\"A relationship between two wandering price series in which some combination of them, such as A − β × B, stays stationary, so gaps between them tend to close.\",\"plain\":\"Two prices that wander but keep a stable gap between them.\",\"related\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"correlation\",\"hedge-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\"},{\"id\":\"z-score\",\"term\":\"z-score\",\"definition\":\"The number of standard deviations a value lies from its mean: (value − mean) ÷ standard deviation.\",\"plain\":\"How many standard deviations a value is from its average.\",\"related\":[\"standard-deviation\",\"pairs-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\"},{\"id\":\"half-life\",\"term\":\"Half-life\",\"definition\":\"For a mean-reverting series, the expected time for a gap to close halfway; for a reversion speed θ per period, about ln 2 ÷ θ.\",\"plain\":\"The average time it takes a gap to close halfway.\",\"related\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"mean-reversion\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\"},{\"id\":\"hedge-ratio\",\"term\":\"Hedge ratio\",\"definition\":\"The number of units of one instrument held against one unit of another so the combination is neutral to their shared moves; in pairs trading, the β in A − β × B.\",\"plain\":\"How many units of one instrument offset one unit of the other.\",\"related\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"hedge\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\"},{\"id\":\"structural-break\",\"term\":\"Structural break\",\"definition\":\"A lasting change in a relationship or process, such as a merger or a new business model, after which estimates from the past no longer apply.\",\"plain\":\"A lasting change that ends a relationship that used to hold.\",\"related\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"correlation-breakdown\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.5\"},{\"id\":\"event-study\",\"term\":\"Event study\",\"definition\":\"A method that averages abnormal returns across many similar events, over a window around each one, and reports the average with a confidence band.\",\"plain\":\"Measuring the average price reaction across many similar events.\",\"related\":[\"abnormal-return\",\"beta\",\"confidence-interval\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.6\"},{\"id\":\"abnormal-return\",\"term\":\"Abnormal return\",\"definition\":\"An asset's return minus the return expected from the market's move, usually beta times the market return. Summed over a window, it gives the cumulative abnormal return (CAR).\",\"plain\":\"The return beyond what the market's move would predict.\",\"related\":[\"beta\",\"event-study\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.6\"},{\"id\":\"beta\",\"term\":\"Beta\",\"definition\":\"How strongly an asset's return has tended to move with the market's: a beta of 1.2 means about 1.2% for each 1% market move.\",\"plain\":\"How much an asset tends to move when the market moves 1%.\",\"related\":[\"abnormal-return\",\"correlation\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.6\"},{\"id\":\"crowding\",\"term\":\"Crowding\",\"definition\":\"Many traders holding the same strategy or position, which shrinks its edge and makes their exits, and their losses, arrive together.\",\"plain\":\"Too many traders using the same strategy, which shrinks its edge.\",\"related\":[\"carry-unwind\",\"adaptive-markets\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.7\"},{\"id\":\"adaptive-markets\",\"term\":\"Adaptive markets\",\"definition\":\"Andrew Lo's view that market efficiency varies over time as participants, competition and conditions change, so edges appear and fade.\",\"plain\":\"The idea that market efficiency changes as participants and conditions change.\",\"related\":[\"crowding\",\"market-regime\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.7\"},{\"id\":\"working-paper\",\"term\":\"Working paper\",\"definition\":\"A research paper circulated publicly before peer review; its methods and results haven't yet been checked by independent referees.\",\"plain\":\"A study published before peer review.\",\"related\":[\"peer-review\",\"replication\",\"evidence-grade\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.8\"},{\"id\":\"replication\",\"term\":\"Replication\",\"definition\":\"An independent repeat of a study, on the same or new data, to see whether its result holds.\",\"plain\":\"Repeating a study independently to see whether the result holds.\",\"related\":[\"working-paper\",\"peer-review\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.8\"},{\"id\":\"peer-review\",\"term\":\"Peer review\",\"definition\":\"Evaluation of a study by independent experts before a journal publishes it. It checks method and reasoning; it doesn't promise the result will hold in the future.\",\"plain\":\"Checking of a study by independent experts before publication.\",\"related\":[\"working-paper\",\"replication\"],\"lesson\":\"3.13.8\"},{\"id\":\"tick-value\",\"term\":\"Tick value\",\"definition\":\"The money one contract gains or loses when price moves one tick: tick size × contract multiplier. LXF's 0.25 tick at 50 vUSD a point is worth 12.50.\",\"plain\":\"What the smallest price step is worth on one contract.\",\"related\":[\"tick\",\"contract-multiplier\",\"point\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\"},{\"id\":\"initial-margin\",\"term\":\"Initial margin\",\"definition\":\"The collateral an exchange or broker requires to open a futures position, set per contract and raised or lowered as volatility changes.\",\"plain\":\"The deposit you need to open a futures trade.\",\"related\":[\"maintenance-margin\",\"variation-margin\",\"margin\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\"},{\"id\":\"maintenance-margin\",\"term\":\"Maintenance margin\",\"definition\":\"The minimum collateral needed to keep a futures position open. Below it comes a margin call, usually to top up to the initial margin, or the position is closed.\",\"plain\":\"The level your margin can't fall below without a top-up.\",\"related\":[\"initial-margin\",\"margin-call\",\"variation-margin\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\"},{\"id\":\"variation-margin\",\"term\":\"Variation margin\",\"definition\":\"The gain or loss on a futures position, paid or received in cash after each day's settlement.\",\"plain\":\"Each day's profit or loss, settled in cash that evening.\",\"related\":[\"initial-margin\",\"maintenance-margin\",\"futures\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\",\"aka\":[\"Daily settlement\"]},{\"id\":\"micro-contract\",\"term\":\"Micro contract\",\"definition\":\"A smaller version of a futures contract, often a tenth of the standard size, on the same underlying and at the same price.\",\"plain\":\"A futures contract a tenth the size of the standard one, for finer sizing.\",\"related\":[\"futures\",\"contract-multiplier\",\"tick-value\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.1\",\"aka\":[\"Micro\"]},{\"id\":\"expiry\",\"term\":\"Expiry\",\"definition\":\"The date a futures or options contract ends and is settled, in cash or by delivery.\",\"plain\":\"The last day a contract exists.\",\"related\":[\"first-notice-day\",\"physical-delivery\",\"futures\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\",\"aka\":[\"Expiration\"]},{\"id\":\"first-notice-day\",\"term\":\"First notice day\",\"definition\":\"The first day a long in a physically delivered futures contract can be assigned delivery. Most traders close or roll before it.\",\"plain\":\"The day a long can start being handed the actual goods.\",\"related\":[\"physical-delivery\",\"expiry\",\"calendar-spread\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\",\"aka\":[\"FND\"]},{\"id\":\"calendar-spread\",\"term\":\"Calendar spread\",\"definition\":\"Buying one expiry and selling another of the same futures contract as a single order. A roll is a calendar spread, priced at the gap between the two months.\",\"plain\":\"One order that sells one month and buys another.\",\"related\":[\"expiry\",\"continuous-contract\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\"},{\"id\":\"continuous-contract\",\"term\":\"Continuous contract\",\"definition\":\"A long price series built by joining successive futures contracts at each roll, usually back-adjusted to remove the roll gaps.\",\"plain\":\"One long chart stitched from many short-lived contracts.\",\"related\":[\"back-adjustment\",\"calendar-spread\",\"expiry\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\"},{\"id\":\"back-adjustment\",\"term\":\"Back-adjustment\",\"definition\":\"Shifting every price before a roll by the roll gap, or scaling it by the price ratio, so a continuous chart has no false jumps. Recent prices stay as traded.\",\"plain\":\"Moving old prices so the chart doesn't jump at each roll.\",\"related\":[\"continuous-contract\",\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.2\"},{\"id\":\"contango\",\"term\":\"Contango\",\"definition\":\"A futures curve with later contracts priced above spot, usually because storage, financing and insurance cost money. A rolled long loses as each contract converges down to spot.\",\"plain\":\"Futures above spot: a rolled long position loses over time.\",\"related\":[\"backwardation\",\"roll-yield\",\"cost-of-carry\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"backwardation\",\"term\":\"Backwardation\",\"definition\":\"A futures curve with later contracts priced below spot, usually when inventories are low and holding the commodity now earns a premium. A rolled long gains as contracts converge up.\",\"plain\":\"Futures below spot: a rolled long gains as they rise toward it.\",\"related\":[\"contango\",\"convenience-yield\",\"roll-yield\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"roll-yield\",\"term\":\"Roll yield\",\"definition\":\"The gain or loss from rolling futures as each contract converges to spot, even when spot doesn't move: about spot ÷ future − 1 per roll.\",\"plain\":\"What rolling futures earns or costs you while spot stands still.\",\"related\":[\"contango\",\"backwardation\",\"carry\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"convenience-yield\",\"term\":\"Convenience yield\",\"definition\":\"The benefit of holding the physical commodity rather than a future, such as keeping a plant running; high when inventories are low.\",\"plain\":\"The value of having the goods in hand now, highest when they're scarce.\",\"related\":[\"backwardation\",\"cost-of-carry\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"cost-of-carry\",\"term\":\"Cost of carry\",\"definition\":\"The cost of holding an asset until a future date: financing plus storage and insurance, minus any income. Fair future ≈ spot + cost of carry − convenience yield.\",\"plain\":\"What it costs to hold something until later: interest, storage and insurance.\",\"related\":[\"contango\",\"convenience-yield\",\"overnight-financing\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.3\"},{\"id\":\"limit-move\",\"term\":\"Limit move\",\"definition\":\"A move to the exchange's daily price limit, beyond which no trades are allowed that day. A market locked at the limit may not trade at all, so stops can't fill.\",\"plain\":\"A day when price hits the exchange's maximum move and trading stops there.\",\"related\":[\"gap\",\"stop-loss\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.4\",\"aka\":[\"Limit up\",\"Limit down\",\"Locked limit\"]},{\"id\":\"inventory-report\",\"term\":\"Inventory report\",\"definition\":\"Scheduled data on how much of a commodity is held in storage, such as weekly oil and gas stock figures. Prices react to the surprise against expectations.\",\"plain\":\"A regular count of how much oil, gas or grain is in storage.\",\"related\":[\"surprise\",\"consensus\",\"economic-calendar\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.4\"},{\"id\":\"physical-delivery\",\"term\":\"Physical delivery\",\"definition\":\"Settling a futures contract by handing over the commodity itself at expiry, rather than paying cash.\",\"plain\":\"Ending a contract by actually delivering the barrels or bushels.\",\"related\":[\"first-notice-day\",\"expiry\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.4\",\"aka\":[\"Physical settlement\"]},{\"id\":\"stablecoin\",\"term\":\"Stablecoin\",\"definition\":\"A crypto token designed to hold a fixed value, usually one US dollar, backed by reserves or by an algorithm. It can lose that value.\",\"plain\":\"A crypto token meant to stay worth one dollar.\",\"related\":[\"de-peg\",\"cryptocurrency\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\"},{\"id\":\"de-peg\",\"term\":\"De-peg\",\"definition\":\"A stablecoin trading away from its fixed value, usually below it, when holders doubt its reserves or its design.\",\"plain\":\"When a 'stable' coin stops being worth its dollar.\",\"related\":[\"stablecoin\",\"counterparty-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\"},{\"id\":\"self-custody\",\"term\":\"Self-custody\",\"definition\":\"Holding your own crypto private keys in a wallet you control, instead of leaving coins with an exchange. It removes venue risk and adds the risk of losing the keys.\",\"plain\":\"Keeping your own keys: no exchange can freeze your coins, and nobody can recover lost keys.\",\"related\":[\"counterparty-risk\",\"cryptocurrency\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\"},{\"id\":\"token-unlock\",\"term\":\"Token unlock\",\"definition\":\"A scheduled release of previously locked tokens, such as team or investor allocations, into the tradable supply.\",\"plain\":\"A set date when new tokens can be sold into the market.\",\"related\":[\"cryptocurrency\",\"economic-calendar\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\"},{\"id\":\"counterparty-risk\",\"term\":\"Counterparty risk\",\"definition\":\"The risk that the venue, broker or custodian holding your assets fails, freezes withdrawals or doesn't pay.\",\"plain\":\"The risk that the firm holding your money or coins fails.\",\"related\":[\"counterparty\",\"segregated-funds\",\"self-custody\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.5\"},{\"id\":\"perpetual-swap\",\"term\":\"Perpetual swap\",\"definition\":\"A futures-like contract with no expiry, mostly on crypto, kept near its spot index by periodic funding payments between longs and shorts.\",\"plain\":\"A crypto future that never expires.\",\"related\":[\"funding-rate\",\"mark-price\",\"futures\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\",\"aka\":[\"Perpetual\",\"Perp\"]},{\"id\":\"funding-rate\",\"term\":\"Funding rate\",\"definition\":\"The periodic rate exchanged between longs and shorts in a perpetual swap, usually every 8 hours and charged on position value. When positive, longs pay shorts.\",\"plain\":\"The fee longs and shorts pay each other every few hours to keep a perpetual near spot.\",\"related\":[\"perpetual-swap\",\"premium-index\",\"overnight-financing\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\",\"aka\":[\"Funding\"]},{\"id\":\"mark-price\",\"term\":\"Mark price\",\"definition\":\"A reference price built from a spot index across venues, used to value perpetual and futures positions and to trigger liquidations.\",\"plain\":\"The fair price a venue uses to value your position, instead of the last trade.\",\"related\":[\"perpetual-swap\",\"liquidation-price\",\"last-price\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\"},{\"id\":\"premium-index\",\"term\":\"Premium index\",\"definition\":\"How far a perpetual swap trades above or below its spot index, as a percentage; the main input to the funding rate.\",\"plain\":\"How much the perpetual trades above or below spot.\",\"related\":[\"funding-rate\",\"perpetual-swap\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.6\"},{\"id\":\"liquidation-price\",\"term\":\"Liquidation price\",\"definition\":\"The price at which a venue force-closes a leveraged position because its margin has fallen to the maintenance level. For an isolated long, about entry × (1 − 1 ÷ leverage + maintenance rate).\",\"plain\":\"The price where the venue closes your trade for you.\",\"related\":[\"isolated-margin\",\"mark-price\",\"margin-close-out\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\"},{\"id\":\"isolated-margin\",\"term\":\"Isolated margin\",\"definition\":\"A margin mode in which each position has its own margin; a liquidation can take only that margin.\",\"plain\":\"Each trade's margin stands alone, so one trade can't drain the account.\",\"related\":[\"cross-margin\",\"liquidation-price\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\"},{\"id\":\"cross-margin\",\"term\":\"Cross margin\",\"definition\":\"A margin mode in which all positions share the account's balance: fewer liquidations from small moves, but one large loss can take the whole account.\",\"plain\":\"All your trades share one pot of margin.\",\"related\":[\"isolated-margin\",\"liquidation-price\",\"margin\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\"},{\"id\":\"insurance-fund\",\"term\":\"Insurance fund\",\"definition\":\"A venue's reserve that absorbs losses when liquidated positions close below zero equity, so traders on the other side still get paid.\",\"plain\":\"The venue's safety pot for liquidations that go below zero.\",\"related\":[\"auto-deleveraging\",\"liquidation-price\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\"},{\"id\":\"auto-deleveraging\",\"term\":\"Auto-deleveraging\",\"definition\":\"When the insurance fund can't cover a liquidation that closed below zero equity, the venue closes opposite positions, usually ranked by profit and leverage, to cover it.\",\"plain\":\"The venue closing winners' positions to cover losses it can't absorb.\",\"related\":[\"insurance-fund\",\"liquidation-price\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.7\",\"aka\":[\"ADL\"]},{\"id\":\"duration\",\"term\":\"Duration\",\"definition\":\"The approximate percentage change in a bond's price for a one-percentage-point change in its yield (modified duration). A duration of 8.95 means about −8.95% if yields rise one point.\",\"plain\":\"How much a bond's price moves, in %, when yields move one point.\",\"related\":[\"yield\",\"dv01\",\"convexity\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.8\",\"aka\":[\"Modified duration\"]},{\"id\":\"dv01\",\"term\":\"DV01\",\"definition\":\"The change in a position's value for a one-basis-point (0.01%) move in yield: about duration × value × 0.0001.\",\"plain\":\"Money gained or lost per 0.01% move in yields.\",\"related\":[\"duration\",\"basis-point\",\"yield\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.8\",\"aka\":[\"Dollar value of a basis point\",\"PVBP\"]},{\"id\":\"convexity\",\"term\":\"Convexity\",\"definition\":\"The curvature of a bond's price–yield relation: duration's straight-line estimate overstates price falls and understates rises, more so for large moves.\",\"plain\":\"Bond prices fall a little less, and rise a little more, than duration says.\",\"related\":[\"duration\",\"yield\"],\"lesson\":\"3.14.8\"},{\"id\":\"option\",\"term\":\"Option\",\"definition\":\"A contract that gives its buyer the right, but not the obligation, to buy or sell an underlying asset at a set price on or before a set date. The buyer pays a premium; the seller takes on the obligation.\",\"plain\":\"A contract giving the buyer a right, but not an obligation, to trade an asset at a set price.\",\"related\":[\"call-option\",\"put-option\",\"strike-price\",\"option-premium\",\"derivative\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"call-option\",\"term\":\"Call option\",\"definition\":\"An option that gives its holder the right to buy the underlying at the strike price, on or before expiry.\",\"plain\":\"The right to buy the underlying at the strike price until or at expiry.\",\"related\":[\"option\",\"put-option\",\"strike-price\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"call\"]},{\"id\":\"put-option\",\"term\":\"Put option\",\"definition\":\"An option that gives its holder the right to sell the underlying at the strike price, on or before expiry.\",\"plain\":\"The right to sell the underlying at the strike price until or at expiry.\",\"related\":[\"option\",\"call-option\",\"strike-price\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"put\"]},{\"id\":\"strike-price\",\"term\":\"Strike price\",\"definition\":\"The fixed price at which an option's holder may buy (call) or sell (put) the underlying.\",\"plain\":\"The price at which an option lets you buy or sell the underlying.\",\"related\":[\"option\",\"in-the-money\",\"out-of-the-money\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"strike\",\"exercise price\"]},{\"id\":\"expiration-date\",\"term\":\"Expiration date\",\"definition\":\"The last day on which an option can be exercised. After it, the option is settled or expires worthless.\",\"plain\":\"The last day an option can be exercised; after it, the option is settled or worthless.\",\"related\":[\"option\",\"time-to-expiry\",\"time-decay\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"expiry\"]},{\"id\":\"option-premium\",\"term\":\"Option premium\",\"definition\":\"The price of an option, paid by the buyer and received by the writer. It equals intrinsic value plus extrinsic value.\",\"plain\":\"The price the option buyer pays and the seller receives.\",\"related\":[\"intrinsic-value\",\"extrinsic-value\",\"option-writer\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"premium\"]},{\"id\":\"in-the-money\",\"term\":\"In the money\",\"definition\":\"An option with intrinsic value: a call whose strike is below the underlying price, or a put whose strike is above it.\",\"plain\":\"An option with intrinsic value: a call with a strike below the price, a put above it.\",\"related\":[\"at-the-money\",\"out-of-the-money\",\"intrinsic-value\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"ITM\"]},{\"id\":\"at-the-money\",\"term\":\"At the money\",\"definition\":\"An option whose strike is at, or very near, the current price of the underlying.\",\"plain\":\"An option whose strike is at or very near the current price.\",\"related\":[\"in-the-money\",\"out-of-the-money\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"ATM\"]},{\"id\":\"out-of-the-money\",\"term\":\"Out of the money\",\"definition\":\"An option with no intrinsic value: a call whose strike is above the underlying price, or a put whose strike is below it. Its whole premium is extrinsic value.\",\"plain\":\"An option with no intrinsic value: a call with a strike above the price, a put below it.\",\"related\":[\"in-the-money\",\"at-the-money\",\"extrinsic-value\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"OTM\"]},{\"id\":\"intrinsic-value\",\"term\":\"Intrinsic value\",\"definition\":\"What an option would be worth if exercised now: max(S − K, 0) for a call, max(K − S, 0) for a put.\",\"plain\":\"What an option would be worth if exercised right now; never below zero.\",\"related\":[\"extrinsic-value\",\"in-the-money\",\"option-premium\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\"},{\"id\":\"extrinsic-value\",\"term\":\"Extrinsic value\",\"definition\":\"The part of an option's premium above its intrinsic value, paid for the time left and the chance of a larger move. It decays to zero at expiry.\",\"plain\":\"The part of the premium above intrinsic value, paid for time and volatility.\",\"related\":[\"intrinsic-value\",\"time-decay\",\"option-premium\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.1\",\"aka\":[\"time value\"]},{\"id\":\"payoff-diagram\",\"term\":\"Payoff diagram\",\"definition\":\"A chart of a position's profit or loss at expiry across possible prices of the underlying, premiums included.\",\"plain\":\"A chart of a position's profit or loss at expiry across possible prices.\",\"related\":[\"option-breakeven\",\"option\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"option-breakeven\",\"term\":\"Option breakeven\",\"definition\":\"The underlying price at expiry at which an option position neither gains nor loses, premium included: strike plus premium for a long call, strike minus premium for a long put.\",\"plain\":\"The price at expiry where an option position neither gains nor loses, premium included.\",\"related\":[\"payoff-diagram\",\"option-premium\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"option-writer\",\"term\":\"Option writer\",\"definition\":\"The seller of an option, who receives the premium and takes on the obligation to deliver or buy the underlying if assigned.\",\"plain\":\"The seller of an option, who receives the premium and takes on the obligation.\",\"related\":[\"assignment\",\"option-premium\",\"option\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\",\"aka\":[\"writer\",\"option seller\"]},{\"id\":\"exercise\",\"term\":\"Exercise\",\"definition\":\"The holder's use of an option's right: buying the underlying at the strike with a call, or selling it at the strike with a put.\",\"plain\":\"Using an option's right to buy or sell the underlying at the strike.\",\"related\":[\"assignment\",\"american-option\",\"european-option\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"assignment\",\"term\":\"Assignment\",\"definition\":\"The notice that obliges an option writer to fulfil the contract after a holder exercises: deliver the underlying at the strike on a call, or buy it on a put.\",\"plain\":\"The notice that obliges an option writer to deliver or take the underlying at the strike.\",\"related\":[\"exercise\",\"option-writer\",\"pin-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"american-option\",\"term\":\"American option\",\"definition\":\"An option that can be exercised on any day up to and including expiry. Most US stock options are American.\",\"plain\":\"An option that can be exercised on any day up to expiry.\",\"related\":[\"european-option\",\"exercise\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"european-option\",\"term\":\"European option\",\"definition\":\"An option that can be exercised only at expiry, such as cash-settled index options. LABX options are European.\",\"plain\":\"An option that can be exercised only at expiry.\",\"related\":[\"american-option\",\"exercise\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"pin-risk\",\"term\":\"Pin risk\",\"definition\":\"The uncertainty, when the underlying closes at or very near a short option's strike at expiry, about whether you will be assigned.\",\"plain\":\"Not knowing whether you will be assigned when the price ends right at a short option's strike.\",\"related\":[\"assignment\",\"strike-price\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.2\"},{\"id\":\"put-call-parity\",\"term\":\"Put-call parity\",\"definition\":\"The no-arbitrage relation between European calls and puts with the same strike and expiry: C − P = S − K·e^(−rT), without dividends.\",\"plain\":\"The fixed link between call, put, underlying and cash prices that rules out riskless profit.\",\"related\":[\"synthetic-position\",\"arbitrage\",\"risk-free-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"synthetic-position\",\"term\":\"Synthetic position\",\"definition\":\"A combination of options, the underlying and cash that copies another position's payoff, such as a long call plus a short put copying a long position in the underlying.\",\"plain\":\"A mix of options, underlying and cash that copies another position's payoff.\",\"related\":[\"put-call-parity\",\"option\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"arbitrage\",\"term\":\"Arbitrage\",\"definition\":\"Locking in a riskless profit from prices that are inconsistent with each other. In liquid markets such gaps close almost instantly, and costs usually exceed what's left.\",\"plain\":\"Locking in a riskless profit from inconsistent prices; in liquid markets it disappears almost instantly.\",\"related\":[\"put-call-parity\",\"spread\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"risk-free-rate\",\"term\":\"Risk-free rate\",\"definition\":\"The interest earned on cash with no default risk, usually a short-term government rate. In option pricing it discounts the strike paid at expiry.\",\"plain\":\"The interest earned on cash with no default risk; one input to option prices.\",\"related\":[\"put-call-parity\",\"black-scholes\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"cash-secured-put\",\"term\":\"Cash-secured put\",\"definition\":\"A short put held with enough cash to buy the underlying at the strike if assigned. By put-call parity its payoff equals a covered call's.\",\"plain\":\"Selling a put while holding enough cash to buy the underlying if assigned.\",\"related\":[\"covered-call\",\"put-call-parity\",\"put-option\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.3\"},{\"id\":\"black-scholes\",\"term\":\"Black–Scholes model\",\"definition\":\"A formula that prices European options from the underlying price, strike, time to expiry, volatility and interest rate, assuming lognormal prices, constant volatility and no jumps.\",\"plain\":\"A formula that prices European options from price, strike, time, volatility and interest rate.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"time-to-expiry\",\"risk-free-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.4\",\"aka\":[\"Black–Scholes–Merton\"]},{\"id\":\"time-to-expiry\",\"term\":\"Time to expiry\",\"definition\":\"The time left until an option expires, written T and measured in years in pricing formulas: 30 days is 30 ÷ 365 = 0.0822.\",\"plain\":\"The time left until an option expires, measured in years in pricing formulas.\",\"related\":[\"expiration-date\",\"time-decay\",\"black-scholes\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.4\"},{\"id\":\"greeks\",\"term\":\"Greeks\",\"definition\":\"Sensitivities of an option's price to its inputs: delta and gamma to the underlying price, theta to time, vega to implied volatility and rho to interest rates.\",\"plain\":\"Measures of how an option's price reacts to changes in price, time, volatility and rates.\",\"related\":[\"delta\",\"gamma\",\"theta\",\"vega\",\"rho\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"delta\",\"term\":\"Delta\",\"definition\":\"The change in an option's price for a one-unit move in the underlying: 0 to 1 for calls, −1 to 0 for puts. It is also the hedge ratio.\",\"plain\":\"How much an option's price changes when the underlying moves by one unit.\",\"related\":[\"gamma\",\"hedge-ratio\",\"delta-neutral\",\"greeks\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\",\"aka\":[\"Δ\"]},{\"id\":\"gamma\",\"term\":\"Gamma\",\"definition\":\"The change in an option's delta for a one-unit move in the underlying. It is largest for at-the-money options close to expiry.\",\"plain\":\"How much delta changes when the underlying moves by one unit.\",\"related\":[\"delta\",\"theta\",\"greeks\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\",\"aka\":[\"Γ\"]},{\"id\":\"delta-neutral\",\"term\":\"Delta neutral\",\"definition\":\"A position whose combined delta is zero, so its value barely changes for small moves in the underlying.\",\"plain\":\"A position whose value barely changes for small moves in the underlying.\",\"related\":[\"delta\",\"delta-hedging\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"delta-hedging\",\"term\":\"Delta hedging\",\"definition\":\"Trading the underlying to bring an options position's delta to zero, and adjusting as delta changes with the price.\",\"plain\":\"Trading the underlying to keep an options position delta neutral as prices move.\",\"related\":[\"delta-neutral\",\"hedge-ratio\",\"gamma-scalping\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\"},{\"id\":\"0dte-option\",\"term\":\"0DTE option\",\"definition\":\"An option traded on its expiration day, with zero days to expiry. With almost no time left, its gamma is extreme and its price swings violently.\",\"plain\":\"An option that expires on the same day it is traded; its price swings violently.\",\"related\":[\"gamma\",\"time-decay\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.5\",\"aka\":[\"zero days to expiry\"]},{\"id\":\"theta\",\"term\":\"Theta\",\"definition\":\"The change in an option's price as one day passes, all else equal. Negative for long options, positive for short ones.\",\"plain\":\"How much value an option loses per day as time passes.\",\"related\":[\"time-decay\",\"gamma\",\"greeks\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\",\"aka\":[\"Θ\"]},{\"id\":\"vega\",\"term\":\"Vega\",\"definition\":\"The change in an option's price for a one-point change in implied volatility. Largest for at-the-money, long-dated options.\",\"plain\":\"How much an option's price changes when implied volatility moves one percentage point.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"greeks\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\",\"aka\":[\"ν\"]},{\"id\":\"rho\",\"term\":\"Rho\",\"definition\":\"The change in an option's price for a one-percentage-point change in interest rates. It matters mostly for long-dated options.\",\"plain\":\"How much an option's price changes when interest rates move one percentage point.\",\"related\":[\"risk-free-rate\",\"greeks\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\",\"aka\":[\"ρ\"]},{\"id\":\"time-decay\",\"term\":\"Time decay\",\"definition\":\"The loss of an option's extrinsic value as expiry approaches, measured by theta. It follows the square root of time, so it accelerates in the final weeks.\",\"plain\":\"The loss of an option's extrinsic value as expiry approaches; fastest near the end.\",\"related\":[\"theta\",\"extrinsic-value\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"gamma-scalping\",\"term\":\"Gamma scalping\",\"definition\":\"Repeatedly re-hedging the delta of a long options position, selling the underlying after rises and buying after falls, to earn from price swings against the option's time decay.\",\"plain\":\"Re-hedging a long options position again and again to capture gains from price swings.\",\"related\":[\"delta-hedging\",\"gamma\",\"theta\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.6\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-skew\",\"term\":\"Volatility skew\",\"definition\":\"Different implied volatilities across the strikes of one expiry. On index and stock options, downside puts usually carry higher implied volatility than calls.\",\"plain\":\"Different implied volatilities across strikes of one expiry, often higher for downside puts.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-smile\",\"volatility-surface\",\"implied-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-smile\",\"term\":\"Volatility smile\",\"definition\":\"A shape of implied volatility across strikes in which both far out-of-the-money puts and far out-of-the-money calls carry more implied volatility than at-the-money options.\",\"plain\":\"A skew where both far-out puts and far-out calls carry higher implied volatility.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-skew\",\"volatility-surface\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"iv-term-structure\",\"term\":\"IV term structure\",\"definition\":\"Implied volatility across expiries for the same underlying. It usually rises with time to expiry in calm markets and inverts in crises.\",\"plain\":\"Implied volatility across expiries; usually rising with time, inverted in crises.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-surface\",\"implied-volatility\",\"event-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-surface\",\"term\":\"Volatility surface\",\"definition\":\"Implied volatility across all strikes and expiries of an underlying, combining the skew and the term structure into one map.\",\"plain\":\"Implied volatility across all strikes and expiries, shown as a single map.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-skew\",\"iv-term-structure\",\"implied-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"iv-rank\",\"term\":\"IV rank\",\"definition\":\"Where current implied volatility sits within its range over the past year, from 0 (the low) to 100 (the high). A context tool, not a tested signal.\",\"plain\":\"Where current implied volatility sits within its past year's range; context, not a signal.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.7\"},{\"id\":\"covered-call\",\"term\":\"Covered call\",\"definition\":\"Owning the underlying and selling a call on it. The premium adds income and a small cushion, but the gain is capped at the strike and almost all of the downside remains.\",\"plain\":\"Owning the underlying and selling a call on it to collect premium, which caps the upside.\",\"related\":[\"call-option\",\"cash-secured-put\",\"collar\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"protective-put\",\"term\":\"Protective put\",\"definition\":\"Owning the underlying and buying a put on it, which sets a floor on losses at the strike plus the premium, even through gaps.\",\"plain\":\"Owning the underlying and buying a put to set a floor under losses.\",\"related\":[\"put-option\",\"collar\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"collar\",\"term\":\"Collar\",\"definition\":\"Owning the underlying, buying a put below the price and selling a call above it, often for near-zero net premium, which limits both losses and gains.\",\"plain\":\"Owning the underlying, buying a put and selling a call to limit both losses and gains.\",\"related\":[\"protective-put\",\"covered-call\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.8\"},{\"id\":\"vertical-spread\",\"term\":\"Vertical spread\",\"definition\":\"Buying one option and selling another of the same type and expiry at a different strike. Its maximum gain and maximum loss are both fixed by the strikes and the premium.\",\"plain\":\"Buying one option and selling another of the same type and expiry at a different strike.\",\"related\":[\"debit-spread\",\"spread-width\",\"defined-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"debit-spread\",\"term\":\"Debit spread\",\"definition\":\"A vertical spread that costs money to open. Its maximum loss is the debit paid; its maximum gain is the spread width minus the debit.\",\"plain\":\"A vertical spread you pay for; the most you can lose is what you paid.\",\"related\":[\"vertical-spread\",\"spread-width\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"spread-width\",\"term\":\"Spread width\",\"definition\":\"The distance between the two strikes of an options spread. It caps what the spread can be worth, and so its maximum gain and loss.\",\"plain\":\"The distance between the two strikes of a spread; it caps gains and losses.\",\"related\":[\"vertical-spread\",\"debit-spread\",\"iron-condor\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"defined-risk\",\"term\":\"Defined risk\",\"definition\":\"A position whose maximum possible loss is known before entry, such as a long option or a vertical spread, as opposed to a naked short option.\",\"plain\":\"A position whose maximum possible loss is known before you enter.\",\"related\":[\"vertical-spread\",\"iron-condor\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.9\"},{\"id\":\"strangle\",\"term\":\"Strangle\",\"definition\":\"Buying an out-of-the-money call and an out-of-the-money put with the same expiry. It costs less than a straddle but needs a bigger move to profit.\",\"plain\":\"Buying an out-of-the-money call and put; cheaper than a straddle but needs a bigger move.\",\"related\":[\"straddle\",\"long-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.10\"},{\"id\":\"long-volatility\",\"term\":\"Long volatility\",\"definition\":\"A position that gains when the underlying moves more than options imply, or when implied volatility rises, such as a long straddle or strangle.\",\"plain\":\"A position that profits when prices move more than options imply.\",\"related\":[\"straddle\",\"strangle\",\"short-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.10\"},{\"id\":\"event-volatility\",\"term\":\"Event volatility\",\"definition\":\"Extra implied volatility in options that expire just after a scheduled event, such as an earnings report. It collapses once the event passes.\",\"plain\":\"Extra implied volatility priced into options that expire just after a scheduled event.\",\"related\":[\"iv-crush\",\"implied-move\",\"iv-term-structure\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.10\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-risk-premium\",\"term\":\"Volatility risk premium\",\"definition\":\"The average amount by which implied volatility exceeds the volatility that later occurs. It pays option sellers on average, as compensation for crash risk.\",\"plain\":\"The average gap by which implied volatility exceeds the volatility that later occurs.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"realised-volatility\",\"short-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\",\"aka\":[\"VRP\"]},{\"id\":\"short-volatility\",\"term\":\"Short volatility\",\"definition\":\"A position that gains when the underlying moves less than options imply, such as a short straddle or iron condor. It earns small, steady amounts and is exposed to sudden spikes.\",\"plain\":\"A position that profits when prices move less than options imply; exposed to sudden spikes.\",\"related\":[\"long-volatility\",\"iron-condor\",\"tail-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\"},{\"id\":\"iron-condor\",\"term\":\"Iron condor\",\"definition\":\"A short out-of-the-money put spread plus a short out-of-the-money call spread on the same expiry: a short strangle with bought wings, so the maximum loss is the wing width minus the credit.\",\"plain\":\"Selling a strangle and buying further-out options as wings, so the maximum loss is capped.\",\"related\":[\"short-volatility\",\"strangle\",\"defined-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\"},{\"id\":\"tail-risk\",\"term\":\"Tail risk\",\"definition\":\"The risk of rare, extreme losses far beyond normal day-to-day moves, the kind a strategy's recent record may not contain.\",\"plain\":\"The risk of rare, extreme losses far beyond normal day-to-day moves.\",\"related\":[\"fat-tails\",\"tail-event\",\"short-volatility\",\"risk-of-ruin\"],\"lesson\":\"4.1.11\"},{\"id\":\"implementation-shortfall\",\"term\":\"Implementation shortfall\",\"definition\":\"The difference between a paper trade filled in full at the decision price and the real result: delay, spread, market impact, fees and the opportunity cost of units that never filled.\",\"plain\":\"The full cost of executing a trade compared with the price when you decided to trade.\",\"related\":[\"decision-price\",\"arrival-price\",\"opportunity-cost\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\",\"aka\":[\"IS\"]},{\"id\":\"decision-price\",\"term\":\"Decision price\",\"definition\":\"The market price, usually the mid, at the moment the decision to trade is made; the reference for implementation shortfall.\",\"plain\":\"The market price at the moment you decided to trade.\",\"related\":[\"arrival-price\",\"implementation-shortfall\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"arrival-price\",\"term\":\"Arrival price\",\"definition\":\"The market price, usually the mid, when an order reaches the market; its gap from the decision price is the delay cost.\",\"plain\":\"The market price when your order reaches the market.\",\"related\":[\"decision-price\",\"slippage\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"opportunity-cost\",\"term\":\"Opportunity cost\",\"definition\":\"In execution, the price move missed on the part of an order that never filled, measured from the decision price.\",\"plain\":\"The gain you missed on the part of an order that never got filled.\",\"related\":[\"implementation-shortfall\",\"partial-fill\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"parent-order\",\"term\":\"Parent order\",\"definition\":\"A large order, set by one decision, that a trader or an execution algorithm splits into smaller child orders over time.\",\"plain\":\"A large order that an algorithm splits into many smaller orders.\",\"related\":[\"child-order\",\"execution-algorithm\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"child-order\",\"term\":\"Child order\",\"definition\":\"One of the smaller orders sent to the market to work a parent order.\",\"plain\":\"One of the small orders sent to the market to fill a parent order.\",\"related\":[\"parent-order\",\"fill\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.1\"},{\"id\":\"temporary-impact\",\"term\":\"Temporary impact\",\"definition\":\"The part of the price move caused by your trading that reverses after you stop, as liquidity refills the book.\",\"plain\":\"The part of your price impact that fades after you stop trading.\",\"related\":[\"permanent-impact\",\"square-root-law\",\"resiliency\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"permanent-impact\",\"term\":\"Permanent impact\",\"definition\":\"The part of the price move caused by your trading that remains after you stop, because other traders infer information from your flow.\",\"plain\":\"The part of your price impact that stays, because the market learned something from your trades.\",\"related\":[\"temporary-impact\",\"square-root-law\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"square-root-law\",\"term\":\"Square-root law\",\"definition\":\"The empirical rule that market impact ≈ Y × σ × √(Q ÷ V): it grows with the square root of order size Q over daily volume V, scaled by daily volatility σ, with Y about 1.\",\"plain\":\"Market impact grows with the square root of order size relative to daily volume.\",\"related\":[\"participation-rate\",\"temporary-impact\",\"permanent-impact\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\",\"aka\":[\"Square-root impact\"]},{\"id\":\"participation-rate\",\"term\":\"Participation rate\",\"definition\":\"Your traded volume as a share of the market's total volume over the same period; 10% means one unit in ten traded is yours.\",\"plain\":\"Your traded volume as a share of the market's volume over the same period.\",\"related\":[\"pov-algorithm\",\"square-root-law\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.2\"},{\"id\":\"execution-algorithm\",\"term\":\"Execution algorithm\",\"definition\":\"A set of rules that splits a parent order into child orders and times them to cut cost against a benchmark, such as TWAP, VWAP, POV or implementation shortfall.\",\"plain\":\"A set of rules that splits and times an order to reduce trading cost.\",\"related\":[\"parent-order\",\"twap\",\"vwap-algorithm\",\"pov-algorithm\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\",\"aka\":[\"Algo\"]},{\"id\":\"twap\",\"term\":\"TWAP\",\"definition\":\"Time-weighted average price: an algorithm that sends equal slices at equal time intervals until the order is done.\",\"plain\":\"An algorithm that trades equal slices at equal time intervals.\",\"related\":[\"vwap-algorithm\",\"execution-algorithm\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\",\"aka\":[\"Time-weighted average price\"]},{\"id\":\"vwap-algorithm\",\"term\":\"VWAP algorithm\",\"definition\":\"An algorithm that sizes its slices to the market's expected volume curve through the day, aiming to fill close to the day's VWAP.\",\"plain\":\"An algorithm that trades in step with the market's expected volume through the day.\",\"related\":[\"vwap\",\"twap\",\"pov-algorithm\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"pov-algorithm\",\"term\":\"POV algorithm\",\"definition\":\"Percentage of volume: an algorithm that trades a fixed share of the market's live volume, such as 10%, so it speeds up and slows down with the market.\",\"plain\":\"An algorithm that trades a fixed share of the market's volume.\",\"related\":[\"participation-rate\",\"vwap-algorithm\",\"execution-algorithm\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\",\"aka\":[\"POV\",\"Percentage of volume\"]},{\"id\":\"timing-risk\",\"term\":\"Timing risk\",\"definition\":\"The risk that price moves against you while part of an order is still unfilled; it grows the longer execution takes.\",\"plain\":\"The risk that price moves against you while part of your order is still unfilled.\",\"related\":[\"opportunity-cost\",\"implementation-shortfall\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"information-leakage\",\"term\":\"Information leakage\",\"definition\":\"Other traders inferring your intentions from the pattern of your orders and trading ahead of you, which raises your cost.\",\"plain\":\"When the pattern of your orders lets others guess what you're doing and trade ahead.\",\"related\":[\"execution-algorithm\",\"high-frequency-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.3\"},{\"id\":\"latency\",\"term\":\"Latency\",\"definition\":\"The delay between an event and your order or cancel reaching the market, through data feeds, your systems and the network.\",\"plain\":\"The delay between something happening and your order reaching the market.\",\"related\":[\"co-location\",\"stale-quote\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"stale-quote\",\"term\":\"Stale quote\",\"definition\":\"A resting order whose price no longer reflects the latest information, so faster traders can trade against it at a profit.\",\"plain\":\"A resting order whose price no longer reflects the latest news.\",\"related\":[\"latency\",\"adverse-selection\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"co-location\",\"term\":\"Co-location\",\"definition\":\"Placing trading servers in an exchange's data centre, next to its matching engine, to cut latency.\",\"plain\":\"Putting trading computers right next to the exchange's, to be faster.\",\"related\":[\"latency\",\"high-frequency-trading\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.4\"},{\"id\":\"trading-halt\",\"term\":\"Trading halt\",\"definition\":\"A pause in trading of one instrument or a whole market, for pending news, a volatility pause or a circuit breaker; it usually ends with a reopening auction.\",\"plain\":\"A pause in trading, often for pending news or a very fast move.\",\"related\":[\"circuit-breaker\",\"limit-up-limit-down\",\"reopening-auction\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"Trading pause\"]},{\"id\":\"circuit-breaker\",\"term\":\"Circuit breaker\",\"definition\":\"A market-wide halt triggered by a large index fall. In the US, S&P 500 falls of 7% and 13% halt trading for 15 minutes before 15:25 ET; a 20% fall closes the market for the day.\",\"plain\":\"A market-wide halt triggered by a big fall in the index, such as 7%, 13% or 20%.\",\"related\":[\"trading-halt\",\"limit-up-limit-down\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"MWCB\",\"Market-wide circuit breaker\"]},{\"id\":\"limit-up-limit-down\",\"term\":\"Limit up–limit down\",\"definition\":\"The US single-stock rule that blocks trades outside price bands around the last 5 minutes' average price, and pauses the stock for 5 minutes if it stays at a band for 15 seconds.\",\"plain\":\"A US rule that pauses a stock when its price hits bands around its recent average.\",\"related\":[\"trading-halt\",\"circuit-breaker\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\",\"aka\":[\"LULD\"]},{\"id\":\"reopening-auction\",\"term\":\"Reopening auction\",\"definition\":\"The call auction that restarts trading after a halt; stops can trigger at its price, far from their levels.\",\"plain\":\"The single-price auction that restarts trading after a halt.\",\"related\":[\"call-auction\",\"trading-halt\",\"gap\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"volatility-auction\",\"term\":\"Volatility auction\",\"definition\":\"A short call auction that replaces continuous trading when a trade would move the price beyond a set tolerance, as on European exchanges.\",\"plain\":\"A short auction that takes over when the price would jump too far.\",\"related\":[\"call-auction\",\"trading-halt\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.5\"},{\"id\":\"benchmark-fix\",\"term\":\"Benchmark fix\",\"definition\":\"A reference price set from trading in a short daily window, such as the WM/Reuters FX fix around 16:00 London; funds trade at it, so volume crowds into the window.\",\"plain\":\"A reference price set in a short daily window, such as the 4 pm London FX fix.\",\"related\":[\"closing-auction\",\"month-end-rebalancing\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\",\"aka\":[\"Fix\",\"London 4 pm fix\"]},{\"id\":\"month-end-rebalancing\",\"term\":\"Month-end rebalancing\",\"definition\":\"Mechanical trades by funds at month or quarter end to bring holdings back to their target weights, often concentrated at the close.\",\"plain\":\"Big scheduled trades by funds at month or quarter end to restore their target mix.\",\"related\":[\"benchmark-fix\",\"closing-auction\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"witching-day\",\"term\":\"Witching day\",\"definition\":\"A quarterly day, the third Friday of March, June, September and December in the US, when stock options, index options and index futures expire together and volume rises.\",\"plain\":\"A day each quarter when many futures and options expire together.\",\"related\":[\"option-pinning\",\"open-interest\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\",\"aka\":[\"Triple witching\",\"Quadruple witching\"]},{\"id\":\"option-pinning\",\"term\":\"Option pinning\",\"definition\":\"The tendency of a stock to close near a strike with large open interest on an expiry day, usually linked to dealers' delta hedging.\",\"plain\":\"A price's tendency to finish near a busy option strike on expiry day.\",\"related\":[\"open-interest\",\"witching-day\"],\"lesson\":\"4.2.6\"},{\"id\":\"family-wise-error-rate\",\"term\":\"Family-wise error rate\",\"definition\":\"The probability of at least one false discovery across a whole set of tests. Bonferroni keeps it at or below the chosen level by dividing the threshold by the number of tests.\",\"plain\":\"The chance of at least one false discovery across a whole set of tests.\",\"related\":[\"multiple-testing\",\"bonferroni-correction\",\"false-positive\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\",\"aka\":[\"FWER\"]},{\"id\":\"reality-check\",\"term\":\"Reality Check\",\"definition\":\"White's (2000) bootstrap test of whether the best of many rules tried on the same data beats a benchmark by more than the best of them would by luck alone.\",\"plain\":\"A test of whether the best of many tried rules is better than luck.\",\"related\":[\"bootstrap\",\"multiple-testing\",\"spa-test\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\",\"aka\":[\"White's Reality Check\"]},{\"id\":\"spa-test\",\"term\":\"SPA test\",\"definition\":\"Hansen's (2005) test for superior predictive ability, a refinement of the Reality Check that stops very poor rules in the family from diluting the test.\",\"plain\":\"A sharper version of the Reality Check.\",\"related\":[\"reality-check\",\"bootstrap\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\",\"aka\":[\"superior predictive ability test\"]},{\"id\":\"sharpe-haircut\",\"term\":\"Sharpe haircut\",\"definition\":\"A reduction of a backtest's Sharpe ratio for the number of tests behind it. Harvey and Liu show it is nonlinear: marginal Sharpe ratios are cut hard, high ones only moderately.\",\"plain\":\"A cut to a backtest's Sharpe ratio for how many tests were tried.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-ratio\",\"multiple-testing\",\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"selection-bias\",\"term\":\"Selection bias\",\"definition\":\"Distortion from keeping only the best-looking results out of many: the selected result is inflated by the luck that got it selected.\",\"plain\":\"Keeping only the best-looking results, which makes them look better than they are.\",\"related\":[\"winners-curse\",\"multiple-testing\",\"survivorship-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.1\"},{\"id\":\"expected-maximum-sharpe\",\"term\":\"Expected maximum Sharpe\",\"definition\":\"The best Sharpe ratio expected from N trials with no edge, by luck alone: about 1.57, 2.28 and 2.53 times the trials' Sharpe spread for N = 10, 50 and 100.\",\"plain\":\"The best Sharpe ratio luck alone would give N trials.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-ratio\",\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\",\"selection-bias\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.2\",\"aka\":[\"E[max]\"]},{\"id\":\"probabilistic-sharpe-ratio\",\"term\":\"Probabilistic Sharpe ratio\",\"definition\":\"The probability that a strategy's true Sharpe ratio exceeds a benchmark, given the measured Sharpe, the number of observations, skewness and kurtosis.\",\"plain\":\"The chance that the true Sharpe ratio beats a chosen benchmark.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-ratio\",\"skew\",\"kurtosis\",\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.2\",\"aka\":[\"PSR\"]},{\"id\":\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\",\"term\":\"Deflated Sharpe ratio\",\"definition\":\"A probabilistic Sharpe ratio whose benchmark is the expected maximum Sharpe of all the trials run, so it corrects for selection as well as for non-normal returns.\",\"plain\":\"The chance a Sharpe ratio beats the best that luck gives all your trials.\",\"related\":[\"probabilistic-sharpe-ratio\",\"expected-maximum-sharpe\",\"trial-count\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.2\",\"aka\":[\"DSR\"]},{\"id\":\"minimum-backtest-length\",\"term\":\"Minimum backtest length\",\"definition\":\"The years of data needed before the best of N zero-edge trials is no longer expected to reach a target Sharpe ratio: (E[max] ÷ target)².\",\"plain\":\"How many years a backtest needs before its Sharpe ratio beats what luck gives N trials.\",\"related\":[\"expected-maximum-sharpe\",\"track-record-length\",\"trial-count\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.3\",\"aka\":[\"MinBTL\"]},{\"id\":\"sharpe-standard-error\",\"term\":\"Sharpe standard error\",\"definition\":\"The typical error of a measured Sharpe ratio: √((1 + SR²/2) ÷ n) for independent returns, in the data's own frequency. For an annual Sharpe from daily data, about 1 ÷ √years.\",\"plain\":\"How far a measured Sharpe ratio may be from the true one; it shrinks with the square root of years.\",\"related\":[\"standard-error\",\"sharpe-ratio\",\"track-record-length\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"track-record-length\",\"term\":\"Track record length\",\"definition\":\"How long a strategy's results have been measured. The error bars on its statistics shrink only with the square root of that length.\",\"plain\":\"How long results have been measured; longer records give tighter estimates.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-standard-error\",\"sample-size\",\"minimum-backtest-length\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.3\"},{\"id\":\"probability-of-backtest-overfitting\",\"term\":\"Probability of backtest overfitting\",\"definition\":\"The share of in-sample/out-of-sample splits in which a strategy family's in-sample winner ranks at or below the median out of sample, estimated by CSCV.\",\"plain\":\"How often the best in-sample variant ranks in the bottom half out of sample.\",\"related\":[\"cscv\",\"strategy-family\",\"overfitting\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.4\",\"aka\":[\"PBO\"]},{\"id\":\"cscv\",\"term\":\"CSCV\",\"definition\":\"Combinatorially symmetric cross-validation: cut the data into S blocks and test every way of using half of them in-sample and the rest out of sample. Eight blocks give 70 splits.\",\"plain\":\"Testing every way to split data blocks into in-sample and out-of-sample halves.\",\"related\":[\"probability-of-backtest-overfitting\",\"cross-validation\",\"in-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.4\",\"aka\":[\"combinatorially symmetric cross-validation\"]},{\"id\":\"strategy-family\",\"term\":\"Strategy family\",\"definition\":\"All the variants of one idea tried during research, such as every parameter setting, filter or lookback. Overfitting tests judge the family, not only its winner.\",\"plain\":\"All the variants of one idea that you tried.\",\"related\":[\"trial-count\",\"probability-of-backtest-overfitting\",\"data-snooping\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.4\"},{\"id\":\"cross-validation\",\"term\":\"Cross-validation\",\"definition\":\"Testing a model in turns on parts of the data (folds) it was not fitted on, then combining the scores.\",\"plain\":\"Testing a model, in turns, on parts of the data it wasn't fitted on.\",\"related\":[\"out-of-sample\",\"data-leakage\",\"purging\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"data-leakage\",\"term\":\"Data leakage\",\"definition\":\"Information from the test period reaching training, for example through labels that overlap the test days, so scores look better than they are.\",\"plain\":\"Test information slipping into training, which makes results look too good.\",\"related\":[\"look-ahead-bias\",\"cross-validation\",\"purging\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"purging\",\"term\":\"Purging\",\"definition\":\"Removing from training every observation whose label window overlaps the test period.\",\"plain\":\"Dropping training days whose labels overlap the test period.\",\"related\":[\"embargo-period\",\"data-leakage\",\"cross-validation\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\"},{\"id\":\"embargo-period\",\"term\":\"Embargo period\",\"definition\":\"A gap after each test fold kept out of training. It covers the days that fed the last test labels and share their serial correlation.\",\"plain\":\"A gap after the test period kept out of training to block leakage.\",\"related\":[\"purging\",\"autocorrelation\",\"data-leakage\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.5\",\"aka\":[\"embargo\"]},{\"id\":\"strategy-capacity\",\"term\":\"Strategy capacity\",\"definition\":\"The trading size a strategy can carry before impact and costs erode its edge. Total profit peaks well before the edge per unit reaches zero.\",\"plain\":\"How much size a strategy can carry before costs and impact eat its edge.\",\"related\":[\"marginal-profit\",\"square-root-law\",\"crowding\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.6\"},{\"id\":\"marginal-profit\",\"term\":\"Marginal profit\",\"definition\":\"The extra total profit from trading one more unit. Total profit peaks where marginal profit falls to zero.\",\"plain\":\"The extra profit from trading one more unit.\",\"related\":[\"strategy-capacity\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.6\"},{\"id\":\"trial-count\",\"term\":\"Trial count\",\"definition\":\"The number of configurations, variants and ideas tried on the same data during research, abandoned ones included. Every honest backtest report states it.\",\"plain\":\"How many variants and tests you tried; every honest report states it.\",\"related\":[\"research-log\",\"multiple-testing\",\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.7\",\"aka\":[\"N\"]},{\"id\":\"validation-dossier\",\"term\":\"Validation dossier\",\"definition\":\"A fixed-format report of one strategy's hypothesis, tests, trial count, DSR, MinBTL, PBO, capacity, decay haircut and verdict, judged against thresholds set in advance.\",\"plain\":\"A fixed report of all tests, thresholds and the verdict for one strategy.\",\"related\":[\"pre-registration\",\"research-log\",\"trial-count\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"decay-haircut\",\"term\":\"Decay haircut\",\"definition\":\"A planned cut to backtest expectancy that allows for edges weakening after discovery. The app plans published ideas at no more than half their backtest figure.\",\"plain\":\"A planned cut to backtest expectancy because edges tend to fade.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-haircut\",\"validation-dossier\"],\"lesson\":\"4.3.7\"},{\"id\":\"value-at-risk\",\"term\":\"Value at Risk\",\"definition\":\"The loss over a set horizon that is exceeded only with a small probability, 1 − α, at a confidence level α. A 95% one-day VaR is beaten on about 1 day in 20; it is not a maximum loss.\",\"plain\":\"A loss level exceeded only with a small probability, such as 5%, over a set period.\",\"related\":[\"expected-shortfall\",\"confidence-level\",\"var-exceedance\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\",\"aka\":[\"VaR\"]},{\"id\":\"expected-shortfall\",\"term\":\"Expected Shortfall\",\"definition\":\"The average loss on the outcomes beyond the VaR threshold, at the same confidence level and horizon. It measures how deep the tail goes, where VaR only marks its edge.\",\"plain\":\"The average loss on the worst days beyond the VaR level.\",\"related\":[\"value-at-risk\",\"fat-tails\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\",\"aka\":[\"ES\",\"CVaR\",\"Conditional VaR\"]},{\"id\":\"confidence-level\",\"term\":\"Confidence level\",\"definition\":\"The probability α, such as 95% or 99%, that a loss stays within the VaR threshold over the horizon.\",\"plain\":\"The probability, such as 95% or 99%, that a VaR threshold is not exceeded.\",\"related\":[\"value-at-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"var-exceedance\",\"term\":\"VaR exceedance\",\"definition\":\"A day, or other period, whose loss is larger than the VaR estimated for it. A correct 95% model has exceedances on about 5% of days.\",\"plain\":\"A day whose loss is larger than the VaR estimate.\",\"related\":[\"value-at-risk\",\"var-backtest\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\",\"aka\":[\"VaR breach\"]},{\"id\":\"var-backtest\",\"term\":\"VaR backtest\",\"definition\":\"Comparing the number of VaR exceedances with the number the model implies, a binomial count: about 12.5 in 250 days at 95%. Far more means the model understates risk.\",\"plain\":\"Checking whether VaR exceedances happen about as often as the model says they should.\",\"related\":[\"var-exceedance\",\"value-at-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"student-t-distribution\",\"term\":\"Student-t distribution\",\"definition\":\"A bell-shaped distribution with fatter tails than the normal; the fewer its degrees of freedom, the fatter the tails. It's used to model returns that include extreme days.\",\"plain\":\"A bell-shaped distribution with fatter tails than the normal, used to model extreme moves.\",\"related\":[\"fat-tails\",\"value-at-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.1\"},{\"id\":\"portfolio-volatility\",\"term\":\"Portfolio volatility\",\"definition\":\"The volatility of a whole book's returns. For two assets, σ_p² = w₁²σ₁² + w₂²σ₂² + 2w₁w₂ρσ₁σ₂, so it depends on weights, volatilities and correlations.\",\"plain\":\"The volatility of a whole book; it depends on weights, volatilities and correlations.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"correlation\",\"exposure-multiplier\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"exposure-multiplier\",\"term\":\"Exposure multiplier\",\"definition\":\"The factor that scales a book's exposure to its volatility target: target volatility ÷ forecast volatility, usually with a cap.\",\"plain\":\"The factor that scales a book's exposure: target volatility divided by forecast volatility.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-target\",\"leverage-cap\",\"portfolio-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"leverage-cap\",\"term\":\"Leverage cap\",\"definition\":\"A ceiling on the exposure multiplier, such as 1.5×, that stops a volatility target from building leverage in calm periods.\",\"plain\":\"A limit on how far a volatility target may scale exposure up.\",\"related\":[\"exposure-multiplier\",\"leverage-creep\",\"leverage\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\"},{\"id\":\"ewma-volatility\",\"term\":\"EWMA volatility\",\"definition\":\"A volatility estimate from an exponentially weighted moving average of squared returns: each older day's weight shrinks by a factor λ, such as 0.94, so recent days count most.\",\"plain\":\"A volatility estimate that weighs recent returns more than older ones.\",\"related\":[\"realised-volatility\",\"volatility-forecast\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.2\",\"aka\":[\"Exponentially weighted volatility\"]},{\"id\":\"gross-exposure\",\"term\":\"Gross exposure\",\"definition\":\"The sum of the absolute values of all positions, longs and shorts both counted as positive. Divided by equity, it's the book's effective leverage.\",\"plain\":\"The sum of all position sizes, with longs and shorts both counted as positive.\",\"related\":[\"net-exposure\",\"effective-leverage\",\"exposure\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"net-exposure\",\"term\":\"Net exposure\",\"definition\":\"Longs minus shorts: the book's overall directional position, in vUSD or as a share of equity.\",\"plain\":\"Longs minus shorts: the book's overall directional position.\",\"related\":[\"gross-exposure\",\"beta-adjusted-exposure\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"beta-adjusted-exposure\",\"term\":\"Beta-adjusted exposure\",\"definition\":\"Net exposure with each position multiplied by its beta, Σ βᵢ × positionᵢ. It estimates how much the book moves with the market.\",\"plain\":\"Net exposure with each position multiplied by its beta.\",\"related\":[\"beta\",\"net-exposure\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"market-neutral\",\"term\":\"Market neutral\",\"definition\":\"A book whose net or beta-adjusted exposure is close to zero, so broad market moves barely change its value. It still carries leg risk.\",\"plain\":\"A book whose net or beta-adjusted exposure is close to zero.\",\"related\":[\"net-exposure\",\"leg-risk\",\"hedge\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"leg-risk\",\"term\":\"Leg risk\",\"definition\":\"The risk that the sides of a paired or hedged position move against each other, so both lose at once. It grows with gross exposure.\",\"plain\":\"The risk that one side of a paired position moves against the other.\",\"related\":[\"gross-exposure\",\"correlation-breakdown\",\"market-neutral\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.3\"},{\"id\":\"risk-parity\",\"term\":\"Risk parity\",\"definition\":\"Portfolio construction in which every asset or strategy contributes the same share of total risk, often levered to reach a return target.\",\"plain\":\"Sizing assets so each one contributes the same share of total portfolio risk.\",\"related\":[\"risk-contribution\",\"inverse-volatility-weighting\",\"leverage\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\"},{\"id\":\"risk-contribution\",\"term\":\"Risk contribution\",\"definition\":\"The share of a portfolio's variance that comes from one position. For uncorrelated assets it's wᵢ²σᵢ² ÷ σ_p²; with correlations, each asset's covariance with the others is included.\",\"plain\":\"The part of a portfolio's total risk that comes from one position.\",\"related\":[\"risk-parity\",\"risk-budget\",\"portfolio-volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\"},{\"id\":\"risk-budget\",\"term\":\"Risk budget\",\"definition\":\"A planned share of total risk for each position, strategy or asset class, used to size them instead of shares of capital.\",\"plain\":\"The share of total risk you allow each position, strategy or asset class to use.\",\"related\":[\"risk-contribution\",\"risk-parity\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\"},{\"id\":\"inverse-volatility-weighting\",\"term\":\"Inverse-volatility weighting\",\"definition\":\"Weights proportional to 1 ÷ volatility, scaled to add up to 100%. For uncorrelated assets, and for any two assets, it equalises risk contributions.\",\"plain\":\"Weighting each asset by one over its volatility, so calmer assets get larger weights.\",\"related\":[\"risk-parity\",\"volatility\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.4\"},{\"id\":\"reverse-stress-test\",\"term\":\"Reverse stress test\",\"definition\":\"A stress test run backwards: start from a loss you couldn't accept, such as 20% of equity, and find the market move or scenario that causes it.\",\"plain\":\"Working backwards from a chosen loss to the market move that would cause it.\",\"related\":[\"stress-test\",\"hypothetical-scenario\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"historical-scenario\",\"term\":\"Historical scenario\",\"definition\":\"A stress scenario that applies the moves of a past crisis, such as the 2010 Flash Crash or the 2015 Swiss franc shock, to today's positions.\",\"plain\":\"A stress test that replays a past crisis, such as the 2010 flash crash, on today's book.\",\"related\":[\"stress-test\",\"hypothetical-scenario\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"hypothetical-scenario\",\"term\":\"Hypothetical scenario\",\"definition\":\"A stress scenario built from an imagined shock, such as a 20% gap, tripled volatility, spreads × 10 or doubled margin, chosen to test what history hasn't shown.\",\"plain\":\"A stress test built from an imagined shock, such as a 20% gap with doubled margins.\",\"related\":[\"stress-test\",\"historical-scenario\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.5\"},{\"id\":\"correlation-regime\",\"term\":\"Correlation regime\",\"definition\":\"A period with a typical pattern of correlations. In calm markets correlations between risky assets are often low; in crises they tend to rise toward 1.\",\"plain\":\"A period with a typical correlation pattern; in crises correlations move toward one.\",\"related\":[\"correlation\",\"correlation-breakdown\",\"effective-bets\"],\"lesson\":\"4.4.6\"},{\"id\":\"mifid-ii\",\"term\":\"MiFID II\",\"definition\":\"The EU's Markets in Financial Instruments Directive II, applied since 2018, which sets the rules for investment firms, client categories, client protection and market conduct across the EU.\",\"plain\":\"The main EU rulebook for brokers and investment firms.\",\"related\":[\"esma\",\"passporting\",\"retail-client\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Markets in Financial Instruments Directive II\"]},{\"id\":\"sec\",\"term\":\"SEC\",\"definition\":\"The US Securities and Exchange Commission, the federal regulator of US securities markets, exchanges and broker-dealers; it oversees FINRA.\",\"plain\":\"The main US regulator for stocks and other securities.\",\"related\":[\"finra\",\"cftc\",\"regulator\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Securities and Exchange Commission\"]},{\"id\":\"cftc\",\"term\":\"CFTC\",\"definition\":\"The US Commodity Futures Trading Commission, the federal regulator of futures, options on futures, swaps and retail forex.\",\"plain\":\"The US regulator for futures and retail forex.\",\"related\":[\"nfa\",\"sec\",\"regulator\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"Commodity Futures Trading Commission\"]},{\"id\":\"nfa\",\"term\":\"NFA\",\"definition\":\"The National Futures Association, the US self-regulatory organisation for futures and retail forex firms, which registers them and enforces its rules under CFTC oversight.\",\"plain\":\"The body that polices US futures and forex brokers.\",\"related\":[\"cftc\",\"finra\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\",\"aka\":[\"National Futures Association\"]},{\"id\":\"passporting\",\"term\":\"Passporting\",\"definition\":\"The right of a firm authorised in one EU member state to offer its services in the others under MiFID II, after notifying its home regulator.\",\"plain\":\"One EU licence lets a firm serve clients across the EU.\",\"related\":[\"mifid-ii\",\"licence-register\",\"regulator\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\"},{\"id\":\"retail-client\",\"term\":\"Retail client\",\"definition\":\"The default MiFID II client category, with the strongest protections, such as CFD leverage caps, negative balance protection and standard risk warnings.\",\"plain\":\"The normal client category, with the most protection.\",\"related\":[\"professional-client\",\"mifid-ii\",\"negative-balance-protection\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\"},{\"id\":\"professional-client\",\"term\":\"Professional client\",\"definition\":\"A MiFID II client category with fewer protections. A retail client can opt up only by meeting at least two of three criteria: frequent large trades, a portfolio over €500,000, or relevant work in finance.\",\"plain\":\"A client category with fewer protections, for experienced investors who meet set criteria.\",\"related\":[\"retail-client\",\"mifid-ii\",\"leverage\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\"},{\"id\":\"appropriateness-test\",\"term\":\"Appropriateness test\",\"definition\":\"A MiFID II check in which a broker assesses whether a client has the knowledge and experience to understand a complex product, such as a CFD, before offering it.\",\"plain\":\"The broker's check that you understand a risky product.\",\"related\":[\"mifid-ii\",\"retail-client\",\"cfd\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.1\"},{\"id\":\"investor-compensation-scheme\",\"term\":\"Investor compensation scheme\",\"definition\":\"A fund that repays clients, up to a set limit, when an authorised investment firm fails and can't return their money or assets. It never covers trading losses.\",\"plain\":\"A safety net if your broker fails, up to a limit. It doesn't cover your trading losses.\",\"related\":[\"segregated-funds\",\"sipc\",\"fscs\",\"counterparty-risk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\"},{\"id\":\"sipc\",\"term\":\"SIPC\",\"definition\":\"The US Securities Investor Protection Corporation, which restores missing securities and cash when a member broker-dealer fails, up to $500,000 per customer, of which $250,000 in cash. It doesn't cover market losses, futures or forex.\",\"plain\":\"The US safety net if a stockbroker fails, up to $500,000.\",\"related\":[\"investor-compensation-scheme\",\"fscs\",\"segregated-funds\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"Securities Investor Protection Corporation\"]},{\"id\":\"fscs\",\"term\":\"FSCS\",\"definition\":\"The UK Financial Services Compensation Scheme, which compensates clients of failed authorised UK firms up to a limit: £85,000 for investments as of October 2026.\",\"plain\":\"The UK safety net if an authorised firm fails.\",\"related\":[\"investor-compensation-scheme\",\"fca\",\"sipc\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\",\"aka\":[\"Financial Services Compensation Scheme\"]},{\"id\":\"hybrid-broker\",\"term\":\"Hybrid broker\",\"definition\":\"A broker that keeps most client trades on its own book (B-book) and passes some, often the consistent winners, to outside liquidity (A-book).\",\"plain\":\"A broker that takes the other side of most clients and passes the winners on to the market.\",\"related\":[\"a-book\",\"b-book\",\"market-maker\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\"},{\"id\":\"asymmetric-slippage\",\"term\":\"Asymmetric slippage\",\"definition\":\"An execution practice in which unfavourable slippage is passed to clients while favourable slippage is kept by the broker; a red flag for unfair execution.\",\"plain\":\"Only bad slippage reaches you. A warning sign.\",\"related\":[\"slippage\",\"best-execution\",\"price-improvement\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.2\"},{\"id\":\"prop-firm\",\"term\":\"Prop firm\",\"definition\":\"A proprietary trading firm. Institutional prop firms trade their own capital through employed traders; retail evaluation firms sell paid tests on simulated accounts.\",\"plain\":\"A firm that trades its own money, or, often today, one that sells trading tests.\",\"related\":[\"evaluation-challenge\",\"funded-account\",\"profit-split\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"Proprietary trading firm\"]},{\"id\":\"evaluation-challenge\",\"term\":\"Evaluation challenge\",\"definition\":\"A paid test with a profit target, loss limits and other rules; passing it earns a funded account, failing it loses the fee.\",\"plain\":\"A paid trading test you must pass to get a funded account.\",\"related\":[\"prop-firm\",\"challenge-fee\",\"profit-target\",\"trailing-drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"Prop challenge\",\"Evaluation\"]},{\"id\":\"funded-account\",\"term\":\"Funded account\",\"definition\":\"The account a trader receives after passing an evaluation, traded under the firm's rules for a profit split; at retail firms it is often still simulated.\",\"plain\":\"The account you get after passing a challenge, often still not real money.\",\"related\":[\"simulated-funded-account\",\"profit-split\",\"prop-firm\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\"},{\"id\":\"profit-target\",\"term\":\"Profit target\",\"definition\":\"The gain, usually a percentage of the starting balance, that an evaluation phase requires before you pass it.\",\"plain\":\"The profit you must reach to pass a phase.\",\"related\":[\"evaluation-challenge\",\"consistency-rule\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\"},{\"id\":\"trailing-drawdown\",\"term\":\"Trailing drawdown\",\"definition\":\"A maximum loss limit whose floor rises with the highest equity (intraday trailing) or the highest closing balance (end-of-day trailing), but never moves down.\",\"plain\":\"A loss limit that follows your best level up, never down.\",\"related\":[\"max-drawdown\",\"evaluation-challenge\",\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\",\"aka\":[\"Trailing loss limit\"]},{\"id\":\"consistency-rule\",\"term\":\"Consistency rule\",\"definition\":\"An evaluation rule that caps the share of total profit allowed from a single day, often under 50%, so one lucky day can't pass the test.\",\"plain\":\"A rule that your best day can't be most of your profit.\",\"related\":[\"profit-target\",\"evaluation-challenge\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\"},{\"id\":\"profit-split\",\"term\":\"Profit split\",\"definition\":\"The share of a funded account's profits paid to the trader, such as 80% or 90%, with the rest kept by the firm.\",\"plain\":\"How profits are divided between you and the firm.\",\"related\":[\"funded-account\",\"prop-firm\",\"payout-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.3\"},{\"id\":\"challenge-fee\",\"term\":\"Challenge fee\",\"definition\":\"The price paid for one attempt at an evaluation, kept by the firm whether the trader passes or fails.\",\"plain\":\"What you pay to try a prop challenge; lost if you fail.\",\"related\":[\"evaluation-challenge\",\"challenge-expected-value\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"challenge-expected-value\",\"term\":\"Challenge expected value\",\"definition\":\"The average result of buying an evaluation: pass probability times expected payouts if funded, minus the fee.\",\"plain\":\"What one attempt at a challenge is worth on average, fee included.\",\"related\":[\"expected-value\",\"challenge-fee\",\"payout-rate\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"simulated-funded-account\",\"term\":\"Simulated funded account\",\"definition\":\"A funded account whose trades never reach a real market; any payouts come from the firm itself, largely funded by evaluation fees.\",\"plain\":\"A 'funded' account that's still a simulation; the firm pays you itself.\",\"related\":[\"funded-account\",\"prop-firm\",\"b-book\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"payout-rate\",\"term\":\"Payout rate\",\"definition\":\"The share of funded traders who actually receive at least one payout. Firms rarely publish it, and claims about it are unaudited.\",\"plain\":\"How many funded traders ever get paid. Rarely published.\",\"related\":[\"funded-account\",\"profit-split\",\"challenge-expected-value\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.4\"},{\"id\":\"capital-gains-tax\",\"term\":\"Capital gains tax\",\"definition\":\"A tax on the profit from selling an asset for more than it cost. Rates, exemptions and what counts as a gain differ by country, product and holding period.\",\"plain\":\"Tax on the profit when you sell something for more than you paid.\",\"related\":[\"after-tax-return\",\"withholding-tax\",\"wash-sale-rule\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"wash-sale-rule\",\"term\":\"Wash-sale rule\",\"definition\":\"A US tax rule that disallows a loss on a security if a substantially identical one is bought within 30 days before or after the sale; the loss is added to the new position's cost.\",\"plain\":\"A US rule: sell at a loss and rebuy within 30 days, and you can't claim the loss yet.\",\"related\":[\"capital-gains-tax\",\"trade-records\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"after-tax-return\",\"term\":\"After-tax return\",\"definition\":\"The return left after taxes are paid; it is the part of a gain that keeps compounding.\",\"plain\":\"What you keep after tax.\",\"related\":[\"capital-gains-tax\",\"cost-drag\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"withholding-tax\",\"term\":\"Withholding tax\",\"definition\":\"Tax deducted at source by a broker, bank or other payer before the money reaches the investor, sometimes as the final tax and sometimes as an advance on it.\",\"plain\":\"Tax taken out before the money reaches you.\",\"related\":[\"capital-gains-tax\",\"trade-records\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"trade-records\",\"term\":\"Trade records\",\"definition\":\"Dated details of every trade, fee, financing charge, currency conversion and cash movement, kept so results can be reviewed and reported for tax.\",\"plain\":\"The full paper trail of your trades and your money.\",\"related\":[\"journal\",\"commission\",\"overnight-financing\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.5\"},{\"id\":\"go-live-checklist\",\"term\":\"Go-live checklist\",\"definition\":\"The written conditions on evidence, process, capital, broker, plan and expectations that must all be met before trading with real money.\",\"plain\":\"Your written list of conditions to pass before you trade real money.\",\"related\":[\"go-live-gate\",\"incubation\",\"risk-capital\",\"minimum-live-size\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"minimum-live-size\",\"term\":\"Minimum live size\",\"definition\":\"The smallest real position size a broker and an instrument allow, used for a first live phase to test execution, costs and emotions while risking little.\",\"plain\":\"The smallest real trade you can place, used to test cheaply.\",\"related\":[\"go-live-checklist\",\"incubation\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"sim-to-live-gap\",\"term\":\"Sim-to-live gap\",\"definition\":\"The expected shortfall of live results against simulated ones, caused by real costs, slippage, partial fills and the emotions of trading real money.\",\"plain\":\"Why real trading does worse than the simulator.\",\"related\":[\"slippage\",\"paper-trading\",\"go-live-checklist\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.6\"},{\"id\":\"no-trade-day\",\"term\":\"No-trade day\",\"definition\":\"A day decided in advance, often at the weekly review, on which you take no new trades because a stand-aside condition applies.\",\"plain\":\"A day you plan not to trade.\",\"related\":[\"stand-aside-rule\",\"stand-aside\",\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.7\"},{\"id\":\"stand-aside-rule\",\"term\":\"Stand-aside rule\",\"definition\":\"A written condition, such as a loss limit reached or an event without a plan, under which you take no new trades until a stated point.\",\"plain\":\"A written rule that says when you don't trade.\",\"related\":[\"no-trade-day\",\"stand-aside\",\"drawdown-tier\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.7\"},{\"id\":\"abstention-accuracy\",\"term\":\"Abstention accuracy\",\"definition\":\"The share of no-edge situations, such as pure-noise market segments, in which you correctly took no trade.\",\"plain\":\"How often you rightly stayed out.\",\"related\":[\"stand-aside-rule\",\"random-walk\"],\"lesson\":\"4.5.7\"},{\"id\":\"constraints-matrix\",\"term\":\"Constraints matrix\",\"definition\":\"A table that maps your time, capital, jurisdiction, drawdown tolerance, skills and measured temperament to the trading styles they allow or rule out.\",\"plain\":\"A table matching your time, money, rules, skills and temperament to suitable trading styles.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"behaviour-audit\",\"swing-trading\",\"scalping\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"behaviour-audit\",\"term\":\"Behaviour audit\",\"definition\":\"A review of measured habits from your own trade journal, such as overtrading, trades opened soon after a loss, moved stops and holding time, used in place of self-description.\",\"plain\":\"A review of the habits your journal measures, such as revenge trades and moved stops.\",\"related\":[\"journal\",\"revenge-trading\",\"overtrading\",\"constraints-matrix\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.1\"},{\"id\":\"session-fit\",\"term\":\"Session fit\",\"definition\":\"How well an instrument's most liquid hours overlap the hours you can actually trade, in your own time zone, including the weeks when daylight-saving changes don't line up.\",\"plain\":\"How well a market's most liquid hours match the hours you can actually trade.\",\"related\":[\"trading-session\",\"session-overlap\",\"daylight-saving-time\",\"tradability-ratio\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.2\"},{\"id\":\"specialisation\",\"term\":\"Specialisation\",\"definition\":\"Trading one style, one or two setups and one or two markets until the sample is large enough to judge whether the edge is real.\",\"plain\":\"Focusing on one style, few setups and few markets until your results can be judged.\",\"related\":[\"playbook\",\"sample-size\",\"statistical-power\",\"no-trade-list\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.3\",\"aka\":[\"Specialization\"]},{\"id\":\"no-trade-list\",\"term\":\"No-trade list\",\"definition\":\"The part of a playbook that lists checkable situations in which no new trade is opened, whatever the setup shows, such as the minutes before a tier-1 release.\",\"plain\":\"A written list of situations in which you never open a trade.\",\"related\":[\"playbook\",\"stand-aside\",\"daily-loss-limit\",\"impact-tier\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"statistical-power\",\"term\":\"Statistical power\",\"definition\":\"The probability that a test detects an effect that is really there. For a trading edge it rises with the number of trades and with the edge's size relative to the spread of results.\",\"plain\":\"The chance that a test detects a real effect; it rises with sample size.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"t-statistic\",\"confidence-interval\",\"false-positive\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.3\"},{\"id\":\"development-pipeline\",\"term\":\"Development pipeline\",\"definition\":\"The staged path of a strategy: hypothesis, rules, backtest, robustness, simulator forward test, minimum live size, gradual scaling and decay monitoring, with a gate at each stage.\",\"plain\":\"The staged path a strategy follows from idea to full live size, with a gate at each stage.\",\"related\":[\"edge-hypothesis\",\"backtest\",\"incubation\",\"kill-criteria\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"kill-criteria\",\"term\":\"Kill criteria\",\"definition\":\"Numeric thresholds, written before going live, at which a strategy stops trading live, such as a drawdown past its 99th Monte Carlo percentile or an expectancy interval entirely below zero.\",\"plain\":\"Numeric limits, set in advance, at which a strategy is stopped.\",\"related\":[\"kill-switch\",\"review-trigger\",\"monte-carlo\",\"percentile\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"review-trigger\",\"term\":\"Review trigger\",\"definition\":\"A threshold, such as a drawdown past the 95th Monte Carlo percentile or an expectancy interval that includes zero after 50 trades, that moves a strategy down one capital stage and starts a full review.\",\"plain\":\"A threshold that moves a strategy down one stage and forces a full review.\",\"related\":[\"kill-criteria\",\"capital-stages\",\"percentile\",\"confidence-interval\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.4\"},{\"id\":\"capital-stages\",\"term\":\"Capital stages\",\"definition\":\"Steps of position size, from the simulator to minimum live size and then 25%, 50% and 100% of target size, each unlocked by sample size, adherence and drawdown, and lost on a review trigger.\",\"plain\":\"Steps of position size, from simulator to full size, each unlocked by evidence.\",\"related\":[\"size-step-up\",\"review-trigger\",\"plan-adherence\",\"go-live-gate\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"notional-account\",\"term\":\"Notional account\",\"definition\":\"The account value used to size trades. Cutting it faster than equity in a drawdown, as the Turtles did by 20% for every 10% down, lowers risk ahead of further losses.\",\"plain\":\"The account value used for sizing; cut after drawdowns so risk falls faster than equity.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown-tier\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"equity\",\"max-drawdown\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"size-step-up\",\"term\":\"Size step-up\",\"definition\":\"A planned increase in position size, typically about 25%, allowed only after a new equity high and once the current stage's criteria are met.\",\"plain\":\"A planned size increase of about 25%, allowed only after new equity highs and stage criteria.\",\"related\":[\"capital-stages\",\"position-size\",\"anti-martingale\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.5\"},{\"id\":\"annual-review\",\"term\":\"Annual review\",\"definition\":\"A yearly review of net, risk-adjusted results against the passive benchmark, with rules, regulation and costs rechecked, ending in a decision: continue, reduce, retire or invest passively.\",\"plain\":\"A yearly check of net, risk-adjusted results against the benchmark that ends in a decision.\",\"related\":[\"benchmark\",\"sharpe-ratio\",\"weekly-review\",\"strategy-retirement\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\",\"aka\":[\"Yearly review\"]},{\"id\":\"rule-change-log\",\"term\":\"Rule-change log\",\"definition\":\"A versioned record of every rule change: the hypothesis, the evidence threshold, the test result, the date and the new plan version.\",\"plain\":\"A versioned record of every rule change, with its reason and its evidence.\",\"related\":[\"research-log\",\"trading-plan\",\"out-of-sample\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"strategy-retirement\",\"term\":\"Strategy retirement\",\"definition\":\"Removing a strategy from live trading for good, when its edge has decayed, its regime no longer appears, or it keeps failing its reviews.\",\"plain\":\"Stopping a strategy for good when its edge is gone or its regime no longer appears.\",\"related\":[\"edge-decay\",\"kill-criteria\",\"annual-review\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\"},{\"id\":\"edge-decay\",\"term\":\"Edge decay\",\"definition\":\"The weakening of a strategy's returns over time, often after it becomes widely known and crowded. Published anomalies earned 58% less after publication on average (McLean and Pontiff, 2016).\",\"plain\":\"The weakening of a strategy's returns over time, often after it becomes widely known.\",\"related\":[\"anomaly\",\"crowding\",\"out-of-sample\",\"strategy-retirement\"],\"lesson\":\"4.6.6\"}],\"concepts\":[{\"id\":\"market-as-auction\",\"name\":\"Market as an auction\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A market price is where the latest buyer and seller agreed; it moves when one side is more eager than the other.\",\"body\":[\"Buyers send orders saying the most they will pay; sellers, the least they will accept. A trade happens only when a buyer's price meets a seller's, and the price on a chart is the last price at which that happened.\",\"When buyers are in more of a hurry, they accept sellers' higher prices and the last price climbs. When sellers hurry, they accept lower bids and it falls. Nobody sets the price; orders do.\"],\"example\":\"Buyers will pay at most 100.30, 100.20, 100.00, 99.95 and 99.80; sellers accept at least 99.70, 99.85, 100.00, 100.05 and 100.25, one unit each. At 100.00, three buyers and three sellers agree, so 3 units trade; at 99.95 or 100.05 only 2 could. Left waiting: a best bid of 99.95 and a best ask of 100.05.\",\"related\":[\"bid-ask-spread\",\"market-order\",\"limit-order\",\"market-participants\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.1\"]},{\"id\":\"market-participants\",\"name\":\"Market participants\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Hedgers, investors, institutions, market makers, speculators and central banks trade for different reasons, horizons and information.\",\"body\":[\"Every trade has two sides, and the other side isn't necessarily wrong. A hedger sells to protect a harvest, a pension fund rebalances, a market maker earns the spread, a speculator bets on a move, a central bank pursues a policy goal.\",\"Institutions worry about the impact of their own large orders; retail traders mostly struggle with costs and their own behaviour. The firms across from a retail trader usually work full time, with better tools and lower costs.\"],\"example\":\"An airline buys oil futures to fix next year's fuel cost. If oil then falls, it loses on the futures but pays less for fuel: the hedge did its job. The trader who sold it those futures made money, and both acted sensibly.\",\"related\":[\"market-as-auction\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.2\"]},{\"id\":\"asset-classes\",\"name\":\"Asset classes\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Families of instruments, such as stocks, forex, futures, crypto, bonds and CFDs, each with its own hours, costs, leverage and risks.\",\"body\":[\"Stocks and ETFs trade in exchange sessions; forex runs 24 hours from Sunday to Friday; futures expire and must be rolled; crypto trades 24/7; bond prices fall when interest rates rise; with a CFD the provider is your counterparty.\",\"The habits of this course transfer to all of them: bid and ask, costs, risk per trade, stops and sizing. The numbers don't: hours, leverage limits, specific costs and expiry differ by class.\"],\"example\":\"On a Saturday only crypto trades. Crypto spot is open 168 hours a week, forex 120, futures about 115 with a daily break, and a US exchange's main session 32.5 (6.5 hours × 5 days). Fewer hours means more chances of a gap when trading reopens.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"trading-costs\",\"market-participants\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"passive-benchmark\",\"name\":\"Passive benchmark\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The buy-and-hold result on the same market, after costs, that any trading result has to beat.\",\"body\":[\"Buying on day one and holding takes no skill and no screen time. So the fair test of any active result is the gap to buy-and-hold on the same market, over the same period, after costs.\",\"Before costs, trading is close to zero-sum around the market's return; after costs it is negative-sum for the median trader. In a study of 66,465 US households (1991–96), the most active fifth earned 11.4% a year against the market's 17.9% (Barber and Odean, 2000).\"],\"example\":\"Dana trades LABX 40 times in a year and makes +14.0% before costs. At about 0.2% of the account per round trip, costs take 40 × 0.2 = 8.0 points, leaving +6.0%. Buy-and-hold made +9.0% after costs, so Dana trails the benchmark by 3.0 points.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"trading-styles\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"trading-styles\",\"name\":\"Trading styles\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Scalping, day, swing and position trading differ in holding period, screen time and sensitivity to costs.\",\"body\":[\"Scalpers hold for seconds to minutes and trade many times a day. Day traders are flat by the close. Swing traders hold for days to a few weeks, position traders for weeks to months, investors for years.\",\"The shorter the hold, the smaller the move each trade aims for and the more often costs are paid, so costs weigh most on the fastest styles.\"],\"example\":\"On LABX, a 0.10 spread is a third of a 0.30 scalping target but a thirtieth of a 3.00 swing target. The scalper also pays it far more often, so costs take a much bigger share of the scalper's results.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"passive-benchmark\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"retail-loss-rates\",\"name\":\"Retail loss rates\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Regulator and academic base rates showing that most retail CFD accounts and almost all persistent day traders lose money.\",\"body\":[\"National regulators' analyses cited by ESMA in 2018 found that 74% to 89% of retail CFD accounts lost money, with average losses of €1,600 to €29,000 per client. The FCA found 82% in a 2016 UK sample, and the AMF about 89% of 14,799 active French clients (2009–2012).\",\"Studies of whole populations agree. In Brazil, 97% of those who day traded futures for more than 300 days lost money; in Taiwan, fewer than 1% of day traders reliably earned returns after fees. These are base rates to start from, not opinions.\"],\"example\":\"A provider's risk warning says 78% of its retail accounts lose money. Out of 500 accounts, that is 500 × 78 ÷ 100 = 390 losing accounts.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"when-not-to-trade\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"when-not-to-trade\",\"name\":\"When not to trade\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Choosing not to trade, or not to trade with real money, is a valid and often rational decision.\",\"body\":[\"Given the base rates, deciding not to day trade with real money, or to invest passively instead, is a reasonable result of learning to trade. The skills this course builds, judging risk and decisions under uncertainty, are useful either way.\",\"Good simulator results don't prove an edge with real money: the market is synthetic, there is no real fear, and the sample is small. If you ever trade real money, use only money you can afford to lose, and only a regulated broker.\"],\"example\":\"After 60 simulated trades you are up 12% and ahead of the benchmark ghost. That's a start, but 60 trades in a synthetic market say little about real money. Not trading your savings for now, or investing passively, is a sound decision.\",\"related\":[\"retail-loss-rates\",\"decision-quality\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"process-scores\",\"name\":\"Process scores\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"MARKET//LAB keeps Decision Quality, Risk Discipline and Simulated Outcome apart and grades only the first two.\",\"body\":[\"Decision Quality grades your reasoning with the information you had when you decided. Risk Discipline grades your risk rules: a stop on every entry, risk within the limit, no widened stop and no bigger size after a loss. Simulated Outcome is the simulated profit or loss: shown, but never graded.\",\"Keeping them apart stops luck from grading you. A trade without a stop that happens to win still breaks Risk Discipline; a well-planned trade that loses can still score well.\"],\"example\":\"A ticket buys 40 LABX at 100.00 with the stop at 98.00. Loss at the stop: 40 × 2.00 = 80.00, plus 2.00 and 1.96 in commission, 83.96 in all, under the 1% limit of 100.00. With the stop on and a reason written before entry, the trade can score well whether it wins or loses.\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\",\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"evidence-grades\",\"name\":\"Evidence grades\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Mechanic and A to F labels showing how well a method, pattern or claim has held up when tested.\",\"body\":[\"Mechanic means true by construction, like the spread. A is robust across markets and decades; B is supported but weaker since publication or limited to some regimes; C is mixed; D is unproven when tested; F loses by design.\",\"A grade describes the evidence, not your future results. Even robust edges shrink once published: across 97 published return predictors, returns were 26% lower out of sample and 58% lower after publication (McLean and Pontiff, 2016).\"],\"example\":\"'A round trip pays the spread' is Mechanic. Diversified trend following over decades is A. Stops clustering just beyond round numbers is B. Elliott waves predicting turning points is D. Doubling your size after each loss is F.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"process-scores\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"outcome-bias\",\"name\":\"Outcome bias\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Outcome bias is judging the quality of a decision by its result instead of by the information and reasoning available at the time.\",\"body\":[\"A result mixes the decision with luck. A sound decision can lose and a poor one can win, so one outcome can't grade the decision. Together they make four boxes: deserved success, bad luck, dumb luck and poetic justice.\",\"People rate the same decision higher when it ends well, even when they say the outcome shouldn't matter (Baron and Hershey, 1988; a preregistered 2023 replication with 692 people). That's why MARKET//LAB grades the process first and shows the outcome separately.\"],\"example\":\"A dice bet pays 10.00 on a 6 and loses 10.00 on anything else. Ana takes it and wins 10.00. The bet loses five times in six, so the decision was poor: her win is dumb luck, not a good decision.\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"counterfactual-thinking\",\"luck-vs-skill\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.1\",\"3.11.3\"]},{\"id\":\"expected-value\",\"name\":\"Expected value\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Expected value is the probability-weighted average result of a bet: what it returns per play over many plays.\",\"body\":[\"Multiply each outcome by its probability and add them up. A coin flip that wins 15.00 or loses 10.00 has an expected value of 0.5 × 15.00 − 0.5 × 10.00 = +2.50 per play.\",\"Expected value describes many plays, not the next one: any single play can still lose. A game can win most of the time and still lose on average if its rare losses are large. An edge is a positive expected value after costs.\"],\"example\":\"Game B wins 1.00 nine times in ten and loses 12.00 once in ten: 0.9 × 1.00 − 0.1 × 12.00 = −0.30 per play. Over 100 plays that is about −30.00, despite 90% wins.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"variance\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"variance\",\"name\":\"Variance\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Variance is how widely results spread around their expected value; short runs can differ a lot from it.\",\"body\":[\"Even a game with a positive expected value has losing stretches. Game A (+15.00 or −10.00 on a coin flip, +2.50 per play) still ends behind about 13% of the time after 20 plays, and about 2% after 100.\",\"The more plays, the closer the average gets to the expected value, but the swings along the way never disappear. Plan for them instead of reading meaning into them.\"],\"example\":\"After 20 plays of game A the expected total is +50.00, yet five runs of 20 plays ended at +100.00, +50.00, +25.00, −25.00 and −50.00. That spread is variance.\",\"related\":[\"expected-value\",\"streaks-and-runs\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"streaks-and-runs\",\"name\":\"Streaks and runs\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"In independent trials, long runs of wins or losses are normal and longer than intuition expects.\",\"body\":[\"Five tails from five given flips is only 3.1% likely, but in 100 flips a run of five or more shows up somewhere 81.0% of the time. At a 40% win rate, a run of eight losses appears in 49.0% of 100-trade sequences.\",\"People writing 'random' sequences switch too often and avoid long runs. A streak on its own doesn't prove a method stopped working, and it doesn't make a win due.\"],\"example\":\"A method that wins 40% of its trades: the chance of at least one run of 6 losses in 100 trades is 87.3%; of 10 losses, 20.5%.\",\"related\":[\"variance\",\"drawdown\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"sample-size\",\"name\":\"Sample size\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Sample size is the number of observations behind a statistic; small samples give wide, unreliable estimates.\",\"body\":[\"A 60% win rate over 20 trades fits any true rate from about 38.5% to 81.5%. At 100 trades the plausible range is 50.4–69.6%, and at 400 trades 55.2–64.8%: four times the trades halves the width.\",\"Judge a method on hundreds of trades, and each single trade on whether it followed the plan. People expect small samples to look like the whole, which Tversky and Kahneman (1971) called the law of small numbers.\"],\"example\":\"A method with no edge reaches 60% or better over 20 trades about one time in four (25.2%). A 60% result needs about 93 trades before a 50% method no longer fits it.\",\"related\":[\"expected-value\",\"luck-vs-skill\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.4\"]},{\"id\":\"random-walk\",\"name\":\"Random walk\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A random walk is a price path in which each step is random; it still draws trends, ranges and levels by chance.\",\"body\":[\"Each step up or down is a coin flip with no memory of the steps before. Yet a few hundred steps draw shapes that look like trends, ranges, breakouts and levels that 'hold'.\",\"So a pattern on a chart proves little by itself. The honest test asks how often it works compared with random data, after costs. In one test, chart gaps 'filled' 83.4% of the time; random levels the same distance away were revisited 83.5% of the time.\"],\"example\":\"Forty one-hour candles, each built from five coin flips of 0.20, climb from 99.40 to 104.40 in nine hours and then range between 104.60 and 105.60. None of it means anything.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"luck-vs-skill\",\"name\":\"Luck versus skill\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"In short runs luck dominates results; skill shows as a consistent process over large samples.\",\"body\":[\"If 1,000 people call LABX's direction for 10 days by flipping a coin, about 55 get 8 or more days right. Over the next 10 days those 'stars' average about 5, like everyone else.\",\"Evidence that separates skill from luck: the same rules over hundreds of trades, results after costs, and a comparison with the benchmark. You control the process, not the luck, which is why MARKET//LAB grades the process.\"],\"example\":\"Of 1,000 coin-flippers, about 55 (5.47%) get at least 8 of 10 days right. About 3 of them do it again over the next 10 days, which is what chance predicts.\",\"related\":[\"survivorship-bias\",\"sample-size\",\"decision-quality\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"survivorship-bias\",\"name\":\"Survivorship bias\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Survivorship bias is seeing only the winners that remain, which makes chance results look like skill.\",\"body\":[\"When many people try something risky, some do well by luck. If you see only those, in ads or on social media, the many who failed are invisible and the winners look talented.\",\"Before believing a record, ask how many started, how many trades it covers, and whether the results are after costs and next to the benchmark.\"],\"example\":\"1,000 people flip coins to call LABX for 10 days. The 55 with 8 or more right post their records; the 945 others don't. Seen alone, the 55 look like experts.\",\"related\":[\"luck-vs-skill\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"0.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"long-and-short\",\"name\":\"Long and short\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A long profits from a rise and closes at the bid; a short profits from a fall and closes at the ask.\",\"body\":[\"A long buys first and sells later; a short sells first and buys back later. Every buy fills at the ask and every sale at the bid, so a long opens at the ask and closes at the bid, and a short does the reverse. The spread costs both directions the same.\",\"A long can lose at most what it paid, because price can't fall below zero. A short loses whatever price rises, with no fixed ceiling, so it needs a stop above the entry, sized like any other trade. Shorting stocks also means borrowing shares and paying a borrow fee; LABX shorts have neither.\"],\"example\":\"Short 30 LABX at the bid, 99.95. The mid falls to 97.00 and you buy back at the ask, 97.05: 30 × 2.90 = +87.00 before commission, +84.04 after fees of 1.50 and 1.46.\",\"related\":[\"bid-ask-spread\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"price-types\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.2\"]},{\"id\":\"price-types\",\"name\":\"Price types\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Last, bid, ask and mid are different prices; charts plot one of them, and stops trigger on the side that closes the position.\",\"body\":[\"The last price is the latest trade; the bid and ask are the quote now; the mid sits halfway between them. Stock charts usually plot the last, many FX and CFD platforms plot the bid, and LABX plots the mid.\",\"Orders don't trigger on the chart. A long's stop triggers when the bid reaches it and a short's when the ask does, half a spread away from the mid. When spreads widen, at the open or around news, a stop can trigger while the chart barely moves.\"],\"example\":\"A short's stop sits at 101.00. The LABX chart peaks at 100.97, but at that moment the ask is 101.02, so the stop triggers and fills at 101.00.\",\"related\":[\"bid-ask-spread\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"long-and-short\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"cost-stack\",\"name\":\"Cost stack\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Spread, commission, slippage, financing and fees that stand between a gross and a net result.\",\"body\":[\"A trade's gross result is the price move times the size. Before any of it is yours, the spread, the commission on each fill, any slippage, overnight financing on leveraged positions and account fees come off.\",\"Commission comes in several models: a percentage of the fill value (LABX: 0.05%, rounded up to the cent), a fixed amount per share or contract, or zero. 'Zero commission' is not zero cost: such brokers earn through wider spreads or payment for order flow.\"],\"example\":\"Long 100 units while the mid rises from 100.00 to 102.00: 200.00 gross. Spread 10.00, commissions 5.01 + 5.10, a 0.03 slip on the exit 3.00 and 2.00 of financing leave 174.89 net.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"slippage\",\"break-even-move\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"break-even-move\",\"name\":\"Break-even move\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The price move needed to cover a round trip's costs before any gain.\",\"body\":[\"A round trip crosses the spread once and pays commission twice, so the mid has to move your way by about the spread plus two fees before closing breaks even: about 0.20 a unit on LABX near 100.00.\",\"The same cost takes a bigger share of a small target: 0.20 is 50% of a 0.40 gain and 5% of a 4.00 gain. Trading often with small targets pays that share again and again, so check that a target is worth the costs before entering.\"],\"example\":\"Long 100 LABX filled at 100.05: at a mid of 100.21 you sell at 100.16, gross 11.00, fees 5.01 + 5.01, net +0.98; at 100.20 the net is −0.02. A short filled at 99.95 breaks even at a mid of 99.80.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"cost-stack\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"point-value\",\"name\":\"Point value\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The money a one-point move is worth for a position: units or contracts times the value per point.\",\"body\":[\"Price moves are counted in ticks (the smallest step, 0.01 on LABX), points (1.00) or, in forex, pips (0.0001 in most pairs). None of these is money until you multiply by the size and by the value per point.\",\"On LABX a point is worth 1.00 vUSD per unit. On 10,000 units of EUR/USD a pip is worth 1.00 USD. A futures contract has a multiplier, the money per point for one contract. The same percentage move can be worth very different amounts, so convert every move into money and into a share of the account.\"],\"example\":\"30 LABX units × 1.25 points = 37.50 vUSD, 0.375% of 10,000.00. 30 pips on 10,000 units of EUR/USD = 30.00 USD. One illustrative LABX future of 100 units: 1.00 vUSD a tick.\",\"related\":[\"position-sizing\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"balance-and-equity\",\"name\":\"Balance and equity\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Balance counts closed results after costs; equity adds the open P&L of current positions.\",\"body\":[\"Balance is deposits plus realised P&L, minus every fee already paid, including the entry commission of a position that is still open. Equity is balance plus the unrealised P&L of open positions, valued at the mid.\",\"Equity is what the account is worth right now, so MARKET//LAB calculates risk percentages on equity. The difference between the two is the open P&L: it becomes realised, and moves into the balance, only when the position closes.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 50 LABX at 100.05: the 2.51 fee takes the balance from 10,000.00 to 9,997.49. With the mid at 99.95 the open P&L is −5.00, so equity is 9,992.49.\",\"related\":[\"risk-per-trade\",\"drawdown\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"order-ticket\",\"name\":\"Order ticket\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The form that turns a decision into an order; checking side, size, type, price and stop prevents costly errors.\",\"body\":[\"The ticket holds the instrument, the side, the size, the order type and price, and the stop and target. Each field carries out one part of the plan; the stop and the size together set the loss if you're wrong.\",\"Most costly mistakes happen here rather than in the analysis: the wrong side, an extra zero, a missing stop. One read from top to bottom against the plan, before pressing Send, catches them.\"],\"example\":\"Plan: buy 40 LABX at market, stop 98.00. Filled at 100.05, the stop loses 85.97 with fees. A ticket with 400 units would lose 859.61: one extra zero, ten times the risk.\",\"related\":[\"stop-loss-order\",\"position-sizing\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"chart-types\",\"name\":\"Chart types\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Line, OHLC bar and candle charts draw the same data with different amounts of detail.\",\"body\":[\"A line chart joins one price per period, usually the close, so it shows the path of closes and drops the open, high and low. An OHLC bar draws a vertical line from the low to the high, with a tick on the left for the open and on the right for the close. A candle shows the same four prices with a filled body between open and close.\",\"Pick the type for the task. A line suits long histories and comparisons; bars and candles suit reading single periods. None of them shows the order of trades inside a period.\"],\"example\":\"Twelve hourly LABX closes peak at 101.80, so a line chart of them never reaches 102.00. Drawn as candles, the same hours show hour 9 trading up to 102.30 before closing at 101.25.\",\"related\":[\"candlestick\",\"timeframe\"],\"missions\":[\"M01\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"candle-aggregation\",\"name\":\"Candle aggregation\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Longer candles are built from shorter ones: first open, highest high, lowest low, last close.\",\"body\":[\"A 4-hour candle summarises four 1-hour candles with four prices: the open of the first hour, the highest high, the lowest low and the close of the last hour. The order of events inside the four hours is lost, so pullbacks fold into the bigger candle.\",\"That's why one market can look choppy on a short timeframe and steady on a longer one. Neither view is wrong; they summarise different stretches of time. Choose the timeframe from how long you plan to hold, before looking for a signal.\"],\"example\":\"Four LABX hours: 100.00/100.60/99.80/100.40, 100.40/101.10/100.20/100.90, 100.90/101.00/99.90/100.10 and 100.10/100.50/99.60/100.30. The 4-hour candle is 100.00/101.10/99.60/100.30: a small bullish candle that hides hour 3's drop of 0.80.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"candlestick\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"volume\",\"name\":\"Volume\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The number of units traded in a period; it measures activity, not direction.\",\"body\":[\"Every unit bought is sold by someone, so volume can't lean towards buyers or sellers. It shows how busy a period was. Read each bar against its recent average: a bar's ratio to the average says more than its height.\",\"Spot forex has no central exchange, so retail FX and CFD charts usually show tick volume: how many times one broker's price changed. Futures and stocks report exchange volume. LABX volume is synthetic.\"],\"example\":\"LABX breaks out of a quiet range on 2,500 units against a 20-hour average of about 1,010: about 2.5 times. Later a news hour trades 2,700 units, the tallest bar, but the average has risen to about 1,290, so it's only about 2.1 times. It closes 0.02 above its open: lots of activity, no direction.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"candlestick\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.4\"]},{\"id\":\"candle-range\",\"name\":\"Candle range\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A candle's high minus its low; the average of recent ranges shows what normal noise looks like.\",\"body\":[\"Measure the range of each recent candle and average them to get the size of a normal candle. Price moves of about that size happen all the time and mean little on their own.\",\"A stop placed closer than one normal candle sits inside that noise and is easily hit. The standard version of this average, ATR, comes later in the course. A candle's range is not the same thing as a range market, the sideways phase.\"],\"example\":\"Ten LABX candles have ranges 0.42, 0.38, 0.55, 0.31, 0.47, 0.36, 0.44, 0.39, 0.52 and 0.36. Their total is 4.20, so a normal candle is 4.20 ÷ 10 = 0.42. A stop 0.20 from entry would sit inside one normal candle.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"candlestick\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"price-gaps\",\"name\":\"Price gaps\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A jump between one candle's close and the next one's open, with no trading at the prices in between.\",\"body\":[\"Gaps happen where trading pauses or jumps: overnight and weekend closes, or sudden news. No order can fill at a price inside a gap, because nothing traded there; orders resting in the gap fill at the next available price instead.\",\"The saying 'gaps always fill' is weak: price returns to many gaps, but it returns to most nearby prices sooner or later, and some gaps are never revisited.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 10 closes at 100.00 and candle 11 opens at 98.80: a 1.20 gap. A long's stop at 99.50 sits inside it; there is no trade at 99.50 to fill it, so it can only fill near the open.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"slippage\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"log-scale\",\"name\":\"Log scale\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A chart scale on which equal percentage moves take up equal heights.\",\"body\":[\"On a linear scale, equal price moves take equal heights, so a move from 20 to 40 looks small next to one from 80 to 160, though both are doublings. A log scale spaces prices by ratio, so the two doublings look the same.\",\"Use a log scale for long histories with large price changes and for comparing markets at different prices. Over a few days of LABX the two scales look almost identical.\"],\"example\":\"LABX goes from 50.00 to 100.00 (+100%), then from 100.00 to 150.00 (+50%). In price both moves are +50.00 and look equal on a linear scale; on a log scale the first is clearly taller.\",\"related\":[\"chart-types\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"chart-limits\",\"name\":\"Chart limits\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A chart records prices and volume, not who traded, why, the path inside a candle or what comes next.\",\"body\":[\"Everything read from a chart is built on prices and, where shown, volume. Motives, the order of trades inside a candle and the next candle are not on it, so any claim about them is interpretation.\",\"Chart examples are often chosen after the outcome was known, and random series draw convincing shapes too. Before trusting a pattern, ask how often it works, on how many charts, after costs, and compared with random.\"],\"example\":\"A textbook chart shows support at 98.00 holding three times before a rise to 101.50. Another chart with the same three touches breaks at the third and falls to 96.80. At the third touch the two looked the same.\",\"related\":[\"candlestick\",\"base-rates\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"hindsight-bias\",\"name\":\"Hindsight bias\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Judging past events as more predictable than they were, once the outcome is known.\",\"body\":[\"Once you see what happened, the path to it looks obvious, and it's easy to believe you would have seen it coming. On a chart, the turning point that looks clear with the whole history visible was one of several possibilities at the right edge.\",\"To counter it, judge decisions only with the candles that were visible at the time, and test ideas on blind charts before believing them.\"],\"example\":\"LABX falls from 102.00 to 98.10 by candle 30, then climbs to 101.00 by candle 40. With the whole chart visible, candle 30 looks like an obvious bottom. At candle 30 itself, the next ten candles could have gone either way.\",\"related\":[\"chart-limits\",\"base-rates\",\"decision-quality\",\"counterfactual-thinking\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"stop-entry-order\",\"name\":\"Stop entry order\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"A buy stop above the price, or a sell stop below it, enters only if a level breaks, then fills at market.\",\"body\":[\"A stop entry waits on the far side of a level. A buy stop sits above the price and triggers when the ask reaches it; a sell stop sits below and triggers when the bid does. Once triggered, it becomes a market order.\",\"Because it fills at market, it can fill worse than its trigger: after a gap, at the open. Limits work the other way round: a buy limit waits below the price for a dip, and one placed above the ask fills at once.\"],\"example\":\"A buy stop for 30 units at 102.10, above a range high of 102.00. The next candle opens with the ask at 102.45, so the order fills there: 0.35 above the trigger, 10.50 of slippage in all.\",\"related\":[\"market-order\",\"limit-order\",\"breakout\",\"slippage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"stop-limit-order\",\"name\":\"Stop-limit order\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"A stop that becomes a limit order when triggered, so it may not fill in a gap.\",\"body\":[\"A stop-limit has two prices: a trigger and a limit. When the trigger is reached, it becomes a limit order that fills only at the limit or better.\",\"For an entry that can be useful: it skips a breakout that runs too far. For protection it's a danger: if price gaps past the limit, nothing sells and the position stays open.\"],\"example\":\"Long from 100.00, protected by a sell stop-limit with trigger 98.00 and limit 97.80. After bad news the bid opens at 97.20: the limit can't fill, and the long stays open while price keeps falling.\",\"related\":[\"stop-entry-order\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"gap-risk\",\"limit-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"oco-order\",\"name\":\"OCO order\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Two linked orders where the fill of one cancels the other, as in a bracket or a two-sided breakout entry.\",\"body\":[\"One-cancels-other links two live orders. A bracket's stop and target are an OCO pair: when the target fills, the stop is cancelled.\",\"OCO also works for entries: a buy stop above a range and a sell stop below it. Without the link, both could fill in a whipsaw, and an exit left behind after a manual close can open a trade nobody decided.\"],\"example\":\"LABX ranges between 99.00 and 101.00. An OCO pair: a buy stop at 101.10 and a sell stop at 98.90, each with its own stop at 100.00. The ask reaches 101.10 and the buy fills; the sell stop is cancelled at the same moment.\",\"related\":[\"bracket-order\",\"stop-entry-order\",\"breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.3.4\"]},{\"id\":\"time-in-force\",\"name\":\"Time in force\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"How long an order stays active: Day, GTC, GTD, IOC or FOK.\",\"body\":[\"Day orders expire when the session ends; good-till-cancelled orders stay until they fill or you cancel them; good-till-date orders, until a date. IOC fills what it can now and cancels the rest; FOK fills everything at once or nothing.\",\"Every resting order is a decision that will execute later without you. A forgotten GTC order can fill in a gap and open a position you no longer planned.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 500 at 100.05, IOC, with 320 units offered at that price or better: 320 fill and 180 are cancelled. The same order as FOK fills nothing.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order\",\"oco-order\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.3.5\"]},{\"id\":\"order-queue\",\"name\":\"Order queue\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Resting orders fill by price, then by time, so a touched price doesn't guarantee a fill.\",\"body\":[\"An order book holds a queue of limit orders at each price. Price-time priority fills the best price first and, at the same price, the oldest order first.\",\"A limit at a popular price may wait behind many units: price can trade at your level and leave before reaching you. A large market order walks the book, filling across several prices at a worse average.\"],\"example\":\"Your buy limit for 100 units joins at 99.90 behind 200 units. Sellers sell 250 units at 99.90: 200 go to the orders ahead, 50 to you. The other 50 keep waiting.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order\",\"slippage\",\"time-in-force\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.3.6\"]},{\"id\":\"initial-risk\",\"name\":\"Initial risk\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The loss at the stop set at entry, costs included; it stays the trade's 1R even if the stop is moved.\",\"body\":[\"Initial risk is units × distance from the entry to the stop, plus the commission at entry and the commission at the stop. It is fixed the moment you enter and becomes the trade's 1R.\",\"Every result is then measured against it. A stop moved further away doesn't create a new 1R: it makes the eventual loss a bigger multiple of the original one, and the journal shows that.\"],\"example\":\"40 units at 50.00 with a stop at 48.50: 60.00 plus 1.00 and 0.97 in commissions = 61.97, the trade's 1R. An exit at 53.00 nets 117.94, so +1.90R. Stopped at a moved stop of 47.00, it loses 121.94: −1.97R.\",\"related\":[\"r-multiple\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.4.2\"]},{\"id\":\"costs-in-r\",\"name\":\"Costs in R\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Round-trip costs as a share of the stop distance; tighter stops make costs a bigger part of every R.\",\"body\":[\"Spread and commissions cost about the same per unit whatever the stop. Divided by the stop distance, they show how much of each R goes to costs before price moves.\",\"Costs also move the break-even: a loss costs the stop plus costs, a win pays the target minus costs. Break-even after costs = (stop + costs) ÷ (stop + target).\"],\"example\":\"Costs 0.20 per unit on LABX at 100.00. With a 2.00 stop they are 10% of R and a 2:1 plan breaks even at 36.7%. With a 0.50 stop they are 40% of R and break-even rises to 46.7%.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.4.6\"]},{\"id\":\"expected-drawdown\",\"name\":\"Expected drawdown\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The drawdown a valid method should be expected to go through, given its risk per trade and normal losing runs.\",\"body\":[\"Losing runs come with any method that doesn't win every time. Their length depends on the win rate and the number of trades; their cost depends on the risk per trade, compounded on what's left.\",\"Knowing the normal depth in advance separates variance from a broken method, and lets you set the depth at which you stop and review before the run arrives.\"],\"example\":\"At a 40% win rate, a run of 8 losses shows up in 49% of 100-trade stretches. At 1% risk it costs 7.7%; at 2%, 14.9%; at 5%, 33.7%.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"variance\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.4.8\"]},{\"id\":\"daily-loss-limit\",\"name\":\"Daily loss limit\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A maximum loss per day, set before the session; when the next trade's risk no longer fits, the day is over.\",\"body\":[\"The limit is written in R or as a percentage of equity before the session starts, and never raised during one. Before each entry, the trade's full risk must fit in the room that's left.\",\"Mistakes cluster after losses: revenge trades, bigger sizes, setups outside the plan. A daily limit ends a bad day before those mistakes can make it worse.\"],\"example\":\"Limit 3% of 10,000.00 = 300.00. Three 1% losses: 100.00, 99.00 and 98.01, so 297.01 used. The next trade would risk 97.02 with 2.99 left: the day is over.\",\"related\":[\"risk-per-trade\",\"revenge-trading\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.4.9\"]},{\"id\":\"swing-points\",\"name\":\"Swing points\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Swing highs and lows marked by a fixed rule and confirmed only after the candles that follow them.\",\"body\":[\"A swing high is a candle whose high is above the highs of the candles on each side; a swing low is the mirror image. This course uses two candles on each side. Swings are the turning points from which trends, ranges, pullbacks and structure breaks are read.\",\"Because the rule looks at the candles after the swing, every swing is confirmed late: with two candles on each side, two candles after it forms. The rule itself is a choice. Wider rules find fewer, bigger swings, so two traders can draw different structures on the same chart. Pick one rule and apply it the same way every time.\"],\"example\":\"Five candles have highs of 100.40, 100.70, 101.10, 100.90 and 100.60. The middle one is a swing high under the 2-candle rule, but only once the fifth candle has closed; before that, 101.10 was just the latest high.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.5.1\",\"L05\"]},{\"id\":\"pullback\",\"name\":\"Pullback\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A counter-move inside a trend, measured against the prior leg; the trend holds while it stays above the last higher low.\",\"body\":[\"A pullback is a move against the trend, inside it: a dip in an uptrend or a bounce in a downtrend. Measure it in price, as a share of the leg before it, or in normal candles. A pullback of 1.20 from a 4.00 leg is 30%.\",\"Depth doesn't decide whether the trend survives; structure does. In an uptrend, a pullback that holds above the last higher low keeps the pattern intact, while one that breaks it is a warning. Ratios such as 61.8% have shown no special power in the evidence this course relies on.\",\"Buying a pullback puts the stop closer than buying after a run, so the same risk buys more units.\"],\"example\":\"A leg runs from 103.55 to 106.00. A pullback to 104.80 gives back 1.20, about 49%, and holds above 103.55. A fall to 101.90 gives back 167% and breaks the last higher low.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"invalidation-level\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.5.3\"]},{\"id\":\"round-numbers\",\"name\":\"Round numbers\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Prices ending in 00 or 50 attract take-profits at the number and stops just beyond it; the effect is real but small.\",\"body\":[\"Traders like easy prices. In one bank's book of 9,667 currency orders, 9.3% of take-profits sat exactly at prices ending in 00, against 4.4% of stop-losses, and buy stops sat just above round numbers about twice as often as just below (Osler).\",\"Take-profits at the number tend to slow price there; stops just beyond it, once triggered, become market orders and can speed a break up. Published support and resistance levels stopped intraday trends 60.8% of the time, against 56.2% for random levels: a real but modest effect, not shown to be profitable after costs (Osler, 2000).\",\"For a stop, the lesson is practical: place it beyond the level and beyond the cluster of stops just past it, not exactly where everyone else's is.\"],\"example\":\"Long at 100.40 above support at 100.00, with a normal candle of 0.40. A stop at 99.95 sits in the cluster under 100.00; a stop at 99.60 clears the level and the cluster by about one normal candle.\",\"related\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"breakout\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"role-reversal\",\"name\":\"Role reversal\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A broken resistance may act as support, and a broken support as resistance; retests can hold or fail.\",\"body\":[\"After a breakout, price often comes back to the level it broke: a retest. Role reversal is the practitioner observation that the old resistance may now act as support, or an old support as resistance. A common explanation is orders left near the old level by traders caught on the wrong side of the break, or who missed it.\",\"The support for role reversal is practitioner experience rather than systematic tests. A retest that wicks into the old zone and closes beyond it is holding; a close back inside the range within a few candles is a failed breakout. Because both endings happen, the plan for each is set before the break.\"],\"example\":\"LABX breaks above 102.00 and rises to 103.20, then dips to 101.90 and closes at 102.30: the retest held. In another ending, it closes at 101.60, back inside the range: the breakout failed, and a stop back inside the range takes the buyer out.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"false-breakout\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.5.6\"]},{\"id\":\"structure-break\",\"name\":\"Structure break\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A trend's structure breaks when price closes beyond its last higher low or its last lower high.\",\"body\":[\"An uptrend is a sequence of higher highs and higher lows. When price closes below the last higher low, that sequence is broken: the first warning that the trend may be ending. A downtrend breaks the same way, with a close above its last lower high.\",\"A break is not yet a reversal. A downtrend's structure also needs a lower high and then a lower low, and many trends pass through a range first. What changes at once is what you trade: setups that need an intact uptrend, such as buying pullbacks, no longer qualify. Smart Money Concepts call this a 'change of character'; it's the same structure under another name.\"],\"example\":\"An uptrend's last higher low is 100.50. A candle closes at 100.24: the structure breaks. A rally then stops at 101.30, under the 102.20 top, and a later close at 99.88 goes under the 100.10 low: now highs and lows both step down.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.5.7\"]},{\"id\":\"multi-timeframe-analysis\",\"name\":\"Multi-timeframe analysis\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Reading context on a higher timeframe and acting on a lower one, with both timeframes fixed in advance.\",\"body\":[\"The same prices can be grouped into candles of different lengths. A higher timeframe, such as 4-hour candles, shows the bigger trend and its levels; the timeframe you trade, such as 1-hour candles, shows where to enter and where the stop goes.\",\"The two often disagree. A fall on the 1-hour chart inside a 4-hour uptrend is usually read as a pullback, as long as the 4-hour structure holds. Trading against the larger structure is possible, but it's a known extra risk.\",\"Every extra timeframe is another chance to find a chart that agrees with you. That's why a plan fixes two timeframes in advance, one for context and one for trading. The approach is a practitioner convention, not a tested edge.\"],\"example\":\"On 4-hour candles LABX makes higher lows at 97.60, 98.40 and 99.20. On 1-hour candles the last 12 fall from 101.40 to 99.98, with lower highs and lower lows. The fall stays above 99.20, so the read is a pullback in an uptrend.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"trend\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.5.8\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-capital\",\"name\":\"Risk capital\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Risk capital is money that could be lost entirely without harming living costs, debts or emergency savings.\",\"body\":[\"Before any rule about risk per trade comes a rule about which money may be at risk at all. Start from savings and take out an emergency fund and every amount already promised, such as tuition or a debt payment. What's left is the most a trading account should ever hold.\",\"Your plan's limits then sit inside it: risk per trade and the daily limit are shares of the trading account, never of money you need.\"],\"example\":\"Savings 20,000; emergency fund of six months' expenses, 12,000; next year's tuition, already promised, 5,000. Risk capital = 20,000 − 12,000 − 5,000 = 3,000. Only those 3,000 belong in a trading account.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.6.2\"]},{\"id\":\"pre-trade-checklist\",\"name\":\"Pre-trade checklist\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A pre-trade checklist is a short yes-or-no list run before every order; any 'no' means no trade, or a resized one.\",\"body\":[\"The list has 5 to 9 questions asked at the moment of decision: is the setup in your playbook, is every condition met, is the stop at the invalidation, is the size within the limit, is the daily limit intact, is no pause running, are you calm enough. Stop, size and limits can be verified from the ticket; the setup, the conditions and your state only you can answer.\",\"Checklists come from aviation and surgery, where they catch routine steps skipped under pressure; the evidence is from those fields, not from trading. They fail when ticked without reading, so the list stays short and each answer is tied to a visible fact.\"],\"example\":\"Six yeses and one 'no': buying 65 units at 50.00 with the stop at 48.00, the loss at the stop is 133.19 with costs, 1.3% of 10,000.00. The fix is size: at 48 units it's 98.36, within 1%. A missing stop, by contrast, can't be resized away: no trade.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"position-sizing\",\"journaling\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.6.3\"]},{\"id\":\"mistake-vs-variance\",\"name\":\"Mistake versus variance\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Separating losses caused by a broken process, which can be fixed, from valid trades that lost, which are the normal cost of trading.\",\"body\":[\"Variance is a trade that followed every rule and still lost; a mistake is a break in the process, such as a missing stop or an oversized position. Rules change only for mistakes, and only through the plan's rule for changes.\",\"A streak of losses is not a mistake by itself. At a 40% win rate, a run of six or more losses shows up in 87.3% of 100-trade sequences, assuming independent results. The journal, not the length of the run, tells the two buckets apart.\"],\"example\":\"A gap through the stop for −1.4R on a trade sized at 1%: variance. A stop moved from 48.00 to 47.00 and a loss of −1.5R where the plan allowed −1R: a mistake, with an error cost of −0.5R.\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"journaling\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"error-taxonomy\",\"name\":\"Error taxonomy\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A fixed list of named process errors used to tag trades and to count what mistakes cost in R.\",\"body\":[\"Naming mistakes the same way every time turns a vague feeling ('I traded badly') into counts: how often you moved a stop away, sized up after a loss or broke the playbook. MARKET//LAB uses names such as Entering without a stop, Risking more than planned, Moving the stop further away, Sizing up after a loss and Breaking your playbook.\",\"Error cost is the loss beyond what the plan allowed. Added up over a week, it shows which rule, kept, would have saved the most.\"],\"example\":\"Moving the stop further away: −2R where the plan allowed −1R, error cost −1R. Sizing up after a loss: −3R where −1R was allowed, −2R. Chasing outside the plan: −1R where the plan said no trade, −1R. Total error cost: −4R.\",\"related\":[\"journaling\",\"decision-quality\",\"mistake-vs-variance\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"process-kpis\",\"name\":\"Process KPIs\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Measures of rule-following, such as stop integrity and risk accuracy, that mean something even on small samples.\",\"body\":[\"Stop integrity, risk accuracy, plan adherence, limit compliance and journal completeness are each the share of trades, or days, that followed a rule. They depend on your actions alone, so 20 or 30 trades already measure them well.\",\"Profit over the same stretch is mostly noise: a system making +0.4R a trade still ends 20 trades with a net loss 12.6% of the time. Goals set on process KPIs can be met every time; goals set on profit invite oversizing.\"],\"example\":\"20 trades: one stop moved away, two oversized, three outside the playbook. Stop integrity 19 ÷ 20 = 95.0%, risk accuracy 18 ÷ 20 = 90.0%, plan adherence 17 ÷ 20 = 85.0%.\",\"related\":[\"decision-quality\",\"journaling\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.6.7\"]},{\"id\":\"emotions-in-trading\",\"name\":\"Emotions in trading\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Fear, greed, hope and regret are normal in trading; strong reactions go with worse decisions, so they are noticed, rated and handed to the plan.\",\"body\":[\"Every trader feels fear near a stop, greed after a run of wins, hope while holding a loser and regret after a missed move. These feelings are information about you, not about the market. The aim is not to stop feeling them but to notice them early and let rules written in advance decide.\",\"In Lo, Repin and Steenbarger (2005), 80 day traders followed for five weeks did significantly worse when their emotional reactions to gains and losses were more intense, and no single 'trader personality' stood out. It is a small study. A check-in before entry and after exit, a number from 1 to 5 and a word, makes your own pattern visible in the journal.\"],\"example\":\"Before entry you rate yourself 2 out of 5, calm. After a stop-out you rate 4 out of 5, regret. Your plan says a check-in of 4 or higher means a pause before the next decision, so you close the ticket and step away.\",\"related\":[\"fomo\",\"revenge-trading\",\"loss-aversion\",\"journaling\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\",\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.7.1\"]},{\"id\":\"gamblers-fallacy\",\"name\":\"Gambler's fallacy\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Expecting a reversal after a streak of independent outcomes; its mirror image, the hot-hand fallacy, expects the streak to continue.\",\"body\":[\"After six losses on a setup that wins half the time, a win feels due. If results are independent, the next trade is still 50%. In 1,000,000 simulated coin flips, heads followed a run of five tails 49.7% of the time. The hot-hand fallacy is the mirror image: after five wins you feel 'on fire' and size up.\",\"Both errors tend to end in the same place: a size set by the streak instead of by the plan. Market results can cluster when conditions change, so a streak is answered with a review rule written in the plan, not with a feeling.\"],\"example\":\"A setup wins 50% of trades at +1.5R and loses 1R. Over 100 trades, a run of six losses in a row is 54.6% likely, so six losses alone don't show the setup broke. The next trade still risks 1%.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"revenge-trading\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.7.3\"]},{\"id\":\"overtrading\",\"name\":\"Overtrading\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Trading more often than the plan's setups allow, which adds costs without adding edge.\",\"body\":[\"Overtrading often starts with boredom, a run of wins or a loss to win back. Each extra trade pays the spread and commission whether or not it has an edge: the costs are certain, the edge is not.\",\"Barber and Odean found that the most active fifth of 66,465 US households earned 11.4% a year against the market's 17.9%; trading costs and frequency explained the gap. Rules such as a maximum number of trades per day, and 'a move that isn't in the plan isn't a trade', tie frequency to setups.\"],\"example\":\"Four extra trades a day with no edge, each costing about 0.10R, cost 0.40R a day: 8R over 20 trading days, roughly 8% of the account at 1% risk per trade.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"action-bias\",\"revenge-trading\",\"fomo\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.7.5\"]},{\"id\":\"action-bias\",\"name\":\"Action bias\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Preferring to act rather than wait, which produces trades taken out of boredom.\",\"body\":[\"After hours with no setup, doing something feels better than doing nothing, and a small move outside the plan starts to look like an opportunity. That is action bias, and it is a common source of off-plan trades.\",\"The counter is to treat waiting as a decision. A logged 'no trade: no setup' is a good decision, and MARKET//LAB's Decision Quality scores it as one.\"],\"example\":\"Three quiet hours, then a 0.30 pop with no pullback and nothing from the playbook. The action-bias trade is to take it 'small'; the plan's answer is to skip it and log the no-trade.\",\"related\":[\"overtrading\",\"decision-quality\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.7.5\"]},{\"id\":\"if-then-rules\",\"name\":\"If-then rules\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Responses decided in advance that link an observable trigger to a concrete action.\",\"body\":[\"An if-then rule, or implementation intention, has two parts: a trigger you can observe, such as a number, an event or a time, and an action you can check, such as pause, skip, close, resize, review or stop for the day. Vague rules such as 'stay calm' leave the decision to the moment when judgement is weakest.\",\"Knowing about a bias doesn't remove it; structure helps. Guard-rails, such as a required stop, a daily lock, a pause timer and preset sizes, make the rule the default.\"],\"example\":\"'Don't chase' becomes: if price is more than one normal candle (0.40) beyond my planned entry, then I don't enter and wait for a pullback.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"cooling-off-pause\",\"fomo\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.7.6\"]},{\"id\":\"cooling-off-pause\",\"name\":\"Cooling-off pause\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"A set pause after a loss or a sharp move, with a fixed routine, before any new decision.\",\"body\":[\"The pause has a length you choose in your plan and a routine that fills it: close the ticket, step away, check in from 1 to 5, reframe, run the checklist, then decide and write it down. A valid setup can wait a few minutes, or another will come.\",\"In a qualitative study at four London investment banks, higher-performing traders relied more on early reframing ('one loss is one draw from many') than on suppression ('I feel nothing'). The 'fresh eyes' question, 'would I open this trade now, at this size?', brings the same calm view to positions already open.\"],\"example\":\"Stopped out for −100.00 on a 10,000.00 account. The plan's pause is 15 minutes. After the routine the setup still passes the checklist, so the re-entry risks 1% of 9,900.00: 47 units with a 2.00 stop, losing 98.66 at the stop with costs.\",\"related\":[\"revenge-trading\",\"if-then-rules\",\"emotions-in-trading\",\"loss-aversion\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.7.7\"]},{\"id\":\"martingale\",\"name\":\"Martingale\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Doubling the stake after each loss so one win recovers everything; with finite capital it ends in ruin.\",\"body\":[\"After n losses a doubling bet is 2ⁿ times the first, and 2ⁿ − 1 times the first is already lost. The first win recovers it all plus one stake, so the method wins small almost every cycle.\",\"In a fair game each cycle's expected result is exactly zero, and costs make it negative. Long losing runs are rare on any one bet but likely over many, and the money runs out before the doubling can continue.\"],\"example\":\"A 10.00 first bet on fair coin flips: 9 losses in a row cost 5,110.00, and the next bet is 5,120.00. Over 1,000 flips, a run of 9 shows up 62.4% of the time.\",\"related\":[\"averaging-down\",\"position-sizing\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.1\"]},{\"id\":\"averaging-down\",\"name\":\"Averaging down\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Adding to a losing position without a predefined limit; martingale in disguise.\",\"body\":[\"Averaging down buys more of a position that is losing, to lower its average price. The stop is usually skipped or moved, so the risk grows exactly when the idea is failing.\",\"A planned split entry is different: sizes, levels and one stop are fixed before the first order, and the total loss at the stop stays inside the budget.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 50 at 100.00 with a stop at 98.00, so 1R = 100.00. Buy 50 more at 98.00 instead of exiting: at 96.00 the loss is 100 × (99.00 − 96.00) = 300.00, 3R.\",\"related\":[\"martingale\",\"position-sizing\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.1\"]},{\"id\":\"grid-trading\",\"name\":\"Grid trading\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Orders at fixed steps around a price, often without stops, that pile up exposure when a trend arrives.\",\"body\":[\"A grid buys at fixed steps below the price and sells each lot a step higher. In a range it closes many small wins; its losing lots stay open.\",\"When price trends away, every step adds units and the open loss grows faster than the fall. The equity curve looks smooth until the trend makes the hidden loss visible.\"],\"example\":\"10 units bought at every 1.00 from 99.00 to 90.00: 100 units at an average of 94.50. At 90.00 the open loss is 450.00, 15 times the 30.00 the grid earned in the range before.\",\"related\":[\"martingale\",\"averaging-down\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.2\"]},{\"id\":\"performance-claims\",\"name\":\"Performance claims\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A performance claim is judged on sample size, costs, period, worst loss, selection and a benchmark comparison.\",\"body\":[\"A win rate or a return means little alone. The sample says whether luck could explain it, costs whether it survives trading, the worst loss whether a high win rate hides rare disasters, and the benchmark whether it beat simply holding the market.\",\"Selection is the hardest part to see: only survivors are shown. Even real edges shrink once published, so a published figure is an upper bound.\"],\"example\":\"Of 1,000 coin-flip systems with no edge, about 28 show +20R or more over 100 trades. Show only those and luck looks like skill.\",\"related\":[\"survivorship-bias\",\"expectancy\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.3\"]},{\"id\":\"broker-due-diligence\",\"name\":\"Broker due diligence\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Checking a broker's licence, client-money rules, protections and execution model before opening an account.\",\"body\":[\"The licence is checked on the regulator's own register, for the exact company and website you'd sign with. Then come client-money rules, compensation schemes and retail protections such as negative balance protection.\",\"Finally the execution model: an A-book broker passes orders to the market, a B-book broker takes the other side, so client losses are its revenue.\"],\"example\":\"Search the firm on the FCA register in the UK or the ASF register in Romania, check that the name and web address match, read how client money is held and whether the broker takes the other side of your trades.\",\"related\":[\"scam-red-flags\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.4\"]},{\"id\":\"scam-red-flags\",\"name\":\"Scam red flags\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Guaranteed returns, bonuses, pressure, offshore-only licences and blocked withdrawals signal a likely scam.\",\"body\":[\"No market return can be guaranteed, and regulated firms don't promise one. Bonuses tie up deposits, deadlines cut your checking short, and offshore-only licences leave little protection.\",\"An offer that reaches you without being asked for is a scam until you've checked it yourself, on the regulator's register.\"],\"example\":\"'30% a month guaranteed, 3 places left today, deposit with our offshore partner for a 100% bonus': at least four red flags in one message.\",\"related\":[\"broker-due-diligence\",\"fomo\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.4\"]},{\"id\":\"effective-leverage\",\"name\":\"Effective leverage\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Position value divided by equity; it sets how hard a price move hits the account, whatever the broker allows.\",\"body\":[\"The broker's maximum leverage is only a ceiling. Effective leverage measures what you actually hold: a 1% move costs 1% of the account for each 1× of effective leverage.\",\"The move that wipes out the account is 100% ÷ effective leverage. Sizing from the stop keeps the planned risk fixed; leverage only decides how easy it is to oversize.\"],\"example\":\"Equity 10,000.00, positions 50,000.00: 5×. A 4% move costs 2,000.00, 20% of the account, and a 20% move wipes it out.\",\"related\":[\"leverage\",\"exposure\",\"margin-close-out\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.5\"]},{\"id\":\"margin-close-out\",\"name\":\"Margin close-out\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The forced closing of positions when equity falls too far below required margin; a failure of sizing, not a stop.\",\"body\":[\"When losses eat into the margin a position needs, the broker may call for funds and then close positions automatically. For EU retail CFD accounts the close-out comes at 50% of the required margin.\",\"Unlike a stop, nobody chose the price: it comes after most of the account is gone, at whatever price is there.\"],\"example\":\"Equity 5,000, 1 lot of EUR/USD at 1.1000 (110,000) at 30:1: margin 3,666.67. The close-out comes at 1,833.33 of equity, a 3,166.67 loss, 63% of the account, after a 2.9% move.\",\"related\":[\"effective-leverage\",\"leverage\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"1.8.5\"]},{\"id\":\"candle-vocabulary\",\"name\":\"Candle vocabulary\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Classifying a candle's body and shadows against recent candles as long, short, doji, marubozu or spinning top.\",\"body\":[\"A candle's size only means something next to its neighbours. The app follows the TA-Lib defaults: a body is long above the average body of the last 10 candles, very long above 3 times it, short below it, and a doji when it's at most 10% of the average range. Wicks are judged the same way.\",\"The names follow from those measurements: a marubozu is long with almost no wicks, a spinning top is short with both wicks longer than the body, a high-wave candle has wicks over twice the body, and the doji family is sorted by its wicks. The thresholds are settings, not truths, and the names describe an hour; they don't predict the next one.\"],\"example\":\"A LABX candle opens at 100.00 and closes at 100.50. After ten candles with bodies averaging 0.20, its 0.50 body is 2.5 times the average: long. After ten candles averaging 0.80, the same body is short.\",\"related\":[\"candlestick\",\"volatility\",\"candle-range\"],\"missions\":[\"M01\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.1\"]},{\"id\":\"rejection-candles\",\"name\":\"Long-shadow candles\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Hammers, shooting stars and their twins: candles whose long shadow marks a price that was rejected.\",\"body\":[\"Two shapes carry four names. A small body near the top with a long lower shadow is a hammer after a decline and a hanging man after a rise. A small body near the bottom with a long upper shadow is an inverted hammer after a decline and a shooting star after a rise. The app wants the long shadow at least twice the body and the other one under a tenth of the average range.\",\"As signals they're unproven. In Bulkowski's US stock data the hammer broke upward first 60% of the time and the shooting star downward 59%, before costs; the hanging man and inverted hammer mostly did the opposite of their names.\"],\"example\":\"Open 99.40, high 99.55, low 98.60, close 99.50 after a decline: body 0.10, lower shadow 0.80 (8 times the body), upper shadow 0.05. A hammer. The identical shape after a rise is a hanging man.\",\"related\":[\"candle-vocabulary\",\"trend\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[\"M01\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.2\"]},{\"id\":\"pattern-context\",\"name\":\"Pattern context\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The checks that decide whether a candle pattern counts: something to reverse, a meaningful location, size and volume.\",\"body\":[\"A reversal pattern needs something to reverse (a decline before a bullish one), a location (its extreme at a support or resistance zone) and usually a confirming close. Size against ATR and volume against its average can be added as further filters.\",\"Whether context improves results is mixed evidence: some tests found an effect, others none, and there is no clean test of the filters. On a random walk no filter helps, because nothing in the past predicts the future.\"],\"example\":\"Two identical hammers: A after a 10-candle decline with its low in a support zone near 98.60; B in the middle of a sideways range. A passes the trend and location checks and waits for confirmation; B fails both and is skipped.\",\"related\":[\"pattern-confirmation\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"trend\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"pattern-confirmation\",\"name\":\"Pattern confirmation\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Waiting for a close beyond the pattern before acting, which raises the hit rate but worsens the entry.\",\"body\":[\"Confirmation means waiting until a later candle closes beyond the pattern: above its high for a bullish pattern, below its low for a bearish one. Some failures are filtered out, but the entry moves further from the stop and closer to the target.\",\"That makes the risk per unit larger, the position smaller and the reward-to-risk lower. The fair comparison between entering early and waiting is expectancy after costs, not win rate.\"],\"example\":\"Hammer low 98.60, stop 98.50, target 101.50. Buying at the hammer's close (ask 99.55) risks 1.05 for 1.95: R:R 1.86, 95 units on a 100.00 budget. After a confirming close at 99.90, buying at 99.95 risks 1.45 for 1.55: R:R 1.07, 68 units.\",\"related\":[\"pattern-context\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"engulfing-pattern\",\"name\":\"Engulfing pattern\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A two-candle pattern in which the second real body fully covers the first, opposite-coloured body.\",\"body\":[\"A bullish engulfing follows a decline: a bearish candle, then a bullish one that opens at or below its close and closes at or above its open, with at least one end strictly beyond. Only bodies count; a candle whose wicks cover the first but whose body doesn't is an outside bar, not an engulfing.\",\"In Bulkowski's data the bearish version reversed 79% of the time yet ranked 91 of 103 for overall performance: the turns were frequent but small. A high hit rate with small moves can lose after costs, so judge it by expectancy.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 1 opens 101.00 and closes 100.20. Candle 2 opens 100.10 and closes 101.10: 100.10 ≤ 100.20 and 101.10 ≥ 101.00, a bullish engulfing. Had candle 2 opened at 100.30, its body wouldn't cover the bottom, whatever its wicks did.\",\"related\":[\"harami-pattern\",\"pattern-context\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"harami-pattern\",\"name\":\"Harami\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A two-candle pattern in which a small real body sits inside the previous, larger body.\",\"body\":[\"A harami is the engulfing turned around: a long body, then a short, opposite-coloured body inside it. If the second candle is a doji, it's a harami cross.\",\"It compares bodies, while the price-action inside bar compares whole ranges, so the two often coincide but not always. Bulkowski's bullish harami reversed 53% of the time, close to a coin flip.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 1 falls from 101.00 to 100.00; candle 2 rises from 100.20 to 100.50. Body 2 sits inside body 1: a bullish harami. If candle 2's high were 101.20, it would still be a harami, but not an inside bar.\",\"related\":[\"engulfing-pattern\",\"candle-vocabulary\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"penetration-patterns\",\"name\":\"Penetration patterns\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Two-candle patterns named by how deep the second candle closes into the first body, from on-neck to piercing.\",\"body\":[\"After a long bearish candle, a bullish candle that opens below its low gets a name from where it closes: on-neck at about the first low, in-neck just inside the body, thrusting up to the middle, piercing past the middle, engulfing at or above the first open. Depth = (close 2 − close 1) ÷ body 1.\",\"Theory calls the first three bearish continuation and the last two bullish reversal, so it flips at one cent past the middle. The data are thin: piercing 64% and dark cloud cover about 60% in Bulkowski's tests, no statistics for the other three.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 1: 101.00 down to 100.00, low 99.90. Candle 2 opens at 99.70. A close at 100.35 is 35% in, a thrusting line; at 100.70 it's 70% in, a piercing line; at 101.00 or above, an engulfing.\",\"related\":[\"engulfing-pattern\",\"candle-vocabulary\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"star-patterns\",\"name\":\"Star patterns\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Three-candle turns with a small, gapped middle candle: morning and evening stars, doji stars, abandoned babies.\",\"body\":[\"A morning star is a long bearish candle, a small body gapped below it, then a bullish candle closing at or above the middle of the first body (Bulkowski; TA-Lib's default asks less). A doji in the middle makes a doji star; a doji gapped beyond both neighbours' wicks makes an abandoned baby. Evening stars mirror them at tops.\",\"The gap rules matter. On a market that trades around the clock candles open where the last closed, so strict stars almost never appear, and relaxing the rule creates a different pattern whose rates are untested. Part of the published rates is built in: the third candle is already the move.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 1: 102.00 → 101.00. Candle 2: 100.80 → 100.70, its body below 101.00. Candle 3 must close at or above 101.50 for Bulkowski, above 101.30 for TA-Lib's default; a close at 101.60 completes the star.\",\"related\":[\"pattern-confirmation\",\"price-gaps\",\"candle-vocabulary\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"confirmed-candle-patterns\",\"name\":\"Confirmed candle patterns\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Multi-candle patterns whose last candle already confirms the move, such as three inside up or three white soldiers.\",\"body\":[\"Three inside up is a harami plus a close above the first candle's open; three outside up is an engulfing plus a higher close; three white soldiers and three black crows are three strong candles in a row. Each ends with its own confirming candle.\",\"Their reversal rates look higher partly by construction: the last candle closes near the pattern's edge, so a break that way needs little more. On a random walk, three inside up 'broke up first' 74% of the time against the harami's 45%, and both lost the costs. Buying at the close also means a wide stop and less room to the next zone.\"],\"example\":\"Soldiers from 99.90 to 102.00, low 99.80. Buy at 102.05, stop 99.70: risk 2.35. Resistance at 103.00 leaves a reward of 0.95, so R:R = 0.40, and 100.00 vUSD of risk buys only 42 units.\",\"related\":[\"pattern-confirmation\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"harami-pattern\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"pattern-name-vs-evidence\",\"name\":\"Names versus evidence\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Judging any pattern by its rule, sample size, base rate, result after costs and scope, not by its name.\",\"body\":[\"Several patterns tested against their own names: the hanging man kept rising about 59% of the time, the inverted hammer kept falling about 65%, the bearish three-line strike broke upward 84%. A name is a story; only data say which way a pattern leans.\",\"Five questions judge any pattern: what is the exact rule, how many cases, what rate compared with 50%, what result after costs, and in which market, timeframe and years. On a random walk, shapes with and without names score the same, about 50%: that's the control a pattern has to beat.\"],\"example\":\"A rare pattern is quoted at 86%. With 14 cases, 12 wins make 86%, and the true rate could be as low as about 60%. The card's second question, how many cases, stops it before the rate is believed.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"sample-size\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"random-walk\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.8\"]},{\"id\":\"alternative-chart-types\",\"name\":\"Alternative chart types\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Heikin-Ashi, Renko and point & figure: ways of drawing price whose plotted values are not traded prices.\",\"body\":[\"Heikin-Ashi candles average real prices: close = (O + H + L + C) ÷ 4, open = the average of the previous HA open and close. They're smoother and lag. Renko draws equal bricks only when price moves a full box, and point & figure stacks Xs and Os by box size and reversal amount; both drop time.\",\"None of their values is a price you could trade, so stops, fills and backtests must use real prices. Predictive claims built on them are unproven, though point & figure signals at least follow fixed rules and can be tested.\"],\"example\":\"Real candle 8: 100.40 / 100.60 / 99.70 / 99.90. HA close = 400.60 ÷ 4 = 100.15, below the HA open of 100.61, so the HA candle is red. An exit on it fills at the real bid, 99.85, not at 100.15.\",\"related\":[\"chart-types\",\"candlestick\",\"trend\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.1.9\"]},{\"id\":\"trendlines-and-channels\",\"name\":\"Trendlines and channels\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Lines through swing points that describe a trend; a break is a warning to confirm, not a signal.\",\"body\":[\"A trendline joins swing lows in a rise or swing highs in a fall. Two points draw it, the second higher (or lower) than the first, and a third touch confirms it. Draw on wicks, judge breaks on closes. A parallel line through the opposite extreme makes a channel. The scale matters: a rise that compounds fits a straight line on a log scale, not on a linear one.\",\"Drawing is subjective: when Osler compared the levels currency firms published, they agreed only about 30% of the time. No robust study shows that a trendline break predicts a reversal (evidence D). Treat a break as a warning and confirm it with horizontal structure, such as a close under the last swing low. Regression channels refit with each new candle, so in a backtest they read the future.\"],\"example\":\"Swing lows at 100.00 (candle 4) and 101.00 (candle 12) give a line rising 0.125 a candle; candle 20's low, 102.00, touches it. Candle 27 closes at 102.77, under the line, but price then goes sideways and never closes under 102.00.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"swing-points\",\"log-scale\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.1\"]},{\"id\":\"chart-pattern-anatomy\",\"name\":\"Chart pattern anatomy\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The parts every chart pattern shares: prior trend, boundaries, volume, breakout, target, invalidation and aftermath.\",\"body\":[\"Bulkowski's anatomy fits every chart pattern: a prior trend to continue or reverse; boundaries with enough touches, at least three on one line and two on the other; volume that usually fades inside; a breakout confirmed by a close beyond a boundary; a target measured from the pattern's height; an invalidation level for the stop; a frequent throwback to the broken edge; and context that shifts the odds.\",\"The statistics attached to each part are base rates with a scope: US stocks, daily charts, measured to the ideal exit. Lo, Mamaysky and Wang (2000) found that patterns carry some information, and warned that what detects an anomaly need not make profits (evidence C).\"],\"example\":\"A rise from 97.00 meets a box: tops 102.00, 101.95 and 102.00, bottoms 100.00 and 100.05. Volume fades from 1,150 to 600, then candle 34 closes at 102.45 on 1,720. The stop goes under the last swing low inside, 101.00; a throwback reaches 102.05 before a high of 103.75.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"false-breakout\",\"invalidation-level\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"breakout-confirmation-filters\",\"name\":\"Breakout filters\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Rules that confirm a breakout, such as a close beyond the level by part of the ATR, trading entry price for fewer false signals.\",\"body\":[\"A close beyond the boundary is the minimum. Filters ask for more: a margin in percent (1% or 3%), a margin in ATR (0.25 to 2 × ATR(14)), two closes in a row, or a gap through the level. Each one skips some false breakouts, and enters the real ones later and at a worse price.\",\"Percentage filters were built for stocks on daily charts. On LABX 1H, 3% of 104.00 is 3.12, about 8 ATR, so it would confirm almost nothing; the app's default is 0.5 × ATR. On a random walk no filter creates an edge: it changes how many trades you take, not their expectancy.\"],\"example\":\"Resistance 104.00, ATR(14) 0.40. Closes at 104.05 and 104.12 fall back inside on the next candle. A 0.5 × ATR filter (104.20) skips both and enters on the 104.32 close, 0.17 later than the first close beyond.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"false-breakout\",\"volatility\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"pattern-target\",\"name\":\"Scaled pattern target\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A target projected from a pattern's height and scaled by how often the full height was reached in past data.\",\"body\":[\"The classic measure rule projects the pattern's height from the breakout: for a head-and-shoulders top, the distance from the head to the neckline, taken off the neckline. Bulkowski's data shows how often that full target was reached, and he recommends scaling it: target = breakout ± height × the share that reached it.\",\"The share differs by pattern and direction: 51% for head-and-shoulders tops, 71% for inverse ones, 78% for rectangles breaking up and 54% breaking down, 46% for flags. These come from US stocks on daily charts. Before any trade, compare the scaled target with the distance to the stop: many patterns offer less reward than risk.\"],\"example\":\"A head-and-shoulders top with its head at 106.00 and its neckline at 103.00 is 3.00 tall. The full target is 100.00; scaled by 51%, it is 103.00 − 1.53 = 101.47.\",\"related\":[\"reward-to-risk\",\"base-rates\",\"chart-pattern-anatomy\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"double-tops-and-bottoms\",\"name\":\"Double tops and bottoms\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Two similar extremes with a valley or peak between them, confirmed only by a close beyond that level.\",\"body\":[\"A double top is two peaks near the same price; the valley between them is the confirmation level, and the pattern exists only after a close below it. A double bottom is the mirror. On LABX 1H the app asks for peaks within 0.5 × ATR(14) of each other, at least 10 candles apart, with a valley at least 3 × ATR deep (an app convention).\",\"Without confirmation, about 60% of twin peaks kept rising in Bulkowski's data. Confirmed Adam & Adam tops failed 25% of the time and reached the full target 64%; Eve & Eve bottoms failed 12% and reached it 65% (evidence C). On a random walk twin peaks are common, and what follows them is the base rate.\"],\"example\":\"Peaks at 105.00 and 104.90 with a valley at 102.00: height 3.00. After a close under 102.00, the scaled target is 102.00 − 3.00 × 0.64 = 100.08; the full height would give 99.00.\",\"related\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"breakout\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"anticipation-vs-confirmation\",\"name\":\"Anticipation versus confirmation\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Entering before a signal completes for a better price, or after it for a higher hit rate, compared by expectancy.\",\"body\":[\"An anticipation entry, such as selling at the second peak of a possible double top, gets a better price and a tighter stop and pays more per win, but many such setups never complete. A confirmation entry waits for the close beyond the level: it wins more often, but enters later, with a wider stop and less of the move left.\",\"Neither is better by default. Compare them by expectancy after costs on the same set of setups, never by win rate alone: a method that wins 65% of the time can still lose money if its wins are small.\"],\"example\":\"Selling at 104.33 near the second peak, with a stop at 105.10, risks 0.77 to make 4.25: about 5.5R before costs. Selling at 101.78 after the confirming close risks 3.32 to make 1.70: about 0.5R.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"double-tops-and-bottoms\",\"false-breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"head-and-shoulders\",\"name\":\"Head and shoulders\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Three peaks with the middle one highest and a neckline through the lows between them, or the inverse at bottoms.\",\"body\":[\"After a rise, the left shoulder, the head and the right shoulder form three peaks: the head highest, the shoulders at similar heights and distances from it. The neckline joins the two lows between the peaks. A close under a flat or up-sloping neckline confirms the top; with a down-sloping neckline, Bulkowski confirms on a close under the right-hand low. The inverse pattern forms at bottoms.\",\"In Bulkowski's data, tops failed 19% of the time and reached the full target 51%; inverse patterns 11% and 71% (evidence C). Lo, Mamaysky and Wang found the shape added information in US stocks from 1962 to 1996, and Chang and Osler found it profitable in 2 of 6 currency pairs. Random walks produce the shape too.\"],\"example\":\"Shoulders at 104.80 and 104.70, the head at 106.00, lows of 103.00 and 103.00. Candle 31 closes at 102.57, under the neckline. Scaled target: 103.00 − 3.00 × 0.51 = 101.47; the fall reached 101.30.\",\"related\":[\"chart-pattern-anatomy\",\"pattern-target\",\"random-walk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.4\"]},{\"id\":\"rectangles-and-triples\",\"name\":\"Rectangles and triple tops/bottoms\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Horizontal ranges with repeated tests of both edges, which remain ranges until a confirmed breakout.\",\"body\":[\"A rectangle is a range between nearly flat support and resistance, with at least three touches on one side and two on the other. A triple top or bottom is the same box named after three tests of one edge: until a close beyond the lowest valley (or the highest peak), it's still a range, and many such ranges break the other way.\",\"In Bulkowski's data, rectangles that formed after a rise broke out upward 63% of the time; upward breakouts reached the full target 78% of the time, downward ones 54%. After a partial rise the breakout went down 75% of the time, and after a partial decline up 79%: a tilt from one data set, not a certainty.\"],\"example\":\"A box from 100.00 to 102.00 is 2.00 tall. Scaled targets: 102.00 + 2.00 × 0.78 = 103.56 upward, and 100.00 − 2.00 × 0.54 = 98.92 downward.\",\"related\":[\"range\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"triangles\",\"name\":\"Triangles\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Converging boundaries (ascending, descending, symmetrical) whose breakout bias comes from data, not from the shape.\",\"body\":[\"An ascending triangle has a flat top over rising lows; a descending one a flat bottom under falling highs; a symmetrical one falling highs over rising lows. Each needs at least three touches on one line and two on the other, and volume usually fades. Extended, the lines meet at the apex.\",\"Bulkowski counted upward breakouts in 63% of ascending, 60% of symmetrical and 53% of descending triangles, so the descending triangle's bearish label doesn't hold. Breakouts usually came about 64–74% of the way to the apex. Near the apex the lines sit closer than a normal candle's range, so a crossing there says little.\"],\"example\":\"A flat top at 104.00 over lows of 100.00, 101.00 and 102.00: the lines meet on candle 35. Candle 23 closes at 104.25, 62.5% of the way from the first low to the apex. Height 4.00 × 70% gives a target of 106.80.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"false-breakout\",\"chart-pattern-anatomy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"flags-and-pennants\",\"name\":\"Flags and pennants\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Short pauses after a steep move whose classic \\\"half-mast\\\" target is reached less than half the time.\",\"body\":[\"A flag is a brief pause with parallel lines sloping against a steep move, the flagpole; a pennant is the same pause with converging lines. On LABX 1H the app asks for a pole of at least 3 × ATR in 8 candles or fewer, and a pause of 5 to 20 candles; longer pauses are rectangles or channels.\",\"The half-mast rule projects the whole pole from the breakout. Bulkowski found flags reached it 46% of the time and pennants 35%; flags failed 44–45% of the time and pennants 54% (evidence D for the rule). Scale the pole before you project it.\"],\"example\":\"Pole from 100.00 to 104.00, breakout at 103.60. Half-mast is 107.60; scaled, 103.60 + 4.00 × 0.46 = 105.44. Price peaked at 105.70.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"breakout\",\"pattern-target\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"measured-move\",\"name\":\"Measured move\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A projection that a second leg will roughly repeat the first; in the data it is usually shorter.\",\"body\":[\"Three swing points define a measured move: the first leg from A to B, then a correction to C. The projection puts D at C plus the length of AB (D100), or at a share of it. In Bulkowski's stock data the second leg averaged +31% against +36% for the first, and a target at 60% of AB was reached 60% of the time (evidence C).\",\"Plan with D60 and treat D100 as the stretch, and check the reward against a stop below C.\"],\"example\":\"A 100.00, B 104.00, C 102.00: AB = 4.00. D60 = 102.00 + 2.40 = 104.40 and D100 = 106.00. Price reached 104.90.\",\"related\":[\"swing-points\",\"trend\",\"pattern-target\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"wedges-and-broadening\",\"name\":\"Wedges and broadening formations\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Converging sloped lines (wedges) or diverging lines (megaphones, diamonds), with weak and mixed statistics.\",\"body\":[\"A wedge has two lines sloping the same way and converging: in a rising wedge the lower line is steeper, in a falling wedge the upper one. Parallel lines make a channel instead. A broadening formation, or megaphone, has diverging lines; a diamond widens, then narrows.\",\"Rising wedges broke down 60% of the time in Bulkowski's data, but 51% of those breakdowns failed to fall 5%, rank 36 of 36 (evidence D for 'bearish'). Falling wedges broke up 68% of the time. Broadening formations and diamonds are rare and drawn subjectively, and their widening swings put stops far away.\"],\"example\":\"A rising wedge 3.00 tall breaks down at 102.00. Scaled by 32%, the target is 101.04. Short at 101.95 with a stop at 103.00, the reward-to-risk is 0.91 ÷ 1.05 = 0.87.\",\"related\":[\"trendlines-and-channels\",\"triangles\",\"reward-to-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.8\"]},{\"id\":\"rounding-and-v-patterns\",\"name\":\"Rounding and V patterns\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Slow bowl-shaped turns and cups that become visible only late, and sharp V turns that can't be seen in real time.\",\"body\":[\"A rounding bottom turns from falling to rising in a slow bowl; a cup with handle adds a short pullback, the handle, in the upper half near the rim. Both confirm on a close above the rim, after much of the rise is done. In Bulkowski's data rounding bottoms failed 4% of the time and cups with handle 5% (evidence C), from months-long patterns on daily and weekly charts.\",\"A V reversal turns sharply with no pause. In real time its low looks like any pause in a fall; only later candles show which it was. Its statistics are measured in hindsight, so using V's as they form has no support (evidence D).\"],\"example\":\"A cup with its rim at 102.00 and its bottom at 97.00 is 5.00 deep. After a close at 102.20 above the rim, the scaled target is 102.00 + 5.00 × 0.61 = 105.05, with the stop under the handle's low, 101.00.\",\"related\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"sample-size\",\"pattern-target\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.9\"]},{\"id\":\"busted-patterns\",\"name\":\"Busted patterns\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Patterns that break out, fail, and then break through the opposite boundary.\",\"body\":[\"A pattern busts when its breakout travels less than 1 × ATR(14) (the app's convention; Bulkowski uses about 10% on daily charts) and price then closes beyond the opposite boundary. A failed breakout, a close back inside, ends the original trade; the bust is a new situation.\",\"Write the plan before the breakout: what you do if it works, if it fails and if it busts. Typically: hold with the stop, exit on a close back inside, then stand aside or trade the bust with its own entry, stop and size. Bulkowski describes strong moves after busts, but his numbers for them aren't reproduced here (evidence C).\"],\"example\":\"An ascending triangle with a flat top at 104.00 breaks out with a close at 104.30 (ATR 0.40). The high reaches only 104.38, candle 27 closes back inside at 103.85, and candle 28 closes at 103.40, under the lower line: a bust.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"scenario-planning\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.2.10\"]},{\"id\":\"dow-theory\",\"name\":\"Dow Theory\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The founding trend framework: three degrees of trend, and the peak-trough rule for when a trend has changed.\",\"body\":[\"Dow Theory sees three degrees of trend at once: the primary trend over months to years, secondary moves against it over weeks to months, and minor day-to-day moves. A trend is assumed to continue until a clear signal says it has reversed. Dow judged trends on closing prices, and wanted two averages and volume to confirm them.\",\"The peak-trough rule makes 'clearly reversed' testable. An uptrend ends when a low breaks the previous swing low; a lower high alone is only a warning. A downtrend ends when a higher low forms and the next rally clears the previous swing high. The signals come late by construction, and the evidence for Dow as a trading system is mixed (C).\"],\"example\":\"LABX makes swing lows at 99.50, 100.30 and 101.10 and swing highs at 101.10 and 102.20, then a lower high at 102.00. The uptrend still stands. Candle 29 falls to 100.97, below 101.10: only now does the peak-trough rule call it over, 1.23 below the 102.20 top.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"trend-definitions\",\"timeframe\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.1\",\"3.11.3\"]},{\"id\":\"trend-definitions\",\"name\":\"Trend definitions\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Working definitions of trend (structure, average slope, average stack, ADX) that can disagree on the same chart.\",\"body\":[\"A trend can be defined by structure (higher highs and higher lows, ended by the peak-trough rule), by the slope of a long average, by a stack of averages (SMA 20 above SMA 50) or by strength (ADX above 25). Each measures something different, so on the same candle they can disagree.\",\"Choose one definition before looking at a chart and keep it. Switching to whichever definition agrees with the trade you want turns analysis into confirmation bias.\"],\"example\":\"At one LABX candle, SMA 20 (104.72) sits above SMA 50 (103.17), which is still rising, so the stack and the slope say uptrend. But a low at 103.22 has broken the 103.67 swing low and ADX(14) is 23.4, so structure and strength say no. Two against two.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"dow-theory\",\"adx-dmi\",\"confirmation-bias\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.1\",\"2.3.6\"]},{\"id\":\"weighted-moving-averages\",\"name\":\"EMA and WMA\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Moving averages that weight recent closes more: faster than an SMA, still lagging, and noisier.\",\"body\":[\"An EMA updates from its last value: new EMA = old EMA + k × (close − old EMA), with k = 2 ÷ (n + 1). A WMA gives the closes weights 1 to n, the newest weighted most. Wilder's smoothing, used in ATR and ADX, is an EMA with k = 1 ÷ n.\",\"On the same jump in price, a WMA moves furthest, then an EMA, then an SMA, but all of them stay behind price. Reacting faster also means reacting to more noise.\"],\"example\":\"Closes 100.00 to 104.00, then 110.00. The 5-period SMA is 104.00. The EMA: 102.00 + (110.00 − 102.00) ÷ 3 = 104.67. The WMA: (101 × 1 + 102 × 2 + 103 × 3 + 104 × 4 + 110 × 5) ÷ 15 = 1,580 ÷ 15 = 105.33.\",\"related\":[\"moving-average\",\"indicator-literacy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"indicator-literacy\",\"name\":\"Indicator literacy\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Every indicator re-weights past prices; its settings are conventions, and less lag always means more noise.\",\"body\":[\"An indicator is a recipe applied to open, high, low, close and volume. It adds no new information; it re-weights what price already showed. Indicators from the same family tend to agree because they are built from the same data.\",\"Default settings such as 14, 20 or 12/26/9 are conventions. Tuning them until the past looks good is the main road to overfitting, and no setting removes the trade-off between lag and noise.\"],\"example\":\"On one LABX trend chart, EMA 20 turns 6 candles after the top with no false signals; EMA 5 turns after 1 candle but gives 8 false signals. On a range chart, EMA 5 gives 26 false signals and EMA 20 gives 16.\",\"related\":[\"weighted-moving-averages\",\"moving-average\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.3\",\"2.3.5\",\"2.3.8\"]},{\"id\":\"ma-crossover\",\"name\":\"Moving-average crossovers\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Signals from price or a fast average crossing a slower one: late in trends and costly in ranges.\",\"body\":[\"A crossover is a change of sign: of close minus an average (a price cross), of a fast average minus a slow one (a double cross), or of SMA 50 minus SMA 200 (golden and death crosses). In a trend there are few signals and they come late; in a range the averages keep meeting, so signals reverse quickly.\",\"Every signal pays spread and commission. Filters such as a band or confirmation candles cut whipsaws but enter later or miss moves. For crossover rules on a single stock index the evidence is mixed (C), and published results largely disappear after costs.\"],\"example\":\"Over 80 candles of a LABX trend, SMA 10 crossed SMA 30 once. Over 80 candles of a range, it crossed 9 times, two of them whipsaws. At about 0.20R per round trip, those 9 signals cost about 1.80R.\",\"related\":[\"moving-average\",\"trading-costs\",\"expectancy\",\"macd\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.4\",\"2.3.5\"]},{\"id\":\"macd\",\"name\":\"MACD\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The difference between the 12- and 26-period EMAs, with a signal line and histogram: a crossover in disguise.\",\"body\":[\"MACD = EMA 12 − EMA 26. The signal line is a 9-period EMA of MACD, and the histogram is MACD minus the signal. A MACD zero cross is exactly a 12/26 EMA crossover; the histogram shows whether MACD is speeding up or slowing down.\",\"MACD is in price units and unbounded, so it has no fixed overbought or oversold levels; PPO divides it by EMA 26 to compare instruments. As a crossover rule its evidence is mixed (C); as an overbought gauge or through divergences it is unproven (D).\"],\"example\":\"EMA 12 104.20 and EMA 26 103.50 give MACD 0.70. With a previous signal of 0.50, the signal is 0.50 + 0.2 × 0.20 = 0.54 and the histogram 0.16. PPO: 0.70 ÷ 103.50 × 100 = 0.68%.\",\"related\":[\"ma-crossover\",\"weighted-moving-averages\",\"indicator-literacy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.5\"]},{\"id\":\"adx-dmi\",\"name\":\"ADX and DMI\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Wilder's directional indicators: +DI and −DI give direction, ADX measures trend strength without direction.\",\"body\":[\"+DM is the up move (high − previous high) when it is positive and larger than the down move; −DM is the mirror, and an inside candle scores 0 on both. Smoothed over 14 candles and divided by true range, they give +DI and −DI. DX = 100 × |+DI − −DI| ÷ (+DI + −DI), and ADX is DX smoothed again.\",\"ADX says how strong a trend is, not which way it goes. Its double smoothing makes it late: as a regime filter the evidence is mixed (C), and DI crossovers as signals are unproven (D). The 20 and 25 thresholds are conventions.\"],\"example\":\"Sums over three candles: +DM 1.20, −DM 0.30, TR 3.00. +DI = 40, −DI = 10, DX = 100 × 30 ÷ 50 = 60. On a LABX fall from 99.66 to 95.01, ADX passed 25 only when price was already at 95.40.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"trend-definitions\",\"volatility\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.6\"]},{\"id\":\"stop-and-reverse-indicators\",\"name\":\"Parabolic SAR and SuperTrend\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Trailing lines that flip with the trend; useful as stops, prone to whipsaws as always-in systems.\",\"body\":[\"Parabolic SAR moves toward price by a growing share of the gap to the extreme point: SAR + AF × (EP − SAR), with AF from 0.02 up to 0.20. SuperTrend sits a multiple of ATR from the candle midpoint and only ratchets in the trend's direction. Both flip when price crosses them.\",\"As always-in systems they whipsaw in ranges, and each flip pays costs; as stand-alone systems the evidence is unproven (D). As trailing stops on a position they simply do what they say.\"],\"example\":\"SAR 99.00, EP 101.00, AF 0.02: next SAR 99.04. A new high at 101.40 raises AF to 0.04: 99.04 + 0.04 × 2.36 = 99.13. SuperTrend with high 101.00, low 100.00, ATR 0.40 and multiplier 3: lower band 99.30.\",\"related\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"range\",\"volatility\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.7\"]},{\"id\":\"ichimoku-basics\",\"name\":\"Ichimoku basics\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Five lines built mostly from high-low midpoints, two of them shifted in time in ways that invite look-ahead errors.\",\"body\":[\"Tenkan and Kijun are the midpoints of the last 9 and 26 highs and lows: Donchian midpoints. Senkou A averages them and Senkou B is the 52-candle midpoint, both drawn 26 candles ahead to form the cloud. Chikou is the close drawn 26 candles back.\",\"Because the lines share their inputs, their agreement is not independent confirmation. There's no robust evidence for Ichimoku as a system (D); in effect it is a channel and breakout method.\"],\"example\":\"9-candle high 104.00 and low 100.00: Tenkan 102.00. 26-candle high 105.00 and low 98.00: Kijun 101.50. Senkou A = 101.75. 52-candle high 106.00 and low 96.00: Senkou B = 101.00, below A, so the cloud is green.\",\"related\":[\"look-ahead-bias\",\"indicator-literacy\",\"breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.8\"]},{\"id\":\"look-ahead-bias\",\"name\":\"Look-ahead bias\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Using information in a test that was not available at the moment the decision would have been made.\",\"body\":[\"A backtest has look-ahead bias when a rule at candle t reads data from after t: a line shifted back in time, like Ichimoku's Chikou, or an indicator that repaints, like ZigZag lines, centred averages, regression channels and swing pivots.\",\"Such tests show profits no one could have made. The fix is to use only as-of values: what the indicator showed at the moment of the decision.\"],\"example\":\"At candle 30, LABX's Chikou line reads 102.39. That number is the close of candle 56, 26 candles later. A rule that buys at candle 30 because Chikou is high is trading on a price nobody had yet.\",\"related\":[\"ichimoku-basics\",\"hindsight-bias\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.3.8\"]},{\"id\":\"oscillator\",\"name\":\"Oscillators\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"An indicator that swings around a centre line or between fixed bounds, such as 0 and 100.\",\"body\":[\"Most oscillators start from momentum: how far price moved over a window. Bounded ones, such as RSI, the stochastic, Williams %R and MFI, rescale it to a fixed range, so fixed lines like 70 and 30 can be drawn. Unbounded ones, such as ROC, MACD, CCI and OBV, have no ceiling: an extreme on one chart is routine on another.\",\"The scale is a construction, not a property of the market. Thresholds are conventions, and what an extreme means depends on the regime: strength in a trend, a possible fade in a range.\"],\"example\":\"On a 48-hour LABX chart, ROC(12) peaked at +1.03% in a calm stretch and at +6.52% in the trend that followed. No fixed line could call both 'overbought'.\",\"related\":[\"momentum-roc\",\"rsi\",\"trend\",\"range\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.1\",\"2.4.2\",\"2.4.3\"]},{\"id\":\"momentum-roc\",\"name\":\"Momentum and rate of change\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Price change over n periods; the technical idea with the strongest evidence, at 3–12-month horizons.\",\"body\":[\"Momentum = Cₜ − Cₜ₋ₙ in price units; ROC = (Cₜ ÷ Cₜ₋ₙ − 1) × 100 in percent. Every oscillator in unit 2.4 is a rescaled version of this change.\",\"Time-series momentum over 3–12 months is grade A: Moskowitz, Ooi and Pedersen (2012) found it positive in every one of 58 futures contracts, and Hurst, Ooi and Pedersen (2017) traced trend following over 110 years. Cross-sectional momentum in stocks goes back to Jegadeesh and Titman (1993). Costs and sharp momentum crashes are the caveats.\",\"None of that covers ROC crosses on hourly candles, which are grade D. A momentum rule needs a market whose moves persist on the horizon you trade.\"],\"example\":\"In the course's simulated markets, 'long while ROC(12) is above 0' made +0.40% a trade after costs in the trend market, against −0.15% for random entries; on the random walk it lost 0.20%, about the cost of a round trip.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"oscillator\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.1\"]},{\"id\":\"rsi\",\"name\":\"Relative Strength Index\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A 0–100 ratio of average gains to average losses, smoothed with Wilder's method.\",\"body\":[\"RSI = 100 − 100 ÷ (1 + RS), with RS = average gain ÷ average loss over n periods, 14 by default. The first averages are simple; after that each one is (previous × (n − 1) + current) ÷ n. With no losses, RSI is 100.\",\"The formula is mechanics; the 70/30 lines are conventions. Brown observed RSI ranges shifting with the trend, about 40–90 in bull markets and 10–60 in bear markets. Using 70/30 crosses as reversal signals is grade D. RSI is not 'relative strength' against another instrument.\"],\"example\":\"Five changes of +1.00, −0.50, +0.80, +0.40 and −0.30: average gain 0.44, average loss 0.16, RS 2.75, RSI(5) 73.33. A next change of +0.20 smooths the averages to 0.392 and 0.128: RSI 75.38.\",\"related\":[\"oscillator\",\"overbought-oversold\",\"divergence\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.2\",\"2.4.4\",\"2.4.5\"]},{\"id\":\"stochastic-family\",\"name\":\"Stochastic, Williams %R and CCI\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Oscillators that place the close within its recent range or against its average; largely the same information.\",\"body\":[\"Stochastic %K = (C − LL) ÷ (HH − LL) × 100 over n candles, and %D averages it over 3. Williams %R = %K − 100, exactly. CCI = (typical price − its average) ÷ (0.015 × mean deviation): unbounded, with ±100 as reference lines.\",\"Their signals are grade D: no robust evidence that crosses of 80/20, −20/−80 or ±100 predict turns, and early signals whipsaw. They behave best in ranges.\"],\"example\":\"Highest high 104.00, lowest low 100.00, close 103.00: %K 75, %R −25. Typical prices 100, 101, 102, 101 and 103: average 101.40, mean deviation 0.88, CCI(5) 121.21.\",\"related\":[\"oscillator\",\"indicator-redundancy\",\"rsi\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.3\"]},{\"id\":\"indicator-redundancy\",\"name\":\"Indicator redundancy\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Indicators built from the same inputs move together, so their agreement is not independent confirmation.\",\"body\":[\"RSI, the stochastic, Williams %R, CCI and MFI all rescale recent highs, lows and closes. When one reads overbought, the others usually do too: one opinion counted several times, which feeds confirmation bias.\",\"Bollinger's advice is to combine indicators that measure different things: momentum with volume, or with volatility. Check a pair's correlation on your own data before treating agreement as evidence.\"],\"example\":\"On a 60-hour LABX chart, RSI and %K correlated at 0.88, %K and %R at 1.00, RSI and CCI at 0.83, but RSI and ATR at −0.13.\",\"related\":[\"confirmation-bias\",\"stochastic-family\",\"oscillator\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.3\",\"2.8.9\"]},{\"id\":\"overbought-oversold\",\"name\":\"Overbought and oversold\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Readings that price has moved far relative to its recent range, not that it must reverse.\",\"body\":[\"Overbought means recent gains outweighed recent losses; it describes the move. Three things stop it working as a sell signal: the regime (in a trend, extremes are normal), the horizon (over 3–12 months, strong past returns tended to continue) and the thresholds (70/30 and 80/20 are conventions).\",\"Decide with the regime first. In a trend, read an extreme as strength; in a range, a fade with a stop is possible; when the regime is unclear, the reading carries no edge.\"],\"example\":\"In the course's simulated markets, shorting when RSI(14) fell back below 70 won 53.5% of 241 trades in the range market (+0.25R a trade) and 24.7% of 1,037 in the trend market (−0.42R).\",\"related\":[\"rsi\",\"trend\",\"range\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.4\"]},{\"id\":\"divergence\",\"name\":\"Divergence\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Price and an oscillator disagree at matching swings; regular divergence suggests reversal, hidden suggests continuation; weak evidence.\",\"body\":[\"Compare the oscillator at the same candles as the price swings. Regular bearish: a higher price high with a lower oscillator high. Regular bullish: a lower low with a higher oscillator low. Hidden divergences claim continuation: a higher price low with a lower oscillator low in an uptrend, and the mirror in a downtrend.\",\"All of it is grade D. Strong trends often print several divergences before the top, so act only after confirmation, a break of price structure or RSI crossing 50, and accept that confirmation costs part of the move.\"],\"example\":\"LABX made highs of 104.95, 106.02 and 106.33 while RSI read 83.86, 77.61 and 70.71: two divergences. Confirmation came at hour 49, a close of 104.84 below the 105.03 pullback low, with RSI at 47.93.\",\"related\":[\"rsi\",\"swing-points\",\"structure-break\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.5\"]},{\"id\":\"historical-volatility\",\"name\":\"Historical volatility\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The standard deviation of past returns, often annualised, used to compare how much prices move.\",\"body\":[\"Take log returns, ln(Cₜ ÷ Cₜ₋₁), compute their standard deviation, and scale by the square root of the number of periods in a year: √252 for daily data. A daily σ of 2% is about 31.7% a year.\",\"Like ATR, it measures size, not direction. ATR works in price units and counts gaps; HV works in percent, so it compares instruments directly.\"],\"example\":\"A daily σ of 1.20% × √252 (15.87) ≈ 19.05% a year.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"volatility-clustering\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.6\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-clustering\",\"name\":\"Volatility clustering\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Busy periods tend to follow busy ones and quiet ones follow quiet ones, so recent volatility forecasts near-term size.\",\"body\":[\"Large moves bunch together in time, and so do small ones. It is one of the most robust facts about prices, grade A, and it's why a recent ATR is a reasonable forecast of near-term candle size, for stops and for sizing.\",\"Clustering is descriptive: it forecasts how much, never which way. Unusually low volatility also tends to revert upward over time, which is the one solid message of a squeeze.\"],\"example\":\"In 8,000 hours of the course's simulated clustering market, after the busiest fifth of 10-hour stretches the next stretch was busier than the median 150 times in 159; after the quietest fifth, 11 times in 160.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"historical-volatility\",\"volatility-squeeze\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.6\",\"2.4.8\"]},{\"id\":\"bollinger-bands\",\"name\":\"Bollinger Bands\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A 20-period average with bands two standard deviations away; touching a band is not a signal.\",\"body\":[\"Middle = SMA(20); bands = middle ± 2σ of the last 20 closes. %b locates price between them, 0 at the lower band and 1 at the upper; BandWidth = (upper − lower) ÷ middle measures their width. 20/2 is a starting point: Bollinger suggests about 2.1 at 50 periods and 1.9 at 10.\",\"Bollinger's own rules: tags are not signals; in trends price walks the band; closes outside the bands are at first continuation; combine the bands with indicators that measure something else. About 88–90% of closes stay inside, not 95%.\"],\"example\":\"SMA(20) 100.00 and σ 0.50 give bands at 99.00 and 101.00. At 100.80, %b = 0.90; BandWidth = 2.00 ÷ 100.00 = 0.02.\",\"related\":[\"moving-average\",\"volatility\",\"volatility-squeeze\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.8\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-squeeze\",\"name\":\"Volatility squeeze\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Unusually narrow bands that tend to precede a larger move of unknown direction.\",\"body\":[\"Bollinger's squeeze is BandWidth at a multi-month low; this course uses the lowest in 120 hours. The TTM version has both Bollinger Bands inside the Keltner Channel.\",\"Low volatility tends to be followed by higher volatility (grade A, descriptive), so a squeeze says: expect a bigger move and size for it. Direction after a squeeze is grade D.\"],\"example\":\"In 8,000 hours of the course's simulated clustering market, 16 of the first 20 squeezes were followed by a wider 10-hour range than the 10 hours before (50.1% at random hours); 10 went up and 10 down.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-clustering\",\"bollinger-bands\",\"volatility-channels\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.8\",\"2.4.9\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-channels\",\"name\":\"Keltner and Donchian channels\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Channels built from ATR around an average (Keltner) or from the highs and lows of recent candles (Donchian).\",\"body\":[\"Keltner, in Raschke's version: EMA(20) ± 2 × ATR(10). Donchian(n): the highest high and lowest low of the previous n candles, with their midpoint. Keltner signals are grade D; channel breakouts are grade C, mixed, and overlap with time-series momentum on diversified futures.\",\"Build a Donchian channel from previous candles only. A channel that includes the current candle lets a backtest know which candle breaks out before it trades: look-ahead bias.\"],\"example\":\"Donchian 20 breakouts in the course's simulated markets, after costs: +0.55R a trade in the trend market and −0.11R on the random walk. With the current candle in the channel, the same tests showed +0.81R and +0.16R.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"breakout\",\"volatility-squeeze\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.4.9\"]},{\"id\":\"volume-analysis\",\"name\":\"Volume analysis\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Reading traded volume as participation and possible confirmation, never as direction on its own.\",\"body\":[\"Volume shows how many units changed hands, so it measures participation: how much interest a move drew. Every unit bought was also sold, so a volume bar never says which side won. Compare each bar with its recent average rather than with the tallest bar on the chart.\",\"The popular claim that volume confirms breakouts has mixed evidence (grade C): in Bulkowski's US stock data, heavy breakout volume improved average results for some patterns only. Test it on the same breakouts with and without a volume filter, and remember that a simulator whose generator links volume to continuation will confirm the filter by design.\"],\"example\":\"LABX breaks out of a 99.65–100.48 range in hour 19 on 2,650 units, RVOL 2.43. Hour 23 trades RVOL 2.10 on news and closes just 0.12 above its open: heavy participation, no direction.\",\"related\":[\"volume\",\"relative-volume\",\"breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"relative-volume\",\"name\":\"Relative volume\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A candle's volume divided by its recent average, flagging unusual activity.\",\"body\":[\"RVOL = this candle's volume ÷ the average volume of the 20 candles before it. 1.0 is normal; 2.0 is twice normal. Because it's a ratio, it can be compared across quiet and busy periods and across instruments, where raw volume can't.\",\"The tallest bar isn't always the most unusual one: after a busy stretch the average rises, so the same volume means less. Intraday, RVOL is better compared with the same hour on earlier days, because volume has a daily rhythm.\"],\"example\":\"Hour 27 trades 2,900 units, the tallest bar of the day, against an average of 1,409: RVOL 2.06. Hour 19 traded less, 2,650, but against 1,092: RVOL 2.43, the most unusual hour.\",\"related\":[\"volume\",\"volume-analysis\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"cumulative-volume-indicators\",\"name\":\"OBV and accumulation/distribution\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Running totals of volume signed by price movement; their direction is read, never their level.\",\"body\":[\"On-balance volume adds a candle's whole volume when it closes above the previous close and subtracts it when it closes below. The accumulation/distribution line instead weights volume by where the candle closed in its own range, from −1 at the low to +1 at the high, so it never sees gaps. Chaikin Money Flow averages that idea over 20 candles; the Money Flow Index applies RSI's recipe to typical price × volume.\",\"Their level depends on the day counting began, and one huge candle can shift it for a long time. There's no robust evidence that their signals predict prices (grade D), and because they share inputs they tend to agree, which isn't independent confirmation.\"],\"example\":\"Closes 100.00, 100.40, 100.20, 100.80, 100.70, 101.30 on volumes 1,200, 800, 1,500, 600 and 2,000 give OBV 1,200 / 400 / 1,900 / 1,300 / 3,300. A day that gaps from 105.00 to 99.00 and closes at 99.80 in a 98.00–100.00 range scores +0.80 on A/D: 'accumulation' on a 4.95% loss.\",\"related\":[\"volume\",\"volume-analysis\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.2\"]},{\"id\":\"vwap\",\"name\":\"VWAP\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The volume-weighted average price since the session open, used above all as an execution benchmark.\",\"body\":[\"VWAP = Σ(typical price × volume) ÷ Σ volume, counted from the session open, with typical price = (H + L + C) ÷ 3. Early in the session it jumps around, because a few candles carry all the weight; it resets at every open because it describes one session's trading.\",\"Its real use is grading execution: a buyer whose average fill is below the session VWAP paid less than the session's average price. As support or resistance it's unproven (grade D); the only plausible mechanism is coordination by algorithms that trade against it.\"],\"example\":\"Typical prices 100.00, 100.50 and 101.00 on 2,000, 1,000 and 500 units: 351,000.00 ÷ 3,500 = 100.29. Buying 3,000 units at an average of 100.26 against a session VWAP of 100.35 beats the benchmark by 0.09 a unit, before 150.40 of commission.\",\"related\":[\"anchored-vwap\",\"trading-costs\",\"volume\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.3\"]},{\"id\":\"anchored-vwap\",\"name\":\"Anchored VWAP\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A VWAP started from a chosen event, showing the average price paid since then.\",\"body\":[\"Anchored VWAP uses the same formula as VWAP, but its sums start at a candle you choose: a swing low, a gap, an earnings release. It reads as the average price of everyone who traded since that moment, which describes their positions in profit or loss.\",\"It's a description, not a forecast. Claims that price respects an anchored VWAP as support or resistance are unproven (grade D).\"],\"example\":\"LABX falls for 12 hours to a low of 100.30, then rises. A VWAP anchored at that low reads 101.27 twelve hours later, with price at 101.90: buyers since the low are, on average, 0.63 ahead.\",\"related\":[\"vwap\",\"swing-points\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.3\"]},{\"id\":\"volume-profile\",\"name\":\"Volume profile\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The volume traded at each price over a window, with its point of control and value area.\",\"body\":[\"A volume profile is a sideways histogram: one bar per price level, its length the volume traded there. The point of control (POC) is the busiest price. The value area starts at the POC and adds the larger neighbouring level, step by step, until it holds 70% of the volume. Peaks are high-volume nodes, dips low-volume nodes.\",\"Claims that high-volume nodes act as support, that price crosses low-volume nodes quickly or that an untouched POC pulls price back are untested (grade D). A profile that includes candles after a turn can't explain that turn: that's look-ahead. Tools working only from candles often approximate by putting each candle's volume at its close, which can move the POC.\"],\"example\":\"Levels 101.00 to 99.50 hold 200, 400, 600, 900, 1,200, 500 and 200 units (4,000 in all). POC 100.00. Adding 100.25, 100.50 and 99.75 reaches 3,200 ≥ 2,800, so the value area is 99.75 to 100.50.\",\"related\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"volume\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.4\"]},{\"id\":\"pivot-points\",\"name\":\"Pivot points\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Levels calculated from the previous session's high, low and close, in several competing formulas.\",\"body\":[\"Classic pivots: P = (H + L + C) ÷ 3, R1 = 2P − L, S1 = 2P − H, R2 = P + (H − L), S2 = P − (H − L), with R3 and S3 further out. Fibonacci pivots space levels at 0.382, 0.618 and 1.0 times the range from P; Camarilla builds eight lines around the close; Woodie weights the close twice; DeMark picks its formula by comparing the close with the open.\",\"There's no robust evidence that pivots predict turns (grade D); any effect would come from traders coordinating on the same lines. Plot several systems at once and price is almost always near one of them, so 'hits' are guaranteed.\"],\"example\":\"Yesterday: H 102.00, L 98.00, C 101.00. P = 100.33, R1 = 102.67, S1 = 98.67, R2 = 104.33, S2 = 96.33. With classic, Fibonacci, Camarilla and Woodie lines together (22), at least one sat within 0.24 of 22 of today's 24 closes; classic alone, 6.\",\"related\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"round-numbers\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"gap-types\",\"name\":\"Gap types\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Common, breakaway, runaway and exhaustion gaps and island reversals, classified by where they occur in a move.\",\"body\":[\"Common gaps sit inside ranges on ordinary volume and fill quickly. Breakaway gaps leave a range on heavy volume and often never fill. Runaway gaps appear mid-trend with no congestion before them. Exhaustion gaps come late, on very heavy volume, and fill soon; you can be sure of one only after the fill. A gap fills when price returns to the last pre-gap extreme.\",\"An island reversal is a group of candles cut off by a gap on each side; the candlestick abandoned baby is its one-candle version. In Bulkowski's tests islands ranked among the weakest patterns, and the claim that a runaway gap marks the halfway point is untested.\"],\"example\":\"LABX ranges near 100.00, gaps out at hour 13 on 2,600 units (breakaway, never filled), gaps again mid-trend at hour 23 on 2,400 (runaway), and late at hour 33 on 3,600, filled two hours later (exhaustion).\",\"related\":[\"price-gaps\",\"gap-risk\",\"breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.6\"]},{\"id\":\"supply-demand-zones\",\"name\":\"Supply and demand zones\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Zones drawn at the base of a strong departure: a popular method without robust evidence.\",\"body\":[\"A zone is named by the move away from its base: a rally away makes a demand zone, a drop away a supply zone. The app's convention: a departure of at least 2 trend candles covering 3 × ATR and breaking a swing, from a base of 1–4 overlapping candles each no wider than 0.6 × ATR; the proximal line on the base's bodies, the distal line on its extreme wick.\",\"The method is grade D: no robust tests, and its story of unfilled institutional orders can't be checked. Its 'fresh zones work best' rule contradicts the S/R rule that more touches make a level stronger. Zones are drawn after the move, so judge them against random zones of the same width, at the first touch.\"],\"example\":\"A drop to 100.40, a base of three candles (bodies up to 100.40, lowest wick 100.05), then a rally from 100.22 to 102.62. Demand zone: proximal 100.40, distal 100.05.\",\"related\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"base-rates\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.7\"]},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-levels\",\"name\":\"Fibonacci levels\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Retracement and extension levels at fixed ratios of a swing, with no evidence that the ratios are special.\",\"body\":[\"Retracements sit at 23.6%, 38.2%, 50%, 61.8% and 78.6% of a swing, measured back from its end; extensions such as 127.2% and 161.8% lie beyond it. 0.618 and 0.382 come from ratios of Fibonacci numbers; 50% comes from Dow Theory, not Fibonacci.\",\"The evidence is grade D: tests found Fibonacci ratios in market swings no more often than chance. Most pullbacks end between a third and two thirds of the move anyway, so the 38.2–61.8% zone catches them by default. Any reaction at a level would come from coordination, not from the mathematics.\"],\"example\":\"Swing 100.00 to 110.00: 23.6% at 107.64, 38.2% at 106.18, 50% at 105.00, 61.8% at 103.82, 78.6% at 102.14. Extensions: 112.72 (127.2%) and 116.18 (161.8%). With 0.38 of tolerance, the three middle levels cover 68% of the 103.33–106.67 stretch.\",\"related\":[\"pullback\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"confluence\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.8\"]},{\"id\":\"confluence\",\"name\":\"Confluence\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Several independent reasons pointing to one price or direction; easy to fake with correlated or numerous lines.\",\"body\":[\"Confluence adds information only if each factor predicts on its own and the factors don't share inputs. RSI and Stochastic, or SMA 20 and EMA 20, are one opinion counted twice; a price level and the volume traded there at least come from different inputs.\",\"Draw enough lines and some always cluster: with round numbers every 0.50, Fibonacci levels, pivots and moving averages, almost any price has two or three 'reasons' nearby. Count the lines near a random price before trusting a cluster near yours.\"],\"example\":\"At 106.40, three lines lie within 0.38 (half an ATR): Fibonacci 38.2% at 106.18, the round number 106.50 and the 20-hour SMA at 106.71. Across that day's range, a price averaged 2.4 such lines and none had zero.\",\"related\":[\"confirmation-bias\",\"fibonacci-levels\",\"pivot-points\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.5.8\"]},{\"id\":\"bar-reading\",\"name\":\"Bar reading\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Classifying bars as trend bars or doji bars, and reading runs of trend bars and the overlap between bars as signs of a trend or a range.\",\"body\":[\"Each bar gets a label from two measurements: the body as a share of the range, and where the close sits. The app calls a bar a trend bar when the body is at least half the range and the close is in the third nearest its extreme, and a doji bar when the body is a quarter of the range or less. Overlap is the share of a bar's range the bar before also covered.\",\"Average overlap under 30% over 10 bars suggests a trend, over 50% a range; three or more trend bars in a row suggest urgency. The measurements are mechanical, but reading them as urgency or balance is Al Brooks' heuristic, without published statistics, and the same bar means different things in a trend and in a range.\"],\"example\":\"Open 100.00, high 101.05, low 99.95, close 101.00: body 1.00 of a 1.10 range (91%), close in the top third, so a bull trend bar. The next bar, 101.00 / 101.25 / 100.45 / 100.90, has a body of 0.10 of 0.80 (12.5%): a doji bar. The two share 0.60, 75% of the smaller range.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"candlestick\",\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\"],\"missions\":[\"M01\",\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.1\",\"2.6.7\"]},{\"id\":\"signal-and-entry-bars\",\"name\":\"Signal and entry bars\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The signal bar sets a stop-entry price one tick beyond it; the entry bar is the bar in which that order fills. A checklist grades the signal bar.\",\"body\":[\"For a long, a buy stop goes one tick above the signal bar's high and the protective stop one tick below its low; the distance between them is the risk. If price never trades through the buy stop, there is no trade. The entry bar's close is the first information about the trade.\",\"The app grades a bull reversal signal bar on five tests: a bull body closing in the top third, a lower wick of at least 25% of the range, an upper wick of at most 15%, a range between 0.5 and 2 × ATR, and a location such as a prior low. That higher scores win more often is a heuristic without published statistics.\"],\"example\":\"Signal bar high 100.80, low 100.20: buy stop 100.81, protective stop 100.19, risk 0.62 a unit. The next bar trades to 101.10 and fills the order: it's the entry bar.\",\"related\":[\"stop-entry-order\",\"invalidation-level\",\"traders-equation\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.2\",\"2.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"traders-equation\",\"name\":\"Trader's equation\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Win probability times reward must exceed loss probability times risk, after costs.\",\"body\":[\"Before taking a setup, multiply the chance of winning by the reward and subtract the chance of losing times the risk, then the costs. Above zero, the setup has an edge on paper; below zero, it loses money however good the chart looks. It is the arithmetic of expectancy, applied to one setup.\",\"The equation is mechanical; the probability you put in is an estimate. Al Brooks pairs setups he rates near 60% with at least 1:1, and most reversals, near 40%, with at least 1:2, but those percentages are his subjective estimates.\"],\"example\":\"P(win) 0.40, reward 2R, risk 1R: 0.40 × 2 − 0.60 × 1 = +0.20R; after costs of 0.05R, +0.15R a trade. At 1:1 the same setup gives 0.40 − 0.60 = −0.20R.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.2\"]},{\"id\":\"inside-outside-bars\",\"name\":\"Inside, outside and narrow-range bars\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Bars contained in, or extending beyond, the previous bar's range, and the narrowest bar of recent bars.\",\"body\":[\"An inside bar's high and low stay within the previous bar's; two in a row are ii, three iii. An outside bar goes beyond both ends of the previous bar. An NR7 bar has the narrowest range of the last seven. A hikkake is an inside bar, a false break of one side, then a close beyond the other side.\",\"A common way to trade a quiet bar is an OCO pair of stop entries one tick beyond each side, each leg stopped at the other entry, sized from the larger distance. The definitions are mechanical; the setups are heuristics without published statistics. What has support is size: quiet stretches tend to be followed by wider ranges.\"],\"example\":\"ii ending with a bar from 100.10 to 100.70: buy stop 100.71, sell stop 100.09, 0.62 of risk either way. With a 100.00 budget and 0.05% commission on each fill, 138 units lose 99.42 at the stop; 139 would lose 100.14.\",\"related\":[\"oco-order\",\"volatility\",\"false-breakout\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.3\"]},{\"id\":\"two-legged-pullback\",\"name\":\"Two-legged pullback\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A pullback that runs in two legs, often ending with a second attempt to resume the trend.\",\"body\":[\"Leg A moves against the trend, a small bounce follows, and leg C makes a new pullback extreme. In a bull trend, the bounce is the first attempt to resume (H1); the bar that trades above the signal bar after leg C is the second (H2).\",\"In Al Brooks' reading, the first attempt often fails, so the second entry is preferred: traders who faded the first attempt are trapped when the second one breaks out. It is a heuristic without published statistics, and it needs a trend to start with.\"],\"example\":\"From a 105.00 high, leg A falls to 103.90; H1 reaches 104.50; leg C falls to 103.70; the next bar trades above the signal bar's 104.00 high. Buy stop 104.01, stop 103.69: 0.32 of risk.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"bar-counting\",\"signal-and-entry-bars\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"bar-counting\",\"name\":\"Bar counting (H1/H2)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Counting attempts to resume a trend during a pullback: H1 and H2 in bull trends, L1 and L2 in bear trends.\",\"body\":[\"In a bull pullback, H1 is the first bar whose high goes above the previous bar's high; after a lower low, the next such bar is H2. Bear trends mirror it with lows. Bars are counted, not swings, and a new trend extreme resets the count.\",\"A count is only a bar beating the previous high, so ranges and random data produce counts all the time. The count means something only after a trend check: higher highs and higher lows first.\"],\"example\":\"Pullback highs 105.00, 104.60, 104.30, 104.50, 104.20, 104.00, 104.35: H1 is the fourth bar (104.50 > 104.30), H2 the seventh (104.35 > 104.00).\",\"related\":[\"two-legged-pullback\",\"trend\",\"range\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"price-channels\",\"name\":\"Tight, broad and micro channels\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Bar-by-bar channel types that grade a trend's strength by pullback size and by the bars' lows or highs.\",\"body\":[\"A tight channel has pullbacks of at most 1 ATR and 3 bars; a broad channel's pullbacks give back more than half the leg before, close to a range; a micro channel has five or more bars without a lower low in a bull trend, or without a higher high in a bear trend.\",\"Channel lines drawn after the fact always fit, because you choose the points they pass through. Only touches after the line was drawn are evidence. The channel types are Al Brooks' heuristics without published statistics.\"],\"example\":\"A line through swing lows 100.20 and 100.70 drawn after bar 11: one later touch, a low of 101.22 against the line at 101.20, then a close under the line on bar 18. Pullbacks of 57% and 55% of the legs before make it a broad channel.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"three-push-wedge\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.5\"]},{\"id\":\"three-push-wedge\",\"name\":\"Three-push wedge\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Three pushes in one direction with fading momentum, read as a possible reversal or, against the trend, as a wedge flag.\",\"body\":[\"Al Brooks' wedge is a count of pushes, each reaching a little further with a shorter leg; the lines don't need to converge. Three pushes up at the end of a rally suggest a possible reversal; three pushes down inside a bull trend make a wedge bull flag, whose buy setup is H3.\",\"It differs from the classic converging-lines wedge, for which Bulkowski published statistics. Brooks' version has none.\"],\"example\":\"Pushes to 101.00, 101.20 and 101.25, from lows of 99.80, 100.40 and 100.75: legs of 1.20, 0.80 and 0.50. Each push gained less.\",\"related\":[\"price-channels\",\"bar-counting\",\"trend\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.5\"]},{\"id\":\"trapped-traders\",\"name\":\"Trapped traders\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Traders caught by a failed move, whose exits can fuel the move against them.\",\"body\":[\"When a breakout fails, the traders who bought it hold losing longs with sell stops back inside the range. As price returns, those stops fill together and add to the drop; the mirror holds for failed breakdowns. The same event is called a 2B, turtle soup, swing failure or liquidity sweep.\",\"The mechanism has support: stop orders cluster beyond round numbers, and price tends to accelerate when it reaches them (Osler). But that cuts both ways: a break through clustered stops can continue as easily as it can fail. Al Brooks' estimate that about 80% of range breakouts fail is a heuristic, and a single unreviewed study of currency pairs found that swept levels predicted continuation, not reversal.\"],\"example\":\"A range from 100.05 to 102.00; bar 15 closes at 102.40 with a low of 101.90; bar 16 closes at 101.80. Breakout longs stopped under 101.90 are out; a fade short from 101.75 has its stop above the failed high, 102.55.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"breakout\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\",\"name\":\"Momentum versus exhaustion\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Reading whether a move is strengthening or tiring from bar size, overlap, pullbacks and climax bars, and stating the result as a probability.\",\"body\":[\"The app's checklist adds a point for each sign of strength (runs of trend bars, overlap under 30%, pullbacks within 1 ATR, closes near the extremes, gap bars) and takes one for each sign of exhaustion (a climax bar of at least 2.5 ATR after 20 or more trend bars, more than 3 ATR from the EMA 20, rising overlap, tails against the move, no follow-through). The score runs from −5 to +5.\",\"The score describes; it isn't a probability. The same big bar can start or end a move, so state a probability and track how well calibrated you are with the Brier score. The checklist items are heuristics; the Brier score is a mechanical scoring rule.\"],\"example\":\"A bar 1.25 tall against an ATR of 0.40, after a 24-bar rally, closing 3.09 above the EMA 20: a climax flag. Forecasts of 0.70 (didn't happen), 0.60 and 0.90 (happened) score (0.49 + 0.16 + 0.01) ÷ 3 = 0.22.\",\"related\":[\"bar-reading\",\"volatility\",\"trend\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.7\"]},{\"id\":\"timeframe-alignment\",\"name\":\"Timeframe alignment\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Using a higher, a trading and a lower timeframe under a hierarchy written before the trade.\",\"body\":[\"Pick three timeframes about 4–6 times apart, such as 4H, 1H and 15m. The higher one gives the direction, the trading one the setup and the stop, the lower one only the timing of the entry; a lower timeframe can't overrule a higher one.\",\"No robust studies show directly that alignment improves results; it's plausible as a trend filter. Each extra timeframe adds choices, and with them the risk of timeframe shopping: switching charts until one agrees with what you wanted to do.\"],\"example\":\"Rule: trade only in the 4H direction, stop on 1H structure, entry timed on 15m. The 4H rises and the 1H shows a sell setup: no trade. A 1H pullback long gets its stop under the last 1H swing low, 101.40.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.6.8\"]},{\"id\":\"entry-techniques\",\"name\":\"Entry techniques\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Anticipation, confirmation and retest entries and their order types, compared in R and expectancy.\",\"body\":[\"The same setup can be entered three ways. Anticipation buys before confirmation, usually with a limit: the best price and the tightest stop, and the lowest hit rate. Confirmation buys after the signal completes, with a stop or market order: a higher hit rate at a worse price. A retest waits with a limit at the broken level: a good price if price comes back, no trade if it doesn't.\",\"Reward-to-risk favours the early entry; hit rate favours the late one. Neither decides alone. Compare entries by expectancy after costs, over many trades, and count the trades a limit never filled.\"],\"example\":\"A double bottom with lows 96.00 and 96.20, peak 99.00 and target 100.95. Anticipation at 96.40, stop 95.80: 7.58R. Confirmation at 99.35, stop 98.20: 1.39R. Retest at 99.10, stop 98.20: 2.06R. All before costs.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order\",\"market-order\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.1\"]},{\"id\":\"pyramiding\",\"name\":\"Pyramiding\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Adding to a winning position while moving stops so total open risk stays within the original R.\",\"body\":[\"Every add raises the loss at the stop unless the stop moves. A pyramid adds only to a winner and lifts the stops, so the worst case if every stop is hit, Σ units × (stop − entry), stays within the original 1R.\",\"Averaging down is the opposite: adding to a loser raises the loss at the same stop beyond the plan. A split entry is different again: the full size and its risk are planned before the first part is bought.\"],\"example\":\"50 at 50.00, stop 48.00: 1R = 100.00. At 54.00, add 25 and lift both stops to 51.00: lot 1 +50.00, lot 2 −75.00, worst case −25.00, a quarter of 1R before costs.\",\"related\":[\"risk-per-trade\",\"r-multiple\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.2\"]},{\"id\":\"profit-targets\",\"name\":\"Profit targets\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Exit levels set by R-multiple, structure or volatility, whose best choice depends on the regime.\",\"body\":[\"A target can be a fixed multiple of R, the next structure level with a small buffer, or a multiple of ATR from the entry. Whichever it is, it's set before entry.\",\"Which pays best depends on how far trades travel. With P the share whose MFE reached k R, and the rest closing at −1R, expectancy is about P × k − (1 − P). Trends favour further targets, ranges closer ones: measure it, don't assume it.\"],\"example\":\"Trending market: MFE reached 3R in 33% of trades, 0.33 × 3 − 0.67 = +0.32R. Range: 3R was reached in 22%, −0.12R, while 1R and 2R both gave +0.20R. Illustrative, before costs.\",\"related\":[\"reward-to-risk\",\"expectancy\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.3\"]},{\"id\":\"chandelier-exit\",\"name\":\"Chandelier exit\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"A trailing stop at the highest high since entry minus a multiple of ATR.\",\"body\":[\"The chandelier hangs k × ATR under the highest high since entry, often with k = 3. New highs lift it, pullbacks leave it where it is, and it never moves down, even when ATR grows.\",\"It's a risk tool, not a predictor: no setting has robust evidence behind it. A wide k holds trends longer and gives back more when it's hit; a tight k exits sooner, often on noise.\"],\"example\":\"Highest high 54.00, ATR 0.80, k = 3: stop 54.00 − 2.40 = 51.60. Hit without a gap, it gives back exactly 2.40 per unit from the high.\",\"related\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"volatility\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.4\"]},{\"id\":\"breakeven-stop\",\"name\":\"Breakeven stop\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"Moving the stop to the entry price after a set gain; it can lower expectancy.\",\"body\":[\"After a gain such as +1R, the stop moves to the entry, so a reversal ends as a scratch instead of a loss. The rule saves losers that had passed +1R and kills winners that dip back to the entry before going on.\",\"Its net effect depends on your trades' paths, so test it with and without on your own record. At the exact entry, the spread and commissions still cost you, and a gap can still fill the stop well below it.\"],\"example\":\"Ten trades, target +2R: 4 winners and 6 losers, +0.20R a trade. With breakeven at +1R, 2 winners are scratched and 1 loser saved: 4 − 5 = −1R, −0.10R a trade.\",\"related\":[\"trailing-stop\",\"expectancy\",\"loss-aversion\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.5\"]},{\"id\":\"scaling-out\",\"name\":\"Scaling out\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Closing a position in parts, which raises the win rate and lowers variance, with an uncertain effect on expectancy.\",\"body\":[\"A trade closed in parts has a blended R: each part's R times its share of the position. Half at +1R and half at breakeven is +0.5R.\",\"Partials turn more trades positive and narrow the spread of results, but they also cap the largest winners. For a system that lives on rare big trades, that can cost more than it smooths.\"],\"example\":\"Runner to +3R in 20% of trades, runner at breakeven 30%, stopped 50%: all-in +0.10R a trade with a standard deviation of 1.51R; half at +1R +0.05R with 1.17R. Illustrative, before costs.\",\"related\":[\"r-multiple\",\"expectancy\",\"variance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.6\"]},{\"id\":\"time-stop\",\"name\":\"Time stop\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"Exiting a trade that has not made set progress within a set number of bars.\",\"body\":[\"A time stop is written before entry: 'If MFE is under 0.5R after N bars, exit at market.' It closes trades that aren't behaving like the setup's winners, for a small loss instead of a full one, and frees capital and attention.\",\"It also cuts some slow winners. N should match the setup's pace and be re-tested on new trades: a line optimised on a few dozen trades is overfitting.\"],\"example\":\"Six trades under 0.5R after 10 bars. Held: one +2R, five −1R, −3.0R. Time stop at about −0.2R each: −1.2R, without the winner.\",\"related\":[\"exit-rules\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.7\"]},{\"id\":\"exit-rules\",\"name\":\"Planned exits\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Exits decided by written rules and tagged with a reason, as opposed to exits driven by fear.\",\"body\":[\"Besides the stop and the target, a plan lists the reasons that justify an early exit: structure broke, an opposite signal, a time stop, an event ahead. Each exit gets its reason as a tag in the journal.\",\"An exit with no tag is a deviation, even when it works out. Over time the tags show whether rules or fear drive your exits. Stops follow the same discipline: they move only toward less risk, never wider.\"],\"example\":\"Three red candles make you nervous and you sell, though nothing in the plan happened: 'no planned reason'. A close under the higher low your setup needed: 'structure broke'.\",\"related\":[\"time-stop\",\"trading-plan\",\"loss-aversion\",\"journaling\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.7\"]},{\"id\":\"mae-mfe\",\"name\":\"MAE and MFE\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The worst and the best unrealised result during a trade, in R, used to review stops and targets.\",\"body\":[\"For a long, MAE = (entry − lowest low) ÷ (entry − stop) and MFE = (highest high − entry) ÷ (entry − stop). MAE shows how much room winners needed; MFE, how far trades travelled.\",\"Plotting MAE against the final result tests a tighter stop: count the winners it would have cut and recompute expectancy. It only means something with enough trades from one setup, and the result has to be re-tested on new trades.\"],\"example\":\"Entry 50.00, stop 49.00, low 49.40, high 52.50: MAE 0.60R, MFE 2.50R. On 20 trades, a stop at 0.70R instead of 1.00R cut one winner and raised expectancy from +0.20R to +0.245R, in the original R.\",\"related\":[\"exit-efficiency\",\"journaling\",\"r-multiple\",\"sample-size\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.8\"]},{\"id\":\"exit-efficiency\",\"name\":\"Exit efficiency\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The share of a trade's best unrealised gain that was actually kept at the exit.\",\"body\":[\"Exit efficiency = realised result ÷ MFE; giveback = MFE − realised result. Averaged by setup, they show which exits leave the most on the table.\",\"100% is impossible without hindsight, since no rule sells the exact top. Compare setups and exit rules with each other, over enough trades, not with perfection.\"],\"example\":\"MFE 2.50R, exit at +1.00R: efficiency 40%, giveback 1.50R.\",\"related\":[\"mae-mfe\",\"trailing-stop\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.7.8\"]},{\"id\":\"market-regime\",\"name\":\"Market regime\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The prevailing behaviour of price, such as trending, ranging or random, which decides which strategies can work.\",\"body\":[\"A trend shows higher highs and higher lows, or lower ones. A range turns at the same two levels with flat averages. A random walk shows neither: swings overlap with no edges and no direction.\",\"Every strategy is a bet on one regime. The same rule can make money in one month and lose in the next without changing at all, because the market did. Label the regime before choosing a strategy, and treat an unclear label as a reason to trade smaller or not at all.\"],\"example\":\"The same moving-average rule: +6.0R over 12 trades in a trending month (5 wins averaging +2.6R), −8.4R over 15 trades in a ranging one (3 wins of +1.2R, 12 losses).\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"range\",\"random-walk\",\"strategy-regime-fit\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.1\",\"2.8.2\",\"2.8.8\"]},{\"id\":\"strategy-regime-fit\",\"name\":\"Strategy–regime fit\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Matching a strategy family to the regime it is built for, and standing aside when the regime is unclear.\",\"body\":[\"Trend following and pullbacks work in trends and lose in ranges. Range trading, mean reversion and failed-breakout fades work in ranges and lose when a trend starts. Breakouts work when a range turns into a trend.\",\"The skill has three steps: diagnose the regime, choose a family or skip, and size for the regime you might be wrong about. On a random walk no rule has an edge, so 'No trade' is the right choice there. The matrix itself is a practitioners' synthesis, evidence grade C.\"],\"example\":\"Guessing among trend, range and no clear regime is right one time in three. Getting 8 or more of 12 segments right by luck alone happens about 1.9% of the time.\",\"related\":[\"market-regime\",\"base-rates\",\"when-not-to-trade\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.1\"]},{\"id\":\"trend-following\",\"name\":\"Trend following\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Rules that trade in the direction of an established trend, cutting losses quickly and letting winners run, usually at a low win rate.\",\"body\":[\"A typical system: price above a rising 50-period average or a fast average above a slow one; entry on the cross or on a close above the 20-candle high; a stop 2–3 ATR away; exit on the opposite cross or a trailing stop.\",\"Win rates of 30–45% are normal: a few large winners pay for many small losses. The system loses in ranges, to whipsaws, and at sharp reversals, and its losing runs are long. Moving-average timing on one market is grade B; diversified across many markets, as time-series momentum, grade A.\"],\"example\":\"7 wins of +3.2R and 13 losses of 1R in 20 trades: +9.4R gross, +7.4R after 0.10R of costs per trade, +0.37R per trade with 65% losers.\",\"related\":[\"market-regime\",\"moving-average\",\"ma-crossover\",\"trailing-stop\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.2\"]},{\"id\":\"trend-pullback\",\"name\":\"Pullback in a trend\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Entering an established trend after a temporary move against it, with a stop beyond the pullback low.\",\"body\":[\"Three parts: a trend filter (price above a rising 50-period average, higher highs and lows), a pullback to the EMA 20 or an old breakout level, and a trigger, such as a close back above the prior candle's high. The stop goes under the pullback low, often minus half an ATR.\",\"Waiting for the pullback buys a better reward-to-risk than chasing, but some pullbacks never come and others turn into reversals: a lower low under the last higher low, or a close under the 50, switches the filter off. Practitioners' method, grade C.\"],\"example\":\"Stop 56.40, target 61.00. Buying the pullback at 57.20 gives 3.80 ÷ 0.80 = 4.75 : 1; chasing at 58.40 gives 2.60 ÷ 2.00 = 1.3 : 1. At 1%, 116 units at 57.20 risk 99.40 with commissions.\",\"related\":[\"trend-following\",\"pullback\",\"invalidation-level\",\"fomo\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.3\"]},{\"id\":\"range-breakout-strategy\",\"name\":\"Range breakout strategy\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Trading a decisive close beyond a tested range edge, with a stop back inside the range and a measured-move target.\",\"body\":[\"The card: a level touched at least twice, a close beyond it (ideally on above-average volume), a stop back inside under the breakout candle, and a target of the range's height added to the level.\",\"Most trades are small losses: breakouts fail most from untested levels, on wicks, on low volume and late in long moves. The reward-to-risk lets about 30% winners break even. Grade C to D for drawn levels; grade B for a rule-based channel breakout on one market.\"],\"example\":\"Range 97.00–100.00, close at 100.15, stop 99.20, target 103.00: 3.0 : 1 before costs. After 0.20 per unit, break-even rises from 25.0% to 30.3%. 95 units risk 99.73.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"false-breakout\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"measured-move\",\"failed-breakout-strategy\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.4\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-contraction-breakout\",\"name\":\"Volatility contraction breakout\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Bracketing a narrow-range period with stop orders on both sides, betting on expansion rather than on direction.\",\"body\":[\"A contraction, such as an NR7, an inside bar or a Bollinger squeeze, is bracketed with a buy stop above and a sell stop below, linked one-cancels-the-other, each with its stop on the opposite side.\",\"Volatility clustering is well documented (grade A), but the directional edge after a contraction is weak after costs (grade C). Tight stops make costs heavy in R, and a sideways resolution can stop both sides when the plan re-arms the other one.\"],\"example\":\"NR7 high 100.60, low 99.70: buy stop 100.65, sell stop 99.65, stops 1.00 away. Costs ≈ 0.20R per side; 90 units per side risk 99.02. Both stopped: −198.04, about 2% of the account.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"volatility-clustering\",\"volatility-squeeze\",\"oco-order\",\"costs-in-r\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.5\"]},{\"id\":\"range-trading\",\"name\":\"Range trading\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Buying near support and selling near resistance in a confirmed range, until a close outside it.\",\"body\":[\"Confirm first: at least two touches on each edge, flat averages, a low ADX. Enter near an edge with a limit or a reversal candle, put the stop just outside it, and exit near the other edge or the middle.\",\"The flip rule: the first confirmed close outside ends range trading, and the breakout card takes over. The biggest losses come from breakouts and gaps through the edge. Small targets make costs heavy. Grade C: it depends on the regime holding.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 97.30, stop 96.40, target 99.70: 2.67 : 1 before costs, 2.0 : 1 after 0.20 per unit; break-even rises from 27.3% to 33.3%. A gap open at 95.80 costs 159.66, about 1.6R.\",\"related\":[\"range\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"limit-order\",\"range-breakout-strategy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.6\"]},{\"id\":\"mean-reversion\",\"name\":\"Mean reversion\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Fading short-term extremes back toward an average, often with a high win rate and rare large losses.\",\"body\":[\"Bollinger reversion buys a close under the lower band in a range and exits at the middle band. RSI(2) buys when RSI(2) drops under 10 with price above the SMA 200, and exits on a close above the SMA 5.\",\"Both win often and lose rarely but big. In a trend, price walks down the band; in a crash, oversold gets more oversold; gaps jump stops. A catastrophic stop, such as 3 ATR, is required on every trade. Grade C for Bollinger reversion, C to B for RSI(2).\"],\"example\":\"15 wins of +0.5R, 4 losses of 1R and one gap of 5R: −1.5R with a 75% win rate. At 75% wins and +0.5R, break-even needs losses averaging 1.5R or less.\",\"related\":[\"return-skew\",\"range-trading\",\"bollinger-bands\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.7\"]},{\"id\":\"return-skew\",\"name\":\"Return skew\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The asymmetry of trade results: many small losses and a few big wins (positive) or the reverse (negative).\",\"body\":[\"Trend following has positive skew: frequent small losses, rare large wins, and long losing runs. Mean reversion has negative skew: frequent small wins and rare large losses, so it looks smooth until the big loss arrives.\",\"The win rate alone says nothing about which you have. Check the average loss against the break-even level, and cap the tail of a negatively skewed rule with a stop.\"],\"example\":\"At a 35% win rate, the chance of at least one run of 8 or more losses in 100 trades is about 69%. At a 75% win rate and +0.5R wins, one 5R loss among 5 losses lifts the average loss to 1.8R, past break-even.\",\"related\":[\"mean-reversion\",\"trend-following\",\"expectancy\",\"streaks-and-runs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.7\",\"2.8.2\"]},{\"id\":\"failed-breakout-strategy\",\"name\":\"Failed breakout strategy\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Trading against a breakout that returns inside the range, with a stop beyond the failed extreme.\",\"body\":[\"Turtle Soup: after a new 20-candle high, with the previous one at least 4 candles earlier, sell just under the old high (valid on that candle only), with the stop just above the new extreme and the far edge of the range as the target. Mirrored for lows.\",\"It mirrors the breakout: same level, opposite bet. The breakout pays when the range ends, the fade when it holds. Choose from the signs (closes back inside, thin volume, a fast return) rather than from the last result. Grade C.\"],\"example\":\"Range 97.00–100.00; a spike to 100.70 closes at 99.80. Sell 99.90, stop 100.80, target 97.60: 2.56 : 1 gross, 2.10 ÷ 1.10 after 0.20 of costs, break-even 34.4%. 99 units risk 99.04.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"range-breakout-strategy\",\"trapped-traders\",\"market-regime\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.8\"]},{\"id\":\"after-cost-evaluation\",\"name\":\"After-cost evaluation\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Judging a strategy by its net result per trade, against the passive benchmark and a random-walk control.\",\"body\":[\"Turn costs into R (cost per unit ÷ stop distance) and take them off the gross expectancy. Then compare the net result with simply holding the market and with the same rule on a random walk, where the gross edge is zero by construction.\",\"Finally, put an interval around the result: about 1.96 × the spread of results ÷ √n either side. Small edges need hundreds of trades, and published edges shrink after publication (McLean and Pontiff: 26% less out of sample, 58% less after publication).\"],\"example\":\"+0.12R gross on a 1.00 stop with 0.20 per unit of costs: −0.08R net, −16R over 200 trades. 50 trades with a 1.44R spread leave an interval of ±0.40R.\",\"related\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"passive-benchmark\",\"random-walk\",\"sample-size\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.8.10\"]},{\"id\":\"market-hours\",\"name\":\"Market hours and time zones\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"When each market's main session runs, converted to your own time zone, including daylight-saving mismatches.\",\"body\":[\"Each market has a main session where most orders meet and liquidity is deepest. Typical hours: US stocks 09:30–16:00 New York, London stocks 08:00–16:30 London, spot FX from Sunday 17:00 to Friday 17:00 New York, crypto all week. In Bucharest that's usually 16:30–23:00 for US stocks and 10:00–18:30 for London.\",\"The US changes its clocks on the second Sunday of March and the first Sunday of November; the EU on the last Sundays of March and October. In the weeks between, New York is 6 hours behind Bucharest instead of 7, so every US time arrives an hour earlier. Platforms may also show server time rather than yours.\"],\"example\":\"Friday 30 October 2026: Europe is on winter time, the US isn't yet. The US open at 09:30 New York is 15:30 in Bucharest, an NFP-style release at 08:30 is 14:30, and FX rollover at 17:00 is 23:00. London's 08:00 is 10:00, as always.\",\"related\":[\"timeframe\",\"fx-sessions\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.1\"]},{\"id\":\"fx-sessions\",\"name\":\"FX sessions and overlaps\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The Sydney, Tokyo, London and New York windows of currency trading, and the busy London–New York overlap.\",\"body\":[\"In New York time, roughly: Sydney 17:00–02:00, Tokyo 19:00–04:00, London 03:00–12:00, New York 08:00–17:00. The London–New York overlap, 08:00–12:00 New York, is typically 15:00–19:00 in Bucharest.\",\"Activity and volatility gather at session opens and in the overlap, and more trading means tighter spreads. Directional 'roles' for sessions, such as London setting the day's high, are folklore without tested evidence. Choose hours by movement per unit of cost.\"],\"example\":\"On a 10-pip target, a 0.8-pip spread in the overlap costs 8% of the target; a 1.6-pip spread at night costs 16%. With illustrative ranges of 42 pips in the overlap and 18 in Asian hours, range per pip of spread is 52.5 against 12.\",\"related\":[\"market-hours\",\"volatility\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.2\"]},{\"id\":\"intraday-cycle\",\"name\":\"Intraday cycle\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The daily pattern of higher volume and volatility near the open and close, with a quieter middle of the day.\",\"body\":[\"In stocks, volume and volatility typically form a U across the session: high after the open, low around midday, rising into the close and its auction. In FX the U appears inside each session.\",\"A fixed stop that fits midday can sit inside the noise at the open. Setting the stop from the hour's ATR keeps it outside the noise, and sizing from that stop keeps the risk constant. In quiet hours the stop is tight, so costs take a bigger share of each R, and without leverage the position cap can bind.\"],\"example\":\"Stop = 2 × the 5-minute ATR, 1% of 10,000.00. At the open (ATR 0.60) the stop is 1.20: 76 units lose 98.76, and costs are 0.17R. At midday (ATR 0.20) the stop is 0.40: the 99-unit cap leaves only 49.49 at risk, and costs are 0.50R.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"position-sizing\",\"costs-in-r\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.3\"]},{\"id\":\"opening-range-breakout\",\"name\":\"Opening range breakout\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Trading a break of the first minutes' high or low; a small edge that the costs at the open can erase.\",\"body\":[\"The opening range is the high and low of the session's first N minutes, N fixed in advance. A long ORB places a buy stop just above the high with the stop-loss at the other edge, and exits at a target or the close. Filters keep it to days in play, with a catalyst and high relative volume, and a range that isn't huge against the daily ATR.\",\"The evidence is grade C. A working paper reported large net returns on US stocks in play, without peer review or an out-of-sample test; an independent replication on index CFDs found a gross edge of about +0.13R per trade that didn't survive spread and slippage. Both peak at the open.\"],\"example\":\"Opening range 99.60–100.40. Entry 100.45, stop 99.55: 99 units risk 99.01. Costs are 0.20 ÷ 0.90 = 0.22R and entry slippage of 0.05 is 0.06R, so a +0.13R gross edge nets −0.15R.\",\"related\":[\"breakout\",\"slippage\",\"intraday-cycle\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.4\"]},{\"id\":\"gap-strategies\",\"name\":\"Gap strategies\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Trading an opening gap's continuation (gap and go) or its retracement (gap fill), or passing.\",\"body\":[\"Gap and go trades with a large gap that has a catalyst and relative volume of about 3× or more, entering on a break of the early range. Gap fill fades a moderate gap with no news while price is still inside last week's range. When neither fits, pass. Both are practitioners' rules, grade C to D.\",\"Many fills need no explanation: on a random walk, the chance of coming back a distance d within the day is 2 × (1 − Φ(d ÷ σ)). A gap-fill rule has to beat that base rate after costs. On a 24-hour chart, a session gap may not exist at all.\"],\"example\":\"LABX closes at 100.00 and opens at 101.00 with no news; the daily standard deviation is 1.60. On a random walk, price touches 100.00 again about 53% of the time. From an open at 102.50 it would be about 12%.\",\"related\":[\"gap-types\",\"random-walk\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.5\"]},{\"id\":\"cost-drag\",\"name\":\"Cost drag\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The share of a trade's average gross profit that costs consume; it rises as targets shrink.\",\"body\":[\"Cost drag = costs ÷ average gross profit per trade. Costs per unit are roughly fixed, so small targets lose a large share: a 0.20 round trip is 67% of a 0.30 target and under 1% of a large swing target.\",\"With an equal stop and target d, break-even after costs is (d + cost) ÷ 2d. At a cost of 0.20 that's 83% for d = 0.30, 60% for 1.00 and 52.5% for 4.00. Scalping sits at the top of that wall, against professionals who earn the spread.\"],\"example\":\"A scalp with a 0.30 target and a 0.30 stop nets 0.10 on a win and loses 0.50 on a loss: break-even is 0.50 ÷ 0.60 = 83.3%. In FX, 1 pip of cost on a 5-pip target is 20% drag; on a 150-pip target, 0.7%.\",\"related\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"trading-costs\",\"trading-styles\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.7\"]},{\"id\":\"fx-rollover\",\"name\":\"FX rollover\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The daily roll of open currency positions at about 17:00 New York time, when spreads often widen.\",\"body\":[\"Spot FX positions still open at 17:00 New York time are rolled to the next value date. In Bucharest that's typically midnight, and 23:00 in the weeks when US and EU clocks are out of step. It also ends the FX trading day, so many platforms close their daily candles then.\",\"Few traders are active at that hour, and spreads often widen for some minutes, so a tight stop can be triggered by the spread alone. Financing on positions held through it comes in 2.14.4; conventions vary by broker.\"],\"example\":\"On Friday 30 October 2026, rollover at 17:00 New York is 23:00 in Bucharest; on Monday 2 November it's back at 00:00. If the spread widens by 4 pips around an unchanged mid price, the bid drops 2 pips, enough to hit a long's stop 2 pips below it.\",\"related\":[\"fx-sessions\",\"market-hours\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.8\"]},{\"id\":\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\",\"name\":\"Weekend and holiday risk\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The risk that price gaps past stops while markets are closed or thin, and the sizing that limits it.\",\"body\":[\"A stop-loss is an order, not a guarantee. When a market reopens after a weekend or a holiday, a stop that price has jumped past fills at the first available price, so the loss can be several times the plan. Holidays also thin the order book, widening spreads and slippage.\",\"For positions held over a weekend, this course sizes from a worst-case gap of 3 × the daily ATR and keeps that loss within 2R. Before holidays it reduces or skips; around FX rollover it avoids tight stops and new entries.\"],\"example\":\"Long 47 at 100.00, stop 98.00, planned loss 98.66. Monday's first bid is 95.50: the loss is 216.10, 2.2 times the plan. Sized for a 6.00 gap (3 × an ATR of 2.00) within 200.00, the position would be 32 units, losing at most 195.11.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"position-sizing\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.9.8\"]},{\"id\":\"news-surprise\",\"name\":\"Expectations versus surprise\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Prices react to the difference between a release and its consensus, often measured as a standardised surprise.\",\"body\":[\"Before a release, the consensus forecast is largely in the price. What moves price on the day is the surprise: the actual minus the consensus. Dividing it by the usual size of surprises gives a standardised surprise, z, which puts different releases on one scale.\",\"At major releases the first move tends to follow the sign of the surprise, but its size is noisy, and small surprises (in this course, |z| under 0.5) give no reliable direction. Which way a surprise pushes a risk asset depends on what the market fears most: inflation or a slowdown.\"],\"example\":\"Consensus 150,000 jobs, actual 210,000: a surprise of +60,000. If surprises usually vary by 60,000, z = +1.0. An actual of 160,000 gives z = 0.17: noise.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"base-rates\",\"expected-value\",\"economic-calendar\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.1\"]},{\"id\":\"economic-calendar\",\"name\":\"Economic calendar\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The schedule of releases with time, impact, previous, consensus and actual values; it predicts volatility, not direction.\",\"body\":[\"Each row of an economic calendar gives an event's time, an impact tier, the previous value and any revision to it, the consensus and, once published, the actual. It tells you reliably when volatility, spreads and gap risk will rise, but not which way price will go.\",\"Times need converting: US releases are set in New York time, and the US and Europe change their clocks on different dates. Impact tiers are editorial and shift with the regime, and unscheduled events never appear on the calendar.\"],\"example\":\"Payrolls at 08:30 New York time: 15:30 in Bucharest, or 14:30 in the weeks when only one side has changed its clocks. A +60,000 headline with −120,000 of revisions nets to −60,000.\",\"related\":[\"news-surprise\",\"news-policy\",\"volatility\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.2\"]},{\"id\":\"news-liquidity\",\"name\":\"Liquidity at news time\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"Around releases spreads widen, depth falls and stops fill at the first available price after the jump.\",\"body\":[\"In the minute before a major release, dealers widen their quotes and pull resting orders. At the number, price can jump across levels where nobody is trading. Liquidity usually returns within a minute or two.\",\"A triggered stop becomes a market order and fills at the first price available, so the loss can exceed the plan. A widened spread can also trigger a long's stop through the bid while the mid price stays above it.\"],\"example\":\"Long 47 LABX at 100.00, stop 98.00, planned loss 98.66. The first bid after the jump is 96.80: the loss is 155.03, 56.37 over plan, about 0.57R of slippage.\",\"related\":[\"bid-ask-spread\",\"slippage\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.3\"]},{\"id\":\"nonfarm-payrolls\",\"name\":\"Nonfarm payrolls\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The monthly US jobs report (payrolls, unemployment rate, wages), with revisions and large sampling noise.\",\"body\":[\"The US jobs report usually comes out on the first Friday of the month at 08:30 New York time. Payrolls come from a survey of firms, the unemployment rate from a survey of households, and average hourly earnings show wage growth. Each month's payrolls are revised in the next two reports.\",\"The surveys carry sampling error: the margin of error on the monthly payrolls change is typically over 100,000 jobs. A report is clearly hot or cold only when all three numbers and the revisions agree; otherwise it's mixed, and the reaction often comes in two waves.\"],\"example\":\"Payrolls 90,000 above consensus, unemployment 0.1 points higher, wages 0.1% softer and revisions of −50,000: one hot reading against three cold ones, so the report is mixed.\",\"related\":[\"news-surprise\",\"economic-calendar\",\"cpi-inflation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.4\"]},{\"id\":\"cpi-inflation\",\"name\":\"Consumer price index\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The monthly US inflation report; markets focus on core month-on-month, and headline and core can disagree.\",\"body\":[\"The consumer price index tracks prices paid by consumers. Headline CPI covers all items; core leaves out food and energy, the most volatile parts. Markets focus on core's month-on-month change, quoted to one decimal, so a miss of 0.1 is typically already large.\",\"When headline and core disagree, the reaction often comes in two waves: the first follows the headline, the second follows core. Waiting a few minutes avoids being stopped out by the first wave.\"],\"example\":\"Core consensus 0.3%, actual 0.2%, with core surprises usually varying by 0.1: z = −0.1 ÷ 0.1 = −1.0, a large cool surprise.\",\"related\":[\"news-surprise\",\"nonfarm-payrolls\",\"central-bank-decisions\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.5\"]},{\"id\":\"central-bank-decisions\",\"name\":\"Central bank decisions\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Rate decisions and guidance move prices by how far they differ from what markets had already priced.\",\"body\":[\"Central banks set their policy rates at scheduled meetings, typically eight a year for the Fed, the ECB, the Bank of England and the Bank of Japan. Well before each meeting, futures prices show what the market expects.\",\"The surprise is the decision minus the probability-weighted expectation. Guidance counts too: a press conference can bring a second, larger move. Hawkish and dovish only matter relative to what was priced.\"],\"example\":\"Markets price a 70% chance of a 25 bp cut: an expected 17.5 bp. A cut surprises by 7.5 bp, dovish; no change surprises by 17.5 bp, hawkish.\",\"related\":[\"news-surprise\",\"economic-calendar\",\"cpi-inflation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.6\"]},{\"id\":\"news-policy\",\"name\":\"News policy\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A rule decided in advance for each event: stand aside, reduce to a gap-safe size, or trade the reaction.\",\"body\":[\"Standing aside is the default: no new orders from a set time before a red event, and flat or within the plan's rule. Reducing keeps a smaller position, sized so that a gap of several ATRs still costs no more than 1R.\",\"Trading the reaction means acting only after the number. The first move tends to follow the surprise, but most of the repricing happens within seconds, before a person can act and at the widest spreads, so the first seconds are no retail edge.\"],\"example\":\"Long from 100.00 with ATR 0.80: a 4 × ATR gap exits at 96.80. 30 units lose 98.96 there, within 1R; 31 would lose 102.26.\",\"related\":[\"position-sizing\",\"gap-risk\",\"news-liquidity\",\"when-not-to-trade\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.7\"]},{\"id\":\"second-move\",\"name\":\"Second move\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Entering after a release only once spreads normalise, on a break of the post-release range.\",\"body\":[\"Wait until the spread is back to at most twice normal, mark the high and low of the first 5 or 15 minutes after the release, and enter on a break of that range in the surprise's direction, with a stop at its other side.\",\"It fails on conflicting details, on small surprises and around press conferences. As a way to cut risk it rates B; as a source of edge, C.\"],\"example\":\"Range 101.00–102.00. Buy the break at 102.05, stop 100.95: 1.10 a unit. 83 units lose 99.73 at the stop; the 2R target is 104.25.\",\"related\":[\"news-policy\",\"breakout\",\"limit-order\",\"news-liquidity\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.8\"]},{\"id\":\"news-risk-checklist\",\"name\":\"News risk checklist\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A short go/no-go list applied before any order near a scheduled event.\",\"body\":[\"Before any order near a red event: know the week's events in your time zone, decide each policy in advance, check that a gap of several ATRs stays within your maximum loss, widen a stop only with a smaller size, wait for normal spreads after the release, reduce before weekends and holidays, know your broker's news rules, and journal the slippage you paid.\",\"No checklist protects you from an unscheduled event. There, only position size and leverage decide the damage.\"],\"example\":\"At 10:1 leverage on a 10,000 account, a 15% gap costs 15,000 and leaves −5,000. At 1:1, it costs 1,500.\",\"related\":[\"pre-trade-checklist\",\"gap-risk\",\"leverage\",\"journaling\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.10.9\"]},{\"id\":\"percent-volatility-sizing\",\"name\":\"Volatility sizing\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Sizing each position so one ATR of movement costs the same share of equity in every instrument.\",\"body\":[\"Choose v, the share of equity one ATR may cost. Units = equity × v ÷ ATR per unit. A wild instrument gets fewer units and a calm one more, so each carries the same weight in the account. With the stop a set number of ATRs away, the risk per trade is fixed too.\",\"ATR is an average of past ranges, so it lags after volatility jumps: resize from what the market is doing now. And equal size per ATR says nothing about correlation. Two positions can be sized perfectly and still be the same bet.\"],\"example\":\"Equity 10,000.00, v = 0.5%: one ATR may cost 50.00. LABX (ATR 2.00): 25 units; LABZ (ATR 0.50): 100 units. With stops two ATRs away and costs counted, 24 LABX and 96 LABZ, each swinging 48.00 per ATR.\",\"related\":[\"position-sizing\",\"volatility\",\"cost-adjusted-sizing\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.1\"]},{\"id\":\"portfolio-heat\",\"name\":\"Portfolio heat\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The total money at risk if every open stop were hit, as a percentage of equity.\",\"body\":[\"Heat adds the open risk of every position, counts pending orders as if filled and keeps a gap allowance for stops at breakeven. It caps the damage of one bad afternoon, when news or a gap hits everything at once.\",\"Before each order, current heat plus the new trade's risk must fit the cap; if it doesn't, resize or skip. Practitioners use caps of about 4–8%. The number is a convention, written into the plan in advance.\"],\"example\":\"Open risks of 1%, 0.5%, 1% and 1.5%: heat 4%. Under a 5% cap, a new LABZ trade planned at 1.5% is cut to 1%: 96 units at 40.00 with a stop at 39.00, losing 99.80.\",\"related\":[\"risk-per-trade\",\"exposure\",\"gap-risk\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.2\"]},{\"id\":\"exposure-limits\",\"name\":\"Exposure limits\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Caps on risk per instrument, per direction and per theme, so that no single idea dominates the account.\",\"body\":[\"Heat caps the total; exposure limits cap how it's spread. A typical policy: at most 2% of risk in one instrument, at most half of the heat in one direction, and one position per group of correlated instruments.\",\"A new trade must fit every limit at once. The tightest one decides whether you accept it, resize it or reject it.\"],\"example\":\"A LABX long already risks 1.5% under a 2% per-instrument cap: a new LABX trade can risk at most 0.5%. A LABY long is rejected while LABX is open, because the two move together.\",\"related\":[\"portfolio-heat\",\"exposure\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.4\"]},{\"id\":\"period-loss-limits\",\"name\":\"Weekly and monthly loss limits\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Caps on realised losses plus open risk over a week or a month, after which new trades stop.\",\"body\":[\"A daily limit stops one bad day; weekly and monthly limits stop a bad run of days. Alexander Elder's 6% rule is the best-known version: this month's losses plus the risk of open trades may not pass 6% of month-start equity.\",\"Counting open risk makes the limit bite before the losses land. The limit resets at the start of the period, never early after a good week, and its size should fit how often you trade.\"],\"example\":\"Month-start equity 10,000.00, cap 6% = 600.00. Lost 350.00, with 200.00 of open risk: 50.00 of room. A full 1% trade no longer fits this month.\",\"related\":[\"daily-loss-limit\",\"portfolio-heat\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.4\"]},{\"id\":\"drawdown-tiers\",\"name\":\"Drawdown tiers\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Pre-set cuts in risk at set drawdown depths, with a rule for when risk returns.\",\"body\":[\"Tiers are written before trading: for example, at −5% risk drops from 1% to 0.5%; at −10% trading stops for a full review; full risk returns once half the drawdown is recovered. Risk only ever goes down as the drawdown deepens.\",\"Calibrate the depths to your system's simulated normal runs, not to round numbers. A stop tier that normal runs often reach stops you for variance; one at or beyond the 95th percentile signals that something may really have changed.\"],\"example\":\"Reference system (40% winners at +2.5R, losers at −1R) at 1% risk, 100 trades: 54.3% of normal runs reach −8% and 30.7% reach −10%. With risk halved from −5%, only 7.6% reach −10%, at the cost of some return.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"expected-drawdown\",\"fixed-fractional-sizing\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.5\"]},{\"id\":\"performance-metrics\",\"name\":\"Performance metrics\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Win rate, payoff, expectancy, profit factor, drawdown and outlier dependence, read as diagnostics with their sample size.\",\"body\":[\"Each metric looks at the journal from one side and hides something: win rate hides payoff, profit factor hides the order of trades, maximum drawdown rests on one episode, and recovery factor grows with the length of the record. Expectancy links them: E = (PF − 1) × (1 − W) × average loss.\",\"Outcome metrics are diagnostics, shown with their sample size and treated with caution under about 30 trades. Process KPIs, such as plan adherence and stop integrity, are the grade.\"],\"example\":\"40 trades: 16 wins (+40R), 24 losses (−24R), best trade +9R. Win rate 40%, payoff 2.5, expectancy +0.40R, profit factor 1.67, outlier dependence 9 ÷ 16 = 56%.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"r-multiple\",\"drawdown\",\"process-kpis\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.6\"]},{\"id\":\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"name\":\"Expectancy confidence interval\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The range in which a system's true average R plausibly lies, given the sample size and the spread of results.\",\"body\":[\"The 95% interval is the mean ± 1.96 × sd ÷ √n. It narrows with the square root of the number of trades: four times the trades, half the width. A setup has shown an edge only once the interval excludes zero.\",\"Small samples prove little: a system with no edge shows +0.40R or better over 30 trades about one time in ten. The calculation assumes independent trades; streaky results make the real interval wider.\"],\"example\":\"+0.40R with sd 1.71R: at 20 trades the interval is −0.35R to +1.15R; at 100, +0.06R to +0.74R. The first sample size that excludes zero is 71 trades.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"base-rates\",\"sample-size\",\"variance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.7\"]},{\"id\":\"sqn\",\"name\":\"System quality number\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Mean R divided by its standard deviation, times the square root of the number of trades (capped at 100).\",\"body\":[\"SQN = √N × mean R ÷ sd of R. With N uncapped it's the t-statistic of the mean, so it reads as how many standard errors the average sits above zero. Bands for a 'good' SQN are practitioners' rules of thumb, not tests.\",\"SQN alone doesn't pick a system. Compare systems by expectancy per month after costs, and set each one next to the passive benchmark, return and drawdown together.\"],\"example\":\"100 trades at +0.40R with sd 1.71R: SQN = 10 × 0.40 ÷ 1.71 = 2.34, p ≈ 0.02. System A (+0.40R, 8 trades a month) beats B (+1.00R, 2 a month) after costs of 0.10R: 2.4R against 1.8R a month.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"passive-benchmark\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.11.8\"]},{\"id\":\"prospect-theory\",\"name\":\"Prospect theory\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"People judge outcomes as gains or losses from a reference point and feel losses more strongly than equal gains.\",\"body\":[\"Kahneman and Tversky (1979, 1992) described three parts. Outcomes are coded as gains or losses from a reference point, often the entry price. Sensitivity diminishes as amounts grow, so people tend to play safe with gains and gamble with losses. And losses weigh more than equal gains: in the 1992 study, about 2.25 times as much.\",\"The pattern is well established (evidence grade A), but the parameters are illustrative: the 2.25 came from 25 students, and studies of professionals sometimes find less. Knowing about it doesn't remove it. Exits set at entry, which don't depend on where the trade stands, are the practical counter.\"],\"example\":\"A sure +50.00 or a 50% chance of +100.00: most people take the sure gain. A sure −50.00 or a 50% chance of −100.00: most take the gamble. Each pair has the same expected value, +50.00 and −50.00.\",\"related\":[\"loss-aversion\",\"expected-value\",\"exit-rules\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.1\"]},{\"id\":\"disposition-effect\",\"name\":\"Disposition effect\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"The tendency to sell winners too early and to hold losers too long, measured as PGR − PLR.\",\"body\":[\"Shefrin and Statman (1985) named it, and Odean (1998) measured it in 10,000 brokerage accounts: on the days they sold, investors realised 14.8% of their paper gains but only 9.8% of their paper losses. The proportion of gains realised (PGR) minus the proportion of losses realised (PLR) is the disposition measure, 0.05 there. Rebalancing and costs didn't explain it (evidence grade B), and its size varies by group.\",\"In trading it shrinks winners and enlarges losers. At a 40% win rate with −1R losses, closing winners at +0.8R instead of a +2.5R target turns +0.40R per trade into −0.28R. Exits set by rules (stop, target, time stop, opposing signal) take the decision out of the moment.\"],\"example\":\"A sample journal: 12 gains realised and 28 left on paper, so PGR = 0.30; 6 losses realised and 34 left, so PLR = 0.15. Disposition = 0.15: gains were sold at twice the rate of losses.\",\"related\":[\"prospect-theory\",\"loss-aversion\",\"exit-rules\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.2\"]},{\"id\":\"overconfidence\",\"name\":\"Overconfidence and calibration\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Being sure more often than you are right, measured by comparing stated confidence with the hit rate.\",\"body\":[\"Overconfidence as miscalibration is a gap you can measure: log a confidence number before each decision, then compare the average with how often you were right. A negative gap, the hit rate below the confidence, is overconfidence; a positive one is underconfidence. Small logs are noisy, so read the gap over many entries.\",\"Its costliest form is trading too much. Barber and Odean found that the most active fifth of 66,465 US households earned 11.4% a year against the market's 17.9%, and that men traded about 45% more than women, which cut their net returns by 2.65 percentage points a year against 1.72 (evidence grade B; some of the trading may have rational reasons).\"],\"example\":\"100 logged calls at an average confidence of 80%, 60 of them right: a gap of 60 − 80 = −20 points. If the same trader goes from 5 to 25 trades a week, costs of 0.20R per trade take 4R more each week.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"trading-costs\",\"overtrading\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.3\"]},{\"id\":\"recency-bias\",\"name\":\"Recency bias\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Giving the last few results more weight than the base rate deserves.\",\"body\":[\"Recent trades come to mind first, and what's easy to recall feels more likely (availability; Tversky and Kahneman, 1974; evidence grade B). After three losses a valid setup feels broken; after three wins it feels certain. Both readings let a handful of trades override the record.\",\"A journal check makes it visible: compare how often you skip valid setups after losses with how often you skip them after wins. A valid setup's base rate doesn't change with the last result, so skipping more after losses is the bias, not caution.\"],\"example\":\"At a 40% win rate, any three given trades all lose 0.6 × 0.6 × 0.6 = 21.6% of the time. A journal showing 5% of valid setups skipped after a win and 40% after two losses shows recency steering the entries.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"sample-size\",\"gamblers-fallacy\",\"streaks-and-runs\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.4\"]},{\"id\":\"regression-to-the-mean\",\"name\":\"Regression to the mean\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Extreme results tend to be followed by more ordinary ones when outcomes contain noise.\",\"body\":[\"Any result mixes edge with luck. An extreme result usually had extreme luck, and luck doesn't carry over, so the next stretch tends to sit closer to the long-run average. It's a property of noisy numbers, not a force that pulls results back.\",\"It explains the 'rookie of the year': among traders with no skill, the best month is luck and fades the next month. It also explains why a setup's hot start rarely lasts. A drop after an extreme run isn't, by itself, proof that something broke.\"],\"example\":\"A setup makes +1.5R per trade over its first 15 trades; a long test puts its edge at +0.3R. The best estimate for the next 15 trades is 15 × 0.3R = +4.5R, not another +22.5R.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"luck-vs-skill\",\"variance\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.4\"]},{\"id\":\"anchoring\",\"name\":\"Anchoring\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Letting an irrelevant number, such as your entry price, pull your judgement.\",\"body\":[\"A number you've seen pulls later estimates toward it, even when it's irrelevant. In Tversky and Kahneman's (1974) study, a wheel of fortune stopping at 10 or 65 moved estimates of the share of African countries in the UN to medians of 25% and 45% (evidence grade A in the lab).\",\"In trading the common anchors are your entry price, an old high and someone else's target. The entry anchor shows in a journal as exits that cluster within 0.10 of entry after a trade has gone against you. The counter is a price, written before entry, that proves the idea wrong.\"],\"example\":\"Bought at 104.00, now 101.20, stop at 100.80. 'I'll sell when it's back to 104.00' lets the entry decide. The plan's stop at 100.80 is the only level that should.\",\"related\":[\"invalidation-level\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"prospect-theory\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.5\"]},{\"id\":\"sunk-cost\",\"name\":\"Sunk cost\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Holding or adding to a position because of what has already been lost on it.\",\"body\":[\"Money already lost is gone whatever you do next; selling only records it. The sunk-cost trap lets it decide anyway: holding a broken idea, adding to bring the average down, or depositing money to 'save' a position. Arkes and Blumer (1985) showed the pattern outside trading: people who paid full price for theatre season tickets went to more plays (evidence grade B).\",\"The fresh-eyes test cuts through it: if you were flat now, would you open this position at this size? If not, close it or cut it to what you would open.\"],\"example\":\"A long is down 0.7R and the 101.00 level the idea needed has broken, though the 100.40 stop hasn't been hit. Fresh eyes say no new long here, so the position goes; the 0.7R already lost doesn't enter the decision.\",\"related\":[\"averaging-down\",\"loss-aversion\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.6\"]},{\"id\":\"house-money-effect\",\"name\":\"House-money effect\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Taking more risk after gains because the profits feel like someone else's money.\",\"body\":[\"Thaler and Johnson (1990) found that after a gain people accept bets they'd otherwise refuse, and that after a loss they seek bets that could get them back to even, the breakeven effect. The evidence comes from hypothetical choices in small samples (evidence grade C). Both are mental accounting: money treated differently by where it came from.\",\"In a journal it shows as risk per trade that rises after wins. Fixed fractional sizing removes it by construction: the same percentage of current equity after a win, after a loss and after a streak.\"],\"example\":\"After three wins the account is 10,400.00. The plan's 1% is 104.00; sizing at 2% 'with the market's money' risks 208.00. A sample journal with 1.6% average risk after wins and 1.0% after losses is the pattern in numbers.\",\"related\":[\"fixed-fractional-sizing\",\"risk-per-trade\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.6\"]},{\"id\":\"tilt\",\"name\":\"Tilt\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"A state in which emotion overrides the plan, visible as a cluster of rule breaks.\",\"body\":[\"The word comes from poker. A feeling can't be seen in a journal, but its effects can: rule breaks that bunch together. This course's tilt index counts the rule breaks in your last 10 trades; it's a process measure defined by the app, not a diagnosis.\",\"The evidence that intense emotional reactions go with worse trading is modest: Lo, Repin and Steenbarger (2005) followed 80 day traders (evidence grade B). Interview research suggests that better traders regulate early, by reappraisal (grade C). The practical answer is structure: a threshold that locks trading, and a reset routine.\"],\"example\":\"Rule breaks at trades 3, 7, 8 and 10 of a session put the tilt index at 4 after trade 10, with 3 breaks in the last 4 trades. A threshold of 3 would have locked trading after trade 8.\",\"related\":[\"revenge-trading\",\"decision-quality\",\"cooling-off-pause\",\"emotions-in-trading\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.7\"]},{\"id\":\"behavioural-guard-rails\",\"name\":\"Behavioural guard-rails\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Pre-set limits such as cool-downs, tilt thresholds and daily locks that make the right action the easy one.\",\"body\":[\"Guard-rails are set before the session, while judgement is clear, and apply on their own: a cool-down after a loss, a lock at a set number of rule breaks in 10 trades, a daily loss lock in R, and a readiness check of sleep, stress and health. Structure beats willpower: knowing a bias rarely removes it, but a rule that triggers by itself doesn't need willpower.\",\"When a guard-rail triggers, a reset routine fills the pause: stop, start the cool-down, breathe and reflect, name the broken rule, run the checklist, then resume at normal size or end the session.\"],\"example\":\"Guard-rails: lock at 3 rule breaks in 10 trades, or at −3R for the day. Trades 4, 5 and 7 break rules while the day reaches −2.9R: the third break locks trading at trade 7, before the loss limit.\",\"related\":[\"if-then-rules\",\"cooling-off-pause\",\"daily-loss-limit\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.7\"]},{\"id\":\"probabilistic-thinking\",\"name\":\"Probabilistic thinking\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Treating each trade as one draw from a distribution and judging results across a series.\",\"body\":[\"An edge is a higher probability over many trades, not a certainty on one. Mark Douglas built a trading mindset on this idea; his framework is a practitioner's advice, not a tested result (evidence grade C), but the arithmetic is exact. A system that wins 40% at +2.5R and loses 60% at −1R, +0.40R per trade, still ends 33.7% of 5-trade stretches and 16.7% of 10-trade stretches in a loss.\",\"Broad framing turns this into practice: commit to a block of 20 trades taken by the rules, then judge the block by process first and net R second.\"],\"example\":\"Five blocks of 20 trades from that system, every rule followed, return +11.5R, +11.5R, +18.5R, −9.5R and −2.5R. The 28.0R gap between the best and the worst is variance; the process score was 100% in each.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"variance\",\"decision-quality\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.12.9\"]},{\"id\":\"setup-card\",\"name\":\"Setup card\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A playbook entry defining one setup's regime, trigger, stop, management, no-trade conditions and statistics.\",\"body\":[\"A card turns a setup into rules anyone could check: name, market and timeframe; the regime it's allowed in; the trigger and order type; the stop rule, from structure or the ATR; targets and management; re-entry; no-trade conditions; a quality grade; the evidence status; and its own statistics.\",\"Each field is a level, a count, a time or a yes or no, so two people would agree on it. Statistics are kept per card, with the sample size and an interval: at 30 trades most intervals still include zero, so specialise in one or two cards until each has about 100 trades.\"],\"example\":\"LABX pullback card: pullback low 56.80, ATR 0.80, so the stop is 56.80 − 0.40 = 56.40. Buy stop 57.20, target the previous swing high 59.60: 2.40 ÷ 0.80 = 3.0R, an A-grade trade. Over 32 trades at +0.35R with a 1.7R spread, the interval runs from −0.24R to +0.94R.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"invalidation-level\",\"sample-size\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.13.2\"]},{\"id\":\"pre-market-routine\",\"name\":\"Pre-market routine\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A fixed preparation sequence before each session: readiness, calendar, regime, levels, scenarios and risk for the day.\",\"body\":[\"The routine runs in the same order every session, in about ten minutes: readiness check, overnight news, the calendar, a regime label, key levels, two or three if-then scenarios, the watchlist, and the day's maximum risk. It doesn't find trades; it finds what's different today, before the first order.\",\"Each step prevents one mistake: the calendar catches releases, the day's risk protects the daily limit, the regime label keeps the wrong card off the chart, levels stop chasing, readiness cuts risk on bad days. The evidence is grade B to C, from planning research and one qualitative study of traders.\"],\"example\":\"14:50 in Bucharest in July: US CPI at 15:30, which is 08:30 in New York. A swing entry at 15:00 with a 0.80 stop would sit through the release, so the routine's scenario says no new orders until it's out and the spread is back to 0.10.\",\"related\":[\"scenario-planning\",\"if-then-rules\",\"daily-loss-limit\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.13.3\"]},{\"id\":\"trading-reviews\",\"name\":\"Daily and weekly reviews\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Short scheduled reviews that grade decisions, find the costliest error and set one process goal.\",\"body\":[\"The daily review takes five to ten minutes: the day's process KPIs, the best and worst decision, each loss sorted as mistake or variance, one lesson, and tomorrow's preparation. It grades decisions, so a skipped trade that kept a rule can be the day's best.\",\"The weekly review slices trades by setup, time of day, instrument and error tag, sets the equity curve against the range expectancy predicts, and ends with one process goal from the costliest error. Trades are replayed blind, stopped at the decision point, so hindsight can't rewrite them.\"],\"example\":\"A week of 25 trades with −4.3R of error cost: chasing in the first 30 minutes −2.4R, early exits −0.9R, a widened stop −0.5R, later chasing −0.3R, size −0.2R. Next week's single goal: in the first 30 minutes, enter only at the card's level.\",\"related\":[\"journaling\",\"decision-quality\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.13.6\"]},{\"id\":\"process-goals\",\"name\":\"Process goals\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Goals you fully control, such as stop integrity, set instead of profit targets.\",\"body\":[\"A process goal names an action and a number the app can measure: a stop before entry on 100% of trades, risk accuracy of at least 95%, the routine in 5 of 5 sessions. You can meet it every week, win or lose.\",\"Outcome goals, such as making 5% a month, depend largely on luck over short periods, and near the end of the period they push toward bigger positions to catch up. Outcomes are read as diagnostics, with their sample size, over many trades.\"],\"example\":\"Risk accuracy over three trades planned at 100.00 and taken at 92.00, 108.00 and 100.00: misses of 8%, 8% and 0%, an average of 5.33%, so 94.7%. A goal of 95% next week is fully in your hands with the sizing tool.\",\"related\":[\"process-kpis\",\"outcome-bias\",\"decision-quality\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.13.7\"]},{\"id\":\"rule-change-governance\",\"name\":\"Rule-change governance\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Changing a rule only through a logged hypothesis, an evidence threshold, a test and a new plan version.\",\"body\":[\"A rule changes in five steps: write the idea down without acting on it, turn it into a hypothesis, set an evidence bar such as 30 to 50 trades or a simulator test, run the test, and adopt the change as a new plan version with a fresh count.\",\"The bar is set before the test, so the result can't move it. Losing streaks with every rule followed are variance, not evidence, and changes made mid-session or after a few losses never get tested.\"],\"example\":\"A pullback card loses three in a row, every rule followed. The idea 'a stop at 1 ATR loses less to noise than 0.5 ATR' goes in the log, the bar is set at 40 trades in the simulator, and only if the test supports it does the card become a new version.\",\"related\":[\"trading-plan\",\"sample-size\",\"mistake-vs-variance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.13.7\",\"3.11.10\"]},{\"id\":\"stocks-and-etfs\",\"name\":\"Stocks and ETFs\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Shares are ownership in a company; ETFs are exchange-traded funds that hold baskets of assets and trade like shares.\",\"body\":[\"A share is a slice of a company, traded on an exchange in set hours that open and close with auctions. Trades settle a day or two later, depending on the market. Outside the main session liquidity is thin and spreads are wider.\",\"An ETF is a fund traded like a share. Dealers create and redeem units against the basket, which keeps the price near the value of the holdings, though gaps open under stress. The fund charges a yearly expense ratio. A leveraged ETF targets a multiple of one day's move, so over longer periods it drifts from that multiple and decays in choppy markets.\"],\"example\":\"An index rises 10%, then falls 9.09%, back where it started. A 2× daily ETF rises 20% to 120.00, then falls 18.18% to 98.18: −1.82% while the index is flat.\",\"related\":[\"corporate-actions\",\"trading-costs\",\"market-hours\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.1\"]},{\"id\":\"corporate-actions\",\"name\":\"Corporate actions\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Dividends, splits and other company events that change prices or share counts without creating value.\",\"body\":[\"On the ex-dividend date a share drops by about the dividend: value moves from the price into cash. A split multiplies the share count and divides the price; a reverse split does the opposite. A secondary offering dilutes existing holders; a spin-off hands them shares in a new company.\",\"Buying just before the ex-date gains nothing before costs and taxes. Charts and backtests need prices adjusted for splits and dividends, or these events look like crashes.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 100 shares at the ask, 50.00, before a 1.00 dividend; sell after the ex-date at the bid, 48.90. Price −110.00, dividend +100.00, commissions 2.50 and 2.45: −14.95 before taxes.\",\"related\":[\"stocks-and-etfs\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.1\",\"3.8.9\"]},{\"id\":\"short-selling\",\"name\":\"Short selling\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Selling borrowed shares to profit from a fall, paying borrow fees and dividends, with no cap on the loss.\",\"body\":[\"The broker locates and borrows shares, sells them for you, and later you buy them back to return them. While the short is open you pay a borrow fee and owe every dividend, and the lender can recall the shares. In the US, after a 10% fall from the prior close, shorts may only sell above the best bid that day and the next.\",\"A short's loss has no ceiling, and a squeeze can open far past the stop. Size so that a realistic gap, not just the stop, is survivable.\"],\"example\":\"Short 48 at 50.00 with a stop at 52.00: 98.45 at risk with commissions. An open at 56.00 costs 290.55. Ten days at a 30% borrow fee and a 0.50 dividend add 19.73 and 24.00.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"position-sizing\",\"long-and-short\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.2\"]},{\"id\":\"forex-quotes\",\"name\":\"Forex quotes, pips and lots\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"How currency pairs are quoted, and how pips and lot sizes turn a price move into money.\",\"body\":[\"A pair quotes one unit of the base currency in the quote currency: vEUR/vUSD at 1.1000 means one vEUR costs 1.1000 vUSD. A pip is 0.0001, or 0.01 in yen pairs; a pipette is a tenth of a pip. Lots are 100,000 units (standard), 10,000 (mini), 1,000 (micro) and, at some brokers, 100 (nano).\",\"A pip on a standard lot is worth 10.00 when the account currency is the quote currency; otherwise convert at the current rate. Size in lots = risk ÷ (stop in pips × pip value per lot), rounded down.\"],\"example\":\"Risk 100.00, vUSD/vJPY at 150.00, stop 40 pips: a pip is 1,000 vJPY = 6.67 vUSD per lot, so 100.00 ÷ (40 × 6.67) = 0.37 lots, rounded down.\",\"related\":[\"position-sizing\",\"bid-ask-spread\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.3\"]},{\"id\":\"overnight-financing\",\"name\":\"Overnight financing\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The daily interest charged or paid for holding leveraged FX or CFD positions past rollover.\",\"body\":[\"FX positions held past 17:00 New York time pay or earn the interest difference between the two currencies, the swap; Wednesday's counts three days for the weekend. CFDs charge a benchmark rate plus the provider's markup on the full exposure, every night.\",\"Financing is small per night and large per month, so count it in R for swing trades. A positive swap isn't free money: carry pays steadily and crashes rarely and hard.\"],\"example\":\"0.40 lots at −8.00 per lot per night, Monday to Monday: 7 nights, −22.40, 0.22R of a 100.00 risk. A 10,000.00 CFD at 5.5% a year costs 271.23 over 180 days.\",\"related\":[\"fx-rollover\",\"trading-costs\",\"cost-stack\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.4\",\"2.14.5\"]},{\"id\":\"cfds-and-indices\",\"name\":\"CFDs and indices\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Index exposure through futures, ETFs, options or CFDs; a CFD is an over-the-counter contract with the provider as counterparty.\",\"body\":[\"Indices are built differently: the Dow by share price, the S&P 500 by market value, the DAX with dividends counted as reinvested. You trade them through futures, ETFs, options or CFDs.\",\"A CFD pays the change in price for a margin deposit. The provider quotes the price and is your counterparty, often keeping trades on its own book. Funding is charged nightly, so CFDs suit short holds; over months an ETF or a future is usually cheaper. CFDs aren't offered to US retail clients.\"],\"example\":\"10,000.00 of LABIX by CFD at 5.5% a year costs 271.23 over 180 days. An ETF's 0.10% fee is 4.93, or 152.88 counting 3% on the cash it ties up.\",\"related\":[\"overnight-financing\",\"leverage\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.5\"]},{\"id\":\"negative-balance-protection\",\"name\":\"Negative balance protection\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"A retail protection that stops an account going below zero; it doesn't limit losses before that point.\",\"body\":[\"For retail CFD clients in the EU and the UK, losses are limited to the money in the account: if a gap closes positions beyond zero, the balance is reset to zero and nothing is owed.\",\"It protects against debt, not against loss. Everything down to zero is still lost, professional clients don't get it, and many places don't require it. Sizing from a stop, not the protection, is what limits a loss.\"],\"example\":\"An account holding 1,000.00 is closed out after a weekend gap at a 1,400.00 loss. With the protection the balance stops at 0.00: 1,000.00 lost, 400.00 not owed.\",\"related\":[\"margin-close-out\",\"leverage\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.6\",\"2.14.8\"]},{\"id\":\"retail-regulation\",\"name\":\"Retail trading regulation\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The dated, per-jurisdiction rules on leverage, close-out, protections and day trading that shape retail trading.\",\"body\":[\"Since 2018 the EU caps retail CFD leverage at 30:1 on major pairs; 20:1 on non-major pairs, gold and major indices; 10:1 on other commodities and non-major indices; 5:1 on shares; 2:1 on crypto. It adds a 50% close-out, negative balance protection and a ban on bonuses. The UK made similar rules permanent in 2019.\",\"The US doesn't offer CFDs to retail clients and caps retail forex at 50:1 on major pairs. In 2026 it replaced the pattern day trader rule with an intraday margin standard, which brokers may phase in until late 2027. Rules change, so check them, and every broker's licence, at the regulator's own register.\"],\"example\":\"With 2,000.00 of margin in an EU retail account: 40,000.00 on a major index CFD (20:1), 10,000.00 on a share (5:1), 4,000.00 on crypto (2:1). In the US: no CFD at all.\",\"related\":[\"leverage\",\"margin-close-out\",\"negative-balance-protection\",\"broker-due-diligence\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"2.14.8\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-pool\",\"name\":\"Liquidity pool\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"A concentration of resting orders (limits, take-profits, stop-losses) at or near a price level.\",\"body\":[\"On a chart, 'liquidity' means orders waiting at a price. Limit entries and take-profits fill passively and absorb the flow that reaches them; stop-losses wait until price touches their trigger and then become market orders that add to the move.\",\"Pools plausibly form where many traders put orders for the same reasons: just beyond range edges and swing extremes, beyond prior-day highs and lows, and at or just past round numbers. That clustering is documented for round numbers (Osler, grade B). A chart never shows the real orders, only where they plausibly sit.\",\"Presence is not attraction. 'Price is drawn to liquidity' has no evidence behind it (grade D): orders wait where people put them, and price reaches them only if it gets there for other reasons.\"],\"example\":\"LABX ranges between 99.20 and 101.00. Longs bought near 99.25 keep sell stops at 99.10–99.19. If price falls there, those stops become market sells and can speed the fall; nothing says price must go there.\",\"related\":[\"stop-loss-order\",\"limit-order\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"round-numbers\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.1\"]},{\"id\":\"stop-clustering\",\"name\":\"Stop and take-profit clustering\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"Conditional orders bunch near round numbers: take-profits at the level, stop-losses just beyond it.\",\"body\":[\"In 9,667 conditional orders at one large FX bank, Osler found take-profits ending exactly in 00 far more often than stop-losses (9.3% against 4.4%). Stop-loss buys sat just above round numbers, 14.4% in xx01–xx10 against 7.4% just below, and stop-loss sells mirrored them just below.\",\"The two clusters act differently. Take-profits are passive limits that tend to stall price at the number; stop clusters, once triggered, add market orders and can speed a break up. Exchange rates spend less time at round numbers, consistent with stops pushing price through them. One bank and one market: grade B.\",\"For your own stop, the practical rule is to sit outside the crowded band and resize so the loss at the stop still fits the budget.\"],\"example\":\"Long at 100.60 with the stop at 99.98, inside the sell-stop band under 100.00. Moving it to 99.87 widens the stop from 0.62 to 0.73, so the size falls from 138 to 120 units to keep the loss, costs included, within 100.00.\",\"related\":[\"round-numbers\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"position-sizing\",\"cost-adjusted-sizing\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.2\"]},{\"id\":\"stop-cascade\",\"name\":\"Stop cascade\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"Triggered stops become market orders that push price into further stops, accelerating the move.\",\"body\":[\"A triggered stop is a market order. In a thin book it takes the nearest offers, then the next ones, so its fills drift away from the trigger: slippage. If those fills reach the next cluster's triggers, more market orders follow, and the move feeds itself.\",\"Osler (2005) describes stop-loss orders as positive-feedback trading that helps trends speed up through clusters and contributes to fat tails (grade B). The mechanism needs no one's intent: 'stop hunt' adds a motive the chart can't show.\"],\"example\":\"1,000 units of buy stops trigger at 100.01. They fill 300 at 100.02, 200 at 100.03, 250 at 100.05 and 250 at 100.08: an average of 100.04. The last fills reach the next cluster at 100.06–100.10.\",\"related\":[\"slippage\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"breakout\",\"round-numbers\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.3\",\"3.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-sweep\",\"name\":\"Liquidity sweep\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Price trades through a level where stops rest, then closes back inside; known only after the close.\",\"body\":[\"A sweep and a breakout start the same way: price trades through a level and sets off the stops beyond it. They differ only at the close. This course's rule: a close back inside within one bar is a sweep; a close beyond with follow-through is a breakout. Before the close, nothing on the chart decides it.\",\"The popular claim that a reversal follows a sweep is grade D: the one systematic test found, an unreviewed 2026 preprint on G10 FX, saw swept levels followed more often by continuation. So a sweep is a classified event, not a signal, and the plan, invalidation and size, is written before the bar closes.\"],\"example\":\"Yesterday's high is 101.00. A candle trades to 101.12 and closes at 100.94: a sweep. Another closes at 101.22 and the next at 101.40: a breakout. Mid-bar, both showed 101.08.\",\"related\":[\"false-breakout\",\"breakout\",\"invalidation-level\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"equal-highs-lows\",\"name\":\"Equal highs and lows\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Two or more swing extremes within a stated tolerance, beyond which stops plausibly rest.\",\"body\":[\"Equal highs are neighbouring swing highs at nearly the same price, with no higher high between them; equal lows mirror them. In classic terms they are double tops and bottoms. 'Nearly' needs a written tolerance, usually a fraction of ATR: the looser the rule, the more pairs it finds.\",\"Stops plausibly rest just beyond such obvious extremes (grade C, extrapolated from round-number data). The claim that equal highs 'will be swept' has no published probability (grade D). The test compares them with random levels at the same distance from price; on a random walk the two break about as often.\"],\"example\":\"ATR 0.60 and a 0.1 × ATR rule give a tolerance of 0.06. Highs at 101.48 and 101.52, 0.04 apart, are equal; at 0.05 × ATR, 0.03, they aren't.\",\"related\":[\"double-tops-and-bottoms\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"reference-levels\",\"name\":\"Reference levels\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Widely watched prices: prior-day and session highs and lows, opening prices and round numbers.\",\"body\":[\"Some prices are on almost every chart: the prior-day and prior-week high and low, the current session's high and low, the opening price and round numbers. Because platforms plot them automatically, many traders see the same lines.\",\"That shared view makes orders around them plausible, a coordination effect (grade C). The claim that price reacts to them at a fixed rate is grade D. As with any level, the test is against random levels at the same distance.\"],\"example\":\"Yesterday LABX ranged from 99.40 to 100.80; today opened at 100.30 and has reached 100.55. The plotted lines are 100.80, 100.55, 100.30, 100.00 and 99.40.\",\"related\":[\"support-and-resistance\",\"round-numbers\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"price-imbalance\",\"name\":\"Price imbalance\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A fast one-sided move that leaves prices with little two-sided trading; not the same as order-flow imbalance.\",\"body\":[\"'Imbalance' is used for two different things. Order-flow imbalance is more aggressive buying than selling at the best prices; it predicts price over the next few ticks, milliseconds to seconds (grade B), a horizon below a retail trader's costs.\",\"Price imbalance describes how price moved: a fast, one-sided run that left some prices with little two-sided trading, often visible as a three-candle gap. It describes the past. That price must return to 'balance' it is a separate claim, and on a null model it matches the base rate.\"],\"example\":\"A candle runs from 100.35 to 101.20 between a candle whose high is 100.40 and one whose low is 100.70. Prices from 100.40 to 100.70 traded only during that one fast candle.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"gap-types\",\"price-gaps\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"fair-value-gap\",\"name\":\"Fair value gap\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A three-candle gap between candle 1's extreme and candle 3's opposite extreme; its revisit rate matches the base rate.\",\"body\":[\"A bullish three-candle gap exists when the first candle's high is below the third candle's low; a bearish one when the first candle's low is above the third candle's high. The gap is the price range between them, and measuring it in ATR lets one filter work across calm and volatile markets.\",\"Identifying it is mechanical. The claim that gaps 'fill' is grade D as information: in a 1,000,000-bar random-walk simulation, 83.4% were revisited within 20 bars, against 83.5% for random levels at the same distance, and a first-touch trade won 50.2% against 50.1%.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 1 high 100.40, candle 2 from 100.35 to 101.20, candle 3 low 100.70: a 0.30 gap. With ATR 0.60, that's 0.5 ATR.\",\"related\":[\"price-gaps\",\"gap-types\",\"base-rates\",\"random-walk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-void\",\"name\":\"Liquidity void\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A price area traded quickly with little volume or time, such as a low-volume node or single prints.\",\"body\":[\"When price runs through an area fast, little volume and little time are spent there. Volume profile shows it as a low-volume node; Market Profile as single prints; a candle chart often as a three-candle gap.\",\"That the area was crossed fast is a fact about the past. That price must come back to 'repair' it is grade D. Such areas can matter for execution, since there were few resting orders there last time, but not as a forecast.\"],\"example\":\"A breakout candle crosses 100.40–100.70 in one bar. On the volume profile those prices show little volume. Price may revisit them, or not; nearby prices get revisited at the base rate either way.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"gap-types\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"zone-grading\",\"name\":\"Zone grading\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Rating supply and demand zones with written criteria before the session, then testing whether grades beat random zones.\",\"body\":[\"A zone grade turns judgment into checkable numbers: departure (the move away, in ATR), time at base (candles in the base), freshness (returns since it formed) and location (distance to a higher-timeframe level). Grades are locked before the session, so they can't be adjusted to the result.\",\"The test compares graded zones with random zones of the same width, because wider zones 'react' more often by width alone. In Osler (2000), firms' strength ratings on published levels added nothing. Zones drawn after the fact look far better than locked ones: that difference is hindsight, not edge.\"],\"example\":\"Locked before the session, 4 of 10 zones reacted: 40%. Redrawn after it, with failures dropped or moved onto the turns, 6 of 7 'reacted': 86%. The same rules, 46 points better on paper.\",\"related\":[\"supply-demand-zones\",\"hindsight-bias\",\"support-and-resistance\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"trapped-inventory\",\"name\":\"Trapped inventory\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The size and average price of positions caught in a failed move, whose stops and break-even exits shape later reactions.\",\"body\":[\"After a failed breakout, the traders who bought it hold positions with a size and an average price. They exit in two places: at their stops, where market orders can speed the move against them, and at break-even, where those without stops sell to get out flat and can stall a rally.\",\"The same mechanism explains role reversal: shorts caught by a broken resistance buy back near break-even on the retest, so buying meets the dip. Trapped traders as fuel is grade C, consistent with Osler's cascades but not tested directly; role reversal through break-even exits is grade C, with mixed evidence.\"],\"example\":\"Trapped buyers: 600 at 101.05, 500 at 101.10, 300 at 101.15, an average of 101.09. 900 have stops under 100.60; the other 500 wait for 101.09.\",\"related\":[\"trapped-traders\",\"stop-cascade\",\"role-reversal\",\"loss-aversion\",\"support-and-resistance\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"market-venue-types\",\"name\":\"Market structures\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Order-driven, quote-driven, brokered and hybrid ways of matching buyers and sellers.\",\"body\":[\"In an order-driven market, orders rest in a central limit order book and match by price, then time. In a quote-driven market, dealers quote a bid and an ask and customers trade against them. In a brokered market, a broker searches for the other side, usually for large blocks. Hybrids combine a central book with market makers that have quoting obligations.\",\"The structure tells you who your counterparty is: another trader on an exchange, the dealer in spot FX, the provider in a CFD.\"],\"example\":\"Spot FX on a retail platform is quote-driven: you buy at your broker's 100.05 ask, and the broker, or its liquidity provider, sells to you. Shares on an exchange match against another participant's order in the book.\",\"related\":[\"market-participants\",\"bid-ask-spread\",\"venue-fragmentation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.1\"]},{\"id\":\"venue-fragmentation\",\"name\":\"Venue fragmentation\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"One instrument trading on many venues, so no single book or feed shows the whole market.\",\"body\":[\"US stocks trade on more than a dozen exchanges, in dark pools and at internalisers, and over a third of volume trades off-exchange. Spot FX has no central book at all, so no consolidated volume exists; retail FX charts show tick volume at one broker.\",\"A screen that shows one venue isn't the market. Routing across venues can find better prices than any one of them offers.\"],\"example\":\"Venue A quotes 99.97 / 100.07 and venue B 99.95 / 100.05. Together the best prices are 99.97 / 100.05, a 0.08 spread. Buying 300 units across both costs 30,018.50, against 30,023.00 on A alone.\",\"related\":[\"market-venue-types\",\"nbbo\",\"volume\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.1\"]},{\"id\":\"limit-order-book\",\"name\":\"Limit order book (L1/L2/L3)\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Resting bids and offers by price: best quotes (L1), size per level (L2) or every order (L3).\",\"body\":[\"Level 1 shows the best bid, the best ask and their sizes. Level 2 adds the total size at every price. Level 3 shows each order separately, with its place in the queue.\",\"Displayed size is an intention, not a commitment: it can be cancelled at any moment. Placing orders you intend to cancel before they fill, to mislead others, is spoofing, and it is illegal.\"],\"example\":\"Best bid 99.95 for 150 units, best ask 100.05 for 120 (L1). Behind the ask, 200 at 100.06 and 300 at 100.08 (L2): 1,820 units offered within 12 ticks.\",\"related\":[\"order-queue\",\"market-depth\",\"limit-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"market-depth\",\"name\":\"Market depth\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Size available near the best prices; it decides how far a market order walks the book.\",\"body\":[\"A market order larger than the best level fills across several prices, so its average price is worse than the best quote. The thinner the book, the further it walks.\",\"Depth is not fixed. Liquidity providers pull size before news and in stress (grade B), so the depth you saw minutes ago may not be there when you trade.\"],\"example\":\"A 500-unit market buy takes 120 at 100.05, 200 at 100.06 and 180 at 100.08: average 100.0648, 7.40 of slippage. After 70% of the depth is pulled, the same order reaches 100.17: average 100.1104, 30.18 of slippage.\",\"related\":[\"limit-order-book\",\"slippage\",\"news-liquidity\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-dimensions\",\"name\":\"Liquidity dimensions\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Tightness, depth, resiliency and immediacy: four separate ways a market can be liquid or not.\",\"body\":[\"Tightness is the spread. Depth is the size near the best prices. Resiliency is how fast the book refills after a large order. Immediacy is how quickly an order can execute.\",\"A market can be strong on one and weak on another: tight but thin, or deep but slow to refill. Check all four before a large order.\"],\"example\":\"A book quotes 0.10 wide with 120 units at the best ask (tight, thin). After a 500-unit buy clears three levels, it takes minutes to refill (low resiliency).\",\"related\":[\"market-depth\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"market-making\",\"name\":\"Market making\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Quoting both sides to earn the spread while carrying inventory and adverse-selection risk.\",\"body\":[\"A market maker's P&L splits into three parts: the spread it captures on each fill, the losses to better-informed traders (adverse selection) and the gains or losses on the inventory it holds.\",\"To manage these risks, market makers widen quotes when informed flow is likely, skew both quotes to shed inventory, and pull them before news. Many today are proprietary high-frequency firms.\"],\"example\":\"60 fills of 100 units at a 0.05 half-spread capture 300.00. Eight fills came just before a 0.30 move against the maker: −240.00. Ending flat, the net is 60.00 before commissions.\",\"related\":[\"adverse-selection\",\"bid-ask-spread\",\"news-liquidity\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"adverse-selection\",\"name\":\"Adverse selection\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Losses a liquidity provider takes when better-informed traders fill its quotes just before price moves.\",\"body\":[\"Anyone who rests an order gives the market a free option: others trade with it when it suits them. Fills cluster on the moves that continue through the order's price, and the moves that turn just before it are missed.\",\"Market makers price this risk into the spread. Retail traders meet it every time a resting limit fills: track the average move one minute after your passive fills.\"],\"example\":\"A buy limit at 99.90. On day A the ask falls through it to 99.50: filled, 0.40 a unit down. On day B the ask stops at 99.91 and LABX rallies to 100.40: no fill.\",\"related\":[\"market-making\",\"limit-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"queue-priority\",\"name\":\"Queue priority\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Matching rules (FIFO, pro-rata) that decide which resting orders at one price fill first, and what resets your place.\",\"body\":[\"Under FIFO, or price-time priority, orders at one price fill in the order they arrived, so the size ahead of you must trade before you fill. Under pro-rata, incoming volume is shared in proportion to size.\",\"Raising an order's size, changing its price or moving it to another account usually sends it to the back of the queue; cutting the size usually doesn't. Each venue publishes its own rules.\"],\"example\":\"340 units ahead of your 100 at 100.25, and 400 trade: FIFO fills 60 of yours. Under pro-rata, with 900 units bid, you'd get 400 × 100 ÷ 900 = 44.\",\"related\":[\"order-queue\",\"limit-order\",\"limit-order-book\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.4\"]},{\"id\":\"hidden-liquidity\",\"name\":\"Hidden liquidity\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Size not shown in the book: icebergs, hidden and pegged orders, and off-exchange liquidity.\",\"body\":[\"Icebergs show a slice and refill it, hidden orders show nothing, midpoint pegs wait between bid and ask, and dark pools display nothing before execution. Each gives up something: priority, price certainty or the chance of a fill.\",\"Hidden size is real liquidity you can't see; displayed size can be liquidity that isn't there. The book is never the whole picture.\"],\"example\":\"The book shows 50 units at 100.00, yet 600 trade there and 50 still show: an iceberg refilling from a hidden reserve.\",\"related\":[\"iceberg-order\",\"limit-order-book\",\"queue-priority\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"iceberg-order\",\"name\":\"Iceberg order\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"An order that shows only a slice of its size and refills the slice from a hidden reserve.\",\"body\":[\"A native iceberg is held by the exchange, which refills the slice at once. A synthetic iceberg is run by a trading platform, which sends a new order after each fill, so the exchange sees separate small orders with short gaps between them.\",\"Either way, each refill joins the back of the queue: showing less hides your size but costs priority every time.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 1,000 at 99.95 with a display quantity of 100: 10 slices, 9 refills, and 9 trips to the back of the queue.\",\"related\":[\"hidden-liquidity\",\"queue-priority\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"maker-taker\",\"name\":\"Maker-taker fees\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Fee schedules that pay rebates to resting orders and charge orders that take liquidity.\",\"body\":[\"A venue with maker-taker pricing pays a small rebate when a resting order fills and charges a fee when an order trades on arrival. Post-only orders guarantee the maker side, at the risk of being rejected.\",\"Over many round trips the difference adds up, but resting to earn the rebate brings the risk of not filling at all.\"],\"example\":\"Rebate 0.002, fee 0.003 a unit. 1,000 units in passively and out aggressively: +2.00 − 3.00 = −1.00. Both aggressive: −6.00. Both passive: +4.00, if both fill.\",\"related\":[\"trading-costs\",\"limit-order\",\"market-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"broker-models\",\"name\":\"Broker execution models\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Agency/STP, ECN, B-book and hybrid ways a broker handles client orders and the risk they create.\",\"body\":[\"Agency or STP brokers pass orders to outside liquidity providers for a commission or markup. An ECN matches many participants in one book. A B-book broker takes the other side, so client losses are its revenue. Hybrids B-book most clients and pass some on.\",\"Regulation manages the B-book conflict rather than banning it. Claims that B-books must hunt stops have no evidence (grade D); execution data is what you can check.\"],\"example\":\"30 scalps of 100 units: STP 10.00 a round trip, B-book 14.00, ECN 8.00. Over 30 trades: 300.00, 420.00 and 240.00.\",\"related\":[\"last-look\",\"broker-due-diligence\",\"leverage\",\"margin-close-out\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"last-look\",\"name\":\"Last look\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A liquidity provider's short window to accept or reject a trade at its quoted price.\",\"body\":[\"During the window the price can move either way. If rejects happen only when it moved in the client's favour, the dealer keeps the gains and passes on the losses.\",\"The FX Global Code's principle 17 says liquidity providers shouldn't trade on the information in a client's request during the window. Read your reject and slippage reports for asymmetry.\"],\"example\":\"120 requests: when price moved the client's way, 18 of 63 were rejected; when it moved the dealer's way, all 57 filled.\",\"related\":[\"broker-models\",\"slippage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"nbbo\",\"name\":\"National best bid and offer\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The best bid and offer across all US exchanges for a stock, taken from the consolidated feed.\",\"body\":[\"Each exchange shows only its own quotes. The NBBO takes the highest bid and the lowest offer among them, and brokers measure fills against it.\",\"The consolidated feed (SIP) is a little slower than exchanges' direct feeds, so a quote on screen can already be stale.\"],\"example\":\"X quotes 99.96 / 100.10, Y 100.00 / 100.08 and Z 99.98 / 100.06. The NBBO is 100.00 / 100.06: 0.06 wide.\",\"related\":[\"venue-fragmentation\",\"best-execution\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"payment-for-order-flow\",\"name\":\"Payment for order flow\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Payments a broker receives for routing client orders to a wholesaler or a venue.\",\"body\":[\"Most US retail market orders go to wholesalers that fill them from their own books, often with small price improvement, and pay the broker for the flow. It is how many zero-commission brokers earn.\",\"Whether PFOF helps or hurts retail traders overall is contested (grade C). It is legal in the US but can't compromise best execution; the EU has banned it, with exemptions only until 30 June 2026.\"],\"example\":\"NBBO 100.00 / 100.06. A wholesaler fills 200 units at 100.05, 2.00 of price improvement, and pays the broker 0.002 a unit: 0.40.\",\"related\":[\"best-execution\",\"trading-costs\",\"nbbo\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"best-execution\",\"name\":\"Best execution\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A broker's duty to seek the most favourable terms reasonably available for each client order.\",\"body\":[\"Best execution is a duty of reasonable diligence, not a guaranteed price. In the US it requires a regular and rigorous review of execution quality, at least quarterly (FINRA 5310), and disclosure of conflicts doesn't satisfy it.\",\"Execution-quality reports show price improvement, fill rate and speed. Compare brokers on net cost: improvement minus commission.\"],\"example\":\"Broker A: no commission, 8.00 of improvement on 4,000 units. Broker B: 20.00 of commission, 36.00 of improvement, 16.00 net. B is better despite the commission.\",\"related\":[\"nbbo\",\"payment-for-order-flow\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.7\"]},{\"id\":\"call-auction\",\"name\":\"Call auction\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Orders gather without matching, then execute at one price that maximises traded volume.\",\"body\":[\"During the call period, exchanges publish the indicative price, the paired volume and the imbalance. At the uncross, all orders that can match trade at one price: where the most units change hands.\",\"Opening and closing auctions set official prices; index funds trade at the close, so it carries a large share of daily volume. Halted markets also reopen through an auction, and stops can trigger at the reopening price.\"],\"example\":\"Volumes that can trade: 300 at 99.90, 600 at 100.00, 900 at 100.10, 600 at 100.20, 400 at 100.30. The uncross is 100.10, with 900 units.\",\"related\":[\"auction-imbalance\",\"market-as-auction\",\"market-hours\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.8\"]},{\"id\":\"auction-imbalance\",\"name\":\"Auction imbalance\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Unmatched buy or sell quantity at the indicative auction price, published before the cross.\",\"body\":[\"An imbalance message tells you which side has more orders than can match at the indicative price, and by how much. Orders on the other side, at the indicative price or better, reduce it without moving the price.\",\"Adding to the imbalance side does the opposite: more orders chase the same counterparties and can push the price against you.\"],\"example\":\"Indicative 100.10, paired 900, imbalance 100 to buy. A sell limit of 100 at 100.10 brings the cross to 1,000 units at 100.10, with no imbalance.\",\"related\":[\"call-auction\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.2.8\"]},{\"id\":\"tape-reading\",\"name\":\"Tape reading\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Reading time-and-sales prints (price, size, aggressor, pace) to describe urgency, not to forecast direction.\",\"body\":[\"The tape lists every trade: time, price, size and, when the feed flags it, the side that crossed the spread. Every unit traded had a buyer and a seller, so size alone has no direction; the aggressor tells you which side was in a hurry.\",\"Pace, the number of prints per second, shows urgency. As a forecast of direction it is a heuristic (grade D): over 20 rounds on a random-walk control, following the pace scored 11 of 20, inside the 6 to 14 range a coin flip gives about 96% of the time.\"],\"example\":\"Bid 99.95, ask 100.05: 300 at 100.05 is buyer-initiated, 500 at 99.95 seller-initiated, and 5,000 at 100.00 can't be classified from the quotes.\",\"related\":[\"trade-classification\",\"bid-ask-spread\",\"volume\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.1\"]},{\"id\":\"trade-classification\",\"name\":\"Trade classification\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Labelling each trade buyer- or seller-initiated, by exchange flag or by rules that misclassify a share of trades.\",\"body\":[\"Some feeds flag the aggressor. Others leave software to guess: the quote rule compares the print with the bid and ask, and Lee–Ready falls back on the tick rule for midpoint prints. Validation studies report accuracy of roughly 75–90% (grade B), worse with fast quotes and hidden or midpoint fills.\",\"Every order-flow tool built on guessed sides, delta, CVD and footprints among them, inherits the error. In spot FX there is no central tape, so retail volume is usually tick volume from one broker.\"],\"example\":\"Over 20 prints, the tick rule disagreed with the engine 4 times, a 20% error: two sells on upticks, one buy on a downtick and one buy at an unchanged price.\",\"related\":[\"tape-reading\",\"bid-ask-spread\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.1\"]},{\"id\":\"depth-of-market\",\"name\":\"Depth of market (DOM)\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A price ladder of resting size, recent trades and working orders that changes as orders are added and pulled.\",\"body\":[\"A DOM ladder shows the size waiting at each price, the last trades beside it and your own orders. Displayed size behaves in four visible ways: pulling, stacking, reloading and absorption.\",\"What the ladder can't show is intent. Displayed size is free to cancel, so reading direction from it is grade D; executed volume is a record. A pulled order isn't automatically spoofing: cancellations are a normal part of trading.\"],\"example\":\"A 2,000-unit offer at 100.20, ten times the usual size, was cancelled with price at 100.17 before a unit traded there. Price went on to 100.28.\",\"related\":[\"order-queue\",\"tape-reading\",\"absorption\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.2\"]},{\"id\":\"footprint-chart\",\"name\":\"Footprint chart\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A bar split by price level that shows volume traded at the bid and at the ask.\",\"body\":[\"Each row of a footprint bar shows bid volume (aggressive sells) × ask volume (aggressive buys) at one price. Bar delta is ask minus bid; the bar's POC is the row with the most volume.\",\"A footprint needs every trade with its aggressor; it can't be rebuilt from OHLCV, and on feeds without the flag it inherits classification errors. A candle that closes higher can still hide negative delta.\"],\"example\":\"A bar from 100.00 to 100.05 traded 505 at the ask and 210 at the bid: delta +295, POC 100.04 with 180 units. The next bar closed 0.03 higher with delta −145.\",\"related\":[\"footprint-imbalance\",\"volume-profile\",\"trade-classification\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"footprint-imbalance\",\"name\":\"Footprint imbalance\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Ask volume at one price far above bid volume one tick lower, or the reverse, often at 3:1.\",\"body\":[\"A buy diagonal imbalance compares the ask at a price with the bid one tick lower; a sell imbalance compares the bid with the ask one tick higher. Three or more in a row on the same side form a stacked imbalance.\",\"The count depends on the threshold you pick, and the claim that stacked imbalances become support is a practitioner heuristic (grade D). An imbalance describes what happened, not what must happen next; test it against random zones of the same width.\"],\"example\":\"Ratios of 3.14, 6 and 6 at 100.02–100.04 stack at 3:1. At 4:1 only two remain and the stack disappears.\",\"related\":[\"footprint-chart\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"cumulative-volume-delta\",\"name\":\"Cumulative volume delta\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Running sum of aggressive buy minus aggressive sell volume from a chosen anchor.\",\"body\":[\"CVD adds each bar's delta to a running total. Its level depends on the anchor, a session open or a date long ago, so only changes are comparable. Unlike OBV, which signs a bar's whole volume by its close, CVD signs each trade by its aggressor.\",\"Delta measures aggression, not the book. Price can rise on flat or negative delta when sellers cancel their offers, and most platform CVDs are approximations that accumulate classification errors.\"],\"example\":\"Deltas +295, +120, −80, +60, −40 give CVD 295, 415, 335, 395, 355. A later rally of 0.30 came with deltas −10, −20, −10 while 1,900 units of offers were cancelled.\",\"related\":[\"footprint-chart\",\"cumulative-volume-indicators\",\"delta-divergence\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.4\"]},{\"id\":\"delta-divergence\",\"name\":\"Delta divergence\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Price makes a new extreme while cumulative delta does not; a fragile move, not a reversal signal.\",\"body\":[\"A delta divergence says a new high (or low) came with less aggression behind it. It is grade D as a signal: many such moves keep going. Price has to confirm, for example by breaking the last swing low.\",\"Check the anchor too. A divergence that crosses a session reset can appear under a session anchor and vanish under a continuous one; inside one session, both anchors agree.\"],\"example\":\"Bar 4's high of 101.10 beat bar 2's 100.80 while CVD reached 395 against 415: a divergence, with no reversal until price confirms.\",\"related\":[\"cumulative-volume-delta\",\"divergence\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.4\"]},{\"id\":\"absorption\",\"name\":\"Absorption\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Heavy aggressive volume at a level that fails to move price because passive orders soak it up.\",\"body\":[\"Absorption is Wyckoff's effort versus result read at one price: lots of aggressive volume, little progress. Often the passive side refills, as an iceberg does.\",\"It describes what has happened, not how much size remains, so it is a grade D heuristic. Every read needs an invalidation: if the level breaks, the absorption failed.\"],\"example\":\"Sellers traded 1,800 units into the 99.50 bid in six minutes, delta −1,500, while price stayed between 99.50 and 99.52. Trades below 99.48 would invalidate the read.\",\"related\":[\"order-flow-exhaustion\",\"invalidation-level\",\"depth-of-market\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.5\",\"3.3.6\"]},{\"id\":\"order-flow-exhaustion\",\"name\":\"Order-flow exhaustion\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Aggressive volume drying up at the extreme of a move.\",\"body\":[\"Exhaustion shows as a new high on thin volume and little delta, or a new low with selling fading. It describes aggression fading at the extreme; as a reversal signal it is grade D.\",\"The read dies when aggression returns: a new high with rising delta is momentum, not exhaustion.\"],\"example\":\"Five rally bars traded 820 to 1,110 units; the sixth made a new high at 101.12 on 340 units and delta +30.\",\"related\":[\"absorption\",\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\",\"invalidation-level\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.5\"]},{\"id\":\"spoofing\",\"name\":\"Spoofing and layering (illegal)\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Placing orders with intent to cancel before execution to mislead others about supply or demand.\",\"body\":[\"US futures law, as amended by Dodd-Frank, bans bidding or offering with the intent to cancel before execution; EU market-abuse rules treat misleading orders as manipulation. Genuine orders at several prices, legitimate layering, are legal.\",\"Surveillance looks for a signature: large orders away from the touch, constant modification, a very high cancel-to-fill ratio and trades on the opposite side. No single statistic proves it; one pulled order seen from outside proves nothing.\"],\"example\":\"Historical case, CFTC v. Sarao: on 6 May 2010 a program's orders were modified more than 81,000 times with 81 lots executed. He paid about $38.6 million and pleaded guilty to spoofing and wire fraud.\",\"related\":[\"depth-of-market\",\"market-participants\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.7\"]},{\"id\":\"market-profile\",\"name\":\"Market Profile (TPO)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A distribution of time at price from 30-minute periods, with point of control, value area and initial balance.\",\"body\":[\"Each 30-minute period that trades at a price adds one letter, a TPO. The row with most letters is the POC; the value area grows from it, adding the larger neighbouring row until it holds 70% of the TPOs.\",\"Day types and the 80% rule are practitioner lore (grade D). One day's profile is noisy, time at price isn't volume at price, and day types are partly subjective; test the claims against random bands and the random-walk control.\"],\"example\":\"A 13-period session gives 42 TPOs. POC 100.5 with 8; adding 100.6, 100.4, 100.7 and 100.3 reaches 30, past 29.4: value area 100.3–100.7.\",\"related\":[\"volume-profile\",\"initial-balance\",\"auction-market-theory\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.8\"]},{\"id\":\"initial-balance\",\"name\":\"Initial balance\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The price range of the first hour of the regular session.\",\"body\":[\"In Market Profile the initial balance is the range of the first two 30-minute periods, A and B. Practitioners classify days by how far price extends beyond it: little on a normal day, one way on a trend day, both ways on a neutral day.\",\"Classifying the day from the initial balance is grade D, and it overlaps with the opening range from 2.9.\"],\"example\":\"Periods A (100.30–100.70) and B (100.10–100.40) give an initial balance of 100.10–100.70. Later extensions to 100.00 and 101.00, with a close at 100.40, make a neutral day.\",\"related\":[\"market-profile\",\"opening-range-breakout\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.8\"]},{\"id\":\"auction-market-theory\",\"name\":\"Auction market theory\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Price moves to find trade: balance means rotation inside value, imbalance means a search for new value.\",\"body\":[\"Auction market theory reads each session as an auction. In balance, price rotates inside the value area; in imbalance, it leaves value to search for a new one, and a new value area forms.\",\"It's a useful vocabulary for describing a session. Its trading rules, such as the 80% rule, are practitioner lore without robust tests (grade D).\"],\"example\":\"A session breaks its initial balance on both sides, then closes at 100.40, inside its 100.3–100.7 value area: balance, a neutral day.\",\"related\":[\"market-profile\",\"market-as-auction\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.3.8\"]},{\"id\":\"method-trial\",\"name\":\"Method trial\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A pre-registered test of a method's claim against a base rate, the R0 control and costs.\",\"body\":[\"A trial turns a claim into a testable statement (rule, event, outcome, horizon, comparison), fixes the parameters and sample size in advance, then runs it twice: on R0, a random walk where no edge can exist, and on a market where the claimed mechanism is present. Costs come off before any verdict, and the result earns an evidence grade.\",\"The order matters. Rules written after the data are fitted to it; a result without the R0 run can't be told apart from the patterns any noise produces.\"],\"example\":\"A head-and-shoulders detector found 3,091 patterns in 10,000 R0 paths. 50.0% closed lower 10 candles after the break, against 49.7% for every candle: the shape appears in noise, the edge doesn't.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"evidence-grades\",\"random-walk\",\"falsifiability\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.1\"]},{\"id\":\"falsifiability\",\"name\":\"Falsifiability\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A claim is testable only if some possible result could show it is wrong.\",\"body\":[\"A falsifiable claim says in advance which outcome would count against it. Escape clauses (relabel the count, the market wasn't 'ready', smart money changed its mind) absorb every result, so the claim can never fail and never be tested.\",\"Degrees of freedom work the same way: anchors, tolerances and alternate labels chosen after the fact let almost any outcome 'fit'.\"],\"example\":\"'A 1-ATR reversal within 20 candles happens more often near 61.8% than near 55%' is falsifiable. 'Wave 3 will be strong, unless the count needs relabelling' is not.\",\"related\":[\"method-trial\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.1\"]},{\"id\":\"fibonacci-clusters\",\"name\":\"Fibonacci projections and clusters\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Three-point projections and overlapping levels from several swings; with enough swings, clusters always exist.\",\"body\":[\"A projection adds a ratio of the A-to-B move to C: C + k × (B − A). A cluster is where levels from several grids, or projections, overlap.\",\"Clusters are guaranteed once enough lines are drawn, so their existence is not evidence. In the lab, Fibonacci levels did no better than control ratios on R0, and only about a point better where 30% of agents traded them.\"],\"example\":\"A = 100.00, B = 110.00, C = 104.00: the 0.618, 1.0 and 1.618 projections are 110.18, 114.00 and 120.18. Grids from 101.50–108.00 and 101.50–110.00 both put a level at 104.75.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-levels\",\"confluence\",\"anchor-selection\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.2\"]},{\"id\":\"anchor-selection\",\"name\":\"Anchor selection\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Choosing which swings to measure from; more candidate anchors make some level fit almost any past turn.\",\"body\":[\"Each pair of swing points gives a new grid of levels. Picking the pair after the turn is hindsight: with enough pairs, one of them always has a level close by.\",\"A fair test fixes the anchor rule mechanically, in advance: for example, swings confirmed by 5 candles on each side and the last finished leg only.\"],\"example\":\"Two lows (100.00, 101.50) and two highs (108.00, 110.00) give 20 levels. One grid puts 26% of prices from 102.00 to 108.00 within 0.15 of a level; four grids put 68% there.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-levels\",\"hindsight-bias\",\"fibonacci-clusters\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.2\"]},{\"id\":\"elliott-wave\",\"name\":\"Elliott Wave Principle\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A framework that labels trends as five-wave impulses and three-wave corrections under three hard rules.\",\"body\":[\"Three rules must hold: wave 2 never retraces more than 100% of wave 1; wave 3 is never the shortest of 1, 3 and 5; wave 4 never overlaps wave 1, except in diagonals. Alternation, extension and Fibonacci ratios are only guidelines.\",\"The rules are definitions and need no test. The forecasts are graded D: several valid counts usually fit the same chart, and a failed count can be relabelled.\"],\"example\":\"Waves of 100.00 → 104.00 → 101.60 → 108.00 → 105.80 → 109.00: wave 2 retraces 60% of wave 1, wave 3 is 160% of wave 1, wave 4 stays above 104.00. A wave 4 at 103.50 would break the count.\",\"related\":[\"trend\",\"invalidation-level\",\"fibonacci-levels\",\"elliott-alternate-count\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.3\"]},{\"id\":\"elliott-alternate-count\",\"name\":\"Alternate wave counts\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Several valid wave counts of the same path, often with opposite forecasts.\",\"body\":[\"Because waves nest and corrections take many shapes, most paths admit more than one valid count. Whatever happens next, one of them 'called' it, which makes the forecasts close to unfalsifiable.\",\"What a count can defend is its invalidation: the price at which its own rules break. That gives a stop and, from the stop, a position size.\"],\"example\":\"One R0 path (104.05, 100.00, 102.38, 100.50, now 101.65) fit a bullish count (wrong below 100.00), a bearish one (wrong above 104.05) and a triangle (wrong above 102.38). A long at 101.70 with a stop at 99.90 sizes to 52 units on a 100.00 budget.\",\"related\":[\"elliott-wave\",\"falsifiability\",\"invalidation-level\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.4\"]},{\"id\":\"wyckoff-method\",\"name\":\"Wyckoff method\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Reading trading ranges as accumulation or distribution through events, phases and three laws.\",\"body\":[\"An accumulation range starts with a selling climax and an automatic rally that set its low and high, then a secondary test; a spring, a sign of strength and a last point of support are supposed to end it. The three laws are supply and demand, cause and effect, and effort versus result.\",\"The schematics are graded D as forecasts: labels are easy to place afterwards. The Composite Man is a study device, not evidence of manipulation. The parts that hold up, relative strength and a reward of at least three times the risk, match better-tested ideas.\"],\"example\":\"A generated range: selling climax at 95.00 on the heaviest volume (4,500), automatic rally to 98.00, secondary test at 95.40, spring to 94.40 closing at 95.30, sign of strength to 98.80.\",\"related\":[\"range\",\"wyckoff-spring\",\"volume-analysis\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.5\"]},{\"id\":\"wyckoff-spring\",\"name\":\"Spring and upthrust\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A break beyond a range edge that closes back inside, usually named only in hindsight.\",\"body\":[\"At the close of the dip, a spring and the start of a breakdown look the same. The name is given after the rally; a dip that keeps falling is called a breakdown instead, so a journal of 'springs' only ever shows successes.\",\"Its base rate comes from the distances: on a random walk, the nearer edge gets hit first more often.\"],\"example\":\"In a 95.00–98.00 range, a dip to 94.40 closes at 95.30. On R0, price reached 98.00 before 94.40 in 25.6% of 20,000 paths; 74.4% broke 94.40 first.\",\"related\":[\"wyckoff-method\",\"false-breakout\",\"base-rates\",\"hindsight-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.5\"]},{\"id\":\"harmonic-patterns\",\"name\":\"Harmonic patterns\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Five-point XABCD structures whose legs must meet Fibonacci ratios, ending in a potential reversal zone.\",\"body\":[\"The Gartley, Bat, Butterfly and Crab each fix ratios for B and D against XA, and for CD against BC. Every ratio comes with an allowed range and a tolerance, which the analyst chooses.\",\"Graded D: no peer-reviewed evidence that the ratios beat chance. On random walks, loosening the tolerance multiplies detections, and reversals at D matched random depths on both R0 and a mean-reverting market.\"],\"example\":\"X 100.00, A 110.00, B 103.82 (0.618), C 107.64, D 102.14 (0.786); CD ÷ BC = 5.50 ÷ 3.82 = 1.44. On 400 R0 charts, detections rose from 29 at ±1% to 1,026 at ±10%.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-levels\",\"fibonacci-clusters\",\"measured-move\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.6\"]},{\"id\":\"ichimoku-system\",\"name\":\"Ichimoku trading system\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The full Ichimoku rule set (cloud filter, Tenkan-Kijun trigger, Chikou confirmation, Kijun exit) tested as one system.\",\"body\":[\"Written mechanically: enter long when price is above the cloud, Tenkan crosses above Kijun and Chikou is above the price 26 candles back; stop just under Kijun; exit on the first close below Kijun. Every line must be read as of the candle.\",\"Trend following as a family has grade A evidence over months; Ichimoku's own signals are grade D. All five lines come from the same highs, lows and closes, so they are not independent confirmations.\"],\"example\":\"Over 300 runs per market, after costs: on a trend market the full system earned 15.7 per 1,000 candles against 16.8 for a plain Kijun cross; on a range market it lost 11.9 against 37.8, mainly by trading far less.\",\"related\":[\"ichimoku-basics\",\"volatility-channels\",\"look-ahead-bias\",\"trend-following\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.7\"]},{\"id\":\"gann-methods\",\"name\":\"Gann methods\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Angles, squares and time cycles from W. D. Gann; partly esoteric and dependent on chart scale.\",\"body\":[\"Gann's tools include the 1×1 and other angles, the Square of Nine and anniversary dates, with a basis described as geometry, astronomy, astrology and time cycles. There is no audited record of his trading.\",\"Angles depend on the chart's scale; the Square of Nine states no testable mechanism. Grade D. The useful habit is to ask what result would show a claim is wrong.\"],\"example\":\"From a 100.00 low, the 1×1 at 0.10 per candle touches lows at 101.00, 102.20 and 103.40. At 0.05 per candle it touches only 102.15; at 0.20, only 101.00.\",\"related\":[\"scale-dependence\",\"falsifiability\",\"spurious-cycles\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.8\"]},{\"id\":\"scale-dependence\",\"name\":\"Scale dependence\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A drawn angle changes meaning when the chart's price or time scale changes.\",\"body\":[\"A 45° line on screen rises one price unit per time unit, and the unit is whatever the chart's scaling makes it. Stretch or squeeze the axes and a different line looks like 45°, touching different candles.\",\"Any tool drawn as an angle or a curve (Gann angles, Fibonacci fans and arcs) must fix its ratio in price per candle before it can be tested.\"],\"example\":\"The same chart: at 0.10 per candle the 1×1 touches three lows; stretched twice as wide, the 45° line rises 0.05 per candle and misses all three.\",\"related\":[\"gann-methods\",\"log-scale\",\"trendlines-and-channels\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.8\"]},{\"id\":\"spurious-cycles\",\"name\":\"Spurious cycles\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Periodic-looking patterns that spectral analysis finds even in pure noise.\",\"body\":[\"Spectral analysis always reports a strongest frequency. On a random walk, that peak mostly reflects the window: longer windows report longer 'dominant cycles'.\",\"A cycle claim needs the trial protocol: a pre-registered period, an R0 run for the base rate, and an out-of-sample check. Most published cycles and seasonal patterns are graded D.\"],\"example\":\"On one R0 path the strongest cycle was 16 candles in the last 64, 43 in the last 128 and 128 in all 256. Across 3,000 R0 windows of 128 candles, the top cycle was 64 or 43 candles 82% of the time.\",\"related\":[\"random-walk\",\"base-rates\",\"gann-methods\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.4.8\"]},{\"id\":\"smart-money-concepts\",\"name\":\"Smart money concepts (SMC/ICT)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Retail vocabulary that renames liquidity, structure and zone ideas around a story of institutional intent.\",\"body\":[\"ICT, the \\\"Inner Circle Trader\\\" mentorship, and the wider SMC movement that repackaged it, give new names to old ideas: stop clustering, Dow structure, supply and demand zones, role reversal, gaps, Fibonacci and session timing. There is no official rulebook; definitions vary by author, year and indicator vendor.\",\"For each term, ask three things: what mechanical fact sits behind it, which classical concept it renames, and what testable claim it makes. The vocabulary is worth knowing because it is everywhere. The story of a single \\\"smart money\\\" steering price is grade D, and the method as a whole has no peer-reviewed support.\"],\"example\":\"\\\"Smart money swept the sell-side liquidity, then delivered price to the order block\\\" decodes to: price ran the stops under a swing low, then rallied back to the base of a demand zone. Only the stops are documented; the intent is not.\",\"related\":[\"claim-layers\",\"intent-attribution\",\"evidence-grades\",\"market-participants\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"claim-layers\",\"name\":\"Mechanics, tendencies, narratives\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Sorting a claim into market mechanics, measured statistical tendencies or untestable stories.\",\"body\":[\"Mechanics are true by construction: a buy market order fills at the ask, a triggered stop becomes a market order. Tendencies are measured rates with exceptions: stops cluster beyond round numbers, volatility rises at session opens. Narratives are stories about intent: \\\"institutions defended the zone\\\".\",\"Each layer is handled differently. Rely on mechanics, test tendencies against their base rate and sample size, and treat narratives as vocabulary, because a story that fits every outcome predicts none.\"],\"example\":\"\\\"Stops above the highs got triggered and price jumped 0.20\\\" is mechanics plus a measurable tendency. \\\"They engineered that liquidity\\\" adds a narrative nobody can check.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"evidence-grades\",\"smart-money-concepts\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"intent-attribution\",\"name\":\"Intent attribution\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Explaining a price move by what \\\"they\\\" intended, which a chart cannot show.\",\"body\":[\"Any outcome can be narrated afterwards: if a level holds, \\\"institutions defended it\\\"; if it breaks, \\\"they were inducing liquidity\\\". An explanation that fits every outcome cannot be wrong, so it cannot predict.\",\"Intent attribution also hurts reviews. \\\"I was the liquidity\\\" turns a loss into a story about others, and skips the questions that help: was the stop where the plan said, was the size right, was the setup in the rules?\"],\"example\":\"A level at 100.00 holds on Monday and breaks on Thursday. One video calls Monday a defence and Thursday a stop hunt. Neither story said in advance which would happen.\",\"related\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"claim-layers\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"break-of-structure\",\"name\":\"Break of structure (BOS)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC name for a with-trend break of the last swing: Dow theory's new high or low.\",\"body\":[\"In an uptrend, a BOS is a break above the latest swing high; in a downtrend, below the latest swing low. It is Dow's continuation, the next higher high or lower low.\",\"Every detector makes three choices: the swing length N, which swing counts as the level, and whether a close or only a wick breaks it. Swings are confirmed only N candles later, so every label lags. Different choices give different labels on the same candles.\"],\"example\":\"With N = 3 and the close rule, a 60-candle chart printed BOS labels at candles 20 and 36, where closes cleared the latest swing highs at 100.67 and 100.96.\",\"related\":[\"dow-theory\",\"swing-points\",\"change-of-character\",\"trend\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.2\"]},{\"id\":\"change-of-character\",\"name\":\"Change of character (CHoCH)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC name for the first counter-trend break of the last protected swing.\",\"body\":[\"In an uptrend, a CHoCH is the first break below the last higher low: the structure break of 1.5 and Dow's first warning that a trend may be failing. MSS adds a failed breakout before it, the classical 1-2-3 reversal.\",\"The label depends on N and on the close or wick rule: one 60-candle chart had 3 CHoCH labels at N = 2, 2 at N = 3, 1 at N = 5, and 4 under the wick rule at N = 3. As a reversal signal it grades D: on a random walk, the next 10 candles went the new way 49.8% of the time, against 49.7% for any candle.\"],\"example\":\"An uptrend's last higher low is 99.21. One candle wicks to 99.15 and closes at 99.21; the next closes at 98.90. The close rule prints the CHoCH on the second candle, the wick rule on the first.\",\"related\":[\"structure-break\",\"dow-theory\",\"swing-points\",\"break-of-structure\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.2\"]},{\"id\":\"displacement\",\"name\":\"Displacement\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC name for a fast run of large trend bars: classical momentum.\",\"body\":[\"Displacement describes a one-sided burst of large-bodied candles, often leaving gaps behind. There is no fixed threshold; strict rule sets use a body of at least 1.5 times the ATR.\",\"As a description of a fast move it is Mechanic. As a sign of institutional intent or of what comes next, it is grade D. Momentum's documented evidence is at horizons of months, not single candles.\"],\"example\":\"A candle opens at 100.71 and closes at 101.31: a body of 0.60, 1.9 times the 0.32 ATR of the 14 candles before it. Under a 1.5 × ATR rule it counts as displacement.\",\"related\":[\"momentum-roc\",\"momentum-vs-exhaustion\",\"break-of-structure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.2\"]},{\"id\":\"inducement\",\"name\":\"Inducement\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC claim that a minor swing is bait whose stops are taken before the real move; the intent is unprovable.\",\"body\":[\"The liquidity words decode to stop clustering and failed breakouts. Buy-side and sell-side liquidity are stops beyond highs and lows; equal highs are a double top; a sweep, turtle soup, the Judas swing and the swing failure pattern are failed breakouts; inducement is a shake-out of trapped traders.\",\"The documented part: stops cluster beyond obvious levels and speed price up when triggered (Osler, grade B). The contradicted part: \\\"after the sweep comes the reversal\\\" (grade D). The untestable part: that anyone set the trap.\"],\"example\":\"In the simulator, with stops resting above the prior day's high, 87.3% of first breaks closed above it, against 66.8% without them. After the sweeps that did close back under, price was lower 10 candles later 46.7% of the time: no reversal edge.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity-sweep\",\"stop-clustering\",\"trapped-traders\",\"intent-attribution\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.3\"]},{\"id\":\"order-block\",\"name\":\"Order block\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC name for the last opposite candle before a strong move: a renamed supply or demand base.\",\"body\":[\"A bullish order block is the last down candle before a displacement that breaks structure; the zone is its body or its full range. Breakers and mitigation blocks are role reversal after a failure; fair value gaps are three-candle gaps.\",\"No peer-reviewed evidence supports the claim that blocks hold. Tested with strict rules on a random walk, blocks reacted as often as random zones at the same distance: 50.3% against 49.6%. Renaming failures as \\\"mitigated\\\" or as breakers makes the claim unfalsifiable. Grade D.\"],\"example\":\"Candle 27 rises from 100.71 to 101.31, 1.9 × ATR, and closes above the 101.06 swing high. The last down candle before it, 100.95 to 100.71, is the block; its body is the zone.\",\"related\":[\"supply-demand-zones\",\"role-reversal\",\"fair-value-gap\",\"zone-grading\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.4\"]},{\"id\":\"premium-discount\",\"name\":\"Premium and discount\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC split of a range at 50%: buy below, sell above; the old 50% rule.\",\"body\":[\"Premium and discount split a dealing range at its midpoint, called equilibrium. Buying only in discount is the 50% retracement rule, which practitioners used long before SMC.\",\"The range itself is a choice: which swing high and low you pick moves the line. Trends can stay in \\\"premium\\\" for a long time, so the split is a filter at best. Grade D.\"],\"example\":\"Range 100.00 to 110.00: equilibrium is 105.00. A long in discount buys under 105.00; a strong uptrend may never come back there.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-levels\",\"optimal-trade-entry\",\"range-trading\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.4\"]},{\"id\":\"optimal-trade-entry\",\"name\":\"Optimal trade entry (OTE)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC retracement band of 62–79%, overlapping Fibonacci 61.8–78.6%.\",\"body\":[\"OTE is the band 62% to 79% of an impulse, measured back from its end, with a 70.5% \\\"sweet spot\\\". It is Fibonacci's 61.8–78.6% band with rounded ratios.\",\"Fibonacci ratios have shown no special power in tests, and OTE inherits that: grade D. It also inherits the anchor problem: a different swing gives a different band.\"],\"example\":\"Impulse 100.00 to 110.00: OTE runs from 103.80 (62%) to 102.10 (79%), and the sweet spot is 110.00 − 0.705 × 10.00 = 102.95.\",\"related\":[\"fibonacci-levels\",\"premium-discount\",\"pullback\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.4\"]},{\"id\":\"killzones\",\"name\":\"Killzones\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC session windows; their higher volatility is real, their directional role is unproven.\",\"body\":[\"Killzones are session opens and overlaps, such as the London open. Volatility and activity really do follow the session clock, with U-shapes in Tokyo and London hours (Ito and Hashimoto): grade A.\",\"The directional roles, such as \\\"London sets the day's high\\\", have no published support: grade D. In a simulated test, breakouts inside the windows moved about a third further, but their direction stayed near 50/50, as outside. Sources also disagree on the windows by 30 to 90 minutes.\"],\"example\":\"Range breakouts inside the windows moved 0.34 over the next 3 candles, against 0.25 outside. Direction: 50.5% against 49.8%, within a ±0.8-point noise band.\",\"related\":[\"fx-sessions\",\"intraday-cycle\",\"market-hours\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"power-of-three\",\"name\":\"Power of three (AMD)\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC claim that each bar runs accumulation, manipulation, distribution; any closed bar can be narrated so.\",\"body\":[\"Power of three reads each candle as accumulation near the open, a false move against the close, then the real move. It renames Wyckoff's cycle and the classic \\\"open, false move, trend day\\\".\",\"Any closed candle with a wick against its close fits the story, so it explains everything after the fact and predicts nothing. Quarterly theory applies the same story to quarters of years, weeks and days. Grade D.\"],\"example\":\"Five daily candles from a random walk all fit: day 2 dips to 99.20 before closing at 100.68, day 5 spikes to 103.53 before closing at 102.94.\",\"related\":[\"hindsight-bias\",\"intent-attribution\",\"candlestick\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"ipda-claim\",\"name\":\"IPDA claim\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The unfalsifiable claim that one algorithm delivers price between liquidity pools and gaps.\",\"body\":[\"IPDA, the \\\"interbank price delivery algorithm\\\", is said to move price between liquidity and gaps on fixed look-back windows. No such algorithm is documented.\",\"It also clashes with what is known about FX: a fragmented market of hundreds of dealers and platforms, where dealers internalise over 80% of client trades (BIS). And as stated, any price path can be called \\\"delivered\\\", so no result could count against it. Grade D.\"],\"example\":\"Price rises to an old high: \\\"the algorithm delivered it to buy-side liquidity\\\". Price falls to a gap instead: \\\"it delivered it to the gap\\\". Both paths fit, so neither tests anything.\",\"related\":[\"market-participants\",\"intent-attribution\",\"smart-money-concepts\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"smt-divergence\",\"name\":\"SMT divergence\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"SMC name for correlated markets disagreeing at a swing: Dow's confirmation principle.\",\"body\":[\"SMT divergence compares two related markets at a swing: one makes a new high, the other doesn't. Dow applied the same check to the Industrials and the Transports.\",\"As a timing signal it has no published support: grade D. Correlations also shift, so a \\\"divergence\\\" may only mean the two markets stopped moving together.\"],\"example\":\"Index A makes a new high at 5,120 while index B stops 0.4% under its prior high. SMT reads a reversal; Dow would call it an unconfirmed high. Neither is a tested signal.\",\"related\":[\"dow-theory\",\"divergence\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"testable-reformulation\",\"name\":\"Testable reformulation\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Rewriting a narrative claim as a rule, an event, an outcome, a horizon and a baseline.\",\"body\":[\"\\\"They swept the lows, now we go up\\\" can't be tested as said. Rewritten as an exact event, an outcome over a fixed horizon and a baseline, it can: run it on a random walk, on a market with the mechanism, and after costs.\",\"Freeze the parameters before the out-of-sample run, report the sample size and noise band, and write in advance the result that would change your mind. On small samples the best of many variants is mostly luck: one rule won 65.1% of 43 trades in-sample and 49.1% of 395 out of sample.\"],\"example\":\"H1: after a trade 1 tick above the prior-day high and a close back under it in the same candle, the next 10 candles are lower more often than after a random candle. Result: 46.7% to 49.1% across three simulated markets, against about 50%.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"sample-size\",\"after-cost-evaluation\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.5.3\",\"3.5.6\"]},{\"id\":\"bond-price-yield\",\"name\":\"Bond price and yield\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Bond prices fall when yields rise, and long maturities move more for the same change in yield.\",\"body\":[\"A zero-coupon bond's price is 100 ÷ (1 + y)^n: the face value discounted at the yield for n years. Raise the yield and every future payment is worth less today, so price and yield always move in opposite directions. That's arithmetic, not a market tendency.\",\"Each extra year is discounted once more, so the same change in yield moves a long bond far more than a short one. Assets whose payoffs lie far in the future, such as fast-growing companies, tend to react like long bonds; that part is a tendency, not arithmetic.\"],\"example\":\"A yield rise from 4% to 5%: a 2-year zero goes from 92.46 to 90.70 (−1.9%), a 10-year from 67.56 to 61.39 (−9.1%), a 30-year from 30.83 to 23.14 (−24.9%).\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"real-yield\",\"yield-curve\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.1\"]},{\"id\":\"real-yield\",\"name\":\"Real yield\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A nominal yield minus expected inflation.\",\"body\":[\"The real yield is what lending earns after inflation: the nominal yield minus the inflation expected over the same horizon. Markets price expected inflation as breakeven inflation, the gap between nominal and inflation-protected bond yields.\",\"Real yields are the cost of holding assets that pay nothing, such as gold. For years gold tended to fall when real yields rose, until heavy central-bank buying broke the link from 2022.\"],\"example\":\"A 10-year yield of 4.50% with 2.50% expected inflation: real yield ≈ 2.00% (exactly 1.045 ÷ 1.025 − 1 = 1.95%).\",\"related\":[\"bond-price-yield\",\"commodities-in-macro\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.1\",\"3.6.5\"]},{\"id\":\"rate-differential\",\"name\":\"Rate differential\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The gap between two currencies' interest rates, a main driver of exchange rates.\",\"body\":[\"A currency pays its holder that currency's interest rate. When one central bank raises rates against another's, holding its currency pays more, money tends to flow in and the pair tends to rise. What matters is the change against what was expected.\",\"The link is moderate (grade B): risk appetite, flows and haven demand can dominate, especially in stress. The same gap is what a carry trade earns, and what it can lose in an unwind.\"],\"example\":\"LCB lifts vUSD rates while VFUN stays at 0%: the differential widens, and LFX, vUSD priced in VFUN, tends to rise.\",\"related\":[\"carry-trade\",\"dollar-smile\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.1\",\"3.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"yield-curve\",\"name\":\"Yield curve\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Yields across maturities; its slope reflects expected policy, growth, inflation and the term premium.\",\"body\":[\"The yield curve plots government bond yields by maturity. Normally longer maturities pay more. A flat curve pays about the same everywhere; an inverted one pays more at the short end, usually because the market expects policy rates to fall.\",\"An inversion has come before every US recession since the 1950s, usually 6 to 24 months ahead, with false alarms (grade B). As a timing signal for stocks it is grade D: equities have often risen for months after an inversion.\"],\"example\":\"3-month 5.10%, LB2 4.80%, LB10 4.20%: 10y–3m = −90 bp and 10y–2y = −60 bp. The curve is inverted.\",\"related\":[\"curve-moves\",\"term-premium\",\"base-rates\",\"business-cycle\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.2\"]},{\"id\":\"curve-moves\",\"name\":\"Steepeners and flatteners\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Bull or bear changes in curve slope, named by which end of the curve moves more.\",\"body\":[\"Bull means yields fall (bond prices rise); bear means yields rise. A steepener widens the 10y–2y spread; a flattener narrows it. So a bull steepener has short yields falling more, a bear flattener short yields rising more, a bear steepener long yields rising more and a bull flattener long yields falling more.\",\"Rate hikes being priced in usually bear-flatten the curve; cuts being priced in usually bull-steepen it.\"],\"example\":\"LB2 −0.60, LB10 −0.20: yields fell and the spread widened by 0.40, a bull steepener.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve\",\"term-premium\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.2\"]},{\"id\":\"term-premium\",\"name\":\"Term premium\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Extra yield investors demand to hold long bonds instead of rolling short ones.\",\"body\":[\"A long yield is roughly the average of expected short rates plus a term premium: pay for locking money up for years and bearing the larger price swings of a long bond.\",\"Heavy bond supply, from large deficits and auctions, or a central bank shrinking its bond holdings can lift the term premium. The curve then steepens with no change in the growth outlook, which muddies its signal.\"],\"example\":\"If short rates are expected to average 3.80% over 10 years and LB10 yields 4.20%, the term premium is roughly 0.40 points.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve\",\"curve-moves\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.2\"]},{\"id\":\"inflation-regime\",\"name\":\"Inflation regime\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The inflation and policy backdrop that decides how assets react to the same data.\",\"body\":[\"Inflation comes from demand, cost shocks, wages and expectations, but markets feel it mainly through the central bank's response. High inflation means a bank fighting it; disinflation lets it ease.\",\"Read the regime before predicting any reaction: from the inflation trend, the bank's guidance, the curve, and above all from how markets reacted to recent data.\"],\"example\":\"Strong retail sales sink LABX 1.1%: the market reads good news as more hikes. That's an inflation-fight regime.\",\"related\":[\"market-regime\",\"regime-dependent-reaction\",\"stock-bond-correlation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.3\"]},{\"id\":\"stock-bond-correlation\",\"name\":\"Stock-bond correlation\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The co-movement of equities and government bonds; its sign changes with the monetary regime.\",\"body\":[\"When growth shocks dominate and inflation is low, bond prices tend to rise as stocks fall: the correlation is negative and bonds hedge equities. When inflation and tight policy dominate, both fall together and the correlation turns positive (grade A, decades of data).\",\"Historical case: in 2022 the S&P 500 lost 18.1% and 20-year US Treasuries 26.1%, double-digit losses in the same year, with inflation the dominant shock.\"],\"example\":\"A 60/40 portfolio with LABX −12% and LBND −8% loses 0.6 × (−12%) + 0.4 × (−8%) = −10.4%.\",\"related\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"inflation-regime\",\"correlation-instability\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.3\",\"3.6.8\"]},{\"id\":\"regime-dependent-reaction\",\"name\":\"Regime-dependent reaction\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The same release moves assets differently depending on what the market fears most.\",\"body\":[\"Strong data lift expected profits and the expected path of rates at the same time. In a growth scare the first effect wins and stocks rise; when the central bank is fighting inflation the second wins: good news is bad news (grade B).\",\"A fixed rule applied in every regime (grade D) fails exactly when the regime changes. Name the regime first, then predict.\"],\"example\":\"Payrolls beat by z = +1.5. Inflation fight: LABX 100.00 → 98.80, with LB2 +0.12. Growth scare: LABX 100.00 → 101.00. Simulator settings.\",\"related\":[\"news-surprise\",\"inflation-regime\",\"market-regime\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.3\"]},{\"id\":\"dollar-smile\",\"name\":\"Dollar smile\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The dollar tends to rise in US booms and in global stress and to fall in synchronised global growth.\",\"body\":[\"On the left of the smile, a US boom draws capital in. At the bottom, growth everywhere sends money abroad. On the right, a global crisis drives demand for safety and for dollars to repay dollar debts.\",\"It's a practitioner framework (grade C). The known exception is a funding-currency unwind: when a low-rate currency in the basket jumps as carry trades close, the dollar index can fall in risk-off.\"],\"example\":\"Risk-off begins and LDX rises to 100.60; then VFUN jumps as carry unwinds and LDX falls to 98.60.\",\"related\":[\"rate-differential\",\"safe-haven\",\"carry-unwind\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"safe-haven\",\"name\":\"Safe haven\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"An asset that tends to gain in stress; which assets play the role changes with the regime.\",\"body\":[\"Government bonds, gold, the dollar and some funding currencies have tended to rise when risky assets fall, as investors seek safety and close leveraged positions.\",\"The role isn't permanent. Bonds failed as a haven in 2022's inflation shock, and the yen's haven role weakened while its rate gap with other currencies was wide.\"],\"example\":\"In the lab's base regime, a risk-off shock lifts LBND, LGLD and LDX while LABX falls.\",\"related\":[\"dollar-smile\",\"risk-on-risk-off\",\"correlation-instability\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.4\",\"3.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"commodities-in-macro\",\"name\":\"Commodities in macro\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Oil, copper and gold as macro signals: supply and growth, industrial demand, real yields and official buying.\",\"body\":[\"Oil is dominated by supply shocks and global growth (grade B). Copper follows industrial demand, which makes it a growth gauge; the copper–gold ratio tends to move with long yields (grade C). Gold reacts to real yields, the dollar, haven demand and central-bank buying.\",\"These drivers shift. From 2022 gold rose despite positive real yields, as central banks bought heavily.\"],\"example\":\"A surprise oil inventory draw, in a synthetic replay: LOIL +2.4% and LCOM, an oil exporter's currency, +0.6% in the first hour.\",\"related\":[\"real-yield\",\"commodity-currencies\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.5\"]},{\"id\":\"commodity-currencies\",\"name\":\"Commodity currencies\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Currencies whose terms of trade follow the prices of their main commodity exports.\",\"body\":[\"When a country's main export gets dearer, its terms of trade improve, national income rises and foreign buyers need more of its currency, so the currency tends to strengthen.\",\"The link is grade C: rate differentials and risk appetite often dominate. Commodity currencies are also high-beta: they tend to fall in risk-off.\"],\"example\":\"Iron ore +20% with import prices flat: the terms of trade rise 20%, and the exporter's currency tends to rise, unless rate differentials pull the other way.\",\"related\":[\"commodities-in-macro\",\"rate-differential\",\"risk-on-risk-off\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.5\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-on-risk-off\",\"name\":\"Risk-on / risk-off\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"A regime in which assets move together with global risk appetite.\",\"body\":[\"In risk-on, investors favour equities, credit and high-yield or commodity currencies. In risk-off they sell them and buy government bonds and havens; carry pairs fall as funding currencies are bought back.\",\"In a crisis, forced selling through margin calls, volatility targeting and funding stress pushes correlations among risky assets toward 1 (grade B). Diversification thins out exactly when you need it.\"],\"example\":\"A risk-off shock in the base regime: LABX, LCOM and LFX fall; LDX, LBND and LGLD rise.\",\"related\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"safe-haven\",\"carry-unwind\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"carry-trade\",\"name\":\"Carry trade\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Borrowing a low-yield currency to hold a high-yield one: small steady gains, rare large losses.\",\"body\":[\"A carry trade earns the rate differential through the swap every day. Over long samples its average return has been positive, with negative skew and crash risk (grade A).\",\"Size carry for the jump, not for the daily gain: a single day's rally in the funding currency can erase more than a year of carry.\"],\"example\":\"50,000 vUSD at a 4% rate gap earns 2,000 a year, about 5.48 a day. A 5% jump in the funding currency costs 2,500: about 456 days of carry.\",\"related\":[\"return-skew\",\"carry-unwind\",\"overnight-financing\",\"rate-differential\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"carry-unwind\",\"name\":\"Carry unwind\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"A fast reversal of carry positions when volatility jumps or the funding currency rallies.\",\"body\":[\"When volatility jumps or the funding currency starts to rise, carry traders close positions at once, buying back the funding currency. The rally feeds on itself as more stops and margin calls are hit.\",\"An unwind can break other relationships: the dollar index can fall in risk-off if the funding currency weighs heavily in its basket. Historical case: in summer 2024, a Bank of Japan rate rise and a jump in the yen forced yen-funded carry trades to unwind within days.\"],\"example\":\"104 synthetic weeks of carry: 78 gains, none above +0.57%. The three worst weeks lost 11.5 points together.\",\"related\":[\"carry-trade\",\"dollar-smile\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"business-cycle\",\"name\":\"Business cycle\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Expansion and contraction phases that are identified reliably only in hindsight.\",\"body\":[\"Textbook phases: early (new orders rebound, claims fall, a steep curve, narrowing spreads), mid (steady growth), late (growth slows, claims turn up, the curve flattens or inverts, spreads widen) and recession (new orders well below 50, rising claims, wide spreads).\",\"Real-time phase calls are grade C: the same three months of data can be a mid-cycle pause or the turn, and only later data tell them apart.\"],\"example\":\"By NBER dating for 1945–2020, US expansions averaged about 64 months and contractions about 10.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve\",\"sector-rotation\",\"credit-spreads\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.7\"]},{\"id\":\"sector-rotation\",\"name\":\"Sector rotation\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Shifting sector leadership through the cycle; the edge shrinks after costs and timing errors.\",\"body\":[\"The textbook map: cyclicals such as consumer discretionary, financials and industrials lead early; technology mid; energy and materials late; staples, utilities and health care in recession.\",\"Research finds a modest edge when phases are dated in hindsight, which mostly disappears after switching costs and realistic timing errors (grade C).\"],\"example\":\"Synthetic: hindsight rotation beats buy-and-hold by 2.4 points a year; four switches at 0.30% cost 1.2; late calls cost 1.0. Edge left: 0.2 points.\",\"related\":[\"business-cycle\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.7\"]},{\"id\":\"credit-spreads\",\"name\":\"Credit spreads\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Corporate minus government yields: a gauge of default risk and financial conditions.\",\"body\":[\"A credit spread is what a company pays over the government to borrow. It widens when lenders fear defaults and narrows as they relax, so it's a core input to financial conditions, along with rates, stocks and the currency.\",\"'Credit leads equities' is a practitioner observation (grade C). Sometimes spreads widen first, sometimes not: test it before relying on it.\"],\"example\":\"In a synthetic stress episode, credit spreads widen from about 1.10% to 2.30% in six days while a volatility index jumps from about 15 to 37.2.\",\"related\":[\"business-cycle\",\"risk-on-risk-off\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.7\"]},{\"id\":\"correlation-instability\",\"name\":\"Correlation instability\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Correlations change sign and size with the dominant shock and often rise toward 1 in crises.\",\"body\":[\"Correlations are footprints of the dominant shock. Growth shocks push stocks and yields the same way, so stock and bond prices move apart; inflation and policy shocks push both prices down together (grade A for regime dependence).\",\"Historical breaks: the stock–bond correlation turned positive in 2022; gold stopped tracking real yields from 2022; the yen's haven role weakened while rate gaps were wide. Risk rule: in stress, size as if correlation were 1.\"],\"example\":\"LABX 5,000 + LBND 5,000. Growth scare: −500 + 200 = −300. Inflation shock: −500 − 400 = −900, 9% of a 10,000 account.\",\"related\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"stock-bond-correlation\",\"portfolio-heat\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.8\"]},{\"id\":\"rolling-correlation\",\"name\":\"Rolling correlation\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"A correlation recomputed over a moving window; short windows are noisy, long ones lag.\",\"body\":[\"A 20-bar rolling correlation rests on 20 pairs of returns. At a true correlation of zero, 95% of such readings fall within about ±0.44; for 60 bars, within about ±0.25.\",\"Short windows flag a regime change sooner but give false alarms; long windows are steadier but late. State the window, and so the number of observations, whenever you quote one.\"],\"example\":\"Synthetic switch at bar 80: the 20-bar correlation first closes above zero at bar 89, the 60-bar at bar 115.\",\"related\":[\"correlation-instability\",\"sample-size\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.8\"]},{\"id\":\"intermarket-relationships\",\"name\":\"Intermarket relationships\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Links among bonds, stocks, commodities and the dollar that hold only within a regime.\",\"body\":[\"The classic map, built in inflation-era markets: stocks and bonds move together; commodities move opposite to bonds and to the dollar. From the late 1990s, with deflation the fear, stocks and bonds decoupled; 2022 flipped them back.\",\"As fixed laws these relationships are grade D; as regime-dependent tendencies, grade C. Check which shock dominates before relying on any of them.\"],\"example\":\"In 2022 stocks and long bonds both fell by double digits: what the classic inflation-era map predicts, and what 2000–2020 experience didn't.\",\"related\":[\"correlation-instability\",\"stock-bond-correlation\",\"market-regime\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.6.8\"]},{\"id\":\"reaction-function\",\"name\":\"Reaction function\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"How a central bank maps inflation, jobs and financial conditions into policy decisions.\",\"body\":[\"A reaction function describes how a central bank turns data into rate decisions. A simple rule, shaped like the Taylor rule, starts from a base rate plus inflation, adds part of the gap between inflation and its target, and subtracts part of the gap between unemployment and its benchmark.\",\"Real central banks don't follow a formula, but markets estimate their reaction functions from past decisions and words. When the weights change, the whole expected path of rates reprices, not just the next meeting. Estimated weights drift, so an old estimate is a risk.\"],\"example\":\"LCB rule: 2.00 + inflation + 0.5 × (inflation − 2.00) − 0.5 × (unemployment − 4.50). With inflation at 2.60% and unemployment at 5.10%: 2.00 + 2.60 + 0.30 − 0.30 = 4.60%.\",\"related\":[\"central-bank-decisions\",\"news-surprise\",\"expected-policy-path\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.1\"]},{\"id\":\"expected-policy-path\",\"name\":\"Expected policy path\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The sequence of future policy rates that markets price across the upcoming meetings.\",\"body\":[\"Rate futures and swaps price an expected policy rate after each coming meeting; together these form the expected path. Short-term yields, such as a 2-year yield, track roughly the average of that path.\",\"A data surprise, or a change in the reaction function, moves the whole path. That's why one release can move a 2-year yield far more than the next meeting alone would justify.\"],\"example\":\"A print 0.30 hotter lifts the LCB's prescribed rate by 1.5 × 0.30 = 0.45. If the market expects that at each of the coming meetings, the whole path shifts up, and LB2 with it.\",\"related\":[\"reaction-function\",\"central-bank-decisions\",\"implied-policy-probability\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.1\"]},{\"id\":\"implied-policy-probability\",\"name\":\"Implied policy probability\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The chance of a rate move derived from rate-futures prices: a price, not a poll.\",\"body\":[\"Rate futures are quoted as 100 minus the average policy rate expected for the month. When a meeting falls inside the month, the days before it carry the old rate, so the rate after the meeting is found by weighting the days. Its distance from today's rate, divided by the size of a step, is the implied probability.\",\"It's a price: it includes risk premia and can swing late, in the quiet period before a meeting. A trade only makes sense where your own view differs from it.\"],\"example\":\"LRF at 95.95 implies a 4.05% average. Meeting on day 16 of 30: r = (4.05 × 30 − 15 × 4.00) ÷ 15 = 4.10%. Hike chance: (4.10 − 4.00) ÷ 0.25 = 40%, not the naive 20%.\",\"related\":[\"expected-policy-path\",\"news-surprise\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.2\"]},{\"id\":\"policy-communication\",\"name\":\"Policy communication\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Statement wording, votes, dissents and minutes, read against what markets expected.\",\"body\":[\"Desks read a policy statement as a list of edits against the last one. A tone scale helps: ±2 for adding or removing a bias toward the next move, ±1 for shifts in risks, timing or strength, 0 for restated data. Votes and dissents show where the next move may go; the minutes, weeks later, show the debate.\",\"Markets react to tone relative to what was priced. A hawkish statement can push yields down if the market had priced something more hawkish.\"],\"example\":\"A statement scores +1. The market had priced +2. Tone minus priced tone is −1: a dovish surprise, so LB2 tends to fall.\",\"related\":[\"central-bank-decisions\",\"news-surprise\",\"reaction-function\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.3\"]},{\"id\":\"dot-plot\",\"name\":\"Dot plot\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Each policymaker's projected policy rate; markets watch the median, which a single dot can move.\",\"body\":[\"A dot plot shows where each policymaker sees the policy rate at coming year-ends, if their own forecast comes true. With 19 participants the median is the 10th dot, and a single dot crossing the middle can move it a full step.\",\"Dots are conditional forecasts, not promises, and their record as predictions is mixed. They move prices only where they differ from the path already priced in futures.\"],\"example\":\"Dots: 2 at 3.75%, 6 at 4.00%, 2 at 4.25%, 7 at 4.50%, 2 at 4.75%. The 10th is at 4.25%. Move one 4.25% dot to 4.50% and the median becomes 4.50%.\",\"related\":[\"expected-policy-path\",\"implied-policy-probability\",\"forward-guidance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.4\"]},{\"id\":\"forward-guidance\",\"name\":\"Forward guidance\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Central-bank communication about the likely future path of policy: calendar, threshold or qualitative.\",\"body\":[\"Calendar guidance names a date, threshold guidance names a level in the data, and qualitative guidance uses words such as 'patient' or 'data dependent'. Credible guidance moves the expected path, and with it longer yields.\",\"Guidance is conditional, not a promise. When a bank abandons it, positions built on it reprice violently. The press conference after a decision can shift guidance and whipsaw prices for 30 to 60 minutes, so the plan for it is written in advance.\"],\"example\":\"'No hike while unemployment is above 5.50%' is threshold guidance. If unemployment falls to 5.40% and the bank still holds, the market has to guess the new rule.\",\"related\":[\"dot-plot\",\"second-move\",\"expected-policy-path\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.4\"]},{\"id\":\"qe-qt\",\"name\":\"QE and QT\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Central-bank bond buying (QE) and balance-sheet runoff (QT), acting mainly through the term premium.\",\"body\":[\"Quantitative easing buys long bonds at scale. It lowers long yields mainly by shrinking the term premium, the extra yield investors want for holding long bonds, and partly by signalling that policy rates will stay low. Estimates for real programmes vary; evidence B.\",\"Quantitative tightening is not the mirror image: runoff is slow and announced in advance, and its estimated effects are smaller and disputed (evidence C).\"],\"example\":\"Synthetic: LB10 at 4.50% = 3.60% expected path + 0.90% term premium. After QE: 3.50% + 0.40% = 3.90%. Most of the 0.60 drop comes from the premium.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve-control\",\"expected-policy-path\",\"central-bank-decisions\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.5\"]},{\"id\":\"yield-curve-control\",\"name\":\"Yield curve control\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Capping a long-term yield by standing ready to buy bonds; exits can gap markets.\",\"body\":[\"Under yield curve control, a central bank caps a long yield and buys whatever bonds it must to hold it. The target stays calm, but pressure builds in the bank's purchases, other maturities and the currency.\",\"When a cap, or a currency peg, ends, the stored pressure can release at once: price gaps to where the market wants it, with nothing trading in between. Positions that rely on the cap must be sized for the break.\"],\"example\":\"Synthetic: LB10 capped at 1.00% holds LBND at 90.53. At the exit LB10 jumps to 1.50% and LBND opens at 86.17, 4.8% lower. 22 units lose 97.87 with costs: within 1% of 10,000.00.\",\"related\":[\"qe-qt\",\"gap-risk\",\"fx-intervention\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.5\"]},{\"id\":\"fx-intervention\",\"name\":\"FX intervention\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Official buying or selling of a currency, usually preceded by escalating verbal warnings.\",\"body\":[\"Before intervening, officials usually escalate their words: watching closely, then calling moves one-sided or speculative, then not ruling out options, then readiness for decisive action. After the last step, action can come at any hour.\",\"An intervention can move a currency a few percent within minutes, but the move often fades if interest-rate differentials don't change (evidence C). Carry positions in that currency need sizing for the jump.\"],\"example\":\"'We stand ready to take decisive action' is the fourth step. A trader holding a carry position in that currency cuts it to a size where a sudden move of a few percent stays within 1R.\",\"related\":[\"yield-curve-control\",\"gap-risk\",\"news-surprise\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.5\"]},{\"id\":\"spike-fade\",\"name\":\"Fading the spike\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Trading against the first event move in expectation of a partial retracement; practitioner lore.\",\"body\":[\"A fade sells the first spike up, or buys the first spike down, betting that the move overshoots. In this course's simulator, fades paid only after small surprises (|z| under 1) with conflicting details: a setting of the generator, not a fact about markets.\",\"In real markets, fading spikes is practitioner lore, evidence D. A fade enters at the widest spreads, against a move that may be a clean repricing.\"],\"example\":\"Across 30 synthetic events, the fade policy lost 1.8R after costs, while flat made 0R and waiting for the second move made 2.4R.\",\"related\":[\"news-policy\",\"second-move\",\"mean-reversion\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.6\"]},{\"id\":\"news-straddle\",\"name\":\"News straddle\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Entry orders on both sides before a release; a spike and a reversal can fill one side and stop it out.\",\"body\":[\"A pending-order straddle puts a buy stop above and a sell stop below the price before a release, often linked as OCO. The spike fills one side with slippage and cancels the other; if the details then reverse the move, the protective stop fills with slippage too.\",\"Evidence for pending straddles is weak, D: they pay the news spread twice. Option straddles buy the expected move, and lose when the realised move is smaller than the implied one.\"],\"example\":\"100 units: the buy stop at 100.40 fills at 100.55, the protective stop at 100.00 fills at 99.90. Price −65.00, commissions 10.03: −75.03, against −50.02 at your own levels.\",\"related\":[\"oco-order\",\"stop-entry-order\",\"slippage\",\"news-liquidity\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.6\"]},{\"id\":\"pre-event-drift\",\"name\":\"Pre-event drift\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Systematic moves before scheduled events, such as the documented pre-FOMC equity drift.\",\"body\":[\"The best-known case: in a sample starting in 1994, a broad US stock index rose 49 bp on average in the 24 hours before scheduled Fed announcements. The cause is debated, and published anomalies tend to weaken: evidence B, with caution.\",\"A small drift is hard to tell from noise. With a drift of 0.10% and a standard deviation of 1.00% per event, t = 2 needs about 400 events: 50 years at 8 meetings a year.\"],\"example\":\"After 40 events, an average of +0.12% with a standard deviation of 1.00% has a standard error of 0.16 and a 95% interval from −0.20% to +0.44%: it includes zero.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"sample-size\",\"buy-rumour-sell-news\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.7\"]},{\"id\":\"buy-rumour-sell-news\",\"name\":\"Buy the rumour, sell the news\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Prices run up into an expected event and reverse when it is confirmed, as positions close.\",\"body\":[\"When good news is widely expected, traders buy early. Once it's confirmed, few buyers are left and the early ones take profits, so price can fall on the news itself.\",\"It happens often enough to have a name, but not reliably enough to trade blindly: evidence C. It's one reason a release that beats the old consensus can still meet selling.\"],\"example\":\"LABX rises 2% into a widely expected rate cut. The cut comes as priced, and LABX falls 1% within the hour as early buyers sell.\",\"related\":[\"market-tell\",\"pre-event-drift\",\"news-surprise\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.7\"]},{\"id\":\"market-tell\",\"name\":\"Reaction tell\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"How price reacts to news hints at positioning: falling on good news suggests a heavy market.\",\"body\":[\"A market that can't rally on good news, and falls hard on bad, is likely already crowded long: buyers are in, sellers are waiting. A market that shrugs off bad news and rallies on good is washed out.\",\"It's a discretionary read, evidence D: one reaction says little, a consistent pattern is a hint. Check it against positioning data where you have it.\"],\"example\":\"A week of synthetic reactions: good news −0.4%, good news −0.2%, bad news −0.9%, good news flat, bad news −0.6%. The reading: heavy.\",\"related\":[\"buy-rumour-sell-news\",\"news-surprise\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.7\"]},{\"id\":\"tail-event\",\"name\":\"Tail event\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A rare, extreme move in which liquidity vanishes and stops fill far from their levels.\",\"body\":[\"In a tail event, such as a currency floor removed without warning or a flash crash, quotes vanish and price jumps to distant orders. Stops become market orders and fill at the first available price.\",\"The survival rules are set in advance: a leverage cap, sizing for a gap scenario rather than for the stop, being flat before big events and long closes, and knowing your broker's protections, such as guaranteed stops and negative balance protection.\"],\"example\":\"Long 800 LPEG at 120.20 with a 0.40 stop, at 9.6 times leverage. A reopening at 102.00 loses 14,560.00 on a 10,000.00 account. Sized for a 15% gap at 1% risk, the position would be 5 units.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"leverage\",\"liquidity-vacuum\",\"negative-balance-protection\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.8\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-vacuum\",\"name\":\"Liquidity vacuum\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"A moment when resting orders disappear and price gaps to the next available bids or offers.\",\"body\":[\"Market makers and other liquidity providers pull their quotes when risk spikes, so the book empties just as orders arrive. Price then jumps across levels where nobody is trading, to the next resting orders.\",\"Stops, which become market orders, fill wherever those orders are. No stop protects against a vacuum: only position size and leverage do.\"],\"example\":\"LPEG's floor breaks and the nearest bids sit near 102.00. A stop at 119.80 fills there, 17.80 below its level.\",\"related\":[\"tail-event\",\"news-liquidity\",\"market-depth\",\"stop-loss-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.7.8\"]},{\"id\":\"financial-statements\",\"name\":\"Financial statements\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The income statement, balance sheet and cash-flow statement, linked through net income.\",\"body\":[\"The income statement covers a period and ends in net income. The balance sheet is a snapshot on one date: assets equal liabilities plus equity. The cash-flow statement shows the cash that actually moved, split into operating, investing and financing.\",\"Net income ties them together. Profit not paid out adds to retained earnings, in equity, and the cash-flow statement starts from it: it adds back non-cash costs such as depreciation and subtracts increases in receivables and inventory. Capex raises PP&E on the balance sheet and leaves as investing cash flow.\",\"Profit is counted when a sale is made and cash when it's paid, so the two can move in opposite directions for quarters at a time.\"],\"example\":\"LABA earns 60m. Depreciation adds back 20m; receivables rise 80m and inventory 30m: operating cash flow −30m. Capex of 15m makes free cash flow −45m, and with no financing, cash falls 45m in a record-profit quarter.\",\"related\":[\"free-cash-flow\",\"earnings-quality\",\"stocks-and-etfs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.1\"]},{\"id\":\"free-cash-flow\",\"name\":\"Free cash flow\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Operating cash flow minus capital spending: the cash a business generates after investing in itself.\",\"body\":[\"Free cash flow is what's left for owners and lenders after the business has paid its suppliers, staff, taxes and its own investment. It's harder to flatter than earnings: it moves only when cash does.\",\"It can be negative for good reasons, such as heavy investment in a growth phase, or bad ones, such as customers who don't pay. Per share, it shows what each owner's slice actually generates, after dilution.\"],\"example\":\"Operating cash flow 140m, capex 55m: free cash flow 85m. With 100m shares, that's 0.85 a share, whatever the adjusted EPS says.\",\"related\":[\"financial-statements\",\"earnings-quality\",\"discounted-cash-flow\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.1\",\"3.8.2\"]},{\"id\":\"earnings-quality\",\"name\":\"Earnings quality\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"How well reported earnings reflect cash and repeatable results, net of adjustments, dilution and buyback effects.\",\"body\":[\"High-quality earnings turn into cash and repeat. Warning signs: adjusted EPS far above GAAP EPS, 'one-off' costs that recur every year, share-based pay that dilutes owners, and receivables or inventory growing faster than sales.\",\"EPS can also grow without the business growing: a buyback shrinks the share count, so the same profit is split fewer ways. And a high ROE can come from debt rather than skill; net debt to EBITDA and interest coverage show which.\"],\"example\":\"LABA and LABB both report adjusted EPS of 2.00. LABA's free cash flow is 2.10 a share; LABB's is 0.80, its GAAP EPS is 1.10 and its share count grows 6% a year. Same headline, different quality.\",\"related\":[\"free-cash-flow\",\"financial-statements\",\"valuation-multiples\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.2\"]},{\"id\":\"valuation-multiples\",\"name\":\"Valuation multiples\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Ratios such as P/E and EV/EBITDA that relate price or enterprise value to a company's fundamentals.\",\"body\":[\"A multiple compresses the market's view of growth, risk and discount rates into one number. P/E relates the share price to EPS; EV/EBITDA relates the whole business, debt and cash included, to operating earnings, so it compares companies with different balance sheets fairly. In one CFA Institute survey, about 93% of practitioners used multiples.\",\"Low isn't automatically cheap: a low multiple can reflect slow growth, high risk or falling profits, and a cyclical company's P/E looks high at the bottom of its cycle. As short-term timing tools, multiples are grade D; value as a long-run factor is graded A/B, with long dry spells.\"],\"example\":\"Market cap 4,000m plus net debt 1,000m: EV 5,000m. EBITDA of 500m gives 10×. A company with the same market cap and 1,000m of net cash has an EV of 3,000m: 6×.\",\"related\":[\"earnings-quality\",\"equity-duration\",\"discounted-cash-flow\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.3\"]},{\"id\":\"equity-duration\",\"name\":\"Equity duration\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"How sensitive a stock's value is to discount-rate changes; higher when cash flows lie further in the future.\",\"body\":[\"A stock's value is the present value of its future cash. The further away that cash lies, the more a change in the discount rate moves the value, as with a long bond. Fast growers, whose cash lies mostly years ahead, have long duration.\",\"With value = cash ÷ (r − g), the closer growth g sits to the discount rate r, the steeper the sensitivity. That's how rate surprises reach stock prices: through the multiples.\"],\"example\":\"Same cash today, rate from 8% to 9%: with growth of 6%, the value falls from 50 to 33.33 times the cash (−33%); with growth of 1%, from 14.29 to 12.50 times (−12.5%).\",\"related\":[\"valuation-multiples\",\"discounted-cash-flow\",\"central-bank-decisions\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.3\"]},{\"id\":\"discounted-cash-flow\",\"name\":\"Discounted cash flow\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Valuing an asset as the present value of its expected future cash flows plus a terminal value.\",\"body\":[\"A DCF forecasts free cash flow for a few years, discounts each year at a rate such as WACC, and adds a terminal value for everything after. The terminal value is often most of the total, commonly over 60–70% (a rule of thumb).\",\"That makes a DCF very sensitive to its two least knowable inputs: the discount rate and long-run growth. It's a model, not a forecast: as a trading signal it's grade D, and it says nothing about when price and model might meet. If you built it yourself, watch for anchoring on it.\"],\"example\":\"Free cash flow of 100m growing 10% a year for five years, WACC 9%, terminal growth 2.5%: 467.2m from the five years plus 1,500.5m of discounted terminal value = 1,967.8m, 76% of it terminal. At 8% WACC: 2,337m.\",\"related\":[\"free-cash-flow\",\"valuation-multiples\",\"implied-expectations\",\"anchoring\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.4\"]},{\"id\":\"implied-expectations\",\"name\":\"Implied expectations\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The growth or outcome that today's price assumes, found by running a valuation model in reverse.\",\"body\":[\"Instead of asking what a company is worth, ask what its price assumes. Fix the valuation at today's price and solve for growth: a reverse DCF. The answer is a claim you can judge against the business, its history and its competitors.\",\"A gap between your model and the price is a disagreement, not a signal. The market may know something you don't, and even if you're right, nothing says when the price will move. Write down what would prove you wrong before acting.\"],\"example\":\"LABB's model gives 1,968m at 10% growth; the market says 2,600m. Keeping WACC at 9% and terminal growth at 2.5%, the price implies about 18.9% growth a year for five years.\",\"related\":[\"discounted-cash-flow\",\"news-surprise\",\"anchoring\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.4\"]},{\"id\":\"earnings-surprise\",\"name\":\"Earnings surprise\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The gap between reported results and expectations; its size and the guidance matter more than a beat itself.\",\"body\":[\"Earnings season compares reported EPS and revenue with the consensus. Beats are the norm: in the S&P 500, about 78% of companies beat on average over five years (FactSet data). So a beat is the base case; what matters is the surprise's size against usual surprises, and guidance.\",\"The bar can sit above the consensus: a whisper number, or what buyers already paid for in a run-up. Reports usually land outside regular hours, so the reaction arrives as an overnight gap that a stop can't stop.\"],\"example\":\"LABB beats, 2.05 against 2.00, and falls 6% after the close. Standardised, a surprise of 0.30 when surprises usually vary by 0.12 gives a SUE of 2.5.\",\"related\":[\"news-surprise\",\"base-rates\",\"gap-risk\",\"earnings-guidance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.5\",\"3.8.6\",\"3.8.8\"]},{\"id\":\"earnings-guidance\",\"name\":\"Earnings guidance\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Management's outlook for future results; changes in it often move price more than the quarter's numbers.\",\"body\":[\"Results and guidance place a report in a 3×3 matrix: miss, in-line or beat, against cut, maintain or raise. Where the two agree, the gap tends to follow them; where they disagree, outcomes spread widely, so flag those cells as uncertain.\",\"Expectations shape every cell. A habitual sandbagger's raise is already priced, so a small raise can disappoint, and a cut smaller than feared can lift the stock. The second wave, during the conference call, can reverse the first. The matrix is grade C, from practitioner sources.\"],\"example\":\"Simulator, 40 reports per cell: 35 of 40 beat-and-raise reports gapped up, median +4.9%; 39 of 40 miss-and-cut reports gapped down. Miss-and-raise: 22 up, 17 down, from −14.1% to +13.8%.\",\"related\":[\"earnings-surprise\",\"second-move\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.6\"]},{\"id\":\"implied-move\",\"name\":\"Implied move\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The move the options market prices for an event, roughly the at-the-money straddle price divided by the share price.\",\"body\":[\"Before a report, options price its uncertainty. The at-the-money straddle for the first expiry after the event, divided by the share price, approximates the move the market expects, up or down.\",\"It's a benchmark, not a forecast. Compare a stock's realised moves with its implied moves over many reports before calling options cheap or rich: the evidence is mixed (grade C), and a few big moves decide the average.\"],\"example\":\"LABB at 100.00 with the straddle at 7.95: an implied move of about ±8%. Over 8 reports, LABB moved 6.8% on average against 8.0% implied, a ratio of 0.85, but two of the moves were 12.4% and 14.0%.\",\"related\":[\"iv-crush\",\"volatility\",\"earnings-surprise\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.7\"]},{\"id\":\"iv-crush\",\"name\":\"IV crush\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The fall in implied volatility once an event's uncertainty is resolved, which lowers option prices.\",\"body\":[\"Implied volatility rises into a scheduled event and collapses once the event resolves the uncertainty. Option prices fall with it, often by more than the stock's move adds.\",\"So buying a call before earnings isn't a cheap bet on a beat: the call can lose even when the stock rises. Selling options to collect the crush wins often and loses rarely but big, when the gap beats the implied move.\"],\"example\":\"A call bought at 3.98 with 7 days left and IV at 72%. After the report LABB is up 3% at 103.00, IV is 30%, and the call is worth 3.50: −12%. It breaks even only above about 103.60.\",\"related\":[\"implied-move\",\"historical-volatility\",\"return-skew\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.7\"]},{\"id\":\"post-earnings-drift\",\"name\":\"Post-earnings announcement drift\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Prices tending to keep moving in the direction of an earnings surprise for weeks; much weaker since publication.\",\"body\":[\"After big earnings surprises, prices tended to keep moving in the surprise's direction for weeks. Ball and Brown (1968) documented it; Bernard and Thomas (1989) found the top-minus-bottom SUE spread positive in 41 of 48 quarters, 1974–1985. About 25–30% of the drift came at the next reports. The usual explanations: underreaction and limits to arbitrage.\",\"The spread fell from about 5% in the 1980s–90s to 3% or less in the late 2010s, and Martineau (2021) finds no drift in large caps since about 2006. What's left lives in small, illiquid, thinly covered stocks, where costs are highest. Grade B, strongly decayed.\"],\"example\":\"Simulator: top-SUE small caps earn a gross +1.10% per 60-day hold. After 0.10% of commissions, the drift breaks even at a 1.00% spread; the small caps' 1.20% spread leaves −0.20% a trade.\",\"related\":[\"earnings-surprise\",\"anomaly-decay\",\"trading-costs\",\"evidence-grades\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.8\"]},{\"id\":\"anomaly-decay\",\"name\":\"Anomaly decay\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The shrinking of a published return pattern out of sample and after publication, as traders learn and costs bite.\",\"body\":[\"Published return patterns shrink. Across 97 predictors, McLean and Pontiff (2016) found returns 26% lower out of sample and 58% lower after publication, consistent with traders learning from the papers. Costs and crowding then take part of what's left.\",\"Treat any published edge as smaller and more crowded than its paper suggests, measure it net of your own costs, and write the rule for stopping before you start, so the results can't bend it.\"],\"example\":\"PEAD's spread fell from about 5% to 3% or less, and in large caps it's about zero since around 2006. A rule written in advance, such as 'stop when the 2-year net expectancy interval includes zero', decides when to quit.\",\"related\":[\"post-earnings-drift\",\"evidence-grades\",\"performance-claims\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.8\"]},{\"id\":\"adjusted-price-data\",\"name\":\"Adjusted price data\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Price history corrected for splits, dividends or contract rolls so charts and backtests show no artificial gaps.\",\"body\":[\"A split divides the price without changing the value; a dividend moves value from the price into cash. Unadjusted, both show up on a chart as drops that never happened to holders, and indicators and backtests react to them.\",\"The fix: adjust every price before the event and nothing after. For a 4-for-1 split, divide earlier prices by 4; for a dividend, multiply earlier prices by (1 − dividend ÷ the previous close). Use adjusted data for signals and actual prices for fills.\"],\"example\":\"LABA closes at 120.00, splits 4-for-1, then pays 0.80 when it trades at 32.00. Bar 30's adjusted close: 120.00 ÷ 4 × 0.975 = 29.25.\",\"related\":[\"corporate-actions\",\"look-ahead-bias\",\"survivorship-bias\",\"moving-average\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.8.9\"]},{\"id\":\"market-sentiment\",\"name\":\"Market sentiment\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"How one-sided expectations are; a condition that shifts reversal odds, not a timing trigger.\",\"body\":[\"Surveys, put/call ratios and positioning data measure how one-sided the crowd's expectations are. Read each against its own history: a bull–bear spread of −30 means little until you know the survey averages +6 with a standard deviation of 15.\",\"Most survey swings follow price. In five synthetic years the spread correlated 0.8 with the past eight weeks' change and 0.02 with the next eight. Extremes raised the odds of a 5% reversal within eight weeks from 33.6% to 41.7%, and in 4 of 12 cases the reading got more extreme first. Evidence grade C.\"],\"example\":\"Bulls 22%, bears 52%: spread −30. With mean +6 and standard deviation 15, z = (−30 − 6) ÷ 15 = −2.4, a bearish extreme for this survey.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"contrarian-signal\",\"recency-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.1\",\"3.9.5\"]},{\"id\":\"contrarian-signal\",\"name\":\"Contrarian signal\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A rule that leans against a sentiment or positioning extreme, best combined with a price confirmation.\",\"body\":[\"A contrarian rule has a condition (a reading beyond about two standard deviations of its own history), a trigger from price, a stop beyond the extreme, an exit, and a size computed from the stop. The extreme alone is not a trigger: in trends, extremes deepen.\",\"In the course lab, on 30 synthetic markets with LABX costs, the naive fade averaged +0.02R in-sample and hit its stop in 63% of trades; the confirmed fade averaged +0.23R and was stopped in 25%. Confirmation misses fast turns. Real-world evidence is grade C: mixed, better with price confirmation.\"],\"example\":\"On a fast turn, selling at once made +1.79R and waiting for a weekly close below the prior week's low made +0.49R. On a slow turn, selling at once was stopped twice, −2R, while the confirmed sale made +1.93R.\",\"related\":[\"market-sentiment\",\"anticipation-vs-confirmation\",\"positioning\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.1\",\"3.9.5\"]},{\"id\":\"commitments-of-traders\",\"name\":\"Commitments of Traders\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The weekly CFTC report of futures positions by trader category, measured on Tuesday and published on Friday.\",\"body\":[\"The CFTC publishes positions in several families: Legacy (commercial, non-commercial, non-reportable), Disaggregated for physical commodities (producer/merchant, swap dealer, managed money, other reportables) and Traders in Financial Futures (dealer, asset manager, leveraged funds, other reportables). Reportable positions usually cover about 70–90% of open interest.\",\"The COT index places a group's net position inside its three-year range, from 0 to 100. Multi-year extremes are a weak-to-moderate contrarian condition (grade C). As a timer the report is weak: weekly, three days old on release, and each group mixes motives. Commercial shorts are mostly hedges.\"],\"example\":\"Managed money in LXO: three-year low −22.0k contracts, high +148.0k, latest +142.1k. COT index = (142.1 + 22.0) ÷ (148.0 + 22.0) × 100 = 96.5, a crowded long.\",\"related\":[\"positioning\",\"market-participants\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.2\"]},{\"id\":\"positioning\",\"name\":\"Positioning\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Who holds what and how forced their flows are; crowded positions amplify moves when they unwind.\",\"body\":[\"Positioning data (COT reports, short interest, days to cover, borrow fees, margin debt) show which side is crowded and how fast it could be forced out. Mechanical flows add to it: trend-following funds, volatility-target funds and dealers' hedging can all amplify a move.\",\"A crowded position is a condition, not a timer. It tells you who would have to sell, or buy back, if the story changes. The forced flow itself is mechanical; positioning as a forecast is grade C.\"],\"example\":\"LABE: 12m shares short, 1.5m traded a day, so 8 days to cover and 30% of a 40m float. A +2% surprise became a +35% squeeze as stops and recalls forced shorts to buy.\",\"related\":[\"commitments-of-traders\",\"short-squeeze\",\"stop-cascade\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.2\",\"3.9.4\"]},{\"id\":\"vix\",\"name\":\"VIX\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"An index of the 30-day volatility priced into S&P 500 options; a measure of priced fear, not a forecast of direction.\",\"body\":[\"The VIX is computed from a strip of S&P 500 options with 23 to 37 days to expiry and quoted as an annualised volatility. Divide it by √12 for a one-month, one-standard-deviation move, and by √52 for a week.\",\"It jumps when stocks fall, as investors pay up for protection, then tends to mean-revert. The index can't be bought directly, VIX products often lose on the roll, and a low VIX means cheap protection, not safety. As a measure it's mechanical; as a signal, grade C.\"],\"example\":\"VIX 28, index 4,000: weekly move = 28 ÷ √52 = 3.88%, or 155.3 points, so the one-sigma week runs from 3,844.7 to 4,155.3.\",\"related\":[\"put-call-ratio\",\"volatility\",\"volatility-clustering\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.3\"]},{\"id\":\"put-call-ratio\",\"name\":\"Put/call ratio\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Put volume divided by call volume, read against its own shifting baseline and mostly at extremes.\",\"body\":[\"The index ratio mostly reflects institutional hedging, the equity ratio is the cleaner speculative gauge, and the total blends both. Each runs at its own typical level.\",\"The total ratio averaged about 0.97 in 2007–2022, with extremes above 1.23 and below 0.72. The rise of same-day (0DTE) options moved the baseline, so fixed thresholds stopped working. Read the ratio against its recent history; as a signal it's grade C.\"],\"example\":\"After the 0DTE boom, a total put/call of 1.30 is beyond the old 1.23 extreme, but not necessarily extreme against the new baseline: compare it with recent months before calling it fear.\",\"related\":[\"vix\",\"market-sentiment\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.3\"]},{\"id\":\"short-squeeze\",\"name\":\"Short squeeze\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A sharp rise driven by short sellers forced to buy back, often far larger than the news that started it.\",\"body\":[\"Squeeze fuel is short interest that can't exit fast: a high share of the float, many days to cover, and a rising borrow fee that signals scarce shares and possible recalls. A small surprise triggers stops; the forced buying lifts price, which triggers more stops.\",\"The forced buying is mechanical; predicting which crowded short squeezes, and when, is grade C. Social-media hype is grade D and easy to fake: late buyers in a pump often become exit liquidity for insiders and early holders.\"],\"example\":\"LABE: 30% of float short, 8 days to cover. A +2% news day led to a 27.00 high, +35%, on volume of 9.4m shares, more than six times normal.\",\"related\":[\"short-selling\",\"positioning\",\"stop-cascade\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.4\"]},{\"id\":\"calendar-effects\",\"name\":\"Calendar effects\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Return patterns tied to dates, such as the turn of the month or seasons, each with its own evidence grade and decay record.\",\"body\":[\"The turn of the month is the best supported (grade B), yet a Fed working paper finds it gone in S&P futures after 1990. Sell in May is B/C, the January effect C, pre-holiday C/D; the Monday effect, the January barometer, the presidential cycle and crypto calendar effects are D. Across published anomalies, returns shrink after publication (grade A).\",\"Calendar scans invite data mining: 30 overlapping tests on pure random walks found a 'significant' pattern in 66% of 2,000 simulated histories. A real effect must come back on a hold-out you check once. Commodity seasons are physical, but the futures curve already prices them.\"],\"example\":\"Twelve independent tests at 5% on data with no effect: the chance of at least one false hit is 1 − 0.95¹² = 46.0%.\",\"related\":[\"evidence-grades\",\"random-walk\",\"sample-size\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.9.6\"]},{\"id\":\"return-distribution\",\"name\":\"Return distribution\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The full shape of outcomes (mean, median, spread, skew and tails), not just the average.\",\"body\":[\"An average tells you what a system earns per trade over many trades. It doesn't tell you what a typical trade looks like, how far results scatter, which side has the long tail, or how extreme the extremes get. Those decide how the system feels to trade and how it can hurt you.\",\"Describe any set of results by its centre (mean and median), its spread (standard deviation), its skew and its tails. When the mean sits above the median, rare large wins carry the system; when it sits below, rare large losses lurk.\"],\"example\":\"The reference system wins 40% of trades at +2.5R and loses 60% at −1R: mean +0.40R, median −1R, standard deviation 1.71R. Most trades lose, yet the system earns, because the rare wins are 2.5 times the losses.\",\"related\":[\"skewness\",\"fat-tails\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.1\"]},{\"id\":\"fat-tails\",\"name\":\"Fat tails\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Extreme moves occur far more often than a normal distribution predicts.\",\"body\":[\"Market returns are not bell-shaped. Mandelbrot (1963) found cotton price changes with a tail parameter near 1.7, where a normal distribution has 2, and Fama found the same long tails in Dow 30 stocks. Multi-sigma days that a normal model calls once-in-millennia recur within years.\",\"The costs are practical: stops get gapped through, risk models built on the bell curve understate losses, the Kelly formula oversizes, and a Sharpe ratio flatters strategies with rare large losses. Plan for the tail, not the average day.\"],\"example\":\"At 1% daily volatility, a normal model puts a five-sigma drop at 2.9 in 10 million days, once in about 13,800 years. A Student-t model with 3 degrees of freedom and the same volatility gives 16 per 10,000 days, once every 2.5 years. In seeded runs of 10,000 days: 0 and 16.\",\"related\":[\"return-distribution\",\"skewness\",\"gap-risk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.1\"]},{\"id\":\"skewness\",\"name\":\"Skewness\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Asymmetry of outcomes: positive skew means rare large wins, negative skew means rare large losses.\",\"body\":[\"Skew says which side of the distribution has the long tail. Trend following tends toward positive skew: many small losses, rare big wins, and long losing streaks to sit through. Mean reversion and option selling tend toward negative skew: many small wins and an occasional loss that erases weeks of them.\",\"Negative skew is dangerous because it hides. A short record may contain none of the rare losses, so the measured volatility, and a Sharpe ratio built on it, look better than the strategy is.\"],\"example\":\"A trend system winning 30% of trades at +3.5R and losing 70% at −1R has a mean of +0.35R, a median of −1R and a skew of +0.87. A premium seller winning 92% at +0.3R and losing 8% at −3R has a skew of −3.1 and a mean of only +0.036R.\",\"related\":[\"return-distribution\",\"fat-tails\",\"trend-following\",\"mean-reversion\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.1\"]},{\"id\":\"square-root-of-time\",\"name\":\"Square-root-of-time scaling\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Volatility grows with the square root of the horizon when returns are independent.\",\"body\":[\"When each day is independent of the last, the variances of daily returns add up, so the standard deviation over n days is the daily one times √n. That's how daily volatility becomes weekly (× √5) or annual (× √252), and how a price cone widens.\",\"The rule needs two things that markets don't always give: independent days and a stable volatility. With positive day-to-day correlation, weeks are wider than the rule says; after a regime switch, a cone measured in calm weather is too narrow.\"],\"example\":\"1% a day is 1% × √5 = 2.24% a week and 1% × √252 = 15.9% a year. With a day-to-day correlation of +0.2, the same market moves about 2.62% a week.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"volatility-clustering\",\"autocorrelation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.2\"]},{\"id\":\"bayesian-updating\",\"name\":\"Bayesian updating\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Combining a base rate with new evidence to get the probability that a hypothesis is true.\",\"body\":[\"Start from the prior: how common real edges are among ideas like yours. A test result then moves that probability according to how often the test passes real edges (its power) and useless ones (its false-alarm rate). Counting 1,000 cases makes the arithmetic easy and keeps the large pool of useless ideas in sight.\",\"Evidence from trades arrives slowly. A few good trades move a belief only a little, and even a real edge can take hundreds of trades to look convincing.\"],\"example\":\"10% of ideas are real; a test passes 80% of real ones and 20% of useless ones. Of 1,000 ideas, 80 real and 180 useless pass: a pass is real 31% of the time. Cut false alarms to 5% and it's 80 ÷ 125 = 64%.\",\"related\":[\"base-rates\",\"statistical-significance\",\"luck-vs-skill\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.4\"]},{\"id\":\"statistical-significance\",\"name\":\"Statistical significance\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Whether a result, such as mean R, is distinguishable from zero given its sample size and variability.\",\"body\":[\"Divide the average by its standard error, sd ÷ √n, to get a t-statistic. Around 2 or more, a result this far from zero would rarely come from a system with no edge; the p-value puts a number on 'rarely'. SQN is the same t-statistic, applied to mean R.\",\"A p-value is not the probability that the edge is real; that also needs a prior. And small edges take many trades: the number needed for t = 2 is (2 × sd ÷ mean)².\"],\"example\":\"+0.2R a trade with sd 1.5R over 50 trades: t = 0.2 ÷ (1.5 ÷ √50) = 0.94, p ≈ 0.35. In 10,000 seeded no-edge samples, 34% landed at least that far from zero. Reaching t = 2 at this edge takes (2 × 1.5 ÷ 0.2)² = 225 trades.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"sqn\",\"bayesian-updating\",\"multiple-testing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.5\"]},{\"id\":\"sharpe-ratio\",\"name\":\"Sharpe ratio\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Excess return per unit of volatility, meaningful only together with its standard error and sample length.\",\"body\":[\"The Sharpe ratio divides the average return above cash by the standard deviation of returns, usually annualised: a daily Sharpe times √252, valid when days are independent. It rewards steady returns and punishes noisy ones.\",\"Read it with its standard error, about 1 ÷ √years with daily data. A short record says little, and a strategy with rare large losses can show a high Sharpe until the loss arrives.\"],\"example\":\"A mean daily excess return of 0.05% with a 1% daily sd is a daily Sharpe of 0.05, or 0.79 a year. With a true Sharpe of 1.0, 10,000 seeded histories gave measured values from −0.11 to 2.12 after 3 years (middle 95%) and from 0.39 to 1.63 after 10.\",\"related\":[\"statistical-significance\",\"skewness\",\"performance-metrics\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.5\"]},{\"id\":\"multiple-testing\",\"name\":\"Multiple testing\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Running many tests makes some look significant by chance, so thresholds must account for every trial.\",\"body\":[\"Each useless rule tested at 5% has a 5% chance to look significant. Test N of them and the chance that at least one does is 1 − 0.95^N: 64% for 20. Every parameter tweak and every discarded rule counts as a test.\",\"Corrections raise the bar. Bonferroni divides the threshold by the number of tests; it controls false discoveries but misses modest real edges. A random-walk control helps too: if the same search finds as many 'discoveries' on random data, yours are noise.\"],\"example\":\"Twenty rules on three years of random-walk data: the best, 20-day momentum, had p = 3.2%. The corrected bar was 5% ÷ 20 = 0.25%. Across 1,000 random-walk series, the same search found a p under 5% in 44.5% of them, and one under 0.25% in 3.1%.\",\"related\":[\"statistical-significance\",\"spurious-correlation\",\"random-walk\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.6\"]},{\"id\":\"spurious-correlation\",\"name\":\"Spurious correlation\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A correlation that appears by chance or through a shared trend and vanishes on new data.\",\"body\":[\"Search enough series and one will line up with yours by chance. The more candidates and the shorter the window, the stronger the best fake match. Series that both trend can also look related when they only share a direction.\",\"The test is new data. A real relationship holds up out of sample; a spurious one falls back toward zero, often at once.\"],\"example\":\"Out of 100 random series, the one that best matched LABX's monthly returns over 24 months had a correlation of 0.52; over the next 24 months, 0.00. Across 1,000 such searches, the best in-sample correlation averaged 0.50 and the out-of-sample one 0.00.\",\"related\":[\"multiple-testing\",\"random-walk\",\"correlation-and-concentration\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.6\"]},{\"id\":\"autocorrelation\",\"name\":\"Autocorrelation\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Dependence of a value on earlier values; its sign separates trending, mean-reverting and random series.\",\"body\":[\"Lag-1 autocorrelation is the correlation of each return with the one before. Positive means moves continue, the fingerprint of a trend; negative means they reverse, the fingerprint of a range; near zero is a random walk.\",\"Trade outcomes can be autocorrelated too. A runs test counts streaks of wins and losses and compares them with what independence predicts. When results cluster, tools that assume independence mislead: Kelly, risk-of-ruin formulas, reshuffled Monte Carlo, the √t rule and annualised Sharpe ratios.\"],\"example\":\"40 wins and 60 losses should form 2 × 40 × 60 ÷ 100 + 1 = 49 runs, with a standard deviation of 4.77. 38 runs give z = −2.30: the outcomes cluster. Only 1.32% of 10,000 random shuffles had 38 runs or fewer.\",\"related\":[\"market-regime\",\"random-walk\",\"volatility-clustering\",\"square-root-of-time\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.10.7\"]},{\"id\":\"edge-hypothesis\",\"name\":\"Edge hypothesis\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A falsifiable statement of why a pattern should pay, under which conditions, and who is on the other side.\",\"body\":[\"Write it before any test: because [mechanism], when [condition], price tends to [effect] over [horizon] in [market or regime]. The other side is [who], because [why]. A hypothesis that names its mechanism and its losers can be checked, and it narrows the search: you test what the story predicts, not every indicator setting.\",\"It must be falsifiable: some result has to count against it. Claims with escape clauses, such as relabelling after the fact, can't fail, so they can't be tested.\"],\"example\":\"After volatility contracts, breakouts in the direction of the higher-timeframe trend continue more often than chance over 10 bars, because stops cluster beyond the range; the other side is traders whose stops are run. Test: +2R before −1R in more than 40% of 20-bar breakouts with a rising 50-bar average.\",\"related\":[\"falsifiability\",\"testable-reformulation\",\"codified-rules\",\"research-log\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.1\"]},{\"id\":\"codified-rules\",\"name\":\"Codified rules\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Rules precise enough that two people, or a program, produce identical trades from the same data.\",\"body\":[\"A codified rule has five parts: a condition, a trigger, a stop, an exit and, while testing, a fixed 1R size. Each part is a number, a level or a yes-or-no test that uses only data known at the decision.\",\"Vague words such as 'strong' or 'recent' let two traders build two different backtests from one idea, and let hindsight pick the better one.\"],\"example\":\"'Buy strong breakouts in an uptrend', read by two traders on the first 40 breakouts of a LABX test: 18 buys and 20 buys, only 13 in common. Codified: close above the 20-bar high, 50-bar average above its value 10 bars earlier, stop 1 ATR(14), exit at +2R or after 10 bars. Any reader takes the same trades.\",\"related\":[\"if-then-rules\",\"edge-hypothesis\",\"look-ahead-bias\",\"setup-card\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.1\"]},{\"id\":\"research-log\",\"name\":\"Research log\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"An immutable record of every hypothesis, test and parameter change, including the number of trials.\",\"body\":[\"The log starts before the first test, with the hypothesis, the prior, who is on the other side, the data and the target regime. Every run after that is a trial: the discarded ones, the extra looks at a hold-out and the walk-forward variants included.\",\"The trial count is what lets anyone judge a result: the best of 10 zero-edge tries shows a Sharpe near 1.5 by luck. Bailey and colleagues point out that a backtest reported without its number of trials can't be assessed for overfitting.\"],\"example\":\"Trials to date: 14. Entry 12 widened the stop from 1 to 1.5 ATR after the hold-out lost money, so the hold-out became in-sample and the system went back to forward testing on new data.\",\"related\":[\"data-snooping\",\"in-sample-out-of-sample\",\"journaling\",\"rule-change-governance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.1\",\"3.11.10\"]},{\"id\":\"fill-assumptions\",\"name\":\"Fill assumptions\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The rules a backtest uses to turn signals into executions; conservative choices avoid flattering results.\",\"body\":[\"Conservative fills: a signal from a bar's close fills at the next bar's open; when the stop and the target share a bar, the stop came first; a limit fills only if price trades through it; large orders may fill only in part.\",\"How much they matter depends on the stop's size relative to the bars. On the course system, with a 1-ATR stop and a 2R target, stop and target never shared a bar. On a tight variant they often did, and the optimistic rules invented most of the edge.\"],\"example\":\"Tight variant, stop 0.5 ATR, target 1R and a 5-bar exit, ten synthetic LABX years: 12 of 136 trades had stop and target in one bar. Gross result: +0.001R a trade with conservative fills, +0.156R with optimistic ones.\",\"related\":[\"slippage\",\"trading-costs\",\"queue-priority\",\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.2\"]},{\"id\":\"breakeven-cost\",\"name\":\"Breakeven cost\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The trading cost at which a strategy's net expectancy falls to zero.\",\"body\":[\"Express costs in R, cost per unit ÷ stop distance; net expectancy = gross expectancy − costs in R. The breakeven cost multiple, gross expectancy ÷ current costs, says how many times today's costs the edge could absorb.\",\"A multiple near 1 leaves no room for a wider spread at the open, slippage on news or a worse broker. Stress every result at double costs.\"],\"example\":\"The course system: +0.255R gross, costs 0.114R a trade, net +0.141R. Breakeven multiple 0.255 ÷ 0.114 = 2.24. At double costs it nets +0.027R; at triple, −0.087R.\",\"related\":[\"costs-in-r\",\"cost-drag\",\"trading-costs\",\"after-cost-evaluation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.2\"]},{\"id\":\"in-sample-out-of-sample\",\"name\":\"In-sample / out-of-sample\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Developing rules on one part of the data and testing them once on an untouched later part.\",\"body\":[\"Split in time order, never at random: develop and tune on the earlier in-sample part, about 70%, and keep the later out-of-sample part locked. Unlock it once, with the pass rule written first.\",\"Any change made after looking at the out-of-sample turns it into in-sample and adds a trial to the log. And a short out-of-sample says little: one year at a Sharpe of 1.0 has a standard error of 1.22.\"],\"example\":\"The course system, lookback tuned on 7 of 10 synthetic years: +0.162R a trade in-sample. The 3 locked years: 24 trades at +0.066R, with a 95% interval from −0.52R to +0.65R. Positive, but not distinguishable from zero.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"statistical-significance\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"overfitting\",\"research-log\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.4\"]},{\"id\":\"overfitting\",\"name\":\"Overfitting\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Fitting rules to the noise of one sample so that results collapse on new data.\",\"body\":[\"The signs: many tuned parameters, few trades for each, a curve that's too smooth. Keep optimised parameters to 3–5 and want many trades for each one.\",\"Selection alone creates impressive numbers: the best of 10 zero-edge strategies shows a one-year Sharpe near 1.55. As filters are added, in-sample results keep rising while out-of-sample results peak early and then fall. Every result should run next to an R0 random-walk control.\"],\"example\":\"On 120 seeded trending series, filters picked one at a time for the best in-sample result raised it from +0.12R to +0.81R a trade over eight filters. Out of sample: +0.08R, a peak of +0.14R at three filters, then +0.07R at eight.\",\"related\":[\"data-snooping\",\"multiple-testing\",\"random-walk\",\"in-sample-out-of-sample\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.5\"]},{\"id\":\"data-snooping\",\"name\":\"Data snooping\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Reusing the same data for many tests or tweaks until something looks good by chance.\",\"body\":[\"Each test on the same data is another draw, and the best of many draws looks good by luck. Filters added to remove particular losing trades are the most common form: the data chose the rule.\",\"The defences: count every trial in the research log, test new ideas on data that played no part in finding them, and run the R0 control, where a snooped rule adds nothing.\"],\"example\":\"100 random moving-average rules on one R0 year: the best scored a Sharpe of 1.93, and +0.25 the next year. Across 500 such runs, the best year-one Sharpe averaged 1.30 and the next year's −0.08.\",\"related\":[\"overfitting\",\"multiple-testing\",\"research-log\",\"random-walk\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.5\"]},{\"id\":\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"name\":\"Walk-forward analysis\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Repeatedly optimising on one window and testing on the next unseen one, then joining the test segments.\",\"body\":[\"Tune on a window, trade the next segment unseen, roll forward and repeat; only the unseen segments count. Walk-forward efficiency, out-of-sample annual return ÷ in-sample annual return, summarises how much survives. 0.5 is a practitioners' rule of thumb, not a standard.\",\"Watch the chosen parameters too: stable choices suggest structure, choices that jump across the range suggest noise. Choose anchored or rolling windows, and their length, before running: tuning the walk-forward is meta-overfitting.\"],\"example\":\"The course system, 4-year rolling windows, six unseen years: lookbacks 22, 22, 25, 20, 20, 20; in-sample +1.74R a year, out of sample +0.89R, WFE 0.51. On an R0 control the choices jumped from 10 to 40, and the unseen years lost 10.42R.\",\"related\":[\"in-sample-out-of-sample\",\"overfitting\",\"parameter-plateau\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.6\"]},{\"id\":\"monte-carlo-resampling\",\"name\":\"Monte Carlo resampling\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Reshuffling or bootstrapping a trade list to see the range of drawdowns and outcomes it could produce.\",\"body\":[\"A reshuffle keeps the same trades in new orders: same total, different drawdowns. A bootstrap draws with replacement, so the total varies too. Plan drawdowns from the 95th percentile and kill switches from the 99th, not from the single backtest.\",\"Monte Carlo can't create an edge and inherits the backtest's fill and cost assumptions. Reshuffling breaks real clustering of results; if they cluster, resample blocks of consecutive trades.\"],\"example\":\"100 trades, 40 at +2.5R and 60 at −1R; the backtest's worst drawdown was 7R. 10,000 reshuffles: median 8.5R, 90th percentile 12R, 95th 13.5R, 99th 16.5R. At 1% risk per trade, plan for about 13.5%.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"expected-drawdown\",\"drawdown-tiers\",\"variance\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.7\"]},{\"id\":\"expectation-corridor\",\"name\":\"Expectation corridor\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The band of results a validated system should produce by chance alone, used to tell variance from breakage.\",\"body\":[\"From the Monte Carlo, or the system's binomial odds, mark the 5th to 95th percentile of the average result after a given number of trades, and the drawdown percentiles. Inside the corridor, keep executing; outside it, investigate before anything else.\",\"The corridor turns 'it stopped working' into a measurable claim instead of a feeling, and it sets the gates and the kill switch for incubation.\"],\"example\":\"Reference system, 40% winners at +2.5R: after 50 trades the average result sits between −0.02R and +0.82R (5th to 95th percentile). After 20 trades a net loss has a 12.6% chance. A live drawdown of 11R is inside a 95th percentile of 13.5R.\",\"related\":[\"mistake-vs-variance\",\"variance\",\"monte-carlo-resampling\",\"kill-switch\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.7\"]},{\"id\":\"robustness-testing\",\"name\":\"Robustness testing\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Checking that results survive perturbations such as higher costs, added noise, other markets and missing best trades.\",\"body\":[\"The stress battery: double the costs, drop the best 5% of trades, add noise to prices, start at a later date, run another market or timeframe. A robust edge weakens; a fitted one collapses. The same battery on the R0 control should fail.\",\"Read outlier dependence by type: trend followers rely on rare large winners by design, while a mean reverter that needs its best trades is fragile.\"],\"example\":\"The course system at lookback 18: base +0.127R; double costs +0.014R; without its best 5 trades +0.025R, profit factor 1.20 → 1.04; noise +0.213R; a year later start +0.103R; another R1 market +0.221R. Five of five positive; the R0 control, one of five.\",\"related\":[\"parameter-plateau\",\"overfitting\",\"performance-metrics\",\"breakeven-cost\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.8\"]},{\"id\":\"parameter-plateau\",\"name\":\"Parameter plateau\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A broad region of parameter values with similar results, preferred over an isolated peak.\",\"body\":[\"Map results across parameter values, stepped in percent (10, 11, 12.1, 13.3…), and choose the middle of a wide region where neighbours behave alike. A cell far above its neighbours is more likely luck.\",\"The course's design rule: the neighbours' average must be at least 80% of the chosen cell. It's a convention, not a law; its job is to make you look at the neighbours before the cell.\"],\"example\":\"Course system lookbacks in 10% steps: 18 earns +0.127R and its neighbours the same, 100%. 24 earns the most, +0.179R, but its neighbours average +0.138R, 77%. 38 earns +0.147R with neighbours at +0.101R, 69%.\",\"related\":[\"robustness-testing\",\"overfitting\",\"walk-forward-analysis\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.8\"]},{\"id\":\"random-entry-benchmark\",\"name\":\"Random-entry benchmark\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Many twins of a strategy with random entries and the same exits, used to measure what the entry adds.\",\"body\":[\"Build 1,000 twins that keep your exits, market and trade count but enter at random. Your percentile among them measures the entry: near 50, it adds nothing; at 95 or more, it probably adds something. Keep buy-and-hold on the chart as the ghost.\",\"Profit can come from the entry, from the exits or from the market's drift. Twins separate the first from the other two.\"],\"example\":\"A breakout system made +17.1% in ten synthetic years while LABX rose 5.7-fold. Its 1,000 twins made −6.9% to +41.8% (5th to 95th percentile), median +15.4%, and the system beat 53.8% of them: the drift did the work.\",\"related\":[\"edge-ratio\",\"passive-benchmark\",\"mae-mfe\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.9\"]},{\"id\":\"edge-ratio\",\"name\":\"Edge ratio\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Average favourable excursion divided by average adverse excursion after entry; random entries score about 1.\",\"body\":[\"Measure MFE and MAE a set number of bars after each entry signal, in ATR, and divide the averages. Exits play no part, so the ratio tests the entry alone.\",\"Compare with random entries on the same data: in a rising market they score above 1 too. Plot the ratio against holding time to see how long the entry's information lasts.\"],\"example\":\"The course system's signals, 10 bars on: MFE 2.32 ATR, MAE 1.61 ATR, e-ratio 1.44. Random entries on the same series: 1.26. By 20 bars the two meet, 1.33 against 1.32.\",\"related\":[\"mae-mfe\",\"random-entry-benchmark\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.9\"]},{\"id\":\"incubation\",\"name\":\"Incubation\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Forward-testing a validated system at small size before scaling, with gates based on statistics and process.\",\"body\":[\"The pipeline: a forward test in the simulator, a small account, size steps, then full size. Each stage adds what a backtest can't show: live fills, your execution and your behaviour in a losing run.\",\"Gates are written before the first trade: after a set number of trades, results inside the Monte Carlo band and process KPIs on target. Size grows on process and statistics, never on profit alone.\"],\"example\":\"Reference system gate: after 50 forward trades, average R between −0.02R and +0.82R and process KPIs on target, then double the size. Kill switch: a drawdown past 16.5R, or an expectancy interval entirely below zero.\",\"related\":[\"kill-switch\",\"expectation-corridor\",\"process-kpis\",\"trading-plan\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.10\"]},{\"id\":\"kill-switch\",\"name\":\"Kill switch\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A pre-written condition that stops a system when its results fall outside what variance can explain.\",\"body\":[\"Set it from the system's own Monte Carlo: a drawdown beyond the 99th percentile, or a 95% interval of expectancy entirely below zero. A trigger inside normal variance, such as the backtest's own drawdown, would stop a healthy system.\",\"Write it before going live and don't move it during a drawdown. When it fires, the system goes back to research, not to a quick tweak.\"],\"example\":\"Reference system: 95th-percentile drawdown 13.5R, 99th 16.5R, so the kill sits at 16.5R or beyond. A kill at the backtest's 7R would have fired in 71.4% of the reshuffled histories of the same trades.\",\"related\":[\"expectation-corridor\",\"incubation\",\"drawdown-tiers\",\"monte-carlo-resampling\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.10\"]},{\"id\":\"premortem\",\"name\":\"Premortem\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Imagining that a plan has already failed and listing the reasons, before committing to it.\",\"body\":[\"Before a system goes live, assume it's six months later and it has failed, and write down why (Klein, 2007): costs were higher live, the regime changed, signals were overridden, the edge was a fit. Imagined hindsight brings up risks that confidence hides.\",\"Each reason becomes a check you can run or a rule in the plan, so the premortem changes what you do, not only how you feel.\"],\"example\":\"Five reasons and their checks: higher live costs → compare fills with the cost model weekly; a regime change → a regime label on every trade; skipped signals → a KPI for signals taken; a fitted edge → R0 control and walk-forward on file; size raised early → size only through the gates.\",\"related\":[\"incubation\",\"confirmation-bias\",\"trading-plan\",\"overconfidence\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.11.10\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-drag\",\"name\":\"Volatility drag\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Swings pull compound growth below the average return, by roughly σ²/2 per period.\",\"body\":[\"Returns compound by multiplying. +50% then −50% averages 0% but leaves 0.75 of the account: a geometric mean of −13.4% per period. Compound growth g sits below the average return μ by about half the variance: g ≈ μ − σ²/2.\",\"Drag grows with the square of the swings, so leverage multiplies it: a 2× daily fund carries four times its index's drag and can lose money while the index ends flat.\"],\"example\":\"μ = 10% a year with σ = 30%: g ≈ 10% − 0.30² ÷ 2 = 5.5%. An index that goes +10% then −9.09% ends flat; a 2× fund ends at 1.20 × 0.8182 = 0.982.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.1\"]},{\"id\":\"time-vs-ensemble-average\",\"name\":\"Time vs ensemble average\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"A trader lives one path over time; the typical path can sit far below the average across many traders.\",\"body\":[\"The ensemble average averages many traders at one moment; the time average follows one trader through many trades. With compounding, a few enormous paths pull the ensemble average up while the median path lags.\",\"Since you live one path, judge a sizing rule by its median and its bad tail, not by its average result.\"],\"example\":\"Reference system at 16% risk, 100 trades, 100,000 simulated paths: the average ends at 481×, the median at 20×, and 13% of paths lose money.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-drag\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.1\"]},{\"id\":\"fixed-ratio-sizing\",\"name\":\"Fixed-ratio sizing\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Adding one lot each time profit grows by another, larger step, so size grows more slowly than profit.\",\"body\":[\"Ryan Jones's fixed ratio sets a delta Δ: lot N needs Δ × N(N − 1) ÷ 2 of cumulative profit, so lots = ½ × (1 + √(1 + 8P ÷ Δ)), rounded down.\",\"No formula gives the best delta. A larger delta adds lots more slowly, cutting both drawdown and growth; in practice it's tied to the drawdown you'd accept per lot.\"],\"example\":\"Δ = 3,000: 2 lots at 3,000 of profit, 3 at 9,000, 4 at 18,000. At 10,000 of profit the formula gives 3.13, so 3 lots.\",\"related\":[\"fixed-fractional-sizing\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.2\"]},{\"id\":\"kelly-criterion\",\"name\":\"Kelly criterion\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"The bet fraction that maximises long-run growth for a known edge; a ceiling, not a target.\",\"body\":[\"For a win rate W and a payoff of b R, Kelly is f* = W − (1 − W) ÷ b. Long-run growth per trade, g(f) = W · ln(1 + f · b) + (1 − W) · ln(1 − f), peaks at f* and crosses zero near twice f*.\",\"Kelly assumes a known edge, independent trades and fixed payoffs. In trading the edge is only estimated and full Kelly's drawdowns are extreme, so it serves as an upper limit.\"],\"example\":\"55% winners at 1.5R: f* = 0.55 − 0.45 ÷ 1.5 = 25%. Growth peaks at 0.0457 per trade; half Kelly gives 0.0344, twice Kelly −0.0041. The reference system's Kelly is 16%, with a median maximum drawdown of 82.7% over 100 trades.\",\"related\":[\"fractional-kelly\",\"position-sizing\",\"expectancy\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.3\"]},{\"id\":\"fractional-kelly\",\"name\":\"Fractional Kelly\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Betting a fraction of Kelly to cut drawdowns and to survive an overestimated edge.\",\"body\":[\"Half Kelly keeps about three quarters of the growth rate with far shallower drawdowns; a quarter is a common choice.\",\"An edge measured in-sample is usually too high. If the true edge is smaller, full Kelly can over-bet into negative growth while a fraction still grows.\"],\"example\":\"Kelly from an estimated 55% at 1.5R is 25%. If the true rate is 45%, 25% gives g = −0.0149 per trade, while half Kelly, 12.5%, still gives +0.0039.\",\"related\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.4\"]},{\"id\":\"optimal-f\",\"name\":\"Optimal f\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"The fixed fraction that would have maximised terminal wealth over a past trade list, scaled by its largest loss.\",\"body\":[\"Each trade's HPR is 1 + f × trade ÷ |largest loss|, and the TWR multiplies them. The f at the TWR peak is optimal f; equity per contract = largest loss ÷ f.\",\"It is fitted to the past: the next largest loss can be bigger, and a short list flatters the edge. Use a fraction of it, chosen by the single loss you can survive.\"],\"example\":\"On +300, −100, +200, −100, −100, +150, −100, +250, −100, +100: optimal f = 0.239, TWR 1.74, one contract per 418 vUSD. A loss of 150 at that f costs 35.9% of equity.\",\"related\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"fractional-kelly\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.4\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-of-ruin\",\"name\":\"Risk of ruin\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The probability that losses reach the drawdown at which you would stop trading.\",\"body\":[\"With even payoffs and a fixed stake, risk of ruin is ((1 − W) ÷ W)^N for N stakes of capital. With fixed fractional sizing the account never reaches zero, so ruin is defined by a drawdown threshold instead.\",\"For a real system, estimate it by Monte Carlo on out-of-sample trades and size so it stays under about 1%.\"],\"example\":\"Reference system, ruin at a 30% drawdown, 100 trades: 0.004% at 1% risk, 3.1% at 2%, 51.8% at 4%. The largest risk that stays under 1% is 1.6%.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.5\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-targeting\",\"name\":\"Volatility targeting\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Scaling exposure so a position's expected swing matches a set budget as volatility changes.\",\"body\":[\"Units = daily budget ÷ forecast daily move. A yearly target becomes daily by dividing by √252. When the forecast doubles, units halve.\",\"Forecasts lag jumps, so losses can exceed the budget for days after a volatility spike; in long calm spells, exposure creeps up. A cap on exposure handles the second; nothing fully removes the first.\"],\"example\":\"10% a year on 10,000.00 is 63.00 a day. With LABX moving 1.50 a day: 42 units. At 3.00: 21 units. At 0.50 the formula asks for 126, but buying power caps it at 99.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"volatility-clustering\",\"position-sizing\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.6\"]},{\"id\":\"equity-curve-filter\",\"name\":\"Equity-curve filter\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Pausing a system while its equity is below its own moving average, while still tracking shadow trades.\",\"body\":[\"The filter bets that a falling equity curve keeps falling. That holds only when outcomes cluster; on independent trades it skips trades with the same edge, and in trend following it tends to miss the big wins that follow losing runs.\",\"Fix the average's length in advance: choosing the best of many lengths is overfitting. Keep shadow trades so you know when to resume.\"],\"example\":\"4,000 simulated lists of 500 trades each: clustered outcomes went from +61R to +87R with the filter, independent ones from +200R to +161.5R, and trend following from +48R to +14R.\",\"related\":[\"drawdown\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.12.7\"]},{\"id\":\"time-series-momentum\",\"name\":\"Time-series momentum\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Going long assets whose own past returns were positive and short those whose past returns were negative.\",\"body\":[\"Each market is judged on its own past: long if its last 12 months beat cash, short if not, with every position sized to the same volatility. Moskowitz, Ooi and Pedersen (2012) found the 12-month rule profitable in all 58 liquid futures they tested, across stock indices, currencies, commodities and bonds: grade A.\",\"Returns tended to persist for 1 to 12 months and partly reverse later. The Turtle rules are one public, crowded version of the idea; their exact parameters faded while the effect survived. Expect low win rates, long losing runs and multi-year flat stretches.\"],\"example\":\"Seeded test, 12 synthetic markets, 30 years: all 12 profitable, against 4 of 12 random-walk controls. The portfolio's Sharpe ratio was 1.13, yet it spent its first 35 months below its first peak.\",\"related\":[\"trend-following\",\"percent-volatility-sizing\",\"cross-sectional-momentum\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.1\"]},{\"id\":\"cross-sectional-momentum\",\"name\":\"Cross-sectional momentum\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Buying recent relative winners and selling relative losers within a universe.\",\"body\":[\"Assets are ranked against each other on past return, usually 12 months skipping the latest one (12-1), because one-month returns tend to reverse. The top group is bought and the bottom group sold, rebalanced monthly. Jegadeesh and Titman (1993) found it in US stocks; Asness, Moskowitz and Pedersen (2013) across eight markets and asset classes. Grade A, decayed but persistent.\",\"Turnover, the cost of borrowing the shorts and crowding eat into it, and it crashes when losers rebound after a panic. A long-only version avoids the short-leg crash but keeps the market's beta.\"],\"example\":\"12-1 return: 12 months +22%, last month −3%: 1.22 ÷ 0.97 − 1 = +25.8%. That ranks above an asset with +30% over 12 months but +12% last month: 1.30 ÷ 1.12 − 1 = +16.1%.\",\"related\":[\"time-series-momentum\",\"momentum-crash\",\"short-selling\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.2\"]},{\"id\":\"momentum-crash\",\"name\":\"Momentum crash\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A sudden large loss for momentum strategies when beaten-down losers rebound sharply after a panic.\",\"body\":[\"After a deep market fall, the past losers are mostly high-beta assets. When the market turns up, they rise most, and the short leg of a winners-minus-losers book loses heavily. Daniel and Moskowitz (2016) found crashes follow market declines, while volatility is high, at the rebound.\",\"Scaling exposure down when the strategy's recent volatility is high reduces the damage; studies of risk-managed momentum found it nearly doubled the Sharpe ratio.\"],\"example\":\"March to May 2009: past losers +163%, past winners +8%. With 1 vUSD on each leg: 0.08 − 1.63 = −1.55 vUSD. In a seeded test, scaling cut a −21.5% crash month to −9.1%.\",\"related\":[\"cross-sectional-momentum\",\"percent-volatility-sizing\",\"tail-event\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.2\"]},{\"id\":\"carry\",\"name\":\"Carry\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The return from holding an asset if prices don't move, such as a rate differential, roll yield or funding; prone to crashes.\",\"body\":[\"Carry appears in every asset class: the swap on a currency pair, roll yield on a future in backwardation, coupon and roll-down on a bond, funding collected by long spot and short perpetual. Koijen, Moskowitz, Pedersen and Vrugt (2018) found it predicts returns across asset classes: grade A.\",\"Carry strategies did poorly at the same times across asset classes, in global recessions. Returns are negatively skewed: many small gains, rare large losses. Leverage has to be chosen from the jump, not from the average year.\"],\"example\":\"A 4% rate differential on 100,000 vUSD earns 10.96 vUSD a day. With a 20% yearly chance of a 10% jump, the 5th-percentile outcome over 10 years is −25.4% at 3× leverage and −61.3% at 5×.\",\"related\":[\"carry-trade\",\"carry-unwind\",\"return-skew\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.3\"]},{\"id\":\"false-discovery-rate\",\"name\":\"False discovery rate\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The share of flagged discoveries that are false.\",\"body\":[\"A 5% test lets about 1 noise case in 20 pass. Scan many noise markets and those false flags pile up, while the number of real effects stays fixed, so a growing share of what you 'discover' is noise.\",\"Confirming each flag once on hold-out data cuts the false share sharply. Calendar effects are where this bites most: every date pattern you look for has a chance to appear.\"],\"example\":\"3 real markets at 70% power give 2.1 true flags; 7 noise markets at 5% give 0.35 false ones: 0.35 ÷ 2.45 = 14%. With 100 noise markets: 5 ÷ 7.1 = 70%.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"random-walk\",\"luck-vs-skill\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.4\"]},{\"id\":\"pairs-trading\",\"name\":\"Pairs trading\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Trading the spread between two related instruments in the expectation that it reverts.\",\"body\":[\"Sell the spread when its z-score passes +2, buy it below −2, exit at 0, stop at about ±3.5, and close anything older than about two half-lives. It needs a cointegrated pair: correlation alone doesn't bring the spread back.\",\"The distance method earned up to 11% a year in US stocks from 1962 to 2002 (Gatev, Goetzmann and Rouwenhorst, 2006), but monthly returns fell from 0.86% to 0.37% to 0.24% in later periods (Do and Faff, 2010): grade B, decayed. Costs are paid on two legs, plus the borrow on the short.\"],\"example\":\"Spread 2.60 above its mean, standard deviation 1.20: z = 2.17, sell the spread. With θ = 0.1 a day, half-life = 0.693 ÷ 0.1 = 6.9 days, so the time stop is about 14 days.\",\"related\":[\"cointegration\",\"structural-break\",\"mean-reversion\",\"short-selling\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.5\"]},{\"id\":\"cointegration\",\"name\":\"Cointegration\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"A relationship in which a combination of two series stays stationary even though each one wanders.\",\"body\":[\"Two prices can each wander like random walks while A − β × B keeps returning to a stable mean. That's cointegration, and it's what makes a spread trade possible. Correlation measures daily co-movement and says nothing about whether a gap closes.\",\"Test the spread itself for stationarity on formation data, then measure its half-life. A pair that tests cointegrated can still break later, so the stop stays.\"],\"example\":\"Seeded pairs: one with prices correlated 0.96 had a spread test t of −2.05 and drifted to 6.2 standard deviations; one correlated 0.93 tested at −3.83, with a 5.9-day half-life, and kept coming back.\",\"related\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"spurious-cycles\",\"correlation-instability\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.5\"]},{\"id\":\"structural-break\",\"name\":\"Structural break\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"A lasting change that ends a previously stable relationship.\",\"body\":[\"A merger, a new business model or a regime change can rewire the link between two prices or the behaviour of one. After it, estimates from the past, the mean, the hedge ratio, the half-life, no longer apply.\",\"A spread blowing through its stop on news is the classic sign. Exit at the stop, don't add, and re-test the relationship on new data before trading it again.\"],\"example\":\"Short a spread at z = 2.21; merger news sends it to 3.92. Exiting there costs 1.71 standard deviations; holding to the 14-day time stop would have cost 3.50.\",\"related\":[\"pairs-trading\",\"correlation-instability\",\"market-regime\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.5\"]},{\"id\":\"event-study\",\"name\":\"Event study\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Measuring the average abnormal return around many similar events, with a confidence band.\",\"body\":[\"For each event, subtract what the market explains (beta × market return) to get the abnormal return, then add it up over a window for the CAR. Average across many events and report the band: one event is an anecdote.\",\"Event effects decay once known. Post-earnings drift is much weaker since publication; the pre-FOMC drift (49 bp in the 24 hours before announcements, 1994–2011) is less reliable since. Write the rule that will stop the strategy before the first trade.\"],\"example\":\"A stock rises 6% while the index rises 1% and its beta is 1.2: abnormal return 6% − 1.2% = 4.8%. In 40 seeded good-news events, the drift after day 0 was +2.58% with a band of −0.24% to +5.40%: not distinguishable from zero.\",\"related\":[\"pre-event-drift\",\"news-surprise\",\"sample-size\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.6\"]},{\"id\":\"strategy-crowding\",\"name\":\"Strategy crowding\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Many traders holding the same strategy, which shrinks its edge and makes losses arrive together.\",\"body\":[\"As a strategy becomes known, more money runs it: its edge shrinks, and its holders share the same exit. When a shock forces some of them to sell, prices move against all of them, triggering more selling.\",\"Published edges shrink: across 97 predictors, returns were 26% lower out of sample and 58% lower after publication (McLean and Pontiff, 2016). Crowded trades fell together around LTCM in 1998, in momentum in 2009 and in the yen carry unwind of 2024.\"],\"example\":\"A seeded R0 control: the best 10 of 200 no-edge strategies showed a Sharpe ratio of 0.87 in-sample and 0.08 out of sample. With a real edge, the best 10 fell from 1.73 to 0.85.\",\"related\":[\"carry-unwind\",\"evidence-grades\",\"strategy-regime-fit\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.7\"]},{\"id\":\"evidence-appraisal\",\"name\":\"Evidence appraisal\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Grading a strategy claim by its sample, costs, trials, out-of-sample tests, replication and mechanism.\",\"body\":[\"Ten checks: sample; status (working paper or peer-reviewed); a true out-of-sample test; realistic costs and fills; the number of trials; sensitivity to parameters; independent replication; the mechanism and who's on the other side; capacity; and decay after publication.\",\"The result is a grade from Mechanic to F, defended in two sentences: what the evidence shows and what's missing. Without independent replication, a claim rarely rises above C.\"],\"example\":\"An opening range breakout paper reported over 1,600% net and a Sharpe of 2.81. An independent replication found the gross edge, about +0.13R a trade, but nothing distinguishable from zero after costs: grade C.\",\"related\":[\"evidence-grades\",\"performance-claims\",\"falsifiability\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"3.13.8\"]},{\"id\":\"futures-contract\",\"name\":\"Futures contract\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A standardised, exchange-cleared agreement to buy or sell an underlying at a future date.\",\"body\":[\"A futures contract fixes everything but the price: the underlying, the size, the tick and the expiry. A clearing house stands between buyer and seller, and the contract's multiplier turns points into money.\",\"You post margin, a small share of the contract's value, so leverage is built in. Size in whole contracts from the stop: risk per contract = stop in points × value per point. When one contract is too big, a micro, a tenth of the size, gives finer steps.\"],\"example\":\"Account 100,000.00, risk 1,000.00, stop 30 points. One LXF risks 30 × 50 = 1,500.00: too much. One LXM risks 150.00, so 6 LXM risk 900.00.\",\"related\":[\"mark-to-market\",\"point-value\",\"leverage\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.1\"]},{\"id\":\"mark-to-market\",\"name\":\"Mark-to-market\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Daily settlement of futures gains and losses in cash, through variation margin.\",\"body\":[\"Each evening the exchange values every position at the settlement price and moves the day's gain or loss in cash. Losses come out of your margin; if it falls below the maintenance level, you top up to the initial level or the position is closed.\",\"The exchange can raise margins when volatility jumps, so a margin call can come even when price hasn't moved. Margin is collateral, not risk: the stop and the size set the risk.\"],\"example\":\"2 LXF on 25,000.00 of margin, maintenance 20,000.00. Day 1: −37.50 points, −3,750.00, 21,250.00 left. Day 2: −40 points, −4,000.00, 17,250.00: margin call, add 7,750.00.\",\"related\":[\"futures-contract\",\"margin-close-out\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.1\"]},{\"id\":\"contract-roll\",\"name\":\"Contract roll\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Closing an expiring futures contract and opening the next one, usually as one calendar-spread order.\",\"body\":[\"Futures expire, so a position held longer than one contract must roll: sell the expiring month and buy the next. One calendar-spread order does both legs at once, crossing one bid/ask instead of two, with no risk of price moving between the legs.\",\"Roll when trading moves to the next month, and always before first notice day on physically delivered contracts. After the roll, move the stop and target by the spread so the risk stays the same.\"],\"example\":\"Long LXF March at 5,000.00, stop 4,960.00. Roll by spread to June at 5,030.00 (spread 30.00): the June stop goes at 4,990.00, still 40 points away.\",\"related\":[\"continuous-contract\",\"term-structure\",\"futures-contract\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.2\"]},{\"id\":\"continuous-contract\",\"name\":\"Continuous contract\",\"category\":\"technical_analysis\",\"summary\":\"A long price series stitched from successive futures contracts and adjusted for roll gaps.\",\"body\":[\"Each contract lives a few months, so long charts join them at the rolls. The two contracts trade at different prices, so an unadjusted join leaves a gap no holder ever had, and breakout rules and indicators trade it.\",\"Back-adjustment adds each roll gap to every earlier price, or scales earlier prices by the ratio, so the series runs on smoothly. Use it for signals and backtests; use the real contract's prices for fills, stops and P&L.\"],\"example\":\"March closes at 5,000.00 and June at 5,030.00 on roll day. A pre-roll close of 4,962.50 becomes 4,992.50; proportionally, 4,962.50 × 5,030 ÷ 5,000 = 4,992.28.\",\"related\":[\"adjusted-price-data\",\"contract-roll\",\"look-ahead-bias\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.2\"]},{\"id\":\"term-structure\",\"name\":\"Term structure\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The curve of futures prices across expiries, in contango or in backwardation.\",\"body\":[\"Plot futures prices by expiry and you get the term structure. In contango later contracts sit above spot, because storage, financing and insurance cost money. In backwardation they sit below, usually when inventories are low and the commodity in hand earns a convenience yield.\",\"A rough fair price: future ≈ spot + cost of carry − convenience yield. The curve also prices known seasons, so a seasonal pattern pays only when the season beats what the curve expected.\"],\"example\":\"Spot 70.00, financing 6% a year (0.35 a month), storage and insurance 0.65 a month: a fair one-month future of 71.00, a contango curve.\",\"related\":[\"roll-yield\",\"contract-roll\",\"commodity-drivers\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.3\",\"3.14.4\"]},{\"id\":\"roll-yield\",\"name\":\"Roll yield\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The gain or loss from rolling futures as they converge to spot, even when spot does not move.\",\"body\":[\"A future converges to spot as it expires. A long that rolls in contango keeps buying above spot and losing the difference; in backwardation it buys below spot and gains it. Per roll, roll yield ≈ spot ÷ future − 1.\",\"It explains why commodity ETFs that hold and roll futures, and long products on VIX futures, can fall while spot is flat. It's a form of carry, and it depends on the curve staying put, which it often doesn't.\"],\"example\":\"Spot stays at 70.00 and the one-month future at 71.00. Each roll returns 70 ÷ 71 − 1 = −1.41%; twelve rolls compound to −15.7%.\",\"related\":[\"term-structure\",\"carry-trade\",\"contract-roll\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.3\"]},{\"id\":\"commodity-drivers\",\"name\":\"Commodity drivers\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Supply shocks, storage, inventories and scheduled reports that move commodity prices.\",\"body\":[\"Each commodity family has its own drivers: OPEC+ output and geopolitics for oil, weather and disease for crops and livestock, heating demand for natural gas, real yields and official buying for gold. Supply shocks arrive as jumps, and volatility stays high after them.\",\"Scheduled reports, such as weekly oil stocks and monthly crop estimates, move prices on the surprise. Daily price limits can lock a market and skip your stop, so positions held through reports are sized for the worst plausible gap.\"],\"example\":\"Long at 500.00, stop 485.00 (1R = 15.00). Two 5% limit-down days lock the market down to 451.25: a 48.75 loss, 3.25R.\",\"related\":[\"commodities-in-macro\",\"term-structure\",\"gap-risk\",\"news-surprise\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.4\"]},{\"id\":\"crypto-market-structure\",\"name\":\"Crypto market structure\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A 24/7 market with fragmented venues, thin weekends, no closing auction and very high volatility.\",\"body\":[\"Crypto never closes: there's no closing auction, and weekends and quiet hours are thin, so spreads widen and stops slip. Trading is split across many venues, some lightly regulated, so prices and reported volumes differ from one to the next.\",\"Volatility is several times that of large stock indices, so the same 1% risk means a much smaller position. Annualise with √365, since it trades every day. In sell-offs, coins tend to fall together.\"],\"example\":\"Risk 100.00 with a 2-ATR stop. LABX (ATR 1.5%): 3,300.00 of stock. LXC (ATR 4%): about 1,250.00. A 4% daily move annualises to about 76%.\",\"related\":[\"counterparty-risk\",\"percent-volatility-sizing\",\"weekend-and-holiday-risk\",\"venue-fragmentation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.5\"]},{\"id\":\"counterparty-risk\",\"name\":\"Counterparty risk\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The risk that the venue, broker or custodian holding your assets fails or freezes them.\",\"body\":[\"Coins or cash held at a venue are a claim on that firm. If it fails, withdrawals freeze and you queue with its creditors. Segregation rules and protection schemes vary, and crypto that isn't a security falls outside the US SIPC scheme.\",\"Counterparty risk is per venue, so cap how much sits on any one. Self-custody removes venue risk and adds your own: lost keys can't be recovered.\"],\"example\":\"10,000.00 held as 4,000.00, 3,000.00 and 2,000.00 on three venues plus 1,000.00 self-custodied: one failure can cost 40%. Capping each venue at 2,500.00 limits it to 25%.\",\"related\":[\"broker-due-diligence\",\"crypto-market-structure\",\"negative-balance-protection\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.5\"]},{\"id\":\"perpetual-swap\",\"name\":\"Perpetual swap\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A futures-like contract with no expiry, kept near spot by periodic funding payments.\",\"body\":[\"A perpetual swap never expires, so it never has to converge to spot. Funding payments between longs and shorts do that job: when the perpetual trades above its spot index, longs pay shorts, and the reverse below it.\",\"Positions are valued at a mark price built from a multi-venue index, which also triggers liquidations, so a wick on one venue's last price doesn't liquidate on its own. Leverage, funding and liquidation rules differ by venue.\"],\"example\":\"Last price on one venue wicks to 28,900 while the mark price dips only to 29,780: a long with liquidation at 29,200, measured on mark price, survives.\",\"related\":[\"funding-rate\",\"liquidation-price\",\"futures-contract\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.6\"]},{\"id\":\"funding-rate\",\"name\":\"Funding rate\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The periodic payment between longs and shorts in a perpetual swap; when positive, longs pay shorts.\",\"body\":[\"Funding is charged on position value, usually every 8 hours. 0.01% per 8 hours is 10.95% a year on the position, and leverage multiplies it as a share of margin. The rate changes every interval with the perpetual's premium to spot.\",\"Persistently positive funding marks crowded longs paying to stay in: a condition, not a trigger (grade C). A basis trade, long spot and short perpetual, earns funding while it's positive and pays when it flips.\"],\"example\":\"A 3× long, 30,000.00 on 10,000.00 of margin, held 14 days at +0.01% every 8 hours: 30,000.00 × 0.01% × 3 × 14 = 126.00.\",\"related\":[\"perpetual-swap\",\"carry-trade\",\"overnight-financing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.6\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidation-price\",\"name\":\"Liquidation price\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The price at which a leveraged position is force-closed because margin falls below the maintenance level.\",\"body\":[\"For an isolated long, liquidation ≈ entry × (1 − 1 ÷ leverage + maintenance margin rate); venues' exact formulas differ. Higher leverage pulls it toward entry: at 50× it's only 1.5% away.\",\"Set the stop first, size from it, then choose leverage: it changes only the margin. Keep liquidation well beyond the stop (this course uses twice the distance), because a wick on the mark price plus a liquidation fee can beat your stop.\"],\"example\":\"LXC at 30,000, stop 3%, risk 100.00: 3,333.33 of LXC. At 10× liquidation is 27,150 (−9.5%), well past the stop; at 25× it's 28,950, only 150 below the stop.\",\"related\":[\"leverage\",\"effective-leverage\",\"margin-close-out\",\"liquidation-cascade\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.7\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidation-cascade\",\"name\":\"Liquidation cascade\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Forced selling that triggers more liquidations in a self-reinforcing chain.\",\"body\":[\"When price falls into a band of liquidation prices, the venue sells those positions at market. The selling pushes price into the next band, and the chain feeds itself, like a stop cascade with leverage.\",\"When liquidations close below zero equity, an insurance fund covers the gap; if it runs out, auto-deleveraging closes profitable opposite positions. Cross margin delays liquidations but can let one loss take the whole account.\"],\"example\":\"From 30,000, longs at 50× liquidate near 29,550 (−1.5%) and at 25× near 28,950 (−3.5%). A 2% drop clears the 50× layer, and its forced selling can carry price on to the next.\",\"related\":[\"liquidation-price\",\"stop-cascade\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.7\"]},{\"id\":\"bond-duration\",\"name\":\"Bond duration\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The approximate percentage price change of a bond for a one-point change in yield.\",\"body\":[\"Duration turns a yield move into a price move: price change ≈ −duration × yield change. Longer maturities and lower coupons mean higher duration and more rate risk.\",\"It's a straight-line estimate. Convexity bends the real curve: falls are a little smaller and rises a little larger than duration says, which matters for big moves.\"],\"example\":\"A 10-year note with a 2.16% coupon at 100.00 has a duration of 8.95. Yields +1 point: estimate −8.95%, exact −8.52%. Yields −1 point: estimate +8.95%, exact +9.42%.\",\"related\":[\"bond-price-yield\",\"dv01\",\"yield-curve\",\"equity-duration\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.8\"]},{\"id\":\"dv01\",\"name\":\"DV01\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The change in a position's money value for a one-basis-point move in yield.\",\"body\":[\"DV01 ≈ duration × position value × 0.0001. It adds across positions, so it shows a book's total rate risk in money, and it sizes rate trades the way a stop sizes a stock trade.\",\"Futures carry their own DV01 per contract. Curve trades weight each leg by DV01 so both legs move the same money per basis point.\"],\"example\":\"1,000,000.00 of a note with duration 8.95: DV01 895.00, so a 25 bp rise costs 22,375.00. Three LXN at 80.00 each lose 2,400.00 on 10 bp; one loses 800.00.\",\"related\":[\"bond-duration\",\"position-sizing\",\"risk-per-trade\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"3.14.8\"]},{\"id\":\"option-contract\",\"name\":\"Call and put options\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"A contract giving the buyer the right, not the obligation, to buy (call) or sell (put) at a set price until expiry.\",\"body\":[\"A call is the right to buy the underlying at the strike price; a put, the right to sell it. The buyer pays a premium for the right and uses it only when it pays. The seller, or writer, collects the premium and takes on the obligation to deliver or buy if assigned.\",\"The buyer can lose at most the premium; that small, fixed cost controlling a whole contract is where options get their leverage. Being right about direction isn't enough: the move must also cover the premium.\"],\"example\":\"A 30-day LABX call with strike 100 costs 2.86, or 28.60 for a 10-unit contract. If LABX ends at 102.00, the call is worth 2.00 and the buyer loses 0.86 a unit despite the rise.\",\"related\":[\"option-moneyness\",\"option-payoff\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.1\"]},{\"id\":\"option-moneyness\",\"name\":\"Moneyness, intrinsic and extrinsic value\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Whether an option is in, at or out of the money, and how its premium splits into intrinsic and time value.\",\"body\":[\"A call is in the money when its strike is below the price, a put when its strike is above it; at the money means the strike is at the price. Only in-the-money options have intrinsic value: max(S − K, 0) for a call, max(K − S, 0) for a put.\",\"Everything in the premium above intrinsic value is extrinsic, or time, value: payment for the chance of a bigger move before expiry. It decays to zero by expiry.\"],\"example\":\"LABX at 100.00, 30 days, IV 25%. The call 95 at 5.97 is 5.00 intrinsic + 0.97 extrinsic. The put 105 at 6.08 is 5.00 + 1.08. The call 105 at 1.08 is out of the money: all 1.08 is extrinsic.\",\"related\":[\"option-contract\",\"option-pricing-model\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.1\"]},{\"id\":\"option-payoff\",\"name\":\"Option payoff at expiry\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The profit or loss of an option position at expiry as a function of the underlying price, with its breakevens.\",\"body\":[\"Four building blocks: long call, short call, long put, short put. A long option's payoff is its value at expiry minus the premium paid; the short position is the mirror image. Buyers can lose only the premium. Writers keep the premium at best and can lose without limit on a short call, or the strike minus the premium on a short put.\",\"Every option position has a breakeven at expiry: strike plus premium for a long call, strike minus premium for a long put. Compute the maximum loss, the maximum gain and the breakeven before any order.\"],\"example\":\"Strike 100, premium 2.86. Long call: breakeven 102.86, max loss 2.86, max gain unlimited. Long put: breakeven 97.14, max loss 2.86, max gain 97.14. A short put can lose 971.40 on one 10-unit contract.\",\"related\":[\"option-contract\",\"r-multiple\",\"exercise-and-assignment\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.2\"]},{\"id\":\"exercise-and-assignment\",\"name\":\"Exercise and assignment\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The holder using the option's right and the writer being obliged to deliver; American versus European timing.\",\"body\":[\"Exercise is the holder using the right: buying at the strike with a call, selling with a put. The exchange then assigns a writer, who must fulfil the contract. American options can be exercised on any day up to expiry; European options only at expiry.\",\"Writers face two practical risks: early assignment on American short calls, typically just before an ex-dividend date, and pin risk when the price closes right at a short strike at expiry.\"],\"example\":\"You wrote a LABX call with strike 100. LABX closes at 104.00 and the holder exercises: you are assigned and must sell 10 units at 100.00 per contract, buying them at 104.00 if you don't own them.\",\"related\":[\"option-contract\",\"option-payoff\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.2\"]},{\"id\":\"put-call-parity\",\"name\":\"Put-call parity\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The no-arbitrage link C − P = S − K·e^(−rT) between European calls, puts, the underlying and cash.\",\"body\":[\"A long call plus a short put with the same strike and expiry is worth S − K at expiry, the same as owning the underlying financed with a loan of the strike. So their prices today must differ by S − K·e^(−rT). With zero rates and S = K, call and put cost the same.\",\"Parity lets you find a missing price and spot inconsistent quotes. But a small gap is rarely free money: three legs pay spreads and commissions, and liquid markets close gaps in milliseconds.\"],\"example\":\"S = 100, K = 100, T = 0.25, r = 4%: K·e^(−rT) = 99.00, so C − P must be 1.00, and the model gives 4.49 − 3.49. At r = 0, a put quoted at 2.50 against a call at 2.86 locks in 0.36, but costs of 0.25 leave 0.11.\",\"related\":[\"synthetic-position\",\"trading-costs\",\"option-pricing-model\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"synthetic-position\",\"name\":\"Synthetic positions\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Combinations of options, underlying and cash that replicate another position's payoff, such as a synthetic long.\",\"body\":[\"Rearranging put-call parity shows which combinations are the same position in disguise. Long call plus short put is a synthetic long; short call plus long put a synthetic short. Long stock plus a long put copies a long call, and long stock plus a short call copies a short put.\",\"The practical lesson: a covered call and a cash-secured put at the same strike have the same payoff and the same downside. Judge positions by their payoff, not by their labels.\"],\"example\":\"Long the LABX 100-call at 2.86 and short the 100-put at 2.86: at expiry the pair is worth LABX − 100 at every price, from −20.00 at 80 to +20.00 at 120, exactly like owning LABX from 100.\",\"related\":[\"put-call-parity\",\"covered-call\",\"option-payoff\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.3\"]},{\"id\":\"option-pricing-model\",\"name\":\"Option pricing inputs (Black–Scholes)\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"How price, strike, time, volatility and rates set an option's value, and where the model's assumptions fail.\",\"body\":[\"Black–Scholes prices a European option from the underlying price, the strike, the time to expiry, the volatility and the rate. A call gains with the price, time and volatility, and loses with the strike. Every input is observable except volatility, the one estimate.\",\"Near the money, price ≈ 0.4 × S × σ × √T, so it grows almost in line with σ and with the square root of time. The model assumes constant volatility, lognormal returns and no jumps; real markets have skew, fat tails and gaps, so treat it as a common language for prices, not a description of reality.\"],\"example\":\"LABX 100.00, 30 days, IV 25%: the 100-call is 2.86, and the quick rule gives 2.87. At IV 35% it's 4.00; with 7 days left, 1.38; at IV 50%, or with 120 days, 5.71.\",\"related\":[\"volatility\",\"fat-tails\",\"gap-risk\",\"implied-volatility\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.4\"]},{\"id\":\"option-delta\",\"name\":\"Delta\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The change in an option's price for a one-unit move in the underlying; also its hedge ratio.\",\"body\":[\"Calls have delta between 0 and 1, puts between −1 and 0; at the money they sit near ±0.5, deep in the money near ±1. A position's delta is contracts × units per contract × delta: the number of units of the underlying it behaves like.\",\"Delta is also the hedge ratio, and roughly the chance of ending in the money. It changes as the price moves, which is gamma's job to describe.\"],\"example\":\"LABX 100.00, 30 days, IV 25%: call deltas 0.93, 0.77, 0.51, 0.26 and 0.10 for strikes 90 to 110. Ten contracts of the 100-call behave like 100 × 0.514 = 51 LABX.\",\"related\":[\"option-gamma\",\"delta-hedging\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"option-gamma\",\"name\":\"Gamma\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The change in delta for a one-unit move in the underlying; largest at the money near expiry.\",\"body\":[\"Gamma bends the option's price curve: a long option gains more on the way up and loses less on the way down than delta alone says. Price change ≈ delta × move + ½ × gamma × move².\",\"Gamma peaks at the money and grows as expiry approaches, because a small move then decides whether the option ends worth something or nothing. That's why same-day options swing so violently, for buyers and for sellers.\"],\"example\":\"The LABX 100-call: delta 0.514, gamma 0.056 with 30 days left. A 1.00 rise adds 0.514 + 0.028 = 0.54, from 2.86 to 3.40. With one day left, gamma is 0.30, 5.5 times higher.\",\"related\":[\"option-delta\",\"option-theta\",\"gamma-theta-tradeoff\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"delta-hedging\",\"name\":\"Delta hedging\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Offsetting an options position's delta with the underlying so small price moves leave the combined value flat.\",\"body\":[\"To hedge, trade the underlying against the position's delta: sell short what a long call position behaves like, or buy what a long put position is short. The combined position is delta neutral, so its result comes from volatility and time rather than from direction.\",\"Neutral doesn't stay neutral. Gamma changes the options' delta as the price moves, so the hedge must be adjusted. A long-gamma position gets longer as the price rises and shorter as it falls.\"],\"example\":\"Long 10 LABX 100-calls, delta 0.514: position delta 51.4, so short 51 LABX. After a 3.00 rise the calls' delta is 0.67: 67 against the short 51, net +16, and the hedge needs re-setting.\",\"related\":[\"option-delta\",\"option-gamma\",\"gamma-theta-tradeoff\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.5\"]},{\"id\":\"option-theta\",\"name\":\"Theta\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The value an option loses per day as time passes, all else equal.\",\"body\":[\"Long options pay theta every day; writers collect it. Time decay follows the square root of time, so an at-the-money option loses value slowly at first and fast in its final weeks.\",\"Positive theta is not free income: it is the payment for being short gamma, and it comes with losses on large moves.\"],\"example\":\"The LABX 100-call at IV 25% loses 0.048 a day with 30 days left, 0.099 with 7 days and 0.261 with 1 day: more than five times the 30-day rate.\",\"related\":[\"option-gamma\",\"option-vega\",\"gamma-theta-tradeoff\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"option-vega\",\"name\":\"Vega\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The change in an option's price for a one-point change in implied volatility.\",\"body\":[\"Vega is largest for at-the-money options with a long time to expiry and small near expiry. A position can gain on direction and still lose if implied volatility falls, as it does after an event.\",\"A day's option P&L splits into delta, gamma, theta and vega terms: dV ≈ Δ·dS + ½·Γ·dS² + Θ·dt + ν·dσ. Reading it this way shows where a result came from.\"],\"example\":\"The 30-day LABX 100-call has vega 0.114. LABX up 2.00 and IV down 3 points in a day: 1.03 + 0.11 − 0.05 − 0.34 = +0.75, against +1.03 from delta alone. Full repricing: +0.77.\",\"related\":[\"option-theta\",\"implied-volatility\",\"iv-crush\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"gamma-theta-tradeoff\",\"name\":\"Gamma–theta trade-off\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Long options pay time decay to own gamma and profit only if realised volatility exceeds implied volatility.\",\"body\":[\"A delta-hedged long option earns about ½ × gamma × move² each day and pays theta. The two balance at a daily move equal to the move implied by the option's volatility, S × σ ÷ √365.\",\"So a hedged option buyer wins when the underlying moves more than implied, and the seller wins when it moves less. Owning an option is a bet on realised against implied volatility, not just on direction.\"],\"example\":\"The 30-day LABX 100-call: gamma 0.056, theta −0.048. Break-even daily move √(2 × 0.048 ÷ 0.056) = 1.31, the same as 100 × 0.25 ÷ 19.1. At 15% realised, about 0.79 a day, the hedged position loses about 0.03 a day.\",\"related\":[\"option-gamma\",\"option-theta\",\"delta-hedging\",\"implied-volatility\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.6\"]},{\"id\":\"implied-volatility\",\"name\":\"Implied volatility\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"The volatility that makes a pricing model match an option's market price: the market's forecast plus a premium.\",\"body\":[\"Put an option's market price into the model and solve for σ: that is its implied volatility. Options are quoted and compared by it, because it strips out price, strike and time.\",\"It is a forecast with a premium built in. On index options it has, on average, exceeded the volatility that followed, which is why selling options has paid on average, with crash risk attached.\"],\"example\":\"The 30-day LABX 100-call trades at 4.00. At IV 25% the model gives 2.86, with vega 0.114: (4.00 − 2.86) ÷ 0.114 = 10 more points, so the implied volatility is 35%.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-surface\",\"implied-move\",\"option-pricing-model\",\"volatility-risk-premium\",\"vix\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-surface\",\"name\":\"Volatility surface\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Implied volatility across strikes (skew) and expiries (term structure), including event-driven bumps.\",\"body\":[\"Across strikes, index and stock options usually show a skew: downside puts carry higher implied volatility, the price of crash protection. Across expiries, the term structure usually rises in calm markets and inverts in crises.\",\"A scheduled event lifts the first expiry after it most, because the event's move is spread over fewer days. Later expiries carry the same event, diluted.\"],\"example\":\"A fictional LABX surface: 30-day IV 31% at strike 90, 25% at 100, 22% at 110. With an event between day 7 and day 14: 7-day IV 22%, 14-day 37.7%, 30-day 30.4%, 180-day 23.6%.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"iv-crush\",\"skewness\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.7\"]},{\"id\":\"covered-call\",\"name\":\"Covered call\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Long underlying plus a short call: collects premium, caps the upside and keeps nearly all of the downside.\",\"body\":[\"Selling a call against stock you own adds the premium to your result and caps your gain at the strike. The premium cushions a fall only by its own size, so almost all of the downside remains.\",\"By put-call parity, a covered call has the same payoff as a cash-secured put. It beats the stock in a range, lags it in a strong rally and loses nearly as much in a crash.\"],\"example\":\"LABX bought at 100.00, call 105 sold at 1.08: max gain 6.08, max loss 98.92, breakeven 98.92. If LABX ends at 112.00 the stock makes 12.00 and the covered call 6.08; at 80.00, −20.00 against −18.92.\",\"related\":[\"synthetic-position\",\"protective-put\",\"option-collar\",\"market-regime\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.8\"]},{\"id\":\"protective-put\",\"name\":\"Protective put\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Long underlying plus a long put: a paid floor on losses that also holds through gaps.\",\"body\":[\"A put lets you sell at its strike however far the price falls, so the loss is fixed at the distance to the strike plus the premium. Unlike a stop-loss, it doesn't depend on a fill, so it holds through overnight gaps and events.\",\"The cost is the premium, spent whether or not the floor is needed. In calm markets that is a steady drag.\"],\"example\":\"LABX bought at 100.00 with the put 95 at 0.97: max loss 5.97, breakeven 100.97. LABX gaps to 88.00: the stop at 95 fills near 88 for about −12.00, the put holds the loss at −5.97.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"option-collar\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.8\"]},{\"id\":\"option-collar\",\"name\":\"Collar\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Long underlying, long put and short call: a capped range of outcomes, often at near-zero net cost.\",\"body\":[\"A collar buys a put below the price and pays for it by selling a call above it. The result is a band: losses floored at the put, gains capped at the call, for little or no net premium.\",\"It suits holding a position you must keep while accepting a cap. A strong rally is where it costs most.\"],\"example\":\"LABX at 100.00, put 95 bought at 0.97, call 105 sold at 1.08: a net credit of 0.11. Max loss 5.00 − 0.11 = 4.89, max gain 5.00 + 0.11 = 5.11, breakeven 99.89.\",\"related\":[\"protective-put\",\"covered-call\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.8\"]},{\"id\":\"vertical-spread\",\"name\":\"Vertical spreads\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Buying and selling options of the same type and expiry at two strikes, with defined maximum gain and loss.\",\"body\":[\"A debit spread costs money: its maximum loss is the debit and its maximum gain the width minus the debit. A credit spread pays you: its maximum gain is the credit and its maximum loss the width minus the credit. The short leg also cuts vega and theta.\",\"Credit spreads win often and lose big; in the model their expected value is about zero before costs. Judge them by expectancy, and size them from the defined maximum loss.\"],\"example\":\"Bull call 100/105: debit 1.78, max gain 3.22, breakeven 101.78; at 1% risk, 100.00 ÷ 17.80 = 5 contracts. Bull put 95/90: credit 0.76, max loss 4.24, wins about 79% of the time, expected value about zero.\",\"related\":[\"expectancy\",\"reward-to-risk\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.9\"]},{\"id\":\"long-volatility\",\"name\":\"Long volatility (straddles and strangles)\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Owning options on both sides to profit from a move larger than the one already priced in.\",\"body\":[\"A long straddle buys the at-the-money call and put; a long strangle buys an out-of-the-money call and put. Both lose at most their cost and profit from a large move either way. The strangle is cheaper but needs a bigger move.\",\"Before events, implied volatility already prices the move, and it collapses afterwards. Buy movement only when your expected move, from evidence, beats the implied move; otherwise the honest choice is no trade.\"],\"example\":\"Event tomorrow, 7-day options at IV 60%: straddle 6.62, breakevens 93.38 and 106.62; strangle 95/105 at 2.80, breakevens 92.20 and 107.80. After the event IV falls to 25%: a 4% move leaves the straddle at 4.35, −2.27.\",\"related\":[\"implied-move\",\"iv-crush\",\"news-straddle\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.10\"]},{\"id\":\"volatility-risk-premium\",\"name\":\"Volatility risk premium\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The tendency of implied volatility to exceed later realised volatility, which pays option sellers on average.\",\"body\":[\"Option buyers pay for insurance. On stock indexes, implied volatility has on average exceeded the volatility that followed: Carr and Wu (2009) find strongly negative variance risk premiums. The evidence for the premium is strong (grade A).\",\"The premium is payment for crash risk, and it is collected as many small gains and given back in rare, very large losses. In February 2018 the VIX rose from 17.31 to 37.32 in one session.\"],\"example\":\"Selling the 30-day LABX straddle collects 5.72 and wins if LABX ends between 94.28 and 105.72. A −25% gap costs 19.28 a unit, 192.80 a contract: years of small premiums can go in one move.\",\"related\":[\"implied-volatility\",\"short-volatility-risk\",\"sharpe-ratio\",\"vix\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.11\"]},{\"id\":\"short-volatility-risk\",\"name\":\"Short-volatility tail risk\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Selling options earns small, steady gains with rare, very large losses, so size comes from stress, not premium.\",\"body\":[\"Short straddles and strangles have open-ended losses; an iron condor buys wings to cap them. Either way, the return series looks smooth until the tail arrives, so its Sharpe ratio flatters it.\",\"Size from a stress scenario you choose in advance, such as a −25% gap, never from the premium collected. Defined-risk structures make that sum exact.\"],\"example\":\"Iron condor 90/95/105/110 for 1.52: max loss 3.48 a unit, 34.80 a contract, so 2 contracts at a 100.00 budget. A short straddle has no maximum: −92.80 a contract in a −15% gap, −192.80 in a −25% gap.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-risk-premium\",\"fat-tails\",\"skewness\",\"tail-event\",\"risk-of-ruin\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.1.11\"]},{\"id\":\"implementation-shortfall\",\"name\":\"Implementation shortfall\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"The full cost of executing a decision versus the decision price: delay, spread, impact, fees and missed fills.\",\"body\":[\"Implementation shortfall (Perold, 1988) compares a paper portfolio, filled in full at the decision price, with the real one. The difference splits into delay (decision to arrival), half the spread, market impact, commission, and the opportunity cost of units that never filled.\",\"Slippage on fills misses the last line, and it is often the largest. Divide the total by the intended trade's value to express it in basis points.\"],\"example\":\"Buy 50,000 LABX, decided at 100.00; arrival 100.04; 40,000 filled at an average of 100.18; close 101.00. Delay 1,600.00, half spread 2,000.00, impact 3,600.00, commission 2,003.60, opportunity 10,000.00: 19,203.60, or 38.4 bps of 5,000,000.00.\",\"related\":[\"market-impact\",\"slippage\",\"trading-costs\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.1\"]},{\"id\":\"market-impact\",\"name\":\"Market impact\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"The price move caused by your own trading, split into a temporary and a permanent part.\",\"body\":[\"Buying pushes the price up and selling pushes it down, because you use up the liquidity waiting in the book. Part of the move fades when you stop and the book refills: temporary impact. Part stays, because others infer information from your trades: permanent impact.\",\"Impact is invisible on small orders and dominant on large ones. Trading more slowly shrinks the temporary part, at the price of timing risk.\"],\"example\":\"A fund buys 50,000 LABX. The mid climbs from 100.00 to 100.32 by the last fill, then settles at 100.21: temporary impact 0.11, permanent impact 0.21.\",\"related\":[\"square-root-impact\",\"implementation-shortfall\",\"market-depth\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.1\",\"4.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"square-root-impact\",\"name\":\"Square-root impact law\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Impact grows roughly with volatility times the square root of order size over daily volume.\",\"body\":[\"The square-root law estimates impact ≈ Y × σ × √(Q ÷ V), with σ the daily volatility, Q the order size, V the daily volume and Y about 1. It is an empirical regularity found in many markets (grade A), not a law of physics; the constant and the fit differ by market.\",\"Because impact per unit grows with √Q, total impact cost grows with Q to the power 1.5: twice the size costs about 2.8 times as much. That is why the size an edge can carry is limited.\"],\"example\":\"LABX: σ = 2%, V = 2,000,000. 50,000 units: 2% × √0.025 = 31.6 bps, about 15,811 vUSD. 200,000 units: 63.2 bps, about 126,491 vUSD: 4 times the size, 8 times the cost.\",\"related\":[\"market-impact\",\"volatility\",\"impact-timing-tradeoff\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.2\"]},{\"id\":\"execution-algorithm\",\"name\":\"Execution algorithms\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Rules that slice a parent order over time or volume, such as TWAP, VWAP, POV and implementation shortfall.\",\"body\":[\"TWAP sends equal slices at equal time intervals. VWAP sizes slices to the expected volume curve. POV trades a fixed share of live volume, such as 10%. An implementation-shortfall algorithm front-loads the order to stay close to the decision price.\",\"Choose by benchmark, urgency and liquidity, and cap the price. Predictable slicing leaks information, so real algorithms randomise sizes and timing.\"],\"example\":\"50,000 LABX on a 2,000,000-unit day: VWAP buys 6,000 in the first half hour and 2,750 at midday; POV 10% buys 24,000 in the first half hour and is done by 10:46.\",\"related\":[\"impact-timing-tradeoff\",\"vwap\",\"implementation-shortfall\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"impact-timing-tradeoff\",\"name\":\"Impact versus timing risk\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Trading faster raises market impact; trading slower leaves the unfilled part exposed to price moves.\",\"body\":[\"Every execution plan balances two costs (Almgren and Chriss, 2000). A single market order pays the most impact but carries no timing risk. A day-long VWAP pays the least impact, but the unfilled part rides the market for hours, so its outcomes spread widely.\",\"No plan wins on both. Urgency, risk limits and your view of where the price is heading decide where on the trade-off you sit.\"],\"example\":\"Over 200 synthetic sessions for 50,000 LABX: a market order costs 134.6 bps every day; POV 10% averages 69.3 with a 95th percentile of 144.7; VWAP averages 38.6 but reaches 213.6.\",\"related\":[\"execution-algorithm\",\"square-root-impact\",\"implementation-shortfall\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.3\"]},{\"id\":\"latency-arbitrage\",\"name\":\"Latency and stale quotes\",\"category\":\"order_types\",\"summary\":\"Faster traders execute against resting orders whose prices have not yet caught up with new information.\",\"body\":[\"When news changes value, every resting order priced before it is stale. Whoever reacts first trades against it: a fast trader can hit your bid within a millisecond, long before a 150 ms cancel arrives. A resting quote is a free option for anyone faster.\",\"Retail traders can't win this race, so they avoid it: cancel resting orders or let them expire before scheduled releases, and price new orders after the news.\"],\"example\":\"You rest a bid of 500 at 100.00 and an offer of 500 at 100.10 through a release. News takes value to 99.80 or to 100.30; either way the stale side is hit, and you lose 500 × 0.20 = 100.00.\",\"related\":[\"adverse-selection\",\"market-making\",\"limit-order\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.4\"]},{\"id\":\"trading-halts\",\"name\":\"Halts, LULD and circuit breakers\",\"category\":\"stop_loss\",\"summary\":\"Rules that pause or stop trading after large moves and reopen through auctions, where stops can fill far away.\",\"body\":[\"US market-wide circuit breakers halt all stocks for 15 minutes when the S&P 500 falls 7% or 13% from the previous close before 15:25 ET, and close the market for the day at 20%. Single stocks have limit up–limit down bands around a 5-minute average; 15 seconds at a band brings a 5-minute pause. Europe uses volatility auctions.\",\"Trading restarts through a reopening auction. A stop triggers at that print and fills near it, however far from the stop. A limit caps the price but may not fill.\"],\"example\":\"LABX pauses at its 90.00 band and reopens at 84.00. A stop at 89.50 fills near 84.00. A sell limit at 85.00 doesn't fill, and the position stays open.\",\"related\":[\"gap-risk\",\"stop-loss-order\",\"call-auction\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.5\"]},{\"id\":\"liquidity-clock\",\"name\":\"Liquidity clock\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Scheduled windows (fixes, month-end, closing auctions, expiries) where volume and spreads change for mechanical reasons.\",\"body\":[\"Some flow is set by the clock: FX funds trade the 16:00 London fix, index funds trade the official close, funds rebalance at month and quarter end, futures and options expire on witching days, and FX spreads widen around the 17:00 New York rollover.\",\"This flow is mechanical, not informed, so it isn't a direction signal. It does change execution cost and slippage, so check the clock before a large order.\"],\"example\":\"In a synthetic LABC session, volume runs at about 120 a minute, jumps to 600 a minute from 15:58 to 16:02 around the fix, and the price ends 0.01 from where it started.\",\"related\":[\"intraday-cycle\",\"option-pinning\",\"call-auction\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"option-pinning\",\"name\":\"Option pinning\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The tendency of prices to settle near strikes with large open interest on expiry days.\",\"body\":[\"On expiry days, optionable stocks close near strikes more often than chance would suggest (Ni, Pearson and Poteshman, 2005; grade B). The usual explanation is dealers' delta hedging: long-gamma dealers sell rallies and buy dips near the strike, damping moves; short-gamma dealers do the opposite and amplify them.\",\"Real studies use thousands of stock-expiry days to see it. Retail 'gamma level' maps are much noisier than the research behind them.\"],\"example\":\"A synthetic test with strike 100: 20 of 50 expiry days closed within ±0.50 (40%), against 13 of 50 normal days (26%). The 14-point gap sits inside an 18-point noise band, so 50 days can't tell.\",\"related\":[\"liquidity-clock\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.2.6\"]},{\"id\":\"reality-check-testing\",\"name\":\"Testing the winner of a search\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Judging the best of many tried rules against the best result expected with no edge (Reality Check, SPA, haircuts).\",\"body\":[\"When you try many variants of an idea and keep the best, the winner was chosen partly for its luck. A single test of the winner is far too lenient, and Bonferroni, which assumes independent tests, is too strict when the variants are near-copies of each other.\",\"White's Reality Check (2000) resamples the returns of every rule tried, recentred to no edge, and records the best result each time; the real winner has to beat that distribution of lucky maxima. Hansen's SPA test (2005) refines it, and Harvey and Liu's haircut turns the same idea into a lower Sharpe ratio.\"],\"example\":\"200 momentum lookbacks on 3 years of random-walk data: the best beat t = 1.96 in 73% of 2,000 seeded searches and Bonferroni's 3.66 in only 1.3%. With 50 lookbacks, the Reality Check gave a random-walk winner of |t| 2.07 p = 0.40, and a trend-regime winner of 3.45 p = 0.033.\",\"related\":[\"multiple-testing\",\"statistical-significance\",\"data-snooping\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.1\"]},{\"id\":\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\",\"name\":\"Deflated Sharpe ratio\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The probability that a strategy's true Sharpe beats the best Sharpe expected by luck from all the trials run.\",\"body\":[\"The best of N trials with no edge shows a Sharpe ratio well above zero: about 1.57 times the trials' Sharpe spread at N = 10 and 2.28 at 50. The deflated Sharpe ratio uses that expected maximum as the benchmark and asks how likely the true Sharpe is to beat it, allowing for track length, skew and fat tails (Bailey and López de Prado, 2014).\",\"A DSR of 0.95 or more is the usual convention for a result unlikely to be a fluke. The threshold is a convention, not part of the derivation, and the DSR is only as honest as the trial count behind it.\"],\"example\":\"Best of 50 backtests, annual Sharpe 1.5 over 3 years of daily data, trials' Sharpe spread 0.5, skew −0.5, kurtosis 5. Benchmark 2.28 × 0.5 = 1.14. PSR against zero 0.994; DSR 0.73. At 421 trials the DSR falls to 0.50.\",\"related\":[\"probabilistic-sharpe-ratio\",\"sharpe-ratio\",\"multiple-testing\",\"reality-check-testing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.2\"]},{\"id\":\"probabilistic-sharpe-ratio\",\"name\":\"Probabilistic Sharpe ratio\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The probability that the true Sharpe exceeds a benchmark, given track length, skewness and kurtosis.\",\"body\":[\"PSR = Φ[(SR − SR*) × √(T − 1) ÷ √(1 − skew × SR + (kurtosis − 1) ÷ 4 × SR²)], with the Sharpe ratio in the data's own frequency and T observations. A longer record raises it; negative skew and fat tails lower it at the same Sharpe.\"],\"example\":\"Daily Sharpe 0.0945 (1.5 a year) over 756 days, skew −0.5, kurtosis 5: PSR against zero = Φ(2.53) = 0.994. With skew +0.5 it would be 0.996.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-ratio\",\"skewness\",\"fat-tails\",\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.2\"]},{\"id\":\"minimum-backtest-length\",\"name\":\"Minimum backtest length\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The data length needed so the best of N zero-edge trials is not expected to reach a target Sharpe by luck.\",\"body\":[\"The best of N zero-edge configurations shows an annual Sharpe near E[max] ÷ √years. It stays above a target Sharpe until the years exceed (E[max] ÷ target)²: the minimum backtest length (Bailey, Borwein, López de Prado and Zhu, 2014). A simpler upper bound is 2 × ln(N) ÷ target².\",\"Read it the other way round as a cap: the data you have limit how many configurations you may try. It is a necessary condition, not proof of an edge.\"],\"example\":\"Target Sharpe 1: 7 tries need 1.9 years, 45 need 5.0, 100 need 6.4 and 1,000 need 10.6. With 4 years of data, at most 25 independent configurations. Seeded check: the best of 45 zero-edge configurations over 5 years averaged 0.99.\",\"related\":[\"deflated-sharpe-ratio\",\"sharpe-ratio-uncertainty\",\"multiple-testing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"sharpe-ratio-uncertainty\",\"name\":\"Sharpe ratio error bars\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The confidence interval around a measured Sharpe ratio, which narrows only with the square root of track length.\",\"body\":[\"For independent returns, the standard error of a Sharpe ratio is √((1 + SR²/2) ÷ n) in the data's own frequency (Lo, 2002). For an annual Sharpe from daily data that is about 1 ÷ √years, so a 95% interval is the Sharpe ± 1.96 ÷ √years.\",\"Autocorrelation breaks the simple scaling. Multiplying a monthly Sharpe by √12 is right only in special cases, and Lo found hedge fund Sharpe ratios overstated by as much as 65%.\"],\"example\":\"A Sharpe of 1.0 over 3 years: 95% interval −0.13 to 2.13; over 10 years, 0.38 to 1.62. Seeded daily histories gave −0.14 to 2.16 and 0.38 to 1.62. Monthly returns with autocorrelation +0.3: √12 overstates the annual Sharpe by about 32%.\",\"related\":[\"sharpe-ratio\",\"autocorrelation\",\"statistical-significance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.3\"]},{\"id\":\"backtest-overfitting-probability\",\"name\":\"Probability of backtest overfitting\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The share of in-sample/out-of-sample splits in which the in-sample winner ranks at or below the median out of sample.\",\"body\":[\"CSCV (Bailey, Borwein, López de Prado and Zhu, 2017) cuts the returns of every variant in a family into S blocks, tries every way of using half of them in-sample, picks the in-sample winner and records its out-of-sample rank. PBO is the share of splits where that rank falls to the median or below.\",\"On pure noise the winner is a coin toss out of sample, so PBO sits near 0.5. A low PBO says the selection carries over; it doesn't prove the edge, which DSR and the other tests judge. The app's convention: 0.50 or more reject, 0.20 to 0.50 incubate, under 0.20 continue.\"],\"example\":\"8 blocks give 70 splits. A family of 10 lookbacks, each traded with and against the move, averaged a PBO of 0.50 over 1,000 seeded random-walk series and 0.105 on a trend regime with a real edge. 49 of 70 splits with λ ≤ 0 is a PBO of 70%.\",\"related\":[\"overfitting\",\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"in-sample-out-of-sample\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.4\"]},{\"id\":\"purged-cross-validation\",\"name\":\"Purged cross-validation\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Time-series cross-validation that removes overlapping labels and adds an embargo so test information cannot leak into training.\",\"body\":[\"When each day's label is built from the days that follow, neighbouring days share most of their labels. Shuffled k-fold cross-validation then trains on near-copies of the test labels and scores well even on noise.\",\"Purging drops training days whose labels overlap the test period; an embargo also drops the days just after each fold. López de Prado (2018) combines them in combinatorial purged cross-validation, which yields many backtest paths.\"],\"example\":\"10-day labels, test fold days 401–500: purge 391–400, embargo 501–510. On 300 seeded random-walk series, a 5-nearest-neighbour model scored 56.0% with shuffled folds and 50.0% with purged, embargoed folds.\",\"related\":[\"look-ahead-bias\",\"autocorrelation\",\"in-sample-out-of-sample\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.5\"]},{\"id\":\"strategy-capacity\",\"name\":\"Strategy capacity\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"The size at which market impact erodes an edge; total profit peaks well before the edge reaches zero.\",\"body\":[\"Impact grows with the square root of size relative to volume, so the net edge per unit falls as size rises. Total profit, size times net edge, rises, peaks where marginal profit is zero, then falls to zero where the edge is gone.\",\"Crowding shrinks capacity from outside: others trading the same signal add their size to your impact. For a small account the practical lesson is not to scale a thin edge into illiquid instruments.\"],\"example\":\"Gross 40 bps, fixed costs 10 bps, round-trip impact 400 × √(Q ÷ V) bps with V = 2,000,000: 396 vUSD per trade at 2,500 units, a peak of 500 at 5,000, 172 at 10,000 and zero at 11,250. A twin fund trading 5,000 alongside cuts the 500 to 86.\",\"related\":[\"square-root-impact\",\"trading-costs\",\"strategy-crowding\",\"anomaly-decay\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.6\"]},{\"id\":\"research-hygiene\",\"name\":\"Validation dossier and pre-registration\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Fixing thresholds, haircuts and verdict criteria before out-of-sample results, then reporting every trial in a fixed dossier.\",\"body\":[\"The validation dossier runs from the hypothesis through rules, costs, in-sample and out-of-sample tests, walk-forward, Monte Carlo, the R0 control, the trial count, DSR, MinBTL, PBO, capacity and the decay haircut to a verdict: deploy to a forward test, incubate longer, or reject.\",\"Pre-registration fixes the thresholds and verdict rules before the out-of-sample unlock, so results can't move the bar. A second unlock, unlogged sweeps, a hypothesis written after the results or a survivors-only universe make a result unreportable. A justified reject is a good outcome.\"],\"example\":\"Rules: deploy needs DSR ≥ 0.95 and PBO < 0.20; reject if DSR < 0.50 or PBO ≥ 0.50. A dossier with 40 trials, Sharpe 1.2 over 5 years, DSR 0.76, PBO 0.14 and MinBTL 3.3 years: incubate longer. A published idea's +0.30R backtest enters the plan as +0.15R.\",\"related\":[\"research-log\",\"edge-hypothesis\",\"incubation\",\"anomaly-decay\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.3.7\"]},{\"id\":\"value-at-risk\",\"name\":\"Value at Risk\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The loss threshold exceeded with a given small probability over a given horizon; not a maximum loss.\",\"body\":[\"VaR at confidence α over a horizon is the loss exceeded with probability 1 − α. Parametric VaR uses a model, z × σ under the normal; historical VaR sorts past P&L and reads off the loss at the 5% or 1% point.\",\"VaR says how often a loss is exceeded, not by how much. Fat tails can make the 95% number look smaller while the 99% tail grows, and scaling by √h assumes independent days. A VaR model is backtested by counting exceedances against the binomial count.\"],\"example\":\"Book 10,000.00 with a daily volatility of 1.5%: 95% VaR = 1.645 × 150.00 = 247 and 99% VaR = 2.326 × 150.00 = 349. Over 250 days a correct 95% model is exceeded about 12.5 times; 20 or more happen only 2.7% of the time.\",\"related\":[\"expected-shortfall\",\"fat-tails\",\"drawdown\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.1\"]},{\"id\":\"expected-shortfall\",\"name\":\"Expected Shortfall\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"The average loss beyond the VaR threshold; a better measure of tail risk than VaR.\",\"body\":[\"Expected Shortfall averages the losses on the days beyond VaR, so it sees how deep the tail goes. Under the normal model, ES = σ × φ(z) ÷ (1 − α): about 2.06σ at 95% and 2.67σ at 99%.\",\"Strategies with rare, large losses, such as selling options, can show a small VaR while their ES is large. Size them with ES limits and stress tests.\"],\"example\":\"Same book: ES 99% = 2.67 × 150.00 = 400 under the normal model, 554 under a Student-t with 4 degrees of freedom. An ES limit of 200.00 allows about 5,000 vUSD of LABX under the normal model and 3,610 under the Student-t.\",\"related\":[\"value-at-risk\",\"fat-tails\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.1\"]},{\"id\":\"portfolio-volatility-targeting\",\"name\":\"Book-level volatility targeting\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Scaling a whole book to a volatility target from forecast volatilities and correlations, within caps.\",\"body\":[\"The book's multiplier is target volatility ÷ forecast book volatility, and the forecast needs weights, volatilities and correlations. A rise in correlation alone can call for a smaller book.\",\"Forecasts lag, so the cut arrives after the first losses. A multiplier cap stops leverage building up in calm spells, and drawdown tiers and gross limits can shrink the book further: the smallest size wins. The evidence is grade B: a risk rule, not an edge.\"],\"example\":\"Two assets at 10% volatility, 50/50: ρ = 0.2 gives 7.75% and a multiplier of 1.29× for a 10% target; ρ = 0.9 gives 9.75% and 1.03×. In 2,000 simulated crises the median loss was 14.8% with no target, 9.2% with volatility forecasts only and 7.8% with volatility and correlation forecasts.\",\"related\":[\"volatility-targeting\",\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"leverage\",\"drawdown-tiers\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.2\"]},{\"id\":\"gross-and-net-exposure\",\"name\":\"Gross and net exposure\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Gross exposure sums absolute positions, net exposure sums signed ones; a book needs limits on both.\",\"body\":[\"Gross exposure adds the absolute size of every position; divided by equity, it's the effective leverage. Net exposure adds positions with their signs: the book's bet on the market as a whole.\",\"Net limits control market risk. Gross limits control leg and correlation risk, when hedges stop hedging. A book needs both, and the fix that shrinks gross is usually the better one.\"],\"example\":\"Equity 50,000; long 60,000 and 40,000, short 70,000: gross 170,000 (3.4×), net +30,000 (0.6×). If the longs fall 5% while the short rises 5%, the book loses 8,500, 17% of equity.\",\"related\":[\"exposure\",\"leverage\",\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"beta-adjusted-exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.3\"]},{\"id\":\"beta-adjusted-exposure\",\"name\":\"Beta-adjusted exposure\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Net exposure with each position weighted by its sensitivity to the market.\",\"body\":[\"Each position is multiplied by its beta before adding: Σ βᵢ × positionᵢ. A high-beta long counts for more than its size, a low-beta long for less.\",\"Betas are estimated from past returns, so the result is a forecast. It answers one question, what a broad market move does to the book, and says nothing about the legs splitting.\"],\"example\":\"Long 60,000 at beta 1.2, long 40,000 at 0.8, short 70,000 at 1.0: 72,000 + 32,000 − 70,000 = +34,000. A 5% market fall costs about 1,700.\",\"related\":[\"gross-and-net-exposure\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.3\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-parity\",\"name\":\"Risk parity\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Weighting assets so each contributes equally to portfolio risk, often with leverage to reach a return target.\",\"body\":[\"Risk parity sizes each asset so it contributes the same share of total risk. For uncorrelated assets, and for any two assets, that means inverse-volatility weights; with more correlated assets, a solver finds them.\",\"The balanced book is calmer than a capital-weighted one, so reaching the same volatility or return takes leverage. That leverage costs financing and hurts most when the assets fall together. For an individual, the useful part is the risk budget. Evidence: grade B.\"],\"example\":\"10% and 30% assets: 75/25 weights give each 50% of the risk and a book volatility of 10.6%. Matching the 50/50 book's 15.8% takes 1.49× leverage.\",\"related\":[\"risk-contribution\",\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.4\"]},{\"id\":\"risk-contribution\",\"name\":\"Risk contribution\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"The share of total portfolio volatility that comes from one position, given weights, volatilities and correlations.\",\"body\":[\"For uncorrelated assets, an asset's risk contribution is its variance term, wᵢ²σᵢ², divided by the portfolio variance. With correlations, its covariance with the other assets is added in.\",\"Contributions add up to 100% and rarely match capital shares: the most volatile holding usually carries far more risk than its weight suggests.\"],\"example\":\"50/50 in a 10% and a 30% asset: 0.0025 and 0.0225 of a total 0.025, so 10% and 90% of the risk. One third each in 16%, 10% and 8% assets: 61%, 24% and 15%.\",\"related\":[\"risk-parity\",\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"exposure\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.4\"]},{\"id\":\"portfolio-stress-testing\",\"name\":\"Portfolio stress testing\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Revaluing a book under historical or hypothetical extreme scenarios, including slower exits and higher margins.\",\"body\":[\"A stress test revalues today's book under extreme scenarios: historical ones, past crises replayed, and hypothetical ones, such as a 20% gap, volatility × 3, spreads × 10, margin × 2 or correlations at 1.\",\"It answers what VaR can't: what happens in conditions the data never showed. A good battery includes the time needed to exit, and the worst scenario sets the size.\"],\"example\":\"A 15,000 long book on 10,000 of equity: a 20% gap costs 3,000, 30% of equity. To keep that scenario within 15%, the book must shrink to 7,500. A 10% gap with three days to exit, prices falling 3% a day, costs 19% instead of 15%.\",\"related\":[\"scenario-planning\",\"gap-risk\",\"value-at-risk\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[\"M04\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.5\"]},{\"id\":\"reverse-stress-test\",\"name\":\"Reverse stress test\",\"category\":\"scenario_analysis\",\"summary\":\"Finding the market move that would cause a chosen loss, instead of the loss from a chosen move.\",\"body\":[\"Instead of asking what a chosen move costs, a reverse stress test asks which move would cause a chosen loss. Loss as a share of equity = leverage × move, so the breaking move = loss limit ÷ leverage.\",\"The answer makes leverage concrete: at 5×, a 4% move costs 20% of equity, a move many markets make in a bad week.\"],\"example\":\"Equity 10,000. At 3× (30,000), a 20% loss needs a 6.7% move; at 5×, 4%. At 2.5×, a 25% loss needs a 10% move.\",\"related\":[\"portfolio-stress-testing\",\"leverage\",\"scenario-planning\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.5\"]},{\"id\":\"correlation-regimes\",\"name\":\"Correlation regimes\",\"category\":\"portfolio_exposure\",\"summary\":\"Correlations change with the regime and rise toward one in crises, shrinking the effective number of bets.\",\"body\":[\"Correlations aren't fixed. In calm markets risky assets often move fairly independently; in crises many investors cut risk at once and correlations rise toward 1. Strategies such as carry, which earn small steady returns, tend to fail together.\",\"So diversification shrinks exactly when it's needed. The combined risk of n equal positions is √(n + n(n − 1)ρ) times one position's risk; size it with crisis correlations, not calm ones.\"],\"example\":\"Five 1% positions: 3.00% of risk and 2.78 effective bets at ρ = 0.2; 4.58% and 1.19 bets at 0.8. Sized at 0.65% each, they stay within 3% even in the crisis.\",\"related\":[\"correlation-and-concentration\",\"correlation-instability\",\"carry\",\"portfolio-stress-testing\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.4.6\"]},{\"id\":\"regulatory-perimeter\",\"name\":\"Regulators and jurisdiction\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Which authority supervises which firm and product where you live, and how to verify a licence.\",\"body\":[\"Each firm and product has a home regulator. In the EU, national authorities such as the ASF in Romania apply MiFID II under ESMA's coordination; the UK has the FCA; in the US, the SEC and FINRA cover securities, the CFTC and NFA futures and retail forex. A firm licensed in one EU state can passport its services to the others.\",\"A licence is verified on the regulator's own register: the number, the exact legal entity, its permissions and its websites must all match. Offshore registrations give retail clients little protection. Every rule is a fact with a jurisdiction and a date, so recheck it; this summary is as of 22 October 2026.\"],\"example\":\"A broker's site shows a Cypriot licence number. On the CySEC register, the number belongs to a company with a different name and website: a clone firm. A real passported firm would also appear on the ASF's register for Romania.\",\"related\":[\"retail-regulation\",\"broker-due-diligence\",\"scam-red-flags\",\"client-categorisation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"us-intraday-margin\",\"name\":\"US intraday margin regime (2026)\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"The FINRA regime that replaced the pattern day trader rule from 4 June 2026, built on intraday margin deficits.\",\"body\":[\"Until 3 June 2026, four or more day trades in five business days in a US margin account made a client a pattern day trader, needing 25,000 dollars of equity. From 4 June 2026 that section of FINRA Rule 4210 is gone.\",\"Instead, brokers track intraday margin: the largest shortfall after a trade that adds risk is a deficit, to be met within 15 business days, and small deficits are exempt. Brokers may keep the old rule until 20 October 2027. As of 22 October 2026; check FINRA's site, since courses written before 2026 still teach the old rule.\"],\"example\":\"In October 2026 a client with 8,000 dollars makes five day trades in a week. There's no 25,000-dollar minimum any more, but if a trade leaves her account short of intraday margin, she must cover the deficit. Her broker may still apply the old rule during the transition.\",\"related\":[\"retail-regulation\",\"leverage\",\"regulatory-perimeter\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"client-categorisation\",\"name\":\"Retail and professional clients\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"MiFID II client categories; opting up to professional removes retail protections such as leverage caps.\",\"body\":[\"Under MiFID II, clients are retail by default and get the strongest protections: leverage caps on CFDs, negative balance protection, standard risk warnings and an appropriateness test for complex products.\",\"A retail client can be treated as professional only by meeting at least two of three criteria: frequent large trades, a portfolio over €500,000, or a year working in finance. Opting up removes the caps and negative balance protection. A broker pushing you to opt up is selling you more risk.\"],\"example\":\"As a retail client, 2,000.00 of margin opens at most 60,000.00 of EUR/USD (30:1). Opted up to professional at 200:1, the same margin could open 400,000.00, and a gap could leave the account below zero with nothing to stop it.\",\"related\":[\"regulatory-perimeter\",\"negative-balance-protection\",\"leverage\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.1\"]},{\"id\":\"client-money-protection\",\"name\":\"Client money protection\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Segregation of client funds and compensation schemes that pay if a firm fails, never for trading losses.\",\"body\":[\"Regulated brokers must keep client money segregated from their own. If a firm fails with client money missing, an investor compensation scheme pays up to a limit: SIPC covers US securities accounts up to $500,000, of which $250,000 in cash; EU schemes pay at least €20,000; the UK's FSCS £85,000 for investments. Limits are as of 22 October 2026; check them.\",\"Schemes never pay for market losses, and many products sit outside them: futures, forex and crypto that isn't a security. When segregation fails, as at MF Global in 2011 or FTX in 2022, clients wait or lose. The broker itself is a counterparty risk.\"],\"example\":\"Your US broker-dealer fails holding 30,000 dollars of your shares and 300,000 dollars of cash. SIPC restores the shares, but cash is covered only to 250,000 dollars: 50,000 is at risk. A 30% fall in the shares' price before the failure is yours either way.\",\"related\":[\"broker-due-diligence\",\"counterparty-risk\",\"broker-models\",\"negative-balance-protection\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.2\"]},{\"id\":\"prop-evaluation-rules\",\"name\":\"Prop evaluation rules\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Targets, daily and maximum loss limits (static or trailing), consistency and minimum-day rules of funded-trader challenges.\",\"body\":[\"A retail evaluation sets a profit target, often 10% then 5%, a daily loss limit measured on equity with open trades, and a maximum loss that is static or trailing. A trailing floor follows the highest equity during the day, or the highest closing balance, and never moves down.\",\"Other rules include minimum trading days, a consistency rule capping the best day's share of profit, bans on news trading, weekend holds, copy trading, grid and martingale, and identity checks before payouts. Rules differ by firm and change often: read the current terms.\"],\"example\":\"50,000 account, 2,500 intraday trailing limit: the floor starts at 47,500. An open trade lifts equity to 51,500, so the floor rises to 49,000. If the trade closes at break-even, only 1,000 of buffer is left; an end-of-day floor would still sit at 47,500.\",\"related\":[\"daily-loss-limit\",\"drawdown-tiers\",\"drawdown\",\"prop-challenge-ev\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.3\"]},{\"id\":\"prop-challenge-ev\",\"name\":\"Expected value of an evaluation\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Pass probability times expected payouts minus fees; negative for most participants.\",\"body\":[\"Buying an evaluation is a bet: EV = P(pass) × expected payouts if funded − fee. The break-even pass probability is fee ÷ expected payouts, and with independent attempts you expect 1 ÷ P attempts per funded account.\",\"Pass probability depends on your expectancy and your risk per trade. Without an edge, more risk raises the pass chance like extra lottery tickets, but the funded account then loses too. Industry pass and payout rates are unaudited estimates, and legal protection for fee payers is unclear.\"],\"example\":\"Fee 500, pass chance 10%, payouts 1,500 if funded: EV = 0.10 × 1,500 − 500 = −350. Breaking even needs 500 ÷ 1,500 = 33.3%. At 10%, expect 10 attempts and 5,000 in fees per funded account.\",\"related\":[\"expected-value\",\"expectancy\",\"prop-evaluation-rules\",\"base-rates\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.4\"]},{\"id\":\"tax-awareness\",\"name\":\"Tax awareness\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Knowing that tax treatment varies by country, product and account type, and where to get an authoritative answer.\",\"body\":[\"Tax on trading depends on where you live, what you trade, how long you hold it, the account type and whether a broker withholds tax at source. Rules change often, so examples from other countries or years can mislead.\",\"Tax slows compounding: a generic 20% on each year's gain turns 10% a year into 8%. Compare strategies after costs and tax. For your own case, ask your tax authority or a qualified adviser; this app gives no tax rates and no tax advice.\"],\"example\":\"From 10,000 at 10% a year for 10 years: 25,937.42 untaxed, 21,589.25 with a generic 20% taken from each year's gain, 4,348.17 less.\",\"related\":[\"trade-records\",\"after-cost-evaluation\",\"journaling\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"trade-records\",\"name\":\"Trade records\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The complete log of trades, fees, financing, currency conversions and cash movements needed for review and tax.\",\"body\":[\"For every trade: date and time, instrument, size, entry and exit prices, commissions, swap or financing, and the exchange rate used to convert the result into your home currency, with its source. For the account: broker statements, deposits and withdrawals.\",\"The record must let you, or a tax authority, rebuild every number. Keep it as long as the law where you live requires, often several years. The journal adds the why: the setup, your state, the grade.\"],\"example\":\"Bought 50 LABX at 97.30, sold at 102.40: gross +255.00 vUSD, commissions 2.44 and 2.56, net +250.00. At 4.60 local units per vUSD on the trade date, that's 1,150.00 local units.\",\"related\":[\"journaling\",\"tax-awareness\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.5\"]},{\"id\":\"go-live-criteria\",\"name\":\"Go-live criteria\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Written conditions on evidence, process, capital, broker and expectations that must hold before trading real money.\",\"body\":[\"Evidence: a validated edge, a forward test inside its Monte Carlo band, and a confidence interval for the average R that excludes zero. Process: stop integrity, risk accuracy, plan adherence and limits on target. Capital: risk capital only, nothing borrowed, emergency savings apart. Broker: licence verified, money segregated.\",\"Plan: minimum live size and written kill criteria. Expectations: live results will be worse than the simulator's, and fixed costs such as platforms and data must be covered too. The decision has three valid outcomes: live at minimum size, not yet, or don't trade live.\"],\"example\":\"60 trades at +0.35R with a 1.5R standard deviation: the 95% interval runs from −0.03R to +0.73R. Zero is inside, so the answer is not yet, however good the discipline.\",\"related\":[\"incubation\",\"risk-capital\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"decision-quality\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.6\"]},{\"id\":\"no-trade-conditions\",\"name\":\"No-trade conditions\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Personal, market, process and financial states in which standing aside is the correct decision.\",\"body\":[\"Personal: tilt, fatigue, illness, a major life event. Market: noise without your setups, a crisis your plan doesn't cover, an event without a rule, thin holiday liquidity. Process: a loss limit or drawdown tier reached, a KPI below its threshold, a setup outside the playbook. Financial: money needed within two years, debts, no emergency fund.\",\"Written as stand-aside rules before the day, these turn abstaining into a scored decision rather than a missed opportunity. In noise, each trade's expectancy is about minus its costs, so staying out is the better play.\"],\"example\":\"Yesterday you hit your daily loss limit, and this morning you want to win it back. That's a personal and a process condition at once: no trades today, and a line in your journal.\",\"related\":[\"when-not-to-trade\",\"daily-loss-limit\",\"drawdown-tiers\",\"tilt\",\"revenge-trading\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.5.7\"]},{\"id\":\"constraints-matrix\",\"name\":\"Constraints matrix\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Mapping time, capital, jurisdiction, drawdown tolerance, skills and temperament to the styles they allow or rule out.\",\"body\":[\"Each row is one constraint and two lists: the styles it points towards and the ones it rules out. Fixed office hours point to swing or position trading with end-of-day orders and rule out scalping and news trading. A small account points to micro contracts and a swing horizon; low drawdown tolerance points to 0.25–0.5% risk and away from low-win-rate trend following.\",\"Time usually rules out the most, so it goes first. Temperament goes last, and only as the journal measures it. The rows are practitioner rules of thumb (grade C): the matrix narrows the choice; your own numbers decide it.\"],\"example\":\"Office hours 09:00–17:00, an account of 3,000, a 10% drawdown limit, and a journal with 9 of 40 trades opened within 10 minutes of a loss: swing trading with end-of-day orders at 0.5% risk. Scalping, news trading and discretionary intraday are ruled out.\",\"related\":[\"trading-styles\",\"behaviour-audit\",\"trading-plan\",\"risk-capital\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.1\"]},{\"id\":\"behaviour-audit\",\"name\":\"Behaviour audit\",\"category\":\"psychology\",\"summary\":\"Measured habits from your own journal (overtrading, revenge trades, moved stops, holding time) used to choose a style.\",\"body\":[\"Self-description is biased: overconfidence and hindsight make most people see themselves as more patient and disciplined than they trade. The journal doesn't. It counts trades opened soon after a loss, stops moved further away, trades per session against the plan, and how long positions are really held.\",\"Where the description and the count disagree, the count goes into the constraints matrix. A trader who closes planned swings within minutes, or revenge trades after losses, has a temperament constraint that points to automated orders, fixed rules and longer horizons.\"],\"example\":\"40 trades in 4 weeks: 9 opened within 10 minutes of a loss (22.5%), 6 stops moved further away, planned swings closed after a median 18 minutes. Whatever the trader believes, the matrix records 'impulsive under stress'.\",\"related\":[\"revenge-trading\",\"overtrading\",\"behavioural-guard-rails\",\"process-kpis\"],\"missions\":[\"M06\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.1\"]},{\"id\":\"tradability-ratio\",\"name\":\"Tradability ratio\",\"category\":\"market_fundamentals\",\"summary\":\"Average true range divided by round-trip cost: a quick measure of how cheaply a market can be traded.\",\"body\":[\"Divide the typical daily move, the ATR, by the full cost of a round trip, spread plus commission both ways, in the same units. The result says how many times its cost a market moves in a day. A higher ratio leaves more room for an edge after costs.\",\"The ratio matters most for short styles. The same cost takes a far bigger share of a small target than of a large one, so scalping needs a much higher ratio than swing trading. The arithmetic is exact; which ratio is 'enough' depends on your targets.\"],\"example\":\"A: 2.40 ÷ 0.20 = 12; B: 1.50 ÷ 0.30 = 5; C: 30.00 ÷ 1.50 = 20. On A, the 0.20 round trip is 4% of a 4.80 swing target but about 17% of a 1.20 scalp target.\",\"related\":[\"cost-drag\",\"trading-costs\",\"volatility\",\"fx-sessions\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.2\"]},{\"id\":\"specialisation\",\"name\":\"Specialisation\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Trading one style, few setups and few markets until the sample is large enough to judge the edge.\",\"body\":[\"An edge belongs to one setup on one market. Trades spread over many setups and markets give each combination a tiny sample, so none of them can ever be judged. The number of trades needed for a t-statistic of about 2 is (2 × sd ÷ mean)², which runs into the hundreds for typical edges.\",\"So specialise: one or two setups on one or two markets, at least 100 trades, or 30 for slow strategies with a backtest behind them. Rank setups by the lower end of their interval, write a no-trade list, and add a second strategy only when each is positive on its own and the two are weakly correlated.\"],\"example\":\"Mean +0.2R, sd 1.5R: n = (2 × 1.5 ÷ 0.2)² = 225 trades, 75 weeks at 3 trades a week. Spread over five setups at the same pace, it would take about seven years.\",\"related\":[\"sample-size\",\"statistical-significance\",\"setup-card\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\"],\"missions\":[\"M10\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.3\"]},{\"id\":\"development-pipeline\",\"name\":\"Development pipeline\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"The staged path from hypothesis to backtest, robustness, forward test, small live size, scaling and decay monitoring.\",\"body\":[\"Eight stages: the hypothesis, with its economic reason; the rules; a backtest with real costs, out-of-sample, walk-forward and an R0 control; robustness across parameters, markets, periods and Monte Carlo; a forward test in the simulator; minimum live size; gradual scaling; and decay monitoring.\",\"Each stage has a gate, and each tests something the earlier ones can't: the idea, then your execution, then real fills and nerves, then size. Several strategies can sit at different stages at once, each with its own review and kill thresholds.\"],\"example\":\"A pullback setup: hypothesis written, rules coded, backtest with costs and walk-forward, robustness passed, 50 simulator trades inside the corridor. It goes live at minimum size; scaling starts only after 50 more trades meet the stage criteria.\",\"related\":[\"edge-hypothesis\",\"walk-forward-analysis\",\"robustness-testing\",\"incubation\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"kill-criteria\",\"name\":\"Review and kill criteria\",\"category\":\"risk_management\",\"summary\":\"Numeric thresholds written in advance that demote or stop a strategy when results leave the expected range.\",\"body\":[\"Two tiers, set from the strategy's own Monte Carlo and expectancy interval before it trades. Review steps it down one capital stage and starts a full review: a drawdown past the 95th percentile, or an interval that includes zero after at least 50 trades. Kill stops live trading: a drawdown past the 99th percentile, an interval entirely below zero, or the regime it was built for no longer appearing.\",\"They are numbers, not adjectives, and they aren't moved during a drawdown. The percentiles come from the data; the choice of the 95th and 99th is a practitioner convention (grade C).\"],\"example\":\"Reference system: review past 13.5R, kill past 16.5R. At 11R, keep trading. At 15R, step down and review. At 18R, stop.\",\"related\":[\"kill-switch\",\"monte-carlo-resampling\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"premortem\"],\"missions\":[\"M07\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.4\"]},{\"id\":\"capital-staging\",\"name\":\"Capital stages\",\"category\":\"position_sizing\",\"summary\":\"Raising size in steps unlocked by sample size, adherence and drawdown, and cutting it automatically on breaches.\",\"body\":[\"Five stages: simulator, minimum live size, then 25%, 50% and 100% of target size. Each step up needs a sample, such as 50 trades at the stage, adherence of 90% or more, the drawdown inside the Monte Carlo band and a new equity high. A review trigger moves you down a stage automatically.\",\"Within a stage, size falls faster than equity in drawdowns and grows about 25% at a time after new highs. Full Kelly is a ceiling, not a target, and large size eventually meets market impact, which grows with order size against volume. These are practitioner conventions (grade C).\"],\"example\":\"Turtle-style de-risking: an account down from 10,000 to 9,000 (−10%) sizes from a notional 8,000, so a 1% risk is 80.00. The reference system's full Kelly is 16%; a 1% risk is about a sixteenth of it.\",\"related\":[\"kelly-criterion\",\"fractional-kelly\",\"drawdown-tiers\",\"position-sizing\"],\"missions\":[\"M05\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.5\"]},{\"id\":\"continuous-improvement\",\"name\":\"Continuous improvement\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"A review cycle from daily to yearly, with evidence-based rule changes and a yearly continue, reduce or retire decision.\",\"body\":[\"Daily and weekly reviews check process. Monthly or quarterly reviews check statistics: the R distribution, MAE and MFE, expectancy per setup with its interval, and drawdown against the Monte Carlo band. The yearly review compares net, risk-adjusted results with the passive benchmark, rechecks rules and costs, and ends with a decision: continue, reduce, retire the strategy, or invest passively.\",\"Rules change only through a logged process: hypothesis, an evidence threshold set in advance, a simulator test and a new plan version. Practice targets the weaknesses the journal measures, not the parts you enjoy.\"],\"example\":\"A year: net +3.1% after costs with a Sharpe of 0.4; the passive benchmark returned +7.8% with a Sharpe of 0.6. Under a rule written a year earlier, 'below the benchmark but positive: reduce', size drops a stage for the next year.\",\"related\":[\"trading-reviews\",\"rule-change-governance\",\"passive-benchmark\",\"decay-vs-variance\"],\"missions\":[],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.6\"]},{\"id\":\"decay-vs-variance\",\"name\":\"Decay versus variance\",\"category\":\"decision_making\",\"summary\":\"Telling a fading edge from normal luck or a regime change with confidence intervals and Monte Carlo bands.\",\"body\":[\"Variance: recent results sit inside the expectancy interval and the Monte Carlo band, with good adherence; keep executing. Decay: a persistent fall across windows and regimes, often after the edge became public or crowded; cut size and revalidate. Regime change: losses only in one regime that has become more common; test a regime filter out of sample.\",\"Edges do decay: published anomalies earned 58% less after publication (McLean and Pontiff, 2016, grade A). But a single bad stretch rarely proves it. Check the interval and the band before calling it.\"],\"example\":\"Expectancy fell from 0.30R to 0.12R over 80 trades with sd 1.5R. Standard error 0.17R, 95% interval −0.21R to +0.45R: it still includes 0.30R, so variance can explain the fall.\",\"related\":[\"anomaly-decay\",\"expectancy-confidence-interval\",\"market-regime\",\"strategy-crowding\"],\"missions\":[\"M02\"],\"lessons\":[\"4.6.6\"]}],\"units\":{}}");
